14 3.2. Optical properties The optical absorption properties of g -C3N4, MoS 2 QDs and MSQD -CN composite s are shown in Fig ure 4 and Figure S 3. For the MoS 2 QDs, it can be observed from Figure S 3 that the peaks at 207 and 230 nm show excitonic features of MoS 2 quantum dots  [36, 37] . The peaks at 380 and 367 nm should be assigned to excitonic absorption bands due to direct band -gap transition at K point with energy split from valence band spin -orbital coupling  [26]. Compared to the absorption edge of bulk MoS 2 and monolayer MoS 2 nanosheets in previous reports  [19, 38] , a large blue -shift was observed due to the strong quantum confinement effect  [39] of MoS 2 QDs as the majority of the particles are within the size of 2-5 nm. The inset image in Figure S 3 exhibits the high stability and good dispersion of MoS 2 QDs in water without noticeable agglomeration for two months. As can be seen from Figure 4a, the steep edge a t around 380 nm should be as signed to the band gap transition of g -C3N4 [40]. For the composite samples, all of their absorption edges present a slight red-shift. In addition, the intr oduction of MoS 2 QDs enhanced both UV and  visible light absorption capacity of the composite samples. It is worth noting that 5 wt% MSQD -CN  outperfo rmed other samples, which might lead to enhanced production of electron -hole pairs and thus a possibly highe r photocatalytic activity. The band -gap energies of all the samples were estimated based on the Kubelka -Munk theory  [9, 40, 41]  following the equation of nh)( = k(h -Eg) (where α,  , k and Eg represent absorption factor, light frequency, proportionality constant and band gap energy , respectively) . Based on the UV-vis diffuse reflectance  spectra  in Figure 4a and Figure S 3, nh)( (n = 2 for direct -gap semiconductors and n=1/2 for indirect -gap semiconductors) versus h   was then plotted in Fig ure 4b and Figure S 3 (inset) with values  shown in Table S1 . The decreased band -gap energy of the com posite with the increasing content of MoS 2-QDs proves that the MoS 2 QDs decoration changed the semiconductor composite properties of g -C3N4 due to mutual ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
