Bifunctional MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 nanohybrids with superior electrochemical energy storage and hydrogen evolution activity Ehtisham Umara, Fozia Shaheena,* , M.Waqas Iqbalb, Atif Mossad Alic, M.A. Sayedc, Attalla F. El-kottd, Eman Kashitae aDepartment of Physics, Government College University Lahore, 54000, Punjab, Pakistan bDepartment of Physics, Riphah International University, Lahore Campus, Pakistan cDepartment of Physics, Faculty of Science, King Khalid University, Abha, 61413, Saudi Arabia dDepartment of Biology, College of Science, King Khalid University, Abha, Saudi Arabia eDepartment of Physics, College of Science, Qassim University, Buraydah, 51452, Qassim, Saudi Arabia HIGHLIGHTS GRAPHICAL ABSTRACT ≡MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 electrodes display su- perb performances in HER and supercapacitors. ≡Real device electrode exhibits Ed of 52.5 Wh/kg and Pd of 837.02 W/kg at 1 A/g. ≡The hybrid type device exhibited capacitance retention of 85 % for 12000 cycles. ≡Ideal behaviors are due to material and channel construction. ARTICLE INFO Keywords: MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4//AC Hybrid supercapacitor Dunn ’s model HER X ray Diffraction Scanning electron microscope MicrostructureABSTRACT Transition metal oxides (TMOs) have garnered considerable attention in the fields of energy storage and sus- tainable energy generation due to their advantageous electrochemical characteristics and diverse array of po- tential uses. Nonetheless, the extensive implementation of these technologies is hindered by inherent limitations, such as electrical conductivity and electrochemical stability, which present considerable obstacles to their commercial viability. A novel hybrid energy storage system has been developed to tackle these challenges, featuring 2D g-C3N4 nanosheet frameworks enhanced with manganese iron oxide (MnFe 2O3), henceforth referred to as MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4. This composite has been meticulously designed for dual-function applications in hybrid- type supercapacitors. The hybrid device exhibits an impressive energy density of 52.5 Wh/kg and a power density of 837.02 W/kg, coupled with exceptional cyclic durability maintained over 12,000 charge-discharge *Corresponding author. E-mail address: foziashaheen@gcu.edu.pk (F. Shaheen). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Journal of Power Sources u{�~zkw! s{yo| kro>! ÐÐÐ1ow�o�t o~1m{y2w{mk�o 2u|{Ð�{� ~ https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpowsour.2025.239005 Received 27 August 2025; Received in revised form 8 November 2025; Accepted 30 November 2025  Journal  of Power  Sources  665 (2026)  239005 Available  online  9 December  2025 0378-7753/©  2025  Elsevier  B.V. All rights  are reserved,  including  those  for text and data mining,  AI training,  and similar  technologies.
