cycles. Moreover, the application of electrochemical kinetic analysis using Dunn ’s model clearly clarifies the respective roles of capacitive (non-faradaic) and diffusion-controlled (faradaic) processes, emphasising the interaction between charge storage mechanisms in relation to scan rate. The fabricated electrodes demonstrated outstanding performance for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) in 1 M KOH, achieving an overpotential of 122 mV at ␀10 mA/cm2, along with a Tafel slope of 123 mV/dec. The electrodes that have been developed demonstrate significant performance, enhancing their potential for future applications in battery-supercapacitor systems and hydrogen evolution reactions. 1.Introduction The demand for advanced energy storage solutions has risen due to the growing popularity of renewable energy sources and the rapid expansion of the portable electronics market [1–3]. Due to their elevated Pd, rapid charge and discharge capabilities, and extended cycling life- span, supercapacitors represent a vital energy storage technology that is indispensable across various application domain [4,5]. Traditional supercapacitors encounter considerable challenges in demanding envi- ronments such as elevated temperatures or harsh mechanical conditions [6,7]. The pursuit of effective and reliable energy storage solutions is limited by two main challenges: the choice of electrode materials and the alteration of particular electrolytes. The significance of these aspects is paramount for supercapacitors, as they have a direct impact on the performance of the device, its range of applications, and the overall energy density [8]. Carbon-based materials provide reliable structures and impressive cycle stability in electrode materials for supercapacitors. Nonetheless, the comparatively low specific capacitance limits their wider utilization [9]. On the other hand, even though transition metal oxide electrodes have a higher specific capacitance, they usually lose their excellent cycling stability (F90 %) after 12,000 cycles [10]. Identifying electrode materials that exhibit outstanding cycle stability and elevated specific capacitance is essential. To minimise ionic diffu- sion limitations and facilitate efficient electrochemical processes in high-rate charge/discharge scenarios, advanced structural designs are implemented to reduce ion diffusion distances. A comprehensive approach that integrates electrode materials exhibiting high electrical conductivity with electrolytes and binders demonstrating superior ionic conductivity is essential for enhancing the energy density of super - capacitors [11]. Hybrid supercapacitors ’ overall performance and effi- ciency are closely linked to the constituent active materials ’ electrochemical activity and microstructural compatibility [12]. Electrochemical HER is an excellent approach for producing green hydrogen with high purity from the electrolysis of water [13]. None - theless, a significant overpotential is necessary to surmount the activa - tion energy barrier for initiating hydrogen evolution, and this has a direct effect on the energy efficiency of the process. Consequently, an effective electrocatalyst becomes indispensable in order to lower this activation barrier. An efficient electrocatalyst, characterized by its exceptional catalytic attributes for the HER, should exhibit a reduced overpotential, rapid electron transfer kinetics (resulting in a low Tafel slope), and exceptional long-term stability. Pt-based materials are well-established electrocatalysts for HERs with very low overpotential and Tafel slope [14,15]. However, the excessive cost of these materials renders them practically nonviable for practical applications. Therefore, designing alternative low-cost materials as efficient electrocatalysts for HERs has drawn huge research attention. Recent studies have identified g-C3N4 as an auspicious support ma- terial for anchoring active TMO species [16–18]. Despite graphene ’s well-known advantages, this increased attention is due to its extraor - dinary properties, such as a high surface area and unique porous ar- chitecture, which in some situations may give advantages surpassing those of graphene [19], such as an ultrathin structure and excellent electrical conductivity [20–22]. Effective anchoring of mixed metal oxides is made possible by g-C3N4 tunable electronic structure, which improves electrochemical stability and sensitivity [23–25]. TMO-anchored g-C3N4 hybrids offer a potential and mostly unexplored method for enhanced supercapacitors and electrocatalysts due to their excellent stability and abundance of active sites [26–28]. Strong inter- facial bonding and heterojunction production are the main causes of the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 nanohybrid ’s enhanced performance. The nitrogen-rich g-C3N4 surface connects to the Mn and Fe centers via metal-N/C-O. Rapid electron and ion migration during electrochemical cycling is made possible by this linkage, which creates an efficient electron-transfer network, greatly lowering charge-transfer resistance and increasing redox kinetics. Additionally, the presence of a built-in electric field at the heterointerface minimises recombination losses and enhances charge separation. The g-C3N4 component functions as a conductive scaffold, enhancing the active surface area, inhibiting MnFe 2O3 aggregation, and maintaining structural integrity. Conse - quently, in comparison to its individual components, the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 hybrid demonstrates enhanced specific capacitance, decreased charge-transfer resistance Rct, and superior cycle durability [29,30]. A multitude of studies has explored the combination of Fe2O3 with various carbonaceous materials, such as rGO, g-C3N4, CNTs, and porous carbon, aiming to improve the energy density and cycling stability of Fe2O3-based supercapacitors [31–33]. Zhang et al. used a straightfor - ward hydrothermal process to create a rGO/Fe 2O3 composite that was used as an active ingredient in screen-printed functional inks, providing a specific capacitance of 703.91 F/g at 1 A/g [34]. Wei et al. demon - strated their potential as effective anode materials in asymmetric supercapacitors by creating hollow acanthosphere-like Fe2O3@C struc- tures (Fe2O3@C-HASs) on carbon fabric using a solvothermal technique and self-sacrificial templating [35]. Amirabad et al. added Zn-doped Fe2O3 to MoS 2@rGO nanosheets to improve the electrochemical per- formance of binder-free electrodes [36]. Zhong et al. created CeO 2–Fe2O3 nanoparticles supported on a porous carbon framework (CeO 2–Fe2O3/C), which produced an amazing specific capacitance of 803 F/g at 1 mV/s [37]. All of these research demonstrate the potential of hybrid systems based on Fe2O3 and carbon for energy storage appli - cations. However, there are yet few thorough studies on Fe2O3/carbon composite electrodes. Thus, the synthesis of Cu–Fe2O3/g–C3N4 com- posites and their assessment as possible electrode materials for high-performance supercapacitors are the main objectives of this work. Higher currents and lower overpotential are the results of using nanomaterials with larger surface areas, which greatly increase electron transfer rates. Improved electrocatalytic activities are a result of research into creating improved electrodes with increased stability and sensitivity. In order to create high-performance supercapattery elec- trodes, a scalable method for creating g-C3N4 nanosheets combined with MnFe 2O3 nanorods is provided. Superior electrochemical performance is achieved by the hybrid ’s strong interfacial coupling, which increases active site density and speeds up electron transmission. The materials make it possible to produce supercapatteries with remarkable Ed and Pd, making them competitive candidates for state-of-the-art electronic devices.E. Umar et al.                                                                                                                                    