2.Experimental section 2.1. Chemicals The supporting file includes a details about the chemicals (S1). 2.2. One-step syntheses of MnFe 2O3 nanorods MnFe 2O3 nanorods were prepared via a one-step hydrothermal approach [38]. Initially, 2.3 g of FeCl 3 was dissolved in 60 mL of deionized (DI) water and stirred continuously for 30 min. Subsequently, 1.2 g of Mn(NO 3)2⋅H2O was added to the solution, followed by 30 min of stirring to ensure complete mixing. NaOH or HCl served as precipitating agents to maintain the stability of the solution ’s pH. The solution un- derwent autoclaving in a 100 mL muffle furnace for a duration of 12 h at a temperature of 160 •C. Upon reaching room temperature, the pre- cipitate underwent a meticulous cleaning process to remove contami - nants, utilising ethanol and deionized water. Finally, the product underwent a drying process for 10 h at a temperature of 80 •C in an oven. 2.3. Syntheses of g-C3N4 nanosheets The synthesis of g-C3N4 was achieved through the direct thermal polymerisation of melamine. 10 g of melamine were subjected to calcination in an alumina-covered crucible at a temperature of 550 •C for a duration of 5 h within a muffle furnace to synthesise bulk g-C3N4, which was subsequently ground into a fine powder. The powder was dissolved in 50 ml of DI water, subjected to ultrasonication for 1 h, and subsequently allowed to settle for 6 h to enable the unexfoliated material to settle. For future applications, the supernatant containing exfoliated g-C3N4 nanosheets was subjected to drying at 120 •C [39]. 2.4. One-step synthesis of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite Initially, g-C3N4 was exfoliated in DI water through sonication for 2 h, to increase the active surface area and promoting better interaction with TMOs. Following this, the exfoliated g-C3N4 was magnetically stirred to ensure uniform dispersion. A 1:1 ratio of MnFe 2O3 was introduced into the g-C3N4 suspension and vigorously stirred for 2 h to ensure thorough mixing [29,40]. The solution was subsequently trans - ferred into an autoclave (100 mL) and subjected to 170 •C for 12 h. After synthesis, the precipitates were washed with DI water, dried at 120 •C, and the process is shown in Scheme 1. 2.5. Fabrication of the MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4 and MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 electrodes Nickel foam (NF) substrates (1 ×1 cm2) were treated with 3 M dilute HCl for 10 min to remove impurities. Following the acid treatment, the NF was thoroughly rinsed with DI water to remove residual acid and ensure surface cleanliness. The cleaned substrates were then dried in a conventional oven at 65 •C. Slurries of MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4, and MnFe 2O3/ g-C3N4 composite materials were prepared for electrode fabrication. NF electrodes were designed with 2 mg of each active material: MnFe 2O3, g- C3N4, and MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 as positive electrode. The slurry was pre- pared with 80 wt% active material, 10 wt% acetylene black, and 5 wt% PVDF in 5 wt% NMP, followed by magnetic stirring for 5 h to ensure uniform dispersion. The prepared slurry was drop onto the pretreated NF surface and dried at 65 •C for 5 h. This same protocol was used for the activated carbon (AC) material. Scheme 1.Synthesis of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite material through hydrothermal method.E. Umar et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Journal  of Power  Sources  665 (2026)  239005 3
