2.6. Characterizations Advanced characterization techniques (XRD, FTIR, SEM, BET, XPS, and EIS) were employed to analyze the active materials. Electrochemical performance was evaluated in 1 M KOH using two- and three-electrode configurations for hybrid-type supercapacitors, and a two-electrode setup for real device testing. In the three-electrode system, the work - ing, counter (Pt wire), and reference (Hg/HgO) electrodes were used. CV, GCD, and EIS measurements were performed, with CV recorded at 5–80 mV/s and GCD at 1–2 A/g to assess charge storage behavior. The voltage window for CV and GCD in the three-electrode cell was opti- mized between 0 and 0.7 V. For energy storage evaluations in the two- electrode configuration, a voltage range of 0–1.7 V was used. The specific capacity (Qs) of the designed electrodes were evaluated through equations (1) and (2)for three and two-electrode configuration systems for the CV and GCD analysis, respectively. Qs⨄ IdV mv(1) QsI×Δt m(2) In equation (1), the integral term denotes the CV curve area, where m and v are the active mass and scan rate, respectively. In equation (2), Δt is the discharge time, and I/m the current density. Eqquations (3) and (4) are used to determine the Ed and Pd. EdQs×ΔV 2×3B6(3) PdEd×3600 Δt(4) Fig. 1.(a) XRD pattern, (b) FTIR spectra of MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4 and MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite. SEM images of (c-c′) MnFe 2O3, and (d-d′) MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4, (e) EDS analysis and (f) elemental mapping of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite.E. Umar et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Journal  of Power  Sources  665 (2026)  239005 4
