The capacitive-diffusion-controlled contributions of the electrodes were assessed using Dunn ’s model (equation (5)). ivk1vk2v1E2(5) The k1v and k2v1/2 shows the capacitive and diffusive contribution, respectively, while equation (6)converts the experimental potential to RHE. ERHE EHg/HgO 0.118 0.059 ×pH                                           (6) Tafel slope values acquired from LSV curves to determining the re- action kinetics and efficiency of electrocatalyst. Lower Tafel slope values indicates faster reaction rates and higher electrocatalyst efficacy (equation (7)). η a b log j                                                                               (7) Where, η overpotential, j current density, a constant, and b  Tafel slope. 3.Results and discussions The crystalline structures of MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4, and MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite were investigated through the X-ray diffraction method (Fig. 1a). The diffraction patterns of MnFe 2O3 nanorods exhibit sharp, intense peaks corresponding to the rhombohedral phase of MnFe 2O3 [ICDD: 04-011-9587] [38]. The XRD pattern of g-C3N4 shows peaks at 14.05•and 26.03•for the (100) and (002) planes (JCPDS No. 01-087-1526), while the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite displays all char- acteristic peaks of both components, confirming its formation [41]. A significant shift of the diffraction peaks towards higher angles was noted following the incorporation of g-C3N4 into the MnFe 2O3 crystal struc- ture. The observed shift is a result of the interfacial strain and slight lattice mismatch caused by the incorporation of g-C3N4. The interaction at the interface of MnFe 2O3 and g-C3N4 promotes charge transfer, resulting in a shift in electron density and alterations in the bonding environment of the MnFe 2O3 lattice. As a result, this process impacts the effective ionic radii and modifies the equilibrium lattice parameters [42]. The FTIR analysis of MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4, and the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite is illustrated in Fig. 1b, emphasising the significant functional groups. A subtle intensity peak observed at 1624 cm␀1 is linked to the asymmetric CO stretching vibration. The broad band at 3323 cm␀1 corresponds to the O␀H group, whereas the sharp peak at 658 cm␀1 indicates the presence of the Fe␀O bond in MnFe 2O3 [38]. The FTIR spectrum of g-C3N4 displays aromatic C–N heterocycles, featuring a broad band at 2890 –3406 cm␀1, which is associated with –NH2 or  N–H groups connected to sp2-hybridized carbon and amines at defect sites. The peaks at approximately 1316 cm␀1, 1394 cm␀1, 1549 cm␀1, and 1637 cm␀1 represent the stretching vibration modes of -CN and -C-N heterocycles. The peak at ~801 cm␀1 is the triazine ring breathing mode, while the ~883 cm␀1 band is from cross-linked heptazine deformation, coinciding with g-C3N4 characteristics [43,44]. The MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite showed low-intensity peaks due to the incorporation of MnFe 2O3 into a g-C3N4 [45,46]. The obtained results validate the successful fabrication of the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite. The surface morphology of MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4, and the MnFe 2O3/g- C3N4 were analyzed using SEM, EDS, and elemental mapping. The SEM images clearly show that the MnFe 2O3 sample exhibits a rod-like and nano-needle structure (Fig. 1c–c′), with the crystallinity of the nano- structures being relatively uniform, though aggregated. The g-C3N4 material displays a nanosheet/flake-like morphology (see our pervisous publish data [41]. The formation of the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite reveals a mixed morphology consisting of nanorods/nanosheets and nanoparticles (Fig. 1d–d′). The TEM image of the composite material confirms a mixed morphology consisting of nanoparticle-like and nanosheet-like structures, indicating that MnFe 2O3 nanoparticles are uniformly deposited on the surface of the g-C3N4 nanosheets (Fig. S1). The EDS analysis demonstrates the distribution of Mn, Fe, O, and C el- ements within the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 (Fig. 1e). These results are consis - tent with the elemental mapping analysis shown in Fig. 1f, further confirming the even distribution of elements across the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 nanocomposite. However, elements such as Na, Cl, and Au detected in the EDX analysis may be attributed to the synthesis precursors and the phytomolecules or coating materials used during sample preparation. High-resolution XPS spectra were employed to examine the oxida - tion states of the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 nanocomposite (Fig. 2a–e). The Mn 2p XPS spectrum (Fig. 2a) exhibits four distinct peaks at 641.44, 642.82, 652.86, and 654.28 eV, which correspond to Mn3(2p3/2), Mn4(2p3/ 2), Mn3(2p1/2), and Mn4(2p1/2), respectively [47]. The high-resolution XPS spectra of MnFe 2O3 (Fig. S2a) reveal that, upon the incorporation of g-C3N4, the Mn 2p1/2 peak exhibits a positive shift of 0.633 eV, while the Mn 2p3/2 peak shifts by 0.786 eV toward higher binding energies. This shift indicates charge transfer from MnFe 2O3 to the more electronegative g-C3N4 across the heterointerface. As shown in Fig. 2b, the Fe 2p spectrum reveals a pair of peaks at 710.44 eV and 712.38 eV for Fe3 2p3/2 and Fe2 2p3/2 and at 723.92 eV and 726.09 eV for Fe3 2p1/2 and Fe2 2p1/2 respectively [48]. In the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite (Fig. S2b), the Fe 2p1/2 peak shows a positive shift of 0.9709 eV, whereas the Fe 2p3/2 peak shifts by 0.467 eV toward lower binding energy. These variations indicate the occurrence of electronic charge transfer from MnFe 2O3 to the g-C3N4 matrix. The 13.72 eV spin–orbit separation between Fe 2p3/2 and Fe 2p1/2 confirms the presence of Fe2O3 [49]. The peaks at 718.95 and 732.6 eV in the high-resolution Fe 2p spectra are attributed to the satellite features of the Fe2O3 electronic structure, further confirming the formation of Fe2O3 [49–51]. As shown in Fig. 2c, the O 1s peak is resolved into two distinct peaks at 530.3 eV, and 531.9 eV, which correspond to the binding energies of peaks to C-O or O-H and lattice oxygen in the MnFe 2O3 composite [52]. The O 1s spetra shows that no prominent peak shifting were observed in the comparision with the MnFe 2O3 prisitine sample (Fig. S2c). The high-resolution C 1s spectrum (Fig. 2d) reveals three distinct peaks attributed to CC, C–C, and C–N bonding environments, which agrees with previous reports [53,54]. Meanwhile, the N 1s spectrum (Fig. 2e) further verifies the presence of three nitrogen species in the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite. The peaks located at 398.758, 399.563, and 400.927 eV correspond to sp2-hybridized nitrogen (N1), tertiary nitrogen (N2), and amino functional groups (N3), respectively [55]. BET isotherms were recorded at 77 K to evaluate the surface char- acteristics of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite (Fig. S3a–b). Extensive infor- mation regarding the surface area of g-C3N4 is available in our prior research [41]. The MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 nanocomposite exhibited a specific surface area of 67.389 m2/g, signifying its mesoporous characteristics and facilitating enhanced electrochemical performance. The MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite exhibits a pore volume of 0.027 cm3/g and a pore diameter of 4.87 nm. The increased surface area results in a greater number of electroactive sites, facilitating accelerated redox processes. These results underscore the promise of customised com- posites for efficient charge storage and electrocatalytic applications. 4.Electrochemical performance 4.1. Three-electrode configuration for energy-storage devices The analysis of EIS offers valuable insights into interfacial charac - teristics, including charge transfer dynamics and mass transport behaviour, which reveal information about the intrinsic electrochemical capabilities of the materials. The EIS spectra, along with the corre - sponding Nyquist equivalent circuit (inset), are presented for the modified electrodes (Fig. S4a–c). The MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite ex- hibits a significantly reduced charge-transfer resistance (Rct) of 1.03 Ω, indicating enhanced interfacial charge transfer capabilities in E. Umar et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Journal  of Power  Sources  665 (2026)  239005 5
