comparison to the separate MnFe 2O3 and g-C3N4 electrodes. Previous research indicates that adding MnFe 2O3 (Rct 3.48 Ω) and g-C3N4 (Rct 1.67 Ω) to the composite framework improves its electrical conduc - tivity and reduces Rct. The improved conductivity reduces overall resistance, facilitating enhanced electrochemical stability. Moreover, the combination of MnFe 2O3 and g-C3N4 features a significant quantity of electroactive sites, facilitating enhanced electrolyte ion adsorption and optimising charge transfer kinetics. The enhanced electrochemical activity of the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite can be explained by the synergistic effects resulting from improved electrical conductivity, strong interfacial contacts, and a larger surface area. The integration of these features facilitates efficient electron transport at the MnFe 2O3/g- C3N4 electrode interface, leading to a notable enhancement in its elec- trochemical performance. The electrochemical behaviour of all fabricated electrodes was sys- tematically investigated using CV, GCD, and EIS in a three-electrode system. CV analysis was conducted within the 0–0.7 V to elucidate the charge storage mechanism-whether faradaic or non-faradaic of Fig. 2.High resolution XPS spectra of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 of (a) Mn 2p spectra, (b) Fe 2p, (c) O 1s, (d) C 1s and (e) N 1s spectra. Fig. 3.CV curves with 3–70 mV/s scan rate of (a) MnFe 2O3, (b) g-C3N4 (c) MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 composite and (d) Comparison of CV curves at 3 mV/s scan rate.E. Umar et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Journal  of Power  Sources  665 (2026)  239005 6
