MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4, and MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 electrodes. The CV profiles in Fig. 3a–c exhibit prominent redox peaks, indicating a battery-type behaviour. Additionally, Fig. 3d presents a comparative CV analysis of the electrodes at 3 mV/s. The Qs of the prepared electrodes were calculated using equation (1). At 3 mV/s, the MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4, and MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 electrodes exhibited specific capacities of 634, 593, and 936C/g, respectively. As the scan rate increased, a declining trend in Qs was observed, attributed to the limited time available for electrolyte ions to interact with the electrode surface effectively. The variation in Qs with scan rate for the respective electrodes is illustrated in Fig. S5a. GCD measurements were carried out across a range of current den- sities within an operational at 0–0.7 V, as illustrated in Fig. 4a–c, further, the electrochemical activity of the fabricated electrodes was evaluated. The presence of distinct charge/discharge plateaus in the curves con- firms the battery-type behavior of the synthesized electrodes (MnFe 2O3, g-C3N4, and MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4), attributed to the redox-active nature of the materials. Additionally, the symmetric nature of the GCD profiles with increasing current density indicates excellent electrochemical reversibility and high rate capability. A comparative analysis at 1 A/g is shown in Fig. 4d, where Qs were calculated using equation (2). Among the electrodes, MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 delivered the highest Qs of 939C/g at 1 A/g, outperforming MnFe 2O3 (720C/g) and g-C3N4 (615C/g). Fig. S5b presents a comparative evaluation at varying current densities, where a decline in Qs was observed with increasing current density. This reduction is attributed to the limited diffusion time for electrolyte ions to effectively interact with the electrode surface at higher current rates. 4.2. Hybrid energy-storage device (MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4//AC) Through the three-electrode electrochemical analysis, MnFe 2O3/g- C3N4 exhibited exceptional performance and was subsequently chosen for use in energy storage. An asymmetric hybrid device was fabricated utilising MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 as the positive electrode and AC as the negative electrode, owing to its exceptional electrochemical behaviour. Fig. 5a illustrates a schematic representation of the device configuration, while Fig. 5b presents a comparison of the cyclic voltammetry of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 with activated carbon at a scan rate of 3 mV/s. In order to conduct a thorough analysis of the electrochemical performance of the fabricated MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4//AC hybrid device, measurements were performed using a two-electrode configuration devoid of a reference electrode. The selection of the operating voltage window was deter - mined by the specific potential ranges of the electrodes: 0–0.7 V for MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 (positive electrode) and 0 to ␀1.0 V for AC (negative electrode). Consequently, a combined cell voltage range of 0–1.7 V was employed for CV analysis across various potential windows. The CV profiles, recorded at 5–80 mV/s, are presented in Fig. 5c. The device exhibits distinct redox peaks and a quasi-rectangular CV shape, indica - tive of combined faradaic and electrical double-layer capacitance behaviour. Additionally, the GCD profiles further corroborate the asymmetric behaviour of the fabricated hybrid device (Fig. 5d). Slow ion diffusion and inadequate electrolyte penetration into the inner active sites may be the cause of the rate capability decline at higher current densities. These factors restrict the complete utilization of the electro - active material during rapid charge –discharge cycles. The device ’s Qs were evaluated and presented in Fig. 5e and f. The CV and GCD analyses show that the device achieved maximum Qs of 175.5C/g, as determined by the GCD curves. The b-values (0.49 –0.68) derived from log(scan rate) vs. log(peak current) analysis confirm the hybrid supercapattery nature of the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4//AC device (Fig. 6a). It achieved 52.5 Wh/kg at 837.02 W/kg (1 A/g), with comparative performance illustrated in the Ragone plot (Fig. 6b) and summarized in Table 1. The outstanding re- sults achieved by this composite indicate its promise as a highly efficient active electrode material for energy storage applications. Fig. 6c illus- trates the device ’s remarkable long-term performance, maintaining 84 % of its initial capacitance and attaining a coulombic efficiency of 90 % after 12,000 galvanostatic charge-discharge cycles. The results under - score the electrochemical stability and durability of the constructed MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4//AC symmetric hybrid supercapacitor. To further support the hybrid nature of the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4//AC device, Dunn ’s model was used to calculate the material ’s capacitive and diffusive contributions, as shown in equation (5). The results of this Fig. 4.GCD analysis of (a) MnFe 2O3, (b) g-C3N4 (c) MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 with various current density and (d) comparison of GCD curves at 1 A/g.E. Umar et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Journal  of Power  Sources  665 (2026)  239005 7
