composite. CV curves (Fig. 7a–f) at scan rates of 5–80 mV/s reveal the composite ’s combined capacitive and diffusion-controlled behavior. Notably, at all scan rates, the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4//AC composite exhibits a dominant capacitive contribution, indicating that the charge storage is primarily governed by a surface-controlled mechanism, which is ad- vantageous for high-rate electrochemical performance. 5.Electrochemical performance of HER Inspired by the abundance of electrocatalytic active sites confirmed by supercapacitor and physicochemical results, the prepared electrodes were analyzed as working electrodes for the HER in 1 M KOH alkaline electrode, demonstrating stability across a wide pH range at 10 mV/s. The polarization curves of all prepared samples are tested in 1 M KOH as shown in Fig. 8a. The MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 catalyst has an overpotential of 122 mV, which is lower than those of MnFe 2O3 (389 mV), and g-C3N4 (506 mV) whereas Pt/C (21.7 mV, as reference HER electrpde) (Fig. 8b). As per the experimental result, the MnFe 2O3 composite material has low over potential compared to g-C3N4 materials due to their heterojunction formed by the combination of hybride material, which played a role in controlling the electronic environment of the active sites. This enhances both the adsorption and activation energies of the intermediates. In addition, the accumulation of MnFe 2O3 within the lamellar g-C3N4 structure can be attributed to partial blockage of its channels, resulting in enhanced loading on the composite surface. Consequently, the active sites become less exposed, leading to decreased activity (overpotential) of the catalyst. The Tafel plot is a basic method for testing HER catalysts. It is often used to study the rate-limiting stages and kinetics of the HER process, giving direct information about how well the catalyst works; smaller Tafel slope values meant that the reaction kinetics were faster. The Tafel slopes were determined and calculated using a polarization curve ac- cording to equation (7). As displayed in Fig. 8c, the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 shows that the smaller Tafel slope of 123 mV/dec compared to MnFe 2O3 (289 mV/dec), and g-C3N4 (328 mV/dec). Thus, the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 experimentally proved to have faster reaction kinetics for the electro - catalytic process. According to the HER catalytic mechanism in KOH with an alkaline solution, the Tafel slope of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 was included at 123 mV/dec, associated with the Volmer␀Heyrovsky mechanism, and it is determined that the Volmer step played a crucial part in the catalytic process. Equations (8)–(10) demonstrate the three stages of HER under KOH with alkaline conditions. By analyzing the Tafel slope value shown in Fig. 8c, we can conclude that the hydrogen evolution process of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 follows the Volmer␀Heyrovsky mechanism [67,68]. H2Oe␀M→MH∗OH␀Volmer step (8) H2OMH∗e␀→H2MOH␀Heyrovsky step (9) MH∗MH∗→H22MTafel step (10) Fig. 8d shows the LSV curve of MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 before and after the stability performance. Virtually, no significant change was observed in the after-stability test, and 123.3 mV overpotential was little bit changes were obsedved. Furthermore, the MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 electrode demon - strates superior HER performance when compared with the previous Table 1 Comparison of this work ’s energy-storage performance with reported studies. Materials Energy density (Wh/ kg)Power density (W/ kg)References MnO 2/MBC 70.2 700 [56] In2O3/Fe2O3 58.29 474 [57] MnO 2@PCs-0.0075 58.01 500 [58] rGO/Fe 2O3-x 114 140 [59] CeO 2@γ-Fe2O3// rGO36.8 700 [60] POR-COF@g-C 3N4- 3024.4 1152 [61] NiS@g-CN 49.54 309 [62] CoMn-Fe/NF 87.80 245 [63] Fe2O3-OV@rGO|| rGO115.6 140 [64] MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4 52.5 837.02 This work Fig. 7.(a–f) CV curves depicting capacitive and diffusion-controlled charge contributions for MnFe 2O3/g-C 3N4//AC at different scan rates.E. Umar et al.                                                                                     