Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Journal of Alloys and Compounds journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/jalcom Research Article Solvothermal temper ature -contr ol of activ e 1T phase in carbon cloth- support ed MoS 2 and Pt-Ni clust er electr odeposition for hydrog en evolution reaction Hien Duy Mai a,b,1, Sangmin Jeong a,c,1, Gi-Nam Bae a, Beom won Seo a, Poong Mo Park a, Ki-Joon Jeon a,d,⁎ a Department of Environmental Engineering, Inha Univ ersity , 100 Inha-ro, Nam-gu, Incheon 22212, the Republic of Korea b NTT Hi-Tech Institute, Nguy en Tat Thanh University , Ho Chi Minh City, Viet Nam c Department of Chemistry , Univ ersity of Massachusetts, Lowell, Lowell, MA 01854, USA d Progr am in Environmental and Polymer Engineering, Inha Univ ersity , Incheon 22212, the Republic of Korea article info Article history : Received 2 December 2022 Received in revised form 4 January 2023 Accep ted 24 January 2023 Available online 26 January 2023 Keywords: MoS 2 Solv othermal approach 1 T phase control 1 T/2 H phase quantification Pt-Ni electrodeposition Hydroge n evolution reactionabstract Establishing synthetic guidance that enables phase optimization with minimized by-products is an im- portant task in modifying atomic arrang ements and electronic structures of MoS 2 through a solvothermal approach. Herein, a synthetic strategy to manipulate the 1 T and 2 H phases within MoS 2 nanosheets on a support carbon cloth (MoS 2/CC) is successfully demonstrat ed via a facile and efficient tempera ture-con - trolled solvothermal method. The 1 T and 2 H in MoS 2/CC are well-char acterized and quantified using Raman and XPS, revealing that MoS 2/CC synthesized at 220 ºC (denot ed as MoS 2/CC(220)) has the highest 1 T phase content (∼ 60 %). The distinctiv e atomic arrang ement of 1 T and 2 H are distinguished by HR-TEM. We also successfully electrodeposit ed Pt-Ni nanoclusters on the 1 T-rich MoS 2 (Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC) , which accounts for a substantially enhanced HER performance. This study highlights the capability of phase manipulation in two-dimensional MoS 2 nanosheets via a one-pot solvothermal approach, which is essential for application-specified phase engineering. © 2023 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserv ed. 1. Introduction Molyb denum disulfide (MoS 2), an emerging two-dimensional (2D) transition metal dichalcogenides, has been a subject of inter est because of the potential applications in various fields (e.g., electron ic [1,2]and optoelectronic devices, [3]gas sensors, [4–6] and electro - cataly sis) [7–1 0]. Structurall y, with the different displacements of the S atoms, MoS 2 exhibits differen t polymorphs, i.e., trigonal pris- matic coordination (2 H) and octahedral coordinat ion (1 T) phases. Of importance, the 1 T with high electron/ion tran sport and con- ductivity , as well as abundant activ e sites is targeted because it is regar ded as a more activ e phase towards electrochemical hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) than the 2 H phase. [11,12]In addition, the enhanced conductivity of 1 T enables the facile introduction of catalytic activ e metal centers on MoS 2 via electrodep osition, thus forming hybrid structures to achieve satisfactory electrochemical performance. How ever , since the thermodynamically stable phase of MoS 2 is 2 H, the preparation of 1 T remains a challenging task. [13]Although sever al post-treatm ent techniq ues (e.g., alkali metal-assiste d inter - calation/e xfoliation, [14]plasmonic hot electron injection, [15] electron-be am irradiation, [16]and substitutional doping [17]) have been devo ted to impro ving the content of metal-like 1 T phase in MoS 2, larger scale synthesis is limited by the comple xity and ha- zardou s materials used in these techniq ues. Therefor e, bottom-up one-po t approaches, such as hydro/sol vothermal methods are con- sidered a more effectiv e straightforw ard strat egy to modify the atomic arrang ements and electron ic structur es of MoS 2. Despite the technical simplicity of the solvothermal approach, there is a major concern in the synthesis of 1 T-MoS 2 because 1 T phase is not only competed with 2 H, but with undesirable Mo xOy (e.g., MoO 2 and MoO 3) as by-products. In this regar d, it is essential to establish synthetic guidance enabling the optimization of 1 T/2 H phase in Journal of Alloys and Compounds 942 (2023) 169035 https ://doi.org/1 0.1016/j.jallcom.2023. 169035 0925-8388/© 2023 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved. ]]]] ]]]]]] ⁎ Corresponding author at: Department of Envir onmental Engineering, Inha University , 100 Inha-ro, Nam-gu, Incheon 22212, the Republic of Korea. E-mail address: kjjeon@inha.ac.kr (K.-J. Jeon). 1 Contributed equally to this work.
