MoS 2 with a lessened quantity of MoO x. To the best of our know l- edge, although solvothermal conditions, such as selection of Mo/S precursors, [18]solvents, [19]and templating agen ts, [20] have been investigated, few reports addre ss the temp eratur e-regulat ed synth - esis, relev ant quantification of 1 T and 2 H, as well as and the competitiv e formation between MoS 2 and Mo xOy via solvothermal approaches. In this study , we demonstrat ed a convenient and efficient sol- vothermal synthetic guidance for optimizing and quantifying the 1 T and 2 H phase within MoS 2 as well as the mechanistic insight into the formation of Mo xOy. The formation of 1 T/2 H-MoS 2 and Mo xOy was well-cha racterized and quantified using Raman and X-ray phot oelectron spectroscop y (XPS). High-resolution tran smission electron microsc opy (HR-TEM) unambiguously revealed the atomic arrang ement and quantification of 1 T-phase fract ion in MoS 2. The optimal 1 T-rich MoS 2/CC was successfully implemented as a sui- table templat e for the electrodeposition of Pt-Ni nanoclusters to form Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC. The 1 T-enhancing conductivity and HER performance of the heterostructur es with differ ent fract ions of 1 T- phase were examined. 2. Material and methods 2.1. Materials Carbon cloth (Kynol, thickness: 0.55 mm, ACC-5092-20) was used as a substrat e. Ammonium sulfate ((NH 4)2SO4, 99.5 %, Sigma- Aldrich), ammonium molybdat e tetrahydrat e ((NH 4)6Mo 7O244 H2O, Sigma-Aldrich), thiourea (NH 2CSNH 2, 99 %, Sigma-Aldrich), platinum (II) chloride (PtCl 2, 98 %, Sigma-Aldrich), nickel(II ) chloride hexahy- drat e (NiCl 20.6 H2O, 98 %, Sigma-Aldrich), boric acid (H3BO3, 99 %, Sigma-Aldrich) were used without further purification. The deionized water was purified by a secondary distillation system for using the experiments. 2.2. Synthesis of MoS 2/CC with controllable 1 T and 2 H phase The carbon cloth (CC) was activ ated by the electrochemistry proce ss in an aqueous solution of ammonium sulfate (0.1 M, 30 mL) at 10 VAg/AgCl for 15 min prior to the growth of MoS 2. The activ ated CC was submerged in an aqueous solution containing (NH 4)6Mo 7O244 H2O (0.1 g) and NH 2CSNH 2 (0.12 g). The reaction was carried out in a Teflon-lined stainless autocla ve at various tem- perat ures (180 ℃, 200 ℃, 220 ℃, 250 ℃, and 280 ℃) for 15 hr. The obtained samples are denot ed as MoS 2/CC( T), where T is the reaction temp eratur e. 2.3. Electrodeposition of Pt-Ni clusters on MoS 2/CC (Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC) The electrodeposition of Pt-Ni nanoclusters on MoS 2/CC was conduct ed based on our report ed protocol. [21]Specifically , Pt-Ni@ MoS 2/CC was obtained by electrodep ositing MoS 2/CC(220) (1 × 2 cm−2) in a 20 mL aqueous solution containing PtCl 2 (0.56 mmol), NiCl 2 (0.56 mmol), and boric acid. The electrodep ositing cell was composed of a MoS 2/CC(220) and a platinum mesh as the cathode and anode, respectiv ely. The electrodeposition of Pt-Ni nanoclusters was carried out at a constant current of − 10 mA cm−2 for 900 s 2.4. Material characterization The morphologies of the prepar ed materials were examined using field emission scanning electron microsc ope (FE-SEM; Hitachi S-430 0, USA) and field emission tran smission electron microscope (FE-TEM; JEM-2 100 F, Japan). Selected area electron diffraction (SAE D) was collected using field emission tran smission electron Scheme 1. Scheme illustrates the synthesis of MoS 2/CC with temper ature-contr olled composition and phase in MoS 2 via a solvothermal approach (Step 1) and the electro - deposition of Pt-Ni nanoclusters on the optimal MoS 2/CC (Step 2).H.D. Mai, S. Jeong, G.-N. Bae et al. Journal of Alloys and Compounds 942 (2023) 169035 2
