microscope (FE-TEM; JEM-2 100 F, Japan). XPS measurements were performed using a K-Alpha spectromet er (Thermo Fisher Scientific, K-Alpha™, USA) with monochr omated Al Kα radiation as the X-ray source for excitation, and the spectra were subjected to charge correction using the C 1 s peak at 248.8 eV. Raman spectroscop y was obtained using a Raman spectromet er (Raman-touch, Nanopho ton, Japan) equipped with a 532 nm solid-state laser sourc e. 2.5. Electro chemical measurements All electrochemical measurements were performed using an electrochemical Ivium potentiostat (Verte x, Ivium Technologies, Nether lands). The HER activity of the cataly sts was examined using a conv entional three-electr ode setup in a 0.5 M H2SO4 as an electro - lyte. A Pt mesh and calomel electrode (Hg/Hg 2Cl2) were employ ed as counter and refer ence electrodes, respectiv ely. Potentials are con- verted to the reversible hydrog en electrode (RHE) based on the Nernst equation (Eq. 1). = + + E E E pH 0.059 RHEHgHgClo HgHgCl2222// (1) where ERHE is the calculated potential vs. RHE, E HgHgClo /22 is the standard potential at 25 °C (0.24 1 V), and EHgHgCl /22 is the experimental potential against the reference electrode Hg/Hg 2Cl2. Linear sweep voltamm etry (LSV) curves were obtained in N2-satu - rated 0.5 M H2SO4 with a scan rate of 5 mV/s. as the potential rang e of between 0 and 0.78 VRHE. The resistance measurement s of all samples were conducted within an enclosed chamber system using a Keithley 4200 at an applied potential from − 6 V to 6 V. 3. Results and discussion The solvothermal synthesis of MoS 2 nanosheets with controllable ratios of 1 T/2 H on activ ated carbon cloth (CC) is schematically il- lustrat ed in Scheme 1. Firstly , hydrophobic commercial CC was electroche mically activ ated by polarizing at 10 V for surface oxida - tion to facilitate the homogeneous coating of MoS 2. The growth of MoS 2 nanosheets on CC was subseq uently performed in an aqueous solution containing (NH 4)6Mo 7O24 and CS(NH 2)2 used as Mo and S prec ursors at different temper atures ranging from 180 to 280 ºC (Step 1 in Scheme 1). For convenience, the samples are denot ed as MoS 2/CC( T) (wher e T is the reaction temper ature). Fig. 1a show s the crystallogr aphic structur e of 1 T and 2 H in MoS 2. Specifically , the coordinat ion geometries of [MoS 6] polyhedr al and stacking se- quences (S-Mo-S) are distinct for 2 H and 1 T in the basal plane and Fig. 1. (a) Schematic models showing the coordination geometry of MoS 6 and the basal and cross-sectional view s of MoS 2 with the 2 H- (left) and 1 T- (right) phases; (b) Raman patterns of MoS 2/CC at different temper atures; (c) Plot of O1/A1 g intensity ratio against reaction temper atures; XPS spectra of (d) Mo 3d and (e) S 2p of MoS 2/CC at different temper atures; (f) Composition of 1 T, 2 H, and Mo xOy of MoS 2/CC at different temper atures; (g) Composition distribution of 1 T and 2 H phase within MoS 2, along with the formation of by-product Mo xOy versu s reaction temper atures.H.D. Mai, S. Jeong, G.-N. Bae et al. Journal of Alloys and Compounds 942 (2023) 169035 3
