cross-sectional view s. These uniq ue structural configurations ac- count for differen t Raman patterns, allowing the tracking of 1 T and 2 H phases in the synthesized MoS 2. Figs. 1b and S1show s the Raman patterns of MoS 2/CC obtained at different temp eratur es. At 220 ºC ≤ T ≤ 250 ºC, the obtained MoS 2 exhibit a mixture of 2 H and 1 T phase. This is evidenced by char acteristic Raman peaks assigned to 2 H (in-plane E1 2 g ∼ 382 cm−1 and out-of-plane A1 g ∼ 409.5 cm-1) [5]and 1 T (J1 ∼ 147 cm−1, J2 ∼ 196 cm−1, and J3 ∼ 330 cm−1). [22] Additional Raman peaks observed aroun d 819 and 993 cm−1 are corresponding to the Mo-O-Mo and O]Mo vibrat ions (denot ed as O1 and O2, respecti vely), [23] inferring that Mo xOy is concurrentl y formed as by-products. To quantify the amount of Mo xOy, the use of absolute Raman intensity of O1 peak is not recommended. This is mainly because the absolut e value is inevitably affected by sub- strat es, sample quality , or laser polarization, which may lead to certain discrepancie s. Inste ad, the O1/A1 g intensity ratio is selecte d as a quantitativ e indicator to assess the ratio of Mo xOy/MoS 2 ratio as O1 and A1 g represents Mo xOy and MoS 2, respecti vely, thus enabling the compositional optimization in MoS 2/CC (Fig. 1c). The O1/A1 g values for MoS 2/CC(1 80) and MoS 2/CC(20 0) are 8 and 11, respec - tively. At higher temper atures (T ≥ 220 °C), the O1/A1 g values drop significantly , implying that the formation of Mo xOy was significantly suppressed presumably because of the higher reacti vity of S2- at higher temper atures that favors the formation of MoS 2 rath er than Mo xOy. Particular ly, at an elev ated temp eratur e of ∼ 280 ºC, none of O1 and O2 vibrations (Fig. 1b) are observ ed in MoS 2/CC(280), imply ing that the formation of MoxOy is complete ly suppressed. How ever , the high temper ature inevitably initiates the phase tran - sition of 1 T to 2 H since 2 H is a more thermodynamicall y stable form. In this report, we found that the temp eratur e around 220 °C yielded the highest portion of 1 T phase MoS 2. We speculate that during the growth stage, the octahedral coordination geometry of Mo sites required for the formation of 1 T phase can be stabilized by the adsorpti on of solvents (e.g., water molecule) that provides an envir onment to maintain the octahedral structure. [19]How ever , at higher temp eratur es the solvent templating effect is not enough to preserv e the octahedral geometry , leading to a tran sformation from 1 T to 2 H. For the competitiv e formation of MoS 2 and Mo xOy at differen t reaction temper atures, the hypotheti cal equations have been proposed as follow s: CS(NH 2)2 + 2H2O → CO2 + 2NH 3 + H2S                                     (1) Mo 7O24−6 + 21S−2 + 48H+ → 7MoS 2 + 24H 2O + 7S                      (2) Mo6++ 6OH- → MoO 3 + 3H2O                                                  (3) Mo4++ 4OH- → MoO 2 + 2H2O                                                  (4) At first, thiourea is subject ed to hydrolysis at high temper atures to generate H2S and NH 3 as sulfur (S−2) and hydro xyl (OH-) sources (Eq. 1). The formation of MoS 2 is attributed to the reduction of Mo6+ Fig. 2. (a,b) SEM and (c,d) TEM images of MoS 2/CC(220). Inset in (d): inter layer spacing of MoS 2 nanosheet; (e) HR-TEM of MoS 2 nanosheet showing 2 H (blue box) and 1 T phase (red box). The yellow and purple spheres in (e) repr esenting S and Mo atoms, respectiv ely, showing the atomic arrang ement difference between 2 H and 1 T phases. The intensity line profiles along the dashed line in the (f) blue and (g) red boxes; (h) Elemental mapping of MoS 2/CC(220) with O (red), S (purple), and Mo (green) elements.H.D. Mai, S. Jeong, G.-N. Bae et al. Journal of Alloys and Compounds 942 (2023) 169035 4
