to Mo4+ by S−2, follow ed by the precipitation of Mo4+ with S−2 to form MoS 2 (Eq. 2). Howev er, the concurrent gener ation of NH 3 from thiourea hydrol ysis also produces OH-, which readil y reacts with Mo6+ or Mo4+ to form Mo xOy (i.e., MoO 3 and MoO 2) (Eqs. 3, 4). Herein, it is experimentally found that the formation of MoS 2 via Eq. 2is more favorable at high temper atures, presumabl y because of the higher reacti vity of S2- at higher temper atures. Mean while, the lower reacti vity of S2- at low temper atur es results in a competitiv e for- mation of Mo xOy. How ever , it should be noted that elevated tem- peratur es lead to the conv ersion of 1 T to 2 H phase; ther efore, optimizing the reaction temper atures is very essential. X-ray phot oelectron spectroscop y was used to further quantify and optimize the composition of 1 T/2 H-MoS 2 as well as Mo xOy in the obtained MoS 2/CC (Fig. 1d,e). The Mo 3d core leve l is deconv o- luted into three main compositions, including 1 T (228.6 and 231.8 eV), [24] 2 H (229.7 and 232.6 eV), [25] and Mo xOy (230.4 and 233.5 and 235.7). [23] The proportion of 1 T, 2 H, and Mo xOy is also estimated from the corresponding Mo 3d XPS and summarized in Fig. 1f. As seen, at T ≤ 200 ºC, the products contain a considerable amount of Mo xOy (∼20–25 %). Mean while, at T ≥ 280 ºC, the per- centage of Mo xOy significantly drops to as low as 2.6 %; how ever , the product is dominated by 2 H phase (∼97.3 %) (Fig. 1f). A similar tren d in the binding energy of MoS 2 is observed from the S 2p (Fig. 1e), in which the 1 T-phase show s peaks at 161.2 and 162.7 eV, and those at 161.9 and 163.3 eV are designat ed to the 2 H-phase. [26] Fig. 1g demonstrat es the composition distribution of 1 T and 2 H phases within MoS 2, along with the formation of Mo xOy versus reaction temper atures. As seen, the optimal solvothermal temper ature is around 220 ºC to fabricate 1 T-rich MoS 2 (∼ 60 % of 1 T phase) with the minimized formation of Mo xOy and this trend is very consistent with the Raman findings, where the optimal temper ature is found to be around 220–250 ºC. This study provides an essential and straightforw ard synthetic strat egy for phase manipulation in two- dimensional MoS 2 nanosheets for targeted applications as well as minimizing the formation of undesirable Mo xOy. The morphology of the optimal sample, MoS 2/CC(220), is in- vestigated using scanning electron micros copy (SEM). As seen in Figs. 2a and S2, the two-dimensional layered nanosheets are uni- formly grown on the entire carbon cloth with high coverag e. High magnification SEM image (Fig. 2b) reveals that the MoS 2 nanosheets align verticall y on CC and connect with lots of nanov oids between them, facilitating the interaction between the electrol yte and elec- trode. To further examine the inter layer structure of MoS 2 nanosheets, transmission electron micros copy (TEM) is utilized (Fig. 2c,d). The MoS 2 nanosheets have a mean size and thickness of around 100 nm and 8 nm, respecti vely (Fig. 2c). The interlayer spacing is estimated to be appr ox. 0.67 nm, which is in good agreement with report ed values for solvothermal-synthesized MoS 2 (inset in Fig. 2d). [22,2 7]High- resol ution TEM (HR-TEM) image provides a clear difference in the atomic arrang ement of 1 T and 2 H phase (Fig. 1e). Specifically , 2 H- MoS 2 (blue box) show s a honey comb structure composed of three Mo atoms and three overlapped S pairs. The intensity distribution (Fig. 2f) demonstrat es a regular Mo-S-Mo order , which is charact er- istic of a 2 H-phase. In contrast, 1 T phase with different S contrasts is unambiguously discriminated from the 2 H-MoS 2 (red box in Fig. 2e). The corresponding intensity profile of 1 T phase (Fig. 2g) reveals the missing S peak between the two neighboring Mo atoms, clearl y de- monstrat ing the existence of 1 T phase. The elemental mapping analy sis (Fig. 2h) show s that sulfur and molybdenum are homo - geneously distributed in MoS 2 nanosheets. Fig. 3. (a,b) TEM and (c) SAED of Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC; (d) Elemental mapping of Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC with Mo (yellow), S (green), Pt (orange), and Ni (blue) elements. H.D. Mai, S. Jeong, G.-N. Bae et al. Journal of Alloys and Compounds 942 (2023) 169035 5
