Heterostructuring catalytic activ e metal species provides en- hanced electrocatal ysis performance. Since the MoS 2 with 1 T phase- rich has higher conductivity , it can serve as a suitable temp late for the electrodeposition of more activ e metal sites. Herein, we deposit Pt-Ni clusters on the optimal MoS 2/CC(220) by electrodeposition to form Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC. The different contrast observed in TEM images (Fig. 3a,b) indicates the successful formation of Pt-Ni nanoclusters on MoS 2/CC. The size distribution of Pt-Ni clusters is aroun d 4 nm. Importantly , the 1 T phase of MoS 2 is still preserv ed after the de- position of Pt-Ni (Fig. S3a). The corresponding Fast Fourie r transform (FFT) (Fig. S3b) and selecte d area electron diffraction (SAED) (Fig. 3c) indicates the existence of MoS 2, Ni0, and Pt0. [28] The Pt and Ni loading of 6.08 % and 3.23 %, respectiv ely, on MoS 2/CC after elec- trodeposition was quantified using TEM-ED X (Fig. 3d, Fig. S4). The Mo 3d and S 2p XPS (Fig. 4a,b) proves the dominant 1 T phase in MoS 2 after electrodepos ition of Pt-Ni clust ers. Fig. 4c and d show s the XPS profiles of Pt and Ni, where the main Pt 4 f and Ni 2p peaks can be proved. The Pt 4 f peak is deconv oluted into signals of Pt0 (71.6 and 74.9 eV) and Pt2+ (72.6 and 76.3 eV) [29,30] while Ni 2p contains a mixture of Ni0 (851.9 and 869.0 eV) and Ni2+ (855.8 and 873.3 eV). [31]This phenomenon is consistent with the dominance of Pt over Ni in the reduction reaction. The prepar ed cataly sts obtained from differ ent reaction tem- peratur es and Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC were utilized as electrocatal ysts for hydrog en evolut ion reaction (HER). As shown in Fig. 5a, the electrical resistance of the samples after coating with MoS 2 with rich 1 T phase show s a remarkable drop in resistance from 60 Ω (bare CC) to 2 Ω (MoS 2/CC(250)). This indicates that the 1 T phase enrichment is es- sential to impro ve the electrical properties, such as resistance and conductivity due to the excellent electron mobility of 1 T phase. Fig. 5b display s the HER activity of different materia ls in 0.5 M H2SO4 as an electrol yte. The MoS 2/CC(220) has higher HER activities than those obtained at different temper atur es. This is mainly because MoS 2/CC has the highest 1 T phase with a minimized amount of 2 H and Mo xOy compared with others. Also, this result indicates that 1 T phase of MoS 2 is essential to impro ve the electroc atalytic perfor - mance. In addition, the electrodeposition of Pt-Ni clust ers on MoS 2 is an excellent strat egy for enhancing the electrochemical activity of the heter ostructure materials. Therefor e, the Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC show s remarkable HER activities with an overpo tential (at −10 mA cm−2) of 154 mV RHE, which is mainly attributed to the synergistic effects. This value is significantly lower than MoS 2/CC(220) (330 mV RHE at −10 mA cm−2). The Tafel slopes for MoS 2/CC(220) and PtNi@MoS 2/CC are 268 and 121 mV dec−1, respecti vely (Fig. 5c), indicating that the electrodeposit ion of Pt-Ni nanoclusters has remar kably promo ted the HER kinetic of the cataly st. We have summarized the relev ant studies to compare the HER activ ity of the Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC and MoS 2/CC(220) (Fig. S5). Although the materials reported in this study do not show the superior HER performance compared with those reported previousl y, we believ e that this study is still worth y as it provides the synthetic guidance to enable optimization and quan- tification of 1 T and 2 H phases in MoS 2 without using any toxic chemicals and to understand the competitiv e formation of Mo xOy versus MoS 2. As shown in Fig. 5d, the HER durability of PtNi@MoS 2/ Fig. 4. XPS spectra of (a) Mo 3d, (b) S 2p, (c) Pt 4 f, and (d) Ni 2p of Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC. H.D. Mai, S. Jeong, G.-N. Bae et al. Journal of Alloys and Compounds 942 (2023) 169035 6
