CC was assessed in 0.5 M H2SO4. After 1000 cycles, PtNi@MoS 2/CC show ed very minor decrease in the HER performance. This is also consistent with the chronoamper ometry at − 0.2 VRHE in which PtNi@MoS 2/CC preserved the HER activity for 35 h. Post-charact er- izations by XPS (Fig. S6) and TEM (Fig. S7) were conduct ed to eval- uate the material after long-term stability tests. XPS data (Fig. S6) revealed that, apart from Ni that show ed a considerab le quantitativ e decrease mainly because of partial Ni dissolution upon exposing to acid media for a prolong ed period, there was negilible differen ce in the quantity , composition, and oxidation states of other elements (i.e., Mo, S, and Pt) before and after stability tests. Based on the Mo 3d (Fig. S6a), the 1 T fract ion was estimated to be 47.9 %, which was slightly decreased from 60 % in the original sample. The morpholo - gical and crystalli nity properties of Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC after stability tests were analyzed by TEM, SAED, EDX mapping (Fig. S7). The data show ed that MoS 2 somewhat preserv ed 1 T phase (Fig. S7b), which is consistent with XPS data. How ever , SAED revealed that the crystal - linity of the metallic species (Pt and Ni) was, to some degree, low- ered after stability tests (Fig. S7c). The EDX mapping indicated that the Ni and Pt loading was decrease d in comparison with those in the origianl sample (Fig. S7d). Importantly , this study allow s the control of phase tran sition in 2D materials, from which more catalyticall y activ e phases (e.g., 1 T) can be target ed via a straightforw ard sol- vothermal synthesis without using any toxic chemicals. This strat egy can be considered a mass prod uction techniq ue in industry for commercialization. Second, the impro ved conductivity of 1 T phase in MoS 2 can be promising to serve as templat es for heter ostructuring higher activ e metal sites with good metal utilization efficiency . 4. Conclusions In summary , we have demonstra ted a convenient and efficient solvothermal synthesis guidance that enables optimization and quantification of 1 T and 2 H phases in MoS 2 as well as the compe - titive formation of Mo xOy versus MoS 2. Temperatu re-corr elated phase quantification in MoS 2 corrobor ated by Raman and XPS re- veals that MoS 2/CC(220) has the highest 1 T phase (∼60 %). The ex- istence of both 1 T and 2 H phases within MoS 2 is unambiguously proved by HRTEM. We also employ ed the 1 T-rich MoS 2/CC as a substrat e for the electrodeposition of Pt-Ni clust ers (Pt-Ni@MoS 2/ CC) and their HER chemistry was much enhanced. We believe that this study allow s the phase manipulation in two-dimensional MoS 2 nanosheets for targeted applications as well as minimizing the for- mation of undesirable Mo xOy. CRediT authorship contribution stateme nt Hien Duy Mai: Carried out the Concept ualization, Writing – original draft and Writing – review & editing, Methodology , Data curation. Sangmin Jeong: carried out the Concept ualization, Writing – original draft and Writing – revie w & editing, Methodology , Data curation. Gi-Nam Bae: carried out the Data curation and Writing – Fig. 5. (a) I-V curve and (b) HER polarization curves of bare CC, MoS 2/CC obtained at different temper atures, and Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC recor ded in 0.5 M H2SO4; (c) Tafel slope of the catalys ts determined from the corresponding LSV in Fig. 5b; (d) LSV of Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC before and after 1000 cycles of continuous operation. Inset in (d): Chronoamper ometry of Pt-Ni@MoS 2/CC at − 0.2 VRHE.H.D. Mai, S. Jeong, G.-N. Bae et al. Journal of Alloys and Compounds 942 (2023) 169035 7
