Molecular Catalysis 516 (2021) 111955 Available online 27 October 2021 2468-8231/© 2021 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.Transition metal atom doped Ni3S2 as efficient bifunctional electrocatalysts for overall water splitting: Design strategy from DFT studies Yibo Chena, Xinyu Zhanga,*, Jiaqian Qinb, Riping Liua aState Key Laboratory of Metastable Materials Science and Technology, Yanshan University, Qinhuangdao 066004, Hebei, China bMetallurgy and Materials Science Research Institute, Chulalongkorn University, Bangkok 10330, Thailand ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Ni3S2 Overall water splitting Bifunctional electrocatalysts DFT study ABSTRACT Exploring stable, inexpensive and highly active bifunctional electrocatalyst for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) is a long-desired topic in the area of sustainable and renewable energy sources. Herein, ten kinds of transition metal (TM Mn, Fe, Co, Cu, Mo, Ru, Rh, Pd, Ir and Pt) were doped in Ni3S2 matrix to design TM-Ni 3S2 candidates, and their catalytic activities for overall water splitting were systematically explored based on density functional theory. Our results demonstrated that doping TM atoms is an effective tactic to boost the catalytic activity of Ni3S2 matrix. Among these candidates, Mn-Ni 3S2, Fe-Ni 3S2, and Ru-Ni 3S2 exhibit the outstanding catalytic activity for water splitting, with the much lower overpotentials being just 0.02/0.29 V, 0.11/0.29 V and 0.01/0.33 V for HER/OER, respectively, which are equivalent to or even superior to the prevailing bifunctional catalysts. Particularly, d-band center was employed to reveal the origin of OER activity. Our findings may open up new routes for the design of advanced Ni3S2-based catalysts for water splitting and realize the wide-range applications of Ni3S2-based catalysts in fields of clean and renewable energy. Introduction The overuse of fossil fuels, the aggravation of energy crisis as well as the deterioration of ecological environment have compelled us to explore the green renewable energy conversion and storage technolo - gies, such as electricity-driven water splitting [1], metal-air battery [2], nitrogen reduction [3], etc. Among these clean and sustainable energy technologies, water-splitting reaction is driven by cathodic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER), which is identified as an outstanding strategy for producing next-generation renewable energy carrier, hydrogen. However, owing to the slow kinetics for these two reactions, the key to electrochemical water splitting is designing the highly efficient electrocatalysts to reduce reaction overpotentials for electrochemical reaction. Currently, the noble metal based-materials, such as Pt, IrO2 and RuO 2, are regarded as the superior electrocatalysts for HER [4]and OER [5, 6], respectively. Nevertheless, the large-scale commercial applications are confined by their inadequacy and prohibitive cost. Regarding unifunctional cata- lysts, the catalytic activity of two separate unifunctional catalysts is generally poor than that of bifunctional catalysts because of different optimal operating conditions [7]. Meanwhile, the mass usage of materials and optimization procedures lead to a high production cost. Hence, achieving stable, affordable, durable and highly efficient bifunctional catalysts for overall water splitting, is a long-standing challenging yet appealing problem to solve. Up to now, continuous efforts have been devoted to explore a variety of inexpensive catalysts as the promising alternatives to noble-metal catalysts, including transitional metal sulfides [8, 9], carbides [10–12], oxides [13, 14]and phosphides [15–17]. Among these alter- natives, transitional metal sulfides, especially including the earth-abundant metal elements [18–20] (e.g., Fe, Co, Ni), have received extensive attentions as a promising catalyst for water splitting. For example, Ni3S2, possessing a continuous network of Ni-Ni bonds, has been demonstrated to be potential catalysts for water splitting owing to its high conductivity. However, the intrinsic activity of Ni3S2 is still inferior to the benchmark noble-metal catalysts. So far, various modi - fication strategies, including heteroatom doping [21, 22], hetero-interface coupling [23, 24], have been extensively devoted to tune the electronic structure of Ni3S2 to improve its catalytic activity. Among these, as an extremely effective method, heteroatom doping [25, 26] has been widely used to increase reactive sites and reduce reaction energy barrier for HER or OER. For example, Kou et al. [27] reported *Corresponding author. E-mail address: xyzhang@ysu.edu.cn (X. Zhang). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Molecular Catalysis u{�~zkw! s{yo|kro>! ÐÐÐ1u{�~zk w�1ow�o�to~1m {y2y{wom�w k~/mk�kwÞ�t�! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcat.2021.111955 Received 11 July 2021; Received in revised form 12 October 2021; Accepted 13 October 2021
