Molecular Catalysis 516 (2021) 111955 2that introducing N modification can significantly enhance the catalytic activity of Ni3S2 nanosheets toward HER. Qin and co-worker [28] pre- pared P doped Ni3S2 nanosheets, which tunes the electronic structure of Ni3S2 and effectively enhances HER activity. Moreover, Qin et al. [29] demonstrated that the overall electronic conductivity and stability for Ni3S2 nanoparticles grown on nickel foam is efficiently improved by incorporating Co and Cu, thus permanently enhancing OER performance of Ni3S2. Even so, it is extremely challenging to systematically screen the novel bifunctional catalysts with superior catalytic activity for HER and OER in experiments, due to expensive raw materials, time-consuming and uncontrollable experimental conditions. Fortunately, multitudi - nous reports have shown that first principle calculation can as serve as a powerful prediction method to screen the promising bifunctional elec- trocatalysts at atomic scale by calculating the adsorption interactions between intermediates and substrate as well as reaction overpotentials. To best our knowledge, the systematic theoretical study on the catalytic activity of Ni3S2-based electrocatalysts for overall water splitting has not been reported up to now. Inspired by the above considerations, with the aim of obtaining the highly efficient bifunctional catalysts, we systematically investigated the HER/OER performances of Ni3S2 doped by ten kinds of transition metal atoms (Mn, Fe, Co, Cu, Mo, Ru, Pd, Rh, Ir and Pt) using first- principles calculations. Here, the structural stability of TM-Ni 3S2, Gibbs free-energy change for H adsorption (ΔG H∗), the energy barrier for water dissociation, potential determining step (PDS) and reaction overpotentials for OER were comprehensively explored. Our results indicate that, among all designed candidates, Mn-Ni 3S2, Fe-Ni 3S2 and Ru-Ni 3S2 exhibit excellent OER performance with the much lower theoretical overpotentials of 0.29, 0.29 and 0.33 V, respectively, and high HER activity coupled with the low overpotentials of 0.02, 0.11, and 0.01 V, respectively, which is comparable to or even outperform the well-developed noble catalysts. Our work may provide theoretical guidance to the design of the advanced bifunctional catalysts and help to design excellent Ni3S2-based catalysts for overall water splitting. Method and computational details The Vienna ab-into simulation package(VASP) [30, 31] code based on the density functional theory (DFT) was employed to realize struc- tural optimization and static self-consistent calculations. The electron exchange-correlation was described by the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) in the form of Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) [32]. The projector-augmented-wave (PAW) [33, 34] potentials were adopted to describe the electron-ion interactions. For all the structural relaxations, long-range van der Waals interactions were corrected by DFT-D3 [35] functional. The plane-waves employed for expanding the Kohn-Sham electron wave-functions have an energy cutoff of 500 eV. The convergence tolerances for energy and force were set to be 10␀5 eV and 0.02 eV/ Å, respectively. In this work, a 2 ×2 ×1 supercell of (001) surface with 6 atom layers containing 40 atoms was constructed as Ni3S2 substrate, since it has been experimentally observed to be an active surface for water splitting [36]. The reciprocal space was sampled with a 3 ×3 ×1 Monkhorst-pack mesh for Ni3S2 surface. In addition, a vacuum region up to 15 Å was employed in the z-direction to avoid the inter- action between two neighboring images. During geometry optimization, the undermost 20 atoms of the slab (bottom three layer) are kept frozen while the three topmost layers are allowed to relax to simulate the surface. The HER performance is assessed via the Gibbs free energy of H adsorption (ΔG H∗), which is defined by the following expression: ΔG H∗ΔE H∗ΔEZPE␀TΔSH (1) where ΔE H∗is the hydrogen adsorption energy and obtained by ΔE H∗ EH∗␀E␀1E2EH2, in which EH∗and E represent the total energy of slab model with and without the adsorbed H atom, respectively, and EH2 denotes the energy of a H2 molecule in the gas phase. ΔEZPE and ΔSH denote the differences in zero-point energy and entropy between the adsorbed state and gaseous state of hydrogen, respectively. T is tem- perature and T 298.15 K in this work. The theoretical exchange current (i0) can be evaluated according to Nørskov ’s assumption [37], as shown below: i0␀ek0 1exp†ΔG H∗†EKBT(2) where k0 is rate constant, which contains all factors related to solvent recombination when protons transfer to catalyst surface. Here, it was assumed to be 1 for illustrative purpose in our computations. As is generally reported in experiments, topical OER process occur - ring at the anode of water splitting in acidic condition, involves four elementary oxidation steps, which can be described as follows: ∗H2Ol→∗OHHe␀(3) ∗OH→∗OHe␀(4) ∗OH2Ol→∗OOHHe␀(5) ∗OOH→∗O2gHe␀(6) here, g and l stand for the gas and liquid phases, respectively. * denotes the activity site on the substrate surface, *O, *OH and *OOH represent the oxygenated groups. It is well established that the Gibbs reaction free energy (ΔG) is an important descriptor for OER process, which is obtained by the computational hydrogen electrode (CHE) model [38] and defined as: ΔGΔEΔZPE␀TΔSΔG pHΔG U (7) where ΔE is the reaction energy obtained from DFT calculations. ΔZPE and ΔS correspond to the change in zero-point energy and entropy at room temperature (T298.15 K), respectively, which are calculated by frequency analysis. ΔGpH is free energy change caused by pH, which can be assessed by ΔGpHKBT×pH×ln10, while the last tem ΔG U refers to the free energy contribution with respect to the electrode po- tential U and the number of electrons transferred ne, which can be determined as ΔG U␀neU. According to Nørskov ’s assumption [39], the OER overpotential (ηOER) in acidic environment is determined by the maximum ΔG of four steps, as computed by: ηOERmax⊔ΔG 1CΔG 2CΔG 3CΔG 4⊓Ee␀1B23V (8) Moreover, to reveal the role of the different TM centers, the d-band center (εd) was calculated as follows: εd⨄Ef ␀∞ερddε⨄Ef ␀∞ρddε(9) where Ef represents the Fermi level, ρd denotes the density of states projected onto a single TM atom d orbital and ε is the energy width of the d orbital. Results and discussion Our obtained lattice constant of Ni3S2 bulk is 4.04 Å, and the opti- mized bond lengths of Ni-S and Ni-Ni are about 2.27 Å and 2.52 Å, respectively, all of which agree well with the experimental data [40]. By cleaving the relaxed Ni3S2 bulk, we constructed three low-index (001) surfaces with different terminations (termination1, termination2 and termination3) and calculated their surface energies (γ) to evaluate the structural stability. The surface energy (γ), is usually calculated by the energy required to split the crystal into two parts along a plane. Here, the surface energy was measured by the excess energy of the surface Y. Chen et al.
