Molecular Catalysis 516 (2021) 111955 3with respect to the bulk and can be defined as follows [19, 41, 42]: γEslab␀nEbulk A␀Eunrelaxed slab␀nEbulk 2A(10) where Eslab and Eunrelaxed slab are the total energies of relaxed slab and un- relaxed slab, respectively. n denotes the number of Ni3S2 units in slab. Ebulk represents the energy of a bulk Ni3S2 formula unit and A is the cross sectional area of the slab. The smaller surface energy means the higher structural stability. As shown in Fig. 1(a), the relative stability trend of Ni3S2 (001) slab was in the order of termination1 (γ1.14 J/m2) F termination3 (γ1.83 J/m2) Ftermination2 (γ2.32 J/m2). Obviously, (001) surface with termination1 possessing the smallest value of γ, is the most stable slab model. Therefore, the (001) slab with termination1 is employed to explore the following catalytic activity. The thermodynamic stability of catalysts plays an important role in catalytic activity and unfavorable binding of TM atoms on substrate usually leads to the loss of catalytic performance. Therefore, we first evaluated the stability of TM-Ni 3S2 substrate (TMMn, Fe, Co, Cu, Mo, Ru, Rh, Pd, Ir and Pt). For TM doped Ni3S2 substrate, two distinct doping sites were taken into account, i.e., Ni vacancy in the first layer and second layer of the defective Ni3S2 substrate, as depicted in Fig. 1(b). Here, to assess the structural stability of TM-Ni 3S2, we first calculated the vacancy formation energy (Ef) of these two defects by Ef Ev ␀Ep  μNi, in which Ev and Ep represent the total energy of Ni3S2 with and without vacancy, respectively. μNi is the chemical potential of Ni atom. The calculated Ef values for Ni vacancy in the first layer and second layer are 0.06 and 0.97 eV, respectively. Generally, smaller Ef value indicates that higher possibility of vacancy formation. Comparing the Ef values of BP with B vacancy (1.66 eV) [43], MoS 2 with S vacancy (5.85 eV) [44], graphene with C vacancy (7.69 eV) [45], it is evident that the Ef values for Ni3S2 with Ni vacancy is much smaller than those, indicating Ni3S2 exhibits greater possibility to form Ni vacancy. Following, we calculated the binding energy (Eb) of TM-Ni 3S2 candidates, which is described as:EbETMCV␀EV␀ETM, in which ETM, ETMCV and EV correspond to the energies of the isolated TM atom, the defective Ni3S2 substrate doped with and without TM atom, respectively. The lower binding energy corresponds to the higher thermodynamic stability. As displayed in Fig. 1(c), the results show that the Eb values of Ni3S2 with TM replacing the Ni atom of second layer are lower than that of TM occupying the Ni vacancy of the first layer, suggesting that TM atom prefers to occupy Ni vacancy in the second layer of Ni3S2(001). Herein, due to the higher thermodynamic stability, the following study on catalytic activity mainly focus on the Ni3S2 doped with TM in the second layer. As is well known, the HER catalytic efficiency can be estimated by the Gibbs free-energy change of the adsorbed H* (ΔG H∗). A larger pos- itive ΔG H∗value suggests that the massive energy needs to be injected in the process of H adsorption on the catalysts surface, while a more negative value indicates that H adsorbed on the catalyst surface is difficult to release, thus inhibiting HER activity. Hence, the ΔG H∗value of an ideal catalyst should be around zero to realize the balance of H adsorption and desorption. For comparison, we first examined three possible adsorption sites of H atom for determining the HER activity of pristine Ni3S2 matrix, involving the outermost Ni site, S site and Ni-Ni bridge site. The optimized geometries of adsorption configuration and the corresponding ΔG H∗are illustrated in Fig.S1(a). The Ni-Ni site is found to have a smallest ΔG H∗value (0.50 eV) than the Ni site (0.67 eV) and S site (1.18 eV), indicating Ni-Ni site is more catalytically active and mainly responsible for the HER, which is consistent with the previous result [19]. For TM-Ni 3S2, four possible Ni active sites, three S sites and the active TM sites have been considered, as presented in Fig.S2. The ΔG H∗values of TM-Ni 3S2 was portrayed in Fig. 2(a), meanwhile the relaxed configurations of the most stable adsorption model were shown in Fig.S3. Obviously, the results reveal that, in the case of Cu-Ni 3S2 and Pd-Ni 3S2, the Ni atoms exhibit stronger active for H atom, while the TM atoms are active sites for other TM-Ni 3S2 candidates. More importantly, the HER catalytic performances of all the candidates, except Cu-Ni 3S2, have been efficiently improved. The ΔG H∗values of TM-Ni 3S2 are characterized as being between -0.15 and 0.51 eV. Note that Ru-Ni 3S2 sample shows the highest HER catalytic activity and the HER catalytic Fig. 1.(a) Side schematic diagrams of Ni3S2 bulk and three Ni3S2 (001) models with different terminations, as well as the corresponding surface energies. (b) Side and top views of Ni3S2 (001) doped with TM on the Ni vacancy in the first and second layers. (c) Binding energies of TM doped on the Ni vacancy in the first and second layers of Ni3S2 (001) surface. Y. Chen et al.
