Molecular Catalysis 516 (2021) 111955 4activity of Ni3S2 matrix has significantly improved with the ΔG H∗values reduced from 0.50 to -0.01 eV after doping Ru atom. Overall, the HER catalytic performances for three best candidates follow the order of Ru-Ni 3S2 (-0.01 eV) FMn-Ni 3S2 (0.02 eV) FFe-Ni 3S2 (0.11 eV), which are comparable to or even outperform than the current prevailing Pt-based catalysts (0.09 eV). However, Cu-Ni 3S2 is eliminated due to the unimproved HER performance compared with Ni3S2. Meanwhile, to intuitively represent and quantitatively evaluate the HER catalytic activity of TM-Ni 3S2, we established the volcano curve, namely, the relationship between the theoretical exchange current density (i0) and the adsorption free energy (ΔG H∗), as plotted in Fig. 2 (b). Note that, Mo-Ni 3S2, with the relatively strong bonding strength to H atom, lies on the left branch of volcano map. By contrary, the remaining candidates are related to the weak adsorption with H atom, locating on the right-hand side of volcano and showing the relatively low exchange currents, especially Pt-Ni 3S2, Pd-Ni 3S2 and Cu-Ni 3S2. Moreover, the closer the exchange current is to the optimal value (the top of volcano plot), the higher the catalytic activity. Notably, Ru-Ni 3S2 and Mn-Ni 3S2 are located in the peak of volcano plot, demonstrating the optimal HER catalytic performance, and they yield the significantly low ΔG H∗values of -0.01 eV and 0.02 eV, respectively, which surpass the noble Pt-based catalysts. Most importantly, the energy barrier for H2O dissociation plays a vital role in alkaline media and a lower energy barrier for H2O disso - ciation is a prerequisite to initiate the alkaline HER. Therefore, we calculated the energy barriers for H2O dissociation on Ni3S2 and TM- Ni3S2 surfaces, as shown in Fig.S4. First, we can find that the difference between their H2O adsorption is very small. However, the energy barrier of H2O dissociation shows significantly difference. Obviously, the pris- tine Ni3S2 exhibits a relatively high energy barrier for H2O dissociation (1.45 eV), indicating sluggish kinetics. Compared with the pure Ni3S2 surface, Cu-Ni 3S2 presents a slightly higher energy barrier of 1.60 eV and Pd-Ni 3S2 shows a similar energy barrier of 1.44 eV. For other TM-Ni 3S2, as we expected, the energy barrier for H2O dissociation significantly decreased, suggesting that doping TM (except Cu and Pd) make it quite convenient for H2O dissociation on Ni3S2 surface. Based on the above results, Cu-Ni 3S2 and Pd-Ni 3S2 were excluded due to the unimproved hydrogen adsorption and H2O dissociation. Overall, these results demonstrate that metal dopants facilitate the H2O dissociation and optimize hydrogen adsorption, which contributes to faster kinetics upon the first stage of HER process and improves the HER activity of Ni3S2. Apart from HER activity, the OER activity of TM-Ni 3S2 candidates was also investigated, and their four-electron reaction free energy pro- files are plotted in Fig. 3, in which the maximum reaction free energy (the potential limiting step) is highlighted in red line and the ideal electrocatalyst is described by the pink step. Meanwhile, the Gibbs free energies of oxygenated intermediates and the optimized intermediates for TM-Ni 3S2 sample are summarized in Table S2 and Fig.S3, respec - tively. Here, to have a comparison, the OER activity of pristine Ni3S2 was first calculated (Fig.S1), yielding a overpotential of 0.68 V, which is agreement with the previous result [19]. Regarding TM-Ni 3S2, the considered adsorption sites for *OH, O* and *OOH intermediates are same as the *H mentioned above (Fig.S2). From Fig.S3, we can find that for all the candidates, both *OH and *OOH favor the Ni2 sites on the surface of TM-Ni 3S2, while the most favorable sites for *O correspond to the bridge site between Ni and S. As shown in Fig. 3, for all candidates, the second step or third step corresponds to the maximum Gibbs free energy difference, which is mainly attributed to the relatively weak *O binding or *OOH binding on catalysts. Moreover, at the equilibrium potential of 1.23 V, although the first and the forth step turn out to be downhill, the second and third step are still endothermic, with the relatively high energy barrier. In case of Mn-Ni 3S2 and Fe-Ni 3S2, the satisfactory catalytic activity was obtained, both with the lowest over- potential of 0.29 V. Beyond that, their free energy diagrams at U0 V are even comparable to the ideal catalyst. Moreover, we found that, for Mo-Ni 3S2, Ru-Ni 3S2 and Pd-Ni 3S2, the third step, where an adsorbed *O groups react with a H2O molecule to form an *OOH specie, is the limiting step, and the corresponding maximum Gibbs free energy change are 2.06, 1.56 and 1.70 eV, respectively. For the remaining TM-Ni 3S2, the second reaction step corresponding to the dissociation of adsorbed *OH into *O species, becomes the most demanding step. Overall, the calculated overpotentials are 0.29, 0.29, 0.37, 0.41, 0.83, 0.33, 0.33, 0.47, 0.36 and 0.35 V for TMMn, Fe, Co, Cu, Mo, Ru, Rh, Pd, Ir and Pt, respectively. Apparently, except Mo-Ni 3S2, the OER overpotentials of other systems decrease significantly, indicating that OER activity of Ni3S2 can be efficiently improved by doping heteroatom. Taken together, Mo-Ni 3S2 is ruled out due to the poor OER performance compared with Ni3S2. Moreover, Mn-Ni 3S2 and Fe-Ni 3S2 exhibit the best OER activity, which is not only significantly superior to the pristine Ni3S2, but also comparable to or even better than many catalysts re- ported at present, such as IrN4@G [46], NiIT monolayer [47], Cr-COFs [48], Ni/g-CN [49]. As known, the binding strength between adsorption intermediates and catalyst is of vital importance to OER electrochemical activity. Too weak adsorption can’t offer sufficient power for catalytic reaction, while the strong binging will seriously hinder catalytic reaction by blocking substrate surface, resulting in the poor catalytic activity. Therefore, to obtain better OER catalytic efficiency, the key is to control the Fig. 2.(a) Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption on TM-Ni 3S2 at U0 V. (b) Volcano curve of exchange current (i0) related to the Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption (ΔG H∗) on TM-Ni 3S2. Y. Chen et al.
