Molecular Catalysis 516 (2021) 111955 6adsorption strength of reaction intermediates neither too strong nor too weak. Many studies suggest that volcano plots have unique advanta - geous in describing OER activity and screening highly efficient OER catalysts. The lower overpotential means the higher catalytic efficiency, that is to say, the peak of volcano map corresponds to the best candidate. Herein, we derived activity volcano plots by adopting OER over- potentials as a function of ΔG∗O␀ΔG∗OH, as shown in Fig. 4. It also can be observed that the optimum ΔG∗O␀ΔG∗OH is about 1.52 eV. Below this optimum value, O* binding on surface is relatively strong, and OER overpotential decreases with the increase of ΔG∗O␀ΔG∗OH, which is dominated by the formation of OOH*. In contrast, when ΔG∗O␀ΔG∗OH F 1.52 eV, overpotential increases with the increase of ΔG∗O␀ΔG∗OH, which is limited by the second reaction step. Regarding Ni3S2 slab, the relatively strong *O binding makes it deviates away from the top of OER activity map, resulting in the formation of OOH* from O* being the rate determining step. Moreover, most candidates are located in the right side of volcano curve, which is mainly ascribed to the relatively weak *O bonding strengths. Only Pd-Ni 3S2 and Mo-Ni 3S2 occupy in the left branch, indicating that *OH binding on catalyst surface is relatively weak. Impressively, Fe-Ni 3S2 and Mn-Ni 3S2 fall in the peak zone and possess the optimal OER catalytic performance, which can compete with and even outperform the currently commercial catalysts IrO2/RuO 2 (0.55/0.42 V) [39, 50]. Telling from the Eqs.(3-7), it can be seen that the OER catalytic ac- tivity is actually determined by the Gibbs free energies of different re- action species, i.e., ΔG∗OH, ΔG∗O and ΔG∗OOH. However, the whole OER reaction can be simplified by establishing the adsorption strength rela- tionship of the oxygenated intermediates. Hence, the scaling relation - ship between ΔG∗OOH and ΔG∗OH was investigated, as plotted in Fig. 5, where the red line corresponds to the scaling fitting between ΔG∗OOH and ΔG∗OH, and their relations can be represented as: ΔG∗OOH 1B10ΔG∗OH2B95. Apparently, ΔG∗OOH and ΔG∗OH display an excellent linear relationship with a relatively high coefficient determination (R20.90). Beyond that, two prominent deviations of ±0.02 eV and ±0.04 eV from the fitted line are also plotted in Fig. 5, as highlighted by the yellow and pink regions, respectively. We noticed that all the can- didates are perfectly distributed in pink zone, indicating that the defined deviation is reasonable and matches well with our designed catalysts. According to the above linear relationship between ΔG∗OOH and ΔG∗OH, the free energy change of four steps (ΔG i) can be described as: ΔG 1ΔG∗OH (11)  ΔG 2ΔG O∗␀ΔG∗OH (12) ΔG 3ΔG∗OOH␀ΔG∗O1B10ΔG∗OH2B95␀ΔG 1␀ΔG 2 (13) ΔG 44B92␀ΔG∗OOH4B92␀1B10ΔG∗OH2B95 (14) From the above Eqs.(11-14), we notice that, based on two indepen - dent variables, i.e., ΔG∗OH and ΔG O∗␀ΔG∗OH, the change of reaction free energies for TM-Ni 3S2 can be obtained. Following the above, the color-filled contour map of the OER limiting overpotential was plotted to evaluate the OER activity of TM-Ni 3S2, as shown in Fig. 6. Obviously, as indicated by yellow line, the 2D activity volcano plot was classified into four regions by reaction free energy changes, which corresponds to different potential determining step (PDS). As indicated in the dark blue part of Fig. 6, the minimum overpotential was predicted to be only 0.27 V under the premise of ΔG 2ΔG 3ΔG 4. Most prominently, all can- didates are dispersed in PDS2 and PDS3, which demonstrates that the Fig. 4.The volcano plot of OER on TM-Ni 3S2 with ␀ηOER vs ΔG∗O␀ΔG∗OH. Fig. 5.Scaling relationship between the Gibbs free energies of adsorbed *OOH (ΔG∗OOH) and *OH (ΔG∗OH) on TM-Ni 3S2. Fig. 6.2D activity volcano showing the OER overpotentials as a function of Gibbs free energies of reaction intermediates. The 