Molecular Catalysis 516 (2021) 111955 7first H2O→*OH and the forth *OOH →O2 reaction steps can easily occur on the whole OER reaction. Firstly, focusing on the pristine Ni3S2, the *O binding strength is stronger than *OOH binding, resulting in the rela- tively poor OER performance with a overpotential of 0.68 V and the generation of *OOH species being PDS. Additionally, Mo-Ni 3S2 deviates obviously from the optimal activity region than Ni3S2, which is not suitable as the promising candidate for OER. Moreover, for most can- didates, such as Rh-Ni 3S2, Cu-Ni 3S2, Ir-Ni 3S2, Co-Ni 3S2 and Pt-Ni 3S2, the PDS for OER is attributed to *OH →*O reaction process, which is inti- mately tied to the weak *O adsorption with respect to *OH adsorption. Particularly, regarding Mn-Ni 3S2, Fe-Ni 3S2 and Ru-Ni 3S2, they lie in the boundary line between PDS2 and PDS3 owing to the small difference between ΔG 2 and ΔG 3, which are very close to the peak of volcano plot, resulting in the outstanding activity with the OER overpotentials of 0.29, 0.29 and 0.33V, respectively. Moreover, Rh-Ni 3S, producing the same overpotential as Ru-Ni 3S2, 0.33 V, also attracted our attention. Taken together, the rate-limiting steps predicted by the 2D activity volcano plot are in well agreement with the results of Gibbs free energy diagram. Insight into the electronic properties of catalysts can guide us to find the physical origin of catalytic activity. As known, d band center( εd) has been widely used as an effective descriptor for binding strength between adsorbate and catalytic surface. Hence, we calculated the εd of all the TM-Ni 3S2 candidates from the corresponding PDOS, as portrayed in Fig. 7. It is obvious that all the candidates show the metallic charac - teristics, which demonstrates that excellent conductivity can provide a prerequisite for rapid electron transfer in the electrochemical process. Moreover, the deeper d-band center and larger d electrons generally lead to the weaker binding between intermediates and catalytic surface. From Fig. 7, a typical characteristic can be observed that with the increased d-electron occupation, εd shows a deeper energy level with respect to the Fermi level, for example, Mn (-0.59) FFe (-1.16) FCo (-1.17) FCu (-2.95); Mo (-0.27) FRu (-1.50) FRh (-1.60) FPd (-2.76); Ir (-2.51) FPt (-2.94). In addition, for the TMs in the same row of the periodic table, except for Pd, a more negative d-band center results in weaker O* binding on TM-Ni 3S2 (Fig.S6), which is accordance with the above description, allowing us to optimize the binding energy to an ideal value for the OER by simply tuning TM atoms doped into Ni3S2. When d- band center is in a certain range, the favorable bonding strength be- tween intermediate and catalyst is conducive to the relatively high catalytic activity. The overpotentials of HER and OER on TM-Ni 3S2 are summarized in Fig. 8. Remarkably, Cu-Ni 3S2 and Mo-Ni 3S2 are excluded due to the unimproved HER and OER activity, respectively. Beyond that, Pd-Ni 3S2 is also eliminated because of the unimproved energy barrier for H2O dissociation. Among all the candidates, Ru-Ni 3S2 and Mn-Ni 3S2 exhibit the extraordinary HER activity with the overpotentilas of 0.01 and 0.02 V, respectively, followed by Fe-Ni 3S2 (0.11 V). In addition, the best OER catalysts are Mn-Ni 3S2 and Fe-Ni 3S2 with the overpotentials of 0.29 V, followed by Ru-Ni 3S2 (0.33 V) and Rh-Ni 3S2 (0.33 V). Overall, Mn-Ni 3S2, Fe-Ni 3S2 and Ru-Ni 3S2 can serve as the highly promising bifunctional catalysts for overall water splitting. Conclusions In summary, we designed a series of promising TM-Ni 3S2 bifunc - tional catalysts for overall water splitting by first-principles calculations. Our results reveal that the catalytic activity of Ni3S2 for HER/OER can be efficiently tuned by doping TM atoms. More importantly, Ru-Ni 3S2 ex- hibits the best catalytic activity toward HER, followed by Mn-Ni 3S2 and Fe-Ni 3S2. Meanwhile, these three catalysts also possess remarkable OER performances, being capable of driving HER/OER with the extremely Fig. 7.PDOS for the d-orbitals of the TM atoms in TM-Ni 3S2 systems. The d band center is marked by the red dashed line, and the Fermi level is set as zero. Fig. 8.Calculated HER and OER overpotentials of TM-Ni 3S2.  Y. Chen et al.
