www.theNanoResearch.com ∣www.Springer.com/journal/12274 | Nano Research  239 Nano Res. 2017, 10(1): 238–246 characteristics is still the most efficient HER catalyst, and it is far superior than other alternative non- precious materials. However, because of its limited resources and high cost, it is imperative to decrease the loading of Pt in the designed electrocatalyst for achieving the ultimate goal of producing cost-effective hydrogen [7–9]. In this regard, downsizing Pt nanoparticles (PtNPs) to achieve a large proportion of surface atoms is a widely adopted strategy. Nevertheless, capping agents used in the colloidal synthesis of PtNPs, which serve to inhibit overgrowth and aggregation, severely block surface-active sites, thereby resulting in decreased electrocatalytic performance [10–12]. Recently, the immobilization of PtNPs on high specific-surface substrates for achieving size control, as well as avoiding the use of capping agents, has attracted considerable attention [13, 14]. Among the substrates reported, carbon with high electrical conductivity and chemical stability, such as graphene, carbon nanotubes, and commercial Vulcan XC-72, has been considered as an ideal substrate for electrocatal ysts [12, 15, 16]. Moreover, experimental and theoretical studies have demonstrated that the doping of carbon with heteroatoms, such as electronegative N, provides a driving force for the nucleation of Pt on the N atom sites, as well as strong affinity between Pt and carbon, resulting in the uniform distribution of PtNPs on carbon with significantly decreased size and enhanced durability [17]. Moreover, the transfer of electrons from PtNP to N-doped carbon results in the modification of the electronic structure of Pt, thereby leading to the intrinsic enhancement of its catalytic activity; this catalytic activity is attributed to the down-shift of the Pt d-band caused by the donation of charge from the N dopant to Pt [18, 19]. In this regard, the co-doping of various atoms in the carbon substrate might create unique Pt electronic structures for the further improvement of its catalytic activity, considering synergis tic coupling effects between the doped hereoatoms and Pt [20–23]. The atomic radius and electron-donating ability of P are greater than that of N, making it a good candidate as a dopant for carbon [23, 24]. Thus far, to the best of our knowledge, ultrathin Pt loaded on carbon co-doped with N and P, has rarely been discussed as a HER  electrocatalyst. Herein, a novel HER catalyst (Pt/NPC) is presented: this catalyst is composed of PtNPs with an average diameter of 1.8 nm that were loaded on a three-dimensional (3D) carbon network co-doped with N and P (hereafter, referred to as NPC). The NPC was prepared by the pyrolysis of polyaniline aerogels, which were obtained from the template-free poly-merization of aniline in the presence of phytic acid [25, 26]. PtNPs were uniformly nucleated and grown on the NPC surface by the addition of NaBH 4 in the reaction without the use of capping agent for maintaining their surface-active sites. Surprisingly, the Pt/NPC thus obtained even with an ultralow   Pt loading of 1.82 wt.%, as compared to commercial 20 wt.% Pt/C (Alfa), exhibited superior catalytic activity and long-term durability. 2 Experimental 2.1 Materials All materials were purchased and used without further purification. Aniline (C 6H7N, AR, ≥ 99.5%) was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. Phytic acid (50%, w/w in water) was pur- chased from Aladdin. Ammonium peroxydisulfate ((NH 4)2S2O4, ≥98%) was purchased from Alfa Aesar. Hydrogen hexachloroplatinate (H 2PtCl 6, 40% Pt) was purchased from Acros Organics. Pt/C (20 wt.% Pt on Vulcan XC-72R) and Nafion (5 wt.%) were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Deionized (DI) water was used in all experiments. 2.2 Synthesis of 3D carbon network co-doped with N and P The polyaniline aerogel was synthesized according to a previously reported method with some modifications. In a typical synthesis, 0.658 mL (0.7 mmol) of phytic acid (50%, w/w in water), 3.5 mL DI water, and 0.458 mL (5 mmol) of aniline were completely mixed under stirring. Second, 0.286 g (1.25 mmol) of ammonium peroxydisulfate was dissolved in 1 mL of DI water by stirring. Both of the above solutions were cooled to 4 °C and then rapidly mixed together. The solution was kept overnight, and the aerogel formed was washed
