| www.editorialmanager.com/nare/default.asp 240 Nano Res. 2017, 10(1): 238–246 by adding DI water for two days. Then, the polyaniline aerogel was freeze-dried for 24 h. Finally, it was heat- treated to 1,000 °C at a heating rate of 3 °C·min−1 and maintained for 2 h under N 2. For comparison, NC was prepared in the same manner, except for replacing phytic acid with 0.625 mL of hydrochloric acid. 2.3 Synthesis of Pt/NPC and Pt/N-doped 3D carbon (Pt/NC) A conventional sodium borohydride reduction method in a hydrous solution was utilized for loading Pt nanoparticles on NPC suppo rt. First, 15 mg NPC was dispersed in a beaker with 14 mL of DI water  by ultrasonic treatment for 1 h. Second, 40 μL of a 0.1 mol·L −1 H2PtCl 6 solution was added into the mixture and stirred for an additional 1 h. Third, the solution was placed in an ice-water bath, and 1 mL of an ice- cooled freshly prepared NaBH 4 solution (2 mg·mL−1) was added with vigorous stirring. After reaction for 1 min, the resulting product was collected by filtration and washed three times with DI water. Finally, the filtered material was dried at 60 °C for 6 h in a vacuum oven. Pt/NPC-2, Pt/NPC-3, and Pt/NC were synthesized by a similar procedure with increased dosage of H 2PtCl 6 and NaBH 4. For Pt/NPC-2 and Pt/NC, 80 μL of a 0.1 mol·L−1 H2PtCl 6 solution was reacted with 1 mL of the NaBH 4 solution (3 mg·mL−1). For Pt/NPC-3, 190 μL of a 0.1 mol·L−1 H 2PtCl 6 solution was reacted with 1 mL of the NaBH 4 solution (5 mg·mL−1). 2.4 Sample characterization The morphologies of the products were examined by transmission electron microscopy (TEM; Tecnai G2 F20 S-Twin) at an acceleration voltage of 200 kV and by scanning electron microscopy (SEM; Quanta 400 FEG) at 20 kV . Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on a Bruker D8 Advance powder XRD system at a scanning rate of 4 °·min −1 from 10° to 80° using Cu-K radiation ( λ = 1.54056 Å). Raman spectra were recorded using a micro-Raman spectroscopy set up (Jobin Yvon LabRam HR800 instrument excited by a He–Ne laser of 532.15 nm). X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were recorded on an PHI 5000 Versaprobe XPS instrument with non-monochromatized Al-K α X-ray as the excitation sour ce. The bulk content of Pt in the samples was measured by inductively coupled plasma-atomic emission spec troscopy (ICP-AES; Vista MPX). 2.5 Electrochemical measurement All electrochemical measurements were performed using a conventional three-electrode system on an electrochemical workstat ion (CHI660E, Shanghai Chenhua, China) in a 0.5 M H 2SO 4 electrolyte. The catalyst loaded on a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) was used as the working electrode, with a diameter of 3 mm and a geometric area of 0.7065 cm 2, while an Ag/AgCl electrode and a platinum foil were used as the reference and counter electrodes, respectively. Before measurement, the GCE was carefully polished and thoroughly rinsed. For preparing an aqueous suspension of the catalysts, 5 mg of the sample and 32 μL of a 5 wt.% Nafion solution were dispersed in 0.968 mL of 4:1 v/v water/ethanol by sonication for at least 30 min. Then, 4 μL of the catalyst ink was loaded on the GCE as the working electrode. Linear scanning voltammetry (LSV) was performed at a scan rate of 50 mV·s −1. All of the reported potentials have been corrected for iR loss, which are expressed versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) according to Eq. (1): Evs. RHE  = Evs. Ag/AgCl  + Eθ Ag/AgCl  + 0.059pH      (1) 3 Results and discussion Figure S1 (in the Electron ic Supplementary Material (ESM)) shows the SEM and TEM images of the prepared NPC substrates. Following the reduction of H 2PtCl 6 by NaBH 4 in the hydrous solution, PtNPs were successfully loaded on NPC, affording Pt/NPC, and the mass content of Pt was quantitatively deter- mined by ICP-AES analysis. In a typical reaction, a Pt/NPC catalyst with a Pt content of 1.82 wt.% was obtained. For comparison, Pt/NC was synthesized by a similar process using N-doped carbon as the substrate (Figs. S2 and S3 in the ESM). As shown in Figs. 1(a) and 1(b), the Pt/NPC electrocatalyst exhibited a 3D porous structure. As shown in the high-angle annular dark-field sca nning TEM (HAADF-STEM) images in Figs. 1(c) and 1(d), monodispersed PtNPs
