| www.editorialmanager.com/nare/default.asp 242 Nano Res. 2017, 10(1): 238–246 ratios of N and P to C were quantitatively determined to be 2% and 0.84%, respectively, from data obtained from XPS. Doping with N and P introduces negatively charged, electron-rich donor states on the carbon surface, which are positioned above the Fermi level and act as local heterogeneou s sites for the nucleation of the deposited Pt; these nucleation sites inhibit the migration and agglomeration of Pt [17]. Characteristic peaks of Pt4f 7/2 and Pt4f 5/2 were observed at 72.2 and 75.4 eV, respectively (Fig. 2(d)). The fitted spectra of Pt 4f shifted to higher binding energies relative to that of bulk Pt, indicating that the electronic structure of Pt is clearly modified as a result of the N and P co-doped carbon substrate. For investigating the electronic state of the Pt species, the XPS spectrum was deconvoluted into 71.8 eV (4f 7/2) and 74.8 eV (4f 5/2), attributed to metallic Pt, and into 72.8 and 76.0 eV, attributed to the 2+ oxidation state of Pt. The relative intensities of Pt0 and Pt2+ were calculated to be 53.4% and 46.6%, respectively [32, 33]. In brief, the above data confirmed that the 3D carbon network was successfully doped with N and P , providing anchoring sites for the nucleation and growth of Pt. Notably, N and P dopants significantly modify the electronic state of PtNP , thereby enhancing the electrocatalytic performance for the HER. The HER electrocatalytic performance of Pt/NPC (1.82 wt.% Pt) was carefull y investigated. Pt/NPC was loaded on the GCE at a density of 0.28 mg·cm −2 and tested in H 2-saturated 0.5 M H 2SO 4 utilizing a typical three-electrode setup. For comparison, NPC, Pt/NC and 20 wt.% Pt/C (Alfa) were also investigated using the same deposition amount and measurement con-dition. Figure 3(a) shows the polarization curves with iR correction. NPC exhibited negligible HER activity, while Pt/NC, Pt/NPC, and commercial 20 wt.% Pt/C exhibited low onset overpotentials of approximately 0 V versus the RHE near the thermodynamic potential of HER. At a geometrical catalytic current density of 20 mA·cm −2, Pt/NC and 20% Pt/C exhibited overpo- tentials of 35.4 and 26.9 mV , respectively. On the other hand, Pt/NPC exhibited a lower overpotential of 21.7 mV at 20 mA·cm −2. Figure 3(b) and Fig. S4 (in the ESM) show the Figure 2  (a) Full XPS spectrum of Pt/NPC and corresponding high-resoluti on XPS spectra of (b) N1s, (c) P2p, and (d) Pt4f in the Pt/NPC electrocatalyst.
