www.theNanoResearch.com ∣www.Springer.com/journal/12274 | Nano Research  243 Nano Res. 2017, 10(1): 238–246 calculated mass specific activities and area-specific activities at different potentials. At an overpotential of 20 mV , Pt/NC exhibited a mass specific activity of 2.1 A·mg −1 Pt and an area-specific activity of 10.0 mA·cm−2, corresponding to values greater than those obtained for commercial 20% Pt/C. By co-doping with N and  P to form Pt/NPC catalysts, HER performance was further improved with a mass specific activity of 3.5 A·mg −1 Pt and an area-specific activity of 18.1 mA·cm−2; these values are 20.6 and 1.9 times greater than those observed for commercial 20% Pt/C (0.17 A·mg−1 Pt and 9.5 mA·cm−2), respectively. Notably, at a high current density of 40 mA·cm−2, Pt/NPC exhibited mass and area specific activities 4.6 and 5.1 times greater than those observed for Pt/NC, respectively. This outstanding electrocatalytic activity of Pt/NPC is attributed to the uniformly dispersed ultrasmall PtNPs (~1.8 nm) on NPC, which provides a large surface area for electrochemical reactions; more importantly, a down- shift of the Pt d-band was observed for the Pt electronic structure modified by N and P dopants, attributed to the donation of charge from the dopant to Pt [33]. Further insights into the detailed mechanism of HER processes were obtained from Tafel plots, determined by the rate-controlling step of the HER. The HER in acidic electrolytes is well known to mainly consist of two steps. The first step is the Volmer reaction, which affords the adsorbed hydrogen atom. The second step is hydrogen desorption, which may proceed via the Tafel or Heyrovsky reaction. Meanwhile, when the Volmer reaction is the rate-limiting step of the HER, a slope of 120 mV·decade −1 should be observed. On the other hand, if the Ta fel or Heyrovsky reaction is the rate-limiting step, Tafel slopes of 30 and 40 mV·decade −1 should be obtained, respectively [34]. In Fig. S5 (in the ESM), the Pt/NPC catalyst exhibited a small Tafel slope of ~36.3 mV·decade−1, comparable to that obtained for 20% Pt /C (Alfa); this result is indicative of a Volmer −Heyrovsky mechanism; the slope obtained is superior to that observed for Pt/NC (~54.8 mV·decade−1), indicative of the absence of P-participated electronic modification. A small value for the Tafel slope renders the Pt/NPC catalyst high HER performance at low overpotentials, making it beneficial for practical applications. Moreover, the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) mea- Figure 3  (a) Polarization curves of NPC, Pt/NC, Pt/NPC, and 20% Pt/C (Alfa); (b) comparis on of the mass specific activity of Pt/NC, Pt/NPC, and 20% Pt/C (Alfa) at various pot entials (vs. RHE). (c) Polarization curves of Pt/NPC before and after CV between − 0.13 and 0.22 V vs. RHE at 100 mV·s−1 for 2,000 cycles. (d) Time dependence of the current density for Pt/NPC at a static overpotential of −8 mV (vs. RHE) for 35 h.
