ical for evaluating the practical application of electrocatalysts for the HER. The long- term cycling robustness of Pt/NPC was measured by continuous cyclic voltammetry (CV) at a high scan rate of 100 mV·s −1 for 2,000 cycles ranging from −0.13 and 0.22 V vs. RHE. As shown in Fig. 3(c), HER activity slightly decreased after 2,000 cycles, indicating superior durability. The morphology of Pt/NPC after durability measurements was also investigated. PtNPs still remained as uniform dispersions without any  agglomeration (Fig. S11 in the ESM). In addition, chronoamperometry at −8 mV (vs. RHE) was perfor- med to further verify the stability of Pt/NPC, and a negligible loss of current density was observed after a successive long-time test period of greater than 35 h (Fig. 3(d)). The strong chemical bond between PtNP and NPC, as well as the chemical inertness of NPC, is responsible for the excellent stability of Pt/NPC. 4 Conclusion In summary, an electrocat alyst composed of PtNPs loaded on a 3D carbon network doped with N and P was developed. Because of the homogeneous dis-tribution of N and P dopants, PtNPs with an average diameter of 1.8 nm were uniformly immobilized on the carbon substrate via high electronic affinity between Pt and C. The as-formed Pt/N PC with a low Pt loading of 1.82 wt.% exhibited a surprisingly high HER per-formance, superior to those observed for Pt/NC and commercial 20% Pt/C. Notably, Pt/NPC exhibited a mass specific activity of 3.5 A·mg −1 Pt, which is 20.6 times greater than that obtained for commercial 20% Pt/C (0.17 A·mg −1 Pt). Furthermore, during chronoamperometry measurement at –8 mV (vs.  RHE), Pt/NPC exhibited superior stability for greater than 35 h with a negligible loss of current density. The strategy utilized a co- doping substrate for decorating ultralow-loaded Pt with an ultrasmall size to achieve high HER catalytic performance results in a significantly high utilization ratio of Pt in this catalyst system, which would open novel avenues for the development of other catalysts, especially for noble-metal-based catalysts. Acknowledgements This work was financiall y supported by National Natural Science Foundation of China (No. 21425103) and Natural Science Foundation of Jiangsu Province (No. SBK201341397). Electronic Supplem entary Material : Supplementary material (SEM, TEM, XPS and electrochemical data) is available in the online version of this article at http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/s12274-016-1281-9.
