Journal of The Electrochemical Society ,166(16) F1314-F1319 (2019) F1315 Figure 1. Chemical structure of the prepared ILs, having the same anion and different cations: (A) 1-butyl-3-ethylimidazolium bromide [Beim][Br] and (B) 1,3-diethylimidazolium bromide [Eeim][Br]. reported results show that changing these parameters affects the ILs adsorption at the electrode’s surface and, consequently, the HER ki-netics, giving a signiﬁcant contribution to the development of moreefﬁcient alkaline water electrolyzers. Experimental Preparation and characterization of the ILs.— Two ILs were pre- pared following the method described in the authors’ previous study. 15 In brief, equimolar amounts of 1-alkylimidazole and 1-bromoalkanemixture in a quartz vessel were irradiated at 240 W and heated at 80°Cfor 7 min using a single-mode microwave reactor. Irradiation time andreaction temperature were increased for 2 min and 10°C for additionalethyl group in the alkyl chain. The resulting mixtures were puriﬁedby liquid-liquid extraction using ethyl acetate. The lower (1-alkyl-3-ethylimidazolium bromide) phase was collected, the solvent removedunder the vacuum and the IL dried for 72 h. This process allowedthe production of high purity 1-butyl-3-ethylimidazolium bromide [Beim][Br] and 1,3-diethylimidazolium bromide [Eeim][Br]. The ILs’ structures (Figure 1) were conﬁrmed by infrared spectroscopy and nu- clear magnetic resonance spectroscopy. 15 Electrochemical measurements.— Electrochemical studies were used to investigate the two prepared ILs as electrolyte additives forthe HER during water electrolysis. The electrolyte solutions were pre-pared by dissolving 1 or 2 vol.% of each IL in 8 M KOH (AnalaRNORMAPUR, 87%) prepared using Millipore water, correspondingto 0.054 μM [Beim][Br] and 0.066 μM [Eeim][Br] or 0.108 μM [Beim][Br] and 0.122 μM [Eeim][Br], respectively. The electrochem- ical measurements were carried out at 25°C in a 125 mL cell usingPAR 273A potentiostat/galvanostat (Princeton Applied Research, Inc.)controlled by PowerSuite software. The standard three-electrode setupwas used, composed of a Pt foil (Metrohm 6.0305.100) with 1 cm 2 of surface area as the working electrode, a 50 cm2Pt mesh (Johnson Matthey) as the counter electrode and a saturated calomel electrode(SCE, Hannah instruments, HI5412) as the reference. Here, Pt has beenused as a model electrode, to make sure that the observed effects weresolely caused by the differences in the electrolyte composition and not due to changes in the electrode material. Electrode potentials were converted to reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) scale by using theformula E RHE=ESCE+0.242+0.059 pH. Thus, all electrode poten- tials given in the manuscript are presented relatively to RHE reference.These potentials are corrected by the positive feedback compensationof the PAR potentiostat; evaluation of the IR-correction based on theEIS data also showed that this ohmic drop is negligible, affecting thetrue working electrode potential by an error less than 1%. The HERperformance was studied by linear scan voltammetry (LSV), scan-ning the potential from the open circuit potential (OCP, ca. 0 V) to−0.38 V at 2 mV s −1, and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). EIS measurements were carried out using a frequency responseanalyzer (Schlumberger SI 1255), at different potentials (from OCPup to−0.38 V), in the 10 −2to 105Hz frequency range, and with an Figure 2. Polarization curves of Pt electrode obtained by LSV at 2 mV s−1in 8 M KOH electrolyte and after addition of (A) 1 vol.% or (B) 2 vol.% of eachstudied ionic liquid. AC potential amplitude of 5 mV . The EIS data were modeled using an equivalent circuit with ZView software (Scribner Associates, Inc.). Results and Discussion The effect of the addition to KOH electrolyte of 1 or 2 vol.% of bromide-based ILs 1-butyl-3-ethylimidazolium bromide [Beim][Br]or 1,3-diethylimidazolium bromide [Eeim][Br] on the HER kinetics was initially investigated by LSV measurements at a scan rate of 2 mV s −1and compared with the case of the IL-free electrolyte. Figure 2 presents the obtained polarization curves, which show that 1 vol.% ofboth ILs, as well as 2 vol.% of [Eeim][Br], do not have a signiﬁcant ef-fect on the electrolysis performance. On the other hand, when 2 vol.%of [Beim][Br] was added to the KOH electrolyte, higher current den-sities associated with the HER are observed. The initial assessment of the effect of ILs addition onto HER ki- netics was done by comparing three key parameters: onset potential,overpotential required to reach the current density of −10 mA cm −2 and Tafel slope. Onset potential, i.e., potential at which current den- sity reaches a value of −1m Ac m−2,16was found to be similar for H E Ri n8MK O H( −0.07 V) and in 8 M KOH +2 vol.% [Beim][Br] (−0.05 V). However, overpotential required to reach current density of−10 mA cm−2was found to be ca. 0.10 V lower in 8 M KOH + 2 vol.% [Beim][Br] ( −0.15 V) than in 8 M KOH ( −0.24 V) evidencing beneﬁcial effect of addition of this IL onto HER efﬁciency. Addition-ally, cathodic current density at a ﬁxed overpotential of −0.20 V in 8MK O H +2 vol.% [Beim][Br] (14 mA cm −2) was found to be double of the value in 8 M KOH (7.0 mA cm−2). From the polarization curves (Figure 2), Tafel plots ( ηvs. log j) can be constructed, as presented in Figure 3.T h eT a f e lp l o t ss h o w correlation coefﬁcients (R2) always higher than 0.99, revealing an excellent adjustment to Tafel analysis. By considering the standardTafel expression, the main HER kinetic parameters obtained for the studied electrolytes are shown in Table I. The addition of the ILs to the IL-free KOH solution inﬂuenced the reaction kinetics and generally led to higher Tafel slopes, except forthe case of 2 vol.% of [Eeim][Br] in which a lower b was obtained.The observed effects of the ILs addition on the charge transfer coefﬁ-cients and j 0values are not very signiﬁcant. Higher Tafel slopes were among the previously reported effects of [Emim]- and salicylate-basedIL additives on the HER kinetics, which also lead to slightly higher ex-change current densities and lower charge transfer coefﬁcients, alongwith higher current densities. 11,17,18 Furthermore, Tafel slope values provide insight into the reaction mechanism. The hydrogen evolution reaction in alkaline solutions isa multi-step process (Eqs. 1–3), 19,20which starts with a discharge, the V olmer step, covering the metal surface with adsorbed protons
