F1316 Journal of The Electrochemical Society ,166(16) F1314-F1319 (2019) Figure 3. Tafel plots for HER at Pt electrode in 8 M KOH electrolyte and after addition of (A) 1 or (B) 2 vol.% of each ionic liquid. (Eq. 1). The reaction may then proceed by one of two possible paths, catalytic recombination of the adsorbed intermediates (MH ads), the Tafel step (Eq. 2), or by an electrodesorption of the adsorbed species via Heyrovský step (Eq. 3).20 H2O+M+e−→MH ads+OH−(V olmer step) [1] MH ads+MH ads→H2+2M/parenleftbig Tafel step/parenrightbig [2] H2O+MH ads+e−→H2+M+OH−/parenleftbig Heyrovsk/primey step/parenrightbig [3] For an αvalue of 0.5 at 25°C, the Tafel coefﬁcient for V olmer, Heyrovský, and Tafel step being the rate-determining step (RDS) is 120, 40 and 30 mV dec−1, respectively.20,21The Tafel slopes obtained were higher than 120 mV dec−1, still suggesting the V olmer step as the RDS of HER in the studied electrolytes. Effects related to dependencyon adsorbed intermediates are generally pointed as the origin of thedifferences between experimental and theoretical Tafel slopes. 22,23 Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy analysis.— The HER was also characterized by impedance measurements in the studiedelectrolytes, performed in a frequency range from 100 kHz to 0.01 Hz,at several applied potentials. The Nyquist and Bode plots obtained at −0.08 V are presented in Figure 4, comparing the impedance response after addition of 1 and 2 vol.% of the ILs with that observed usingthe IL-free KOH solution. Figure 4shows several components of the impedance response, which is greatly affected by the ILs addition. The high-frequency intersection with the Z’ axis for all the elec- trolytes (inset in Figure 4) is attributed to the sum of the resistances of the wiring, the electrolyte, and the electrode material. 11,24All mea- surements were performed in the same conditions, only changing theelectrolyte composition, and therefore it may be concluded that theresistance of the 8 M KOH electrolyte was not signiﬁcantly affectedby the ILs addition. Table I. Kinetic Parameters of HER at Pt Electrode in 8 M KOH and After Addition of 1 or 2 vol.% of Each Tested Ionic Liquid. Electrolyte b/mV dec−1α j0/mA cm−2 8 M KOH 203 0.29 0.63 8MK O H +1v o l . % [Beim][Br] 241 0.25 0.84[Eeim][Br] 369 0.16 3.008MK O H +2v o l . % [Beim][Br] 232 0.25 1.54[Eeim][Br] 186 0.32 0.42Other contributions to the impedance of the system are usually found in similar impedance measurements11,25and are suggested by the Nyquist and Bode plots in Figure 4. The high-frequency relaxation, which is visible in the inset of Figure 4A, in the case of [Beim][Br]– added electrolyte, may be related to the charge transfer process. In the impedance spectra of the other electrolytes, the semi-circle regarding the high-frequency contribution is hidden by the largestone at intermediate frequencies, with the corresponding peaks in thephase angle Bode plots being merged, as was previously reported forsalicylate-based ILs. 18At intermediate frequencies, the dominant re- laxation is probably associated with the adsorbed H. This relaxationshows a remarkable decrease after addition of both quantities of bothILs. A three-fold decrease in all the electrolytes upon addition of2 vol.% of both ILs is observed. A low-frequency contribution is alsosometimes observed, assigned to the oscillation of the H 2concen- tration at the electrode/electrolyte interface.11,25However, the semi- circles regarding this low-frequency relaxation were not usually wellresolved, related to high instability of the measurements for frequen-cies below 0.1 Hz, which originated from the presence of increasedamounts of gas bubbles in the vicinity of the electrode. 18No inductive loop was observed at low frequencies, as it was expected since Tafelanalysis suggests the V olmer step as the RDS. 26 It is clear from Figure 4that the overall impedance of the system associated with the HER is decreased in the electrolytes containing the bromide-based ILs. The sum of the resistances of the high andintermediate frequency relaxations is related with the HER kineticsand its reciprocal value is directly related to the faradaic current den-sity; therefore, the strong reduction of the impedance, if completelyunderstood, has the potential to lead to improvement of the HER per-formance. A detailed analysis of the data is therefore needed in order to an- alyze each of these relaxations individually and to access the effectof the ILs addition in each of them. The impedance data were theninterpreted using the well-known equivalent circuit of Armstrong andHenderson, 20,27,28presented in Figure 5. In this circuit, R sis the ohmic resistance of the solution, R ctis the charge transfer resistance related with the reaction at the working electrode, C dlis the double-layer ca- pacitance of the working electrode, R pis the mass transfer resistance of the adsorbed intermediate H ads, also known as pseudo-resistance, and C pis the pseudo-capacitance of the working electrode. Since the low-frequency relaxation was not observable in most of the obtainedspectra, it was not considered in the ﬁtting of the impedance data us-ing the Armstrong and Henderson equivalent circuit. The obtainedvalues for the ﬁtting parameters of the EIS data are presented inTable II. From the analysis of Table II, it may be seen that the resistance of the electrolyte solution increased with the addition of 1 vol.% of theionic liquids, as previously reported for salicylate-based electrolytes, 18 However, the values observed after addition of 2 vol.% of the ILsare similar to those of the IL-free electrolyte. For both quantities ofILs, higher electrolyte resistances were observed after the addition of[Beim][Br], as compared to [Eeim][Br]. On the other hand, R ctand R pare substantially reduced in IL- added solutions. Despite that the lowest R ct, 2.73/Omega1cm2, is observed using 1 vol.% of [Beim][Br], increasing the amount of IL additivesgenerally leads to lower resistances. This is observed particularly inthe case of R p, which decreased from 165 /Omega1cm2in IL-free KOH to values lower than 50 /Omega1cm2in the additive containing electrolytes. These observations are in agreement with the higher current densityobserved after addition of 2 vol.% of [Beim][Br], as shown in thepolarization curves of Figure 2, suggesting that, at least in the case of this IL, the IL additives indeed enhance the charge and mass transferprocesses for the HER. Regarding the capacitance values, the capacitance of the double layer, C dl, was in the range of 58.1–160 μFc m−2in the additive containing electrolytes, close to previously reported values,11,18,27,29 and always lower than the value of 194 μFc m−2observed for the IL- free KOH solution. The pseudo-capacitance generally showed slightlylower values after IL-addition, of the order of 10 −4Fc m−2, as was ob-
