Journal of The Electrochemical Society ,166(16) F1314-F1319 (2019) F1317 Figure 4. Nyquist (A, B) and Bode (C, D) plots of Pt electrode obtained at −0.08 V in 8 M KOH electrolyte and after addition of (A, C) 1 or (B, D) 2 vol.% of ILs. Insets show the high-frequency region in detail. served for this contribution when using [Emim]- and salicylate-based added electrolytes.11,18 The dependence of the impedance on the applied potential is pre- sented in Figure 6, in the Nyquist (A,B) and Bode (C,D) plots of the [Beim][Br]–added and the IL-free KOH electrolytes. The general ob- servations are similar in the case of the addition of [Eeim][Br]. Theﬁtting results regarding both studied ILs are shown in Table III. The resistance of the electrolyte, R s, seems to slightly increase with the applied potential in all cases, which should be related tothe increasing amount of gas bubbles in the solution. Furthermore,there is no considerable effect of the additives in R sin the entire potential range. On the contrary, it is clearly shown in Figure 6and Table IIIthat the overall impedance systematically decreases with the increasing applied potential in the three electrolytes and that the IL-additives contribute to lower overall impedance, mainly at low applied Figure 5. Armstrong and Henderson equivalent circuit used to ﬁt the data, where R sis the ohmic resistance of the solution, R ctis the charge transfer resistance, C dlthe double layer capacitance, R pthe pseudo-resistance and C p the pseudo-capacitance.potentials. Indeed, the two HER-related resistances, R ctand R p, clearly decrease with applied potential, with the effect of the IL-additives onboth being noticeable at low overpotentials. Regarding the capacitance, C dlshowed values around 150 μF cm−2, with no clear effect of the applied potential, whereas C pis slightly higher, around 500 μFc m−2, and revealed some increasing trend with the increasing applied potential. The IL-additives seem tohave little effect on the capacitance, which, nevertheless, shows somehigher values in the IL-containing electrolytes. The effect of ILs onto HER kinetics has been reported to origi- nate from both the effect of cation and anion. In terms of structuralorganization, ILs are likely to be spatially heterogeneous. Namely,both experimental study and simulations evidenced the existence ofheterogeneous domains in 1,3-dialkyl imidazolium salts formed by Table II. Parameters Obtained from The Impedance Data at −0.08 V for the IL-free KOH Electrolyte and After Addition of 1 and 2 vol.% of the Two ILs at 25°C. Rs//Omega1cm2Rct//Omega1cm2Cdl/μFc m−2Rp//Omega1cm2Cp/μFc m−2 8 M KOH 0.48 40.14 194 165.6 363 8MK O H +1v o l . % [Beim][Br] 0.73 2.730 58.1 109.1 468[Eeim][Br] 0.66 27.40 160 118.0 2798MK O H +2v o l . % [Beim][Br] 0.50 17.56 159 52.90 351[Eeim][Br] 0.48 14.40 117 47.04 323
