F1318 Journal of The Electrochemical Society ,166(16) F1314-F1319 (2019) Figure 6. Nyquist (A,B) and Bode (C,D) plots at 25°C and several applied potentials for the electrolyte with the addition of 2 vol.% [Beim][Br] (A,C) compared with those for the IL-free KOH electrolyte (B,D). side-chain aggregation of the alkyl groups with the cation ring and anions homogeneously arranged. Thus, ILs of the imidazolium groupcan be described as “nanostructured” ﬂuids. This spatial heterogene-ity enlightens the dependence of the density, viscosity, polarity, andconductivity on the alkyl chain length and anion nature. 30Herein, the bromide anion was common, while the cation differed for the twoILs. The increase of the alkyl chain length on N-1 atom of the im- idazolium cation changes the physicochemical properties of the IL, leading to increased viscosity as well as to lower density and electri-cal conductivity. 14On the other hand, increase of the alkyl chain length on N-3 atom has the opposite effect, leading to decreased viscosity butincreased electrical conductivity. In the present study, the ILs have dif- Table III. Parameters Obtained from the Impedance Data at Several Applied Potentials for the IL-Free KOH Electrolyte and After Addition of 2 vol.% of [Beim][Br] and [Eeim][Br]. Applied Potential/V R s//Omega1cm2Rct//Omega1cm2Cdl/μFc m−2Rp//Omega1cm2Cp/μFc m−2 8MK O H −0.08 0.48 40.1 194 166 363 −0.18 0.53 13.9 176 29.1 420 −0.28 0.51 4.93 133 4.13 402 −0.38 0.68 1.13 117 0.15 2310 8MK O H +2 vol.% [Beim][Br] −0.08 0.50 17.6 159 52.9 351 −0.18 0.53 9.06 228 15.4 727 −0.28 0.53 7.27 139 5.96 513 −0.38 0.57 1.59 154 1.09 699 8MK O H +2 vol.% [Eeim][Br] −0.08 0.48 14.4 117 47.0 323 −0.18 0.50 11.7 190 22.0 503 −0.28 0.49 3.42 147 2.82 439 −0.38 0.54 1.03 186 0.64 814ferent length of the alkyl chain on the N-1 atom and the same one on the N-3 atom. The higher current densities observed during the ca-thodic polarization curves recorded in 2 vol.% [Beim][Br] comparedto 2 vol.% [Eeim][Br] (in 8 M KOH) were not expected, having inmind the lower electrical conductivity of the former. This ﬁnding sug-gests that IL’s viscosity and conductivity are not determining factorsin the electrochemical reaction rate. Furthermore, imidazolium-based ILs are known to create orga- nized layers at the electrode/electrolyte interface that were reportedto notably affect the electron transfer kinetics. 30Cation orientation at the electrode surface (vertical, ﬂat, etc.) changes with the potentialapplied to the electrode, with that change further depending on the an-ion and electrode material nature. Thus, it can be assumed that cationadsorption at the electrode and/or cation-metal complex adsorbed in-termediate species account for the differences in the two IL-additives’effect on HER. Namely, surface pre-adsorption of the IL additives wasreported to stabilize the intermediate H atoms generated during theV olmer step and to modify adsorption and charge transfer processesat the metal/electrolyte interface. 11 Conclusions Two bromide-based ILs, 1-butyl-3-ethylimidazolium bromide [Beim][Br] and 1,3-diethylimidazolium bromide [Eeim][Br], wereprepared and investigated as electrolyte additives for the HER in alka-line water electrolysis. Lower overpotential values along with highercurrent densities were obtained after addition of 2 vol.% of [Beim][Br].Generally, the bromide-based additives led to slightly higher Tafelslopes, not signiﬁcant effects on the charge transfer coefﬁcient and toa decrease of the j 0values. On the other hand, impedance measurements revealed a dominant relaxation at intermediate frequencies, related with the adsorbed H, aswell as a strong decrease of the overall impedance of the system in theIL-added electrolytes, showing their ability to improve the electrolyteproperties and to lead to better alkaline electrolysis performances. Although Pt was used in this study as a model electrode, literature reports show an increase of HER efﬁciency with the use of IL additivesand Ni-based electrodes compared to the case of the same IL additivesand Pt electrode. 10Furthermore, Ni electrodes potential window in the presence of IL additives was reported to be notably wider compared tothat of other lower-cost electrodes. 31Thus, it is believed that the herein studied ILs would also lead to improved performance of Ni-basedelectrodes for HER. Further efforts will be devoted to the investigationof the performance of several types of advanced low-cost electrodematerials, including Ni-based ones, on the HER in the developed IL-added electrolytes. Acknowledgments The authors would like to thank Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia (FCT, Portugal) for contract no. IST-ID/153/2018 (L. Amaral) and no. IST-ID/156/2018 (B. Šljuki´ c), as well as for con- tract no. IF/01084/2014/CP1214/CT0003 under IF2014 Programme(D.M.F. Santos). ORCID Diogo M. F. Santos https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7920-2638 César A. C. Sequeira https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7556-2858 Milan Vraneš https://orcid.org/0000-0001-8259-7549 Slobodan Gadžuri´ c https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8145-5239 References 1. M. David, C. Ocampo-Martínez, and R. Sánchez-Peña, J. Eng. Storage ,23, 392 (2019). 2. R. S. El-Emam and H. Ozcan, J. Cleaner Product. ,220, 593 (2019). 3. D. M. F. Santos, B. Šljuki´ c, C. A. C. Sequeira, D. Macciò, A. Saccone, and J. L. Figueiredo, Energy ,50, 486 (2013).
