can be improved through optimizing geometric morphology and electronic structure of the substrate. [13–16] In this regard, nanos- tructured conductive substrates are beneﬁcial to enhanced electro- catalytic performance because of their large surface area and efﬁcient charge transfer path. [17–19] . Metals, metal oxides, covalent-organic frameworks, metal–or- ganic frameworks, and carbon-based materials have been widely employed as substrates to disperse single-atom or nanocluster cat- alysts. [20–24] Among them, carbon-based supports have been highlighted for their high electronic conductivity, cost- effectiveness, and excellent electrochemical stability. [17,25–29] Recently, carbon nanotube (CNT) has been demonstrated as promising substrate material, due to their large speciﬁc surface area, and incomplete coordination of atoms on the surface, leading to an increased active site. [30–32] Remarkable efforts have been made in developing high-performance CNT-supported electrocata- lysts. Lin et al. synthesized N-doped CNT aerogels with Fe single atoms homogeneously dispersed, achieving outstanding Oxygen Reduction Reaction (ORR) performance. [33] Tavakkoli et al. anchored Pt single atoms and nanoclusters on single-walled CNTs, revealing that single-walled CNTs can immobilize Pt atoms more efﬁciently than graphene. [34] However, CNTs reported previously have disordered structure. An ordered CNT array is desired to expe- dite mass transfer by facilitating electrolyte access into active sites and accelerating release of the accumulated gas bubbles during electrocatalysis, especially when operated in high current density condition. [35–37] . Herein, we have successfully synthesized highly ordered verti- calN-doped CNTs on carbon cloth (V-CNTs/CC) using a templated hydrothermal method. To verify the structural advantages of ordered CNTs, hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) performances of well-dispersed Pt nanoclusters are investigated, in comparison with that of Pt/disordered CNTs. As expected, Pt@V-CNTs/CC exhi- bits much more enhanced HER activity than Pt/disordered CNTs. By experiments and simulations, we have demonstrated the ordered CNTs have short electron transport paths, high charge transfer efﬁ- ciency, and excellent gas diffusion capability. 2. Materials and methods 2.1. Preparation of samples Preparation of V-CNTs/CC :Firstly, a commercial carbon cloth was immersed in ethanol and acetone solution respectively to remove oil stains and impurities. After that, about 20 nm of ZnO seed layer was grown on the surface of clean carbon cloth by atomic layer deposition (ALD) (Picosun SUNALE R-200). The V-CNTs/CC was acquired by a two-step process with hydrothermal and chemical vapor deposition (CVD). In the hydrothermal process, 0.025 mol/L Zn(NO 3)2/C16H2O (AR /C2199.0 %,Sinopharm Group) and Hexamethylenetetramine (AR /C2199.0 %,Sinopharm Group) were mixed and stirred for 10 min. The mixture was transferred to a 50 ml reactor in the oven at 95 ℃for 10 h to obtain the ZnO NWs/CC. Following, the precursor was prepared by placing ZnO NWs/CC in a mixed solution of 10 ml DI, 20 ml ethanol and 100 mg Dopamine hydrochloride (AR /C2198 %, aladdin) in the oven at 95℃for 10 h. In the CVD process, the precursor was set in the tube furnace with Ar/H 2atmosphere, and the heating rate was 2℃min/C01and was further increased to 800 ℃for 3 h to gain the V-CNTs/CC. Preparation of CNTs/CC: The CNTs/CC was received by our pre- vious work. [38]. Preparation of Pt@CC, Pt@V-CNTs/CC and Pt@CNTs/CC: Prior to ALD, the CC/V-CNTs/CNTs were rinsed repeatedly with ethanol and deionized water (DI) to remove surface contaminants. Thenthese samples were dried at room temperature after they were transferred to the ALD chamber. Pt Atomic layer deposition was obtained by sequential exposing the samples to MeCpPtMe 3(Strem Chemicals, 99 %) and ozone. Each ALD cycle consisted of an expo- sure time of 10 s, a nitrogen purge time of 25 s and a pulse time of 0.5 s for MeCpPtMe 3at 60 /C176C, and an exposure time of 10 s, a nitro- gen purge time of 25 s, and a pulse time of 1 s for O 3. The ALD chamber was maintained at 280 /C176C. Ultrahigh purity nitrogen (99.999 %) was used as the purge and carrier gas. Finally, 50 cycles of Pt were deposited on the surface of these samples by the ALD method. 2.2. Materials characterizations The morphologies and elemental distribution of samples were characterized by SEM (Geminutesi SEM 300) and TEM (FEI Talos F200X). XPS (Kratos AXIS Ultra DLD spectrometer) was used to investigate the composition and chemical state of surface elementswith Pt@V-CNTs/CC and V-CNTs/CC. The phase of the samples was identiﬁed by X-ray diffraction (Bruker D8 Advance) the K awave from 10/C176to 80/C176. 2.3. Electrochemical measurements The electrochemical measurements were conducted using the electrochemistry workstation (CHI 760E) with a voltage window of/C00.5 to /C00.1 V. A three-electrode cell system was employed by incorporating an Ag/AgCl electrode as the reference electrode, a platinum sheet as the counter-electrode and the as-prepared electrodes (Pt@CC, Pt@V-CNTs/CC and Pt@CNTs/CC) as the working electrodes. High-purity N 2gas was employed to purge the system for 15 min before hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) test. Electro- chemical impedance spectra (EIS) was measured at /C00.283 V for HER with a frequency range from 10-1to 105Hz. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) with the scan rate of 50 mV s/C01was tested in 0 . 5MH 2SO4solution. The stability of a catalyst was studied by chronoamperometry at /C00.283 V for 25 h. 2.4. Simulation of the electric ﬁeld contribution in COMSOL The simpliﬁed 3D structure models between the electrodes with array/disordered structure and electrolyte based on COMSOL was established to clarify the structure effect in charge transport/ the release of gaseous products taken the HER as the typical reac- tion. Particularly, the sizes with these simulations are based on physical sizes according to SEM images. Meanwhile, these models only show that an ideal situation does not fully reﬂect the actual system. 1 M H 2SO4was ﬁlled in the gap between electrodes. The electrochemical HER process was simulated steady-state in a three-dimensional space region (300*300*400 nm) and calculated by using a secondary electrochemical module. The physical param- eters of the two models are consistent. 3. Results and discussion Fig. 1 a schematically illustrates the preparation of V-CNTs on Carbon Cloth (CC) (V-CNTs/CC) by template-assisted method and subsequent carbonization treatment. Initially, one-dimensional ZnO nanowire arrays (NWs) were grown vertically on carbon ﬁbers via ALD and a facile hydrothermal method, which were identiﬁed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Fig. S1 a and b show that the ZnO seed layer was uniformly deposited. ZnO NWs with diam- eters ranging from 50 to 80 nm and lengths ranging from 2 to 3 lm are ordered distributed on CC. Subsequently, dopamine hydrochlo- ride was coated on the surfaces of the NWs as a carbon precursor inH. Zhang, Y. Liu, X. Xu et al. Materials & Design 224 (2022) 111329 2
