a neutral environment. Carbonization was conducted in the Ar/H 2 atmosphere to enhance the graphitization of CNTs as well as to remove the ZnO template, which was conﬁrmed by X-ray diffrac- tion measurement (XRD) ( Figure S2 ). In comparison to the ZnO NWs, the CNTs/CC have a shrinking diameter. Fig. 1 b and c, show that oriented and ordered V-CNTs/CC with diameters of around 30–50 nm were anchored on the nanoﬁbers, displaying superior conformality. For comparison, the typical disordered CNTs grown on carbon cloth (labeled as CNTs/CC) were prepared ( Fig. S3 a and b), and the long CNTs chaotically dispersed on the CC. These unique V-CNTs/CC with high speciﬁc surface area and shorter lengths can remarkably facilitate the accessibility of active sites, enhance elec- trons/reactants’ transport capability, and increase the release of gaseous products.To validate the structural advantages of ordered V-CNTs/CC, a typical hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) with Pt nanoclusters as catalyst was conducted as a proof of concept. An equal amount of Pt nanoclusters were deposited on V-CNTs/CC and CNTs/CC by ALD. Nitrogen/carbon defects are essential for immobilizing and forming dispersed platinum active sites while preventing aggrega- tion, and theoretical simulations have demonstrated that these sites have low energy barriers. Pt is mainly deposited at sites of N defects. The low-magniﬁcation transmission electron micro- scopy (TEM) image shows that the Pt nanoclusters with diameters of around 1–3 nm are homogeneously distributed on V-CNTs/CC (Fig. 1 d). In addition, it also displays that the thickness of the V- CNTs/CC is 3 nm, which is beneﬁcial to electron transport. The high-resolution TEM image ( Fig. 1 e) of the Pt nanoclusters presents Fig. 1. (a) The Schematic diagram of preparation with V-CNTs/CC. (b, c) SEM of V-CNTs/CC. (d, e) TEM images of Pt@V-CNTs/CC.H. Zhang, Y. Liu, X. Xu et al. Materials & Design 224 (2022) 111329 3
