a lattice stripe spacing of 0.226 nm, corresponding to the (111) crystal plane of the face-centered cubic phase of Pt. [39] The selected area electron diffraction pattern in the inset of Fig. 1 e indi- cates that the Pt nanoclusters are polycrystalline. STEM image and the corresponding energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) element map- pings demonstrate the uniform distribution of Pt, N and C elements in Pt@V-CNTs/CC ( Fig. S4 a-d), further illustrating the successful incorporation of Pt and N into V-CNTs/CC, where the N-doping can construct strong Lewis base sites, improving the interface con- tact of electrode/ electrolyte and reducing the contact resistance. [40] In addition, the Pt loading of the samples was accurately determined with inductively coupled plasma (ICP), in which The Pt weight-loading of the reference catalysts: Pt/C, Pt@CC, Pt@CNTs/CC and Pt@V-CNTs/CC are 0.02, 0.03, 0.04 and 0.06lgc m/C02, respectively. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra of Pt@V-CNTs/ CC and V-CNTs/CC were exploited to investigate the chemical state and electronic structure change of surface elements after Pt incor- poration. Survey spectra ( Figure S5 ) show the presence of O, C and N elements in V-CNTs/CC and CNTs/CC. The radio of N in V-CNTs and CNTs is 2.08 % and 10.85 %. The contents of Pyrrolic N, Pyridinic N and graphitic N in V-CNTs are 48.33 %, 16.35 % and 35.33 %, respectively. Meanwhile, the contents of Pyrrolic N, Pyridinic N and graphitic N in CNTs are 43.81 %, 38.23 % and 17.96 %, respec- tively. The high-resolution C XPS spectrum ( Fig. 2 a) of V-CNTs/CC can be split into three dominantly peaks at 284.6, 285.35 and 287.72 eV, corresponding to C AC/C@C, CAN, and C AO, respec- tively. [41] Although the CNTs/CC has a higher ratio of pyridinic-Nand graphitic N in comparison to V-CNTs/CC, the Pt is more easily anchored on V-CNTs/CC due to the well-dispersed 3D array CNTs (V-CNTs) can expose effectively the nitrogen defect sites, while the disordered CNTs are stacked and entangled with each other, and the active sites are hidden, which can be veriﬁed by the ICP results. The Pt incorporation induced the shift of C 1s peak of Pt@V-CNTs/CC, impacting the electronic structure of the V-CNTs/ CC. As for N 1s XPS spectrum ( Fig. 2 b) of the V-CNTs/CC, three peaks located at 398.59, 400.86 and 404.63 eV are assigned to the pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, and graphitic N, respectively. [42] It is worth noting that the position and shape of the N 1s peaks signif- icantly changed in Pt@V-CNTs/CC, owing to pyridinic-N can gener- ate numerous defects and active sites, showing a good binding afﬁnity for Pt. [43] The Pt 4f XPS spectra ( Fig. 2 c) of the Pt@V- CNTs/CC consisted of two pairs of doublets root in the spin–orbit splitting of the 4f 5/2 and 4f 7/2. The major peaks (74.77 eV and 71.21 eV) were attributed to metallic Pt, and the minor peaks (75.55 eV and 71.82 eV) were assigned to oxide species. [44] The positive shift to higher binding energies in the Pt 4f peaks for Pt@V-CNTs/CC compare to Pt/CC ( Figure S6 ) further conﬁrms the strong metal-support interaction between Pt and N, modifying the electronic and chemical states of Pt, which enhance the cat- alytic property of the Pt@V-CNTs/CC. The Pt is easily oxidized on the CC sample due to the simple structure of the CC. Therefore, the degree of Pt2+on Pt@CC is very much more pronounced than for Pt@V-CNTs/CC. The ratios of Pt2+/Pt0in Pt@V-CNTs/CC, Pt@CNTs/CC and Pt@CC were 2 ( Fig. 2 c), 1 ( Fig. 2 d) and 9 ( Fig- ure S6 ), respectively ( Figure S6 ). Compared with Pt@CNTs/CC, Fig. 2. The XPS spectra of (a) C 1s and (b) N 1s for Pt@V-CNTs/CC and V-CNTs/CC. (c) Pt 4f for Pt@V-CNTs/CC. (d) Pt 4f for Pt@CNTs/CC.H. Zhang, Y. Liu, X. Xu et al. Materials & Design 224 (2022) 111329 4
