Pt@V-CNTs/CC has a higher ratio of Pt2+/Pt0because the Pt on an ordered nanotube is more easily oxidized than the disorder nanotubes. HER performance of the Pt@V-CNTs/CC electrode was evaluated using a typical three-electrode system. The Pt@CNTs/CC and Pt@CC were tested for comparison. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves indicate that Pt@V-CNTs/CC displays an excellent catalytic activity ( Fig. 3 a), showing an onset potential of zero, and lower overpotential with 24.34 mV vs RHE at 10 mA cm/C02, over that of the Pt@CNTs/CC (57.09 mV) and Pt@CC (66.59 mV). Speciﬁcally, Pt@V-CNTs/CC demonstrates signiﬁcant advantages at a large cur- rent density with 59.83 mV vs RHE at 100 mA cm/C02much better than the Pt@CNTs/CC (77.66 mV) and Pt@CC (135.78 mV). The good HER property can be attributed to the vertically ordered structure promoting the rapid exchange of ions and accelerating the diffusion rate of the gas. In addition, the HER performance of Pt30@V-CNTs/CC, Pt50@V-CNTs/CC and Pt70@V-CNTs/CC was measured to determine the optimal platinum loading, as shown inﬁgure S7 , clearly, the Pt50@V-CNTs/CC showed the best catalytic performance. The Tafel slope for Pt@V-CNTs/CC is only 27 mV dec-1 (Fig. 3 b), which is smaller than that of Pt@CNTs/CC (29 mV dec-1), Pt@CC (33 mV dec-1) and Pt/C (38 mv dec-1) suggesting the Volmer-Tafel mechanism [45,46] and enhanced HER kinetic for Pt@V-CNTs/CC. The stability of Pt@V-CNTs/CC was assessed by i-t chronoamperometric tests ( Fig. 3 c). The Pt@V-CNTs/CC electrode displays an ignorable loss of current density after 25 h in contrast to that of the Pt@CNTs/CC, validating its superb stability. Further- more, the HER activity of the Pt@V-CNTs/CC is superior to most of the reported literature ( Table S1 ).Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was carried out at a voltage of /C00.283 V to analyze the reaction kinetics, as shown inFig. 4 a. Both of the Nyquist plots display a typical feature of semicircle, representing the charge transfer resistance. [47,48] The EIS was modeled with a proper Randle equivalent circuit shown in the inset of Fig. 4 a, where RXrepresents the contact resis- tance between the Pt and supports and the electrolyte resistance, Rctrepresents the charge transfer resistance. As shown in Fig. 4 b, an obvious decrease in the RXwas observed for Pt@V-CNTs/CC (3.252 X) compared with Pt@CNTs/CC (3.743 X), indicating the good contact between Pt and V-CNTs. In addition, the Rctof Pt@V-CNTs/CC (0.09 X) was smaller than that of the Pt@CNTs/CC (0.13 X), which can be attributed to ordered CNTs being uniformly dispersed on the CC matrix. Fig. 4 c and d display the advantages of building order architectural arrays for realizing highly efﬁcient and durable catalytic reactions. The short CNTs of the Pt@V-CNTs/CC shorten electron transport distance and accelerate electron collec- tion efﬁciency. While the interlaced disordered CNTs of the Pt@CNTs/CC with tanglesome electron transport path increases the electron recombination rate. As for ion transport, the volume space between arrays can be used as the access for electrolyte ion transport, [49–51] which is further elucidated by the effective diffusion coefﬁcient [52,53] : Deff¼Dee s where Derepresents the intrinsic diffusion coefﬁcient; erepresents the electrode porosity, and srepresents the tortuosity factor. Since Deis constant, and the difference of the evalue for Pt@V-CNTs/CC and Pt@CNTs/CC is negligible. Obviously, Pt@CNTs/CC has a high s Fig. 3. (a) HER (after iR corrected) polarization curves of Pt/C(Pt/C was prepared on carbon cloth by drop coating), Pt@V-CNTs/CC, Pt@CNTs/CC and Pt@CC in 0. 5MH 2SO4. (b) Tafel plots derived from the corresponding HER polarization curves. (c) i-t chronoamperometric curve for Pt@V-CNTs/CC and Pt@CNTs/CC during HER pr ocess for 25 h. (d) Comparison of the overpotentials at 10 mA cm/C02and the Tafel slope with recently reported HER catalysts in acidic conditions.H. Zhang, Y. Liu, X. Xu et al. Materials 