value, leading to a low effective diffusion coefﬁcient. In addition, the ion diffusion time in the 3D nanostructures can be obtained by the ﬂowing equation [54,55] : t¼L2 D where Lrepresents the ion diffusion length, and Drepresents the ion diffusivity. Pt@V-CNTs/CC presents a short ion diffusion length compared with Pt@CNTs/CC, demonstrating a short ion diffusion time and a faster ion diffusion rate. COMSOL simulations were conducted to visualize the local hydrogen ion density distribution in Pt@V-CNTs/CC and Pt@CNTs/CC. The speciﬁc COMSOL model is shown in Figure S8 . For an HER reaction, the horizontal cross-sections ( Fig. 4 e and f) show that the hydrogen ion with 1 M is homogeneous throughout the ordered CNTs, demonstrating the excellent ion permeability of the architectural arrays. In contrast, the ions concentration ﬂuctu- ates in a wide range of 0.2–1 M for the chaotic structure because the interlaced microstructure hindered the mass transfer of elec- trolytes. The hydrogen ion distribution diagram of another hori- zontal cross-section ( Figure S9 a and b) and the longitudinalcross-sections ( Figure S8 c and d) further conﬁrm this phenomenon. The active materials tend to peel off from supports during the diffusion of large amounts of gas, leading to the deterioration of electrocatalytic performance. [56] Therefore, the gas transport per- formance within electrodes is crucial for promoting the reaction kinetic. [57,58] To validate the experimentally measured HER per- formance of the Pt@V-CNTs/CC electrode, we conducted a COMSOL simulation to elucidate the underlying mechanisms of gas release. Figure S10 shows the calculated distributions of H 2at equilibrium in HER devices consisting of Pt@V-CNTs/CC and Pt@CNTs/CC elec- trodes in a 1 M H 2SO4electrolyte. The H 2distribution diagram of the longitudinal cross-sections ( Fig. 5 a and b) shows that the H 2 distribution between the oriented arrays is very uniform, and high concentration gas distribution was observed near the top of V- CNTs due to gas accumulation. While, for disordered CNTs, the dis- tribution of H 2is extremely uneven. The hydrogen distribution curve at the top of V-CNTs is shown in Fig. 5 c, a regular hydrogen distribution curve shows a high concentration of hydrogen dis- tributed at the top of the V-CNTs due to the gas accumulation, indi- cating the excellent gas released property of the 3D order nanoarrays structure. In the case of the disordered CNTs ( Fig. 5 d), Fig. 4. (a) EIS Nyquist plots and (b) the comparison of RXand Rctfor Pt@V-CNTs/CC and Pt@CNTs/CC. Diagram of ion/electron diffusion in (c) Pt@V-CNTs/CC and (d) Pt@CNTs/ CC. The hydrogen ion distribution simulations of around (e) Pt@V-CNTs/CC and (f) Pt@CNTs/CC.H. Zhang, Y. Liu, X. Xu et al. Materials & Design 224 (2022) 111329 6
