the H 2with a low concentration is irregularly distributed, which will affect the intensity of subsequent reactions due to the insufﬁ- cient exposure of the active sites. The H 2distribution diagram of the horizontal cross-sections ( Figure S10 and S11) further conﬁrms the uniformity of gas distribution in Pt@V-CNTs/CC compared with Pt@CNTs/CC. The enhanced catalytic property and stability also derive from the support material’s morphology due to nanostructure can facil- itate bubble convection away from the catalyst surface and prevent the bubbles from accumulating and damaging the electrode. Gen- erally, large bubbles will pin at the planar electrode surface ( Fig- ure S12 ).[35] Because of the effect of surface adhesion, reducing the effective electrode surface area. As shown in Fig. 5 e, bubble size gradually increases during the gas migration from the root to the top of the V-CNTs. The ordered void distribution provides a regular path for bubble migration. 3D nanostructures, particularly NWs and CNTs, tend to burst these more giant bubbles due to surface tension. [59] Most importantly, the adequate transportation of the bubbles away from the electrode surface provides an excellent solid /C0liquid contact interface for the catalytic reaction. In con- trast, the disordered Pt@CNTs/CC with increased effective elec- trode surface area can also burst the gas bubbles. However, the intricate arrangement of the CNTs leads to a complex bubble trans- port path, in which the bubbles are blocked in the process of trans- mission and small bubbles are captured to form large bubbles, resulting in catalytic instability and decreased HER performance (Fig. 5 f). As shown in Fig. 5 g-i and Video S1, the large bubbles and slow gas release rate were observed in the Pt@CC catalyst due to the large bubble adhesion force on a planar electrode. To see if there is a difference in bubble size and formation for the same geometric current density, we observed the bubble situation of different structures as shown in the video S2 at 100 mA cm/C02. Pt@V-CNTs/CC produces a large number of bubbles with small sizes. At the same time, the bubbles of Pt@CNTs/CC are slightly lar- ger. Simultaneously,the bubble formation rate of Pt@CC is rela- tively slow. Meanwhile, the corresponding voltage–time diagram (Figure S13 ) at constant current density indicates that Pt@CC cor-responds to a larger voltage. Both the Pt@V-CNTs/CC and Pt@CNTs/CC catalysts release the tiny bubbles, beneﬁting from the puncturing effect of 3D structure on bubbles. In addition, the intensity of bubble release for Pt@V-CNTs/CC is much higher than that of the Pt@CNTs/CC catalysts, which can be attributed to the architectural arrays providing fast gas transport path. 4. Conclusion In summary, we have successfully fabricated N-doped vertically aligned CNTs anchored on carbon cloth as a superior support for embedding single-atom or nanocluster catalysts. The well- dispersed Pt nanoclusters at this V-CNTs exhibit much better HER performance (24.34 mV vs RHE at 10 mA cm/C02and 59.83 mV vs RHE at 100 mA cm/C02) compared with that of Pt/disor- dered CNTs (57.09 mV vs RHE at 10 mA cm/C02and 77.66 mV vs RHE at 100 mA cm/C02). Through structural characterizations and COM- SOL simulations, we have attributed the improved HER to synergis- tic effects: (1) The short CNTs/CC facilitate the rapid electron transfer transporting from materials catalysts to the collectors, preventing the electron recombination. (2) The highly aligned architectures of V-CNTs can more active sites to electrolyte and facilitates ion transport. Simultaneously, ordered void distribution facilitates the ion transport. (3) The hierarchically ordered sup- ports can expedite the release of accumulated bubbles, especially at a high current density state. The V-CNTs/CC designed in this work can serve as general support for various single-atom or nan- ocluster catalysts for high-performance electrocatalysis. In addi- tion, the diameter of the nanotubes is another crucial point inﬂuencing ion and bubble transport. Maybe smaller nanotubes can more easily facilitate the transport of ions and bubbles. Data availability Data will be made available on request. Fig. 5. The concentration of H 2is the distribution of its formation and diffusion stability on the surface of (a) V-CNTs/CC and (b) CNTs/CC. The concentration distribution of H 2 from left to right above the XZ cross section along the X-axis with (c) Pt@V-CNTs/CC and (d) Pt@CNTs/CC. Diagram of bubble diffusion on (e) Pt@V-CNTs/C C and (f) Pt@CNTs/ CC surface. The optical image of (g) Pt@V-CNTs/CC, (h) Pt@CNTs/CC and (i) Pt@CC with a constant voltage of 300 mV in 0.5 M H 2SO4.H. Zhang, Y. Liu, X. Xu et al. Materials & Design 224 (2022) 111329 7
