Applied Surface Science 597 (2022) 153554 2various modification strategies have been consistently reported over the years. Among several reported photocatalysts, metal molybdates are gaining significant attention nowadays. Their high electronic and atomic stability, non-toxicity, and unique photoluminescence properties make them competitive for solar energy conversion and storage appli - cations. In particular, silver molybdates (Ag2MoO 4) are of great interest for photocatalysis applications owing to their exotic electronic structure, high electrical conductivity, better optical properties, and high aqueous stability. In recent years, various attempts have been made to synthesise morphologically diverse silver molybdates (Ag2MoO 4) nanostructures and they have been studied for various photo-oxidative applications ranging from dye and antibiotic degradation to wastewater treatment. For instance, Kumar et al, have developed potato-like silver molybdate (Ag2MoO 4) microstructures by hydrothermal approach and tested for ciprofloxacin (CIP) degradation under UV light illumination. [2] Simi- larly, Sousa et al have synthesised β-Ag2MoO 4 microcrystal and studied RhB degradation under UV light. These reports focus on the photo- oxidative nature of Ag2MoO 4 under UV light. [3] However, it was observed that the decoration of Ag nanoparticles (NPs) on Ag2MoO 4 could extend light absorptivity in the visible region. Li et al have syn- thesised Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 nanocomposite by in-situ reduction of cubic Ag2MoO 4 by NaBH 4 and demonstrated CIP degradation under visible light illumination. [4] The improved photocatalysis performance was mainly attributed to the surface plasmon resonance effect of Ag NPs that extends light absorptivity and simultaneously enhances electron-hole generation. Similarly, different heterojunctions of Ag2MoO 4 such as Ag2MoO 4/AgBr/Ag, [5] Ag@Ag 2MoO 4/AZO, [6] and WO 3/butterfly- like Ag2MoO 4 [7] further demonstrated the novel applicability of Ag2MoO 4 for photocatalytic application. However, the functional ac- tivity and photocatalytic mechanism of the Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 nano- composite have never been examined for hydrogen production. The p- type semiconducting nature of Ag2MoO 4, the non-availability of a pro- ton reduction site, high charge carrier recombination efficiency, and inappropriate band edge potential make it less competitive for photo - catalytic hydrogen production. Therefore, it is significantly important to propose a new facile method to fabricate Ag2MoO 4 based nano- composite with sufficient electron-hole separation and proton reduction active sites. One possible solution towards efficient electron-hole sepa- ration is to create anion defects on the oxide surface wherein holes are trapped and provide free electrons. The increased oxygen vacancies affect the crystallinity of the compound, leading to the formation of a crystalline core and an amorphous shell. This disorder –order junction prevents the photogenerated charge carrier recombination and accel - erates the photo-redox reaction in photocatalysis. Therefore, to intro- duce the oxygen vacancies in the Ag2MoO 4 nanostructure, phosphomolybdic (PMA) was intently selected as the molybdenum- precursor solution in the current manuscript. The PMA is a class of metal –oxygen clusters which are profoundly used to create control anion vacancies in the nanostructure. Simultaneously, its superior reversible-redox properties, high aqueous stability, and excellent electron-capture properties will strengthen the photocatalytic properties of the nanocomposite. For instance, Sha et al, have reported enhanced photocatalytic hydrogen production with POM (Polyoxometalates - class of well-defined metal –oxygen cluster anion) incorporated Ag2PO4/GO (Graphene oxide) composite. [8] Zareie-Darmian et al. used theoretical and practical methodologies to introduce the electronic structure of β- Ag2MoO 4 and Ag2O nanocrystals for the application of electrochemical water splitting and CO2 reduction. [9] Furthermore, the addition of a co- catalyst enhances the rate of hydrogen production by trapping the photogenerated electrons for reduction reactions. Among the transition metals, platinum (Pt) is widely used for hydrogen evolution activity due to higher work function and lower M␀H bond strength. Additionally, noble metal (Pt) is used for hydrogen production, which enhances the active site for photocatalytic performance by inhibiting the recombi - nation of photo-generated charge carriers. Photo-deposited Pt NPs on Ag2MoO 4 surface were used as co-catalysts in the current composite. In this study, we have developed spherical shaped Ag2MoO 4 micro - particles by using a simple and novel UV-assisted photoreduction tech- nique by using PMA and silver nitrate as molybdenum and silver precursors. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first time we report the application of Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 catalytic properties for photocatalytic hydrogen production. Moreover, the synthesis of spherical Ag2MoO 4 using the photoreduction method is unique and will significantly contribute towards the development of a morphological diverse Ag2MoO 4 framework for various photocatalytic applications. The physico-chemical characteristics of the spherical Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 com- posite were thoroughly investigated by XRD, SEM, TEM, FT-IR, and XPS analytical characterization techniques. Subsequently, the performance of Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 composites for photocatalytic water reaction is studied using ethanol as sacrificial donor and Pt-as co-catalyst under UV–visible light irradiations. Further, the controlled spherical morphological characteristics of Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 using a relatively simple approach for fabrication provide an opportunity for creativity for energy application. 2.Methods and materials 2.1. Materials and general synthetic strategy Dodeca molybdophosphoric acid (H3MO12O40P⋅xH2O, PMA 99%), silver nitrate (AgNO 3 – 99%), ethanol (C2H5OH) used for the prepara - tion of photocatalyst were of analytical grade. procured from Merck India Pvt Ltd. These chemicals were used without any further purifica - tion. The water used for synthesis and evaluation experiments was deionized water collected from Millipore Q water system. 2.2. Preparation of Agx(I)/PMo 12 heterostructure 2.2.1. Preparation of precuror solution PMA (0.1 M) solution was deaerated with nitrogen gas for 30 min under continuous stirring (400 rpm) at 25 ±5 •C. After purging, solu- tion was covered and stored in dark place. 2.2.2. Preparation of nanocomposite The Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 catalyst was prepared by conventional photo - deposition method. PMA (100 mL), AgNO 3 (5 M) and ethanol (5 mL) were mixed under continuous stirring for 10 min, then allowed to age for 30 min and the mixture turns mixture slightly cloudy. This suspension was irradiated with UV–Vis light (F280 nm, 450 W medium pressure lamp) for 2 h at the temperature of 25 ±5 •C. The resulting colour changes from yellow to greyish black indicating the formation of Agx(I)/ PMo 12 nanocomposites. The suspension was filtered by pyrex filter as- sembly with washing using ethanol and recovered sample is dried at 60 •C for 24 h. 2.3. Photocatalytic hydrogen production experimental setup The experiments of photocatalytic hydrogen generation were per- formed in a 500 mL double jacketed, three necked and immersion well lamp photochemical reactor (Lelesil Innovative Systems, India). A 450 W medium pressure lamp (Hg lamp) was used as the light source (immerged middle of the reactor). The effective irradiation area for the reactor was ~ 75 cm2. The reactor was connected to the chiller to maintain the room temperature and cool off lamp. The hydrogen evolved from the system under continuous stirring was analysed by Gas Chromatography (Shimadzu GC-2014) equipped with a thermal con- ductivity detector (TCD). The column used was molecular sieve-5 Å and carrier gas was nitrogen. The samples were injected in continuous mode by using fully automatic Gas Sampling Valve (Valco make) connected to the GC system. A. Anjum Moinuddin et al.
