Applied Surface Science 597 (2022) 153554 32.4. Photocatalytic hydrogen generation experiments 2.4.1. Photocatalytic experiments with different % loading of Pt The photocatalytic experiments were performed in the setup as dis- cussed above in section 2.3. The reactions were performed in 400 mL aqueous solution containing 5% ethanol, 150 mg of Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 pho- tocatalyst loaded with 0.31%, 0.62%, 1.25% and 1.87% of platinum. Before the reaction, the reactive suspension was deaerated with nitrogen gas for 0.5 h to maintain inert atmosphere free from oxygen and other gaseous. The suspension was then irradiated under 450 W Hg medium pressure mercury lamp (MPML), and the generated hydrogen was ana- lysed using a gas chromatograph (GC- Shimadzu 2014, nitrogen as a carrier gas). 2.4.2. Photocatalytic experiments with different concentration of ethanol water ratio The photocatalytic experiments with different concentration of ethanol water were performed by using different concentration of ethanol (0%, 5%, 25%, 50%, 75% and 100%) with fixed dose of photocatalyst. 2.5. Instrumentation and their methods The surface topology and the arrangement of NPs at an atomic level was analysed by a high-resolution transmission electron microscope (HR-TEM, FEI TECHNAI G2 F20 at 200 kv). The samples for electron microscopy were prepared by dispersing the powder form of catalyst sample in ethanol and loading a very dilute suspension on carbon-coated Cu grids. The crystal structure of a nanocomposite was analysed using powder X-ray diffraction (XRD, RIGAKU MiniFlex II) with Cu-K α radi- ation at 30 kV and 15 mA with a monochromator. The range of 2θ was 20 to 90•, the scanning speed was 3•. min␀1. The standard JCPDS database was used to retrieve the crystallographic nature of the analyzed sample. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, X-Ray001 400 µm) was used to study the composition of elements and the chemical states of nanocomposite. Binding energies were normalized by the adventitious carbon (C 1 s – 286.4 eV) as a reference. The Fourier transform infrared spectrometry (FT-IR, BRUKER Model – 152 Vertex-70 spectrophotom - eter) was used to obtain information about the functional groups present in the nanocomposites using KBr pellet technique. Photoluminescence (PL) analysis was done by SL170 (ELICO) spectrofluorometer. The band structure and optical properties of the synthesized nanocomposites was examined by UV–visible diffuse reflectance spectroscopy (UV–Vis-DRS, Agilent, Cary 5000). The photocatalytic experiment was performed in triplicate and by computing the standard deviation values, error bars assigned. 3.Results and discussion 3.1. Synthesis of Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 nanocomposite The Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 and Pt-Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 nanocomposite was obtained by novel photoreduction approach under the illumination of UV–visible light, schematically represented in Fig. 1. It is known that diffusion rate, irradiation time, intermolecular force of attraction and reduction kinetics play a significant role to determine the morphology of composite. Particularly, in the current approach, slow diffusion rate of metal ions would lead to the formation of Ag- MoO 4- units and growth of these unit would results into spherical morphology. However, comparatively weak reduction kinetics and larger diffusion length may not able to reduce all metallic ions and hence, partially reduced means Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 framework developed in current synthesis protocol. The platinum NPs (Pt) were further deposited on Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 composite using photo-deposition method to enhance photocatalytic performance by reducing the recombination rate. The physico-chemical characterization of resultant Pt/Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 com- posite has been described as follow: 3.2. Crystallographic study of the photocatalyst The X-ray diffraction pattern of Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 and Pt/Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 were illustrated in Fig. 2 (a) and (b) respectively. The diffraction peaks situated at 2θ 31.84 ⁰, 33.29 ⁰, 38.62 ⁰, 47.83 ⁰, 50.9⁰ and 55.80 ⁰ can be indexed to (311), (222), (400), (422), (511) and (440) facets of cubic β-Ag2MoO 4 with lattice constant of a b c 0.931 nm (JCPDS # 08–0473). [5] While, XRD signals located at 2θ 38.11 ⁰, 44.27 ⁰ and 64.42 ⁰ corresponding to (111), (200) and (220) facets represent the formation of metallic Ag NPs on silver molybdate surface (JCPDS # 04–0783). [10] However, characteristics peak of metallic Pt was remained undetected in diffraction profile of Pt/Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 [Fig. 2 (b)], due to lower abundance and weak resolution of instruments. The absence of extra diffraction peaks corroborates the purity of synthesized nanocomposites. 3.3. Morphology and transmission electron microscopy Surface morphology of as-synthesized Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 composites was studied with scanning electron microscope. The well-developed Fig. 1.Schematic representation of synthesis of Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 and Pt-Ag/Ag 2MoO 4.  A. Anjum Moinuddin et al.
