Applied Surface Science 597 (2022) 153554 7PL emission intensity has been observed upon Pt NP incorporation in Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 composite that indicates the effective charge transport from semiconductor to metal domain. Being Pt as electron sink, it leads to quenching in emission intensity which could beneficial for efficient proton reduction at metallic Pt interface. [21–23] 3.6. Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution Photo-assisted water splitting activity of PMA based as-synthesized composites was tested under illumination of UV–visible light (450 W) using ethanol as a sacrificial donor, the obtained trend of hydrogen evolution is presented in Fig. 6. It is observed that water-splitting ability of bare PMA is so weak which gets profound with the incorporation of metallic Ag and Pt NPs. The photocatalyst Ag/PMA wherein metallic Ag acts as co-catalyst have exhibited HER of 8.4 ×10-3 mmol/h while Pt/PMA composite delivered 1.7 ×10-2 mmol/h of hydrogen. This obvious increment indicated the higher proton reduction ability of metallic Pt compared with Ag. It is well known that metallic platinum acts as an electron sink that provides a more active site for hydrogen production. [24,25] In addition to individual performance, a synergy of metallic Ag and Pt with PMA has been studied by evaluating in-situ Ag/Pt/PMA and recovered Pt- Ag/PMA i.e. Pt-Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 nanocomposite performance for photo - catalytic H2 evolution under similar reaction conditions. The Pt-Ag/ Ag2MoO 4 have exhibited the highest hydrogen evolution of 6 ×10-2 mmol/h which is 1.49 times higher than Ag-Pt/PMA in-situ composite (4 ×10-2 mmol/h) and much higher than the rest of PMA based composite. In Pt/Ag/Ag 2MoO 4, subsequently, photo-deposited Pt NPs may accel - erate its performance compared to Ag/Pt/PMA in-situ composite. The well-separated metallic Pt domain on Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 surface can lead to efficient charge separation and provides more active sites for hydrogen reduction. [26] On contrary, simultaneously uncontrolled growth of Pt and Ag NPs in Ag/Pt/PMA in-situ composite may hinder many hydrogen active sites of Pt and hence, comparatively less hydrogen evolution was detected. Under rapid screening, Pt/Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 emerged as a photo - catalyst with the highest water splitting activity. The loading of co- catalysts i.e. metallic Pt was optimized by testing the performance of Ptx Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 (x ␀0 to 1.8 w/w %) nanocomposites for hydrogen production, a respective trend in hydrogen evolution is presented in Fig. 7. The maximum H2 yield of 0.4 mmol/h/g was observed with Pt0.3/ Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 (% w/w – 0.3 %) which subsequently decreased with the increment in Pt loading. The decline in hydrogen evolution could be due to the irregular growth of Pt clusters on Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 surface. It is well established that increased Pt size can dramatically restrict its proton reduction ability. [27] Along with co-catalyst, sacrificial donors also plays a key role in photocatalytic reaction as it inhibits the recombina - tion rate of photogenerated charge carriers (e- and h) by consuming photogenerated holes. [28] The effect of sacrificial donors on the hydrogen production was analysed with Pt0.3/Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 nano- composite and illustrated in Fig. 8. Among different sacrificial electron donors, ethanol proved as an effective sacrificial electron donor as its low dissociation energy makes it as the most viable option compared to other. [29] Moreover, it is investigated that the interaction of valence band holes with ethanol and water mixtures lead to the formation of Hions which on reduction increases the hydrogen yield [30] as per the following reac- tions: C2H5OHH2O⎬⎬↗hνEPt2H22CO2CH4 Furthermore, the effect of ethanol concentration on photocatalytic of Pt0.3/Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 nanocomposite are studied and represented in Fig. 9. It observed that increased concentration of ethanol has restricts hydrogen evolution rate owing to excessive accumulation of ethanol oxidised product on catalytic surface that deteriorates the active sites. Fig. 7.Effect of concentration (wt %) of Pt on Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 nanocomposite (a) Cumulative hydrogen productivity (mmols) against reaction time (min) (b) Bar graph mmols/h/g against Pt loading. Fig. 8.Effect of different sacrificial electron donors on synthesized Ag/ Ag2MoO 4 nanocomposite in presence of 0.31% of Pt. A. Anjum Moinuddin et al.
