ee of segregation is stronger in homonuclear bonds than the heteronuclear bonds. The generated exciton is effectively shuttled between these two islands, the electrons are effectively trapped on metallic Pt while holes are consumed by the sacrificial donor. (iv) The shuttling of electrons between Ag and Pt has strengthened light-absorbing property and proton reduction ability, hence, the highest hydrogen production was witnessed with the catalyst Pt0.3/Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 composite. 4.Conclusion Photocatalysis is recognized as a promising future technique to address the environmental issues. This work reports process for the synthesis of Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 (Ag/PMA) nanocomposites and their photo - catalytic activity for water splitting hydrogen generation. This photo - catalyst was synthesized by a new facile approach of simple and environment- friendly in-situ photodeposition method, where the mixing of [PMo 12O40]3- and Agin an ethanol-aqueous suspension at ambient condition and illuminating the mixture with MPML is the major single step. The as prepared catalyst Ag/Ag 2MoO 4 showed the best photo - catalytic performance of 5.7 ×10-2 mmol/h/g using sacrificial donor ethanol which improved further to 0.4 mmol/h/g of hydrogen using Pt as a co-catalyst. The photo induced electrons were moved from Ag2MoO 4 to metallic Ag, and combined with the Ag plasmonic holes. The Ag plasmonic electrons were trapped by Ag2MoO 4 and it increase the photogenerated reactive site for hydrogen production on the surface of nanocomposite. Meanwhile, the photogenerated electrons from Ag/ Ag2MoO 4 which transfer to Pt were used to reduce Hto H2, thus they showed efficient and feasible photocatalytic performance for hydrogen production without any corrosion up to 24 h. CRediT authorship contribution statement Afsha Anjum Moinuddin: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. Abhay Vijay Kot- kondawar: Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Writing – review & e