Fuel 331 (2023) 125971 2the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) at the anode electrode and the other is the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at the cathode electrode [16]. When compared with the other methods, alkaline electrolysis has the advantage of producing high purity of hydrogen (99.999 %) with simple cell designs [17]. However, the electrolysis of water has a disadvantage due to their high energy consumption and low hydrogen generation rate. It only contributes about 4 % of the global hydrogen production. Therefore, as an alternative way to increase efficiency and reduce energy consumption, many researchers are looking to reduce energy consumption by developing low-cost electrode components and catalysts [18,19] . In recent years, the electrodes utilized in electrochemical energy conversion applications have been prepared by a 3D printing method, which is known as AM. 3D printing technology has many advantages, such as low cost, being environmentally-friendly, and rapid production of complex geometries with minimal waste [20,21] . Many different materials, like metals, polymers, and ceramics are used to construct 3D printed products. Fused deposition modeling (FDM) has become popular in the manufacture of fabricating materials widely used in various fields, including the health, automotive sector, aerospace, energy, and elec- trochemical energy conversion applications [22–25]. Recent studies indicated that carbon-based nanomaterials reinforced with polymeric filaments, such as PLA or Acrylonitrile Butadiene Styrene (ABS) pave the way for use as raw materials in the manufacture of 3D printed products. Carbon-based nanomaterials, such as graphene, carbon black (CB), and carbon nanotubes (CNT) are widely used to form conductive filaments [26–28]. Commercially available 3D printable conductive polymer fil- aments for 3D printing of electrodes are graphene-based PLA (Black Magic from Graphene 3D Lab) and CB-based PLA (Proto-Pasta ® from Proto-Plant) and they are commercially available [29]. The performance of 3D printed specimens often depends on extrusion temperature, layer height, and printing speed. Taking into account considering the con- ductivity of a thermoplastic polymer filled with CB or CNT, the pro- duction conditions or coatings on the samples have an important place [30–32]. It is reported that carbon-based polymer composites have several advantageous compared to the preparation of other products because they can be printed with a 3D printer, and easy to product manufacturing. Using carbon nano-based 3D filaments, studies on 3D printed electrodes, circuits, sensors, and similar components are very popular [33,34] . There are several studies that have indicated that the printing of objects using different printing parameters contributes to the changes in the mechanical and physical properties of conductive PLA filaments. For instance, Gao et al. [35] tested two types of commercially- available different conductive PLA filaments using various printing pa- rameters. The main purpose of their work was to examine the effects of 3D printing parameters, like temperature, layer height, extrusion, and movement speeds on the resistance of conductive PLA filaments. In the analysis results, they stated that printing temperature, layer height, and orientation significantly affected the conductivity of PLA filament. They also determined that changing the print parameters could affect the conductivity when printing different geometries, such as electrodes, sensors, and strain gauges. Tirado-Garcia et al. [36] investigated the mechanical, thermal, and electrical properties of 3D printed objects which were prepared by using conductive CB based PLA filament. They printed the objects by considering three different printing conditions for PLA filament direction (longitudinal – 0•, oblique – 45•, and transverse – 90•). In the results, they stated that the specimen geometry according to the printing direction of the conductive PLA affects the conductivity and thermal properties. They also said that it becomes heterogeneous when the printing angle is increased with relative to the longitudinal direction of the sample geometry. The surface of the 3D printed electrodes may be easily changed by the electrochemical deposition method and the con- ductivity properties of the electrodes may be improved by increasing their electrochemical performances. Novel electrode materials are ex- pected to have high kinetic activity, like noble metals (Pt, Pd, Ru, etc.), which are the most efficient catalysts due to the high bond energy between the metal surface and hydrogen atoms. However, these metals are not preferred in large-scale industrial applications and electro - chemical applications due to their high prices and rarity [37,38] . Therefore, Ni-based electrodes have attracted considerable interest due to their high chemical stability, low cost compared to other transition metals, and low HER overpotentials. The kinetic activity of HER can be increased by using electrodes with porous or rough surfaces and surface modified electrode materials. The coating of electrode surfaces with transition metal-based materials and noble metals reduces the over- potential of the electrodes and increases the catalytic activity of the electrodes. The coating of Ni or its alloys on the electrodes significantly accelerates HER. Moreover, efficient, and inexpensive catalysts need to be synthesized for HER to reduce the overpotential without using more noble metal additions. Thus, these noble metals should be used with non-noble metal addition like pure Ni catalysts to obtain cheaper and stable electrodes for alkaline media. The use of transition metals such as Fe, Ni, Co, and Mo or their alloys as electrocatalysts is highly effective for HER in alkaline media. It is also stated that the inclusion of non- metals additives such as B, S, and P in metal compounds increases ki- netic activity of HER. For this reason, the coating of the binary or ternary Ni alloy may increase the kinetic performance of HER at low over- potentials [39–41]. For example, Crespo et al. [42] prepared a series of bimetallic nanoparticles with different NiPt molar ratios (1:1, 2:1, and 3:1) to study HER kinetics. They stated that the NiPt coated samples exhibited a different surface morphology for both Pt and Ni. They interpreted that the increase in the kinetic performance for HER is due to the higher electrochemical active surface area. Du et al. [43] demon - strated a one-step chemical reduction strategy to produce NiPt sup- ported reduced graphene oxide (rGO) as electrocatalysts for HER in an alkaline medium. They prepared graphene-supported NiPt alloys with Pt/Ni molar ratios of 1:0, 3:1, 3:2, 1:1, and 0:1. They concluded that NiPt alloys prepared at different molar ratios show higher kinetic activity than commercial Pt/C and graphene-supported Pt electrodes for HER in an alkaline medium. They also stated that NiPt alloys have excellent stability for HER and NiPt alloys can be used for electrocatalyst appli - cations in the alkaline medium. Eiler et al. [44] prepared Ni-Pt thin films by the electrodeposition method using an aqueous solution. They ob- tained NiPt thin films by varying the deposition potential, time, and Ni content in the aqueous solution. They stated that the NiPt thin films were uniform, and their thickness varied between 200 and 300 nm. They also declared that Ni-rich, Ni-Pt thin films were a promising electro - catalyst for HER. Therefore, they can be used as electrocatalysts in fuel cells. Fiameni et al. [45] deposited Pt on Ni electrodes (commercially purchased porous and foam electrode) and investigated the HER per- formance of NiPt electrodes in 1 M KOH solution for alkaline electro - lyzers. They determined that the surface areas of the Pt-modified Ni electrodes were higher in the Ni foam electrodes. They mentioned that the Ni foam electrodes had better kinetic activity and high Pt surface area compared to other electrodes. However, it was also possible to design high-performance electrodes with simple methods instead of conventional electrodes to increase their kinetic activity and conduc - tivity values for HER. To the best of our knowledge, the HER kinetics of NiPt coated 3D printed graphene-based electrodes in an alkaline elec- trolyte (1 M KOH) have not been investigated. In this study, electrochemical properties of NiPt coated 3D printed graphene-based electrodes were co-deposited with platinum (Pt) for HER in the alkaline medium. The physical properties of the 3D printed electrodes were determined by using FE-SEM, FE-SEM/EDX, FE-SEM mapping, XRD and XPS analysis. The electrochemical performance of the 3D printed electrodes were measured using LSV, CV, and EIS tech- niques. The stability and polarization of the electrodes were measured by using CA and Tafel analysis, respectively. B. Hüner et al.
