Fuel 331 (2023) 125971 32.Experimental procedures 2.1. Chemicals and materials To prepare the graphene-based 3D printed electrodes, commercial conductive graphene-based PLA filament (electrical resistivity: 0.60 Ω cm) was obtained from Black Magic 3D (New York, USA). The electro - deposition of Nickel was prepared with the following chemicals using an Ni Watt bath containing: Nickel sulfate hexahydrate (NiSO 4⋅6H2O – CAS No:10101-97-0), Nickel (II) chloride hexahydrate (NiCl 2⋅6H2O – CAS No:7791-20-0), and Boric acid (H3BO3 – CAS No:10043-35-3). The Pt electrodeposition was conducted using the following chemicals pur- chased from Germany (Merck): Potassium chloride (KCl – CAS No:7447- 40-7), Potassium (IV) hexachloroplatinate (K2PtCl 6 – CAS No:16921-30- 5), and Potassium hydroxide (KOH – CAS No:1310-58-3). All the chemicals analytically pure and utilized as received without any puri- fication. Deionized distilled (DI) water (resistivity: 18.25 MΩcm) ob- tained from a Milli-Q ® water purification system was employed to prepare all bath solutions. 2.2. Fabrication and electrochemical coating of electrodes The preparation of the 3D printed electrodes was fabricated ac- cording to a procedure similar to our previous studies [46]. In brief, the electrodes were designed in 50 mm ×5 mm x1 mm (length ×width × thickness) using SolidWorks 2018 computer-aided design (CAD) soft- ware and the active surface area of the electrode was set to 25 mm2 (5 mm ×5 mm). Electrodes were fabricated with an Ultimaker2 printer using the FDM technique and Black Magic 3D conductive graphene- based PLA filament (commercially purchased from 3D Compare). Before the electrodeposition process, a series of cleaning procedures were applied to the surfaces of the electrodes which are prepared by using the 3D printing method. The electrodes were firstly cleaned in the ultrasonic bath with ethanol for 5 min. Then, electrodes were washed with deionized (DI) water and dried with nitrogen gas (N2). The elec- trochemical coating process was provided by applying a constant voltage with a potentiostat/galvanostat system (Metrohm-Autolab PGSTAT204) using the two-electrode chronoamperometric technique. A 3D printed graphene-based electrode with 25 mm2 active surface area and a Pt sheet (99 % purity) in 0.1 mm thickness with 1 cm2 surface area were used as the working electrode and counter electrode, respectively. The bath solutions were prepared by high purity deionized water (Milli- Q®) and all the electrochemical tests were conducted at room temper - ature (25 •C). The details of the bath composition for the coatings was summarized in Table 1. All coating bath solutions were prepared by 50 mL. The coating process of 3D printed graphene-based electrodes was conducted in two steps. (i) Firstly, 3D printed electrode was immersed in a nickel-plating bath as a cathode, and it was coated with nickel under 10 V constant voltage for 600 s. (ii) After that, Ni coated 3D printed electrode was immersed in a platinum (Pt) bath as a cathode, and it was co-deposited on the electrode under 10 V constant voltage for 3600 s. The Ni coated 3D printed electrodes which were coated with different Pt metal ratios were named as NiPt1, NiPt2, and NiPt3. Required chemical compositions for the coating procedure of the electrodes were deter - mined by the different metal ratios [47,48] . The amount of deposition of 3D printed electrodes was calculated from Equation (4) according to Faraday ’s law: WItMA nF(4) where W (g) is the amount of the deposition, t (s) is the time in seconds, I (A) is current, MA (g/mol) is the molecular weight of the metal. n and F represent the number of moles of electrons, and Faraday constant (96485 C), respectively. 2.3. Determination of the electrochemical properties of electrodes Electrochemical measurements of all prepared electrodes were made with the use of a computer-controlled potentiostat –galvanostat (Met- rohm –Autolab/PGSTAT204) electrochemical workstation and experi - mental data were recorded and evaluated with NOVA 2.1.5 software. The measurements were performed with a typical three-electrode sys- tem and the 3D printed electrode was used as a working electrode. The Ag/AgCl electrode that was saturated with 3 M KCl was utilized as a reference electrode and Pt wire was used as the counter electrode. Ex- periments were conducted in a solution of 1 M KOH (pH:13.99) in the alkaline medium. During the electrochemical measurements, the elec- trolyte was kept in an N2 gas environment to remove dissolved oxygen. The applied potential was converted from E vs Ag/AgCl to a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) using Eq. (5) as given below: ERHEEAgEAgCl0B1970B059pH (5) The electrochemical measurements of NiPt coated 3D printed graphene-based electrodes were investigated by LSV, CV, EIS, CA, and Tafel analysis. LSV curves were performed with a potential range from ␀0.5 to 0 V vs Ag/AgCl at a scan rate of 50 mVs␀1. CV curves were performed with a potential range from ␀1.6 to 1.6 V vs Ag/AgCl at a scan rate of 100mVs␀1. EIS measurements were recorded versus the open circuit potential (OCP) in a frequency range of 1 kHz to 0.1 Hz and voltage amplitude of 10 mV [43]. Stability tests were conducted with potential of ␀0.05 V for 20 h. Polarization curves were conducted with a potential range from ␀0.4 to 0.2 V vs Ag/AgCl at a scan rate of 10 mVs␀1 [49]. Tafel polarization analysis is the most effective method to deter - mine the corrosion properties of coated electrodes. Kinetics of HER of the coated electrodes were determined from the following Equation (6): ηβaβclogI (6) where η, βc, βa, and I represent overpotential (mV), cathodic Tafel slope (mVdec-1), anodic Tafel slope (mVdec-1), and current density (mA/cm2), respectively. The exchange current density (Jo) and the transfer coeffi - cient (α) were calculated using Equation (7) and Equation (8), respec - tively as given below: logJo␀βa βc(7) α2B303RT βcF(8) where T (K), R, and F are the temperature of the ambient and the gas constant (8.314 J/Kmol), and Faraday ’s constant (96485 C), respec - tively [50]. Generally, the mechanism of HER reactions in alkaline media consists of three main steps, as given below [51]: Volmer Reaction BH2Oe␀→HadsOH␀(9) Heyrovsky Reaction BHadsH2Oe␀→H2OH␀(10) Tafel Reaction B2Hadse␀→H2 (11) The first step is the Volmer reaction that involves the generation of an adsorbed hydrogen atoms intermediate (Hads) at the low over- potentials. The second step of the HER mechanism is the Heyrovsky reaction, which is the electrochemical desorption of adsorbed hydrogen Table 1 Bath composition for the coating. Electrode Bath Composition Ni 30 g/L NiSO 4⋅6H2O 1 g/L NiCl 2⋅6H2O 1.25 g/L H3BO3 NiPt1 0.176 g/L K2PtCl 6 7.46 g/L KCl NiPt2 0.352 g/L K2PtCl 6 7.46 g/L KCl NiPt3 0.528 g/L K2PtCl 6 7.46 g/L KCl  B. Hüner et al.
