Fuel 331 (2023) 125971 4atoms. The third step is the Tafel reaction, which is the chemical desorption process with the composition of adsorbed hydrogen atoms [52]. 2.4. Surface characterization of the electrodes Surface morphologies of the 3D printed graphene-based electrodes were examined with FE-SEM images. The average weight values of the chemical composition of the electrode samples were analyzed with the EDX connected to the FE-SEM device. The specimen ’s crystalline phases were measured using XRD measurements. XRD measurements were conducted using a Bruker AXS-D8 diffractometer at a scan rate of 0.02• min␀1 with Cu Kα radiation anode. The average grain size (D) was calculated using the Scherrer equation for NiPt coated electrodes: DnmKλ FWHM Cosθ(12) where FWHM, K, θ, and λ represent full width at half maximum of peaks in radian, Scherrer constant (0.94), the Bragg angle in radians and X-ray wavelength (λ:1.54056 Å for Cu Kα), respectively. In addition, the chemical structure of each element in the NiPt coated 3D printed elec- trodes were investigated by XPS (Specs-Flex-Mod Systems PHI 5000 Versa Probe) using an Al anode Kα with an energy of 1486.6 eV. 3.Results and discussion 3.1. Morphology and composition of the 3D printed electrodes FE-SEM image and particle distributions of the NiPt alloys deposited on the electrochemically 3D printed graphene-based electrodes can be seen in Fig. 1. Fig. 1(a) and Fig. 1(b) show FE-SEM images of electrodes and particle distributions on the 3D printed electrode surface, respec - tively. As seen in Fig. 1(a), NiPt alloys are deposited on the electrode. Moreover, with the increase of Pt content, the morphology of the NiPt coated 3D printed electrodes did not change significantly except for the increase of grain sizes. According to Faraday ’s law, the amount of deposited metals on the surface of NiPt1, NiPt2 and NiPt3 coated elec- trodes has been determined as 0.05218, 0.05431, and 0.05821 g/cm2, Fig. 1.Surface properties of the NiPt coated electrodes a) FE-SEM images b) particle size distribution histograms of the electrodes.  B. Hüner et al.
