Fuel 331 (2023) 125971 5respectively. As seen in Fig. 1(b), the particle size of the NiPt coated electrodes varies from 30.65 nm to 36.47 nm. The FE-SEM/EDX elemental mapping analysis of the NiPt coated electrodes can be seen in Fig. 2. Ni (red) and Pt (green) have a uniform distribution in agreement with the amount of NiPt alloy content, which means that there is a homogeneous structure of NiPt coated electrodes. Thus, the elemental maps of the NiPt coated electrodes have confirmed the co-existence of Ni and Pt elements in the electrode surface. More - over, the surface elemental composition of the NiPt coated electrodes was determined by FE-SEM/EDX analysis. The results of the FE-SEM/EDX of the samples can be seen in Fig. 3. As seen in Fig. 3, the main peaks are composed of Ni and Pt according to the FE-SEM/EDX results of the NiPt coated 3D printed electrodes with different molar ratios. The O peak may be due to contamination of the coating bath during the electrodeposition of the electrodes. While the highest Ni content is in the NiPt1 coated electrode (82.01 wt%), the lowest Ni content is observed in NiPt3 electrode sample (66.14 wt%). When the amount of Pt in the coating bath is increased, it is seen that weight percentages of Pt deposition on the electrodes are measured as 11.28 %, 23.44 %, and 29.03 % by weight in the NiPt1, NiPt2, and NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrodes, respectively. According to Fig. 3, it is determined that the composition of NiPt alloys coated at different rates is compatible with co-deposition. The XRD patterns of the NiPt coated 3D printed graphene-based electrodes in the range of 2θ 20•␀90•can be seen in Fig. 4. The obtained XRD patterns of the 3D printed electrode samples are matched with the JCPDS card number. All diffraction peaks in the XRD patterns of the NiPt coated 3D printed electrodes are indexed to JCPDS card numbers 00–001-1194, 01–087-0646, and 00–004-0802. The XRD pat- terns show that the Ni and Pt coatings are completely metallic state and oxide peaks (NiO, PtO, etc.) are not visible. It can be observed that the NiPt diffraction peaks increase as Pt concentration increases in the coating bath. As can be seen from Fig. 4, the NiPt coated electrodes present diffraction peaks of the Ni and Pt located at 2θ 44.73•, 52.11•, and 76.70•for the (111), (200), and (220) planes, respectively. Ac- cording to XRD results, the electrochemical coating of Pt on Ni-coated electrodes show that shifts occur in the diffraction peaks of Pt at higher 2θ values. It can be an alloy formation upon to the incorporation of Ni into the Pt structure. These diffraction peaks may demonstrate the solubility of Ni in the Pt lattice and the structure of a single-phase Ni-Pt alloy due to lower diffraction angles [53].Therefore, the addition of Pt elements is compatible with the Ni lattice, and it causes formation of the NiPt alloy on the coated surface. Moreover, the intensity of the (111) plane has increased gradually with the increase of Pt contents. Accord - ing to the Debye-Scherrer formula, the average crystalline sizes of the NiPt1, NiPt2, and NiPt3 coated electrodes in the (111) plane were calculated as 32.12, 34.33, and 36.4 nm, respectively. This result also has indicated that the amount of Pt in the coating bath solution is changed by the variation of the molar ratio. To further examine the existence of Ni and Pt structures and their valence states in the NiPt coated 3D printed electrodes, XPS analyses were performed as shown in Fig. 5. Ni2p and Pt4f peaks are occurred in the XPS spectrum in Fig. 5(a), demonstrating the evolution of Pt and Ni in the NiPt coated 3D printed graphene-based electrodes. As can be clearly seen in Fig. 5(a), the asset of Ni and Pt species in the electrodes is observed in the Ni2p and Pt4f peaks at 872 and 74 eV, respectively. The presence of the O1s peak could be occurred by the adsorption of oxygen from contamination of the coating bath. Moreover, the spectrum of the C1s of the NiPt coated electrodes occurred at a peak of 282.5 eV, belonging to the C–C [54]. Fig. 5(b) and Fig. 5(c) show the XPS contrast spectrum of Pt4f and Ni2p peaks in the NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrode, respectively. The Pt4f spectra show the main peaks corresponding to Pt4f5/2 and Pt4f7/2. On the other hand, the Ni2P spectra can be seen in the main peaks corre - sponding to NiP1/2 and NiP3/2. As can be seen in Fig. 5(b), it is stated that the most intense peaks at 71.2 eV (Pt4f7/2) and 74 eV (Pt4f5/2) correspond to the metallic Pt(0) state. Moreover, the peaks for metallic Ni(0) state (Fig. 5(c)) are observed at 853 eV (Ni2p3/2) and 872 eV (Ni2p1/2). Similar results are reported by other groups who determined that coexist with Ni(0) and Pt(0) on the NiPt coated electrode surface [55–57]. The deposition of Ni and Pt on the surface of the 3D printed electrode can result in a charge transfer from Ni to Pt and thus lead to a higher binding energy shift of Ni2p [58]. It can be seen from the above results that metallic Ni(0) and Pt(0) are successfully obtained by the electrodeposition method. Fig. 2.FE-SEM/EDX mapping analysis of NiPt coated electrodes for a) NiPt1, b) NiPt2, and c) NiPt3 samples. B. Hüner et al.
