Fuel 331 (2023) 125971 63.2. Electrochemical measurements of the electrodes Fig. 6 shows the CV measurements of the uncoated and NiPt coated 3D printed graphene-based electrodes. As can be seen in Fig. 6, the electrochemical behavior of the electrode samples is different from the uncoated 3D printed electrode. The oxidation and reduction peaks of the NiPt coated 3D printed electrodes can be seen in the CV curve. The presence of peaks in the CV curve of the NiPt coated 3D printed elec- trodes may be due to the Pt and Ni reducing to different oxidation states. Hydrogen adsorption/desorption of the NiPt coated 3D printed elec- trodes was observed between ␀1.2 V and ␀0.4 V in the forward (anodic) scan and in the backward (cathodic) scan. Corresponding peaks about ␀0.701 V and ␀0.372 V in anodic scanning and corresponding peaks at about ␀1.16 V and ␀0.931 V in the cathodic scanning. This can be caused by desorption and adsorption of the hydrogen on the Pt surface [59]. Moreover, peak current was developed by increasing the Pt con- tent in the NiPt alloys and it reached the highest value in the NiPt3 coated electrode. In the order of the peak backward (cathodic) current Fig. 3.FE-SEM/EDX results of the NiPt coated electrodes a) NiPt1, b) NiPt2, and c) NiPt3.  B. Hüner et al.
