Fuel 331 (2023) 125971 8of 268.8 mV (10 mA/cm2). Moreover, the stability tests reveal that the NiPt3 electrode contributes to better stability and lower overpotential with higher Pt content. The above results show that the NiPt3 electrode has an excellent kinetic activity for HER compared to other electrode samples. EIS measurements of the uncoated and NiPt coated 3D printed electrode samples can be seen in Fig. 8. The Nyquist plots of the 3D printed electrodes and the electrical equivalent circuit (EEC) model of the EIS analysis can be seen in Fig. 8(a) and Fig. 8(b-c), respectively. As seen in Fig. 8(b-c), the EEC model elements of the EIS curves are composed of Rs, Rad, Rct, and two constant phase elements (CPEs). Among them, Rs is the resistance of the solution (1 M KOH), Rad and Rct represent hydrogen adsorption and resistance charge transfer resistance, respectively. It is stated that the diameter of the semicircle in the Nyquist plot represents the Rct during electrochemical reactions [47]. CPE 1, CPE 2, and CPE 3 are constant phase elements of the double-layer capacitance. n represents parameters related to CPE and the value of n range from 0 to 1. While n is equal to 0 for an ideal capacitor, n is equal to 1 for ideal resistance. The electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) is a fundamental electrochemical feature of an electrode inter- face. It is also known as the electrode surface area that is available for charge transfer. The values of ECSA depend on the electrochemical re- action that occur in the electrode interface. The EIS technique is a useful approach for the characterization of the electrode/electrolyte interface, and it provides an insight into the catalytic properties of an electro - chemical reaction. To calculate the ECSA, double-layer capacitance (Cdl) can be determined by means of the EIS technique. The Cdl obtained from the EIS fitting parameters is calculated by using as given below Equation (13) [60]: Cdl⌈ CPE R␀1 sR␀1 ct1␀n⌉1En (13) The ECSA of the NiPt coated 3D printed electrode samples can be calculated from electrochemical Cdl according to the following Equation (14): ECSACdl Cs(14) where Cdl and Cs represent the double-layer capacitance and the specific capacitance, respectively. Cs is known as the specific capacitance of the electrode samples and corresponds to the capacitance of the electrode surface per unit area. The value of Cs is taken as 0.04 mF/cm2 in an alkaline medium (1 M KOH) based on previously reported values [61,62] . As seen in Fig. 8(a), it is determined that the diameter of the semi- circle decreases for all of the NiPt coated electrodes with increasing current density. Furthermore, it is determined that the EIS curves of the NiPt coated electrodes shifted towards the real axis. This state can be explained by the combination of the charge transfer resistance of the semicircle in the high-frequency region and the double-layer capaci - tance [63]. The parameters obtained by fitting the Nyquist plots with the EEC model are given in Table 2. The kinetic performance of HER changes depends on Rad and Rct. According to Table 2, the CPE 1 and Rct values of the uncoated 3D printed electrode are 1.98E-05 and 6.0946 kΩ, respectively. It is seen that these values decrease when the 3D printed graphene-based electrodes are coated with Ni and Pt. In Table 2, it is found that the Rct values of the NiPt1, NiPt2, and NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrodes are 0.1051, 0.1046, and 0.1036 kΩ, respectively. The Rct value of the NiPt3 coated electrode is much lower than the uncoated 3D printed electrode. Thus, the NiPt3 coated electrode provides better electron transfer kinetics. Moreover, the low Rct value of the NiPt3 coated electrode shows the excellent electron transfer between the electrode and the electrolyte. These results show that alloying Ni and Pt increases the electrical conductivity and enhances the interfacial transfer kinetics of the 3D printed graphene-based electrodes. Among these electrode samples, the NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrode has faster charge transfer kinetics. Therefore, the NiPt3 coated 3D printed Fig. 6.The CV curves of NiPt coated electrodes. Fig. 7.(a) LSV curves of the NiPt coated electrodes and (b) Mass activities of the NiPt coated electrodes.  B. Hüner et al.
