 the energy required for HER activity. According to these results, it can be interpreted that the electrochemical adsorption and the rate of a cathodic HER reaction at the electrode interface is improved. More - over, the protection efficiency (PE) of the coated electrodes is calculated using Equation (15) [64]: PE%Iuncoated␀Icoated Iuncoatedx100% (15) Here, Iuncoated and Icoated represent the corrosion current density of the uncoated and coated electrodes, respectively. According to Fig. 8(c), whereas the NiPt1 coated 3D printed electrode has the lowest PE of 80.20 %, the NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrode has a PE of 82.20 %. These results show that increasing the amount of Pt in the coating bath is Fig. 9.Kinetic activities of NiPt coated electrodes in alkaline medium (1 M KOH) (a) The polarization curves of the NiPt coated electrodes, (b) Jo values of the electrodes, (c) PE values of the electrodes, and (d) stability tests of electrodes. Table 3 Kinetic parameters of the uncoated electrode and NiPt coated 3D printed elec- trodes for HER. Electrode Ecorr (mV) Icorr (mA) βc (mV/ dec) βa (mV/ dec) α η (mV) Uncoated  ␀284.31 5.014E- 02  369.7  283.2  0.159  346.5 NiPt1  ␀252.25 9.945E- 03  147.3  11.7  0.401  134.3 NiPt2  ␀248.37 9.227E- 03  141.4  10.20  0.418  129.9 NiPt3  ␀219.84 8.945E- 03  139.1  9.40  0.425  128.4  B. Hüner et al.
