Fuel 331 (2023) 125971 11effective in protected the electrodes. To investigate the catalytic stability of the NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrode, the CA measurements have been conducted for 20 h at ␀0.05 V. As seen in Fig. 9(d), the current density of the NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrode has a higher value than the other electrode samples during the whole CA testing period. At the end of the CA measurement of the NiPt3 electrode, the current density is around 51.56 % and 30.21 % higher than NiPt1 and NiPt2 coated electrodes, respectively. It can be clearly seen that NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrode has shown an enhancement in the kinetic activity throughout the stability test. Thus, it is obvious that NiPt3 coated electrode has exhibited better stability towards HER than other 3D printed electrode samples. A comparison of the NiPt coated electrode with noble/non-noble metals catalysts from the literature are listed in Table 4. It can be clearly seen that the electrochemical performance of NiPt alloy as electrode or catalyst is widely investigated, but this state is not common for the 3D printed electrode of the graphene-based PLAs. It is previously reported that Ni-based bimetallic alloys, particularly those combined with Pt, Pd, Fe, and Mo can be efficient and have higher ki- netic activity. Adjusting and optimization of the interaction between metal alloys and support materials (electrodes) play an important role in improving their kinetic activities for HER reaction. Ali et al [66]. re- ported that the HER catalytic activity of Ni–Pt Island ’s nanoparticles are analyzed by changing their size from 1.5 nm to 3.8 nm. As a result, they interpreted those different sizes of Ni-Pt island nanoparticles show different HER activity in alkaline media. In addition, the smaller the size of Pt island particles, the lower the Pt:Ni ratio at the particle surface and the higher the kinetic activity. Also, they stated that with increasing Pt island ’s size, the onset potential for HER at 10 mA/cm2 is 345 mV for the larger 3.8 nm Pt islands. They interpreted that the changes in HER ac- tivity of Ni-Pt island nanoparticles with different Pt island sizes can be clarified by the differences in the surface area of the Pt and Ni interfaces on the particle surface. On the other hand, supporting material struc- tures play an important role in determining the average size of the metal particles on them, and it greatly can affect the kinetic performance and stability of catalysts. In particular, graphene is known as the supporting material for metals and their alloys among many carbon materials, due to its high surface area, high corrosion resistance under electrochemical conditions (acidic or basic), and low cost. For example, Du et al [43]. obtained graphene oxide (rGO) supported Ni-Pt alloys as catalysts for HER under alkaline conditions (0.1 M KOH). All the rGO-supported al- loys are prepared by controlling the molar ratios of the bimetallic pre- cursors. They examined that HER activity in 0.1 M KOH solution and found that the onset potential of the Pt-Ni (1:1)/rGO catalyst is 147 mV. In conclusion, they stated that depend on the design of hybrid materials, specific catalysts exhibit higher kinetic activities against HER reaction among all catalysts. Moreover, the excellent HER performance might result from the large surface area of rGO-NiPt catalysts and the syner - gistic effect between rGO, Ni and Pt particles. Graphene-based NiPt coated electrode indicated relatively lower onset potential than that of PdNi/C [68], Ni-Fe-Pt [69], Ni-NWs [71], and Ni-Fe-Sn [72] (Table 4). HER kinetic activity in alkaline or acidic environments of graphene- based 3D-printed electrodes can be improved by using larger specific surface areas, lower resistance materials, and preparation electrodes in different shapes with 3D printing (square, circle, and triangular porous structures etc.). Meanwhile, after NiPt coating on the graphene-based 3D printed electrode, the kinetic activity of HER has been enhanced. On the other hand, it is possible to further increase the catalytic per- formance by improving factors such as optimizing the molar content of Ni/Pt in the coating bath solutions and printing the electrodes at a different layer thickness. In this study, as seen in the comparison be- tween graphene-based 3D-printed electrodes and other catalysts or electrodes with similar and different chemical compositions, it is observed that the onset potential values of this study are close to other reported studies from the literature. 4.Conclusions The electrochemical coating process, which is performed to increase the kinetic activity and corrosion resistance of electrodes, has an important place in electrochemical energy conversion applications. In particular, the platinum group metals (Pt, Pd, etc.) and transition metal- based elements (Ni, Cu, etc.) are the most active metals for HER. In this study, Ni and Pt were deposited electrochemically on 3D printed elec- trodes and electrochemical performances of the electrodes was investi - gated in alkaline media using 1 M KOH solution. NiPt alloys can be considered as an alternative to Pt catalyst for HER in electrolyzers, because of high kinetic activity and stability. According to the FE-SEM results, with the increase of Pt content in the coating solution, there was no significant change in the surface morphology of the NiPt coated electrodes, except for the increase in grain sizes. The existence of peaks of Ni and Pt in the XRD patterns of the electrodes showed that the NiPt coated 3D printed graphene-based electrodes have a crystalline struc- ture with grain sizes varying from 30.65 nm to 36.47 nm. This indicates that the Pt coating with different molar ratios may have changed the morphology of the electrodes, and thus significantly increased the active surface area, which is useful for the kinetic activity of NiPt coated 3D electrode on HER in alkaline media. When the Ni coated 3D printed electrode is co-deposited with Pt, the current density value increases at a constant voltage value. In the NiPt1 and NiPt3 electrode samples, as seen in the CV analysis, the current density of the NiPt coated electrodes has increased from 61.46 to 121.67 mA/cm2. Among the NiPt coated elec- trodes, it was seen that the NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrode had three times higher current density than the NiPt1 coated electrode. The EIS results show that the diameter of the semicircle decreases for all of the NiPt coated electrodes with increasing current density, and it was observed that the EIS curves shifted towards the real axis. It caused a significant decrease in the charge transfer resistance and hydrogen adsorption resistance in the high-frequency region of the electrodes. In addition, the NiPt3 coated 3D printed electrode demonstrated the Table 4 Comparison of HER activity with other reported noble and non-noble metals for Ni-based catalysts in alkaline or acidic medium. Catalyst/ Electrode Electrolyte Current Density (mA/cm2) Onset Potential for HER (mV) Tafel Slope (mV/ dec) Ref. Graphene- based NiPt coated electrode 1 M KOH 10 268 139.1 This Study Pt2@Ni-rGO 1 M KOH 10 ~200 327.9 [65] Ni-Pt islands (3.8 nm) 0.1 M KOH 10 345 150 [66] Ni/5Pt 1 M KOH 10 46 59 [67] Pd30Ni70/C 1 M KOH 10 393 291 [68] Pd50Ni50/C   327 311 Pt-Ni (1:1)/rGO 0.1 M KOH 10 147 72 [43] Ni-Fe-Pt 1 M KOH 10 463 81 [69] NiMo 1 M KOH 10 268 135 [70] Ni NWs 30 wt% KOH 10 295 129 [71] NiW NWs 50–50 280 114 NiZn NWs 50–50 276 104 Ni-Fe-Sn 30 wt% KOH 10 333 47.3 [72] Pt/3D Ni (ED-6) 30 wt% KOH 10 NA 152.23 [73] Pt modified Ni Foams (0.5 mg) 1 M KOH 100 114 75 [45] Mesoporous % 95Ni/Pt 0.5 H2SO4 10 140 63 [44] Ni3Pt 0.5 H2SO4 10 NA 200 [42]  B. Hüner et al.
