Research briefingVisualization of protein motions using temperature- jump crystallo - graphy An infrared laser-induced temperature jump provides a rapid and broadly applicable perturbation to protein dynamics. Temperature-jump crystallography was paired with time-resolved X-ray crystallography to study the dynamic enzyme lysozyme. Measurements with and without a functional inhibitor revealed different patterns in the propagation of motion throughout the enzyme.The challenge Protein dynamics are important for func- tion, yet characterizing those dynamics at high spatial and temporal resolution remains challenging 1. Time-resolved crystal- lography (TRX) offers insight into protein motion but most experiments have focused on photoactive proteins, using pulsed lasers to initiate coordinated motion within the ensemble of crystallized molecules 2. Despite the power of TRX to visualize protein mo - tions, most proteins are not light sensitive, limiting the scope of the method 2. A major direction in the field of TRX is the discov - ery of structural perturbations that can be applied to any protein for time-resolved measurements of structural dynamics. The solution Changing the temperature of a macromolecu-lar system alters the distribution of conforma-tional states at equilibrium. For decades, this principle has been exploited to study chemi-cal dynamics using ‘temperature-jump’ meth-ods, whereby the temperature of a system is rapidly changed, followed by time-dependent measurements as the system relaxes to a new thermal equilibrium 3. More recently, modest temperature changes were shown to alter the conformational ensembles of crystallized proteins 4. Inspired by this work, we combined temperature-jump with X-ray crystallography to develop a universal method for mapping the dynamics of macromolecules at high spatial resolution. To perform our measurements, we devel- oped a pump–probe experiment that paired a nanosecond pulsed infrared laser with serial femtosecond crystallography at the Spring-8 Angstrom Compact Linear Accelerator (SA - CLA) X-ray free-electron laser. The enzyme lysozyme was selected as a target because of its well-characterized dynamics, ease of crystallization and strong X-ray diffraction. We sampled three pump–probe time delays (20 ns, 20 μs and 200 μs), and used the result- ing high-resolution (1.6 Å) diffraction data to analyse time-dependent changes in the crys-tallographic electron density relative to the enzyme’s ground state at ambient tempera-ture. Following a temperature jump of ~20 °C, short-amplitude motions, including atomic vibrations and sidechain rotamer intercon-versions, were excited within 20 ns. These fast motions were widespread, but non-uniform, throughout the molecule. At longer pump–probe time delays, short-amplitude motions mostly dissipated into larger-scale, coordi-nated shifts of atoms, representing confor-mational rearrangements near the active site (Fig. 1a) that recapitulate motions calculated using an anisotropic network model.To confirm whether the observed motions were related to lysozyme’s catalytic function, we repeated the measurements in the presence of chitobiose, an inhibitor that binds to the lysozyme active site and arrests a conforma-tional change required for the catalytic cycle. When the inhibitor was bound, the dissipation of short-amplitude, fast motions to large-scale conformational changes appeared restricted, with more consistent signals between the 20-ns and 200-μs time delays for the inhibitor-bound enzyme than for the apoenzyme (Fig. 1b ). Our results demonstrate that a temperature jump is a viable and generalizable perturbation for studying protein dynamics using TRX. Future directions The demonstration of temperature-jump crystallography opens the door for use of TRX to study the dynamics of virtually any protein of interest. Having established the utility of temperature-jump crystallography, we are now leveraging it to study how dynamics drive biological functions in diverse protein systems. Temperature-jump crystallography could also enable investigation of protein conformational states that are not highly populated at equilibrium but might be impor-tant targets for therapeutic modulation. Nevertheless, there are limitations. To broad- en the method scope, it is essential to democ-ratize instrumentation for temperature-jump crystallography, so that it is available to the wide user base of X-ray facilities. Additionally, motions triggered by temperature jumps are typically distributed throughout the unit cell and occur in only a small fraction of the crystallized molecules, presenting challenges for modelling the conformational changes. Better computational approaches are needed to reliably extract and model the small signal changes observed during experiments. Temperature-jump X-ray crystallogra- phy also offers the opportunity to directly investigate coupling between internal protein motions and solvent dynamics. TRX experi- ments provide insight into molecular mo - tions compatible with the crystal lattice, such as internal movements of atoms within the protein scaffold. By contrast, time-resolved temperature-jump solution X-ray scattering experiments can be used to map larger-scale motions incompatible with the crystal lattice, including reorganization of the solvation lay - er 5. Performing analogous temperature-jump studies on a molecular system in the crystal-line and solution phases should enable us to correlate the timescales of changes in protein solvation with internal protein motions. Alexander M. Wolff & Michael C. Thompson University of California, Merced, Merced, CA, USA.This is a summary of: Wolff, A. M. et al. Mapping protein dynamics at high spatial resolution with temperature-jump X-ray crystallography. Nat. Chem. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41557- 023-01329-4 (2023). Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Published online: 18 September 2023 Check for updates Nature Chemistry | Volume 15 | November 2023 | 1497–1498 1497
