RefeRences 1. van den Bedem, H. & Fraser, J. S. Integrative, dynamic structural biology at atomic resolution—it’s about time. Nat. Methods 12, 307–318 (2015). A review outlining a broad variety of approaches to studying protein dynamics. 2. Schmidt, M. Time-resolved macromolecular crystallography at modern X-ray sources. Methods Mol. Biol. 1607 , 273–294 (2017). A review highlighting challenges and developments in methods for time-resolved crystallography. 3. Gruebele, M., Sabelko, J., Ballew, R. & Ervin, J. Laser temperature jump induced protein refolding. Acc. Chem. Res. 31, 699–707 (1998). A review covering infrared-induced temperature jumps coupled with spectroscopy to study proteins. 4. Keedy, D. A. et al. Mapping the conformational landscape of a dynamic enzyme by multitemperature and XFEL crystallography. eLife 4, e07574 (2015). This paper reveals the effects of temperature on protein structures at thermal equilibrium. 5. Thompson, M. C. et al. Temperature-jump solution X-ray scattering reveals distinct motions in a dynamic enzyme. Nat. Chem. 11, 1058–1066 (2019). This paper reports the use of temperature-jump paired with solution X-ray scattering to gain insight into functional protein dynamics.expeR t opinion “This methodology extends the current toolbox of time-resolved pump–probe experiments and will potentially be applicable to any protein, not only those that are light sensitive. The methodology could thus be important to the entire time-resolved serial crystallography field. By applying this method to the model protein lysozyme, the authors convincingly show that the triggered structural change can be rationalized in a functional framework.” Martin Weik, Institut de Biologie Structurale, Grenoble, France. Behind the papeR This research project, which lasted nearly a decade, was a lesson in humility. As crystallographers with a longstanding interest in protein dynamics, we were enamoured with time-resolved experiments but felt jealous because we never worked on photoactive systems. Therefore, we sought to generalize the technique and selected temperature as the perturbation method. We knew it was going to be challenging, but didn’t fully understand that we were immersing ourselves into the world of experimental physical chemistry. We quickly learned that time-resolved crystallography has more in common with pump–probe laser spectroscopy than it does with traditional, single-crystal X-ray diffraction. The subsequent years required patience and tenacity as we, the structural biologists, learned to think like physical chemists and design a new experiment. The motivation to succeed spawned many collaborations, and took us to X-ray facilities around the world. The results are a testament to the hard work and generosity of everyone involved. M .C.T. fRom the editoR “Using infrared laser pulses, the researchers trigger a temperature jump in microcrystalline lysozyme for which they can monitor structural changes using time-resolved serial femtosecond crystallography. Atomic vibrations occur on the nanosecond timescale, which evolve into structural fluctuations coupled to the active site on the sub-millisecond timescale. When this work came in to us, it was clear that such a method could prove useful in studying the structural dynamics of any protein.” Stacey Paiva, Senior Editor, Nature Chemistry. a Loop 97–100 backbone modelling Residue 23 side-chain modellingw (F20ns – Fdark2) w (F200us – Fdark2) Conf A initial model and refined Conf B initial model Conf B refined (all timepoints) Conf A initial model and refined Conf B initial model Conf B refined (all timepoints)+ 0.04 e– Å–3 – 0.04 e– Å–3+ 0.04 e– Å–3 – 0.04 e– Å–3ANM vectors | ∆IADDAT | (AU)Apo 200 µs–20 ns CHI 200 µs–20 ns 20 ns Apo CHI Apo CHI200 µsAverage IADDATAverage IADDAT (AU)b 0.40.6 0.5 0.4 0.3 0.2 0.1 00.3 0.2 0.1 0 0.6 0.5 0.4 0.3 0.2 0.1 0 Fig. 1 | Lysozyme dynamics induced by a temperature jump. a, Alternative conformations of lysozyme were explicitly modelled (purple) based on time-resolved difference electron density and refined (green). These alternative conformations represent fast (20 ns) sidechain rotation of residue 23 (left), and slower (200 μs) rearrangement of a loop spanning residues 97–100 (right). Vectors (pink) correspond to an anisotropic network model (ANM) based on the major conformation refined against ambient temperature data (black). Difference electron density maps at a contour level of ±0.04 e– Å–3 are also shown. b , The spatial distribution of electron density changes following a temperature jump is non-uniform (where IADDAT is the integrated absolute difference density above threshold), and changes more as a function of pump–probe time delay for the apoenzyme than for the inhibitor-bound (CHI) enzyme. © 2023, Wolff, A. M. et al., CC BY 4.0 .figuRe Nature Chemistry | Volume 15 | November 2023 | 1497–1498 1498"
