Sue‐Faye	Ng	et	al.	/	Chinese	Journal	of	Catalysis	47	(2023)	150–160	1 51 temperature	due	to	the	emission	of	heat‐trapping	greenhouse gases	[1,2].	Additionally,	our	no n‐renewable	fossil	fuel	reserv es are	predicted	to	be	depleted	by	2060	at	our	current	rate	of	us‐age.	These	issues	have	spurred	the	global	search	for	sustainabl e energy	sources,	such	as	solar	energy,	to	replace	traditional	hy ‐ drocarbon	fuels	[3–5].	In	particular,	solar‐driven	water	split‐ting	for	the	production	of	green	hydrogen	allows	us	to	achieve	green	 power	generation	 by	shif ting	our	reliance	away	from non‐renewable	 fossil	 fuels	 [6–9].	 Presently,	 alkaline	 water	electrolysis	is	said	to	play	a	key	role	in	the	industry	to	prod uce clean	H 2	by	treating	excreted	alkaline	water	while	serving	as	a common	reaction	condition	for	fundamental	studies.	However,	the	reaction	efficiency	of	most	catalysts	is	far	from	that	of	t he noble	metal	Pt.	Therefore,	the	fundamental	study	of	alkaline	hydrogen	evolution	reaction	(HER)	is	crucial	to	elucidate	reac‐tion	 mechanisms	 for	 the	 development	 of	 high‐performance	catalysts.	Meanwhile,	the	HER	mechanism	in	acidic	medium	has	lower	overpotentials	and	less	complex	reaction	mechanisms	than	alkaline	media.	Unfortunate ly,	the	operational	condition	o f acid‐based	cells	results	in	the	vaporization	of	electrolyte,	wh ich can	contaminate	the	produced	hydrogen	gas	[10]. Amongst	the	semiconductor	na nomaterials,	2D	carbon	ni‐ trides	have	been	outlined	as	a	prospective	metal‐free	photo‐catalyst	 due	 to	 its	 superior	 stability,	 high	 sur‐face‐area‐to‐volume	 ratio	 and	 tunable	 surface	 engineering	[11–13].	 In	 particular,	 graphitic	 carbon	 nitride	 (g‐C 3N4)	 has been	highly	regarded	for	its	high	thermal	and	chemical	stabil‐ity,	non‐toxicity,	and	affordability.	Unlike	the	UV‐light	activ e commercial	TiO 2	catalyst,	g‐C 3N4	is	active	in	the	visible	light region	(380–700	nm)	due	to	its	band	gap	position.	This	has	allowed	 for	 its	 successful	 imp lementation	 in	 photocatalytic water	splitting	[14–17],	CO 2	reduction	[18–22],	and	other	en‐ vironmental	 applications	 [23–27].	 Despite	 these	 promising	properties,	in	its	pristine	form,	the	application	of	g‐C 3N4	is	lim‐ ited	by	the	drawbacks	of	rapid	recombination	of	photogener‐ated	charge	carriers,	resulting	in	low	photocatalytic	efficienc y and	insufficient	active	sites	for 	the	adsorption	and	activation 	of reactants	[28,29]. Recently,	low	C/N	atomic	ratio	carbon	nitride	allotropes,	such	as	C 3N7	[30],	are	emerging	to	surpass	the	intrinsic	draw‐ backs	of	the	g‐C 3N4.	The	2D	nitrogen‐rich	C 3N5	has	shown	to	be a	prospective	successor	to	g‐C 3N4	owing	to	its	excellent	elec‐ tronic	properties,	and	ample	N‐active	sites	[31].	The	1	triazol e and	2	triazine	moiety	in	C 3N5	also	induces	more	electrons	in	the π–π	conjugation	compared	to	g‐C 3N4,	which	is	attributed	to	the electron	contribution	of	the	 sp2	hybridized	N	on	triazole	moiety [32].	As	such,	the	emergence	of	carbon	nitride	allotropes	rep‐resents	a	new	paradigm	of	nanoengineering	through	chemical	composition	tuning.	C 3N5	has	shown	a	remarkable	performance in	photo‐	and	electrocatalytic	CO 2	reduction	[33,34],	hydrogen evolution	 [35–37],	 and	 other	 environmental	 applications	[38–40].	 Although	 there	 have	 been	 previous	 studies	 on	 the	catalysis	of	C 3N5	allotrope	[35,41–43],	these	research	works	are either	conducted	through	experimental	performance	analysis	or	computational	electronic	prop erty	analysis.	Therefore,	there i s 	a 	l a c k 	o f 	u n d e r s t a n d i n g 	t o w a r d s 	t h e 	u n d e r l y i n g 	r e a c t i o n 	pathway	 on	 carbon	 nitride	 allotropes	 and	 the	 effect	 of	non‐metal	dopants	on	the	catalytic	active	sites. Apart	from	that,	surface	modification	in	the	form	of	dopants have	shown	great	potential	to	tune	the	band	gap	of	the	semi‐conductor,	increase	the	catalyti c	active	sites,	and	redistribut e electron	 hole	 pairs	 to	 reduce	 charge	 carrier	 recombination	[44–49].	Recently,	boron	has	been	a	popular	dopant	choice	to	be	incorporated	into	carbon	nitride	due	to	its	electron‐deficie nt nature	which	allows	it	to	have	a	strong	interaction	with	the	lone	pair	electrons	of	water	molecules	[50–52].	The	electronic	configuration	of	boron	(2 s 22p1)	also	allows	it	to	interact	with reactants	 such	 as	 N 2	t h r o u g h 	t h e 	“ a c c e p t a n c e ‐ d o n a t i o n ” 	o f electrons	due	to	the	three	half	occupied	and	one	empty	orbital	[53].	 In	 this	 work,	 boron	 atoms	 were	 introduced	 to	 C 3N5 through	substitution	of	C/N	atom	or	interstitial	doping.	Boron	has	a	similar	atomic	radius	(85	pm)	to	C	(70	pm)	and	N	(65	pm),	thereby	allowing	for	ease	of	substitution.	Although	Sun	 et al.	[54]	have	published	a	first‐principles	study	on	boron‐doped C 3N5,	their	work	focused	on	property	analysis	such	as	band structure,	density	of	states	(DOS),	charge	distribution	and	op‐tical	 properties.	 The	 reaction	 kinetics	 of	 the	 water	 splitting	reaction	is	still	unknown.	Therefore,	this	work	aims	to	close Wee‐Jun	Ong	(School 	of	Energy	and	Chemical 	Engineering, 	Xiamen	University 	Malaysia)	received his	B.Eng.	and	Ph.D.	in	chemical	engineering	from	Monash	Univer sity.	He	is	presently	an	Assis‐ tant	Dean	and	Associate	Professor	in	the	School	of	Energy	and	C hemical	Engineering	at	Xiamen University	Malaysia	(XMUM).	From	2016	to	2018,	he	was	a	scienti st	at	Institute	of	Materials Research	and	Engineering	(IMRE), 	Agency	for	Science,	Technology 	and	Research	(A*STAR)	in Singapore.	Starting	from	2021,	he	becomes	a	Director	of	the	Cen ter	of	Excellence	for	NaNo Energy	&	Catalysis	Technology	(CONNECT)	at	XMUM.	In	2019,	he	wa s	a	visiting	scientist	at Technische	Universität	Dresden,	Germany	and	a	visiting	professo r	at	Lawrence	Berkeley	Na‐ tional	Laboratory	(LBNL),	USA.	His	research	interests	focus	on	 tunable	design	of	nanostruc‐ tured	materials	(i.e.,	2D	nanoarc hitectures	and	carbon‐based	su bstrates)	for	photocatalytic, photoelectrocatalytic,	and	electrochemical	H 2O	splitting,	CO 2	reduction,	N 2	fixation	and	alcohol oxidation.	Apart	from	these,	his	most	recent	progresses	include 	the	3D	printing	nanotechnolo‐ gy	 as	 well	 as	 microwave	 plasma	 methane	 cracking	 for	 graphene	 an d	 hydrogen	 produc‐ tion/storage.	He	has	coauthored	more	than	120	peer‐reviewed	pap ers	and	received	over 16000	citations	and	a	H‐index	of	56	to	date.
