152	 Sue‐Faye	Ng	et	al.	/	Chinese	Journal	of	Catalysis	47	(2023)	150–160 this	research	gap	and	uncover	the	underlying	HER	pathway	on carbon	nitrides	and	to	find	suitable	structures	that	can	repre‐sent	acidic	and	alkaline	media	in	experimental	application. Herein,	boron‐doped	C 3N5	will	be	evaluated	through	both substitutional	and	interstitial	doping	models	for	the	HER.	The	formation	energy	calculations	will	serve	as	the	first	screening step,	 followed	 by	 properties	 investigation	 on	 the	 band	 gap,	density	 of	 states	 and	 optical	 properties.	 At	 the	 end,	 the	Volmer‐Tafel	and	Volmer	Heyrovsky	HER	mechanism	in	both	acidic	and	alkaline	environments	will	be	studied	on	the	bo‐ron‐doped	C 3N5	models	by	evaluating	the	free	energy	pathway. As	a	result,	the	specific	introduction	of	boron	can	improve	the electronic	distribution,	optical 	properties	and	reduce	the	ther ‐ modynamic	energy	barrier	of	C 3N5. 2.		Computational 	details All	of	the	calculations	were	implemented	by	the	Vienna	ab initio	simulation	package	(VASP) 	based	on	density	functional t h e o r y 	( D F T ) 	u s i n g 	t h e 	p r o j e c t e d 	a u g m e n t e d 	w a v e 	( P A W ) 	pseudopotentials	 [55].	 The	 exchange‐correlation	 functional	was	described	by	the	generalized	gradient	approximation	in	the	form	of	Perdew‐Burke‐Ernzerhof	(GGA‐PBE)	[56].	For	the	e l e c t r o n i c 	a n d 	i o n i c 	s e l f ‐ c o n s i s t e n t 	c a l c u l a t i o n s , 	t h e 	c o n v e r ‐gence	criteria	for	energy	and	force	were	set	to	10 –5	eV	and	0.02 eV	Å–1,	respectively.	The	C 3N5	was	built	in	a	2	×	2	×	1	supercell with	a	vacuum	of	20	Å	in	z	direction	to	decrease	the	interac‐tions	between	neighboring	images.	Then,	a	boron	atom	will	e i t h e r 	b e 	i n t r o d u c e d 	t h r o u g h 	s u b s t i t u t i o n 	w i t h 	a 	C 	a t o m 	(B C‐C3N5)	or	an	N	atom	(B N‐C3N5),	or	through	interstitial	doping (BIn‐C3N5).	A	2	×	2	×	1	Monkhorst‐Pack	k‐point	mesh	was	em‐ ployed	for	sampling	the	Brillouin	zone	[57].	Charge	transfer	calculation	in	the	form	of	Bader	charge	analysis	was	conducted	using	the	code	developed	by	Henkelman	and	co‐workers	[58]. The	formation	energy	of	the	boron‐doped	C 3N5	monolayer was	calculated	as: EfC	=	E(BC‐C3N5)	–	(E(C3N5)	–	μC	+	μB)		 	 	 (1) EfN	=	E(BN‐C3N5)	–	(E(C3N5)	–	μN	+	μB)	 	 	 (2) EfIn	=	E(BIn‐C3N5)	–	(E(C3N5)	+	μB)	 	 	 	 (3) Eqs.	(1),	(2)	and	(3)	are	used	for	C‐substituted	boron	do‐ pant,	N‐substituted	boron‐dopant 	and	interstitial	boron	dopant positions,	respectively.	 E(BC/BN/BIn‐C3N5)	and	 E(C3N5)	are	the total	energies	of	the	corresponding	boron‐doped	C 3N5	mono‐ layer	and	pristine	C 3N5	monolayer,	respectively.	 μC,	μN	and	μB are	the	chemical	potentials	of	carbon,	nitrogen	and	boron	at‐o m s , 	w h i c h 	w e r e 	o b t a i n e d 	f r o m 	g r a p h e n e , 	N 2	 and	 α‐boron crystal,	respectively	(Table	S3). The	adsorption	energies	( Ead)	of	various	adsorbed	species were	calculated	to	evaluate	the	stability	of	adsorption	configu ‐ ration.	The	equation	is	 calculated	as	follows: Ead	=	Eadsorbate/surface 	–	Eadsorbate	–	Esurface		 	 (4) The	HER	pathway	for	the	reaction	kinetics	are	calculated	as follows, Acid H+	+	*	+	e−	 → 	 H * 	 					( 5 ) H*	+	H+	+	e–	→	H2	+	*	 	 	 	 	 (6) H*	+	H*	→	H 2	 + 	 2 * 				 	 	 	 	 ( 7 ) 	Base H2O	+	*	+	e–	→	H*	+	OH–	 					( 8 ) H*	+	H 2O	+	e–	→	H2	+	*	+	OH–	 				( 9 ) 2H*	+	2OH–	→	H2	+	2*	+	2OH–				( 1 0 ) Whereby	Eqs.	(5‒7)	refers	to	the	Volmer,	Heyrovsky,	and Tafel	 mechanism	 in	 acidic	 media,	 respectively,	 while	 Eqs.	(8‒10)	refers	to	the	Volmer,	Heyrovsky	and	Tafel	mechanism	in	alkaline	media,	respectively.	The	Gibbs	free	energy	change	i s calculated	as	∆ G	=	∆E ad	+	∆ZPE	–	 T∆S	+	∆GpH,	where	∆ZPE	and ∆S	are	the	zero‐point	energy	and	entropy	change,	respectively. ∆GpH	is	dependent	on	the	H+	concentration	and	is	calculated	as ∆GpH	=	–kTln[H+]	=	kTln10	×	pH,	where	 k	is	the	Boltzmann	con‐ stant	[59,60].	The	influence	of	∆ GpH	is	negligible	for	pH	=	5.0 and	8.0	and	is	therefore	not	considered	in	this	study.The	tem‐perature	T 	is	set	to	298.15	K. 3.		Results 	and	discussion 3.1.		Formation 	energy	of	boron‐doped	C 3N5 Fig.	1	shows	the	tri‐ s‐triazine	structure	used	for	g‐C 3N4	and triazole‐triazine	structure	used	for	the	pristine	C 3N5	structure. The	 triazole	 and	 triazine	 refers	 to	 the	 5	 membered	 and	 6	membered	heterocyclic	moieties	in	the	C 3N5	structure,	respec‐ tively	[32].	The	boron‐doped	C 3N5	models	were	built	by	either substituting	one	boron	atom	for	a	C/N	atom	or	filling	a	boron	atom	at	the	interstitial	space	of	the	2	×	2	×	1	supercell	of	C 3N5. The	substitutional	doping	posi tions	chosen	for	this	work	in‐ clude	the	4	kinds	of	N	atom	positions	(N1,	N2,	N3,	N4)	and	1	C	atom	position	(C1).	The	interstitial	doping	structure	(In)	was	obtained	by	occupying	a	B	atom	in	the	interstitial	space	en‐closed	by	the	heptazine	units	(Fig.	1(b)). The	 formation	 energies	 were	 calculated	 to	 evaluate	 the most	likely	doping	site	for	the	boron‐dopant	on	C 3N5,	which were	shown	in	Fig.	2.	Apart	for	boron‐doped	on	the	N1	posi‐tion,	all	formation	energies	are	negative	and	range	from	–1.28	to	–3.04	eV.	This	suggests	that	the	doping	positions	are	exo‐thermic	 and	 thermodynamically	 spontaneous	 except	 for	B N1‐C3N5	[61].	The	substitution	of	boron	for	the	center	N	atom leads	 to	 charge	 deficiency	 and 	 lattice	 deformation	 of	 the tri‐s‐triazine	ring	of	C 3N5,	making	it	thermodynamically	unfa‐ vorable.	Therefore,	B N1‐C3N5	will	be	excluded	from	the	later calculations.	 Meanwhile,	 the	 introduction	 of	 boron	 into	 the	interstitial	space	leads	to	stru ctural	deformation,	whereby	bo‐ ron	is	integrated	into	the	ring	after	optimization.	The	smalles t formation	energy	was	obtained	for	B N4‐C3N5	(–3.04	eV),	indi‐ cating	that	this	is	the	most	stable	doping	position. 3.2.		Work	function Work	function	( Φ)	is	defined	as	the	minimum	energy	re‐ quired	for	an	electron	to	escape	from	the	Fermi	level	into	vac‐ uum.	The	equation	 Φ	=	Evac	–	Efermi	was	used	to	calculate	the work	function,	where	E vac	and	E fermi	refer	to	the	vacuum	and fermi	level,	respectively.	From	Table	1	we	can	observe	that	the introduction	of	boron	into	C 3N5	leads	to	a	decreased	work	func‐ tion	except	for	the	B N4‐C3N5	model.	In	particular,	the	interstitial
