Sue‐Faye	Ng	et	al.	/	Chinese	Journal	of	Catalysis	47	(2023)	150–160	1 53 boron‐doped	model	has	the	lowest	work	function	at	4.36	 Φ	due to	the	upshifted	fermi	level.	The	decreased	work	function	can be	ascribed	to	the	improved	ability	of	electron	transport	to	surface	that	can	partake	in	th e	catalytic	reaction	[62,63]. 3.3.		Band	structure	and	density	of	states The	band	gap	position	for	g‐C 3N4	and	C 3N5	was	calculated	to be	1.19	and	1.91	eV,	respectively	(Fig.	S1	and	Fig.	3(a)).	Our	results	reflect	the	findings	of	C 3N5	with	the	triazole‐triazine framework.	Although	the	undere stimation	of	band	gap	is	very common	in	GGA‐PBE	function,	the	relative	value	of	g‐C 3N4	and C3N5	is	still	meaningful	to	be	compared.	As	for	the	boron‐doped models,	the	band	gap	is	narrowed	for	all	the	models	except	for	B N3‐C3N5	and	B N4‐C3N5	(Figs.	3(d)	and	(e)).	Additionally,	we	can observe	the	impurity	states	for	all	boron‐doped	models	except	for	B C1‐C3N5	and	B In‐C3N5	(Figs.	3(b)	and	(f)).	Depending	on	the position	of	the	impurity	states	relative	to	the	VB	and	CB,	they could	act	 as	the	reduction	or	oxidation	 site	 to	improve	the	photocatalytic	reaction.	The	pho togenerated	electrons	(holes) will	be	trapped	by	the	impurity	state	that	is	positioned	closer relative	to	the	CB	(VB),	which	will	improve	charge	separation	and	act	as	a	reduction	(oxidation)	site.	However,	an	impurity	level	at	the	middle	of	the	band	gap	is	considered	a	deep	impu‐rity	energy	level,	which	can	act	as	the	recombination	center	fo r photogenerated	charge	carriers	[64,65].	It	can	be	observed	that N‐doped	C 3N5	have	impurity	states	near	the	middle	of	its	band gap	(Figs.	3(c)‒(e)),	indicating	that	it	is	a	deep	impurity	ene rgy level	that	is	not	effective	for	 the	separation	of	charge	carriers. Meanwhile,	the	effect	of	boron	dopant	in	B N3‐C3N5	is	an	up‐ shifted	VB	and	a	shallow	impurity	state	near	the	VB	(Fig.	3(d)) , which	can	act	as	a	hole	trapping	center	to	accept	the	electrons (provide	holes)	for	photooxidation.	The	shift	of	CBM	and	VBM	of	the	boron‐doped	models	can	be	observed	in	Fig.	3(g).	It	can	be	noted	that	the	downshift	of	CBM	in	B N2‐C3N5	may	lead	to	the decrease	of	reduction	ability. The	partial	density	of	states	(PDOS)	of	pristine	C 3N5	reveals that	the	valence	band	is	occupied	by	N	atoms,	while	carbon	atoms	 contribute	 to	 the	 conduction	 band	 (Fig.	 S2(a)).	 Since	boron	is	an	electron	acceptor,	the	introduction	of	boron	into	C 3N5	will	result	in	an	p‐type	acceptor	impurity	level	above	the VB	[66].	The	PDOS	results	reveal	that	the	impurity	state	for	boron‐substituted	models	(Fig.	S2(b)–(f))	is	mainly	contributed from	N	and	B,	which	reveals	the	strong	interaction	of	the	N–B	bonds. 3.4.		Optical	properties The	optical	property	of	a	photocatalyst	is	an	important	con‐ sideration	factor	as	a	semiconductor	that	can	utilize	a	broad light	range	is	able	to	produce	more	electron‐hole	pairs	for	sub ‐ sequent	reactions	[67].	Therefore,	the	optical	properties	of	al l models	were	calculated	and	are	shown	in	Fig.	4.	It	can	be	ob‐ BC1-C3N5 BN1-C3N5 BN2-C3N5 BN3-C3N5 BN4-C3N5 BIn-C3N5 Absence of B-N bond Fig.	2.	Formation	energies	of	boron‐dopant	on	various	sites	on	C 3N5. The	C,	N	and	boron	atoms	are	represented	in	brown,	grey,	and	gr een, respectively. (a) (b) N1N2 C1 N3N4 In Fig.	1.	Top	and	side	view	of	g‐C 3N4	(a)	and	triazole‐triazine	C 3N5	(b)	framework	marked	with	positions	used	for	boron‐doping.	The 	C	and	N	atoms	are represented	in	brown	and	grey,	respectively. Table	1 Work	function	( Φ)	of	boron‐doped	C 3N5	and	pristine	C 3N5	models. Model	 WF	(Φ) BC1‐C3N5	5 .71 BN2‐C3N5	5 .76 BN3‐C3N5	 5.02 BN4‐C3N5	 5.88 BIn‐C3N5	 4.36 C3N5	5 .80
