Sue‐Faye	Ng	et	al.	/	Chinese	Journal	of	Catalysis	47	(2023)	150–160	1 55 thermodynamic	step	for	product	desorption. 3.6.		Reaction	kinetics DFT	 calculations	 were	 also	 performed	 to	 elucidate	 the mechanistic	insights	of	boron‐dopant	on	the	HER	mechanism over	pristine	C 3N5.	The	Volmer‐Tafel	and	Volmer	Heyrovsky mechanism	for	both	acidic	and	alkaline	conditions	(Fig.	6)	was	calculated	for	this	section.	For	the	acidic	Volmer	mechanism,	hydrogen	adsorption	free	energy	(Δ G H*)	is	used	as	the	key	de‐ scriptor	for	HER	activity	[68].	A	positive	value	for	hydrogen	adsorption	represents	high	thermodynamic	barrier	for	hydro‐gen	adsorption,	while	a	negative 	value	represents	low	thermo‐ dynamic	barrier	for	hydrogen	d esorption.	The	optimum	value for	|Δ G H*|	should	be	around	zero.	This	is	reflected	by	the	Δ GH*	of benchmark	Pt/C	at	–0.09	eV	[69].	As	the	introduction	of	boron	also	 influences	 the	 electronic	 properties	 of	 the	 surrounding	atoms,	the	Δ G H*	was	calculated	on	the	boron	site	as	well	as	the surrounding	 C	 and	 N	 atoms	 (Fig.	 S3,	 Tables	 S4–S8).	 It	 was	found	that	the	Δ G H*	for	BN2‐C3N5	and	B N4‐C3N5	was	closest	to	0 on	the	B	atoms,	whereas	the	B In‐C3N5	and	B C5‐C3N5	models	had the	lowest	Δ GH*	on	the	neighbouring	N	atoms.	The	model	with the	best	performance,	B N3‐C3N5	had	the	lowest	|Δ GH*|	on	the neighbouring	C	atom.	The	lowest	|Δ GH*|	for	all	models	were shown	in	Table	S9	and 	Fig.	7(a).	From	Fig.	7(a),	all	boron‐doped models	 showed	 a	 decreased	 activation	 energy	 compared	 to	pristine	C 3N5	(1.12	eV).	Amongst	all	the	models	B N3‐C3N5	had	the	best	performance	with	a	Δ GH*	of	0.11	eV. Furthermore,	in	order	to	understand	the	HER	process	as	a whole,	 we	 have	 calculated	 the	 Volmer‐Tafel	 and	Volmer‐Heyrovsky	mechanism	on	B N3‐C3N5.	As	shown	in	Fig. 6(a),	the	Volmer	mechanism	in	acidic	media	consists	of	a	com‐bination	of	proton	and	electron	on	the	surface	to	form	H*.	In	the	Heyrovsky	mechanism,	a	proton	electron	pair	will	react	with	H*	in	the	previous	Volmer	step	to	form	H 2*.	In	the	Tafel mechanism,	another	H*	is	formed	on	the	surface	and	will	com‐bine	with	the	neighboring	H*	in	the	subsequent	step.	B N3‐C3N5 has	a	limiting	energy	of	0.79	eV	for	the	Tafel	mechanism	(H*	+	H*	→	H 2),	which	is	lower	than	that	of	pristine	C 3N5	(0.97	eV,	H+ +	e–	→	H*).	(Fig.	7(b)).	This	also	is	lower	than	the	energy	change of	Tafel	step	calculated	on	the	surface	of	precious	metal	Pt	(111)	 (0.85	 eV)	 [70],	 which	 de m o n s t r a t e s 	t h e 	h i g h 	p e r f o r ‐ mance	of	the	metal‐free	boron‐doped	C 3N5	system.	As	for	the Volmer‐Heyrovsky	 mechanism	 in	 acidic	 media,	 the	 PDS	 for	B N3‐C3N5	and	pristine	C 3N5	is	the	H 2	formation	step	(H*	+	H*	→ H2)	and	the	H*	adsorption	step	(2H+	+	2e–	→	H*	+	H+	+	e–),	of which	the	required	energy	is	0.35	and	0.97	eV,	respectively.	This	indicates	that	the	introduc tion	of	boron‐dopants	results	i n a n 	i m p r o v e d 	r e a c t i o n 	k i n e t i c s 	f o r 	b o t h 	V o l m e r ‐ T a f e l 	a n d 	Volmer‐Heyrovsky	mechanism	in	acidic	media. In	alkaline	conditions,	an	impor tant	factor	for	HER	activity	is BC1-C3N5 C3N5 BN2-C3N5 BN3-C3N5 BN4-C3N5 BIn-C3N5H2O H2 Fig.	5.	Adsorption	energy	for	H 2O	and	H 2	molecule	on	different	bo‐ ron‐doped	C 3N5	models	and	pristine	C 3N5.	Blue	and	yellow	represen t H2O	and	H 2	adsorption	energies,	respectively. Fig.	6.	S c h e m a t i c 	o f 	H E R 	p a t h w a y 	f o r 	V o l m e r ‐ H e y r o v s k y 	a n d Volmer‐Tafel	pathway	in	acidic	(a)	and	alkaline	(b)	media	on	B N3‐C3N5 model.	The	Tafel	pathway	is	denoted	with	grey	arrows	and	circle s.	The C,	N,	H,	O,	and	boron	atoms	are	represented	in	brown,	grey,	red ,	white, and	green,	respectively. Fig.	7.	Reaction	kinetics	for	Volmer	mechanism	for	boron‐doped	C 3N5 and	pristine	C 3N5	(a)	and	Volmer‐Heyrovsky	and	Tafel	mechanism	for BN3‐C3N5	and	original	C 3N5	(b)	in	acid	conditions.	Tafel	pathway	is	de‐ noted	in	light	grey.	The	inset	of	the	Fig.	is	the	reaction	path way	on BN3‐C3N5.
