156	 Sue‐Faye	Ng	et	al.	/	Chinese	Journal	of	Catalysis	47	(2023)	150–160 the	energy	barrier	for	water	dissociation	[71].	As	shown	in	(Fi g. 6(b)),	the	alkaline	HER	Volmer	mechanism	consists	of	the	dis‐ sociation	of	H 2O	into	OH–	and	H*.	In	the	Heyrovsky	mechanism, the	H*	will	then	bind	to	another	proton	from	H 2O	and	gain	an electron	from	the	catalytic	surface,	forming	H 2.	In	the	Tafel mechanism,	two	adsorbed	H*	dissociated	from	H 2O	molecules	will	bind	to	each	other	on	the	surface	to	form	H 2.	Amongst	all models	shown	in	Fig.	8(a).	B N3‐C3N5	had	the	best	performance in	the	Volmer	mechanism	for	HER	under	alkaline	condition	with	a	low	PDS	of	0.43	eV	at	the	OH –	dissociation	step.	This	is attributed	to	the	moderate	adsor ption	of	intermediates	on	the BN3‐C3N5	surface.	In	contrast,	B In‐C3N5	fared	worse	than	pristine C3N5	(1.11	eV)	with	a	PDS	of	2.21	eV	at	the	OH–	dissociation step	due	to	the	strong	chemisorption	bond	between	the	inter‐m e d i a t e s 	a n d 	t h e 	c a t a l y t i c 	s u r f a c e . 	T h i s 	p h e n o m e n o n 	w a s 	eluded	in	Section	3.6	from	the	strong	binding	energy	between	the	surface	of	B In‐C3N5	and	the	H 2O	molecule.	From	Fig.	S5,	we can	observe	that	the	boron‐dopant	acts	as	the	adsorption	site	for	H*,	while	the	neighboring	C	atom	binds	to	the	*OH.	The	en‐ergy	barrier	for	the	Tafel	mechanism	(H*	+	*H 2O	+	e–	→	2H*	+ *OH–)	is	lower	on	the	B N3‐C3N5	model	(0.87	eV)	than	pristine C3N5	(1.94	eV),	which	appears	at	the	second	H 2O	dissociation step	(H*	+	*H 2O	+	e–	→	2H*	+	*OH–)	and	the	H 2*	formation	step (2H*	→	H 2*),	respectively.	Meanwhile,	the	energy	barrier	for the	Heyrovsky	mechanism	for	both	pristine	C 3N5	(2.13	eV)	and BN3‐C3N5	(0.47	eV)	is	at	the	H 2*	formation	step	(H*	+	*H 2O	+	e– →	H2*	+	*OH–).	Therefore,	the	B N3‐C3N5	is	determined	to	de‐ crease	 the	 thermodynamic	 energy	 barrier	 of	 the	Volmer‐Heyrovsky	 and	 Volmer‐Tafel	 mechanism	 for	 HER	 in	both	acidic	and	alkaline	media. In	order	to	evaluate	the	effect	of	boron‐dopant	on	the	elec‐ tron	distribution,	the	Bader	charge	of	C 3N5	and	B N3‐C3N5	was calculated.	From	the	Bader	charge	analysis	(Tables	S12	and	S13),	it	was	found	that	boron	doping	led	to	a	shift	in	the	elec ‐ tronic	structure	of	C 3N5.	The	boron‐dopant	in	B N3‐C3N5	has	a positive	charge	(+1.27|e|)	while 	the	original	N	has	a	negative charge	(–0.19|e|)	in	pristine	C 3N5.	Additionally,	we	can	observe a	more	negative	charge	on	the	atoms	surrounding	boron	in	B N3‐C3N5	compared	to	C 3N5	(Figs.	9(a)	and	(b)).	Therefore,	the shift	in	surface	charge	distribution	is	a	contributing	factor	t o Fig.	8.	Reaction	kinetics	for	(a)	Volmer	mechanism	of	HER	on	B N3‐C3N5 catalysts	and	(b)	Volmer‐Heyrovsky	and	Tafel	mechanism	of	HER	on BN3‐C3N5	in	alkaline	condition.	Tafel	pathway	is	denoted	in	light	grey. The	right	side	of	the	Fig.	is	the	schematic	of	the	reaction	mec hanism	on the	catalyst	surface.	The	inset	of	the	Fig.	is	the	reaction	pat hway	on BN3‐C3N5. +1.27 e-1.10 e-0.74 e +0.25 e-0.19 e-1.01 e-0.20 e +0.97 e(a) (b) -1.35 e-1.29 e-0.82 e +1.83 e +1.47 e-1.01 e -0.35 e -1.29 e +1.55 e-1.07 e +0.18 e +0.49 e+1.56 e -1.05 e +0.34 e +1.11 e(c) (d) (e) (f) Fig.	9.	Bader	charge	analysis	on	B N3‐C3N5	(a),	C 3N5	(b),	B In‐C3N5	(c),	B C1‐C3N5	(d),	B N2‐C3N5	(e),	and	B N4‐C3N5	(f)	models.
