Sue‐Faye	Ng	et	al.	/	Chinese	Journal	of	Catalysis	47	(2023)	150–160	1 57 the	enhanced	HER	mechanism	of	B N3‐C3N5	as	it	changes	the binding	energy	of	intermediates	to	allow	for	easier	product desorption. For	comparison,	the	Bader	charge	of	the	other	boron‐doped C3N5	models	(B In‐C3N5,	BC1‐C3N5,	BN2‐C3N5,	BN4‐C3N5)	was	also calculated	(Figs.	9(c)–(f),	Tabl es	S14–S17)	to	study	the	influ‐ ence	of	charge	and	adsorption	energy	of	reactants	and	inter‐mediates.	It	was	found	that	the	boron	atom	had	a	highest	posi‐tive	charge	in	B In‐C3N5	at	+1.83|e|,	whereas	B N3‐C3N5	had	the lowest	positive	charge	at	+1.27|e|.	Meanwhile,	the	surrounding	N	atoms	in	B In‐C3N5	also	had	a	more	negative	charge	(–1.34|e|). Based	on	the	Bader	analysis,	a	higher	net	charge	implies	a	larg e c h a r g e 	t r a n s f e r 	b e t w e e n 	t h e 	a d s o r b a t e 	a n d 	c a r b o n 	n i t r i d e 	model,	which	is	an	indication	of	higher	adsorption	energies	[ 7 2 , 7 3 ] . 	T h e r e f o r e , 	i t 	c a n 	b e 	i n f e r r e d 	t h a t 	t h e 	h i g h 	p o s i t i v e	charge	of	boron	atom	in	the	B In‐C3N5	is	a	contributing	factor towards	the	high	energy	barrier	for	product	desorption	in	the	HER	mechanism.	Furthermore,	a	larger	Bader	charge	transfer	can	result	in	the	strong	adsorption	of	H	atom	and	a	higher	Δ G H* value	[74]. 4.		Conclusions In	conclusion,	the	effect	of	boron	doping	position	on	the electronic	and	catalytic	properties	of	C 3N5	were	investigated through	first‐principles	calculation.	Through	formation	energy calculation,	the	most	of	the	introduction	of	boron	into	C 3N5	was perceived	to	be	thermodynamica lly	favorable	due	to	the	stabi‐ lization	of	B–N	bond.	The	most	stable	doping	position	was	de‐termined	to	be	B N4‐C3N5	as	it	had	the	lowest	formation	energy of	–3.04	eV.	Due	to	the	high	concentrated	doping,	the	introduc‐t i o n 	o f 	b o r o n 	l e d 	t o 	t h e 	a p p e a r a n c e 	o f 	d e e p ‐ l e v e l 	i m p u r i t y 	states	for	all	nitrogen	doped	models.	However,	the	B N3‐C3N5 model	had	an	impurity	state	near	the	VB,	which	can	act	as	a	hole	trapping	center	to	enhance	charge	separation.	From	the	optical	analysis,	it	was	found	t hat	the	adsorption	energy	was increased	at	around	the	1.8–3.1	eV	(400–689	nm)	range	for	all	boron‐doped	models,	which	indicates	in	the	boron	doping	can	increase	the	efficiency	of	light	utilization	for	the	photocatal ytic HER.	Furthermore,	the	HER	pathways	of	on	boron‐doped	C 3N5 models	were	also	calculated	to	estimate	the	HER	properties.	In	g e n e r a l , 	t h e 	b o r o n 	d o p i n g 	c a n 	r e d u c e 	t h e 	H E R 	e n e r g y 	f o r 	Volmer	mechanism	under	acidic	condition.	The	B N4‐C3N5	and BIn‐C3N5	m o d e l s 	h a d 	a 	h i g h e r 	P D S 	f o r 	t h e 	a l k a l i n e 	V o l m e r mechanism	compared	to	pristine	C 3N5.	Remarkably,	the	Gibbs free	energy	of	hydrogen	bonding	(Δ GH*)	of	BN3‐C3N5	(0.11	eV)	is comparable	to	the	benchmark	Pt/C	catalyst	(–0.09	eV),	which	is	a	testament	to	its	performance	as	a	non‐metal	HER	catalyst	alongside	other	catalysts	such	as	boron	nitride	(Δ G H	=	2.66	eV) [75]	 and	 graphene	 oxide	 (Δ GH	= 	1 . 1 3 	e V ) 	[ 7 6 ] . 	M e a n w h i l e , BN3‐C3N5	displays	a	good	performance	in	acidic	media	through the	Volmer‐Tafel	and	Volmer‐Heyrovsky	mechanism	with	PDS	of	0.79	and	0.35	eV,	respectively.	The	B N3‐C3N5	models	also shows	 a	 decreased	 energy	 barrier	 for	 Volmer‐Tafel	 and	Volmer‐Heyrovsky	mechanism	in	alkaline	media,	with	a	PDS	of	0.47	and	0.87	eV,	respectively.	 Thus,	it	can	be	concluded	that the	introduction	of	boron‐dopant	improves	the	reaction	kinet‐ics	of	HER	in	alkaline	conditions	by	serving	as	a	binding	site	 for H*	in	N	substituted	boron	doped	C 3N5	models	and	OH*	inter‐ mediates	for	B C1‐C3N5	and	B In‐C3N5	models.	Overall,	these	find‐ ings	will	be	insightful	towards	 future	research	on	carbon	ni‐ tride	allotropes	for	photo‐	or	electro‐	driven	catalytic	applic a‐ tions.	As	a	future	recommendatio n,	other	elements	can	also	be introduced	alongside	boron	to	regulate	the	electronic	structure of	carbon	nitride.	For	instance,	Ding	et	al.	have	noted	that	th e introduction	of	fluoride	(F)	into	boron	doped	g‐C 3N4	systems can	facilitate	the	separation	of	electrons	and	holes	due	to	the ir matching	 electron	 orbit	 [64].	 Meanwhile,	 the	 sulfur‐boron	codoped	system	can	lead	to	a	bandgap	narrowing	effect	due	to	the	p‐orbital	hybridization	between	C,	N,	and	S	atoms.	Notably, a	different	choice	in	co‐dopant	can	give	rise	to	dissimilar	pro d‐ uct	 selectivity	 in	 multiple	 product	 applications	 such	 as	 CO 2 reduction	[77].	Therefore,	there	is	a	great	potential	in	the	ex ‐ ploration	of	different	codoping	systems	by	screening	through	computational	analysis	in	the	future. Electronic 	supporting 	information Data	such	as	the	chemical	potential	of	molecules,	band	gap positions,	density	of	states,	adsorption	configuration	and	Gibb s free	energies	of	the	adsorbed	states	are	available	in	the	elec‐ tronic	supplementary	information. Author	contributions Sue‐Faye	 Ng:	 conceptualization,	 methodology,	 validation, formal	 analysis,	 investigation,	 data	 curation,	 writing‐original draft,	writing‐review	and	editing.	Xingzhu	Chen:	conceptualiza‐ tion,	methodology,	validation,	formal	analysis,	writing‐review	and	editing.	Joel	Jie	Foo:	conceptualization,	methodology,	vali ‐ dation,	formal	analysis,	writing ‐review	and	editing.	Mo	Xiong: validation,	 investigation,	 data	 curation,	 resources,	 writ‐ing‐review	and	editing.	Wee‐Jun	Ong:	validation,	investigation, data	curation,	resources,	supervision,	writing‐review	and	edit‐ing,	funding	acquisition,	project	administration. Conflicts	 of	interest There	are	no	conflicts	of	interest	to	declare. References [1] Assessing 	the	Global	Climate	in	2021,	in:	NOAA	National	Centers for	Environmental	Information,	 2022. [2] L.	Ai,	S.‐F.	Ng,	W.‐J.	Ong,	 ChemSusChem ,	2022,	15,	e202200857. [3] H.	Mai,	T.	C.	Le,	D.	Chen,	D.	A.	Winkler,	R.	A.	Caruso,	 Chem.	Rev., 2022,	122,	13478–13515. [4] A.	K.	Singh,	K.	Mathew,	H.	L.