2D phosphides heterostructures on titanium microfiltration membrane for enhanced ampere-level current density overall seawater splitting Wenjing Dai1,2, Xin Wang1,2, Yulong Ma1,3, Sisi He1, Ming Chen1,3, Zhaohui Yin1,2, Shuheng Tian4, Maolin Wang4, Shixiang Yu4, Hang Zhang1,2, Yuanzhe Wang1, Hong Wang5, Jianxin Li5, Faming Gao1, Bowen Cheng2, Yun Wang   6 ✉, Zhen Yin   1,3 ✉, and Ding Ma   4 ✉ 1 Tianjin  Key  Laboratory  of  Brine  Chemical  Engineering  and  Resource  Eco-utilization,  College  of  Chemical  Engineering  and  Materials Science, Tianjin University of Science and Technology, Tianjin 300457, China2 State Key Laboratory of Biobased Fiber Manufacturing Technology, Tianjin University of Science and Technology, Tianjin 300457, China3 Haian Institute of High-Tech Research, Nanjing University, Haian 226600, China4 Beijing  National  Laboratory  for  Molecular  Sciences,  College  of  Chemistry  and  Molecular  Engineering,  Peking  University,  Beijing  100871, China5 State  Key  Laboratory  of  Separation  Membranes  and  Membrane  Processes,  Separation  Membrane  Science  and  Technology  International Joint Research Centre, Tiangong University, Tianjin 300387, China6 Centre for Catalysis and Clean Energy, School of Environment and Science, Gold Coast Campus, Griffith University, Southport, Queensland 4222, Australia Cite this article:  Nano Research , 2025,  18, 94907061.  https://doi.org/10.26599/NR.2025.94907061 ABSTRACT: The  advancement  of  direct  seawater  electrolysis  is  a significant step towards sustainable hydrogen production, addressing the critical  need  for  renewable  energy  sources  and  efficient  resource utilization.  However,  direct  seawater  electrolysis  has  to  face  several challenges posed by the corrosiveness of highly concentrated chloride and the  competitive  chlorine  evolution  reaction  (ClER).  To  overcome  these issues,  we  designed  a  novel  NiP 2@CoP  electrocatalyst  on  a  porous titanium  microfiltration  (Ti  MF)  membrane.  The  obtained  bifunctional NiP2@CoP catalyst outperforms the Pt/C and IrO 2, as evidenced by its low overpotentials of 192 and 425 mV at a current density of 500 mA·cm−2 for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) in alkaline seawater (1 M KOH + 0.5 M NaCl), respectively. Especially, only 231 and 569 mV overpotentials are required at the current density of 1500 mA·cm−2 towards HER and OER in alkaline seawater, respectively. More importantly, no ClER was observed, demonstrating its excellent selectivity to OER. The selection of porous Ti MF membrane as an electrode substrate further enhances the performance by providing a robust structure that promotes the fast generation and release of gas bubbles. Our promising outcomes obtained with NiP 2@CoP catalysts on Ti MF support, therefore, pave the way for the commercial viability of direct seawater electrolysis technologies at industrial-level current densities. KEYWORDS: direct  seawater  electrolysis,  porous  titanium  microfiltration  membrane,  phosphides  heterostructures, NiP2@CoP electrocatalyst, excellent selectivity to oxygen evolution reaction (OER) 1    Introduction Hydrogen  as  a  secondary  energy  source  with  a  high  combustionvalue,  low  carbon  emissions  and  zero  carbon  utilization  has attracted  growing  attention  [1−3].  The  hydrogen  production  via water electrolysis has also been used to store intermittent renewable energy sources, like solar and wind power [4]. Alkaline electrolysis presents  an  appealing  approach  for  water  splitting,  as  it  eliminates the  concerns  associated  with  acid  corrosion.  Up  to  date, commercial  water  electrolysis  technologies  exclusively  rely  on freshwater  as  the  primary  source  [5−8].  However,  the  freshwater resources are limited worldwide. In comparison, seawater resources Received: August 5, 2024; Revised: September 23, 2024 Accepted: October 2, 2024 ✉Address  correspondence  to Yun Wang, yun.wang@griffith.edu.au; Zhen Yin, yinzhen@tust.edu.cn; Ding Ma, dma@pku.edu.cn Open Access Research Article 94907061 (1 of 11) Nano Research , 2025, 18, 94907061
============================================================
[Page 2/11]
============================================================
are  abundant,  accounting  for  approximately  97%  of  all  water resources  on  the  Earth  [9].  In  this  regard,  using  seawater  as  the primary  source  for  water  electrolysis  is  the  future  direction  for hydrogen energy development. Nonetheless, the intricate solutes in natural  seawater  (such  as  Na+,  K+,  Mg2+,  Ca2+,  Cl−,  SO42−,  etc.)  have detrimental  effects  on  the  catalytic  activity  and  durability  of electrocatalysts [10, 11]. In particular, the competitive nature of the chlorine  evolution  reaction  (ClER)  hinders  the  progress  of  the oxygen  evolution  reaction  (OER)  and  leads  to  the  formation  of chlorine-containing products, which lowers the overall efficiency of seawater  electrolysis  [12, 13].  Hence,  it  is  desirable  to  design  and prepare  the  highly  efficient  and  highly  selective,  robust electrocatalysts for direct seawater electrolysis in alkaline. To  date,  noble-metal-based  materials  are  still  the  most  efficient catalysts  for  water  electrolysis,  including  Pt/C  for  the  hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and Ir/RuO 2 for the OER. The substantial costs  and  scarcity  of  noble  metal  catalysts,  combined  with  their singular  functionality  for  OER  and  HER,  impede  their  widespread industrial application [14, 15]. Non-noble metal-based bifunctional catalysts  have  emerged  as  prominent  candidates  to  lower  the  cost, but the high hydrogen binding energy of transition metals, e.g., Ni, hinder  their  H 2 release  efficiency  in  HER  [16−18].  Negatively charged  phosphorus  can  effectively  capture  protons  and significantly  modulate  the  d-band  centers  of  metal  atoms, weakening  the  chemical  bonds  between  the  metal  and  H,  thus facilitating H 2 desorption on transition metals [6]. Thus, transition metal  phosphides  (TMPs)  have  been  developed  and  exhibited remarkable  electrolytic  activity  and  stability  due  to  their  adaptable electronic structures and distinctive physicochemical properties [19, 20]. However, the utilization of metal phosphides for overall water splitting has been demonstrated to be constrained by the limitation of a solitary active site [21]. Recently,  several  strategies  have  been  developed  to  enhance  the overall catalytic activity of TMPs. Morphology controlling of TMPs is proposed to increase the surface area of TMPs, create more active sites  and  gas  channels,  and,  even  fabricate  two-dimensional  (2D) ultrathin nanosheets (NSs) [3, 15, 22]. Wang et al. reported that the 2D Ru/B-CoP had an excellent performance in seawater electrolysis [3].  The  introduction  of  guest  metal  atoms  is  another  way  to enhance the activity of TMPs by modulating the charge distribution and  synergy  of  heterogeneous  surface  [23, 24].  Lv  et  al.  reported  a three-metal  phosphide  (FCNP@CQDs)  with  an  open  nanotubular structure was prepared with Fe-metal-organic framework (MOF) as a  precursor  via  etching  and  phosphorization  processes,  which exhibited superior OER and HER activities at low current densities (268 mV overpotential for OER at 20 mA·cm−2) in alkaline seawater [25]. The interaction between multiple components can regulate the electronic  and  band  structures  of  TMPs  by  enabling  electron transfer  between  various  constituents  and  significantly  adjust  the physicochemical  properties  of  their  interface  [26−28].  Li  et  al. accomplished  the  construction  of  a  heterogeneous  structure  of C@CoP-FeP/FF  by  growing  Co-MOF in-situ on  Fe  foam  (FF), followed  by  high-temperature  phosphorization,  demonstrating excellent  seawater  electrolysis  performance  (1.73  V  for  the  overall seawater  splitting  voltage  at  100  mA·cm−2)  [29].  Still,  the  poor corrosion  resistance  of  the  substrate  limited  the  durability  of C@CoP-FeP/FF in alkaline salt water and resulted in a sharp drop of  activity  in  seawater  electrolysis.  Obviously,  the  activity  and stability  of  2D  heterogeneous  phosphides  cannot  meet  the requirements  of  practical  applications  and  there  is  still  significantpotential  for  improvement.  Moreover,  for  the  direct  seawater electrolysis, the 2D heterogeneous phosphides are less explored. Herein, we constructed a 2D bimetallic phosphide electrocatalyst (NiP 2@CoP)  with  abundant  interfacial  sites  through  an in-situ growth  process.  The  microfiltration  (MF)  titanium  membrane  is selected  as  an  electrode  substrate  due  to  its  microscale  porous structure, high mechanical strength and strong corrosion resistance [30, 31].  After  loading  ultrathin  NiCo 2O4 NSs  as  a  precursor  on  a MF  titanium  membrane,  the  NiP 2@CoP  was  then  prepared  by  a phosphorization treatment. The obtained NiP 2@CoP Ti membrane electrode demonstrates extremely low overpotentials of 222 mV for HER  and  509  mV  for  OER  at  a  high  current  density  of 1000  mA·cm−2 and  remains  operable  for  400  h  without  significant degradation. 2    Results and discussion 2.1    Synthesis and structure characterization Figure 1(a) illustrates the synthesis scheme of NiP 2@CoP NSs being anchored  on  the  MF  Ti-membrane  substrate  to  obtain  a  three- dimensional (3D) self-supported electrode. Firstly, the precursor of ultrathin  NiCo 2O4 NSs  is  loaded  on  the  pores  surface  of  the  Ti membrane  via  an in-situ growth  process,  i.e.,  hydrothermal method. Subsequently, the crystal structure of NiCo 2O4 is obtained through  calcination,  confirmed  by  the  X-ray  diffraction  (XRD) pattern  (Fig.  S1  in  the  Electronic  Supplementary  Material  (ESM)). By  employing  NaH 2PO4 as  the  phosphorus  source,  the  NiP 2@CoP NSs  is  prepared  via  a  partial  phosphorization  process  of  NiCo 2O4 precursor  under  Ar  flow  (Fig.  S2  in  the  ESM).  Similar  procedures are employed to prepare the monometallic nickel phosphide (NiP 2) and  cobalt  phosphide  (CoP),  respectively.  The  XRD  pattern  of  the NiP2@CoP/Ti  electrode  confirmed  the  co-existence  of  CoP  and NiP2 on Ti membrane (Fig. S3 in the ESM). To avoid the influence of  the  Ti  substrate,  the  powder  of  NiCo 2O4 (the  precursor  of NiP2@CoP)  is  prepared  through  a  similar  process  without  the  Ti membrane.  The  obtained  NiP 2@CoP  powder  showed  the diffraction peaks matching those of NiP 2 and CoP, consistent with the sample prepared on the Ti membrane (Fig. S4 in the ESM). In addition,  the  NiCoP  as  a  control  catalyst  is  prepared  via  the complete phosphorization process (Fig. S5 in the ESM). The  scanning  electron  microscope  (SEM)  images  reveal  the smooth and clean surface of Ti membrane (Fig. S6 in the ESM) and the uniform distribution of NiCo 2O4 NS precursor with a thickness of approximately 7 nm (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The porous nature of the  NiCo 2O4 NSs  could  be  identified  in  the  transmission  electron microscope  (TEM)  image  (Fig.  S8  in  the  ESM).  High-resolution TEM  (HRTEM)  images  show  that  the  nanosheet  has  a  lattice distance of 0.20 nm, corresponding to NiCo 2O4 (400) (Fig. S9 in the ESM).  After  the  phosphorization  process,  the  2D  ultrathin  sheet- like  nanostructure  and  the  porous  structure  is  preserved  in  the NiP2@CoP phosphide (Figs. 1(b) and 1(c) and Fig. S10 in the ESM), which is beneficial for the diffusion of electrolytes and the release of bubbles during reaction [32, 33]. The NiP 2-CoP interface is clearly demonstrated  in  the  HRTEM  images  (Figs.  1(d) and 1(e)),  where the lattice of 0.196 and 0.223 nm could be attributed to CoP (112) and  NiP 2 (211),  respectively.  The  scanning  TEM  (STEM)  image and the corresponding energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mappings also confirm the uniform dispersion of Ni, Co and  P  elements  (Figs.  1(f) and 1(f1)−1(f 3).  NiCoP,  NiP 2 and  CoPDai et al. Nano Research |  Vol. 18, No. 1 Nano Research , 2025,