samples were also prepared via similar procedures on Ti membrane (Figs.  S11–S16  in  the  ESM).  While  NS  structure  is  observed  in NiCoP  and  NiP 2 samples,  CoP  shows  a  needle-like  nanostructure, which is similar to its precursor. It was noted that the morphology of  the  precursor  was  well  preserved  on  the  membrane  pores  even after the phosphorization treatment. X-ray  photoelectron  spectroscopy  (XPS)  was  employed  to investigate  the  chemical  states  and  compositions  on  the  surface  of membrane  electrodes.  The  Ni,  Co,  P  and  O  elements  are prominently  presented  in  the  full  survey  XPS  spectrum  of NiP2@CoP  (Fig.  2(a)).  The  formation  of  NiP 2,  CoP  and  NiCoP  as the control samples are also verified with their corresponding XPS spectra. The high-resolution XPS spectra of Co 2p for NiCoP, CoP and  NiP 2@CoP  are  shown  in Fig.  2(b),  which  can  be  divided  into two  shake-up  satellites  (defined  as “Sat.”)  and  two  spin-orbit doublets.  The  peaks  at  778.5  and  793.5  eV  are  attributed  to  Co3+ 2p3/2 and Co3+ 2p1/2, whereas the peaks at about 782.1 and 797.9 eV belong  to  the  Co2+ 2p3/2 and  Co2+ 2p1/2,  respectively.  Especially,  the peak of Co3+ 2p3/2 centered at 778.5 eV is ascribed to the formation of Co–P bonds. The peak of Co2+ 2p3/2 at 782.1 eV is related to the oxidation state of Co, resulting from the formation of Co-PO x [34, 35]. In comparison to NiCoP and CoP, the binding energy of Co 2p in NiP 2@CoP exhibit a positive shift of 0.50 eV. The increase in Co 2p binding energy reflects a decrease in electron density around Co atoms, which is likely due to electron transfer from Co to the NiP 2 phase.  The  Ni  2p  spectra  of  NiP 2@CoP,  NiP 2 and  NiCoP  are presented in Fig. 2(c). The deconvolution of the Ni 2p spectrum ofNiCoP  reveals  Ni–P,  Ni2+ and  sat.,  while  NiP 2@CoP  and  NiP 2 exhibit  higher  oxidation  states  of  Ni3+.  The  existence  of  the  higher valence  state  of  Ni3+ is  advantageous  for  the  OER  [36−38].  The peaks  of  NiP 2@CoP  in  the  Ni  2p  spectra  shift  towards  lower binding  energy  compared  to  NiP 2.  The  Ni–P  are  located  at 853.3  and  870.7  eV.  As  a  reference,  the  Ni2+ peaks  at  857.1  and 875.0 eV and the Ni3+ peaks at 859.9 and 877.0 eV. The Co 2p and Ni  2p  peak  shifts  indicate  the  presence  of  charge  redistribution between NiP 2 and CoP at the interface, with electron transfer from Co to Ni in NiP 2@CoP. As  shown  in Fig.  2(d),  three  peaks  are  identified  in  the  high- resolution P 2p spectra. Two peaks at binding energies of 129.2 and 130.2  eV  are  well  assigned  to  P  2p 3/2 and  P  2p 1/2,  respectively.  The peak  at  about  134.6  eV  is  related  to  PO x or  P–O  species  due  to possible  oxidation  at  the  surface.  However,  for  the  NiP 2@CoP,  the positive shift of P 2p 3/2 and 2p 1/2 is observed relative to that of pure- NiCoP,  pure-CoP  and  NiP 2,  supporting  that  the  charge redistribution  occurs  owing  to  the  strong  interaction  of  the  NiP 2 and  CoP  at  the  interface  of  NiP 2@CoP,  which  would  significantly affect the electrocatalytic activity [39, 40]. 2.2    Electrocatalytic performance The  electrochemical  performance  of  as-prepared  electrocatalysts loading on the Ti MF membrane (TM) electrodes was investigated via  a  three-electrode  system  to  explore  the  water  splitting application.  The  bare  Ti  MF  membrane  electrode  exhibited minimal catalytic current across the examined potential range (Fig. Figure 1    (a)  Schematic  illustration  of  the  synthesis  procedure  for  the  NiP 2@CoP  catalyst;  (b)  SEM,  (c)  TEM,  (d)  and  (e)  HRTEM,  (f)  and  (f 1)–(f3)  STEM  images  and corresponding elemental mapping of Ni, Co and P for NiP 2@CoP NSs arrays.Nano Research |  Vol. 18, No. 1 Dai et al. 94907061 (3 of 11) Nano Research , 2025, 18, 94907061
