S17  in  the  ESM).  Figure  S18  in  the  ESM  shows  the  OER performance of the synthesized catalysts in 1 M KOH solution. The NiP2@CoP  exhibits  the  lowest  overpotentials  compared  to  that  of NiCoP, CoP, NiP 2 and NiCo 2O4. The overpotentials for NiP 2@CoP at  200,  1000  and  1500  mA·cm−2 are  347,  472  and  541  mV, respectively.  To  further  comprehend  the  advantages  of  bimetallic hybrid phosphide electrocatalysts, the electrochemical performance was  assessed  in  alkaline  simulated  seawater  (1  M  KOH  +  0.5  M NaCl). The performance of the electrocatalysts is compared to that of  control  samples,  including  NiCoP,  CoP,  NiP 2,  NiCo 2O4 and commercial IrO 2. Figure 3(a) displays the OER polarization curves of these samples. The cyclic voltammetry (CV) curve of NiP 2@CoP has  an  anodic  peak  at  1.35  V  due  to  the  oxidation  of  the  Co  ions before OER, which can be illustrated by the CV curve of CoP. The overpotentials  for  NiP 2@CoP  at  500,  1000  and  1500  mA·cm−2 are 425,  509  and  569  mV  in  alkaline  seawater,  respectively, demonstrating  the  lowest  overpotentials  than  the  value  shown  by NiCoP, CoP, NiP 2, NiCo 2O4 and commercial IrO 2. In Fig. 3(b), the Tafel  slopes  of  NiP 2@CoP,  NiCoP,  CoP,  NiP 2 and  NiCo 2O4 are 44.7,  48.54,  95.43,  60.01  and  124.67  mV·dec−1,  respectively, confirming  that  NiP 2@CoP  exhibits  the  fastest  OER  reaction kinetics. Figure  3(c) shows  the  overpotential  comparison  of NiP2@CoP,  NiCoP,  CoP,  NiP 2 and  NiCo 2O4 at  different  current densities. When the current density is below 500 mA·cm−2, the OER performances of NiP 2@CoP, NiCoP and NiP 2 are closer, which are better  than  that  of  CoP,  NiCo 2O4 and  IrO 2.  When  the  current  is higher  than  500  mA·cm−2,  the  electrocatalytic  performance  of NiP2@CoP is significantly better than that of other phosphides andoxides.  Especially,  the  overpotential  of  NiP 2@CoP  is  569  mV  at 1500 mA·cm−2, much lower than that of NiCoP (654 mV) and NiP 2 (750  mV),  demonstrating  dramatically  improved  OER  activity  of NiP2@CoP  at  high  current  densities  in  alkaline  seawater. Considering  that  the  loading  of  the  catalyst  has  an  effect  on  the current density of the catalytic process, the metal mass-normalized current  density  is  more  reliable  to  explore  the  activity  of  catalysts. As  shown  in  Fig.  S19  in  the  ESM,  NiP 2@CoP  has  the  best  mass activity with an overpotential of 300 mV at 500 A·g metal−1. To explore the  practical  feasibility  of  2D  NiP 2@CoP,  the  actual  seawater solution  (1  M  KOH  +  Bohai  seawater)  was  also  used  for  the  OER test (Fig. S20 in the ESM). It is seen that the NiP 2@CoP still exhibits the best electrochemical performance of OER compared with other control samples in actual seawater. The overpotential of NiP 2@CoP is 520 mV at 1000 mA·cm−2, much lower than that of NiCoP (548 mV),  NiP 2 (620  mV),  CoP  (670  mV)  and  NiCo 2O4 (820  mV). These  results  further  confirm  the  significantly  improved  OER activity  of  NiP 2@CoP  at  high  current  densities  in  actual  seawater, demonstrating  the  great  promising  potential  of  2D  hybrid NiP2@CoP for seawater splitting. The turnover frequency (TOF) is an essential parameter used to evaluate  the  intrinsic  activity  of  a  catalyst  [41].  As  shown  in Fig. 3(d), the TOF value of NiP 2@CoP is 0.249 s−1 at 1.6 V, 4.0, 2.9, 19.2 and  24.9  times  higher  than  that  of  NiCoP  (0.062  s−1),  NiP 2 (0.087 s−1), CoP (0.013 s−1) and NiCo 2O4 (0.010 s−1), respectively. At 1.7  V,  the  TOF  value  of  NiP 2@CoP  is  0.809  s−1,  5.2,  5.1,  21.9  and 19.3  times  higher  than  that  of  NiCoP  (0.155  s−1),  NiP 2 (0.158  s−1), CoP  (0.037  s−1)  and  NiCo 2O4 (0.042  s−1),  respectively.  The Figure 2    XPS spectra of NiP 2@CoP, CoP, NiP 2 and NiCoP. (a) Wide-scanning XPS spectra and high-resolution XPS spectra of (b) Co 2p, (c) Ni 2p and (d) 