significantly  higher  TOF  values  indicate  a  greater  intrinsic  activity towards  OER  for  the  NiP 2@CoP  electrocatalyst.  Furthermore,  the electrochemical  active  surface  area  (ECSA)  of  these  electrocatalysts was also calculated according to the double-layer capacitance (C dl). The Cdl of  NiP 2@CoP,  NiCoP,  CoP,  NiP 2 and  NiCo 2O4 are  50.1, 83.6, 84.5, 53.5 and 42.5 mF·cm−2 according to their corresponding CV  curves  (Fig.  S21  in  the  ESM),  respectively. Figure  3(e) depicts the  linear  sweep  voltammetry  (LSV)  curves  normalized  by  the ECSA, confirming the superior intrinsic activity of NiP 2@CoP. The different  performances  between  bimetallic  phosphide  and monometallic  phosphides  suggest  that  the  synergy  between  CoP and NiP 2 components can be responsible for the enhanced intrinsic activity  towards  OER,  indicating  the  pivotal  role  of  the  two- dimensional  ultrathin  porous  heterostructure.  Moreover,  the  OER catalytic  activity  of  NiP 2@CoP  catalyst  is  comparable  or  much superior compared with most of the reported TMP and other best OER  electrocatalysts,  especially  achieving  a  high  current  density  of 1000 mA·cm−2 with extremely lower overpotential (Fig. 3(f)). The long-term stability of the electrocatalyst is another significant criterion  for  the  application  of  direct  seawater  electrolysis. Figure 3(g) displays  the  current  density  curve  (ranging  from  50  to 400  mA·cm−2)  over  time  with  the  NiP 2@CoP  electrocatalyst.  The overpotential of the NiP 2@CoP catalyst keeps nearly constant in the corrosive electrolyte solution at a current density of 50 mA·cm−2 for 100  h,  demonstrating  its  remarkable  stability  in  alkaline  saline water.  It  is  worth  noting  that  the  chronopotentiometry  curves  arerelatively  smooth  without  distinct  fluctuations  during  the  OER operation  at  high  current  densities  from  100  to  400  mA·cm−2.  In addition,  for  2D  NiP 2@CoP  heterostructures  after  OER,  the nanosheet structure is still observed although the possible formation of  NiOOH/CoOOH  is  found  on  the  surface  (Figs.  S22  and  S23  in the  ESM),  demonstrating  excellent  long-term  stability  of morphology even with high current density of OER. Stimulated  by  the  recent  studies  on  TMP-based  HER  catalysis since  the  P  can  capture  H+ from  the  solution  to  improve  the  HER activity  [3, 6, 42],  the  HER  performance  of  the  NiP 2@CoP electrocatalyst  was  also  investigated.  In  1  M  KOH  solution  at  high current  densities  (>  1000  mA·cm−2),  the  HER  activity  sequence  of the  samples  is  found  to  be  NiP 2@CoP  >  NiCoP  >  CoP  >  NiP 2 > NiCo 2O4 (Fig.  S24  in  the  ESM).  In  an  alkaline  simulated  seawater electrolyte  (1  M  KOH  +  0.5  M  NaCl),  NiP 2@CoP  demonstrates superior  HER  performance,  even  surpassing  the  commercial  Pt/C. From Fig. 4(a), the overpotentials of NiP 2@CoP, NiCoP, NiP 2, CoP and  NiCo 2O4 are  150,  161,  217,  145  and  240  mV,  respectively,  at 200 mA·cm−2. When the current density reaches 1500 mA·cm−2, the overpotentials  of  NiCoP,  NiP 2,  CoP  and  NiCo 2O4 increase  to  299, 464,  348  and  519  mV,  respectively.  By  contrast,  the  NiP 2@CoP maintains  a  smaller  overpotential  (231  mV)  at  1500  mA·cm−2 (Fig. 4(b)), demonstrating superior HER activity at ampere-level current. According  to Fig.  4(c),  the  NiP 2@CoP  also  exhibits  the  smallest Tafel  slope  (34.19  mV·dec−1),  further  demonstrating  their  fastest HER kinetics and their superior HER performances even compared Figure 3    OER performance in 1 M KOH + 0.5 M NaCl. (a) Polarization curves, (b) Tafel plots and (c) overpotential at the different current densities. (d) TOF plots and (e)  polarization  curves  normalized  by  ECSA  of  NiP 2@CoP,  NiCoP,  NiP 2,  CoP  and  NiCo 2O4.  (f)  Comparison  of  the  state-of-the-art  catalysts  as  for  overpotentials  at different current densities. (g) Time-dependent overpotential curve under the different current densities.Nano Research |  Vol. 18, No. 1 Dai et al. 94907061 (5 of 11) Nano Research , 2025, 18, 94907061
