to  the  benchmark  Pt/C  catalyst  (41.12  mV·dec−1).  Remarkably, NiP2@CoP also has a notably higher TOF value. Its highest value of 3 s−1 at an overpotential of 231 mV is 8.5, 20.4 and 24.6 times higher than  that  of  NiCoP,  NiP 2 and  CoP,  respectively  (Fig.  4(d)). Furthermore,  NiP 2@CoP  shows  a  superior  ECSA-normalized current  density  and  mass  activity  compared  to  NiCoP,  NiP 2 and CoP  at  the  same  potential  (Fig.  4(e) and  Fig.  S25  in  the  ESM).  In the  actual  seawater  solution  (1  M  KOH  +  Bohai  seawater),  the NiP2@CoP  also  exhibits  the  best  electrocatalytic  activity  for  HER compared with other control samples in actual seawater (Fig. S26 in the  ESM).  The  overpotential  of  NiP 2@CoP  is  402  mV  at 1500  mA·cm−2,  much  lower  than  that  of  NiCoP  (440  mV),  CoP (416  mV),  NiP 2 (564  mV)  and  NiCo 2O4 (606  mV),  further demonstrating significantly improved HER activity of NiP 2@CoP at ampere-level current densities in actual seawater. Consequently, the best  HER  performance  of  NiP 2@CoP  indicates  that  the heterojunction  interface  between  NiP 2 and  CoP  is  the  primary active  site  for  HER.  Previous  studies  demonstrate  that  the  charge transfer  at  the  heterojunction  interface  can  effectively  coordinate the  charge  density  around  the  active  sites  to  reduce  the  hydrogen evolution  reaction  overpotentials  [6, 43−45].  As  suggested  by  the XPS  data,  the  charge  transfer  between  the  NiP 2 and  CoP components  elevates  the  electrocatalytic  performance  of NiP2@CoP, surpassing most catalysts reported in the literature (Fig. 4(f)). Chronoamperometry  tests  were  performed  to  illustrate  the exceptional HER stability of NiP 2@CoP, indicating that NiP 2@CoPcan sustain stability for 400 h at current densities ranging from 50 to  400  mA·cm−2 (Fig.  4(g)).  Moreover,  the  SEM  and  TEM  images demonstrate that the morphology and structure of used NiP 2@CoP after  the  continuous  operations  of  HER  is  negligible  even  after 300  h  of  HER  operation,  confirming  the  long-term  stability  of NiP2@CoP in the HER process (Figs. S27 and S28 in the ESM). To  investigate  the  kinetics  of  the  electrocatalysts  at  the  interface between  the  electrode  and  electrolyte,  electrochemical  impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were conducted in the simulated alkaline seawater (Fig. S29 in the ESM). Owing to the ultrathin and unique  porous  structure  of  NiP 2@CoP,  this  catalyst  exhibits  the lowest charge transfer resistance (R ct, 5.6 Ω) based on the fitted EIS data. It is consistent with the lower overpotential and smaller Tafel slope  shown  for  the  NiP 2@CoP.  The  porous  configuration  allows for efficient charge transfer and improved electrolyte diffusion [27, 46].  On  the  other  hand,  the  electron  redistribution  at  the  interface boosts  the  conductivity  of  NiP 2@CoP  [26].  To  further  analyze  the reaction kinetics of NiP 2@CoP, we employed the Bode phase plots of  the  EIS  measurements  at  different  overpotentials.  NiCoP  was used  as  a  control  to  highlight  the  advantages  of  the  NiP 2@CoP heterostructure  (Fig.  S30  in  the  ESM).  By  fitting  the  Nyquist  plots with  a  dual-parallel  equivalent  circuit  model,  we  identified R1, which represents the resistance to the adsorption of OH− species on the  catalyst  surface  (Fig.  S31  in  the  ESM).  To  quantify  the  OH− adsorption  kinetics,  we  plotted  logR 1 against  the  overpotential.  As shown  in  Fig.  S32  in  the  ESM,  NiP 2@CoP  exhibits  a  lower  slope compared to NiCoP, indicating accelerated OH− adsorption kinetics Figure 4    HER  performance  in  1  M  KOH  +  0.5  M  NaCl.  (a)  Polarization  curves,  (b)  overpotential  at  the  current  density  and  (c)  Tafel  plots.  (d)  TOF  plots  and  (e) polarization  curves  normalized  by  ECSA  of  NiP 2@CoP,  NiCoP,  NiP 2,  CoP  and  NiCo 2O4.  (f)  Comparison  of  the  state-of-the-art  catalysts  for  overpotentials  at 1000 mA·cm−2. (g) Time-dependent overpotential curve under the different current densities.Dai et al. Nano Research |  Vol. 18, No. 1 Nano Research , 2025, 18, 94907061 94907061 (6 of 11)
