[47].  This  indicates  that  the  heterostructured  NiP 2@CoP  exhibits enhanced  OH− adsorption  properties,  which  could  potentially improve its catalytic performance. 2.3    Sources of electrochemical activity To  explore  the  active  sites  of  NiP 2@CoP  involving  the  OER  and HER  processes,  the in-situ Raman  measurement  was  conducted during  LSV  tests  at  a  low  scanning  rate  of  1  mV·s−1.  Figure  S33  in the ESM shows the Raman bands specific to NiP 2/CoP for the OER process,  which  are  evident  without  applied  potential.  The NiP2@CoP exhibits obvious Raman peaks at 265, 351 and 447 cm−1, corresponding  to  the  Ni–P  and  Co–P  vibrations  in  the  NiP 2 and CoP  phases  [27].  When  the  potential  is  increased  from  1.15  to 1.25  V,  the  phosphide  vibrations  disappear.  By  contrast,  the  Ni–O and  Co–O  vibrations  of  the  (Ni,  Co)OOH  located  at  464.4  and 558.3 cm−1 are discerned [48, 49]. Furthermore, the intensities of the Ni/Co–O  peaks  are  gradually  strengthened  with  the  increase  of potential  from  1.25  to  1.45  V  and  finally  become  stable  above 1.55  V.  These  results  indicate  the  formation  of  active NiOOH/CoOOH  owing  to  self-reconstruction  of  heterogeneous interface  (above  1.23  V)  at  the  surface  of  NiP 2@CoP,  leading  to remarkable  OER  performance.  XPS  was  employed  to  further elucidate the chemical state of NiP₂@CoP after OER (Fig. S35 in the ESM). The Co 2p spectrum revealed the presence of Co3+, which is characteristic of CoOOH. In the Ni 2p spectrum, a notable increase in  the  Ni3+ peak  was  observed  after  OER,  indicating  an  oxidation state increase of Ni, which corresponds to the formation of NiOOH [33, 36].  These  results  confirm  that  NiP₂@CoP  undergoes  areconstruction  to  NiOOH/CoOOH  post-OER.  Moreover,  the surface  transformation  of  phosphides  is  beneficial  to  improve  the stability  of  NiP 2@CoP  during  the  oxidation  reaction  since  the formation  of  a  negatively  charged  anion  layer  can  repel  Cl− to enhance  the  corrosion  resistance  of  the  NiP 2@CoP  electrocatalyst [12, 27].  By  contrast,  the  NiP 2@CoP  during  HER  process substantially  retains  the  structure  of  metal  phosphides  in  the potential  range  from −0.1  to −0.55  V.  (Fig.  S34  in  the  ESM).  The good stability of NiP 2@CoP supports that the active site for HER is within the catalyst. 2.4    Overall seawater splitting To  investigate  the  performance  of  overall  seawater  splitting,  a standard two-electrode electrolyzer was used with NiP 2@CoP/Ti as both  the  cathode  and  anode  (Figs.  5(a) and 5(b))  in  simulated alkaline seawater (1 M KOH + 0.5 M NaCl). The electrode pair of the  benchmark  Pt/C||IrO 2 (loading  on  the  Ti  MF  membrane)  was also  employed  for  comparison.  The  electrolyzer  with NiP2@CoP||NiP 2@CoP  achieves  high  current  densities  of  200,  500 and 1000 mA·cm−2 at voltages of 1.90, 2.03 and 2.16 V, respectively, much  lower  than  the  noble  metal  Pt/C||IrO 2 (2.28  V  at 300 mA·cm−2) (Fig. 5(b)). At the same time, NiP 2@CoP||NiP 2@CoP remains  highly  active  in  alkaline  seawater  (1  M  KOH  +  Bohai seawater), reaching 1000 mA·cm−2 at 2.25 V (Fig. S36 in the ESM). The  gases  of  hydrogen  and  oxygen  were  collected  via  gas chromatography  at  current  density  of  100,  200  and  500  mA·cm−2, respectively,  for  measurement  of  the  faradaic  efficiency  (Fig.  5(c) and Fig. S37 in the ESM). The faradaic efficiency for hydrogen and Figure 5    Overall seawater splitting performance. (a) Schematic illustration of the electrolyzer. (b) Polarization curve, (c) Faraday efficiency (FE) at current density of 100, 200 and 500 mA·cm−2 and (d) long-term stability test of the electrolyzer performed at a constant current density of 1000 mA·cm−2 for NiP 2@CoP||NiP 2@CoP. The solution of 1 M KOH + 0.5 M NaCl was used as electrolyte.Nano Research |  Vol. 18, No. 1 Dai et al. 