oxygen  production  is  close  to  100%.  The  produced  hydrogen  and oxygen  volume  ratio  is  2:1,  indicating  the  exclusive  occurrence  of HER and OER reactions in the overall seawater electrolysis. Figure S38 in the ESM shows the experiment of detecting hypochlorite in the  electrolyte  after  overall  seawater  electrolysis  using NiP2@CoP||NiP 2@CoP  membrane  electrode.  No  hypochlorite  was detected in the electrolyte solution, further confirming the absence of  the  ClER.  Therefore,  NiP 2@CoP  exhibits  extremely  high selectivity  of  OER.  The  stability  of  NiP 2@CoP||NiP 2@CoP  on  Ti MF  membrane  electrodes  is  verified  at  a  constant  current  density (1000 mA·cm−2) for 300 h (Fig. 5(d)). The Pt/C||IrO 2 exhibited rapid downward  stabilization.  The  small  degradation  demonstrates  the outstanding  stability  of  NiP 2@CoP||NiP 2@CoP  at  high  current densities and its robust corrosion resistance in seawater. 2.5    Advantage  of  membrane  electrode  for  seawater electrolysis Depositing  the  catalyst  onto  a  suitable  support  can  significantly enhance its activity and stability [50, 51]. The selection of a support becomes  more  essential  for  electrocatalytic  seawater  splitting.  This is because seawater has a more complex chemical composition than fresh water. Its high salt concentration can cause the corrosion and degradation  of  electrocatalysts  [12, 52, 53].  Over  the  last  decade, porous  reticulated  nickel  foam  (NF)  has  been  widely  used  as  a common  electrode  substrate  due  to  its  affordability,  favorable conductivity and ability to hold an increased amount of catalyst [54, 55].  However,  NFs  are  vulnerable  to  corrosion  and  have  limited durability when exposed to seawater [53, 56, 57]. As a result, porous Ti MF membranes have emerged as a promising alternative due to their  great  strength,  high  conductivity  and  strong  corrosion resistance [31, 58]. To  demonstrate  the  unique  advantage  of  porous  TM  for seawater  splitting  used  in  this  study,  common  Ti  plate  (TP) electrode  substrates  is  selected  to  prepare  the  corresponding NiP2@CoP/TP  through  the  same  preparation  procedures.  The electrochemical performance of NiP 2@CoP/TM and NiP 2@CoP/TP in 1 M KOH + 0.5 M NaCl electrolyte solution is shown in Fig. S39 in  the  ESM.  For  OER,  the  overpotentials  at  1500  mA·cm−2 are  657 and  1392  mV  for  NiP 2@CoP/TM  and  NiP 2@CoP/TP  electrodes, respectively. The HER overpotentials at 1500 mA·cm−2 are 244 and 578 mV for NiP 2@CoP/TM and NiP 2@CoP/TP, respectively. To understand the origin of the outstanding performance of TM support. The contact angles were measured and shown in Fig. S40 in the ESM, which suggest hydrophobicity across the two substrate surfaces.  Figure  S41  in  the  ESM  shows  the  contact  angles  after loading  NiP 2@CoP  catalyst  on  the  TM  and  TP,  along  with observations  on  bubble  generation  and  detachment.  It demonstrates the superior hydrophilicity of NiP 2@CoP nanosheets since  the  substrates  transform  from  original  hydrophobic  to hydrophilic after loading it. Especially, the contact angle cannot be observed  for  the  super  hydrophilic  NiP 2@CoP/TM,  demonstrating the  high  loading  capability  of  the  catalyst  due  to  the  porous structure of the MF membrane. The superior hydrophilicity of the catalyst  can  facilitate  contact  between  catalytic  active  sites  and electrolytes,  promoting  rapid  electrolyte  diffusion  at  high  currents to  enable  efficient  activities  at  high  current  densities  [14]. Additionally, the bubbles on the surface of NiP 2@CoP/TM are tiny and dense. In contrast, the bubbles on NiP 2@CoP/TP are large and sparse.  These  results  confirm  that  the  catalytic  surface  of NiP2@CoP/TM  exhibits  better  hydrophilicity  to  facilitate  fastelectrolyte diffusion and rapid bubble release, especially at ampere- level  high  current  conditions  [59, 60].  On  the  contrary,  the  gas bubble accumulation on the surfaces of NiP 2@CoP/TP may occlude the  active  sites,  hamper  electrolyte-catalyst  interaction,  elevate resistance  and  ultimately  diminish  catalytic  activity  [27, 61].  As  a result, the superior electrochemical performance of NiP 2@CoP/TM can  be  attributed  to  the  unique  porous  nature  of  the  titanium  MF membrane,  which  promotes  the  fast  generation  and  release  of  gas bubbles to improve electrocatalytic activity and stability. 3    Conclusions In  summary,  a  novel  self-supported  and  bifunctional  electrode through  the  integration  of  2D  NiP 2@CoP  and  a  porous  Ti  MF membrane  was  designed  and  synthesized.  The  NiP 2@CoP  catalyst showcases  exceptional  HER  and  OER  performances  in  alkaline seawater.  The  obtained  NiP 2@CoP  catalyst  achieves  a  current density of 500 mA·cm−2 with overpotentials of 192 and 425 mV for HER and OER in simulated alkaline seawater (1 M KOH + 0.5 M NaCl),  respectively.  Especially,  at  a  high  current  density  of 1500  mA·cm−2,  the  catalyst’s  overpotentials  are  only  231  and 569  mV  towards  HER  and  OER,  respectively,  which  significantly surpasses  those  of  traditional  benchmark  Pt/C  and  IrO 2 catalysts. The  synergistic  effects  of  the  bimetallic  phosphides  on  their interface  are  believed  to  optimize  the  electronic  structure  of  the active  sites  and  improve  overall  catalytic  activity.  Moreover,  the porous  structure  of  Ti  MF  membrane  support  can  enable  the  fast generation  and  release  of  gas  bubbles,  which  further  benefit  the direct  seawater  electrolysis  at  high  current  densities.  This  research lays  the  groundwork  for  future  developments  in  hydrogen production from seawater, offering a pathway towards cost-effective and durable seawater-to-H 2 conversion systems. Electronic  Supplementary  Material: Supplementary  material (experimental section, photos of samples, XRD patterns, SEM/TEM images, LSV, CV, EIS, in-situ Raman and contact angle) is available in  the  online  version  of  this  article  at https://doi.org/10.26599/ NR.2025.94907061. Data availability All  data  needed  to  support  the  conclusions  in  the  paper  are presented  in  the  manuscript  and  the  Electronic  Supplementary Material.  Additional  data  related  to  this  paper  may  be  requested from the corresponding author upon request. Acknowledgements The  authors  gratefully  acknowledge  the  financial  support  of  the Natural  Science  Foundation  of  Tianjin  for  Distinguished  Young Scholar  (No.  20JCJQJC00150),  Basic  Research  Program  of  Jiangsu Province  (No.  BK20241845),  the  National  Natural  Science Foundation of China (No. 21872104), Tianjin Research Innovation Project  for  Postgraduate  Students  (No.  2022BKY135),  and  the National Key Research and Development Program of China (Nos. 2020YFA0211003 and 2020YFA0211002). Declaration of competing interest All  the  contributing  authors  report  no  conflict  of  interests  in  this work. Dai et al. Nano Research |  Vol. 18, No. 1 Nano Research , 2025, 18, 94907061 94907061 (8 of 11)
