Short Communication 2D WS 2nanosheet supported Pt nanoparticles for enhanced hydrogen evolution reaction Yunxia Zhanga,b,c, Junqing Yana,b,c,**, Xianpei Rena,b,c, Liuqing Panga,b,c, Hong Chena,b,c, Shengzhong (Frank) Liua,b,c,d,* aKey Laboratory of Applied Surface and Colloid Chemistry, National Ministry of Education, PR China bShaanxi Engineering Lab for Advanced Energy Technology, PR China cSchool of Materials Science &Engineering, Shaanxi Normal University, Xi 'an, 710119, PR China dDalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Dalian National Laboratory for Clean Energy, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian, 116023, PR China article info Article history: Received 26 November 2015 Received in revised form 17 August 2016Accepted 25 August 2016Available online xxx Keywords: WS 2nanosheet Platinum nanoparticles Adsorption-photoreductionHERabstract By taking advantage of large speciﬁc surface area and large number of reaction sites of the2D WS 2nanosheet (NS), we developed a simple adsorption-photoreduction method to load Pt nanoparticles (NPs) on the NS carrier. The WS 2NSs were soaked in KPtCl 4solution for sufﬁcient amount of time to ensure the adsorption equilibrium of the Pt ions on the WS 2NS substrate. Upon photolysis and H 2post-treatment, uniformly dispersed Pt0NPs (~2 nm in size) were harvested. The hybrid structure containing 0D Pt0NPs and 2D WS 2thin-layers showed distinctly enhanced electrocatalytic activity for hydrogen evolution reaction(HER) with the overpotential at a current density of 10 mA cm /C02reduced to 80 mV compared with commercial Pt/C electrode. The synergetic effect of 0D and 2D structures may encourage designing new catalysts systems for special application. ©2016 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. Introduction Recently, the rapidly excessive consumption of the conven- tional fossil fuels has caused severe environmental contami- nation and it has become apparent that our world demands for an alternative clean energy. Hydrogen is a leading candi-date for it is clean, efﬁcient and sustainable. Compared withthe commercial steam-reformed hydrogen generationprocess, hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) by electro- catalytic water splitting has been proven to be a renewable, sustainable and environmental friendly method [1e4]. There is no doubt that Pt-group metals are the best HER catalysts.However, their scarcity and high costs have hindered theirbroad applications. Even though there have been tremendousresearch to develop effective electrocatalysts with minimal Ptloading, it remains to be a challenge to achieve good catalyticactivity using minimized Pt [5e7]. To date, several efforts have *Corresponding author . Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Dalian National Laboratory for Clean Energy, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Dalian, 116023, PR China. **Corresponding author . Shaanxi Engineering Lab for Advanced Energy Technology, PR China. E-mail addresses: junqingyan@snnu.edu.cn (J. Yan), szliu@snnu.edu.cn (S.(F). Liu). Available online at www.sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/he international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2016) 1 e6 http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2016.08.225 0360-3199/ ©2016 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. Please cite this article in press as: Zhang Y, et al., 2D WS 2nanosheet supported Pt nanoparticles for enhanced hydrogen evolution reaction, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2016), http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2016.08.225
