been devoted toward above mentioned aim by means of constructing hybrid structure based on minimal Pt and two- dimensional (2D) conductive substrate materials [8e11]. For example, Zhai et al. demonstrated enhanced activity formethanol oxidation by loading Pt nanoclusters on MoS 2/gra- phene nanosheets [12]. Li et al. described a two-step process to fabricate Pt NPs and MoS 2NSs on carbon ﬁbers as an efﬁcient HER catalyst [13]. Zhang et al. demonstrated epitaxially grown of Pt on 2D MoS 2NS with respectable HER activity [14]. It should be mentioned that the 2D nano-materials such as Tungsten disulﬁde (WS 2) with nanometer thickness have been discovered with ultrafast electron transport [15], large speciﬁc surface area [16]and extremely large number of reaction sites [17]. The mentioned intrinsic fortes suggest the possible application of loading uniformly dispersed ultraﬁne metalnanoparticles and excellent catalytic reaction. Herein, for theﬁrst time, we take a facile method to synthesize WS 2NS supports followed by one simple adsorption-photoreductionstrategy for platinum nanoparticles loading. As a result, thewell dispersed 0Pt NPs with the size of 2 nm were supported on the surface of WS 2nanosheet. Moreover, the 0D and 2D nanocomposites demonstrated signiﬁcantly promotion ofHER activity compared to with commercial Pt/C and other WS 2-based electrocatalyst. In addition, the adsorption- photoreduction method for Pt NPs decorated on WS 2NS also provides a new strategy for the facile and high-efﬁciencypreparation of 0D-2D nanocomposites materials. The syner-getic effect of 0D and 2D structures may encourage designingnew catalysts systems for special application. Experimental Preparation of WS 2, Pt/WS 2 WS 2NSs were synthesized using a method reported in liter- ature [18]. The Pt NPs on 2D WS 2NS hybrid nanocomposite was prepared by an in-house developed adsorption-photoreduction method. 3.1 mg WS 2was added into 10 mL of 0.2 mM K 2PtCl 4solution containing 0.3 mM trisodium cit- rate. The mixture was treated using ultrasonication for 30 minand then stirred for ca. 5 h to reach the adsorption- dissocia-tion equilibrium. The mixture was then irradiated with a150 W halogen lamp for 2 h. The resultant solution wascentrifuged at 8000 r.p.m for 15 min; the Pt/WS 2hybrid nanomaterial (~10 wt% Pt in Pt/WS 2) was obtained. The black products were heated in a tube furnace at 200/C14C for 2 h under active ﬂow of H 2/Ar (5 vol% H 2) mixture at 100 sccm (cubic centimeter per minute) to form the ﬁnal product for the HERapplication. Material characterizations Field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) was performed on a SU8020; Atomic force microscopy (AFM) im- ages were obtained by a Bruker Multimode 8. A JEM-2100 wasused for transmission electron microscopy (TEM) studies; X-ray diffraction (XRD) was acquired with a DX-2700; The X-raydiffraction (XRD) was acquired on a DX-2700 and elementalanalysis was conducted using an AXIS ULTRA X-rayphotoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) using monochromatic Al Ka X-ray source. Electrochemical characterization Electrochemical measurements were carried out on an IM6ex (Zahner, Germany) workstation with the standard three-electrode cell. The Ag/AgCl (in 3.5 M KCl solution) electrodeand platinum foil were used as the reference electrode and thecounter electrode, respectively. A glassy carbon (GC) electrodewith catalyst loading was used as the working electrode. Toprepare the GC electrode, 4 mg of catalysts and 80 mLo f5w t% Naﬁon solution were dispersed in 1 mL of a water/ethanol (4:1(v/v)) mixed solvent under active sonication for 30 min to forma homogeneous solution; 5 mL portion of the resulting solution dispersion was drop cast onto a GC electrode before being dried at room temperature. The commercial Pt/C catalyst (10% Pt on activated charcoal) loaded onto the GC in the same wayat the same Pt loading. The electrochemical impedancespectroscopy (EIS) was carried out at h¼0.2 V from 10 /C02e106Hz with an AC voltage amplitude of 5 mV. Results and discussion The XRD patterns of WS 2and Pt/WS 2are shown in Fig. 1 A. The diffraction peaks at 14.3/C14, 32.7/C14, 43.9/C14, and 58.4/C14are attributed to the (002), (100), (006), and (110) lattice planes of the hexag-onal structure of WS 2(JCPDS No. 08-0237). Obviously, the full- width-at-half-maximum of the (002) Bragg peak shows thelower signal related to the standard signal pattern, suggesting the WS 2multilayer structure [4]. According to Scherer equa- tion based on the (002) peak, the thickness of synthesized WS 2 can be calculated to be ca. 4.6 nm, indicating seven WS 2 monolayers. On the other hand, for the Pt-WS 2sample, no metallic Pt or PtO xsignals can be detected according to the above XRD results, suggesting the Pt NPs are well disperse onthe WS 2NS carrier [19]. The surface and sub-surface states of the WS 2and Pt/WS 2 samples are examined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy(XPS) as shown in Fig. 1 BeD.Fig. 1 B shows two peaks at 162.1 eV and 163.1 eV that are indexed to S 2p 1/2and S 2p 3/2, suggesting the existence of the S2/C0species in the samples [20,21] .Fig. 1 C gives the W 4f region results, two binding en- ergy peaks centered at 32.8 eV and 35 eV are detected andassigned to the typical signals of W 4þ4f7/2and W4þ4f5/2, respectively. Obviously, no obvious binding energy shift of W4f and S 2p can be observed for the pure WS 2and Pt/WS 2 samples, and here result suggests the similar chemical envi-ronment for the two elements. The Pt 4f XPS spectra of Pt/WS 2 inFig. 1 D shows the characteristic of metallic Pt signals, which are located at 70.7 and 73.9 eV. The morphology and the size of the studied samples are examined by FESEM, AFM and TEM. As seen from Fig. 2 A and B, the WS 2sample is composed of large quantities of uniform WS 2NSs. It is clear that these NSs are ca. 2 nm in thickness and connected with each other, suggesting WS 2sample indeed the multilayer structure. The TEM images in Fig. 2 D further support the multilayer structure. For the Pt/WS 2 sample, Fig. 2 C shows the FESEM image with theinternational journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2016) 1 e6 2 Please cite this article in press as: Zhang Y, et al., 2D WS 2nanosheet supported Pt nanoparticles for enhanced hydrogen evolution reaction, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2016), http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2016.08.225
