corresponding EDX mapping results, which display uniform distribution of W, S and Pt elements [8].Fig. 2 E shows a higher resolution TEM image of the sample. Obviously, uniform dis- tribution of dark dots with the uniform size of ca.2 nm on the WS 2can be observed. Fig. 2 F shows lattice fringes of a repre- sentative Pt NP with the spacing ca. 0.22 nm well in line withthe interplanar spacing of metal Pt (111) facet. As mentioned in the above text, the 2D structure is chosen for its large speciﬁc surface area and large number ofadsorption and reaction sites. To ensure homogeneousdispersion and adsorption of the Pt NPs on the WS 2NS, the Pt precursor and WS 2is mixed and actively stirred for as long as 5 h when the mixture is illuminated under the halogen lampfor 2 h, the semiconductive WS 2NS is excited to produce electrons and holes. The electrons, as strong reducing agent, would reduce the adsorbed PtO xto form metallic Pt NPs. The out-layer PtO xNPs on the WS 2are ﬁnally reduced to metallic Pt0(Fig. 1 D) by H 2at elevated temperature.The nanohybrid material was tested as the HER catalyst. Fig. 3 A shows a typical three-electrode system with the N 2- saturated 0.5 M H 2SO4electrolyte. The commercial Pt/C was used as a reference electrode. As shown in the polarization curves (I eV plot) ( Fig. 3 A), the GC alone shows a straight line at 0 current across the tested voltage range, meaning no HERactivity. While the bare WS 2gives a high overpotential 380 mV at current density of 10 mA cm/C02, the Pt/WS 2hybrid shows much reduced overpotential as low as 80 mV. The Pt/Celectrode shows superior HER activity with negligible over-potential as expected. However, at the same potential, the Pt-WS 2catalyst afforded higher current density than did the commercial Pt/C. On the other hand, Table 1 provides a summary of the overpotential using a variety of different catalysts, it is clear that the Pt/WS 2hybrid is superior compared to WS 2-based peers ( Table 1 ). We attribute the 