FULL PAPER 3D carbon foam supported edge -rich N-doped MoS 2 nanoflakes for enhanced electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution Xueying Jia,[a] Hongyuan Ren,[a] Hanbin Hu,[a] and Yu -Fei Song*[a] Abstract:  Molybdenum disulfide (MoS 2) is one of the most promising alternatives to the Pt -based electrocatalysts for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). But its performance is currently limited by the insufficient active edge sites and poor electr on transport. Hence , enormous efforts have bee n devoted to constru cting more  edge active sites and improving conductivity to obtain enhanced electrocatalytic performance . Herein, the 3D carbon foam  (denoted as CF)  supported edge -rich N-doped MoS 2 nanoflak es were successfully fabricated by using the commercially available polyurethane foam ( PU) as 3D substrates and PMo 12O403- clusters (doneted as PMo 12) as Mo source via redox polymerization and  followed by  sulfurization. Owing  to the uniform distribution of nanoscale Mo sources and 3D carbon foam substrate, the as -prepared MoS 2-CF composite  possessed well - exposed active edge sites and enhanced  electrical conductivity. Systematic investigation demonstrated that the MoS 2-CF compo site showed high HER performance with a low overpotential of 92 mV in 1.0 M of KOH and 155 mV in 0.5 M of H 2SO 4 at a current density of 10 mAcm-2, respectively.  This work offers a new pathway for rational design of the MoS 2-based HER electrocatalysts. Introduction Driven by global energy demands and environmental consideration, electrochemical water splitting is one of the important tasks for hydrogen production.[1-3] Due to their good catalytic efficiency and structural stability for HER, transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) have attracted extensive  attention as alternatives to the Pt -group electrocatalysts.[4-6] In general, it is relatively beneficial for the HER process in acidic solution, whereas the electrochemical water splitti ng in industry is usually operated in strong alkaline solution.[7] Currently , the electro catalytic performance of MoS 2 is not unsatisfactory, which is even worse in alkaline solution than in acidic solution. [8] Accordingly, it is importance to develop hig h performance MoS 2- based catalysts for HER process both at acidic and alkaline solutions.  Theoretical studies proved that the high HER activity of MoS 2 mostly originates from the edge sites, which is comparable to that of Pt -group catalysts.[8-10] However, the electrocatalytic activities of the bulk MoS 2 electrocatalysts are unsatisfied  owing to the insufficient active edge sites of MoS 2 and poor electri cal conductivity .[11-12] For overcoming their drawbacks , many conductive substrate s have been employed to synthesize MoS 2- based catalysts to  obtain more active sites and enhanced electrical conductivity , such as graphene, carbon nanotubes, nickel foam and so on.[14-21] They can not only improve the electrical conductivity  but also promote the dispersion of  MoS 2. As such, more active edge sites can be  exposed . [22, 23] Recently , it has been demonstrated that substrates with  3D structure are more efficie nt than those with 2D structure for HER process . This is b ecause that, compared with the 2D substrates, the electrocatalysts with 3D structure exhibit  more contact area with the electrolyte, less aggregation and maintain superior intrinsic properties of MoS 2.[24-27] For example, Piao et al.  reported that the MoS 2-coated macroporous carbon foam/ N-doped graphene (CF -NG@MoS 2) 3D nanostructures showed super ior HER performance with low overpotential and small Tafel slope.[28] Sung  et al.  successfully synthesized the 3D carbon/MoS 2 by an in-situ method, which was motivated by the graphene ribbon synthesis process.  Their cross -connected and porous structure led to the high HER activity.[29] Nevertheless , the preparation process for 3D sbustrates is generally complication and tedious . It remains a great challenge to prepare  highly efficient and low - cost MoS 2-based electrocatalysts immobilized on the 3D substrate , in particular, to use them directly as stable binder -free HER electrodes . [30, 31 ] Among many 3D substrates , the open -cell flexible polyurethane foam (PU) is one of the most diverse engineering polymers and consists of the chain with repeating urethane units.[32] Due to its industrial availability and 3D macroporous structure , it can be easy to obtain interconnected pores of carbon foam after pyrolysis of polyurethane foam, which is a low -cost and facile approach to large scale production  of 3D carbon substrate . [33-36] Herein, the N-doped MoS 2/carbon foam (MoS 2-CF) was prepared by using the commercially available PU as 3D substrates and the PMo 12 as Mo source via redox polymerizati on and followed by sulfurization.  The PU foam as template offered a 3D macroporous structure and interconnecting interface for supporting MoS 2 nano flakes . Due to the unique 3D carbon foam and uniform distribution of PMo 12 precursor , the as -prepared MoS 2-CF obtained  well-exposed active sites . Besides, the 3D carbon substrate allowed the infiltration of electrolytes to exposed active sites of MoS 2 while offered  efficient electr on transport  as confirmed by the reduced charge -transfer resistance (R ct).The MoS 2-CF composite exhibited excellent electrocatalytic activity for HER both in 0.5  M H 2SO 4 and 1 .0 M KOH.  [a] X. Jia, H. Ren, H. Hu, Prof. Dr. Y.-F. Song State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology Beijing 100029 P. R. China. E-mail: songyf@mail.buct.edu.cn Supporting information for this article is given via a link at the end of the document. 10.1002/chem.201904669 Accepted Manuscript Chemistry - A European Journal This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
