FULL PAPER Results and Discussion The hierarchical synthe tic strategy for MoS 2-CF was presented  in Figure 1. First of all, commercially available, open -cell and flexible polyurethane foam was selected as the 3D scaffold. The PMo 12 precursor was evenly deposited on the PU foam framework by redox polymerization with pyrrole  (Py) monomer (denoted as PU - PPy-PMo 12). The d igital photograph s of fabrication process w ere shown in the Fig ure S1. As shown in Fig ure S1a, direct sulfurization of PU can result in the formation of bulk carbon materials . But , when PU was coated with PPy -PMo 12, further sulfurization of the resulting PU -PPy-PMo 12 can lead to the formation of MoS 2-CF. This result indicated the PPy -PMo 12 provide d effective protect ion and support for  the PU substrate. Figure 1. The hierarchical fabrication process for the preparation of the MoS 2- CF composite. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of PU -PPy- PMo 12 (Figure 2a) showed that the typical 3D macroporous structure with a smooth skeleton. After the polymerization  process , the resultant PU-PPy-PMo 12 composite turned  rough, tiny PPy- PMo 12 were  evenly dispersed  on the surface of the PU. (Figure 2b). HRTEM image s of the PU -PPy-PMo 12 (Figure 2c) clearly showed the uniform distribution of PMo 12 clusters on the coating layer of PU . Figure 2. SEM images of (a) PU and (b) PU -PPy-PMo 12; (c) HRTEM image of PU-PPy-PMo 12; (d) SEM , (e) HRTEM , and  (f) AFM image of MoS 2-CF. The ATR-FTIR patterns confirmed the presence of PMo 12 and polypyrrole (PPy) in the PU -PPy-PMo 12 composite (Fig ure S2). As shown in  Figure S2, the composite displayed four peaks at 1063, 965, 870, and 787 cm-1, which can be assigned to ν(P -O), ν(Mo - Od), ν(Mo -Ob-Mo), and ν(Mo -Oc-Mo) of PMo 12, respectively.[37] The vibration centered at 1550 cm-1 (C=C), 1320cm-1 and 1200cm-1 can be attr ibuted to ν(C=C), ν(C -H), and ν(C -N), respectively, indicating the PPy was coated on the surface of the porous PU substrate.[38] Besides, the XPS spectra obtained for the H3PMo 12O40, PPy -PMo 12 and PU -PPy-PMo 12 were shown in Figure S4. Compared with the H 3PMo 12O40, the Mo 3d spectra of PU-PPy-PMo 12 was deconvoluted into four peaks, corresponding to Mo6+ (232.6 eV , 235.7 eV) and Mo4+ (231. 2, 234. 1 eV), respectively. It indicated that the PMo 12 can be used as oxidizing agent for the p olymerization of Py and partially converted into “heteropoly blue” . As shown in Fig ure 2d, the SEM image of MoS 2-CF showed that the resulting composite retained the original porous structure of the PU template. And the MoS 2 nanoflakes were loaded on the surface of the 3D carbon substrate.  Besides, HRTEM image illustrated that MoS 2 with a few layers (≤5 layers)  formed on 3D carbon foam (Fig ure 2e). Contrast experiment  of the sulfurization products of PPy -PMo 12 sphere (denote d as MoS 2-CS, Figure S3) showed the thicker MoS 2 layers than that of MoS 2-CF. This result implied that the 3D carbon substrate  of PU  effectively restrained the aggregation of MoS 2 nanoflakes. Atomic force microscopy (AFM) was conducted to determine the thi ckness of the MoS 2 nanoflakes on the carbon foam. The thickness of MoS 2 was approximately 3.5 nm, confirming that the MoS 2 nanoflakes were ultrathin without aggregation (Fig ure 2f). Figure 3. (a) Raman spectra of MoS 2-CF (b) XRD patterns of MoS 2, MoS 2-CS and MoS 2-CF. (c) High -resolution core -level XPS spectra for N1s of MoS 2-CF composite. (d), (e) High -resolution core -level XPS spectra for Mo 3d and S 2p corresponding peaking fitting analysis of MoS 2-CF and MoS2 -CS, respe ctively. (f) Mo K -edge FT -EXAFS of MoS 2-CF. The chemical composition and phase identification of MoS 2- CF was subsequently obtained by Raman scattering analysis. 10.1002/chem.201904669 Accepted Manuscript Chemistry - A European Journal This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
