FULL PAPER Raman signals at 402 and 378 cm-1 (Figure 3a) for MoS 2-CF were attributed to out -of-plane A1g and in -plane E12g phonon modes of 2H-MoS 2, respectively.[39] Moreover, it is well -known that the A1g mode represented edge -terminated structures, whereas E12g mode was preferentially excited for terrace -terminated structures.[40] Accordingly, the integrated  intensity ratio of the E12g and A1g can reflect the structural edge termination of MoS 2. Notably, the MoS 2-CF displayed the ratio of 0.46, which was lower than those of MoS 2-CS (0.48) and MoS 2 (0.59), indicating the MoS 2-CF possessed more edge sites. Moreover, the slight shift of Raman peaks can be explained by the  presence of strain - induced changes on the 3D substrate.[41-42] Figure 3b showed the XRD patterns of MoS 2-CF, MoS 2-CS, pure MoS 2, respectively. The XRD patterns of MoS 2-CF and MoS 2-CS showed two characteristic peaks at 32.7°  and 58.7° compared with 2H -MoS 2 phase (JCPDS 75 -1539), corresponding to the (100) and (110) planes, respectively. However, the peak of MoS 2-CF and MoS 2-CS located at 14.1°  obviously decreased compared with that of p ure MoS 2. It indicated that the growth of the crystal face (002) of MoS 2-CF was restrained, resulting from uniformly distributed PMo 12 as precursor. In general, (002) plane of MoS 2 is the inert plane for HER, but the MoS 2 can easily stack together along th e (002) plane, leading to the decreas e of the active sites.[43, 44]  Hence, the MoS 2-CF with low aggregation degree of (002) plane can be exposed more active sites for HER. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were employed to analyze the surface chemistry of the MoS 2-CF composite. As shown in Fig ure 3c, the peak located at 396.8 eV was attributed to Mo -N bonds , demonstrating the formation of N- doped MoS 2.[45] Moreover, the extended X -ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) was carried out to  evaluate the local atomic structure of Mo. As shown in Fig ure 3f, the Fourier transform (FT) curves of MoS 2-CF composite presented a shoulder peak at around 1.4 Å corresponding to Mo -N bond.[46] And two main peaks centered at around 1.9 Å and 2.8 Å attrib uting to Mo -S and Mo-Mo bond can be observed in MoS 2-CF.[47] These results confirmed the formation of N-doped MoS 2 on carbon foam . It has been reported that N-doping strategy can improve the intrinsic electrocatalytic of MoS 2 S-edge for HER and enhance the conductivity of MoS 2 basal plane. [48] Besides, the C  1s XPS spectrum of the MoS 2-CF composite (Fig ure S5) showed two strong peaks of C -C/C=C species (284.6 eV) and C -N species (285.7 eV) and a relatively weak peak of O -C=O (289.2 eV).[49] Figure 3d exhibited that the Mo peak of MoS 2-CF at 232.5 eV and 229.3 eV, arising mostly from its 3d 3/2 and 3d 5/2 single doublet as characteristics of the +4 oxidation state.  Compared with MoS 2- CS, XPS peaks of the MoS 2-CF shifted toward lo wer binding energy, suggesting the increase of electron concentration with enhanced electron transfer between  carbon substrate and MoS 2.[50] More importantly, the exposure of unsaturated edge sites was verified by the XPS patterns of MoS 2-CF and MoS 2-CF in Figure 3e. Two distinct XPS peaks of MoS 2-CF and MoS 2-CS centered at 162.3 eV and 163.5 eV can be attributed to the basal plane S2- ligands of 2H -MoS 2. However, in the XPS pattern of MoS 2-CF, additional two peaks can be observed at 164. 8 eV and 163.7 eV, which can be assigned to the unsaturated bridging disulfide S 22- in the MoS 2 edge sites. This result clearly indicated the presence of more unsaturated edges active sites in MoS 2- CF.[51] To evaluate the performance of the designed MoS 2-CF foam, we performed comprehensive electrochemical measurements for MoS 2-CF, MoS 2-CS, pure MoS 2, and Pt/C. The electrocatalytic HER performance of all samples in alkaline solution using a typical three -electrode setup was investigated.  Figure 4a showed the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) polarization curves of all samples. At a current density of 10 mA· cm-2, the MoS 2-CF, MoS 2- CS, MoS 2 exhibited overpotentials of 92 mV, 244 mV, 26 3 mV, respectively. The HER activity enhancement of the composite can be related  to the thin layer N-doped MoS 2-CF with more exposed active site s. Moreover, the Tafel plots were derived to analyze the quantitative kinetics of the HER process in Fig ure 4b. The Tafel curve of the MoS 2-CF showed a slope of 7 4 mV·dec-1. This result suggested that the hydrogen evolution process for MoS 2-CF followed the Volmer -Heyrovsky mechanism and the rate-determining step was the hydrogen generation.  The HER pathway mainly involved two steps: H2O adsorption on the (H2O+e→Hads+OH-), and redu ced to form hydrogen atoms adsorbed on the active site  of MoS 2, followed by H 2 formation and desorption (H ads+H2O+e → H2+OH-).[52] The Nyquist plots (Figure S6) indicated the expected semicircular features associated with charge transfer resistances in the HER process.  The MoS 2-CF electrode displayed the charge transfer resistance (Rct) of 5.5 Ω, which was lower than those of MoS 2-CS (6.1 Ω) and MoS 2 (6.4 Ω). This result showed that the MoS 2-CF exhibited  the lowest charge -transfer resistance and enhanced conductivity, which enabled efficient interfacial electron transport without a binder or conductive additive for HER. Figure 4. (a) HER polarization curves ( iR-corrected) of MoS 2-CF, MoS 2-CS, MoS 2 and P t/C, in 1.0 M KOH. (b) Corresponding HER Tafel plots in 1.0 M KOH. (c) HER polarization curves ( iR-corrected) of MoS 2-CF, MoS 2-CS, MoS 2 and 10.1002/chem.201904669 Accepted Manuscript Chemistry - A European Journal This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
