FULL PAPER Pt/C, in 0.5 M H 2SO 4. (d) Corresponding HER Tafel plots in 0.5 M H 2SO 4. (e) Determination of the double -layer capacitance C dl of MoS 2-CF, MoS 2-CS, MoS 2 both in acidic and alkaline conditions. (f) Cycle performance of MoS 2-CF in 1.0 M KOH and 0.5 M H 2SO 4. The HER activity of the MoS 2-CF composite  was further studied by electrochemical measurements in the N 2-saturated 0.5 M H 2SO 4 electrolyte. As shown in Fig ure 4c and 4d, the MoS 2-CF delivered excellent HER activity in an acidic solution with overpotential of 155 mV at a current density of 10 mA· cm-2, and a Tafel slope of 96 mV· dec-1, outperforming the MoS 2-CS (overpotential of 201 mV)  and many other reported MoS 2-based HER catalysts  (Figure 5). The double -layer capacitance (C dl) of the as -prepared various MoS 2-based  electrocatalysts  in this work were determined  through the cyclic voltammetry in 0.5 M H 2SO 4 and 1.0 M KOH electrolyte, respectively. The capacitive current was plotted as a function of scan rate to obtain  the C dl values of MoS 2- CF (Fig ure 4e and Fig ure S7), which were 2.37 mF  cm-2 in 0.5 M H2SO 4 and 2.69 mF  cm-2 in 1.0 M KOH , respectively. In contrast, pure MoS 2 only showed  Cdl values of 0.48 and 0.71 mF  cm-2 in 0.5 M H 2SO 4 and 1.0 M KOH. The above results indicated  that for MoS 2-CF, few  layers MoS 2 with 3D carbon foam exposed more catalytically active  surface area .[62] Moreover, to test the catalytic durability of composite, the overpotential of MoS 2-CF exhibited negligible increase at 10 mA cm-2 both in 1.0 M KOH and 0.5 M H2SO 4 solution after 2000  cycles (Fig ure 4f and Figure S6 ), indicating that the MoS 2-CF composite was a stable electrocatalyst both in alkaline and acidic solution . Figure 5. Comparison of HER performance of some reported MoS 2-based catalysts in 1.0 M KOH and/or 0.5M H 2SO 4 at the current density of 10 mA · cm-2. Conclusions  In this work , the immobilization of PPy -PMo 12 onto 3D macroporous PU substrate resulted in the formation of novel PU-PPy-PMo 12 composite  by facial redox polymerization . After that, N-doped MoS 2 on 3D carbon foam , denoted as MoS 2-CF, was successfully prepared from PU -PPy-PMo 12 via sulfurization. The uniformly distributed PMo 12 precursor on 3D substrate prevented serious aggregation  of MoS 2 nanoflake s, and thereby increas ed the exposed active sites  of MoS 2-CF. Furthermore, the 3D carbon substrate  led to enhanced  electron transport  properties. The as -prepared MoS 2-CF exhibited low overpotential of 92 mV in 1.0 M KOH and 155 mV in 0.5 M H2SO 4 solution, respectively. The super ior HER activity may originate from both the exposed active edge sites and enhanced  electrical conductivity.  To the best of our knowledge, this is one of the best MoS 2-based HER electrocatalysts reported so far. This work offers a new opportunity for exploring convenient and economic methods to design novel MoS 2-based electrocatalysts. Experimental Section Materials. All chemicals were of analytical grade and were used as received without any further purification. Preparation of PU -PPy-PMo 12. The commercial flexible polyurethane foam (PU) was cut into a 40× 10× 40 mm block and put in 200 mL phosphomolybdic acid (PMo 12, 3.6 g) aqueous solution. In addition, 420 μL pyrrole (Py) was dissolved in 50 mL ethanol, and then dropwise added to the  H3PMo 12 (PMo 12) aqueous solution. The mixture was stirred for 24 hours under ambient conditions. Finally, the foam was washed with deionized water and dried at 60 ℃ for 24 hours to obtain the PU -PPy-PMo 12 foam. Preparation of MoS 2-CF composite. The MoS 2-CF was obtained via a high-temperature vapor phase sulfurization process. The PU -PPy- PMo 12 composite precursor was cut into a 30× 10× 10 mm  block, placed on a corundum boat and put in a furnace. Then, another boat containing 0.9 g sulfur powders was put in the upstream side. The furnace was first heated up to 600 oC with a heating rate of 1 oC· min-1 for 300 min. Then, the temperature was incr eased to 800  oC with a heating rate of 5 oC·min- 1 for 60 min under nitrogen atmosphere. After that, the furnace was then slowly cooled down to room temperature. Preparation of MoS 2/carbon sphere (MoS 2-CS) composites. The PPy-PMo 12 sphere precursor was pre pared with pyrrole monomer and PMo 12 via a redox polymerization method as the previous report.[63] Py monomer solution by dispersing 420 μL of Py in 50 ml ethanol was slowly added to 200 mL of a 0.02 M solution of PMo 12. The mixture was stirred for 24 hours at room temperature and then the black precipitate was filtered, and washed repeatedly with water and ethanol to obtain the PPy-PMo 12 sphere. To prepared the MoS 2-CS, the P Py-PMo 12 sphere precursors were sulfurized in N 2 atmosphere. Preparation of MoS 2. The MoS 2 was prepared with H 3PMo 12 and sulfur powders  via high sulfurization under 600 oC for 300 min and then 800 oC for 60 min . 10.1002/chem.201904669 Accepted Manuscript Chemistry - A European Journal This article is protected by copyright. All rights reserved.
