of 49░mV decade-1. This present work provides new insight into the large-scale production of chemically active MoS 2-based catalysts for HER. Keywords:  ; MoS 2C; andle soot; M; agnetron‐sputtering;  Hydrogen  evolution  reaction (HER) 1 Introduction With the increased energy crisis and environmental pollution as well as the rise of population, it is imperative to find new re newable energy to replace the traditional non-renewable fossil energy. Hydrogen energy has been considered as a perfect fuel due to its purity and high energy density [1–4]. Among the many hydrogen production technologies, electrolytic hydrogen genera tion from water has become the most promising green hydrogen production technology because its apparatus is easy to operate, and it has a high energy conversion rate [5–6]. According to the thermodynamic theory, the theoretical de composition voltage of water is 1.23 ░V, which is the minimum voltage that must be provided during water electrolysis [7–8]. However, for many reasons, such as the polarization of the electrode and the drop of resistance voltage, the required decomposition voltage often reaches as high as 1.8 ░V during the actual production process. Noble me tals and their derivatives such as Pt and RuO 2 are commonly utilized as hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) catalysts respectively and show excellent performance and stability [9–11]. However, the scarcity and high cost of the noble metals restrict their large-scale industrial applications. As a result, it is currently necessary to exploit cost-effective alternative electrode materials with higher catalytic activity, commendable stability, and materials that are widespread on earth. Currently, transition metal chalcogenides [12–15], phosphides [16–17], carbides [18], nitrides [19–22], and other hybrids [23–29] have been investigated wildly as efficient electrocatalysts for HER. Among these materials, MoS 2 has drawn great attention for its potential to provide more active edge sites given that it has a lamellar, hexagonal structure that is similar to graphite . Alongside the many active edge sites [30–34], the electrical transport of the catalyst also plays an important role in HER performance [35–37]. Recent publications have demonstr ated that the electrocatalytic HER performance of molybdenum disulfide is strongly dependent on the phase structure; the metallic 1 ░T-MoS 2 has a better catalytic performance of the HER than the semiconductive 2H-MoS 2 because of its excellent electrode kinetics, remarkable intrinsic electrocatalytic HER activity, and fast charge transfer [38–43]. Also, researchers have improved the catalytic performance of MoS 2 further through various methods, such as the recombination of other materials, the design of nanotopography and electronic structure doping modification as well as edge active point exposure [44–52]. For example, Dai’s group synthesized MoS 2 on the graphene successfully and improved the catalytic activity of materials by utilizing the high specific surface area and high electron conductivity of graphene fully [36]. It is of great interest to synthesize electrocatalyst materials with 3D nanostructures, that make them promising electrode materials for HER in electrochemical water splitting [53–60]. The preparation of MoS 2 nanoparticle electrocatalysts is done mainly through incipient wetness impregnation and electro-deposition. However, the ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
