synthesis of 3D nanostructure electrocatalysts is still a challenge for industrial application owing to the complicated preparation process, with the parameters not easy to manipulate, restricting the large-scale production. Herein, we increased both the conductivity and number of active edge sites of MoS 2 simultaneously using the simple magnetron sputtering [61] approach with candle soot [62] as the template supported on the Ni foam [63]. Carbon particles [64] possess high electrical conductivity and large surface-area, making them ideal supports for the construction of functional nanostructures fo r applications in electrochemical energy conversion and storage, and the catalytic performance of MoS 2 based candle soot spongy nanostructures in HER is better than that of pure MoS 2. The synthesized 3D MoS 2/candle soot composited displayed a remarkable hydrogenation performance due to its abundant active sites, an extraordinary surface wettability on the Ni foam, and an excellent electrical conductivity. 2 Experimental section 2.1 Materials preparation Ni foam was obtained from Tianjin Aiweixin Chemical Technology Co., Ltd. (Tianjin, China). Candle soot was deposited on clean Si (110) substrates and the distance between the candle flame and the silicon wafer was about 5 ░cm, and the uniform baking time was 10 ░seconds. Before deposition, the substrate was successively ultrasonic cleaned by acetone, alcohol, and deionized water to remove contaminants. 2.2 Synthesis of MoS 2 nanosheets The 3D flower-like MoS 2 was grown on candle soot substrate using DC/RF reactive magnetron-sputtering (JPG-450A) in pure argon (99.995%) atmosphere at room temperature. Initially, the main chamber was evacuated to a pressure of 2 ░×░10-4 Pa using a turbo molecular pump, and the working pressure was maintained at 5.0░×░10-1 Pa. MoS 2 layer was deposited on candle soot substrate by sputtering a MoS 2 target (99.99%, 75 ░mm diameter and 5 ░mm thick) using radio frequency (RF) power. The distance between substrate and target was ~15 ░cm. The target was pre-sputtered for 20 ░min at lower power to remove impurities present on the target surface. The substrate holder was rotated at 6 rmp to ensure uniformity of deposition. The deposition time was 10 ░s and 40░min respectively. All the depositions were performed keeping optimized fixed flow rate of 30 ░sccm argon. The power of MoS 2 target was kept 80 ░W. 2.3 Synthesis of MoS 2/candle soot/Ni foam electrode First, MoS 2 / candle soot powder binder in isopropanol was dispersed to form a homogeneous slurry. Then, the slurry was spread on Ni foam with a geometric area of 1░cm2 and dried at 50 °░C for 10░h. 2.4 Characterizations The crystalline phase of the sample was analyzed by powder XRD measurements with a XRD-7000X X-ray diffractometer using Cu-K α radiation and performed at a ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
