scanning rate of 0.07° s-1. The morphologies were examined by scanning electron microscopy (ZEISS Gemini 500) at  an accelerating voltage of 15 ░kV. The microstructure of the sample was further characterized by TEM and HRTEM measured at 200 ░kV with a JEM-2100 ░F field-emission transmission electron microscope. The composition and chemical state of the sample was analyzed by X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS), using a Thermo Fisher ESCALAB Xi ░+░with monochromatic Al K α source. Raman spectra was collected from a laser Raman spectrometer with a laser of 532 ░nm. The contact angles of water droplets (2.5 ░μL) on samples were measured using a contact angle meter (KRUSS DAS30). 2.5 Electrochemical measurements To prepare the working electrodes, all the power samples (4 ░mg) were first dispersed in the mixture of water and ethanol (4:1, v/v, 980 ░μL), followed by the addition of Dupont Nafion PFSA Polymer (20 ░μL, 5░wt %). Then, the mixed solution was sonicated for 1 ░h to acquire a homogeneous catalyst suspension. Finally, 20 ░μL of suspension was cast onto the surface of the glassy carbon electrode (5 ░mm in diameter) with a loading of 0.407 ░mg/cm2 and Ni Foam (10 ░×░10 mm2) with a loading of 0.08 ░mg/cm2 respectively. The Pt/C electrode was prepared by casting the commercial 20 ░wt % Pt/C for comparison. 2.6 Electrochemical measurements All electrochemical experiments were performed in a three-electrode system at room temperature on a CHI660D electrochemica l workstation (Chenhua, China). The modified Ni foams were used as a working electrode, a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) was utilized as the reference electrode, and a carbon rod was used as the counter electrode. The electrolyte was 1.0 ░M KOH bubbled with nitrogen for 60░minutes prior to polarization and maintained above the liquid level during all electrochemical measurements. The scan rate in the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was 5░mV / s. Nyquist plots were obtained at the open circuit potential and various HER overpotentials versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) in the frequency range from 100 ░kHz to 0.1 ░Hz. To evaluate the ECSA of the materials, the electrochemical double-layer capacitance (C dl) was calculated, the CV was conducted within non-faradaic region at the scan rates from 100 to 200 ░mV s-1. The durability was evaluated by the potential-time curves under a constant current density of 20 ░mA cm-2. All the potential values were converted to values with reference to RHE according to E(RHE)=E(SCE) + 0.059░pH░+░0.242. ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
