3 Results and discussion Recently, it is acknowledged that the incorporation of catalyst with special structures such as hierarchical [17,24,25,37,59], cavate or uniformly dist ributed architectures with conductive frameworks [36,37,54] can improve the HER performance distinctly. Based on this insight, three-dimensional MoS 2 sheets with nanometer scale may have better catalytic efficiency and activity [53]. The synthetic strategy for MoS 2 with candle soot as template by magnetron sputtering is schematically illustrated in Fig.1 Fig. 1, from which candle soot was directly prepared on the monocrystalline wafer in the initial stage. Then MoS 2 nanosheets were deposited on the candle soot via a magnetron sputtering system equipped with a pure MoS 2 target. During the deposition, the Mo atoms and S atoms liberated form the target under the bombardment of argon ions, the coordinated atoms reassembled on the surface of the candle soot substrate resulting in the formation of 3D hybrid composites nanosheets. The crystal structure and composition of the sample were first investigated by the X-ray diffraction (XRD) technique. Fig.2 Fig. 2 shows the XRD pattern of the as-prepared 3D MoS 2/Candle Soot composite. The typical diffraction peaks locate at 14.4, 32.7, 39.6, and 60.1 ° correspond to the (002), (100), (103), and (110) planes of MoS 2 (JCPDS No. 37-1492), respectively. Because the candle soot is amorphous, no other diffraction peaks were observed. The MoS 2 was successfully fabricated on the candle soot and the phase purity of the as-prepared sample is assumed to be high. The chemical state and surface elemental composition of the sample was examined by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) ( Fig.3 Fig. 3 a‐d). Fig.3a gives the XPS full survey spectrum, indicating the existence of elements C, Mo, S with a small amount of O. The corresponding peaks could be observed at binding energies of 284.6 (C 1s), 161.5 (S 2p), 231.0 (Mo 3d) and 529.1 eV (O 1s), respectively. An atomic Mo/S proportion of 0.43 was detected in the sample , consistent with the theoretical value of pure MoS 2. Oxygen at 8.3 at.% was also present, which can be ascribed to the existence residual oxygen in the magnetron sputtering chamber. According to previous reports, the Mo 3d peaks of 1 ░T- MoS 2 shifted to lower binding energies (~1 ░eV) with respect to the corresponding peaks of 2H-MoS 2 [65].As depicted in Fig.3b, Mo 3d 3/2 and 3d  5/2 peaks with the respective binding energies of 232.8 and 229.7 ░eV are ascribed to 2H-MoS 2, and at 228.9 ░eV for 3d 5/2 and at 231.9 ░eV for 3d 3/2 are ascribed to 1 ░T-MoS 2 [44]. And the characteristic peak located at 226.9 ░eV is corresponding to the binding energies S 2 ░s. It is worth mentioning that two weak peaks at 233.6 and 236.1 ░eV are the characteristic peak of MoO 3, owing to the small amount of oxide species during the sputtering process. This result suggests that Mo4+ dominates in the product. Similarly, the S 2p spectra can be deconvoluted into two doublets, the peaks located at 162.6 ░eV and 164.1 ░eV , corresponding to the 2p 3/2 and 2p1/2 orbitals of 2H-MoS 2. Furthermore, the binding energies at 161.6 ░eV and 163.2 ░ eV are attributed to 1 ░T- MoS 2. As shown in Fig.3d, in the high-resolution C 1s spectrum of the sample, one main peak with binding energy of 284.6 eV is assigned to C-C bond, and the peaks located at 284.9 eV is ascribed to C. The high-resolution spectra of XPS further confirm the existence of MoS 2 in the hybridized samples, and ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
