these results are quite consistent with the XRD analysis of the 3D MoS 2/candle soot composite. The morphology and microstructure of the as-synthesized samples of candle soot, pure MoS 2 deposited on the Si wafer and MoS 2 deposited on the candle soot were investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Fig.4 Fig. 4a clearly shows that the structure of candle soot is composed  of micrometer ‐sized coral structure and nanometer ‐sized clusters. The carbon particles cross‐linked together forms the framework  resemble  spongy construction.  In Fig.4b, the typical two-dimensional MoS 2 nanosheets are dense and uniform on the Si substrate. As shown in Fig.4c and d, MoS 2 demonstrate a 3D flower-like spherical profiles with diameters about 200-300 ░nm and are consisted of thin nanosheets showing thickness around 10 ░nm. In contrast Fig.4a and d, the diameter of the MoS 2 is much larger than the candle soot particles. The nanosheets of MoS 2 encapsulate the candle soot particles, and the topography of the MoS 2/candle soot also presents macroporous features and chain-spherical structure. The tiny plate-like subunits of the MoS 2/candle soot interconnect with each other on the surface of the carbon particle ball, and the other end of the sheet subunits extend almost vertical to the surface of the carbon ball and avoid the a disordered stacking of MoS 2 layers. It is well known that the size and morphology of nanostructures significantly influence the performance of electrocatalysis, due to nanoscale sheet structure might be beneficial in exposing more  edges as active sites. It is worth noting that the surface of nanosheets is not flat and smooth, but rather uneven, such a structure can further increase the solid/liquid contact area of the catalytic electrode in the electrolyte. In addition, compared to the size of the flower-like MoS 2 prepared by other methods, the size of the three-dimensional flower-like molybdenum disulfide can be easily controlled by magnetron sputtering through controlling process parameters such as deposition time and sputtering power, which is convenient to the modulation of morphology for various needs applications. To investigate the spatial distribution of Mo, S and C, elemental mapping analysis in transmission electron microscope (TEM) mode was carried out on several MoS 2/candle soot particles. A bright field image and EDX mapping (Fig.S1) confirms the uniform distribution of Mo and S surround around the carbon particles. The observation reveals that the flower-like MoS 2 particles are densely deposited on the candle soot particles. TEM and HRTEM images of pure candle soot and MoS 2/candle soot sample are shown in Fig.5 Fig. 5, revealing that the crystallized MoS 2 with thin-layers has covered on the surface of amorphous candle soot. Fig.5a and d, present the typical TEM, HRTEM images and selected area electron diffraction  (SAED) patterns of the pure candle soot. The soot particulates  are amorphous,  and the average size was about 15‐20░nm. In TEM and HRTEM images of Fig.5b, c, e and f, uniform flower-like nanoparticles of MoS 2/candle soot can be clearly observed. The HRTEM images of MoS 2/candle soot reveal that the surface of candle soot is composed of thin interwoven nanosheets, layered MoS 2 is directly grown on the carbon particles compactly with clear edges, which is consistent with SEM images of MoS 2/candle soot composite ( Fig.4c, d). As can be seen in Fig.5e and f, clear interlayer spacing of 0.62 ░nm is observed, which corresponds to the (002) ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
============================================================
[Page 8/22]
============================================================
crystal plane of MoS 2. The MoS 2 nanosheets mainly comprise less than 10 layers as shown in Fig.5e, and most of nanosheets in the MoS 2 nanoflowers are relatively thin. The fewer layer MoS 2 nanosheets possesses, the improved wettability and larger contact interfaces for MoS 2/candle soot will be, which will be key factors in improving their HER performance. In addition, SAED inset image in Fig.5b shows three strong diffractions, which can be readily assigned to (002), (100) and (110) planes of MoS 2 phase. The results are highly in line with crystallographic planes of MoS 2 from XRD. Interestingly, according to the HRTEM image shown in Fig.6 Fig. 6, expanded  interlayered  spacing around 0.79░nm and defects (Fig.6a and b) for the sample are also detected. The enlargement of MoS 2 interplanar distance between two adjacent (002) planes suggests the existence of metallic 1 ░T-MoS 2 in MoS 2/candle soot, which is agreeing well with the XPS results. The formation of metallic 1 ░T phase is caused by the bombardment of Ar ions which carrying certain kinetic energies could effectively trigger the lateral sliding of top S-layer to form 1 ░T-phase nanostructures [66]. Furthermore, due to S atoms inevitably escape from the lattice during the deposition process, some S vacancies genera ted. Most of these S vacancies occur at the boundary between the 2H phase and the 1 ░T phase. Because of the localized stress caused by S vacancies, the 1 ░T phase is pinned to make it stable [67]. The growth process of MoS 2 slabs on the surface of candle soot can be well illustrated by changing the deposition time (Fig.S2). Fig.S2a represents the sample with the deposition time is 10 ░s and b is 40 ░min. According to Fig.S2a, only a small portion of the candle soot is deposited with MoS 2. The MoS 2 is adopted heterophase epitaxial growth on the candle soot. At the initial stage of deposition, MoS 2 starts to nucleate on the amorphous carbon film in the shape of a spherical cap. As the deposition process progresses, many three-dimensional island-shaped MoS 2 nucleus are nucleated on the amorphous carbon film, then grow to form a polycrystalline film with rough surface. Interestingly, as deposition time increases, a multitude of defects (yellow arrow in Fig.S2b and Fig.5f) and steps (dark arrow in Fig.S2b and Fig.5f) appeared in the sample. During the deposition process, the newly deposited surface will also be etched by the Ar ions, the bombardment etching promotes the generation of defects and steps. When the Ar ions or knock-on atoms (Mo or S) possess enough kinetic energy, they will strike atoms out of their initial lattice positions and cause secondary collisions (Fig.S3). By this means, collision cascades develop and the primary knocked atom absorbs most of the energy. The resulting strong atomic motion along the pathway of the ions leads to a rearrangement of the lattice atoms [68]. As the flux of the impinging ions are increased, atomic displacements produced in the collision cascades result in an increasing number of residual interstitials and vacancies [69]. Therefore, the amount of Ar ions in the deposition process plays an important role in the generation of defects and can be controlled by changing the working pressure. Fig.S4 illustrates the HRTEM of MoS 2/candle soot formed at 0.3 ░Pa (Fig.S4a) and 0.7░Pa (Fig.S4b). It is very clear that the MoS 2 nanosheets uniformly disperse on the surface of candle soot with flat lattice stripes under low working pressure. The lattice becomes extremely distorted and the defects increase significantly as the working pressure increase to 0.7 ░Pa. When the working pressure increases, the S vacancies ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
============================================================
[Page 9/22]
============================================================
will continue to increase, eventually destroy the lattice structure, and at the same time, the stress is released and the 1 ░T phase will disappear. In order to test electrocatalytic performance, the powder sample obtained by the deposition reaction was brushed on pure nickel foam by a slurrying, brushing, and solidification method to prepare an electrode  for subsequent electrochemical testing. The electrocatalytic HER performance of the MoS 2/candle soot/Ni Foam (NF ░+░CS ░+░MoS 2) was first investigated in 1 ░M KOH using a standard three-electrode setup with a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) as the reference electrode and a graphite rod as the counter electrode. Optimal HER activity  is observed with a working pressure of 0.5░Pa on the Ni foam substrate (Fig.S5) with adequate deposition time (Fig.S6). For comparison, a series of reference samples, including pure Ni foam (NF), Ni foam deposited pure candle soot (NF ░+░CS) and commercial 20% Pt/C (on NF), were also examined. Fig.7 Fig. 7a shows the liner sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of all the samples with a scan rate of 5 ░mV/s at room temperature. As expected, the 20% Pt/C exhibits expected HER activity with a near zero overpotential. The MoS 2/candle soot/Ni foam (NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2) catalyst shows higher catalytic activity with small overpotentials of 56 ░mV at the current density of 10 ░mA/cm2 for HER than bare nickel foam (269 ░mV), Ni foam/candle soot (267 ░mV) catalysts in 1 ░M KOH, respectively. The overpotential of the NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2 sample is smaller than some of the best MoS 2 based HER catalysts that have been developed by other groups (Table S1). Furthermore, the cathodic curren t rises rapidly at more negative potentials for the NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2 sample. To further study the HER kinetics of the catalysts, Tafel plots are fitted to the linear portions of the Tafel plots to the Tafel equation ( η= b×log j + a, where j is the current density and b is the Tafel slope), which can be used for the quantitative kinetics analysis of the HER. A lower Tafel slope means a more efficient kinesis of H 2 evolution at a constant increase of overpotential. As shown in Fig.7b, the Tafel slopes calculated after linear fitting to each region are 30, 49, 122.5 and 123 ░mV/dec for Pt/C, NF░+░CS░+░MoS 2, NF░+░CS and bare nickel foam electrodes, respectively. According to the mechanism of HER, there are three principle steps in alkaline electrolytes, including the V olmer, Heyrovsky and Tafel steps. The theoretical V olmer reaction is the initial discharge step with the Tafel slope is 120 ░mV/dec, followed by Heyrovsky and Tafel reactions with Tafel slope are 40 ░mV/dec and 30 ░mV/dec, respectively. The observed Tafel slope value of NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2 is smallest, suggesting that the V olmer- Heyrovsky reac tion mechanism dominates HER process. Thus, the Tafel slope also confirms the better HER performance of the 3D MoS 2/candle soot/Ni foam composite, in good agreement with the LSV data. The HER performance of MoS 2/candle soot and pure candle soot is also investigated and compared in Fig.S7 with those of commercial 20% Pt/C by casting the catalysts with the same mass loading of 0.407 ░mg/cm 2 on the glassy carbon electrode. Although Pt/C catalyst performs best among these candidates, the MoS 2/candle soot reveals competitive reaction activity with an ultralow η10 of 61░mV and a small Tafel slope of 52.3░mV/dec, and the results is not very different from using Ni foam as a ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
============================================================
[Page 10/22]
============================================================
substrate. The electron transfer rate during the HER process is also confirmed by the electrochemical impedance spectrosc opy (EIS) result. Nyquist plots of NF░+░CS░+░MoS 2, NF░+░CS and NF are presented in Fig.7c. The semicircular diameter (nyquist plots yield charge-transfer resistance (Rct)) values in the order: NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2 (3.1Ω), NF░+░CS (9.3Ω), NF (9.9 Ω). This is consistent well with the results of LSV . It is well fixed that the Rct associated with the electrocatalysis kinetics, and the lower Rct value signifies a faster reaction rate. Thus, the fast charge transfer during the electrocatalytic reaction could contribute to the superior electrocatalytic activity of the MoS 2/candle soot/Ni foam composite. The sample constructed with 1 ░T- and 2H- mixed phase MoS 2 displays much smaller impedance than pure 2H-phase MoS 2, due to its higher intrinsic conductivity of 1 ░ T-phase. The 1 ░T-phase MoS 2 reveals a metal-like conducting feature, which electroconductivity is about six times than the semiconducting 2H-phase. On the other hand, the self-supported candle soot exhibits a graphene-like structure (Fig.S8), which contributes to the charge transfer throughout the interface. The decrease of the electron transfer resistance is conduc ive to the electron transport in the electrocatalytic process, thereby improving the catalytic efficiency in the reaction kinetics, and the enhanced conductivity is a nother main reason for the decrease of the Tafel slope. In short, the composite structure has been enhanced in terms of electron transport, and the rate of reduction of hydrogen ions at the solid-liquid interface has increased, which has a positive effect on the hydrogen evolution reaction. To elucidate the possible origin for the superior activity of NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2, the electrochemical surface area (ESCA) of each sample is evaluated. The double-layer capacitance (C dl), which is in proportion to ESCA, is acquired by deriving from the cyclic voltammetry (CV) curves versus the different scan rate ( Fig.8 Fig. 8). As expected, NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2 sample shows the largest C dl value (52.39 ░m░F cm-2) than NF░+░CS (0.13░m░F cm-2) and NF (0.377 ░m░F cm-2), suggesting its enriched and more exposed catalytically active sites for electrocatalysis during HER. Surface wettability of bare nickel foam and NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2 is displayed in Fig.8. The contact angle of bare nickel foam was 121.5 ° (Fig.9 Fig. 9a), indicating its hydrophobic property intrinsic. In contrast, the water droplet on the NF ░+░CS░+░ MoS 2 was rapidly disappearing as soon as it contacted the surface ( Fig.9b-d), suggesting the superhydrophilicity of the sample. The behavior of water droplet on the surface can be influenced by the micro-na nostructure, which may contribute to the access of the electrolyte into the electrocatalyst and the hydrogen release to enhance the HER performance. In principle, the high electrochemical water splitting performance is related to many factors, such as electrical conductivity, active areas and so on. The outstanding catalytic performance of NF ░+░CS░+░MoS 2 for HER may be attributed to the following aspects: Firstly, the 3D flower-like morphology which the nanosheets arrays are vertically grown on the candle soot to expose more active sites, promoting the HER activity. The comparison results of the electrocatalytic activities between the 3D MoS 2/candle soot/Ni foam composite and other MoS 2 reported literature indicated the ACCEPTED MANUSCRIPT
