3D transition metal boride monolithic electrode for industrial hectoampere-level current anion exchange membrane water electrolysis Juan Zhang , Qikang Wu, Jian Song , Chenyang Xu, Shengpeng Chen , Yanhui Guo* Department of Materials Science, Fudan University, Shanghai 200433, PR China ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Industrial hydrogen production Anion exchange membrane water electrolysis Transition metal borides Chemical corrosion engineering Bifunctional electrode ABSTRACT High performance, cost-efficient anion exchange membrane water electrolysis (AEMWE) is of great current in- terest for industrial-level hydrogen production. However, the lack of active and robust catalytic electrode severely impedes the development of this technology. Herein, a versatile strategy of 3D hierarchical porous monolithic electrode enabling industrial hectoampere-level current AEMWE is successfully explored for the first time. By a facile electroless plating technique coupled with corrosion engineering process, a series of low-cost and highly active 3D transition metal boride (etched-TMB, TMNi, Co, NiP, NiMo, CoP, CoMo, CoNi) cata- lytic electrodes have been prepared. A distinctive hierarchically structured etched-NiPB@MS alloy monolithic electrode exhibits a superior bifunctional hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER) catalytic activity and large-current stability, which derive from enhanced intrinsic activity, sufficient electrochemical active sites, mechanical stability as well as efficient gas/liquid transport pathways. An AEMWE electrolyzer with 10×10cm2 etched-NiPB@MS as both anode and cathode works efficiently at large current of 100A (1Acm␀2) and reaches a H2 production rate of 41.78 Lh␀1, verifying its huge potential for industrial hydrogen production. This study paves out a new approach for high-efficient catalytic electrode and industrial- level current AEMWE. 1.Introduction Electrochemical water electrolysis driven by renewable energy (solar/wind) has been regarded as a promising approach to large-scale hydrogen production, which handling the looming energy shortage and environmental pollution [1–3]. Currently, three common low-temperature water electrolysis technologies of alkaline water elec- trolysis (AWE), proton exchange membrane water electrolysis (PEMWE), and anion exchange membrane water electrolysis (AEMWE) are widely studied [4–8]. Amongst, the emerging AEMWE retains the low-cost benefits of AWE and high efficiency of PEMWE, is regarded as a promising technology for industrial hydrogen production [9–11]. Up to now, extensive efforts have been pursued to develop practical electro - catalyst and AEMWE. However, there is still some critical technical challenges for AEMWE system, especially in the device electrolysis ef- ficiency and durability at high industrial current density [12–15]. To date, in a typical preparation of catalytic electrode for AEMWE, powder catalysts coupling with binder are commonly loaded on planar current collectors or membrane through spraying or screen-printing approach [16]. However, low electrocatalyst utilization, high charge resistance, poor gas/liquid transfer and weak stability due to physical contact exclude powder catalytic electrode from industrial long-term application [17]. Currently, 3D porous monolithic AEMWE electrodes which has great potential to satisfy all the practical requirement of high intrinsic activity, low gas/liquid transportation resistance, excellent chemical and mechanical stability, high conductivity and easy fabrica - tion are attracting intensive attention [18–21]. Normally, 3D AEMWE electrode was fabricated by growing electrocatalysts on 3D substrate. However, the choice of 3D substrates remains quite limited to conduc - tive metal, e.g., nickel, titanium, iron in the form of foam [22]. Con- structing 3D monolithic electrode by growing conductive electrocatalyst on insulating substrate will greatly expand the substrates to cheap and eco-friend substrates, thus achieve practical significance and environ - mental benefit [23,24] . Recently, various conductive non-noble metal electrocatalysts, such as transition metal chalcogenides [25], carbides [26], nitrides [27], phosphides [28], and borides [29] have been extensively reported for water electrolysis, allowing a straightforward construction of *Corresponding author. E-mail address: gyh@fudan.edu.cn (Y. Guo). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Nano Energy u{�~zkw! s{yo|kro>! ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2zkz{o z! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.nanoen.2024.109923 Received 3 March 2024; Received in revised form 29 May 2024; Accepted 23 June 2024   Nano  Energy  128 (2024  ) 109923 Available  online  27 June 2024 2211-2855/©  2024  Elsevier  Ltd. All rights  are reserved  , including  those  for text and data mining  , AI training  , and similar  technologies.
============================================================
[Page 2/10]
============================================================
monolithic AEMWE electrodes using an insulating substrate. Among the earth-abundant conductive electrocatalysts, transition metal boride (TMB) alloys have attracted extensive interest due to high intrinsic catalytic activity, high conductivity and low cost [30,31] . Depositing TMB on polymer has been proved to be an efficient strategy to make practical electrode for AWE [32–34]. 3D TMB monolithic catalytic electrode with integration of catalysis and gas diffusion competent which has the great potential to enable industrial AEMWE still remains to be developed. Herein, a novel strategy of 3D hierarchical porous monolithic elec- trodes enabling industrial hectoampere-level current AEMWE was suc- cessfully realized. All-in-one simple technique of electroless plating coupled with chemical corrosion engineering has been exploited to construct a series of binder-free monolithic 3D TMB alloy catalytic electrodes on melamine sponge (etched-TMB@MS). These etched- TMB@MS catalysts exhibited superior bifunctional HER and OER cata- lytic activity, and excellent stability at industrial ampere-level current density, which derived from enhanced intrinsic activity, sufficient electrochemical active sites, mechanical stability as well as efficient gas/ liquid transport pathways. A distinctive bifunctional etched-NiPB@MS electrode affords 100mAcm␀2 at overpotential of only 76mV for HER and 278mV for OER, and achieves 100mAcm␀2 current density at low voltages of 1.61 V for overall water splitting in 1M KOH solution at 25•C. An AEMWE electrolyzer with 10×10cm2 etched-NiPB@MS as both anode and cathode works efficiently at large current of 100A and reaches a H2 production rate of 41.78 Lh␀1, verifying its huge potential for industrial hydrogen production. This work provides new insights into the exploration of efficient and robust electrode towards industrial AEMWE system, and the generality of this method in promoting the catalytic performance of TMBs alloy may attract board interest towards practical water electrolysis. 2.Results and discussion A series of etched-TMB@MS were synthesized by a facile electroless plating and subsequent chemical etching process (corrosion engineer - ing), as illustrated in Fig. 1a and Fig. S1. Firstly, TMB alloy was formed and deposited on the activited-MS substrate to make pristine TMB@MS within a short electroless plating time. Secondly, the etched-TMB@MS alloy electrode was fabricated via a chemical corrosion engineering using aqueous HCl solution. After chemical etching, inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) measurement revealed the leaching of TM and B species from the TMB@MS electrode into HCl solutions, while Ni and P element in the non-boride electrode of NiP@MS pecu- liarly persists against dissolution (Table S1). Moreover, to demonstrate its scalability for industrial application, 10 ×10cm2 etched-NiPB@MS alloy electrode (as a representative example) was also prepared using the above method (Fig. 1b and f). The morphology and microstructure evolution of the optimized etched-NiPB@MS alloy were manifested by field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM). The pristine NiPB alloy layer grown densely and uniformly on the 3D open-pore MS skeleton via electroless plating method, presenting the slightly rough nodule-like surface structure (Fig. 1d). This surface morphology is known to be greatly detrimental for gas/liquid transfer capability and electrochemical active sites exposition [32]. For etched-NiPB@MS, the surface structure changed dramatically from micro-nodule to mesoporous flower-like nanosheet structure (Fig. 1h) and the average thickness of the catalyst layer reduced apparently from 15.13 μm to 14.06 μm (Fig. 1c and g). The flower-like nanosheet structure section is 1.50 μm in thickness (Fig. 1i). Intriguingly, the chemical etching can also exfoliate other TMB surface layer finely (Fig. S2a-i). Furthermore, the corresponding element map- ping results show uniform distribution of Ni, P, B and O elements on the surface of the etched-NiPB@MS alloy electrode (Fig. S3). After the chemical etching treatment, an improvement of hydrophilicity was observed by the decrease of the contact angles from 76.5•for NiPB@MS to 49.2•for etched-NiPB@MS (Fig. S4). The Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) results (Fig. S5) show the increase of the specific surface area from 0.12 m2 g␀1 to 0.63 m2 g␀1 after chemical etching, indicating the production of large numbers of micro- and nano-pores on the surface of etched-NiPB@MS electrode. However, no obvious morphology change could be observed after corrosion treatment of NiP@MS (Fig. S2j and k). Transmission electron microscope (TEM) images (Fig. 2a and c) further revealed the porous structure and interconnection of the thin Fig. 1.(a) Schematic illustration of the synthesis for etched-TMB@MS. (b, f) Photographs of pristine NiPB@MS and etched-NiPB@MS with the dimensions of 10 × 10cm2, 5×5cm2. (c) The cross-section of pristine NiPB@MS and (d, e) its FESEM images. (g) The cross-section of etched-NiPB@MS and (h, i) its FESEM images. J. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Nano  Energy  128 (2024  ) 109923 2
