nanosheets in etched-NiPB@MS. High-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images (Fig. 2b and d) and the corresponding selected-area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns (top insets of Fig. 2b and d) indicated the co-existence of major amorphous phase and localized nanocrystalline phase in both pristine NiPB@MS and etched-NiPB@MS. The interplanar crystal spacing of 0.203 nm in small grains corresponds to the (111) plane of Ni (PDF#70 –0989). Combined with the X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns in bulk samples, no apparent diffraction peak from 10•to 80•was detected, further suggesting the major amorphous phase (Fig. S6). Remarkably, the diffraction ring intensity of etched-NiPB@MS decreased and long- range ordered lattice fringes of small grains weakened, indicating that the chemical etching was accompanied by an exfoliation process for TMB alloy electrode, which reduced the crystallinity of nanocrystalline phase. Compared with crystallization, amorphous phase will lead to a higher surface density of unsaturated sites that can act as active catalytic centers, thus lay a favorable impact on the catalytic performance [35]. The energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) analysis (Fig. S7) uncovers that the Ni, P and B atomic percentage decrease upon surface recon - struction, in agreement with the above ICP-MS results. The elemental mappings visualize the multiple atomic constitution including Ni, P, B and O and their uniform distributions in etched-NiPB@MS (Fig. 2e-i). Predictably, there is no obvious change in crystallinity and morphology between NiP@MS and etched-NiP@MS (Fig. S8), which is consistent with the SEM results. More insight into the surface components and chemical states of the obtained catalysts was gained from X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). For pristine NiPB@MS, the doublet Ni 2p peak (Fig. 3a and S9b) split into Ni 2p3/2 (855.53 eV) and Ni 2p1/2 (873.21 eV) corresponding to oxidization of Ni (Ni2) on the surface of the catalyst. The Ni 2p peaks located at around 851.93 and 869.16 eV suggested the presence of metallic nickel (Ni0). The high-resolution B 1s spectrum (Fig. S9c) could be deconvoluted into two peaks at 187.84 and 191.74 eV, and the former was ascribed to metallic borides (M-B) while the latter could be assigned to oxidized borates species (BO x). After chemical etching, the intensity of metallic nickel (Ni0) peaks weakened, while the Ni 2p peaks of etched-NiPB@MS alloy was positively shifted by 0.35 eV as compared with that of the pristine samples. The peaks of B 1s in etched-NiPB@MS alloy exhibited a slight negative shift of around 0.33 eV as compared to that of NiPB@MS alloy. The similar phenomenon was also observed in other etched-TMB@MS alloys (Fig. S9b and c). This may be ascribed to the transit B site, which has an intrinsic electron deficiency, acting as an “electron sink” to facilitate more electron flow from the Ni site [22,36] . Besides, the O 1s peak at 530.7 eV was assigned to the oxygen va- cancies, while the peaks at 529.8 eV and 532.2 eV were ascribed to oxygen atoms bound to metals and hydroxyl species (M-OH) of surface-absorbed water molecules (Fig. 3b and S9d), respectively. We noted that the ratio of oxygen vacancies in etched-NiPB@MS is obvi- ously higher than that in pristine NiPB@MS. The electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy was conducted to further verify the oxy- gen vacancy levels (Fig. 3c). The EPR signal intensities of the etched-NiPB@MS at g2.004 which correspond to structural defects induced oxygen vacancies was manifestly higher than that of pristine NiPB@MS, in line with the XPS results. It is noteworthy that the O va- cancies originated from surface reconstruction could play important roles in regulating the electronic environment to benefit the intrinsic catalytic activity [37,38] . Interestingly, the P 2p spectrum of etched-NiPB@MS alloy possessed a negative shift of 0.4eV as compared to that of pristine NiPB@MS alloy (Fig. S9e), indicating more electron was transferred from Ni to P after chemical etching. This may be ascribed to the strong influence of P on the electronic structure of etched-TMB@MS. The incorporation of non- metal modulator P in etched-TMB@MS further regulates the activity Fig. 2.TEM image and high-resolution TEM image of (a) (b) pristine NiPB@MS alloy and (c) (d) etched-NiPB@MS alloy. Insets in (b) and (d): SAED pattern and corresponding autocorrelated lattice fringe pattern of the yellow-dotted box. (e) Dark-field STEM and corresponding elemental mapping images of etched-NiPB@MS for (f) Ni Kα, (g) P Kα, (h) B Kα, and (i) O Kα. J. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Nano  Energy  128 (2024  ) 109923 3
