of Ni active site, which is affected jointly with the synergistic effect of B as suggested in previous studies [22], resulting in significant enhance - ment on the intrinsic activity of etched-TMB@MS electrode toward both HER and OER [28]. Likewise, the phenomenon was also observed in other etched-TMB@MS alloys (etched-NiMoB@MS) (Fig. S9f). Details of XPS analysis for Mo 3d spectrum in etched-NiMoB@MS is described in the Supporting Information. To gain a deeper understanding of the electronic structure and local coordination environment, X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) of etched-NiPB@MS and NiPB@MS together with reference samples of Ni foil and NiO were further characterized. Ni K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra of these samples (Fig. 3d) displays that the near-edge energy of etched-NiPB@MS is between pristine NiPB@MS and NiO, which confirms that the valence state of Ni elements in etched-NiPB@MS contributed by electronic regulation is higher than that in NiPB@MS and lower than that in NiO. These results may derive from partial electron transfer from Ni atoms to B upon surface recon - struction, in line with the XPS results analysis. The R-space spectra of Ni in etched-NiPB@MS have three dominant peaks at 2.23, 2.25 and 2.45 Å assigned to Ni-P, Ni-B and Ni-Ni peak, fitting deliver a coordination number (CN) of 0.3±0.1, 0.2±0.0 and 1.5±0.3 (Table S2), respectively. Compared with NiPB@MS (3.6±1.0), NiO (12.5±1.5) and Ni foil (≪12), the surface of Ni atoms in etched-NiPB@MS exhibited a low CN (1.5±0.3) (Table S2). The main amorphous phase with localized nano- crystalline and metal vacancies-rich structures originated from the Ni leaching of etched-NiPB@MS may contribute to its ultralow CN, which makes a great contribution to its high oxygen-containing intermediates adsorption capability and further the promotion on the catalytic activity [39,40] . Besides, the Ni-Ni bond length of about 2.45 Å determined by the EXAFS curve of the etched-NiPB@MS was shorter than that in the NiPB@MS (Fig. 3e). This result re-confirms the partial electrons transfer from the Ni sites to B during the surface reconstruction, which is also verified by the corresponding k space oscillation (Fig. 3f). Among the wavelet transforms (WT) for the k2-weighted Ni K-edge EXAFS curve of the etched-NiPB@MS as well as pristine NiPB@MS, Ni foil and NiO (Fig. 3g-j). The WT contour plots of Ni-Ni bond in etched-NiPB@ exhibit one intensity maximum at 7.0Å␀1, and presents between the Ni foil (pristine NiPB@MS) and NiO. This could be attributed to an apparent Fig. 3.High-resolution XPS spectra of Ni 2p (a) and O 1s (b) for pristine NiPB@MS and etched-NiPB@MS alloy. (c) EPR spectra for pristine NiPB@MS and etched- NiPB@MS alloy. (d) Normalized Ni K-edge XANES spectra of Ni foil, NiPB@MS, etched-NiPB@MS and NiO. (e) The corresponding k2-weighted FT profiles in R-space from EXAFS at Ni K-edge collected on Ni foil, NiPB@MS, etched-NiPB@MS and NiO. (f) The K-edge extended oscillation curves K2 χ(k) for Ni foil, NiPB@MS, etched- NiPB@MS and NiO. Wavelet transforms results of the Ni K-edge EXAFS for: (g) Ni foil, (h) NiPB@MS, (i) etched-NiPB@MS and (j) NiO. J. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Nano  Energy  128 (2024  ) 109923 4
