local and unsaturated coordination for the etched-NiPB@MS. The HER and OER performance of the etched-NiPB@MS were eval- uated via a standard three-electrode configuration in H2-saturated 1.0M KOH solution and O2-saturated 1.0M KOH solution, respectively, along with the B-free counterpart (etched-NiP@MS and Ni foam) for com- parison. Firstly, the polarization curves of pristine NiPB@MS and different samples treated in HCl solution with different concentration are shown in Fig. S10a and S11a. The HER polarization curves (Fig. 4a) indicated that the etched-NiPB@MS has an outstanding HER activity with ultralow overpotentials of 51 and 76mV to afford the current densities of 50 and 100mAcm␀2, respectively, superior to those of etched-NiP@MS (η50 171mV, η100 210mV) and etched- Ni foam (η50 241mV, η100 308mV) as well as recently reported other state- of-the-art transition metal-based HER electrocatalysts (Fig. 4i). It is noteworthy that etched-NiPB@MS alloy also has better performance as compared to that of etched-NiB@MS (η50 100mV, η100 128mV). This is attributed to the further systematic and rational modulation of the electronic structures of etched-NiPB@MS by incorporating non- metal (e.g., P) modulators, resulting in significant enhancement on the its intrinsic activity toward HER, as mentioned in the XPS analysis. Besides, the Tafel slope of 41.32 mV dec␀1 for etched-NiPB@MS was lower than those of etched-NiB@MS (59.31 mV dec␀1), etched-NiP@MS (122.41 mV dec␀1), and etched-Ni foam (260.99 mV dec␀1), indicating the rapid reaction kinetics and the reaction process follows the Volmer- Heyrovsky mechanism with the Heyrovsky as the rate-determining step (RDS) (Fig. 4c) [41,42] . The flower-like nanosheet structure and elec- tron modulation in the etched-NiPB@MS promotes the dissociation of water and increases the formation rate of adsorbed hydrogen (H*) in- termediates [43]. To get further insight into the superior HER perfor - mance of the as-fabricated catalysts, double-layer capacitance (Cdl), which was positive correlation with the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA), was estimated in a non-Faradaic potential range at various scan rates in 1.0M KOH (Fig. S12). Compared with etched-NiP@MS, the Cdl value of etched-NiPB@MS is significantly increased from 23.91 to 55.19 mF cm␀2 after the chemical etching process, signifying the increased exposed active sites, which is in line with the BET results. To better demonstrate the intrinsic catalytic activity of etched-NiPB@MS alloy, the ECSA-normalized polarization curves toward HER have been calculated. The resulting ECSA-normalized polarization curves show the same tendency as the geometric-surface-area polarization curves, and etched-NiPB@MS bears the best ECSA-specific activity (Fig. S13a). The superior ECSA-specific activity of etched-NiPB@MS catalysts over NiPB@MS also suggests the promotion of the intrinsic activity rather than merely the active sites during the chemical etching treatment. Moreover, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was employed to further investigate the HER kinetics (Fig. 4b and Table S3). Fig. 4.HER and OER performance of etched-Ni foam, etched-NiP@MS, etched-NiB@MS, and etched-NiPB@MS in 1.0M KOH solution: (a) iR-corrected HER po- larization curve, (b) EIS Nyquist plots of the catalysts at HER, (c) Tafel plots of HER, (d) iR-corrected OER polarization curve, (e) EIS Nyquist plots of the catalysts at OER, (f) Tafel plots of OER, chronopotentiometric measurements of long-term stability of etched-NiPB@MS at the current density of 600mAcm␀2 for (g) HER and (h) OER, insets in (g) and (h): polarizations curves of etched-NiPB@MS before and after stability test for HER and OER, (i) overpotentials to afford 100mAcm␀2 for different bifunctional electrocatalysts. (Fig. 4b and e inset: the equivalent circuit used for the EIS fitting (top)). J. Zhang et al.                                                                                                 