Etched-NiPB@MS alloy has the smallest charge transfer resistance at the high frequencies region (Rct0.30 Ω) among all the counterparts, indi- cating its fastest charge transfer ability in HER process. The second semicircle at low frequencies region is believed to be associated to the kinetics of Volmer step (Fig.S14) [41–43]. Apparently, etched-NiPB@MS shows a significant lower adsorption resistance (Rp0.76 Ω) than etched-Ni foam (Rp14.02 Ω), etched-NiP@MS (Rp1.75 Ω) and etched-NiB@MS(R p1.12 Ω), further indicating that etched-NiPB@MS is more favorable for the water dissociation step [44, 45].The electrochemical durability of etched-NiPB@MS electrode to- ward HER was investigated at a large current density of around 600mAcm␀2 in 1M KOH solution. The negligible degradation and fluctuation of current density were present during 40h stability tests (Fig. 4g). Furthermore, the polarization curves after HER long-term stability tests are similar to the initial polarization curves before tests (the insets of Fig. 4g), confirming the outstanding HER durability of etched-NiPB@MS electrode. The OER performance of etched-NiPB@MS electrocatalyst together with the comparative samples was also explored in 1.0M KOH. The overpotential (η50 246mV, η100 278mV) of etched-NiPB@MS at 50 and 100mAcm␀2 was much smaller than etched-NiB@MS (η50  298mV, η100 311mV), etched-NiP@MS (η50 311mV, η100  332mV) and etched-Ni foam (η50 362mV, η100 397mV) (Fig. 4d). This outstanding activity was also superior to most recently reported transition metal based OER electrocatalysts (Fig. 4i). The Tafel slope of etched-NiPB@MS was determined to be 43.88 mV dec␀1, which is smaller than those of etched-NiB@MS (51.34 mV dec␀1), etched- NiP@MS (60.93 mV dec␀1) and etched-Ni foam (118.60 mV dec␀1), indicating that etched-NiPB@MS has the best reaction kinetics of OER (Fig. 4f). Besides, according to the microkinetic analysis of previous studies, the Tafel slope of etched-NiPB@MS is close to a featured value of 40mV dec␀1, suggesting that the rate-determining step (RDS) of etched-NiPB@MS may associated with the electron-proton reaction (O∗OH␀→OOH∗e␀) by the classical Butler-Volmer kinetics [46, 47]. Meanwhile, the etched-NiPB@MS displayed the largest Cdl value (68.91 mF cm␀2, Fig. S15), which demonstrated the most potential catalytic sites. Similarly, the smallest Rct value (0.13 Ω) of etched-NiPB@MS for OER demonstrates lowest charge transfer barrier (Fig. 4e, S14 and Table S4). Moreover, the evolution of reactants on the catalyst surface could be described by adsorption resistance (RP). The RP of etched-NiPB@MS (0.41 Ω) was lower than that of etched-Ni foam (22.03 Ω), etched-NiP@MS (0.73 Ω) and etched-NiB@MS (0.50 Ω), revealing the faster kinetics for adsorption of reaction intermediate during the OER, which be in favor of overall catalytic driving force [45]. The ECSA-normalized LSV curves of etched-NiPB@MS also displayed the lowest overpotential among the studied catalysts during OER (Fig. S13b ), demonstrating the best intrinsic OER activity. These results also strongly verified the positive effect of chemical corrosion engi- neering on the electronic regulation and surface reconstruction of the etched-NiPB@MS. The long-term OER durability was also investigated at a large current density of around 600mAcm␀2 in 1M KOH solution (Fig. 4h), and the etched-NiPB@MS exhibited great stability toward OER at large current density. From the above sections, it is well established that the bifunctional etched-NiPB@MS electrode exhibits considerable improvement in the performance over other electrodes. The probable mechanisms of enhanced catalytic activity are as followed. Firstly, the flower-like nanosheet structure of the etched-NiPB@MS could enhance the elec- trochemical surface area to expose more active sites and accelerate the mass/charge transfer. Secondly, the strong electronic interaction be- tween Ni, P and B in the etched-NiPB@MS and its low coordination environment unveiled by the above XPS and XAS analysis adjusts the charge distribution at Ni and B sites. This electron modulation promotes the synergistic division of hydroxylation and hydrogenation at Ni and B sites, and electron-deficient Ni and electron-rich B are more favorable to the adsorption of key OH* and H* intermediates after chemical etching, thus facilitating the dissociation of water molecules during the Volmer process [48,49] . Moreover, the electron modulator P and O vacancies originated from surface reconstruction could play important roles in regulating the electronic environment of the etched-NiPB@MS, which may optimize the adsorption of O-containing intermediates (O*, OH*, OOH*), resulting in significantly enhanced OER catalytic activity [37, 38]. To elucidate the generality of the 3D hierarchical porous monolithic electrode strategy, five other TMB@MS electrodes, including etched- NiMoB@MS, etched-CoB@MS, etched-CoPB@MS, etched-CoMoB@MS and etched-CoNiB@MS, were prepared and investigated. The HER and OER performance of pristine and etched NiMoB@MS, CoB@MS, CoPB@MS, CoMoB@MS and CoNiB@MS are displayed in Fig. S10 and S11. The pristine NiMoB@MS, CoB@MS, CoPB@MS, CoMoB@MS and CoNiB@MS require the overpotentials of 115 and 348mV, 265 and 366mV, 159 and 355mV, 93 and 296mV, 203 and 362mV at 100mAcm␀2 for HER and OER, respectively. After chemical etching, etched-NiMoB@MS, etched-CoB@MS, etched-CoPB@MS, etched- CoMoB@MS and etched-CoNiB@MS exhibited excellent HER and OER performance (Fig. S16). These results clearly demonstrated the effi- ciency of the strategy. Given the outstanding catalytic activity and durability of the etched- NiPB@MS catalyst in the three-electrode configuration, a two-electrode electrolyzer employing etched-NiPB@MS catalysts as both anode and cathode was constructed to evaluate electrocatalytic performance for overall water splitting in 1M KOH solution at 25 •C (Fig. 5a). The etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS (Fig. 5b and S17a) requires low voltages of 1.61 and 1.80 V to reach current densities of 100 and 600mAcm␀2, respectively, which is much lower than that of etched- NiB@MS || etched-NiB@MS (1.67 V, 1.90 V), etched-NiP@MS || etched-NiP@MS (1.78 V, 2.08 V), etched-Ni foam || etched-Ni foam (2.0V, 2.65 V) as well as pristine NiPB@MS || NiPB@MS (1.74 V, 1.98 V). Meanwhile, etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS electrolyzer is also superior to most of the other bifunctional electrocatalysts in terms of the overall water splitting performance (Fig. 5d and Table S5). The long-term durability of etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS was evaluated at constant potential (Fig. 5c). It can be clearly observed that the current density of 100 and 600mAcm␀2 present negligible decay for 140h in 1M KOH solution, indicating its excellent stability. To further understand the outstanding electrode durability of the etched- NiPB@MS in alkaline conditions, the structural and morphological of the electrodes after long-term stability were characterized. The flower- like nanosheet surface of the etched-NiPB@MS after both HER and OER stability was almost perfectly maintained (Fig. S18), and the co- existence of main amorphous phase and localized nanocrystalline phase also remains unchanged (Fig. S19). Ni, P and B species still pre- served the initial oxidized and elemental states after HER and OER ac- cording to the XPS measurements (Fig. S20). It is worth noting that the peak of elemental nickel (Ni0) almost vanished after long-term OER, which was attributed to elemental nickel being oxidized during the OER process. And the peaks of phosphorus and boron in oxidized and elemental states weaken after the HER and OER test for 140h, which may be the result of inevitably partially leaching out [22]. The similar electronic state, crystal structure and surface morphology of etched-NiPB@MS further demonstrated that the catalyst obtained by chemical etching exhibits remarkable stability. Meanwhile, five other etched-TMB@MS electrodes also exhibited excellent overall water splitting activity and stability during two-electrode electrolyzer test as shown in Fig. S17. For practical application, large-scale and low-cost synthesis of the 3D monolithic TMB electrode is required. To show the scale-up production capability, large-area etched-NiPB@MS (5×5cm2, 10×10cm2) were prepared by electroless plating coupled with chemical corrosion engi- neering (Fig. 1f). The large-area etched-NiPB@MS electrode has iden- tical morphology over the whole substrates to that of the small ones (1× 0.5cm2) (Fig. S21). The price of etched-NiPB@MS electrode is J. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Nano  Energy  128 (2024  ) 109923 6
