estimated to be around 127.66 US$ m␀2 (Table S6, based on factory raw material costs), an order of magnitude lower than that of commercial Pt/ C electrode (≪8800 US$ m␀2) and the same order of magnitude as that of commercial Raney Ni (≪129.77 US$ m␀2) (Table S7). Low cost and facile manufacturing without complex procedure and high time-energy input supports the potential for large-scale application of the 3D monolithic TMB electrodes. Beyond testing in the lab-scale homemade cell, the pair was assem - bled in a scale-up AEM water electrolyzer to further evaluate the feasi- bility for industrial applications. The schematic diagram and photograph of the AEM electrolyzer are shown in Fig. 6a and b, respectively. The electrolyzer consists of four parts: bipolar plate with serpentine channels, gaskets to avoid the short circuit caused by direct contact between the two plates and avoid the gas/liquid leakage, 5 × 5cm2 etched-NiPB@MS electrodes for HER and OER, and 7 ×7cm2 commercial Sustainion ™ X37–50 RT anion exchange membrane to allow OH- pass from cathode to anode and prevent intersection of H2 and O2. The electrochemical properties were evaluated by feeding 1.0M KOH aqueous medium at 30mLmin␀1 circulated by a peristaltic pump in dynamic mode. Positively, the etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS (25cm2 electrode area) supported a current of 25A at 1.98 V, and the current can be up to 50A at a cell voltage of only 2.43 V for the AEM water electrolyzer in 1M KOH solution at 25 •C, which precedes the pristine NiPB@MS || NiPB@MS (25A, 2.29 V) and commercial Ni foam || Ni foam (25A, 3.10 V), and even superior to the benchmark Pt/C || RuO 2 (25A, 2.19 V) (Fig. 6d). The AEM electrolyzer operating system using etched-NiPB@MS as both cathode and anode can achieve a hydrogen production rate of up to 10.45 Lh␀1 at a cell voltage of 1.98 V, which is 1.59 times that of the Pt/C, 2.46 times that of the NiPB@MS and 12.45 times that of the commercial Ni foam (Fig. 6e). Furthermore, it only takes 4.73 kWh Nm␀3 to produce hydrogen under a current density of 1Acm␀2, while the Pt/C requires 5.24 kWh Nm␀3, NiPB@MS re- quires 5.50 kWh Nm␀3 and Ni foam requires 7.42 kWh Nm␀3, which means that using etched-NiPB@MS as cathode and anode would save 2.69 kWh Nm␀3 per cubic meter of hydrogen produced as compared to Ni foam (Fig. 6f). A key criterion for evaluating the viability of elec- trocatalysts in practical applications is the durability at high industrial current. The long-term stability test of the scale-up etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS AEM water electrolyzer was further inspected by a current of 25A (current density of 1Acm␀2), which was maintained in the cell voltage of about 1.98 V without noticeable degradation for 160h tests at room temperature (Fig. 6h). Moreover, multi-step chro- nopotentiometry test for the AEM electrolyzer from 5 to 25A was also performed (Fig. 6g) and the excellent stability of the etched-NiPB@MS within a wide range of current will be beneficial for its practical hydrogen production driven by an unstable wind energy and solar Fig. 5.(a) Graphical illustration of overall water splitting system. (b) Polarization curves of overall water splitting using different electrodes as both cathode and anode. (c) Stability test of overall water splitting based on etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS under different cell voltages (100 mAcm␀2 and 600mAcm␀2 at 1.61 V and 1.80 V). (d) Summary of recent reported bifunctional electrocatalysts and their corresponding cell voltage at a current density of 100mAcm␀2 (Table S5). (Sample size n3 for (b) error bars correspond to the standard deviation of three independent measurements). J. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Nano  Energy  128 (2024  ) 109923 7
