energy. Faradaic efficiency (FE) of the AEM water electrolyzer was measured using the drainage method (Fig. S22a). The results indicated that the amounts of H2 and O2 gas produced nearly match the theoretical volume ratio of 2:1, indicating a high FE (Fig. S22b ). Moreover, the scale-up AEM water electrolyzer employing 10×10cm2 etched- NiPB@MS electrode as both cathode and anode was further assembled (Fig. 6c). Excitingly, it can drive a large current of 100A at a low cell voltage of about 2.23 V for 26h with negligible degradation of voltage and reaches a H2 production rate of 41.78 Lh␀1 (Fig. S23). The per- formance of catalyst-assembled electrolyzers reported in literatures was summarized as shown in Fig. 6i and Table S8 and S9. Notably, the etched-NiPB@MS alloy electrode employed in AEM electrolyzer stand out among noble and non-noble electrode systems reported for other advanced AEM electrolyzers. Considering the above attractive advan - tages, the 3D monolithic TMB electrode demonstrates the potential to serve as practical AEMWE electrodes for industrial hydrogen production. 3.Conclusion In summary, this study has presented a versatile strategy of 3D hi-erarchical porous monolithic electrodes that enables industrial hectoampere-level current AEMWE. By a facile electroless plating technique coupled with corrosion engineering process, a series of 3D TMB@MS alloy monolithic catalytic electrodes up to maximum size of 10×10cm2 (i.e., etched-NiPB@MS, etched-NiB@MS, etched- NiMoB@MS, etched-CoB@MS, etched-CoPB@MS, etched-CoMoB@MS and etched-CoNiB@MS) have been successfully constructed. The porous architecture of the optimized etched-NiPB@MS framework not only promoted the exposure of the active sites but also afforded highly effi- cient transport pathways for gas/liquid. Furthermore, the enhanced oxygen vacancies modulate local coordination environments and elec- tronic structure of transition metal and non-metal species in etched- NiPB@MS. As a result, the etched-NiPB@MS exhibits superior cata- lytic activity and excellent long-term stability as both cathode and anode toward overall water splitting, requiring only 1.61 V to afford current densities of 100mAcm␀2 in 1M KOH solution at 25•C. In addition, the cost of etched-NiPB@MS is also much lower than that of noble metal- based materials and even Raney Ni, showing great commercial pros- pects. Impressively, AEMWE system using the etched-NiPB@MS bifunctional electrodes up to 10×10cm2 exhibited high electrolysis efficiency and durability at large current up to 100A, producing Fig. 6.Schematic illustration (a) and photograph (b) of AEM water electrolyzer. (c) Scaled-up manufacturing of AEM water electrolyzer. (d) Polarization curves of AEM water electrolyzer recorded in 1M KOH solution. (e) Comparison of H2 production rate for commercial Ni foam || Ni foam, Pt/C || RuO 2, NiPB@MS || NiPB@MS and etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS at different cell voltage. (f) Comparison of electric energy required for 1m3 H2 generated for etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS, NiPB@MS || NiPB@MS, Pt/C || RuO 2, and Ni foam || Ni foam. (g) Multi-step chronopotentiometry testing and (h) long-term stability test of the etched-NiPB@MS || etched-NiPB@MS in the AEM water electrolyzer at a fixed current of 25A without iR compensation in 1.0M KOH at 25℃ (inset: photograph of AEM water electrolyzer components). (i) Performance comparison of etched-NiPB@MS with the electrodes employing in other AEM water electrolyzers reported in the literatures (Table S8 and S9). (Sample size n3 for (d) error bars correspond to the standard deviation of three independent measurements). J. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Nano  Energy  128 (2024  ) 109923 8
