A comparative study of hydrogen evolution on Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs in acid solution Lazar Rako /C20cevi/C19ca, Jelena Golubovi /C19cb, Dana Vasiljevi /C19c Radovi /C19cc, Vladimir Raji /C19ca, Svetlana /C20Strbacb,* aINS Vin /C20ca, Laboratory of Atomic Physics, University of Belgrade, Mike Alasa 12-14, 11351 Belgrade, Serbia bICTM-Department of Electrochemistry, University of Belgrade, Njego /C20seva 12, 11000 Belgrade, Serbia cICTM- Department of Microelectronic Technologies, University of Belgrade, Njego /C20seva 12, 11000 Belgrade, Serbia highlights graphical abstract /C15Pt is electrochemically deposited on GC with a high loading of48.1 wt%. /C15Pt is spontaneously deposited on the GNPs with a low loading of4.6 wt%. /C15HER activity of Pt/GNPs exceeds that of Pt/GC in an acid solution. /C15The HER overpotential for Pt/GNPs is lower than for Pt/C. /C15HER proceeds through different reaction mechanisms on Pt/GCand Pt/GNPs. article info Article history:Received 13 June 2023Received in revised form19 July 2023Accepted 21 July 2023Available online 08 August 2023 Keywords: PlatinumGlassy carbonGraphene nanoplateletsHydrogen evolution reactionAcid solutionabstract We present a comparative study of the electrocatalytic performance of catalysts obtainedby electrochemical and spontaneous deposition of Pt on glassy carbon (GC) and graphene nanoplatelets (GNPs) supports. Pt/GC, with high Pt loading, obtained by electrochemical deposition of Pt, and Pt/GNPs, with low Pt loading, obtained by spontaneous deposition,show the highest activity for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) in acid solution. AFMimages of Pt/GC reveal that the GC coverage by Pt nanoparticles is 87%, while SEM imagesof Pt/GNPs do not show the presence of Pt species due to their small amount. XPS analysisconﬁrms that the amount of Pt is 48.1 wt% in Pt/GC and 4.6 wt% in Pt/GNPs. The HER ac-tivity of both catalysts is equally high, showing onset potentials close to 0.0V (vs. RHE). TheTafel slopes of 30 mV/dec for Pt/GC and 37 mV/dec for Pt/GNPs indicate different HERmechanisms. ©2023 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. *Corresponding author . E-mail address: sstrbac@tmf.bg.ac.rs (S./C20Strbac). Available online at www.sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/he international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 1240 e1254 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2023.07.231 0360-3199/ ©2023 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
