1. Introduction Due to the increasing demand for renewable energy, clean energy technologies, and the capability of using fuel cells asgreen devices for energy conversion, electrocatalysts forhydrogen evolution reaction (HER) are receiving increasingattention [ 1]. Up-to-date studies of HER electrocatalysts involved polycrystalline and single crystal precious metals, non-precious metals, alloys, bimetallic systems, and metal nanoparticles supported by various substrates [ 2e7]. Design principles for HER electrocatalysts assume the establishment of the relation between the thermodynamicsand kinetics of the reaction and the electronic and structuralproperties of the catalyst [ 8,9]. Regarding the desired low overpotential, Pt group metals are the best catalyst for HER,among which platinum exhibits the highest activity due to theoptimal hydrogen adsorption energy [ 10,11]. Since the lack of stability and the high price of Pt are the main obstacles to itscommercial use, the rational design of low-cost and efﬁcient electrocatalysts is the requirement of today. One way is to reduce the Pt content and increase the efﬁciency of its utili-zation by using Pt nanoparticles supported by cheaper andconductive substrates with a large surface area, such asvarious carbon-based materials [ 12e15]. The most common methods for obtaining nanoparticle- based catalysts are chemical synthesis [ 16e19] including recently reported successive ionic layers deposition (SILD)[20], atomic layer deposition (ALD) [ 21] and electrochemical deposition [ 22e25]. The HER activity of these catalysts de- pends on the morphology, particle size and shape, their dis- tribution, the number of active sites, and the surface-to- volume ratio, which are controlled by the proper choice ofpreparation conditions [ 3,26]. Eco-friendly and low-cost syn- thesis of Pt nanoparticles immobilized on various carbonsupports, such as graphene oxides, carbon nanohorns (CNHs),and carbon nanotubes (CNTs), produced electrocatalysts withlow loading of Pt nanoparticles (~1 wt%) [ 13]. They exhibited high HER activity, which involved low onset potential, highcurrent density, excellent durability, and stability. Cuboid-likeplatinum nanocrystals (Pt-CNSs) supported by reduced gra-phene oxide (rGO) showed higher HER electrocatalytic activity and better stability than pure Pt-CNSs, with great potential for application in fuel cells [ 27]. Pt nanoparticles supported by high-defective graphene nanospheres (GNs) are a promisingcatalyst for water-splitting applications due to their high HERactivity and stability in an acid solution [ 28]. Pt nanoparticles supported by Ketjen Black (KB) synthesized using thechemical-free solution plasma sputtering (SPS) methodexhibited enhanced HER activity and stability than a com-mercial Pt/C catalyst [ 29]. Pt/NPC catalyst consisting of Pt nanoparticles dispersed over a three-dimensional carbonnetwork co-doped with N and P, with an ultralow Pt loading of 1.82 wt% exhibited better HER performance than a commer- cial 20% Pt/C [ 30]. Pt-based catalyst with pseudo-atomic-scale dispersion of Pt on single-walled carbon nanotubes (SWNTs)showed extraordinarily high electrocatalytic activity for HER,close to or even higher than that of Pt/C [ 31,32]. The onion-like nanospheres of carbon (OLC) are used to anchor atomicallydispersed Pt for the synthesis of Pt/OLC catalyst for HER. Inacid solution, the Pt/OLC catalyst with a low Pt loading of 0.27 wt% shows excellent activity and better performance for HER, compared to graphene-supported single-atom catalystwith a similar Pt loading, and comparable to a commercial Pt/C catalyst with 20 wt% Pt [ 33]. Pt/CNTs catalysts consisting of the ultraﬁne Pt nanoparticles (NPs) decorated carbon nano-tubes (CNTs) shows excellent HER activity in acidic media,which originates from a combination of more active sitesprovided by ultraﬁne Pt NPs, and good CNTs conductivity [ 34]. A catalyst with a unique Pt eC3 coordinated conﬁguration obtained by trapping isolated Pt atoms on a defective gra-phene support (Pt@DG) exhibits remarkable HER activity, approximately 18 times higher compared to commercial 20 wt % Pt/C in both acid and alkaline solutions [ 35]. Graphene nanoplatelets (GNPs) supported platinum nanoparticles, Pt/GNPs, are promising electrocatalysts for polymer electrolytemembrane (PEM) fuel cells [ 36]. In this work, we aim to examine and compare the HER activity of different catalysts consisting of Pt supported onglassy carbon (GC) and graphene nanoplatelets (GNPs) in a0.5 M H 2SO4solution. The preparation of Pt/GC electrodes and Pt/GNPs electrodes involves the electrochemical and sponta-neous deposition of Pt on GC and GNPs substrates. Morpho- logical and chemical characterization of the obtained Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes will include microscopic and spec-troscopic techniques. Electrochemical characterization andtesting of HER activity will include standard electrochemicaltechniques. 2. Experimental 2.1. Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes preparation A glassy carbon disc, 5 mm in diameter (geometric area0.196 cm 2), mounted in a Teﬂon holder, was used as a sup- porting electrode for electrochemical measurements. GC disc,7 mm in diameter (geometric area 0.385 cm 2), was used for ex- situcharacterization. GC electrode was polished mechanically with alumina slurry on a cloth polishing pad and then rinsedin an ultrasonic Milli Q water bath. Before each Pt deposition,the bare GC electrode was cycled in a deaerated 0.5 M H 2SO4 solution. The working Pt/GC electrodes were prepared by the elec- trochemical deposition of Pt on GC. Pt nanoparticles were deposited from 10/C04MH 2PtCl 6þ0.05 M H 2SO4solution at a constant applied potential for different times. The concen-tration of the depositing solution for electrochemical deposi-tion was chosen as optimal to avoid very slow or very fastdeposition of Pt. The preparation of the Pt/GC electrodes bythe electrochemical deposition of Pt on a bare GC electrodewas described in detail in our previous paper [ 37]. For the investigation of HER, different Pt/GC electrodes were preparedby the electrochemical deposition of Pt on GC at a potential of0.15 and 0.20 V (vs. RHE) for 30 min and at a potential of 0.15 V (vs. RHE) for 60 min. For comparison, Pt was also spontane- ously deposited on GC. Due to the rather slow spontaneousdeposition of Pt, the chosen concentration of the depositingsolution was 10 /C03MH 2PtCl 6þ0.05 M H 2SO4. The deposition was carried out for 60 min.international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 1240 e1254 1241
