GNPs/GC substrate was prepared by spreading ﬁve drops (25ml) of GNPs suspension (2 g GNPs powder in 1.0 L of ethanol solution, previously sonicated for 4 h) over GCsupport. The working Pt/GNPs electrodes were prepared byelectrochemical and spontaneous Pt deposition on previ-ously prepared GNPs/GC substrate electrode. As in theprevious case, electrochemical deposition was performedusing 10 /C04MH 2PtCl 6þ0.05 M H 2SO4solution at 0.15 V for 60 min. Spontaneous Pt deposition on GNPs/GC was per-formed from 10 /C03MH 2PtCl 6þ0.05 M H 2SO4for 60 min deposition time. 2.2. Characterization of Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes Atomic force microscopy (AFM) technique was used tocharacterize the Pt/GC electrode, obtained by electro-chemical deposition of Pt on GC. Since AFM is not suitablefor very rough surfaces, such as that consisting of thedeposited Pt nanoparticles on a rather rough GNPs/GC sup-port, a Field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM)with an energy dispersive x-ray spectrometer (EDS) was used to characterize Pt/GNPs electrodes obtained by both electrochemical and spontaneous deposition of Pt. Besides,for Pt/GC electrode surface, obtained by spontaneous Ptdeposition, the AFM imaging was not reliable enough, sincethe deposited Pt nanoparticles were very small and did notclearly differ from the relatively rough GC substrate.Therefore, SEM/EDS was also used in this case. Althoughthese techniques provided some insight into surface chem-istry, x-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was usedadditionally for direct insight into the chemical composition,the oxidation state of the elements, and the nature of the chemical bonds. AFM imaging of as-prepared Pt/GC was performed in a tapping mode using an NTEGRA Prima atomic force micro-scope (NT-MDT, Moscow, Russia) with an NT-MDT NSGO1silicon, n-type, antimony-doped cantilever with Au reﬂectivecoating. Simultaneously recorded AFM images in height andphase mode enabled observation of surface morphology andcontrast of chemically different regions in the same imagingarea, respectively. The sensitivity of the phase shift to thesurface chemical composition enables an accurate estimationof the coverage deﬁned as the fraction of the GC substrate surface covered by the deposited Pt nanoparticles. Imaging of the morphology and the chemical composition with the elemental mapping of the Pt/GC, obtained by aspontaneous deposition, and Pt/GNPs electrode surfaces, ob-tained by both electrochemical and spontaneous Pt deposi-tion, was performed using FESEM/EDS spectrometer (FEI Scios2, Thermo Fisher Scientiﬁc, Waltham, MA, USA). FESEM im-ages and EDS spectra were recorded at the pressure of1/C210 /C04Pa with an electron beam voltage of 10 kV. XPS survey spectra of all investigated Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes, and high-resolution spectra of Pt 4f, C 1s, and O 1s lines, were recorded using SPECS System (SPECS Surface Nano Analysis GmbH, Berlin, Germany). The spectra were obtainedwith AlK asource (1486.74 eV) at 12.5 kV and 32 mA, at a pressure ranging from 3 /C210 /C08to 2/C210/C09mbar. The sample charging was neutralized by SPECS FG15/40 ﬂood gun. All peakpositions are given relative to C 1s at 284.8 eV.2.3. Electrochemical measurements The electrochemical measurements were performed usingPine Instruments Potentiostat. All experiments were carriedout in a three-electrode cell in a deaerated 0.5 M H 2SO4 working solution. Bare GC, GNPs/GC, Pt/GC, and Pt/GNPs wereused as working electrodes, while Pt wire and Ag/AgCl (3 MKCl) were used as counter and reference electrodes, respec-tively. The electrode potential values were recalculated rela-tive to the reference hydrogen electrode (RHE). All measurements were conducted at room temperature. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was used for the electrochemical characterization of the obtained electrodes, and Linear sweepvoltammetry (LSV) for the investigation of their electro-catalytic activity for HER in 0.5 M H 2SO4solution. All cyclic and linear voltammograms were recorded at a scan rate of 50 and10 mVs /C01, respectively, after conditioning at a potential prior to hydrogen evolution to reduce the fraction of Pt oxide. Thecurrent density values are given relative to the geometricsurface area of the working electrodes (0.196 cm 2). 2.4. Materials and chemicals A glassy carbon disc (Alfa Aesar) was used as a support. GNPssuspension was prepared from graphene nanoplateletspowder (surface area 750 m 2/g, Sigma-Aldrich) and ethanol (Merck). H 2SO4(Merck) and Milli-pure water were used to prepare the working solution. H 2PtCl 6(aq) (MaTeck) and suprapure H 2SO4(Merck) were used to prepare the depositing solution. For the electrochemical measurements, the elec- trolyte was deaerated by high-purity nitrogen (Messer, 99.9995%). 3. Results and discussion 3.1. Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes characterization Morphological and chemical characterization was performedfor Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes, obtained by electrochemical Pt deposition at 0.15 V and by spontaneous Pt deposition for the same deposition time of 60 min. 3.1.1. Surface morphology imaging and chemical analysis of Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes The surface morphology of the Pt/GC electrode, obtained by electrochemical Pt deposition on a GC support, was evaluatedat a nano-scale by imaging in the height AFM mode. Thechemical difference between the GC substrate and the Pt de-posit was visualized by simultaneous imaging in the phaseAFM mode. Fig. 1 shows height and phase AFM images of the Pt/GC electrode surface. Height AFM image in Fig. 1 a shows that the GC surface is covered with densely packed electrochemicallydeposited Pt nanoparticles, giving the estimated average sur-face roughness of 15.3 nm. The individual Pt nanoparticle sizeis approximately 50 nm and the size of agglomerated onesreaches 200 nm, according to a rough estimate from the cross-section along the line from the height AFM image in Fig. 1 b. The Pt nanoparticle height distribution obtained from theinternational 