onsecutive scans, Pt 4f doublet emergesfrom the noise level. Such peaks cannot be deconvoluted, norcan the amount of the deposited Pt be estimated. Thedeconvolution of C 1s line, Fig. 5 e, resembles that of bare GC and, compared to the previous case, shows an additional peakat 287.0 eV, which corresponds to the sp 2carbon in C ]C bond [42,43]. The O 1s line, Fig. 5 f, is ﬁtted to only one component corresponding to adsorbed oxygen (or water), while thecomponent corresponding to PtO cannot be resolved due to the small amount of Pt. High-resolution spectra of Pt 4f, C 1s and O 1s lines for Pt/ GNPs, obtained by electrochemical and spontaneous deposi-tion of Pt on GNPs/GC support, are given in Fig. 6 . For Pt/GNPs electrode, obtained by electrochemical depo- sition of Pt, Fig. 6 a shows high-resolution spectra of Pt 4f doublet lines. The relative intensity of the Pt 4f lines is muchlower compared to Pt/GC, obtained under the same depositionconditions, indicating a lower amount of Pt. Binding energiesof the components corresponding to metallic Pt (71.6 eV for Pt4f 7/2and 75.0 eV for Pt 4f 5/2), as well as Pt oxide (72.3 eV for Pt 4f7/2and 75.3 eV for Pt 4f 5/2) are upshifted compared to the same for Pt/GC, meaning that the deposited metallic Pt as wellas PtO (or PtOH) are more strongly bound to GNPs than to GC.Fig. 6 b shows that the deconvolution of the C 1s line gives four components. The ﬁrst component at 284.7 eV can be ascribed to both sp 2carbon in C ]C and sp3carbon in C eC bonds, [42,43]. The component at 285.4 eV corresponds to carbon in the CeO bond, and a wider one with a maximum peak in- tensity at 288.4 eV corresponds to carbon in the O eC]O bond [43]. The low intensity component at 289.0 eV, can be attrib- uted to a satellite peak [ 42]. O 1s line, Fig. 6 c, is deconvoluted in tree components. The ﬁrst component at 533.0 eV correspondsto adsorbed oxygen or water, the second at 531.7 eV corre-sponds to oxygen in the O eC]O bond [ 44], and the third one with