d 0.24 V originate from weakly and stronglyadsorbed hydrogen spices, respectively. The formation ofPtOH begins after the double-layer potential region, atapprox. 0.50 V, and proceeds with a low current density up toapprox. 0.8 V. At higher potentials, the formation of PtO be-gins and proceeds up to 1.20 V. In the reverse scan, thereduction of Pt oxide occurs, and all reduction peaks appearat the same potential of 0.78 V, although with different cur-rent densities. Assuming that one hydrogen atom adsorbs on a single Pt atom, a ratio between the total charge passed during theanodic hydrogen desorption on Pt/C and polycrystalline Pt(210 mCc m /C02) gives the electrochemically active surface area (ECSA). For Pt/GC electrodes obtained after 30 min of Ptdeposition at 0.20 and 0.15 V and 60 min at 0.15 V, the ECSA is0.094, 0.123, and 0.184 cm 2, or 48, 63, and 94%, respectively, of the hydrogen atom coverage concerning the geometric sur-face area of 0.196 cm 2. The hydrogen coverage of 94% on Pt/GC obtained after 60 min of Pt deposition at 0.15 V is slightlyhigher than the 87% Pt coverage estimated from the phase AFM image. Assuming that H coverage on Pt is generally lower than a monolayer (0.77 ML on bare Pt [ 49]), this difference stems most likely from the increased roughness after Ptdeposition. On the other hand, the difference between GC andPt/GC surface roughness cannot be obtained with sufﬁcientTable 1 eAtomic and weight percentage of the elements for Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs obtained by the electrochemical, and Pt/GNPs by a spontaneous Pt deposition. Line Pt/GC Edep ¼0.15 V Pt/GNPs Edep ¼0.15 V Pt/GNPs spontaneous At% Wt% At% Wt% At% Wt% C 1s 83.3 44.1 76.3 57.3 84.5 80.3 O 1s 11.1 7.8 18.0 18.0 15.2 15.1Pt 4f 7/2 5.6 48.1 1.3 15.9 0.3 4.6 Fig. 7eCyclic voltammograms of different: (a) Pt/GC; (b) Pt/GNPs electrodes. CVs are recorded in 0.5 M H 2SO4at a sweep rate of 50 mV/s.international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 1240 e1254 1249
