The current density of 10 mA cm/C02is reached at /C00.308 V, signiﬁcantly more negative than in the previous cases. This means that such electrode cannot be considered as a prom- ising HER catalyst. For HER on all tested Pt/GC electrodes, obtained by elec- trochemical deposition, a Tafel slope of about /C030 mV/dec is obtained, Fig. 8 b. According to Butler-Volmer kinetics, the Tafel slope of 120, 40, and 30 mV/dec means that the dischargereaction (Volmer step), electrochemical desorption reaction(Heyrovsky step) and recombination reaction (Tafel step) arethe rate-determining steps, respectively [ 50e52]. In this case, the obtained Tafel slope assumes that the Volmer reaction isthe ﬁrst step, followed by the Tafel reaction as the rate- determining step, meaning that the hydrogen evolution re- action on Pt/GC electrodes occurs through the Volmer - Tafelmechanism, which is in accordance with HER mechanism onPt (110) [ 11]. Different Tafel slope of /C060 mV/dec, for HER on Pt/ GC, obtained by spontaneous Pt deposition, implies that theadsorption of hydrogen, as a reaction intermediate followsFrumkin rather than Langmuir adsorption isotherm, similarlyas reported for Pt(111) [ 11]. Fig. 8 c shows LSV curves for HER on Pt/GNPs electrodes obtained by electrochemical and spontaneous deposition of Pton GNPs/GC support. The onset potential for HER on the Pt/ GNPs, obtained by electrochemical Pt deposition is /C00.004 V, and for spontaneously deposited Pt it is close to 0.00 V, almostequal to the thermodynamically reversible hydrogen evolu-tion potential. The potential for a current density of10 mA cm /C02is/C00.057 V for electrochemically deposited Pt, and/C00.054 V for spontaneously deposited Pt. The Tafel slope for hydrogen evolution on Pt/GNPs electrode, obtained by bothelectrochemical and spontaneous Pt deposition on the GNPssubstrate, is /C037 mV/dec, Fig. 8 d, which differs from that for Pt/GC, indicates different reaction pathways. Namely, the ﬁrststep is the discharge reaction (Volmer step), and the electro- chemical desorption reaction (Heyrovsky step) is the rate- determine step, meaning that HER on Pt/GNPs occursthrough the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism, which is inaccordance with HER mechanism on Pt(100) [ 11].Among all tested Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes, the lowest absolute values for both the onset potential and the potential for the current density of 10 mA cm /C02are obtained for HER on Pt/GC electrode, obtained by electrochemically deposited Pton the GC substrate and for Pt/GNPs electrode, obtained byspontaneously deposited Pt on GNPs substrate. Between thesetwo electrodes, the Pt/GNPs, obtained by spontaneous Ptdeposition shows better activity, making it the most active ofall tested electrodes for the hydrogen evolution reaction in0.5 M H 2SO4solution. A direct comparison of the difference in CVs for the most active Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs electrodes and theirHER activities are presented in Fig. 9 a and b, respectively. The stability of the Pt/GC electrode obtained by the elec- trochemical deposition of Pt at 0.15 V for 60 min and the Pt/ GNPs electrode obtained by spontaneous deposition of Pt for60 min was tested by chronoamperometry. Measurementswere performed in 0.5 M H 2SO4solution at a constant applied potential of /C00.02 V for Pt/GC and /C00.03 V for Pt/GNPs during permanent rotation of the electrode with a rotation speed of2500 rpm. Fig. 10 a and b shows CA curves recorded during 3 h for Pt/GC and Pt/GNPs, respectively. Although the loading of Pt is much higher on Pt/GC than on Pt/GNPs, their HER activities are comparable, and only slightlyenhanced on Pt/GNPs. Metallic Pt, which according to XPS results dominate in both cases, is identiﬁed as the main active species for HER [ 8]. According to XPS, Pt 4f 7/2binding energy for the fraction of metallic Pt, indicates its stronger interactionwith GC than GNPs substrate. On the other hand, a higher Pt4f 7/2binding energy for PtO (or PtOH) on GNPS than GC in- dicates stronger PtO-GNPs interaction. Most likely, the elec-trochemical reduction of PtO (or PtOH) fraction before HERmeasurements gives rise to the more metallic Pt active siteswith a stronger interaction with GNPs than GC. According tothe obtained Tafel slopes, the adsorption of hydrogen isstronger on GNPs, causing its desorption (Heyrovsky) step to be the rate-determining step in the HER reaction mechanism. In addition, Table 2 shows a comparison of HER activity on Pt-based catalysts supported by different carbon-basedmaterials. Fig. 9eComparison of the electrochemical behavior of Pt/GC, obtained by electrochemical deposition at 0.15 V, and Pt/GNPs, obtained by spontaneous Pt deposition, in 0.5 M H 2SO4solution: (a) cyclic voltammograms; (b) HER activity.international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 1240 e1254 1251
