HER activity of Pt/GNPs for 10 mA cm/C02approaches that of the commercial Pt/C electrode (E ¼/C00.034 V) [ 56] and exceeds the polycrystalline platinum (E ¼/C00.060 V) [ 60]. Also, it shows similar or slightly better activity than the electrodes made ofdifferent Pt nanoparticles on other carbon supports, as showninTable 2 . 4. Conclusion We have investigated HER activity of different Pt/GC and Pt/ GNPs catalysts, obtained by electrochemical and sponta- neous deposition of Pt on GC and GNPs supports. Pt/GCcatalyst consisting of large Pt nanoparticles electrochemi-cally deposited on glassy carbon and Pt/GNPs with a lowamount of Pt spontaneously deposited on a graphene nano-platelets support showed the best HER activity in sulfuricacid solution. The main results concerning these two cata-lysts are as follows. - AFM images of the Pt/GC electrode surface revealed the presence of large Pt nanoparticles with an average size of50 nm and coverage of 87%.- SEM images of the Pt/GNPs electrode surface do not reveal Pt species due to their small size, but EDS analyses conﬁrmed the presence of 4.5 wt% (0.3 at%) Pt. - XPS analysis in both cases conﬁrmed the presence of Pt in two oxidation states, Pt 0and Pt2þ, with 48.1 wt% (5.6 at%) in Pt/GC and 4.6 wt% (0.3 at%) in Pt/GNPs. - CVs in 0.5 M H 2SO4solution of Pt/GC are typical of bare Pt, while that of Pt/GNPs shows only an indication of thepresence of Pt. - The electrocatalytic activity for HER is high in both cases, showing the onset potentials almost equal to the thermo-dynamically reversible potential. The Tafel slopes of 30mV/dec for Pt/GC and 37 mV/dec for Pt/GNPs indicate that the reaction occurs through Volmer-Tafel and Volmer- Heyrovsky mechanisms, respectively. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competingﬁnancial interests or personal relationships that could haveappeared to inﬂuence the work reported in this paper.Table 2 eComparison of the HER catalytic activity on Pt-based catalysts supported by different carbon-based materials in 0.5 M H 2SO4. Catalyst Wt% E onset (V) E (V) for j ¼10 mA cm/C02Tafel slope (mV dec/C01) Ref. 40 wt% Pt/C 40.0 ~0.00 e 32 [ 13] Pt-CNSs/RGO 46.9 /C00.018 e 29 [ 27] Pt/GNs 14.7 /C00.01 /C00.025 33 [ 28] PtNPs/CNFs 7.0 /C00.055 /C00.175 50 [ 53] Pt-CQDs/Gr- C400 0.15 e /C00.038 40 [ 54] Pt-CNTs ee /C00.041 49 [ 55] Pt/NCNTs 0.74 ~0.00 /C00.040 33 [ 56] Pt@DNHC 8.6 e /C00.041 29 [ 57] Pt/CNTs-ECR 0.20 e /C00.034 26 [ 58] Pt50-PG 12.0 e /C00.044 63 [ 59] 20 wt%Pt/C 20.0 ~0.00 /C00.034 31 [ 56] Pt(poly) 100.0 ~0.00 /C00.060 60 [ 60] Pt/GC 48.1 /C00.003 /C00.056 30 This work Pt/GNPs 4.6 ~0.00 /C00.054 37 This work Fig. 10 eChronoamperometry curves recorded in 0.5 M H 2SO4at a constant applied potential for: (a) Pt/GC, obtained by electrochemical deposition at 0.15 V; (b) Pt/GNPs, obtained by spontaneous Pt deposition.international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 1240 e1254 1252
