Catalysis Science & technology  ARTICLE This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 20 xx  J. Name ., 2013, 00 , 1-3 | 2 Please do not adjust margins Please do not adjust margins structure and rich chemistry 15 . For instance, several semiconducting TMDCs such as MoS 2, MoSe 2, WS 2 and WSe 2 undergo a transition from indirect to direct bandga p, while going from bulk to monolayer 22 . The relatively high mobility of charge carriers in TMDCs has opened up new possibil ities of employing these materials in a variety of applicati on 14,27 . Moreover, because of the varying population of elec trons in the d-orbitals for different transition metal, we can ob serve various electronic properties ranging from metals, semiconductors, insulators to superconductors 15 . These features of TMDCs offer opportunities for fundament al and technological research in a variety of fields inclu ding solar cells, photo detectors, electronic devices, topolog ical insulators. The photocatalytic property of TMDCs is  equally important in order to split water after getting sol ar irradiated due to the optimum band gap that it possess 28-35 . The recent experimental progresses reported cost ef fective and better HER catalytic  efficiency of MoS 2 as compared to most of the nonprecious metals 36,37 . It has been observed in the previous studies that only the edge sites of 2H  phase of MoS 2 are responsible for its catalytic activity and req uire rational  design of materials to increase the numbe r of exposed edge sites38. Recent theoretical investigations have shown that PtS 2 and WS 2 monolayers are having the required photocatalytic band gap and suitable band edge alig nment for HER and have solvation enthalpies significantly grea ter than that of HgS 21 . In addition, it has been experimentally determined that nano sheets coupled with reduced gr aphene oxide and nano flakes of WS 2 exhibit promising catalytic activity toward HER 39,40 . This prepares the background motivation of our study to tune the HER activity of PtS 2 and WS 2 using density functional theory (DFT) based electr onic structure calculations. We have thoroughly investig ated the effect of foreign elements like Ag, Au, Hg, Pd, Ir,  Rh and Rh on the catalytic activity for these two monolayers. We  have also shown that the van der Waals (VDW) interactions are important enough to accurately predict the adsorpti on energy for the individual catalytic surfaces. Computational Methodology The crystal and electronic structures of pristine a nd functionalised platinum disulphide (PtS 2) and tungsten disulphide (WS 2) are determined using DFT 41  calculations by employing the Vienna ab-initio simulation package ( VASP) 42,43, where the ion-electron interactions are described u sing Projected Augmented Wave (PAW) 44 method. The exchange- correlation for electrons is represented using gene ralised gradient approximation (GGA) functional of the Perd ew-Burke- Erzenhoff (PBE) 45 form. Due to the fact that  the dispersive forces are not well defined in the standard DFT and  a non-local term needs to be added to account for such dispersi ve forces, we employed the latest dispersion correction (D3) 46 of Grimme throughout our calculations to correctly describe t he adsorption energy. A kinetic energy cut-off of 500 eV is used for plane wave basis set with the energy convergenc e of eV 10 1.0 5−× . For structural optimization, eV/Å 0.02  has been used as the force convergence criteria. To obtain t he adsorption energy of H 2 on various functionalised surfaces, we used a 133××  supercell containing 27 atoms (9 transition metal and 18 S atoms). For Brillouin zone sampling,  144×× Monkhorst-Pack 47 K-mesh is used to generate the k-points. Periodic boundary conditions are employed in all di rections and 22 Å of thick vacuum are used in the z-directio n to avoid the interaction between slabs. While performing the optimization initially H 2 molecule are placed at varying distances (3 – 4.2 Å) from the sandwiched transitio n metal layer. All calculations have been performed using s pin polarization. The band gap of pristine  PtS 2 and WS 2 are determined using hybrid HSE06 48 functional. While as density of states (DOS) have been calculated with GGA function al using 188××  k mesh. Results and Discussions Crystal structure and band structure of PtS 2 and WS 2 The crystal structure of 2D PtS 2 and WS 2 are shown in Fig. 1. The ground state structure of PtS 2 exists in 1T phase and that of WS 2 in Fig. 1  The crystal structure of PtS 2 in 1T phase (a) Top and (b) side view and that of WS 2 in 2H phase (d) Top and (e) side view. Band structure of (c) PtS 2 and (f) WS 2 calculated using HSE06 functional. The colour of the elements is designated as follows : maroon for Platinum, Red for Tungsten and green for Sulphide. Page 2 of 6 Catalysis Science & Technology Catalysis Science & Technology Accepted Manuscript Published on 09 January 2017. Downloaded by University of California - San Diego on 09/01/2017 11:49:47. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C6CY02426B
