s observed that VBM and CBM shifts towards the lower energy region in these sys tems as Table 1  Optimised bond lengths (S—X: X= Ru, Rh, Pd, Ag, Ir , Au, Hg) and work function of functionalized PtS 2 and WS 2. Impurity PtS 2                                            WS 2 S—X (Å) Φ  (eV) S—X (Å) Φ (eV) pristine  2.389  6.280  2.407  5.632 Ru 2.384 5.880 2.380 4.234 Rh 2.370 6.222 2.413 4.507 Pd 2.390 6.276 2.484 4.836 Ag 2.494 5.624 2.562 5.047 Ir 2.374 6.062 2.390 4.304 Au 2.483 5.200 2.572 4.894 Hg  2.601  5.482  2.626  5.198 compared to the pristine PtS 2 DOS. In case of the other dopants, neither such electronic states inside the band gap has been observed nor they effect the band extrema. For  WS 2, we found that there is a significant effect of dopants  on DOS. In the case of WS 2, there is more significant effect on the DOS and it has been seen that all dopants shift the VBM  toward more negative energy value. This strong influence o f dopants on the band gap of PtS 2 and WS 2 lead to the possible tuning of the electronic properties of both the pseudo-monola yers and consequently the HER activity as well. HER  activity on Nano structured PtS 2 and WS 2 The photocatalytic water splitting is one of the im portant reactions that can be divided into two half-reactio ns: HER and oxygen evolution reaction (OER). The overall HER tak ing place at the electrode in contact with the electrolyte co nsists of two reaction mechanisms in acidic media, viz . Volmer–Tafel and Volmer–Heyrovsky mechanism. The Volmer reaction inv olves the reduction of proton to produce adsorbed hydroge n on the electrode surface (H + (aq)  + e – → H ads ). After Volmer step, the reaction can follow the Tafel or Heyrovsky step to complete HER. In the Volmer-Tafel reaction mechanism, two ads orbed hydrogen atoms on the electrode surface adjacent to  each other combine to produce hydrogen molecule (H ads  + H ads  → H2). While in the Volmer-Heyrovsky reaction mechanism , a proton from water reacts with one of the adsorbed h ydrogen atom to make H 2 (H + (aq) + H ads  + e – → H 2). Regardless of the pathway followed by HER, both the mechanisms reveal that the adsorption energy of the hydrogen atom plays a decisive role in determining the catalytic activity on the c atalyst surface. The adsorption energy of H 2 in turn is related to the adsorption free energy (HG∆ ) of H 2 on the electrode (catalyst) surfaces and is broadly accepted to be a  precise descriptor for a catalyst toward HER activity 25,50,51 . Detailed explanation on the implications of the adsorption f ree energy as a descriptor for HER is provided in ref. [52]. Th e adsorption free energy is calculated by the following equation : H ZPEadsH STEEG ∆−∆−=∆          (1) where Eads  is the adsorption energy and ΔE ZPE  is the zero-point energy difference of H 2 in the adsorbed and gas phase state with its values ranging from 0.01 to 0.04 eV. The t hird term represents the difference in entropy of H 2 molecule in the adsorbed and gas phase and its approximate value is  0.4 eV 23 . The adsorption free energy therefore is written as 24. 0+=∆adsHEG . For an optimal HER catalytic material, Δ GH should be zero and hence the adsorption energy Eads  should be of the order of –0.24 eV, where the binding strengt h of hydrogen is neither too strong nor too weak. Page 4 of 6 Catalysis Science & Technology Catalysis Science & Technology Accepted Manuscript Published on 09 January 2017. Downloaded by University of California - San Diego on 09/01/2017 11:49:47. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C6CY02426B
