Fuel 415 (2026) 138444 Available online 22 January 2026 0016-2361/© 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.Full Length Article A computational screening study of Dual-Metal M1M2@1T-MoS 2 with various coordination environments as catalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction Tingting Lia,b,1, Guohao Yina,1, Haiyan Zhua,b,* , Shanlin Chenb, Chou Wua, Shaobo Jiaa, Ghulam Meeladia, Yawei Lic,* , Yixuan Huangd aShaanxi Key Laboratory for Theoretical Physics Frontiers, Institute of Modern Physics, Northwest University, Xi’an, Shaanxi 710069, China bInstitute of Yulin Carbon Neutral College, Northwest University, Xi’an, Yulin 719000, China cSchool of Energy, Power and Mechanical Engineering, Institute of Energy and Power Innovation, North China Electric Power University, Beijing 102206, China dDepartment of motion picture photography & animation, Xi’an Academy of Fine Arts, Xi’an 710199, China ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Density Functional Theory (DFT) Free Energy Prioritization First-Principles Calculations Electrochemical Hydrogen Evolution Reaction (HER) Dual-Atom Catalysts (DACs) Composite Modulation StrategyABSTRACT 1 T-MoS 2-based dual-atom catalysts (DACs) generally demonstrate high reactivity toward the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). However, the experimental development of highly efficient HER catalysts derived from DACs remains both time-consuming and costly. In this study, we employed a hydrogen adsorption free-energy prior- itization strategy, based on density functional theory (DFT), to systematically screen HER-active DACs. Taking the absolute hydrogen adsorption free energy of Pt (111) (0.09 eV) as a performance benchmark, 11 DACs with potentially high catalytic activity were initially identified. Furthermore, by introducing nonmetal atoms (N and P) to modulate the coordination environment, we constructed 66 M1M2/X2@1T-MoS 2 (X N, P) configurations. Among these, FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 was identified as the most promising catalyst, exhibiting an optimal hydrogen adsorption free energy (ΔGH* ≪0). This work establishes a class of highly efficient HER catalysts through a DFT- based free-energy screening strategy and uncovers a novel multi-center synergistic catalytic mechanism. These findings provide a rational design paradigm for multi-center synergistic catalytic materials and offer significant theoretical insights into the HER mechanism. 1.Introduction The global energy supply crisis is intensifying, accompanied by mounting ecological and environmental pressures. In this context, hydrogen energy has emerged as a promising clean energy carrier owing to its high energy density, favorable calorific value, and zero carbon emissions during utilization. It is widely regarded as a viable pathway to meet the urgent demand for sustainable and green energy in human society [1]. Among various hydrogen production strategies, water electrolysis has attracted increasing attention because of its environ - mental benignity and sustainability [2]. Within this process, the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) serves as a key half-reaction, playing a pivotal role in advancing clean energy conversion. Owing to its envi- ronmental friendliness, operational safety, and system stability, HER has been extensively investigated. Nevertheless, platinum-based and alloy catalysts —currently the most efficient HER electrocatalysts —suffer from high cost and scarcity of natural reserves, which severely hinders their large-scale deployment [3]. Therefore, the rational design of alternative electrocatalysts that combine low precious-metal loading with high catalytic activity and long-term stability is of critical impor - tance for accelerating the development and industrialization of hydrogen energy technologies [4–6]. In recent years, single-atom catalysts (SACs) have attracted consid - erable attention owing to their maximized atomic utilization efficiency, which has been demonstrated to markedly enhance the electrocatalytic activity for HER [7,8] . Nevertheless, SACs often suffer from limited thermodynamic stability; because of their high surface energy, isolated metal sites tend to aggregate during catalytic processes [9–12]. In contrast, dual-atom catalysts (DACs) offer greater structural tunability of the active sites and enable the optimization of intermediate *Corresponding authors.. E-mail addresses: zhuhaiyan@nwu.edu.cn (H. Zhu), lywiailyw@gmail.com (Y. Li). 1These authors contribute equally to this article. Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Fuel u{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2q�ow https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fuel.2026.138444 Received 8 December 2025; Received in revised form 11 January 2026; Accepted 16 January 2026
