Fuel 415 (2026) 138444 2adsorption and desorption through synergistic interactions between paired atoms, thereby significantly boosting catalytic performance. Consequently, DACs have emerged as highly promising HER electro - catalysts and have received increasing research interest in recent years [13,14] . Moreover, introducing dual-metal doping into DAC structures can not only further regulate the adsorption states of intermediates and reactants but also modulate the charge distribution of metal active sites via element-specific electronic interactions, thereby generating diverse synergistic effects [15]. Furthermore, previous studies have demonstrated that 1 T-MoS 2 exhibits excellent stability under harsh acidic and alkaline conditions, making it a widely employed support material for electrocatalysts [16]. In addition, its unique electronic structure can effectively facilitate the HER process [17–19]. Building upon these advantages, we designed dual-atom catalysts (DACs) with 1 T-MoS 2 as the substrate and applied a density functional theory (DFT)-based free-energy prioritization strat- egy to systematically screen HER-active DACs. Using the absolute hydrogen adsorption free energy of Pt (111) (0.09 eV) as a benchmark, 11 dual-metal combinations with potentially high activity (e.g., FeFe, FeCo, FeRu) were initially identified. To further tune the local coordi - nation environment, this study simultaneously integrates the bimetallic combinations of five key transition metals (Fe, Co, Ni, Ru, Pt) with the systematic coordination environment modulation of two nonmetallic atoms (N, P) for synergistic screening, thereby constructing a total of 66 candidate catalysts (Fig. 1). Through comprehensive evaluation, FeRu /P2@1T-MoS 2 was identified as the most promising catalyst, exhibiting nearly optimal Gibbs free energy (ΔGH* ≪0). This study highlights the effectiveness of the DFT-based free-energy prioritization strategy in accelerating DACs design and demonstrates that adsorption energy serves as a reliable descriptor with strong predictive capability and broad application potential. 2.Computational details In this study, all spin-polarized density functional theory (DFT) cal- culations based on first principles were carried out using the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP) [20]. The interactions between ions and electrons were treated using the projector augmented-wave (PAW) method, while the electronic exchange –correlation energy was described by the Perdew –Burke –Ernzerhof (PBE) functional within the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) framework [21–23]. A plane-wave cutoff energy of 500 eV was applied. A 4 ×2 ×1 supercell model was adopted for the 1 T-MoS 2 substrate, with the lattice param - eters of a 12.896 Å and b 11.253 Å. To avoid spurious interactions between periodic slab models, a vacuum spacing of 15 Å was introduced along the c-axis.The DFT-D3 correction method was employed to accurately account for the long-range van der Waals interactions between the catalyst and adsorbates. For the geometry optimization, we selected a series of k- points for convergence testing (Fig. S1). For all structural optimizations, a 2 ×2 ×1 Gamma-centered k-point mesh was adopted for Brillouin zone sampling, For band structure calculations, a denser 7 ×7 ×1 K- point grid was employed, while the density of states was computed with a 15 ×15 ×1 Gamma-centered mesh. To assess the thermodynamic stability of the optimized structures, ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) simulations were performed in the canonical (NVT) ensemble. In addition, the Crystal Orbital Hamiltonian Population (COHP) was calculated using the LOBSTER code to quantify and analyze bonding and antibonding interactions between atoms. To further investigate chemi - cal bonding at the molecular level, geometry optimizations and fre- quency calculations were carried out with Gaussian 16 at the PBE level of theory. All elements were described using the def2-SVP atom- centered basis set within the PAW framework. The thermodynamic and electrochemical stability of the materials were evaluated through the formation energy (Ef) and dissolution po- tential (Udiss). EfEcat-Eslab-EM n(1) UdissU0 dissmrtalCbulk␀EfEne (2) Here, Ecat, Eslab, and EM represent the energies of M1M2/X2@1T-MoS 2 (X P, N), P or N-coordinated defective 1 T-MoS 2, and isolated metal atoms, respectively. U0 diss(metal, bulk) denotes the standard dissolution potential of the bulk metal, and n represents the number of electrons involved in the dissolution process. According to the Sabatier principle [24], the adsorption energy of reaction intermediates governs the catalytic performance, typically exhibiting a volcano-type relationship [25]. The adsorption energy of key intermediates reflects the strength of interaction between the cata- lyst and reactants, thereby serving as a critical descriptor of catalytic activity. Consequently, evaluating the adsorption energy of HER in- termediates is essential for identifying efficient DAC catalysts. The adsorption energy (Eads) of HER intermediates on the DACs was calcu - lated as follows: EadsEtotal-Esubstrates -1 2EH2 (3) where Eads represents the adsorption energy of the HER intermediate (H*), Etotal denotes the energy of the optimized adsorption system, and Esubstrates is the energy of the substrate model. The solvent effect was considered by using the implicit solvent model of VASP-sol plug-in unit [26], and the the dielectric constant was set to 80F/m [27].The adsorption energy of intermediate in the solvent is given by the following formula: Esol adsEsol total-Esol substrates -1 2EH2 (4) According to the Sabatier principle, the adsorption site exhibits the highest catalytic activity when ΔGH* ≪0 [28,29] . Therefore, we further evaluated the HER performance of the DACs based on the calculation of ΔGH*: ΔGH*EadsΔEZPE␀TΔSH* (5) where ΔEZPE and ΔSH* represent the differences in zero-point energy and entropy between the adsorbed hydrogen atom and the gaseous hydrogen molecule, respectively. And T denotes the temperature (298.15 K). The charge density difference (Δρ) between the adsorbed interme - diate H* and the M1M2@1T-MoS 2 substrate was calculated as follows: Δρρtotal␀ρsubstrates␀ρH* (6) Fig. 1.Schematic diagram of the catalyst screening process, structure and performance, the left part presents the screening process of the catalyst, while the right part shows the configuration of the target catalyst.T. Li et al.
