Fuel 415 (2026) 138444 43.Results and discussion 3.1. High-Throughput computational screening of DACs Previous studies have reported that dual-atom catalysts (DACs) composed of Fe, Co, Ni, and Ru exhibit promising activity toward OER or HER [30,31] . On this basis, we initially constructed 11 candidate DACs, namely FeFe, FeCo, FeRu, FeNi, CoCo, CoNi, CoRu, NiNi, NiRu, RuRu, and PtPt. The optimized structural models of these 11 DACs are pre- sented in Fig. 2. In this study, a screening methodology based on prioritizing ΔGH* was employed. Specifically, adsorption energy calculations were extended by introducing ΔGH* as the key descriptor for systematically screening DACs. A standard screening window of |ΔGH*| between –0.2 eV and 0.2 eV was adopted to identify promising HER catalysts [32–34]. Fig. 3illustrates the high-throughput computational workflow used to screen 1 T-MoS 2-supported bimetallic M1M2 catalysts (transition metals including Fe, Co, Ni, Ru, etc.). The goal of this strategy is to efficiently identify cost-effective catalysts with HER performance potentially exceeding that of platinum. The screening process proceeds as follows. First, combinations of relatively inexpensive and abundant transition metals were evaluated, and those with |ΔGH*| F0.2 eV were eliminated, as such deviations from the optimal adsorption strength are unfavorable for efficient HER catalysis. Next, a stricter criterion (|ΔGH*| ≼0.09 eV) was applied to the remaining candidates to select catalysts whose hydrogen adsorption behavior is comparable to or superior to that of platinum. For materials not meeting these criteria, the local coordination environment was modulated by introducing heteroatoms to further optimize their elec- tronic properties and adjust adsorption energies. Through this multi- level screening strategy combined with structural optimization, bime - tallic catalysts with both low cost and high HER activity were ultimately identified. As shown in Fig. 4, two catalysts —FeRu@1T-MoS 2 (␀0.17 eV) and FeFe@1T-MoS 2 (␀0.15 eV)—exhibit ΔGH* values within the target range, indicating their potential for high HER activity. However, their catalytic performance still falls short of that achieved by noble-metal catalysts such as platinum. Inspired by previous studies [35], they modulated the electronic structure of these DACs by partially substituting nitrogen atoms in the M␀N4 coordination sites with less electronegative nonmetal atoms (e.g., phosphorus), thereby optimizing their catalytic behavior. To further implement this optimization strategy and maximize the room for performance improvement, we did not limit ourselves to the partial substitution of a single phosphorus atom. Instead, we achieved precise regulation of the local coordination environment of 1 T-MoS 2 by systematically varying the content ratio and coordination mode of ni- trogen and phosphorus (see Fig. 5), and finally constructed 66 different candidate models. Gibbs free-energy analysis of hydrogen adsorption identified nine catalysts with HER activity comparable to that of Pt (111) (ΔGH* ␀0.09 eV): CoCo/N 6@1T-MoS 2 (␀0.079 eV), FeCo/ P10@1T-MoS 2 (␀0.089 eV), RuCo/P 6@1T-MoS 2 (0.043 eV), RuRu/ P2@1T-MoS 2 (␀0.045 eV), FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 (0.008 eV), FeNi/ P10@1T-MoS 2 (0.045 eV), RuNi/P 2@1T-MoS 2 (0.044 eV), RuNi/ P6@1T-MoS 2 (0.073 eV), and RuNi/P 10@1T-MoS 2 (0.040 eV). The free energy distribution curves corresponding to catalysts in different coor- dination environments are provided in the Supplementary Information (Fig. S2). 3.2. Stability screening Through the screening process, nine promising candidate electro - catalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction were identified. To further assess their thermodynamic and electrochemical stability, the formation energy (Ef) and dissolution potential (Udiss) were employed as evaluation criteria [36]. In general, a more negative Ef and a more positive Udiss indicate superior stability, the detailed data on formation energy and dissolution potential for all 66 configurations are provided in Table S1. In this study, priority was given to catalysts with ΔGH* ≪0 as the benchmark for HER activity. As shown in Fig. 6(a), although the stability (in terms of Ef and Udiss) of FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 is not optimal, it still falls within an acceptable range, and it exhibits significant advantages in catalytic performance. To further examine its robustness, ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) simulations were performed at 500 K to evaluate kinetic sta- bility. As shown in Fig. 6(b), the limited energy fluctuations and negli- gible structural deformation after 10 ps of simulation confirm that both energy and temperature remained within a constrained range throughout the AIMD process. These results demonstrate the excellent kinetic stability of this material. Considering both catalytic performance and stability, FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 was ultimately identified as the most promising HER electrocatalyst. Based on the continuous solvation model, we also considered the influence of the solvent on the adsorption behavior between FeRu/ P2@1T-MoS 2 and intermediate. As shown in Table S2, the result Fig. 3.Screening workflow for M1M2@1T-MoS 2 catalysts. Fig. 4.Screening of 11 DACs based on | ΔGH* |D0.20 eV.T. Li et al.
