Fuel 415 (2026) 138444 5indicates that the solvent effect induces a ΔGH* shift of 0.004 eV, which demonstrates that in the system of this study, the impact of the solvent effect on the hydrogen adsorption free energy is relatively weak and the core conclusions remain unaffected. 3.3. Origin of the catalytic activity Previous computational studies have established the outstanding catalytic activity and stability of the optimal electrocatalyst FeRu/ P2@1T-MoS 2. Concurrently, a comparative analysis of the adsorption free energies at different potential active sites confirms that the current structure corresponds to the optimal adsorption configuration, with relevant details provided in Table S3 of the Supporting Information. Nevertheless, a deeper understanding of its electronic structure and the origin of its catalytic activity remains essential. To address this, we conducted a comprehensive analysis aimed at elucidating the hydrogen reaction mechanism and electronic characteristics of the active sites in FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2. Specifically, we calculated and analyzed its the energy barriers of the HER reaction pathway, band structure, partial density of states (PDOS), projected crystal orbital hamiltonian popula - tion (pCOHP), and charge density difference. The reaction energy barrier and corresponding free energy diagrams for FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 (see Fig. S3 in the Supporting Information) demonstrate that for the HER proceeding via the Volmer-Tafel pathway, the adsorption strength of H* is close to the ideal value, rendering the Volmer step highly facile. The major energy barrier of the reaction originates from the formation of the bis-H* intermediate species, yet the overall energy barrier (0.357 eV) remains at a relatively low level, which collectively indicates that this catalyst possesses excellent HER catalytic performance. The band structure of FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 reveals multiple energy bands crossing the Fermi level, confirming its distinct metallic character (Fig. 7a). This metallicity facilitates rapid electron transport, thereby enhancing charge-transfer efficiency during electrocatalysis. The non- zero density of states at the Fermi level further verifies the material ’s excellent electrical conductivity, making it highly suitable for catalytic applications. Projected density of states (PDOS) analysis indicates that hydrogen adsorption on the FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 surface is primarily facilitated by the hybridization of the H–s orbital with the d orbitals of the metal atoms (Ru, Fe) and the s/p orbitals of the coordinated P atoms. As illustrated in Figs. 7b, a pronounced overlap between the H–s and Ru–d orbitals is observed near the Fermi level, signifying strong s-d hybridi - zation. In contrast, the overlap between the H–s and Ru–s/p orbitals is considerably weaker, implying that the d orbitals are the principal contributors to hydrogen adsorption at the Ru sites. Concurrently, the PDOS for Fe–H interactions reveals a moderate overlap between the H–s and Fe–d orbitals near the Fermi level, confirming the involvement of Fe atoms via their d orbitals. Furthermore, strong overlap peaks between the s/p orbitals of P and the H–s orbital form distinct bonding states, demonstrating a robust P–H interaction through s-p hybridization. To further elucidate the bonding interactions, we integrated the pCOHP of FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 to obtain the ␀ICOHP values, thereby quantitatively evaluating the bonding strength between H and Ru, Fe, and the top-site P atom. As shown in Fig. 8, the calculated ␀ICOHP values are ␀1.585 eV, ␀0.025 eV, and ␀3.909 eV, respectively. These ICOHP results quantitatively confirm that the P–H bond ex- hibits the strongest bonding interaction within the system, as evidenced Fig. 5.Structural diagrams of diatomic catalysts in different coordination environments. Fig. 6.a) The formation energy and dissolution potential of the 9 DACs. b) The variations of energy during the 10 ps AIMD simulation.T. Li et al.
