Fuel 415 (2026) 138444 7donation from P, thereby optimizing the local electronic environment of the active sites. Meanwhile, the Ru–H bond demonstrates a moderately strong interaction, primarily originating from metal d–H s orbital hy- bridization as per PDOS analysis. This is consistent with its negative ICOHP value, which confirms a bonding character. The small absolute magnitude of this value, however, indicates that Ru's role, while stabi- lizing, is secondary to that of P. Although the ICOHP value for the Fe–H bond is negligible, the PDOS analysis unequivocally reveals a non-zero s- d orbital interaction, confirming Fe's participation in the adsorption process. This indicates that Fe primarily functions as an electronic fine- tuner within the bimetallic site, indirectly modulating the electronic structures of the Ru and P atoms to optimize the overall adsorption performance. In summary, the hydrogen adsorption mechanism on the FeRu/ P2@1T-MoS 2 system operates through a multi-center cooperative model. This synergy involves a dominant contribution from the strong P–H bond, a key stabilizing role from the medium-strength Ru–H bond (via s-d hybridization), and a modulating effect from the weak Fe–H interaction that fine-tunes the electronic structure. This multi-faceted cooperation enables the precise optimization of the hydrogen adsorp - tion free energy (ΔGH*). Building upon the PDOS and COHP results, we further performed a charge density difference (CDD) analysis. As shown in Fig. 9(a), signif - icant electron redistribution upon hydrogen adsorption primarily occurs among the H, P, and Ru atoms. Specifically: i) A dense and directionally distinct region of electron accumulation (blue) and depletion (green) forms between H and P, indicating a strong interaction. ii) A discernible but weaker electron accumulation region appears between H and Ru, with lower intensity and smaller spatial extent compared to the P–H region. This observation is consistent with the moderate Ru–H bond strength inferred from the ␀ICOHP value. iii) No direct electron redistribution is detected between H and Fe. However, interconnected redistribution between Fe and P suggests that Fe indirectly modulates the electronic environment through electron exchange with P. Further Bader charge analysis, presented in Fig. 9(b), corroborates the proposed multi –center synergistic mechanism. The results indicate that during the catalytic process, the adsorbed H* gains 0.948 e, with the electrons predominantly supplied by adjacent P atoms (losing 0.669 e) and Ru atoms (losing 0.332 e). Notably, P atoms account for more than half of the total charge transferred, quantitatively confirming their central role as the primary electron donors. Although the net charge variation on Fe atoms is minor (a gain of 0.082 e), distinct charge rearrangement is observed between Fe and neighboring P atoms. This suggests that Fe atoms contribute mainly by optimizing the electronic environment of the active sites through long –range electronic modulation. Overall, the CDD and Bader charge analysis provides a direct confirmation of the PDOS and COHP findings: hydrogen adsorption is governed by a multi-center cooperative mechanism in which P serves as the dominant adsorption site, Ru provides secondary stabilization, and Fe acts as an electronic modulator. This triatomic synergy surpasses the conventional dual-metal paradigm and offers new insights into the design of efficient multi-center catalytic systems. 4.Conclusions Through density functional theory (DFT) calculations, we system - atically explored the potential of P-doped dual-atom catalysts as elec- trocatalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). By incorporating non-metal atoms (P and N) to modulate hydrogen adsorption strength, we constructed 66 types of M1M2/X2@1T-MoS 2 (X P, N) materials. Among these, FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 was identified as the most promising HER catalyst, with a calculated ΔGH* value close to zero. In this system, the hydrogen adsorption mechanism transcends the conventional dual-metal-center model, evolving into a novel multi- center synergistic adsorption mode. Specifically, P atoms dominate adsorption through strong P–H bonding, Ru atoms provide moderate Ru–H interactions that fine-tune and stabilize the adsorption strength, and Fe atoms indirectly regulate the electronic environment via long- range electronic effects. This synergistic interplay enables precise opti- mization of hydrogen adsorption free energy (ΔGH*), thereby achieving superior HER catalytic activity. This study not only identifies FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2 as a promising HER catalyst candidate but also integrates bimetallic combination screening with nonmetallic (N/P) coordination environment modulation to construct a high-throughput screening system based on metal-nonmetal synergistic regulation. This system enables the precise fine-tuning of the electronic structure of active sites, thus providing a paradigm for the rational design of next-generation electrocatalysts. Author Contributions: Tingting Li:Conceptualization:Ideas; formulation or evolution of overarching research goals and aims. Guohao Yin:Data curation:Management activities to annotate (pro- duce metadata), scrub data and maintain research data(including soft- ware code, where it is necessary for interpreting the data itself) for initial use and later re-use. Shanlin Chen:Formal analysis:Application of statistical, mathemat - ical, computational, or other formal techniques to analyze or synthesize study data. Haiyan Zhu:Funding acquisition:Acquisition of the financial support for the project leading to this publication. Chou Wu:Methodology:Development or design of methodology; creation of models. Shaobo Jia:Project administration:Management and coordination responsibility for the research activity planning and execution. Ghulam Meeladi:Investigation:Conducting a research and investi - gation process, specifically performing the data collection. Yawei Li:Software:Programming, software development; designing Fig. 9.a) Charge density difference of FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2. The isosurface value is set at ±0.002 e/Å3. The blue regions represent electron accumulation (Δρ F0), while the green regions represent electron depletion (Δρ D0). b) Bader charge analysis of H adsorbed on FeRu/P 2@1T-MoS 2.T. Li et al.
