ARTICLE  Journal Name 2 | J. Name ., 2012, 00, 1-3 This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 20 xx Please do not adjust margins Please do not adjust margins  electrocatalyst based on NiPt hybrid has not been reported so far. In this study, we have, for the first time, synthesized carbon nanotube (CNT)-supported Pt/NiO/Ni (Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT) hybrid electrocatalysts by inter-dispersed NiO and Ni onto CNT through substrate-enhanced electroless deposition (SEED),30, 31 followed by calcination in Ar/H 2 (v/v 50:10) atmosphere, and then decorated NiO/Ni/CNTs with ultrasmall Pt nanoparticles (NPs, ~2 nm) by a galvanic displacement reaction. The hierarchically  structured Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT electrocatalysts thus prepared possessed a large number of active sites, including NiO/Ni surfaces , Pt-Ni interfaces and selectively exposed Pt (111) facets, for ORR, OER and HER . Consequently , the resultant Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT hybrid electrocatalysts exhibit ed excellent performance for ORR, OER and HER - similar to the benchmark RuO 2 for OER, and even superior to the commercial Pt/C for ORR and HER in an alkaline medium. Furthermore , two Zn-air batteries based on the Pt/NiO/Ni/CNTs air electrode for ORR and OER could successfully self-power an overall water splitting process using the same Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT electrocatalyst for OER and HER, demonstrating great potential for practical applications. This work rep orts the first NiPt hybrid with a low Pt loading for ORR/OER/HER trifunctional electrolysis in Zn-air batteries and water splitting processes, providing a facile approach to trifunctional catalysts from low-cost earth- abundant materials for efficient energy conversion and storage. Results and discussion Synthesis and characterization Fig. 1 schematically shows the procedure for preparing Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT catalysts: Ni was first deposited on CNTs through a galvanic displacement reaction between Ni2+ and Zn substrate by SEED.30, 31  During the SEED process, Ni(OH) 2 also formed due to a pH increase caused by the galvanic displacement between H+ and Zn (ESI†,Fi g. S1). After sonication and drying in air at 70º C, we then obtained the Ni(OH) 2/Ni/CNTs powder with Ni and Ni(OH) 2 particles supported by CNTs , along with some ZnO impurities (Fig. 1a). Thereafter, the Ni(OH) 2/Ni/CNTs powder was converted into NiO/Ni/CNTs by annealing at 950º C in Ar/H 2 atmosphere, which was accompanied by the remov al of ZnO (Fig. 1b, c) . By stirring NiO/Ni/CNTs powered in an aqueous solutions of K 2PtCl6 with different concentrations of 1 5.4, 3 0.8 and 61.5 μmol L-1, we finally obtained Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT-1, Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT-2 and Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT-3 (Fig. 1d), respectively, for subsequent electrocatalysis (Fig. 1e) . Details for all the reactions involved in this process could be found in  the ESI†. As shown in Fig. 2a, X-ray diffraction ( XRD ) patterns of Ni(OH) 2/Ni/CNTs exhibit ed a typical face-centered cubic structure (fcc) of Ni (JCPDS No. 04-0850), indicating Ni was successfully deposited on CNTs. Some weak peaks from Ni(OH) 2/Ni/CNTs indicated the presence of ZnO (JCPDS No. 36- 1451) and Ni(OH) 2∙0.75H 2O (JCPDS No. 38-0715). The peaks of ZnO disappeared in NiO/Ni/CNTs due to the removal of ZnO after the thermal annealing . It was worth to note that no NiO peak was observed, suggesting the amorphous state of NiO in NiO/Ni/CNTs , as also evidenced by a high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image and X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) – see below . Although no distinct Pt peak was observed in t he XRD patterns for the Fig. 1 Schematic  representation of the synthetic  process . (a) Deposition of Ni  and Ni(OH) 2 onto CNTs supported by a Zn plate  via SEED . (b) Ni(OH) 2/Ni/CNT s powered was obtained after sonication and drying. (c) Ni(OH) 2/Ni/CNT s was converted into NiO/Ni/CNT by  annealing in Ar/H 2 at 950 ºC . (d) Ultrasmall Pt NPs were decorated on the NiO/Ni/CNTs by galvanic displacement reaction to obtain the Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT catalyst . (e) the resultant Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT catalyst  was used  as an OER/HER bifunctional catalyst for overall water splitting, which was powered by two Zn -air batteries using the same Pt/NiO/Ni/CNT c atalyst  as the air electrode  for ORR and OER . Page 2 of 11 Nanoscale Nanoscale Accepted Manuscript Published on 10 June 2020. Downloaded by Uppsala University on 6/10/2020 12:56:23 PM. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/D0NR03378B
