3d metals is a one of the most promising approaches to improve the electrochemical properties of Pt-based cata- lysts, reduce their production costs, and improve durability by mitigating structural changes over time [ 9–11]. For these purposes, the studies have revealed that Pt nanopar- ticles are commonly alloyed with the ﬁrst-row transition metals (such as Ni, Cu, Co, or Fe) [ 5,6,12–14]. Accord- ingly, the literature reports that incorporating Cu and Ni into Pt to create an alloy not only reduces costs but also leads to the formation of nanoparticles with long-term structural stability [ 1,8,14,15]. PtCuNi nanoalloys are promising catalysts for electrochemical reactions such asHER and OER [ 10,16]. This preference arises from the high catalytic activity achieved through synergistic inter- actions between Pt, Cu, and Ni metals. Stabilization of Ptactive sites via electron transfer facilitates water dissocia- tion processes. From a cost-effectiveness standpoint, given the high cost of Pt, alloying it with cheaper metals such asCu and Ni reduces the overall cost without sacriﬁcing performance. Furthermore, these nanoalloys often exhibit high structural stability and can have small particle sizes,increasing the reaction surface area. These factors make PtCuNi nanoalloys advantageous compared to other cata- lysts in terms of both production costs and performance. Recent advancements have shown that replacing monometallic catalysts with binary and ternary nanoalloys signiﬁcantly enhances catalytic efﬁciency against targetedspecies [ 17–19]. This can be attributed to the strain effects created at the atomic level and electronic transfer between the distinct metals. The morphology and composition ofnanocatalysts also signiﬁcantly inﬂuence their electrocat- alytic activity and stability. To improve Pt-based nanocatalysts for the electrocatalytic applications, variousfactors such as shape, size distribution, density, interpar- ticle distance, electrochemical active surface area (ECSA), and steps in the formation mechanism need thorough adetailed examination [ 5,18–22]. Over the past decade, various morphologies of PtM (M: Ni, Co, Cu, etc.) alloys have been widely investigated forthe catalytic applications [ 23–26]. While PtM alloys demonstrate efﬁciency in breaking the HO–H bond, they have exhibited limited capability to transform adsorbed Hintermediates to H 2gas. Among these alloys, ternary PtCuNi nanocatalysts have been studied, including nano- cubes [ 10], nanoframe [ 27], and nanochains [ 5] types of structures. Cao et al. conducted a study about the exami- nation of ternary PtCuNi nanocatalysts with a face-cen- tered cubic crystal phase doped with a small amount of Ptfor HER, revealing that their speciﬁc activity surpassed that of bimetallic nanocatalysts [ 5]. A study revealed that the PtCu 0.5Ni0.5/C catalyst exhibited a speciﬁc activity nearly 9.2 times higher than that of commercial Pt/C [ 19]. Moreover, Wei et al. reported a signiﬁcant enhancement incatalytic performance, achieving a Tafel slope of 24.1 mV dec-1and a remarkably low overpotential of 15 mV at 10 mA cm-2[10]. The superior performance was linked to the uniform structure of the ternary PtNiCu alloy, a higherdensity of surfactant sites, and enhanced metal–metal interactions, leading to a characteristic Tafel slope. In recent years, signiﬁcant research has focused on thestructural, morphological, and catalytic characteristics of PtCuNi nanoalloys. However, detailed examination of structural properties utilizing Rietveld reﬁnement analysis, including crystalline structure, lattice structure identiﬁca- tion, d-spacing evaluation, and crystallite size analysis, remains scarce. Furthermore, although the catalytic per- formance of PtNiCu alloys has been explored for both the HER and OER processes, comprehensive investigation intocatalytic performances and understanding intermetallic effects between Pt, Cu, and Ni of under varying atomic ratios in both acidic and alkaline media continues to pre-sent a challenge. In this work, we describe a facile synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys with varying Cu, Pt, and Ni composi-tions, allowing the production of nanoalloys without oxides at reduced temperatures [ 28,29]. The one-pot fabrication of ternary PtCuNi NPs offers a promising strategy fordeveloping high-performance and long-lasting catalysts for HER and OER, offering advantages in terms of synthesis simplicity, compositional control, catalytic performance,and structural stability compared to the multiple steps synthesis methods. PtCuNi nanoalloys reveal a cubic crystal phase with adjustable lattice parameters and smallparticle sizes. Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloys shows superior speciﬁc activity and stability toward HER in alkaline electrolyte, while Pt 19Cu36Ni45exhibited excellent cat- alytic ability for OER in acidic media compared to Pt/C catalysts. The enhanced catalytic activity is attributed to synergistic effects between Pt, Cu, and Ni, facilitatingwater dissociation and electron transfer in both acidic and basic pH conditions. 2 Experimental 2.1 Chemicals Nickel(II) acetylacetonate (Ni(acac) 2, 97.0%), copper(II) acetylacetonate (Cu(acac) 2, 98.0%), platinum(II) acety- lacetonate (Pt(acac) 2, 97.0%), polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), sodium borohydride (NaBH 4Sigma-Aldrich C98%) and dichloromethane were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich. Sodium hydroxide (NaOH, and C98%), N,N-dimethyl- formamide (DMF, C99.8%), ethylene glycol (EG, C99.8%), and ethanol (extra pure) were also obtained from ISOLAB. 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–10189A facile synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys 10173 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
