2.2 Syntheses of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys The synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys was conducted by using a modiﬁed polyol process. The atomic ratio of metals chosen was determined to ensure that the catalystexhibits maximum activity and selectivity in the target reaction, minimizes unwanted side reactions, and forms the most suitable active sites at the atomic level. This selectionwas made based on up-to-date scientiﬁc ﬁndings in the lit- erature, experimental data, and computational predictions [30,31]. These sources collectively guided the determination of the optimal atomic ratios of Pt, Cu, and Ni in the ternary nanoalloys to enhance catalytic performance, stability, and structural properties in both acidic and alkaline media. In thismethod, each chemical reagent plays a critical role: EG acts as both the solvent and the primary reducing agent at high temperatures, converting metal ions into their metallic form.NaOH adjusts the pH to alkaline conditions, facilitating the reduction process and inﬂuencing the morphology of the yielded nanoalloys. PVP functions as a surfactant and sta-bilizing agent, preventing particle agglomeration by adsorbing onto the nanoalloy surface. Finally, NaBH 4serves as a strong reducing agent, particularly during the initialreaction stages, to ensure rapid and complete co-reduction of the metal precursors, leading to a more uniform nucleation process for the formation of nanoalloys. Therefore, thesynthesis was carried out by increasing the Pt content and adjusting the Cu or Ni content accordingly in order to investigate determine optimum catalyst ratio for HER andOER activity. For the Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloys, an 0.329 g, 0.057 g, and 0.100 g of Pt(acac) 2, Cu(acac) 2, and Ni(acac) 2 metal salts were dissolved completely in 30 ml DMF and 60 ml of EG (0.620 mol) under vigorous magnetic stirring, followed by a 10 min. sonication in an ultrasonic bath at room temperature. Subsequently, 0.115 g of PVP(0.0053 mol), serving as a surfactant to ensure the stabi- lization and the colloidal dispersion of the alloy nanoparti- cles, and 0.866 g of NaOH (0.0265 mol), employed for pHadjustment, were introduced into the metal precursor’s solution. The reaction system was sealed, and the tempera- ture was raised to 120 /C176C under a ﬂow of Argon gas. At this point, 1.092 g of NaBH 4(0.0354 mol) was added dropwise to the solution. The solution was further heated to 175 /C176C and kept at this temperature for 90 min, followed by rapidcooling to RT. The solution underwent washing with etha- nol/dichloromethane and subsequent forced separation of PtCuNi nanoalloys in a centrifuge (9000 rpm, 6 min). 2.3 CharacterizationA Hitachi HT7700 transmission electron microscope (TEM) in high-resolution (HR) mode at 120 kV was usedto analyze the morphology and size of the ternary PtCuNinanoalloys. HRTEM images were acquired using a Hitachi HF5000 equipped with a high-brightness cold ﬁeld emis- sion gun (FEG), high-stability, automated aberration cor- rector, and operated at 200 kV applied acceleratingvoltage. The analysis of the images was performed using Gatan Digital Micrograph /C210software. The XRD patterns of the nanoalloys were collected using a PANalyticalEMPYREAN X-ray diffraction instrument operated at a voltage of 45 kV and current of 40 mA with Cu-K aradi- ation ( k= 1.54 A ˚). Inductively coupled plasma-mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) with a PerkinElmer NexION 2000 P and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS)was used to determine the chemical composition of the nanoalloys. For the ICP-MS characterization, approxi- mately 50 mg PtCuNi powders samples were prepared induplicate and the samples were extracted with appropriate acids (HNO 3/HCl/H 2O2). Furthermore, X-ray photoelec- tron spectroscopy (XPS) with Thermo K-Alpha radiation at1486.6 eV was applied to study the material’s chemical states and surface composition, utilizing the C 1s peak at 284.5 eV for reference to adjust the binding energies. 3 Results and discussion 3.1 Structural characteristics XRD patterns and Rietveld reﬁnement analysis of ternary Pt 9Cu39Ni52,Pt12Cu53Ni35,P t 19Cu36Ni45, and Pt 58Cu15Ni27 nanoalloys were studied in the range of 2 h=3 0/C176–100/C176and are depicted in Fig. 1A–D demonstrating the experimental data (black circles), best ﬁt (red line), difference (blue line) and Bragg reﬂections (red bar), respectively. The Rietveldreﬁnement analysis of all prepared ternary nanoalloys revealed characteristic peaks associated with the fcc phase, with a space group of Fm 3m. All the parameters which used and obtained from the Rietveld reﬁnement analysisfor the samples are provided in Tables S1 and S2 of the supporting information. With the exception of the Pt 9Cu39Ni52nanoalloys, all other ternary PtCuNi nanoal- loys exhibited a single fcc-Pt 1Cu1Ni1phase in the fcc structure. The Pt 9Cu39Ni52sample in Fig. 1A exhibited Fm3msymmetry group, and the peak marked by ‘*’ in XRD pattern indicated the characteristics of the Ni phase [111] at 45.13 8[32,33]. As depicted in Fig. 1B, the ﬁve diffraction peaks at 2 h= 42.88 8, 49.94 8, 73.31 8, 88.86 8, and 93.97 8for the ternary Pt 12Cu53Ni35nanoalloys corre- spond to the (111), (200), (220), (311), and (222) crystal planes of the Pt 1Cu1Ni1fcc phase. Given that the peak positions lie between the standard metallic Pt, Cu, and Nipeaks and no additional peaks corresponding to other phases of these metals are observed, it can be concluded 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–1018910174 I. B. Isik et al. 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
