that three metals form a solid-solution (alloy). As it can be concluded by Fig. 1C, D, the higher mass ratio of Pt in the nanoalloy, the larger peak width and the lower peak intensity, suggesting that the particle size was smaller thanthat of other ternary nanoalloys. XRD peak intensities and full width at half maximum (FWHM) values provide valuable insights into the degree of crystallinity and crys-tallite size of NPs. Speciﬁcally, the intensity of the (111) diffraction peak is associated with the degree of atomic ordering and preferred orientation along that crystal plane[34,35]. Higher peak intensity typically indicates better crystallinity and a higher degree of structural order within the corresponding planes. For the Pt 9Cu39Ni52, Pt12Cu53Ni35,P t 19Cu36Ni45, and Pt 58Cu15Ni27samples, the (111) peak intensities are *7000, 7500, 5500, and 2800 a.u., respectively. The signiﬁcantly lower peak intensityobserved for Pt 58Cu15Ni27suggests reduced crystallinity and possibly smaller crystallite size, as supported by the broader FWHM (Table S1). This may also reﬂect a higherdegree of structural disorder or the inﬂuence of surface roughness and NP dispersion, which can attenuate XRD signal intensity [ 35]. The composition of the nanoalloy, its crystal formation, symmetry group, d-spacing, and lattice parameters,determined by Rietveld analysis, were complemented by the average particle sizes from TEM images of Pt 9Cu39Ni52,Pt12Cu53Ni35,P t 19Cu36Ni45, and Pt 58Cu15Ni27 nanoalloys, are presented in Table S1. The lattice param- eters for pure Cu, Pt, and Ni are 3.6150 [ 36], 3.923 [ 37], and 3.5238 A ˚[36], respectively. The shift observed at XRD peaks towards lower 2 hindicated a lattice expansion for PtCuNi, increasing from a=b=c= 3.6150 to a= b=c= 3.717 A ˚, in relation to the Pt content percentage in the alloy. A comparable trend was seen in the d-spacing of PtCuNi nanoalloys. As shown in Table S1, the lowest crystal and particle sizes were found to be 1.59 ±0.39 and 3.62±0.01 nm for the Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloy, respec- tively. All these results support the formation of PtCuNi nanoalloys. Lattice strain values were calculated based on XRD data to assess their inﬂuence on catalytic performance, and the results are summarized in Table S1. Lattice strain is a critical structural parameter that arises from lattice mis-matches, atomic size disparities, or alloying effects, and it can signiﬁcantly affect the electronic conﬁguration and surface reactivity of catalytic materials. In metal nanoal-loys, particularly those based on Pt, strain-induced distor- tions modulate the Pt–Pt and Pt–M (M = Cu, Ni) bond Fig. 1 A –DDiffraction patterns using Cu K aX-rays representing the data distributed as the ’standard’ data (black circles), Rietveld analysis: best ﬁt (red line), difference (blue line), and the positions of all possible Bragg reﬂections are shown by the red row of verticaltick marks below the proﬁle of observed data for Pt 9Cu39Ni52,P t 12Cu53Ni35,P t 19Cu36Ni45, and Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloys, respectively 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–10189A facile synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys 10175 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
