lengths and alter the d-band center of Pt atoms, thereby tuning the adsorption energies of key intermediates involved in electrocatalytic reactions such as HER and OER [ 38]. Moderate to high lattice strain can enhance catalytic activity by optimizing binding energies, improv- ing charge transfer kinetics, and increasing the density of active sites. However, excessive strain may introducecrystal defects or lattice instability, potentially compro- mising long-term structural durability [ 39]. In this study, increasing Pt content in the PtCuNi system led to a pro- gressive rise in lattice strain: 20.00 910 –3for Pt9Cu39Ni52, 24.65 910–3for Pt 12Cu53Ni35, 40.73 910–3 for Pt 19Cu36Ni45, and 42.36 910–3for Pt 58Cu15Ni27. Among these, Pt 58Cu15Ni27exhibited the highest lattice strain, correlating strongly with its superior catalytic per-formance in HER and OER measurements. This suggests that the pronounced lattice distortion enhances both the geometric and electronic structure of the active Pt(111)surface, facilitating optimal interaction with adsorbates such as H*, OH*, and OOH*. Moreover, the simultaneous decrease in crystallite size (from 3.51 nm in Pt 9Cu39Ni52to 1.59 nm in Pt 58Cu15Ni27) suggests increased surface area and a greater density of strained surface atoms, further contributing to the improved electrocatalytic behavior. Figure 2A, B demonstrates the corresponding size dis- tribution histogram, ﬁtted for two different peaks (ﬁt 1: Ni (blue) and ﬁt 2: PtCuNi (green), using the log–normalfunction (black line: cumulative ﬁt peak). The mean par- ticle sizes of Ni and PtCuNi nanoparticles were computed as 8.58 and 3.23 nm, respectively. The calculated meanparticle sizes for Pt 12Cu53Ni35,P t 19Cu36Ni45, and Pt58Cu15Ni27nanoalloys were found to be 6.64, 6.53, and 3.62 nm (Fig. 2C–H and Table S1), respectively. The stoichiometric calculations of the atomic percentages of PtCuNi nanoalloys were performed using EDS and ICP- MS data (Table S4). The EDS spectra of Pt 9Cu39Ni52, Pt12Cu53Ni35,P t 19Cu36Ni45and Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloys (Fig. S1A–D), obtained from various regions, were aver- aged to determine the stoichiometries of the nanoalloys. Asa result, the presented nanoalloy compositions are not just due to the use of different amount of metal precursors, these are the exact alloy compositions formed during thepresented synthesis protocol. The morphology of Pt 9Cu39Ni52, Pt12Cu53Ni35, Pt19Cu36Ni45, and Pt 58Cu15Ni27intermetallic particles was assessed by TEM, and the recorded TEM images along with the associated particle size histograms are shown in Fig. 2. In TEM images (Fig. 2A, C, E, G), all nanoparticles exhibited spherical shape on cloudy backgrounds attributable to the existence of PVP employed as a surface layer agent [ 40]. Moreover, the histograms representing particle size in Fig. 2B, D, F, H, which are constructed by analyzing approximately 350 individual particles from theassociated TEM images, indicate that nanoalloys have an expected Gaussian-type curve for the particle size distri- bution, except Pt 9Cu39Ni52showing two different curves. The lower Pt content and excess Cu/Ni precursors in thePt 9Cu39Ni52alloy likely promote Ostwald ripening due to slower nucleation kinetics and higher atomic mobility of Cu/Ni [ 41]. This mechanism can explain the unbalanced particle size distribution (3–9 nm) observed in this com- position compared to Pt-rich alloys (e.g., Pt 58Cu15Ni27)i n our case. HRTEM analysis was employed to investigate the ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys (Fig. 3A). The lattice spacing in the observed region was calculated to be 0.214 nm, which lies between the lattice spacings of the individual pure metal components in fcc phase (Cu: 0.209 nm, Ni:0.203 nm, and Pt: 0.227 nm) [ 42], indicating successful alloy formation. This d-spacing matches the (111) crystal planes of the fcc PtCuNi nanoalloy, as conﬁrmed by theXRD analysis. The elemental composition of the nanoalloy was analyzed using high-angle annular dark-ﬁeld scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) com-bined with EDS. As illustrated in Fig. 3B, Cu, Pt, and Ni were regularly disseminated across the nanoalloy, con- ﬁrming the generation of a homogeneous alloy structure.Consistent with prior studies, Pt-Ni and Pt-Cu NPs exhibit similar behavior as Pt’s strong metal–metal bonding restricts atomic diffusion, limiting growth. Pt’s largeratomic radius induces lattice strain, better tolerated by smaller particles. Additionally, Pt’s higher reduction potential leads to earlier ion reduction and increasednucleation, creating more seeds that compete for precur- sors, further suppressing growth [ 43]. XPS characterization was performed to elucidate the chemical nature and environment of PtCuNi nanoalloys. In Fig. 4, the resolved spectra of the Pt 4f, Cu 2p, and Ni 2p regions are depicted. The high-resolution XPS spectra of Pt4f core-levels (Fig. 4A) exhibit mainly dual-peak pattern, with the prominent inﬂuence of the spin–orbit coupling structure 4f 7/2—4f 5/2. The Pt 4f peaks in as-synthesized PtCuNi nanoalloys exhibited a shift to higher binding energy with increasing Pt content. Notably, the Pt58Cu15Ni27intermetallic particle displayed the most signiﬁcant shift, approximately 0.45 eV higher than metallic Pt in Pt/C catalyst (4f 7/2: 70.90 eV) [ 42,44]. This alteration in the binding energy of the metal core levelsuggests a concurrent shift in the d-band center in relation to the Fermi level [ 45]. Charge transfer phenomena, involving electron migration from copper to platinum andnickel to platinum, are responsible for the observed shift toward higher binding energies [ 32]. This shift is expected to enhance the electro-catalytic capabilities of Pt-basedmaterials. However, in PtCuNi nanoalloys, the spectra reveal a distinctive peak attributed to the overlap with the 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–1018910176 I. B. Isik et al. 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
