charge transfer from Cu to Pt was further substantiated by the Cu 2p spectrum, where discernible shift of peaks towards the lower binding energies were observed, as depicted in Fig. 4B. Upon examining the high-resolution Ni 2p core spectra of all nanoalloys in Fig. 4C, the main peak appeared at*856.0 eV, identiﬁed as Ni2?, accompanied by a prominent satellite at 860.0 eV, as expected. Notably, in Pt12Cu53Ni35and Pt 58Cu15Ni27, only peaks centered at 852.0 and 852.5 eV, respectively, were observed, indicat- ing the presence of Ni0[48]. Remarkably, no substantial shift in binding energies was observed in the Ni 2p spectra.This observation suggests that, despite its lower abundance in the nanoalloys, Cu serves as a more potent electron donor to Pt than Ni. The integrated ﬁndings from XRD, TEM, HRTEM, EDS, and XPS analyses collectively reveal the alloyed structure of Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanocatalyst is formed. Speciﬁ- cally, the prevalence of Ni2?species in notable abundance suggests a higher concentration of nickel in regions prox- imal to the surface. Furthermore, the observed shifts inbinding energies serve as clear evidence of electron transfer occurring from copper to platinum. The detected binding energy shift in Pt 58Cu15Ni27, with a particle size of 3.62 nm, is attributed to the realignment of the d-band center with respect to the Fermi level and the combined interaction between Ni and Cu atoms. This conﬁrms theassertion that Pt 58Cu15Ni27demonstrates encouraging cat- alytic abilities resulting from cooperative interactions within the nanoalloys. 3.2 HER activity of the ternary PtCuNi nanocatalysts under alkaline and acidicenvironments The electrocatalytic performance of ternary PtCuNi for HER was initially investigated by studying the CV curves, depicted in Fig. 5A, B, at a scan rate of 50 mV s -1at RT in both 0.5 M KOH and 0.5 M H 2SO4solutions. Catalytic ink preparation, electrode preparation, and electrochemical measurements details are given in Sect. 2.1as presented in the Supporting Information. In addition, water splittingexperiments were performed employing the Pt–Cu–Ni electrocatalysts .The overall water splitting activity was evaluated under both alkaline (0.5 M KOH) and acidic(0.5 M H 2SO4) conditions using a scan rate of 50 mV s-1 under RT, and the resulting polarization plots are shown in Fig. S3. In 0.5 M KOH solution (Figs. 5A, and S3A), CV curve of Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloy exhibited a hydrogen desorption/adsorption behavior between 0.01 and 0.8 V (vs. RHE), suggesting that Pt atoms on the particle surfaceplay a critical role. CV curves for all ternary PtCuNi cat- alysts demonstrated a wide current peak within the range of0.05 to 0.6 V (vs. RHE) in 0.5 M H 2SO4(Figs. 5B and S3B), consistent with the voltametric results in 0.5 M KOH. Comparing the CV curves of nanoalloys with dif- ferent Pt ratios in both alkaline and acidic media, it isevident that a broader current peak is observed in the hydrogen desorption/adsorption regime with an increasing Pt ratio in the PtCuNi nanoalloy. The calculated ECSAvalues, based on the hydrogen adsorption area for 0.5 M H 2SO4and 0.5 M KOH electrolyte solutions, were demonstrated in Fig. 5C. As particle size decreased, the ECSA value increases [ 49–51], and thus the Pt 58Cu15Ni27 catalysts with the smallest particle size exhibited the highest ECSA value under alkaline and acidic conditions. The Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalyst exhibited the highest ECSA value of 36.25 m2g/C01 Ptin 0.5 M KOH environment, while the Pt 12Cu36Ni35has the highest ECSA value with 13.74 m2g/C01 Ptin 0.5 M H 2SO4solution. Figure 6A, B illustrates LSV curves of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys in 0.5 M KOH and 0.5 M H 2SO4media for the HER region, respectively. Onset potentials and overpo- tentials ( g) measured at 10 mA cm-2for all samples were determined from the LSV curve and are summarized in Fig. 6E, F to enable a quantitatively comparison of activ- ities in 0.5 M KOH and 0.5 M H 2SO4media, respectively. It should be noted that gis a sum of several contributions, including but not limited to activation (electrode), con- centration, and sometimes nucleation or reaction overpo- tentials. Among these, only the activation overpotential directly reﬂects the intrinsic kinetics of the electrodereaction, whereas concentration overpotential is related to mass transport limitations. Therefore, when discussing electrochemical kinetics, it is more rigorous to speciﬁcallyrefer to the activation overpotential as the kinetic descriptor, rather than the overall overpotential, which also includes non-kinetic contributions. Thus, in the discussionof electrochemical kinetics, we focused on comparing the activation overpotential values. In all cases, the Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloy exhibited the most superior HER activity among all the tested catalysts. Speciﬁcally, the gat 10 mA cm-2and onset potentials of Pt 58Cu15Ni27 nanoalloy were found to be 61 and 43 mV in 0.5 M KOH and 62 and 51 mV in 0.5 M H 2SO4, respectively. When comparing gmeasured at 10 mA cm-2and onset potentials of all ternary PtCuNi catalysts, it was observed that theyrequired lower potentials under alkaline and acidic condi- tions, following this order: Pt 58Cu15Ni27\ Pt19Cu36Ni45\Pt12Cu53Ni35\Pt9Cu39Ni52. Also, Pt58Cu15Ni27,P t 19Cu36Ni45, and Pt 12Cu53Ni35catalysts requiring 61, 67, and 77 mV, respectively, exhibited close potential demands to achieve 10 mA cm-2in 0.5 M KOH compared to the 20 wt% Pt deposited on carbon black (Pt/ C) catalyst, which required 46 mV to attain a current 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–10189A facile synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys 10179 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
