and Ni atoms into Pt alloys signiﬁcantly alters Pt’s elec- tronic structure via lowering d-band center of Pt concern- ing the Fermi levels [ 55]. Thus, hydrogen adsorption reaction becomes easier on Pt sites due to the downshift ofd-band center. Although Pt tends to adsorb hydrogen, it exhibits limited activity in water dissociation. On the other hand, Cu and Ni initiate water dissociation and serveelectron donor for Pt, as conﬁrmed XPS results. While they play important role in breaking HO -H bonds, they are less efﬁcient for H 2gas production [ 5,56–59]. According to measurements, ternary PtCuNi catalysts displayed superior HER catalytic performance in relation to the Pt/Ccatalyst. Tafel slopes were computed from LSV curves in both alkaline and acidic media to investigate the kinetics of theHER process. The potentials were plotted against the log- arithm of current density, as depicted in Fig. 6C, D. In alkaline conditions, the HER proceeds through either theVolmer–Tafel mechanisms or the Volmer–Heyrovsky mechanisms [ 60–63]. Tafel slopes for the Pt 58Cu15Ni27, Pt19Cu36Ni45,P t 12Cu53Ni35, and Pt 9Cu39Ni52catalysts were determined to be 40, 49, 47, and 51 mV dec-1, respectively, in the 0.5 M KOH solution. These values are notably lower than that of Pt/C (63.8 mV dec-1)[59] and reveal that Pt 9Cu39Ni52,P t 12Cu53Ni35,P t 19Cu36Ni45, and Pt58Cu15Ni27catalysts aligning with the Volmer–Hey- rovsky mechanism (Fig. 6C). Besides, in 0.5 M H 2SO4 (Fig. 6D), Pt 9Cu39Ni52exhibited the smallest Tafel slopes of 40 mV dec-1, while Pt 12Cu53Ni35,P t 19Cu36Ni45, and Pt58Cu15Ni27exhibited Tafel slopes of 64, 66, and 62 mV dec-1, respectively. All ternary catalysts in acidic solution followed the Volmer–Heyrovsky mechanism. Notably, Pt9Cu39Ni52exhibited the smallest Tafel slope of 40 mV dec-1in the acidic medium, but the largest Tafel slope of 51 mV dec-1in the alkaline medium. The variations in Tafel slopes between alkaline and acidic media could beattributed to variations in the rate-limiting step [ 5,54,64]. The mass activity (MA) and speciﬁc activity (SA) were determined by dividing the current by the Pt mass on theelectrode and the electrochemical surface area of Pt, respectively. The MA, SA and ECSA values for ternary PtCuNi samples in 0.5 M H 2SO4and 0.5 M KOH envi- ronments are provided in Table 1. According to Table 1, the Pt 19Cu36Ni45provided the highest MA and SA values of the catalyst in 0.5 M KOH and H 2SO4solutions. The Pt58Cu15Ni27catalyst with 58 wt% Pt loading showed the lowest speciﬁc activity of 1.16 in 0.5 M KOH medium. The SA values of commercial Pt/C have found as 0.5 and2.07 mA cm -2in 0.5 M KOH and 0.5 M H 2SO4media [53]. All of our ternary PtCuNi catalysts exhibit superior speciﬁc activity compared to the commercial Pt/C catalyst. The ECSA values (m2g/C01 Pt) for the HER region werecalculated for all alloy catalysts, presented in Table 1. The Pt12Cu53Ni35exhibits the maximum ECSA value at 13.74 m2g/C01 Ptin the acidic medium, while the Pt 58Cu15Ni27has the highest ECSA value with 36.25 m2g/C01 Ptalkaline medium. The turnover frequency (TOF) values, as shown in Table S5, calculated using current density (100 mV for- ward from onset potential) and ECSA values for the PtCuNi trimetallic catalysts, exhibit clear pH- and com-position-dependent trends, highlighting their electrocat- alytic potential for the HER under different conditions. In alkaline media (0.5 M KOH), TOF values increased from0.771 to 2.934 s -1with increasing Pt content in the structure. In acidic medium (0.5 M H 2SO4), a notable en- hancement in TOF was obtained for Pt 12Cu53Ni35with 4.786 s-1, this improvement is consistent with the faster proton reduction kinetics under acidic conditions, where the availability of H?ions accelerates hydrogen adsorption and evolution [ 65,66]. The observed trend also suggests that the presence of Cu and Ni may induce favorable ligand and strain effects that enhance catalytic performance underacidic conditions [ 67]. The TOF values are lower than those typically reported for commercial Pt/C catalysts, which can range between 0.1 and 1 s -1depending on pH and surface structure [ 65]. The results support the potential of these trimetallic systems as cost-effective alternatives to high-Pt-content catalysts, with performance tunability through careful control of composition and surface morphology. The durability of the Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanocatalyst in both alkaline and acidic solutions was explored by conducting chronoamperometry measurements at a constant voltage of-0.3 V (vs. RHE), as shown in Figs. S4A, B, respectively. The Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanocatalyst maintains following a test duration of 1000 s in alkaline and acidic solutions 79% and38% of initial current density. Li et al. tested stabilities of PtNiCu and commercial Pt/C catalysts in a N 2-saturated 1.0 M KOH medium at 50 mV s-1(vs RHE) and found as 84% and 20%, respectively [ 58]. When compared with Pt58Cu15Ni27nanocatalyst, it exhibited better stability than commercial Pt/C. To determine the interfacial charge transfer dynamics and ion diffusion characteristics of the synthesized elec- trocatalysts during the HER, electrochemical impedancespectroscopy (EIS) measurements were conducted in both 0.5 M KOH and 0.5 M H 2SO4electrolytes. The measure- ments were performed at an applied potential of 0.1 V overa frequency range of 10 5to 0.01 Hz, both before and after chronoamperometry-based stability testing [ 68,69]. The impedance data were ﬁtted using an equivalent circuitmodel (Fig. S9) comprising the solution resistance ( R 1), charge transfer resistance ( R5), constant phase element 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–10189A facile synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys 10181 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
