(modeled by C7/Yo7and its dispersion factor A8) repre- senting the non-ideal double-layer capacitance, and the Warburg element ( W8) accounting for ion diffusion pro- cesses. The corresponding Nyquist plots are presented inFig. S5A, B in 0.5 M KOH and C, D 0.5 M H 2SO4solu- tions, and the impedance spectra were ﬁtted using the equivalent circuit model illustrated in Fig. S9. Theextracted ﬁtting parameters are summarized in Table S6. In the alkaline medium, Pt 58Cu15Ni27exhibited a remarkably low charge transfer resistance ( R5= 5.59 X) after pro- longed HER operation, accompanied by a signiﬁcantly enhanced double-layer capacitance ( C7= 115 mF). This indicates an increased ECSA and improved interfacial conductivity, which are indicative of accelerated HER kinetics. The substantial decrease in R5following operation suggests enhanced catalytic activation and possibly surface restructuring that promotes electron transfer [ 70]. Pt19Cu36Ni45also showed a low post-operation R5value (7.47 X), conﬁrming its efﬁcient HER behavior under alkaline conditions. In the acidic medium, Pt 12Cu53Ni35 and Pt 19Cu36Ni45retained relatively low charge transfer resistances after stability testing (9.36 and 7.54 X, respectively), indicating sustained catalytic activity and good tolerance to acidic corrosion. Conversely,Pt 9Cu39Ni52experienced a dramatic increase in R5(669.1 X) post-operation, which is likely due to surface passiva- tion or the formation of inactive oxide layers that impedeelectron transfer—a known degradation pathway in acidic environments [ 71]. Additionally, the observed trends in W 8 suggest composition-dependent ion diffusion characteris- tics. Lower W8values, particularly after HER operation, reﬂect reduced diffusion impedance and more facile mass transport within the catalyst layer. The behavior of Yo7and its A 8further indicates variations in interfacial roughness and non-ideal capacitive behavior, both of which inﬂuence charge storage and transport dynamics. Higher values ofYo 7andA8[72] generally correspond to improved surface conductivity and better wettability, which can facilitate efﬁcient HER performance [ 73].3.3 OER activity of ternary PtCuNi nanocatalysts both under alkaline and acidic environments We assessed the OER activities of the ternary PtCuNi electrocatalysts under alkaline and acidic environments toevaluate their performance. Figures 5A, B and S3A, B illustrate the occurrence of the Pt-O evolution reaction over the potential range of 0.9–1.8 V (vs. RHE) in 0.5 M KOHand 0.5 M H 2SO4media. The CV curves of the ternary PtCuNi catalysts exhibit slight variations, reﬂecting the diverse electrochemical phenomena occurring on the sur-face of the alloy, which are inﬂuenced by the Pt ratio in parallel to HER results. Moreover, as observed in Fig. 5A, B, Pt 58Cu15Ni27exhibited larger peak areas compared to Pt9Cu39Ni52,P t 12Cu53Ni35, and Pt 19Cu36Ni45nanocata- lysts, potentially due to differences in Pt content and par- ticle size within the structure [ 74]. LSV curves of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys under acidic and alkaline solutions for the OER region are displayed in Fig. 7A, C, respectively. Table 2summarizes the OER performance metrics, including onset potential (V), and potential at a current density of 50 mA cm-2of ternary PtCuNi catalyst under alkaline and acidic solutions. Asseen in Fig. 7A, C and Table 2, the Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalyst provides a low onset potential in both acidic and alkaline electrolyte solutions. The potential at a current density of50 mA cm -2value is observed in between 1.36–1.54 V and 1.44–1.56 V in 0.5 M H 2SO4and 0.5 M KOH solu- tions, respectively. On the other hand, Pt 9Cu39Ni52catalyst also exhibits low onset potential of 1.42 V in acidic elec- trolyte. Similar onset potential of Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalyst (1.36 V vs. RHE) with commercially available RuO 2and IrO 2for OER in 0.5 M H 2SO4was found to be 1.35 and 1.30 V, respectively [ 75]. The Tafel slopes, derived from LSV curves, are pre- sented in Fig. 7B, D in alkaline and acidic media, respec- tively. The Tafel slopes of Pt 9Cu39Ni52in alkaline and acidic media were 60 and 44 mV dec-1, respectively. These ﬁndings highlight the favorable OER performance ofTable 1 The MA and the SA for ternary PtCuNi catalysts in 0.5 M H 2SO 4and 0.5 M KOH media for HER Nanoalloys 0.5 M H 2SO 4 0.5 M KOH MA @ -0.05 V (mA g/C01 Pt)SA @ -0.05 V (mA cm-2)ECSA ðm2g/C01 PtÞMA @ -0.05 V (mA g/C01 Pt)SA @ -0.05 V (mA cm-2)ECSA ðm2g/C01 PtÞ Pt9Cu39Ni52 0.11 3.31 3.41 0.31 4.48 7.01 Pt12Cu53Ni35 0.60 4.36 13.74 0.33 5.67 5.84 Pt19Cu36Ni45 0.70 10.66 6.54 0.38 4.20 9.11 Pt58Cu15Ni27 0.47 4.49 10.48 0.42 1.16 36.25 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–1018910182 I. B. Isik et al. 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
