the ternary PtCuNi alloy catalysts, underscoring the sig- niﬁcance of incorporating Ni into the structure. The Pt58Cu15Ni27catalyst exhibited the lowest Tafel slope of 38 mV dec-1in acidic medium. In comparison, the Tafel slope of commercial Pt/C catalyst are 193.3 mV dec-1in acidic electrolyte [ 76] and 130.2 mV dec-1in alkaline electrolyte [ 77], and ternary PtCuNi alloy catalysts with lower Tafel slopes exhibit good OER activity. Tafel slope of RuO 2and IrO 2in 0.5 M H 2SO4determined to be 90 and 85 mV dec-1for OER, respectively [ 75]. The TOF values of the PtCuNi alloy for OER samples were calculated in both 0.5 M KOH and 0.5 M H 2SO4 electrolytes using current density (100 mV forward from onset potential) and carbon electrode surface area. As shown in Table S5, the highest TOF was observed for Pt9Cu39Ni52with values of 0.027 s-1in KOH and 0.045 s-1in H 2SO4with a lowest Pt content. Other com- positions such as Pt 12Cu53Ni35and Pt 19Cu36Ni45exhibited lower TOFs, with values ranging from 0.008 to 0.026 s-1 in KOH. The Pt 58Cu15Ni27sample showed the lowest OER activity, with TOFs of 0.005 s-1(KOH) and 0.007 s-1 (H2SO4). For comparison, the TOF values of IrO 2- RuO 2@Ru(3:1), Ir 3RuO 2, and IrO 2catalysts have been reported in H 2SO4and calculated as 0.039, 0.023 and 0.017 s-1at 1.55 V (vs. RHE), respectively [ 78]. The TOFvalues compared to IrO 2-RuO 2-based catalysts Pt9Cu39Ni52,P t 12Cu53Ni35, and Pt 19Cu36Ni45catalysts demonstrated similar values in 0.5 M H 2SO4. The chronoamperometry test of all PtCuNi catalysts was conducted to evaluate OER activity at a ﬁxed potential of 1.65 V (vs. RHE) in acidic and alkaline electrolytes. Fig- ure S7A–H demonstrates the normalized current density vstime. The results reveal that the Pt 9Cu39Ni52,P t 12Cu53Ni35, Pt19Cu36Ni45, and Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalysts maintain after a test period of 2000s in 0.5 M KOH solution 43%, 52%,68%, and 42% of initial current density, respectively. The Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalyst retains 44% of its original current density after 1000 s of testing in 0.5 M H 2SO4, whereas the Pt9Cu39Ni52,P t 12Cu53Ni35, and Pt 19Cu36Ni45catalysts in acidic solution show poor stability outcomes. To further elucidate the charge transfer behavior and ion transport characteristics of the Pt–Cu–Ni electrocatalysts toward the OER, EIS measurements were performed after chronoamperometric stability testing in both alkaline(0.5 M KOH) and acidic (0.5 M H 2SO4) electrolytes at an applied potential of 0.1 V (Fig. S8A–D). The extracted ﬁtting parameters are summarized in Table S7, post-sta-bility data in alkaline media revealed that Pt 12Cu53Ni35and Pt58Cu15Ni27retained notably low charge transfer resis- tance values ( R5= 9.05 Xand 20.31 X, respectively), Fig. 7 A ,CLSV curves of ternary PtCuNi electrocatalysts taken at scan rate of 50 mV s-1at room temperature; B,DTafel slopes calculated from LSV curves in alkaline and acidic media for OER, respectively 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–10189A facile synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys 10183 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
