suggesting efﬁcient interfacial electron transfer and enhanced OER kinetics following extended operation [ 79]. These samples also exhibited elevated double-layer capacitance values ( C7= 140.4 and 58.23 lF), which are positively correlated with increased ECSA, implying the exposure of more active catalytic sites and improved electrode/electrolyte interaction [ 80]. In acidic medium, Pt58Cu15Ni27again demonstrated the most favorable per- formance, exhibiting the lowest post-operation charge transfer resistance ( R5= 5.12 X), thereby conﬁrming its robust catalytic activity across a broad pH range. The improved performance in both electrolytes highlights thecompositional synergy between Pt, Cu, and Ni, which may facilitate electronic modulation of surface states and opti- mization of the d-band center, contributing to favorableadsorption energies for OER intermediates. The Warburg impedance values for these two compositions also indi- cated reduced ion diffusion resistance, supporting moreefﬁcient mass transport within the porous electrode struc- ture. Simultaneously, elevated Yo 7and optimal A8values reﬂected enhanced interfacial capacitance and reducedsurface heterogeneity, consistent with improved charge accumulation and conductivity at the interface. Interest- ingly, extremely high R 9values were observed for some catalysts, particularly Pt 12Cu53Ni35in acidic medium (R9= 121.9 M X), potentially indicating the formation of surface oxides or passivating layers that hinder faradaicprocesses [ 81]. These resistive features may arise from Ni or Cu surface segregation and subsequent oxidative degradation in low-pH environments [ 80,82]. Table S8 presents comprehensive data on the structural characteristics, particle dimensions, onset potentials (mV), overpotentials at 10 mA cm -2, and Tafel slope of ternary PtCuNi nanocatalysts in alkaline and acidic media for HER, alongside comparative results from existing literature and our study. Notably, most ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys display a fccstructure, which underscores their structural homogeneity and stability. Morphological attributes signiﬁcantly inﬂuence the catalytic efﬁcacy of nanocatalysts, inﬂuencing key parameterssuch as overpotential, onset potential, and Tafel slope.Speciﬁcally, smaller crystal and particle sizes coupled with larger lattice parameters tend to enhance HER activity [ 83]. Comparison of overpotential values across different ternary PtCuNi catalysts in alkaline medium, summarized inTable S8, the lowest overpotential values are observed in the order: Pt 28Ni72\Pt58Cu15Ni27\Pt25Cu75\Pt/C\ Pt19Cu36Ni45\Pt9Cu4Ni87\Pt12Cu53Ni35\Pt9Cu39Ni52 [10,24,59,84]. Additionally, the lowest overpotential values in the acidic medium are Pt/C \PtNiCu-light \PtNiCu- dark\Pt58Cu15Ni27\Pt19Cu36Ni45\Pt13Cu73Ni14/CNF@CF \ Pt12Cu53Ni35\Pt9Cu39Ni52[10,54]. Evaluating Tafel slopes in H 2SO4, results show close comparison across cat- alysts in Table S8, with the following order: PtNiCu- light\PtNiCu-dark \Pt75Cu17Ni8\Pt/C\Pt13Cu73Ni14/ CNF@CF \Pt9Cu39Ni52\Pt58Cu15Ni27\Pt19Cu36Ni45\ Pt9Cu4Ni87[5,10,54]. The lowest Tafel slope of 40 mV dec-1 for Pt 58Cu15Ni27in 0.5 M KOH and 37 mV dec-1for Pt 25Cu75 in 1 M KOH was obtained, demonstrating Pt 58Cu15Ni27 superior catalytic performance. This catalyst, characterized by a remarkably small particle size of 3.62 nm, demonstrates exceptional efﬁciency in the alkaline environment for the HERregion. For the OER, the Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalyst displays promising performance in alkaline conditions relative to the commercial Pt/C catalyst, evidenced by its minimal overpo-tential value. Among the studied electrocatalysts, Pt 58Cu15Ni27 exhibited the most efﬁcient overall water splitting perfor- mance in both alkaline and acidic media. This superior activity is attributed to its bifunctional electrocatalytic behavior, enabling it to effectively facilitate both the HERand the OER under varying pH conditions. The low onset voltage and high current densities observed in two-elec- trode measurements further conﬁrm its remarkable chargetransfer capability and surface reactivity. Such perfor- mance highlights the synergistic effect of its ternary com- position and optimized surface properties, which enhanceboth catalytic activity and stability. Therefore, Pt 58Cu15Ni27stands out as a highly promising candidate for practical water electrolysis applications, offering a cost-effective and efﬁcient route for sustainable hydrogen production. Relative to the commercial Pt/C catalyst, the superior catalytic performance of Pt 58Cu15Ni27is due to its better chemical stability and the synergistic effects of its com- position. TEM analysis in Fig. S2A–P revealed that whilethe Pt 9Cu39Ni52and Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanocatalysts maintained their morphology and stability after exposure to both alkaline and acidic media, Pt 12Cu53Ni35and Pt 19Cu36Ni45 exhibited changes in particle size and surface decomposi- tion (Note S1). XPS analysis revealed stable oxidation states in the alkaline medium but pronounced oxidativeshifts for Pt and Ni of all nanoalloys in the acidic medium, as shown in Fig. S6 (Note S2). The Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloyTable 2 Summary of OER performance of ternary PtCuNi cat- alysts in 0.5 M H 2SO 4and 0.5 M KOH media Catalysts In 0.5 M H 2SO 4 In 0.5 M KOH Onset Pot. (V)Pot. (V) at 50 mA cm-2Onset Pot. (V)Pot. (V) at 50 mA cm-2 Pt9Cu39Ni52 1.42 1.66 1.51 1.50 Pt12Cu53Ni351.46 1.78 1.57 1.56 Pt19Cu36Ni451.54 1.82 1.52 1.44 Pt58Cu15Ni271.36 1.63 1.53 1.47 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–1018910184 I. B. Isik et al. 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
