was identiﬁed as the most efﬁcient catalyst for electro- chemical hydrogen production, exhibiting the lowest overpotentials of 61 mV in 0.5 M KOH and 62 mV in 0.5 M H 2SO4at 10 mA cm-2, along with low onset potentials of 43 and 51 mV, respectively. These values are close to that of commercial Pt/C (46 mV in KOH), highlighting its superior HER activity. For the OER, thesame catalyst showed low onset potentials of 1.36 V in 0.5 M H 2SO4and 1.44 V in 0.5 M KOH, comparable to commercial RuO 2(1.35 V) and IrO 2(1.30 V). The enhanced HER performance is attributed to Pt as the primary active site for hydrogen adsorption, while theincorporation of Cu and Ni lowers the d-band center of Pt, weakening Pt–H bonding and facilitating hydrogen desorption. Cu and Ni also aid water dissociation, espe-cially in alkaline media, by acting as electron donors and accelerating H–O bond cleavage. For OER, Ni and Cu act as the main active centers, promoting intermediate oxi-dation, while Pt enhances stability and electronic con- ductivity. The synergistic effect of the three metals enables high bifunctional activity in both acidic andalkaline environments. The enhanced catalytic performance of PtCuNi nanoal- loys in both the HER and OER can be attributed to thesynergistic electron transfer among Pt, Cu, and Ni atoms, which optimizes the electronic structure and surface chemistry of the catalyst. The incorporation of Cu and Niinto the Pt lattice modiﬁes the d-band center of Pt, thereby tuning the hydrogen binding energy to an optimal range. This balanced adsorption strength facilitates the Volmerstep (proton adsorption and electron transfer), followed by the Heyrovsky or Tafel steps (hydrogen molecule forma- tion), thus improving the overall HER kinetics. In thissystem, H ?ions preferentially adsorb on Pt sites, while OH-ions interact more favorably with Ni and Cu sites, collectively reducing the energy barrier of the HER pro-cess. The combined electronic effects and structural sta- bilization provided by this ternary alloy system lead to enhanced catalytic activity and durability compared tomonometallic or binary analogs [ 33]. For the OER, Pt contributes by adjusting the electronic structure and oxy- gen intermediate binding energies through alloying with Niand Cu. This modiﬁcation facilitates the adsorption and desorption of key oxygen-containing species (e.g., OH - and O 2) during the reaction. In PtNiCu nanoparticles, Ni and Cu typically serve as the primary active centers for the electrochemical conversion of OH-to O 2, while Pt further tunes the electronic conﬁguration of the catalyst by opti-mizing the d-band center. This enhances the adsorption of critical intermediates such as *OH and *OOH, which are essential to the OER pathway [ 85]. The structural stability imparted by Pt is crucial for maintaining catalyst durabilityduring OER, whereas Ni and Cu predominantly drive the catalytic activity [ 86]. 4 Conclusion We reported a facile synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoal- loys with various compositions, primarily cubic nanocata- lysts, using a one-pot synthesis approach to evaluate their performance towards OER and HER in alkaline and acidic electrolytes. Among the range of catalysts examined, Pt 58Cu15Ni27exhibited superior HER and OER activities attributed to its larger lattice parameter, d(111)-space, and small crystal size. The overpotentials at 10 mA cm-2and onset potentials of Pt 58Cu15Ni27nanoalloys for HER were found to be 62 and 51 mV in acidic conditions and 61 and 43 mV in alkaline conditions, respectively. Tafel slope analysis revealed that the Pt 58Cu15Ni27,P t 19Cu36Ni45, Pt12Cu53Ni35, and Pt 9Cu39Ni52catalysts followed the Volmer–Tafel mechanism under acidic and alkaline elec- trolytes. Enhanced catalytic activity observed inPt 58Cu15Ni27nanocatalysts can be attributed to electron transfer from copper to platinum, enhancing the catalytic potential of Pt within the ternary nanoalloys, as corrobo-rated by XPS analysis. Additionally, chronoamperometry testing demonstrated stable HER activity for the Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalyst in both acidic and alkaline solutions. Additionally, Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalyst exhibited superior OER activity, requiring only 140 mV versus RHE as the mini- mal overpotential to reach a current density of10 mA cm -2in alkaline electrolyte. Notably, in acidic medium, the Pt 58Cu15Ni27catalyst displayed afﬁrmative OER efﬁciency with a Tafel slope of 38 mV dec-1. Comparative EIS results before and after stability tests revealed that Pt 12Cu53Ni35and Pt 58Cu15Ni27maintained low charge transfer resistance and favorable capacitivebehavior, indicating robust interfacial electron transfer and sustained electrochemical activity. In contrast, other com- positions showed signiﬁcant increases in resistance ordecreases in capacitance, suggesting surface degradation or passivation effects under prolonged OER conditions. Acknowledgements This work was ﬁnancially supported by Cukurova University, Adana, Turkey, under Scientiﬁc Research Funding No: FBA-2021-14176. O ¨.M. thanks to the Turkish Academy of Sciences (TUBA) for the ﬁnancial support. Author contributions Ilknur Baldan Isik participated in the inves- tigation, data curation, methodology development, visualization, and contributed to writing—review and editing. Zafer Eroglu contributed to the investigation, visualization, and writing—review and editing.Dogan Kaya and Onder Metin were responsible for conceptualization,funding acquisition, provision of resources, writing—original draft, and writing—review and editing. Faruk Karadag was involved in 1 Rare Met. (2025) 44(12):10172–10189A facile synthesis of ternary PtCuNi nanoalloys 10185 18677185, 2025, 12, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1007/s12598-025-03579-2 by CochraneChina, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
