solutions to meet the sustainable development of hydrogen production [ 6,7]. Numerous semiconductor photocatalysts have been developed for photocatalytic hydrogen production[8e10]. Among them, metal sulﬁde is considered to be the most potential visible-driven photocatalyst due to its narrowband gap and suitable band structure [ 11e14]. Mn xCd1-xS, ZnxCd1-xS, Cu xCd1-xS and other polymetallic sulﬁde solid so- lutions are widely used in photocatalytic hydrogen productiondue to their advantages such as wide optical response,adjustable band edge position, small photo-corrosion andstrong photocarrier separation ability [ 15e18]. Since the ﬁrst reported application of Mn xCd1-xS for pho- tocatalytic hydrogen production in 2010, it has gained much attention for its wide solar spectral response, exceptionalresistance to photo-corrosion and tunable electronic structure[16,19]. Up to now, Mn xCd1-xS has been synthesized in numerous ways, with different synthesis methods and Sprecursors leading to different crystalline phases, morphol-ogies and applications. The hexagonal phase is easily syn-thesized using Thiourea (TA) and L-Cysteine (L-Cys) as S precursors and the cubic phase using Thioacetamide (TAA) asS precursors [ 20]. The external features of Mn xCd1-xS can be basically classiﬁed into several types: nanoparticles [ 21], nanowires [ 22], nanosheets [ 23], hollow spheres [ 24], and ﬂoral spheres [ 25]. Liu et al. synthesized 3D ﬂower-like Mn 0.8Cd0.2S microspheres assembled from nanosheets by a solvothermalmethod assisted by polyethylpyrrolidone [ 23]. It showed good catalytic activity for H 2precipitation and CO 2reduction. Huang et al. prepared Mn xCd1/C0xS( 0/C20x/C201) nanowires in ethylenediamine solvent by solvothermal method [ 22]. The hydrogen formation rate of Mn 0.2Cd0.8S reached 5.88 mmol g/C01h/C01in the sacriﬁcial solution of Na 2SeNa2SO3. Lai et al. synthesized for the ﬁrst time a series of Mn xCd1-xS solid-solution hollow spheres with superior rhodamine b degradation activity using a hydrothermal method assisted by L-glutathione [ 24]. It is well known that the size and morphology of a catalyst is one of the most important factors affecting the catalyticperformance of a semiconductor [ 24,26], and regulating the size of catalyst to promote the charge transport and masstransfer process is one of the effective strategies to improvethe catalytic performance [ 27]. So, controlling the size and morphology of Mn xCd1/C0xS is the key to improving its photo- catalytic activity. Numerous experimental studies haveshown that three-dimensional dendritic structures have morephotoactive sites that absorb more visible light, resulting in the separation of electron and hole pairs, which leads to a signiﬁcant reduction of composite electron-hole pairs andthus higher photocatalytic performance of the catalyst itself[28,29]. Most Mn xCd1/C0xS solid solutions exhibit irregular granular and rod-like morphology to date [ 22,30e32], while their low speciﬁc surface area and susceptibility to photo-corrosion greatly limit their photocatalytic hydrogen precipi-tation activity. Accordingly, it is crucial to develop Mn xCd1-xS with high speciﬁc surface area and photogenerated carrierseparation capability. Synthesis of Mn xCd1-xS with a dendritic 3D dendritic structure is an attractive option. In this work, Mn xCd1-xS photocatalysts with a leaf-shaped 3D dendrimer structure were synthesized by a simple hydro-thermal method without using templating agents andsurfactants. Obtained Mn xCd1-xS shows high speciﬁc surface area and high photogenerated carrier separation performance for photocatalytic hydrogen precipitation. A series of charac-terization techniques were used to systematically charac-terize the physical and chemical properties of Mn xCd1/C0xS with leaf petal three-dimensional dendrite structure. Hydrogenproduction was tested in the presence of Pt co-catalyst andsacriﬁcial agent solution. The highest hydrogen productionrate is 1360 mmolg /C01h/C01, and the AQY at 450 nm is 3.64%. In addition, the catalyst has excellent cyclic stability. In addition,a feasible mechanism of photocatalytic hydrogen productionfrom Mn xCd1/C0xS with leaf-shaped 3D dendrimer structure is proposed. The synthesis method avoids the high cost caused by the use of templating agents while giving Mn xCd1/C0xS a high speciﬁc surface area, which is in line with the concept of greenchemistry. This work provides a feasible strategy for theconstruction of Mn xCd1/C0xS solid solutions with new morphology. 2. Experimental section 2.1. Synthesis of Mn xCd1-xS with leaf-shaped 3D dendrite structure A certain amount of C 4H6MnO 4/C154H2O, 5 mmol CdCl 2$2.5H 2O and 4 mmol CH 4N2S were dispersed in 50 mL of deionized water and then stirred incessantly at room temperature for2 h. The reaction solution was then placed in a Teﬂon high-pressure reactor at 150 /C14C for 15 h. After cooling to room temperature, the solid samples were separated by centrifu-gation, washed several times with deionized water andethanol alternately, and dried at 60 /C14C for 12 h to obtain Mn xCd1-xS with leaf-shaped 3D dendrite structure. Further- more, the amount of manganese acetate tetrahydrate wasadded to meet the ratio of its amount of substance to that ofcadmium chloride as 1:9, 3:7, 5:5, named as LMCS-1, LMCS-2,LMCS-3, respectively. CdS was prepared without the additionof C 4H6MnO 4/C154H2O and MnS was prepared with the addition of 5 mmol C 4H6MnO 4/C154H2O and without the addition of CdCl 2$2.5H 2O when other reaction conditions were kept con- stant. The preparation process is shown in Fig. 1 . 2.2. Photocatalytic test and characterizations More particulars are shown in the supporting information (SI). 3. Results and discussion X-ray diffractometer was used to test the crystal structure of CdS, LMCS-1, LMCS-2, LMCS-3, and the crystal structure and phase analysis results were presented in Fig. 2 . The diffraction peaks of CdS are observed to be consistent with the hexagonalphase of h-CdS (JCPDS 77 e2306) [ 33]. The diffraction patterns of LMCS-1, LMCS-2 and LMCS-3 are similar and consistentwith the diffraction peaks of CdS. As shown in Fig. 2 b, the diffraction peaks are slightly shifted toward higher diffractionangles with the increasing of manganese content added, andthe corresponding crystalline phase gradually changes frominternational journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 988 e997 989
