h-CdS to g-MnS (JCPDS 40 e1289) [ 34]. The XRD diffraction peak is slightly shifted to the higher diffraction angles because the larger Cd2þ(0.97 /C23A) is replaced by the smaller Mn2þ(0.46 /C23A) in the lattice in the Mn xCd1-xS solid solution, leading to theincrease of lattice deformation [ 35]. These results indicate that Mn xCd1-xS with leaf-shaped 3D dendrite structure solid solu- tion has been successfully prepared, rather than a simplemixture of h-CdS and g-MnS [ 36]. During the process of MnS preparation, TA was seriously mineralized, resulting in almostno MnS observed in the XRD pattern, only Mn 3O4(Fig. S1 ). Similar phenomena also appeared in other literature [ 20]. The surface morphologies of CdS, LMCS-1, LMCS-2 and LMCS-3 were observed by SEM and correspond to Fig. 3 aed, respectively. CdS consists of a large number of leaf-shaped structures with 6 e10mm branches in length. When Mn was added to the system to construct Mn xCd1-xS, a three- dimensional dendritic structure appeared, while the leafmorphology remained unchanged. With increasing Mn con-tent, the leaf shape gradually changed to rod-like, but thethree-dimensional dendritic structure remained. In the detaildiagrams of LMCS-2 ( Fig. 3 e and f), clear leaf lines and uneven surfaces can be observed. This structure may provide a largerspeciﬁc surface area and a richer active site. The trans-formation of leaves into rod-like shape in LMCS-3 may be oneof the reasons for the weak photocatalytic performance. TEM images show that the leaves of LMCS-2 are thick in the middle Fig. 1 eSchematic illustration of the formation process over foliated dendrite Mn xCd1-xS. Fig. 2 eXRD of CdS, LMCS-1, LMCS-2, LMCS-3 and standard card of CdS and MnS and details of XRD at 2 q¼24.8/C14, 26.5/C14. Fig. 3 eSEM images of (a) CdS, (b) LMCS-1, (c) LMCS-2, (d) LMCS-3 and (e, f) Detail SEM images of LMCS-2.international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 988 e997 990
