and thin at the edges ( Fig. 4 aec). Furthermore, it can be observed from HRTEM images ( Fig. 4 d) that the clear lattice fringes of LMCS-2 with a spacing of 0.359 nm correspond to(100) crystal planes [ 24], and correspond to diffraction peaks at 2q¼24.84 /C14in XRD images. The STEM-EDX image of each element ( Fig. 4 f) shows that Cd, Mn and S elements are uni- formly distributed on the LMCS-2 surface, indicating that auniform solid solution is formed, which is also consistent withXRD results. The chemical composition and chemical state were investigated by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) mea- surements. The coexistence of Mn, Cd and S elements in LMCS-2 can be seen in Fig. 5 a XPS full spectrum. Fig. 5 bed shows the high-resolution XPS spectra of Cd, Mn and S byLMCS-2. Fig. 5 b shows two characteristic peaks of Mn 2p at 652.60 eV and 637.59 eV, belonging to 2p 1/2and 2p 3/2of Mn2þ [23]. The two peaks centered at 412.08 eV and 405.33 eV in the Cd 3d XPS analysis belong to Cd 3d 3/2and Cd 3d 5/2, respec- tively, indicating the presence of Cd in the form of Cd2þ[16]. In Fig. 5 d, the banding energies of the S 2p peaks were 162.89 eV and 161.69 eV, attributed to the S 2p 1/2and S 2p 3/2signals, respectively, indicating the presence of S in the form of S2/C0in LMCS-2 [ 36]. The light absorption capacity of CdS, LMCS-1, LMCS-2 and LMCS-3 was analyzed by UV evis-DRS. In the UV evisible diffuse reﬂection absorption spectrum ( Fig. 5 a), the absorp- tion side band of Mn xCd1-xS is redshifted and the light ab- sorption capacity is enhanced with the increase of manganeseaddition. The Eg of each catalyst can be obtained by drawing with Tauc formula ( ahn)n¼A( hn-Eg), where A, a, h and v are deﬁned as a constant, optical absorption coefﬁcient, Planck 's constant, and photon frequency, respectively; n is 2 forMn xCd1-xS[33]. The Eg values of CdS, LMCS-1, LMCS-2 and LMCS-3 are 2.25 eV, 2.23 eV, 2.22 eV, 2.21 eV, respectively(Fig. 6 b). The band gap of Mn xCd1-xS with foliated three- dimensional dendrite structure is similar to that in the liter-ature [ 37,38]. It can be seen that Mn xCd1-xS with leaf-shaped 3D dendrite structure have narrower band gaps, whichmeans that the electron transition energy decreases, so pho- tocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction is more likely to occur under the same conditions. The photocatalytic hydrogen production performance of CdS, LMCS-1, LMCS-2 and LMCS-3 loaded 1.5 wt%Pt by lightreductionwas evaluated on the sacriﬁcant solution of 0.1 MNa 2S and 0.3 M Na 2SO3. As can be observed in Fig. 7 a, CdS has a low photocatalytic HER rate of 470 mmolg/C01h/C01. The photo- catalytic activity of HER of Mn xCd1-xS solid solution photo- catalyst with leaf-shaped dendrite structure was signiﬁcantlyenhanced. The photocatalytic HER of LMCS-2 is the highest,reaching 1360 mmolg /C01h/C01, which is 3 times that of CdS. Fig. 7 b shows that hydrogen production augments with the exten- sion of reaction time. In order to investigate the reasons forthe deterioration of the hydrogen production performance ofLMCS-3, BET analysis of the catalyst was carried out and theN 2uptake and desorption curves and pore size distribution of the catalyst are shown in Fig. S2 . It was found that the speciﬁc Fig. 4 e(a, b) TEM images of LMCS-2; (c) Detail diagram of Figure b; (c)HRTEM images of LMCS-2; (d) STEM image and element mapping of Mn, Cd and S over LMCS-2.international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 988 e997 991
