surface area of LMCS-3 (21.24 m2g/C01) was smaller than that of LMCS-2 (25.60 m2g/C01), which may lead to a reduction in hydrogen production active sites and hence hydrogen pro-duction performance ( Table S1 ). In addition, we measured the photocatalytic hydrogen production activity of LMCS-2 indifferent sacriﬁcant solutions ( Fig. S3 ). Under the equal test conditions, the HER of LMCS-2 in 20 vol% lactic acid solution (LA) was 570 mmolg /C01h/C01, and that in 10 vol% triethanolamine solution (TEOA) was 910 mmolg/C01h/C01, both of which were lower than that in sulfur-rich solution. In addition, theapparent quantum yield of the system was measured by UVevisible diffuse reﬂection absorption spectroscopy. As shown in Fig. 7 c, it is worth noting that when LMCS-2 is in Na2SeNa2SO3sacriﬁcial agent with Pt loading of 1.5 wt%, the AQY at 400 nm, 420 nm, 450 nm and 550 nm were 0.94%, 1.69%,3.64% and 2.19%, respectively. To measure the stability of the leaf-shaped 3D dendritic structure Mn xCd1-xS, cyclic experiments were carried out using LMCS-2 as a model catalyst, and the results are shown inFig. 7 d. With the extension of light time and the number of Fig. 6 e(a) UV eVis absorption spectra and (a) Tauc plots of all samples (a:CdS, b:LMCS-1, c:LMCS-2, d:LMCS-3). Fig. 5 eXPS spectra of (a) total spectrum, (b) Mn 2p, (c) Cd 3d, (d) S 2p for LMCS-2.international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 988 e997 992
