cycles, the hydrogen production maintained a steady in- crease, and the activity did not decrease in the four cycles. In addition, XRD patterns after four cycles of experimentsshowed that the lattice and crystalline phase of the LMCS-2catalyst did not change, indicating its strong chemical stabil-ity, and the characteristic peaks of undetected Pt were prob-ably low amount loading Pt ( Fig. S4 ). SEM results revealed that the leaf structure and three-dimensional structure of LMCS-2were preserved intact at the termination of the cyclingexperiment ( Fig. S5 ). TEM and STEM-EDX images showed that the structure of LMCS-2 was well maintained, and Pt particleswere uniformly distributed on the catalyst surface ( Fig. S6 ). SEM and TEM results show that LMCS-2 has a stable micro- structure. In addition, lattice stripes of Pt particles (d¼0.202 nm) on the surface of LMCS-2 can be clearly seen in the HTEM images, corresponding to the (111) face of Pt [ 39], indicating that Pt was deposited on the (102) crystal face ofLMCS-2 ( Fig. S6d ). As listed in Table S2 , the photocatalytic performance of LMCS-2 was compared with that of somepreviously reported sulﬁde photocatalysts. LMCS-2 can pre-sent some advance. The separation ability of photogenerated electrons and holes is one of the crucial factors affecting the performance ofphotocatalytic hydrogen evolution. In this study, the photo- chemical response, electrochemical impedance and photo- luminescence spectra of the catalyst were tested to evaluatethe carrier separation and transport efﬁciency. As can be seenfrom the i-t curve of transient photocurrent response ( Fig. 8 a), the photoelectric response capacity of LMCS-1, LMCS-2 andLMCS-3 are stronger than that of CdS, among them, LMCS-2has the strongest photoelectric response capacity, indicating that LMCS-2 has strong photogenerated carrier separation ability [ 40,41]. The interfacial charge transfer and separation efﬁciency of CdS, LMCS-1, LMCS-2 and LMCS-3 were studiedby EIS experiments. As shown in Fig. 8 b, the Nyquist circle diameter of LMCS-1, LMCS-2 and LMCS-3 are smaller than thatof pure CdS, indicating that the new Mn xCd1-xS solid solution photocatalyst has faster interfacial charge transfer andeffective charge separation [ 42,43]. Among them, the Nyquist radius arc of LMCS-2 is the smallest, indicating that the elec-tron transfer impedance is the weakest and the photo-generated electrons are more likely to diffuse to the catalystsurface. Radiation compounding of electron-hole pairs in semiconductors results in photoluminescence (PL), the in- tensity of which is a function of the carrier compounding rate,with lower intensities indicating that electrons and holes areless likely to compound [ 44,45]. It can be observed from the PL diagram ( Fig. 8 c) that LMCS-2 has the weakest ﬂuorescence response intensity, indicating that LMCS-2 has stronger elec-tron and hole separation ability [ 46,47]. In addition, hydrogen evolution overpotential is a factor affecting the photocatalytichydrogen evolution kinetics, the lower the overpotentialmeans that hydrogen evolution reaction is more likely tooccur [ 48]. The polarization curves of FTO electrodes coated with CdS and Mn xCd1-xS samples are shown in Fig. 8 d. LMCS- 1, LMCS-2 and LMCS-3 had lower overpotential than CdS, andLMCS-2 has the lowest overpotential. Obviously, the LMCS-2sample showed strong electrocatalytic hydrogen evolutionactivity in 0.2 M Na 2SO4electrolyte, and the Ag/AgCl potential was/C01.12 V at the output current of 1.2 mA cm/C02. Obviously, Fig. 7 e(a) Hydrogen production and (b) hydrogen production rate of various photocatalysts; (c) The AQY of LMCS-2; (d) Cyclic tests of hydrogen production performance.international journal of hydrogen energy 51 (2024) 988 e997 993
