Article A general approach to high-entropy metallic nanowire electrocatalysts Yingjun Sun,1,3Wenshu Zhang,1,3Qinghua Zhang,2Yingjie Li,1Lin Gu,2and Shaojun Guo1,4,* SUMMARY High-entropy alloys (HEAs) hold great promise for efﬁcient catalyst discovery in a virtually unlimited compositional space. The control-lable incorporation of multiple metal elements into low-dimensionalnanomaterials with tailored structure merits untold scientiﬁc andapplicable potential; however, it remains a great challenge usingprevious high-temperature synthe tic techniques. Hence, we report a general reduction-diffusion method for constructing a library of atomic-thick Pt-based HEA nanow ires (NWs) with up to ten compo- nents. We have identiﬁed that the initial formation of Pt NWs andthe nucleation rate of other transition metals are the key points informing a uniform HEA single-phase solid solution. Our methodcan be used to synthesize 26 kinds of multimetallic NWs, including17 HEAs. The high-entropy design can introduce severe latticedistortion in NWs, thereby altering the strain distribution and elec-tronic structure, which enables the HEA NWs with speciﬁc compo- nents to exhibit outstanding catalytic performance in hydrogen oxidation reaction and hydrogen evolution reaction. INTRODUCTION Platinum (Pt)-based alloys with tailored structures are promising catalysts for electrochemical energy conversion and storage devices, such as proton exchangemembrane fuel cells, proton exchange membrane water electrolyzers, and metal-air batteries. 1–4The improved catalytic properties of PtM catalyst (M = transition metals) originate from the varied distribution of d-electron in Pt modulated by ligand effect and strain effect.5–8However, the ability to tune the electronic structure of Pt is impeded by the limited compositional scope of M, which makes it difﬁcult to maxi-mize the catalytic performance. 9,10 Multi-metal alloy materials containing three or more elements provide a new oppor- tunity to solve this problem due to their expansive and underexplored compositionalspace as well as the synergistic effects among the metals. 11–13Many experimental and theoretical studies point out that multi-metal alloy can provide a stronger ligandeffect than a single or bimetallic catalyst, thereby reaching a higher activity. 14–16 Remarkably, with the number of elements conﬁned in alloy increasing, the mixingentropy of the system will enlarge, known a s the high-entropy effect, which can form a more stable structure in terms of thermodynamics ( DG mix=DHmix–TDSmix) and kinetics.17–21The high-entropy alloy (HEA), generally composed of at least ﬁve components with a 5%–35% molar ratio for each constituent, often has a morepositive effect on catalytic performance, derived from the interatomic d-band ligand effect or crystal lattice strain effect compared with low-entropy alloy (LEA)materials. 22–25New strategies for metal HEA recently reported are carbon-ther- mal-shock,26fast-moving bed pyrolysis,27microwave heating,28electrodepositionPROGRESS AND POTENTIAL HEAs materials have great promise in catalysis and energy applications. However, currentHEAs nanocrystals are usuallyprepared at high temperatures,which largely restricts thestructure and functionmodulation. Herein, we present ageneral method for constructing a library of atomic-thick HEA NWs up to ten dissimilar elements atlow temperatures. We elucidatethe reduction-diffusion growthmechanism of these HEA single-phase solid solutions, in which theinitial formation of Pt NWs andnucleation rate of other transition metals are pivotal. Using this new method, we synthesized 26 typesof multi-component nanowires,containing 17 types of HEA NWswith desirable compositions. Wedemonstrate that Pt-based HEANWs show obvious latticedistortion for modulating strain distribution and electronic structure, thus leading tooutstanding hydrogen oxidationand hydrogen evolution catalyticp e r f o r m a n c er e l a t i v et ol o w -entropy alloy. Matter 6, 193–205, January 4, 2023 ª2022 Elsevier Inc. 193ll
