method,29and dealloying method,30etc. However, they either need a high temper- ature to overcome the reaction energy barrier or produce relatively large-sizenanomaterials not suitable for catalysis. Moreover, the research on Pt-based HEAnanomaterials is still scarce because of the increased difﬁculties of mixing multipleelements uniformly at the nanoscale. 31,32To the best of our knowledge, searching for a general method to synthesize the ultrathin HEA nanomaterials with a well- deﬁned structure and adjustable composition at low temperatures remains a grand challenge. Herein, we report a general approach to synthesizing a library of Pt-based multime- tallic entropy-controlled nanowires (NWs) with 3–10 components (Pt, Rh, Mo, Ir, Co,Ru, Fe, Ni, Mn, and Cr) by a new reduction-diffusion method. Unlike previous high-temperature processes, our synthetic method can mix multiple elements evenly toform structural and compositional controlled HEA NWs at low temperature (180 /C14C–220/C14C), which relied upon the initial nucleation of atomic-thick Pt NWs in the presence of carbonyl compound and surfactants, and the following reductionand diffusion of other atoms into the pre formed NWs. We demonstrate that the pre- sent method can be extended to synthesize more types of Pt-based HEA NWs withtunable stoichiometric ratios. We found t hat the high-entropy effect, originating from the distorted lattice, affects the ele ctronic structure of Pt, which endows HEA NWs of speciﬁc components with outstanding catalytic performance for hydrogenoxidation reaction (HOR) and hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) catalysis. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION Synthesis and characterization of HEA NWs Pt-based multimetallic NWs (from ternary to denary components) were obtained by a simple reduction-diffusion method. In a typical procedure, a mixture of metal precur-sors (Pt and M, M = Mo, Rh, Ir, Ru, Co, Fe, Ni, Mn, or Cr, among others) with a speciﬁcratio dissolved in oleylamine were added into a pre-ultrasound oleylamine solutioncontaining dodecyl trimethyl ammonium chloride (DTAC) and glucose. After beingcompletely dissolved, the above mixture was further heated at 180 /C14C–220/C14Cf o r 60–90 min, respectively, yielding the multimetallic high-entropy NWs ( Table S1 ). Figures 1 A–1H show high-angle annular dark-ﬁeld scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) mapping images of eight types of Pt-based multime-tallic NWs (from ternary to denary). Large-area TEM and STEM images of these sam-ples are shown in Figures S1–S8 . These multi-metallic alloys all have a feature of the 1D structure. The ultrathin 1D NWs morphology has no obvious change with thenumber of components increasing to 10, except that the diameter of these NWsslightly increases. STEM energy-dispers ive X-ray spectroscopy (STEM-EDS) map- pings at high magniﬁcation of multimetallic NWs show that each of the elements is homogeneously distributed through out the entire NW without any apparent elemental segregation or phase separatio n. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of these NWs demonstrates that all of them have a face-centered-cubic ( fcc) structure, revealing the successful synthesis of single-phase solid solution alloys ( Figure S9 ). The multimetallic nature of these NWs is conﬁrmed by the corresponding STEM- EDS analysis ( Figures S10–S17 ) and inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) ( Table S2 ). It has been recognized that HEA normally con- tains ﬁve or more elements. In all cases, the atomic ratio of each element in these H E AN W s( d e n o t e da sx - H E AN W s ,x=5 ,6 ,7 ,8 ,9 ,1 0 )i sc o n s i s t e n tw i t ht h e deﬁnition of HEA (5%–35%). For example, the atomic ratio of Pt/Mo/Rh/Ir/Ru/Co/Fe/Ni/Mn/Cr in 10-HEA NWs is estimate d to be 18/15/16/11/6/9/6/9/5/5.1School of Materials Science and Engineering, Peking University, Beijing 100871, China 2Beijing National Laboratory for Condensed Matter Physics, Institute of Physics, Chinese Academy of Scie