10-HEA NWs negatively shifts by 0.2 eV ( Figure S23 ), revealing that electrons trans- fer from other elements to Pt in 10-HEA NWs. Formation mechanism of HEA NWs Herein, the elements alloyed with Pt possess different atomic radii (1.24–1.39 A ˚), reduction potential ( /C00.74 to 1.64 V versus the standard hydrogen electrode), and crystal structures ( fccand hcp), creating critical obstac les in forming single-phase solid solution. The overwhelming consensus is that concurrent reduction of all-metalprecursors holds the key to the formation of single-phase solid solutions, otherwiseresulting in phase separation in alloys. 33We challenge this conventional wisdom by conducting a series of temperature/time-dependent experiments to investigate the synthetic mechanism; herein, we chose the most complex 10-HEA NWs as a model. The reaction solution quickly changed to black once the temperature reached 150/C14C. The products collected at different temperatures (150/C14C, 170/C14C, 200/C14C, and 220/C14C) were ﬁrst characterized by TEM ( Figures 3 A–3E) and ICP ( Figure 3 F). TEM images show that some NWs with varying lengths were formed at 150/C14Cw i t ht h e /C2470% Pt Figure 2. Morphology and structure characterization of 3-NWs and 10-HEA NWs (A–C) HAADF-STEM (A), FFT images (B), and cell model (C) of 3-PtRhMo NWs. (D–F) HAADF-STEM (D), FFT images (E), and cell model (F) of 10-HEA NWs.(G) AC-HAADF-STEM image and (H) corresponding strain ( ε) map of 10-HEA NWs taken along the x-[111] direction calculated from (G) by GPA method. (I) XAFS of 3-PtRhMo NWs and 10-HEA NWs.ll 196 Matter 6, 193–205, January 4, 2023Article
