A series of control experiments were further conducted to reveal the synthetic mechanism by tuning the use of Mo(CO) 6,g l u c o s e ,D T A C ,a n do l e y l a m i n e .W e found that the CO in situ produced during the reaction process cannot only act as a strong reductant to promote the nucleation of metal atoms, because Ru,Mn, and Cr metal precursors cannot be reduced in the absence of Mo(CO) 6, but it also works as a structure-directing agent to form 1D nanostructure and inhibits the spreading growth or self-seeding nucleation of other metal atoms (Figures S24 and S25). In addition, both the appropriate amount of glucose ( Fig- ure S26 ), resulting in a proper reduction rate, and the use of DTAC and oleylamine solvent ( Figures S27 and S28) are also the key to forming high-quality HEA NWs with a speciﬁc length. The above results reveal that the formation of HEA NWsconsists of the initial formation of ultrathin Pt NWs and subsequent formation ofuniform multi-metal alloy driven by combining the reduction with the atomic diffu-sion process ( Figure 3 G). A general method for HEA NWs As can be seen, the kinetics of nucleation and growth of Pt NWs is the key to success- fully obtaining HEA NWs at low temperatures. To obtain more types of multi-element alloy, we choose some elements (M) that have the potential to react withPt precursor. After screening ( Figures S29 and S30), all these elements can be divided into two categories: ones that reduce faster (e.g., Cu, Pd, Ag, Au) and theothers that reduce slower (e.g., Rh, Fe, Co, Ni, Mn, Cr, Ir, Ru) than Pt ( Figure 4 A). O b v i o u s l y ,i ti su n a v a i l a b l et oo b t a i nN Ws t r u c t u r ei ft h ee l e m e n tc h o s e nh a sar a p i d nucleation rate. Based on the above analysis, we have created 17 kinds of HEA NWs (composed of Pt, Mo, as well as other elements, theoretically, more than 200) at random by adjustingthe ratio/variety of metal precu rsors and reaction temperature ( Figures 4 Ba n d S31). To conﬁrm the reliability and universality of our synthetic strategy, we randomly select two of them for structural and compositional characterization: 5-PtRuMoIrCo HEA NWs ( Figure S32 ) and 7-PtMoCoFeNiMnCr HEA NWs ( Fig- ure S33 ). STEM-EDS mapping, corresponding STEM-EDS analysis, XRD patterns, and ICP results demonstrate the successful formation of HEA NWs preparedby this method. Our unique reduction-diffusion process offers a general,effective, low-temperature strategy for the design and synthesis of 1D HEA NWs(Table S4 ). HEA NWs for HOR and HER catalysis As a proof of concept, we selected two types of HEA NWs with speciﬁc components as advanced catalysts for the fundamental reactions related to fuel cells and waterelectrolyzers: HOR and HER. Electrochemical energy conversion and storagedevices, such as fuel cells and water electrolyzers, are vital in constructing a low-carbon world. 34,35However, HOR and HER in an alkaline environment are orders of magnitude slower than in the acid,36–38which requires high-loading Pt-based noble metals. Especially, the low resistance to CO poisoning of Pt severely hinderstheir further application. 39–42Therefore, developing highly efﬁcient Pt-based elec- trocatalysts with high CO tolerance is an urgent need for those electrochemical devices. The quinary Pt 30Rh27Mo 17Ir16Ru10HEA NWs (5-HEA NWs) were synthesized through this reduction-diffusion method and further employed as an HOR catalyst (Figures 5 A, 5B, and S34). For comparison, the 3-Pt 71Ru18Mo 11NWs/C (3-PtRuMo NWs), commercial PtRu/C, and commercial Pt/C were also prepared. Figure 5 Cll 198 Matter 6, 193–205, January 4, 2023Article
