shows the polarization curves of different catalysts in 0.1 M KOH with saturated H 2at a rotating speed of 1,600 rpm. The current densities increase faster against the applied potential for the 5-HEA NWs than those of the 3-PtRuMo NWs/C, PtRu/C,and Pt/C catalysts, indicating the higher HOR activity for the HEA catalysts. Fig- ure S35 shows the electrode geometrical area n ormalized kinetic current density (j k) versus the potential through the Butler-Volmer ﬁttings for different catalysts. The value of exchange current ( i0) of 5-HEA NWs/C is estimated to be 1.25 mA, about 1.2, 1.,3 and 2.9 times higher than those of 3-PtRuMo NWs, PtRu/C, and Pt/C. Furthermore, j kand j0were normalized by the loading amount of Pt and Ru (de- noted as jk,m,and j0,m, respectively) to quantitatively compare the HOR activity of each catalyst ( Figure 5 Da n d Table S5 ). As shown in Figure 5 D,j0,mof 5-HEA NWs was calculated to be 0.7 A mg/C01 Pt+Ru with impressive improvement factors of 1.3, 2.8, and 6.4 over 3-PtRuMo NWs/C, PtRu/C, and Pt/C. Furthermore, the jk,mof 5-HEA NWs for HOR can reach 5.8 A mg/C01 Pt+Ru at 50 mV versus reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), 1.4, 4.2, and 16.2 times higher than those of 3-PtRuMo NWs/C,PtRu/C, and Pt/C catalysts, respectively. Figure 4. Expanded synthesis of Pt-based multimetallic NWs (A) The choice of elements that have potential catalytic activity for HEAs. (B) Ratio distribution bars of each element in 26 kinds of multimetal lic Pt-based NWs synthesized in this work.ll Matter 6, 193–205, January 4, 2023 199Article
