Synthesis of PtRhIrRuCoFeNiMnCrMo nanowires (denary HEA NWs, 10-HEA NWs) In a typical synthesis of 10-HEA NWs, Pt(acac) 2(10 mg), Rh(acac) 3(4 mg), Mo(CO) 6 (20 mg), Ir(acac) 3(8 mg), Ru(acac) 3(8 mg), Co(acac) 3(6 mg), Fe(acac) 3(6 mg), Ni(acac) 2(4 mg), Mn(acac) 3(6 mg), Cr(acac) 3(6 mg), and OAm (4 mL) were added into a 20-mL vial and dissolved by sonication. Then, 4 mL of OAm containing 60 mg DTAC and 10 mg glucose was added to the above solution, and the resulting solution was transferred into an oil bath at a tempera-ture of 80 /C14Cf o r5m i n ,h e a t e dt o2 2 0/C14C ,a n dk e p ta tt h a tt e m p e r a t u r ef o r 90 mins. The ﬁnal product was collected by centrifugation and washed with acyclohexane/ethanol mixture (v:v = 1:1) three times. The resulting HEA NWswere redispersed in 5 mL cyclohexane for further use. In a similar syntheticmethod, we extended to synthesize other multimetallic NWs via changing the pre- cursor ratio, reaction temperature, and time. The experimental details are summa- rized in Table S1 . Characterization TEM images were taken on a HITACHI H-7700 at 100 kV. AC-HAADF-STEM and cor- responding elemental analysis were conducted on JEM ARM200F TEM instrumentsat an acceleration voltage of 300 kV. Power XRD patterns were collected usinga PANalytical-XRD instrument equipped with a Cu radiation source ( l= 0.15406 nm) at a voltage of 40 kV and a current of 30 mA. The elemental composi-tions of catalysts were determined by the I CP-OES (Agilent 5110). XPS spectra were carried out on Thermo Scientiﬁc ESCALAB 250 XI, and the binding energies were allcalibrated to C 1s adventitious carbon at 284.8 eV. Electrochemical measurements The as-synthesized nanowires were loaded o n carbon (Ketjen black, KB) before elec- trochemical tests. Taking the 10-HEA NWs/C as an example, the catalyst was pre- pared by mixing 10-HEA NWs with KB previously dispersed in cyclohexane, andfurther sonicated for 1 h. The ﬁnal electrocatalyst was collected by centrifugationand washed with ethanol three times, and then treated with acetic acid to clearthe surfactants. After drying at ambient conditions, the catalyst was dispersed in amixture of isopropanol, ultrapure water, and Naﬁon solution (v/v:v = 1:1:0.04) toget a homogeneous ink (1 mg mL /C01) by ultrasonication. The loading amount of active precious metal of electrocatalysts was determined by ICP-OES, and the re- sults are listed in Table S5 . Electrochemical tests were performed on a CHI 760e electrochemical work- station from Chenhua Instruments with a typical three-electrode system. Theworking electrode was fabricated by dropping the catalyst ink onto a glassy carbonelectrode (5 mm, 0.196 cm 2). A platinum plate and a saturated calomel electrode served as counter and reference electrodes, respectively. All thepotentials were calibrated to an RHE. As for HOR, the polarization curves were carried out in H 2-saturated 0.1 M KOH electrolyte at a scan rate of 5 mV s/C01 and a rotational rate of 1,600 rpm, with 95% iR compensation. The CO anti-poisoning tests were performed in 0.1 M KOH purged with saturated1,000 ppm CO/H 2, and the voltage for the stability test was 0.1 V. The HOR kinetic parameters were determined using the Koutecky-Levich (K-L) equation, asfollows, 1 j=1 jk+1 jd; (Equation 1)ll 202 Matter 6, 193–205, January 4, 2023Article
