Keywords: Dendritic PtFeRhRu nanoalloyPt-M dual siteElectronic effect Water splitting Hydrogen evolution reactionAlkaline media10 mA cm /C02) and Tafel slope (21.1 mV dec/C01). At a potential of /C00.07 V, the mass activity of the PtFeRuRh nanoalloy is 7.04 A mg Pt/C01, it is 6.97 times that of commercial Pt/C. The den- dritic PtFeRuRh nanoalloys exhibit a negligible decrease in activity after 20 h of continuoustesting at 10 mA cm /C02/100 mA cm/C02and 3000 cyclic voltammetry cycles. In a practical application, the cell voltage of a PtFeRhRu ( /C0) || IrO 2(þ) couple is 1.568 V at 10 mA cm/C02with almost 100% faradaic efﬁciency. The turnover frequency of the PtFeRhRu electrocatalyst at70 mV was 78.5 s /C01, which is 11.71 times as large as that of commercial Pt/C (6.7 s/C01). ©2021 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. Introduction Hydrogen (H 2) gas is widely used in proton-exchange mem- brane fuel cells (PEMFCs) and the hydrogenation industry.Currently, H 2is mainly produced for large-scale commercial use via coal gasiﬁcation and steam methane reforming. However, the implementation of these two methods inevi- tably leads to carbon emissions and the production of CO impurities; moreover, both these methods rely on fossil re-sources. CO is highly toxic to Pt- and Pd-based catalysts, whichare the most active catalysts in PEMFCs and the hydrogena-tion industry. Therefore, procedures to get rid of the releasedCO are generally necessary, resulting in increased operationcosts. Clean H 2generation can be achieved by water electrol- ysis viathe electrochemical hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). Generally, HER conducted in an alkaline electrolyte maybe described as follows: H 2þ2OH/C042H2Oþ2e/C0. Further- more, the overall reaction comprises the Tafel, Volmer and/or Heyrovsky microscopic processes (i.e. H 242Hads;H2þOH/C0 4HadsþH2Oþe/C0and/or H adsþOH/C04H2Oþe/C0)[1e6]. Therefore, the adsorption/desorption of H adson the Pt active site is crucial for H 2generation. Another key stage of the described approach involves the electrocatalyst-activatingwater (H 2O) so that the HO eH bond is cleaved; this process is the rate-determining step in alkaline electrolytes, whichcauses H 2formation to be orders of magnitude slower in an alkaline medium when compared with that in an acidic me-dium [ 7e12]. Thus, the rational design of Pt-based electro- catalysts that not only display an excellent ability to activate H 2O but are also characterised by an optimal H ads/OH/C0 ads adsorption ability is highly desirable for the preparation of highly efﬁcient and durable HER electrocatalytic systemsworking in alkaline electrolytes [ 13e16]. Currently, defect engineering and surface modiﬁcation of Pt nanocrystals with transition metals are considered to beeffective strategies to improve the utilisation efﬁciency perunit of Pt; these two strategies do not just create abundantsurface active sites in the catalysts but they also generateadvantageous electronic effects originating from nearbyatoms viaelectron orbital hybridisation at the atomic level and allow the formation of dual active sites that induce syn- ergistic catalytic effects [ 17e31]. However, the use of exotic transition metals may reduce platinum use, alleviating theproblem of the scarcity of Pt resources. Majority of the tran-sition metals (e.g. Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Ru and Rh) may provide sitesthat are more oxophilic than pure Pt [ 32e49]; on such sites,more H 2O molecules would dissociate to generate H adsin alkaline electrolytes, achieving higher HER activity. Despitethe progress achieved by research pioneers, the low utilisationefﬁciency per unit of Pt, high overpotentials and the low durability of Pt-based electrocatalysts still remain obstacles to the large-scale commercialisation of HER systems. In this study, we developed a series of hierarchical archi- tectures constructed from the dendritic-quaternary PtFeRuRh,ternary PtFeRh or PtFeRu and binary PtFe nanoalloys. Subse-quently, we investigated the electrochemical HER perfor-mance in the alkaline electrolyte of these species andcompared the obtained values with that of commercial Pt/C(20 wt%, Johnson Matthey). All obtained Pt-based samplesshowed better HER performance than commercial Pt/C. Spe-ciﬁcally, the quaternary PtFeRuRh nanoalloy exhibited much lower overpotential, much higher values for both mass ac- tivity (MA) and speciﬁc activity (SA) and much longer dura-bility than commercial Pt/C. When coupling commercial IrO 2 with the PtFeRuRh nanoalloy to assemble a water-splittingdevice, evidence indicates that high turnover frequency(TOF) values were achieved as well as nearly 100% faradaicefﬁciency and low cell voltage. Experimental section Materials Chloroplatinic acid hexahydrate (H 2PtCl 6$6H2O, 99%), Iron ace- tylacetonate (Fe (acac) 2, 99.95%), Rhodium (III) chloride hy- drate (99.9%) ，Ruthenium (III) chloride hydrate (99.9%) and n- octylamine (98%) were purchased from Aldrich. Hexadecyltrimethyl ammonium Bromide (CTAB ，99%) was purchased from Aladdin. Ascorbic acid (AA, 99.7%) was obtained fromKermel. 20% Pt/C was purchased from Johnson Matthey. IrO 2 was purchased from Alfa-Aesar. Alkymer alkaline membrane was purchased from EVE Institute of New Energy Technology. Characterizations The morphology were analyzed by a ZEISS sigma ﬁeld- emission SIGMA þX-Max 20 scanning electron microscopy (SEM) system equiped with energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) de-tector at 5 kV and a FEI Tecnai G2 F20 S-Twin high-resolutiontransmission electron microscope (HRTEM). The inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) analysis was performed on iCAP 7200 (Thermo Fisherinternational journal of hydrogen energy 46 (2021) 11573 e11586 11574
