Scientiﬁc). The X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were measured by a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer with Cu K a radiation ( l¼1.5418 /C23A) with graphite monochromator (40 KV, 40 mA). The X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were takenusing a K-Alpha þX-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (Thermo Fisher, USA) with non-monochromatic Al-K aradiation (1486.6 eV) at a power of 150 W. Electrochemical measurements The CHI 760E electrochemical analyzer (Chenhua, Shanghai)was used to perform all electrochemical measurements in aconventional three electrode system at room temperature, inwhich a graphite rod and Hg/HgO (with 1 M KOH) were used asthe counter electrode and the reference electrode, the workingelectrode was a glassy carbon (GC) rotating disk electrode(RDE, diameter: 5 mm, area: 0.196 cm 2; PINE, USA). Polariza- tion curves were obtained using LSV with a scan rate of10 mV s /C01. The HER electrocatalytic performance was measured at room temperature in N 2-purged aqueous elec- trolyte of 1 M KOH without iR compensation in a range of /C00.65 V to þ0.05 V vs. RHE, the loading amounts of Pt were around 9 mgc m/C02. Potential cycles from /C00.2 to 0.1 V at a sweep rate of 100 mV s/C01and the long-term chro- nopotentiometry test performed at 10/100 mA cm/C02in 1.0 M KOH aqueous solution at 1600 rpm at room temperature wereused to evaluate the durability of catalysts. Their electro-chemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was collected at theoverpotential of /C00.05 V in a frequency range from 100 kHz to 10 mHz in 1 M KOH (pH ¼14.0) aqueous electrolyte. Synthesis of the hierarchical-architecture dendritic- quaternary PtFeRuRh, ternary PtFeRh or PtFeRu and binaryPtFe nanoalloys Typically, depending on the nanoalloy to prepare, aqueous solutions of H 2PtCl 6(0.25 mL; 0.1 M) and RhCl 3(0.5 mL; 0.01 M) and/or RuCl 3(0.05 mL; 0.1 M) were added dropwise into 6.2 mL of the n-octylamine solution containing 2.1 mg of Fe (acac) 2, 36.4 mg of CTAB and 18 mg of AA at room temperature undermagnetic stirring for 10-min, followed by 10-min sonication. The mixture thus obtained was placed in a 10-mL Teﬂon-lined stainless-steel autoclave, which was heated at 190 /C14C for 8 h. The products thus obtained were then collected, washed withethanol and subjected to several centrifugation cycles at9000 rpm. The synthesis of binary PtFe nanoalloys wasconsistent with the above procedure in the absence of RuCl 3 and RhCl 3. Results and discussion Structure characterisations The scanning electron microscopy images ( Fig. 1 aand S1a ) indicate that the prepared products were characterised byhierarchical architectures; the products were composed of Pt,Fe, Rh and Ru ( Fig. S1b ) in the following atomic ratios: Pt: Fe: Rh: Ru ¼0.710: 0.161: 0.097: 0.032. In all the cases, four peaks appeared in the compounds’ X-ray diffraction patterns(Fig. 1 b). They were assigned to the (111), (200), (220) and (311) facets of a face-centred cubic ( fcc) structure; notably, these peaks shifted slightly relative to the standard peaks of fccPt (JCPDS-04-0802), indicating that the prepared productsexhibited an alloy structure. In addition, the observedcompressive lattice strain ( Table S1 ) of the PtFeRhRu nano- alloy is expected to weaken the adsorptive strength of thePteH adsbonds and facilitate H 2generation. The characteris- tics of the high-angle annular dark-ﬁeld scanning trans-mission electron microscopy (HAADF eSTEM) images ( Fig. 1 c) of all the products indicate that the hierarchical architecturescomprised dendritic building blocks; importantly, dendritic nanostructures have been proven to be excellent electro- catalysts because of the abundance of surface active sitessuch as low coordination atoms and interfaces [ 50e58]. The line-scanning EDX spectrum of PtFeRhRu ( Fig. 1 d) indicates that the dendritic block comprised Pt, Fe, Rh and Ru, con-ﬁrming that the product was a nanoalloy. EDX element map-ping ( Fig. 1 eei) conﬁrmed that Pt, Fe, Rh and Ru were evenly distributed in the dendritic block, conﬁrming that the den-dritic block is a quaternary PtFeRhRu nanoalloy that, by thealloying effect, would change the coordination environmentof Pt, produce a strong electronic effect at an atomic level and result in the construction of heteroatom dual active sites of the ‘Pt eM( M ¼Fe, Rh or Ru)’ type, improving the electro- catalytic performance of the system. The characteristics of the high-resolution transmission electron microscopic images ( Fig. 2 ) of all the products indi- cate that the dendritic block are formed by the interconnectedsub-8.0 nm grains, resulting in abundant interfaces ( Fig. 2 a) and low coordination atoms. The spacing of lattice fringes(Fig. 2 c) is approximately 0.224 nm that is similar to the (111) lattice spacing of fccPt; notably, the Fourier transform pat- terns further conﬁrmed the mentioned similarity ( Fig. 2 d, inset). The XPS evidence ( Fig. 3 aed) indicates that the near- surface area of the dendritic block comprises Pt, Fe, Rh andRu, leading to an ensemble effect that may enhance theelectrocatalytic performance [ 59,60]. In addition, the metal states of these elements coexist with the oxidation states. Themetal state of Pt is obviously dominant relative to the oxida-tion state of this species, and the binding energy of Pt had apositive movement, indicating that Pt centres had strongelectronic interactions with the nearby exotic atoms. The XPSvalence band spectra ( Fig. S2 ) of Pt conﬁrmed that the d-band centre of Pt had shifted downﬁeld [ 61e64], and the calculated value of the Pt d-band centre positions for the PtFeRhRu alloy was/C04.06 eV, whereas that for Pt black was /C03.77 eV. Thus, the evidence suggests that the Pt eH adinteraction would be weakened in case of the PtFeRhRu alloy with respect to the Ptblack case, accelerating hydrogen generation. The XPS dataalso reﬂect the fact that a large number of dual ‘Pt eM( M ¼Fe, Rh or Ru)’ active sites would be produced near the surface ofthe catalyst. Furthermore, in case of Fe, Rh and Ru, theoxidation states are clearly dominant, which would enhancethe oxophilic effect with H 2O, because of the lone pair elec- trons on the oxygen atom, thereby favouring H 2O dissociation and H 2generation. And the near-surface alloying of the hierarchical-architecture PtFeRhRu nanoalloy causes Pt andthe Fe, Rh or Ru to have the nearest distance, which shouldinternational journal of hydrogen energy 46 (2021) 11573 e11586 11575
