114.4 mA cm/C02, respectively, which are 7.53, 7.65 and 8.30 times as large as the corresponding values measured forcommercial Pt/C (5.48, 8.45 and 13.78 mA cm /C02). The SA values (Table S2 andFig. 4 c) for the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy calculated on the basis of the ECSA obtained in the HClO 4solution/by CO stripping in 0.1 M HClO 4solution were 10.50/11.32, 16.45/17.74 and 29.13/31.40 mA cm/C02at/C00.05,/C00.07 and /C00.1 V versus the RHE, respectively. These values are 11.54/11.79, 11.67/11.83 and 12.72/12.87 times as large as the corresponding parame- ters measured for commercial Pt/C (0.91/0.96, 1.41/1.50 and2.29/2.44 mA cm /C02) under the same overpotential. Such data further reﬂect the contributions of near-surface dual ‘Pt eM (M¼Fe, Rh or Ru)’ active sites because of the alloying effect. The PtFeRhRu nanoalloy displayed MA values of 4.49, 7.04 and12.24 A mg Pt/C01at/C00.05, /C00.07 and /C00.1 V @ RHE, respectively. These values are 2.39, 2.37 and 2.23 times as large as the cor-responding values measured for the PtFeRh nanoalloy (1.88,2.97 and 5.48 A mg Pt/C01), 3.71, 3.70 and 3.84 times as large as those measured for the PtFeRu nanoalloy (1.21, 1.90 and 3.18 A mg Pt/C01), 6.80, 5.72 and 5.36 times as large as those measured for the PtFe nanoalloy (0.66, 1.23 and 2.28 A mg Pt/C01), and 6.90, 6.97 and 7.46 times as large as those measured for Pt/C (0.65, 1.01and 1.64 A mg Pt/C01). The Tafel slope, which quantitatively re- ﬂects the reaction kinetics of HER, was observed to have avalue of 21.1 mV dec /C01for the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy ( Fig. 4 d). In contrast, this parameter had values of 28.7, 36.0, 51.5 and90.9 mV dec/C01for the PtFeRh nanoalloy, the PtFeRu nanoalloy, the PtFe nanoalloy and Pt/C, respectively, indicating fasterreaction kinetics associated with the use of the PtFeRhRunanoalloy [ 23,67,68]. Markovic, Schmickler and Trasatti co- workers had showed that the activity of HER of Ru or Rh wassuperior to that of Fe by experiments or theoretical calculation[69e71], when adding Rh and/or Ru into PtFe to form nano- alloy, the activity was superior to PtFe, which showed a pos- itive effect, was consistent with their studies and further validated their studies. Notably, the exchange current density ( Fig. 4 e) of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy was 1.39 mA cm /C02, which was larger than those measured for the PtFeRh nanoalloy (0.91 mA cm/C02), the PtFeRu nanoalloy (0.82 mA cm/C02), the PtFe nanoalloy (0.79 mA cm/C02) and Pt/C (1.18 mA cm/C02), indicating the supe- rior intrinsic electrocatalytic activity of the PtFeRhRu nano-alloy [ 32,48,72]. The fast HER kinetics associated with the use of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy as catalyst was further veriﬁed byconducting electrochemical impedance spectroscopy mea- surements ( Fig. 4 f); in particular, the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy displayed the lowest value for the charge-transfer resistance(RCT) (13.9 U); this value is 159.2 Ulower than that measured for Pt/C (173.1 U), implying a fast electron transfer in the re- action catalysed by the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy, beneﬁting elec-trocatalytic activity [ 23,73,74]. Fig. 2 e(aed) HRTEM images of the hierarchical-architecture quaternary PtFeRhRu nanoalloys. (The inset in (d) is the Fourier transformation (FT) patterns.).international journal of hydrogen energy 46 (2021) 11573 e11586 11577
