The chronopotentiometric curves recorded at the current density maintained at 10 mA cm/C02(Fig. 5 a) exhibit that the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy shows long-term durability for a 20-hcontinuous testing. The hierarchical-architecture PtFeRhRu,PtFeRh, PtFeRu and PtFe nanoalloys exhibit higher HER dura-bility than Pt/C, indicating that the presence of Fe or Fe alongwith Rh and/or Ru in the near-surface area of the catalyst maynot just enhance the activity of the catalyst but also itsdurability. The quaternary PtFeRhRu nanoalloys exhibit sig-niﬁcant durability. For instance, as can be evinced from Fig. 5 b, for the Pt/C, there has a potential drop of 237 mV after 10 h and 266 mV after 20 h, however, for the PtFeRhRu nano-alloy, there is only 52 mV potential drop after 10 h and 103 mVpotential drop after 20 h. Furthermore, the initial LSV curveand the re-recorded LSV curve after 20 h of the PtFeRhRunanoalloy almost coincide ( Fig. 5 c), while the overpotential at 10 mA cm /C02for the Pt/C increases by about 23 mV. And after 20 h, the MA of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy can remain 93.7%(Fig. 5 d), while that of the Pt/C has a big drop, only remains 62.9%. The results from an accelerated durability testcomprising 3000 cyclic voltammetry (CV) cycles also indicate that the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy is much more durable than Pt/C. After 3000 CV cycles, only a negative shift of 6 mV wasobserved at 10 mA cm /C02for the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy; for Pt/C, the negative shift in potential was 25 mV ( Fig. 5 e). Moreover, the MA of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy decayed only by 0.5%,whereas that of Pt/C decayed by 35.2% ( Fig. 5 f). The decline inMA may be due to the loss of ECSA. As can be evinced from Fig. S8 and Table S3 , the ECSA of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy underwent losses of 11.1% and 17.1% after a 20-h test and 3000CV cycles, respectively, whereas for Pt/C, the correspondinglosses were 48.1% and 30.1%, respectively. In addition, the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy displayed much su- perior durability to commercial Pt/C when being subjected to acurrent density of 100 mA cm /C02for 10 h ( Fig. 6 a). As can be evinced from Fig. 6 b, in the case of Pt/C, an MA loss of 35.1% was observed after 10 h; however, in the case of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy, only an MA loss of 5.2% was observed after 10 h. Furthermore, the initial LSV curve of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloyalmost coincided with the corresponding curve recorded after10 h ( Fig. 6 c); by contrast, in the case of Pt/C, the overpotential at a current density of 10 mA cm /C02increased by about 26.0 mV after 10 h. After 20 h working at 100 mA cm/C02, the MA of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy displayed a value equal to 92.0% of thatmeasured initially. ( Fig. 6 d). After stability tests of 3000 CV cycles, 10 mA cm /C02for 20 h and 100 mA cm/C02for 20 h ( Fig. S9 ), there is no obviously change in the morphology of PtFeRhRunanoalloy, which is still the hierarchical structure. However, as can be seen from the EDX spectra, the atomic ratio of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy had changed due to the electrochemicaldealloying [ 75,76], which may be the reasons for the decrease of catalytic activity. The system comprising the PtFeRhRu ( /C0) || IrO 2(þ) couple delivered a current density of 10 mA cm2when a potential of Fig. 3 eXPS spectra of the dendritic PtFeRhRu nanoalloys. (a) Pt 4f region, (b) Fe 2p region, (c) Rh 3d þPt 4d region, (d) Ru 3dþC 1s region. (The red dashed line in (a) represents the position of the standard pure Pt.). (For interpretation of the references to color in this ﬁgure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)international journal of hydrogen energy 46 (2021) 11573 e11586 11578
