only 1.568 V was applied ( Fig. S10 ), a much more advantageous situation than that of the benchmark Pt/C ( /C0) || IrO 2(þ) couple (1.616 V). The TOF values, calculated based on references[77,78], were then measured to obtain further information on the intrinsic per-site activity based on the ECSA. The TOFvalues of the hierarchical-architecture PtFeRhRu, PtFeRh,PtFeRu and PtFe nanoalloys were consistently higher thanthose of Pt/C at various potentials ( Fig. S11 and Table S4 ). The PtFeRhRu nanoalloy displayed the largest TOF value, i.e. 78.5 s /C01at 70 mV, which is 11.71 times as large as the bench- mark Pt/C (6.7 s/C01), further indicating that the presence of Fe, Rh and/or Ru in the near-surface area of the catalyst canintrinsically enhance the catalytic activity. The overall water-splitting process was performed to evaluate the practicalapplication of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy as catalyst ( Fig. 7 ); the amounts of H 2and O 2obtained based on water drainage were quite consistent with the theoretical values (solid line inFig. 7 f), indicating a nearly 100% FE, which conﬁrms the efﬁ- cient electron utilisation of the hierarchical-architecturedendritic-quaternary PtFeRuRh nanoalloy. Overall, the experimental results detailed above demonstrate that the hierarchical-architecture PtFeRhRu nanoalloy prepared maybe a promising electrocatalyst for industrial H 2production in an alkaline electrolyte. Conclusions A series of hierarchical architectures constructed by dendritic-quaternary PtFeRuRh, ternary PtFeRh or PtFeRu and binary PtFe nanoalloys was successfully fabricated by imple-menting a general liquid-phase method. These nanoalloyswere prepared as potential catalysts for the generation of H 2in KOH solution. The dendritic shape of the building block andalloyed crystal structure caused the hierarchical-architecturequaternary PtFeRuRh nanoalloy to acquire many advanta-geous traits (i.e. abundant surface defects, dual ‘Pt eM( M ¼Fe, Rh or Ru)’ active sites, high oxophilic and electronic effectsand low RCT), which enhanced the HER kinetics and acceler-ated H 2generation. The MA values of the PtFeRhRu nanoalloy at/C00.05//C00.07//C00.1 V were 4.49/7.04/12.24 A mg Pt/C01, indicating a substantial improvement in the utilisation per unit of Pt relative to Pt/C. The PtFeRhRu nanoalloy also displays anexcellent durability compared to commercial Pt/C. In an actual Fig. 7 e(aee) Digital photos of generated H 2and O 2during 1200 s with the interval of 300 s. (f). Experimentally measured H 2 and O 2production versus theoretically calculated quantities.international journal of hydrogen energy 46 (2021) 11573 e11586 11582
