1 A green approach for enhancing the electrocatalytic  activity and stability of NiFe 2O4/CB 1 nanospheres towards hydrogen production 2 3 Tshimangadzo S. Munonde 1,2 , Haitao Zheng 1* , Mphoma S. Matseke 1,2 , Philiswa N. Nomngongo 2,  4 Yi Wang 3, Panagiotis Tsiakaras 4,5,6* 5 1 Energy Centre, Council for Scientific and Industria l Research (CSIR),  6 P.O Box 395, Pretoria, 0001, South Africa. 7 2 Department of Applied Chemistry, University of Joha nnesburg, Doornfontein Campus,  8 P.O Box 17011, Johannesburg, 2028, South Africa. 9 3 School of Chemical Engineering and Technology, Sun Yat-sen University, Zhuhai, 519082, PR China. 10 4 Laboratory of Electrochemical Devices based on Sol id Oxide Proton Electrolytes, 11 Institute of High Temperature Electrochemistry (RAS ), Yekaterinburg 620990, Russia. 12 5 Laboratory of Materials and Devices for Clean Energ y, Ural Federal University, 19 Mira Str., 13 Yekaterinburg 620002, Russia. 14 6 Laboratory of Alternative Energy Conversion Systems , Department of Mechanical Engineering, 15 School of Engineering, University of Thessaly, Pedi on Areos, Volos383 34, Greece. 16 17 Abstract 18 Among the exfoliation processes applied on layered materials, it is the first time to explore the 19 ultrasonic exfoliation in water for improving the c atalytic properties of NiFe 2O4/CB (CB=carbon 20 black) nanospheres towards the electrocatalytic hyd rogen evolution reaction (HER) in acidic media. 21 It is found that after exfoliation, the overpotenti al of HER on NiFe 2O4/CB nanospheres is improved 22 by about 90 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm –2. Moreover, the exfoliated NiFe 2O4/CB 23 nanospheres are not only more stable than the comme rcial Pt/C catalyst, but also they exhibit an 24 overpotential improvement of about 100 mV at 50 mA cm -2, after 6000 CV cycles.  25 It is also found that the ultrasonic process causes  uniformed NiFe 2O4/CB particles, an increase of the 26 electrochemical active sites, enriched Fe 2+  ion and Fe 3+ occupied on  tetrahedral sites on the surface 27 layer of the NiFe 2O4/CB nanospheres, as resulted from the analysis with  XPS, FTIR etc., leading to 28 a higher activity and excellent durability. Further more, the approach also provides new insights on 29 processing  of materials in a green route.   30 Keywords:  Hydrogen evolution reaction; Ultrasonic exfoliation ；NiFe 2O4, Nanospheres; Acid media. 31 *Corresponding authors: hzheng@csir.co.za (H. Zheng ) Tel: +27 12 841 3050; Fax: +27 12 841 2135. 32 tsiak@uth.gr (P. Tsiakaras), Tel.: +30 24210 74065,  Fax: +30 24210 74050. 33 34
