2 1.  Introduction  1 It is widely known that hydrogen production through  the electrochemical reduction of water is a 2 clean process compared to the steam reforming proce ss [1,2]. Hydrogen evolution reaction 3 (HER) takes place in acid or in alkaline media, and  as a fundamental reaction in the water 4 splitting process, requires a large applied overpot ential, causing a low energy conversion 5 efficiency [3,4]. Therefore, it is desirable to dec rease the overpotentials required for HER, as 6 this can be satisfactorily achieved by using an eff icient catalyst such as Pt/C that can operate at 7 nearly zero overpotentials with a significant HER r ate [5]. However, as known, Pt is expensive 8 and scarce in nature, facts that limit its practica bility on a large-scale use in the hydrogen 9 production industry. MoS 2 nanomaterials constitute typical non-noble based m etal catalysts for 10 HER in acidic media [6], which exhibited good HER a ctivity and durability. In the past decade, 11 a series of investigations have been devoted on the  development of new catalytic materials (new 12 preparation methods, doping strategies, etc.) for h ydrogen evolution reaction (catalytic rate and 13 stability enhancement) [5,7,8]. Recently, Nickel ha s shown more applicability than Mo due to its 14 excess abundance than Mo. Indeed, a number of Ni al loys, such as Ni-P (Ni 2P, NiP 2) [9-11], 15 Ni−Mo [12], Ni−Mo/W−P [13], have been thoroughly in vestigated as electrocatalysts for HER. 16 However, in acid media these Ni-based catalysts are  unstable.  17 Spinel ferrites-MFe 2O4 (M = Ni, Co, Cu, etc.) have also attracted remarka ble attention due to 18 their natural ferromagnetism, expandable oxygen lat tice and substitutable metallic sites [14-17]. 19 Amongst these nano-crystalline ferrites, spinel str uctured nickel ferrites (NiFe 2O4) have gained 20 significant interest due to their: i) tuneable stru cture, ii) high electrochemical stability, iii) 21 catalytic behaviour, iv) low cost and v) high abund ance in nature [18,19]. NiFe 2O4/graphene has 22 been reported for HER in acidic medium [20,21].  23 The exfoliation process has been explored for layer ed materials like graphene to accomplish 24 their full potentials [22]. It is believed that exf oliation can improve the thermoelectric efficiency 25 [23], reduce the residual conductance of bulk mater ial [24], boost surface effect and enhance 26
