3 electronic property [25]. However, the reported exf oliation processes involve the time- 1 consuming mechanical processes [26] and environment al sensitivities (chemical exfoliation) 2 [27-29]. Sonochemistry, regarded as green chemistry , is a discipline concerned with the 3 utilization of ultrasonic cavitation to promote che mical reactions and mass transfer. It has been 4 explored in nanomaterials synthesis [30], metal sur face treatment [31], environmental 5 remediation and pollution prevention [32] etc., due  to its potential to shorten reaction cycles, use 6 of inexpensive reagents, and favourable physical co nditions. It is evidently acknowledged that 7 the ultrasonic irradiation of an aqueous based reac tion expedites the chemical reactions and 8 constructs a special reaction field for the develop ment of various materials [33-36]. 9 In this work, we explored an ultrasonic process to exfoliate NiFe 2O4/CB (CB = carbon black) 10 nanospheres in water dispersions. Surprisingly, the  process remarkably improves the HER 11 performance of NiFe 2O4/CB nanospheres in acidic solution.  To the best of  our knowledge, no 12 report has been generated on the ultrasound exfolia tion of NiFe 2O4/carbon black nanospheres, 13 aimed at expanding their electrocatalytic propertie s for HER. 14 2. Experimental section 15 2.1 Synthesis of NiFe 2O4/CB 16 Systematically, 0.8 g Ni(NO 3)2.6H 2O (Sigma-Aldrich) and 1.4 g FeCl 3.6H 2O (Merck, South Africa) 17 were added to a mixture solvent solution of 70 mL e thylene glycol (Merck, South Africa) and 30 18 mL deionised water, and then stirred for 15 min. Af ter the ultrasonication of 250 mg Vulcan XC- 19 72R carbon in 10 mL deionized water, the as-obtaine d carbon black solution was added to the above 20 mentioned solution and then stirred for 30 min. The n, the addition of 3.1 g urea (ACE, South Africa) 21 with a 30 min stirring was to ensure that the preci pitation of the metal salts is achieved. The mixtur e 22 was then heated to 150 ºC for 18 h under stirring o n a stainless steel Teflon-lined autoclave. After 23 filtering and washing the as-obtained precipitate w ith deionised water and ethanol, the product was 24 dried at 60 ºC overnight. The exfoliation process w as done following our earlier method [37]. 25
