4 Typically, NiFe 2O4/CB was dispersed in deionised water and sonicated for 10 min, with the colloid 1 solution filtered and dried at 60 ºC overnight.  2 2.2 Physicochemical characterization 3 The crystal structure, chemical composition and mor phology of the synthesized materials were 4 studied using; Rigaku Ultima IV X-ray diffractomete r with Cu-Kα radiation ( λ = 0.15406 nm, 5 operating at 40 mA and 30 kV), Perkin Elmer spectru m 100 Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) 6 spectrometer (Waltham, MA, USA), SPECS PHOIBOS 150 electron energy analyser (XPS), 7 operated at room temperature, using an Al–K α monochromatized photon source (h ν = 1486.71 8 eV), transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-210 0, JEOL,  Japan) and scanning electron 9 microscopy (SEM, Auriga Cobra FIB FE-SEM, Germany) coupled with an energy dispersive x- 10 ray spectroscopy (EDS). 11 2.3 Electrochemical measurements 12 Electrochemical performance evaluations were done o n a Potentiostat/Galvanostat (AUTOLAB, 13 PGSTAT 302), equipped with a three-electrode electr ochemical cell, employing a platinum rod 14 as a counter electrode, Ag/AgCl as a reference elec trode and the catalyst loaded glassy carbon 15 electrode (GCE) as a working electrode. Prior to lo ading, the GCE (area = 0.0707 cm 2) was 16 polished with alumina powder and cleaned with deion ised water and ethanol. Then, the catalyst 17 ink, prepared by dispersing 5 mg electrocatalyst ma terial on a prepared Nafion based solution 18 (H 2O: 2-propanol: Nafion with ratio 3:1:0.02) in an ul trasonic bath, forming a 2.5 mg ml -1 19 sludge, was loaded (loading ~ 0.2 mg cm -2). Prior to LSV measurements, 30 CV scans were 20 done at 5 mV s -1 to activate the electrocatalysts. All potential qu antities were valuated versus 21 RHE (Reversible Hydrogen Electrode). 22 3. Results and Discussion 23 To evaluate the crystal structure of the prepared m aterials, XRD was analysed on the as- 24 synthesized materials and shown on Figure 1. As seen, the 2 θ values of the XRD patterns on the 25
