11 The deconvolution on the Ni 2p 3/2 and Ni 2p 1/2 orbitals resulted in two peaks with shake-up satell ite 1 peaks (labelled as Sat.) for both materials. The tw o deconvolutions are associated with Ni 2+ ions in 2 the octahedral (B) and tetrahedral (A) sites. The h igh-resolution scan of Fe 2p on both materials is 3 shown in Fig. 7(c). Fe 2p peaks have a significantly split spin orbit  of Fe 2p 3/2  (711 eV)  and Fe 2p 1/2 4 (724.4 eV) with two satellite features. The two orb itals yield the spin-energy separation of 13.2 eV 5 on both materials, which shows the character of Fe.  The same values of the spin-energy separation 6 on Ni 2p or Fe 2p, on both materials, has further i dentified the crystal structure of NiFe 2O4/CB 7 remained after exfoliation.  The Fe 2p 3/2  and Fe 2p 1/2 spectra were deconvoluted to three peaks that  8 can be ascribed to the Fe 2+ , Fe 3+  (B) and Fe 3+ (A) ( Fig. 7(c)), disclosing the inverse spinel structure 9 of NiFe 2O4. The binding energy values associated with tetrahe dral and octahedral coordinated Ni 2+ 10 and Fe 3+ are consistent with the values reported in literatu re [51,52]. Fig. 7(d) shows three oxygen 11 contributions of the deconvoluted O 1s centered app roximately at 530, 532 and 533 eV in both 12 materials. The peaks can be associated with the lat tice oxygen (O l) from Fe-O/Ni-O [53,54], surface 13 labile oxygen O - (O s) [55,56] and O2- from physi- and chemisorbed water [38], respectivel y.  14 All elements’ quantitative analysis from the XPS sp ectra are listed in Table S2 , Table S3  and 15 Table S4 . To feature the effect of exfoliation on the catio ns distributions, the quantitative 16 analysis is summarized in Table 2. If the valence of some Fe ions is +2 instead of + 3, the 17 oxygen vacancy would be created on site A of NiFe 2O4 [57]. From Table 1, it can be seen that 18 after exfoliation, the ratio of Fe 2+ /Fe 3+  ion was increased from 0.28 to 0.35,  which also 19 corresponds to the increased intensity of 420.51 cm-1 band in the FTIR. On the other hand, to 20 balance the deficit of positive charges resulting f rom the reduction of Fe 3+  to Fe 2+ , there is a 21 probability of surface oxygen vacancies being gener ated [58]. Therefore, ultra-sonication boosts 22 the formation of oxygen vacancies at site A. On the  other hand,  the ratios of  Fe 3+ (B)/Fe 3+ (A) and 23 Ni 2+ (B)/Ni 2+ (A) were decreased after exfoliation, as shown in Table 2 . This mean that ultra- 24 sonication enhance the stability of structure of Ni Fe 2O4, since B site cations (Fe-O) are less 25 stable than A site cations (Fe-O, Ni-O).  26
