16 6000 cycles with Pt/C electrocatalysts as a referen ce (Fig. 10 ). After 6000 cycles, both the Exf- 1 NiFe 2O4/CB and commercial Pt/C electrocatalysts afforded s imilar i-V curves as the initial tests. 2 However, It is noteworthy that, the Exf-NiFe 2O4/CB showed a great improvement on the 3 overpotential from 317 to 224 mV at a current densi ty of 50 mA cm -2, whilst the commercial 4 Pt/C catalyst showed a decrease in the  activity wi th the overpotential increasing from 149 to 5 172 mV to achieve a current density of 50 mA cm -2. On the other hand, after 6000 cycles, the 6 limited current density was slightly increased on t he Exf-NiFe 2O4/CB, whilst the commercial 7 Pt/C electrocatalyst had kept same current density before and after 6000 cycles. The stability of 8 Exf-NiFe 2O4/CB nanospheres is much higher than the reported re sults in the literature, with 9 most reported materials showing degraded activity a fter 1000 cycles as compared in Table 3. 10 11 12 Figure 10 . Polarization curves of Exf-NiFe 2O4/CB and Pt/C electrocatalysts in 0.5 M H 2SO 4 before 13 cycling and after 6000 cycles. 14 From above the results of electrochemical performan ce, Exf-NiFe 2O4/CB electrocatalyst showed 15 comparable activity to those reported in the litera ture ( Table 3 ) though the as-synthesized 16 materials still show lower activity than that of Pt /C. The enhanced catalytic activity of 17 NiFe 2O4/CB electrocatalysts after exfoliation can be ascri bed to the ultrasonic cavitation effect. 18 During the ultrasonic process, the contact of the c avitation bubbles with a solid surface led to the 19
