18 materials. However, the ligand effect might be invo lved due to the Fe ions and O ions changes in the 1 surface of the NiFe 2O4 after exfoliation, which possibly alternates the el ectronic structure of the 2 catalyst surface on the exfoliated material. On the  other hand, carbon black also play an important 3 role. Besides improving intrinsic conductivity of N iFe 2O4, Carbon additive has been reported to 4 enhance metal oxides’ OER-activity due to its abili ty to disproportionate hydroperoxide (HOO −) as 5 an OER intermediate.  XAS measurements have identif ied that the processing of materials with 6 carbon induces a change in the material’s electroni c structure [82-84]. 7 4. Conclusions   8 The ultrasonic exfoliation was successfully applied  for the HER catalytic activity improvement of 9 NiFe 2O4/CB nanospheres, prepared using a simple hydrotherm al method without further calcination. 10 The ultrasonic exfoliation resulted in: i) the enha ncement of the amount of active species on the 11 surface of NiFe 2O4/CB nanospheres as confirmed by the increased ECSA,  which stimulated 12 their electrocatalytic activity for HER; and ii) th e broken down of agglomerated particles. In a 13 0.5 M H 2SO 4 aqueous  solution, the Exf-NiFe 2O4/CB (Exf = exfoliated) nanospheres could afford a 14 10 mA cm –2 current density at a relatively low overpotential ( 186 mV); which is lower compared 15 to the overpotential (274 mV) required to reach a 1 0 mA cm –2 current density on the un-exfoliated 16 NiFe 2O4/CB  nanospheres. Furthermore, the Exf-NiFe 2O4/CB  nanospheres demonstrated superior 17 durability, operating for 6000 cycles with improved  overpotential affording a current density of 50 18 mA cm –2 at the expense of 100 mV overpotential. For commerc ial Pt/C catalyst, the activity for 19 HER was decreased by about 20 mV. The ultrasonic me thod for the synthesis and post treatment 20 provide new and reliable possibilities for the deve lopment and processing of materials.  21 