INORGANIC CHEMISTRY FRONTIERS RESEARCH ARTICLE Cite this: Inorg. Chem. Front. , 2024, 11, 8789 Received 1st August 2024, Accepted 27th October 2024 DOI: 10.1039/d4qi01947d rsc.li/frontiers-inorganicA highly active and durable NiMoCuCo catalyst with moderated hydroxide adsorption energy for eﬃcient hydrogen evolution reaction † Hyeonggeun Choi, ‡aSeunghwan Jo, ‡aKi Hoon Shin,aHeeYoung Lim,b Liting Zhang,aKeon Beom Lee,aYoung-Woo Lee *band Jung Inn Sohn *a Nonprecious NiMo composites are promising hydrogen evolution reaction electrocatalysts due to their Pt-like catalytic surfaces. However, because of hydroxide adsorption in alkaline media, the oxidative elution of Mo deteriorates structural and catalytic stability. Herein, a Cu and Co incorporated NiMo com-posite (NiMoCuCo) is prepared as an active and durable hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) electrocatalyst by direct electrochemical deposition. The low electronegativities of Cu and Co e ﬀectively reduce the charge valence state of the metals and the zeta potential of the electrocatalysts, ameliorating surfaceelectronegativity. NiMoCuCo shows a low overpotential of 53 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm −2and a slight overpotential increase of 0.01 mV h−1(1.8%) after a long-term stability test for 100 h at a current density of 100 mA cm−2, outperforming NiMo and NiMoCu. Ex situ analyses demonstrate that NiMoCuCo exhibits a reduced charge valence state of Mo without signi ﬁcant degradation after the long-term stability test. Furthermore, Co in NiMoCuCo acts as the OH adsorption site on behalf of Mo, owing to the reduced surface electronegativity of Mo and strong OH a ﬃnity. This results in a balance between water dis- sociation and HER kinetics of NiMo composites, leading to excellent HER activity and stability. Introduction Hydrogen has attracted great interest as an alternative energy resource to reduce carbon emissions while satisfying the growing energy demand.1–5Electrochemical water electrolysis provides high-purity hydrogen without carbon emissionthrough the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at the cathode, utilizing transition metal-based catalysts. 6–11For practical water electrolyzer systems, Pt-group composites have been widely utilized as highly active and robust HER electrocatalysts.12–14Particularly, in alkaline media, Pt group composites with balanced H and OH adsorption energies caneﬀectively dissociate water molecules, leading to excellent cata- lytic activity toward the alkaline HER. 15–19However, these Pt group composite-based water electrolysis systems entail high production costs due to the scarcity and cost of noble metals.20,21Thus, developing low-cost and e ﬃcient HER elec-trocatalysts is paramount for the large-scale application of electrochemical water electrolysis. Recently, nonprecious NiMo composites have been exten- sively studied as e ﬃcient HER electrocatalysts that are compar- able to the Pt group electrocatalysts.22,23Based on the theore- tical and experimental studies, the NiMo site possesses near-zero H adsorption energy, indicating that such NiMo sites can lead to excellent reaction kinetics that are comparable to those of Pt. 24,25Moreover, Pourbaix decomposition and adsorption- free energy calculations have demonstrated that the Mo site is thermodynamically favored for water dissociation and OH adsorption in alkaline media.26–28Unfortunately, the unba- lanced adsorption energy of reactive OH, which is much stron- ger than that of reactive H, results in excessive OH adsorption on the Mo sites, causing reduced catalytic activity and stabilityby blocking active sites and causing the oxidative elution of Mo. 29,30Therefore, for e ﬃcient alkaline HER, OH adsorption on NiMo composites should be deliberately moderated tobalance hydrogen adsorption energy. Thus far, heteroatoms with low electronegativity have been proposed to modify OH adsorption on the catalytically active site by reducing theaﬃnity to negatively charged OH. 31–33Nevertheless, balancing water dissociation and HER kinetics is still challenging for the practical application of nonprecious HER electrocatalysts dueto the di ﬃculty of balancing the H and OH adsorption.†Electronic supplementary information (ESI) available. See DOI: https://doi.org/ 10.1039/d4qi01947d‡Both authors contributed equally to this work.aDivision of Physics and Semiconductor Science, Dongguk University-Seoul, Seoul 04620, Republic of Korea. E-mail: junginn.sohn@dongguk.edu bDepartment of Energy Engineering, Soonchunhyang University, Asan 31538, Republic of Korea. E-mail: ywlee@sch.ac.kr This journal is © the Partn er Organisations 2024 Inorg. Chem. Front. ,2 0 2 4 , 11,8 7 8 9 –8796 | 8789 Published on 29 October 2024. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 3/29/2026 5:28:07 PM. View Article Online View Journal | View Issue
