Herein, a NiMo composite incorporated with Cu and Co (NiMoCuCo) is proposed and demonstrated as an e ﬃcient alkaline HER electrocatalyst using a direct electrochemicaldeposition method. It is demonstrated that incorporating the low electronegativity of Cu (1.90) and Co (1.88) gradually decreases the charge valence state of the metals and the zetapotential of the electrocatalysts. 32–34It is further revealed that the strong OH a ﬃnity of Co additionally contributes to the HER performance of NiMoCuCo, outperforming the Cu-incor-porated NiMo composite (NiMoCu) and NiMo composite (NiMo). In a 1 M KOH electrolyte, NiMoCuCo requires a low overpotential of 53 mV and shows a Tafel slope of 34.2 mV dec −1 at a current density of 10 mA cm−2, demonstrating the improved HER kinetics. At a high current density of 100 mA cm−2, NiMoCuCo shows long-term stability for 100 h with a slightoverpotential increase of 0.01 mV h −1(1.8%). In contrast, NiMoCu and NiMo show significant overpotential increases of 0.43 mV h−1(27.3%) and 0.97 mV h−1(51.3%), respectively. Ex situanalyses demonstrate that while NiMo and NiMoCu still suﬀer from significant oxidative elution of Mo, Mo in NiMoCuCo shows a reduced valence state without significantelution. The stabilized Mo is attributed to the alteration of the OH adsorption site to Co in place of Mo, resulting in a balance between water dissociation and HER kinetics. Experimental Electrochemical deposition A direct electrochemical deposition was conducted in a two- electrode system using a pair of nickel foam (Ni foam) electro- des to prepare the electrocatalysts. All the Ni foam electrodeswere pretreated in an ultrasonic bath using acetone, 3 M HCl, ethanol, and D.I. water, in turn. At a current density of −50 mA cm −2, metal salts in the prepared solutions were subjected to reductive deposition on the Ni foam surface for 10 min at room temperature. For the preparation of NiMo, NiMoCu, and NiMoCuCo solutions, equivalent amounts of Ni(NO 3)2·6H 2O, Na2MoO 4·2H 2O, Cu(NO 3)2·3H 2O, and Co(NO 3)2·6H 2O were dis- solved in 50 mL of D.I. water to a total amount of 6 mM. The electrochemically deposited Ni foams were dried at 60 °C anddirectly used for the characterization and electrochemical measurements. The NiMo, NiMoCu, and NiMoCuCo loading masses were measured as 1.1, 1.2, and 1.2 mg cm −2, respectively. Characterization Field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, JEOL, JSM-7800F Prime), field emission transmission electron microscopy (FE-TEM, JEOL, JEM-F200), and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) were used to investigate the surfaceand microstructure with elemental distribution. X-ray di ﬀrac- tion patterns of the prepared electrocatalysts were obtained by X-ray di ﬀractometry (XRD, Miniflex 600, RIGAKU). Raman spectroscopy (HEDA, WEVE) was used to identify the Raman signals of the prepared electrocatalysts. XPS and ICP-AES ana-lyses were performed to trace the charge valence state and atomic ratio of the elements in the prepared electrocatalysts. For the ICP-AES analysis, the electrocatalysts were chemicallydissolved into 0.5 M HNO 3solution and diluted with D.I. water to achieve a neutral pH. The zeta potentials of the pre- pared electrocatalysts were measured using a zeta potentialanalyzer (ELSZ2000, OTSUKA). Electrochemical measurements The electrochemical properties were obtained at room temp- erature using an Autolab Potentiostat (Metrohm, PG-STAT302N). Hg/HgO and a carbon rod were used as the reference and counter electrodes. The prepared electrocatalystswere chemically activated in Ar gas-purged 3 M KOH for 5 min and rinsed with D.I. water before being used as working elec- trodes. Commercial PtRu/C loaded on Ni foam was used forcomparative analysis. For the HER measurements, 10 mg of PtRu/C was homogeneously mixed with 950 μL of isopropanol and 50 μL of 5 wt% Nafion solution and cast on the Ni foam and dried at 60 °C. The potential values were obtained at a scan rate of 1 mV s −1and calculated with respect to the revers- ible hydrogen electrode (RHE) using the Nernst equation asfollows: E RHE¼Erefþ0:059/C2pHþE° ref Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was used to measure the solution resistance ( Rs) and charge transfer resis- tance ( Rct) values of the prepared electrocatalysts at −0.1 V versus RHE in the frequency range from 10 kHz to 10 mHz. The obtained solution resistance values were used for the iR-correction of the corresponding polarization curves. Double-layer capacitance ( Cdl) was estimated by measuring the gradient of capacitive current density against scan rates. The obtained Cdlwas used to calculate the electrochemical active surface areas (ECSAs) using the equation below: ECSA ¼Cdl=Cs where Csstands for the capacitance of an atomically smooth surface area in alkaline media, with a value of 0.04 mF cm−2.35 The mass activity and turnover frequency (TOFs) were calcu- lated as follows: Mass activity ¼j=mass TOFs ¼j=2/C2F/C2n where jstands for current, Frefers to the Faraday constant (96 485 C mol−1), and nrefers to the number of active sites (mol). The nvalues of the prepared electrocatalysts were calcu- lated by dividing the loading masses by the molar weight times the atomic ratio. The faradaic e ﬃciency was calculated as follows: Faradaic efficiency ð%Þ¼2/C2F/C2nH2=j/C2t where nH2stands for the evolved H 2gas (moles) using the water displacement method, and trepresents the measured time.Research Article Inorganic Chemistry Frontiers 8790 |Inorg. Chem. Front. ,2 0 2 4 , 11,8 7 8 9 –8796 This journal is © the Partner Organisations 2024 Published on 29 October 2024. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 3/29/2026 5:28:07 PM. View Article Online
