Results and discussion Fig. 1a displays the electrocatalysts prepared by direct electro- chemical deposition on the Ni foam electrode and their surface electronegativities. FE-SEM and FE-TEM measure- ments identified that NiMoCuCo (Fig. 1b and c), NiMoCu, andNiMo (Fig. S1 †) exhibit nanoparticle shapes of approximately 100 nm in size. The equipped EDS confirmed that the metal cations were well dispersed on the Ni foam electrode, indicat-ing the homogeneous deposition of metal cations. High- resolution TEM and fast Fourier transform techniques showed that the nanoparticles possess a disordered atomic arrange-ment without indicating a clear crystal structure (Fig. 1d and S1†). Along with the TEM results, the XRD patterns showed no crystalline phase with the exception of Ni metal (JCPDS #04-0850) belonging to the Ni foam electrode (Fig. 1e). NiMo exhibited three broad Raman signals at around 280, 660, and 820 cm −1, representing the Mo –O stretching modes of MoO 3(Fig. 1f).36,37These Raman signals gradually decreased in NiMoCu and NiMoCuCo, and two di ﬀerent peaks of the oxygen-deficient MoO 3−xat 390 and 890 cm−1gradually increased simultaneously. Furthermore, the ICP-AES and zeta potential analyses confirmed that while the atomic ratio of Mo was constant, the zeta potential gradually decreased from 5.6to−10.9 and −12.2 for NiMo, NiMoCu, and NiMoCuCo, respectively (Fig. 1g). These results indicate that the decrease in the Mo –O bond and zeta potential is attributable to the sub- stitution of low electronegative Cu (1.90) and Co (1.88) for Ni (1.91) in NiMo. The prepared electrocatalysts were subjected to XPS analysis to investigate the surface charge valence. As shown in Fig. 2a, while the intensity of zero-valent Ni 0(852.5 eV) increased, those of Ni2+(855.2 eV) and Ni3+(857.1 eV) decreased in both NiMoCu and NiMoCuCo.22–26Similarly, the low valent Mo0(at 227.0 and 231.0 eV) and Mo4+(at 229.0 and 232.6 eV) increased, as the high valent Mo5+(at 230.5 and 234.0 eV) and Fig. 1 (a) The preparation and physicochemical properties of the electrocatalysts. (b) Field emission scanning electron microscopy (left) and energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (right) images of NiMoCuCo. (c) Field emission transmission electron microscopy (FE-TEM), and (d) high-resolution TEM image of NiMoCuCo. The inset shows a fast Fourier transform image of the same region. (e) X-ray di ﬀraction patterns and (f) Raman spec- troscopy image of the prepared electrocatalysts. (g) Inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectrometry (ICP-AES) and zeta potential result s of the prepared electrocatalysts.Inorganic Chemistry Frontiers Research Article This journal is © the Partn er Organisations 2024 Inorg. Chem. Front. ,2 0 2 4 , 11,8 7 8 9 –8796 | 8791 Published on 29 October 2024. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 3/29/2026 5:28:07 PM. View Article Online
