Mo6+(at 231.6 and 234.9 eV) decreased (Fig. 2b).22–26The quantitative calculation confirmed that the average charge valence states of Ni and Mo were gradually reduced from 1.91and 5.05 for NiMo to 1.68 and 4.26 for NiMoCu, to 1.48 and 3.44 for NiMoCuCo, respectively (Fig. 2c). The Cu 2p and Co 2p XPS spectra demonstrated the existence of Cu and Co withcharge valence states of Cu 0(at 932.2 and 952.0 eV), CuO x(at 934.2 and 953.8 eV), Co0(at 778.0 and 793.6 eV), Co2+(at 781.2 and 796.8 eV), and Co3+(at 779.7 and 794.9 eV), respectively (Fig. S2 †).38–41Furthermore, the O 1s XPS spectra of the pre- pared electrocatalysts exhibited a gradual increase in the inten- sity of electron-deficient O−/O22−species as Cu and Co were incorporated into NiMo (Fig. 2d).42,43These results indicate that Cu and Co reduce the charge valence state of Ni and Mo and increase unpaired electrons in oxygen due to a decrease inelectron transfer from metal to oxygen, enhancing catalytic activity. 42–46 At room temperature, the electrocatalysts were evaluated in 1 M KOH under a three-electrode system composed of the pre- pared electrocatalysts, Hg/HgO, and carbon rods as working, reference, and counter electrodes, respectively. Fig. 3a showsthe polarization curves that reveal the catalytic performance of the prepared electrocatalysts in the HER region. To achieve a current density of 10 mA cm −2, NiMoCuCo required a lower overpotential of 53 mV compared to NiMoCu (116 mV) and NiMo (132 mV). The Tafel slopes were found to be 34.2, 40.4, and 47.5 mV dec−1for NiMoCuCo, NiMoCu, and NiMo, respectively (Fig. 3b). EIS measurements confirmed that Rctof NiMoCuCo (3.1 Ωcm−2) was much lower than that of NiMoCu Fig. 2 X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra of the electro- catalysts in the (a) Ni 2p and (b) Mo 3d regions. (c) Calculated average valence state of Ni and Mo in the prepared electrocatalysts. (d) O 1s XPS spectra of the electrocatalysts. Fig. 3 (a) Polarization curves of the prepared electrocatalysts in the HER region. (b) Tafel plots around the current density of 10 mA cm−2. (c) Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy results of the prepared electrocatalysts. (d) Electrochemical double-layer capacitances and calculate d electrochemical active surface areas (ECSAs) of the prepared electrocatalysts. (e) Polarization curves normalized by the ECSAs of the prepared ele c- trocatalysts. (f) Radar plots with respect to current density, speci ﬁc activity, mass activity, turnover frequency at −0.1 V versus RHE and reciprocal of the charge transfer resistance of the prepared electrocatalysts.Research Article Inorganic Chemistry Frontiers 8792 |Inorg. Chem. Front. ,2 0 2 4 , 11,8 7 8 9 –8796 This journal is © the Partner Organisations 2024 Published on 29 October 2024. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 3/29/2026 5:28:07 PM. View Article Online
