(25.8 Ωcm−2) and NiMo (26.9 Ωcm−2), as shown in Fig. 3c. Furthermore, NiMoCuCo exhibited comparable HER perform- ance to the state-of-the-art commercial PtRu/C and Pt/C withrespect to overpotential (17 and 15 mV), Tafel slope (29.3 and 64.4 mV dec −1), and Rct(1.2 and 1.8 Ωcm−2). These results demonstrate that the surface electronegativity modificationusing low electronegative heteroatoms e ﬀectively enhanced the catalytic performance of the NiMo-based electrocatalysts. The catalytic activity of the prepared electrocatalysts was further estimated using the intrinsic properties. In the non-far- adaic region, cyclic voltammetry curves were obtained at scan rates ranging from 1 to 10 mV s −1(Fig. S3 †). NiMoCuCo exhibi- ted a larger double-layer capacitance (19.5 mF cm−2) compared to NiMoCu (9.3 mF cm−2) and NiMo (6.7 mF cm−2), as shown in Fig. 3d. Using the double-layer capacitance values, the ECSAvalues were determined to be 487.5, 232.5, and 167.5 cm 2for NiMoCuCo, NiMoCu, and NiMo, respectively. Moreover, the polarization curves normalized with the ECSAs demonstratedthat NiMoCuCo possesses a more catalytic active area and intrinsic activity than NiMoCu and NiMo (Fig. 3e). The intrin- sic activity of the prepared electrocatalysts was further evalu-ated with respect to the loading mass and the number of active sites (Fig. S4 †). As shown in Fig. 3f, NiMoCuCo showed superior extrinsic and intrinsic activities compared to NiMoCuand NiMo. To demonstrate the catalytic stability, the prepared electro- catalysts were subjected to chronopotentiometry measure-ments at a constant current density of 100 mA cm −2(Fig. 4a).NiMo showed a short period of catalytic stability with a degra- dation rate of 0.97 mV h−1(51.3%) for 20 h. In contrast, the catalytic stabilities of NiMoCu and NiMoCuCo were main-tained for 100 h with degradation rates of 0.43 (27.3%) and 0.01 (1.8%) mV h −1, respectively. Moreover, it was found that the stability-tested NiMoCuCo exhibited a lower overpotentialincrease of 2 mV at a current density of 100 mA cm −2com- pared to NiMo (115 mV) and NiMoCu (22 mV) (Fig. 4b). The faradaic e ﬃciency of NiMoCuCo was evaluated to be almost 100% (Fig. 4c). Furthermore, NiMoCuCo demonstrated com- parable and superior HER performance and stability to the recently studied electrocatalysts (Table S1 †). The stability-tested electrocatalysts were subjected to post- mortem analyses to demonstrate the structural stability and the reaction mechanism. XRD and FE-TEM analyses confirmedthat the amorphous structure of NiMoCuCo was well-main- tained (Fig. S5 and S6 †). After the long-term stability test, the FE-SEM analysis identified no significant degradation in thesurfaces of NiMoCu and NiMoCuCo (Fig. S7 †). In contrast, NiMo showed degraded porous morphology with a pore size of about 100 nm on the surface of NiMo, which was associatedwith the significant oxidation of Mo (Fig. 4d and S5 †). The ICP-AES and XPS analyses demonstrated that the decreased atomic ratios of Mo were 19.2, 10.3, and 0.7%, while the di ﬀer- ences in the average charge valence state of Mo were +0.36, +0.94, and −1.53 for NiMo, NiMoCu, and NiMoCuCo, respect- ively (Fig. 4e). Given these results, the oxidative elution of Mowas not su ﬃciently suppressed in NiMoCu by simply modify- Fig. 4 (a) Chronopotentiometry results of the prepared electrocatalysts at a current density of 100 mA cm−2. (b) Polarization curves of the prepared electrocatalysts before (solid line) and after (dashed line) the long-term stability test. (c) Calculated evolved H 2moles and faradaic e ﬃciency. (d) XPS spectra of the long-term stability tested electrocatalysts in the Mo 3d region. (e) ICP-AES results and calculated average valence state of Mo before and after the long-term stability test. (f) Schematic representations of the water dissociation and hydrogen evolution reactions in NiMoCuCo.Inorganic Chemistry Frontiers Research Article This journal is © the Partn er Organisations 2024 Inorg. Chem. Front. ,2 0 2 4 , 11,8 7 8 9 –8796 | 8793 Published on 29 October 2024. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 3/29/2026 5:28:07 PM. View Article Online
