ing the surface electronegativity. On the other hand, NiMoCuCo e ﬀectively stabilized the surface Mo. The XPS spectra confirmed no significant di ﬀerences in the charge valence states in the Ni 2p and Cu 2p regions (Fig. S8 †). Meanwhile, the charge valence state of Co3+was dominant in the long-term stability-tested NiMoCuCo, indicating that thecharge valence state is increased. Furthermore, ex situ Raman spectroscopy analysis demonstrated that while Mo –O bond Raman signals disappeared, Raman peaks appeared around580 and 700 cm −1, corresponding to Co –O bonds (Fig. S9 †).34 These results demonstrate that the OH adsorption site is altered to Co on behalf of Mo due to the synergistic e ﬀect of the surface electronegativity modification and the strong OH aﬃnity of Co. The incorporation of Co acting as an OH adsorp- tion site balances water dissociation and HER kinetics overNiMoCuCo, which facilitates e ﬃcient HER (Fig. 4f). Conclusions In this work, a Cu and Co-incorporated NiMo composite was prepared using direct electrochemical deposition for e ﬃcient alkaline HER. Owing to the low electronegativities of Cu andCo, the charge valence states of the metals and the zeta poten- tial were gradually reduced. NiMoCuCo required a low overpo- tential of 53 mV and a low Tafel slope of 34.2 mV dec −1at a current density of 10 mA cm−2in 1 M KOH. Furthermore, NiMoCuCo showed a low degradation rate of 0.01 mV h−1 (1.8%) after a long-term stability test for 100 h at a current density of 100 mA cm−2, outperforming NiMo (0.97 mV h−1, 51.3%) and NiMoCu (0.43 mV h−1, 27.3%). The ex situ analyses demonstrated that incorporating Cu does not su ﬃciently sup- press the oxidative elution of Mo unless Co is incorporated simultaneously. After the long-term stability test, NiMoCuCo exhibited a reduced charge valence state of Mo without signifi-cant changes in morphology and atomic configuration. Meanwhile, the Co in NiMoCuCo showed an oxidized charge valence state, which is attributed to the adsorbed OH duringthe alkaline HER. The OH adsorption site alteration resulted in a balance between water dissociation and HER kinetics, which led to excellent HER activity and the stability ofNiMoCuCo. Our findings provide insight into a novel strategy for elaborating catalytically active sites by modulating the surface electronegativity for the e ﬃcient and stable alkaline HER. Author contributions H. Choi: investigation and formal analysis; S. Jo: investigation,methodology, and writing –original draft, review & editing; K. H. Shin: investigation; H. Lim: investigation; L. Zhang: formal analysis; K. B. Lee: formal analysis; Y.-W. Lee: formalanalysis and supervision; and J. I. Sohn: conceptualization, supervision, review and editing.Data availability The data supporting this article have been included as part of the ESI. † Conﬂicts of interest There are no conflicts to declare. Acknowledgements This research was supported by the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) funded by the Ministry of Scienceand ICT (RS-2024-00344256). It was also supported by the Nano & Material Technology Development Program through the National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) funded bythe Ministry of Science and ICT (RS-2024-00446825). This research was also supported by the Soonchunhyang University Research Fund. References 1 W. Ye, Y. Yang, M. Arif, S. Yang, X. Fang, M. A. Mushtaq, X. Chen and D. Yan, Fe, Mo-N/C hollow porous nitrogen-doped carbon nanorods as an e ﬃcient electrocatalyst for N 2reduction reaction, ACS Sustainable Chem. Eng. , 2020, 8(42), 15946 –15952. 2 M. Yue, H. Lambert, E. Pahon, R. Roche, S. Jemei and D. Hissel, Hydrogen energy systems: A critical review of technologies, applications, trends and challenges,Renewable Sustainable Energy Rev. , 2021, 146, 111180. 3 M. G. Rasul, M. A. Hazrat, M. A. Sattar, M. I. Jahirul and M. J. Shearer, The future of hydrogen: Challenges on pro-duction, storage and applications, Energy Convers. Manage. , 2022, 272, 116326. 4 