we used a thin wire electrode 30 μm in diameter. Two peaks of voltammograms reported so far at highly acidic concentrations correspond to the voltammograms in Figs. 1ao r 1b. There is no effect of chloride ion on the redox reactions, as shown for the dotted wave of Fig. 1in 0.5 M KCl solution. Figure 2shows voltammograms at the Pt wire electrode for several scan rates. Since the maximum current at the wire electrode atv<8Vs−1was less than 10 μA in magnitude, deformation of the voltammograms by IRdrop can be neglected. There appeared two reduction waves at v>1Vs−1and one oxidation wave for any scan rate. The ﬁrst reduction wave was vague for v<0.2 V s−1, but was recognized for v>1Vs−1. The second wave was drawn out as with a diffusion tail. In contrast, the oxidation wave exhibited a peak, and then attenuated with the voltage. A voltammetric feature can be speci ﬁed by a plot of the peak potentials, Ep, against logarithmic scan rates, as shown in Fig. 3. Values of Epboth for the reduction of the oxidation waves keep constant for v<2Vs−1, suggesting the absence of kinetic complication. No peak of the ﬁrst reduction wave (b) was found forv<1Vs−1.Epof the second reduction peaks (a) was shifted linearly with log vforv>3Vs−1, exhibiting the slope −60 mV, which will be discussed in the context of the negative capacitance later. The shift of the oxidation (c) for v>2Vs−1is caused by the participation in the reaction relevant to the ﬁrst reduction wave. Conﬁrmation of the rate-determining step needs quantitative analysis of variations of peak currents with the scan rates. We denote the reduction peak currents at the ﬁrst and the second wave as Ip1andIp2, respectively. Figure 4shows the dependence of Ip2(disk) at the 1.6 mm disk electrode on v1/2for slow scan rates. The peak currents (a) were proportional to v1/2forv<0.15 V s−1, indicating a diffusion-controlled step. The slope was consistent with the value of 0.465 F3/2c*(D/RT)1/2for the diffusion current of the one-electron transfer reaction at D=9.3×10–5cm2s−1,a s predicted from reaction 1per H+. The lower deviation from the proportionality at v>0.15 V s−1may be ascribed to a contribution of the negative capacitance,26,27which diminished Ip2(disk) at v=1 Vs−1by 40%. Figure 5shows the variation of Ip1(wire) with v, exhibiting the proportionality. Therefore, the ﬁrst wave should be attributed to the reduction of adsorbed species. Since the peak current was indepen- dent of concentrations of HCl more than 1 mM, it may be caused by the saturated adsorbate. On the assumption of the absence ofinteraction among the adsorbate, the peak current density is expressed by j p=F2Γv/4RT, where Γis the surface concentration. The proportional constant of the plot of jpvsvgives Γ=1.2×10–9 mol cm−2or (0.3 8nm)2per adsorbate, which is close to the densityfor a monolayer adsorption. Since this value includes the charge by the negative capacitance as has been observed for adsorbed speciesFigure 1. Voltammograms of 1.0 mM HCl +0.5 M KCl solution at the Pt disk electrode 1.6 mm in diameter for v=(a) 0.01, (b) 0.1, (c) 0.5 and (d) 1.0 Vs−1. The dotted wave is in 0.5 M KCl solution at v=1.0 V s−1. Figure 2. Voltammograms of 1.37 mM HCl +0.5 M KCl solution at the Pt wire electrode for v=(a) 0.1, (b) 2, (c) 4, (d) 6 and (e) 8 V s−1. Figure 3. Scan rate dependence of the peak potentials of (b) the ﬁrst and (a) the second reduction waves and (c) the oxidation wave at the 30 μm wire electrode. Figure 4. Variations of the peak currents, Ip2, at (a) the 1.6 mm disk and (b) the 30 μm wire electrode with v1/2in solution of 1.37 mM HCl.Journal of The Electrochemical Society , 2022 169036510
