DLCs as if a conventional DLC would take a negative value. A hydrogen ion is accompanied with a counterion to form Prto keep electric neutrality, and reach the electrode just before the reduction(C). Since the P ris extinguished after the reduction (D), the difference in the two ﬁelds is kPr(k=1/εo), which is the same as that between (A) and (B). ThePris exhibited as a result of the charge transferred to the solution in order to make the energetic gradient smooth. The smoothing is brought about generally by entropic dissipation. Therefore, the suppression of faradaic currents by the negative capacitance is based on the thermodynamic principle. Since kPr includes the electrode potential, its value may be proportional to log(cb) as well as the logarithmic reduction current, as is predicted from the Nernst equation. Then, the effective ﬁeld,E2-kPs-Pr,i n Fig. 7C has been observed to be shifted logarithmically with vin Fig.3a. In order to con ﬁrm whether Crxis really brought about by reaction 1, we evaluated Crxfor several values of cbat the wire electrode. Voltammetric features were almost the same as in Figs. 1 and2. Variations of Ip2with v1/2were similar to those in Figs. 4and 6. Figure 8shows dependence of Crxthus obtained on cb. The proportionality of the plot indicates that Crxshould be caused by the reduction of H+. This behavior has been found also in (ferrocenyl- methyl)trimethyl ammonium (abbreviated as FcTMA),26as shown in Fig. 8. The twice the value of the slope for H+may be ascribed to the difference in the intensity of the dipoles of H+-Cl−and Fc+-Cl−. Since the negative capacitance is brought about by the charge transfer reaction, it has no direct relation with a degree of adsorption. The slope value, 150 μFc m−2mM (H+)−1, is much larger than values of double layer capacitance (ca. 30 μFc m−2). Because the units in the comparison are different, it is meaningless to compare only the values. The hydrogen bond exhibits the energy ten times larger than the ﬁeld-orientation energy of water dipoles in double layers. Therefore, only a small part of water dipoles can participatein the double layer capacitance. In contrast, all the reacting hydrogen ions on the electrode cause the negative capacitance with minor effect of the hydrogen bond. The negative capacitance resembles a pseudocapacitance formally in that it is coupled with faradaic reactions. Since the capacitive current suppresses the faradaiccurrents, it is useless in development of energy storage. There are some reports 29,30on negative capacitance relevant to double layers. A necessary condition of a negative capacitance is anenhancement of applied electric ﬁeld rather than relaxation of the ﬁeld. Since adsorption by electrostatic interaction works as the relaxation, it does not generate any negative capacitance. Possible sources of the negative capacitance are generation of charge by faradaic reactions, by electromagnetic waves, by adsorption throughchemical bonds, and by turbulence ﬂow.Although Butler-Volmer kinetics also deviates theoretically the linearity of peak currents with v 1/2and provides a linear potential shift with log( v),31it is not valid for real electrochemical reaction.32,33Our experimental results in Figs. 3,6and 8are inconsistent with the behavior complicated by the Butler-Volmer kinetics. It is interesting to examine whether the present data are satis ﬁed with the Heyrovsky step (H ad+H++e−↔H2) or not. The Heyrovsky step is invalid at the ﬁrst reduction potential because H 2 is not yet generated at this potential. If H adis alive at the second reduction potential, H+in solution may react at the electrode to yield H2without inconsistency through the Heyrovsky step. A required condition is to keep the generation of H adthrough reaction 2during the formation of H 2. Since H adtakes high concentration (0.3 M), the Heyrovsky step may occur readily. Therefore, the Heyrovsky step isplausible. The oxidation of H 2is not simple reverse of the reduction of H+, as can be predicted from the asymmetric voltammograms in Figs. 1 and2. The difference in the peak potentials between the oxidation and the reduction in Fig. 3indicates that the oxidation does not occur at a two-electron transfer step.21Since the oxidation peak was almost the same as the potential of the ﬁrst reduction peak for H++e−→ Had, the oxidation step can be deduced to be H ad-Had→Had-Had++ e−. When H ad+gets high enough density for the saturation, it is desorbed to solution, causing further oxidation of left H ad. Since a loss of H ad+is compensated by diffusion of H 2from the solution, the oxidation peak currents were approximately proportional to v1/2at slow scan rates. The peak potential for v>2Vs−1was shifted in the positive direction with an increase in vin Fig. 3. This may be ascribed to kinetics of the desorption relevant to interaction amongH ad+. It is dif ﬁcult to analyze the oxidation voltammograms, partly because concentrations of the reactant, H 2, depend on the reduction process, and partly because a gas state of H 2is ambiguous in the form of either bubbles or the hydrated.21 Conclusions The reduction peak current of H+to H 2is composed of the diffusion current proportional to v1/2and of the negative capacitance proportional to v. The observed current is smaller than the diffusion- current by the capacitive contribution. Since the capacitance valuesare proportional to concentration of H+, they should be brought about with the redox reaction. They are twice the negative capacitance values of FcTMA. The negative capacitance causes the potential shift with v. The participation in Crxis not speci ﬁc to the reduction of H+, but is observed with other charge transfer reactions by rapid measure- ments. It obeys the following general rule that a dynamic phenom- enon is necessarily associated with energetic dissipation in the direction of enhancing the entropy. Only the Heyrovsky step of the three suggested steps can be realized voltammetrically. This conclusion has not resulted from ambiguous potential shift complicated by adsorption, but has done from the dependence of the peak currents on the scan rates. The reduction of H+to H 2occurs as the second wave at the one-electron transfer reaction under the diffusion-control through the Heyrovskystep. In contrast, the adsorbed species H ad+is reduced at the potential more positive than the equilibrium potential for H+/H2 because of adsorbed concentrations as high as 0.3 M of H ad+. ORCID Yuanyuan Liu https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0616-8376 Koichi Jeremiah Aoki https://orcid.org/0000-0001-7378-5329 Jingyuan Chen https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5042-6179 References 1. B. E. Logan, B. Hamelers, R. Rozendal, U. Schröder, J. Keller, S. Freguia, P. Aelterman, and K. Rabaey, Environ. Sci. Technol. ,40, 5181 (2006).Figure 8. Variation of Crxwith bulk concentrations of (a) HCl and (b) FcTMA.Journal of The Electrochemical Society , 2022 169036510
