ARTICLE  Journal Name 4 | J. Name ., 2012, 00 , 1-3 This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 20 xx Please do not adjust margins Please do not adjust margins platform for physisorption of a free-base porphyrin  monolayer (e.g. , a 2H-TPyP monolayer) at the solid-liquid interfac e. An Au(111) substrate was used for understanding molecu lar self- assembly of porphyrin/graphene at electrochemical interfaces. 33-35  The reconstruction of the Au(111) surface results in an unique structure, leading to a predic table fashion against applied potentials, 36  which is distinguishable from graphene (or graphite). Chemical vapour deposition (CVD)- grown graphene was transferred onto an Au(111) subs trate and thermally annealed as described in the methods section. Single-layer graphene on Au(111) was successfully o bserved in solution (Fig. 2a, b), where the honeycomb pattern of the hexagonal graphene lattice was  overlapped on the reconstructed stripes of Au(111), which was clearly distinguishable from bare Au(111) and multilayer gr aphene on Au(111) (Fig. S1a, b in the ESI) showing a superlatt ice pattern and  consistent with a previous report. 37  Moiré structures of graphene on Au(111) due to the lattice mismatch of graphene and Au(111) varies with the lattice rotation. 37  Fig. 2c shows the schematic structure of graphene deposited on surfac e- reconstructed Au (111). To observe the adsorption b ehaviour of 2H-TPyP molecules onto single-layer graphene on Au(111), we used several potentials within the range of −0.2 V SCE  ~ 0.65 VSCE . According to previous literature, 33-34  2H-TPyP molecules can form a monolayer at a potential more negative t han the potential at the point of zero charge (pzc) for a s ubstrate in electrolyte solution. For example, the pzc of Au(11 1) is around 0.3 V SCE  in 0.1 M H 2SO 4 solution, which resulted in 2H-TPyP molecules assembling into a monolayer at 0.0 V SCE  and multilayers (or aggregated layers) at 0.5 V SCE .33  When sample potential was held at 0.0 V SCE  during scanning after application of a voltage pulse of 0.5 V SCE  (Fig. 2d), 2H-TPyP molecules assembled into an ordered adlayer on graphene (mark ed with a white arrow) while they aggregated on Au(111) (ma rked with a black arrow). Fig. 2d was imaged a certain region  where graphene was torn to measure the thickness of porph yrin- adsorbed graphene on Au(111) against the rest of th e surface. In the line profile, the apparent thickness of porp hyrin- adsorbed graphene was 0.35 nm from the rest of the surface that could have porphyrin molecules. Considering th e thickness of single-layer graphene (0.34 nm), the l ine profile indicated the formation of an ordered porphyrin lay er on graphene (Fig. S1c in the ESI). The line profile fr om the zoomed-in image of the aggregated region (Fig. S1d in the ESI) was consistent with that in Fig. 2d. Meanwhile, the  aggregated porphyrin molecules on bare Au(111) disappeared aft er application of a 0.65 V SCE  pulse (Fig. S1e in the ESI) that can lift the reconstructed Au(111) and form Au islands (0.25  nm high indicating the step depth of Au(111)), 34  whereas the graphene- covered Au(111) still showed the herringbone patter n. Two possibilities for the disappearance of porphyrin mo lecules can be suggested: (1) the aggregated (disordered) porph yrin molecules may form clusters on Au islands after the  lifting of the reconstructed Au(111); (2) the porphyrin molecu les may be desorbed from the surface after oxidation. 33-34  In Fig. 2e, 2H-TPyP molecules were clearly observed on graphene  at 0.0 VSCE , and the line profile showing the lateral size of 2H-TPyP (approximate 2 nm) suggested a flat-lying assembly,33  where the apparent height of a 2H-TPyP molecule was obser ved by approximate 0.08 nm. Accord ing to the previous paper, 33  the apparent height of a 2H-TPyP monolayer on Au(111) a t the solid-liquid interface was measured by approximate 0.12 −0.15 nm. Considering the experimental condition ( e.g ., tunnelling set point), the cross-sectional line profile throug h the porphyrin adsorbed single-layered graphene sheet in dicated that porphyrin assembled in a monolayer on graphene . 33-34 The monolayer was maintained in the potential windo w from −0.2 V SCE  to 0.5 V SCE , which is the important finding for porphyrin/graphene systems showing the structural s tability against electrocatalytic reactions. From a direct o bservation of 2H-TPyP molecules adsorbed on graphene at the solid -liquid interface (Fig. S2 in the ESI), molecular lines on graphene/Au(111) appeared at 0.2 V SCE  and disappeared 0.55 VSCE , repeatedly. Consequently, graphene-2H-TPyP intera ctions can play a predominant role in the formation of mol ecular layers, and graphene can function as a platform for  the formation of a 2H-TPyP monolayer (consisting of app roximate 2.5 x 10 13  molecules cm -2) at the solid-liquid interface. Page 4 of 12 Nanoscale Nanoscale Accepted Manuscript Published on 23 February 2017. Downloaded by University of Newcastle on 26/02/2017 19:36:11. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C6NR09428G
