ARTICLE  Journal Name 8 | J. Name ., 2012, 00 , 1-3 This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 20 xx Please do not adjust margins Please do not adjust margins electrochemical proton adsorption and the proton at tack to evolve H 2 could occur consecutively such as a heterolytic H 2- evolving process.9,51  Overall results in our system showed that the HER activity increases as the strength of the M- H bond increases,52  corresponding to the electronegativity of metal atoms (Ni = 1.9, Zn = 1.6, Pt = 2.3 for Pauling electronegativity 53 ).49  Pt-porphyrin/graphene showed the best performance towards HER due to its high electronegat ivity that stabilizes the network structures of active si tes. The repeated cycles in a potential range revealed the electrocatalytic stability of a surface-immobilized  Pt- porphyrin/graphene system (Fig. S7 in the ESI). Interfacial charge transfer related with HER activi ty Surface charge separation due to electronegativity differences between nitrogen and carbon atoms at π-π stacked interfaces can influence the electrocatalytic activity of grap hene electrode surfaces. The electrocatalytic activity o f M- porphyrin/graphene electrodes can be attributed to changes in charge density on adjacent atoms due to the formati on of M- N4. Thus, investigations of the charge transfer betwe en porphyrin and graphene (or M-porphyrin and graphene ) are needed. To understand interactive charge transfer a t the interface between the adsorbed porphyrins and the g raphene substrate, the electronic characteristics of graphe ne were systematically investigated by using field effect t ransistor (FET) devices. Fig. 5a-c shows typical changes in transfe r characteristics of graphene FETs for each step of t he sequence process from annealing of pristine graphene, porphy rin ( e.g ., 2H-TPyP, 2H-TAPP, and 2H-TPP) adsorption on graphen e, and metalation of porphyrins with Ni, Zn, and Pt on gra phene. All charge transfer measurements were conducted under a vacuum after a certain stabilized condition such as  long evacuated time over several hours. In the first ste p, pristine graphene samples were treated by rapid thermal anne aling at 200 °C for 30 s. For reliable comparison, we chose bare graphene samples exhibiting minimum current densiti es flowing between drain and source electrodes at a si milar gate voltage ( i.e. , the charge neutrality point or Dirac point), whic h are slightly located at positive gate voltages due to the electropositive (electron acceptor) doping effect i n graphene during transfer of CVD graphene. 54  The value of the charge neutrality point is normally very sensitive to expe rimental environments such as adsorption of water or oxygen,  so that the same batch of graphene FETs was used and charac terized in a vacuum after keeping a vacuum condition over s everal hours. In the second step, adsorption of porphyrin molecules led to electropositive doping of graphene; 2H-TPyP and 2H- TAPP induced large drifts in the charge neutrality point to positive voltages, while 2H-TPP induced relatively small drifts. We believe that there are three factors that contri buted to charge transfer between graphene and the adsorbed porphyrin layer: (1) intermolecular hydrogen bondin g between the substitutes ( e.g. , pyridine groups in 2H-TPyP) of neighbouring porphyrins, (2) lone-pair electrons in  the central N4 units, and (3) electronegativity of the central N 4 units. Page 8 of 12 Nanoscale Nanoscale Accepted Manuscript Published on 23 February 2017. Downloaded by University of Newcastle on 26/02/2017 19:36:11. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C6NR09428G
