A multi-interface SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite for enhanced electrocatalytic water splitting Priyanshu Chaubey , Subhajit Sarkar , Prashant Kr. Sharma* Functional Nanomaterials Research Laboratory, Department of Physics, Indian Institute of Technology (Indian School of Mines), Dhanbad 826004, JH, India ARTICLE INFO Keywords: SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite Coral-shaped nanoflowers Nanocubes Oxygen evolution reaction Hydrogen evolution reaction Overall water splittingABSTRACT In water splitting, the noble metal-based catalysts (IrO 2, RuO 2, Pt, etc.) are being replaced by non-noble, readily available, and cost-effective transition metal-based nanostructures. In the same pursuit, this work presents a novel bifunctional {Hydrogen Evolution Reaction (HER) and Oxygen Evolution Reaction (OER)} SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 multi-interface heterostructure nanocomposite. The SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite, synthesized through facile sonochemical method, has a coupled morphology of coral-shaped nanoflowers modified with nanocubes. The significantly exposed surface area of this nanocomposite and the synergistic interactions among SnO 2, Co3O4, and Mo2S3 were the cause of its interesting surface morphological characteristics and enhanced electrocatalytic activity. For OER and HER, overpotentials of 192.3 mV and 204.98 mV at 10 mA/cm2, and the significantly reduced Tafel slopes of 99 mV/dec and 102.8 mV/dec were obtained in 2.0 M KOH and 1.0 M H2SO4, respectively. This SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite requires a cell voltage of 1.45 V only for water electrolysis at 10 mA/cm2. An extraordinary stability of 72 hours was noted for overall water splitting (OWS) while the current density barely changes. After the OWS, the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite modified electrode showed good stability, opening up novel possibilities for this multi-interface heterostructure SnO 2- Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite in the advancement of commercial energy-producing and energy-storage technologies. 1.Introduction The diminishing availability of fossil fuels, the rising energy con- sumption demands, and the negative environmental implications of using non-renewable resources are some of the major issues we will eventually face in the twenty-first century. Alternative energy sources for addressing energy demands include sustainable energy sources, such as wave, tidal, and solar energies, although their availability is incon - sistent due to geographical and seasonal variations [1,2]. Thus, elec- trochemical water splitting presents an extremely promising technique that relies heavily on the production of highly effective and plentiful electrocatalysts for OWS in order to produce sustainable hydrogen en- ergy on a large scale. Two half-cell reactions, water reduction and water oxidation, are used to process water splitting. Water is oxidised at the anode (OER) and reduced at the cathode (HER) by this process. The schematic illustration of electrolyser design and the reactions that occur at the cathode and anode in different electrolyte media is discussed in the supplementary information. OER kinetics are typically slower due to O-O bond formation and the breaking of -OH bonds. Therefore, in OER process a larger amount of energy (higher overpotential) is required above the theoretical breakdown voltage of water (1.23 V). Enhancing the electrical efficiency for the production of O2 requires efficient OER electrocatalysts [3]. Although the commercially available noble metals Pt/C and RuO 2 have been identified as standard HER and OER catalysts, respectively, their limited availability, high cost, and poor stability at high current densities have prevented their wide usage [3]. Therefore, the development of reliable and reasonably priced electrocatalysts that may successfully substitute noble metals remains necessary. Many non-noble electrocatalysts, including transition metal dichal - cogenides, oxides, nitrides, phosphides, and carbides, have received a great deal of attention as effective electrocatalysts. Mo-based electro - catalysts are considered to be the most promising choice because of their excellent performance and the adjustable structure. Mo-based sulphides, such as MoS 2, are among the most researched Pt substitutes for HER because of their suitable Gibbs free energy (ΔGH*) of 0.08 eV [3,4]. As, the alkaline solutions have a low hydrogen ion concentration, MoS 2 can form a strong bond with -OH, boosting the resistance for water disso - ciation [3]. Therefore, MoS 2 has a number of disadvantages, including *Corresponding author. E-mail address: prashant@iitism.ac.in (P.Kr. Sharma). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Surfaces and Interfaces u{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1�mt ozmont~om�1m{ y2u{�~zkw2 ��~qkmo�/k zn/tz�o~qkm o� https://doi.org/10.1016/j.surfin.2025.107099 Received 23 January 2025; Received in revised form 16 June 2025; Accepted 1 July 2025  Surfaces  and Interfaces  72 (2025)  107099 Available  online  1 July 2025 2468-0230/©  2025  Elsevier  B.V. All rights  are reserved,  including  those  for text and data mining,  AI training,  and similar  technologies.
