poor intrinsic conductivity, inert catalytic activity, fewer active sites at the edge, and extreme sluggishness in alkaline environments [3–8]. On the other hand, Molybdenum sesquisulfide (Mo 2S3) is a quasi-one-dimensional compound of the molybdenum chalcogenide family that has received very little attention as an electrocatalyst for HER [9]. When used as a HER electrocatalyst for practical applications, Mo2S3 outperforms MoS 2 in terms of stability and catalytic activity in acidic environments [4]. Mo2S3 exhibits significantly higher conduc - tivity than MoS 2. In the meantime, compared to pure Mo metal, Mo2S3 has shorter Mo-Mo bonds, which could result in delocalised electrons and electronic states that might be used to adjust H* and OH* adsorption and desorption behaviour and improve HER dynamics in alkaline con- ditions as well [10–14]. Xiaowen Zhou et al., has reported Urchin-shaped Mo2S3 which re- quires an overpotential of 257 mV at 10 mV/cm2 for HER and the calculated Tafel slope was 64 mV/dec [9]. Mo2S3/NiMo 3S4 hetero - structural nanocomposite was synthesized by Tong Wu et al. The required OER/HER overpotential values at respective current densities of 10, 100, and 1000 mA/cm2 were measured as 173 mV/32 mV, 256 mV/124 mV, and 390 mV/174 mV. Also, an excellent OWS electro - catalytic performance was recorded with the cell potential of 1.639 V@500 mA/cm2 and 1.662 V@1000 mA/cm2 with a successful longer durability testing of 100 hours. This makes Mo2S3@NiMo 3S4 among the top non-noble electrocatalysts for electrocatalytic water splitting [10]. Similarly, for OER, Cobalt-based composites show outstanding electro - catalytic properties such as low Tafel slopes with enhanced kinetics of the catalytic reaction. However, the poor durability in long-run catalytic processes limits their OER efficiency. In order to address this issue, creating a composition with additional compounds that will generate a heterostructure interface is a useful strategy [15,16]. Accordingly, due to their outstanding performance in optical and electrical devices, Co3O4 and SnO 2 have drawn great attention of the researchers. The most widespread uses of Co3O4 and SnO 2-based semiconductors are reported in electrocatalysis, gas sensors, photocatalysis, lithium-ion batteries, transparent electrodes, and supercapacitors [17–23]. Also, the availability of morphological varia - tions of SnO 2 like nanorods, nanosheets, nanoribbons, hollow spheres, nanoflowers, and nanotube shapes [24–29] make SnO 2 an effective framework to enhance the durability of various compounds into work - ing devices. J Milikic et al. has synthesised and compared the electro - catalytic properties of spherical mesoporous Co3O4 and Co3O4/SnO 2 composite. 407 mV and 487 mV OER overpotentials were required by spherical Co3O4 and mixed Co3O4/SnO 2 compound, respectively, at 10 mA/cm2 along with the corresponding Tafel slopes of 107 mV/dec and 100 mV/dec [16]. For OER, Norah Alwadai et al. has reported an overpotential of 360 mV@10 mA/cm2 in alkaline media by using SnO 2/MnO 2 nanocomposite with a low Tafel slope of just 38.9 mV/dec [30]. N. Bhuvanendran et al. have reported Co3O4 spinel oxides having reduced overpotential of 460 mV for OER and 363 mV for HER with longer durability [31]. Similarly, J. Duan et al. have synthesised ceria nanoparticles and Co3O4 nanowires (CeO x@Co 3O4/NF) using nickel foam (NF) resulting in a smaller Tafel slope of 41 mV/dec and 106 mV/dec and a lower overpotential of 252 mV and 106 mV at 10 mA/cm2 for OER and HER, respectively [32]. These literatures confirm that although there are few reports on the Co3O4 and SnO 2-based semiconductors and there composites, but, still the multi-interface heterostructure assembly of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite as a bifunctional OWS electrode material is unexplored up to this point. Therefore, in the present work, for the first time, the novel multi-interface heterostructure assembly of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite has been synthesized by the chemical route. The syner - gistic interactions among SnO 2, Co3O4, and Mo2S3 provide numerous surface-active redox sites and enhanced electronic movements, as a result this synthesised multi-interface SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite shows excellent performance for water electrolysis in alkaline media. The minimal bifunctional overpotential values with this SnO 2- Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite were recoded as 192.3 mV@10 mA/cm2 for OER and 204.98 mV@10 mA/cm2 for HER with the respective Tafel slopes of 99 mV/dec and 102.8 mV/dec. The observed lower over- potential values and enhanced stability in both HER and OER demon - strate the superiority of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite in electrocatalytic water splitting. Furthermore, SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite requires as low as only 1.45 V of cell voltage at 10 mA/cm2 current density during OWS. This makes multi-interface heterostructure assembly of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite potentially an effective electrocatalyst for energy conversion and storage applications underly - ing the novelty and importance of the present work. 2.Experimental section 2.1. Materials and reagents Dimethyl sulfoxide [DMSO, (CH 3)2SO] and sodium molybdate (Na2MoO 4⋅2H2O) were from Thermo Fisher brand. Sodium hydroxide (NaOH) pellets, cobalt nitrate hexahydrate [Co(No 3)2⋅6H2O], and po- tassium hydroxide (KOH) pellets were from Sisco Research Laboratory (SRL) brand. Stannous chloride pentahydrate (SnCl 4⋅5H2O) was from Central drug house (CDH) brand and Thiourea (CH 4N2S) was purchased from Merck Specialties. Sulfuric acid (H2SO4) was purchased from Rankem Chemicals whereas, ethanol (C2H5OH) was purchased from Changshu Hongsheng Fine Chemicals. All the Chemicals were of analytical reagent grade and were used without further purification. De- ionized (DI) water was used where ever required. 2.2. Synthesis of materials 2.2.1. Synthesis of Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers 3.52 g of Na2MoO 4⋅2H2O and 6.1 g of CH4N2S were added to a so- lution having 40 ml of ethanol and 40 ml of DI water. The mixture was kept for 2 hours with continuous stirring [34]. The resulting solution was then transferred to an autoclave at 200 •C for 12 hours. Multiple washings with ethanol and DI water were done resulting in the black powder of Mo2S3 after drying at 60 •C for 8 hours. 2.2.2. Synthesis of SnO 2 -Co3O4 nanocubes SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes were synthesised using sonochemical process adopting few modification to the earlier research [33]. 1.28 g of Co (No3)2⋅6H2O and 3.78 g of SnCl 4⋅5H2O were added in 60 ml of DI water. 3.6 g of NaOH solution in 30 ml of DI water was added in the previously prepared mixture while continuous stirring. Finally, ultrasonication was done for 30 min to produce a pink-coloured precipitate of CoSn(OH) 6. After, washing with ethanol and DI water the precipitate was dried overnight at 50 •C. Subsequently, after 2 hours of calcination at 500 •C the SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes were produced. 2.2.3. Synthesis of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite This SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite was synthesized using three different methods, viz, solid-state, hydrothermal and sonochemical methods. A detailed discussion of the precursor amount, temperature and other chemical conditions for solid-state and hydrothermal syn- thesis methods have been provided in Section 2of the supplementary information. The comparative FESEM morphological analysis of the synthesised SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite is presented in Fig. S2 of the sup- plementary information. Figs. S2(a-c) illustrate the complete structural transformation of the original SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubic morphology, along with the complete degradation of the coral-shaped Mo2S3 nano- flowers in the solid-state synthesis method. In contrast, Figs. S2(d-f) depict the morphological characteristics of the hydrothermally syn- thesised SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite. The aggregation of SnO 2- Co3O4 nanocubes and the wrapping of Mo2S3 nanosheets is evident in Figs. S2(d) and (e). A magnified view of the enclosed region in Fig. S2(e) P. Chaubey et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  72 (2025)  107099 2
