has been provided in Fig. S2(f), which further justifies the inhomoge - neous distribution of SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes over the agglomerated Mo2S3 nanosheets. Moreover, in third (sonochemical) method, an equiweight ratio (0.5 g each) of the pre-synthesized Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers and SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes was precisely selected to facilitate the uniform synergistic chemical interactions while preserving their inherent morphological characteristics. 0.5 g of the above prepared Mo2S3 was added to 35 ml of DI water and stirred continuously for 1 hour. 0.5 g of SnO 2-Co3O4 powder was then added and stirring was continued for additional 1 hour. To ensure homogeneous diffusion and interfacial adhesion between the SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes and Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers, an intensive stirring process was conducted for 2 hours. Subsequently, a sonochemical treatment of 2 hours was employed, for the surface decoration of Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers with SnO 2- Co3O4 nanocubes. Multiple washings with ethanol and DI water were done resulting in the black powder of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite after drying at 60 •C for 8 hours. This process resulted in the formation of the desired multi-interface SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite, characterized by a hierarchically coupled architecture comprising of coral-like nanoflowers adorned with nanocubes. Based upon the discussion and observed results of these three different methods, viz, solid-state, hydrothermal and sonochemical methods, we have selected sonochemical method for the synthesis of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite over the other methods. Further - more, all the chemical equations corresponding to each step of the re- action Scheme 1are provided in the supplementary information (from equations 18 to 23). 2.3. Characterization techniques The crystal structure of the prepared powder samples was analysed by a Rigaku Smartlab high resolution X-Ray diffractometer (HRXRD) using 3 kW, Cu Kα X-ray radiation (λ 1.54 Å) as the source. The guidance software of SmartLab Studio II and a HyPix-3000 2D hybrid semiconductor detector -were used in this HRXRD system. Field emis- sion scanning electron microscope (FESEM, Sigma 300, Zeiss Germany) was used for morphological analysis having 20 V to 30 KV of accelera - tion voltage. High resolution transmission electron microscope (HRTEM, Talos F200 X G2, Thermo Scientific) was used with 200 KV of accelerating potential. The elemental compositions were studied by energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) attached with TEM having approximately 136 eV energy resolution for Mn-K α with 10 Kcps (output). X-ray photoemission spectroscopy (XPS, PHI 5000 versa probe III) having Al Kα X-ray source was used to analyse the elemental prop- erties. Iterative Shirley function was used for the background subtrac - tion during XPS analysis. The pass energy, time per step, and eV step used for the survey scan were 280 eV, 20 ms, and 0.5, respectively. For the elemental scan 55 eV of pass energy, 50 ms time per step, and 0.1 eV step were used. For detecting elemental concentrations, inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES, model- iCAP 7400 Duo, Thermo Fischer Scientific, USA) was used. For electro - chemical studies, like linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), cyclic voltam - metry (CV), chronoamperometry (CA), and multistep chronopotentiometry (MCP), PalmSens3 potentiostat system (The Netherlands) was used. For electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) studies, metrohm autolab PGSTAT204 electrochemical worksta - tion was used. A frequency range of 100 MHz to 100 KHz at 10 mV amplitude potential was employed for performing EIS. 2.4. Electrochemical measurements Three electrode system was used for the electrochemical measure - ments having a counter electrode (Pt), a reference electrode (Ag/AgCl), and the samples-modified pencil graphite lead (PGL, of 0.5 mm diam - eter) as a working electrode. These pencil leads were inserted in a micropipette and 0.4 cm of the pencil lead was kept outside. The elec- trocatalytic ink was prepared by mixing 90 wt % of the synthesised Scheme 1.Schematic representation for the synthesis of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite.P. Chaubey et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  72 (2025)  107099 3
