of various constituent elements of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite. Fig. 1(d) shows the survey spectrum of the SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite, which represents the existence of Co 2p, Mo 3d, S 2 s, S 2p, O 1 s, and Sn 3d core elemental states with the inset displaying their respective atomic percentages. The XPS spectra of Co 2p is pro- vided in Fig. 1(e). Two prominent peaks can be seen at 779.20 eV and 794.81 eV corresponding to Co32p3/2 and Co32p½ [19,39,40], and their satellite peaks appeared at 783.7 eV and 802.87 eV, respectively [41]. The energy difference between the Co32p3/2 and Co32p1/2 peaks is 15.61 eV, which corresponds to Co3O4 phase [23]. The Mo 3d XPS result is provided in Fig. 1(f). While the peak at 234.32 eV belongs to Mo6, the appearance of peaks at 224.82 eV and 227.19 eV is related to S 2s. The deconvoluted peaks at 228.3 eV and 231.01 eV belong to Mo 3d5/2 and Mo 3d3/2, respectively, highlighting the evidence of Mo3in the nanocomposite [35–38]. The XPS result of S 2p can be best fitted into four peaks as shown in Fig. 1(g). The deconvoluted peaks at 159.92 eV and 161.11 eV are due to S 2p3/2, the 164.67 eV peak is assigned to S 2p1/2, governing the existence of S2- in the composite, and the final peak at 167.61 eV is a satellite peak [36–38]. In Fig. 1(h), the O 1s spectrum is displayed. The lattice metal-oxygen linkages are demonstrated by the peak at 529 eV, while the surface adsorbed species are responsible for the peak at 530.05 eV [41]. Fig. 1(i) displays the XPS result of Sn 3d spectra. The deconvoluted peaks at 485.31 eV and 493.73 eV, respec - tively, are the result of Sn 3d5/2 and Sn 3d3/2, both of which demonstrate the formation of Sn4in the composite [25]. The energy difference of 8.42 eV between these two peaks further justifies the presence of Sn4 oxidation state in the composite [27]. So, the observed XRD and XPS results are consistent and confirm the successful synthesis of SnO 2-- Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite. 3.2. FESEM results The surface morphology of the prepared samples were confirmed using FESEM analysis. Fig. 1(j, k) present the FESEM images of Mo2S3, which confirms the coral-shaped nanoflower-like morphology. Several ultrathin two-dimensional nanosheets, having 35 nm average thickness, have been combined to form these Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers. The standalone single Mo2S3 nanoflowers has an average diameter of 2–3 μm. The Mo2S3 nanoflower synthesis process described above is quite reliable because it replicates the same morphology when Mo2S3 nano- flowers were synthesised a second-time, as demonstrated in Fig. S3(a) of the supplementary information. SnO 2-Co3O4 has a well-defined nano- cubical morphology, as displayed in Fig. 1(l). The average dimension of these nanocubes is determined to be between 350–400 nm. Further - more, Fig. 1(m) demonstrates the aggregation of numerous tiny and ultrafine SnO 2 and Co3O4 nanoparticles forming the nanocubical ar- chitecture. This synthesis process for SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes is very reproducible as the same morphology was replicated again in a second- time synthesis approach, as provided in Fig. S3(b) of the supplementary information. The FESEM result of the synthesised SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite is given in Fig. 1(n-p). The SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes are inserted/stacked on the borders of the Mo2S3 nanoflowers. The extremely flat and symmetric geometry of SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes helps in enhancing the electronic conductivity and thus is expected to further enhance the HER and OER catalysis processes. Similarly, the Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers provide a highly porous architecture with enhanced active surface area, which will further facilitate the mass transport of reactants and products. SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite has retained the parental morphologies with a coupled framework of coral-shaped nanoflowers modified with nanocubes, forming a very promising architecture for enhanced electrocatalytic activity. 3.3. TEM results TEM analysis provides a further comprehension and insight of the morphological results. TEM results of the synthesised Mo2S3 nanoflowers and SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes have been provided in Figs. S4 and S5 of the supplementary information, respectively, with their cor- responding detailed discussion. This result was in accordance with FESEM observations. Fig. 2(a) displays the TEM result of SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite, which clearly shows the coupled morphology of Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers further decorated with SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes. The distribution of smaller nanoparticles of SnO 2-Co3O4 over the petals of Mo2S3 nanoflowers has been displayed in the enclosed area of Fig. 2(b). Furthermore, the indicated regions 1, 2, and 3 of the HRTEM image 2(c) highlight the existence of multi-phases of Mo2S3, SnO 2 and Co3O4. This result further lies in the agreement of well syn- thesised multi-interphase assembly of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite. HRTEM result of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite is shown in Fig. 2(d) with the enclosed regions 1, 2, and 3, belonging to the (␀101) plane of Mo2S3, (110) plane of SnO 2, and (111) plane of Co3O4, respectively. Fig. 2(e), 2(f) and 2(g) show the enlarged view of these enclosed regions 1, 2, and 3, respectively, with the inset showing (I) Fast Fourier transform (FFT) and (II) inverse FFT results. SAED analysis of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite is shown in Fig. 2(h), which represents (␀101) plane of Mo2S3, the (110), (101), and (211) planes of SnO 2, and the (440) plane of Co3O4. These outcomes further support the XRD results of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite explained earlier. EDX result shows the successful incorporation of all the individual ele- ments in SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite (Fig. S6 of the supplemen - tary information). Further, the HAADF-STEM picture of the SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite is shown in Fig. 2(i). The elemental mapping of Co, Mo, O, Sn, and S elements, is shown in Fig. 2(j-n). The overall elemental mapping is provided in Fig. 2(o), which also justifies that Sn, Co, Mo, S, and O are all uniformly distributed in this synthesised SnO 2- Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite. 3.4. Electrochemical analysis One of the main stages before conducting an electrochemical investigation is optimising the analytical electrocatalytic parameters such as mass loading, scan rate, and electrolyte concentration. After optimization studies (shown in the supplementary information, Fig. S7), 25 mg mass loading, 50 mV/s scan rate, and 2.0 M KOH electrolyte were finally employed for all the OER studies of the prepared electrode ma- terials. Similarly, after optimization studies (shown in the supplemen - tary information, Fig. S8), 25 mg mass loading, 50 mV/s scan rate, and 1.0 M H2SO4 electrolyte were finally employed for all the HER studies. 3.4.1. OER results The CV scans were recorded at various scan rates from 2 mV/s to 50 mV/s in 2.0 M KOH electrolyte solution. The CV results of Mo2S3, SnO 2- Co3O4, and SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 are given in Fig. 3(a), (b), and (c) respectively. The electrochemical double-layer capacitance (Cdl) value of each prepared electrocatalyst has been provided in Fig. 3(d). As shown, Mo2S3 and SnO 2-Co3O4 have respective Cdl values of 0.43 mF and 0.44 mF, respectively. However, SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite has a larger Cdl value of 6.34 mF which confirms the availability of enlarged active sites for the electrocatalytic activities. Similarly, Mo2S3 and SnO 2-Co3O4 have the respective electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) of 21.5 cm2 and 22 cm2, whereas, SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite has a comparatively higher ECSA value of 317.25 cm2. This superior ECSA value of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite further corroborates the large number of active sites availability for the OER phenomenon. The LSV polarisation results shown in Fig. 3(e) have been used to compare the overpotential values of all the synthesized materials. The obtained overpotentials of Mo2S3 and SnO 2-Co3O4 are 431.9 mV and 461.8 mV at 10 mA/cm2, respectively. Still, the pristine Mo2S3 and SnO 2-Co3O4 convey a lower response as OER electrocatalysts. Inade - quate charge transfer between the electrode and reaction sites may be the cause of this. The overpotential value of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 P. Chaubey et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  72 (2025)  107099 5
