nanocomposite was significantly lowered to 192.3 mV at 10 mA/cm2 with the combined integration of coral-shaped Mo2S3 nanoflowers and SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes. The Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflower plus nanocubical SnO 2-Co3O4 morphologies in the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite have an additional role for making a successful exposure of many adsorption-desorption active sites and, providing a sufficient electrocatalytic area for the diffusion of in-situ produced oxygen. The exchange current density (Jo) is an important parameter to know the inherent electrocatalytic applicability of materials. The calculated Jo value of 2.56 mA/cm2 for the synthesised SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite is the least compared to Mo2S3 (3.89 mA/cm2), and SnO 2- Co3O4 nanocubes (3.55 mA/cm2). The lowest Jo value of SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite compared to other samples further signifies the optimised internal OER kinetic applicability of this nanocomposite. Additionally, a comparative analysis of the J₀ values with the previously reported electrocatalysts in the literature is provided in Table ST1 in the supplementary information, which further confirms the superior OER performance of our SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite with respect to the other reported materials. Moreover, the Tafel slope is another essential parameter to consider while researching the kinetics and mechanism of OER. The Tafel plot is given in Fig. 3(f). The obtained OER Tafel slope of 99 mV/dec for the synthesised SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite is the least compared to Mo2S3 (219.42 mV/dec) and SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes (303.14 mV/dec). The lowest OER Tafel slope value of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite further justifies its enhanced OER reaction kinetics compared to other counterparts. EIS was used to study the kinetic behaviour of the synthesized electrode materials, with a particular focus on charge transfer resistance (Rct). This Rct value was determined from the diam - eter of the semicircle observed in the Nyquist plot which is depicted in the Fig. S9(a) of the supplementary information. An enlarged view of the enclosed region of Fig. S9(a) is presented in Fig. S9(b), which also in- cludes an equivalent circuit model having solution resistance (Rs), a constant phase element (CPE), and Rct in a series-parallel configuration. The least arc diameter of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite in the ob- tained EIS plot confirms that SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite has the smallest value of Rct. The synergistic interactions among SnO 2, Co3O4, and Mo2S3 at various interfaces ultimately enhance the OER electronic conductivity and hence lower the Rct value in the nanocomposite. As illustrated in Fig. 3(g), a comparative bar plot of OER overpotential and Tafel slope value highlights the superior properties of SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite compared to its pristine counterparts of Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers and SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes. The error values of this bar plot have been determined from the mean standard deviation (±SD, N 3). Furthermore, CA stability testing was conducted for the SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite in 2.0 M KOH electrolyte at a potential of 0.74 V (vs. Ag/AgCl) and is shown in Fig. 3(h). Almost constant current density Fig. 3.CV plots of (a) Mo2S3; (b) SnO 2-Co3O4; (c) SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite; (d) Δj vs. scan rate plot; (e) OER polarisation plots; (f) OER Tafel plot; (g) comparative bar plot of OER overpotential and Tafel slope values for Mo2S3, SnO 2-Co3O4 and, SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite; (h) CA measurement of SnO 2- Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite over a period of 24-hours; (i) OER polarisation curve before and after 24-hours stability analysis of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite.P. Chaubey et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  72 