enhanced HER kinetics for SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite due to fast rise in the catalytic current towards HER. This suggests the domi - nating behaviour of the Volmer-Heyrovsky reaction pathway, which is the rate-determining step in the HER process [42,43]. This is due to the synergistic sway of Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers and SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes. The increased porosity, numerous active sites, and the sur- face area created by the bundled effect of Mo2S3 and SnO 2-Co3O4 in the nanocomposite material are responsible for the faster reaction kinetics. Moreover, to study the kinetic behaviour of these synthesized electrode materials, EIS was employed and the associated results are provided in Fig. S9(c) of the supplementary information. The enlarged view of the enclosed region in Fig. S9(c) is presented in Fig. S9(d), which also in- cludes an equivalent circuit diagram consisting of Rs, CPE, and Rct in a series-parallel configuration. Notably, the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite exhibited the smallest semicircular diameter in the EIS profile, signifying the lowest Rct value than the pristine components. This EIS result further compli - ments the HER Tafel slope analysis. The charge redistribution in SnO 2- Co3O4 and Mo2S3 interfaces reduces the HER kinetic barrier for the diffusion of H* intermediates at the electrode-electrolyte interface. As illustrated in Fig. 4(g), a comparative bar plot of HER overpotentials at 10 mA/cm2 and Tafel slope values highlights the superior properties of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite compared to its pristine counter - parts of Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers and SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes. The error values of this bar plot have been determined from the standard deviation (±SD, N 3). Moreover, as illustrated in Fig. 4(h), the CA stability measurement was performed at a potential of ␀0.5 V (vs. Ag/ AgCl) to validate the long-term endurance of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite in HER process. After continuous 24-hours run, the material nearly maintains its original electrocatalytic behaviour for HER. The LSV polarisation curves before and after the 24-hours CA test have been provided in Fig. 4(i). This also demonstrates that the over- potential value changes very little at lower and higher current densities, confirming long term stability of the prepared SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite during HER. 3.4.3. Interfacial mechanisms Based on band alignment positions of the two semiconductors, type- II (staggered type) heterojunction theory is the most fascinating among researchers to explain the enhanced mechanisms at the interface. The type-II heterojunction theory predicts the positions of the conduction band (CB) and valence band (VB) at the interface of two semiconductors. According to this theory, the CB of semiconductor A will lie between the CB and VB of semiconductor B. Based on these band positions and Fermi level (Ef) alignment, electron transfer will take place from B to A and holes will be transferred from A to B. Charge recombination processes are avoided so that the oxidation and the reduction process may take place simultaneously and separately at these two semiconductor heter - ojunctions [44–46]. A schematic diagram of the position of CB, VB, and the position of Ef (shown by a dotted line) at the Co3O4/SnO 2 interface is provided in Fig. 5(a). The CB position of n-type SnO 2 is lower compared to that of p- type Co3O4. So, a p-n junction is formed at the Co3O4/SnO 2 interface. When a potential is applied, the electrons will be transferred from the CB of Co3O4 to the CB of SnO 2. In contrast, the holes from the VB of SnO 2 will move into the VB of Co3O4 because of the energy differences. This phenomenon will happen till a charge equilibrium is attained at the Fermi level. Now, because of the concentration gradient generated, a built-in internal electric field will be formed in this charge depletion region at the interface of Co3O4/SnO 2. This internal electric field further ensures a sufficient spatial charge separation and avoids the charge recombination processes. As a result, the accumulation of electrons will be increased in the CB of SnO 2, and the collection of holes will be enhanced in the VB of Co3O4. These electrons and holes directly get involved in the HER and OER reactions, and hence oxidation takes place at Co3O4 and reduction takes place at SnO 2, respectively [47,48]. So, in this way, the formed Co3O4/SnO 2 heterojunction will ultimately enhance the OER and HER processes simultaneously by avoiding charge recombination. Similarly, at the Co3O4/Mo 2S3 heterojunction interface, a built-in electric field will also be developed from Mo2S3 to Co3O4 direction because of the metallic or n-type nature of Mo2S3 and p-type nature of Co3O4 {Fig. 5(b)}. This internal electric field will ensure the spatial charge separation and further add up in the charge transfer process. The schematic diagram of the phenomenon occurring at the interface of SnO 2/Mo 2S3 type-II heterojunction has been shown in Fig. 5(c). The direction of internal electric field formed in the depletion region will be from Mo2S3 to SnO 2. Again, the spatial charge separations and the accumulation of excess electrons in the CB of Mo2S3 and holes in the VB of SnO 2 will catalyse the HER and OER processes, respectively, at Fig. 5.The synergistic interfacial mechanisms for electrocatalytic reactions at (a) Co3O4/SnO 2 interface; (b) Co3O4/Mo 2S3 interface and; (c) SnO 2/Mo 2S3 interface.P. Chaubey et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  72 (2025)  107099 9
