Mo2S3/SnO 2 interface. These synergistic interactions occurring at the multiple hetero-interfaces of this SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite are responsible for enhancing the OER and HER processes in comparison to the pristine Mo2S3 nanoflowers and SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubes. To justify these interpretations for the enhanced electrochemical activity of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite, an experimental study for comparative valence band maximum (EVmax) analysis and Fermi energy (Ef) shift evaluation was conducted using XPS measure - ments. These obtained results have been provided in Fig. S10 of the supplementary information. The electronic structural modifications after combining the Mo2S3 and SnO 2-Co3O4 can be well observed in these XPS results. The measured Ef -EVmax values were 0.05 eV for metallic-like Mo2S3, 2.01 eV for SnO 2-Co3O4, and 1.93 eV for SnO 2- Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite. The insertion of metal-like Mo2S3 at the interfaces of SnO 2-Co3O4 has lowered the Fermi level positions towards the valence band maxima and hence this Ef -EVmax difference was reduced to 1.93 eV [10]. These electronic alterations at the multiple interfaces of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite generate numerous electron-hole pairs and avoid charge recombination losses to enhance its superior catalytic efficiency. 3.4.4. OWS results The SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite-based anodic and cathodic electrochemical cell assembly was used for water electrolysis in 2.0 M KOH alkaline solution (denoted as SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 || SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3). As observed in the inset of Fig. 6(a), bubbles were produced from both the electrodes after applying an external potential. As dis- played in Fig. 6(a) a minimal cell potential of 1.45 V at 10 mA/cm2 was needed for water electrolysis. This is comparatively better than the conditions of an ideal electrolyser (Pt as cathode and IrO2 as anode), which requires a higher cell voltage of 1.59 V at 10 mA/cm2 current density [49]. The 48-hours of CA investigation was also used to assess the stability of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite. The CA result given in Fig. 6(b) shows almost constant current density. Additionally, water splitting LSV polarisation curves before and after 48-hours of CA sta- bility testing was displayed in Fig. 6(c), which suggests that the elec- trodes modified with SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite have almost unchanged OWS electrocatalytic behaviour, even after a 48-hours continuous operation. This further suggests that the SnO 2-Co3O4-- Mo2S3 nanocomposite has promising electrode properties for bifunc - tional electrolytic water splitting applications. 3.4.5. Stability testing Stability testing is one of the most important criteria for claiming the sustainability of the electrode materials in water electrolysis applica - tions. Therefore, a 72-hours of OWS stability testing was carried out at 1.5 V, as shown in Fig 7(a). The two-electrode system built by SnO 2- Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite over nickel foam (NF) substrate is shown in the inset of Fig. 7(a), and it demonstrates the evolution of large density of hydrogen (H2) and oxygen (O2) gas bubbles. By closely examining Fig. 7(a) it was analysed that the current density drops from 30 mA/cm2 to 20 mA/cm2 in the first 13 hours, and it stays constant at 20 mA/cm2 for the remaining hours of the stability investigation. XRD study was conducted following this 72-hours of OWS stability testing to analyse any phase transformations within this SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite. The obtained XRD results are presented as Fig. S11 (a-c) of the supplementary information. The high intensity XRD peaks in Fig. S11(a) appearing at 44.5•, 51.8•, and 76.3•belong to (111), (200), and (220) planes of metallic nickel in the NF substrate [42]. The other diffraction peaks appearing at 9.26•, 44•, 64.4•, and 76.5•are well indexed to (001) plane of Mo2S3, (400) and (440) planes of Co3O4, and (321) plane of SnO 2, respectively. These results are in accordance with the XRD observations for the freshly prepared SnO 2-- Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite before OWS study as reported in Fig. 1(C). However, the magnified view of the (400) diffraction peak of Co3O4 at 2θ 44•{Fig. S11(b)}, shows reduced crystallinity after this 72-hours stability test. Similarly, a noticeable decrease in the diffraction in- tensity of (321) plane of SnO 2 at 76.5•{Fig. S11(c)} is observed. This shows very minor degradation in few phases of the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite after stability test. Moreover, as shown in Fig. 7(b, c), FESEM analysis after this 72- hours stability testing confirms that the nanocubical morphology of SnO 2-Co3O4 component in the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite is retained. However, due to prolonged electrochemical interactions under highly alkaline conditions, a discernible increase in the surface rough - ness was observed. In contrast to the observed clear ultrathin nanosheets of Mo2S3 nanoflowers {Fig. 1(n, o)}, deformed architecture of Mo2S3 coral-shaped nanoflowers and a few Mo2S3 nanosheets were observed after the stability test (Fig. 7(b, c)). This deterioration led to a significant reduction in the number of available HER and OER surface-active sites, ultimately contributing to the observed decrease in current density within the initial 13-hours interval [50,51]. After stability test, TEM analysis was further conducted to study the morphological transformations in the SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nano- composite and is presented in Fig. S12 of the supplementary informa - tion. Fig. S12(a, b) display that the nanocubic architectures of the parent SnO 2-Co3O4 remained mostly unaffected within the final SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite even after the post-stability testing. However, the coral-like nanoflower structures of Mo2S3 were found deformed in the final SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite after the post-stability testing. These findings corroborate FESEM results shown in Fig. 7(b, c). Fig. S12 (c, d) clearly highlights the disintegration of Mo2S3 nanoflowers within the final SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite after the post-stability testing. Additionally, increased surface roughness in the SnO 2-Co3O4 nanocubic components was observed within the final SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite {Fig. S12 (e, f)} after prolonged stability test in Fig. 6.(a) OWS polarisation curve; (b) 48-hours of CA stability testing; (c) OWS polarisation result before and after 48-hours stability analysis of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite.P. Chaubey et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  72 (2025)  107099 10
