the depletion of Co3species as active OER sites attributes to the attenuation of this OWS performance. The Mo 3d XPS results for before and after the stability study are shown in Figs. S14(c) and S14(d), respectively. The deconvoluted peaks at 230.64 eV and 233.36 eV are due to Mo33d3/2 and Mo6in Fig. S14(c), respectively. In contrast, Fig. S14(d) has only the Mo3peak appeared at 231.13 eV because several defects were generated by the morphological destruc - tion of Mo2S3 nanoflowers, which has altered the nearby electronic environment. Fig. S14(e) and S14(f) represent the Sn 3d XPS images before and after stability analysis, respectively. The Sn43d5/2 and Sn4 3d3/2 spin doublets are represented by the peaks at 485.96 eV and 494.30 eV, respectively, in Fig. S14(e). Likewise, the Sn43d5/2 and Sn43d3/2 doublets appeared at 484.25 eV and 492.96 eV in Fig. S14(f) with slightly shifted positions towards lower binding energy. Addition - ally, the area ratio of Sn43d5/2 to Sn43d3/2 was dropped from 0.91 to 0.79. The charge transfer effects and the decreased relative content of Sn4brought about by the morphological destructions are the reasons behind this. The respective S 2p XPS analysis is shown in Figs. S14(g) and S14(h) for the stability measurements taken before and after. As shown in Fig. S14(g), the XPS peaks at 162.69 eV, 164.82 eV, and 167.37 eV are attributed to S2- 2p3/2, S2- 2p1/2, and the satellite peak, respec - tively. Fig. S14(h) displays the satellite peak at 167.5 eV, while the S2- peak occurred at 161 eV has very low peak intensity due to the dis- integrated morphology of Mo2S3 nanoflowers. The O1s XPS results analysed before and after the stability tests are shown in Figs. S14(i) and S14(j), respectively. Fig. S14(i) has the XPS peaks at 529.9 eV and 532.2 eV due to the metal-oxygen bonds and the surface adsorbed (-OH) spe- cies. After the stability measurement, the 529.31 eV and 530.9 eV peaks were preserved in Fig. S14(j). The area ratio of the metal-oxygen bond of the lattice to surface adsorbed oxygen-containing functional groups (-OH) was decreased from 1.01 to 0.95 after this long-run stability testing. This observation further justifies the fact that it is very obvious for Co and Mo transition metal cations to electrochemically interact with these surface-adsorbed OH- anions and form Co-OH/CoOOH and Mo-oxide/hydroxide species. Therefore, this electrode-electrolyte con- tact for a longer duration negatively affects their electrocatalytic performance. Furthermore, ICP-OES testing was conducted to evaluate the elemental leaching of Co, Mo, and Sn metals in the KOH alkaline envi- ronment and the results are summarized in Table ST2 of the supple - mentary information. Freshly prepared KOH electrolyte and the solution collected after 72-hours of stability testing were used for a comparative elemental leaching study. The ppm concentration of K remained nearly unchanged, whereas the concentrations of Co, Sn, and Mo were increased following the stability test, which strongly correlates with the FESEM and TEM observations. These morphological deformations lead to the loss of highly active catalytic sites and the decline in current density within the initial 13 hours of stability testing. A 72-hours multistep chronopotentiometry (MCP) analysis was conducted to evaluate the long-term OWS stability of the SnO 2-Co3O4- Mo2S3 nanocomposite at the higher current densities of 100 mA/cm², 150 mA/cm², and 250 mA/cm², each applied for an interval of 24 hours. For this experiment, the electrocatalytic ink was drop-casted over the NF substrate having (0.3 ×0.2) cm dimension. The corresponding two- electrode-based OWS electrochemical performance result is presented in Fig. S15 of the supplementary information. These MCP results exhibited stable current densities with recorded OWS cell potential re- quirements of 1.99 V, 2.13 V, and 2.35 V at 100 mA/cm2, 150 mA/ cm2, and 250 mA/ cm2, respectively. Furthermore, theoretical energy con- sumption analysis (kWh/m3 H2) at different current densities were performed using equations (28–31) of the supplementary information, and the obtained result has been provided in Table ST3. Following this MCP stability study, the used electrode was subjected to the ECSA cal- culations in KOH electrolyte. CV scans were recorded within the non- Faradaic region at scan rates ranging from (5 to 25) mV/s, and the Fig. 7.(a) 72-hours long run CA stability testing curve of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite with the inset having large number of H2 and O2 bubbles at the nickel foam (NF) electrodes; (b, c) FESEM images of SnO 2-Co3O4-Mo 2S3 nanocomposite after 72-hours stability testing (rescaled-enlarged representations of the individual scale bars have been provided for clear visualization).P. Chaubey et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  72 (2025)  107099 11
