www.advancedsciencenews.com www.afm-journal.de conﬁgurationofthismaterial,whichpromotestheHERperfor- mance to the highest level because of the induced favorable en- ergybarrierforwaterdissociationandadsorptionfreeenergyof H*.Asaresult,theHERoverpotentialofFeCoNiMnRu/CNFin 1MKOHat100mAcm−2was55and174mVlowerthanthatof FeCoNiCrRu/CNFandFeCoNiCuRu/CNF,respectively. While actually, the uplift of HER performance for high entropy alloys is the result of a synergistic interaction be- tween multiple active sites and reaction intermediates driven by electronegativity-dominated electronic conﬁguration,[18]and unraveling the mechanism is crucial for the structural design and performance enhancement of high-entropy materials. In this work, according to Pauling electronegativity scale ( 𝜒p), La (𝜒p=1.10),Mn( 𝜒p=1.55),Cr( 𝜒p=1.66),Cu( 𝜒p=1.90)and Mo ( 𝜒p=2.16) with diverse electronegativities are selected to regulatetheelectronicstructureofhollowcarbonsphere(HCS) supported high entropy alloy (HEA@HCS) material of quater- nary PtFeCoNi@HCS, and a series of resulted quinary (PtFe- CoNiX@HCS, X =La, Mn, Cu, Mo) and senary (PtFeCoN- iCuY@HCS, Y =Cr, Mn, Mo) HEA@HCS materials are re- ported.AsCuandCrareintroducedintoPtFeCoNi@HCSinse- quencetoyieldPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS,thelocalelectronicstruc- ture of the material is optimized, leading to a multi-site syner- gisticeﬀectthatenablesanimprovedHERperformancewithan overpotential of 29 mV at 10 mA cm−2, and its durability is evi- dentlybetterthanthatofcommercial20%Pt/C.Basedonexperi- mentscoupledwithdensityfunctionaltheory(DFT)calculations, themulti-sitesynergisticeﬀectwithinthePtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS materialwaselucidatedintermsofbothstructuralcomposition andelectrocatalyticprocess. 2.ResultsandDiscussion 2.1.MaterialsCharacterization Monodisperse polystyrene (PS) spheres (Figure S1, Supporting Information) were used as a soft template to synthesize hollow carbonspheres(HCS)(Figure S2,SupportingInformation).The HCS-supportedhigh-entropyalloymaterial(HEA@HCS)ofPt- FeCoNiCuCr@HCSwaspreparedthroughaone-stepsolvother- mal method as depicted in Figure1a, wherein multiple metal ionswerereducedtoformHEAnanoparticlesdepositedonHCS surface.X-raydiﬀraction(XRD)wasconductedtounveilthecrys- talstructureoftheas-synthesizedPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSandthe detailed information is shown in Figure 1b. The three distinct diﬀraction peaks located at 2 𝜃of 41.6 °, 48.5 °, and 71.3 °can be attributed to (111), (200), and (220) facets of Cu3Pt (PDF #35– 1358),respectively,suggestingahomogeneousface-centeredcu- bic(FCC)conﬁgurationwithinPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSwhichre- sembles that of Cu3Pt alloy. However, careful observation ﬁnds thatthediﬀractionpeaksinPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSslightlyshift toward lower 2 𝜃values compared to those of standard Cu3Pt (PDF#35–1358),implyingtherelaxationofcrystallatticeinduced byembeddingofmultiplemetalatomsinCu3Pt.Theseresultsre- vealahomogeneousphaseofPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSwithPtand Cuformingthebasiccrystallatticeasfundamentalelements. The morphology of PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS was observed us- ing a scanning electron microscope (SEM), transmission elec- tron microscope (TEM), and high-angle annular dark ﬁeld-STEM(HAADF-STEM).PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSpreservesthein- herent 3D structure of HCS (Figure 1c), ensuring the distribu- tion of HEA nanoparticles with a diameter of ≈5n m( F i g u r e s S3a,b, Supporting Information) throughout the carbon shell (Figures1d,e). High-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) image of PtFe- CoNiCuCr@HCS nanoparticle and the corresponding diﬀrac- tion ring (Figure 1f) indicate that the lattice fringes with spac- ingof0.225and0.193nmcanbeascribedtotheexpanded(111) and (200) facet of Cu3Pt (PDF #35–1358), which is consistent with the result of XRD pattern. Furthermore, HAADF-STEM images and the corresponding energy dispersive spectroscopy (EDS)elementalmappings(Figure 1g;FiguresS3candS4,Sup- portingInformation)demonstratetheuniformdistributionofPt, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, and Cr forming PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS nanopar- ticles.Theseresultsprovideadditionalcompellingevidencesup- portingthehomogeneousstructureintheas-preparedPtFeCoN- iCuCr@HCSmaterial. The elemental composition in PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS (Table S1,SupportingInformation)wasdetectedusinginductivelycou- pledplasmaatomicemissionspectrometry(ICP-AES),anditre- veals the weight percent of 7.33% for Pt, 0.92% for Fe, 1.04% for Co, 2.23% for Ni, 3.13% for Cu, and 0.26% for Cr in PtFe- CoNiCuCr@HCS (Figure S5a, Supporting Information), which means an atomic fraction of 0.51, 0.22, 0.24, 0.52, 0.67, 0.06% for Pt, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu and Cr (Figure S5b, Supporting Informa- tion), respectively. Apparently, the atomic ratio of Pt and all the other metals of Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, and Cr is approach to 1: 3, con- sisting of the atomic ratio of Pt and Cu in Cu3Pt. The result of ICP-AES meets well with the XRD pattern that Pt and Cu play a crucial role as fundamental elements to form the basic crys- talstructureofhighentropyalloys,whileFe,Co,Ni,andCrare morelikelytosubstituteCuatominCu3PttogeneratePtFeCoN- iCuCr@HCSmaterialduetotheirsimilarchemicalpropertiesas transitionmetals.[19] X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) was employed to fur- ther explore the electronic and coordination structure of Pt- FeCoNiCuCr@HCS, and the X-ray absorption near-edge struc- ture (XANES) spectra of Pt L3-edge are depicted in Figure2a. The white line intensity and pre-edge energy of PtFeCoN- iCuCr@HCSisawayfromPtO2,butcomparabletothatofPtfoil, suggestingametallicstateofPtinPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS.[20]In addition, the Fourier-transformed (FT) k3-weighted extended X- rayabsorptionﬁnestructure(EXAFS)spectracouldprovidethe information of coordination structure. In the EXAFS spectra of Pt L3-edge (Figure 2b,c), the prominent peak at 2.64 Å in PtFe- CoNiCuCr@HCS is originated from the ﬁrst-shell Pt ─Pt bond, andcarefulanalysisrevealsthattheﬁrst-shellPt ─PtbondinPt- FeCoNiCuCr@HCS exhibits shift toward higher R-value com- pared to that of 2.57 Å in Pt foil, which could be attributed to themultiplemetalatomsembeddedinthelatticeofthematerial. Moreover,theabsenceofPt-OshellinPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSin- dicatesawell-maintainedalloyingstructure.[21]Theseﬁndingsin EXAFSspectrawerefurthervalidatedthroughwavelettransform (WT)mapsofPtL3-edge(Figure 2d),wheretheintensityaround k value of 8.6 Å−1in PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS closely resembles that observed in Pt foil, indicating the metallicity of PtFeCoN- iCuCr@HCS. In the XANES spectra of Cu K-edge (Figure 2e), thepre-edgeofPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSisobviouslydiﬀerentfrom CuO,butexhibitssimilaritytotheobservedpre-edgeinCufoil, Adv. Funct. Mater. 2025,35,2414554 ©2024Wiley-VCHGmbH 2414554(2of11) 16163028, 2025, 5, Downloaded from https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/adfm.202414554 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
