t is observed that PtFeCoNiCu@HCSexhibitsahigher Cdlvalueof1.64mFcm−2 thanPtFeCoNi@HCS(0.57mFcm−2),PtFeCoNiLa@HCS(0.20 mF cm−2), PtFeCoNiMn@HCS (0.41 mF cm−2) and PtFeCoN- iMo@HCS(0.22mFcm−2),implyingthelargerECSAandmore availableactivesitesexposedonitssurfaceforHERprocess. To gain in-depth insight into the inﬂuence of various metals onHERperformance,EISNyquistplotsoftheHEA@HCSma- terials(Figure 3d)wereanalyzedwiththeequivalentresistance- capacitance circuits (Figures S9andS10, Supporting Informa- tion), where Rs is ohmic resistance, CPE1 and CPE2 are con- stant phase elements, Rct1and Rct2are charge transfer resis- tance. The ﬁtted values of total charge transfer resistance (Rct) consisting of both Rct1and Rct2are listed in Table S2(Support- ingInformation),andthelowerRctvalueofPtFeCoNiCu@HCS (18.8Ω) compared to that of PtFeCoNi@HCS (48.9 Ω), PtFe- CoNiLa@HCS(1536.0 Ω),PtFeCoNiMn@HCS(243.9 Ω)andPt- FeCoNiMo@HCS(165.0 Ω)indicatesafasterchargetransferki- neticsinPtFeCoNiCu@HCSduringHERprocess. The aforementioned electrochemical results conﬁrm that the electronegativityofatomshasasigniﬁcanteﬀectontheHERper- formance of PtFeCoNi@HCS, and Cu possesses superior capa- bility in regulating the electrocatalytic activity compared to La, Mn,andMo. To further improve the HER performance of PtFeCoN- iCu@HCS material, Cr ( 𝜒p=1.66), Mn ( 𝜒p=1.55) and Mo (𝜒p=2.16) were introduced, respectively, to create senary PtFe- CoNiCuY@HCS (Y =Cr, Mn, Mo). XRD patterns (Figure S11, Adv. Funct. Mater. 2025,35,2414554 ©2024Wiley-VCHGmbH 2414554(5of11) 16163028, 2025, 5, Downloaded from https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/adfm.202414554 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
