www.advancedsciencenews.com www.afm-journal.de performance when compared with commercial 20% Pt/C cat- alyst, as evidenced by LSV (Figure S15a, Supporting Informa- tion), Tafel slope (Figure S15b, Supporting Information), and Rct(FiguresS16andS17, Supporting Information), and its Cdl value (1.86 mF cm−2) is 62% higher than that of 1.15 mF cm−2 for commercial 20% Pt/C catalyst (Figures S18andS19,S u p - porting Information). All these results verify the superior capa- bility of Cr atom in regulating the electrocatalytic performance of high entropy alloy materials toward HER. When compared tothe reportednoble-metal-based materials as HERcatalysts in literatures,[8,26–38]theas-preparedPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSexhibits distinct advantages in terms of overpotential and Tafel slope (Figure4f;TableS3,SupportingInformation). The durability of PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS as HER electrocata- lyst was evaluated by using CV cycles and the results are dis- played in Figure 4galong with that of 20% Pt/C for compar- ison. It shows much better stability with overpotential loss of o n l y5m Va t1 0m Ac m−2after 15000 CV cycles, surpassing that of more than 60 mV at similar conditions for commer- cial 20% Pt/C (Figure 4h). The anti-degradation property of Pt- FeCoNiCuCr@HCS compared to 20% Pt/C catalyst can be at- tributed to the slow diﬀusion of atoms in its structure, as the highentropyeﬀectcouldreducetheGibbsfreeenergyandpro- mote the formation of homogeneous phase, resulting in a sta- ble crystal structure.[25]To characterize the structure of PtFe- CoNiCuCr@HCS following durability test, carbon paper was employed as the working electrode for catalyst ink deposition, and XRD, SEM, TEM as well as XPS measurements were con- ducted. Given that Naﬁon was incorporated as a binder dur- ing the electrochemical experiments, the XRD patterns of Pt- FeCoNiCuCr@HCS on carbon paper before and after durabil- itytestareillustrated(Figure S20,SupportingInformation),and it is observed that PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS retains its diﬀraction peaks consistent with that before durability test, suggesting a well-preservedcrystalstructure.SEM(Figure S21,SupportingIn- formation) and TEM (Figures S22a,b, Supporting Information) images demonstrate a complete 3D spherical structure of Pt- FeCoNiCuCr@HCS, with HEA nanoparticles attach on the sur- face of carbon shell. In HR-TEM images (Figures S22c,d,S u p - portingInformation),thedistinctcrystallatticecanbeattributed to the well-maintained (111) facet of PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS. In addition, EDS result exhibits all the elements of Pt, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu and Cr in PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS after the durability test (FigureS23,SupportingInformation).Notably,theFelementde- tectedinEDSisfromNaﬁon,whichisalsoevidentintheX-ray photoelectronspectroscopy(XPS)(Figure S24,SupportingInfor- mation).However,onlyPtandCuelementsaredistinctlyobserv- able in the XPS spectrum of PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS due to the interference from Naﬁon. Given that Pt and Cu constitute the fundamentalframeworkofPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS,theirpredom- inantmetallicstate(Figures S24b,c,SupportingInformation)fur- thercorroboratesthestructuralstabilityofPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS afterdurabilitytest. 2.3.AnalysisofElectronicConﬁguration The electronic conﬁguration surrounding the active sites is a comprehensiveoutcomeresultingfromtheinteractionsamongmultipleatoms,anditcouldbemodulatedbythetypeofmetal- licelement.Togainanin-depthunderstandingoftheimpactof Cr on electronic structure in high entropy materials, XPS spec- tra of both PtFeCoNiCu@HCS and PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS were recorded as shown in Figure5. In Pt 4f spectra (Figure 5a), the twopeaksofPtFeCoNiCu@HCSlocatedat71.0and74.4eVare assigned to Pt04f7/2and Pt04f5/2, respectively, conﬁrming the metallic state of Pt in this material. Notably, the binding en- ergy of Pt04f7/2and Pt04f5/2in PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS shows a negative shift toward 70.9 and 74.3 eV, respectively, indicat- ingareconstructionoftheelectronicconﬁgurationinPtFeCoN- iCuCr@HCSandmoreelectronsaregatheredatPtsiteafterin- corporatingCratomduetotherelativestrongabilityofPtatom for electron-trapping. In Fe 2p spectra (Figure 5b), the peaks at ≈709.6and720.2eVofPtFeCoNiCu@HCSareattributedtoFe0 2p3/2andFe02p1/2,respectively.WhileinPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS, thebindingenergyofFe02p3/2undergoesadecreaseto709.4eV and that of Fe02p1/2shifts to 720.1 eV, also suggesting an elec- tronicstructurereconﬁgurationandanelectronaccumulationon Fe site induced by Cr incorporation. Similarly, in Co 2p spectra (Figure5c),theCo02p3/2andCo02p1/2inPtFeCoNiCu@HCSat 779 and 793.7 eV, respectively, shift to lower binding energy of 778.7 and793.5 eVinPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS.TheNi02p3/2and Ni02p1/2(Figure5d) are away from that of 852.8 and 872.0 eV in PtFeCoNiCu@HCS approaching 852.7 and 871.9 eV, respec- tively,inPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS.InCu2pspectra(Figure 5e),Cu0 2p3/2(932.1eV)andCu02p1/2(951.8eV)inPtFeCoNiCu@HCS shiftto932.0and951.7eVinPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS,respectively, andthedominantpeaksimplythewell-maintainedmetallicstate ofCuinbothmaterials.ThedownshiftinbindingenergyofCo 2p, Ni 2p, and Cu 2p orbitals also indicates an increased elec- tron density surrounding Co, Ni, and Cu sites introduced by the incorporation of Cr, mainly due to their superior capabil- ity to capture electrons than Cr. Moreover, the two prominent peaksinCr2pspectrum(Figure 5f)ofPtFeCoNiCuCr@HCSat ≈577.2and586.8eVarefromCr3+2p3/2andCr3+2p1/2,respec- tively, providing evidence supporting the single chemical state of chromium, and suggesting a preference for Cr site to supply electrons within PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS, consequently resulting inadecreaseofelectrondensityaroundit.TheseXPSresultsre- veal the reconstruction of the electronic conﬁguration induced byCratomincorporation,wherelocalelectronsescapefromthe CrsiteandgraduallyaccumulateonadjacentPt,Fe,Co,Ni,and Cusites.ThiselectronredistributionmightaﬀecttheHERelec- trocatalyticperformanceasitcouldpromotetheoptimizationof adsorption/desorptionenergybetweenintermediatesandactive sites.[39] Densityfunctionaltheory(DFT)calculationswereperformed to further clarify the inﬂuence of Cr on the electronic conﬁg- uration of the metal sites within PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS and the intrinsic mechanism for HER performance enhancement. The atomic models of quinary PtFeCoNiCu@HCS material (FigureS25, Supporting Information) were constructed based ontheexperimentalﬁndingsdiscussedabove,andthe(111)crys- talplane(Figure S26,SupportingInformation)waschosenasthe adsorption/desorption surface for hydrogen intermediates tak- ingintoaccounttheXRDpatternandcrystalorientationobtained fromHR-TEMimage.Moreover,theatomicmodelsofthe(111) crystal plane for senary PtFeCoNiCuCr@HCS material Adv. Funct. Mater. 2025,35,2414554 ©2024Wiley-VCHGmbH 2414554(7of11) 16163028, 2025, 5, Downloaded from https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/adfm.202414554 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
