www.advenergymat.de www.advancedsciencenews.com© 2018 WILEY-VCH Verlag GmbH & Co. KGaA, Weinheim 1800575 (3 of 7) Information). The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern (Figure S4, Supporting Information) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) (Figure S5, Supporting Information) verify the chemical component of intermediate products. The scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images of NH 4VO 3, VO@GC, and V 8C7@ GC NSs/NF are presented in Figure 1b–d, respectively. The enlarged images in Figure 1f,g suggest that V 8C7@GC NSs are well-regulated networks consisting of one layer of interleave nanorods and carbon film is uniformly covered onto interweaved networks. The detailed representations of the carbon layers in Figure S6 (Supporting Information) intuitively prove that inter - weaved V 8C7 networks are enveloped by double-deck carbon layers, which not only provides large specific surface areas and plentiful pores in favor of the permeation of electrolyte but also adjusts the growth rate of V 8C7 crystal on behalf of the forma- tion of single crystal interweaved networks. Besides, nitrogen isothermal adsorption-desorption measurement of V 8C7 without carbon coating and V 8C7@GC was conducted and displayed in Figure S7 (Supporting Information). It is visible that the specific surface area increases from 14 to 34 m 2 g−1 after carbon coating, which contributes to improving the HER performances of V 8C7. For directly exploring the intrinsic electronic structure and forecasting the catalytic activities of cubic V 8C7 with different facets exposed, taking the typical (100), (110), and (111) as examples, DFT computation is conducted via establishing theo-retical model (Theoretical Section in the Supporting Informa-tion). The crystal structure of cubic V 8C7 is shown in Figure 2a and Table S1 (Supporting Information), in which C atom is octahedrally coordinated to neighboring V atoms. Owing to the diversely stoichiometric ratio of vanadium carbide, monodis-persed V 8C7 obtained via powerful ultrasonic is further exactly examined by XRD and all the diffraction peaks could be identi-fied to the cubic V 8C7 (JCPDS No. 89-1096) (Figure 2b). Specifi- cally, the typical diffraction peaks indexing to the (320), (520), and (542) planes confirm the successful synthesis of V 8C7. Figure 2c,d delivers the total density of states (DOS) and the corresponding crystal structures of different surfaces, from which we could determine that V 8C7 bulk and crystal exposed with different planes all exhibit typical metallic behavior, guar - anteeing the rapid migration of electron from the electrode surface to the inner crystal and further promoting the highly electrocatalytic performances (Figure S8, Supporting Infor - mation). The surface composition and electronic structure of V 8C7 are further surveyed by XPS since they play a key role in HER. The C V bond in 285.5 eV and V C band in 513.6 eV jointly prove the successful fabrication of vanadium carbide (Figure 2e,f).[19] Besides, no signal for vanadium oxide indi- cates that the V 8C7 is exempt from oxidation, which may be due to the fact that interweaved V 8C7 networks are enveloped by double-deck graphitized carbon films. Generally, HER activity could be described by the adsorption energy of H atom on this system. Thus, the Gibbs free energy of atomic hydrogen (∆G H*) on the surface is usually considered as an effective factor for evaluating HER activity and the optimal ∆G H* should approach to 0 eV.[20] Besides, it is proved that (110) surface has the smallest ∆G H* in contrast with those of (100) and (111) facets (Table S2 and Figure S9, Supporting Information). Sur - prisingly, the |∆G H*| of V 8C7@GC NSs/NF exposed with (110) Adv. Energy Mater. 2018, 1800575 Figure 2. a) The structure of V 8C7, brown and red spheres denote C and V atoms, respectively. b) XRD pattern of the V 8C7@GC NSs (which is obtained via ultrasonic treatment for precise representation). c) DOS of V 8C7, exposed with (100), (111), and (110) planes, respectively. The Fermi energy is set to zero. d) The (100), (111), and (110) surfaces of V 8C7 crystal. XPS spectra of e) C 1s and f) V 2p in V 8C7@GC NSs/NF.
