A novel K 2Ti8O17nanorod photocatalyst rich in surface OH groups for efﬁcient hydrogen production by water splitting Zhuobin Yua,b, Ruiyi Wanga,**, Jianfeng Jiac, Yong Yuand, Eric R. Waclawike, Zhanfeng Zhenga,* aState Key Laboratory of Coal Conversion, Institute of Coal Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences, Taiyuan, 030001, China bTaiyuan University of Technology, Taiyuan, 030024, China cThe School of Chemical and Material Science, Shanxi Normal University, Linfen, 041004, Shanxi, China dXi 'an Thermal Power Research Institute Co. Ltd, Xi 'an, 710032, China eSchool of Chemistry, Physics and Mechanical Engineering, Queensland University of Technology, Brisbane, QLD, 4000, Australia article info Article history: Received 28 May 2018 Received in revised form 23 July 2018Accepted 28 July 2018Available online xxx Keywords: K 2Ti8O17 PhotocatalystWater splittingTreatment temperatureHydroxyl radicalabstract K2Ti8O17nanorods have been synthesized by hydrothermal method and its photocatalytic performance of water splitting under UV irradiation was studied in detail. The photo-catalytic activity has been studied in the light of the effect of metal nanoparticles deco-ration and interface structure. The hydrogen evolution rate of K 2Ti8O17sample modiﬁed by Pt cocatalyst is approximately 390.17 mmol mol catalyst/C01h/C01, which is about 300 times higher than that of bare K 2Ti8O17photocatalyst. Remarkably, the amount of surface OH groups over K 2Ti8O17samples have a positive correlation with the hydrogen evolution rate, which resulted from various thermal annealing temperature. It is suggested that more hydroxyl radical generated over K 2Ti8O17sample result in higher holes consumption via methanol oxidation reaction, which favors the electron-hole separation and thereby exhibit betterphotocatalytic performance for hydrogen evolution. ©2018 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. Introduction Nowadays, a promising member of novel functional material family, namely, potassium titanate with the general formula of A 2TinO2nþ1(1<n<9), has received more and more atten- tion as an important inorganic material in view of variablestructure [1], excellent mechanical properties [2], special functional properties and biocompatibility [3e5]. Interest- ingly, the chemical behavior and structures for potassiumtitanate is different when the “n”is changed, such as, K 2Ti2O5 and K 2Ti4O9have layered structure while K 2Ti6O13and K2Ti8O17have tunnel structures [6]. Typically, K 2Ti8O17,a s displayed in Fig. 1 a, was synthesized ﬁrstly by Tournoux [7], *Corresponding author . State Key Laboratory of Coal Conversion Institute of Coal Chemistry, Chinese Academy of Sciences, P. O. Box 165, Taiyuan, Shanxi, 030001, PR China. **Corresponding author . E-mail addresses: wangruiyi@sxicc.ac.cn (R. Wang), zfzheng@sxicc.ac.cn (Z. Zheng). Available online at www.sciencedirect.com ScienceDirect journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/he international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2018) 1 e10 https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.07.169 0360-3199/ ©2018 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. Please cite this article in press as: Yu Z, et al., A novel K 2Ti8O17nanorod photocatalyst rich in surface OH groups for efﬁcient hydrogen production by water splitting, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2018), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.07.169
