which has been used as reinforcement materials, frictional materials [8], potassium-ion batteries [9], and photocatalyst due to its stable tunnel structure and chemical stability [10]. Furthermore, K 2Ti8O17sample can transformed to octahedral anatase titania particles, which acts as photocatalyst for oxidative decomposition of acetic acid and water splitting[11e16]. At present, K 2Ti8O17has been successfully prepared by many methods, including solid state calcination method[7,9,17] , ﬂux growth [6,18,19] , microwave assisted high tem- perature method [20], melt reaction [21], and hydrothermal reaction [22,23] . To our knowledge, the photocatalytic per- formance of K 2Ti8O17photocatalyst has not been reported for water splitting so far, which is important to be applied in theﬁeld of renewable energy [24,25] . In this work, K 2Ti8O17nanorods were prepared by low-cost hydrothermal process, and its photocatalytic performance was evaluated for water splitting. Meantime, the effect ofnoble metals decoration on the photocatalytic performance ofK 2Ti8O17samples was discussed. More importantly, it is found that the hydrothermal treatment introduces a great deal ofhydroxyl groups on the photocatalyst surface, which hassigniﬁcant effect on the photocatalytic performance ofK 2Ti8O17.Experimental section Sample preparation All chemicals were purchased from Sinopharm ChemicalReagent Co. Ltd without further puriﬁcation. K 2Ti8O17(labeled as KTO) sample was synthesized by hydrothermal method. Inbrief, titanium oxysulfate (TiOSO 4$xH2O, 1.92 g) were added to aqueous KOH solution (1, 3, 5, 7, and 9 M, 50 mL) and keptstirring for 1 h. Then, the solution was placed in a PTFE-linedautoclave (100 mL in volume) for 24 h at 180 /C14C. After the hydrothermal processing, products were washed with deionized water until pH ¼7 and then washed with ethanol, collected by centrifugation, and dried in air at 80/C14C for 12 h. To investigate the effect of treatment temperature, KTO wascalcined in air at 300, 500, 700, and 900 /C14C for 2 h with a heating rate of 5/C14C/min, which were labeled as KTO-300, KTO-500, KTO-700, and KTO-900, respectively. To investi-gate the samples stability, KTO was placed in NaOH solution(5 M, 20 mL) or HCl solution (6 M, 20 mL) with stirringcontinuously for 12 h, then washed by Milli-Q water and driedat 80 /C14C overnight. Fig. 1 e(a) Crystal structure, (b) XRD patterns, (c) UV-vis-DR spectra and (d) schematic diagram of the DOS of K 2Ti8O17(KTO) sample.international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2018) 1 e10 2 Please cite this article in press as: Yu Z, et al., A novel K 2Ti8O17nanorod photocatalyst rich in surface OH groups for efﬁcient hydrogen production by water splitting, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2018), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.07.169
