Characterization The X-Ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on a MiniFlex II diffractometer with Cu K aradiation ( l¼1.5418 /C23A). The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and energy-dispersive spectroscopy (EDS) were operated 200 kV (JEM-2100F). Nitrogen sorption isotherms were carried out throughan automatic adsorption instrument (Micromeritics, TriStar II3020 analyser) operated at liquid nitrogen temperature (77 K).The UV-vis diffuse reﬂectance (UV-vis-DR) spectrometer (Shimadzu UV-3600) was used to obtain UV-vis-DR spectra of samples. The X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) were per-formed using a Thermo ESCALAB 250 spectrometer with an AlKaX-ray source (h n¼1486.6 eV). The binding energy was calibrated using the C1s peak at 284.6 eV. Photocatalytic performance evaluation for water splitting The photocatalytic H 2generated for water splitting was eval- uated in a closed gas circulation device. In brief, 100 mg photocatalyst, 200 mL methanol aqueous solution (containing15% methanol in volume), and proper amounts of metal pre-cursors Pt (H 2PtCl 6), were mixed under N 2atmosphere for in situ platinum photodeposition and carried out at room tem-perature under magnetic stirring. Other metallic cocatalystwere generated in the similar manner by Au (HAuCl 4), Cu (Cu(NO 3)2), Co (Co(NO 3)2), or Ag (AgNO 3) instead of H 2PtCl 6, respectively. A 300 W Xe lamp (CEL-HXF300, Beijing ChinaEducation Au-light Co., Ltd) (320 <l<420 nm), I ¼50 mW/cm 2 was used as light source. During the reaction, the gas samples were analyzed by gas chromatography (GC-7920, Beijing China Education Au-light Co., Ltd). Results and discussion The XRD patterns of as-prepared samples in different levels ofaqueous KOH solution are shown in Fig. S1 . It is observed clearly that the concentration of aqueous KOH solution has asigniﬁcant effect on the formation of products. The XRD pat- terns of samples generated without adding KOH solution and in 1 M KOH solution could be indexed as reported data TiO 2 (ICDD PDF card No. 21-1272) and K 2Ti4O9(ICDD PDF card No.27-0447), respectively. On the other hand, the XRD patternsof samples generated in aqueous KOH solution (3 M or 5 M)could match well with published data K 2Ti8O17(ICDD PDF card No. 41-1100). In addition, it is obviously found that the XRDpatterns of samples generated in KOH solution (7 M and 9 M)are different with samples generated in KOH solution (3 M and5 M), which indicates that there present other characteristicdiffraction speaks besides the pattern of K 2Ti8O17(ICDD PDF card No.41-1100) in the XRD pattern of samples generated in 7 M or 9 M KOH solution contains. The XRD pattern and reﬁnement proﬁles of K 2Ti8O17(KTO) sample, synthesized in 5 M KOH solution by hydrothermalmethod, are shown in Fig. 1 b, and the structure reﬁnements based on X-ray data are summarized in Table S1 . The good ﬁts are obtained with residuals factor R p¼11.33% and weighted residuals factor Rwp¼8.41%, which reveals that the XRD pattern could well indexed as K 2Ti8O17(monoclinic phase,space group C2/m, lattice parameters a¼15.678 /C23A,b¼3.775/C23A, c¼11.951 /C23A), which is consistent with the reported data (ICDD PDF card No. 41-1100) even though it possess poor crystal-linity. The grain size is approximately 13.5 nm correspondingto (020) plane on the basis of the Scherer 's equation. The physicochemical properties of KTO (5 M NaOH) sample wouldbe conducted for further study owing to the pure phase ofK 2Ti8O17. The KTO sample is stable when calcined at elevated tem- peratures or treated in acid and basic solutions. It is found thatthe formation of products strongly depend on treatmenttemperature ( Fig. S2 ). As a consequence, there is no obviously difference in XRD patterns of samples treated with below 500 /C14C except that the peaks attributed to ( /C0204) and (400) plane for KTO-500 would occur. It is also observed that KTOshould transform into other titanate phases when increasingtreat temperature. Obviously, the characteristic diffraction ofKTO sample treated at 700 /C14C could be indexed as published data K 2Ti2O5(ICDD PDF card No.51-1890) with strong crystal- linity, which indicates that phase transformation occurs fromK 2Ti8O17to K 2Ti2O5when treatment temperature increases from 500 to 700/C14C. Likewise, there is other diffraction char- acteristic peak of titanate (K 2Ti4O9) besides K 2Ti2O5in sample treated at 900/C14C. The results demonstrate treatment tem- perature above 500/C14C on samples could result in the change of crystal lattice parameters. There is no signiﬁcant difference inXRD patterns of samples when KTO is treated by 5 M NaOH or6 M HCl ( Fig. S3 ), which presents a good stability of tunnel-like K 2Ti8O17. The UV-vis-DR spectra of KTO sample is displayed in Fig. 1 c. It is observed that there is the strong absorption in UV region with the absorption edge located at approximately420 nm. The band gap (Eg) of semiconductive KTO photo-catalyst is about 3.28 eV calculated by the following formula, a(hn)¼A (hv-Eg) 1/2, where ais the absorption coefﬁcient, h nis the photon energy and A is a constant [18]. As shown in Fig. 1 d, the corresponding valence band positon and conduction bandposition of KTO photocatalyst is located at 2.64 eV and/C00.64 eV, respectively, which is theoretically speculated using the equation related to Mulliken electronegativity (SupportingInformation). The transmission electron microscope (TEM) and high resolution TEM (HRTEM) images of KTO sample are shown inFig. 2 . It is obviously observed that there are a large number of nanorod whose diameter is in the range between 2 nm and6 nm and length varies from 50 to 120 nm in Fig. 2 a. The lattice spacing is 0.78 nm and 0.31 nm, which corresponding to (200) plane and (310) plane of KTO in Fig. 2 b, respectively, which agrees with the XRD results in Fig. 1 b. The survey and high resolution X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS) of KTO sample was displayed in Fig. 3 . The characteristic peaks of K 1s locate at 292.1 eV and 294.9 eVcould be attributed to potassium exists in the form of K þwhile the characteristic peaks of Ti 2p 3/2at 457.5 and Ti 2p 1/2 463.5 eV could belong to Ti4þin the titanate [26,27] . In addition, in the O 1s XPS spectra of KTO sample, deconvoluted threecharacteristic peak at approximately 529.3 eV, 531.3 eV and 532.9 eV, were classiﬁed into O eTi species, surface hydroxyl (OH) species and loosely bound oxygen on the surface,respectively [28e30]. The results are in accordance with theinternational journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2018) 1 e10 3 Please cite this article in press as: Yu Z, et al., A novel K 2Ti8O17nanorod photocatalyst rich in surface OH groups for efﬁcient hydrogen production by water splitting, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2018), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.07.169
