noticed that Pt/KTO photocatalyst exhibit the highest photo- catalytic activity for hydrogen evolution, and there is a smalldifference between Pt/KTO photocatalyst and Au/KTO pho-tocatalyst. In contrast, there is an apparently decrease for Cu/ KTO and relatively lower level for Ag/KTO, and Co/KTO sam- ples in the hydrogen evolution rate. The similar phenomenahave been reported elsewhere [12,31] , which could be related to that chemically stable Pt and Au would exist as the puremetal (Pt 0and Au0) while Cu, Ag and Co should remain both metallic and oxidized forms that could correlate with highoverpotential [12,31,32] . Moreover, the other reason that de- termines the hydrogen evolution rate for KTO photocatalystmodiﬁed metal cocatalyst should be attributed to their dif-ference in work function [11]. The photocatalytic activity for water splitting, which depends on the content of Pt cocatalyst in Pt/KTO photocatalyst, is illustrated in Fig. 4 b. It is noted that there is a remarkable difference in the rate of hydrogen evo-lution between bare KTO photocatalyst and KTO modiﬁedwith Pt cocatalyst. As a result, the optimum content of Pt is3 wt. % Pt in Pt/KTO photocatalyst, which exhibit the highesthydrogen evolution rate and is about 300 times high than bare KTO photocatalyst. The cocatalyst of Pt play an important rolein capturing electron and decreasing over-potential forhydrogen over-potential [33]. Simultaneously, the 3 wt.% Pt/ KTO nanoparticles photocatalyst are further analyzed in (Fig. S4 ), which indicates that mean nanoparticle size of deposited Pt cocatalyst is approximately 2.41 nm. The hydrogen evolution rate for KTO photocatalyst is about 390.17 mmol mol catalyst/C01h/C01(0.53 mmol/g catalyst /h) and there is little difference in hydrogen evolution rate compared withKTO (3 M) ( Fig. S5 ). Meanwhile, it is found that the catalytic performance KTO sample is quite outstanding as P25(690.05 mmol mol catalyst/C01h/C01, namely 8.63 mmol/g catalyst /h, Fig. S6 ) in the hydrogen evolution rate for water splitting. On the contrast, the hydrogen evolution rate for K 2Ti4O9(1 M) and K2Ti8O17(7 M) including K 2Ti8O17and other phase is 9.63 and 221.88 mmol mol catalyst/C01h/C01(Fig. S5 ), respectively. In a word, it is observed that differences do exist in hydrogen evolutionrate for samples synthesized in different KOH solution, whichshould account for the concentration of KOH solution has a Fig. 4 e(a) The H 2evolution rate over KTO photocatalyst modiﬁed with metal, (b) The effect of Pt loading on the H 2evolution rate under UV irradiation. Fig. 5 e(a) The H 2evolution rate of Pt/KTO, Pt/KTO-300 and Pt/KTO-500, (b) Recyclability and stability of Pt/KTO photocatalyst for water splitting.international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2018) 1 e10 5 Please cite this article in press as: Yu Z, et al., A novel K 2Ti8O17nanorod photocatalyst rich in surface OH groups for efﬁcient hydrogen production by water splitting, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2018), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.07.169
