decisive role in determining the formation of products shown in (Fig. S1 ). The results indicate that crystal structure of samples determine the difference of photocatalytic activityfor water splitting. In addition, there are minor discrepanciesin hydrogen evolution rate between KTO and KTO treated with HCl or NaOH solution in ( Fig. S5 ), which ascertains the good stability of KTO photocatalyst with tunnel structure. The effect of calcination temperature on the KTO sample prepared in 5 M KOH solution was further studied since itshighest hydrogen evolution rate. It is apparently found thatthe hydrogen evolution rate for water splitting decreases withincreasing post-treatment temperature, which follows in theorder of KTO-500 <KTO-300 <KTO. The hydrogen evolution rate of KTO is 400 times larger than KTO-500. There isnegligible difference in the hydrogen evolution rate for Pt/KTO photocatalyst and recycled photocatalyst in Fig. 5 b, which indicated photocatalytic deactivation is not presented and the photocatalytic performance is maintained after foursuccessive cycles. It is observed that there are apparentlydiscrepancies in the hydrogen evolution rate for water split- ting between KTO, KTO-300 and KTO-500 photocatalyst indespite of the minor differences in S BET, pore volume, and band gap ( Table 1 ). Meanwhile, it is found that the catalytic performance KTO sample could not outweigh P25 (690.05 mmol mol catalyst/C01h/C01, namely 8.63 mmol/g catalyst /h, Fig. S6 ) in the hydrogen evolution rate for water splitting. Even though, the results indicate that K 2Ti8O7has become a new member of photocatalysts for photocatalytic watersplitting. Furthermore, K 2Ti8O17with unique tunnel-like present good stability when it is treated by 5 M NaOH or6 M HCl ( Fig. S3 ). What is more, interface structure of K 2Ti8O17 could be regulated easily by thermal annealing treatment, which would determine in photocatalytic activity for watersplitting. Given that treatment temperature plays a