It can be observed obviously that there are difference in the valence band (VB) maximum energy among KTO, KTO-300,KTO-500, which is 0.53 eV and 0.63 eV more positive forKTO-300 and KTO-500 than KTO in Fig. 6 a, respectively [34].I n addition, the O1s XPS spectra of KTO sample could arecomposed of three characteristic peak at approximately529.3 eV, 531.3 eV and 532.9 eV, which could be ascribed to O e Ti species, surface hydroxyl (OH) species, loosely bound oxy-gen on the surface, respectively [28,29] .I nFig. 6 b, the O1s at round 532.9 eV of KTO-300 and KTO-500 samples disappears,which could be attributed to remove the surface adsorbed water with increasing calcination temperature [35]. Moreover, the intensity of the characteristic O1s at 531.3 eV decreaseswith increasing treatment temperature for KTO samples, aslisted in Table 1 , which indicates the amount of surface OH groups decrease in the order of KTO (55 at.%) >KTO-300 (45 at.%) >KTO-500 (35 at.%), which follows a similar trend interms of hydrogen evolution rate for water splitting. The re- sults indicate the amount of surface OH groups have a positive correlation with the hydrogen evolution rate, which agreeswith reported literature [28]. The reasons could be attributed to that photocatalytic oxidation of methanol by means of thedirect hole oxidation mechanism [36,37] , as shown in Fig. 9 . The results reveal more amounts of surface OH groups overKTO photocatalyst would generate more OH radical (hydroxylradical) by the reaction between OH group and photo-generating hole, which is beneﬁcial to prevent photo-generating electron and hole from recombining. Conse-quently, KTO sample exhibit better photocatalytic perfor- mance for water splitting under UV radiation. The photoluminescence (PL) spectra of KTO samples is shown inFig. 6 c, the emission peak at 390 and 470 nm should be assigned to the emission of the band gap transition and thecharge transfer transition of an oxygen vacancy trappedelectron [38,39] . The intensity of PL increase in the following order: KTO-500 >KTO-300 >KTO, which further conﬁrms that the charge separation is weakened with increasing treatmenttemperature. In addition, the hydroxyl radical over KTOsamples is analyzed by terephthalic acid as the trapping agentto react with hydroxyl radical to generate 2- hydroxyterephthalic acid ( Scheme S1 in the Supporting In- formation). The photoluminescence (PL) emission spectra ofﬂuorescent 2-hydroxyterephthalic acid (HTA) produced isdisplayed in Fig. 6 d. It is clearly found that the amount of hydroxyl radical of KTO photocatalyst is far greater than thatof KTO-300 and KTO-500, and decreases in the order ofKTO >KTO-300 >KTO-500, which is in accordance with the amount of surface OH groups over KTO photocatalyst. Why has KTO sample more OH groups? Literature show that the amount of the oxygen vacancy over photocatalyst hasa positive correlation with the content of OH groups over photocatalyst by adsorbing water [40], therefore, it is neces- sary to detect the amount of the oxygen vacancy. It is wellestablished that oxygen vacancy is easily hunted by theadsorbed molecular oxygen to generate superoxide radicalanion (O 2/C15⁻), then nitroblue tetrazolium (NBT) is chosen to analyze the production of O 2/C15⁻(Scheme S2 in the Supporting Fig. 7 e(a) UV-vis-DR spectra of NBT with KTO, KTO-300 and KTO-500 samples. Fig. 8 eIn situ IR spectra of KTO showing the surface OH regeneration ability: (a) KTO sample, (b) KTO sample with water under UV radiation.international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2018) 1 e10 7 Please cite this article in press as: Yu Z, et al., A novel K 2Ti8O17nanorod photocatalyst rich in surface OH groups for efﬁcient hydrogen production by water splitting, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2018), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.07.169
