Information) [41], which is listed in Fig. 7 . The result reveals that the increasing maximum absorbance at 259 nm in the UV-vis-DR spectra of NBT over KTO samples in the order of KTO <KTO-300 <KTO-500, which indicates that KTO possess more oxygen vacancy than KTO-300 and KTO-500 and agreeswith the above results [42,43] . Thus, KTO photocatalyst with more OH groups could be attributed to that it has more oxygenvacancy and more easily absorbs water. Moreover, theinfrared spectroscopy (IR) is carried out to conﬁrm the amountof surface OH groups over KTO samples ( Fig. S10 ). It is shown that there exist different peak intensity at around 3200 cm /C01 and 1650 cm/C01, which assigned to the O eH stretching modes and O eH bending modes, respectively, while no signiﬁcant change was observed in other frequency regions [44]. The in- tensity at 3200 cm/C01and 1650 cm/C01decrease with increasing heat treatment temperature, which indicates that the amountof surface OH groups agrees with the XPS results ( Fig. 6 b). The intensities of the characteristic OH group absorption bands (3200 cm /C01and 1650 cm/C01) gradually decreases as a function of increasing irradiation time without markedly di-versity of other absorption peaks, and it is also observed thatthe absorption peak at 1650 cm /C01disappears under 20 min of irradiation in Fig. 8 a. On the contrary, the absorption peaks at about 3200 cm/C01and 1650 cm/C01almost remain unchanged as a function of increasing irradiation time without markedly difference in other absorption peaks in Fig. 8 b, which conﬁrms that KTO sample has good surface OH regeneration abilitywhen KTO sample is contact with water under UV irradiation.Therefore, KTO photocatalyst could remain photocatalyticactivity for water splitting, which is consistent with the re-sults in Fig. 5 b. On the basis of the above results, the possible photo- catalytic mechanism for water splitting is shown in Fig. 9 . Under illumination, the photo-generating electron would beexcited from the valence band to the conduction band of KTO photocatalyst and transfer to Pt cocatalyst deposited on KTOsample, which could result in H þreduction to H 2. Further- more, no hydrogen is detected when water is replaced by acetonitrile or pure methanol solution, which is consistent with previous publications [31]. The results indicate that the Hþion comes from H 2O by means of reacting H 2O with photo- generated hole. Meantime, KTO photocatalyst with more ox-ygen vacancy would generate more OH groups easily by wateradsorption. Then, there occurs that the reaction between thephoto-generating hole in the valence band and OH group overKTO photocatalyst to form hydroxyl radical. Finally, hydroxylradical reacted with CH 3OH (sacriﬁcial agent) to generate CO, CH4, and CO 2(detected by GC analysis as shown in Fig. S11 ) with $CH2OH radical as the intermediate. In this process, the desorbed $CH2OH radical intermediate over KTO would be ruptured and recombined to produce CO and CH 4, while the absorbed $CH2OH radical may further oxidized to produce formic acid and ﬁnally CO 2,C H 4[31,32,45] . There is obvious difference in the hydrogen evolution rate for water splittingamong KTO, KTO-300 and KTO-500 samples, which could ac-count for the various amount of surface OH groups. Thereason could be attributed to that more amount of surface OHgroups over KTO photocatalyst would generate more hydroxylradical, which is beneﬁcial to promote the reaction of hy-droxyl radical and CH 3OH to form CO, CH 4,C O 2. The amount of surface OH groups over photocatalyst have a positive rela- tionship with the hydrogen evolution rate for water splitting.Consequently, KTO photocatalyst exhibited better photo-catalytic performance for water splitting under UV radiation. Conclusions In summary, K 2Ti8O17nanorods material has been prepared by hydrothermal method and its applicability to photo- catalytic hydrogen evolution was studied for the ﬁrst time. The concentration of KOH solution and 