A phosphorene-like InP 3monolayer: structure, stability, and catalytic properties toward the hydrogen evolution reaction † Abdul Jalil,aZhiwen Zhuo,aZhongti Sun,aFang Wu,bChuan Wang *c and Xiaojun Wu *a Exploring a new phase of two-dimensional (2D) materials with novel electronic and catalytic properties is of particular importance for both fundamental research and practical applications in clean energy. Herein, wereport a new phase of the InP 3monolayer with a phosphorene-like structure for the hydrogen evolution reaction on the basis of density functional theory calculations. Our results demonstrate that 2D phosphorene-like InP 3(P-InP 3) can be obtained by exfoliating the (0 /C01 1) surface of the InP 3bulk with a cleavage energy of 1.08 –1.37 J m/C02, which is more stable energetically than the 2D graphene-like InP 3 (G-InP 3) monolayer by /C00.17 eV per atom. The P-InP 3monolayer is a semiconductor with a direct band gap of 0.53 eV and anisotropic carrier mobility, while its bilayer and trilayer are metallic. In particular, theP-InP 3monolayer, bilayer and trilayer show high catalytic activity toward the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) comparable to Pt and 1T0-MoS 2on the basis of the calculated Gibbs free energy. Besides, the G-InP 3bilayer and trilayer also exhibit similar catalytic activity. The calculation of the microkinetic process of the HER indicates that both Volmer –Tafel and Volmer –Heyrovsky reactions are possible, but the latter one is more feasible for both P-InP 3and G-InP 3. The P-InP 3monolayer is structurally dynamically stable, con ﬁrmed with the calculated phonon spectrum and Born –Oppenheimer molecular dynamics simulation in an aqueous environment at 300 K. These ﬁndings imply the great potential of the 2D P-InP 3monolayer in electronics, optoelectronics, and catalytic HER. 1. Introduction Since the discovery of graphene, two-dimensional (2D) mate- rials, such as h-BN, transition metal disul des (TMDs), ger- manene, phosphorene, carbon nitride, borophene, and silicene,have been investigated intensively due to their inimitableproperties and promising applications in electronics, spin-tronics, and catalysis. 1–15In particular, diverse phase structures of 2D materials with tunable electronic and magnetic properties signicantly extend their applications. For instance, 2D TMDs,such as MoS 2,W S 2, and ReS 2, could exhibit trigonal prismatic (H), octahedral (T), or distorted octahedral phase structures.16–20 Various phase structures of 2D materials can be obtained by cleaving di ﬀerent surfaces of their bulk counterpart or con- verted into each other viacarrier doping or strain. With diﬀerent phase structures, 2D TMD monolayers could exhibit metallic, semiconducting, or magnetic behavior, which can beused for electronics, spintronics, and catalysis. 16–18,21Thus, it is of particular interest to explore new possible phase structureswith intriguing properties of 2D materials to develop 2Dmaterial-based nanotechnology. Indium phosphide (InP 3) is a layered bulk material, which wasrst synthesized in 1980s under high pressure and can be quenched to ambient conditions.22The InP 3bulk is metallic with a symmetry of R/C223m(Group No. 166), sharing the same structures as those of SnP 3and GeP 3, which can be derived from the structure of arsenic by systematically replacing every fourthatom in the As layer by a metal atom and the rest by phospho-rous. 22,23Very recently, 2D InP 3monolayers with a graphene-like (G-InP 3) structure have been theoretically predicted by exfoli- ating the layered InP 3bulk.24Diﬀerent from its bulk counter- part, the 2D G-InP 3monolayer is an indirect band gap semiconductor with high and isotropic electron mobility,implying its applications in electronics and optoelectronics. 24–26aHefei National Laboratory for Physical Sciences at the Microscale, CAS Key Laboratory of Materials for Energy Conversion, Synergetic Innovation of Quantum Information & Quantum Technology, School of Chemistry and Materials Sciences, University of Science and Technology of China, Hefei, Anhui 230026, China. E-mail: xjwu@ustc.edu.cn bCollege of Information Science and Technology, Nanjing Forestry University, Nanjing, Jiangsu 210037, P. R. China cInstitute of Advanced Synthesis, School of Chemistry and Molecular Engineering, Jiangsu National Synergetic Innovation Center for Advanced Materials, Nanjing TechUniversity, Nanjing 211816, Jiangsu, P. R. China. E-mail: ias_cwang@njtech.edu.cn †Electronic supplementary information (ESI) available: Exfoliation energy calculation, phonon spectrum, molecular dynamics simulation, deformation charge density, electronic band structures, elastic constants, and adsorption energy of hydrogen. See DOI: 10.1039/c9ta08612aCite this: DOI: 10.1039/c9ta08612a Received 7th August 2019 Accepted 5th December 2019 DOI: 10.1039/c9ta08612a rsc.li/materials-a This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2019 J. Mater. Chem. AJournal of Materials Chemistry A PAPER Published on 09 December 2019. Downloaded by UNIVERSITY OF NEBRASKA on 1/2/2020 9:38:51 PM. View Article Online View Journal
