In addition, theoretical studies have shown that the G-InP 3 monolayer can be used as a reversible anode material for ultrafast charging lithium or sodium ion batteries and a multi-function device containing both photovoltaic and thermoelec-tric characteristics at the same time. 25–28 In this work, however, we reported a 2D phosphorene-like InP 3(P-InP 3) monolayer by cleaving the (0 /C01 1) surface of the InP 3bulk based on rst-principles calculations. Our results demonstrate for the rst time that P-InP 3is more energetically stable than the G-InP 3monolayer. The P-InP 3monolayer is a semiconductor with a direct band gap of 0.53 eV and aniso-tropic behavior in carrier mobility, while its bilayer and trilayerare metallic. Both phonon spectrum and Born –Oppenheimer molecular dynamics simulation at a temperature of 300 Kconrm the structural stability of the P-InP 3monolayer. In particular, the P-InP 3monolayer, bilayer, and trilayer as well as the G-InP 3bilayer and trilayer exhibit higher catalytic activity toward the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), comparable to Pt and 1T0-MoS 2monolayers. 2. Methods Allrst-principles calculations are performed by using the density functional theory (DFT) method implemented in the plane-wave basis Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP).29,30The projector augmented wave (PAW) method is applied to describe the electron –ion interaction. The plane- wave energy cuto ﬀis 500 eV. The generalized gradient approx- imation of Perdew –Burke –Ernzerhof31(PBE) with correction of the optB88-vdW method and DFT + D3 method is applied todeal with the long-range vdW interactions. The rst Brillouin zone integration using the Monkhorst –Pack scheme 32is sampled with a k-point of 7 /C27/C21 and 14 /C214/C21 for opti- mization of the P-InP 3and G-InP 3structure, respectively, while it is 6 /C26/C21 and 4 /C24/C21 for the upper limit value of exfo- liation energy calculations with a supercell of 1 /C21/C24 and 1 /C2 1/C22 for the P-InP 3and G-InP 3structure with the frozen atom model, respectively. Note that pure DFT always underestimatesthe band gap of the semiconductor; the Heyd, Scuseria, and Ernzerhof screened hybrid density functional method 33(HSE06) with dispersion correction within the optB88-vdW method isused 34,35for the calculation of the band structure, optical properties and electron hole motilities. The force convergence isset to 0.01 eV /C23A /C01. The vacuum size is chosen as 20 /C23A. The phonon, infrared (IR) and Raman spectra are calculated byusing the nite displacement method implemented in the CASTEP 36package. The HER performance is evaluated with the free energy of adsorbed hydrogen atom under equilibrium conditions,37,38 which is de ned as DGH¼Eads+DZPE/C0TDS (1) where Eadsis the adsorption energy of the hydrogen atom, DZPE is the change of zero-point energy of absorbed H and that of freeH 2(g),TDSis the di ﬀerence of entropy of absorbed H and that of H2(g). We employ the ZPE and TS of free H 2gas molecule fromprevious work by NØrskov et al. ,39while for adsorbed H, the free energy corrections were calculated using the corresponding formula including ZPE and Sobtained from T. Vegge's work40 using vibrational frequency based on the harmonic approxi- mation. The Eads,DZPE and TDSare calculated with the following equations Eads¼E*H/C0ES/C01/2EH2(2) DZPE¼ZPE *H/C01/2ZPE H2(3) TDS¼TSvi-*H/C01/2TSH2(4) where E*Hand ESrepresent the total energy of InP 3with and without adsorbed H, respectively, and EH2is the energy of the hydrogen molecule in the gas phase. ZPE *Hand ZPE H2represent the zero-point energy of absorbed H and that of free H 2(g), respectively. TS vi-*His the vibrational entropy of absorbed H while TSH2is the entropy of H 2(g) of 0.41 eV.39 3. Results and discussion 2D layered structures can be cleaved from their bulk materials by various physical techniques, such as liquid and mechanical exfoliation.41P-InP 3can be obtained by exfoliating the (0 /C011 ) surface of the InP 3bulk. As shown in Fig. 1a and b, the opti- mized structures imply that P-InP 3has a phosphorene-like structure,42while G-InP 3possesses a buckled structure.25Both of them can be considered as an arrangement of In atoms andP 6rings in a 2D lattice. The relaxed structure of the P-InP 3monolayer belongs to the space group C2/m(No. 12) with lattice parameters of a¼8.15/C23A andb¼7.56/C23A and thickness of the P-InP 3monolayer dz¼2.68/C23A. The G-InP 3monolayer belongs the space group P3m1 (No. 164) with an optimized lattice constant of a¼b¼7.52/C23A, and the thickness is dz¼1.190 /C23A, agreeing well with previous result.24The In–P bond lengths range from 2.64 to 2.66 /C23A, and P –Pb o n d lengths range from 2.22 to 2.27 /C23A. In comparison, the In –Pa n d P–P bond lengths in the G-InP 3monolayer are 2.59 and 2.24 /C23A, similar to previously reported values.25In addition, the structural parameters are calculated with the HSE06 + optB88-vdW method, as summarized in Table S1 (see ESI †), and are similar with those results with the PBE + optB88-vdW method. Interestingly, the Fig. 1 (a) and (b) are the top and side views of G-InP 3and P-InP 3 monolayers, respectively. (c) The exfoliation of G-InP 3and P-InP 3from the InP 3bulk, respectively. Blue and green balls represent P and In atoms, respectively. J. Mater. Chem. A This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2019Journal of Materials Chemistry A Paper Published on 09 December 2019. Downloaded by UNIVERSITY OF NEBRASKA on 1/2/2020 9:38:51 PM. View Article Online
