formation energy of P-InP 3is smaller than that of G-InP 3by 0.17 eV per atom, particularly due to the bilayer structure in the P- InP 3monolayer, which presents more long-distance interactions between In and P atoms (about 2.96 /C23A). To explore the possibility of fabricating the InP 3monolayer from its layered bulk, the exfoliation energy of P-InP 3from the (0/C01 1) surface of the InP 3bulk is calculated. Here, the exfoli- ation energy is calculated with di ﬀerent models, including the ideal exfoliation model,43which considers all structures fully relaxed to calculate the energy change to obtain an ideal value, and the frozen atom model, which keeps all atoms frozenduring the exfoliating process to evaluate the upper limit value.More details of the exfoliation energy calculation are shown inFig. S1 and Table S2 in the ESI. †The ideal exfoliation value of the P-InP 3monolayer is 1.08 J m/C02, while it is 1.10 J m/C02for G- InP 3, quite close to that of previous work with DFT + D3 (1.06 J m/C02).44The calculated upper limit value of exfoliation energy of P-InP 3is 1.38 J m/C02, larger than the ideal value of 0.30 J m/C02, but close to that of G-InP 3(1.32 J m/C02).24This value is also compa- rable to that of other layered materials, such as GeP 3(1.14 J m/C02),45CaP 3(1.30 J m/C02),46and GeS (0.52 J m/C02),47implying the great possibility of exfoliating the P-InP 3monolayer from its bulk. Additionally, the ideal exfoliation energy of the P-InP 3 monolayer exfoliated from its bilayer and trilayer is 0.97 and1.04 J m /C02, slightly smaller than that from its bulk (1.08 J m/C02). To investigate the lattice dynamic stability of the P-InP 3 monolayer, phonon dispersion calculation was performed by using the nite displacement method as implemented in CASTEP. The calculated phonon spectrum is displayed inFig. S2a (see the ESI †). There are 24 vibration modes in the P- InP 3monolayer with C2/m(No. 12) symmetry, including three acoustic and twenty-one optical branches with four modes ofvibrations Ag, Bg, Au, and Bu, as given below: G acoustic ¼Au + 2Bu Goptic¼7Ag + 4Au + 5Bg + 5Bu The three acoustic branches include the in-plane longitu- dinal acoustic (LA), transverse acoustic (TA) and out-of-planeacoustic (ZA) ones. The LA and TA branches have a lineardispersion and higher frequency than the ZA one around G. 48,49 The highest vibrational frequency is 427 cm/C01, which is comparable to that of the G-InP 3(430 cm/C01) structure (as shown in Fig. S2b †) and the reported values for GeP 3(480 cm/C01)45and phosphorene (450 cm/C01),50indicating the mechanical robust- ness of the bonding in P-InP 3. The Raman and IR spectra of the P-InP 3monolayer are calculated to reveal their interaction with light. The inelasticscattering of light is de ned by spatial derivatives of macro- scopic polarization, and the shi of phonon energy is de ned by vibrational frequency. 50The spatial derivatives of macroscopic polarization are obtained by calculating the polarization of each displacement with a linear response formalism method51 implemented in CASTEP. Among four modes of vibrations, Auand Bu vibration modes are IR active modes, while Ag and Bgare Raman active modes. The calculated Raman and IR inten- sity graphs of the P-InP 3monolayer are displayed in Fig. 2a and c. There are 12 and 9 active modes observed in the Raman andIR spectra. In the IR spectra, the ve most intense peaks are observed at frequencies of 106.58, 135.54, 158.14, 289.34 and340.84 cm /C01, respectively. For the Raman spectra, the most intense active modes are located at frequencies of 406.69,335.08, and 276.41 cm /C01. Besides, the calculated Raman and IR spectra of G-InP 3are shown in Fig. 2b and d, respectively. Two peaks in the IR spectrum at frequencies of 41.291 and 258.76 cm/C01and three peaks in the Raman spectrum at frequencies of 422.43, 289.27 and 91.76 cm/C01are observed, respectively. Therefore, P-InP 3and G-InP 3can be distinguished with the measured IR and Raman spectra in experiment. Next, Born –Oppenheimer dynamics (BOMD) simulation in the NVT ensemble was performed to verify the thermo-dynamicstability of the P-InP 3monolayer. The simulated temperature of the whole system is set as 300 and 500 K. The time step is 2 fs and the total time is 5 ps. A supercell, containing 48 P and 16 Inatoms, is employed. The structure snapshot at 5 ps simulationtime implies that P-InP 3could retain its phosphorene-like puckered structure at 500 K, suggesting the strong bondingcharacteristic in the P-InP 3monolayer as shown in Fig. S3. † The electron localization function (ELF)52is calculated to analyze the nature of bonding in both G-InP 3and P-InP 3mono- layers, as shown in Fig. 3. The electrons are localized around P atoms and delocalized around In atoms. Partial electron transferoccurs from In to P atoms, resulting covalent In –Pb o n d sw i t h minor ionicity. The deformation charge density of both P-InP 3 and G-InP 3structures present similar results of covalent bonding of In –Pb o n d sa n dP –P bonds, as shown in Fig. S4 (see the ESI †). Bader charge analysis53shows that In atoms transfer 0.82 e to P atoms and each P atom gains /C240.27 e in P-InP 3,w h i l eI na t o m s transfer 0.70 e to P-atom and each P atom receives 0.23 e in G- InP 3, as illustrated in Fig. S5 (see the ESI †). Fig. 4a displays the calculated electronic band structures of the P-InP 3monolayer. The P-InP 3monolayer is a direct band gap semiconductor. The calculated energy band gap is 0.014 eV Fig. 2 The calculated IR spectra of (a) P-InP 3and (b) G-InP 3and Raman spectra of (c) P-InP 3and (d) G-InP 3are displayed, respectively. This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2019 J. Mater. Chem. APaper Journal of Materials Chemistry A Published on 09 December 2019. Downloaded by UNIVERSITY OF NEBRASKA on 1/2/2020 9:38:51 PM. View Article Online
