with the PBE + optB88 method. Note that the PBE functional always underestimates the band gap of the semiconductor,which can be signi cantly improved with the hybrid HSE06 functional. Our HSE06 calculation implies that the energy bandgap of the P-InP 3monolayer is 0.53 eV. In contrast, the G-InP 3 monolayer is an indirect band gap semiconductor with anindirect band gap of 1.14 eV. 24The calculated density of states (DOS) and DOS projected on atoms indicate that the valencebands are mainly contributed by P atoms, while conductionbands are mainly contributed by In atoms around the Fermienergy level, as shown in Fig. 4b and S6 (see the ESI †). This is also con rmed from the plotted charge distribution pro les of the valence band minimum (VBM) and conduction band maximum (CBM), as shown in Fig. 4c. The energy band gap of the P-InP 3monolayer can be further modulated by applying external strain, as shown in Fig. S7 (see the ESI †). With an external strain ranging from /C05% to 5%, the electronic band structure of the P-InP 3monolayer retains its major character- istics, while the energy band gap varies from 0.22 to 0.70 eV, asillustrated in Fig. S7. †Note that the dynamic stability of the P- InP 3structure remains stable under 5% compression and tensile uniaxial strain, as shown in Fig. S7. † The calculated work function ( F)f o rt h eP - I n P 3monolayer is 5.15 eV ( vs.the VBM), comparable to that of black phosphorus42 (5.16 eV) and bilayer MoS 2structure54(5.12 eV). In addition, the predicted carrier mobility of P-InP 3shows an anistro pic behaviour, which is di ﬀe r e n tf r o mt h a to fG - I n P 3.24The carrier mobilitycalculation is based on the deformation potential theory (DPT) method,55which only considers the longitudinal acoustic (LA) phonon e ﬀect, which possibly results in overestimation.56The electron mobility in the zigzag direction (1229 cm2V/C01s/C01)i sa b o u t three times that along the armchair direction (364 cm2V/C01s/C01) and the hole mobilities in di ﬀerent directions are close to those of electrons. The electron mobility in the zigzag direction of P-InP 3is about 11 times that of G-InP 3while the hole mobility is smaller than that of G-InP 3.24The calculation details of carrier mobility are summarized in the ESI. † To shed light on the mechanical stability of G-InP 3and P- InP 3monolayers, their elastic properties are calculated, as summarized in Table S4. †The elastic constants satisfy the Born stability criteria57as C 11/C0C12>0 ,C 11+2 C 12> 0 and C 66>0 , indicating the mechanical stability of P-InP 3and G-InP 3 monolayers. The two dimensional Young's modulus of P-InP 3 along the xandydirections (Y 11and Y 22) is 28.1 and 58.5 N m/C01, respectively, slightly larger than those of phosphorene (21.9 and 56.3 N m/C01).58These values are lower than those of the MoS 2 monolayer (120 N m/C01)37and graphene (335.0 N m/C01).59,60 Besides, Y 11]Y22of G-InP 3is almost the average value of Y 11 and Y 22of P-InP 3. The calculated mechanical properties imply that both P-InP 3and G-InP 3monolayers are so 2D structures. Next, we consider the catalytic performance of G-InP 3and P- InP 3monolayers toward the HER. We consider all possible adsorbed con gurations of the H atom on G-InP 3and P-InP 3 monolayers, as shown in Fig. S9. †The most favorable adsorp- tion site is the top of the P atom for both P-InP 3and G-InP 3 monolayers since the P atom decreased the electron occupationof the d-orbital of metal atoms due to fewer valence electrons. 61 The calculated adsorption energies are presented in Table 1 andFig. 5. Fig. 5 The adsorption sites f (a) P-InP 3and (b) G-InP 3structures. Charge density of (c) P-InP 3and (d) G-InP 3structures.Table 1 Adsorption energies Eads(eV), Gibbs free energy DGH(eV), charge gained of hydrogen atom c (e) and bond length L(/C23A) of hydrogen atom with the adsorbed atom Adsorption sites Eads DGH cL In (P-InP3) 0.78 0.97 0.26 1.769 P1 (P-InP3) /C00.17 0.10 0.35 1.431 P2 (P-InP3) 0.15 0.41 