The calculated Gibbs free energy ( DGH) of adsorbed H atoms on P1, P2 and In atom (P-InP 3, a supercell 9 times the primitive cell, which is denoted as r, containing 2 In atoms and 6 P atoms) is 0.10, 0.41, and 0.97 eV, respectively. The calculatedGibbs free energies of H on P and In atoms on the G-InP 34r supercell are 0.36 and 0.96 eV, respectively, of which that on P ofG-InP 3well agrees with the reported value (0.41 eV).62The calculated Gibbs free energy implies that the active site towardthe HER on both G-InP 3and P-InP 3monolayers is P atoms. Note that the value of free energy DGHshould be nearly close to zero for an ideal catalyst of the HER. The calculated Gibbs freeenergy on P-InP 3is as low as DGH¼0.10 eV for the P top site, which is much smaller than that of P on the phosphorene (1.12eV) 63and MoP (001) surface ( /C00.36 eV).64This value is compa- rable to that of other HER catalysts, such as Pt ( /C00.16 eV),621T0- MoS 2(0.13 eV),65the 2H-1T hybrid phase of MoS 2monolayers (/C00.13 eV),66Mo 2S6nanowires ( /C00.05 eV),67and 1T-WS 2nano- sheets under strain (0.09 to 0.49 eV),21implying that P-InP 3has high catalytic activity toward the HER. Interestingly, Bader charge analysis reveals that the hydrogen atom gains 0.28 e and 0.33 e from In and P of the G-InP 3structure and 0.26 e, 0.35 e and 0.34 e for the In atom, P1 site and P2 site of P-InP 3, respectively, in agreement with the fact that the interaction of In /C0H is generally weaker than H /C0P on both two structures. The di ﬀerential charge densities for H absorption on di ﬀerent P sites are shown in Fig. 5b –d, sug- gesting the good gain of electrons from the P to H atom.Furthermore, the calculated Gibbs free energy of H adsorptionon P-InP 3with di ﬀerent functional methods, which present similar results, is shown in Table S5. † To examine the dependence of DGHon the hydrogen coverage ratio, di ﬀerent sized supercells with a single adsorbed H atom at the P1 site of P-InP 3are considered. From the initial H coverage ratio of 1/18 for all P1 sites on a 9 rsupercell to a higher coverage ratio of 1/8 (4 r) and 1/4 (2 r), the DGHon 9r, 4rand 2 ris 0.10, 0.20 and 0.26 eV, respectively, as shown in Fig. 6a, which suggests that DGHincreases with the increase ofH coverage ratio. At a coverage ratio of 1/16 of P1 sites, the H atom gains a charge of 0.34 e and it is 0.28 e and 0.31 e for a coverage ratio of 1/8 and 1/4, respectively. The bond length ofP–H hardly changes, with only a di ﬀerence of 0.002 ˚A. Besides, the calculations with PBE and DFT + D3 methods agree withthese results well, with a tiny di ﬀerence of less than 0.02 eV, as shown in Fig. S11a. † The e ﬀect of strain on 2D dimensional materials has been proven to be an e ﬀective way to tune the chemical and physical 68 properties and HER performance.69,70Hence we have applied biaxial strain on the P-InP 3monolayer (4 rsupercell) as shown in Fig. 6b. In the test strain range from 0% to 6%, the energychange of P-InP 3follows a parabolic trend (Fig. S10 †), suggest- ing that there is no mechanical failure in the strain range. Thetrend of the DG Hvalue to applied strain is in a negative corre- lation where DGHdecreases with increasing strain, from 0.20 eV to 0.02 eV for a strain from 0% to 6%. For the strain range from 0% to 6%, the bond length of P –H decreases slightly from 1.431 /C23A to 1.429 /C23A and the gained charge on H increases from 0.28 e to 0.34 e. Additionally, the calculations with PBE and DFT + D3methods well con rm these results, with a slight di ﬀerence of less than 0.03 eV. Furthermore, the layer dependent HER performances of G- and P-InP 3structures are studied by testing the bilayer and trilayer. Interestingly, di ﬀerent from the semiconducting properties of G- & P-InP 3monolayers, their bilayers and trilayers are all metallic, as shown in Fig. S12 and 13, †which can enhance the electron transport for better HER performance. Inenergy, the bilayers are more stable than the monolayers, andthe trilayers are the best, as shown in Fig. 6c and Table S6. † Compared to the P-InP 3monolayer, the formation energy is lower: 37, 115, 119 and 163 meV per atom for the bilayer andtrilayer of G-InP 3and the bilayer and trilayer of P-InP 3, respec- tively. Based on the ideal exfoliation model, their exfoliation energy is calculated as well; all are slightly larger than those oftheir monolayers. It is 1.14, 1.10, 1.17 and 1.19 J m /C02for the G- InP 3bilayer and trilayer and P-InP 3bilayer and trilayer, respectively. The DGHresults for the 4 rsupercell of both G-InP 3 and P-InP 3are all negative, ranging from /C00.04 to /C00.16 eV, as shown in Fig. 6d. Speci cally, the DGHof the bilayer of P-InP 3 Fig. 7 The reaction energy barrier of Tafel and Heyrovsky steps of the HER on P-InP 3and G-InP 3. The ﬁnal states are all set as 0 eV. Fig. 6 (a) The Gibbs free energy of P-InP 3with di ﬀerent H coverage ratios, of which ris the primitive cell, (b) the Gibbs free energy of P- InP 3under biaxial strain from 0% to 6%, (c) Layer dependent energy of G- and P-InP 3structures and (d) the Gibbs free energy of P- and G- InP 3bilayers and trilayers. This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2019 J. Mater. Chem. APaper Journal of Materials Chemistry A Published on 09 December 2019. Downloaded by UNIVERSITY OF NEBRASKA on 1/2/2020 9:38:51 PM. View Article Online
