and G-InP 3is/C00.14 and /C00.13 eV, respectively, and it is /C00.03 and/C00.16 eV for the trilayer of G-InP 3and P-InP 3, respectively. Considering both conductivity and absolute value of DGH, the bilayer and tri-layer of both types of structures likely have higher HER catalytic performance than their monolayer. The calcula- tions are carried out and similar results to PBE and DFT + D3methods are obtained. The microkinetic process of the HER is investigated with both Volmer –Tafel and Volmer –Heyrovsky reactions. Here, a partially geometric optimization method to scan the potentialenergy surface is applied to evaluate the reaction barrier. Thedetails are shown in the ESI. †As shown in Fig. S15, †the reac- tion barrier E bof the Volmer step ( *+( H++e/C0)/H*) on both monolayers is quite small, i.e.0.17 eV for G-InP 3and 0.01 eV for P-InP 3, respectively. As shown in Fig. 7 and S16 –S19,†theEbis 0.89 and 0.40 eV for the Tafel step (2H */H2+*) and Heyr- ovsky step (H *+H++e/C0/H2+*) on P-InP 3, respectively, and the corresponding values are 0.83 and 0.68 eV on G-InP 3, respectively. These values are smaller than or comparable tothose of the Volmer –Heyrovsky reaction on MoS 2,i.e.a theoret- ical value of 0.78 eV (ref. 71) and an experimental value of 0.86 eV,72suggesting that reactions are feasible in experiment. Considering the reaction barrier of the elementary steps, theVolmer –Heyrovsky process is more favorable than the Volmer – Tafel process for both structures. Furthermore, the chemical stability of both P-InP 3and G- InP 3monolayers in an aqueous environment is veri ed by means of ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) simulations. Both structures are placed in water at room temperature (set as 300 K) for 5 ps. The snapshot of the structures a er annealing is shown in Fig. 8. 4. Conclusion In summary, we report a phosphorene-like InP 3monolayer with lower formation energy than the previously reported G-InP 3 structure. Both BOMD simulations and phonon spectrum calculations imply the lattice dynamic stability of the P-InP 3 monolayer. P-InP 3is a semiconductor with a direct bandgap of 0.531 eV and high carrier mobility comparable to that of blackphosphorene, which can be modulated with external strain. Thecalculated Gibbs free energy for G- and P-InP 3structures, including the monolayer, bilayer and trilayer, implies that the P- InP 3monolayer, bilayer and trilayer as well as the bilayer and trilayer of G-InP 3present high catalytic activity toward the HER, which is comparable to that of platinum and 1T0-MoS 2. The calculation of the microkinetic process of the HER indicatesthat Volmer –Tafel and Volmer –Heyrovsky mechanisms are possible, and the latter one is more favorable for both P-InP 3 and G-InP 3. This study implies that the new phase of P-InP 3 possesses great potential in application in electronics and as anHER catalyst. Conﬂicts of interest There are no con icts to declare. Acknowledgements This work is supported by the MOST (2016YFA0200602, 2018YFA0208603), the NSFC (21573204, 21421063), the Stra- tegic Priority Research Program of CAS (XDB01020300), theFundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities, theNational Program for Support of Top-notch Young Professional,the External Cooperation Program of BIC CAS(211134KYSB20130017), and USTCSCC, SCCAS, Tianjin, andShanghai Supercomputer Centers. References 1 K. S. Novoselov, D. Jiang, F. Schedin, T. J. Booth, V. V. Khotkevich, S. V. Morozov and A. K. Geim, Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. U. S. A. , 2005, 102, 10451. 2 L. Song, L. Ci, H. Lu, P. B. Sorokin, C. Jin, J. Ni, A. G. Kvashnin, D. G. Kvashnin, J. Lou, B. I. Yakobson andP. M. Ajayan, Nano Lett. , 2010, 10, 3209 –3215. 3 H. Liu, J. Gao and J. Zhao, J. Phys. Chem. C , 2013, 117, 10353 – 10359. 4 W. Jiang, H. Wang, X. Zhang, Y. Zhu and Y. Xie, Sci. China: Chem. , 2018, 61, 1205 –1213. 5 S. Z. Butler, S. M. Hollen, L. Cao, Y. Cui, J. A. Gupta, H. R. Guti ´errez, T. F. Heinz, S. S. Hong, J. Huang and A. F. Ismach, 