This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2021 Chem. Commun., 2021, 57, 13792–13795 |  13793electrochemically polymerized to form PANI on the carbon paper and according to ref. 5 the type of PANI was consideredas emeraldine salt. Then, the Pt was loaded with a currentdensity of /C03.75 mA cm /C02to form the CP-PANI-Pt electrode. For comparison, an electrode with Pt catalysts (CP-Pt) wasprepared using a similar process without PANI coating. The PANI was characterized by FTIR spectra (Fig. 1B). The weak and broad peak at 3200–3400 cm /C01is assigned to the N–H stretch- ing vibration. Due to the polymerization, some of the N–Hgroups are transformed into C–N QC and the number of N–H groups reduces, resulting in a weak peak for the N–H stretchingvibration. The peak around 3000 cm /C01is assigned to the stretching vibration of the C–H on the benzene ring. The strongpeaks between 1600 and 1200 cm /C01result from the bending vibration of the benzene ring and the stretching vibration ofCQN, which confirms the polymerization of aniline into PANI. Specifically, the peak at 1630 cm /C01is assigned to the stretching vibration of C QN and the peaks centred at 1600 and 1560 cm/C01, respectively, are assigned to the bending vibration of the benzene ring. Therefore, during the activation of anelectronic field at 0.75 V, the aniline was successfully trans-formed into PANI. Furthermore, compared with the FTIR spectra of commercial PANI (Fig. S1, ESI †), the main peaks of the as-prepared products are consistent, which further con- firmed the formation of the PANI. 5Fig. 1C shows the SEM image of the PANI on the carbon paper and the formation of thePANI framework structure under the electronic field in 30 min,hinting at the possibility to tailor the electrode surface. Asshown in Fig. 1D and E, the Pt loading on the bare CP is aflower-shape with small plates, while on the PANI surface some of the Pt grew with spheres combining the plates, which illustrates the different growth process of Pt on the PANI layers.The XRD patterns of CP and CP-PANI-Pt are displayed in Fig. S2(ESI† ) and the peak centred at 26 1is assigned to the (002) facet of graphite carbon, while the PANI is considered as amorphous.The diffraction peaks of Pt are not observed in the XRDpatterns, which might be because of the small size and lowcontent. By using a scanning speed of 0.5 1min /C01, the XRD patterns of CP and CP-PANI-Pt were collected from 36 to 42 1 (Fig. 2A). As presented in the XRD pattern of CP-PANI-Pt, a cleardiffraction peak centred at 39 1is observed, matching the (111) facet of metallic Pt. In the same 2-theta range, there are nodiffraction peaks in the XRD pattern of CP and therefore theXRD characterization proved that the particles on the fiberswere metallic Pt. The TEM image of Pt on PANI (Fig. 2B) showsthat the Pt NPs consisted of aggregated smaller Pt NPs with5–10 nm diameter. The lattice distance was measured as 0.20 nm (Fig. 2C), matching the (111) facet of Pt. Simulta- neously, as shown in the HRTEM images (Fig. 2D), the Ptnanoparticles contain large lattice disorder, which would pro-vide active sites for electrocatalysis. Fig. S3 (ESI† ) shows the survey XPS of CP-PANI-Pt and the presence of the C, N, and Pt elements. As shown in Fig. S3(ESI† ), the peak at 70 eV is assigned to the Pt(0), which demonstrates the metallic Pt loading on the surface of the CPs. However, the high resolution XPS of Pt 4f (Fig. 3A) was deconvoluted into peaks in the range between 71 and 77 eV.The peaks centred at 74.7 and 71.4 eV are attributed to the 4f 7/2 and 4f 5/2peaks of Pt(0) respectively. The peaks with higher binding energy (centred at 76.7 and 72.7 eV respectively) areassigned to Pt–N, which resulted from the coordination of thePt and PANI. The C 1s peaks at 286.9 and 285.4 eV are assignedinto the C–N and C QC respectively, which negatively shift to 286.9 and 285.4 eV after loading metallic Pt. As shown in Fig. 1 (A) The Schematics of electropolymerization of PANI on the sur- face of CP and the Pt loading to prepare the CP-PANI-Pt electrode, (B) the FTIR spectrum of PANI, and (C) the SEM image of PANI. (D) The SEM image of CP-Pt, and (E) CP-PANI-Pt. Fig. 2 (A) The XRD pattern of CP-PANI-Pt, (B) the TEM image of PANI-Pt, and (C and D) the HRTEM images of Pt nanoparticles loaded on the PANI. Fig. 3 (A) The high resolution XPS of Pt 4f. (B and C) The high resolution XPS of PANI-Pt and PANI: (B) C 1s, (C) N 1s.Communication ChemComm Published on 29 November 2021. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 3/29/2026 2:23:58 AM. View Article Online
