13794 |  Chem. Commun., 2021, 57, 13792–13795 This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2021Fig. 3C, the peaks at 400.2 and 401.9 eV are assigned to the N 1s of –NH– and –NH+–respectively. After loading Pt, the N 1s peaks decrease to 399.6 and 401.1 eV, which illustrates the stronginteraction between Pt and N. The controlled loading of PANI was investigated to deter- mine the possibility of tailoring the electrocatalysis perfor-mance. As shown in Fig. 4A, the bare carbon paper consistedof smooth fibers. After loading the PANI in 0.5 min, suchchannels are filled with the PANI (Fig. 4B) and the PANI layersbecome thicker by prolonging the loading time to 3 min (Fig. 4C). The PANI nanorods grow from the carbon fiber surface in 10 min (Fig. 4D) and then form a PANI frameworkin 30 min (Fig. 4E). The FTIR spectrum of these samples wascollected and as shown in Fig. S4 (ESI †), the peaks of the bending vibration of the benzene ring and the stretchingvibration of C QN became stronger as the loading prolonged, confirming the growth of a PANI layer. What’s more, the loadedPt on the surface of the PANI-modified electrode is very diﬀerent, though the Pt is loaded under the same conditions. Pt nanoplates were obtained on the bare carbon fiber (Fig. 4F)while Pt nano-pyramids were obtained in the case on the PANI(0.5 min) layer (Fig. 4G). By loading Pt onto the thicker PANIlayer, the formed Pt is spherical with nanoplates and dispersedon the surface of the fibers uniformly (Fig. 4H). On the thickerPANI layer with nanorods, the loaded Pt NPs are found to growon the PANI nanorods (Fig. 4I). As shown in Fig. 4I, the Pt dispersed on the carbon fiber without observing the PANI nanorods. The morphology comparison of CP-PANI(10 min)and CP-PANI(10 min)-Pt indicates that the Pt cations arecaptured by the PANI nanorods and then are reduced intometallic Pt. As shown in the SEM images of CP-PANI(30 min)-Pt(Fig. 4J), the Pt grows on the PANI frameworks, which furtherproves the Pt cation adsorption by PANI before metallic Ptformation. The controllable PANI loading influenced the Pt growth, hinting at the importance of the surface modification of electrodes. However, the morphologies of Pt vary in anundetected path, which might be due to the complex structureof the PANI. The PtCl 4/C0 6coordinates with the –NH–group ofPANI by ligand exchange and consequently, the different struc-tures of these PANIs would influence the coordination and connection between Pt and PANI. The PANI modified surface resulted in the different Pt growth, hinting that the CP-PANI-Ptelectrodes would perform different electrocatalysis. As dis-played in Fig. 4K, the CP-Pt exhibited activity for the hydrogenevolution reaction (HER) with 0.05 V RHEof onset potential and the current density reached 42 mA cm/C02at 0.2 V RHE.B y modifying the electrode surface with PANI, the loaded Pt showsa higher performance. The CP-PANI (0.5 min)-Pt shows obvious higher activity for the HER and the CP-PANI (3.0 min)-Pt exhibits the highest activity with 0.009 V RHEof onset potential and 85 mA cm/C02of current density at 0.20 V RHE. Nevertheless, the electrode modified by a thicker PANI layer exhibitsdecreased activity. The obtained CP-PANI (30 min)-Pt electrodeexhibits very poor activity. In order to confirm the activity of theloaded Pt, different amounts of Pt were loaded and used for theHER. Fig. S5 (ESI† ) shows that the HER activity of the CP-PANI- Pt electrodes increases on using a larger amount of Pt, con-firming the PANI enhancement. The series of CP-PANI-Ptelectrodes also exhibit higher activity for the HER than theCP-Pt electrode. Furthermore, the Pt content on the series ofelectrodes was measured by ICP (Table S1, ESI †). Although the Pt content loaded on the PANI layer is a little more than that onthe bare CP supports, the polarization curves with mass currentdensity of a series of electrodes also confirmed the catalytic activity enhancement from the PANI layers (Fig. S6, ESI †). As reported, the PANI was taken as supported materials would bring the synergetic effect because of the electron transportingenhancement from the N–Pt. 18In addition, the wetting surface of the electrodes was also considered to have an important rolein the catalysts. 19,20Fig. S7A (ESI †) show that the contact angles decrease as the PANI grows and the surface becomes hydro-philic after growing PANI for 3 min. Furthermore, after loading Pt, the contact angles of the electrode surface remain well and show good durability in acid and alkaline solution (Fig. S7B–D,ESI†). Besides, the electocatalytic sites are measured by the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) (Fig. S8, ESI †) and the CP-PANI(3 min)-Pt has the highest ECSA, illustrating themost reaction sites of CP-PANI(3 min)-Pt among the as-obtained electrodes. Fig. S9 (ESI †) shows the LSV curves of the HER comparison before and after 500 cycle tests and the close LSV curves indicate the good stability of the CP-PANI-Pt electrodes. For the case of the ethanol oxidation reaction (EOR),the CP-PANI-Pt electrode also exhibits higher activity than theCP-Pt electrode. Fig. 4L shows the CV curve of the variouselectrodes and the oxidation peaks at 603 and 1036 mV SCEare assigned to the direct oxidation to form CO 2and indirect oxidation to form CO. As shown in Fig. 4L andFig. S10 (ESI† ), the oxidation current density produced on the CP-PANI(3.0 min)-Pt is much larger than that on CP-Pt, illus-trating the ethanol oxidation enhancement from PANI modifi-cation. Fig. S11 (ESI †) shows the EOR performance on the CP- PANI(3.0 min)-Pt with various ethanol concentrations. Theoxidation current and desorption current exhibit a positiverelationship with the ethanol concentration, proving the high Fig. 4 (A–E) The SEM images of CP (A) and CP-PANI (B–E). (F–J) The corresponding SEM images of CP-Pt and CP-PANI-Pt. (K) The HER performance of CP-Pt and a series of CP-Pt and CP-PANI-Pt. (L) Theethanol oxidation reaction on the CP-PANI (3 min)-Pt and (M) the XRD patterns before and after EOR.ChemComm Communication Published on 29 November 2021. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 3/29/2026 2:23:58 AM. View Article Online
