This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2021 Chem. Commun., 2021, 57, 13792–13795 |  13795activity of the CP-PANI(3.0 min)-Pt electrode. The performance with different PANI layer thicknesses was also studied. Fig. S12 (ESI† ) shows that the higher activity of CP-PANI-Pt increases and then decreases on increasing the PANI layer thickness. For the thinner PANI layer modified electrode, the hydrophilicproperties of the CP-PANI-Pt surface are improved and conse-quently the ethanol is absorbed on the surface more easily,resulting in a higher oxidation current. Compared with otherreported studies, the CP-PANI-Pt electrode in this work showsgood catalytic performance (Table S2, ESI †). Meanwhile, the CP- PANI-Pt electrodes are stable after 500 cycles (Fig. S13, ESI †). The XRD pattern (Fig. 4M) comparison before and after a10 hour test in the EOR shows that Pt retained its phase well,hinting at the better stability with PANI modification. The SEMimages of the electrode also confirm that the morphologies wellremained after the EOR test (Fig. S14, ESI †). It is reported that the conductive polymer modified surface would protect the Ptnanocrystal catalysts from aggregation. 18,21Hence, PANI mod- ification would enhance the electrocatalysis by promoting thehydrophilic properties and avoiding the aggregation of thecatalysts. However, the thicker PANI layer exhibits a super-wetting surface, and the capacitance of PANI played a mainrole and limited the electrocatalysis. Furthermore, electro-chemical impedance spectroscopy (ESI †) was performed (Fig. S15, ESI†). As shown in Fig. S15 (ESI †), the ohmic resistances of these electrodes are very close but their capacities are the main factors to reduce the catalysis process. Hence, the PANI with certain thickness enhanced the electrocatalytic activity andstability. In summary, we have investigated the modification of the electrode surface with conductive polymer, PANI, whichresulted in the loaded Pt growing in a diﬀerent way due tothe complex structure of PANI with diﬀerent polymerizationdegrees. By employing the CP-PANI-Pt electrodes for the HER and EOR electrocatalysis, the PANI layer enhanced the perfor- mance of the CP-PANI-Pt viapromoting the hydrophilic proper- ties of the electrode surface, promoting dispersion of theloaded Pt, and enlarging the ECSA. This work provided apossible strategy to modify electrodes and then boost theirperformance in electrocatalysis. This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (51973118, 21805193, and 52003160), the Science and Technology Innovation Commission of Shenzhen (JCYJ20190808113005643, JCYJ20170818093832350, JCYJ20180507184711069, JCYJ20180305125319991, and JCYJ20190808114803804),the Key-Area Research and Development Program of GuangDongProvince (2019B010929002 and 2019B010941001), and the Program for Guangdong Introducing Innovative and Enterpreneurial Teams (2019ZT08C642). Conflicts of interest There are no conflicts to declare. Notes and references 1 S. Ghosh, S. Bera, S. Bysakh and R. N. Basu, Sustainable Energy Fuels, 2017, 1(5), 1148–1161. 2 S. Ghosh, S. Bera, S. Bysakh and R. N. Basu, ACS Appl. Mater. Interfaces , 2017, 9(39), 33775–33790. 3 W. Liu, L. Yu, R. Yin, X. Xu, J. Feng, X. Jiang, D. Zheng, X. Gao, X. Gao, W. Que, P. Ruan, F. Wu, W. Shi and X. Cao, Small , 2020, 16(10), e1906775. 4 W. Liu, D. Zheng, L. Zhang, R. Yin, X. Xu, W. Shi, F. Wu, X. Cao and X. Lu, Nanoscale , 2021, 13(5), 3019–3026. 5 R. Djara, Y. Holade, A. Merzouki, N. Masquelez, D. Cot, B. Rebiere, E. Petit, P. Huguet, C. Canaﬀ, S. Morisset, T. W. Napporn, D. Cornu and S. Tingry, J. Electrochem. Soc. , 2020, 167(6), 066503. 6 N. Deogratias, M. Ji, Y. Zhang, J. Liu, J. Zhang and H. Zhu, Nano Res. , 2015, 8(1), 271–280. 7 D. Zheng, L. Yu, W. Liu, X. Dai, X. Niu, W. Fu, W. Shi, F. Wu and X. Cao, Cell Rep. Phys. Sci. , 2021, 2(6), 100443. 8 W. Liu, D. Zheng, T. Deng, Q. Chen, C. Zhu, C. Pei, H. Li, F. Wu, W. Shi, S. W. Yang, Y. Zhu and X