REVIEW A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals Yun-Rui Li, Ming-Xuan Li, Shu-Na Li, Yu-Jie Liu, Juan Chen, Yao Wang* Received: 28 September 2020 / Revised: 10 November 2020 / Accepted: 11 November 2020 /C211Youke Publishing Co., Ltd. 2021 Abstract Today, nanocrystals enclosed by high-index facets (HIFs) are attracting widely attentions of researchers due to their tremendous potential in the ﬁeld of catalysis,especially in electrocatalysis, such as electro-oxidation of small organic molecule (such as formic acid, methanol, and ethanol), oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), hydrogenevolution reaction (HER), as well as the oxygen evolution reaction (OER). However, the practical applications of nanocrystals enclosed by HIFs still face many limitationsin preparations of advanced electrocatalysts, including preparation strategy, limited life-time and stability. The development of advanced electrocatalysts enclosed withHIFs is crucial for solving these problems if the large-scale application of them is to be realized. Herein, we ﬁrstly detailedly demonstrate the identiﬁcation methods ofnanocrystals enclosed by HIFs, and then preparation strategies are elaborated in detail in this review. Current advanced nanocrystals enclosed by HIFs in electrocatalyticapplication are also summarized and we present represen- tative achievements to further reveal the relationship of excellent electrocatalytic performance and nanocrystals with HIFs. Finally, we predict the remaining challenges and present our perspectives with regards of designstrategies of improving electrocatalytic performance of Pt- based catalysts in the future. Keywords Nanocrystals with high-index facets; High surface free energy; Electrocatalytic performance; Water splitting; Fuel cell 1 Introduction With the growth of energy demands and increasing pres- sure of reducing the greenhouse gas, the water electrolysisand fuel cell are attracting considerable attention as green and highly efﬁcient sustainable energy storage and con- version technologies [ 1–5]. The design and preparation of high-performance catalysts is an important crucial research area for their extensive applications [ 6,7]. In general, the properties of the nanocatalysts, such as chemical structure,composition, size and morphology determine the catalytic performance in electro-catalysis. As is well-known, the electrocatalytic activity, stability, anti-poisoning, and selectivity strongly depend on the surface structures of particles because of the preferentially reaction on the sur-face. Previous studies have been reported that the nanocrystals (NCs) enclosed by HIFs usually perform a better catalytic properties in comparison with NCsenclosed by low-index facets ({100}, {110}, and {111} facets) [ 8,9]. The HIFs possess the high density of low- coordinated atoms (such as atomic steps, ledges, andkinks), which are more active for breaking chemical bonds, leading to the signiﬁcant enhancement of catalytic activi- ties. However, the Pt-based NCs of HIFs possess a highsurface energy and are thermodynamically metastable, leading to a big challenge in their shape-controlled syn- thesis. To overcome this challenge, kinetic-thermodynamic Yun-Rui Li and Ming-Xuan Li have contributed equally to this work Y.-R. Li, M.-X. Li, S.-N. Li, Y.-J. Liu, J. Chen State Key Laboratory of Heavy Oil Processing, College ofChemical Engineering and Environment, China University ofPetroleum, Beijing 102249, China Y. Wang* Department of Chemistry, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084,Chinae-mail: yaowang@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn 1 Rare Met. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12598-021-01747-8 RARE METALS
