control of crystal growth is indispensable and is currently realized mainly by electrochemical methods and surfac- tant-based wet chemical approaches. Today, many syn- thetic strategies have been developed for the formation ofNCs enclosed by HIFs, such as (i) wet chemical method, (ii) electrochemical synthesis strategies, (iii) high temper- ature alloying and dealloying approach and (iv) micro-wave-assisted method. In the past decades, a large number of researches have been devoted to the exploration on correlation between NCs enclosed with HIFs and applica- tions in the electrocatalytic reactions, including electro- oxidation of small organic molecule (such as formic acid,methanol, and ethanol), ORR, HER, as well as the OER. However, the generation of well-controlled NCs covered with HIFs is still challenging because of their high surfacefree energy and amounts of undercoordinated surface atoms. Further studies reported that NCs enclosed by HIFs usually suffered from thermodynamically instability, whichis easy to cause the surface restructure and rearrangement in electrolytes attributed to the presence of high active unsaturated atoms and the high surface energy, ﬁnallyleading to the degradation of catalytic performance. In this review, as shown in Fig. 1, we ﬁrstly expounded thedeﬁnition of HIFs and summarized the several fabrication methods of NCs with HIFs. And then we reviewed recent applications in energy and environment catalysis based on NCs with HIFs. Lastly, the future perspective about designconcept of NCs with HIFs was demonstrated. 2 Identiﬁcation methods of NCs with HIFs The enhanced electrocatalytic activity of NCs with HIFs is ascribed to the atomic steps, edge parts and kinks with low coordination numbers, thus it is of great signiﬁcance tounderstand the general identiﬁcation and preparation strategies of HIFs [ 10,11]. In order to investigate the information of HIFs, the catalysts need to be characterizedby transmission electron microscopy (TEM), high resolu- tion TEM (HRTEM) and selected area electron diffraction (SAED). In this section, we summarize two methods toidentify Miller indices of the HIFs: (i) microfacet method, (ii) measuring the projection angle by Steno’s law. Firstly, the HIFs can be composed of multiple low-index planes,including terraces and steps. The facets can be represented using the microfacet method by low-index facets [ 12–14]. Fig. 1 Overview of topics covered in this review 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
