The microfacet method is deﬁned n(htktlt)9(hsksls), meaning natomic width of ( htktlt) terraces separated by monatomic ( hsksls) steps [ 15,16]. Based on the data of Table 1[16], it is easy to ﬁnd the appropriate microfacet description of HIFs. In accordance with HRTEM images or atomic arrangement of HIFs, we can ﬁnd out the low-index of terraces and steps, and then describe the Miller indiceswith microfacet notation in Table 1. For instance, the HIFs {553} can be expressed as 4 (111) 9(110), including a terrace in four atomic width of (111) and monatomic step of (110) [ 17]. Likewise, the {332} composed of 5 (111) 9(110), indicating a terrace in ﬁve atomic width of (111) separated by (110) steps, as shown in Fig. 2a. Luo and Shen [ 18] have successfully prepared concave PtCu 2 octopod nanoframes bounded by HIFs of {553}, {331}, and {221}, as shown in Fig. 2b. The HIFs composed of (111) terraces and (110) steps, which are usually repre- sented by n(111) -(111) or equivalently ( n-1) (111)- (110). Shao et al. demonstrated the arrangement of the platinum atoms of n(111) -(111) surfaces enclosed by {331} and {221} facets, as shown in Fig. 2c[19]. The HIFs are composed of (111) and (110), as terraces and steps. According to literatures, the Miller indices of HIFs can be determined by Steno’s law [ 20,21]. The index of facets is measured by calculating the projection angle between two adjacent facets through the appropriate crystal zone axis direction. The variety of angles of the projection ismeasured along different crystal axis directions and com- paring with theoretical values, which can identify the Miller indices of the facets [ 22,23]. The projection angle and corresponding Miller indices of NCs with HIFs are shown in Table 2[15]. The strategies of identiﬁcation for noble metals enclosed with HIFs are also adaptable toseveral non-precious metal oxides, such as Cu 2O, TiO 2, Fe2O3, ZnO, SnO 2and other metal oxides [ 20,24–29].Zhang et al. reported that the nanocubic and HOH Pt–Ni were enclosed by {521} and {532} HIFs using hydrothermal method. The measurement methods of Miller indices are based on the measured angles [ 30]. The mea- sured projection angles along the [011] direction are shown in Fig. 3a, b. Han et al. prepared the convex polyhedral Au@Pd core–shell NCs bounded predominantly by {532}HIFs [ 31]. Through the measured projection angles along different direction, the Miller indices of HIFs obtained are shown in Fig. 3c–e. Based on above discussions, it should be noted that there are mainly two general approaches to identify the Millerindices of HIFs. The strategies of identiﬁcation are not only applicable for precious metals, but also can be used to identify the HIFs of non-noble metal oxides. Theseapproaches need to select suitable particles or HRTEM images, which can ensure the accuracy of Miller indices measurement. In generally, researchers combined the twoapproaches to identify the Miller indices of HIFs more accurately. 3 Preparation strategies of NCs with HIFs The nanoparticles with HIFs possess high surface energy, leading to the instability of HIFs in the growth process. Therefore, it is a challenging task to synthesize and stabi-lize the structure and composition of NCs by HIFs. In this section, we conclude the important factors of developing NCs with HIFs including: (i) wet chemical method, (ii)electrochemical synthesis strategies, (iii) high temperature alloying and dealloying approach and (iv) microwave-as- sisted method. 3.1 Wet chemical method The wet chemical method, as a facile and simple method, refers to the chemical reduction of metal ions in an aqueous solution, which has been widely applied in preparation ofmetal nanocomposites. Cui et al. [ 32] demonstrated that the ternary AuPtNi multimetallic nanostructures (NSs) with rich defects such as twins, stacking faults, and atomic stepswere synthesized using Ag nanowires (NWs) as templates through a simple wet-chemical method, as shown in Fig. 4 [32]. In general, from the insight of preparation strategy, the wet chemical method also contains: (i) seed-mediated method for high-index facts, (ii) selectively etching the metal atoms, and (iii) selecting the appropriate reducingagent and capping agent.Table 1 Different types of nanopolyhedron with HIFs and microfacet notation High-indexed polyhedronZone Surface facets Miller index (nC2)Microfacet notation Tetrahexahedron (THH)[001] { hk0}24 (h[k[0)(n,n-1,0) n(110) 9(100) (n,1 ,0 ) n(100) 9(110) Trapezohedron (TPH)[011] { hkk}24 (h[k[0)(n?1, n-1, n-1)n(111) 9(100) (2n-1, 1, 1) n(100) 9(111) Trisoctahedron (TOH)[110] { hhl}24 (h[l[0)(n?1, n?1, n-1)n(111) 9(110) (2n-1, 2n-1, 1)n(110) 9(111) Hexoctahedron (HOH)/{ hhl}48 (h[k[l[0)// 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
