3.1.1 Seed-mediated method for high-index facts At present, seed-mediated method is one of the methods to prepare the NCs with HIFs in aqueous solution. The pro- cess is mainly divided into two steps: ﬁrstly, the synthesis of NCs seeds; secondly, the seeds were added into solutionincluding metal precursor, reducing agent and morphology directing agent, which results in directing the heteroge- neous nucleation of newly formed atoms and then thepreparation of nanoparticles [ 33]. The main advantage of seed-mediated method is that a variety of new morpholo- gies structure can be obtained through adding precursorsand reducing agents based on any existing nanocrystals. Kim et al. [ 31] successfully developed the Au@Pd core– shell NCs with HIFs of {1253}. The catalysts were syn-thesized through simultaneous reduction of Au and Pd ions in the presence of octahedral Au NCs seeds using a seed- mediated synthetic method. Based on characterizationresults, ﬁrstly the octahedral Au NC seeds transformed into rhombic dodecahedral NCs, and then into convex NCs withhexoctahedron-like arms through the preferential deposi- tion of metals onto the surfaces of the growing seeds. Wang et al. [ 34] demonstrated two types of Au–Pd core–shell NCs by seed-mediated method in the presence of Autetrahexahedral and trisoctahedral NCs with HIFs. The Pd shell was enclosed by HIFs of {730} and {221}, which grew along on the facets of Au seeds uniformly. In addi-tion, the HIFs were preserved in reduction reaction solu- tion. Lee et al. successfully prepared a variety of Au@Pd trisoctahedral, hexoctahedral and tetrahexahedral catalystswith different HIFs using trisoctahedral gold NCs seeds [15]. Concave TOH Au NCs were selected as the template because their concave shape and protruded corners offer aversatile geometry for tailoring a variety of HIFs. The Au@Pd polyhedral was enclosed by a class of HIFs of {221}, {552} and {331}, which resulted from theheteroepitaxial growth of Pd layers along the facets of gold NCs seeds under careful control of the growth kinetics. The surfactants and the process of oxidative etching both play vital role in forming the HIFs in the seed- Fig. 2 a Atomic model and cross section of atomic arrangement. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 17]. Copyright 2016, Wiley– VCH. bHRTEM image of PtCu 2CONFs and atomic arrangement of HIFs. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 18]. Copyright 2016, American Chemical Society. cCross section of atomic arrangement of n(111)–(111) surface. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [19]. Copyright 2015, American Chemical Society 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
