structure. Xiong et al. reported a simple approach to syn- thesis Pt branched nanocrystals with controllable numbers of branches by tuning the concentration of additional HCl [45]. The amount of HCl has a signiﬁcant effect on the system, mainly including three factors. Firstly, the HCl can tune the crystallinity of seeds and affect the atomic addi- tion. With the increase in HCl concentration, the branchesof Pt nanocrystals were adjusted from 4 to 8. More importantly, the oxidation and etching of HCl can control the reduction rate of the Pt precursor, regulating the growthrate of atoms addition. As the amount of HCl changes, the oxidation etching has a certain effect on the generation of Pt atoms. Finally, the presence of HCl can provide thegrowth sites for Pt atoms. However, the excessive HCl will produce a covering effect, thereby reducing the reaction rate. Overall, one hand, appropriate amount of chloride ions is prone to the reduction of metal precursors, forming the various possible morphologies. On the other hand,excessive Cl -is selectively adsorbed on to facets, resulting in covering effect and inﬂuencing the reaction rate. The selectively etching process can produce catalysts with various morphologies including nanocages, nano-frames, nanoconcaves with HIFs. Shen et al. reported that the Pt–Cu–Rh heterogeneous NCs (HNs) with Cu rich core were accomplished through one-pot method [ 46]. The Pt– Cu–Rh heterogeneous nanocages (HNCs) were enclosed by {331}, {332}, {553}, {210} and {410} plans, which were obtained by selectively removing the Cu-rich core from Pt–Cu–Rh HNs via acetic acid etching. The Cu atoms will not react directly with acetic acid, but they could be easily oxidized by oxygen, and then the oxidized species werereadily dissolved in the acid solution. Wang et al. [ 47] have synthesized the Pt 3Ni alloy nanoframe catalysts with {510}, {310}, {410} and {720} by colloidal synthesis approach via carbon monoxide etching at elevated tem- peratures. The