which formation is attributed to the preferential etching pathway and mild annealing temperature. The surfactants and reducing agents play an effect on forming HIFs in the selectively etching process.Researchers combined both merits to explore the formation of high-index crystal facets. Jin et al. prepared the deeply excavated Pt 3Co nanocubes (Pt 3Co DENCs) with {510} and {710} HIFs by one-pot solvothermal method [ 40]. The HIFs of Pt 3Co DENCs were formed, which would be attributed to the facets of capping from CTAC and oxida-tive etching from Cl -/O2pairs, respectively, as shown in Fig. 6. In the reaction system, the presence of deionized water can provide more O 2and increase the number of Cl-/ O2pairs, thereby increasing the corrosion rate, and then accelerating the formation of concave structure and deeply excavating architecture. In addition, the metal oxides withHIFs can be obtained through similar approach. Guo’s group successfully reported that multifaceted Cu 2O enclosed with a series of HIFs was accomplished throughaltering the time of selective oxidative etching, which could facilitate the formation of HIFs [ 48]. 3.1.3 Selecting appropriate reducing agent and capping agent It has been well established that reducing agent and cap- ping agent have a signiﬁcant effect on the nucleation andgrowth rates of NCs, which can facilitate the formation of HIFs. The presence of reducing agent can strongly coor- dinate with metal ions and then decrease the redox poten- tial of different metals. The capping agents can selectivelyabsorb the different surface of NCs, which dominates the growth of speciﬁc facets. The different halide ion can be used as capping agents to selectively absorb on certainfacets of metals, or coordinated with metals, which can inﬂuence the reaction rate and form the special structures with HIFs. Xu et al. [ 30] successfully developed concave- nanocubic (CNC) and HOH Pt–Ni alloy NCs enclosed by HIFs of {610}, {510}, {410} and {720} through one-pot fabrication method. The HIFs were obtained by adjustingthe concentration of glycine, which can affect the reduc- tion rate of Pt (IV) and Ni (II) species. The low con- centration of glycine can result in forming CNC Pt–Nialloy by self-assembly, while the HOH Pt–Ni alloy can be controlled by crystal growth at high concentration of glycine. In addition to the amount of reducing agent, capping agents can also affect the formation of high-index crystal facets, such as cetyltrimethyl ammonium bromide (CTAB),CTAC and iodine ions (I -). Gong et al. [ 49] demonstrated that the Pt–Ni cross double dumbbell-like nanostructures (Pt–Ni CDDNs) enclosed by {511}, {311}, {911} and{711} HIFs were synthesized by one-pot solvothermal method. The CTAC serves as the surface capping to Fig. 6 a TEM and bHAADF-STEM images of Pt 3Co DENCs; cHRTEM image of Pt 3Co DENC and atomic arrangement of facets and damount of deionized water affecting morphologies of nanoparticles. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 40]. Copyright 2017, American Chemical Society 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
