selectively adsorb on the (100) facet. In addition, the concentration of CTAC can inﬂuence the morphologies ofPt–Ni nanostructures. The presence of CTAC promotes the formation of branched structures along the \100[direc- tion of the initial Pt–Ni nanostructures, and then acceler-ates the formation of HIFs. Wu et al. [ 50] successfully synthesized Pt–Pd symmetry-broken concave nanocube (SBCNCs) structures with a series of {610}, {510}, {720}and {830} HIFs via one-pot method. In the reaction sys- tem, the I -selectively adsorbs on the (100) facets, which decreases the surface diffusion rates and leads to direc-tional growth along the vertexes and pile-up of new atoms at the corner of the cubes, and then it promotes the for- mation of asymmetry concave nanocubes. In accordance with results of literatures, reducing agents and stabilizers play positive effect in forming the high- index faceted NCs, and then it is signiﬁcant to combine themerits. Huang et al. [ 51] have fabricated PtPb concave CNCs bounded by {520} HIFs through a one-step wet- chemical method, as shown in Fig. 7. The lead (II) iodide (PbI 2) was used as the metal precursors, oleylamine (OAm) and oleic acid (OA) mixture as solvents and surfactants, respectively, the CTAB as the structure-directing agent andglucose as reducing agent in the synthesis process. The quantity of reducing agent would affect the reduction reaction rate of the precursors, and then inﬂuence themorphology of NCs. In addition, the presence of I -could promote the directional growth of nanoparticles into con- cave structure.According to above results, both reducing agents and capping agent have a certain effect on the formation ofHIFs. First, the concentration of the reducing agent can narrow the gap between the reduction potentials of differ- ent metal precursors, thereby improving the reduction ratesof metals. Secondly, the presence of capping agent can selectively absorb on a certain surface, and then change the path of nanocrystal nucleation and growth. Therefore,combining their advantages can better promote the for- mation of HIFs. 3.2 Electrochemical synthesis strategies In the absence of surfactants or ligands, the electrochemi- cal method can fabricate the nanocrystals with controlled morphologies in comparison with the traditional wet- chemical methods. Luo et al. [ 52] developed a facile electrochemical method to convert Pt wire into Pt concave icosahedra and nanocubes on carbon paper through linear sweep voltammetry scanning and altering electrochemicalsynthesis solution, as shown in Fig. 8. More importantly, the adjusting of electrode potential is favorable for the construction of nanocrystalline surface structure. Sun et al.proposed that tetrahexahedral platinum NCs bounded by HIFs {730} were accomplished through electrochemical square-wave potential method for the ﬁrst time [ 53]. The synthetic process is mainly composed of two separate steps: (i) the Pt nanospheres were formed through the process of electrodeposition; (ii) the THH NPs were Fig. 7 a HAADF-STEM images and bHRTEM images of PtPb CNCs; catomic model of PtPb (520) facets and dgrowth mechanism of PtPb CNCs. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 51]. Copyright 2017, American Chemical Society 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
