prepared through reduction of dissolved platinum ion under lower potential of square wave treatment. Followed the similar electrochemical square wave potential strategy, they accomplished the Pd THH, Rh THH, PdPt alloy andPtRh alloy THH NCs enclosed with HIFs [ 54–57]. In addition to the preparation of different metals THH NCs, the different morphologies NCs with HIFs can be obtainedby adjusting the conditions, such as trape-zohedron, hex- octahedron and triambic icosahedron [ 38,58–61]. It is remarkable that the lower potential of square wave shouldbe selected at the potential of metal precursor reduction and sluggish speed. The studies above indicated that the electrochemical square-wave potential method possessesgeneral applicability, variety and controllability for syn- thesizing the noble NCs with HIFs. The electrochemical method is partially suitable for the preparation of metal oxides with HIFs. Liu et al. [ 62] demonstrated that the concave octahedrons bounded by {344} HIFs convered byCu ﬁlms were accomplished through electrochemical ter- med cyclic scanning electrodeposition approach at room temperature. 3.3 High temperature alloying and dealloying approaches In addition to the above methods, high-temperature deal- loying approach is recognized as the novel method forsynthesizing nanoparticles with HIFs, which has aroused interest in application. In this approach, the metal nanoparticles with foreign shape-regulation metals werealloyed and dealloyed to fabricate high-index facets under elevated temperature thermal treatment. Meanwhile, the foreign metal with low evaporation temperature was cho-sen preferentially to synthesize bimetallic NPs based on noble metals. Moreover, under thermal annealing, the nanoparticles are favorable for forming heterostructuresdue to the different miscibility of metals. The process of alloying/dealloying is not only conducive to the growth of surface, but also can reconstruct the internal of nanoparti- cles extensively, which facilitates the formation of high-index facets [ 63–65]. Huang et al. [ 66] reported that the tetrahexahedral particles composed of (Pt, Pd, Ru, Ni, Co) enclosed by HIFs of {210} through alloying/dealloyingwith trace metals (Bi, Sb, Pb or Te) were prepared, which can stabilize the HIFs at elevated temperature thermal treatment (900–200 /C176C), as shown in Fig. 9[66]. The foreign metals (Sb, Bi, Pb and Te) with low evaporation temperature evaporated and moved to the location of metal salts, which could promote the formation of particles withHIFs under thermal treatment. Subsequently, in 2020, it was demonstrated that multimetallic Pt-based and Rh- based tetrahexahedral particles with HIFs were accom- plished through alloying with transition metals and modi- fying surface with Bi at high temperature, and then thecolloidal was ﬁrst prepared and transformed into target by two separate steps [ 67]. Meanwhile, Huang’s group [ 68] synthesized a library of multimetallic (Pt, Pd, Rh and Au)HIF-ed tetrahexahedral nanoparticles by alloying/dealloy- ing with Bi at high temperature. Above results of literatures indicated the effectiveness of novel solid-state shape reg-ulation strategy for preparing nanoparticles with HIFs. This strategy can provide greater new notion and method for designing electrocatalysts with HIFs and excellent catalyticefﬁciencies, which can enhance the composition, surface state and size of catalysts. 3.4 Microwave-assisted method In addition, the microwave irradiation method has been also used to synthesis the metal nanomaterials with HIFs. The crystals nucleate rapidly in a short time, which mainly depend on the adsorption of microwave and high energy Fig. 8 a Schematic illustration of preparation Pt concave icosahedra and nanocubes, bTEM image of Pt concave icosahedra and cTEM image of Pt concave nanocubes. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 52]. Copyright 2018, Springer 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
