4.1 Methanol oxidation reaction (MOR) Direct methanol fuel cells (DMFCs) are applied in auto- mobiles and portable power sources due to the convenience of storage, low price and high energy density of methanol[84]. Although PGMs have been identiﬁed as the most ideal catalytic materials for MOR, there are many side reactions and the toxic intermediate prevents the reactionfrom proceeding, which is shown below [ 85]: PtþCH 3OH!Pt/C0CHO adsþ3Hþþ3e/C0ð1Þ Pt/C0CHO ads!Pt/C0CO adsþHþþe/C0ð2Þ PtþH2O!Pt/C0ðOHÞadsþHþþe/C0ð3Þ Pt/C0CO adsþPt/C0ðOHÞads!2PtþCO 2þHþþe/C0ð4Þ Pt/C0CHO adsþPt/C0ðOHÞads!PtþPt/C0ðCOOH Þads þHþþe/C0 ð5Þ Pt/C0ðCOOH Þads!PtþCO 2þHþþe/C0ð6Þ Therefore, the sluggish kinetics of methanol oxidation reduces the current density tremendously [ 86]. In order to solve this issue, it is of great signiﬁcance to carry outgeometric engineering design of the nanometarials, aiming to promote this structure sensitive reaction [ 87]. In general, the HIFs with lower interplanar spacing and non-densely packed atoms enhance methanol oxidation more obviously.For example, Gong et al. [ 49] demonstrated a one-pot solvothermal method for the growth of Pt–Ni cross double dumbbell-like nanostructures (Pt–Ni CDDNs) (Fig. 10a–f), which enhances the catalytic properties of MOR signiﬁcantly compared with the Pt–Ni nanocubes that they synthesized at the same time. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) results reveal that HIFs can increase the utilization of Pt atoms, leading to the increase inelectrochemical surface area (ECSA) (Fig. 10g). The low lattice spacing and lattice-mismatch strain brought about by HIFs reduce the adsorption of toxic intermediate, all ofwhich promote the oxidation of methanol (Fig. 10i, j). In addition, Zhang et al. [ 84] proposed a hydrothermal method to synthesize pure platinum concave cubes(Fig. 10k). The surface of this tetrahedral structure is rich in different high-index { hk0} facets ( h[k[1), which is conducive to MOR. As shown in Fig. 10l, m, the current density can reach 3 times that of commercial Pt/C, which is relatively rare for shape-controlled pure Pt catalysts to Fig. 10 a –cTEM images and dHAADF-STEM image and elemental mapping images of Pt–Ni CDDNs; eatomic-resolution TEM image of surface on nanosheet; f3D schematic model of Pt–Ni CDDNs; g–jelectrochemical measurements of as-prepared Pt–Ni CDDNs, Pt–Ni NDs and commercial Pt/C catalysts. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 49]. Copyright 2019, Elsevier. kSynthesis schematic diagram, TEM image and electrocatalytic performance of Pt CNC; CV curves for electro-oxidation of lmethanol and mformic acid by Pt CNCs, commercial Pt black, and Pt/C. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 84]. Copyright 2012, American Chemical Society 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
