achieve such activity. The subsequent work has further improved the MOR performance by combining Pt with other elements and modifying the HIFs [ 81,88,89], which also proves the broad application prospects of HIFsengineering in the design of DMFCs. 4.2 Formic acid oxidation reaction (FAOR) PdþHCOOH !Pd/C0ðCOOH Þ adsþHþþe/C0ð7Þ Pd/C0ðCOOH Þads!PdþCO 2þHþþe/C0ð8Þ PdþH2O!Pd/C0ðOHÞadsþHþþe/C0ð9Þ Pd/C0ðOHÞadsþPd/C0ðCOOH Þads!2PdþCO 2þH2O ð10Þ Direct formic acid fuel cells (DFAFCs) are also identiﬁed as efﬁcient energy conversion devices because of its highenergy density and portability. Formic acid usually transfers two electrons on the anode to generate protons and CO 2and the reaction mechanism is simpler than that of methanol [ 90]. The mechanism of the reaction is as follows. For FAOR, Pt atoms are prone to cutoff C–O bond at low potential, resulting in more CO adsorpted on the sur- face of the active sites, which reduces the reactionperformance [ 91]. As a substitute, Pd atoms can easily break the O–H bond and promote the direct dehydro- genation of formic acid to CO 2, thus improving the anti- toxicity of FAOR [ 91,92]. Therefore, Pd is more used in the oxidation of formic acid. However, for the FAOR on the Pd surface, the Pd crystals with HIFs are not superior to low-index crystal faces. Yu et al. [ 92] found that as crystal face index increased, the activity of Pd catalyzing FAOR decreased, which is a special case of the oxidation of small organic molecules (Fig. 11a). In other words, with the increase in step content, the activity of FAOR decreased (Fig. 11b, c). Density functional theory (DFT) calculations also show that the dehydrogenation of formic acid on the surface of Pd is the control step of FAOR. Compared with the ﬂat Pd (100), the Pd crystal with HIFs (take stepped Pd(310) as an example) has a stronger adsorption of COOH, which hinders FAOR [ 91]. 4.3 Ethanol oxidation reaction (EOR) Ethanol is a kind of small organic molecule that can be easily converted from biomass. Compared with methanol, ethanol has higher energy density (8 kWh /C1kg -1) and stronger resistance to CO poisoning, which makes it beconsidered as a promising power source in fuel cells. In general, there are C1 and C2 pathways in the EOR. For C1 Fig. 11 a Linear sweep voltammograms of electrooxidation of formic acid, methanol, ethanol, n-propanol, and ethylene glycol on Pd NCs; bnormalized peak current density vs normalized density of step sites using the values of THH{1130} as reference, where slopes reﬂect surface-structure sensitivity of reactions; cdependence of peak current density on density of step sites. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 92]. Copyright 2019, American Chemical Society 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
