reaction mechanism, the product is generally acetaldehyde (CH 3CHO) and acetic acid (CH 3COOH), of which only 2–4 electrons are transferred. However, when the ethanol molecule is completely oxidized to CO 2via the C2 path- way, it can transfer up to 12 electrons [ 93], which greatly increases the current density, as shown in the following formula: C1 pathway: CH 3CH 2OHþ3H2O!2CO 2þ12Hþþ12e/C0ð11Þ CH 3CH 2OHþ5H2O!2HCO2/C0 3þ14Hþþ10e/C0ð12Þ CH 3CH 2OHþ5H2O!2CO2/C0 3þ16Hþþ12e/C0ð13Þ C2 pathway:CH 3CH 2OHþ3H2O!2CH 3COOH þ4Hþþ4e/C0ð14Þ CH 3CH 2OH!CH 3CHOþ2Hþþ2e/C0ð15Þ In order to promote the complete oxidation of ethanol to CO 2, the cleavage of the C–C bond is particularly important for the reaction process. The high-density step sites and kink sites contained in the HIFs of nanocrystal can also promote the rupture of the C–C bond, thuspromoting the complete oxidation of ethanol and increasing the speciﬁc activity of EOR. For example, the tetrahexahedron of Pt nanocrystal with {730} facets can even reach 4.6 times that of commercial Pt/C [ 53]. Besides, Zhang et al. [ 94] reported that the EOR catalytic activities of Pt nanocrystal surfaces decrease in the sequence Fig. 12 HRTEM image of edge region of a, b Pt (211) and c, d Pt (411); eelectrocatalytic properties of concave Pt NCs with Pt (411) facets, multipod Pt NCs with Pt (211) facets, Pt nanocubes and commercial Pt/C. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 94]. Copyright 2012, Tsinghua University Press. f, g Illustration and hiTEM images of sub-10 nm THH Pt NCs synthesized via an electrochemically seedmediated method, where Pt nanoparticles (3.2 nm) supported on graphene were used as crystal seeds andthen grew into THH Pt NCs (9.5 nm) through an electrochemical square-wave potential treatment; TEM images and size histograms ofcommercial Pt/C jbefore and kafter electrochemical SWP treatment; lcyclic voltammograms of commercial Pt/C before and after SWP treatment for ethanol oxidation; mremaining activity of THH-Pt/G, Pt/C-SWP, and Pt/C for ethanol oxidation after 1000 potential cycles. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 95]. Copyright 2016, American Chemical Society 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
