{411} [{211} [{100} (Fig. 12a–d), which also indicates that the higher the Miller index is, the better the oxidation of ethanol is (Fig. 12e). Liu et al. [ 95] compared the stability of Pt tetrahexahedral and ordinary Ptnanocrystal loaded on the same type of activated carbon (Fig. 12f–i). After 1000 potential cycles, the HIFs nanocrystal lost 28% of its initial activity while commonPt/C lost 51% of its activity (Fig. 12j–m), and the HIFs are still retained (Fig. 12j), which demonstrates that nanocrystal with HIFs can also improve the stability of the EOR. 4.4 Ethylene glycol oxidation reaction (EGOR) Direct ethylene glycol fuel cells (DEGFCs) have also attracted the interest of researchers in recent years. As a model reaction, the research of EGOR is beneﬁcial to the anode reaction of various fuel cells. As an organic mole-cule containing a C–C bond and two hydroxyl groups, its oxidation reaction mechanism may be quite complicated [82], the possible pathway is shown below CH 2OHCH 2OHþH2O!HOCH 2COO/C0þ5Hþþ4e/C0 ð16Þ CH 2OHCH 2OH!HOCCOH þ4Hþþ4e/C0ð17Þ HOCH 2COO/C0!2COH xþH2O ð18Þ HOC ¼COH !2COH x ð19Þ CH 2OHCH 2OH!2COH x ð20Þ COH x!COþxHþþxe/C0ð21Þ COH xþH2O!CO 2þyHþþye/C0ð22Þ PGMs-based materials are considered to be the most ideal catalysts for glycol oxidation due to their excellent ability to break C–C bonds and C–H bonds. Among them,the Pt crystal with HIFs has garnered signiﬁcant interest because of the enhanced catalytic activity. Du et al. [ 96] reported a kind of excavated and dendritic PtCo nanocubeswith HIFs (Pt–Co EDNCs) as efﬁcient catalysts to oxidation ethylene glycol. The speciﬁc activity of Pt–Co EDNCs can achieve 7.68 mA /C1cm -2, which is 2.12 times that of commercial Pt/C. In addition, its speciﬁc activity is also higher than that of Pt–Co NCs without HIFs, which can attribute to the high energy facets andundercoordinated atoms on the surface of Pt–Co EDNCs. Combining HIFs engineering with other strategies can further improve the oxidation performance of small organicmolecules. Alloying with other elements is an ideal choice. The introduction of other elements can not only cause compressive strain in the unit cell of PGMs [ 75], but also reduce the density of the d-band center of the Pt atom [88,97], thereby reducing the adsorption of intermediateand accelerating the oxidation of small organic molecules. Xiang et al. [ 98] successfully synthesized PtLa NCs with HIFs, among which Pt 10La5is 1.46 times more active than pure Pt. The introduction of La can maintain the samemorphology as pure Pt nanocrystal and maintain the inherent advantages of HIFs strategy. In addition, La has a synergistic effect with Pt, which promotes the cleavage ofC–C bonds and further enhances the oxidation activity of ethanol. The assembly strategy of active sites on the sur- face of HIFs is another feasible way. Wang et al. [ 82] introduced Mo atoms into the surface of Pt 3Mn CNCs as a reassembly of the active sites. Compared with Pt 3Mn CNCs without Mo, Mo–Pt 3Mn CNCs show good stability and maintains the initial morphology after long-term sta- bility reaction (Fig. 13a–h). The DFT calculation results show that compared with the Mo atoms inside the Pt 3Mn CNCs, the Mo–Mn bond formed by the Mo atoms on the surface can inhibit the dissolution of Mn in the electro-chemical reaction, thereby maintaining the morphology and catalytic performance of the Pt 3Mn CNCs. Modiﬁca- tions such as in situ etching of high-index crystal faces arealso a good way to improve performance. Qin et al. [ 72] etched the HIFs surface, which was achieved by the gal- vanic replacement reaction between PtCl 62-and Fe gen- erated by the reduction of excess FeCl 3. As shown in Fig. 13i–k, electrochemical in-situ Fourier transform infrared reﬂection (FTIR) spectra indicate that both Pt 3Mn CNCs and Pt 3Mn UCNCs exhibited good CO 2selectivity during the MOR process, but Pt 3Mn UCNCs can produce CO 2at a lower potential (0.3 V), which shows that the modiﬁcation strategies can effectively improve the anti- toxicity of the HIFs of PGMs. All these studies provide strong support for the further application of PGMs catalystswith HIFs. 5 NCs enclosed with HIFs for ORR Molecular oxygen is essential for human survival and has been known as a natural oxidant for years, yet our ability to control the reduction of oxygen gas effectively in aqueous medium is still limited. The sluggish kinetics of reducingoxygen gas electrocatalytically in an acidic environment is a major hurdle that needs to be overcome to develop cost- effective proton exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs)for various clean energy applications [ 99–105]. In recent years, a substantial amount of research effort has been devoted to the design and synthesis of electrocatalysts forORR from both computational work and study of model systems of extended single crystals of platinum alloys [106,107]. Among them, the NCs enclosed by HIFs have a enormous potential in the ORR. Herein, we discuss recent 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
