normal octahedral Pt 2Co alloy NCs with the same size (11.4 m2/C1g-1 Pt). This result reveals that the excavated octahedral structure exposes more active sites, which arebeneﬁcial for further adsorption/desorption during the catalytic process. The PtCo sample shows the extraordinary catalytic activity and durability toward ORR in alkalineenvironment. In the ORR test, the half potentials ( E 1/2) for the excavated octahedral PtCo and normal octahedral Pt2Co are almost the same (around 0.91 V), but they exhibited a remarkable positive shift of 60 mV compared with that of Pt/C (0.85 V) (Fig. 15f). This result highlights the high ORR activity of PtCo alloy catalysts. The speciﬁcactivity of PtCo/C is 1.85 mA /C1cm -2, which is far higher than that of Pt/C (0.25 mA /C1cm-2) though lower than that of Pt2Co/C (2.41 mA /C1cm-2) (Fig. 15g). Note that in spite of a lower speciﬁc activity, the excavated octahedral PtCo NCs exhibited the superior mass activity to the octahedral Pt2Co/C (Fig. 15g) due to the excavated morphology. Xu et al. [ 30] reported a nanocubic and HOH Pt–Ni NCs by facile one-pot hydrothermal synthesis. The obtainedHOH PtNi NCs were covered by HIFs and expose many stepped surface atoms. Further electrochemical measure- ment of the ORR indicated that the speciﬁc activities (at0.90 V (vs. RHE)) of the HOH (1.08 mA /C1cm -2), nanocu- bic (0.93 mA /C1cm-2), and CNC Pt–Ni alloy NCs (0.44 mA /C1cm-2) are 5, 4 and 2 times, respectively, as high as that of Pt/C (0.22 mA /C1cm-2), as based on the ECSA, demonstrating the highly enhanced activities of Pt–Ni alloy NCs. The above results revealed that HOH Pt–Ni alloyNCs with HIFs consisting of high-density atomic steps and kinks exhibit enhanced catalytic activity per unit surface area for the oxidation of small organic molecules, such asmethanol and formic acid, as well as the ORR, compared to that of commercial Pt black and Pt/C catalysts. As we know, low-dimensional (low-D) NCs (such as NWs, nanosheets) with HIFs possess greater potential in comparison with high-dimensional NCs. On the one hand, the low-D NCs with HIFs have a higher atomic utilization,stable structure and better electrical conductivity. On the other hand, the design and fabrication of low-D NCs (such as NWs, nanosheets) with HIFs also face more challenges due to high surface free energy. Luo’s group [ 110] reported a new class of Pt 3Fe zigzag- like NWs (Pt-skin Pt 3Fe z-NWs) with stable HIFs and nanosegregated Pt-skin structure (Fig. 16a–c), which are capable of substantially boosting electrocatalysis in ORR. Fig. 15 a SEM and bTEM images of excavated octahedral PtCo alloy NCs; c,dHRTEM images (left) and corresponding structure models (right) of an individual excavated octahedral PtCo alloy NCs viewed along [011] direction and [001] direction, respectively; ereal Co contents of PtCo alloy NCs synthesized with different ratios of precursors. Insets are corresponding TEM images of individual PtCo alloy NCs; fORR polarization curves of Pt/C, Pt 2Co/C and PtCo/C recorded at room temperature in an O 2-saturated 0.1 mol /C1L-1HClO 4aqueous solution at a scan rate of 10 mV /C1s-1and a rotation rate of 1600 r /C1min-1;gcomparison of speciﬁc activity and mass activity of different catalysts toward ORR at 0.9 V (vs. RHE). Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 109]. Copyright 2017, Elsevier 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
