These unique structural features endow the Pt-skin Pt 3Fe z-NWs with a mass activity of 2.11 A /C1mg-1and a speciﬁc activity of 4.34 mA /C1cm-2for the ORR at 0.9 V (vs. RHE), which are the highest in all reported PtFe-based ORR catalysts (Fig. 16d, e). DFT calculations reveal a combi- nation of exposed HIFs and formation of Pt-skin structure, leading to the optimal oxygen adsorption energy due to the ligand and strain effects, which is responsible for theenhanced ORR activities. In contrast to previously reported HIFs-based catalysts, the Pt-skin Pt 3Fe z-NWs maintain ultrahigh durability with little activity decay and negligiblestructure transformation after 50,000 potential cycles. In addition, Tian’s group [ 111] reported one-dimen- sional (1D) bunched Pt–Ni alloy nanocages with a Pt-skinstructure for the ORR that display high mass activity (3.52 A/C1mg -1 Pt) and speciﬁc activity (5.16 mA /C1cm-2), or nearly 17 and 14 times higher than those of commercial Pt/C catalyst. This catalyst exhibited high stability with negli- gible activity decay after 5 9104cycles. Both the exper- imental results and theoretical calculations reveal the existence of fewer strongly bonded Pt–O sites induced by the strain and ligand effects. Moreover, the fuel cellassembled by this catalyst delivers a current density of 1.5 A/C1cm-2at 0.6 V and can operate steadily for at least 180 h. 6 NCs enclosed with HIFs for HER The NCs with HIFs for the HER are of considerable attractive attention due to the excellent electrochemicalstability, highly competitive electrocatalytic activity, as well as the low overpotential. Liu et al. [ 112] synthesized the concave Pd@PdPt octa- hedral core–shell alloy structures through the one-pot syn- thesis method by using the EG as a reducing agent, the PVP and CTAB as the structural directing agents. These structurescombine the advantages of core–shell, alloy and concave octahedron structures, showing a remarkable improved HER electrocatalytic activity with a 