Transition metal-based materials (such as oxide, sulﬁde, phosphide and elenide) have attracted considerable inter- ests arising from the outperformance in electrochemical storage and energy conversion technologies. In light of theprevious works, two main routes are adopted for designing efﬁcient electrocatalyst with the best HER performance. One is increasing the number of active sites of the catalystvia morphology control. The other route is improving the electrical contact to these high active sites. Feng et al. [116] reported the Ni 3S2/NF via in situ growth of { /C22210} high-index faceted Ni 3S2nanosheet arrays on nickel foam (NF), which signiﬁcantly showed electrocatalytic activity and stability for both HER and OER. Subsequently, Denget al. [ 117] used the low-temperature method to synthesize the TiO 2@Ni 3S2core-branch arrays with Ni 3S2nanoﬂake branches composed of { /C22210} HIFs. Combining the advantages of rich active sites, high active surface area and uniform porous structure, the TiO 2@Ni 3S2catalysts per- formed the signiﬁcant electrocatalytic properties withlower potential and Tafel slopes for both HER and OER than other Ni 3S2counterparts. Zhang et al. [ 118] reported the CoP ultrathin porous ultrathin nanosheets with sub-1.1 nm thickness and exposed {200} crystal facets via a synthesis chemical transformation strategy through phosphidation of Co 3O4 precursors, which exhibited a low overpotential (only 56 and 131 mV are required for current densities of 10 and 100 mA /C1cm-2, respectively) and a small Tafel slope of 44 mV /C1dec-1in acidic condition. DFT calculations showed that the best performance arised from the abundant high reactive sites, appropriate structural disorder, short elec- tron/carrier transfer distance and large speciﬁc surface areas of the porous ultrathin 2D structures. Li et al. [ 119] reported the highly mono-disperse CoP NCs with abundant surface phosphide atoms on exposed {211} crystal planes by a robust solution-phase route, which presented highlyefﬁcient bifunctional catalytic activity toward ORR and HER in alkaline media. Shen et al. [ 120] synthesized the porous NiO NWs with composed of inter-connected NCsand numerous pores via an electrospinning method, exhibiting the best electrocatalytic performance compared to the commercial NiO nanoparticles. Introducing the carbon nanomaterials to construct cata- lyst/carbon hybrids are an efﬁcient way to enhance the electrical conductivity of electrocatalysts due to enrichcatalytic active sites and improve electrical coupling [121,122]. Zou et al. [ 123] reported that the CoP nanoparticles/carbon nanosheets (CoP NPs/CNSs) hybridstructures performed remarkably superior catalytic prop- erties than the CoP nanostructures enclosed by single or a few low-index facets toward the HER, ORR and OER inthe alkaline condition. Kou et al. [ 124] synthesized thecarbon-cohered high-index {222} faceted tantalum carbide NCs (TaC NCs@C) through a novel chlorine-assisted ‘‘micro-cutting-fragmentation’’ strategy by incomplete chlorination toward bulk TaC (Fig. 18). The TaC NCs@C catalysts showed remarkable HER performance with an overpotential of 146 mV to achieve a current density of 10 mA /C1cm -2, attributing to the exposure of the dominant HIFs of {222} on surfaces of TaC NCs. The cubic pyrite-phase transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) as the potential HER catalysts have attracted sig- niﬁcant interests arising from the abundant reserves, environmentally-friendly, unique 3d electron number andspecial e gorbitals. Li et al. [ 125] reported the synthesis of ultrathin metallic CuFeS 2nanosheets (NSs) with an aver- age sheet thickness of less than 2.5 nm with rich exposed high-index {0 /C2224} facets, exhibiting high HER performance with an onset potential of 28.1 mV and a small overpo- tential of 88.7 mV at 10 mA /C1cm-2. The ultrathin nanosheet structure and high density of S2-active sites on the exposed {0 /C2224} facets HIFs are beneﬁting for the fast transformation efﬁciency of adsorbed atomic hydrogen into H2. 7 NCs enclosed with HIFs for OER The OER is considered as difﬁcult reaction because of its complicated four electron transfer process, which isimportant for water splitting and energy storage [ 126–130]. In general, the four electron transfer process requires high overpotential in order to push the reaction smoothly. In this case, the rational use of effective catalyst is particularly important in terms of lowering the overpotential. Thus,highly active catalysts are welcomed [ 131,132]. Accord- ingly, considerable research efforts have been devoted to enhancing its activity via lots of different strategies,especially in composition regulation, structural regulation size regulation, etc. [ 133,134]. Previous research has reported that the undercoordinate atoms on crystallizedfacets are active sites for the OER [ 127]. The HIFs possess a high density of low-coordinated atoms (such as steps, edges and kinks), leading to catalytically more active sitesfor electrochemical reaction. Recently, the catalysts with HIFs perform well in some application. In particular, the exploration of highly active and with HIFs electrocatalyststhrough a controllable route was widely used for MOR and ORR, especially like Pt recious metals catalyst. In recent years, high-index catalysts are also increasingly being usedin OER fortunately, especially in non-precious metals catalyst. In addition, nanocatalysts with HIFs are accom- panied by high surface energy, which can reduce theenergy of whole reaction and play an important role in 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
