amples of {110} (8.42 mF /C1cm-2) and {111} (5.53 mF /C1cm-2) were tested much smaller than that of {112} (22.13 mF /C1cm-2) in double-layer capacitance (Cdl) tests. They attributed these excellent OER perfor- mance parameters to the ultrathin porous structure, {112} facets with higher density of Co2?active sites and oxygen vacancies on surface. All the factors mentioned above cansigniﬁcantly enhance the charge transfer rate and increase the number of active sites during the OER process. Among the numerous non-noble metal catalysts, Ni- based catalysts generally perform well in OER reactions, with the beneﬁts of relative earth-abundance [ 138]. At the same time, NCs with HIFs can offer more atomic steps thanlow-index facets because of its at least one index larger than unity. Hence, in 2015, Feng et al. [ 116] reported Ni 3S2 nanosheet arrays enclosed with { /C22210} supported on nickel foam (NF), dubbed Ni 3S2/NF for the ﬁrst time. It is proved that the material exhibited efﬁcient and ultrastable electro- catalytic activity in both HER and OER. The HIFs wereconﬁrmed by HRTEM. An angle of 70.7 /C176was observed Fig. 18 a Schematic formation of TaC NCs@C by a subtle chlorine-assisted micro-cutting fragmentation strategy; bTEM and cHR- TEM images of TaC NCs@C; dpolarization curves of commercial 20% JM Pt/C, TaC NCs@C and bulk TaC; eoverpoten- tial@10 mA /C1cm-2compared to other samples obtained at different temperatures; fnormalized XRD-derived peak intensity as a function of overpotential for different facets, where low-index (111) facet is used as base line. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [124]. Copyright 2017, Elsevier 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
