Similar to the activity trend of Ni-based metal sulﬁdes catalysts, the OER performance of Ni-based metal carbides with HIFs was enhanced efﬁciently. So far, the research on OER catalysts with HIFs mainly focused on non-preciousmetal, but the precious metal catalysts have rarely been reported. 8 Design principle and perspective of NCs enclosed with HIFs 8.1 Shape and composition modulating strategyMonometallic Pd and Pt catalysts have attracted signiﬁcant attention due to the effective electrocatalytic activity forboth the anode and the cathode of PEMFCs. Specially, the HIFs of Pt and Pd monometallic NCs contain a high den- sity of low-coordinate atomic steps and kinks and thusshow the superior catalytic activity relative to the low- index faces of the same metals. Particle morphologies and chemical composition play a crucial role in the electro- catalytic activity [ 142,143]. In the decade years, a number of strategies such as electrochemical method and wet chemical synthesis were employed to construct the various morphologies PGM-based electrocatalysis with enclosedby HIFs. Sun et al. reported the single-crystal tetrahexahedral Pt nanoparticles (THH Pt NPs) enclosed by 24 HIFs such as {730}, {210} and {520} surfaces through the synthesis of shape-controlled electrochemical method (Fig. 23a–e) [53]. The activity and stability of THH Pt NPs were sig- niﬁcantly improved for both electro-oxidation of formic acid (FAOR) and ethanol (EOR) compared to those of Ptnanospheres and commercial Pt/C catalyst (Fig. 23f–i). Remarkably, the morphology of THH Pt NPs showed no visible change after electro-oxidation reaction, indicating Fig. 23 a TEM image of THH Pt NPs recorded along [001] direction; bcorresponding SAED pattern; chigh-resolution TEM image recorded from boxed area marked in a;datomic model of Pt (730) plane with a high density of stepped surface atoms; eHRTEM image recorded from another THH Pt NPs to reveal surface atomic steps in areas made of (210) and (310) subfacets; ftransient current density curves of formic acid oxidation at 0.25 V; gpotential-dependent steady-state current density (left, recorded at 60 s) of formic acid oxidation on THH Pt NPs, Pt nanospheres, and commercial Pt/C catalyst, and the ratios R(right) between that of THH with latter two, respectively; htransient current density curves of ethanol oxidation at 0.30 V for THH, Pt nanospheres and Pt/C catalyst; ipotential-dependent steady-state current density (left) of ethanol oxidation on THH Pt NPs, Pt nanospheres and commercial Pt/C catalyst, and ratios R(right); jschematic illustration of different effect of oxygen adsorption/desorption on surface structure of (111) and (730) of Pt. Reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 53]. Copyright 2007, American Association for the Advancement of Science 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
