that the THH Pt NPs enclosed by HIFs possessed the chemical stability. The oxygen species (O adand OH ad) tend to diffuse or dope into the surface atoms of Pt low-index planes to generate the Pt–O lattice by place-exchangebetween oxygen and surface Pt atoms because of the larger coordination numbers in the low-energy surface, which could cause the destruction of surface structure (Fig. 23j), leading to the decrease in activity and stability during the long-time test. Subsequently, they prepared the high-yield trapezohedral Pt NCs (TPH NCs) enclosed by HIFs of {522} by a direct square wave electrodeposition method, which showed superior enhanced electro-oxidation cat-alytic activity of C1 molecules (CO, CH 3OH, HCOOH) compared to commercial Pt/C [ 59]. Sun’s group [ 56] lso reported the THH Pd enclosed by {730} HIFs via theelectrodeposition method for the EOR. The positive and negative peak current densities of ethanol oxidation on the THH Pd are 4.4 and 5.9 times higher than those of thecommercial Pd black catalyst, respectively. Stability was reported to be improved, with only 0.5% and 25% losses of initial catalytic activity in the positive- and negative-goingpotential scans after 1000 potential cycles, respectively. Xia et al. showed that the Pd CNCs surrounded by high- index {730} facets presented higher FAOR catalyticactivity compared to the conventional nanocubes [ 144]. In addition, the initial structures of Pd CNCs were preserved from destruction in the electro-catalytic reaction. Zhanget al. [ 84] synthesized the highly selective Pt CNCs with HIFs via an environmentally wet-chemical route in a simple aqueous solution. The Pt CNCs showed theimprovement of speciﬁc activities toward the MOR and FAOR with respect to commercial Pt black and Pt/C cat- alysts. Zhang et al. [ 145] reported the synthesis of cubic concave Pd NCs with HIFs of {730} by reducing H 2PdCl 4 with ascorbic acid in the presence of two capping agents(CTAC and CTAB) at room temperature, which exhibitmuch better electrocatalytic activity and stability than the commercial Pd-black catalyst toward the EOR. The same group also successfully synthesized the high-yield Pt NCswith surrounded by a series of { hkk} HIFs including {211} and {411} through the solvothermal method, which showed much better EOR catalytic property [ 94]. Recently, Huang et al. [ 66] reported a highly general and scalable method for synthesizing monometallic THH NPs (Pt, Pd, Rh, Ni and Co) and a library of bimetallic NPsenclosed by {210} HIFs through alloying/dealloying by introducing trace elements foreign metals (Bi, Sb, Pb or Te) to stabilize HIFs during high temperature thermaltreatment (900–1200 /C176C). Subsequently, they synthesized multimetallic heterostructured nanoparticles consisting of Pt, Pd, Rh and Au and investigated the suitability offorming HIFs by altering the nanoparticle composition andsize [ 68]. They found that this strategy for inducing HIFs is regardless of the initial nanoparticle morphology due to thermodynamically immiscible metal upon thermal annealing. The catalytic activities of foreign metals modi-ﬁed Pt-based, Rh-based and Pd-based for electrooxidation are much higher tha those of commercial Pt/C and Pd/C catalysts [ 66]. 8.2 Alloying and composition modulating strategyTo further improve the chemical activity and stability of NCs, a feasible approach is to synthesize high-index-faceted bimetallic or multimetallic PGM-based nanocata- lysts by introducing other metallic elements selectively. The alloying of different metal components could effec-tively boost the performance of nanocrystal catalysts due to the synergy effect of HIFs and electronic structure of the alloy, as illustrated in Fig. 24. Many PGM-based alloy nanocatalysts enclosed by HIFs have been reported with signiﬁcant enhancement of electrooxidation activity with respect to the pure Pt counterparts with HIFs, which isgenerally ascribed to the increased Pt utilization efﬁciency and the optimized surface adsorption energies originating from the synergies of ligand effects, electronic effects, aswell as the geometrical effects. Lu et al. [ 35] reported the fabrication of high yield Au–Pd core–shell THH heterostructures enclosed by {730} facets by using Aunanocubes as the structure-directing cores. These THH NCs possessing entirely HIFs presented a signiﬁcantly better electrocatalytic activity than concave octahedral andoctahedral Au–Pd heterostructures bounded by low-index facets. Yu et al. [ 15] prepared the high yield polyhedral shape-controlled Au@Pd NCs with three different classesof HIFs, including concave TOH NCs with { hhl} facets, concave hexoctahedral (HOH) NCs with { hkl} facets, and THH NCs with { hk0} facets. All the Au@Pd NCs with HIFs exhibited more active for FAOR than those conven- tional cubic and octahedral structures enclosed by low-in- dex facets. However, these core–shell bimetallic NCs possess the quite thick shell layer, which may result in the decrease of alloying effects, causing the degradation of catalyticactivity [ 54,146]. These are due to that the catalytic reactions are much more sensitive to the surface or near- surface composition rather than bulk composition. Honget al. [ 146] studied the inﬂuence of atomic distribution on the catalytic properties of bimetallic NCs enclosed by low- index facets toward FAOR. The octahedral Au–Pd alloy,Au@Pd core–shell, Pd and Au NCs with similar size were synthesized under the similar experimental conditions. Further investigation showed that electrocatalytic perfor-mances of the various catalysts follow the order Au–Pd 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
