alloy[Au@Pd core–shell [Pd[[ Au NCs. This enhanced catalytic reactivity of the Au–Pd alloy NCs was due to the modiﬁed electronic structure and excellent CO removal ability after alloying with Au. Great progress hasbeen made on the Pt–M bimetallic nanocatalysts enclosed by HIFs, including Pt–Pd, Pt–Ni, Pt–Co, Pt–Cu, Pt–Sn. Deng et al. [ 54] reported the alloying THH Pd–Pt NCs enclosed by HIFs via the electrochemistry method. These THH Pd–Pt NCs showed at least 3 and 6 times higher than the THH Pd and commercial Pd black catalysts toward to the FAOR, respectively. Zhan et al. [ 147] demonstrated the facile synthesis of Pd–Pt CNCs enclosed by HIFs for MORpreferential oxidation of CO than commercial Pt/C. In addition, the Pd–Pt CNCs presented composition-depen- dent catalytic activity, with Pd: Pt mass ratio of 40: 60performing the 4.6 times enhancement of speciﬁc activity compared to commercial Pt/C due to the presence of HIF on the surface and the electronic/synergistic effect betweenPd and Pt. Despite the alloying, PGM-based bimetallic NCs can effectively boost the catalytic properties of nanocrystalcatalysts, the ordered design and synthesis of shape-con- trolled alloy NCs with HIFs are still a challenge due to the difﬁculties in controlling high energy surfaces in thepresence of two metals with different nucleation and growth habits. Additionally, due to the scarcity of precious Pd and Au metals, alloying Pt with non-noble metal (suchas Cu, Co, Ni, Mn, Fe) has been found to be an effective way for optimizing the electrocatalytic structure and pro- moting the kinetics of electrooxidation. Xu et al. [ 30] reported the CNC and HOH Pt–Ni alloy NCs enclosed byHIFs via a wet-chemical method by simply adjusting the addition of glycine, which exhibited the better catalytic activity and stability for both MOR and FAOR with respect to Pt–Ni nanocubes and commercial Pt/C. Pt–Cu CNCsenclosed by HIFs of {511} were prepared by Qi et al. [148], which presented the substantially enhanced elec- trocatalytic activity and stability toward to MOR comparedwith the commercial Pt/C. Wan et al. [ 149] reported the Pt– Cu nanocuboctahedrons with concave surfaces via a one- pot solvothermal co-reduction method, which showed better enhanced electrocatalytic activity and durability for the MOR than Pt–Cu spherical and the pure Pt catalysts.Du et al. [ 40] synthesized the deeply excavated Pt 3Co nanocubes bounded with HIFs through a facile one-pot method. These excavated structures exhibited muchimprovement of electrocatalytic performance for both MOR and FAOR. Huang et al. [ 51] reported that the PtPb CNCs with exposed {520} facets exhibited high electro-catalytic stability and activity for MOR, 2.16 and 4.62 times higher than those of conventional PtPb NCs and commercial Pt/C catalysts, respectively. Chen’s group[150] has demonstrated the synthesis of concave PtZn polyhedral NCs with superior electrocatalytic activity for MOR. Mechanistic investigations demonstrated that theelectronic interactions between Pt and alloyed metals account for the enhancement of performance. Specially, the inert of non-noble metals could cause the downshift of thed-band center of Pt and modify the electronic structure of Pt, which might reduce the adsorption energies of CO on Pt by a compressive strain effect, thus resulting in the goodstability and activity. Huang et al. [ 67] synthesized a series Fig. 24 Illustrations of electronic effect, synergistic effect, high-energy sites and strain effect of PGM-based alloy nanostructures with enclosed by HIFs 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
