of Pt–M (M = Co, Ni and Cu, molar ratio of 1:1) and Rh– M (M = Ni and Co, molar ratio of 1:1) THH alloy with HIFs by Bi modiﬁcation at 900–1000 /C176C. The exploration showed that the efﬁciently catalytic activities and stabilitieswere achieved relative to those of commercial Pt (or Rh) and THH Pt (or Rh) catalysts with HIFs for MOR, FAOR and EOR. However, most of Pt-based non-precious metal alloy catalysts were vulnerable to suffer from the degradation of nanostructures arising from leaching non-noble elements, which will lead to the insufﬁcient stability property, thus causing the decrease in activity. To address this challenge,the shape-controlled PGM-based non-noble trimetallic alloy has been proposed. In contrast to mono- and bi- metallic NCs, the trimetallic NCs may possess an evengreater degree of catalytic activity and selectivity due to that more variables are available for tuning. Yin et al. [ 151] reported that the activity of Pt–Pd–Cu CNCs enclosed by{hk0} was improved. More recently, Zhang’s group [ 152] has developed a general strategy assisted by dual reduc- tants and dual protecting agents for preparing Pt-basedtrimetallic alloys with HIFs including CNC Pt–Ni–Cu, CNC Pt–Mn–Cu [ 89] and unique concave cube (UCNC) Pt–Fe–Mn [ 72] bounded by HIFs and found that their activities and stabilities for electrooxidation were superior that that of binary CNC and commercial Pt/C. Further electrochemical in situ FTIR experiments exhibited thathigher CO 2productivity could be observed on the Pt–Fe– Mn UCNC catalyst, implying that the Pt–Fe–Mn had a strong anti-poisoning ability. The introduction of the thirdelement can tune the electronic and geometric properties of the electrocatalysts in a synergistic manner. Alloying noble metals with earth-abundant transition metal elements canefﬁciently promote the performance of electrocatalytic reactions while decreasing cost.8.3 Surface/interface engineering strategy Despite the enhanced catalytic activity and stability of PGM-based nanocatalysts with HIFs are achieved com- pared to the low-index noble, preserving the HIFs is knownto be challenging in the long-time tests [ 153]. In addition, the insufﬁcient activity, ready poisoning and deactivation by CO intermediate still hinder the further application ofmetallic alloy catalysts for the electrooxidation. As shown in Fig. 25, surface/interface engineering has recently emerged as an effective way to improve the activity andstability of the PGM-based nanocatalysts bounded with HIFs. In fact, the introduction of carbon-support can result in the improvement of activity and durability due to theinteraction between catalyst and support. Sun et al. repor- ted that the EOR activity of Pt HIFs supported on carbon black was improved compared to that of the conventionalPt/C [ 154]. The results of situ FTIR demonstrated that the HIF-Pt/C catalysts exhibited the enhanced reactivity for alleviating the adsorption between the intermediates andcatalysts, which improved the anti-poisoning properties, resulting in the improvement of electrocatalytic perfor- mance. Qin et al. [ 155] reported the Pt–Co NCs/rGO hybrids via the one-pot synthesis by distributing Pt–Co alloy NCs with bounded by HIFs on rGO. These Pt–Co NCs/rGO hybrids present enhanced catalytic properties forthe FAOR due to the synergy of the HIFs and the electronic effect of the intimate contact/interaction between Pt–Co alloy and rGO. In addition, the rGO as a morphologycontroller played the key role in morphology controlling of Pt–Co binary alloys with HIFs. Introducing the metal hydroxides as the support is also a plausible way to tailorthe surface chemical composition, atomic arrangement and thus the catalytic performance. Yu et al. [ 88] combined well-deﬁned Pt–Ni CNCs enclosed by high-index HIFs Fig. 25 Schematic illustration for tuning surface/interface of electrocatalyst, where image for heteroatom doping was reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 97] Copyright 2018, Royal Society