with Ni(OH) 2nanosheets via a facile hydrothermal method. Implanting ‘‘active auxiliaries’’ (such as Ru, Mo, Fe, Sn) onto the surface of PGM-based NCs are another effectiveway to stabilize the crystal surface structure of PGM-based NCs due to the synergetic interaction between ‘‘active auxiliaries’’ and surface [ 156,157]. Previous studies have reported ‘‘active auxiliaries’’ doped onto the surface of PGM-based NCs bounded with low-index facets, which presented a higher performance. Wang et al. [ 82] reported a new strategy by doping the non-noble metals or nonmetals as ‘‘active auxiliaries’’ into the near-surface of the PGM-based NCs enclosed by HIFs to construct a stable novel- structured catalyst possessing with enhanced performance for EGOR. These Mo atoms were doped into the crystalsurface of Pt–Mn CNCs, which can alter the physico- chemical properties such as composition and structure of nanocrystal surface, thus promoting the catalytic activity.They also reported the isolated Ru atoms (Pt 3Mn–Ru) and Ru nanoparticles (Pt 3Mn@Ru) by inseting Ru onto Pt 3Mn catalysts via wet-chemical method and the former exhib-ited superior electrocatalytic activity, durability and CO- resistance ability for EGOR [ 158]. 8.4 Geometry structure engineering strategy One effective way to decrease the cost of electrocatalysts and improve the atomic utilization efﬁciency of nanocrystals is to construct the low-D nanostructures, as illustrated in Fig. 26. The 1D PGM-based NWs, especiallyPt-based alloy NWs, are generally considered as promising direction for state-of-the-art electrocatalysts due to the special physicochemical properties including the large high surface area, quick electron/mass transfer for high con-ductivity, abundant highly active atomic steps and HIFs, as well as good resistance to dissolution, Ostwald ripening and aggregation. Previous researches reported that theseadvantages could effectively improve the catalytic activity and stability of electrocatalysis. In recent years, many researches have been devoted to designing and synthesiz- ing the new class of Pt-based alloy NWs with exposed high active surfaces for simultaneously improving the utilizationof Pt atoms and enhancing catalytic properties [ 159–162]. Bu et al. [ 163] reported the synthesis of a class of hierarchical Pt–Co NWs via a robust large-scalable wet-chemical approach. Remarkably, the hierarchical Pt 3Co NWs exhibited the ordered intermetallic structures. The Pt- based nanomaterials with ordered structures and deﬁnitecomposition usually showed higher activity and durability than the conventional disordered alloys because of the higher anti-dissolution ability of the transition metalsoriginated from the stronger Pt–M interaction. In addition, these hierarchical Pt–Co NWs also possessed Pt-rich sur- face, high density active, as well as HIFs. All advantages ofhierarchical Pt–Co NWs caused the signiﬁcant enhance- ment of catalytic properties for MOR and EOR. The hierarchical Pt–Co NWs also showed the 39.6 times higherspeciﬁc activity and 33.7 times higher mass activity than commercial Pt/C catalysts for ORR, respectively. By using DFT simulations, they found that the enhanced factor of Fig. 26 Schematic illustration for tuning adsorption reaction sites of electrocatalysts, where image for nanocage was reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 46]. Copyright 2018, Wiley–VCH; image for multiframe was reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 159]. Copyright 2018, American Chemical Society; image for nanosponge was reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 160]. Copyright 2016, American Chemical Society; image for ultrathin NWs was reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 161]. Copyright 2018, Royal Society of Chemistry; image for porous NWs was reproduced with permission from Ref. [ 162]. Copyright 2017, Wiley–VCH 1 Rare Met.A review of energy and environment electrocatalysis based on high-index faceted nanocrystals
