catalytic performance was due to the presence of three-fold hollow active sites on the Pt-rich HIFs. The same group demonstrated an ultrathin PtNi NW mediated strategy for creating a new class of core–shell trimetallic PtNiPd NWsenclosed by HIFs for superior electrocatalysis [ 164]. These PtNiPd core–shell NWs are composed of interconnected NWs with majority of Pd as the core and PtNi as the shell.The initially formed ultrathin PtNi NWs acted as the growth seeds at the early stage and subsequently the reduction and diffusion of Pt and Ni occur to form the PtNi shell by introducing the Pd precursors. By combining the advantages of 1D morphology, HIFs, core–shell trimetallicalloy structures, and optimal elementary composition, the PtNiPd NWs exhibited superior catalytic activity and durability toward liquid fuel oxidation (EGOR and glyceroloxidation reaction (GOR)) and ORR than commercial Pt/C catalysts. Mao et al. [ 165] reported an efﬁcient synthesis of ultrathin Pt–Mo–Ni NWs containing an ultrathin and ultralong nanostructure with a diameter of *2.5 nm and length of several micrometers through H 2-assisted solution route (HASR). The results of HAADF-STEM showed that these Pt–Mo–Ni NWs possessed a rough surface structure surrounded by high-density low-coordinated atomic steps,ledges and kinks. Such Pt-based transition metal alloy ultrathin 1D NWs exposed by high active surface could effectively improve catalytic performance of EOR com-pared to commercial Pt/C and Pt black catalysts. They also explored the function of transition metal Ni and Mn. The introduction of Ni metals into Pt lattice can promote theadsorption of –OH species and the oxidation of the poi- soned CO intermediates at a lower potential, resulting in the enhancement of catalytic activity. The Mo metals as astabilizer can preserve the Pt and Ni atoms from dissolution and diffusion, leading to the improvement of durability. Shi et al. [ 166] reported the intermetallic Pd 3Pb nano- wire networks (IM-Pd 3Pb NNs) through a synthesis of one- step wet-chemical strategy at a low temperature for 1 h. The enhancement activity of EOR was achieved. Afterlong-term operation stability, they found that such IM- Pd 3Pb NNs bounded with more active step atoms possessed higher CO-resistance ability and durability. Luo et al. [ 110] synthesized a stable HIF Pt skin onto 1D Pt 3Fe zigzag-like NWs (Pt-skin Pt 3Fe z-NWs) by simultaneously reducing the Pt and Fe precursors and thermally annealing thez-NWs at high temperature. These unique Pt-skin Pt 3Fe z-NWs/C catalysts exhibited an excellent catalytic perfor- mance for both MOR and EOR. The Pt-skin Pt 3Fe z-NWs/ C catalysts showed 4.8 times higher mass activity and 5.8 times higher speciﬁc activity than commercial Pt/C toward the MOR, respectively. After 5000-s chronoamperometrictests, the retention of initial activity was higher than that of the commercial Pt/C, as well as the pristine Pt 3Fe z-NWs/ C. Tang et al. [ 167] prepared the screw-like PdPt NWs enclosed by HIFs via a programmed potential electrode-position method without using any templates and surfac- tants at room temperature conditions. These screw-like PdPt NWs showed the enhanced catalytic activity anddurability for both MOR and EOR. Further investigation revealed that the improvement of CO-tolerance ability is achieved. Chang et al. [ 168] demonstrated the synthesis of the PtNi 3ﬁshbone-like NWs (PtNi 3-FBNWs) with rough surfaces (backbone) and extended polyhedral structures(side chain). The rough surfaces showed the feature of richer Pt and HIFs. Such structures exhibited the best cat- alytic activity and signiﬁcant stability toward MOR.Compared with commercial Pt/C, the PtNi 3-FBNWs pre- sented the 4.76 times higher mass activity and 3.02 times higher speciﬁc activity. In addition, the Pt-based 3D nanoframe architectures are attracting considerable interests as the enhanced electro- catalysis arising from the large surface area, excellentﬂexibility, high porosity, as well as the advantage of trapping the gaseous reactants into the nanoconﬁned spaces. The vertex-type-selective growth of metallicnanohorns on a central nanocrystal was employed for the synthesis of vertex-type-selective Pt–Cu–Rh heterogeneous nanocages (HNCs) via a one-pot solvothermal synthesis byWang et al. [ 46]. These Pt–Cu–Rh HNCs are composed of a Rh-decorated Pt–Cu rhombic dodecahedral nanocage and six Pt–Cu–Rh nanohorns sticking out from the {100} ratherthan {111} vertices of central rhombic dodecahedron. Such architectures presented the abundant concave/convex sites and various HIFs around the surface, resulting in signiﬁ-cant catalytic performance for EOR with respect to com- mercial Pt/C catalysts, as well as dramatically enhanced anti-poisoning capacity. Huang et al. [ 169] reported the vertex-reinforced quaternary PtCoNiMo hexapod nano- skeletons (NSs) enclosed by HIFs for efﬁcient electro- catalysis of MOR. These unique structures are composed ofcore, ﬁrst-layer feet and second-layer feet. Compared with PtCoNi NPs, the vertex-reinforced PtCoNiMo hexapod NSs exhibited an efﬁcient atomic utilization because of thehighly open structures and abundant high active sites. They found that the electron transfer and mass exchange were accelerated in electrochemical tests, which caused theenhancement of catalytic activity. The enhanced stability and anti-poisoning ability were also achieved. Yang et al. [71] synthesized a series of PtCuCo NFs bounded with HIFs by a microwave-assisted one-pot approach, which performed improved activity and durability for both MOR and FAOR. 1 Rare Met.Y.-R. Li et al.
