9 Summary and outlook For the past decade, the explorations of NCs with HIFs have already opened a novel avenue to design advancedelectrocatalysis with high-performance. This review has summarized the recent research progress on NCs with HIFs for electrooxidation (such as MOR, EOR, EGOR), oxygenreduction and electrochemical water splitting (HER and OER), including the synthesis of catalysts and the design strategies at nanoscale for the improvement of electrocat-alytic performance (such as activity and stability). Herein, we discussed the design synthetic strategies of nanocata- lysts enclosed by HIFs, including selective etching, surfacecapping, kinetic control, and template direction, as well as the high-temperature alloying and dealloying. We dis- cussed a connection between the shape or morphologytaken by a nanocrystal and the growth mechanism. All the synthetic methods of NCs with HIFs mentioned in this review have their own unique advantages, as well as lim-itations, respectively. Among them, the electrochemical and wet-chemical methods have been widely used for the synthesis of noble metal catalyst and non-noble metalcatalyst with HIFs. However, the general and simple methods to prepare electrocatalysts with HIFs remain highly challenging, especially for proton exchange mem-brane fuel cells and electrolyzed water. For all these, the main reason is that the HIFs usually disappear during the growth of nanocrystals because of the higher surface energies. Recent studies have demonstrated that HIFs could be stabilized by the selective adsorption of speciﬁc chem-ical species in solution synthesis via a wet-chemical route. We also summarized several strategies for tuning the properties of the nanocatalysts with HIFs, including shapeand size controlling, alloying and dealloying, surface and interface engineering, composition tuning, as well as the dimension engineering. The performances of each strategyand the possible reasons for the enhanced electrochemical activity and stability have also been demonstrated. Among them, controlling the shape and thus the facets of metalnanocrystals has long been considered as a powerful mean for tuning their catalytic performance and improving their activity and stability in a wide variety of applications. Inaddition, the NCs enclosed with amounts of undercoordi- nated surface atoms and well-deﬁned atomic arrangement could provide a new model surface for viewing the struc-ture-performance functionality at the atomic level, which requires precise and continuous stability in the surface structure of NCs. Thus, the size modulating of the NCswith HIFs, especially small size, \10 nm NCs, is still an important challenge for bimetallic or trimetallic NCs. Moreover, to systematically tune the surface atom structureof catalysts to alter the surface physico-chemical properties can become more effective practically. However, theexceptional properties of NCs with HIFs have been mostly demonstrated in the laboratory; it is vital to explore syn- thesis technologies for scaling-up to large batch production and manufacturing. In conclusion, synthesis strategy,composition, size, morphology and surface structure play important roles in improving the properties of electrocatalysts. Acknowledgements This work was ﬁnancially supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (No. 22008135) andthe China Postdoctoral Science Foundation (No. 2020M670345). References [1] Banham D, Ye S. Current status and future development of catalyst materials and catalyst layers for proton exchange membrane fuel cells: an industrial perspective. ACS Energy Lett. 2017;2(3):629. [2] Yu L, Zhou TT, Cao SH, Tai XS, Liu LL, Wang Y. Sup- pressing the surface passivation of Pt–Mo nanowires via con- structing Mo–Se coordination for boosting HER performance. Nano Res. 2020. https://doi.org/10.1007/s12274-020-3269-8 . [3] Wang Y, Wang DS, Li YD. A fundamental comprehension and recent progress in advanced Pt-based ORR nanocatalyst