Review Article Muhammad Murad, Salman Khan, Noureen Amir Khan, Nisar Khan, Muhammad Yasin, Kashif Ali, Zonish Zeb, M. K. M. Ali and Muhammad Azam Qamar* A review of transition metal compounds-carbon hybrid electrocatalysts for sustainable hydrogenproduction through water splitting https://doi.org/10.1515/revic-2025-0074 Received July 28, 2025; accepted November 22, 2025; published online December 15, 2025 Abstract: Designing e ﬃcient, a ﬀordable, and durable elec- trocatalysts to drive water splitting is crucial for addressing the energy crisis and environmental issues. Transition metal-based materials are widely used for hydrogen and oxygen evolution reactions due to their tunable activity and abundance. However, challenges like low conductivity, agglomeration, and poor stability persist. Transition metalcompounds have the potential to provide a solution by integrating carbon materials. This review examines the integration of transition metal compounds (TMCs) with carbon nano ﬁbers (CNFs) and carbon nanotubes (CNTs) to enhance the e ﬃciency of electrochemical water splitting, a vital process for sustainable hydrogen production. The mechanisms of hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and ox- ygen evolution reaction (OER) are discussed, emphasizing the role of electrocatalysts in mitigating overpotentials associated with these reactions. TMCs, when combined with CNFs and CNTs, create synergistic e ﬀects that improve re- action kinetics and increase active sites for catalysis. This review highlights recent advancements in the development of hybrid nanocomposites, detailing how their unique physicochemical properties facilitate e ﬀective electrontransfer and enhance electrocatalytic activity and the sta- bility of these integrated materials under harsh electro- chemical conditions. Through a comprehensive examination of recent studies, this review aims to provide insights into the design and optimization of advanced electrocatalysts for eﬃcient water splitting. Keywords: electrocatalysis; water splitting; transition metal compounds; carbon nanomaterials; hydrogen production 1 Introduction The global energy landscape is undergoing a transformative shift towards sustainable and renewable energy sources in response to the escalating climate crisis and the depletion of fossil fuels. Among various innovative renewable energy technologies, electrochemical water splitting has emerged as a promising method for hydrogen production, which is heralded as a clean fuel alternative that can help mitigate greenhouse gas emissions. Additionally, water splitting can be directly integrated with solar, wind, and other intermit-tent renewable energy sources, making it a cornerstone technology for decarbonizing global energy systems. The process involves the decomposition of water into hydrogen (H 2) and oxygen (O 2) gases through two key half-reactions; the HER at the cathode and the OER at the anode. Achieving eﬃcient water splitting requires the application of e ﬀective electrocatalysts that can lower the activation energy of these reactions, thereby improving overall energy e ﬃciency.1,2 Transition metals and compounds, and carbon-based nanostructures, particularly CNFs and CNTs, have gained signiﬁcant attention as potential candidates for electro- catalysts in water-splitting applications. Transition metals such as nickel, cobalt, and iron, and their compounds such as oxides, sul ﬁdes, phosphides, and carbides, are known for their catalytic properties, while carbon nanomaterials o ﬀer unique structural and electrical characteristics that enhance the performance of these metals. The integration of TMCs*Corresponding author: Muhammad Azam Qamar , Institute of Innovation Materials and Energy, School of Chemistry and Materials Science, Yangzhou University, Yangzhou, 225002, P.R. China,E-mail: qamariub@gmail.com. https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7645-7133 Muhammad Murad, Nisar Khan, Muhammad Yasin, Kashif Ali and Zonish Zeb, Institute of Innovation Materials and Energy, School of Chemistry and Materials Science, Yangzhou University, Yangzhou, 225002, P.R. China Salman Khan, School of Mechanical Engineering, Sichuan University, Chengdu 610065, ChinaNoureen Amir Khan, Government City Girls College, Gulbahar No.2, Peshawar, Pakistan; and Higher Education Archives and Libraries Department, Peshawar, Khyber Pakhtunkhwa, Pakistan M. K. M. Ali, Physics Department, College of Science, Imam Mohammad Ibn Saud Islamic University (IMSIU), Riyadh, 13318, Saudi ArabiaRev Inorg Chem 2025; aop Open Access. © 2025 the author(s), published by De Gruyter. This work is licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License.
