with CNFs and CNTs provides a synergistic e ﬀect that not only enhances electrocatalytic activity but also improves stability and conductivity, addressing some of the limitations associated with traditional catalyst materials.3–5 Recent advancements in nanotechnology have enabled the synthesis of various carbon nanostructures that exhibit high surface area, tunable morphology, and exceptional mechanical properties. These attributes are crucial forfacilitating mass transport and charge transfer during the electrochemical reactions involved in water splitting. Furthermore, the incorporation of transition metal com- pounds into carbon matrices can signi ﬁcantly alter the electronic structure and surface chemistry, optimizing the catalyst ’s performance. For example, the presence of active sites for hydrogen adsorption can be enhanced through the strategic design of the catalyst architecture, leading to a reduction in overpotentials associated with both HER and OER. 6–9 Theoretical studies and experimental ﬁndings indicate that the e ﬃciency of electrocatalysts is closely linked to their intrinsic properties, such as electronic conductivity, surface area, and the nature of the active sites. In this context, the Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption ( ΔGH) on the catalyst surface plays a pivotal role in determining the re- action kinetics. A suitable ΔGHallows for e ﬃcient hydrogen evolution while ensuring rapid desorption of molecular hydrogen from the catalyst surface.10,11 Despite of advantages, the practical deployment of wa- ter splitting is hindered by several challenges. The OER is kinetically sluggish due to its multi-electron transfer pro- cess, leading to large overpotentials and low overall energy eﬃciency. The HER,12although faster, also faces challenges related to electrode stability in both acidic and alkaline media. Moreover, most state-of-the-art catalysts rely on scarce and expensive noble metals such as platinum, ruthenium, and iridium, which limit scalability. Other ob- stacles include electrode degradation, corrosion of cell components, and the high cost of electrolyzer systems. Consequently, ongoing research focuses on developing cost-eﬀective, earth-abundant catalysts with high activity and long-term durability, as well as optimizing reactor designs and energy integration strategies to make water splitting economically viable for large-scale hydrogen production. 13,14 This review aims to provide a comprehensive overview of the current state of research on transition metal and compound-based carbon nano ﬁbers and nanotubes as elec- trocatalysts for e ﬃcient water splitting. This review dis- cusses the fundamental mechanism of water splitting, HERand OER, explore the synthesis and characterization of various TMCs-CNFs/CNTs based catalyst systems, and high- light the recent advancements in enhancing electrocatalyticperformance. Furthermore, address the future directions and potential pathways for overcoming the existing chal- lenges in the ﬁeld, with a focus on the integration of novel materials and innovative designs that promise to push the boundaries of water-splitting e ﬃciency. 2 Fundamental understanding of electrochemical water splitting The process of splitting water molecules into H 2and O 2by means of an electric current is known as electrochemical water splitting. There are two half-cell reactions involved inthis process; one at the anode and one at the cathode. In order to electrolyze water at the cathode and anode, two half processes are needed; the hydrogen evolution reaction and the oxygen evolution reaction. 15,16A device known as an electrolyzer, which is made up of an anode, cathode, and electrolyte, is where the water splitting process takes place shown in Figure 1.17In the electrolytic water splitting pro- cess, the cathode and anode surfaces yield pure hydrogen and oxygen gases, respectively. The anode and cathode un-dergo a variety of electrochemical reactions, though, depending on the electrolytes ’varying pH values. 18 In acidic electrolytes, HER :4H++4e−=2H2E=0 V (1) OER :2H2O–4e−=O2+4H+E=1.23 V (2) In basic electrolytes, HER :4H2O+4e−=2H2+4OH−E=0.828 V (3) OER :4OH−−4e−=2H2O+O2E=0.402 V (4) Overall reaction, 2H2O=2H2+O2E=1.23 V (5) Regardless of whether the electrolyte is alkaline or acidic, the predicted thermodynamic voltage of water split- ting at 298 K and 1 atm is 1.23 V. The operating voltage for electrolyzing water, however, is much greater than 1.23 V. One de ﬁnition of the redundant potential is the over- potential caused by the intrinsic activation impediment.2 Eop=E+Ea+Ec+ErE=1.23 V (6) where E ris the excess potential generated by electrolyte diﬀusion, surface polarization (capacitance), and other counter-electromotive force sources, and E ais the anode ’s overpotential to perform OER, and E cis the cathode ’s overpotential to perform HER. Of them, E aand E care important for the electrocatalyst ’sa c t i v i t yt o w a r d sO E R2 M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon
