and HER at the anode and cathode, respectively. As a result, using electrocatalysts as the anode and cathode materials can sharply reduce E aand E cwhile simulta- neously enhancing water splitting ’s electrocatalytic activ- ity to increase e ﬃciency. 2.1 Mechanism of the hydrogen evolution reaction HER is a two-step cathodic reaction with favorable kinetics. HER uses the Volmer step when the environment is acidic. Before being removed from the catalyst surface by the chemical combination of Tafel or electrochemical Heyrovsky processes, hydrogen ions ﬁrst transform into an intermedi- ate hydrogen atom (H*) by absorbing an electron and adsorbing on the catalyst surface. The Volmer –Heyrovsky mechanism and the Volmer –Tafel mechanism are the two ways that HER proceeds in an acidic and an alkaline envi-ronment, respectively. 19,20 The HER pathway in an alkaline environment is similar to that in an acidic one, with the exception that in an alkaline environment, water molecules produce intermediate atomic hydrogen. The electrochemical reduction of H 2O into absorbed OH and H* during the Volmer process takes place in an alkaline environment as opposed to an acidic one. It is more di ﬃcult to break the H –O–H bond in the solution un- der these conditions prior to H* adsorption at the catalystsurface. The HER is more suited to an acidic environment due to its H +level Figure 2A.21 The HER reaction mechanism in di ﬀerent electrolyte is described below: In neutral and basic electrolyte, H2O+*+e−→H*+OH−(7) Tafel reaction:2H*→H2 (8) Heyrovsky reaction: H3O++e−+H*→H2+H2O (9) In acidic electrolyte, Volmer Reaction: H3O++*+e−→H*+H2O (10) Tafel reaction: 2H*→H2 (11) Heyrovsky reaction: H3O++e−+H*→H2+H2O (12) The catalyst ’s electrical and surface structure has a signiﬁcant impact on the hydrogen evolution reaction ’s ki- netics. The Volmer step becomes the rate-determining step in the event of poor hydrogen adsorption on the catalyst surface, producing a Tafel slope of about 120 mV/dec. More surfaces, edges, and cavities in catalysts improve charge transfer and hydrogen adsorption, increasing e ﬃciency. The Tafel step, on the other hand, regulates the kinetics if hydrogen adsorption is strong on active sites. This results in high surface concentrations of H*, which encourage thecombining of neighboring hydrogen atoms to produce hydrogen gas, producing a Tafel slope of 30 mV/dec. The reaction speed is controlled by the Heyrovsky reaction, which produces a Tafel slope of 40 mV/dec when the con- centration of intermediate hydrogen atoms is low. When Heyrovsky or Tafel reactions are rate-determining, increasing surface roughness accelerates the water-splitting rate. 22,23The volcano curve, which links exchange current density with ΔGH, explains how important the Gibbs free energy ( ΔGH) of hydrogen atoms on the catalyst surface is for electrocatalytic activity. To facilitate electron-proton trans-fer and ensure that the bond is weak enough for quick H 2 Figure 1: Illustration of water splitting and the involved mechanisms, HER and OER. The * present the catalytic reaction site on thecatalysts 17Copyright © 2025, Wiley- VCH GmbH.M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 3
