release, an e ﬃcient HER electrocatalyst must establish a suﬃcient bond with adsorbed hydrogen. Catalysts should ideally be placed close to the vertex of the volcanic curve, where ΔGHapproaches zero.11,24 2.2 Mechanism of the oxygen evolution reaction In contrast to HER at the cathode, the reaction pathway of OER at the anode is a four-electron transfer process withseveral intermediates. The mechanism and reaction pathway of OER are more complex than those of HER because of its slow kinetics. The OER process can be broadly characterized from the beginning and ﬁnal products as fol- lows, taking into account the solution ’s varying acidity and alkalinity: the H 2O molecule loses electrons to produce O 2 and H+in an acidic solution, while OH−loses electrons to produce H 2O and O 2in an alkaline solution.25,26 In an acidic solution,H2O+M=M−*OH+H++e−(13) M−*OH=M−*O+H++e−(14) M−O*+H2O=M−*OOH +H++e−(15) M−*OOH =M+O2+H++e−(16) In an alkaline solution, M+OH−=M−*OH+e−(17) M−*OH+OH−=M−O*+H2O+e−(18) M−O*+OH−=M−*OOH +e−(19) M−*OOH +OH−=M+O2+H2O+e−(20) Many intermediates, such as O*, *OH, and *OOH, are observed to occur during the entire OER process. O 2can be readily produced from metal oxide surfaces, according toreports. Furthermore, the electrocatalyst ’s electrical struc- ture can in ﬂuence its capacity to adsorb *OH and generate *OOH, which primarily a ﬀects the OER performance. It is Figure 2: Illustration of the mechanisms and performance of catalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and the oxygen evolution reaction (OER). A. The HER pathways in acidic and alkaline medium. B. The mechanism of OER in an acidic and alkaline medium. C. The volcano plot for the hydrogen evolution reaction on metal electrodes in acidic conditions depicts the relationship between the logarithm of the exchange current densit y( j0) and the metal –hydrogen (M −H) bond energy for various metal surfaces36Copyright © 2010, American Chemical Society. D. Volcano plot demonstrates the relationship between the rate of O 2oxidation on transition metal oxide surfaces and the change in enthalpy in acidic and basic solutions. Measurement of overpotential in relation to 0.1 mA cm−2current density36Copyright © 2010, American Chemical Society.4 M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon
