3 Synthetic strategies of TMCs and carbon nanomaterial hybrids The rational construction of transition-metal compounds- carbon nanomaterial hybrids specially, CNFs and CNTs hasbecome a central theme in modern energy-chemistry research because the integration of high-activity TMCs with highly conductive and mechanically robust carbonscaﬀolds synergistically boosts electron transport, exposes abundant active sites, and suppresses structural degrada- tion during electrocatalytic water splitting. 9,45 –47The syn- thesis strategies employed to fabricate the TMCs-CNFs andCNTs heterostructures are critical in determining their structural integrity, surface characteristics, and their elec- trochemical or catalytic performance. Several syntheticstrategies have been developed to construct the TMCs-basedcarbon nanostructure with the microspheric morphology, such as electrospinning, which o ﬀers a direct route to continuous, binder-free nano ﬁber mats in which TMC nanoparticles are uniformly anchored along graphitic ﬁ- bers, ensuring rapid charge collection and facile electrolyte inﬁltration. 48,49Hydrothermal/solvothermal approaches exploit mild yet tunable solvent environments to crystallizeTMCs directly onto carbon cloth, graphene, or nanotubes ornanﬁbers, a ﬀording precise control over size, phase, and edge-rich morphologies. 9,50Chemical vapour deposition (CVD) enables conformal growth or encapsulation of TMCswithin carbon shells at elevated temperatures, yielding dense heterointerfaces and atomically thin protective layers that stabilize catalytic surfaces under harsh operating con-ditions. 51,52Microwave-assisted synthesis further shortens reaction times to minutes while uniformly heating the entire precursor, resulting in highly crystalline TMCs dispersed on porous carbon with minimal energy input.53,54Beyond these mainstream routes, alternative strategies are increasinglyvaluable: valence engineering tailors the oxidation states of metal centers via post-reduction or doping, 52sol-gel chem- istry a ﬀords molecular-level mixing of metal and carbon sources to create mesoporous composites,55and template- assisted pyrolysis converts pre-shaped precursors into hi- erarchical architectures with tunable pore size and surface area.56Collectively, these complementary synthetic toolkits provide versatile, scalable pathways to engineer TMC –car- bon hybrids with optimized electronic structures, maxi- mized active-site density, and exceptional durability foreﬃcient electrocatalytic hydrogen and oxygen evolution. The comparison of di ﬀerent synthetic strategies is listed in Table 1.3.1 Electrospinning The synthesis of transition metal compounds and carbon hybrids via electrospinning for water splitting is a promisingapproach to develop e ﬃcient and cost-e ﬀective electro- catalysts. 57This method leverages the high surface area and conductivity of carbon nanomaterials, combined with the catalytic properties of transition metal compounds, to enhance the performance of water splitting reactions.58The electrospinning technique allows for the uniform distribu- tion of metal nanoparticles within the carbon matrix, improving the catalytic activity of the catalysts.59For example, Woo et al.48demonstrated a straightforward approach that combines electrospinning and pyrolysis to fabricate carbon nano ﬁbers embedded with Co –CeO 2 nanoparticles (Co –CeO 2@CNF) for e ﬃcient HER, OER, and overall water splitting. First, a PAN/DMF precursor solution was electrospun at 27 kV and 1.5 mL h−1for the HER catalyst and at 15 kV and 0.8 mL h−1for the OER catalyst. Second, theTable :The comparison of synthetic strategies for transition-metal compounds and carbon nanomaterial with advantages and limitations. Synthetic methodsAdvantages Limitations Electrospinning Produces continuous, high-surface-area CNF mats with TMCs evenly embedded along ﬁber axesRequires high- temperature calcination (> °C) to convert poly- mer to carbon and pre-cursor to TMC, riskingﬁber 