collected ﬁbers were stabilized at 280 °C in air for 3 h, then carbonized at 900 °C under N 2for 5 h to yield Co –CeO 2@CNF for the HER and NiFe@CNF for the OER. The synthesized nanoﬁbers exhibit a one-dimensional morphology with an average diameter of about 840 nm. Scanning electron mi- croscopy (SEM) analysis reveals a rough surface texture uniformly decorated with Co –CeO 2nanoparticles embedded throughout the carbon nano ﬁber matrix, which enables the catalyst to deliver excellent HER, OER, and overall water splitting performance. Zhong et al.60reported the synthesis of Fe-doped NiS 2crystals grown on the surface of carbon nanoﬁbers encapsulated with NiFe alloy nanoparticles ((Ni,Fe)S 2/NiFe-CNFs) via an electrospinning –calcination – vulcanization process for boosting the OER. NiFe-CNFs were fabricated through an electrospinning-carbonization pro- cess in which the precursor solution of NiFe –CNF was elec- trospun under a high-voltage power supply of 16 kV, followed by drying at 45 °C in a vacuum oven to obtain Ni(CH 3COO) 2/Fe(acac) 3-PAN membranes. Subsequently, the obtained membranes were pre-oxidized and then carbon- ized at 800 °C under an Ar atmosphere for 2 h, after which the NiFe –CNF membranes were immersed in aqueous thiourea and hydrothermally treated at 200 °C for 18 h to yield (Ni,Fe)S 2/NiFe-CNFs. The resultant composite possesses a continuous, interconnected carbon-nano ﬁber sca ﬀold with a diameter of ≈400 nm that is densely decorated with cube-like Fe-doped NiS 2crystals with a ≈0.283 nm lattice, while NiFe alloy nanoparticles remain embedded; EDXmapping con ﬁrms uniform Ni/Fe/S distribution and strong cube –ﬁber contact. This morphology supplies a conductive 3-D network for rapid electron transport, a high density of exposed Fe-doped NiS 2active sites, and robust cube –ﬁber adhesion that prevents aggregation, together reducing the OER overpotential to a low value and ensuring long-term stability Figure 3A. Electrospinning strategy was used to synthesize CoFe 2O4nanoparticles uniformly embedded within one-dimensional N-doped carbon nano ﬁbers (CoFe 2O4@N-CNFs), resulting in a nanohybrid with excep- tional oxygen evolution reaction (OER) performance.49The synergistic integration of catalytically active CoFe 2O4with conductive N-doped carbon nano ﬁbers delivers low over- potential, high current density, a small Tafel slope, and outstanding long-term stability in alkaline media, surpass- ing both individual components and commercial RuO 2cat- alysts. Liu et al.61synthesized Fe, Co-co-doped Ni 5P4 hierarchical cube-on-sheet nanoarrays grown on coal-basedcarbon nano ﬁbers FeCo –Ni 5P4@C-CNFs through an electro- spinning approach followed by a hydrothermal process.OC-PAN nano ﬁbers were electrospun, pre-oxidized, and calcined at 800 °C for 2 h to yield C-CNFs. The C-CNFs were then hydrothermally treated at 120 °C for 6 h to grow Ni(OH) 2nanosheets, onto which NiCoFe PBA nanocubes were co- precipitated at room temperature. The resulting composite and NaH 2PO2were subsequently calcined to obtain FeCo – Ni5P4@C-CNFs. The resultant composite possesses a dense forest of vertically aligned nanosheets whose surface is uniformly decorated with ≈120 nm NiCoFe PBA nanocubes. High-resolution TEM reveals lattice fringes of 0.196 nm (Ni3P4300) and 0.217 nm (Ni 5P4211) within the particles, and EDS mapping con ﬁrms the homogeneous distribution of Fe, Co, Ni, and P throughout the nanosheets. This hierarchical cube-on-sheet morphology enlarges the electrochemically active surface area, exposes abundant dual-metal-doped active sites, and provides superhydrophilic yet aerophobic pathways for rapid electrolyte penetration and facile gas bubble release. Consequently, it enhances intrinsic activity and mass-transfer kinetics, signi ﬁcantly lowering the OER overpotential Figure 3B. Another study reported the fabri- cation of Ni@NiO nanoparticles embedded in carbon nano- ﬁbers (Ni@NiO/CNFs) via an electrospinning –carbonization process.62The precursor solution of Ni@C was electrospun at 21 kV and 0.5 mL min−1, dried at 45 °C, then calcined at 900 °C for 2 h under an inert atmosphere to yield Ni@C nano ﬁbers. Theﬁbers were subsequently treated at 400 °C in air to oxidize surface Ni to NiO, washed, and dried to obtain 3Ni@NiO/CNFs. The resultant composite possesses a coral- like, cross-linked nano ﬁber morphology with an average diameter of ≈145 nm and lengths up to tens of micrometers, producing a high aspect-ratio 3-D sca ﬀold uniformly deco- rated with 13.5 nm Ni@NiO nanoparticles embedded in a graphitic carbon matrix. This structure enhances electrical conductivity, exposes abundant Ni/NiO active sites, and creates mesopores, collectively lowering charge-transfer resistance and facilitating rapid electrolyte penetration and gas bubble detachment, thereby lowering the overpotential and enhancing durability Figure 3C. A self-standing nitrogen-doped hollow carbon nano ﬁber membrane embedding Co/Co 2P heterojunctions (Co/Co 2P-NCNFs-H) was fabricated through continuous coaxial electrospinning. The resulting composite exhibits uniform hollow channels (200 – 250 nm diameter, 30 –50 nm wall thickness) and a high spe- ciﬁc surface area (254 m2g−1, which enhances charge- transfer e ﬃciency and mass transport. The uniformly dispersed Co/Co 2P heterojunctions within the N-doped car- bon framework create strong interfacial electronic synergy, thereby optimizing both the HER and OER.63Furthermore, self-supported NiCo alloy-embedded carbon nano ﬁbers NiCo –NCNF for e ﬃcient water splitting,47RuNi nano- particles embedded in N-doped carbon nano ﬁbers (RuNi- NCNFs) as a robust bifunctional catalyst for e ﬃcient overall water splitting,64nitrogen-doped carbon nano ﬁber-con ﬁned Ni2P/Ni 3N nanoparticles (Ni 2P/Ni 3N/NCNF) for e ﬃcient urea-M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 7
