erarchical morphology maximizes the electrochemically active surface area, exposes abundant triphasic hetero- interfaces, and provides continuous conductive pathways via the graphitic N-doped carbon skeleton. Consequently, itenhances charge-transfer e ﬃciency and lowers the reaction energy barriers for both the hydrogen evolution reaction and the oxygen evolution reaction, leading to outstanding bifunctional water-splitting performance with low over- potentials Figure 4C. Furthermore, a biomass-derived CoS@CNFs hybrid membrane with a layered core –shell structure, synthesized via carbonization followed by hy- drothermal treatment, has been reported as a highly active hydrogen evolution catalyst across a wide pH range. Usingsilk-ﬁbroin protein as a sustainable precursor, the growth and morphology of CoS nanoparticles on CNFs were pre- cisely tuned by adjusting cobalt-nitrate and thioureaconcentrations, enabling controllable exposure of active sites. 70Another study introduced a CuS/CdFe 2O4composite integrated with CNFs to create a hierarchical electrocatalyst that exhibits enhanced conductivity, structural stability, and abundant active sites for overall water splitting.71Addi- tionally, the rapid, one-step hydrothermal synthesis of cubic NiO/troilite-FeS hybrids embedded within a three- dimensional carbon-nanotube framework has yielded acost-eﬀective, highly active bifunctional electrocatalyst; the NiO/FeS@CNT composite demonstrated remarkable perfor- mance in alkaline media, achieving low overpotentials for both hydrogen and oxygen evolution reactions. 72 3.3 Chemical vapor deposition The chemical vapor deposition (CVD) method is a versatile and e ﬀective technique for synthesizing transition metal compounds and carbon nanomaterials composites. This method involves the pyrolysis of hydrocarbon gases in the presence of transition metal catalysts, leading to the for- mation of CNFs and CNTs composites. The CVD process ishighly adaptable, allowing for the precise control of exper- imental variables such as temperature, catalyst type, and gas composition, which are crucial for optimizing the properties of the resulting materials for speci ﬁc applications like water splitting. 73,74CVD can deposit a ﬁlm of solid material on a heated surface from a chemical reaction in the vapor phase. The versatile process can result in nano/microstructured coatings, powders, ﬁbers, as well as multiphase compounds from metals, metal oxides, as well as nonmetallic elementssuch as carbon and silicon. The advantage of CVD includes high throughput due to high deposition rate, as well as fabrication of single/multiphase nanomaterials. CVD process has been given enormous attention, owing to the possibility of mass production of nanomaterials; nevertheless, the mechanism of powder synthesis kinetics is still not completely known for this technique. 75,76Majumdar et al.51 reported the synthesis of Mo 2C nanoparticles anchored on carbon nanotubes grown over graphite felt (Mo 2C@CNT/GF) via magnetron sputtering and CVD for e ﬃcient HER. Graphite felt was sputter-coated with Al/Fe seed layers,treated in a CVD furnace at 800 °C under C 2H2/H2/Ar to grow dense CNTs, magnetron-sputtered with Mo, then calcined at 960 °C under Ar to carburize Mo into Mo 2C, yielding Mo 2C@CNT/GF. The composite presents a hierarchically porous 3-D sca ﬀold whose carbon ﬁbers are densely wrap- ped by two CNT populations-shorter sparse CNTs in the core and longer oriented CNTs along the periphery with 4.7 nm Mo 2C nanoparticles uniformly anchored on their surfaces, maximizing electrochemically active surface area,M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 9
