orted Co-NC/CF electrocatalyst via 60-s microwave pyrolysis of Co-MOF/CF inside a graphene “microreactor ”. Co-MOF nanosheets grown on carbon ﬁber paper were encased in graphene powder and treated with microwave irradiation at ≈1,000 °C for 60 s under Ar, yielding Co-NC/CF in which the ultrafast heating reduced Co2+to metallic nanoparticles while carbonizing the ligands into graphitic layers without collapse. The composite, with vertically aligned nanosheet morphology that retains the precursor ’s architecture, has its surface covered with numerous ultrashort, highly graphi- tized carbon nanotube protrusions. High-resolution TEM shows metallic Co nanoparticles (lattice fringe 0.20 nm, (111) plane) intimately embedded within 0.37 nm (002) graphiticcarbon layers, creating an open 3D network with abundant mesopores and continuous macrochannels that facilitate rapid electrolyte in ﬁltration and ion di ﬀusion. This hierar- chical morphology, coupled with a high carbon content (∼66 wt %) that prevents Co aggregation and the presence of N-doped graphitic layers that optimize electronic structure, yields an exceptionally large electrochemically active sur- face area and low charge-transfer resistance; these charac-teristics enhance the OER and HER catalytic activities Figure 6A. Microwave-assisted synthesis of Ni 3S2nano- structures ( ∼8 nm) anchored on electrospun N-doped carbon nanoﬁbers, optimized to produce amorphous, partially crystalline, and crystalline phases. Comprehensive charac- terization con ﬁrmed that the partially crystalline Ni 3S2 supported on carbon nano ﬁbers exhibited the best oxygen evolution reaction (OER) performance, attributed to its high electrochemically active surface area, N-doped conductive support, and mixed-phase structure.54Su et al.81reported the microwave-assisted synthesis of a three-dimensional hier-archical CoS 2/MoS 2nanocomposite on carbon- ﬁber cloth (CoS 2/MoS 2/CC) for HER application. CC was immersed in a Mo source solution and treated at 180 °C for 12 h under mi- crowave irradiation to grow MoS 2nanosheets; the resulting MoS 2/CC was then soaked with Co and S precursors and again treated at 180 °C for 9 h to deposit CoS 2nanoparticles, aﬀording binder-free CoS 2/MoS 2/CC after rinsing and vac- uum drying at 120 °C. The composite exhibits densely inter- woven ﬂower-like MoS 2nanosheets whose curled petals create abundant mesopores, while uniformly dispersed CoS 2 nanoparticles are anchored on each sheet and furtherwrapped by ultrathin MoS 2layers. TEM con ﬁrms the compact MoS 2petals with an interlayer spacing of 0.62 nm (002 planes), and HRTEM reveals intimate lattice matching between CoS 2(0.24 nm, 210 plane) and the surrounding graphitic MoS 2layers, establishing continuous electron pathways. This hierarchical morphology-characterized by high surface area, open 3D pores, and strong interfacial contact-enhances electrochemical accessibility, accelerates electron/proton transport, and minimizes aggregation,leading to superior HER activity Figure 6B. 3.5 Other methods Besides the methods of synthesis mentioned above, other methods for TMCs-CNFs and CNTs, such as valence engi- neering strategy, sol –gel synthesis, in-situ reductions, and template-assisted pyrolysis, have been reported; these methods lead to the formation of functionalized TMCs-CNFs and CNTs materials e ﬃcient for electrocatalytic water splitting, o ﬀering tunable electronic structures, abundantM. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 11
