Transition metals and compounds (TMCs)-based cata- lysts, such as those integrated with carbon nano ﬁbers and carbon nanotubes, present a signi ﬁcantly more cost- eﬀective alternative to traditional noble metal catalysts such as Ir, Ru, and Pt. The primary advantage lies in the abundance and low cost of transition metals such as nickel, cobalt, iron, and molybdenum, which are widely available and inexpensive compared to precious metals. Additionally,the synthesis methods for TMC-CNFs and TMC-CNTs, are scalable and do not require the high-purity precursors often necessary for noble metal catalysts, further reducing pro- duction costs. 61,91,92 Transition metals and their compounds such as sul ﬁdes, phosphides, oxides and carbides, possess tunable d-orbitals and multivalent redox states that confer moderate hydrogen and oxygen adsorption energies, endowing them with intrinsic electrocatalytic activity for both hydrogen and ox- ygen evolution reactions. Their variable oxidation states facilitate rapid electron transfer and intermediate stabili-zation, while nanostructuring or single-atom dispersion maximizes active-site density without compromising struc- tural integrity. 93–96Carbon nano ﬁbers and nanotubes, in turn, exhibit continuous graphitic networks that deliver exceptional electrical conductivity (>104Sc m−1), hierarchical meso- and macroporosity that accelerates electrolyte in ﬁl- tration and gas release, and outstanding mechanical ﬂexi- bility that withstands vigorous bubble evolution and long- term cycling.97,98Integrating these transition-metal chalco- genides, phosphides or oxides with carbon nano ﬁber or nanotube sca ﬀolds through electrospinning, chemical vapor deposition, hydrothermal, or microwave-assisted routes, creates synergistic electrodes in which the carbon frame- work acts as a conductive highway and corrosion barrier while the anchored TMC nanoparticles supply abundant, well-dispersed active sites.7,8,99This intimate heterointer- face reduces charge-transfer resistance, tunes adsorption energetics, suppresses particle aggregation, and stabilizes catalytic phases, yielding bifunctional electrodes that ach- ieve low HER and OER overpotentials and prolonged dura-bility for e ﬃcient overall water splitting. This review mainly aims to show a series of classi ﬁcations of the TMCs/CNFs and CNTs hybrid electrocatalysts for water splitting in detail in the following section. 4.1 Unique characteristics of transition metals and their compounds In the ﬁeld of electrocatalysis, transition metals and their compounds (TMCs) have attracted a lot of attention because of their roles in the OER and the HER. These reactions areessential to a number of energy conversion procedures, such as fuel cells, which transform chemical energy into electrical energy, and water splitting, a crucial technique for produc- ing hydrogen.100Because of their particular electronic con- ﬁgurations, oxidation states, and catalytic behaviors, TMCs have unique electrochemical features that make them appropriate candidates for these crucial reactions.101,102 As discussed above HER involves the reduction of pro- tons (H+) to produce H 2. This reaction can be performed in acidic or alkaline media, and the e ﬃciency of HER is largely dependent on the choice of catalyst. Noble metals like plat- inum are often regarded as the gold standard due to their high catalytic activity and stability. However, the scarcity and cost of Pt have driven researchers to explore alternative catalysts, particularly non-precious metal catalysts (NPMCs) derived from transition metals.103TMCs such as nickel (Ni), cobalt (Co), and molybdenum (Mo) have demonstrated promising HER activity.104,105Nickel-based catalysts, for example, particularly those modi ﬁed with phosphides or sulﬁdes, have shown signi ﬁcant promise due to their favorable electronic properties and ability to facilitate e ﬃ- cient proton reduction.106Cobalt phosphide (CoP) is one such compound that has emerged as an e ﬀective catalyst for HER, exhibiting performance levels comparable to those of noble metals. The intrinsic properties of TMCs, such as their ability to exist in multiple oxidation states, allow for versatile interaction with protons, enhancing their catalytic e ﬀec- tiveness. The electrochemical properties of TMCs for HERare in ﬂuenced by several factors, including their surface morphology, electronic structure, and the presence of active sites. The nanoscale structuring of catalysts can signi ﬁcantly enhance their performance by increasing the surface area available for proton adsorption. 107,108For instance, the use of nanostructured Ni and Co compounds has demonstrated improved HER activity due to the high density of active sites. Moreover, the electronic structure of TMCs can be tuned through alloying or doping, optimizing the binding energies of hydrogen and thereby enhancing reaction kinetics.105,109 Compared to HER, the OER involves the oxidation of water to produce oxygen gas (O 2). OER is often considered the more challenging of the two reactions due to its complex multielectron transfer process and the high overpotential required for e ﬃcient catalysis. The stability of the catalyst under oxidative conditions is crucial, as many materials can degrade or lose activity over time. Transition metal oxides, such as cobalt oxide (Co 3O4), manganese oxide (MnO 2), and iridium oxide (IrO 2), have been extensively studied for their OER performance. Co 3O4, in particular, is recognized for its high catalytic activity and stability in alkaline conditions, making it a favored choice for electrochemical water splitting applications.110IrO 2is14 M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon
============================================================
