another highly e ﬀective OER catalyst, especially in acidic environments, due to its robust performance and resistance to corrosion.111The electrochemical properties of TMCs for OER are primarily determined by their oxidation states and the ability to stabilize these states during reaction cycles. Transition metals can exist in various oxidation states, allowing them to participate e ﬀectively in the electron transfer processes necessary for OER. For example, cobaltcan transition between Co(II) and Co(III) during the OER, enhancing its activity. 112The ability to maintain structural integrity and catalytic activity under harsh oxidative con- ditions is critical for the long-term performance of OER catalysts. Moreover, the surface area and morphology of TMCs signi ﬁcantly in ﬂuence their catalytic e ﬃciency. The development of porous structures or nanoscale materials can provide a large number of active sites for the reaction, thereby improving overall performance. Recent studies have shown that modifying the surface characteristics of TMCs through techniques such as electrospinning or tem-plating can lead to enhanced OER activity. 113 4.2 Unique characteristics of carbon nanoﬁbers and nanotubes for HER and OER Carbon nanoparticles are excellent candidates to assist the electrocatalysts for HER and OER because they typically havehigh conductivity and exceptional structural stability. Heteroatom-doped carbons have also demonstrated inherent electrocatalytic activity for water splitting appli- cations, including phosphorus, boron, Sulphur, and nitro- gen. Many carbon-based materials with varied electronic structures and interfacial e ﬀects have been created so far for both HER and OER, including CNFs, CNTs, GE, GDY, CDs, and MOFs-derived C. Each form of carbon nanomaterial has its own bene ﬁts. 114,115 Carbon nano ﬁbers (CNFs) and carbon nanotubes (CNTs) are two distinct forms of carbon nanomaterials that play asigniﬁcant role in electrochemical reactions, particularly HER and OER. The primary structural di ﬀerence between them is that CNFs feature a ﬁbrous morphology with di- ameters in the range of tens of nanometers, providing a large surface area bene ﬁcial for reactant adsorption. In contrast, CNTs can be single-walled or multi-walled, characterized by a cylindrical shape and exceptional mechanical strength. This structural distinction in ﬂuences their electrochemical performance; CNTs exhibit superior electrical conductivitydue to their sp 2hybridized carbon bonding, which enhances charge transfer during reactions. While CNFs typicallypossess a higher density of surface functional groups that can promote catalytic activity, CNTs can achieve high cata- lytic performance through functionalization, where various groups are introduced to create active sites for HER and OER. Stability is another crucial factor, with CNTs generally demonstrating higher thermal and chemical stability, mak- ing them more durable in harsh electrochemical environ- ments compared to CNFs. However, CNFs can be engineeredfor improved stability through composite materials. Both nanomaterials have been employed as supports for various catalysts in HER and OER, with recent advancements focusing on hybrid nanocomposites. This synergy allows for the development of catalysts that leverage the high surface area of CNFs and the robust electrical properties of CNTs, making them highly e ﬀective in applications aimed at sus- tainable energy conversion. 116,117 4.3 The integration of TMCs with carbon nanoﬁbers and nanotubes materials for water splitting TMCs exhibit exceptional electrocatalytic e ﬃciency in water splitting, while carbon nanomaterials have exceptional conductivity and structural stability. As a result, combining TMCs with CNFs and CNTs o ﬀers HER and OER a lot of possible uses.118,119By hybridizing TMCs with CNFs and CNTs, a distinctive interface can be created, leading toincreased exposed surface area and active sites. In HER and OER processes, the enhanced water splitting performance is facilitated by the increased active sites at the interface, which encourage the adsorption of reactants and in- termediates. Additionally, the special physicochemical characteristics of carbon nanomaterials (CNFs and CNTs) and their powerful interactions with TMCs will result in an eﬀective electron transfer process that optimizes the ΔG H* andΔGO*–ΔGHO*for HER and OER, respectively, which is advantageous for enhancing the electrocatalytic activity.13 On the other hand, the incorporation of TMCs with carbon nanomaterials can also improve the long-term stability by reducing the oxidation and aggregation of the active in- gredients by coating or supporting the carbon material shell. For instance, eco-friendly electrospun Co 6W6C, N-doped carbon nano ﬁbers (Co 6W6C–N@CNFs) with PAN as the pre- cursor of N and C and no additional excrescent carbon ad- ditives have been created for HER applications. The special carbon nano ﬁber structure facilitates electron transport and prevents metal catalysts from clumping together andcorroding during HER. With low overpotentials of 86 and 116 mV at η 10and small Tafel slopes of 85 and 101 mV dec−1inM. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 15
