acidic and alkali, respectively, and exceptional long-term stability at least 20 h and 12 h at η10in acidic and base, respectively, the electrocatalysts thus exhibit superior cat- alytic properties.120Similarly, a large number of other ref- erences have been reported for e ﬃcient HER and OER catalysis in recent years. The synthetic method, structure and electrochemical performance of the TMCs/CNFs and CNTs hybrid electrocatalysts are compared in Tables 2 and 3. 4.4 TMCs/CNFs heterostructures for water splitting Among the most common types of carbon nanomaterials, CNFs are characterized by their huge surface area and thin shape, which often results in good electrical conductivity. Furthermore, heteroatom-doped CNFs, which o ﬀer addi- tional active sites for electrolytic water splitting, are readilysynthesized during the CNF synthesis process. 145,146More- over, there is a strong interaction between the two compo- nents and a synergistically increased electrocatalytic performance when TMCs and CNFs are integrated, either as an encapsulation state or as a core –shell structure. As eﬀective electrocatalysts for HER and OER, the creation of hybrid materials comprising inexpensive and highly active TMC electrocatalysts and CNFs has drawn a lot of inter- est.64,147,148Herein, this review summarizes the preparation methods, as well as HER, OER and overall water splitting properties of diverse types of TMCs integrated with CNFs. Inaddition, the in ﬂuence of the composition, architecture, and interface synergistic e ﬀect on their electrochemical perfor- mance have been discussed. 4.4.1 Hybridizing transition metal with CNFs for HER and OER A signi ﬁcant number of metal/CNF hybrids have been created in recent years by carbonizing polymeric nano ﬁbers with metal precursors. For instance, the bacterial cellulose (BC)@Co(OH) 2aerogel was pyrolysed in an Ar environment to create CNFs@Co. With tiny mesopores that range in size from 4 to 10 nm and macropores that range in size from 50 to 80 nm, the as-prepared CNFs@Co still has a porous structure that can o ﬀer eﬀective channels for exposing a wider surface area and the interchange of reactant ions and gas products. Additionally, because of the inherent ﬂexibility of CNFs and the 3D network that CNF aerogels create, CNF@Co shows an unexpectedly high compression tolerance. Moreover, with an onset potential of roughly 0.445 V versus Ag/AgCl, CNF@Co exhibits outstanding electrocatalytic activity to- wards the oxygen evolution process. It can reach a currentdensity of 10 mA cm−2in 1.0 M KOH with just a low over- potential of 314 mV. The CNF@Co catalyst also shows outstanding stability against water oxidation, even sur- passing previously documented cobalt-based electro- catalysts and commercial IrO 2and RuO 2catalysts.149Because of the synergistic impact that results from the hybridization of many metals, multimetal alloys have been regarded as a class of superior electrocatalysts when compared to singlemetal catalysts. 150,151 To further enhance the electrocatalytic performance of the metal/CNTs, the e ﬀective strategy is the fabrication of bimetallic materials composed of one noble metal and a transition metal to achieve a coupling e ﬀect. For example, Li et al.64synthesized RuNi/N-doped CNFs (NCNFs) via elec- trospinning for applications of overall water splitting. Compared to samples of Ru-NCNFs and Ni-CNFs, the opti- mised RuNi-NCNFs with a molar ratio of Ru: Ni = 1: 1 and a carbonization temperature of 800 °C show a rough surface with more exposed active sites. In both alkaline and acidicenvironments, the as-synthesized RuNi-NCNFs exhibit su- perior Pt-like electrocatalytic activity for the hydrogen evo- lution reaction. Additionally, the RuNi-NCNFs demonstrate exceptional oxygen evolution reaction activity, achieving a current density of 10 mA cm −2in an alkaline electrolyte with an overpotential of 290 mV. Remarkably, an electrolyser with RuNi-NCNFs as the anode and cathode catalysts only needs a cell voltage of 1.564 V to drive a current density of 10 mA cm−2in an alkaline medium, which is lower than the benchmark of Pt/C ‖RuO 2electrodes because of both the HER and OER performance. The Ru1Ni1-NCNFs catalyst exhibited outstanding HER and OER stability, showing no signi ﬁcant performance degradation after 6,000 and 4,500 CV cycles in LSV measurements and maintaining a steady current during a chronoamperometric test at a 40 mv and 290 mV over- potential respectively, outperforming most recent HER cat- alysts. When compared to the Pt/C –RuO 2electrodes, the overall water splitting device based on the RuNi-NCNFs ex- hibits higher durability. In both alkaline and acidic envi- ronments, the as-formed RuNi-NCNFs exhibit superior Pt-like electrocatalytic activity for the hydrogen evolution re- action. Additionally, the RuNi-NCNFs demonstrate excep- tional oxygen evolution reaction activity, achieving a current density of 10 mA cm −2in an alkaline electrolyte with an overpotential of 290 mV. Remarkably, an electrolyzer with RuNi-NCNFs as the anode and cathode catalysts only needs a cell voltage of 1.564 V to drive a current density of 10 mA cm−2in an alkaline medium, which is lower than the benchmark of Pt/C ‖RuO 2electrodes because of both the HER and OER performance. When compared to the Pt/C –RuO 2 electrodes, the overall water splitting device based on the RuNi-NCNFs exhibits higher durability Figure 7A. E ﬃcient16 M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon
