activity. As a result, NiCo –NCNF exhibited low over- potentials of 65 mV for HER and 124 mV for OER at 10 mA cm−2, delivering a remarkable combination of high catalytic activity, stability, and favorable reaction kinetics. These ﬁndings highlight NiCo –NCNF as a promising bifunc- tional electrocatalyst for e ﬃcient water-splitting and sus- tainable energy technologies. 4.4.2 Hybridizing transition metal oxide with CNFs for HER and OER Transition metal oxides are a popular area of research due to their low cost, excellent stability, customized design, andchangeable redox characteristics. This can greatly enhance the metal oxides ’electrochemical performance by control- ling their shape, composition, electronic structure, and contact e ﬀect.152For example, Co 3O4nanoparticles sup- ported on acidi ﬁed N-doped CNFs (Co 3O4/N-ACCNF) have been created as an e ﬀective OER electrocatalyst using a hy- drothermal process and electrospinning.153The results show that the homogeneous distribution of crystalline Co 3O4 nanoparticles 20 –30 nm in size on the surface of N-ACCNFs is facilitated by the acidic N-CNFs. The high OER performance of the as-prepared Co 3O4/N-ACCNF is demonstrated by its overpotential of 310 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm−2, which is signi ﬁcantly lower than that of individual Co 3O4 Figure 7: Schematic illustration of the synthesis processes and characterization of Ru 1Ni1-NCNFs and h-Co 3O4/CeO 2@N-CNFs. A (a) Schematic illustration for the fabrication process of RuNi-NCNFs electrocatalysts (b) SEM image of Ru 1Ni1-NCNFs (c) TEM image of Ru 1Ni1-NCNFs (d) SEM image of Ru-NCNFs (e) TEM image of RuNi-NCNFs (f) Ru 3p XPS spectra of Ru 1Ni1-NCNFs and Ru-NCNFs (g) Ni 2p XPS spectra of Ru 1Ni1-NCNFs and Ni-NCNFs (h) the HER polarization curves for as-prepared catalysts at a scan rate of 1 mV s−1(i) the OER polarization curves with a mass loading of 0.612 mg cm−2loaded on an RDE electrode (j) LSV plots of overall water splitting in a two-electrode system64