nanoparticles, N-ACCNFs, Co 3O4/CNFs, and even commercial IrO 2catalyst. This is due to the strong interface interaction between the Co 3O4nanoparticles and N-ACCNFs. It is eﬀective to fabricate a hybrid to modify the inter- face and electrical structure of the Co 3O4materials in order to increase their OER e ﬃciency. For example, using a two- step technique that included electrospinning and carbon- ization, Li et al.14combined Co 3O4and CeO 2to create a hol- low Co 3O4/CeO 2heterostructure embedded in N-CNFs (h-Co 3O4/CeO 2@N-CNFs). The SEM and TEM images show the hollow porous structure of the Co 3O4/CeO 2hetero- structure in the NCNFs conductive network, which is created by the Co 3O4and CeO 2materials di ﬀusing at di ﬀerent rates. Because of its porous nature, high redox capacity, and abundance of oxygen vacancy defects, the new hollow Co 3O4/ CeO 2heterostructure can optimize the adsorption binding energy of the intermediate and customize the conversion of Co3O4to hydroxide/oxyhydroxide, improving OER perfor- mance. In addition to increasing electrical conductivity andlowering charge transfer resistance, conductive CNFs can also stop Co 3O4/CeO 2nanoparticles from clumping together and act as a bridge for mass and electron transit. Addition- ally, by controlling the electron con ﬁguration of the nearby carbon, the N dopant added to CNFs can increase the number of catalytic active sites. With a low overpotential of 310 mV at 10 mA cm−2and a current density of 50.5 mA cm−2at 1.7 V in 0.1 M KOH solution, the resulting h-Co 3O4/CeO 2@N-CNFs demonstrated excellent OER performance, outperformingCo 3O4@N-CNFs, CeO 2@N-CNFs, and commercial RuO 2cata- lysts by a signi ﬁcant margin. Furthermore, compared to the RuO 2catalyst (57.7 % loss), the produced h-Co 3O4/ CeO 2@N-CNFs exhibit greater long-term stability with just 1.2 % loss in the current retention after 11.1 h Figure 7B. Enhancing catalytic e ﬃciency for overall water splitting requires electrocatalysts with optimized structural and morphological characteristics. One-dimensional (1D) nano- structures are particularly promising due to their high surface area and interconnected architecture, which outperform zero-dimensional (0D) and two-dimensional(2D) counterparts depending on material type, synthesis technique, and structural con ﬁguration. cobalt ferrite (CoFe 2O4, CFO) nanoparticles were incorporated into carbon nanoﬁbers (CNFs) through a combination of hydrothermal synthesis and electrospinning. The hydrothermally pre- pared CoFe-MOF precursor was embedded within a poly- mer matrix, electrospun, and subsequently annealed at 600 ° C, 700 °C, and 800 °C to obtain CFO@CNFs-600, CFO@CNFs- 700, and CFO@CNFs-800, respectively. Among these,CFO@CNFs-800 demonstrated superior oxygen evolution reaction (OER) activity, achieving a low overpotential of 300 mV at 10 mA cm −2, a charge transfer resistance (Rct) of1.5Ωcm2, and a Tafel slope of 42 mV dec−1. Furthermore, CFO@CNFs-800 exhibited excellent durability, maintaining stable performance over 48 h of continuous testing, high- lighting its potential as an e ﬃcient OER electrocatalyst.154 Designing heterointerfaces between metals and metal compounds is an e ﬀective approach to enhance electro- catalyst performance. However, achieving a high degree of integration between dissimilar metal components remainschallenging, as they often tend to segregate during synthesis. Carboxyl-functionalized carbon nanotubes were employed to stabilize metal ions, enabling the controlled construction of abundant Ni |MnO heterointerfaces within porous carbon nanoﬁbers (Ni |MnO/CNF) through a combined electro- spinning –calcination process. The resulting Ni |MnO/CNF catalyst exhibits exceptional bifunctional electrocatalytic activity for both the oxygen reduction reaction and oxygen evolution reaction (OER). The outstanding electrochemical performance is attributed to the synergistic e ﬀects of oxygen-de ﬁcient Ni |MnO heterointerfaces, strong Ni –Mn alloying interactions, and the conductive, porous one- dimensional CNF framework. 58Integrating NiFe 2O4nano- particles (NPs) into a carbon nano ﬁber (CNF) framework oﬀers a highly promising strategy for developing advanced materials for electrochemical energy applications. Embed- ding NiFe 2O4within CNFs enhances catalytic activity by increasing the density of active sites, improving charge transfer, and enhancing structural stability. The high sur- face area of CNFs enables uniform nanoparticle dispersion,thereby maximizing exposure of catalytically active sites, while the conductive, interconnected CNF network facili- tates e ﬃcient electron transport between the NiFe 2O4NPs and the electrode interface. Additionally, encapsulation within CNFs mitigates particle agglomeration and detach- ment during operation, improving long-term durability. NiFe 2O4CNF composites were synthesized and systemati- cally evaluated for their bifunctional electrocatalytic activity toward the HER and OER. SEM analysis con ﬁrmed the uni- form distribution of NiFe 2O4nanoparticles across the CNF matrix, with no evident agglomeration. High-magni ﬁcation imaging further veri ﬁes the successful integration of NiFe 2O4into the CNFs, a feature expected to enhance elec- tron transport pathways and increase the accessible surface area, thereby improving catalytic performance. Compara- tive electrochemical testing against pristine CNFs and pure NiFe 2O4nanoparticles revealed that the composite exhibited superior catalytic performance, demonstrating enhanced activity, stability, and durability under electrochemical conditions. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) measurements,conducted at a scan rate of 10 mV s −1, reveal that the NFOCN11 nanocomposite exhibits the lowest onset potential (1.38 V vs RHE) among all tested samples, highlighting its20 M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon
