superior electrocatalytic activity due to the greater exposure of active sites.123 4.4.3 Hybridizing transition metal sul ﬁde with CNFs for HER and OER Transition metal cations and strongly bonded negative S anions make up transition metal sul ﬁdes. There are two primary categories of metal sul ﬁdes in general. The mate- rials of the 2D layered structure are one. MoS 2is the most common example of them, having a large number ofexposed S edge sites that enhance electrocatalytic activ- ity. 155,156Metal sulphides such as NiS 2and CoS 2that include orthorhombic marcasite-type and cubic pyrite-type struc- tures are another example.157Most metal sulphides, how- ever, are semiconductors with limited electrical conductivity, which must be combined with a highly conductive substrate to improve the ability to transport charges.158Thus, CNFs are a promising candidate to address this issue. The bio-inspired (BC) aerogel is typically utilized to create CNFs, which can serve as an e ﬃcient electrocatalyst support. Guo and colleagues used a hydrothermal process to create MoS 2/CNFs by combining the BC-derived CNFs with (NH 4)2MoS 4.159When compared to single MoS 2nano- particles, the as-prepared MoS 2/CNFs exhibit greater HER activity, indicating the synergistic action of MoS 2and CNFs. To further improve the HER performance of MoS 2/CNF, Wen et al.3have devised a two-step method for the preparation of Pd16S7/MoS 2/CNFs using Sulphur vapor-assisted chemical vapor deposition and electrospinning. In the CNFs, thePd 16S7/MoS 2nanocrystals are embedded and widely distributed. The formation of Pd –S–Mo nanointerface in Pd16S7/MoS 2core/shell heterostructures e ﬃciently controls MoS 2’s electron orbital and reveals additional Sulphur va- cancies, which are hydrogen evolution reaction active sites. Beyond colloidal fabrication, the self-supported Pd 16S7/MoS 2/ CNFs may be utilized directly as electrode materials. An electrode with superior hydrophobic qualities can enhance the stability of hydrogen evolution and speed up bubble desorption during the reaction. A Tafel slope of 113 mV dec−1 and a modest overpotential of 83 mV at a geometric current density of 10 mA cm−2is provided by the Pd 16S7/MoS 2/CNFs electrode, indicating a greater intrinsic activity (88 mV at 1m Ac m−2ECSA) and exceptional endurance. Fe-doped NiS 2 crystals anchored on CNFs and encapsulating NiFe alloy nanoparticles ((Ni,Fe)S 2/NiFe-CNFs) were synthesized via an electrospinning –calcination –vulcanization process. The incorporation of Fe into NiS 2and the strong synergistic interaction between NiFe-CNFs and surface sul ﬁdes provide abundant active sites, SEM analysis and TEM investigationsrevealed that numerous nanoparticles are uniformly dispersed both inside and on the surface of CNFs in all NiFe- CNFs, Ni-CNFs, and Fe-CNFs samples, con ﬁrming the method ’seﬀectiveness in preventing nanoparticle aggrega- tion. After vulcanization, some nanoparticles remain embedded within the ﬁbers, while larger polyhedral parti- cles form on their surface, indicating the successful forma- tion of (Ni, Fe)S 2/NiFe-CNFs. The catalyst achieves a low overpotential of 287 mV at 30 mA cm−2and maintains high stability with minimal current loss after 48 h, outperforming several noble-metal-based catalysts. Furthermore, a two- electrode system using (Ni,Fe)S 2/NiFe-CNFs as the anode and commercial Pt/C as the cathode delivers 10 mA cm−2at a low cell voltage of 1.54 V, surpassing the performance of con- ventional RuO 2‖Pt/C electrolyzers Figure 8A.60A simple approach was developed to synthesize high-index Cu 3Pd13S7 nanoparticles directly on three 3D CNFs via electrospinningcombined with CVD. The high-index facets with abundant active sites, the large surface area of the 3D CNFs, and thestrong electronic interactions among Cu –Pd–S bonds collectively enhance electrocatalytic activity. The resulting Cu 3Pd13S7/CNFs exhibit outstanding performance, achieving low overpotentials of 52 mV for the hydrogen evolution reaction and 240 mV for the OER at 10 mA cm−2. Encapsula- tion within CNFs prevents nanoparticle aggregation and corrosion, ensuring excellent stability in both acidic and alkaline environments.73Heteroatom-doped carbon mate- rials show promise for HER and OER catalysis but often facechallenges in synthesis complexity and durability. To address this, a ZIF-67/PDA/BC precursor was prepared using polydopamine-modi ﬁed bacterial cellulose as a template, enabling the synthesis of NiCo-MOF/PDA/BC with tunable Ni/ Co ratios and subsequent conversion into a porous NiCo- NSC@CBC composite. The optimized NiCo-NSC@CBC-1:4 re- tains a nanotube structure and Co 9S8active phase while beneﬁting from the synergistic e ﬀects of its bimetallic sul- ﬁde heterostructure and self-supporting architecture. As a bifunctional HER/OER electrode, it delivers low over- potentials of 119 mV for HER and 370 mV for OER at10 mA cm −2, demonstrating excellent activity and long-term stability.127 Nickel-based chalcogenides have emerged as promising electrocatalysts for overall water splitting due to their favorable chemisorption energies for oxygen- and hydrogen-containing intermediates. About ∼8n m N i 3S2 nanostructures were synthesized on electrospun N-doped carbon nano ﬁbers using microwave-assisted synthesis, yielding amorphous, partially crystalline, and crystallinephases. Comprehensive characterization revealed that the partially crystalline Ni 3S2supported on carbon nano ﬁbers exhibited superior OER performance attributed to its mixed-M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 21
