phase structure, high conductivity, N-doped support, and large electrochemically active surface area. This catalyst achieved a low overpotential of 270 mV, a Tafel slope of 51 mV dec−1, and low charge transfer resistance (1.08 Ω), while maintaining over 95 % Faradaic e ﬃciency and excel- lent stability for both OER and HER. In alkaline media, the hydrogen evolution reaction is hindered by the need for simultaneous water dissociation and hydrogen desorption.However, factors such as nanostructuring, optimized metal loading, and heteroatom doping signi ﬁcantly modify surface properties and electronic structure, enhancing catalytic ac- tivity. The observed increase in overpotential and Tafel slope highlights the strong dependence of HER kinetics on metal content, with an 8 % metal loading o ﬀering the most favor- able pathway for hydrogen reduction, likely due to optimal spacing between adsorbed hydrogen atoms. This composi- tion, identi ﬁed as a-Ni 3S2@CNF, was con ﬁrmed as one of the top-performing catalysts Figure 8B.544.4.4 Hybridizing transition metal phosphide with CNFs for HER and OER In addition to transition metal sul ﬁdes, metal phosphides have been determined to be the preferred electrocatalyst for water splitting. Metal phosphides can be shown to be more eﬀective HER or OER catalysts than metal oxide and sul ﬁdes because both P and transition metal can be utilized as activesites. 160,161Additionally, compared to their oxides, metal phosphides exhibit better conductivity and faster electron transfer ability. These factors have led to the increased in- terest in metal phosphide/CNF hybrids and their potential uses in water splitting. Mo et al.162used direct pyrolysis reduction of electrospun Fe3+/Ni2+/polyacrylonitrile precur- sorﬁbres, followed by in situ phosphorization, to create a nitrogen-doped carbon nano ﬁber embedded with ultra ﬁne graphene layers-wrapped FeNiP nanoparticles (FeNiP@N- CFs). The as-obtained FeNiP@N-CFs exhibit exceptional Figure 8: Elec