electrocatalytic activity, as evidenced by a low overpotential (300 mV at 10 mA cm−2), a small Tafel slope of 47 mV dec−1, and remarkable long-term stability in 1.0 m KOH for the oxygen evolution reaction. These features are made possible by the high electrochemical activity of in situ formed FeNiP/ FeNiOOH core –shell nanoparticles during the test, a strong coupling with a few layers of graphene shell, and synergis- tically enhanced electronic conductivity of the N-dopednanoﬁber matrix. This work o ﬀers a general method for creating hybrid electrodes for electrochemical water oxidation using double-metal phosphide and graphitised carbon nano ﬁbers that have improved conductivity and catalytic activity. Bimetallic phosphides, which integrate the metalloid properties of monometallic phosphides with tailored elec- tronic and geometric structures at active sites, have gained signiﬁcant attention as e ﬃcient electrocatalysts for water splitting. However, conventional synthesis methods often involve toxic reagents, corrosive conditions, and high tem-peratures, leading to increased particle size and reduced catalytic surface area. To address these limitations, ultra- ﬁne NiCoP nanoparticles were in-situ embedded within P, N-codoped carbon nano ﬁbers (NiCoP@PNCNF) through a facile electrospinning approach utilizing phytic acid as a phosphorus source. The resulting hierarchical structure eﬀectively con ﬁned the bimetallic NiCoP nanoparticles, yielding catalysts with outstanding activity toward both the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER, η 10= 98 mV) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER, η10= 260 mV) in alkaline media, demonstrating low overpotentials and exceptional dura- bility. X-ray absorption ﬁne structure (XAFS) analysis revealed that this enhanced performance originated from the heteroatom-doped carbon nano ﬁbers, which improved corrosion resistance, electrical conductivity, and prevented nanoparticle agglomeration, and strong synergistic elec- tronic interactions between Ni, Co, and P atoms that modi- ﬁed the local coordination environment. Binding energy analysis showed a positive shift in Co peaks, indicating electron transfer from the bimetallic species to neighboringatoms, while P 2p 3/2and 2p 1/2peaks were negatively shifted, conﬁrming electron acceptance by P atoms. This electron redistribution enhanced proton adsorption on negatively charged P sites, thereby accelerating HER kinetics and contributing to the superior bifunctional catalytic perfor- mance of NiCoP@PNCNF.163Developing cost-e ﬀective, sta- ble, and e ﬃcient electrocatalysts for the HER and OER remains a key challenge for overall water splitting. A hier- archical CoP/carbon nano ﬁber (CoP/CNF) composite was synthesized and evaluated as a bifunctional catalyst. The integration of two-dimensional CoP nanosheets with one- dimensional CNFs created a conductive network withabundant active sites, enhancing charge transfer and mass transport. As a result, the optimized CoP/CNF catalyst ach- ieved low overpotentials of 225 mV for HER and 325 mV for OER at 10 mA cm−2, and enabled overall water splitting at 1.65 V to deliver the same current density. This facile syn- thesis strategy demonstrates signi ﬁcant potential for designing high-performance, non-noble metal electro- catalysts for sustainable energy applications.164In another study, A hierarchical CoP-on-Ni-foam (CoP/NF) composite was synthesized via a three-step MOF-derived route room- temperature precipitation of Co-MOF nanorods, alkaline etching to ultrathin Co(OH) 2nanosheets, and low- temperature phosphidation that preserves the porous nanosheet architecture. This creates abundant edge defects and continuous electron pathways. The optimized CoP/NF electrode delivers outstanding HER performance with overpotentials of only 41, 55, and 84 mV at 10 mA cm−2in 1 M KOH, 1 M PBS, and 0.5 M H 2SO4, respectively and an OER overpotential of 317 mV at 50 mA cm−2in 1 M KOH. When used as a bifunctional, pair, overall water splitting is ach- ieved at a record-low cell voltage of 1.54 V 10 mA cm−2, sur- passing the Pt/C ‖IrO 2benchmark and showing negligible decay after 3, 000 CV cycles or 28 h continuous operation. Post-electrolysis XPS con ﬁrms robust CoP stability for HER and in-situ formation of CoOOH active species for OER, Figure 9A.165 Designing e ﬃcient and durable non-precious metal catalysts for the hydrogen evolution reaction remains a keychallenge in electrocatalysis. In this work, MoP nano- particles uniformly anchored on N, P-doped porous carbon nanoﬁbers (MoP/NPCNF) were synthesized as a binder-free, self-supported HER catalyst. The electrospun, sponge-like carbon nano ﬁber framework o ﬀers aﬂexible, porous, and conductive network that enhances gas di ﬀusion and elec- tron transport. The optimized MoP/NPCNF catalyst demon- strates excellent HER activity across a wide pH range, requiring overpotentials of 192 mV (0.5 M H 2SO4), 139 mV (1.0 M KOH), and 227 mV (1.0 M PBS) at 10 mA cm−2, while maintaining exceptional stability for over 200 h. Densityfunctional theory (DFT) calculations con ﬁrm that Mo served as the primary active site, with a hydrogen adsorption free energy ( ΔG*H) of 0.127 eV, approaching thermos neutrality and surpassing that of N-graphite, NP-graphite, and pristine MoP. The synergy between Mo 3d orbitals and the 2p orbitals of C, N, and P atoms contributes to the material ’s metallic conductivity and enhanced charge transfer. Di ﬀerential charge density analysis further reveals stronger electron redistribution at Mo sites compared to P sites, explaining thesuperior catalytic activity. Additionally, heteroatom doping signiﬁcantly modi ﬁes the electronic structure of the carbon framework, further optimizing HER kinetics. These ﬁndingsM. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 23
