and hierarchical structure modulation.61 4.4.5 Hybridizing transition metal carbide with CNFs for HER and OER Transition metal carbides, with their distinct electrical structure and earth abundance, have been used as a novelclass of promising electrocatalysts for both HER and OER in alkaline media. For HER or OER applications, several common transition metal carbides, including Mo xCy,W xCy, FexCy,N i xCy, and V xCy, have been produced in recent years.92,166,167With a d-band electronic structure that is comparable to that of the Pt-group metal, Mo 2C is one of the most typical carbides among them. To improve their electrochemical performance, Mo 2C materials are frequently hybridized with other conductive nanomaterials,such as CNFs, due to their structural ﬂexibility. For example, Mo 2C-encapsulated in multi-channel hollow CNFs (Mo 2C-MCNFs) are a bifunctional electrocatalyst for both HER and OER, generating overpotentials of 114 mV and 320 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm−2by an electro- spinning and pyrolysis technique. The electrolyzer that uses Mo 2C-MCNFs as both the anode and the cathode has a good overall water splitting performance with a cell voltage of1.68 V. 4 Making a heterostructure of transition metal/metal carbides embedded in CNFs is a promising way to further improve the HER and OER activities of the metal carbide materials. Through electrospinning and subsequent carbonization procedures, Li et al.130created Ni –Mo 2C nanoparticles embedded in N-doped carbon nano ﬁbers (Ni/ Mo 2C-NCNFs). For water splitting, the as-prepared Ni/ Mo 2C-NCNFs exhibit exceptional bifunctional electro- catalytic performance, exhibiting overpotentials of 143 mV and 288 mV to reach the current density of 10 mA cm−2for OER and HER in 1 M KOH solution, respectively. In addition, the entire water splitting system made of Ni/Mo 2C-NCNFs acting as the anode and cathode produces a low voltage of 1.64 V at a current density of 10 mA cm−2, which is on par with the Pt/C ‖RuO 2electrode standard. The excellent elec- trocatalytic activity of the Ni/Mo 2C-NCNFs electrocatalyst can be attributed to the following reasons: (1) the intrinsic activity of Mo 2C and the synergistic e ﬀect between Mo 2C and Ni contribute to the high HER and OER e ﬃciency; (2) CNFs can provide the fast charge transfer pathway and protect Ni – Mo 2C nanoparticles from agglomeration and oxidation; the N doping can modulate the electronic structure of the adja- cent carbon, which can improve the conductivity of the electrocatalyst. In conclusion, the combination of CNFs and TMCs exhibits outstanding electrocatalytic e ﬃciency for HER, OER, and overall water splitting due to its structural stabil- ity, large number of exposed active sites, and improved charge transfer capabilities. Further control of their composition, architectural, electrical, and interface struc-tures is expected to result in a markedly enhanced electro- chemical performance. Carbon nano ﬁbers (CNFs) embedded with a Co 6Mo 6C2– Mo 2C bimetallic carbide heterostructure (Co 6Mo 6C2–Mo 2C/ CNFs) were synthesized via electrospinning followed by high-temperature carbonization. The strong synergistic coupling between Co 6Mo 6C2and Mo 2C phases, combined with the mesoporous structure and excellent conductivity of CNFs, signi ﬁcantly enhances charge transfer and reaction kinetics. As a result, the optimized catalyst demonstrates outstanding bifunctional activity, achieving overpotentials of 94 mV for HER and 295 mV for OER at 10 mA cm−2inM. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 25
