alkaline electrolyte. An alkaline electrolyzer assembled with this catalyst as both electrodes delivers 1.66 V at 10 mA cm−2, highlighting its potential for e ﬃcient water electrolysis.168 The development of e ﬃcient and low-cost electrocatalysts for the HER, OER, and oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) is vital for advancing clean energy storage and conversion systems. A Co –Co6Mo 6C2heterostructure supported on car- bon nano ﬁbers (Co –Co6Mo 6C2/CNFs) was synthesized via a four-step method and evaluated as a tri-functional electro- catalyst. The strong interfacial electronic coupling between Co and Co 6Mo 6C2eﬀectively modulates the electronic structure, resulting in remarkable activity with over- potentials of 91 mV for HER and 293 mV for OER at 10 mA cm−2. An electrolyzer assembled with Co –Co6Mo 6C2/ CNFs achieved overall water splitting at 1.70 V (10 mA cm−2) with excellent long-term stability. XPS analysis revealed a ∼0.3 eV negative shift in Mo binding energy compared to MoC/CNFs, indicating a lower Mo valence state, while Co 2p peaks exhibited a positive shift. These results con ﬁrm strong charge redistribution at the Co/Co 6Mo 6C2interfaces, where electron transfer from Co to Mo optimized the adsorption – desorption behavior of reaction intermediates, thereby enhancing intrinsic catalytic activity.91A bamboo-like Co – MoC/NCNF composite was fabricated by electrospinning ZIF-67 and MoO 2(acac) 2into PAN nano ﬁbers, followed by pre-oxidation and carbonization. The ultra ﬁne≈5n mM °C and metallic Co nanoparticles are simultaneously embedded in nitrogen-doped carbon nano ﬁbers, creating abundant Co→MoC electron transfer pathways that down-shift the d-band center from −0.76 eV (MoC/NCNF) to −1.42 eV and reduce the water-dissociation barrier to 0.40 eV. These electronic and structural advantages deliver benchmark HER activities of 86, 116 and 145 mV at 10 mA cm−2in 1 M KOH, 0.5 M H 2SO4and 1 M PBS, respectively, with negligible loss after 5,000 cycles or 100 h chronopotentiometry. For OER in 1 M KOH the same electrode needs only 254 mV at 10 mA cm−2(77 mV dec−1Tafel), outperforming RuO 2. When assembled as both anode and cathode, the Co –MoC/NCNF electrolyzer drives overall water splitting at 1.59 V(10 mA cm −2) and maintains stable operation for >100 h, conﬁrming that the in-situ-generated CoOOH and MoC syn- ergistically optimize *H and *OH adsorption –desorption and thus a ﬀord superior bifunctional performance Figure 10A.92 A heterostructured Mo 2C–MoC catalyst embedded in nitrogen-doped carbon nano ﬁbers (Mo 2C–MoC/NCNF) was synthesized via electrospinning followed by annealing. The precursor solution, prepared using ammonium heptamo- lybdate as a Mo source and polyacrylonitrile as C and Nsources in DMF, was electrospun into ﬁbers and annealed under N 2at 700, 800, and 900 °C to achieve controlled phase formation, yielding Mo 2C–MoC/NCNF (700 °C), Mo 2C/NCNF(800 °C), and Mo –Mo 2C/NCNF (900 °C). This straightforward synthesis approach enables phase engineering of Mo-based carbide electrocatalysts through temperature adjustment while maintaining a robust one-dimensional architecture. Among these, Mo 2C–MoC/NCNF exhibited superior HER performance, achieving overpotentials of 106.8 mV and 169.1 mV at current densities of 10 and 50 mA cm−2, respec- tively, with stable operation for 24 h in alkaline electrolyte.The enhanced activity is attributed to the synergistic e ﬀect of the Mo 2C–MoC heterostructure, which optimizes hydrogen binding energy, and the nitrogen-doped carbon nano ﬁbers, which provide high conductivity and a large surface area. This work demonstrated a cost-e ﬀective, scalable strategy for designing high-performance HER catalysts for sustain- able hydrogen production Figure 10B.8Molybdenum carbide (Mo 2C), known for its platinum-like electronic properties, is a promising substitute for precious metal catalysts but faces challenges due to ine ﬃcient synthesis methods and limited catalytic activity. a Mo 2C–CoNi heterostructure embedded in carbon nano ﬁbers (Mo 2C–CoNi@CNFs) was fabricated through a simple electrospinning and pyrolysis process, serving as a high-performance bifunctional electrocatalyst for the hydrogen evolution reaction and oxygen evolution reaction (OER). The Mo 2C@CNFs exhibited a relatively smooth surface, whereas the Mo 2C–CoNi@CNFs displayed numerous nanoparticles distributed across the nano ﬁber surfaces. This di ﬀerence is attributed to the higher metal precursor concentration used for Mo 2C–CoNi@CNFs, resulting in greater nanoparticle loading and surface expo- sure following carbonization. In contrast, the lower metal content in Mo 2C@CNFs led to minimal particle formation, producing a smoother ﬁber surface. The optimized catalyst achieved low overpotentials of 128 mV. and 306 mV (OER) at 10 mA cm−2in 1 M KOH, with Tafel slopes of 74.2 mV dec−1 and 72.4 mV dec−1, respectively, and demonstrated excellent long-term stability exceeding 100 h. The superior perfor- mance arises from the synergistic interactions between Mo 2C and CoNi nanoparticles and the high conductivity of carbon nano ﬁbers, which enhance charge transfer and active site utilization.169 4.5 TMCs/CNTs heterostructures for water splitting CNTs, another signi ﬁcant class of carbon materials, have a unique hollow structure with sp2-hybridized carbon. They have enough surface area to contact the electrolyte and good electrical conductivity. TMCs can also be loaded inside the CNT cavity, which shows a strong con ﬁnement e ﬀect to improve the electrocatalytic performance as compared to26 M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon
