agglomeration and corrosion by the carbon nanotubes, they are able to attain a desired level of stability over a period of 12.5 h. CNT-encapsulated Ni 3Co alloy nanoparticles are shielded from agglomeration and corrosion, allowing for a desired level of long-term stability over 12.5 h. The Ni atom can be added to modify the active center ’s electrical struc- ture and the intermediate ’s chemical adsorption energy. By altering the electronic structure of the neighboring carbonand the adsorption free energy of H, the N dopant can also increase the number of active sites and improve chemical and thermal stability. More intriguingly, it appears that the interconnection between the CNTs ’“branch ”and “trunk ”in this 1D/1D structure can reduce the impedance to charge transfer. Each of these factors plays a part in the improved water splitting e ﬃciency. FeCo alloy nanoparticles encapsulated in nitrogenated carbon nanotubes (Fe xCox-NCNTs) have been created recently using Fe/Co salts and dicyandiamide as precursors. The CNTs guarantee the FeCo alloy nanoparticles ’electro- catalytic activity at all pH levels in addition to o ﬀering strong conductivity. In order to achieve a current density of 10 mA cm−2in acidic, alkaline, and neutral solutions, respectively, Fe 0.4Co0.6-NCNTs with an ideal Fe/Co ratio of 2: 3 need overpotentials of 50, 157, and 202 mV, indicating their exceptional all-pH HER e ﬃciency.171 Apart from transition metal nanomaterials, metal phosphide nanoparticles have also been incorporated into carbon nanotubes (CNTs) for the purpose of water splitting.Yang et al. 170create a technique for creating homogeneous NiFeP nanoparticle-encapsulated nitrogen-doped bamboo- like carbon nanotubes (NBCNT) (NiFeP@NBCNT). Excellent long-term stability and unusually high activity in alkaline solution (180 mV @ 10 mA cm−2and a low Tafel slope of 59 mV dec−1) are demonstrated when the OER is catalysed by the as-fabricated nanocomposites. It is determined that the turnover frequency for NiFeP@NBCNT at overpotential of 350 mV is 3.38 s−1. Its outstanding performance suggests that the synergistic e ﬀect of NiFeP ’s inherent catalytic ac- tivity and pyrrolic nitrogen adulteration in the catalystpromotes the OER activity of NiFeP@NBCNT Figure 11A. NiCu-MOF@MWCNTs electrocatalyst by anchoring well- dispersed multi-walled carbon nanotubes onto Ni –Cu MOF nanoblocks via a simple solvothermal method was devel- oped. The composite exhibited outstanding bifunctional ac- tivity in alkaline media, requiring only 78 mV for HER and 220 mV for OER at 10 mA cm −2, with stable performance over 24 h. Its superior e ﬃciency is attributed to the strong syn- ergy between MWCNTs and bimetallic Ni –Cu nanoblocks, demonstrated its potential as a high-performance water- splitting catalyst.172An orthorhombic-CoTe 2|P heterophase conﬁned in hierarchically porous carbon/CNTs (o-CoTe 2|P@HPC/CNTs) was synthesized via a vacancy-occupation- driven polymorphic transformation. Triggered by P- ﬁlling of Te-vacancies, the hexagonal CoTe 2lattice (h-CoTe 2) slips and distorts into the orthorhombic phase, generating enlarged lattice strain ( ≈1.2 %), residual Te-vacancies, and P –Co bonding. These structural changes jointly expose more Co- active edges, accelerate charge transfer, and tune the elec- tronic structure, delivering an exceptional OER over-potential of only 241 mV at 10 mA cm −2superior to h-CoTe 2 (308 mV), IrO 2@C (332 mV), and all reported telluride cata- lysts. DFT calculations reveal that the P-doping lowers the *OOH formation barrier ( ΔG = 1.021 →0.793 eV), while re- sidual Te-vacancies further shrink it to 0.712 eV by local charge re-distribution that weakens Co –OOH binding. The hierarchical carbon/CNT matrix additionally provides a 262 m2g−1surface area, rapid mass transport, and robust 24- h stability, collectively accounting for the outstanding OER performance, Figure 11B.173Nitrogen-doped carbon nanotube-supported CoS 2/NiS 2heterostructures (CoS 2/ NiS 2@N-CNTs) were designed to create e ﬃcient bifunctional active sites for water splitting. The catalyst demonstrates excellent performance, achieving overpotentials of 126 mV for HER and 205 mV for OER at 10 mA cm−2in 1 M KOH, along with low Tafel slopes of 71 and 82 mV dec−1, a high electro- chemically active surface area (1,030 cm2), and low charge- transfer resistance (41 Ω). When assembled as both anode and cathode, it delivers 1.53 V for overall water splitting and maintains stability for 23 h with negligible performance loss,attributed to its unique heterostructure and strong electron transport properties. 5 4.5.2 Construction of catalytic TMCs on CNT surfaces Enhancing the electrocatalytic activity can also be achieved by altering the catalysts on the surface of carbon nanotubes (CNTs) to reveal more active sites than with the nano- particle-in-nanotubes con ﬁguration. For instance, as a high- performance electrocatalyst for HER, Pan et al.6synthesized Co2−xNixP/CNTs, which are cobalt nickel phosphide nano- particles supported by carbon nanotubes. It was discovered that the HER activities are signi ﬁcantly in ﬂuenced by the Co/ Ni molar ratio. With an overpotential of 91 mV at a currentdensity of 10 mA cm −2in 0.5 M H 2SO4solution, the optimized Co1.6Ni0.4P/CNTs exhibit the best HER performance. This is signiﬁcantly lower than that of other controlled samples, such as the single Co 2−xNixP/CNTs. CNTs and the produced Co1.6Ni0.4P nanoparticles exhibit a potent synergistic impact. According to the XPS analysis, the HER activity is increased by the surplus electrons, which are equivalent to the positive charge that Co 1.6Ni0.4P provides close to the P atom. The electrons from CNT are easily transferred to the metal28 M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon
