Moreover, MWCNTs have been chosen to be treated with varying amounts of H 2SO4and H 2O2in order to create MWCNTs that are strongly oxidized, mildly oxidized, and weakly oxidized (sMWCNTs, mMWCNTs, and wMWCNTs). Lu et al.176described the production of Co 3O4/MWCNTs using a wet-chemical method. The synergistic impact between the two components is responsible for the signi ﬁcantly greater electrocatalytic activity of Co 3O4/mMWCNTs compared to individual Co 3O4nanoparticles and bare mWCNTs. The structural degradation and poor charge transfer of sMWCNTs, as well as the reduced Co 3O4loading brought on by the lack of oxygen-containing functional groups on the surface of wMWCNTs, are the other reasons why the Co 3O4/ mMWCNTs exhibit higher catalytic activity than the Co 3O4/ sMWCNTs and Co 3O4/wMWCNTs. It is commonly recognized that even slight oxidation will reduce the conductivity and damage the CNTs ’original structure. However, MWCNTs, on the other hand, have a multilayer structure. Because of this,the inner layer structure will not be disrupted during the surface oxidation process, which contributes to their sur- face ’s favorable conductivity and abundance of oxygen- containing functional groups. This conclusion can be conﬁrmed by the comparison result for the formation of Co3O4/mildly single-walled CNTs (Co 3O4/mSWCNTs). Layered double hydroxides (LDHs) are regarded as one of the most sophisticated electrocatalytic materials becauseof their easy fabrication and compositional and structural ﬂexibility. 177More active site exposure, improved charge transfer between CNTs and LDHs, and less structural dam- age to the LDH nanosheets during the electrocatalytic pro- cess are all possible with the combination of LDH nanosheets and CNTs. For example, Qiu and co-workers developed CoMn-LDH nanosheets anchored on the CNTs (CoMn-LDH/ CNT), showing the remarkable OER performance with an overpotential of 355 mV at 10 mA cm−2and a small Tafel slope of 45 mV dec−1in 0.1 M KOH.178X