and scalable in situ self-assembly method for preparing a Ce- doped NiFe-LDH nanosheet/nanocarbon (NiFeCe-LDH/CNT, LDH = layered double hydroxide and CNT = carbon nano- tube) hierarchical nanocomposite for excellent OER.179Ac- cording to experimental ﬁndings, doped Ce into NiFe-LDH/ CNT nanoarrays results in lattice defects, electrochemical surface area, a strengthened speci ﬁc surface area, and electron transport between the LDH nanolayered structureand the CNT framework. With a low starting potential (227 mV) and Tafel slope (33 mV dec −1), the NiFeCe-LDH/CNT nanocomposite exhibits greater OER electrocatalytic activity compared to the most non-noble metal-based OER electro- catalysts that have been documented. This is due to the eﬀective synergy. Thus, the Ce-doped LDH nanocomposite is a promising catalyst to broaden the ﬁeld of lanthanide- doped layered materials for e ﬀective water-splitting elec- trocatalysis with scale-up potential due to its exceptional catalytic ability and easy normal temperature synthesis conditions. A nanocage-structured Fe-doped CoS 2catalyst interwoven with carbon nanotubes (FCS/CNT) was devel- oped as a highly e ﬃcient bifunctional electrocatalyst. The nanocage design provides a large surface area with abun- dant active sites, while CNT integration prevents CoS 2ag- gregation and improves electrical conductivity, facilitating rapid charge transfer. Fe doping optimizes intermediate adsorption energies, further enhancing catalytic perfor- mance. Under high redox potentials, CoS 2undergoes in situ transformation to CoOOH, a highly active phase for oxygenevolution. By ﬁne-tuning CNT content, the catalyst achieves impressive activity with overpotentials of 143 mV for HER and 265 mV for OER, along with excellent stability, main- taining over 90 % of current density for both reactions after 36,000 s of continuous operation Figure 12B. 180An optimized Se-doped CoS 2@MoS 2heterostructure anchored on CNTs demonstrated outstanding bifunctional electrocatalytic ac- tivity, achieving low overpotentials of 85 mV for HER and 240 mV for OER at 10 mA cm−2. The catalyst shows excep- tional durability, maintaining stability for over 145 h, outperforming many comparable materials. Its superiorperformance arises from strong interfacial synergy be- tween CoS 2and MoS 2, a high density of active sites, and enhanced electron transfer. Additionally, amorphous Se incorporation modi ﬁes the crystallinity and electronic structure, optimizing intermediate adsorption and activa- tion energy barriers to further boost catalytic e ﬃciency.9 The development of e ﬃcient bifunctional electrocatalysts for HER and OER is essential for advancing sustainable en- ergy technologies such as overall water splitting. A sulfur-modiﬁed CoFe 2O4/Co7Fe3composite integrated with multi- walled carbon nanotubes (S –CoFe 2O4/Co7Fe3/MWCNTs) demonstrated enhanced catalytic performance, achievinglow overpotentials of 250 mV for OER at 10 mA cm−2and 270 mV for HER at 50 mA cm−2, along with small Tafel slopes of 77 mV dec−1and 70 mV dec−1, respectively. The catalyst exhibits excellent durability in alkaline media and enables overall water splitting at a cell voltage of 1.52 V at 10 mA cm−2, highlighting its practical applicability. Combining cost-e ﬀectiveness, structural optimization, and high activity, this material o ﬀers strong potential for large- scale clean energy production.181 5 Future research trends of industrial water splitting Developing electrocatalysts with high intrinsic activity and strong mechanical stability under industrial operating con- ditions is a signi ﬁcant challenge. Minimizing technical costs through reasonable system design is also key to reducingoverall costs and improving electrolysis e ﬃciency. This section will discuss the development and design of electro- catalysts and the optimization of electrolysis systems to facilitate the transition from laboratory research to indus- trial applications. 5.1 Considerations for a more accurate evaluation criterion There is a signi ﬁcant discrepancy between laboratory testing environments, evaluation indicators, and actual in-dustrial production conditions. In laboratories, over- potentials ( η) at 0.5/1 mA cm −2(ηinitial) and 10 mA cm−2(η10) are typically used to assess the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and hydrogen evolution reaction12activities. Howev- er, industrial applications of alkaline water electrolyzers (AWE) and polymer electrolyte water electrolyzers (PEWE) require catalysts with excellent performance at high current densities (>500 mA cm−2), such as η500 or even η1000, as these indicate high hydrogen yield and can reduce capital outlay. Additionally, the ratio of overpotential to current density ( ∼η/∼log j) and its change with current density (j) should be considered for evaluating practical application potential. Secondly, Electrochemical impedance spectros- copy179characterizes the kinetics of OER and HER by measuring solution resistance (Rs) and charge transfer resistance (Rct). While much reported data involves IRs compensation to eliminate voltage drop caused by Rs, this can lead to misjudgment of actual catalytic performance, especially under high currents, where energy consumption due to Rs is inevitable. Therefore, voltage drop cannot beM. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 31
