ignored when evaluating pract ical catalytic performance. Furthermore, under industr ial operating conditions, numerous oxygen and hydrogen bubbles can cause prob- lems such as bubble coverage, di ﬃculty in bubble detachment, and gas accumulat ion, leading to high trans- mission loss, low active site u tilization, and unstable sys- tem performance. Evaluating the catalyst surface ’s bubble management capability, inc luding electrode interface wettability, underwater hydr ophilicity/hydrophobicity, and bubble adhesion force, i s crucial. Also, Laboratory stability tests often use continuous cyclic voltammetry (CV) scanning and chronopotenti ometry/chronoamperometry at low potential/current. To better re ﬂect industrial con- ditions, catalyst stability should be assessed under high current (>500 mAcm−2), high temperature (>60 °C), strong acid/base (pH < 0 and pH > 14), and high pressure (0.1 –0.3 MPa). Quantitative evaluation methods like DV (average voltage degradation) and DA (ratio of DV to initial voltage) can be used to measure energy consumptionspeed and activity degradatio n rate over long-term oper- ation. Catalysts for renewable energy-driven hydrogen production also need to adapt to constantly ﬂuctuating currents. 5.2 Theoretical insights into the catalytic mechanism under industrial conditions Understanding the catalytic process and identifying active sites is crucial, especially as the active phase of catalysts often undergoes structural reconstruction during reactions. Traditional ex situ characterization (pre- and post-reaction states) has limitations, including contamination during storage/sample delivery and di ﬃculty in detecting short- lived intermediates, which restricts accurate understandingof catalytic mechanisms. Moreover, in-situ characterization techniques such as infrared spectroscopy, Raman spectros- copy, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy, synchrotron radia- tion technology, X-ray di ﬀraction, and transmission electron microscopy are vital. These methods can monitor structural evolution in real-time, identify key intermediates and active species, and reveal changes in electronic con ﬁgurations, thereby providing more accurate insights for designing advanced catalysts. Despite advancements, signi ﬁcant dif- ferences still exist between experimental setups for in-situ / operando techniques and industrial electrolysis systems.Continued optimization of experimental methods is needed to more accurately monitor dynamic catalyst evolution and gain deeper insights.5.3 Accelerating the selection and design of advanced and recyclable catalysts The traditional “trial and error ”method for developing high- performance electrocatalysts is laborious and time- consuming. High-throughput screening methods canrapidly identify target materials and screen HER and OER catalysts with excellent activity. To promote the sustainable development of water electrolysis, catalyst recyclability should be considered from the early design stage. This in- cludes evaluating the recyclability, value, and treatment methods of constituent materials to enable resource regeneration. 5.4 Preparation of high-performance catalysts from waste secondary resources Combining solid waste recycling with electrocatalyst syn- thesis can simultaneously utilize waste resources and pro- duce high-performance catalysts. The emerging in-situ repair and regeneration processes o ﬀer a pathway for remediating waste catalysts. By using pretreated waste cat- alysts as raw material and supplementing missing metal salts, the catalytic performance can be restored via in-situ calcination or electrochemical reactions. This is particularly important for precious metal-based catalysts due to their scarce resources. 5.5 Constructing energy-saving water electrolysis systems The eﬃciency of water electrolysis is limited by the slow kinetics of the anodic OER, requiring an input voltage greater than 1.7 V for high current densities. Furthermore,O 2products have low added value, can form explosive gas mixtures with hydrogen, and the reactive oxygen species generated during OER can degrade proton exchange membranes. A rational design involves replacing OER with thermo- dynamically and kinetically more favorable anode reactions to construct new hybrid electrolysis water systems. This can not only reduce energy consumption but also e ﬀectively treat hazardous waste or yield high-value-added oxidationproducts, o ﬀering signi ﬁcant economic and developmental potential.32 M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon
