5.6 Focus on the circular ecology of the catalytic process Attention must be paid to the ecological cycle of the elec- trolysis system, whether conventional or hybrid, as they use large volumes of acidic, neutral, and alkaline electrolytes. The electrolysis process consumes vast amounts of wa- ter, requiring monitoring of system pH changes and timely replenishment. Under industrial conditions, corrosion of electrolytic cells, cathodes, and anodes can lead to electrolyte pollution, necessitating regular impurity removal from waste electrolytes. Moreover, hybrid electrolysis devices, besides electrolyte pH changes, require the separation and collection of organic products in the anode chamber, and the recovery and treatment of residual organic waste electro- lytes are crucial. Thus, Hydrogen energy is an important green energy carrier for a carbon-neutral society, and waterelectrolysis is a preferred low-carbon method for producing high-purity H 2. 6 Conclusion and future prospects In conclusion, the integration of transition metal compounds with carbon nano ﬁbers and carbon nanotubes presents a compelling strategy for enhancing the e ﬃciency of electro- chemical water splitting. The unique properties of thesehybrid materials, including superior electrical conductivity, high surface area, and structural stability, contribute to their eﬀectiveness as electrocatalysts. Recent advancements have demonstrated that this synergistic approach not only im- proves catalytic performance but also ensures long-term stability and durability under operational conditions. As research continues to explore innovative synthesis methods and the optimization of catalyst architectures, these hybrid systems hold signi ﬁcant promise for advancing sustainable hydrogen production technologies. The ongoing develop- ment of cost-e ﬀective and e ﬃcient catalysts is essential for overcoming current challenges and realizing the potential of water splitting as a viable renewable energy source. Looking ahead, the ﬁeld of electrocatalysis for water splitting holds immense potential for innovation and advancement. Future research should focus on several key areas to further enhance the e ﬃciency and practicality of transition metal compound (TMC) and carbon nano ﬁber (CNF) and carbon nanotube (CNT) hybrid catalysts. One promising direction is the exploration of novel materials andcomposite structures that can provide superior catalytic activity while minimizing the reliance on precious metals. This includes the development of earth-abundant transitionmetal catalysts that exhibit comparable performance to their noble metal counterparts. Additionally, the optimization of synthesis methods for TMC-CNF and TMC-CNT hybrids is crucial. Techniques such as electrospinning, templating, and 3D printing can be employed to create tailored architectures that maximize active site exposure and enhance mass transport properties. Understanding the interactions at the nanoscale level be-tween TMCs and carbon materials will be essential for designing catalysts with improved stability and reactivity. Moreover, integrating advanced characterization tech- niques, such as in situ spectroscopy and microscopy, will provide deeper insights into the catalytic mechanisms and degradation pathways under operational conditions. This knowledge can guide the design of more robust catalysts that maintain performance over extended periods. Another important avenue is the exploration of oper- ando conditions that mimic real-world applications. Research should aim to evaluate the performance of thesecatalysts under varying pH levels, temperatures, and elec- trolytic environments to better understand their applica- bility in practical settings. Finally, interdisciplinary collaboration between mate- rials science, chemistry, and engineering will be pivotal in driving forward the development of next-generation elec- trocatalysts. By addressing the existing challenges and leveraging new technologies, the future of water splitting technologies can pave the way for sustainable hydrogenproduction, contributing signi ﬁcantly to the global transi- tion towards clean energy. Research ethics: Not applicable. Informed consent: Not applicable. Author contributions: All authors have accepted re- sponsibility for the entire content of this manuscript and approved its submission. Use of Large Language Models, AI and Machine Learning Tools: None declared. Conﬂict of interest: The authors state no con ﬂict of interest. Research funding: None declared. Data availability: Not applicable. References 1. Kamaruddin, H.; Jianghong, Z.; Yu, L.; Yuefan, W.; Yizhong, H. A Review of Noble Metal-Free High Entropy Alloys for Water Splitting Applications. J. Mater. Chem. A 2024 ,12(17), 9933 –9961. 2. Sun, H.; Xu, X.; Kim, H.; Jung, W.; Zhou, W.; Shao, Z. Electrochemical Water Splitting: Bridging the Gaps Between Fundamental Researchand Industrial Applications. Energy & Environmental Materials 2023 ,6 (5), e12441.M. Murad et al.: Transition metal compounds-carbon 33
