Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 Available online 26 March 2022 1364-0321/© 2022 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.A review on the electrocatalytic dissociation of water over stainless steel: Hydrogen and oxygen evolution reactions A. Razaa, K.M. Deenb, E. Asselinb, W. Haidera,* aSchool of Engineering and Technology, Central Michigan University, Mt. Pleasant, MI, 48859, USA bDepartment of Materials Engineering, The University of British Columbia, Vancouver, BC V6T 1Z4, Canada ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Electrocatalysis Water splitting Butler-volmer equation Hydrogen evolution reaction Oxygen evolution reaction Surface modification ABSTRACT For the hydrogen economy to be viable, new, and efficient production techniques are of prime importance. Water electrolysis offers high production of hydrogen but due to slow reaction rates on many electrode surfaces, electrocatalysts are needed. However effective electrocatalysts, such as platinum and rhenium, may be impractical for economic operation. Therefore, research in this area has been focused on finding materials that can replace these expensive electrocatalysts. The electrocatalytic behaviour of stainless steel towards water dissociation is presented. The use of two widely available and comparatively inexpensive stainless steels i.e., 304 and 316L, in a variety of forms, i.e., mesh, solid electrode, and adsorbed nanoparticles is discussed. Results of microscopic characterization are compiled to illustrate how surface modification of these substrates affects their electrocatalytic ability. The crystallographic orientations i.e. (111) and (220) in the microstructure of stainless steel are believed to be effective in catalytic dissociation of H2O. The catalytic activity and long-term stability measurements of stainless steels have yielded results similar to or sometimes better than-those of the noble electrocatalysts. The review briefly captures the current progress in HER and OER electrocatalysis on stainless steels and highlights the possible research solutions to overcome existing challenges i.e., lack of active centers, the surface modification needed, poisoning of active species and an overall low stability, the solution to which could make stainless steel a viable replacement for the precious metals electrocatalysts. 1.Introduction Energy demands continue to increase globally [1,2]. Currently, 65% of the global energy demand is dependent on non-renewable fossil fuels [3]. However, this breakdown in the source of energy is variable. For example, a study in 2017 said that in 2014, approximately 14.1% and 85.9% of total primary energy was generated from Renewable Energy (RE) and Non-Renewable Energy (NRE), respectively [4,5]. For the past few decades, a significant amount of research has focused on developing renewable resources, but their distribution and chronological avail- ability are the major challenges to their implementation on a large scale [6]. Hydrogen (H2) gas is recognized as a green energy source due to its high specific energy [7,8], generation of environment-friendly end products [9,10], and abundance on earth [11,12]. Hydrogen can be produced via water electrolysis. Water electrolysis consists of applying a potential across two electrodes such that the anodic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) occurs on one surface and the cathodic hydrogen evo- lution reaction (HER) occurs on the other. The magnitude of the applied potential depends on the selectivity and catalytic properties of the electrode material(s) [13]. Hydrogen can be stored as a fuel and used in transportation [14] and power generation in internal combustion en- gines [15] or turbines [16]. Generally, H2 is generated through reduction processes i.e. reduction of H(in acidic medium) and/or H2O (in alkaline medium) [17], however, compared to the alkaline conditions, the acidic environment is considered unsuitable for HER due to the limited stability of the cata- lysts [18]. The most efficient and commercially available electrocatalysts for the OER are iridium and ruthenium, while platinum-based materials are commonly used for the HER [19]. However, these electrocatalytic ma- terials belonging to platinum group metals are expensive. This limits their widespread application in economical hydrogen production. Therefore, the quest to find suitable and cheaper alternatives is ongoing [20]. Stainless steel (SS), because of its corrosion resistance and me- chanical properties, is ubiquitously used in the aerospace [21], auto- motive [22], construction [22] and chemical industries [23]. In the past few decades, many studies described the electrocatalytic behavior of SS *Corresponding author. E-mail address: haide1w@cmich.edu (W. Haider). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews u{�~zkw! s{yo|kro>! ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2~�o~! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.rser.2022.112323 Received 14 November 2021; Received in revised form 17 February 2022; Accepted 23 February 2022
