Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 2towards the HER and OER. Although different methods i.e. steam methane reforming [24–26], gasification of coal [27–29], utilization of biomass [30–32], nuclear energy [33–35], and electrolysis [36–38] have been proposed for hydrogen production based on low overall hydrogen cost, and improved efficiency [39–41], water electrolysis remains the best method because hydrogen is produced from H2O and the only by-product is pure oxygen [42]. In electrolysis, hydrogen and oxygen are produced when water molecules are broken down by the application of electric current [43]. In an alkaline electrolysis cell, two electrodes are immersed in a 20–30% KOH solution. At the anode, water oxidation produces O2 and at the cathode surface, the reduction of water into H2 takes place. A membrane separates the two electrodes and restricts the electron transport and physical mixing of hydrogen and oxygen gases [44]. On the other hand, in a solid oxide fuel cell, water is dissociated at high temperatures (F800 •C). This thermodynamically favoured water splitting results in large volumes of hydrogen [45]. The proton exchange membrane cell uses a solid sulfonated polystyrene membrane that provides high proton conductivity, limits the gas crossover and can be used at high-gas pressure [46]. To reduce overpotentials and to improve energy efficiency, the ki- netics of both OER and HER can be increased by the use of active electrocatalysts. It has been shown that the reaction overpotential for the OER could be as large as ~245 mV more than that of the HER [47]. This higher overpotential is primarily associated with the multi-electron transfer intermediate reactions in which a rate-determining step that dictates the overall reaction rate is of prime importance. The desired function of an efficient electrocatalyst is to speed up the rate-limiting reaction thus enhancing the overall OER [48,49]. In general, an effi- cient electrocatalyst for water electrolysis should possess the following characteristics [50–56]. (i)An active surface that provides better attachment of electro - catalyst on the surface and fast removal of the products (ii)The catalyst should be chemically, electrochemically, and me- chanically stable and must withstand aggressive process conditions (iii) Must present low overpotentials for OER and HER  (iv) The low material cost, simple synthesis and post processing steps at commercial scale are also the key factors of consideration in selecting electrocatalysts (v) Must have a lower energy barrier for intermediate adsorbed species i.e., H*, O*, HO*, and HOO* etc. (vi) Compatibility and sustainability of the hydrogen production systems for its continuous production, storage, and supply to the consumers As far as the cost of the material is concerned, it upderpins the whole prospect of using SS as an electrocatalyst. Even as a raw material with no surface modifications or treatments, the per kilogram price for both SS- 316 and SS-304 is well within the $5 bracket [57–60]. This is almost 4 times less than the average rate for 1 kg of nickel [61]. By comparison, the noble metals are much higher in price with platinum and rhenium costing $30900 [62] and $1300 per kilogram [63] respectively. Keeping in mind the base price of the material, it becomes evident that SS is by far the most affordable, even from a laboratory usage perspective. Fig. 1 shows a comparative cost analysis of per kg H2 yield from additive manufactured (AM) 316L as well as from Pt/IrO 2 based electrocatalysts and it can be seen at roughly $1/kg of H2, the use of AM SS may prove to be the most economical. By contrast, in 2020, the hydrogen production cost has been reported to be in between $4 and $6 per kg of H2, whereas in 2019, it was ranging $2.5 –6.8 per kg of H2 [64]. Furthermore, the production of hydrogen from water splitting is also affected by the cost of energy required to operate the electrolyzer. Athough a greater usage of electrolzer cuts down the fixed cost added by its construction, the recurring operating cost still contributes to the total cost. It has been calculated however that the optimal ~3000 –6000 h operation of the electrolyzer minimizes the overall production cost. It has also been noticed that at the operation D2000 h, the operating cost of the electrolyzer becomes higher, thereby rendering the whole process economically non-viable. By the use of conventional processes, approximately half of the cost involved in the production of H2 is associated with the electricity cost ($46 –57/MWh el) [65]. On the other hand, owing to the low cost of alkaline water electrolyzers, i.e. relatively low construction cost, flexibility in the upscaling of the process and longevity of the setup make the overall water splitting process less expensive [66]. Furthermore, commercially, the efficiency of the alka- line electrolyzers ranges between 43 and 69% with an average cost of ~ $1257/kW el, where the capital costs have been reported to be around $457 –1029/kW el [67]. Fig. 2 shows the cost comparison of the different hydrogen generation processes i.e., steam methane reforming (SMR), coal gasification (CG), partial oxidation of hydrocarbons (POX), biomass Abbreviations CSS Cratered Stainless Steel CV Cyclic Voltammetry DFT Density Functional Theory ECSA Electrochemically Active Surface Area FESEM Field Emission Scanning Electron Microscopy FE Faradaic Efficiency GF Graphite Felt HER Hydrogen Evolution Reaction HSSF Heat Treated Stainless Steel Felt LSV Linear Sweep Voltammetry NWSSF Nonwoven Stainless Steel Fabric OER Oxygen Evolution Reaction RHE Reference Hydrogen Electrode RDS Rate Determining Step SSF Stainless Steel Felt SLM Selective Laser Melting SSM Stainless Steel Mesh XRD X-ray Diffraction XPS X-Ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy ZPE Zero Point Energy Fig. 1.Comparison of AM-316L with Pt/IrO 2 electrocatalysts.  A. Raza et al.
