Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 3gasification (BG), wind-based electrolysis (W-EL) and hydro-based electrolysis (H-EL). It is evident that compared to other processes, water splitting via H-EL is the most economical. Although this process suffers from the problem of a lower plant capacity, yet it yields sub- stantially low equivalent CO2 emissions i.e. 0.01 kg/Mj at 52% effi- ciency [68,69]. Based on these qualities, the water electrolysis, or in this case the alkaline water electrolysis is considered commercially viable. As far as the reason for selection of material is concerned, the ubiquitous availability of SS, low cost, manufacturability and its sta- bility are the primary reasons for its selection as an HER/OER electro - catalyst. WORLD6, a model used for investigating possibilities and making predictions for sustainable use of resources has been used by researchers for making predictions regarding the availability and the subsequent fall in the supply to demand ratio. Keeping in view the limited supply of the rare-earth and precious metals and to minimize the carbon emissions, the recycling of metals and promotion of the circular economy has been encouraged in recent years. It has also been reported that if the optimized recycling protocols for the production of SS are promoted, its continuous supply can be ensured for the next 30 years. The low cost and abundant availability of the SS constituent metals i.e. Ni, Cr, Mn, Nb, Mo, V and Co (compared to the Pt, Ir, and Pd etc.) confirm the sustainable supply of raw materials for the construction of SS based electrolyzers. It has been reported that the prime alloying element of SS i.e. Ni will not face a decline in supply before 2050, after which the Mn–Cr based steels could be used. The replacement of Ni with Nb, Mo, V and Co in SS, however, will not be able to bring the same properties in SS. As for Cr and Mn however, their availability can be ensured up to year 2300 [70]. Based on these factors as well as ease in fabrication, the SS is considered as a sustainable material compared to Pt, Ir, and Ru electrocatalysts. It is also important to mention that the chemical and heat treatments and other surface modifications negligibly influence the overall cost of SS and do not affect its affordability. Furthermore, electrodeposition of layers of platinum on SS substrate not only inceases the conductivity but also decreases the cost of the whole assembly [71]. The same idea of deposition of nanoparticles on cast steel has also been discussed and found to be economically viable when compared to wrought counter - parts [72]. All these instances have been compiled to justify the use of SS as a widespread and economical electrocatalyst for water splitting. This review discusses in detail all the possibilities whereby SS-316 or SS-304 can be used for electrocatalysis. First of all a brief overview of the fundamentals and the governing reaction mechanisms has been given, followed by a description of selectivity-activity-stability Gordian knot. Parameters of an ideal electrocatalyst have also been listed afterwards. In the majority of the sections, the use of SS as an electrocatalyst for water splitting in various forms and through different treatments has been discussed. With regards to shape and form, SS can be used as a mesh, foil, substrate or in the form of nano particles on another sub- strate. In surface modifications, SS can be subjected to a wide variety of oxidations i.e. sulphidization, phosphodization, nitridation and electro- oxidation. Various heat treatments can be applied to the SS substrate to enhance its electrocatalytic properties. Cellular and hatched cellular structures resulting from additive manufacturing have also been found to be useful in boosting the electrocatalytic response. Water splitting process yields H2 and O2, which are both important and are in high demand. To classify any material as an effective catalyst, many different aspects must be considered which can serve as a guide for future researchers. There are however some features of great importance that are often overlooked. Since porosity of the surface is directly linked to the surface area and indirectly related to the electrocatalytic activity of the electrocatalyst, this is one area which could benefit from more research. By introducing a certain level of porosity on the surface, high electrocatalytic activity can be achieved by increasing the ECSA [73,74]. Another approach can be to achieve the same ECSA by growing nano- particles of rare earth metals. Finally, the inherent porosity of cellular structure of AM produced materials can also be used to increase the ECSA. During AM processing of SS, the electrocatalytic activity may be improved by manipulating bulk density or porosity, which can be ach- ieved by improving the ECSA for the generation of hydrogen and oxy- gen. SS-316 electrodes with 3D structures could also be easily built by AM processes and these structures may be helpful in enhancing the electrocatalytic active sites for water splitting. Layered double hydrox - ides (LDH) deposited on SS, despite having low electrical conductivities, show high electrocatalytic performance [75,76]. That is why further research involving a combined use of LDH deposits on the porous structures of SS (produced via AM) could also be incorporated in the future studies. Furthermore, by tuning the microstructure through different surface or chemical treatments, different phases could be ob- tained that may have a tendency to significantly improve the electro - catalytical behavior of SS produced via AM. A proper understanding of the electrocatalytic mechanism is also required. Furthermore, despite conceiving countless new electrocatalyst materials, stable performance during continous and intermittent operating cycles still needs to be achieved [51]. Since a large portion of electrocatalysts involves surface modified materials or nano particles decorated on a substrate, the major problem faced is the detachment or contamination of those particles. This in turn leads to a loss of electrocatalytic activity over the long run. That is why these proposed solutions could benefit from more research. All of these points serve as a roadmap for future research in the field of electrocatalysis. This review describes the potential use of stainless steel as sustain - able electrocatalyst for water splitting and for the generation of both H2 and O2 gases, which differentiates this study from other published studies. The selection of SS instead of existing and well-established Fig. 2.Cost analysis of different hydrogen production processes.  A. Raza et al.
