Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 4precious metal based electrocatalysts like Pt, Ir, and Ru etc. has been proposed based on its low cost, mechanical integrity, stability, fabric - ability and the ease in the modification of its structural design during AM processes. Different strategies i.e. addition of alloying elements, microstructural variations during AM, post heat-treatments, and surface texturing of SS substrate are possible, which could justify its use over other electrocatalytic materials that may be reported in the literature. Owing to the process condition of AM, the SS structures may show promising electrochemical stability in aqueous media and improved electrocatalytic performance for both HER and OER under ambient conditions. Due to the non-homogeneous heating and cooling cycles during AM processing of SS, the formation of dendritic and fine cellular shaped microstructure enhance its electrochemical stability and elec- trocatalytic performance for HER and OER. Various strategies have to be applied to address the stability challenges of wrought SS i.e., deposition of electrocatalytic materials and surface modifications to improve their performance in alkaline water electrolyzers. Furthermore, research in- dicates that 4% of the hydrogen produced through electrolysis is due to unnecessary energy consumption amounting to 2.05 kW h/m3H2. There is a strong possibility of of alleviation of this challenge by using the AM process because of its ability to produce highly porous parts with increased specific surface area as well active regions for HER to proceed [77]. Another attribute of AM SS as a electrocatalyst is its values of overpotential for HER i.e. 280 mV [78] and OER i.e. 310 mV to get the targeted current density of 10 mA cm␀2, which are reasonably close to those for Pt-based electrocatalysts varying between 122 and 218 mV for HER [79] and 270 mV for OER [80]. All of these attributes make AM SS and in general, SS, a highly suitable candidate for electrocatalysis and have been discussed in this review. 2.Electrochemistry of the HER & OER 2.1. Fundamentals of the HER The HER (2H2e– → H2) occurs at the cathode in water elec- trolysis. The HER involves an intermediate reaction during a two- electron transfer. Lowering the overpotential and increasing the effi- ciency of the electrolysis is dependent on the electrocatalyst. The small overpotential and facile HER on Pt have been studied rigorously, how- ever, Pt is not only expensive but it is also a relatively scarce metal [81]. In an alkaline medium, during HER, the generation of His considered the rate-determining step (RDS) that requires additional energy to break the –O–H bond and to produce Hunder these condi - tions, the HER either follows the Volmer –Heyrovsky (R1 and R2) or the Volmer –Tafel (R1 and R3) reaction sequences as shown schematically in Fig. 3. Volmer Step: H2O e␀→ H* OH␀(R1) Heyrovsky Step: H* H2O e– → H2 OH␀(R2) Tafel step: 2 H* → H2                                                                                   (R3) Theoretically, the overall rate of the HER is determined by evalu - ating the rate of Volmer (vV), Heyrovsky (vH) and Tafel (vT) steps indi- vidually as described by equations E1, E2 and E3. vVko VH2O1␀θH√ e␀βVFη RT⇑ ␀ko ␀VOH␀θH⌊ e1␀βVFη RT⌋ (1) vHko HH2OθH√ e␀βVFη RT⇑ ␀ko ␀HOH␀1␀θH⌊ e1␀βHFη RT⌋ (2) vTko Tθ2 H␀ko ␀T⌊PH2 Po⌋ 1␀θH2(3) Where, θH is the fraction of catalyst ’s surface covered with the adsorbed H* species and ko V, ko H, and ko T are standard rate constants (in cm/sec) for Volmer, Heyrovsky and Tafel processes, respectively. ko ␀V, ko ␀H, and ko ␀T are the standard rate constants for the reverse reactions (R 1–3) on the surface of a catalysts. Based on the rate of the electron transfer reactions (Volmer and Heyrovsky steps) total faradaic current density (j) of the overall HER reaction can be calculated by using equation (E4). By determining the rate constants o