al possible mechanisms for the OER in acidic or alkaline con- ditions have been proposed in the past [100]. The OER reaction is mainly dependent upon the pH of the solution. For instance, in acidic conditions, the water is dissociated into protons and oxygen whereas the hydroxyl ions are oxidized to water and oxygen in alkaline conditions. However, the OER is also hindered due to the specific adsorption of intermediate species on the catalyst surface [101]. The formation of oxygen from the intermediate species (MO) follows two possible routes i. e. the direct combination of MO to generate O2(g) and/or producing MOOH followed by the decomposition into O2(g) as depicted in Fig. 4 [102]. The equilibrium half-cell potentials (EO a) of the OER under standard conditions are given in R7 and 8. 4OH␀↔2H2OO24e␀EO a0B404 VpH14 (R7) 2H2O↔4HO24e␀EO a1B23VpH0 (R8) 2.3. Energetics of the HER & OER 2.3.1. Energetics of the HER Over an electrocatalyst surface, the HER involves three reaction steps i.e. adsorption, dissociation of intermediate species, and formation of a hydrogen molecule. In general, the effectiveness of a catalyst and re- action sequence is estimated by calculating the adsorption (Ead), and Table 1 Brief overview of some base metal electrocatalytic materials used for HER. Material Electrolyte Concentration HER Overpotential (V) vs. RHE Current Density mA.cm␀2 Stability Limit (hrs) Ref 316L foil 6 M NaOH n/a 100 216 [90] TiO 2 coated NiTi foil 0.5 M H2SO4 ␀565 0.007  [91] TiO 2 coated NiTi foil with 1% tensile strain ␀472 0.009 TiO 2 coated NiTi foil with 2% tensile strain ␀371 0.021 TiO 2 coated NiTi foil with 3% tensile strain ␀260 0.097 W2C nanosheets H2 saturated 0.5 M H2SO4 274 0.19  [92] W2C nanosheets with 2% Fe 197 0.22 W2C nanosheets with 2% Co 157 0.41 W2C nanosheets with 2% Ni 57 0.79 Pt/C  39 0.92 FeNi 2P nanosheets 1 M KOH 116 10 12 [93] FeNiSe 2 nanosheets 147 10 Ni/NiO 1 M KOH 160 10 10 [94] NiFe –P/IF 1 M KOH 220 200 20 [95] NiS deposits on 30