Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 6activation energies of water dissociation (Eac) in addition to the evalu - ation of the adsorption-free energy of H(ΔGH). For instance, a ΔGH  value close to zero is an indication of facile hydrogen production at the catalyst surface [18]. However, a highly negative ΔGH corresponds to the strong binding of intermediate H* with the surface or simply high- lights the difficulty in H-atom desorption. On the other hand, positive ΔGH represents the inability of the catalyst to stabilize intermediate species for an electron transfer process and ultimately limits the HER. In other words, an efficient catalyst should exhibit sufficiently strong binding with the intermediate adsorbed species to promote proto - n/electron transfer but simultaneously must be weak enough to facili - tate accelerated H2 evolution to avoid excessive surface coverage by the intermediate species [103]. Generally, compared to the ΔGHof 0.09 eV for Pt [87], the catalytic materials that present ΔGHD0.2 eV, exhibit an appreciable exchange current density (io) and are considered suitable candidates for HER [104]. On the other hand, the ease in breaking H–O–H bonds is estimated from the smallest Eac value. Based on this, an efficient catalytic material should present the lowest absolute value of Ead, Eac, and ΔGH. In acidic media, both ΔGHand Ead are adequate proxies for the HER activity of a catalyst. However, Eac (activation en- ergy for water dissociation) is a key parameter in determining the kinetic activity of the HER over a catalyst surface in alkaline media [105]. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations have been performed to develop free energy diagrams to predict the kinetic barriers and to identify the RDS involved during HER over different materials. For instance, DFT calculations have been utilized to establish that out of 271 different structures, the HER activity over the Co2B2, Os/Co 2B2, Co–Ni2B2, Pt–Ni2B2, and Mn–Co2B2 was found to be much higher than on Pt metal [106]. Recently, DFT and machine learning were combined to assess the HER activity and thermal stability of 264 single loaded MXene structures based on ΔGHand cohesive energy values [107]. It has been reported that 21 MXene structures had high catalytic activity for the HER compared to Pt [87] as indicated from their | ΔGH| D0.09 eV and higher thermal stability as evident from the large cohesive en- ergy ranging from 5.9 to 8 eV/atom. These studies explore the suitable candidate materials that present very large catalytic activity in terms of HER, however, there exists a gap in the clear understanding of the HER process mechanism on SS both in acidic and alkaline conditions and detailed DFT calculations at atomistic scales are very much needed to determine the ΔGHof an RDS and other intermediate steps. It has been reported that in a multistep reaction ΔG◦ ⋃ΔG◦ i but (EℑℑEℑ i), where i is the ith step [108]. In such a multistep reaction, RDS must be ten times slower than the slowest step. It follows that EℑℑEℑ RDS. In a multistep reaction, the β and α do not coincide. The differ - ence between β and α has also been explained, where the first refers to single-step reactions, whereas the latter parameter is for complex re- actions [109]. In simple words, α is a multistep version of β. The sym- metry factor β relates to the physical chemistry of the initial electron transfer step, whereas the transfer coefficient α relates to the rate of the reaction and the total number of electrons preceding the RDS [109]. Although Lee et al. [87] worked on the HER of heteroatom-doped graphene and the OER of heteroatom-doped C3N4, they still used the same Volmer-Heyrovsky and Volmer-Tafel mechanisms as proposed in section 2.1. As seen in Fig. 5, it is found by the Nernstian behavior of the HER, the reaction tendency can be calculated by calculating the Gibbs free energy of the intermediate state, ǀΔGH*ǀ. The Pt catalyst is consid - ered favourable for the HER due to its low ǀΔGH*ǀ value (0.09 eV) [110]. In this work, the Volmer-Tafel-Heyrovsky mechanism was used to calculate the ΔGVolmer H* (ƟH1*), ΔGVolmer H* (ƟH2*), ΔGHeyrovsky H* (ƟH1*) and ΔGHeyrovsky H* (ƟH1*) by considering the definite concentration of hydrogen intermediate species at the ƟH1* and ƟH2* active sites as indicated in Fig. 3. The ΔGH* of the intermediate H* species can be calculated by using E6. ΔG H*ΔE H*ΔE ZPE␀TΔSH (6) Where ΔEH* and ΔEZPE are the hydrogen adsorption energy and zero- point energy (ZPE) between the adsorbed state and the gas phase, respectively. The entropy of the adsorbed ½ H2 at a given temperature can be calculated by ΔSH ␀1/2 S0 H2 in which S0 H2 refers to the entropy of H2 at STP [87]. 2.3.2. Energetics of the OER Rossmeisl et al. [48] and Ping et al. [111] proposed a possible route for the OER, composed of four proton-electron transfer steps as described by reactions R9-12 [112]. For an ideal catalyst, the overall overpotential for the OER (R13) calculated from reaction free energies of all four electron/proton transfer reaction steps (ΔG1, ΔG2, ΔG3 and ΔG4) is 4.92/4 1.23 V. H2O(l) (*) ⇌ HHO* e␀ΔG 1                                              (R9) HO* ⇌ HO* e␀ΔG 2                                                         (R10) O* H2O(l) ⇌ HHOO* e␀ΔG 3                                          (R11) HOO* ⇌ HO2(g) e␀(*) ΔG 4                                            (R12) Fig. 5.Schematic representation of the change in Gibbs free energy of intermediate H* species involved in the Volmer-Heyrovsky and Volmer-Tafel mecha - nisms [87]. A. Raza et al.
