ntial (ηOER) of the OER and is designated as a potential-determining step (PDS). For instance, under standard conditions, theoretically, the overpotential of the OER (ηOER) is calculated using E7 [114]. ηOERΔG iCmax\ e␀1B23V (7) Recently the free energy (ΔGi) was calculated for the intermediate steps which were involved during the progress of the OER on 15 different single-atom transition metals including Ti, V, Mn, Fe, Ni, Co, Cu, Mo, Ru, Pd, Ag, Pt, Au, Zn and Cr by using DFT calculations and ML predictions [115]. Stainless steels contain Fe, Cr and Ni as major alloying additions, the benefit of the transition metals is the presence of the d-orbital that supports the easy exchange of electrons and facilitates the breaking and formation of intermediate OH*, O* and OOH* bonds on the surface. As predicted from the ΔGi, the RDS over Fe is R12 (as shown above) whereas the conversion of intermediate O* into OOH* determines the overall rate of OER on Cr and Ni surfaces. However no information on the combined effect of the catalisys of O2 evolution is available for these transition metals that exist in stainless steel as a solid solution. 2.4. Key performance indicators for electrocatalysts Several important factors, i.e. the nature of the electrocatalytic ma- terial, electrolyte, overpotential, Tafel slope, selectivity, cyclic perfor - mance, stability including chemical, thermal and physical, and activity, influence the overall kinetics of the HER and OER processes. The per- formance of the catalyst is also affected by the crystal structure, con- ductivity, surface wettability, and contact angle of the electrolyte. One of the most important parameters is the output current density, and 10 mA cm␀2 is considered the benchmark value to evaluate the over- potential of the HER and OER. A catalyst presenting an overpotential in the range of 300–400 mV is considered excellent. Thus, an ideal elec- trocatalyst is known for its selectivity, stability and activity in combi - nation. Fig. 6 illustrates the schematic of the selectivity-activity-stability relationship in the form of a Gordian Knot [116]. To get a brief idea of selectivity, the electrolysis of seawater was conducted and it was found that the chlorine evolution reaction (ClER) and hypochlorite formation were the two major reactions competing with the OER [117]. Since both of these reactions were two-electron reactions, they were considered to be more feasible than the four-electron OER. Here it was noted that while the OER was thermo - dynamically feasible the ClER was kinetically more feasible. This raises an important need for developing electrocatalysts that have a high selectivity for the OER during electrolysis in such conditions. The conductive indium doped tin oxide (ITO), and Cu/Ti foil are considered unidirectional porous electrodes. Because the 3D electrodes e.g. carbon paper/cloth and Ni foam, provide multidirectional channels for electrolyte flow. The widely used glassy carbon (GC) electrode is easy to handle but exhibits limited catalyst loading capacity (~1 mg cm␀2) Fig. 6.The selectivity-activity-stability Gordian Knot with the text in triangles explaining the parameter and the text in squares describing the factors influencing the performance. A. Raza et al.
