Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 8[118]. GC electrodes loaded with different catalytic materials having effective activity towards HER are listed in Table 2. To stabilize the electrocatalytic material on the GC electrode surface, a suitable binder is always required. Binders can block the active sites and may hinder the ionic diffusion within the porous structure of a catalyst, which are their main disadvantages [119]. Also, the catalyst is applied on the surface of the GC electrode and the catalyst layer could peel off due to the reactions occurring on the surface, i.e. H2 or O2 evolution, which may lead to poor catalyst stability. This can, however, be solved by surface pre-treatment to promote the attachment of the catalyst layer. Catalytic stability has been mentioned by researchers in two different contexts i.e. thermal [120,121] and oxidative [122]. It is affected by reactions between the electrolyte and the catalyst, availability of active sites and the presence of loose nanoparticles of narrow size distribution leading to the blockage of active sites [123]. Typical catalyst materials tend to be more stable in alkaline media compared to acidic environments. The manufacturing process also influences stability. For instance, directly deposited mate- rials have been found more stable compared to pasted ones. In many studies, the stability of the catalyst is evaluated within a short time by a chronopotentiometry that does not represent the long-term stability under actual conditions. Operando characterization techniques were studied which were found to be helpful to gain an understanding of the structure-property relationship for catalysts [136]. Operando characterization i.e. XRD, XPS and Raman spectroscopy etc. coupled simultaneously to electro - chemical testing make it possible to explain the relationship between structure and electrochemical properties of the catalyst. Since the changes in the structure of a catalyst are reasonably unpredictable and affect its performance, operando characterization could be beneficial. It was found that dynamic structural changes have a profound effect on the activity, selectivity and stability of the electrocatalyst. Li et al. [137] explained this by showing that these properties depend on the nature of active sites, their accessibility and the transport of active species to- wards them. The transport of active species was found to depend on the porous structures on the surface. The operando characterization tech- niques have been explained to assess the structural, compositional, chemical and physical properties. The operando techniques are not only able to comment more thoroughly on the active sites but also detect reaction intermediates and detailed reaction mechanisms [138]. These techniques include microscopy, X-Ray based spectroscopy, Mossbauer spectroscopy, X-Ray absorption spectroscopy and X-Ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy technique. 2.5. Figure of merit A figure of merit was proposed by Tahir et al. [139], which is useful for estimating the performance of an electrocatalyst based on over- potential. The figure is based on the overpotential required to generate 10 mA/cm2 of current at t 0 and t 10 h: these time points represent the activity and stability of the catalyst, as shown in Fig. 7. Depending on the overpotential at 10 mA/cm2, the catalysts are categorized as ideal (200 –300 mV), excellent (300 –400 mV), good (400 –500 mV) and satisfactory (F500 mV). Any catalyst material that has an overpotential F500 mV within 10 h of operation is not considered attractive for the OER because it has poor long-term stability. Any cat- alytic material that has an overpotential within the 300–400 mV range (at 10 mA/cm2) is considered ‘very good ’ for the OER. 3.Use of stainless steel as an electrocatalyst 3.1. Stainless steel substrate as an electrocatalyst Over the years, many researchers have shown that the electro - catalysis of the OER by Ni can be improved when it is alloyed with iron [140]. Stainless steels (such as the austenitic AISI 304, 316, and 316L grades), which contain Fe and Ni, may be suitable catalytic materials for improved water dissociation. It has been noticed that the electro - catalytic ability of stainless steel has not been studied rigorously in the past [141]. In addition to the bulk alloy chemistry, stainless steels are covered by a thin passive oxide/oxy-hydroxide surface film that may itself be cat- alytic for the OER [142]. A stainless steel scrubber of grade 434L was used as an economical electrode for water splitting in an alkaline medium [143]. Cyclic vol- tammetry demonstrated that the water-splitting occurred on the surface Table 2 Various electrocatalytic materials for HER, detail of loading capacity and overpotential for the listed current output. Material Electrolyte Concentration (M) Overpotential mV Current Density mAcm-2 Catalyst Loading Capacity mg-cm-2 Ref Fe–Ni–S nanosheets 0.5 M H2SO4 105 0.02 0.254 [124] Ni–Mo–S nanosheets on carbon fiber 0.5 M H2SO4 200  0.52 [125] CoMoS 3 0.5 M H2SO4 112, 171 1, 10 0.5 [126] Co9S8@MoS 2 on carbon nanofibers 0.5 M H2SO4 190 10 0.212 [127] Zn0.3Co2.7S4 0.5 M H2SO4 80, 90, 85 10 0.285 [128] 129, 144, 136 1 VS2nanosheets 0.5 M H2SO4 ␀68 10 0.01 [129] MoS 2 on reduced Graphene Oxide 0.5 M H2SO4 1000  0.28 [130] WS2 @ P,N,O doped Graphene film 0.5 M H2SO4 125 10 0.113 [131] Mo2C with nitrogen rich carbon nanolayers 0.5 M H2SO4 60 1 0.28 [132] 124 10 Mo2C nanoparticles in C matrix 0.5 M H2SO4 78 0.178 0.25 [133] Mo2C nanowires 0.5 M H2SO4 130  0.21 [134] Carbon supported Mo0.06W0.94C 0.5 M H2SO4 220  0.7 [135] Fig. 7.The figure of merit for an OER catalyst [139].  A. Raza et al.
