Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 9of the stainless steel at ~1.8 V. The OER occurred above 1.59 V vs. RHE. On the other hand, the current response at potential D␀0.2 V vs. RHE corresponded to the HER on the surface of the stainless steel scrubber. The overpotentials required for the HER and OER were 260 mV and 418 mV, respectively, which were noticeably better than other conventional and more costly materials. The two-electrode SS scrubber system pro- duced a current output of 10 mA/cm2 at 1.98 V cell voltage without iR compensation. OER on stainless steel was investigated and an overpotential of ƞ  0.37 V at 10 mA/cm2 in 1 M KOH solution was reported [23]. A Tafel slope of 30 mV/decade and the long-term stability of SS were promising according to these authors. A cyclic voltammetry (CV) scan showed a prominent anodic peak (Epa) at approximately 1.47 V vs. RHE. Similarly, a cathodic peak (Epc) at 1.37 V corresponds to the quasi-reversibility of the NiO/NiO X redox process. The presence of NixFeyOz/Co xFeyOz, and FeO x/NiO x composites on the surface of stainless steel also presented an effective catalytic activity towards the OER. Similarly, as shown in Fig. 8, the Tafel plot also showed an exponential change in overpotential with current density in a range from 250 to 360 mV. Kahnamouei et al. [144] formed iron/nickel oxyhydroxide dopped nanocones of nickel deposited on a SS substrate. The developed electrocatalyst had a low overpotential of 254 mV to achieve the benchmark current density of 10 mA cm␀2 whilst maintaining a high degree of stability. A N-doped car- bon framework decorated with NiFe particles was developed as a cata- lyst for the OER. It presented a low overpotential of 260 mV at 10 mA cm␀2. Further, it was proposed that the high catalyst stability and ac- tivity could be achieved by controlling the carbon layer thickness [145]. Coatings like silicon dioxide (SiO 2), titanium dioxide (TiO 2), zirco- nium dioxide (ZrO 2) and aluminium oxide hydroxide (AlOOH) have been applied on SS substrates, which presented 100–200 mV lower overpotential than bare SS for the HER [146]. This behavior was attributed to the propensity of adsorbed hydrogen on the surface of the oxide layers and its rapid conversion to H2. In another study to improve the stability of SS, NiS and NiS2 layers were introduced. Owing to the increased ECSA, a low overpotential of 258 mV and decrease in charge transfer resistance from 231.5 to 186.1 Ω for SS and sulphidized SS respectively to 11.1 Ω for the SS with NiS and NiS2 was exhibited by these catalytic surface layers towards HER. Table 3 summarizes some recent advances in the development of surface modified SS electro - catalysts, which had promising features in terms of HER and OER processes. HER on stainless steel 304, 316 and 430 electrodes in alkaline media (NaOH and KOH) was discussed using cyclic voltammetry analysis [152]. To observe the stability of the 316L toward the HER, the CV scans were obtained in 1.0 M KOH and NaOH solutions as shown in Fig. 9. The well-defined anodic and cathodic peaks centred at approximately ␀0.9 V vs. SCE correspond to the reversible conversion of Ni into α-Ni(OH) 2 and vice versa [153]. Before and after the HER, a slight difference in the current response was observed in the KOH solution. However, in the NaOH solution, a significantly large current response (at potential D ␀1300 mV) associated with the HER was observed. These authors sug- gested that metallic hydrides (NiH x) could form on the surface of SS during the HER process. This was not observed in the case of CV scans obtained in KOH solution because Ni in SS tends to adsorb hydrogen atoms in NaOH solutions. 3.2. Surface modification of stainless steel to improve catalytic performance 3.2.1. Surface oxidation processes The use of oxidized 304L as a water oxidation catalyst was reported by Schafer et al. [154]. The 304L was oxidized by Cl2 for exposure times varying from 50 min to 300 min. It was seen that CV curves shifted to lower potential as the chloride treatment was increased. The oxidation could have contributed to an increase in the surface area, which resulted in higher values of current because a notable chemical change was inevitable after the activation of the surface due to the treatment. It was also seen that overpotential had an inverse relation with the surface treatment time. This also resulted in perfect catalytic stability in the neutral regime for tens of hours of operation, as obvious from the stable OER currents they observed [155]. Various other studies have also shown high electrocatalytic activity for stainless steel as an electrocatalyst [23,154,156], especially focusing on oxidation. Tang et al. [157], and Zhong et al. [142] proved that surface modification methods like etching and oxidation produce morphological and compositional changes on the surface of SS that in- crease its water splitting performance. For instance, Balogun et al. [158] demonstrated the use of n- and p-doping in the etched 304 for improved water splitting in an alkaline electrolyte. Nitrogen and phosphorous atoms were doped on the surface through annealing in a controlled environment of NH3 and PH3. This environment was created by decomposing NH4H2PO2⋅H2O at a temperature of 400 •C. A bifunctional electrocatalyst produced by phosphorous doping on Co–Fe–B yielded the target current density of 10 mA cm␀2 at low overpotentials of 225 mV and 173 mV for OER and HER, respectively. It was further determined that a voltage of approximately 1.68 V was required to operate the cell by using this bifunctional electrocatalyst [159]. Stainless steel mesh (SSM) has been used as a bifunctional catalyst through extensive surface modification, as shown in Fig. 8 [78]. SSM was subjected to chemical etching in a bath of 2 M HCl. This resulted in highlighting the active centers on the surface of the material. SSM was anodized to be used as an anode (SSM-A) and then reduced at a high temperature to work as a cathode (SSM-AR). To make it capable of being used as an OER catalyst, anodizing at 5 V for 5 min was carried out in 7.5 M NaOH solution. For its use as an electrocatalyst, the anodized SSM was annealed at 400 •C for 1 h. This process is illustrated graphically in Fig. 10. As shown in Fig. 11, XPS analysis revealed that the chromium signal disappeared in the anodized SSM-A. Similarly, the oxidation of nickel to the following forms i.e. Ni(II)(OH) 2 or Ni(III)OOH was seen. This was believed to be a result of partial Cr leaching and migration of nickel to the surface under the given anodic conditions. This validated Wang et al.’s [160] hypothesis that nickel and iron layered hydroxides were the active chemical sites for the OER and slightly reduced iron and nickel oxides were the active sites for the HER. The formation of RuO 2–FeOOH nanosheets on an iron substrate has also been discussed [161]. The improved hydrophilicity of the surface ensured perfect interaction of the electrolyte with the surface and resulted in spontaneous release of H2 gas. The nanosheets required a low overpotential of 30 mV to achieve the current density of 10 mA cm␀2 in 1 M KOH, whereas an overvoltage of 230 mV was required by these nanosheets on the iron substrate. An OER catalyst was synthesized by Lee et al. [162] through Fig. 8.LSV of SS obtained in 1 M KOH [23].  A. Raza et al.
