Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 10electro-oxidation of a 304 foil, where the current density was varied between 1 and 6 A cm␀2 for 2 h in 7 M KOH at a pH of 14–15. Electro-oxidation resulted in the formation of many cratered structures in the nickel deposits. In Tafel plots, both samples had the same trend albeit in a lower current regime, but the cratered stainless steel (CSS) showed a higher Tafel slope of 340 mV/dec as compared to that of bare 304 (185 mV/dec). The sample subjected to oxidation at 6 A cm␀2 for 2 h provided the highest OER activity, whereby the lowest overpotential of 504 mV was achieved at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 in 0.5 M bicarbonate solution saturated with CO2. The overpotential remained stable for ~18 h in the bicarbonate solution but increased to η ~700 mV in the phosphate-containing electrolyte. This showed that the se- lection of anions (bicarbonate or phosphate) influenced the stability of the CSS catalyst [162]. The presence of a Ni-rich environment was suspected to be the major cause of the formation of a cratered structure. To elucidate this surface modification in a strongly oxidizing medium, the “dissolution-precipitation ” mechanism [154] is schematically shown in Fig. 12. In this mechanism, the Cr, Ni, and Fe species present on the surface of SS are dissolved during the oxidation process in strong alka- line media. However, the Ni species are re-precipitated due to their low solubility [163]. Raman spectroscopy was also performed to examine the active spe- cies present on the surface of cratered stainless steel (CSS) (Fig. 13). The characteristic bands that are attributed to Ni–O vibrations were ana- lysed. In the case of the CSS sample, two characteristic bands at 555 and 700 cm␀1 were observed. In general, α-Ni(OH) 2 or β- Ni(OH) 2 exhibit Table 3 Kinetics of OER and HER on the surface of stainless steel based electrocatalysts. Material Electrolyte Overpotential (mV) Potential for Water Splitting