Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 11Ni–O vibrational bands in the range of 445–465 cm␀1. It has been noted that the Ni–O vibration mode indicates water oxidation. The high- intensity band at the 555 cm␀1 corresponded to the NiOOH phase in which the Fe ions were incorporated with the Ni, and showed the high OER activity [162]. The stability of the electrode as an OER catalyst was assessed at a potential of 0.8 V vs Hg–HgO in 1 M KOH solution [163]. All electrochemical tests were conducted in a solution of 5 M LiOH to replicate the conditions in a Li-air battery. The materials were found to be stable over 3000 h of usage. The surface film contributing to the stable behavior was composed of 83 at. % nickel oxides, 10 at. % iron oxides and 7 at. % chromium oxides and was due to the dissolution and precipitation at the anodic sites. It was also seen that the electrode was consumed by a maximum of 315 nm. The oxide film was passivating, which was indicative of its stable behavior and strong performance as a catalyst. This was considered by the authors to be a benefit compared to catalysts with deposited films, in which damage to the layer leads to irreversible loss in catalytic ability. Raman spectroscopy was used to study phases associated with Ni-based compounds as shown in Fig. 14. The spectra were compared to that obtained from a film formed on the surface of pure nickel. The peaks observed at the same wavelength demonstrate the presence of similar major compounds at the surface of both electrodes. The wavelengths ν1 460 cm␀1 and ν2 525 cm␀1 were found to be associated with α-Ni(OH) 2 s as per Deabate et al. [164]. Fig. 15 shows the changes in oxidation peak at 0.45 V vs. Hg/HgO/1 M KOH for 316L exposed to 5 M LiOH [163]. This peak was attributed to the change from Ni(OH) 2 to NiOOH. The negative shift of the peak was related to the formation of γ-NiOOH/ α-Ni(OH) 2 in the layer [165]. Initially thin, the growth of this layer was suspected to reduce the dehydration of Ni-hydroxides. This could alternatively favour the in- crease of γ-NiOOH/ α-Ni(OH) 2 contents in the surface film compared to β-NiOOH/ β-Ni(OH) 2. It was said that a synergic effect known as the hypo hyper d inter bonding effect [166] was responsible, whereby cat- ions could only get into contact with their closest neighbours due to the Fig. 11.XPS Spectra of SSM-316L, SSM-A and SSM-AR showing the (a) Ni2p, (b) Fe2p, and (c) O1s spectral lines [78]. Fig. 12.Cratered structure on SS surface: (a) Oxygen evolution on SS surface at low current, (b) ‘dissolution-precipitation ’ process at high current, (c) formation of cratered structure [162]. Fig. 13.Raman spectra of the different samples showing various band intensity ratios [162]. A. Raza et al.
