Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 12dispersion of Fe and Cr. The second reason was attributed to the change in electrochemical surface area due to a change in roughness [163]. Fig. 15 shows a rise in the intensity of the electrochemical peaks. Since this intensity was dependent on the quantity of NiII or NiIII con- tained in the catalytic layer, this change provided an accurate descrip - tion of the growth of the layer. Ni oxides were not found in the layer but the analysis of the oxygen content in the film, however, showed an α-Ni (OH) 2 phase. According to Tarfela et al. [167], however, the Ni (OH) 2/NiOOH-based catalysts are unstable on a long-term basis [168]. 3.2.2. Phosphodization, nitridation & sulphidization processes Many researchers have applied surface modification methods to not only increase the surface activity of bifunctional electrodes but also avoid the problem of catalyst flaking off of the substrate surface [169]. A simple method of phosphodization using N2 gas at 400 •C was used to improve the electrocatalytic ability of stainless steel mesh (SSM) by Gao et al. [170]. An alkaline electrolyzer utilizing an SSMNi-P (SS Mesh phosphodized) cathode and SSM-Ni (SS Mesh phosphorous free) anode was made that presented an 10 mA cm␀2 current density at a potential of 1.62 V. Fig. 16a and b displays the SEM images of pristine SSM and SSM-Ni, respectively. Compared to the elemental mapping of SSM, the elemental mapping of SSM-Ni showed that the voids inherently found in SSM fibres were eventually filled with Ni as shown in Fig. 16b. The structure of the SSM-Ni-P shows that the voids were filled with Ni and P atoms, as shown by the blue regions in Fig. 16c. Phosphodization was confirmed by the mapping spectrum in Fig. 16d. Elemental mapping in Fig. 16e␀j also confirmed that the voids were filled with Ni and P. Ni was due to treatment in 0.5 M NaNO 3 and 2 M NiCl 2⋅6H2O for 120 s. SSM-Ni proved to be the best electrocatalysts compared to SSM-Ni-P and SSM due to the generation of active sites during chemical bath deposi - tion (CBD). Furthermore, the Tafel slope of SSM-Ni was the smallest (42 mV dec␀1), while that of SSM (49 mV dec␀1) was even smaller than that of SSM-Ni-P (43 mV dec␀1), which showed that phosphodization did not significantly aid in the electrocatalysis. A recorded Nyquist plot showed that the charge transfer resistances in both SSM-Ni-P and SSM were nearly the same as shown in Fig. 17, which provided further evidence for the insignificant effect of P. Xing et al. [171] however found that by playing with the phos- phodization time, the electrocatalytic behavior could be augmented. Ni–P nanorods with 2 h of phosphodization displayed an overpotential of 270 mV to achieve a current density of 30 mA/cm2 for the OER and an overpotential of 158 mV to deliver a current density of 10 mA/cm2 for the HER. By comparing the three catalysts, it was found that SSM-Ni-P ach- ieved 10 mA cm␀2 current density at an overpotential of 149 mV, which had the best HER performance compared to SSM-Ni and SSM that pre- sented 346 and 402 mV overpotentials, respectively. Polarization scans and EIS were carried out to assess the reaction rates. Fig. 18 shows the HER Tafel plots of SSM, SSM-Ni, and SSM-Ni-P electrocatalysts. The Tafel slope of SSM-Ni-P was 80 mV dec␀1, which was significantly smaller than those of SSM (136 mV dec␀1) and SSM-Ni (145 mV dec␀1). Moreover, the relatively lower charge transfer resistance (Rct) of the SSM-Ni-P compared to SSM-Ni and SSM further confirmed its improved kinetic response. Sulfidization, phosphodization and nitridation treatment of stainless steel 316 foil (SSF) have also been studied in detail [172]. The formation of a sulphide layer resulted in the improvement of the electrocatalytic activity. An overpotential of η 136 mV for HER and η 262 mV for OER was needed to achieve a current density of j 10 mA/cm2. The sulphide layer was developed by 3 g of sulphur upstream of the SSF in a tube furnace. The heating range was between 400 and 600 •C for 1 h. In a two-electrode configuration, the sulfidized stainless steel foil SSFS catalyst supported 10 mA cm␀2 at 1.64 V, which was ~220 mV lower than that of unmodified 316, because of the micro-spherical morphology of the sulfidized surfaces. The elemental mapping showed that the mi- crospheres were comprised of iron and nickel sulphide both of which have been reported to show good electrocatalytic activity. The equiva - lent circuit model of the EIS spectra of SSFS and SSF showed charge transfer resistances of 0.00027 Ω for SSFS and 3.43 Ω for SSF, demon - strating the high OER performance of the former. A comparison of the electrochemical parameters obtained from the fitting of impedance spectra is given in Table 4. The electrocatalytic behavior of SS electrodeposition with nickel sulphides (NiS) has also been examined (Fig. 19) [96]. SS was used as an anode with Pt mesh as an electrode in a NiSO 4 bath. This resulted in the formation of NiS and NiS2 on the surface. Afterwards the sample was sulphidized in low vacuum and at 300 •C in the presence of sulphur powder particles. Afterwards, the samples were used as HER electro - catalysts and it was found that the reaction mechanism for the HER followed the Volmer-Heyrovsky route. The first step involved the adsorption of hydrogen on the surface. This was followed by the Heyr- ovsky step which yielded H2 on the electrocatalyst. The methodology resulted in promising results with an overpotential as low as 258 mV, which was smaller than both SS (457 mV) as well as sulphidized SS (494 mV). 3.2.3. Hydrothermal processing of stainless steel It has been speculated that a decrease or complete elimination of the Cr content from stainless steel leads to an increased OER activity. On the other hand, stainless steels with Cr are considered vital for large-scale electrolysis because the formation of Cr oxides ensures high corrosion resistance. Anantharaj et al. [173] corroded SS-304 hydrothermally in a mixture with an equal concentration of KOH and NaOCl at 180 •C to enable Cr to diffuse out of the surface. The effect of Cr on the deterio - ration of surface activity was evaluated by XPS analysis. High-resolution Fig. 14.Raman spectra of a 316L stainless steel surface and pure Ni electrode after 250 h of operation (5 M LiOH; 0.8 V vs. Hg/HgO - 1 M KOH) [163]. Fig. 15.Increase of the OER catalytic activity of a 316L electrode in 5 M LiOH during the first 144 h [163]. A. Raza et al.
