Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 143.3. 3D structures on stainless steel Self-supported catalysts have also been designed by merging active species into 3D structures such as nickel foams and stainless steel meshes, which can enhance electro-activity by providing high catalyst adhesion, easy mass transfer and a good electrode contact [174]. 3.3.1. Ni/Fe layered double hydroxides (LDH) The hydrogen and oxygen escape rate during water electrolysis was studied from 3D non-woven stainless steel (NWSS) [175]. In this study, a suspension of water, cellulose and stainless steel fabric (SSF) was filtered and a paper-like intermediate was formed by pressing and drying at 110 •C. This intermediate was heat-treated at 1050 •C which evaporated the cellulose and resulted in the NWSS fabrics (NWSSF). Gas escape rate was found to be directly dependent on the pore size and fibre diameter. It was reported that the hydrophilicity of the surface should aid in the escape of small bubbles. Structures like nano-needles, nano-flakes, or nano-cones were suggested [176]. The small bubbles after growing large were reported to get stuck in the pores [177]. It was concluded that the longer the residence time of gas bubbles in the porous structure, the poorer the catalytic performance [175]. An optimized structure was made by growing flakes of iron nickel- layered double hydroxide on the surface of NWSSF. The electro - catalytic activity of FeNi LDH@NWSSF towards OER was investigated in 1 M KOH. It was seen that FeNi LDH@NWSSF required a lower over- potential of 210 mV and 110 mV for the OER and HER, respectively, to achieve the current density of 10 mA/cm2 compared to two alternative electrodes, which had been calcined. An oxidation peak at 1.43 V Fig. 18.(a-I) HER and (a-II) OER LSV curves of SSM, SSM-Ni, and SSM-Ni-P. (b) HER and (c) OER Tafel plots of SSM, SSM-Ni, and SSM-Ni-P [170]. Table 4 Comparison of Electrocatalytic Properties of SSF and SSFS obtained from th