to stem from the refined microstructural features which were linked to the additive manufacturing process. A large number of grain boundaries not only assisted in activating the reactants but also pro- vided a higher degree of active sites. This feature too was related to the manufacturing process. A cellular structure originating from the fast solidification in the additive manufacturing process was observed to develop with an average size of about 800 nm as shown in Fig. 22c. The geometry of this structure was dependent on the laser power and scan speed of the AM process [182,183]. It was also seen that the high dis- locations due to thermal stresses corresponded to stored energy which in turn increased the number of active areas. The layout of the research is illustrated in Fig. 23. As explained above, AM 316 samples were used as base material and subjected to chemical etching in a solution of 1:2:2 ratio of HCl, HNO 3, CH3COOH and 3 drops of C3H8O3 to expose and increase the active sites i.e. the grain boundaries in the structure. Furthermore, the cellular structure also served as points for selective etching. After etching, the material was used as an anode in the electrochemical measurements in 1 M KOH solution with graphite rod and HG-HgO as counter and reference elec- trodes, respectively. The activity of the additively manufactured – electrochemically treated (AM-T) 316L stainless steel was investigated in 1 M KOH solu- tion. Fig. 24a shows the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves of the Fig. 20.High-resolution Cr 2p3/2 spectra of (a) bare SS and (b) SS-12, respectively [173]. Fig. 21.(a) Polarization curves (b) Tafel plots of FeNi LDH@NWSSF, FeNi LDH@NF and FeNi LDH@SSF for OER at a scan rate of 5 mV/s [175].  A. Raza et al.
