Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 183.3.4. 3D nanoparticles on stainless steel Co3O4 electrocatalyst films were also successfully fabricated by Jeon et al. [186] on SS by a paste coating method in which they used Nafion as a binder. The resulting Co3O4 films were not efficient but stable as an electrocatalyst. Generally, such oxide layers are prepared at temperatures around 500 •C but due to the damage incurred by the surface layer at these elevated temperatures, low-temperature methods are preferred. The electrocatalytic response of the surface films produced at different temperatures, i.e. 50, 150 and 300 •C, was investigated. The micro - structure of the films heat-treated at different temperatures is shown in Fig. 27. The morphology of the nanoparticles was not affected by heat treatment temperature up to 300 •C due to the stability of the Nafion matrix as shown in Fig. 26 b and c. A significant change in the morphology of nanoparticles was observed after heat treatment at 500 •C suggesting the decomposition of the Nafion matrix, as high- lighted in Fig. 27d. As seen in Fig. 28, the Co3O4 film produced at 50 •C had high OER and HER rates. However, the increase in heat treatment temperature adversely affected the H2 evolution rate on the surface of the Co3O4 film. It was further noticed that the decreased rate of H2 evolution was not caused by either deactivation of the Co3O4 film anode or degradation of the stainless steel substrate at high heat treatment temperatures, but rather was associated with the physical detachment of the nanoparticles from the surface due to the degradation of Nafion at high temperature. Ni(OH) 2 nanoparticles have been prepared on SS nanoparticles and deposited on a 3D Ni foam substrate [187]. The X-ray diffraction (XRD) data showed an absence of Ni(OH) 2 peaks, which were attributed to the small size and disordered nature of Ni(OH) 2 particles on the stainless steel nanoparticles (SSNP). Fig. 29a shows the CV of a-Ni(OH) 2 deposit made on stainless steel (SS) and nickel foil substrates in which conver - sion to NiOOH can be deduced from the anodic peak. However, the anodic peak and cathodic peak of Ni(OH) 2 deposited on SS shifted to positive values by 70 and 45 mV, respectively. This anodic shift could be because of Fe-doping of the Ni(OH) 2 which has a detrimental effect on the OER capability [188,189]. It can be concluded that the formation of Ni(OH) 2 particles and their subsequent oxidation increase the OER ki- netics, a fact that is evident from the steep increase in current density at ~1.45 V vs. RHE [187]. The smaller size of the first semi-circle in the Nyquist plots in Fig. 29b is a clear indication of a low charge transfer resistance between the deposit and the substrate whilst the smaller diameter of the second semi- circle indicates the improvement in the kinetics of the OER. Fig. 30 shows that the SSNP/Ni(OH) 2 was better than electrophoretically deposited bare SSNP as well as electrodeposited bare Ni(OH) 2 whereby deposition was made under similar conditions at 200 V for 2 min. The resulting surface only needed an overpotential of 255 mV for a current density of 10 mA cm␀2. The formation of nanostructures of the Ni(OH) 2 surface could have contributed to increasing the ESCA. The double-layer capacitance suggested similar values for pure Ni(OH) 2 and the SSNP/Ni (OH) 2 hybrid where ECSA is typically calculated as the ratio of Cdl and specific capacitance (Cs) i.e. capacitance of an atomically smooth layer of the material [187] indicating a direct relation between Cdl and ESCA. Fig. 27.SEM images of Co3O4 films produced at (a) 50, (b) 150, (c) 300, and (d) 500 •C [186]. Fig. 28.The hydrogen production rate on the Co3O4 films and SS 316L stain- less steel substrates prepared at 50, 150, and 300 •C [186]. A. Raza et al.
