hat such nanostructures on the surface not only increase the ECSA but also provide additional latching points for microbes. This fact was validated by Guo et al. [198] and Herbert et al. [199] who discussed the effect of electrode surface modification on microorganism-electrode interaction and abiotic sulfide oxidation rates and mechanisms in mine tailings respectively. Polarization curves for the three different cathode materials (HSSF, SSF and GF) were also conducted before inoculation and after the operation at the cathode potential of ␀0.8 V. The three materials were found to be distinctly different in their HER trends as shown in Fig. 34a. HSSF had a lower onset potential of ␀0.6 V for the HER as compared to that of SSF i.e. ␀0.8 V. The heat treatment boosted the SSF performance in HER as well as methane production at more negative potentials of ␀1.1 and ␀1,3 V. After biofilm growth, the polarization trends of all three materials were largely overlapping as shown in Fig. 34b. The onset potential for all the cathode materials was around ␀0.8 V. There was no difference in the cathodic current of HSSF before and after inoculation. However, after inoculation, the cathodic current of GF and SSF became almost similar to that of HSSF, which meant that the biofilm acted as an effective electrocatalyst for hydrogen or methane production. 4.Overpotential measurements The overpotential is a critical parameter to scrutinize the performace of a catalyst. As already mentioned in the preceding sections, the po- tential required for water spliiting is 1.23 V at 298 K. Due to barriers imposed by activation energy however, a higher potential termed as overpotential is normally required for the reactions to proceed [200]. Zeng et al. [201] highlight the importance of overpotential by linking it to the energy efficiency. Overpotential and efficiency have been shown to have an inverse relation with a higher overpotential indicating a less efficient OER or HER. Since overpotential in linked with the activity, stability of the elctrocatalsyt as well as the efficiency of the reactions, Niu et al. [202] stress on the proper documentation of the overpotential values. Keeping in view the discussion above, we can conclude that amongst other parameters (current density, Tafel slop, turnover fre- quency, onset potential, and stability), overpotential is one of the pri- mary parameters that determine whether a material is an electrocatalyst, the corresponding values of other important factors from previous literature are summarized in Table 5. According to Klug et al. [203], the inverse relation between current density and overpotential is not an appropriate criteria in the selection of electrocatalysts. In heterogeneous electrocatalysis, the dependence of reaction rate on binding energies of the formed intermediates was a good indicator for predicting the catalytic performance of various ma- terials as discussed in section 2.3. It was further explained that the synergistic association between turnover frequency (TOF) and over- potential was dependent on certain parameters, for example electrolyte Fig. 31.A generic schematic for the understanding of a bifunctional electrocatalyst. A. Raza et al.
