Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 23influence on the pitting and re-passivation potentials of stainless steel [206]. Based on potentiodynamic polarization test results, Lothongkum et al. [207] utilized the potential-pH diagram for 316L stainless steel that was exposed to air saturated aqueous media containing 0 and 5000 ppm of Cl␀. The solutions had oxygen concentrations in the range of 7.8–8.5 ppm. At each pH (between 2 and 12), the potential regimes within Ecorr and primary passivation potential (Epp), Epp and (protection potential) Epr, Epr and transpassive potential (Et) and above Et were defined as general corrosion, perfect passivation, imperfect passivation, and pitting corrosion regions, respectively. Thermodynamically, the HER and OER are expected at potentials beyond the H2O stability region. However, the progress of these reactions on an electrode surface is kinetically controlled by many factors as discussed above. 6.Current challenges and proposed solutions i. The reaction chemistry dictates that to be considered efficient in hydrogen production, an electrocatalyst has to overcome the activation energy. It is, however, difficult to quantify the acti- vation energy barrier as it has a direct dependence on the surface properties of the electrode material. Using an efficient electro - catalyst can lower the activation energy and may significantly increase the reaction rate. The rate-determining step (RDS) is directly related to the Gibbs free-energy change (ΔGmax) and is important to understand the reaction sequence. As per our knowledge, no study describes the calculation of ΔGi for an RDS over a stainless steel electrode. Rigorous analysis of the free en- ergy calculation of the intermediate electron/proton transfer processes occurring on the single-atom transition metals i.e., Fe, Cr and Ni has been conducted. But there still exists a gap in the available information about reaction sequence and identification of the RDS over SS. In other words, quantitative information of the ΔGi of the associated intermediate steps and overall activa - tion energy of HER and OER over the SS surface is not available. For this reason, detailed density functional theory (DFT) calcu - lations for the estimation of ΔGi, RDS and overall activation en- ergy of the HER and OER processes must be carried out for SS surfaces. ii.The HER mechanism in an alkaline environment must be eluci- dated through DFT calculations which can better inform how different SS surface conditions relate to the HER catalytic response. Electrochemical, compositional testing and micro - structural analysis is necessary for validating such studies. iii. In the alkaline medium, the active sites are blocked by the OH␀ ions that lower the catalytic activity and increase the over- potential. Produced O2 blocks the reaction interface and hinders the OH␀adsorption and electron transport in the electric double layer (EDL). This problem could be solved by exploiting the surface chemistry of the materials and making use of porous structures and the specific shape and geometry of the pores, which could prove to be helpful in the release of the gas mole- cules, thereby evading any entrapment and blockage. iv. The combination of two catalyst materials has been proposed to improve the slow reaction rate of HER in an alkaline environ - ment. For instance, the surface treatment of Pt by Ni(OH) 2 and/or by adding Mn3O4 in/on a Ni-based catalyst could initially disso - ciate water to generate hydrogen intermediates, followed by adsorption and recombination to generate H2 gas at the active sites. v. In addition to the exploration of new electrocatalyst materials, research should also incorporate the in-depth study of the reac- tion mechanism of the HER/OER processes that occur on the surface of already existing hetero-structured multicomponent electrocatalysts. vi. Adhesion of certain intermediate species i.e. OOH (ads) or OH␀ ions on the surface of various catalysts could block the active sites, thereby hindering the OER and imposing a challenge to the overall kinetics of water dissociation. To better understand the adsorption process and surface activity, DFT calculations and molecular dynamic simulations could be beneficial to explore effectiveness of stainless steels as electrocatalytic materials. Fig. 35.Plot of TOF and microviscosity in 0%, 25%, 50% and 80% butanedi - nitrile in acetonitrile [203]. Fig. 36.Formation of a catalytic wave for an electrocatalyst [204]. Fig. 37.Polarization curves of Co2P@CP before and after 2000 cycles in acidic and alkaline media [205]. A. Raza et al.
