Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 24vii. The operational stability of the electrocatalytic materials including the support are of prime importance in addition to their intrinsic catalytic performance. More research on the use of additively manufactured SS and their stability in the highly oxidizing/reducing conditions in both acidic and alkaline media must be conducted to evaluate their service longevity. Tech- niques that explain the use of activity/stability correlation to evaluate the structure decomposition/amorphization process, may be employed. viii. Increasing the surface area of the catalyst is mostly considered a viable solution to increasing the efficiency. Modification of microstructural features, surface texturing via electron or laser beams, implantation of various ions on the surface and genera - tion of porous stainless steel structures could further extend their electrocatalytic performance and may be investigated in the future. 7.Concluding remarks The current trends and recent progress in the SS-based electro - catalysts for the HER and OER are summarized in this review. At present, the increased interest in the development of low-cost and earth- abundant elements containing electrocatalysts demands further work and in-depth characterization. Also, to improve the catalytic activity of SS-based materials, the possible routes are identified as (1) surface treatment i.e. phosphodization, nitridation, chemical etching, sulphid - ization (2) sensitization of active sites via nanostructuring i.e., creation of 0 to 3D nanoporous architectures (3) using the different materials i.e. graphene, Ni foam, and carbonaceous materials doped/decorated SS and (4), efficient use of bifunctional surfaces to make the electro - catalytic process as economical as possible. Although several strong electrocatalysts have been developed for the HER as well as the OER reaction, there remain several areas that need scientific exploration. A summary of the issues being faced and the probable solutions is presented in Fig. 38. The deposition of catalyst material on easily accessible and economical materials is of supreme importance. The presence of com- posite materials on stainless steel was also found to play its part in the oxygen evolution reaction. Surface modifications in form of chemical treatment, as well as surface oxidation, were also studied. Phosphori - zation, nitridation as well as sulphidization of surfaces has also been studied. These processes, despite their simplicity, impart favourable properties to the surface. In all cases discussed above, the overpotential i.e. the defining character of an electrocatalyst, was found to be lower compared to the untreated surfaces. Based on these results, the surface modifications mentioned above can increase the ECSA and enhance the availability of active species. The catalytic materials should be explored at an atomistic scale. For instance, distribution of charge, the possible reaction routes, identifi - cation of intermediate species and energetics of both the HER and OER must be investigated in detail. It has occasionally been noted that the conclusive decision between the HER mechanism i.e. Volmer-Heyrovsky and/or Volmer-Tafel steps have not been made. To predict and explain the catalytic performance of the catalytic material, the incorporation of DFT and molecular dynamic simulation studies could be beneficial to support the experimental observations. In almost all research, overpotential at 10 mA/cm2 and the η are considered the yardstick. However, other factors like turnover fre- quency, selectivity, activities and onset potential are rarely reported but these are necessary to validate the performance of a catalyst. The actual reaction mechanism for OER and HER has been reported by very few researchers. It has also been seen that almost none of the researchers have related the HER and OER reactions to the Pourbaix diagram of stainless steel. Different factors govern the calculation of reaction energies. Reactions can vary based on whether the surface is clean metal or there are pre- adsorbed species. Another thing that needs more inclusion in mainstream research in Fig. 38.Current problems in the development of low cost and efficient electrocatalysts & their probable solutions.  A. Raza et al.
