Renewable and Sustainable Energy Reviews 161 (2022) 112323 25water splitting is the volcano plot. The catalytic volcano plots demand appropriate interaction between the catalyst surface and the in- termediates formed. These intermediates should have an optimal bind- ing with the surface. The normal binding free energy of intermediates is used for the x-axis and current density on the y-axis. It shows the theoretical overpotential required to make the reaction run optimally. Not much research can be seen utilizing the volcano plots to their fullest. 8.Summary & future outlook Through this review, the superiority of SS over other commonly used catalysts has also been established. Although there are other materials like nickel foam, copper foam or carbon paper with good conductivity and high surface area, SS gains an even bigger advantage over these materials as well, primarily because of its ubiquitous availability, the possibility of usage in different shape and form i.e. sheet, foam, nano- structures and low price. Despite the inherent lack of sites on bare SS, the material can still be used in a wide range of electrocatalytic systems after minor surface treatments. A simple example of this statement can be given by the formation of an ultra thin layer of nickle oxide on the surface of SS-304 which yields appreciable electrocatalytic properties. Furthermore, SS by nature has high ratio of transition metals like Ni, Cr in its bulk, from which Ni in particular can help in self healing of the catalyst [208]. All of these factors make SS a suitable candidate for electrocatalytic water splitting. Furthermore, as discussed above, SS is superior to other materials in terms of price as well, which is one of the major factors in deciding whether or not a technique is feasible. Due to low price of the material, as well as processing and treatment, SS is the ideal candidate for the said discussion. Future prospects, include deeper insights into the improvement of electroconductivity of the electrocatalyst, increase of redox centers in addition to high corrosion resistance at high operating potentials. It is suggested that for accelerated charge transfer for HER and OER on the surface of a catalytic active materials, the improved electronic conduc - tivity of the metallic support and intermediate layers must be ensured. Furthermore, investigation of AM cellular structures as well as devel - opment of porous structure vis AM could be carried out further to probe the use of the surface features for electrocatalysis by effectively increasing the ECSA. Moreover, surface texturing and roughening through laser ablation as well as other high energy beam processes like electron beam melting and laser beam melting themselves can be used to introduce features and explore the effect of surface roughness on the electrocatalytic behavior of the SS. Different approaches ranging from doping an element with higher conductivity to a complete or partial morphological change can be employed. It has been noticed that during the OER process under oxidizing conditions i.e., positive applied po- tentials, the electrocatalysts tend to dismantle or leach out from the substrate. This necessitates the development of highly stable electro - catalytic materials that could withstand highly aggressive operating conditions. Incorporation of other noble elements into the electro - catalyst, formation of composites with graphene and CNTs as well as introduction of a buffer layer between the electrocatalyst and substrate have also been known to improve catalyst durability. This technique could also be utilized in the usage of SS as an electrocatalyst. Through alloying addition of different elements in SS, the composition and structure of the material can be manipulated and their effect on the electrocatalytic behavior can be investigated. Finally, from a design perspective, in situ assessment of the reaction mechanism through techniques like scanning probe microscopy (SPM) and XPS has been suggested. Finally, through this review, it is shown that several SS based HER electrocatalysts have great potential for their use as OER or even oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) catalysts. These multifunctionality of SSnot only reduces the cost but also clarify numerous complexities assicated with the conventional catalysts, i.e., durability, activity and above all the cost-effectiveness. Other areas in which further research on the bifunctionality of the catalysts are their improved stability in water having wide variartions in pH, must have simple and economical preparation procedure and should present high current density at low overpotentials. Hence, it is suggested that rigorous research must be conducted to develop efficient SS based bifunctional electrocatalysts. Credit author statement Ali Raza: Writing – original draft, Visualization. Kashif Mairaj Deen: Writing – review & editing, Visualization, Supervision. Edouard Asselin: Writing – review & editing Waseem Haider: Writing – review & editing, Project administration, Conceptualization, Resources.. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgements This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. References [1]Lewis JI, Chai Q, Zhang X. Chapter 17 - low-carbon electricity development in China: opportunities and challenges. In: Sioshansi FP, editor. Generating electricity in a carbon-constrained world. Boston: Academic Press; 2010. p. 473–500. [2]Zohuri B. 2 - nuclear fuel cycle and decommissioning. In: Khan SU-D, Nakhabov A, editors. Nuclear reactor technology development and utilization. Woodhead Publishing; 2020. p. 61–120. [3]Nikolaidis P, Poullikkas A. A comparative overview of hydrogen production processes. Renew Sustain Energy Rev 2017;67:59