ChemistrySelectResearch Article doi.org/10.1002/slct.202405005 www.chemistryselect.org ASelf-SupportedElectrodefortheAlkalineHERBasedon ElectrodepositionofPlatinumonMetalFiberFelt BiyingRen,*[a]HuanZhang,[a]XiangkunJia,[a]JianPingGao,[a]JunXie,[a]andXikuiWang[a] Electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution is an important topic in the preparation of low-carbon green hydrogen energy sources. This paper reports an efficient electrocatalytic electrode using platinum on a stainless steel fiber felt (SSF) substrate by the electrodeposition method. The surface of SSF is loaded with electrodeposited Pt nanoparticles, which enhance the electro- chemical properties of SSF. The overpotential η10 of the synthe-sizedelectrodeforhydrogenevolutionreaction(HER)in1MKOH is just 21 mV, indicating that it has excellent electrocatalytic per- formance. Long-time durability tests showed good stability of the synthesized electrode. All these excellent properties indicate that the prepared self-supported electrodes are promising for a new direction in industrial water electrolysis. 1.Introduction Withthedevelopmentofindustry,theenergyproblemisbecom- ing more and more serious. As a sustainable, nonpolluting, and clean energy source with high energy density, hydrogen has been recognized as a promising and competitive green source.[1–4]The hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) of electrolytic water is one of the most efficient methods to obtain carbon- neutral green hydrogen.[4–6]Current research on hydrogen pro- duction from electrolyzed water focuses on electrocatalytic hydrogen production in alkaline environments.[7,8]However, lower efficiency and higher costs are still the main reasons for the limitations of this technology in practical applications.[9] For the present, reducing the overpotential of the elec- trode contributes greatly to the improvement of the efficiency of hydrogen production during the electrolysis of water. Effi- cient electrocatalysts are essential for accelerating the reactions throughout the water separation process.[7,10–12]Currently, noble metal-basedelectrocatalysts(suchasplatinum)arecharacterized by high current density, small Tafel slope, and low overpotential, therefore, they are used as excellent HER electrocatalysts.[13,14] 316L stainless steel is an excellent metal material with high strength, high mechanical properties, high electrical con- ductivity, corrosion resistance, and low cost.[15–17]Nickel, iron, chromium, and other elements in stainless steel can provide more active sites for the HER reaction, so stainless steel is an excellent substrate for alkaline water separation, and as a sub- strate, it can make the electrode have better electrochemical activity.[7,18,19]Stainless steel fiber felt (SSF) has a complex inter- locking 3D pore structure.[20–22]It still maintains most of the [a]B. Ren, H. Zhang, X. Jia, J. Gao, J. Xie, X. Wang Western Metal Materials Co.Ltd., Xi’an, Shaanxi 710201, China E-mail:byebeing@163.com jszx@c-wmm.com Supporting information for this article is available on the WWW under https://doi.org/10.1002/slct.202405005good properties of stainless steel.[23–25]In addition, the three- dimensional structure of the SSF facilitates mass transfer in the electrolytic water reaction, so the metal fiber felt can be used as an electrode substrate in alkaline electrolytic water.[26] Electrodeposition is the electrochemical deposition of metal ions from solution onto a substrate material to form a metal or metal alloy with the advantage of producing precious metal nanomaterials with high surface area and high activity, and the operation is simple and green. Qian et al.[27]improved the hydrogen precipitation catalytic performance of composite catalysts by forming nanostructured noble metal particles (Ag, Au, and Pt) on 3D-structured nickel foam by electrodeposition. As an effective 3D hydrogen evolution cathode, the nanoscale Pt-particle-decorated 3D porous nickel electrode demonstrates the highest catalytic activity with an extremely low overpoten- tial of −0.045 V for hydrogen production. Naseemah et al.[28] successfully developed a highly active platinum nanoparticles- decorated stainless-steel mesh (Pt/SSM) electrocatalyst for the HER using electrodeposition. The Pt/SSM electrocatalyst demon- strated superior HER performance compared to bare SSM, with a remarkably low overpotential of 121 mV and a Tafel slope of 40 mV·dec−1in 0.5 M H 2SO4.J i a ne ta l .[29]presented a facile electrodeposition and annealing procedure for the fabrication of Ni-Mo-Cu catalysts for hydrogen evolution reaction. The Ni- Mo-Cu catalyst exhibited a reduced overpotential of 143 mV to generate a current density of 10 mA·cm−2in 1 M KOH at 25 °C. In this work, the electrode is prepared by electrodepositing Pt nanoparticles on a conductive substrate of SSF. The elec- trode exhibited prominent electrocatalytic performance for HER in alkaline electrolyte. This work provides more possibilities to obtain electrodes with good catalytic performance. 2.ExperimentalSection 2.1. Chemical Materials Potassium hydroxide (KOH), potassium chloride (KCl), sulfuric acid (H2SO4), and anhydrous ethanol (CH 3CH2OH) were purchased from ChemistrySelect 2025,10, e202405005 (1 of 6) © 2025 Wiley-VCH GmbH
