ChemistrySelectResearch Article doi.org/10.1002/slct.202405005 Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd., China. Chloroplatinic acid (H2PtCl6·6H2O) was obtained from Annagy. The 316L SSF (moBZ100D) and TC4 turning fibers were purchased from Xi’an Filter Metal Materials Co., Ltd., C paper (TGP-H-060) was purchased from Toray Company (Japan). 2.2. Materials Synthesis Electrodeposition was performed at 25 °C in a 150mL electrolytic bath. A metal fiber felt substrate (316L stainless steel fiber felt, TC4 turning fiber, and C paper, etc.) served as the working electrode, a platinum sheet served as the counter electrodes, and a saturated calomel electrode served as the reference electrode. The electrolytes were aqueous KCl solutions containing 0.01, 0.05, 0.1, and 0.15 mM Pt metal ions, respectively. All metal fiber felt substrates were cut into 1 cm ×2 cm samples and ultrasonically cleaned in 1M KOH and acetone for degreasing treatment. The working electrode with an explore area of 1 cm ×1 cm was immersed into the plating solution. Then the i-t curves were scanned at the reduction potential, and samples were electrodeposited for 60 min. The deposited samples were recorded as Pt-SSF-0.01, Pt-SSF-0.05, Pt-SSF-0.1, and Pt-SSF-0.15 depending on the concentration of the plating solution. 2.3. Characterizations The XRD analysis of as-synthesized electrode was carried out using Rigaku with Cu K αirradiation ( λ=1.5418 Å). SEM (ZEISS Supra 55) was used to characterize the morphology of as-synthesized products. Electrochemical testing was performed using a three-electrode systemwithaCHI660eelectrochemicalworkstation(ShanghaiChen- hua Instruments Co., Ltd.) in 1 M KOH, in which a platinum sheet electrode was used as the counter electrode, the prepared sample as the working electrode, and a saturated calomel electrode as the reference electrode. The LSV was measured at a scanning rate of 5 mV·s−1.T h e electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) was assessed from electrochemical double layer capacitance (Cdl). The Cdl of the sam- ples was obtained by testing the cyclic voltammetry curves of the faradaic region at different scan rates. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were performed over the test fre- quency range of 0.01–10,000 Hz, and the results were fitted using Nova 2.1. Chronic potentiometric durability tests were tested at 10 mA·cm−2f o r1 2h . 3.ResultsandDiscussion 3.1. Characterization Figure1a,bshows the SEM images of pristine SSF at different magnifications. It can be clearly seen that the SSF is a 3D struc- ture composed of fiber filaments with an average diameter of 24 μm. The 3D pore structure inside the SSF is favorable for mass transfer and charge transfer in the HER reaction.[26]As illustrated in Figure 1c,d, after 60 min of electrodeposition, the macroscopicmorphologyofthefiberfelthasnotchanged,main- taining the three-dimensional porous morphology of the SSF. The inset of Figure 1dshows uniform deposition of Pt nanopar- ticles on fiber filaments. Figure 1e,fdemonstrates the SEM test Figure 1. SEM images: (a, b) pristine SSF with different magnification; (c, d) Pt-SSF-0.1 with different magnification; and (e, f) Pt-SSF-0.1 at high resolution. of Pt-SSF-0.1 at higher resolution. The size and distribution of Pt particles on the surface of the stainless steel fiber filament can be clearly observed from the figure. As illustrated in Figure 1e,f, the fiber surface was covered by a uniform and dense layer of Pt nanoparticles, and the average particle size of the deposited Pt nanoparticles was measured to be about 35.8 nm, indicat- ing that the electrodeposition method can effectively prepare self-supported electrodes. FigureS1illustrates SEM images of the cross-section of the modified stainless steel fiber wires with different magnifications. As illustrated in Figure S1a, we can observe that the surface of stainless steel fiber wire of the typical sample pt-SSF-0.1 is covered by a uniform Pt coating. The cross-section dimensions of the modified stainless steel fiber wires were approximately 24.8 μm, which is consistent with the SEM test results in the arti- cle. In the enlarged Figure S1b, we can clearly observe that a layer of Pt plating uniformly covered the surface of the fiber fil- ament, and the thickness of the electrodeposited Pt plating is measured to be about 37.4 nm. Moreover, as shown in Figure 2a,b,as m a l la m o u n to fP tw a s foundintheEDSanalysisresultsofsamplePt-SSF-0.1,whichindi- cates that this experiment successfully loaded platinum on the surface of SSF. Figure3shows that no impurity phase was detected in the XRD test results of the sample and corresponds to the austenitic phase of stainless steel. Combined with the SEM and EDS test results, it shows that the electrodeposition method can success- fully prepare self-supported metal fiber felt electrodes.[26]It is worth mentioning that the characteristic diffraction peaks of Pt are not observed in the XRD patterns because the characteristic diffractionpeaksofPtareweakerthanthoseofstainlesssteel.[30] ChemistrySelect 2025,10, e202405005 (2 of 6) © 2025 Wiley-VCH GmbH 23656549, 2025, 7, Downloaded from https://chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/slct.202405005 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
