ChemistrySelectResearch Article doi.org/10.1002/slct.202405005 Figure 2. (a, b) EDS images of Pt-SSF-0.1. Figure 3. XRD patterns of samples with different loadings. Table 1. HER performance comparison table of catalysts with 3D sub- strates. Electrode Overpotential (mV)Tafel slope (mV·dec−1)Ref. Pt-SSF-0.1 21 67 This manuscript SSF@NiFe-120 100 120 [ 7] CuNPs/CNFs 61 152 [ 14] Pt/porous nickel 45 135.59 [ 27] Pt/SSM 121 40 [ 28] Ni-Mo-Cu 143 43 [ 29] CoP–FeP/CC 71 67 [ 32] 3.2. HER Activities The electrocatalytic HER performance of the SSF self-supported electrodes was tested at 25 °C 1 M KOH solution. Figure 4ashows their LSV curves on the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) scale. Pristine SSF has an overpotential of 489 mV ( η10). In sharp contrast, the overpotentials of the Pt-SSF-0.01, Pt-SSF-0.05, Pt- SSF-0.1, and Pt-SSF-0.15 are 302, 31, 21, and 152 mV, respectively. It can be clearly seen that Pt-SSF-0.1 has excellent electrocat- alytic performance, showing better HER performance than many currently reported catalysts with 3D substrates (Table 1). The syn- ergistic interaction between the platinum nanoparticles and theSSF substrate may facilitate the electrochemical adsorption of the intermediate active material, resulting in the enhancement of the HER properties of the material.[7] Based on the Tafel plot (log j- η) the relevant mechanism for HER can be determined. The Tafel equation is as follows: η=blogj+a (1) wherejis the current density, ηis the overpotential, and bthe Tafel slope. It is known that lower the Tafel slope, more favorable the electrocatalytic reaction proceeds and faster the electrocatalytic kinetics. The equation for the relationship between the rate- determining step of the HER and the Tafel slope is given below: Volmer : catalyst +H2O+e−→OH−+catalyst − Had(∼120mV ·dec−1)( 2 ) Heyrovsky : catalyst −Had+e−+H2O→catalyst +OH−+ H2(∼40mV ·dec−1)( 3 ) Tafel : 2catalyst −Had→2catalyst +H2(∼30mV ·dec−1)( 4 ) whereH adindicatestheadsorbedhydrogenatomonthecatalyst surface. As illustrated in Figure 4b, it can be seen that compared with pristine SSF (288 mV·dec−1), Pt-SSF-0.01 (230 mV·dec−1), Pt- SSF-0.05 (80 mV·dec−1), and Pt-SSF-0.15 (137 mV·dec−1), Pt-SSF-0.1 had the lowest Tafel slope (67 mV·dec−1), which indicated that the Pt-SSF-0.1 sample had the best HER kinetics. According to Equations ( 2), (3), and (4), the Tafel slope values of Pt-SSF-0.1 are in the range of 40–120 mV·dec−1, indicating that the HER pro- cess follows the Volmer–Heyrovsky mechanism.[26,31,32]In order to further investigate the relationship between Pt loading on fiber felt and electrodeposition, we prepared samples with dif- ferent electrodeposition times (0.5, 1, 1.5, and 2 h) under the same electrodeposition conditions. The samples with different electrodeposition times were noted as Pt-SSF-0.5 h, Pt-SSF-1 h, Pt-SSF-1.5 h, and Pt-SSF-2 h. We have measured the weight of fiber felt loaded at different electrodeposition times, the results are shown in Table S2. As can be seen from Table S2, ChemistrySelect 2025,10, e202405005 (3 of 6) © 2025 Wiley-VCH GmbH 23656549, 2025, 7, Downloaded from https://chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/slct.202405005 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
