ChemistrySelectResearch Article doi.org/10.1002/slct.202405005 Figure 4. (a) LSV and (b) corresponding Tafel plots of samples with different loadings. Figure 5. (a) LSV and (b) corresponding Tafel plots of samples based on different 3D materials. (c) EIS Nyquist plots of samples based on different 3D materials. (d) Electrochemical double layer capacitance of samples. after electrodeposition, the Pt loading of Pt-SSF-0.5 h, Pt-SSF- 1 h, Pt-SSF-1.5 h, and Pt-SSF-2 h was about 0.14, 0.43, 0.56, and 0.73 mg·cm−2, respectively. As shown in Figure S3andS4,t h e results of SEM and HER tests show that excessive electrode- position time leads to excessive thickness and agglomeration of the Pt coating on the fiber surface, which inhibits the catalytic electrocatalytic performance of the electrode. More- over, excessively thick Pt coatings lead to excessive costs that are not conducive to the balance between catalytic activity and cost-effectiveness. Therefore, in combination with the SEM results, the samples electrodeposited for 1 h had the best performance. On the other hand, as shown in Figure 5, we further inves- tigated the electrochemical properties of samples based on different 3D materials in 1 M KOH.It can be seen from Figure 5athat the HER catalytic activity of Pt-TC4-0.1 and Pt-C paper-0.1 is lower than that of Pt-SSF- 0.1. Meanwhile, the overpotential of Pt-SSF-0.1 is very close to that of Pt ( η10=22 mV),[32]while the overpotentials of Pt-C paper-0.1 (44 mV) and Pt-TC4-0.1 (52 mV) are much higher than that of Pt-SSF-0.1. As shown in Figure 5b, the Tafel slopes of both Pt-C paper-0.1 (72 mV·dec−1) and Pt-TC4-0.1 (99 mV·dec−1) are lower than those of Pt-SSF-0.1, indicating that their reaction kinetics are slower than those of Pt-SSF-0.1.[26]It can be seen from Figure 5cthat the Pt-SSF-0.1 sample shows the lowest Rct (68.9 /Omega1), while Pt-TC4-0.1 (554 /Omega1) and Pt-C paper-0.1 (4910 /Omega1) have greater transfer resistance, which indicates that Pt-SSF-0.1 has the lowest charge transfer resistance in HER electrocatalysis. It is well known that the number of active sites tends to be proportional to the catalyst surface area; therefore, the ChemistrySelect 2025,10, e202405005 (4 of 6) © 2025 Wiley-VCH GmbH 23656549, 2025, 7, Downloaded from https://chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/slct.202405005 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
