after 60 min of electrodeposition. Stability is another important property to evaluate an elec- trocatalyst. In order to evaluate the stability of self-supported electrode, chronoamperometric measurements are conducted. A ss h o w ni nF i g u r e 6a, the overpotential of Pt-SSF-0.1 is always less than those of the electrodes with the other two materials as substrates, indicating that Pt-SSF-0.1 has the most excellent elec- trochemical performance. On the other hand, there is no observ- able variation in chronopotentiometric durability test curves for Pt-SSF-0.1, and electrochemical performance of Pt-SSF-0.1 did not exhibit any significant decrease after 12 h test (Figure 6b), whichmeansthatPt-SSF-0.1hasagoodelectrochemicalstability. Figure6c,dshow the SEM images of Pt-SSF-0.1 after the 12 h sta- bility test with different magnifications. Figure 6dand the inset of Figure 6dare enlargements of Figure 6c. It can be seen from Figure6d, that after a 12 h chronopotentiometric durability test in 1 M KOH, the original morphology of the Pt-SSF-0.1 has not changed. The Pt deposited on the electrode surface has some- what cracked after the stability test, but on the whole it is still ChemistrySelect 2025,10, e202405005 (5 of 6) © 2025 Wiley-VCH GmbH 23656549, 2025, 7, Downloaded from https://chemistry-europe.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/slct.202405005 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
