1 Article type: Full paper A self -supported high -entropy metallic glass with nanosponge  architecture for efficient hydrogen evolution under alkaline and acidic conditions Zhe Jia1, Keita Nomoto1,2, Qing Wang3,4, Charlie Kong5, Ligang Sun6*, Lai -Chang Zhang7, Shun -Xing Liang7, Jian Lu4,8*, Jamie J. Kruzic1* 1School of Mechanical and Manufacturing Engineering, University of New South Wales (UNSW Sydney), Sydney NSW 2052, Australia 2Australian Centre for Microscopy & Microanalysis, and School of Aerospace, Mechanical and Mechatronic Engineering, University of Sydney, Sydney 2006, Austra lia 3Laboratory for Microstructures Institute of Materials Science, Shanghai University, Shanghai 200072, China 4Hong Kong Branch of National Precious Metals Material Engineering Research Center, and Department of Mechanical Engineering, City University of  Hong Kong, Hong Kong SAR, China 5Electron Microscope Unit, University of New South Wales ( UNSW Sydney) , Sydney NSW 2052, Australia 6School of Science, Harbin Institute of Technology, Shenzhen 518055, China 7School of Engineering, Edith Cowan University, 270 Joondalup Drive, Joondalup, Perth, WA 6027, Australia 8Centre for Advanced Structural Materials, City University of Hong Kong Shenzhen Research Institute, Greater Bay Joint Division, Shenyang National Laboratory for Materials Science, Shenzhen, 518057 China *Corresponding authors. Email: j.kruzic@unsw.edu.au (J.J.K.);  jianlu@cityu.edu.hk  (J.L); sunligang@hit.edu.cn (L.S.) Keywords:  high-entropy metallic glass; lattice distortion ; chemical complexity; metallurgy; electrocatalysis Developing highly efficient and durable electrocatalysts for the  hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) under both alkaline and acidic media is crucial for future development of a hydrogen economy . However, state-of-the-art high -performance electrocatalysts recently developed are based on carbon carriers mediated by binding noble elements and their complicated processi ng methods are a major impediment to commercialization.  Here, inspired by the recently promising high -entropy concept with inherent multinary nature and glassy alloy  with chemical homogeneity and tunability, we present a scalable strategy to alloy five equiatomic  elements, PdPtCuNiP, into a high -entropy metallic glass (HEMG) for HER in both alkaline and acidic
============================================================
[Page 3/28]
============================================================
2 conditions. Surface dealloying of the HEMG create s a nanosponge -like architecture  with nanopores and embedded nanocrystals that provide abundant active sites to achieve outstanding HER activity.  The obtained overpotentials at the current density of 10 mA cm-2 are 32 and 62 mV in 1.0 M KOH and 0.5 M H 2SO 4 solutions, respectively, outperforming most currently available electrocatalysts. Density functional theory (DFT) reveal s that a lattice distortion and chemical complexity  of the nanocrystals lead to a strong synergistic effect on electronic structure that further stabilizes the H* adsorption/desorption. This HEMG strategy establishes a new  paradigm for designing compositionally complex alloys for electrochemical  reactions. 1. Introduction Hydrogen is widely recognized as an appealing clean energy storage medium to take the place of traditional fossil fuels due to its sustainability , high energy density, and eco-friendly properties[1]. Among the wide range of hydrogen generation processes, electrochemical water splitting is particularly attractive because of its high performance, low -cost, simplicity , and zero carbon emissions[2]. However, a current challe nge for this technology is the development of high-performance catalysts  that 1) can be manufactured and implemented at large -scale and 2) are compatible in pH -universal electrolytes so that they can  be directly employed for hydrogen generation with a high energetic efficiency[3]. Great research effort has been dedicated to develop novel electrocatalytic materials through wet chemistry[4] or chemical vapor deposition[5], including  single atoms[6], quantum dots[4], binary  alloys[7], and various dimensional layere d[8] or core -shell[9] structured /nanostructured  catalysts. While t hese materials provide excellent lab oratory  scale performance  owing to their large specific surface area s[10], well-tuned  electronic structure s[11], and well-controlled nanostructures[12], they are heavily dependent on carbon carriers mediated by binding elements . This limitation is a major impediment to achieve industrialization of water electrolysis  and the challenge of  how to design and manufacture self-supported three -dimensional (3D) electrode materials  at scale has not ye t been overcome . In contrast, 3D nanoporous metallic materials can be fabricated at industrial scale and  have demonstrated attractive performance as multifunctional catalysts in a wide range of
============================================================
[Page 4/28]
============================================================
3 electrochemical applications[13]. Additional attractive properties include fast electron transp ort, reactive selectivity, and a flexible, self-supported architecture that together make alloy electrodes  ideal for manufacturing commercial electrodes  at scale . To date, a number of binary alloy s[14] or multi -phased alloy  systems[15] have been employed as the precursors to fabricate noble metal -based  nanoporous materials. However, in the present work we propose a novel high-entropy alloying approach whereby the multicomponent nature enables fine tuning of the nanopore and nanophase distrib ution  to enable elect rochemical  performance that greatly exceeds previously developed  nanoporous metallic catalysts . A key novelty of our approach is to begin with a high -entropy metallic glass (HEMG) ribbon  that provide s a mechanically strong and flexible substrate  onto which a well -controlled nanoporous and multiphase surface structure can be developed. Recently, m etallic glass (MG)  ribbons , with their unique short -range ordered and long - range disordered atomic structure s, have garnered  considerable interest for  catalytic applications  on their own merits[16]. Their multicomponent nature , chemical homogeneity , high atomic diffusivity, metastable nature, and excellent mechanical properties offer great potential as nanoporous catalysts , such as  has been reported for the Ni-Zr-Ti[17], Pd-Ni-P[18], Pd-Cu-Ni[19], Pt-Ni-Cu-P[20], and Au-Cu-Si[21] MGs . While tremendous research progress has been made using traditional MG compositional designs, the fabrication of nanoporous architecture s through chemical dealloying is in fact limited  by their single principal  component design (i.e., other elements are minorly alloyed). This limitation motivates the high -entropy compositional design we have developed  in the present work. High -entropy alloys (also referred to as c ompositionally complex alloys ) have attracted great research interest  for various applications owing to their unique  mechanical and physicochemical properties[22]. The ir inherent multinary nature and ability to decompose into multi -phased structures  make them ideal for fabrication of nanoarchitectured catalysts . A few excellent multi -phased high-entropy  alloys , including Fe-Co-Ni-Al-Ti[23], Al-Ni-Co-Ir-Mo[24], Fe-Co-Ni-Cr-Nb[25], and Cu-Al-Ni-Mo-Fe[26] have been successfully fabricated in porous architectures that present  excellent performance in electrochemical water splitting. However, the pore size,  morphology and distribution are highly dependent on their un control lable  phase
============================================================
[Page 5/28]
============================================================
4 structures and grain boundaries , which  greatly limit the potential for further enhancement of their electrocatalytic activity.  Thus, the advancement of nanoporous metallic electrocatalys ts require s a bold step forward to develop a  rational alloy design strategy to create  self-supported 3D electrode materials  at scale . Here, inspired by both the advantages of glassy alloy  ribbons  and the novel high -entropy compositional design concept, we have developed a new HEMG  possessing five equiatomic  elements  for efficient hydrogen evolution  in both alkaline and acidic conditions . The high -entropy concept was incorporated into MG ribbons to achieve homogeneously distributed elements at the atomic level that could be partially dealloyed to give unsurpassed nanostructural control of the surface layer. The flexible free -standing HEMG ribbon with an equiatomic  composition of Pd 20Pt20Cu20Ni20P20 was manufactured by a scalable and simple melt-spinning technique. The superior synergistic catalytic function of the  quinary  PtPdCuNiP HEMG was initially emphasized by comparison to two traditional quaternary MG with the atomic compositions of Pd40Cu30Ni10P20 and Pt 57.5Cu14.7Ni5.3P22.5. Afterwards, a simple one - step dealloying process of the nonfunctional Cu and Ni elements was employed  to enlarge the specific surface area . By this dealloying strategy, we successfully prepared a 3D self -supported HEMG ribbon with a nanosponge -like surface morphology that achieve d unsurpassed electrochemical HER activity in 1.0 M KOH solution compared to the past scalable - manufactured alloy catalysts . Furthermore , our fabricated nanoporous HEMG ribbon demonstrate d excellent HER activities in both alkaline and acidic  conditions, which is also unachievable in the past alloy systems , i.e., the HER activities of the past alloy catalysts were mostly favorable in only acidic or alkaline condition . The mechanism for the enhanced catalytic performance was found to involve nanocrystallization (  10 nm) of Pt5Pd3P2 and Pt 3Pd2 induced in the top most, Cu and Ni depleted,  nanoporous layer . Density functional theory (DFT) simulation revealed that the interstitial solid solution  of P atoms  in the Pt -Pd sublattice g ave rise to a slight lattice distortion which stabilize d hydrogen  proton ( H*) adsorption/desorption and remarkably promote d hydrogen generation for  a long service under both alkaline and acidic media .
