5 2. Results and discussion 2.1. Preparation and structural features Figure 1a shows the schematic  of the surface morphological evolution during the dealloying process , in which the flexible as -spun Pd20Pt20Cu20Ni20P20 HEMG ribbon was employed as a precursor. The amorphous structure and the original ultra -smooth surface of the as -spun HEMG ribbon were  confirmed by X-ray diffraction (XRD) and scanning electron microscope (SEM) in Fig ure S1 and Figure S 2a. The equiatomic  nature  was confirmed  in the matrix and on the surface by  inductively coupled plasma - optical emission spectrometry (ICP -OES) and X -ray photoelectron spectro metry  (XPS) , respectively  (Figure S3). After chemical dealloying by 70 wt.% nitric acid for 60 min at room temperature , the surface morphology of the HEMG ribbon evolved to a nanoporous architecture with interconnected nanopores and ligaments  (Figure 1b and Figures S2b,c). The ultra-fine size of the nanopores was less than 10 nm and they were uniformly distributed on the  ribbon  surface. Figure 1c presents a 3D atomic force microscope (AFM) image of the dealloyed HEMG ribbon, further verifying the 3D nanosponge -like surface morphology. The dealloyed HEMG ribbon  surface exhibited increased  concentrations of Pd and Pt relative to the Cu and Ni elements , demonstrating the Cu and Ni atoms  were gradually dissolved during the dealloying process to create  the 3D nanosponge surface architecture (Figure S3). To determin e the surface chemical state evolution of the HEMG  ribbon  during the dealloying process, we also carried out XPS analysis for the elements of Pt, Pd, Cu, Ni, P , and O as shown in Figures 1d,e and Fig ure S4. The Pt 4f high -resolution XPS spectra of the as -spun HEMG consist ed of two major  peaks at the binding energies of 71.4 and 73.1 eV , which are assigned to be metallic Pt (Pt0) and Pt2+, respectively  (Figure 1d). It is noteworthy that the Pt2+ binding energy of the dealloyed HEMG with the value of 72.8 eV exhibit ed a slightly negative shift compared to that of as -spun HEMG . Similar ly, the binding energies of Pd 3d at 335.9 and 337.1 eV  for the as -spun HEMG , which correspond to metallic Pd (Pd0) and Pd2+, respectively, also present ed a negative shift to 335.8 and 336.9 eV for the dealloyed HEMG  (Figure 1e). The Cu 2p XPS spectra of the as -spun HEMG show ed peaks at 932.0 and 934.2 eV corresponding to metallic Cu (Cu0) and Cu2+, respectively (Figure S4a).  The Ni 2p XPS spectra of the as -spun
============================================================
[Page 7/28]
============================================================
6 HEMG exhibit ed binding energies at 852.6, 856.0 and 857.6 eV that could  be assigned to Ni0, Ni2+, and Ni3+, respective ly (Figure S4b). Interestingly, it was found that the negative shifts of the Cu2+, Ni2+, and Ni3+ peaks were  also observed on the nanosponge -like HEMG ribbon. Such results suggest that 1) the metallic  atoms  were  gaining electrons to enrich their electron density[27] and a robust electron hybridization phenomenon was occur red during the formation of the nanoporous structure[17]; 2) the dealloying process promote d the atomic diffusion  rate and gave rise to an atomic re-arrangement[17] (details in the following  spatial structure section ); 3) the metal sites were  hydroxyl acceptor s and form ed M-OH after the dealloying treatment[28]. However, t he major function of the Cu and Ni elements was to be selectively dissolved for  the formation of nanoporous  structure  (Figure S3 ); thus, their contribution to the alteration of the electronic structure was confined. The P 2p binding energies for P3- and phosphate (PO 43-) exhibit ed a significantly positive shift from 129.8  and 132.5  eV to 130.1 and 133.8  eV , respectively  (Figure S4c), suggesting the  P atoms were involved in the dealloying and atomic re-arrangement process es (details in the following  spatial structure section) . It is well accepted that the  presence of  PO 43- on an electrocatalyst surface can play an essential contribution to accelerate  the water dissociation step[29] and reduce the intermediate H* adsorption  energy[30] during hydrogen production. However, in the present work we also find a strong  role of P3- that will be discussed in detail in the following DFT section.  The binding energies of O 1s at 531.4 and 532.4 eV , which can be  assigned to the M -OH and adsorbed O, also present ed a positive shift to 531.9 and 533.7 eV ,  respectively  (Figure S4d) . The variation of this surface oxidation state was considered as another contribution to the positive shift of PO 43-. More importantly, it was found that the proportion of the M -OH for the dealloyed HEMG is significantly improved compared to that of the as -spun HEMG, indicating  the formation of the metal hydroxides during the dealloying process , which is in agreement with the above XPS analysis of the metal elements.
