7 Figure 1. Conceptional design and surface characterization of the nanoporous PdPtCuNiP HEMG.  a, Schematic illustration of the preparation process of the nanosponge -like HEMG. b, SEM and c, 3D AFM images of the dealloyed  HEMG ribbon surface showing the nanosponge morphology. High -resolution XPS profiles of d, Pt 4f and e, Pd 3d spectra for the as -spun and nanosponge -like HEMG ribbon surfaces. To reveal the nanostructure of the dealloyed surface , an aberration -corrected h igh-angle annular dark -field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF - STEM) image of a cross -section of the nanosponge is shown in Fig ure 2a. It is noteworthy that the top nano porous layer with a thickness of ~20 nm consist ed of a large number of  nanopores with pore diameters less than 10 nm . The nanopores were  interconnected between the  sponge -like ligaments. The matrix of the dealloyed HEMG ribbon still present ed an amorphous struct ure as identified by the diffuse ring in  the selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern shown in Fig ure 2a inset . Figure 2b shows the HAADF -STEM image and elemental mapping results of the cross - sectional structure. It is clear that the elements of Pt, Pd, and P were  enriched  at the surface of the nanosponge layer  while the five elements of Pt, Pd, Cu, Ni and P  were  homogeneously distributed in the underlying amorphous ribbon . These results are  in good agreement with the XPS and ICP -OES analyses shown in Fig ure S3. More details with respect to the cross -sectional nano porous  structure and elemental distribution can be found in Figure S5 and Figure S6.
