8 It is noteworthy that the elemental mapping results in Figure 2b and Figures S6d,e exhibited extremely weak signals of Cu and Ni elements and they even cannot be detected in the top porous layer. The major function of Cu and Ni elements was to be selectively dealloyed to form a homogeneously distributed nanopores on the top surface layer, which is mainly ascribed to the unique feature of the chemical  homogeneity in MGs[18]. For example, the porous/nanoporous morphology and distribution of other types of crystalline alloys are highly dependent on their phase morphology  and grain boundaries  which can be difficult to control. In contrast, the atoms targeted for dealloying are homogeneously distributed  in MGs  which gave a homogeneously distributed nanopore structure . The second function of Cu and Ni elements was to promote the formation of self - constructed nanocrystals around the nanopores, which is attributed to the high atomic diffusion rate[31] and metastable nature[32] of the glassy alloys. The loss of Cu and Ni elements promoted the internal atomic diffusion from a metastable amorphous state to a more stable nanocrystalline state.  It is well accepted that the electrochemical reaction only occurs at the reactive interfaces to achieve water molecule dissociation and H* adsorption/desorption. Therefore, it is deduced that the active sites predominantly contributing to the electrochemic al HER in this work were from the self -constructed Pd and Pt -rich nanocrystals embedded around the nanopores. The contribution of Cu and Ni elements to the reaction was considered to be negligible . Furthermore , high -resolution HAADF -STEM characterization of the sponge ligaments (Figures 2c-e) revealed nano crystalline  structures were formed with  crystallographic plane spacings measured to be  0.17, 0.14 (Fig ure 2d) and 0.23 nm ( Figure 2e) for the (220) , (331) , and (111) planes , respectively. Selected area of the nanocrystals in Figures 2c -e exhibited an extremely weak signal for the Cu and Ni elements according to the elemental mapping results in Figure 2b and Figure S6d,e , respectively , indicating that the nanocrystals were Pd and Pt - rich. Such results  further verify  the negligible role of Ni and Cu at the nanosponge surface and that the self-constructed Pd and Pt rich nanocrystals would provide the major contribution to the HER activity.  To further uncover the details of the Pd and Pt -rich nanocrystals, electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) analysis and DFT simulations were combined with the previously described XPS results , as described below.
