9 Figure 2. Characterization of spatial structure of the nanoporous PdPtCuNiP HEMG.  a, Aberration -corrected HAADF -STEM image of a cross -section of the nanosponge structure. The inset shows the corresponding SAED pattern of the predominantly amorphous matrix.  b, HAADF -STEM image and elemental mapping results of a cross -section of the nanosponge structure showing the elements of Pt, Pd, P are in higher concentration at the surface of the nanoporous structure. c, High -resolution HAADF -STEM image revealing nanocr ystalline Pt5Pd3P2 in the nanosponge area. d, Fast fourier transform (FFT) pattern of the area in ( c). e, High -resolution HAADF -STEM image of the selected nanosponge  area, showing the nanocrystalline structure of Pt 3Pd2. To help determine the atomic compositions of the two Pd, Pt -rich nanocrystals, the EELS spectrum  is shown  in Fig ure 3. Figure 3b shows the EELS mapping results of the Pt, Pd, Ni, Cu elements distributed in the top nanosponge layer. It is clear that the Ni and Cu elements presented a negligible atomic distribution around the nanopores , which is in good agreement with the EDS elemental mapping results in Figure 2b and Figure s S6d,e , and further evidence that the observed nanocrystals in Figures 2c ,e were Pd and Pt -based . The EELS spectrum was acquired between 302 -2348 eV and the elemental atomic concentration for the Pd and  Pt-based nanocrystals was initially deduced as shown in Fig ure 3c and Figure S3 . Note that an elemental
