19 surface d, without and e, with first neighboring coordinat ion of P respectively. Red and blue colors represent the depletion and accumulation of electrons  with the unit of e/ Å3, respectively. 3. Conclusion In this work, the high-entropy alloy concept was incorporated into metallic glass catalysts  to achieve homogeneously distributed elements at the atomic level that could be partially dealloyed  to give unsurpassed nanostructural control of the  surface layer.  Using this novel strategy, we have successfully developed a flexible freestanding high-entropy metallic glass (HEMG)  ribbon with equiatomic  composition and tunable nanostructures that can be directly used as an efficient and reliable electrode in electrochemical hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) . The novel HEMG ribbon with a nanosponge -like surface morphology displays an outstanding HER activity in both alkaline and acidic  conditions , i.e., the achieved overpotentials at  the current density of  10 mA cm-2 are 32 mV and 93 mV in 1 M and 0.1 M KOH solutions as well as 62 mV and 73 mV in 0.5 M and 0.05 M H 2SO 4 solutions. Moreover, the nanosponge -like HEMG ribbon possesses an ultra-reliable HER performance  that can be employed for ~100 h with a negligible elemental leaching  and structural deterioration , suggesting their great potentials in applied prospects . Density functional theory (DFT) calculation and experimental character ization reveal that the excellent HER performance of the nanosponge -like HEMG ribbon is attributed to the nanocrystals generated during the dealloying process that possess  a slight lattice distortion caused by an interstitial solid solution of the P atoms in the P t-Pd sublattice . These P interstitials improve the  electronic structure to stabilize hydrogen proton  (H*)  adsorption/desorption . Our study presents  a novel alloy design strategy for high-entropy materials that have superior performance for  widespread electro catalytic applications. Future attentions toward HEMG materials should be paid on the development of their other structural and functional  applications. 4. Materials and Methods Materials : Raw metal elements of pure palladium (Pd), platinum (Pt), copper (Cu), nickel (Ni), phosphorus (P) were purchased from China Rare Metal Material Co., Ltd and mixed to atomic  compositions of Pd 20Pt20Cu20Ni20P20, Pd 40Cu30Ni10P20 and Pt 57.5Cu14.7Ni5.3P22.5 for MGs
============================================================
[Page 21/28]
============================================================
20 preparation. Boron trioxide (B 2O3) supplied from China Rare Metal Material Co., Ltd was employed as a fluxing agent. Analytical grade chemicals including nitric acid (HNO 3, 70 wt.%), potassium hydroxide (KOH), sulfuric acid (H 2SO 4) and absolute  ethanol were supplied by Chem -Supply. Milli -Q water (18.2 M  cm) was employed throughout this work for chemical dilution. Materials preparation : The MG ribbons were prepared by our previously reported large - scalable melt -spinning technique[41]. Specifically, three master alloys (~20 g) with the given nominal compositions were initially prepared under an argon (Ar) atmosphere in a fused silica tube and then treated in a high purity dehydrated B 2O3 flux at 1200 oC to avoid surface oxidation. To ensure the elements formed a homogeneous distribution the master alloys were re -melted at least four times. Afterwards, the ribbon samples were obtained via melt -spinning onto a single rotating copper (Cu) wheel with an esti mated cooling rate of ~105 K/s. The as -spun MG ribbons with an approximate thickness of 30 m were cut into 0.2 × 1 cm2 pieces for direct use as working electrodes in the electrochemical testing. The chemical dealloying process of Pd20Pt20Cu20Ni20P20 HEMG was carried out in HNO 3 (70 wt.%) solution for 60 min at room temperature. Then, the dealloyed HEMG ribbons were ultrasonicated in absolute  ethanol and Milli -Q water four times to remove residual chemical substances. The dealloyed HEMG ribbons were preserv ed in absolute ethanol solution until subsequent electrochemical testing. Materials characterization : Surface morphology was carried out using a  field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, AURIGA, Carl Zeiss) and an atomic force microscope (AFM, Di mension ICON AFM, Bruker). The surface chemical compositions and valence states were measured by an X -ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS, ESCALAB250Xi, Thermo Fisher Scientific) coupled with Ar+ depth -profile sputtering. An ultraviolet photoelectron spectrometer (UPS, ESCALAB250Xi, Thermo Fisher Scientific) was used to measure the electronic levels of the as -spun and dealloyed HEMG ribbons. Microstructural analysis was conducted using a double aberration -corrected transmis sion electron microscopy (TEM, Themis Z, Thermo Fisher Scientific) operated at 300 kV acceleration voltage. High -angle annular -dark field scanning TEM (HAADF -STEM) images were acquired using ~50 pA probe current, 17.9 mrad convergence angle, and a 100 - 200 mrad collection angle. Energy -dispersive
============================================================
[Page 22/28]
============================================================
21 X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) acquisition parameters were ~200 pA probe current, 20 ms dwell time, 20 keV energy range. More than 1 million output counts were collected to map the elemental distribution. Electron energ y loss spectroscopy (EELS) was performed in the STEM mode using a Gatan Quantum ER/965 GIF with a screen current ~50 pA, probe convergence semi -angle  = 17.9 mrad, probe collection semi -angle  = 41.6 mrad, camera length 37 mm, and entrance aperture 5mm. The EELS on the nanosponge structure was acquired at an energy dispersion of 1 eV/channel to collect a large field of view (2048 eV) in the EELS spectrum. DualEELS  mode, which allows to acquire both low -loss and high loss spectra at the same time, was app lied to calibrate an energy shift during the acquisition and to remove any signal from plural scattering. For the estimation of the specific atomic composition, we used a standard elemental quantification routine in Gatan DigitalMicrograph software. A Hart ree-Slater model for theoretical cross -section calculation as well as a power -law model for background subtraction were used for the quantification. Pd -M4,5 edge (~335 eV), Ni -L2, L3 edges (~872 eV and ~855 eV), Cu -L2, L3 edges (~951 eV and ~931 eV), and P t-M4, M 5 edges (~2202 eV and ~2122 eV), were selected. The TEM specimens were prepared by a standard lift -out method[42] in a plasma focused ion beam scanning electron microscope (PFIB -SEM, Helios G4 PFIB UXe , Thermo Fisher Scientific). To protect the sample surface from the Xe -ion beam, a ~1.0 µm thick layer of Pt/C was deposited on the surface of the material. The incident angle of the ion beam to mill the TEM lamella was restricted within ±2° from the paral lel angle to avoid a potential damage from the ion beam. An X -ray diffractometer (XRD, Smartlab, Rigaku) with a monochromator was employed using Cu -K radiation to identify the amorphous structure of the as -spun and used HEMGs. The nominal composition of t he as -spun HEMG ribbon was initially dissolved in aqua regia and then measured by inductively coupled plasma - optical emission spectrometry (ICP -OES, Optima ICP -OES Spectrometer, PerkinElmer) with a 10 times diluted solution. The leached ions after 100 h stability test were also tested by ICP -OES. Electrochemical measurements : A standard three -electrode electrochemical station (CHI 750E) was employed for testing the electrochemical activities, in which a MG ribbon was directly used as the working electrode, a graphite rod was employed as the counter electrode, and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) was utilized as the reference, respectively.  Pt sheet and
============================================================
[Page 23/28]
============================================================
22 commercial Pt/C nanoparticles (20 wt.%, Sigma -Aldrich) were used for HER  performance comparison. The Pt sheet with a geometric area of 1 × 1 cm2 was directly used as the working electrode. With respect to the Pt/C catalysts preparation, 2 mg Pt/C nanoparticles were initially dispersed into  a mixture solution containing 390 L water/ethanol solution (3:1, v/v) and 10 L Nafion (5 wt.%, Sigma -Aldrich) under 1 h ultrasonication for the preparation of Pt/C ink. Afterwards, 10 L Pt/C ink was loaded onto a glassy carbon electrode (GCE) with a di ameter of 5 mm and surface area of 0.196 cm2 by naturally drying at the room temperature. The electrochemical measurements were conducted in electrolytes of 0.1 and 1.0 M KOH solutions as well as 0.05 and 0.5 M H 2SO 4 solutions , where the electrolytes were initially treated by purging  N2 for 30 min for the purpose of removing the dissolved O 2. The obtained potentials were calibrated with a reversible hydrogen electrode ( E vs. RHE) according to the Nernst equation with a correction of iR loss. Geometric surface area was employed to calculate the current density. Polarization curve was measured by linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) with a scan rate of 5 mV s-1. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was conducted in the frequency range between 0.1 to 105 Hz. Double -layer capacitance ( Cdl) was measured by cyclic voltammetry (CV) at sweep rates of 10 – 100 mV s-1 at non -faradaic overpotentials. The value of electrochemical surface area (ECSA) was calculated by the equation of ECSA = Cdl/Cs, where Cdl is the measured double -layer capacitance and Cs is the specific capacitance. The Cs value was assumed as 0.04 mF cm-2 in this work owing to the flat surface of the ribbons[17, 23]. A static current density of  20 mA cm-2 was employed by a chronoamperometry method for the stability testing in different electrolytes. DFT calculation method : All calculations were performed using the Cambridge Sequential Total Energy Package (CASTEP) module in Materials Studio. Generalized gradient approximation (GGA) in the form of Perdew -Burke -Ernzerhof (PBE) functional was employed for the DFT exchange corre lation energy[43] and ultrasoft pseudo -potentials was used for the core el ectrons[44]. A plane -wave basis set with a cutoff energy of 400 eV was assigned and the self-consistence field (SCF) tolerance was 10−5 eV/atom. The Brillouin zone is sampled by a Monkhorst -Pack grid[45]. Ge ometry optimization of atomistic models was conducted with energy, force and displacement tolerances of 10-6 eV/atom, 0.02 eV/Å and 0.001 Å, respectively.
