23 The Gibbs free energies for hydrogen proton adsorption ( ∆G𝐻∗) were calculated from the given equation of ∆G𝐻∗=∆E𝐻∗+∆ZPE −T∆S, where ∆E𝐻∗, ∆ZPE, T and ∆S represent binding energy, zero point  energy change, temperature and entropy change of H* adsorption system, respectively. Since the vibrational entropy of H* in the adsorbed state is negligible, ∆S can be obtained from the following equation of ∆S=S𝐻∗−1 2S𝐻2≈−1 2S𝐻2 , where S𝐻2  is the entro py of H 2 in the gas phase at standard conditions. In addition, ∆ZPE can be calculated by the equation of ∆ZPE =ZPE 𝐻∗−1 2ZPE 𝐻2. Hence, the free energy of the adsorbed state can be calculated using the following simplified equation[46] of ∆G𝐻∗=∆E𝐻∗+0.24 eV. The plot of electron density difference is obtained from the following equation: ∆𝜌=𝜌𝑠𝑜𝑟𝑏𝑎𝑡𝑒 @𝑠𝑢𝑏𝑠𝑡𝑟𝑎𝑡𝑒 −(𝜌𝑠𝑜𝑟𝑏𝑎𝑡𝑒 +𝜌𝑠𝑢𝑏𝑠𝑡𝑟𝑎𝑡𝑒 ) where 𝜌𝑠𝑜𝑟𝑏𝑎𝑡𝑒 @𝑠𝑢𝑏𝑠𝑡𝑟𝑎𝑡𝑒  is the electron density of the total sorbate and substrate system  while 𝜌𝑠𝑜𝑟𝑏𝑎𝑡𝑒  and 𝜌𝑠𝑢𝑏𝑠𝑡𝑟𝑎𝑡𝑒  are the unperturbed electron densities of the sorbate and substrate. Theoretical modeling : Since the lattice constants of pure Pt and Pd are very similar ( aPt = 3.923 Å and aPd = 3.891  Å), two atomistic models were constructed using a = (aPt+aPd)/2 = 3.907 Å with face centered cubic (FCC) structure both for Pt 3Pd2 and Pt 5Pd3P2 nanocrystals. According to the elemental and structural analysis from the experimental results, the DFT model s of Pt 5Pd3P2 and Pt 3Pd2 crystals were constructed via partially replacing Pt atoms by Pd atoms in an FCC Pt crystal and the P atoms were inserted into the crystal as interstitial atoms. Note that we also attempted to model the Pt 5Pd3P2 crystal with substitutional P atoms in the FCC Pt crystal. However, the P atoms were not inclined to stay at substitutional sites during structural optimization, indicating that such configuration was unrealistic. Instead, a stable Pt5Pd3P2 model was achi eved by introducing an interstitial solution of P atoms. Thus, our DFT models were obtained from the partial replacement of Pt atoms by Pd atoms and interstitial additions of P atoms into the FCC Pt crystal  (structural files are provided in supplementary materials) . In addition, it is well accepted that the spin -polarization effect should be taken into account for alloys that naturally include ferromagnetic metals, such as Fe, Co, Ni elements[47]. In our cases, the Pt 5Pd3P2 and Pt 3Pd2 crystals were deduced as the possible active sites that were employed for the DFT modelling. While the as -spun HEMG ribbon contains 20% at. of Ni, its concentration at the top surface is negligible after the dealloying treatment. Therefore, the spin-
