24 polar ization effect of the Pt 5Pd3P2 and Pt 3Pd2 crystals on the hydrogen adsorption energy was ignored in this work. Structure optimization were performed for these models to optimize the crystal structure, with k points set as (3×3×3). Then the (111) surfaces w ere cleaved from the optimized periodic structures with a vacuum layer of ∼20 Å. These models were further geometrically optimized and utilized to study the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) performance of Pt 3Pd2 and Pt 5Pd3P2 nanocrystals, with k points se t as (3×3×1). Acknowledgements The authors acknowledge the facilities and the scientific and technical assistance of Microscopy Australia at the Electron Microscope Unit (EMU) within the Mark Wainwright Analytical Centre (MWAC) at UNSW Sydney. The authors acknowledge use of facilities and the assistance of Dr. Dorothy YU and Dr. Yu WANG in the Solid State & Elemental Analysis Unit at Mark Wainwright Analytical Centre at UNSW Sydney. The authors acknowledge the use of the facilities and the scientifi c and technical assistance of the Microscopy Australia node at the University of Sydney (Sydney Microscopy & Microanalysis).  This work was supported by the National Key R&D Program of China (Project No. 2017YFA0204403);  the Major Program of National Natura l Science Foundation of China: NSFC 5159089 2; Hong Kong Innovation and Technology Commission via the Hong Kong Branch of National Precious Metals Material Engineering Research Center (grant number 9448003); the Australian Research Council (Discovery Grant DP180101393); the UNSW Faculty Funded Postdoctoral Research Fellowship. Author contributions Z.J., L.G.S., J.L., and J.J.K. designed the research; Z.J., K.N., Q.W., C.K., L.C.Z., and S.X.L. prepared and characterized the samples; K.N. carried out HAADF -STEM and EELS experiments; C.K., and Z.J. conducted AFM, SEM and TEM; L.G.S. carried out DFT simulations; All authors analyzed the data and discussed the results. All authors reviewed and contribute