| www.editorialmanager.com/nare/default.asp 2 Nano Res. energy density and low environmental impact [4 6]. Among various approaches, the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), in which water is split into H 2 and O 2 with the aid of electrocatalysts, has attracted great interest, because of its ease and generality [4, 6]. Mechanistically, the HER begins with an adsorption step (the Volmer reaction), H + e → H ad, wherein a proton is adsorbed on the catalyst surface and reduced to a hydrogen atom. The subsequent desorption step may proceed via either the homolytic Tafel reaction, 2H ad → H 2, or the heterolytic Heyrovsky reaction, Had + H+ + e– → H 2 [7]. An optimal catalyst should exhibit an intermediate Gibbs free energy change ( ΔGM-H) for atomic hydrogen adsorption. If this energy is too small, the driving force will not be great enough to trigger the Volmer reaction. If the adsorption energy is too large, the subseque nt Tafel and/or Heyrovsky reaction will be slow [8, 9]. Identification of a material with moderate Gibbs free energy changes for atomic hydrogen adsorption/ desorption is therefore a requirement for effective hydrogen evolution. Pt, which is best known for optimizing these energies, is the premier catalyst   of the HER in terms of fast reaction rates, low Tafel slopes, and near zero overpotentials [10 13]. The major barriers to widespread utilization of Pt are its prohibitively high cost and finite abundance. Thus, development of new, cheap catalysts that exhibit similar or even better performance than Pt has been a longstanding focus of researchers in the chemistry community [14]. Two strategies have been pursued to address this need. The first involves replacement of Pt with abundant, non-precious materials such as MoS 2 [15 18], WC [19, 20], MoSe 2 [21, 22], nickel [23 25], or cobalt [2628]. Unfortunately, these electrocatalysts have not produced a significant improvement in performance. The second and more promising approach is to manipulate the reaction pathway such that the Volmer and Heyrovsky (or Tafel) reactions occur at different regions on a nanocomposit e catalyst [14, 29]. We describe herein a stepwise designed Rh-Au-Si electrocatalyst that follows the second strategy. Rh and Si were selected as active materials for the Volmer and Heyrovsky reactions, beca use of their demonstrated activities in these processes [14]. After determining the  materials involved in the adsorption and desorption steps, the questions of increasing electrical conductivity and reducing catalyst cost remain. Au was selected  as the material to bridge the Rh and Si domains due to its high conductivity and relatively low cost. In addition, Au binds hydrogen atoms weakly [15] and exhibits good chemical compatibility with Rh and  Si, which may enhance the migration of adsorbed hydrogen atoms from the Rh to Si surfaces. A reaction pathway is proposed based on density functional theory (DFT) calculations. Ternary composite Rh-Au-Si catalysts have been successfully designed and experimentally synthesized. These catalysts exhibit excell ent activity that surpasses that of 40 wt.% Pt/C at large overpotentials. At the optimum Rh:Au:Si mass rati o of 2.2:28.5:69.3, the catalyst cost is significantly less than that of 40 wt.% Pt/C. The ternary Rh-Au-Si catalyst also exhibits good durability, because of its ability to be regenerated by rapid diffusion of adsorbed  hydrogen atoms from Rh to Si. When Si atoms are poisoned or oxidized by hydroxyl ions, diffusing hydrogen atoms may react with hydroxyl to expose active sites by producing water molecules [14]. The results of our research open n e w  d i r e c t i o n s  i n t o  t h e  s t e p w i s e  d e s i g n  o f  h i g h l y  efficient catalysts for the HER. 2 Experimental 2.1 Materials HAuCl 4·4H 2O was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. RhCl 3·3H 2O was donated by Aladdin Industrial Co. Nafion (5 wt.%) was obtained from Sigma Aldrich. The commercial Pt/C catalyst (nominally 40 wt.% Pt on carbon black) was obtained from Alfa Aesar. Other reagents were of analytical grade and used without further purification. Doubly distilled water was used in all experiments. 2.2 Synthesis of Rh-Au-Si nanowire (NW), Au- SiNW, Rh-Au, and Au catalysts SiNWs (30 mg) were etched in a 1.2-mL 5% HF aqueous solution to form Si H bonds on the surface. The etched nanowires were immersed in aqueous solutions containing different concentrations of Au 3+
