www.theNanoResearch.com ∣www.Springer.com/journal/12274 | Nano Research  3 Nano Res. and Rh3+ and stirred for 60 min to form the Rh- Au-SiNW catalysts. Rh-Au nanocomposites and Au nanoparticles were obtained by etching Rh-Au-SiNW and Au-SiNW catalysts, respectively, with excess 5% HF aqueous solution. 2.3 Characterization The phase and crystallography of the catalysts were established by X-ray powder diffraction (XRD, Philips X'pert PRO MPD diffractometer) with Cu K α radiation (λ Cu = 0.15406 nm). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM), high resolution TEM (HRTEM), high angle annular dark field scanning transmission electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM), and elemental mapping by energy dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS) were conducted by use of an FEI Tecnai F20 transmission electron microscope at an accelerating voltage of 200 kV. The Au and Rh contents of the catalysts were determined with a Hitachi 180-80 atomic absorption spectrometer. The chemical states of the catalysts were studied using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) on a Kratos AXIS UltraDLD ultrahigh vacuum surface analysis system with Al K α radiation (1,486 eV). 2.4 Preparation of the working electrodes Working electrodes were fabricated as follows: Catalysts (4 mg) were dispersed in 0.9 mL of 5:1 v/v water/isopropanol solvent followed by addition of 100 μL of Nafion solution (0.5 wt.%). The mixture was agitated ultrasonically to achieve a homogeneous suspension, and 4.5 μL of the suspension was loaded onto a 3-mm diameter glassy carbon electrode (loading ~0.255 mg·cm –2). 2.5 Electrochemical measurements All electrochemical experiments were carried out on a CHI 750 C electrochemical workstation and employed a three-electrode system at room temperature. The modified glassy carbon electrode was the working electrode. A saturated calomel electrode (SCE) and  a platinum wire were used as reference and counter electrodes, respectively. H 2SO 4 (0.5 M) was the suppor- ting electrolyte in all experiments. Potentials were referenced to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) by the following equation E(V vs. RHE) = E(V vs. Hg/Hg 2Cl2) + 0.245 + 0.0591pH 3 Results and discussion Theoretical simulations were conducted to investigate the activation barriers of the Rh-Au-Si catalysts. Figure 1 illustrates our prop osed five-step process on a Rh-Au-Si catalyst, which differs from the previous two-step HER reaction pathway [7] and contains the following elements: (i) Adsorption of a hydrogen atom on the Rh surface by the Volmer reaction, (ii)–(iv) diffusion of a hydrogen atom from Rh to Si via the Au surface, and (v) desorption of hydrogen gas from the Si surface by the Heyrovsky reaction. Information concerning the model used in the DFT calculations is listed in Table S1 (in the Electronic Supplementary Material (ESM)). Structures of the bimetallic Rh-Au models are shown in Fig. S1 (in the ESM). The calculated binding energy of a H atom to the Au(111) surface is 0.01 eV . As shown in Table S2 (in the ESM), the diffusion of a hydrogen atom from the Au(111) to a Si(111)-(2x1 ) surface is an exothermic reaction with a small activation barrier (0.19 eV, Fig. S2 in the ESM). The small diffusional barrier is consistent with the small binding energy of H· to the Au surface. The rate-determining step fo r the diffusion of adsorbed hydrogen atoms is therefore the migration of H atoms from the Rh to the Au surface. A Rh-Au-Si nanocomposite was prepared to verify the predictions of the simulations by loading Rh and Au nanoparticles onto silicon nanowires via reduction of the corresponding metal ions with Si H bonds [30]. SiNWs were obtained by a high temperature method Figure 1 Schematic representation of the HER pathway on a Rh-Au-Si catalyst.
