d continues to increase with increasing overpotent ial, which demonstrates that the stepwise designed electrocatalyst improves efficiency. According to the principle of stepwise design, different components of the catalyst are responsible for different steps in the HER process. Thus, it is meaningful to explore the optimum dosage for Rh-Au-SiNW catalysts. Different Rh-Au-SiNW nano-composites were prepared and employed as electro- catalysts (Fig. 3(a) and Fig. S5(a) in the ESM). The HER activity of Rh-Au-SiNW catalysts increases initially with increasing Rh content and reaches saturation  at an optimal Rh:Au:Si mass ratio of 2.2:28.5:69.3 (Rh-Au-SiNW-2), which is the best composition in terms of catalytic performance. The catalytic activities of pure Au, SiNWs, Rh-Au, and 29.2 wt.% Au-SiNW catalysts were also invest igated. Pure Au and SiNW catalysts display negligible HER activity (Fig. S5(b)  in the ESM). Rh-Au-SiNW nanocomposites are much better than the Rh-Au catalyst, while the 29.2 wt.% Au-SiNW catalyst exhibits poor catalytic performance (Fig. 3(a)). The above results indicate that each com- ponent of the catalyst is essential for completion of an effective HER pathway. The corresponding Tafel plots (Fig. 3(b) and Fig. S6 in the ESM) were derived from LSV curves (Fig. 3(a)) by fitting their linear portions to the Tafel equation, V = a + b·log(– j), where V is the overpotential, b is the Tafel slope, and  j is the measured current density. A smaller Tafel slope indicates better HER performance, because it corresponds to a greater increase in reaction rate with increasing overpotential. Although the Rh- Au-SiNW-2 catalyst displays a larger overpotential (62 mV) than 40 wt.% Pt/C (44 mV) at 10 mA·cm –2, the former exhibits a lower Tafel slope (24.0 mV·dec–1), which indicates better performance at higher current
