andESI-MS measurements. Ionchromatography analysis exhibited asignal atretain timeofca.4.18min,which suggests thatthecounter-anion inπ-2isHCOO␀(Fig- ureS9).Theappearance ofCu(I)andHCOO␀inπ-2may beduetotheredox reaction ofCu(II) withDMF. In solvothermal condition, DMFoftendecomposes togenerate NH(CH3)2andCO.Thelattercouldreduce Cu(II) toCu(I) during theinsituconstruction ofπ-2.[9]Based onthethese results, aswellasthoseofinfrared spectra (Figure S10), thermogravimetric analysis (Figure S11)andelemental anal- ysis(seeSupporting Information), theformula ofπ-2canbe determined as{[Cu(Bcp)2](HCOO)} ·{2DMF·8H2O}. Thermogravimetric (TG) analysis indicated thatthe guestmolecules inπ-2canbereleased when heated to 160°C.Thennoweight losswasobserved tillto220°C (Figure S11). Thepowder XRD patterns ofπ-2after activation aresimilar tothosebefore activation (Figure S12), preliminarily proving theintegrity oftheporous framework ofπ-2.Variable-temperature powder XRDpatterns ofπ-2 showed thatwiththeincrease oftemperatures, thediffrac- tionpeaksat2θover2.5°gradually weaken, whiletheoneat 2.5°doesnotchange, eventhetemperature over90°C (Figure S13).Structurally, thediffraction peakat2θof2.5° corresponds tothe(111)crystal facetofπ-2,thatis,the openhexagonal faces(Figure S14),whichindicates thatthe nanocage windows andporestructure ofπ-2werenot destroyed afteractivation. The1Hmagic angle spinning nuclear magnetic resonance (1HMASNMR) spectroscopy measurements werecarried outtofurther illustrate the structural integrity ofπ-2.Asshown inFigure S15,the1H MASNMRspectra ofπ-2afteractivation isalmost thesame asthatbefore activation, welldemonstrating theframework integrity ofπ-2afteractivation. Thisresult wasfurther confirmed bygassorption tests(seetheSupporting Information). Asshown inFigure S16aandb,although the desolvated π-2cannotadsorb N2andAr,itcanuptake CO2 atdiverse temperatures. Forexample, at195K,the adsorption amount ofCO2reaches 79.02cm3/g,solidly evidencing thepermanent porosity inπ-2.Based onthis adsorption isotherm, theporedistribution of1–3nmcould beestimated (Figure S16c),whichisconsistent withthepore sizemeasured fromthecrystal structure. Itshould benoted thatatanytested temperature, thesorption isotherms assume largehysteresis. Thismaybeascribed tothespecial quadrupole moment ofCO2(␀1.4×10␀39Cm2).Ithasbeen reported thatthequadrupole moment ofCO2caninduce theframework tointeract withCO2molecules, thusincrease theCO2binding andadsorption.[10]Besides goodthermal stability, π-2alsoshows goodchemical stability inH2Oand low-polarity organic solvents (seetheSupporting Informa- tion).Afterimmersed inseveral testedsolvents for24h,the framework ofπ-2canretain intact(Figure S17).Moreover, π-2showsgoodstability inacidicandbasicaqueous solution (pH3–13;Figure S18).Thegoodchemical stability ofπ-2 maybeascribed totheBcpligands around theCu(I)centre, which canprovide ahydrophobic environment andprotect theCu␀Nbonds frombeingattacked byH1/OH␀/H2O.Itis well-known thatπ-πstacking interaction isatypeofweak noncovalent interaction (D2.0kJ/mol) insupramolecularchemistry. Getting stable porous supramolecular frame- works dependent onmerely π-πstacking interactions isstill agreatchallenge. Theemergence ofπ-2,asupramolecular- nanocage-based πframework, willgiveresearchers new insights onweaksupramolecular interactions. Theconcept ofcollective weakness making strength isalsosuitable for construction ofstableporous supramolecular materials. Because ofthegoodstability, thecapacities ofπ-2for encapsulating guestmolecules weretentatively tested. First, theiodine capture by π-2wasinvestigated (seethe Supporting Information). Theresults showthat π-2cannot onlycapture theiodine inorganic solution (I2),butalsoin aqueous solution (I3␀).Asshown inFigure S19a, the adsorption ofπ-2toI2inn-hexane solution canbedirectly andclearly observed bythecolorofsolution, whichquickly changes fromdarkpurple, tolightpurple andcolorless with theincrease ofthesoaking time.Thecorresponding UV/Vis spectra showtheconsistent results, where theI2adsorption peakat522nmquickly decreases withthesoaking time. Particularly, attheinitial 5minutes, theadsorption peak sharply decreases (Figure S19b). Thisresult indicates that theI2adsorption rateisveryfastatthebeginning. Quantitatively, onthebasisofthestandard curveofI2inn- hexane (Figure S20),theI2concentration decreases from 10.0to0.5mMafter π-2soakedfor3minandto0.04mM for20min(Figure S19c), indicating thatalmost alltheI2 (99.6%)inthen-hexane hasbeencaptured byπ-2.Besides thefastadsorption dynamics toI2,π-2alsoshowshighiodine uptake. InI2-KIaqueous solution, theiodine uptake ofπ-2 canreachashighas1.8g/g(Figure S21).Theadsorption kinetics anduptake capacity ofπ-2toiodine isobviously superior tomostreported porous materials.[11]Theexcellent iodine uptake ofπ-2canbeattributed tothelargepore volume andtheinteractions between thearomatic ringsof π-2andiodine.[12]Thelargecavity ofπ-2wasfurther assessed byadsorption ofmethyl orange, adyemolecule withmolecular sizeof~1.3nm(seetheSupporting Informa- tion). Asshown inFigure S22,thecavities ofπ-2can accommodate methyl orange molecules, which canalsobe detected bythecolorchange andUV/Vis spectra. Thecolor ofthemethyl orange aqueous solution gradually changes fromorange redtoorange, andfinally tonearly colorless after48h(Figure S22a). Thecorresponding UV/Vis spectra showthatthecharacteristic peakofmethyl orange at 465nmnearly disappeared (Figure S22b). Alltheseobserva- tionsindicate thatalmost allthemethyl orange insolution hasbeenadsorbed byπ-2.According tothestandard curve ofmethyl orange inaqueous solution (Figure S23),the methyl orange concentration decreases from 311.7 to 37.6μMafter π-2soaked for24handto6.5μMfor48h, corresponding to97.9%methyl orange inaqueous solution captured byπ-2(Figure S22c). Thepowder XRDmeasurements showthatthepatterns ofπ-2aftereither iodine capture ormethyl orange encapsulation aresimilar tothoseofsimulated one(Figur- esS24–25), illustrating that π-2cankeepstable after encapsulation ofiodine andmethyl orange. TheAuger electron spectroscopy (AES) measurement ofπ-2after iodine adsorption shows thatthevalence ofthecopper inπ- Angewandt e ChemieResearch Article Angew .Chem. Int. Ed. 2025, 64, e202413413 (5 of 9) © 2024 Wiley-VCH GmbH 15213773, 2025, 1, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/anie.202413413 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [29/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
