catalytic activity. TheH2generation ratereaches ashighas 4087.3 μmol/g/h (Figure 4;TableS5,Entry2),muchhigher thanthoseofPtNPs(6.9μmol/g/h) and π-2(150.8μmol/g/h) under thesamecondition (Table S5,Entry1–3).Based on theamount ofPt,thehydrogen evolution rateswere calculated tobeanunprecedented valueof524012 μmol/gPt/ h(λF320nm)and117822 μmol/gPt/h(λF420nm).These values would make Pt@π-2 aunique photocatalyst among reported crystalline photocatalysts forhydrogen evolution (Table S6).[13]Theextremely highphotocatalytic activity of Pt@π-2 maybeascribed totheultrafine sizeofPtNPs.The ultrafine sizenotonlyendows thePtwithhighactivity, but alsoenable thePthighly todisperse ontothesurface ofthe nanopore ofπ-2andmakefulluseofPtinphotocatalytic hydrogen evolution. Inaddition, theextremely highphoto- catalytic activity ofPt@π-2 alsoindicates thatthePtNPs within thenanopores ofπ-2couldeffectively promote the separation ofthephoto-generated electron andholeinπ-2, thusgreatly boosting thehydrogen evolution. Itshould be noted thatthehighly photocatalytic performance was achieved intheabsence ofanyadditional photosensitizers, whichhighlights π-2isreallyanexcellent photocatalyst. Control experiments demonstrated thatwithout Pt@π-2, BIHorlightillumination, noH2wasdetected, indicating thatallthesefactors areindispensable tothephotocatalytic hydrogen evolution (Table S5,Entry4–6).Besides excellent activity, Pt@π-2 alsoexhibits gooddurability forphoto- catalytic hydrogen evolution. Asshown inFigure S39,after fourcatalytic cycles, thephotocatalytic activity ofPt@π-2 in hydrogen evolution doesnotshowremarkable reduce. The Ptcontent, powder XRD patterns, andmorphologies of Pt@π-2 aftercyclicreactions aresimilar tothoseofPt@π-2 freshly prepared (Table S7,andFigures S40–41), which further confirms therobustness ofPt@π-2. Inaddition, it wasfound thattherewerenegligible PtandCucontents in thereaction solution afterfourrunsofphotocatalytic hydrogen evolution (Table S7),andalmost noBcpligand wasdetected inthisreaction solution (Figure S42).These observations further evidence that Pt@π-2 isarobust photo- catalyst forhydrogen evolution. Wealsochecked the possible photocatalytic activity ofthereaction solution after fourrunsofphotocatalytic hydrogen evolution reactions (Pt@π-2 wasremoved bycentrifugation), theresultshows thatnegligible H2wasdetected (Table S8).Thisresultnot onlyevidences therobustness ofPt@π-2, butalsoelucidates thatthephotocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction over Pt@π-2 occurs inthesolid-state, rather thansolution-state. Toexplore theeffectofporeconfinement ofπ-2onthe photocatalytic activity, Pt/π-2, inwhich mostPtNPswere deposited ontheoutersurface ofπ-2andthePtcontent is similar tothatinPt@π-2, wasprepared byaliquidreduction method (seetheSupporting Information; Figure S43a). Additionally, Pt&π-2, physical mixture ofpre-synthesized Pt NPsand π-2,where allPtNPsweredeposited outersurface ofπ-2andthePtcontent issimilar tothatinPt@π-2, was alsoobtained (seetheSupporting Information; Figure S44a). Thephotocatalytic activity ofboth Pt/π-2 and Pt&π-2 for hydrogen evolution wastested under thesamecatalytic condition asPt@π-2. Theresults showthatthehydrogenevolution ratesofPt/π-2 and Pt&π-2 are1038.3 and 269.7μmol/g/h, respectively (Figure 4;TableS5,Entry7–8). These values aremuch lower thanthatofPt@π-2 (4087.3 μmol/g/h), which illustrates thattheporeconfine- mentofπ-2tothePtNPsisimportant forthephotocatalytic activity. Inaddition, Pt/π-2 and Pt&π-2 showobviously lowerstability than Pt@π-2; itwasobserved thattheparticle sizeofPtobviously become bigger afterphotocatalysis compared withthosefreshly prepared (Figures S43band 44b),despite thepowder XRDpatterns ofPt/π-2 and Pt&π- 2aresimilar tothosefreshly prepared (Figures S45–46). Therefore, theporeconfinement couldnotonlycontribute toobtain ultrafine PtNPswithhighactivity anddurability, butalsoreinforce theinterfacial interactions between the poresurface andPtNPs,thusfacilitate fortheelectron transfer from π-2toPtNPs.Inaddition, thephotocatalytic hydrogen generation testofthephysically mixed PtNPs, BcpandCu(I)(Pt&Bcp&Cu(I); TableS5,Entry9)wasalso carried out.Theresults show thatPt&Bcp&Cu(I) has photocatalytic activity forhydrogen evolution, withthe hydrogen evolution rateof90.8μmol/g/h, alsomuchlower thanthatofPt@π-2 (4087.3 μmol/g/h). Thisobservation suggests thatthecoordination assembly ofCu(I)andBcpis beneficial forenhancing thephotocatalytic hydrogen evolu- tionactivity. Toestimate thethermodynamic driving force and elucidate thepossible catalytic mechanism, the (photo␀ )redox potentials ofthebare[Cu(Bcp)2]1complex weredetermined byUV/Vis absorption, emission spectra andcyclicvoltammetry (CV),(Figures S47–48).[14]Asshown inTableS9,theoxidation potential ofBIHwasmore negative thantheexcited-state reduction potential of[Cu- (Bcp)2]1*,implying thatthethermodynamic driving forceis enough forBIHtotransfer electron toexcited [Cu(Bcp)2]1 *.[15]Thisindicates areductive quenching mechanism for [Cu(Bcp)2]1*.Thus, thepossible reaction pathways of Pt@π-2, Pt/π-2 and Pt&π-2 forH2evolution wereproposed. Upon illumination, [Cu(Bcp)2]1wasexcited toproduce [Cu(Bcp)2]1*,which wasquenched byBIHtoform[Cu- (Bcp)2].Theresulting Cu(Bcp)2]transfers electrons toPt NPs,where theadsorbed H1wasreduced toH2.[15b,16] Thephotoluminescence (PL)spectra, photocurrent, and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) ofPt@π-2, Pt/π-2, Pt&π-2 and π-2werefurther measured (seethe Supporting Information). ThePLspectra ofPt@π-2, Pt/π-2 and Pt&π-2, especially for Pt@π-2, show significantly damped emission incontrast toπ-2,indicating thatthe recombination ofthephotogenerated electrons andholesis enormously restrained afterintroduction ofPtNPs(Fig- ure5a).Transient fluorescence spectroscopy measurements demonstrated thatthePLlifetimes ofPt@π-2, Pt/π-2, Pt&π- 2and π-2are604,416,350and244ps,respectively (Figure 5b,TableS10).These results further illustrate that theintroduced PtNPscouldserveasanelectr