1A theoretical study on mechanism of hydrogen evolution on non-precious partially oxidized nickel-based heterostructures for fuel cells Xinju Pan, Gang Zhou* State Key Laboratory of Chemical Resource Engineering, Beijing University of Chemical Technology, Beijing 100029, People’s Republic of China (Dated: December 3, 2017) It is desirable, yet challenging, to utilize nonUprecious metals instead of nobleUmetals as efficient catalysts in the renewable energy manufacturing industry. By using first principles calculations, we study the structure characteristics of partially oxidized nickelUbased nanoheterostructures (NiO/Ni NHSs), and the interfacial effects on hydrogen evolution. The origin of enhanced hydrogen evolution performance is discussed at the microscopic level. This study identifies two types of active sites of exposed Ni surface available for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). One is the hcpUhollow sites near the perimeter boundary that exhibit more excellent HER performance than platinum (Pt), and the other the second nearest neighbor fccUhollow sites away from the boundary that have similar performance to Pt. The interfacial effects are resulting from the competitive charge transfer between NiO and Ni surface in NHSs, and enhance the reactivity of NiO/Ni NHSs by shifting the dUstates of surface atoms down in energy. The illumination of the mechanism would be helpful for the design of more efficient and cheap transition metalUbased catalysts. *EUmail: gzhou@mail.buct.edu.cn. Page 1 of 14 Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Accepted Manuscript Published on 21 February 2018. Downloaded by University of New England on 24/02/2018 09:07:09. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C7CP08112J
