2Introduction Ultrathin metal films of only a few atomic layers have recently attracted increasing interest as efficient electro catalysts,1−6 especially for water splitting for hydrogen evolution in both acid and alkaline media,7 due to high surface/volume ratio and excellent electronic transport property. The former guarantees a large number of reaction centers, while the latter supplies a sufficient quantity of highly mobile electrons or holes for the redox reaction occurring at the electrodes. To solve the century problem that fossil fuels are diminishing, hydrogen and solar energy have been put on the agenda of sustainable development a long time ago.8−10 Up to the present, a potential means of producing highUpurity hydrogen is to electrochemically split water into hydrogen and oxygen in an electrolyzer.11,12 Among these metallic electrodes in use, it is recognized that platinum (Pt) is the most efficient electrocatalyst for hydrogen evolution. However, the scarcity and cost of Pt severely limit its largeUscale commercial application for electrolysis.13 For this purpose, many attempts have been performed to reduce costs, such as PtUbased alloying14,15 and looking for alternatives.16,17 Among these candidates, nonUprecious transition metals possess high conductivity available for electrodes, despite having poor hydrogen evolution performance.18,19 At this rate, how to improve their hydrogen evolution performances is of great importance in reality but remains a challenge. Fortunately, with the development of nanotechnology, some nonUprecious metal films have been fabricated by the generalized synthetic strategy,20U22 most typically for nickel (Ni) nanosheets. However, Ni is not a suitable catalyst for water splitting or hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) due to high over potential (~ 200 mV),23,24 which is consistent with the volcano curve prediction.18,19 Recent experiments showed that oxide/metal or hydroxide/metal heterostructures could enhance the hydrogen evolution performance of the whole system in water splitting due to the interfacial effects or the interaction between the two parts.25,26 More precisely, Dai et al. achieved nickel oxide/nickel (NiO/Ni) nanoheterostructures (NHSs) on carbon nanotube sidewalls by the thermal decomposition of nickel hydroxide precursors, followed by partial reduction of NiO. They found that NiO/Ni NHSs have highly effective electrocatalytic activity for the HER, similar to Pt, and further proposed that the exposed NiO/Ni interfaces might be synergistically active sites for the HER, because the NiO shell incompletely covers on the reduced Ni core.20 Contrary to this strategy, Sun et al. first synthesized an ultrathin singleUcrystalline Ni nanosheet through in situ topotactic reduction from nickel hydroxide precursors, and then partially oxidized the Ni sheet to form similar NiO/Ni NHSs, which are reported as efficient Page 2 of 14 Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Accepted Manuscript Published on 21 February 2018. Downloaded by University of New England on 24/02/2018 09:07:09. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C7CP08112J
============================================================
[Page 4/15]
