3water splitting electrocatalysts for the HER, too.21 The synthetic strategy for unique NHSs is of great significance for achieving the goal that utilizes nonUprecious metals instead of noble metals as electrocatalysts for the HER. It is also expected to pave the way for future development of transition metalUbased electrodes for water splitting. However, explanations based on interfacial effects of NiO/Ni NHSs, although conceivable, have not been clearly studied in these reports.20,21 In particular, at the microscopic level, the details of oxideUmetal interactions and the interfacial effects of NHSs on the HER process and performance still remain obscure. Therefore, here we use the first principles calculations to study the interface characteristics of such NiO/Ni NHSs, and confirm that partial oxidization of Ni substrates is crucial for ensuring the highly efficient HER performance. That is, monodispersed NiO deposited on Ni substrates is a prerequisite for the uniqueness of NiO/Ni NHSs. Interfacial effects on the HER performance of NiO/Ni NHSs are illustrated by the identification of reactive sites and electronic structure analysis. Methodology and calculation model All calculations and electronic structure analysis are carried out within the spinUpolarized density functional theory (DFT) employing the Vienna abUinitio simulation package (VASP).27 The generalized gradient approximation (GGA) in the revised PerdewUBurkeUErnzerhof form (RPBE)28 and the projector augmented wave (PAW) method29,30 are used to describe the electron exchangeUcorrelation energy and the electronUionic interaction, respectively. A planeUwave cutoff energy of 500 eV is applied for all atoms. The dipole correction is used in all cases. The periodically repeated slab consisting of three atomic Ni layers and singleUlayer NiO belt, in which NiO only covers half of the Ni(111) surface, is adopted to mimic partially oxidized NiO/Ni NHSs (see Fig. 1). More precisely, we choose a p (5 x 2) supercell for the Ni(111) surface and a p (1 x 3) unit cell for the NiO nanobelt. In this case, the lattice mismatch along the b axis direction is ~ 3.8%. This result is compatible with the experimental phenomena, which NiO in situ deposited on Ni sheet or core.20,21 A vacuum region of 15 Å between the repeated slabs is utilized to prevent the spurious interaction. Integration over the Brillouin zone is done using MonkhorstUPack kUpoint mesh of 3 x 3 x 1 through the calculations. During the optimization, the bottom two Ni layers are kept fixed, while the top one Ni layer, partially covered NiO and adsorbed H are fully relaxed until the residual force on each ion is less than 0.03 eV/ Å. To compare with the previously calculated results about the HER of metals by Nørskov,19 here we Page 3 of 14 Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Accepted Manuscript Published on 21 February 2018. Downloaded by University of New England on 24/02/2018 09:07:09. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C7CP08112J
