4do not take into account the effect of Hubbard U. The integral adsorption energy of the H atom on the electrode that reflects the activity of the HER19 is defined by ∆EH = 1/n(Esurf+nH − E surf   − n/2E(H2))                 (1) where n is the number of H atoms in the calculations. Esurf+nH and Esurf are the energies of the adsorbed H and pristine Ni(111) surfaces, respectively. The calculation of free energy for the adsorbed H, ∆G H*, in the HER follows the definition described by Nørskov in Ref.19. ∆G H* = ∆EH + ∆EZPE  −T∆SH                                        (2) Where ∆ EZPE and ∆ SH are the difference in zero point energy and H entropy between the adsorbed and the gas phase.19 T is the HER temperature, which is set to 300 K in our calculations. The * denotes a site on the surface. Noted that we also test the adsorption energies, ∆ EH, of the H atom on the 4Ulayer, and 5Ulayer Ni slabs under the same optimization conditions and criteria. Results show that ∆EH is regardless of the number of layers of Ni slabs, ~ U0.46 eV . Fig. 1   Atomic structure of NiO/Ni nanoheterostructures (NHSs) under our investigation. The top and side views are shown on the left and right, respectively. In the top view, the arrows of a and b donate two base vector directions of the twoUdimensional slab, respectively. In NiO/Ni NHSs, in situ formed NiO from Ni substrates via incomplete oxidation partially covers on the Ni surface, having the stepUlike perimeter boundary (along the b axis direction). The big and small blue balls and the red balls represent the Ni atoms of substrates and oxide, and the O atoms, respectively. Results and discussion First of all, our simulations show that when the cubic NiO bulk is cut into the film with a few atomic layers, stratification will occur, that is, a flat NiO layer stacking on the other one with a van der Waals separation. However, as they are placed on Ni substrates, which follows the in situ oxidation, layered structure is prohibited and a dense nanobelt is formed on the Ni surface. There is an indication of the Page 4 of 14 Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Accepted Manuscript Published on 21 February 2018. Downloaded by University of New England on 24/02/2018 09:07:09. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C7CP08112J
