5substrate effects, which dominate the formation of NiO cluster for NHSs, consistent with the experimental results.21 So we use the singleUlayer NiO on Ni substrates as a representative, and expect that the conclusions drawn be unchanged and universal for similar NiO/Ni NHSs, i.e., NiO clusters or nanoparticles (NPs) on Ni substrates. From Fig. 1, we can see that the Ni and O atoms in the singleUlayer NiO will be relative misalignment, that is, the O atoms are close to the Ni surface and then the chemical bonds are formed, whereas the Ni atoms are far away from the surface. As a result, two atomic layers of O (lower) and Ni (upper) are present. The interface is characterized as the formation of OUNi bonds, like as Ni(OH) 2 or NiUAl hydroxides on Pt nanoparticles.31,32 In this case, NiO/Ni NHSs are divided into three regions: exposed Ni substrates, stepUlike perimeter boundary, NiO. The main features of the three regions are, respectively, pure Ni(111) surface, O atoms along the boundary, loose Ni atomic layer. Representative sites * for H adsorption after the reduction of protons in the HER are shown in the top view in Fig. 1. In detail, there are the hcpUhollow site near the boundary (H h), the second nearest neighbor fccUhollow site (H fs) and the fccUhollow site at the boundary (H fb) for exposed Ni(111) surface (Fig. 2(a)U(c)), the top site of O (T O) at the boundary (Fig. 2(d)), and the hollow site of Ni atomic layer (H NiO) and the top site of O (T O(NiO) ) for NiO (Fig. 2(e),(f)). Table 1 lists the associated integral hydrogen adsorption energies ∆ EH, and the difference of zero point energy, ∆ EZPE, between the adsorbed H and the gas phase on the aboveUmentioned adsorption sites of NiO/Ni NHSs. Table 1.  Calculated hydrogen adsorption energies ∆ EH, and the difference of zero point energy, ∆ EZPE, between the adsorbed H and the gas phase on different adsorption sites of NiO/Ni NHSs, as shown in Fig. 2. The units are eV in this table. Adsorption Site T O(NiO)