8interface interaction as supposed by Dai.20 On the other hand, this is also the motivation of developing heterostructures, that is, performing their respective duties and achieving performance complementary. Specific to the present study, from the interface structure that the O atomic layer is dominant (Fig. 1), we can affirm that once nanoUscale NiO forms, a certain number of electrons spontaneously transfer from the Ni(111) surface to NiO via the interfacial O atoms due to the electronegativity difference between the O and Ni atoms. Bader charge analysis shows that the electronUdonating number of Ni surface (0.287U0.365 e) is much less than that of Ni in NiO (0.728U0.653 e). This implies that exposed Ni surface in NHSs still maintains the nature of metal, and then can act as an electrode continuously supplying the electrons to protons from solution, then reduce them to H atoms, finally form H 2 gas. The whole HER process is H+ + eU + * → H*, H* + H* → H 2 + *, might following the VolmerUHeyrovsky mechanism.36 More precisely, the electronUdonating ability of the H h site (0.734e) is somewhat stronger than that of the H fs site (0.581e), also showing better reductive property of the former than the latter. Electron density differences (Fig. 4) indicate the charge redistributions of NiO, interface, and Ni substrate surface once NiO/Ni NHSs construct. From it, we can see that the electron transfer proceeds, at either H h or H fs sites, from the Ni substrate surface to the NiO across the interface. Therein, the former is more pronounced than the latter. As a result, when the proton H+ is attracted by the electronUdeficient Ni substrate surface, even though it still gains the electrons from the Ni substrates and then is reduced to be the H atom, the effects occurring at the H h site is obviously stronger than those of the H fs site. This is not only consistent with the free energy changes for hydrogen evolution depicted in Fig. 3, but also compatible with the results of the hybridizations of NiU d states with HU s states for these two reaction sites (Fig. 5). Furthermore, electron density differences also reinforce the fact that NiO affects the electron states of exposed Ni(111) surface by an indirect way, that is, the competitive charge transfer between NiO and Ni surface occurring at the interface O atoms, and the O atoms at the boundary basically do not participate in the reaction. And simultaneously, the protons can be completely reduced at the two active sites (H h and H fs) for the HER. Fig. 4   Electron density differences of the reduction of protons occurring at the (a) H h and (b) H fs sites. For the Page 8 of 14 Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Accepted Manuscript Published on 21 February 2018. Downloaded by University of New England on 24/02/2018 09:07:09. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C7CP08112J
