10minority spinUdown channel crosses the Fermi level, indicative of the metallic characteristics. This means that NiO/Ni NHSs is fully capable of using as an excellent electrode in electrochemical reactions. In addition, the hybridizations of NiU d states with HU s and OU p states are not very pronounced, showing that a complete HER occurs, not involving in the O atoms of NiO. This result is consistent with the VolmerUHeyrovsky mechanism of the HER. And this also means that the H + H recombination is easily occurred at the exposed Ni surface due to the small diffusion barrier of the H atom between the two neighbor available sites, which is attributed to decreased integral H adsorption energy. Crucially, owing to the interfacial effects, resulting from the competitive charge transfer between NiO and Ni surface, the shift of the dUband center of exposed Ni(111) surface is clearly visible. Typically, the downshift of the dUband center of H h site, relative to the pure Ni surface, is up to 0.15 eV (for the minorityUspin states), which facilitates the HER activity because of a negative shift of the electroUreduction onset potential and/or higher electroUreduction current at a given potential. In reality, the similar modification effects on the HER of metallic catalysts via epitaxial oxides have been encountered in the ORR of amorphous boron oxideUcovered Pt nanoparticles.39,40 Furthermore, we take into account the effect of the coverage of the adsorbed H on the integral adsorption energy, ∆ EH. More precisely, for the H h site the ∆ EH are U0.21 eV for 0.5 monolayer (ML) and U0.15 eV for 1 ML, respectively, while for the H fs site, the ∆ EH are U0.34 eV for 0.33 ML and U0.27 eV for 1 ML. The trend that the ∆ EH decreases with the increase of H coverage for NiO/Ni NHSs is consistent with those of all metals considered.19 Since the NiO is a wellUknown Mott insulator—a strongly correlated system, we also check carefully the influence of an effective Hubbard U ( = 4.0 eV , which was used in previous calculations31). As expected, some results for NiO have changed. Typically, the free energy for the adsorbed H, ∆G H*, on the H NiO site in the HER is decreased from 0.51 eV (without the U correction) to 0.21 eV (with the U correction). Even so, this also means that the NiO does not supply the active sites for the HER. Comparatively speaking, the main conclusions (i.e., mechanism and physical origin) for NiO/Ni NHSs for the HER remain unchanged, that is, the Ni substrates available for the HER. The reason is that the NiO is very smaller than Ni substrates in the NHSs system, no matter the size or the number of atoms. Conclusions Our first principles calculations show that in NiO/Ni NHSs used in water splitting for hydrogen Page 10 of 14 Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Physical Chemistry Chemical Physics Accepted Manuscript Published on 21 February 2018. Downloaded by University of New England on 24/02/2018 09:07:09. View Article Online DOI: 10.1039/C7CP08112J
