11evolution, only the hollow sites of exposed Ni(111) surface near the boundary have more excellent or similar HER performances than or to the Pt. The HER process follows the VolmerUHeyrovsky mechanism. The synergistical effects at the interface of NiO/Ni NHSs are manifested as the competitive charge transfer between NiO and Ni surface. The existence of NiO shifts the dUstates of surface Ni atoms down in energy, and thus enhances the reactivity of exposed Ni surface and increases the active sites availability for the HER. We can affirm that in the synthetic strategy/actual operation, the partial oxidization for Ni substrates or incomplete reduction for NiO is a prerequisite for unique NiO/Ni NHSs, which are capable to be used as the highly efficient electrocatalysts for the HER. The research that sheds light on this issue would be helpful to popularize the partial oxidation strategy to other nonUprecious transition metalUbased catalyst design for fuel cells. Related works such as CoO/Co NHSs and so on are in progress. Notes The authors declare no competing financial interest. Acknowledgements This work was supported by the National Natural Science Fundamental of China (Grant Nos. 21431003 and 21541004), and the Fundamental Research Funds for the Central Universities (buctrc201514). Our calculation works were completed on the “Explorer 100” cluster system of Tsinghua National Laboratory for Information Science and Technology. References 1 X. Huang, S. Tang, X. Mu, Y. Dai, G. Chen, Z. Zhou, F. Ruan, Z. Yang and N. Zheng, Nat. Nanotechnol ., 2011, 6, 28−32. 2 F. Saleem, Z. Zhang, B. Xu, X. Xu, P. He and X. Wang, J. Am. Chem. Soc ., 2013, 135, 18304−18307. 3 H. Duan, N. Yan, R. Yu, C. Chang, G. Zhou, H. Hu, H. Rong, Z. Niu, J. Mao, H. Asakura, T. Tanaka, P. J. Dyson, J. Li and Y. Li, Nat. Commun ., 2014, 5, 3093. 4 H. Liu, F. Nosheen and X. Wang, Chem. Soc. Rev ., 2015, 44, 3056−3078. 5 H. Zhang, ACS Nano ., 2015, 9, 9451−9469. Page 11 of 14 Physical Chemistry Chemical 