16108 |  J. Mater. Chem. C, 2024, 12, 16108–16119 This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2024 Cite this: J. Mater. Chem. C, 202 4, 12, 16108A thienothiophene and anthracene based functional hyperbranched polymer: synthesis,photophysical properties and photocatalytic studies † Recep Isci, ‡aHakan Bildirir, ‡*bcDilara Gunturkun,aMiguel Gomez-Mendoza,b Marta Liras, bVı´ctor A. de la Pen ˜a O’Shea band Turan Ozturk *ad Conjugated polymer photocatalysts have been receiving extensive attention in the field of photocatalytic hydrogen evolution, owing to their tunable molecular structures and electronic properties. Herein, wereport a hyperbranched conjugated polymer, containing thienothiophene and anthracene units ( TT-Ant ), synthesized viaPd(0) catalyzed Suzuki coupling. Its structural, photophysical and electrochemical features were investigated by using UV-vis and fluorescence spectroscopy, cyclic voltammetry (CV) andX-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution tests, combining the material with two diﬀerent additives, resulted in high hydrogen production rates from water. A steady state production rate of around 286 mmol g /C01h/C01for its hybridization with TiO 2was recorded, which is more than 3 times that for pristine TiO 2under the same conditions. Moreover, the combination of the polymeric material with platinum (1% wt) resulted in a maximum rate value of 700 mmol g/C01h/C01. The surface properties of the latter combination before and after the reaction were studied by scanningelectron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM), which demonstrated successfulPt deposition on the surface of the polymer. This work may provide a new strategy to construct stablephotocatalysts with thienothiophene and anthracene cores as active sites for eﬃcient catalytic reactions in energy conversion applications. Introduction Conjugated polymers are attractive materials for electronic applications owing to their electron rich backbone, which caneasily be manipulated by combining various electron rich/poormonomers through numerous reaction techniques. 1Although most of those electronic applications require good solubility,this property is not essential for several applications. 2–5More- over, solubility can be a disadvantage in some cases. Forexample, soluble organic electrode materials for battery appli-cations suffer from leaching during charge–discharge cycles. 6 Additionally, the heterogeneous nature of the non-solublematerials is an advantage for applications like photocatalysisas it allows the removal and reuse of the catalyst easily. 7 Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution from water (HER) is one of the most attractive sub-fields of catalysis since it involvesproduction of green energy from a highly abundant and sus-tainable source as an alternative to fossil fuels. 8Extended polymeric structures are intriguing materials for the hetero-geneous HER, thanks to their facile electronic tunability and high hydrothermal stability. 9,10Indeed, hydrogen production using polymeric photocatalysts has been improved from a few mmols to hundreds of mmols in the last decade.11–14In addi- tion to producing champion catalysts for the HER, designingnew materials to investigate the structure–property relationshipis also of great interest in this field (see Table S1 for recentexamples, ESI †). 15General key design parameters for a success- ful catalyst are adjusting hydrophilicity to promote interactions with water16–18and adjusting HOMO–LUMO levels by combin- ing donor–acceptor co-monomers.19–21Specifically, regulating the electron delocalization viamanipulating heteroatoms,22,23 tuning the amount of electron withdrawing groups along the polymeric backbone,24,25independent integration of active donor–acceptor groups for individual hydrogen and oxygenaIstanbul Technical University, Chemistry Department, 34469, Maslak, Istanbul, Turkey. E-mail: ozturktur@itu.edu.tr bPhotoactivated Processes Unit, IMDEA Energy Institute, Parque Tecnolo ´gico de Mo´stoles, 28935, Mostoles, Spain cElectrochemical Processes Unit, IMDEA Energy Institute, Parque Tecnolo ´gico de Mo´stoles, 28935, Mo ´stoles, Spain dTUBITAK UME, Chemistry Group Laboratories, 41470, Gebze, Kocaeli, Turkey †Electronic supplementary information (ESI) available. See DOI: https://doi.org/ 10.1039/d4tc02568g‡These authors contributed equally.Received 19th June 2024, Accepted 31st August 2024 DOI: 10.1039/d4tc02568g rsc.li/materials-cJournal of Materials Chemistry C PAPER Published on 02 September 2024. Downloaded by Istanbul Technical University on 5/16/2025 9:43:39 AM. View Article Online View Journal | View Issue
