This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2024 J. Mater. Chem. C, 2024, 12, 16108–16119 |  16109production,26and forming push–pull–pull systems for an enhanced charge separation27are some recent examples. Additionally, amount of the catalyst,28pH of the media,29and further processing of the catalyst such as exfoliation or nanostructuring30,31are the physical/environment al investigations regarding HER performance. Moreover, forming bulk-heterojunctions to tune electronic features similar to organic solar cells is also effective for the HER,32–34even for total water splitting,35which is a highly desired function for polymeric photocatalysts. Thienothiophenes are fused p-extended structures consist- ing of two thiophene rings; therefore, their structure has fourdiﬀerent isomers. Among them, thieno[3,2- b]thiophene (TT) has the greatest significance as it provides the best electrondelocalization. 36It has an electron-rich, rigid and flat molecular structure with two sulfur heteroatoms, which provides remark-able optic/electronic and electrochemical oxidation/reductionproperties. 37–45These features make derivatives of TT very useful building blocks for optoelectronic/electronic applications, parti- cularly energy production and storage.46–61Having three rigid fused benzene rings, anthracene (Ant) is the least conjugated but the most soluble and the most stable member of the acenefamily (pentacene, tetracene and anthracene), to which a highconsideration has been given within conjugated organicmaterials. 62,63Particularly, its unique structural properties and photophysical behaviors make Ant derivatives very interestingfor light driven applications such as photocatalysts and photon upconversion. 63–67Similar to TTs, Ant derivatives were also introduced to hyperbranched polymeric backbones, which exhibited interesting properties in many applications rangingfrom sensing to photophysics. 68–72 In this study, the design and synthesis of a new hyper- branched TT and Ant based conjugated polymer ( TT-Ant) was portrayed. Its optical and electronic properties were investi-gated using cyclic voltammetry (CV), and UV-vis and fluores- cence spectroscopy, which was found to be feasible to produce hydrogen (H 2) photocatalytically. The hybrid of the polymer with TiO 2(1/9, w/w) resulted in an average H 2production of around 286 mmol g/C01h/C01over 5 hours (more than 3 times compared to pristine TiO 2under the same conditions), which reached a maximum value of 360 mmol g/C01h/C01in 2 hours. On the other hand, combining TT-Ant with 1% (wt.) platinum (Pt) as a co- catalyst improved H 2production up to 700 mmol g/C01h/C01under UV light irradiation, the average of which was 660 mmol g/C01h/C01 over a 5-hour period. Microscopic investigations viascanning electron microscopy (SEM) and transmission electron microscopy(TEM), regarding Pt deposition, showed a homogeneous distribu- tion along the structure with s mall amount of agglomeration. Experimental section Materials All the reagents were purchased from Aldrich and Acros and used without further purification. All the solvents, used inthe syntheses, were technical grade and freshly distilled priorto use. The solvents, used in spectroscopic measurements, werespectroscopic grade. Flash chromatography was performedusingr0.063 mm silica gel. Instrumentation and testing method To determine the electrochemical bandgap of TT-Ant , cyclic voltammetry (CV) measurement was conducted in a mixture of acetonitrile/dichloromethane (1/1), containing tetrabutylammo-nium hexafluorophosphate (TBAPF 6) (0.1 M) as a supporting electrolyte at a scan rate of 50 mV s/C01, using platinum (Pt) and silver (Ag) wires as working and reference electrodes, respectively. CV data were examined on a Princeton Applied Research Versa STAT 3. Fluorescence spectra were recorded on a HITACHI F-4500fluorescence spectrophotometer. UV-vis measurements wererecorded on a HITACHI U-0080D spec trophotometer. X-ray photo- electron spectroscopy (XPS) measurements were performed on aThermo Scientific K-Alpha X-ray photoelectron spectrometer withan aluminum anode (Al Ka –1468.3 eV). Agilent 490 micro-GC wasused for HER measurements. XRD measurements were recorded on a D.10.3.C.118.M224 PANalyti cal XRD spectrometer. Gas sorp- tion measurements were run in a Micromeritics TriStar II Plus. A PerkinElmer Lambda 1050 UV/Vis/NIR was used to record %reflectance, and Spectragryph 70was employed to process the data. A Bruker AVIII/HD spectrometer (100 MHz for13C) was used for solid-state cross-polarization magic angle spinning (CP-MAS) NMR spectroscopy.13CN M Rs p e c t r aw e r er e c o r d e do naV a r i a n 126 MHz for NMRs of monomers. Field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM) and energy dispersive X-ray spectro- scopy (EDX) analysis were performed by using JEOL JSM-7900Fequipped with ULTIM Max 170 fr om Oxford Instruments. A JEOL JEM 1400 (at 120 kV) and an Oxford Instruments EDSTEM XPLORE detector, using Aztec software, were employed torecord transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and the corres-ponding EDX data, respectively. Time-resolved photolumines- cence was recorded through the time correlated single photon counting (TC-SPC) te chnique in a Mini Tau system having a band pass filter in the range of 475–525 nm and using an EPL-375 pspulsed diode laser with emission at 372 nm as an excitationsource (Edinburgh Inst ruments). The corresponding fitting curves were adjusted viaa multi-exponential function taking into account the lifetime of the lamp ( 61.2 ps). A StellarNet UVNb-50 radiometer connected to an optical fibre was used to detect thephoton flux for the photonic effi ciencies, calculated using the standard formula. 73 Photocatalytic experiments HER experiments were conducted following the literature method.72The polymer (25 mg) was sonicated in a mixture of MeOH/water (4/6 mL), and added into a previously dilutedH 2PtCl 6(aq.) solution (mixture of 83.33 mL, 15 mM H 2PtCl 6 (aq.) + 50 mL water), after which water (61 mL) and MeOH (9 mL) were introduced to obtain 117 mL of water + 13 mL of MeOH in total. It was found to be more eﬃcient comparedto direct addition of the solid into the reaction media. Asimilar approach was applied for the TiO 2hybrid of TT-Ant without adding Pt. The mixture was constantly degassed untilno H 2and other ambient gas peaks were observed on thePaper Journal of Materials Chemistry C Published on 02 September 2024. Downloaded by Istanbul Technical University on 5/16/2025 9:43:39 AM. View Article Online
