16110 |  J. Mater. Chem. C, 2024, 12, 16108–16119 This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2024chromatogram. Additionally, the mixture was constantly stirred around 400 rpm. Afterwards, a UV lamp (a 150 W medium- pressure Hg immersion lamp) was turned on. The process was monitored using an Agilent 490 micro-GC, equipped with amolecular sieve column and a TCD detector, collecting achromatogram every E3.8 minutes. Synthesis of the polymer TT-Br3 74–78and Ant-Boron245were synthesized following our previous and the literature reports. To a mixture of TT-Br3 (0.27 g, 0.600 mmol) and Ant-Boron2 (0.17 g, 0.400 mmol), dissolved in THF (30 mL) and degassed for 45 min with N 2, was added K 2CO3(2.5 mL, 2 M) and Pd(PPh 3)4(0.0060 mmol). The reaction mixture was left stirring for 2 days at 90 1C, after which the precipitate obtained was filtered and washed with various solvents (water, THF, and methanol). Following Soxhlet extrac-tion with MeOH and THF (1 day each), it was dried undervacuum at 90 1C overnight to obtain the title compound as a greenish-grey powder in 80% yield. Preparation of TiO 2and TT-Ant hybrid TiO 2was prepared on the TT-Ant hybrid according to the literature method.79A mixture of TT-Ant (10 mg) and TiO 2 (90 mg, PC500, calcinated at 400 1C prior to use), was ground in a mortar and the resultant powder was dispersed in a mixture of water/acetonitrile (1/1, v/v), which was sonicated for 15 minutes. The solvent was then evaporated and the powderwas dried at 100 1C under vacuum. Results and discussion TT-Ant was synthesized by applying a Pd(0) catalyzed Suzuki coupling reaction between brominated TT ( TT-Br3 ) and boro- nated Ant ( Ant-Boron2 ) (Scheme 1). TT-Ant was obtained as a non-soluble precipitate due to its rigidity and extended cross-linking. Thus, the final material was collected by filtration, andfurther purified by Soxhlet extraction using MeOH and THF assolvents. Comparison of 13C nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectrums of the monomer in solution and TT-Ant in the solidstate indicated successful polymerization (Fig. 1a). Despite thecrosslinking in three directions, thanks to the tribromo mono- mer TT-Br3 ,N 2gas sorption measurements resulted in a negli- gible Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area as was observed from the poor N 2adsorption trend (Fig. 1b). This could be due to the presence of more linear segments formed duringthe reaction between TT-Br3 and Ant-Boron2 since a-positions of thienothiophene (TT) are more reactive compared to thep-bromo phenyl substituent of TT-Br3 . 80 Morphology of TT-Ant was examined by using a powder X- ray diﬀraction (pXRD) method as it is important for the HERperformance, 32,81which demonstrated an amorphous structure (Fig. S1, ESI †). X-ray photoelectric spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was performed to investigate its composition and chemicalstate (Fig. 2a and b). The peaks, recorded from the XPS studiesat 284.5 and 286.4, were assigned to the aromatic carbons of thesystem, among which the latter is due to the carbons of TTunits. 82,83The peak at 289.7 eV was attributed to carbonyl carbons, and their presence is probably due to the trappedimpurity within the hyper crosslinked TT-Ant (i.e.carbonates of K 2CO3stuck during the reaction).82,84The deconvolution of S 2p s h o w e dt w oc u r v e so ft h eS2 p 3/2peak at 163.8 eV and the S 2p 1/2 peak at 165.1 eV, indicating the presence of the C–S–C moietiesbelonging to the TT rings. 82,83Thus, it was concluded that the photoelectron signals and the binding energy (BE) values, arisenfrom TT-Ant polymer, corresponded to its S and C elements. Photophysical properties (UV-vis absorption and emission spectra) of TT-Ant were investigated in a dispersed solution in THF at room temperature (Fig. 3a and b and Table 1). Itsmaximum p–p* absorption ( l max) and the corresponding emis- sion wavelengths appeared at 404 and 486 nm (ex. at lmax), respectively, producing a high Stokes shift of 82 nm, whichcould be due to a fast relaxation from the excited state to theground state as a result of intramolecular energy-transfer between the TT and anthracene groups. Moreover, the Stokes shift of photoluminescence emission could imply a recombina-tion possibility of photo-induced electrons and holes throughfluorescence radiation along the high dimensional backbone.The optical band gap ( E optic)o f TT-Ant was calculated to be 2.72 eV from the onset threshold wavelength of the absorption Scheme 1 Synthesis of hyperbranched TT-Ant .Journal of Materials Chemistry C Paper Published on 02 September 2024. Downloaded by Istanbul Technical University on 5/16/2025 9:43:39 AM. View Article Online
