16112 |  J. Mater. Chem. C, 2024, 12, 16108–16119 This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2024spectra at 451 nm. The solid-state diffuse reflectance (DR) spectrum of TT-Ant was recorded and converted to Kubelka– Munk units for a better comparison with the UV-vis spectrum ofTT-Ant in THF dispersion. A similar DR response to the UV-vis was recorded in THF dispersion, demonstrating two broadabsorptions at 258 nm and 414 nm (Fig. 3b). A close value of2.51 eV band gap was calculated by using Tauc-plot derivedfrom DR data (Fig. S2, ESI †). The ionization potential (IP) and electron aﬃnity (EA) play roles in charge injection and charge transport of active materi- als in device fabrication. Even though the comparison of electrochemically detected EA and IP to the HOMO–LUMOlevels needs to be carefully approached, it is common practiceto calculate the band gap electrochemically ( E elec).85The elec- trochemical features of TT-Ant were investigated from its dis- persion in dichloromethane containing tetrabutylammoniumhexafluorophosphate (TBAPF 6) as an electrolyte. Cyclic voltam- metry (CV) measurements were conducted at a scan rate of 50 mV s/C01, using Pt wires as counter and working electrodes and Ag wire as a reference electrode. The oxidation and reduction onset potentials ( E(onset)ox ,E(onset)red ) were observed to be 1.18 and /C00.79 V, respectively (Fig. S3, ESI †). On the basis of the oxidation onset potential, the HOMO energies of TT-Ant were calculated to be /C05.58 and /C03.61 eV, respectively, using the equations of HOMO = /C0(Eonset(ox) + 4.40) (eV) and LUMO = /C0(E(onset)red + 4.40) eV (Table 1). The electrochemical band gap was calculated to be 1.97 eV from Eelec= IHOMO– LUMOI equation. Ground-state geometric optimization of TT-Ant was per- formed using density functional theory (DFT) at the B3LYP/6-31G (d,p) level, Gaussian 16 software (Fig. 4a). 86The calcu- lated highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) and thelowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) energy levels werecalculated to be /C05.13 and /C01.87, respectively (Fig. 4b). While the HOMO electrons were distributed mainly on the anthracenepart, located at the para position of the phenyl moiety of the TTcore, the LUMO was found to be delocalized through theanthracenes and the TT ring. On the basis of the optimized ground-state geometry, time- dependent DFT (TD-DFT) calculations were conducted in THF toinvestigate the absorption properties and theoretical band gap(Table 2). While the energy diﬀerence between the HOMO and LUMO levels ( D H–L) was calculated to be 3.26 eV, considering the lmax, the optical band gap ( Eg) was estimated from lonset of the UV-vis curve as 2.29 eV. The calculated absorption wave- length was centered at 400 nm (Fig. 4c), which was found to be ingood agreement with the experimentally obtained UV-visspectrum.Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) experiments were per- formed in a 3-neck reactor, which was illuminated by 150 W medium pressure Hg immersion lamp under the similar con- ditions reported previously. 79The system was cooled to 20 1Ct o prevent any possible eﬀects of the elevated temperatures fromthe light source. The carrier gas was argon, and the producedH 2was measured directly by a micro GC connected to the system. The initial experiments, involving only a mixture of TT- Antwith 10% MeOH (as a sacrificial agent), did not produce any detectable H 2. Thus, the polymeric material was combined with other (co)catalysts to facilitate the HER performance(Fig. 5a). Initially, TT-Ant was combined with TiO 2(TiO 2@TT- Ant) to form a hybrid photocatalyst. Even though the maximum production was 360 mmol g/C01h/C01in 2 h (Fig. 5b), the average H2production rate was found to be around 286 mmol g/C01h/C01as calculated from the amount accumulated over 5 h (correspondsto a photonic eﬃciency of 0.11%), which is 3 times higher thanthe average production of pristine TiO 2(87mmol g/C01h/C01, photonic eﬃciency 0.03%) under the same conditions (Fig. 5a). Time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) studies for TiO 2, TT-Ant , and their hybrid TiO 2@TT-Ant were conducted under UV light irradiation (at 372 nm) through a band-pass filtercentered at 500 nm, where both TiO 2and TT-Ant absorb and emit light, respectively (more details can be found in theExperimental part and Fig. S4, ESI †). Decrease of the fluoresce lifetime of TiO 2when it is mixed with TT-Ant (i.e.in the case of TiO 2@TT-Ant ) was observed probably due to the electron transfer from TiO 2to the polymeric backbone (Fig. S4a, ESI †). Such quenching was also reported previously with thiophenicpolymers. 87Considering these structural and spectral simila- rities, the Z-scheme mechanism88(Fig. S4b, ESI †) was proposed for the HER of TiO 2@TT-Ant under the UV light irradiation. On the other hand, partial segregation of the polymer and TiO 2composite was observed at the end of the reaction, which was probably leading to the unsteady H 2production rates when compared to other two competitors (Fig. 5b). Regardingan in-depth understanding, field emission scanning electronmicroscopy (FE-SEM) and the following elemental mappingwith energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) of TiO 2@TT- Ant, before and after the HER, were compared (Fig. S5–S8, ESI†). Despite the presence of encapsulated TT-Ant particles in both cases, the observed morphology became more similar to pristine TiO 2particles after the HER experiment (Fig. S5, S7 and S9, ESI †), which could be due to the segregation of the poly- meric particles and TiO 2during the HER. Besides TiO 2, Pt was also combined with TT-Ant to observe its eﬀect on hydrogen production. Addition of 1 w% Pt to TT-Ant in a mixture of methanol/water ( Pt@TT-Ant , see the Experimental Table 1 Optical and electronic data of TT-Ant Polymer UV maxa(nm) UV onset (nm) Fl maxa(nm) Stokes shift (nm) Dnb(cm/C01)Eopticc(eV) HOMOd(V) LUMOe(V) Eelecf(eV) TT-Ant 404 451 486 82 4176 2.72 /C05.58 /C03.61 1.97 aAbsorption and emission maxima i