This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2024 J. Mater. Chem. C, 2024, 12, 16108–16119 |  16113part for the details) led to a H 2production of 540 mmol per gram catalyst in the first hour, and the maximum H 2production rate reached 700 mmol g/C01h/C01after 2 h (Fig. 5a and b). The average H2production rate was around 660 mmol g/C01h/C01over 5 h of run (Fig. 5a), which corresponds to a 0.24% of photonic eﬃciency (Fig. 5d). Moreover, switching the light source oﬀ for 15 minafter the irradiation of an hour indicated that the H 2production of the system is a light-driven process since no H 2was evolved without any light exposure (Fig. 5c). Two independent long-termexperiments revealed a linear drop on the performance of Pt@TT- Ant,l e a d i n gt oH 2production rate around 400 mmol g/C01h/C01after 18 h (Fig. S10, ESI †). The reduction of the performance is highly probably due to casting-up of the photocatalyst on the reactor wall(Fig. S11c, ESI †) or diminishing of the sac rificial agent (MeOH) in time rather than decomposition since the decrease is very linearand the recorded infra-red (IR) spectrums before and after reac-tion of the catalyst were nearly the same (Fig S11 and S12, ESI †).The only significant diﬀerence between the IR spectrums wasthe peak around 1260 cm /C01, which could be due to the photodeposi- tion of Pt along the polymeric catalyst, Pt@TT-Ant (Fig. S12, ESI †).89,90 Interactions between the Pt co-catalyst and the main photo- catalyst play important role for the HER performance.91,92To investigate the behavior of the added Pt on TT-Ant , the Pt loaded samples were characterized by FE-SEM, transmissionelectron microscopy (TEM) and EDX spectrums. The backscat-tering images captured by using FE-SEM revealed Pt depositionon the surface of TT-Ant (i.e.Pt@TT-Ant ) as bright spots compared to the parent material (Fig. 6a and b, see Fig. S13 and S14 in the ESI †for more images). The overall SEM-EDX measurements on those bright segments confirmed Pt deposi- tion (Fig. S15 and S16, ESI †). In addition to detecting Pt on the surface of the polymeric catalyst, some residual Pd trappedinside the compound was also observed. Although the eﬀectof the residual Pd to the photocatalytic performances has beena discussion in the field either for linear 93or crosslinked94 polymers,95,96it appears that the trapped Pd is not active in our case since H 2production was not observed with the pristine TT-Ant without any Pt co-catalyst. A similar postulate was also made in the literature that the outer-sphere polymer/co-catalystinteractions plays a role in the HER while trapped co-catalystsare inactive. 97This could be speculated that water molecules cannot penetrate through the hydrophobic structure of the Fig. 4 (a) Optimized geometry of TT-Ant at the B3LYP/6-31G (d,p) level. (b) HOMO and LUMO diagrams calculated at the B3LYP/6-31G (d,p) level of theory. (c) The theoretical UV spectrum of TT-Ant . Table 2 The HOMO and LUMO energy level, orbital energy diﬀerences (DH–L) and absorption values calculated at the TD-B3LYP/6-31G (d,p) level of theory PolymerHOMO (eV)LUMO(eV)D H–L (eV)lmax (nm)lonset (nm)Eoptic (eV) TT-Ant /C05.13 /C01.87 3.26 400 540 2.29Paper Journal of Materials Chemistry C Published on 02 September 2024. Downloaded by Istanbul Technical University on 5/16/2025 9:43:39 AM. View Article Online
