16114 |  J. Mater. Chem. C, 2024, 12, 16108–16119 This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2024polymer to interact with the encapsulated Pd residues. On the other hand, the activity of in situ photo deposited Pt co-catalyst is attributed to their placement on the outer shell of the polymericmaterials. The argument was also supported by the TEM images,showing that the Pt particles are well distributed over thepolymeric photocatalyst (Fig. 6c, d and Fig. S17 and S18, ESI †). That being said, the spotted agglomeration helped us to run asuccessful TEM-EDX, which indicated a high population of thePt co-catalyst on the surface of Pt@TT-Ant (Fig. S19, ESI †). Conclusions In this work, a novel conjugated hyperbranched polymer, TT- Ant,h a v i n gt h i e n o [ 3 , 2 - b]thiophene (TT) and anthracene (Ant) groups, was designed and synthesized successfully. The poly-merization took place between the tribromo derivative of TT andthe diboronic acid ester functionalized Ant through Pd(0) cata-lyzed Suzuki coupling in 80% yield. Electronic and optic proper- ties of the polymer were investigated using UV-vis and fluorescence spectroscopy, cyclic voltammetry and solid-statediﬀuse reflectance spectroscopy. Solid, solution and predictedcomputational UV values were found to be in good agreement.The HER experiments, conducted using 1/9 (w/w) composite ofTT-Ant with TiO 2, resulted in a maximum 360 mmol g/C01h/C01H2production over two hours, the average production of which was286mmol g /C01h/C01over 5 h of accumulation, which is more than 3 times that of pristine TiO 2under the same conditions. Moreover, tests with 1 wt.% Pt co-catalyst produced a maximum 700 mmol g/C01h/C01H2, and the calculated average value over a 5 h run was around 660 mmol g/C01h/C01. Microscopic investigations before and after Pt addition revealed that the metal particles are well-distributed on the surface of the polymeric material making itan eﬃcient catalyst for photocatalytic hydrogen production fromwater. The work presented herein is a step forward in facilitating one of the best-performing building blocks in photovoltaics, namely, thienothiophene (TT), 37in the HER field, given the current perspectives in materials chemistry focusing on thecombination of both fields. 35Moreover, the work may help the development of anthracene-based porous polymers since themoiety is interesting for triplet–triplet annihilation photonupconversion (TTA-UC), 98which can enhance the efficiencies of such photocatalytic and photovoltaic processes.99 Data availability The authors confirm that the data supporting the findings ofthis study are available within the article and its ESI. †Raw data Fig. 5 (a) HER performance of TiO 2,TiO 2@TT-Ant ,a n d Pt@TT-Ant . (b) The change of H 2production rate of TiO 2,TiO 2@TT-Ant ,a n d Pt@TT-Ant specifically for the detected amount on the individual measurement ( ca.3.8 min time interval) (c) Light on–off HER experiment of Pt@TT-Ant.( d )% photonic efficiencies of TiO 2,TiO 2@TT-Ant ,a n d Pt@TT-Ant .Journal of Materials Chemistry C Paper Published on 02 September 2024. Downloaded by Istanbul Technical University on 5/16/2025 9:43:39 AM. View Article Online
