Applied Surface Science 631 (2023) 157577 Available online 20 May 2023 0169-4332/© 2023 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.Full Length Article A two-electron migration mechanism co-constructed by Kpassivation and defect engineering for efficient TiO2 photocatalytic hydrogen evolution Xinyu Xiaoa, Xing Wanga,b, Yun Lic,*, Yao Lia, Guangwei Suna, Ying Hana,b,* aLiaoning Key Lab of Lignocellulose Chemistry and BioMaterials, Liaoning Collaborative Innovation Center for Lignocellulosic Biorefinery, College of Light Industry and Chemical Engineering, Dalian Polytechnic University, Dalian 116034, China bState Key Laboratory of Biobased Material and Green Papermaking, Qilu University of Technology, Shandong Academy of Sciences, Jinan, 250353, China cChemistry & Chemical Engineering of College Yantai University, Yantai, 264005, China ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Kpassivation layer Defect healing Oxygen vacancies B-doping engineering Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction ABSTRACT As a promising candidate for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction, Pt/TiO 2 photocatalysts with low cost and high activity are considered to be one of the most ingenious strategies for obtaining both high-purity hydrogen and high-value products using inexhaustible sunlight as the sole driving force. In this work, a TiO2 microsphere with internal defect engineering co-constructed by oxygen vacancies (Ov) and B-doping was pre- pared by using a one-step solvent volatilization method, whose surface was cleverly modified by the K passivation layer. Compared with the hydrogen evolution activity of pure TiO2 (2.7 μmol/g/h), that of HB-TiO 2 was improved by 460% (124 μmol/g/h), while that of KHB-TiO 2 was improved by 1300% (352 μmol/g/h). The prominent performance is attributed to the accelerated carrier separation induced by the internal defect- engineering and the efficient two-electron migration mechanism assisted by the Kpassivation layer. On the premise of not destroying the internal defect engineering that facilitates carrier separation, the fascinating surface Kpassivation mechanism inhibits carrier recombination and improves visible light utilization by healing the electronic structure of the TiO2 surface and then using Pt-Ti metal bonds to directly migrate two- electron to Pt for the hydrogen evolution reaction. Our work demonstrates the promising strategy for the modification of defective TiO2 microspheres with Kpassivation layer, opening a new avenue for the con- struction of a two-electron migration mechanism for efficient hydrogen evolution reaction. 1.Introduction The global energy shortage, environmental pollution and greenhouse effect caused by the overconsumption of non-renewable fossil fuels have been recognized as serious issues that threaten the whole human society [1–3]. As a new generation of renewable and clean energy, the efficient exploitation of green hydrogen has been paid extensive attention by numerous researchers [4,5] . Direct conversion of inexhaustible solar energy into hydrogen source is considered as an appealing and prom - ising route for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution [6]. However, the currently reported photocatalysts are severely limited in large-scale in- dustrial production and practical applications due to their low effi- ciency, instability, and high price [7]. Fortunately, the emergence of various semiconductors has provided important theoretical technical support for the development of burgeoning photocatalysts by virtue of their effective utilization of visible light [8]. In particular, the abundant TiO2 has evolved as a popular photocatalyst due to its relatively large band gap (3.2 eV) and low conduction potential [9]. Its unique advan - tages are as follows: (1) The controllable morphologies can provide sufficient active sites for various applications; (2) The ionic radius of Ti4is close to that of most metal cations, which facilitates the imple - mentation of heteroatom-doping technology; (3) The adjustable energy band structure promotes the free electron storage in the conduction band; (4) The introduction of defect engineering such as oxygen va- cancies (Ov) enhances visible light absorption by building a new band gap in the forbidden band. The design of high-performance photocatalysts requires not only a precise regulation of the composition and electronic structure, but also *Corresponding authors at: Chemistry & Chemical Engineering of College Yantai University, Yantai, 264005, China. (Y Li).Liaoning Key Lab of Lignocellulose Chemistry and BioMaterials, Liaoning Collaborative Innovation Center for Lignocellulosic Biorefinery, College of Light Industry and Chemical Engineering, Dalian Polytechnic University, Dalian 116034, China. (Y Han). E-mail addresses: liyun605@126.com (Y. Li), hanying@dlpu.edu.cn (Y. Han). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Applied Surface Science u{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2k|���m! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apsusc.2023.157577 Received 7 April 2023; Received in revised form 3 May 2023; Accepted 19 May 2023
