Applied Surface Science 631 (2023) 157577 2the exploration of the dynamical behavior of photogenerated electrons/ holes by combining the modern instruments characterization (such as transient fluorescence spectroscopy, transient absorption spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy) with the simulation results of theoretical calculations [10]. Numerous researches have shown that the construction of defect engineering (Ov and heteroatom-doping) facili - tates the carrier separation in TiO2, providing abundant free electrons for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution [11–15]. It is noteworthy that the two-electron migration mechanism plays an integral role in the hydrogen evolution reaction [16]. The defect structure on the TiO2 surface will promote the photoelectrons recombination and restrict the two-electron migration to Pt to participate in the hydrogen evolution reaction [17]. Considering that, the surface healing about defective TiO2 is a necessary strategy to improve the activity of hydrogen evolution reaction, ensuring that the surface defects are healed to an electronic structure that facilitates two-electron migration without destroying the internal defect engineering [18–20]. Herein, we reported a TiO2 microsphere with Ov and B-doping en- gineering, whose surface was modified by Kpassivation layer. The intervention of TiO2 internal defect engineering contributes to accel - erate carrier separation and improve the visible light utilization, while the two-electron migration mechanism constructed with the aid of the Kpassivation layer enhances hydrogen evolution activity. Structural and morphological characterizations, electron reduction experiments and theoretical calculations were used to verify the mechanism of action of the Kpassivation layer and the reaction principle of photocatalytic hydrogen evolution. Compared with the hydrogen evolution activity of pure TiO2 (2.7 μmol/g/h), that of HB-TiO 2 was improved by 460% (124 μmol/g/h), while that of KHB-TiO 2 was improved by 1300% (352 μmol/ g/h). The KHB-TiO 2 is 51 times more active than commercial P25 in the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction. Our work not only provides a feasible surface modification strategy for the construction of a two- electron migration mechanism for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution, but also reserves technical support for the rational design and applica - tion of high-performance photocatalysts through a combination of modern instrumental characterization and theoretical calculations. 2.Preparation and characterization 2.1. Preparation of materials HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 were obtained by adding 5 mmol of sodium borohydride or potassium borohydride to 1 ml of tetrabutyl titanate and stirring for 30 min, then dropping into 50 ml of anhydrous ethanol and volatilizing at indoor temperature for 4 h. The solid products were annealed at 450 •C (N2 or Air), whose products are named as TiO2 (air atmosphere), HB-TiO 2 (N2 atmosphere) and KHB-TiO 2 (N2 atmosphere), respectively. 2.2. Characterization of materials The detailed parameters related to the structure and physicochem - ical properties of materials, electrochemical tests, and photocatalytic experiments were listed in the supplementary information (S2.1-S2.7). 3.Results and discussion The synthesis strategies of TiO2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 are shown in Scheme. 1, where the formation of Ov and B-doping engineering rely on the switching of annealing atmosphere (air or N2). The core of the sur- face passivation technology lies in the introduction of Kpassivation layer with defect-healing function through the targeted selection of the raw material and well-designed preparation process. The formation of Ti-H bonds and Ti-B bonds with the participation of NaBH 4 and KBH 4 achieves substitution of H and B for O, while they can be healed to Ti-O bonds in air to construct defect-free TiO2 [21]. The formation of Ti-O v and the retention of Ti-B bonds are involved in the design of defect engineering with the assistance of N2 protection [22]. Notably, Kwith a diameter of 0.133 nm is restricted to enter the Ti-O structural units (Ti: 0.068 nm), resulting in that Kis gradually deposited on the surface of B-doped TiO2 with Ov to form Kpassivation layer, which can heal the electronic structure of TiO2 to assist in establishing a two-electron migration mechanism for efficient hydrogen evolution. Simulta - neously, the retained internal defect engineering is not affected by the surface Kpassivation layer, which accelerates the separation of Scheme 1.Synthesis strategies of TiO 2, HB-TiO 2, and KHB-TiO 2.  X. Xiao et al.
