Applied Surface Science 631 (2023) 157577 4photogenerated carriers [11,23] . The effects of Ov, B-doping engineer - ing and Kpassivation are analyzed by comparing the hydrogen evo- lution activities of TiO2, HB-TiO 2, KHB-TiO 2 prepared in different annealing atmospheres, respectively. The XRD diffraction peaks of TiO2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 all correspond to anatase-TiO 2 (JCPDS No.21 –1272), as shown in Fig. 1a-c. Besides, the XRD pattern of KHB-TiO 2 shows the characteristic diffrac - tion peaks of rutile-TiO 2 (JCPDS No.21 –1276), and the crystalline phase composition of KHB-TiO 2 is similar to that of commercial P25-type photocatalyst [11,24] . Obviously, the diffraction peaks of impurities attributed to B2O3 and TiB2 are absent in all samples, which proves that the stability of the product compositions and the homogeneity of B- doping engineering. It is noteworthy that the characteristic diffraction peaks of HB-TiO 2 shifts to the direction of high angle leading to a decreased lattice constant, which is induced by the presence of Ov in the crystal structure that trigger the collapse of crystal unit cells [25]. Simultaneously, the B-doping engineering with small ionic radius (0.02 nm) is one of the inducing factors for the occurrence of this phenomenon. Moreover, a diffraction peak shift similar to that of HB- TiO2 occurs in KHB-TiO 2, presumably the surface deposition of Kdoes not interfere with the defect engineering induced by Ov and B-doping. The XRD spectra shows that Kdoes not enter the KHB-TiO 2 lattice, but only forms passivation layers on the surface [26]. The defect engineering in HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 was corroborated by Raman, FTIR spectroscopy and thermal weight loss analysis. As shown in the Raman spectra (Fig. 2a), the blue shift of the Eg1 absorption peaks of HB-TiO 2 (146 cm␀1) and KHB-TiO 2 (146 cm␀1) are found when they compare to TiO2 (141 cm␀1). The Ti-O bond stretching vibration frequencies in the FTIR spectra are decreased (HB-TiO 2: 584 cm␀1, KHB- TiO2: 577 cm␀1, and TiO2: 601 cm␀1, respectively). Meanwhile, almost the same mass loss (200 –400 •C, 2%) is observed in the thermal weight loss analysis. The above characterization analysis confirms that the Ov and B-doping engineering in KHB-TiO 2 are involved in the construction of defect engineering which will not be damaged by the Kpassivation layer, keeping the lattice spacing and atomic spacing constant [27]. The surface morphologies of TiO2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 were Fig. 3.(a-c) SEM images and (d-f) TEM images (scale bar 1 μm) of TiO 2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2, respectively; (g-h) EDS patterns of KHB-TiO 2 and HB-TiO 2.  X. Xiao et al.
