Applied Surface Science 631 (2023) 157577 5investigated by SEM and TEM, as shown in Fig. 3a-f. The results show that the three samples have uniform spherical morphology with a diameter of about 2 μm [28]. The Ov and B-doping engineering drive the microsphere surface tend to granular distribution (Fig. 3b and e) and the size remains almost constant, while the deposition of Kenhances the granularity of the microsphere surface (Fig. 3c). Moreover, it is clearly observed from the EDS energy spectrum that the B elements located in the KHB-TiO 2 covered with Kpassivation layers maintain a certain mapping intensity when it compares to that in HB-TiO 2, confirming that the retention of the B-doping engineering. As shown in Fig. 3g and h, the B element is uniformly distributed and no impurities are generated. The reason why there is no Napassivation layer in HB-TiO 2 is as follows. The ionic radius of K(0.133 nm) is similar to that of O2– (0.140 nm), while the ionic radius of Na(0.095 nm) differs significantly from that of O2–. The differences in the number of electron layers and ionic radius result in that the coordination capacity of Kand Ov is stronger than that of Na[29]. It is worth noting that the above analysis of the morphology and element distribution on the sample surfaces could not probe in depth the effect of the Kpassivation layer on the B-doping engineering. The intervention of Kpassivation layer will not heal the internal defect engineering co-constructed by Ov and B-doping, which can be judged by the XRD, Raman, FTIR spectra and thermal weight loss curves. There - fore, the further analyses are paid attention to the effect of Kpassiv - ation layer on B-doping engineering. To investigate the effect of Kpassivation layer on B-doping engi- neering, Ti 2p and O 1 s of TiO2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 are shown in the XPS spectra (Fig. 4a and b). The binding energies of Ti 2p in HB-TiO 2, KHB-TiO 2 are shifted toward lower values compared to TiO2, which probably attributed to the synergistic effect of the formation of Ti3with B-doping engineering to occupy the coordination orbitals of O elements [30]. The surface deposition of Kslightly increases the binding energy value of Ti 2p in KHB-TiO 2, demonstrating that the mechanism of K passivation layer to assist in the defect healing is probably by reducing the electron cloud density around Ti3. Simultaneously, the binding energy peak area ratio of Ov is decreased, which is related to the fact that Koccupies the free electrons adsorbed by the Ov and suppresses the number of unpaired electrons in the structure [31]. In the XPS spectra of B 1 s (Fig. 4d), almost no signal of element B was detected in KHB-TiO 2, which indicates that the Kpassivation layer achieves an all-around uniform coverage of the B-doping engineering. Furthermore, compared with HB-TiO 2, the large amount of B-doping engineering in KHB-TiO 2 is retained, which verifies the exceptional anti-interference of B-doping engineering with the intervention of Kpassivation layer (Fig. 4c). As shown in Fig. 5a, the hydrogen production efficiencies of HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 are 124 μmol/g/h and 352 μmol/g/h (λ F420 nm), which are higher than TiO2 by 460% and 1300%, respectively (all samples are deposited with 1% Pt). Meanwhile, compared with com- mercial P25 and Black-TiO 2, the hydrogen production activities of KHB- TiO2 are enhanced by 510% and 720%, respectively (Fig. 5b). Obvi- ously, Ov and B-doping engineering enhance the effective utilization of visible light by TiO2, and the construction of defect engineering assist Fig. 4.(a-c) Ti 2p, O 1 s and surface B-doping engineering ratios of TiO 2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 in the XPS spectra; (d) B 1 s of HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 in the XPS spectra. X. Xiao et al.
