Applied Surface Science 631 (2023) 157577 6HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 to maintain stable catalytic activity in the cycling experiments in Fig. S1 [32,33] . The above results indicate that the surface Kpassivation based on defect engineering is a feasible strategy to enhance the photocatalytic activity and hydrogen production efficiency. In this work, two-electron photocatalytic reduction reactions are designed (Fig. 5c and Fig. S2), whose catalytic activities are deter - mined by the degradation efficiency of two-electron methylene blue and the single-electron rhodamine-6 G. Notably, the construction of defect engineering in HB-TiO 2 accelerates the carrier separation efficiency in the two-electron reduction reaction, but its lower degradation efficiency than that of Black-TiO 2 is attributed to the inhibition of the two-electron migration mechanism on Pt by the Ov and B-doping engineering. In contrast, none of the samples exhibits photocatalytic activity in the single-electron reduction reaction. It is inferred that both HB-TiO 2 and Fig. 5.(a) Hydrogen production efficiencies of TiO 2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 in different times periods; (b) Hydrogen production efficiencies of different samples in 1 h; (c) Photocatalytic degradation efficiencies of methylene blue by KHB-TiO 2, HB-TiO 2, Black-TiO 2 and photolysis; (d) Photoelectron migration mechanism of KHB- TiO 2 and HB-TiO 2. Fig. 6.(a) and (b) Pt 4f and Ti 2p of KHB-TiO 2 in XPS spectra; (c) Assisted mechanism of Kon the migration of photoelectrons from TiO 2 to Pt; (d) EDS mappings of Pt in HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2. X. Xiao et al.
