Applied Surface Science 631 (2023) 157577 7KHB-TiO 2 possess the ability to enrich the two-electron to Pt surface, unfortunately, the Ov and B-doping engineering drive the two-electron separation on Pt surface and accelerate the recombination of carriers without the intervention of the Kpassivation layer [23]. It follows that the Kpassivation mechanism has a unique advantage in improving the hydrogen production efficiency of the KHB-TiO 2, as shown in Fig. 5d. The Kdeposited on the KHB-TiO 2 surface heals the structural defects of the missing two-electron migration mechanism on the surface caused by the adsorption of Ov on free electrons and the induction of electron cloud density enhancement triggered by B-doping engineering, ensuring that the electronic structure of the KHB-TiO 2 surface is the same as that of the TiO2 without defects. When a large number of two-electron induced by Ov and B-doping engineering migrate to the KHB-TiO 2 surface, the stable electronic structure healed by the Kpassivation layer will directly transfer the two-electron to Pt and be used for photocatalytic hydrogen evolution. The changes in chemical state of HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 whose surfaces are deposited with Pt are investigated by XPS spectra. The Pt 4f in the XPS spectra shown in Fig. 6a and Fig. S3, only Ptδis detected but not Pt0, indicating that the Pt is uniformly dispersed, which is also confirmed by the EDS in Fig. 6d [34]. The Ti 2p binding energy of KHB- TiO2 whose surface was deposited with Pt shifts 0.4 eV to lower energy levels (Fig. 6b), and the Pt deposition enhances the electron cloud density of the surface. This is attributed to the strong metal-support interaction (SMSI) to form the Pt-Ti metal bonds [35]. As shown in Fig. 6c, the Pt deposition sites are adjacent to the Ti atoms, while the K is mainly deposited in the vicinity of the B-doping sites and Ov, which means that Kwill preferentially match the free electrons. In contrast, the Ti 2p binding energy of HB-TiO 2 shifts to higher energy level by 0.1 eV after the deposition of Pt (Fig. S4). The B-Ti-Pt and Ov-Ti-Pt structures act as transport channels for the photogenerated electrons to move to- ward the B-doping sites and Ov, which reduces the electron cloud den- sity of the TiO2 surface and inhibits the fast migration kinetic process of photogenerated electrons on Pt. Therefore, compared with HB-TiO 2, KHB-TiO 2 exhibits more excellent photocatalytic hydrogen evolution activity with the assistance of Kpassivation layer. In the UV diffuse reflectance spectrum (Fig. 7a), the band gap widths of TiO2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 essentially remain the same. The dif- ference is that a new band gap provided by Ov is detected in the visible region (λ F420 nm) for HB-TiO 2, which will enhance the visible light utilization in synergy with the B-doping engineering [36]. The band gap structure of KHB-TiO 2 detected which is identical to that of TiO2 in the visible region confirms that Kpassivation layer possesses the defect- healing ability to homogenize the electronic structure of the KHB-TiO 2 surface with that of TiO2. The energy level structures shown in Fig. 7c correspond to the valence band position given by the valence band spectrum (Fig. 7b). Although the Kpassivation layer heals the defects of the KHB-TiO 2 surface, its internal defect structure is still retained, where the new band gap (1.05 eV) co-established by the Ov and B- doping engineering in the valence band enhances the carrier separation efficiency and visible light absorption [3]. The retention of internal defects is also confirmed by the electron paramagnetic resonance spec- trum (Fig. 7d), where the signal provided by Ov is still detected at G  2.003 [4]. Meanwhile, the valence band shifted to higher energy levels Fig. 7.(a-d) The UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra, valence band spectra, energy level structures and electron paramagnetic resonance spectra of TiO 2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2, respectively. X.