Applied Surface Science 631 (2023) 157577 9enhances the storage of internal carriers. It is noteworthy that the po- sitions of the conduction band and valence band of KHB-TiO 2 are located exactly between TiO2 and HB-TiO 2, which may be caused by the coex- istence of the complete electronic structure on the surface and the in- ternal defect engineering. And the passivation mechanism of Kis precisely to heal the distorted electronic structure on the surface on the basis of retaining the internal defects. The separation process of photogenerated carriers in different sam- ples is further illustrated by transient fluorescence spectra as shown in Fig. 8b, where the fluorescence lifetimes of TiO2, HB-TiO 2 and KHB-TiO 2 are 6.36 ns, 10.24 ns and 12.63 ns, respectively [11]. The results indi- cate that the photogenerated carriers on the surface of KHB-TiO 2 are efficiently separated, while the Ov and B-doping engineering accelerate the carrier separation and recombination on the surface of HB-TiO 2. And KHB-TiO 2 exhibits the smallest semicircular arc and the lowest electron transfer impedance in the AC impedance spectra (Fig. 8a). Meanwhile, the photocurrent of KHB-TiO 2 is 3.52 μA/cm2 (Fig. 8c), which is much higher than that of HB-TiO 2 (1.38 μA/cm2) and TiO2 (0.27 μA/cm2). Moreover, the fluorescence intensity of HB-TiO 2 is higher than that of KHB-TiO 2 in the photoluminescence spectrum (Fig. 8d), indicating that the rate of recombination between electrons and holes on the surface of HB-TiO 2 is faster, which is attributed to the synergistic effect of Ov and B-doping engineering [27]. Density of states calculation based on the first principle is used to investigate the effect of Kdeposition on Ov and B-doping engineering. In order to facilitate the analysis of the effect of the defect engineering on the energy band structure, the Fermi energy level is defined as 0 eV during the calculation. As shown in Fig. 9a, the Fermi energy level of anatase-TiO 2 is located at the bottom of the conduction band and be- longs to the N-type semiconductor, and its forbidden band width of about 3.2 eV is consistent with that reported in previous researches [37]. The construction of Ov in the TiO2 introduces a new band gap in the forbidden band (Fig. 9b), which corresponds to a new absorption peak in the visible region in the UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectrum (Fig. 7a) [38]. The deposited K, which takes the vicinity of the B-doping sites and Ov as the main deposition sites, will drive the absorption peak attributed to the Ov to move towards the valence band, resulting in a decrease of the surrounding electron cloud density and inhibiting the generation of holes (Fig. 9c). Similarly, the B-doping engineering drives the conduction band to move to a higher energy level (1.3 eV), which is the main reason for the increase in the number of electrons in the anion-doped TiO2 (Fig. 9d) [39]. The tendency of the conduction band to move to higher energy level (0.4 eV) will be weakened by the Kdeposited near the B-doped sites (Fig. 9e). In addition, the B-doping engineering will induce the appearance of a prominent small absorption peak at the top of the TiO2 valence band (the orange circle in Fig. 9d), which is caused by the electron migration driven by the B-Ti 3 structural unit from the valence band to the conduction band [40]. The defect structure co-constructed by the Ov and B-doping engi- neering in the TiO2 structural units enable the tendency of the con- duction band move to higher energy levels to show an extreme value (2.2 eV) (Fig. 9f). And the deposition of Kin the vicinity of both defect structures suppresses the tendency of the conduction band to move to higher energy levels, corresponding to a value decaying to 0.2 eV. However, the new band gap constructed by Ov and the density of states absorption peak induced by B-doped engineering are retained in the internal structure (Fig. 9g), which is consistent with the above analysis that Kpassivation only heals the electronic structure of TiO2 surface and does not destroy the internal defect engineering. It is noteworthy that the absorption peaks generated by Ov are shifted toward the valence band, while the absorption peaks induced by B-doping engineering are shifted toward the conduction band[41,42] . Therefore, the Kpassiv - ation mechanism is shown in Fig. 9h. Kcan balance the free electrons generated by the Ov, preventing the free electrons in the conduction band from participating in the carrier separation and inhibiting the generation of hole induced by the B-doping engineering. Theoretical calculations demonstrate that the deposited K, which takes the vicinity of the B-doping sites and Ov as the main deposition sites, to constructs K- Ov-Ti and O-K-B-Ti chemical bonds, healing the electronic structure of the TiO2 surface (Fig. 9h) [43]. Furthermore, the B-Ti 3 electron density is attenuated by the deposited K, thus inhibiting the recombination of surface photoelectrons. 4.Conclusion In this work, a well-designed TiO2 microspheres with internal defect engineering co-constructed by Ov and B-doping, whose surface is accu- rately adhered by the Kpassivation layer, was prepared for efficient photocatalytic hydrogen evolution. The introduction of the Ov and B- doping engineering can provide the free electrons for the photocatalytic hydrogen evolution process by promoting carrier separation, while K deposited on the TiO2 surface acts as a medium to rapidly transfer two- electron to Pt, thus realizing efficient utilization of visible light. The mechanisms of internal defect-engineering and Pt-Ti metal bonds acting on carrier separation and free electron transport were verified by XPS and theoretical calculations. The electron reduction experiments clarify that the intervention of Kpassivation layer contributes to the con- struction of a two-electron migration mechanism. Based on the density of states calculations, an in-depth elaboration of the principle of the K passivation layer to heal the electronic structure of the KHB-TiO 2 surface and retain the internal defect engineering is given. The core of the technology of Kpassivation layer is to inhibit the increase of electron cloud density caused by B-doping engineering and balance the free electrons adsorbed by Ov. The KHB-TiO 2 is 130 times more active than TiO2 and 51 times more active than commercial P25 in the photo - catalytic hydrogen evolution reaction. What ’s truly fascinating about the Ksurface passivation technology is that it provides a novel surface modification strategy for TiO2 as a promising photocatalyst for efficient hydrogen evolution. CRediT authorship contribution statement Xinyu Xiao: Investigation, Data curation, Writing – original draft. Xing Wang: . Yun Li: . Yao Li: . Guangwei Sun: . Ying Han: Supervision. Declaration of Competing Interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Data availability The data that has been used is confidential. Acknowledgments This research was funded by National Natural Science Foundation of China (No. 22278046), Liaoning Education Department Project (Nos. J2020038 and LJKZ0531), Liaoning University Innovation Talent Pro- gram in 2020, the Special Financial Grant from the China Postdoctoral Science Foundation (No. 2020 T130464) and Dalian high level talent innovation support program (Dalian Youth Science and Technology Star Project Support Program) (No. 2021RQ110), the Foundation (No. GZKF202212) of State Key Laboratory of Biobased Material and Green Papermaking, Qilu University of Technology, Shandong Academy of Sciences, Supported by Graduate Innovation Fund of Dalian Polytechnic University. X. Xiao et al.
