Chemical Engineering Journal 431 (2022) 133287 Available online 3 November 2021 1385-8947/© 2021 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.A WO x mediated interface boosts the activity and stability of Pt-catalyst for alkaline water splitting Di Yina, Yun-Dong Caoa, Dong-Feng Chaid, Lin-Lin Fana, Guang-Gang Gaoa,*, Ming-Liang Wanga, Hong Liua, Zhenhui Kangb,c,* aSchool of Materials Science and Engineering, University of Jinan, 250022, Jinan, China bInstitute of Functional Nano & Soft Materials (FUNSOM), Jiangsu Key Laboratory for Carbon-Based Functional Materials & Devices, Soochow University, 199 Ren’ai Road, Suzhou, 215123, Jiangsu, China cMacao Institute of Materials Science and Engineering, Macau University of Science and Technology, Taipa 999078, Macau SAR, China dCollege of Chemistry and Chemical Engineering, Qiqihar University, 161006, Qiqihar China ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Polyoxometalate Bifunctional electrocatalysts Alkaline water splitting Nanodot WO x interface ABSTRACT Water splitting offers a clean way to produce hydrogen, in which, an efficient catalyst interface is one of the key factors to boot the activity and stability. Here, using polyoxotungstate (POT) as precursor, Pt-based bifunctional catalysts were fabricated, which are composed of Pt, WO x and graphene (Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO). The Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO (1.58% Pt-loading) catalyst shows an enhanced catalytic activity, namely, overpotentials of 37 mV for Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and 174 mV for Oxygen evolution reaction (OER), and achieves a working voltage of 1.55 V at 10 mA cm␀2 for water splitting in alkaline-medium. Electrochemical analyses and DFT calculation suggest that the WO x mediated interface (Pt0⋅⋅⋅O ␀W) plays a key role to enhance the electron transfer and reduce the activation energy for both HER and OER. Moreover, this kind of Pt0⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface enhances the Pt based catalyst stability in alkaline-medium significantly. 1.Introduction Electrochemical water splitting, including the two half-reactions of water splitting reaction, Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and Oxy- gen evolution reaction (OER), is the most prospective method of hydrogen production [1–8]. Pt is generally regarded to be one of the best catalysts for HER, especially in acid media [9–18]. In alkaline electro - lyte, OER has a high reactivity, while, the present catalysts need a low interface electron transition barrier to improve their catalytic activity. An efficient way to enhance the kinetics of Pt in alkaline media is a combination of Pt with the metal oxide substrates. Among many metal oxides, WO 3 is known to be capable to constitute a non-stoichiometric and electrically conducting HxWO 3 compound in solution which makes WO 3 as a potential HER electrocatalyst [19–22]. The electron transition band-gap between oxygen 2p and tungsten 3d orbitals of WO 3 is cramped due to the small band-gap between oxygen p-band center and Fermi level [23,24] . For OER, the O2␀species can occur the adsorption or desorption on the WO 3 lattice, resulting in an easily occurred electron exchange between the oxygen p-band center and the Fermi level of WO 3 [25]. WO 3 should be a promising co-electrocatalyst that facilitates charge transfer in electrochemical reactions. However, WO 3 often forms a Schottky barrier at the surface-interface between electrocatalyst and electrode or electrolyte, which leads to a poor overpotential for HER or OER process [26]. On the other hand, both theoretic and laboratorial achievements indicate that the production of oxygen vacancies (OV) on the surface of the electrocatalyst can change the electronic construction, charge denseness and conversion in the p-band center of WO 3, thus increasing the inherent electron delivery frequency and catalytic per- formance [27,28] . While, the utility of oxygen vacant WO 3␀x for elec- trocatalytic water splitting, especially loading on a higher conductivity surface, remains unreported. In this system, it is anticipated that the contact of Pt0 particles on the WO 3 at atomic level, which will enhance the activity of active sites and reduce the interface electron transition barriers significantly. As an important polyoxometalate (POM) family [29–32], poly- oxotungstate (POT) can be converted into more stable WO 3 species. *Corresponding authors at: School of Materials Science and Engineering, University of Jinan, 250022, Jinan, China (G.G. Gao); Institute of Functional Nano & Soft Materials (FUNSOM), Jiangsu Key Laboratory for Carbon-Based Functional Materials & Devices, Soochow University, 199 Ren’ai Road, Suzhou, 215123, Jiangsu, China (Z.H. Kang). E-mail addresses: mse_gaogg@ujn.edu.cn (G.-G. Gao), zhkang@suda.edu.cn (Z. Kang). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Chemical Engineering Journal u{�~zkw! s{yo| kro>! ÐÐÐ1 ow�o�to~1m{y 2w{mk�o2mou! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2021.133287 Received 26 September 2021; Received in revised form 25 October 2021; Accepted 27 October 2021
