Chemical Engineering Journal 431 (2022) 133287 2During the transformation of POTs, the reduction of tungsten atoms can partly induce the W–O bonding breakage to facilitate the oxygen va- cancies on WO 3 surface. Especially, the coordination between PtII ion and POT anion allows their atomic level contact, which boosts the further reduction of PtII ion to form small Pt0 nanoparticles on the WO 3 surface. In this paper, a new structure-defined cis-platin modified Dawson-type POM complex of K6[P2W17O61{cis-Pt (NH 3)2}2] 32H 2O (P2W17Pt2) has been successfully fabricated by reaction of cis-platin and mono-lacunary Dawson-type POM. Then, through photochemical reduction, the in-situ generated P2W17Pt2@rGO hybrid can be achieved by anchoring POM on the reduced graphene (rGO) substrate. In the subsequent calcination process, the P2W17Pt2@rGO hybrid forms a new ternary hybrid of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO, which exhibits excellent electro - catalytic water splitting performance due to the combination of surface small Pt0 nanoparticles on the WO 3␀x substrate, which shows the better HER and OER activities than that of 20% commercial Pt/C in alkaline medium. This work unveils an interface effect mechanism to design highly efficient Pt catalyst with Pt ␀WO x ␀C interface for alkaline water splitting which facilitates the electron transfer and the ultra-low electron transition barrier for HER and OER, and results in an activity surpassing that of commercial 20% Pt/C with Pt O ␀C interface. 2.Experimental section 2.1. Reagents and materials Graphene oxide (GO, 99%), Pt/C and cis-platin (cis-Pt (NH 3)2Cl2) were obtained from Aladdin Ltd. Silver nitrate (AgNO 3) and sodium tungstate (Na2WO 4) was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. Isopropyl alcohol was purchased from Tianjin Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute. Deionized water was used in all experi - ments. There was no further purification of all the reagents and solvents. 2.2. Synthesis of P2W17Pt2 AgNO 3 (0.136 g, 0.8 mmol), cis-Pt (NH 3)2Cl2 (0.121 g; 0.4 mmol) and Na2WO 4 (0.132 g, 0.4 mmol) was dissolved in 8 mL water (pH 3.7). After stirring for 1 day at 25 •C, a yellow precipitate was formed. The precipitate was removed off through a membrane filter (0.2 μm). The solution kept stirring for 12 h in an ice bath, and then crystallized in dark for one week to obtain colorless crystals of K6[P2W17O61{Pt(NH 3)2}2] 32H 2O (P2W17Pt2) (yield 25% based on Pt). The lattice and coordinated water molecules in P2W17Pt2 were determined by thermogravimetric (TG) analysis (Fig. S1). Elemental Anal. Calcd for K6H76N4Pt2O93P2W17 (5432.56): H, 1.41; N, 1.03; Pt, 7.18; W, 57.53 (%). Found: H, 1.48; N, 1.09; Pt, 7.24; W, 57.57 (%). 2.3. Synthesis of P2W17Pt2@rGO, Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO and WO3␀x@rGO An aqueous solution containing 0.1 mg mL␀1 GO, 0.25 mM P2W17Pt2 and 2.5 mL isopropanol was added into a 1 cm thick quartz cuvette and illuminated by UV light for 10 min. Then, the precipitate was centri - fuged and rinsed with ethanol for three times. After being dried under vacuum for 5 h, the P2W17Pt2@rGO composite can be obtained. By calcination of P2W17Pt2@rGO at 900 •C for 2 h in argon atmosphere (the heating rate was 3 •C min␀1), the Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO catalyst was obtained. The WO 3␀x@rGO sample was obtained by the above experimental method, in which P2W17Pt2 was replaced by P2W17. 2.4. Synthesis of other contrastive samples The Pt-WO 3 composite was obtained by calcination of P2W17Pt2 at 900 •C for 2 h in argon atmosphere. Pt/C was dispersed into aqua regia for 2 days to obtain the carbon matrix sample. 2.5. Electrochemical tests HER and OER electrocatalytic performance of the fabricated elec- trodes was tested on an electrochemical workstation (CHI 660E) in a three-electrode configuration. The loading of the electrocatalyst on GCE surface is about 0.2 mg cm␀2. All reported potentials in this paper were calibrated to RHE. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were carried out for the sheet samples (15 MPa for 30 s, thickness of 1 mm) sandwiched between two platinum electrodes (3 mm diameter). The cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests for the solid samples were also conducted by two platinum electrodes, in which each powder sample (30 mg) mixed with 8 mg NaClO 4, 15 μL water and 30 μL ethanol. 2.6. Characterization instruments Single-crystal diffraction data were collected on an Agilent Tech- nology Eos Dual system with focusing multilayer mirror optics and a Mo Kα source of λ 0.71073 Å. The morphologies of the samples were studied on the scanning electron microscopy (SEM, JEOL JSM-6330F), transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL 2010, 200 kV). The X- ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were performed on Rigaku/Max-2550 with Cu Kα radiation (λ 1.54060 Å). The element distribution was measured by Energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) on JEOL JSM-6330F SEM. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) scans were carried on multifunctional imaging electron spectrometer (Thermo ESCALAB 250XI). The elemental analyses of H and N were conducted on a Vario EL III elemental analyzer, and those of W and Pt were analyzed on a Jarrel- AshJ-A1100 (ICP) atomic emission spectrometer. The Electron para- magnetic resonance spectrum (EPR) was carried out using an Endor - spectrometer (JEOL ES-ED3X). 3.Results and discussion 3.1. Structural description of P2W17Pt2 As shown in the Fig. S2, single-crystal X-ray structure analysis in- dicates that P2W17Pt2 is composed of the mono-lacunary [P2W17O61]10␀ polyoxoanion, which is derived from classical saturated Dawson type [P2W18O62]6␀precursor by leaving of one W O group. Two dech- lorinated cis-platin molecules just sit on the vacancy site parallelly and are stabilized by Pt–O bonds. The distances of Pt–O bonds are in the range from 1.96 Å to 2.06 Å, which are closed to the reported values for cis-platin-POM complexes (1.99 ~ 2.01 Å) [33]. The crystallographic data is listed in Table S1. It should be noted that only Keggin-type POM- cis-platin complexes have been obtained the single-crystal structures. While, P2W17Pt2 is the first example of Dawson type Pt-substituted POM compound. The Pt–O bonding distances of P2W17Pt2 are slightly longer than those of Keggin-type POM- cis-platin complexes [34]. This feature may relate to the fact that the structure of Dawson type polyoxoanion is slightly looser than those of Keggin polyoxoanions. As shown in Fig. S2, the P2W17Pt2 polyoxoanion are interconnected by Kions (center of pink polyhedron) to form 3D framework. 3.2. Synthesis and characterization The synthetic procedure of electrocatalyst comprised of Pt-WO 3␀x nanodots anchored on rGO is diagramed in Scheme 1. At the first step of the synthesis, P2W17Pt2 is anchored on the exterior of rGO by a photo - reduction evolution. During this reaction, the conversion of GO to rGO is accomplished by the assistance of photo-reduced P2W17Pt2. In order to prove that the P2W17Pt2 has been successfully anchored on the surface of rGO, powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) spectra were carried out. Fig. S3 illustrates the PXRD patterns of GO nanosheets and P2W17Pt2@rGO hybrid. The characteristic reflection peak of GO located at 2θ 10.6• attaches to the atomic-level defects (sp3 hybridization bonding), which D. Yin et al.
