Chemical Engineering Journal 431 (2022) 133287 3is caused by oxygen-containing functional groups affiliated on each side of the graphite film [35]. However, the characteristical diffraction at 10.6•is fully eliminated, and a fresh wide peak can be noticed at about 25.1•on rGO, which suggests the successful preparation of rGO through the reduced P2W17Pt2 [36]. Besides, the broad diffraction peak at 25.1• indicates the order of the sample along the stacking direction is poor, indicating that the sheet is mostly constituted of separate layers of rGO [37]. So as to study the surface characteristic changes of the synthesized P2W17Pt2@rGO, Raman tests were carried out. Raman scattering spec- troscopy can generally provide valuable information on carbon and reflect the structural changes from GO to rGO. Fig. S4 reveals the Raman shifts of GO and P2W17Pt2@rGO. The G band at 1582 cm␀1 and the D band at 1342 cm␀1 are perceived for the GO sheets. After anchoring P2W17Pt2, the G band of rGO in P2W17Pt2@rGO diverts from 1582 cm␀1 to 1593 cm␀1 as a result of the fact that the distribution of oxygen- involving functional groups is decreased and that the graphene system is reconstructed [38]. The ID/IG intensity ratio of rGO in P2W17Pt2@rGO (1.02) is higher than that of GO (0.848), which reveals that the surface oxygen-involving functional groups are decreased by the assistance of P2W17Pt2 [39]. This reduction process is found to be fast, with the light brown color of GO turned into black of rGO after 2 h reaction. The color change is presented in Fig S5, indicating that GO in P2W17Pt2@rGO is reduced to rGO [40]. This effect is compatible with the consequences of PXRD and Raman characterization. Under UV light, P2W17Pt2 can be effortlessly reduced in the existence of a sacrifice-agent due to the fact that it possesses excellent redox properties. Then the reduced P2W17Pt2 can further react with oxygen-containing functional groups on GO to recover its original state. To confirm the existence of P2W17Pt2, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) exploration was also executed. The survey spectrum of P2W17Pt2@rGO confirms the existence of W, Pt, O, and C composition (Fig. S6). The aforementioned analyses indicate the efficient anchor of P2W17Pt2 on the surface of the rGO substrate. Due to the existence of PtII ion, but not the Pt0 metallic phase, P2W17Pt2@rGO does not show the substantial HER or OER activities. As a result, the following calcination treatment is necessary for obtaining the desired Pt0 nanosized particles on the surface of tungsten oxide. After P2W17Pt2@rGO precursors were heated to 900 •C in Ar for 2 h, new Pt0-containing nanocomposite of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO can be acquired. The structure of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO was characterized in detail by PXRD pat- terns. As displayed in Fig. S7, the observed peaks located at 23.08, 23.58, 24.27, 26.55, 28.84, 33.31, 34.15, 35.59, 42.47, 47.37, 48.35 and 49.93•are indexed to (002), (020), (200), (120), (112), (022), (202), (122), (023), (004), (040) and (400) facets of WO 3, respectively (PDF#83 –0950). No diffraction peaks of metallic Pt0 can be found in the PXRD pattern, illustrating that the size of Pt0 is too small to be detected by the PXRD measurement. The morphologies and structures of P2W17Pt2@rGO nanosheets and Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO nanocomposite were further explored by SEM and TEM (Fig. 1a-1d and S8). The SEM portraits show the surface topographies of P2W17Pt2@rGO (Fig. S8) and Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO (Fig. 1a). We can obvi- ously see that the large-scale rGO nanosheets of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO present crumpled waves compared with original GO nanosheets, indicating the efficient reduction of GO. Meanwhile, typical TEM and HRTEM images of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO (Fig. 1b–1d) show that the nanodots with an approximately 20 ~ 30 nm in diameter is uniformly dispersed on the outer side of rGO, which is consistent with the SEM image. HRTEM image indicates the lattice fringes with the interspace of 0.365 nm, consistent with (200) the lattice face of WO 3. And the spherical Pt0 Scheme 1.Schematic illustration of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO fabrication. Fig. 1.SEM (a), TEM (b), HRTEM (c, d) images and elemental mapping images (e) of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO.  D. Yin et al.
