Chemical Engineering Journal 431 (2022) 133287 4nanoparticles (ca. 2 nm) are highly scattered on WO 3 exterior. Furthermore, Fig. 1d shows a further structural analysis of the Pt0 nanoparticles. The lattice fringes with the interspace of 0.224 nm are ascribed to the Pt0 (111) lattice face. It confirms the crystalline char- acteristic of Pt0 particles. The existence of Pt0 is additionally affirmed by the energy dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS) (Fig. S9). The corre - sponding elemental mapping images show a satisfactory clear overall arrangement of C, O, Pt, and W (Fig. 1e). Element analysis further shows the content information of each element in the Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO hybrid (Table S2), in which the ratio of Pt is about 1.58%. This result is consistent with the ICP results (Table S3). XPS is used for characterizing the component and the valent condi - tion of the elements in the Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO hybrid. As shown in Fig. 2a, the main bound energies of W 4f7/2 and W 4f5/2 is evaluated to be 35.3 eV and 37.3 eV separately with the ΔE of 2.0 eV, demonstrating the 6 valent condition of W [41,42] . This is consistent with the recorded detail of W 4f7/2 and W 4f5/2 in WO 3 [43]. In addition to the above peaks, the other two peaks are 33.9 eV and 31.5 eV, respectively, which correspond to the W5and W4states in the oxide, respectively [44,45] . Corre - spondingly, the XPS spectrum of O 1 s can be fitted as three divisions (as shown in Fig. S10), which are ascribed to oxygen-vacancy species (OV: 531.4 eV), lattice oxygen species (OL: 530.6 eV) and the chemisorbed oxygen species (OC: 532.5 eV) [46,47] . The above results show that there are OV species on the surface. In addition, the Pt 4f spectra (as shown in Fig. 2b) of the Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO shows that Pt atoms are in a reduced state with a bound energy of Pt 4f5/2 at 74.1 eV and Pt 4f7/2 at 70.8 eV, which is close to that of metallic Pt0. This result suggests that the PtII coordinated with POTs has been reduced. Moreover, contrasted with Pt/C, the bound energy (4f7/2) for Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO displays a slight passive skewing of ca. 0.4 eV, reflecting strong interactions between Pt0 and WO 3␀x species. Such a phenomenon has been beforehand noticed for the noble metal supported on metal oxide substrates, such as Pt/ Fe3O4 [48], Au/Fe 2O3 [49] and Au/SnO 2 [50] etc. The change in binding energy of precious metal is considered to be the result of metal- carrier interactions, which leads to the charge transmission from the carrier to the metal [48–51]. Whereafter, Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) as a practical way for probing the unpaired electron has also been applied to study oxygen vacancies in Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO. No EPR signal can be detected for WO 3 owing to the existence of W6with saturated coordination mode (Fig. S11). In comparison, the strong paramagnetic EPR signal is observed for Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO, indicating the reduction of W6to form W5that may involve to the occurrence of oxygen vacancy. To explore the interface effect on the electrochemical activity, the CV and the EIS measurements were also conducted for different samples. As can be seen from Fig. 3a, the CV curves display the obvious redox peaks from the voltage range of ␀0.2 V to 0.6 V for both Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO and WO 3␀x@rGO. In contrast, no obvious redox peaks can be observed in both Pt/C and the carbon black substrate (without Pt0). Such results indicate that Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO possesses more active redox sites due to that Pt0 is located on the WO 3␀x surface to construct a more active Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface. It can be also inferred that the redox processes (two redox couples of 0.37 V/0.30 V and 0.05 V/0.15 V as shown in Fig. 3a red line) of WO 3␀x are conducive to increase the electron transfer paths for Pt0 reactive site, by which the activation energy of the catalytic reaction can be lowered. Moreover, the EIS analysis reveals that the conductivity of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO is the lowest one (1.8 Ω) as compared with the carbon black substrate (6.3 Ω), WO 3␀x@rGO (7.1 Ω) and even Pt/C (2.4 Ω) samples (Fig. 3b). On one hand, this result indicates that the oxygen vacancy defects can promote the conductivity and reduce the resistance of charge transfer process. On the other hand, due to the existence of WO 3␀x interval layers, the Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface shows the low electron transition barrier and provides a favorable HER and OER kinetics (Fig. 3c). The above results indicate that the Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface of Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO is more efficient to promote the catalytic activity than that of Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface for commercial 20% Pt/C, which opens a new strategy to fabricate highly active Pt0 electrochemical catalysts based on an interface structure design. Moreover, the electrochemical double-layer capacitance (Cdl) anal- ysis was performed to evaluate the electrochemical surface area (ECSA) of synthesized catalysts (Fig. S12 and Fig. S13). The Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO catalyst delivers a higher Cdl value of 3.49 mF than that of Pt-WO 3 (1.46 mF) and Pt/C (2.63 mF), indicating that the introduction of Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface could substantially increase the active site density of the Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO catalyst. 3.3. Electrochemical performance The catalytic performance of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO for HER is carried out by evolving linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) measurements in the freshly obtained 0.5 M H2SO4 aqueous solution at a scan rate of 5 mV s␀1. For contrast, the Pt-WO 3 composite has been obtained by calcina - tion of P2W17Pt2 under high temperature. The characterizations of the prepared Pt-WO 3 composite are displayed in Fig. S14. As we can see that Pt-WO 3 has the phase of WO 3 with non-uniform morphology. Fig. 4a shows the LSV curves of Pt-WO 3, P2W17Pt2@rGO, Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO and Pt/C. Among these samples, Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO exhibits an ultrahigh ac- tivity with the lowest overpotentials of 13 mV and 73 mV at 10 and 100 mA cm␀2, respectively, which are both smaller than those of 20% Pt/C (29 mV and 96 mV at 10 and 100 mA cm␀2, respectively).