Chemical Engineering Journal 431 (2022) 133287 5the Tafel plots of Pt-WO 3, P2W17Pt2@rGO, Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO and Pt/C, which are evolved from the LSV curves (Fig. 4a). The Tafel slope value (35 mV dec-1) for Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO is the smallest one as compared to that of other samples. This value is even lower than that of Pt/C (45 mV dec␀1). So as to examine the durability of the catalysts, CV studies have been performed with Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO. As exhibited in the inset of Fig. 4c, the polarization curve after recycling 5000 cycles holds an almost identical curve to that of the beginning measurement. Also, Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO displays an enduring durability in the process of 14 h managed-current splitting at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 (Fig. 4c). All these results confirm that Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO nanocomposite displays an enduring durability in the course of electrocatalysis. The application of electrocatalysts in electrolytes with wide pH values broadens their application range. As a result, the HER activities of the samples are also evaluated in 0.5 M Na2SO4 (pH 7) and 1.0 M KOH (pH 14) elec- trolytes. As denoted in Fig. 4d and Fig. S15, Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO shows higher current density in both neutral and basic solutions. It can be seen that Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO keeps higher HER efficiency (16 mV and 37 mV at 10 mA cm␀2) than that of 20 wt% Pt/C (45 mV and 71 mV at 10 mA cm␀2) in 0.5 M Na2SO4 and 1.0 M KOH, respectively. Typically, the increase of the Pt utilization efficiency is a central challenge for all re- ported Pt-based catalysts, which demands a major leap in the Pt mass activity [52,53] . Hence, in this case, the mass activity was investigated for Pt-driven reactions. Fig. S16 shows the comparison of mass activity that is normalized for all of the samples to the Pt loading at η value of 50 mV. As can be observed, Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO reaches 16.0 A mg␀1 Pt, 15 A mg␀1 Pt and 14 A mg␀1 Pt under acidic, neutral and alkaline conditions, respectively. These values are 23, 30 and 46 times greater than those of Pt/C. Since electrocatalysts are usually used under alkaline conditions, the electrocatalytic stability of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO was examined by the method of chronoamperometric response in 1.0 M KOH (Fig. S17). Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO displays an enduring durability over 12 h of continuous operation at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2. After the stability test, the structural information of the Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO catalyst was further studied by PXRD analysis (Fig. S18). It can be observed that the process of electrocatalysis does not result in the shift for the diffraction peaks. XPS analyses before and after electrocatalytic reaction were also carried out (Fig. S19). Notably, XPS analysis clearly reveals that the W species almost retain their peak locations, while the peaks at the binding energy of 70.8 eV and 74.0 eV of the Pt 4f have positive shift due to the partial oxidation of Pt during the process of electrocatalysis. These findings suggest that the Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO catalyst can maintain long-term sta- bility in practical applications. It is desired that the OER electrocatalysts coordinate with HER Fig. 3.Cyclic voltammograms (a) and EIS curves (b) of different catalysts. (c) Schematic diagram for Pt/C and Pt-WO 3␀x@GO interfaces.  D. Yin et al.
