d by applying Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO as the anode and cathode in 1.0 M KOH for overall water splitting. As demonstrated in Fig. 6a, to achieve a current density of 10 mA cm␀2, a potential of about 1.55 V is needed, which is extremely superior than that of the commercial Pt/C (1.60 V, as shown in Fig. 6a). This electrochemical-behavior is compared with noble-metal-based electro - catalysts. For instance, Ir-based catalyst reported by Zhou et al. can reach an applied potential of 1.60 V at 10 mA cm␀2 in 1.0 M KOH electrolyte, which can be employed over 5 h (Table S4). For Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO, the bubbles are generated on both electrodes at 1.55 V (the inset of Fig. 6a). Moreover, the chronoamperometric response is performed to evaluate the durability of the Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO at a constant potential of 1.55 V (Fig. 6b). It is measured that 93.3% of the initial potential is maintained even after 14 h. These results suggest that the stability is significantly enhanced by the interactions between the Pt0 nanoparticles and WO 3␀x matrix. In order to explore the surface effective species of the electrocatalysts under the processes of HER and OER, in situ Raman-electrochemical spectral studies of controllable electrochemical overpotentials were conducted in 1.0 M KOH electrolyte. The Raman frequencies at 2100 cm␀1 under the reaction process as shown in Fig. S23 can be ascribed to the Raman shifts of Pt ␀H* species [54,55] . The vibration intensities of Pt ␀H* become larger when the applied negative potentials increase during HER process. The formed species of Pt ␀H* can further react with the neighboring water molecules to generate H2. For OER, the OH␀ group from the electrolyte is adsorbed on the surface site of the catalyst forming H*O ␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO intermediates, which are then succes - sively transformed into *O ␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO and H*OO ␀Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO. Finally, the formed reactive H*OO ␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO intermediate can react with OH␀groups to produce O2. The proposed -13-12-11-10 -0.1 0.0 