Chemical Engineering Journal 431 (2022) 133287 7HER and OER processes in this work are as follows: HER process (cathode): H2O Pt e␀→ H*␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO OH␀ H2O H*␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO e- → Pt H2 OH␀ OER process (anode): Pt-WO 3␀x OH␀→ H*O ␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO e␀ H*O ␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO OH␀→ O*␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO H2O e␀ O*␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO OH␀→ H*OO ␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO e␀ H*OO ␀Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO OH␀→ Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO O2 H2O e␀ 3.4. DFT calculations First-principles calculation derived from density functional theory (DFT) was carried out to clarify the origin of the prominent HER and OER activity of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO. Generally, dissociation of water to form H* and OH* intermediates is the key process for alkaline water splitting. Firstly, the adsorption and dissociation of H2O are determined. The adsorption configuration and adsorption energy (Eads) of H2O on different sites of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO are illustrated in Fig. 7a. The calcu - lated Eads of H2O molecules on Pt, W and O (Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface) sites of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO is ␀0.15 eV, ␀0.10 eV and ␀0.36 eV, respectively. The O sites (Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface) display a higher binding strength than that on Pt or W site, indicating that the H2O molecules prefer to adsorb on the interface O sites of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO. Furthermore, as the most important step, the energy barriers of water dissociation for WO 3 and pristine Pt0 were both higher (F0.8 eV) than that of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO, as shown in Fig. S24 and Fig. S25, which hinders the O–H bond breaking in water to form H* and OH* intermediates and leads to sluggish kinetics. Notably, in the case of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO, the water dissociation energy barrier is calculated as 0.68 eV on the Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W site. This result demonstrates that the Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface in Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO are more effective active centers for cleaving O–H bond to form OH* and H*. The configuration- determined optimization further demonstrates that the dissociative OH* can be adsorbed on another Pt site preferentially (Fig. 7b). This adsorption configuration can reduce the competitive adsorption of OH* in the subsequent adsorption/desorption process of H* on the catalyst exteriors, by which the excellent HER performance can be obtained. This result is similar to the previous report that the elongated Pt–O bond in polyoxometalate can accelerate the coupling of electron and proton and leads to an enhancement of HER activity than that of Pt/C catalyst [56]. Further, the adsorption configuration of OER intermediates (i.e., OH*, O* and OOH*) and their related Gibbs free energy (ΔG) on the surface of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO, Pt-WO 3@rGO, WO 3 and Pt were also explored using DFT calculations (Fig. S26 and S27). Fig. S16 shows the calculated ΔG diagrams along the OER pathway for Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO, Pt- WO 3@rGO, WO 3 and Pt, respectively. The ΔG values for the formation of OH*, O*, OOH*, and O2 on the surface of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO are 0.63, 2.09, 3.57, and 4.92 eV, respectively. In contrast, the ΔG values are 0.75, 2.27, 3.82, and 4.92 eV, respectively, on the surface of Pt. It should be noticed that Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO exhibits satisfactory ΔG values for the combination of OH*, O*, and OOH* than that of Pt, WO 3 or Pt- WO 3@rGO, indicating a higher beneficial OER kinetics for Pt- 1.0 1.2 1.4 1.6 1.804080120 E vs. RHE (V)j( mcAm-2)Initial After 5000 cycles(c)-0.5 0.0 0.5 1.0 1.51.41.51.6 P2W17Pt2@rGO 403 mV dec-1 Pt-WO3 354 mV  dec-1     Pt/C 65 mV dec-1 Pt-WO3-x@rGO 48 mV  dec-1 Log[j (mA cm-2)])V(EHR.svE(b) 1.0 1.2 1.4 1.6 1.8 2.004080(a) E vs. RHE (V)j( mcAm-2)Pt-WO3-x@rGO Pt/Cx Pt-WO3 P2W17Pt2@rGO 708090100110 20 18 16 14 12 10 8(d) Time (h)j(m mcA-2) 024691.4%Fig. 5.(a) Polarization curves of different catalysts in 1.0 M KOH for OER measurements. (b) Tafel slops corresponding to curves provided in (a). (c) The OER polarization curves of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO before and after 5000 cycles in the alkaline condition. (d) The i-t curve of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO with a long-time test in the alkaline conditions. D. Yin et al.
