Chemical Engineering Journal 431 (2022) 133287 8WO 3␀x@rGO catalyst. For the four-reactions steps, the third step of converting O* to OOH* needs the largest ΔG (ΔG3 1.48 eV) for Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO catalyst, resulting that it is the rate control step. The ΔG of Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO at every response path is much lower than that of Pt- WO 3@rGO without OV, indicating an advantageous adsorptive process of intermediate products on Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO. The above calculated re- sults point out that OER is more thermodynamically favorable on the Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO surface which is in agreement with the electrochemical measurements. 4.Conclusion In this work, with the cis-platin modified Dawson-type POM as pre- cursor, uniformly distributed Pt0 nanoparticles have been decorated on the surface of WO 3␀x via an in-situ reduction-calcination approach. The formed Pt-WO 3␀x@rGO nanocomposite (1.58% Pt-loading) shows overpotentials of 37 mV and 174 mV for the process of HER and OER in an alkaline medium, respectively. For overall water splitting, Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO achieves a satisfactory working voltage of 1.55 V to receive the current density of 10 mA cm␀2. It has been demonstrated that the Pt0⋅⋅⋅O ␀W interface is conducive to the electron transfer and the lower activation energy for alkaline HER and OER. Also, the DFT calculations reveals that the Pt⋅⋅⋅O ␀W site in the interface of Pt- WO 3␀x@rGO facilitates the cleavage of H–O bond in the process of alkaline water splitting. The present work not only unveil the interface effect for Pt0-based catalyst but also provides the insights for designing highly efficient electrocatalysts for the alkaline water splitting. CCDC1984028 contains the supplementary crystallographic data for this paper. These data can be obtained free of charge from The Cam- bridge Crystallographic Data Centre via www.ccdc.cam.ac.uk/data_ - request/cif. Declaration of Competing Interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationshi