Platinum nanoparticles supported on titanium carbide MXene and reduced graphene oxide for efficient hydrogen evolution in alkaline medium Farah Ezzah Ab Latifa , Abdul Hadi Ismaila,b , Arshid Numana,c,* , Muhammad Norhaffis Mustafaa , Abdul Hanana , Haizum Aimi Zaharind aSunway Centre for Electrochemical Energy and Sustainable Technology (SCEEST), Faculty of Engineering and Technology, Sunway University, No. 5 Jalan Universiti, Bandar Sunway, 47500, Petaling Jaya, Selangor Darul Ehsan, Malaysia bCentre of Innovative Nanostructures & Nanodevices (COINN), Universiti Teknologi PETRONAS, 32610, Seri Iskandar, Perak Darul Ridzuan, Malaysia cDepartment of Applied Physics, Saveetha School of Engineering, Saveetha University (SIMATS), Chennai, India dFuel Cell Institute, Universiti Kebangsaan Malaysia, Selangor Darul Ehsan, 43600, Bangi, Malaysia ARTICLE INFO Keywords: MXene rGO Electrochemical water splitting Electrocatalyst Affordable and clean energy Net-zero emissionABSTRACT Addressing the challenges of developing electrocatalysts with excellent conductivity, stability, and catalytic performance through rapid and cost-effective synthesis method is crucial for realizing green hydrogen ’s po- tential. In this study, a rapid microwave-assisted hydrothermal approach was employed for the simultaneous incorporation of titanium carbide (Ti3C2Tx) MXene, in situ reduction of graphene oxide, and self-deposition of platinum nanoparticles (Pt NPs). The structural analysis confirmed the presence of reduced graphene oxide (rGO), Ti3C2Tx, and Pt NPs, while morphological characterization revealed thin and fractured layers with uni- formly distributed white particles, representing well-deposited Pt NPs. Further, the electrochemical evaluation identified 10 as optimal weight loading of Pt NPs, producing Pt NPs supported on Ti3C2Tx and rGO electro - catalyst, with a low overpotential of 233 mV to achieve 10 mA/cm2, Tafel slope of 182 mV/dec, a high elec- trochemical surface area of 59.25 cm2, and very minimal charge transfer resistance, respectively. Overall, physicochemical characterizations confirm the successful synthesis of the electrocatalyst, underscoring its promise as a durable electrocatalyst for hydrogen evolution reaction towards sustainable energy applications. 1.Introduction The growing urgency for clean and renewable energy has captivated researchers ’ attention for decades. However, in recent years, the focus on clean energy has intensified due to climate change mitigation and the depletion of fossil fuel reserves [1]. With advancements in technology, electrochemical water splitting has emerged as a viable and scalable alternative to fossil fuels for hydrogen production [2,3]. This process, commonly called green hydrogen production, is particularly attractive because it relies on water as a resource and generates zero carbon emissions. Hydrogen also possesses an exceptionally high energy density of 120–140 MJ/kg, making it ideal for sustainable and clean energy [4]. Consequently, the growing demand for hydrogen has surged, inviting experts to explore innovative and efficient water-splitting technologies. Among these, water electrolysis which involves splitting of water into hydrogen and oxygen, emerged as a promising approach. Generally, the production of hydrogen is known as the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at cathode side, while the production of oxygen is known as the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) at anode side, as two key reactions of electrochemical water splitting [5,6]. While the platinum on carbon (Pt/C) remains a benchmark for HER, its high cost and scarcity [7] have driven extensive research into alternative electrocatalysts that can rival or even surpass the performance of Pt-based catalysts while maintaining cost-effectiveness and long-term stability of the electrocatalyst. A newly discovered material in a two-dimensional (2D) family known as MXene has been well-recognized in energy storage applica - tions. MXene with the general formula of Mn1XnTx, where M is for the transition metal, X is for the nitrogen or carbon, and Tx denotes the surface termination groups such as –O, –F, –Cl or –OH [8]. The commonly reported MXene, Ti3C2Tx, has garnered massive recognition as a promising electrocatalyst for the HER, attributed to its excellent electrical conductivity, abundant surface termination groups, unique *Corresponding author. Sunway Centre for Electrochemical Energy and Sustainable Technology (SCEEST), Faculty of Engineering and Technology, Sunway University, No. 5 Jalan Universiti, Bandar Sunway, 47500, Petaling Jaya, Selangor Darul Ehsan, Malaysia. E-mail address: numana@sunway.edu.my (A. Numan). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect International Journal of Hydrogen Energy u{�~zkw! s{yo|kro>! ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2so https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2025.151393 Received 5 May 2025; Received in revised form 26 August 2025; Accepted 4 September 2025  International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 Available  online  11 September  2025 0360-3199/©  2025  Hydrogen  Energy  Publications  LLC. Published  by Elsevier  Ltd. All rights  are reserved,  including  those  for text and data mining,  AI training,  and similar  technologies.
