layered structure, high surface area, and tunable surface chemistry [9, 10]. Furthermore, the tunable surface chemistry of MXene presents an opportunity for researchers to explore its potential as an HER electro - catalyst. For example, the Ti3C2Tx MXene synthesized through microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating using sodium hydroxide (NaOH) as an etching agent by Kajal Mahabari and team has successfully demonstrated an improvement in the HER performance with an over- potential of 140 mV [11]. Similarly, Muhammad Yameen Solangi and the team have successfully fabricated an electrocatalyst for the HER with an overpotential of 380 mV by incorporating the Ti3C2Tx MXene with the cobalt hydroxide Co(OH) 2 [12]. Additionally, MXenes based on transition metals, such as titanium, chromium and vanadium, have been combined with Co3, exhibiting excellent conductivity, enabling the capability to be developed as an HER electrocatalyst [13]. In addition to MXene, cerium oxide, which is a rare metal, has also been integrated with cobalt phosphate [14] and manganese-cobalt sulfide [15], demonstrating an excellent electrocatalyst performance. Similar to rare metal, MXene can serve as an excellent conductive framework for the composite, facilitating ion migration while promoting the exposure of the active sites [16]. Moreover, the presence of oxygen vacancies plays a vital role in boosting the adsorption and desorption of hydrogen during the HER catalytic activities by promoting electrons to the lower Fermi level, which thereby facilitates the electron transport and the ion ex- change between the active sites [17]. Despite the numerous advantages of the MXene, it often faces structural degradation, which diminishes its active site exposure and hinders its excellent intrinsic properties [10]. Implementing rGO as an electrocatalyst has demonstrated excellent electrocatalytic activity toward the HER. The excellent conductivity and large surface area of rGO provide an ideal platform for catalytic activity during the HER. For example, Mahaptra et al. have demonstrated the good electrical conductivity of rGO integrated with the perovskite neodymium cobalt oxide, able to improve the electrocatalytic properties of the HER [18]. Additionally, rGO has shown the ability to serve as a scaffold for the NPs, effectively preventing the agglomeration scenario, enhancing catalytic activity and ensuring long-term stability [19]. For instance, the ability of N-doped rGO to act as a framework for Cu-doped Co3-xO4 NPs has created an electrocatalyst toward the HER with high electrocatalytic activity and excellent stability [20]. Moreover, the ox- ygen functional groups on rGO surface provide additional active sites for HER catalytic activity. For instance, integration of the rGO with the iron-oxide nanostructure had demonstrated an HER electrocatalyst capable of delivering an overpotential of 300 mV at 10 mA/cm2 [21]. Recently, another 2D material coming from transition metal dichalco - genides, MoS 2, was integrated with the rGO and has demonstrated excellent electrocatalytic activity for the HER, which requires an over- potential of 242 mV at 10 mA/cm2 [22]. Notably, Pt NPs are widely recognized for their outstanding elec- trocatalytic performance for the HER. While their high surface area contributes significantly to enhancing charge transfer and active site availability, their superior inherent catalytic behavior boosts HER per- formance. Specifically, Pt NPs show optimal hydrogen adsorption en- ergy and favorable reaction kinetics during the HER, facilitating the ion adsorption/sorption process [7]. While previous studies have exten - sively explored the MXene-based and rGO-based catalysts for HER ap- plications, there has been limited investigation into the potential of 2D MXene-based heterostructures as an electrocatalyst for HER. Particu - larly, the unique combination of Ti3C2Tx MXene and rGO offers distinct advantages, wherein the abundant hydrophilic groups of Ti3C2Tx enhance dispersion, while rGO’s high surface area prevents the unde- sirable restacking of layered structures. Additionally, the strategic incorporation of Pt NPs within the heterostructure creates a synergistic effect, facilitating proton reduction and enhancing the HER catalytic activity. Herein, this study has introduced a novel Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electro - catalyst for the HER through a facile and rapid microwave-assisted hy- drothermal approach. The technique involves combining Ti3C2Tx, GO, and Pt salt solutions, with varying Pt loadings of 5, 10, and 15 wt%, resulting in three distinct electrocatalytic composite. As-synthesized electrocatalysts were systematically characterized to assess their struc- tural, compositional, chemical bonding, and electrochemical properties toward the HER. The optimal as-synthesized electrocatalyst exhibits superior catalytic activity for HER with an overpotential value of 233 mV, current density 10 mA/cm2 and a Tafel slope of 182 mV/dec. The overall schematic of this work is presented in Fig. 1. To date, the hy- bridization of Pt NPs with a 2D MXene-rGO heterostructure for HER applications has not been reported, marking a novel advancement in electrocatalyst design. This innovative Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO composite le- verages the exceptional conductivity of MXene, the high surface area and structural support of rGO, and the superior electrocatalytic activity of Pt NPs. The synergistic interaction among these materials signifi - cantly enhanced electrocatalytic performance, holding this novel com- posite as a promising next-generation electrocatalyst for energy conversion and storage applications, particularly in the pursuit of effi- cient and sustainable hydrogen production. 2.Experimental 2.1. Materials and methods Titanium carbide MAX phase (Ti3AlC2, 325 mesh, 99 % purity) was purchased from Xiamen Tob New Energy Technology Co., LTD. Lithium fluoride powder (LiF, 300 mesh, 99.99 % trace metal basis), graphite (99.99 % trace metal basis), chloroplatinic acid (H2PtCl 6, 99.99 % trace metal basis), and hydrochloric acid (HCl, 37 % purity) were procured from Sigma Aldrich, United States. Ethanol (C2H6O, 99.7 %) was ob- tained from R&M Chemicals, Malaysia. A potentiostat Metrohm Autolab PGSTAT 204 electrochemical setup with NOVA 2.0 as an operating software was used to evaluate the electrochemical analysis of HER. The glassy carbon electrode (GCE, d 3 mm), Nafion solution (5 %), alumina slurry, Pt wire, a mercury/mercury oxide (Hg/HgO), and micro cloth were bought from Metrohm, Malaysia. All solutions were prepared with deionized water (DI, resistivity 18.2 MΩ. Cm). 2.2. Synthesis of Ti3C2Tx Using our previously reported method, MXene was produced through its parent Ti3AlC2 MAX phase [23]. Typically, LiF/HCl solution was prepared by dissolving 0.648 g of LiF in 10 mL of 6 M HCl through magnetic stirring for 10 min. Subsequently, 0.5 g of Ti3AlC2 MAX powder was gradually added to the LiF/HCl solution under continuous stirring at room temperature for 30 min. The mixture was then ultra- sonicated in a bath sonicator for another 30 min. Afterwards, the mixture was poured into a Teflon vessel and subjected to microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating at 40 •C for 10 min, with a heating rate of 1.5 •C/min. The resulting mixture was thoroughly washed and centrifuged using DI water and ethanol several times until the pH reached ~7. The supernatant was collected and ultrasonicated in a bath sonicator under an Argon atmosphere for 1 h to delaminate the MXene layers. The resulting supernatant was centrifuged at 10,000 rpm for 5 min before freeze-drying. The obtained powder was subsequently labelled as Ti3C2Tx. 2.3. Synthesis of rGO Initially, graphene oxide (GO) was synthesized from graphite through a modified Hummer ’s method [24]. 25 mg of GO was dispersed in 12.5 mL of DI water through ultrasonication for 1 h. The resulting homogenous GO dispersion was then transferred into a Teflon vessel and subjected to microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating at 120 •C for 20 min, with a heating rate of 4 •C/min. Subsequently, the solution ob- tained was washed and centrifuged with DI and ethanol for several cy- cles to remove impurities. Finally, the obtained precipitate was F.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 2
