freeze-dried to yield the powder form for further analysis. 2.4. Synthesis of Ti3C2Tx/rGO The Ti3C2Tx dispersion was prepared by dispersing 25 mg of Ti3C2Tx powder in 12.5 mL DI through ultrasonication under an Argon atmo - sphere for 15 min. Meanwhile, the GO solution was prepared by dispersing 25 mg of GO powder in 12.5 mL DI through an ultra- sonication bath for 1 h. Subsequently, the Ti3C2Tx dispersion gradually dropped into the GO solution under continuous stirring at room tem- perature. The mixture was then magnetically stirred for 1 h, followed by nitrogen purging for 15 min under ultrasonication. Then, the mixture was transferred into a Teflon vessel and subjected to microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating at 120 •C for 20 min, with a heating rate of 4 •C/ min. The resulting mixture was washed by centrifugation with DI for three cycles, followed by ethanol and with DI water again. The precip - itate of the mixture was then freeze-dried and denoted as the Ti3C2Tx/ rGO. 2.5. Synthesis of Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO The Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst was prepared through the magnetic stirring of Ti3C2Tx, GO, and H2PtCl 6 solution. Initially, the Ti3C2Tx solution (25 mg in 12.5 mL DI) was gradually added into the GO solution (25 mg in 12.5 mL DI) under continuous stirring for 1 h. Sub- sequently, H2PtCl 6 solution (1.25 mL, 2 mg/mL) was added dropwise to the mixture while stirring continuously for 30 min. The resulting mixture was then transferred into a Teflon vessel and subjected to microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating at 120 •C for 20 min, with a heating rate of 4 •C/min. The product was then purified by washing with DI water for three cycles, followed by washing with ethanol and DI water again. Finally, the precipitate was freeze-dried to obtain the powdered electrocatalyst labelled 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO. Similar steps were repeated using 2.5 and 3.75 mL of H2PtCl 6 solutions, designated 10Pt/ Ti3C2Tx/rGO and 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, respectively. 2.6. Characterizations of the Ti3C2Tx, rGO, Ti3C2Tx/rGO and Pt/ Ti3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst X-ray diffraction (XRD) characterization was employed to examine the crystalinity of Ti3C2Tx and rGO and composites using an Empyrean (PANalytical) X-ray diffraction instrument. The Cu Kα radiation was subjected to the precursors and the synthesized samples with a wave - length of 1.5406 Å at 45.0 kV and 40.0 mA, between the angles of 5.0• and 80.0•, with a step size of 0.02•. Based on the XRD patterns, the lattice spacing of the samples was calculated using Bragg ’s Law (Eqn (1)), as stated following: nλ2dsinθ (1) where n is the order of the reflection (typically equivalent to 1), λ is the wavelength of the X-ray incident, d is the spacing of the sheets, and θ is the angle (radians) of the incidence from the lattice plane. Additionally, the morphology features were examined using FEI Quanta 400F Field- Emission Scanning Electron Microscopy (FESEM) equipped with Ox- ford Instruments INCA 400 X-Max Detector Electron Distribution Spec- troscopy (EDX). The microstructural analysis of Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO was performed using Talos L120C Transmission Electron Microscope (TEM) operated at an accelerating voltage of 120 kV. Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO elec- trocatalyst ’s surface chemistry and composition were evaluated using a Fig. 1.Overall schematic illustration of this study.F.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 3
