working electrode, a Hg/HgO electrode as a reference, and a Pt wire as a counter electrode. The catalyst solution was prepared by dispersing the catalyst powder in DI water at a 2 mg/mL concentration. Subsequently, 10 μL of a Nafion solution (5 %), acting as a binder, was added to the mixture and ultrasonicated in a bath sonicator for 1 h. Afterwards, 10 μL of the electrocatalyst ink was drop-cast onto the polished surface of GCE and allowed to dry in an oven at a temperature of 39 •C for ~20 min. The coated GCE was then evaluated for the electrocatalytic activity of HER in 1 M KOH using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) at a scan rate of 5 mV/s within negative potential window, accordingly. The charge transfer resistance (Rct) values vs reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) of the electrocatalyst were analyzed by employing the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) over a frequency range of 100 kHz to 0.1 Hz, with a sinusoidal potential of 10 mV. The stability of the electro - catalyst for HER was assessed through chronopotentiometry at a con- stant current density of 10 and 20 mA/cm2 for 32 h. Additionally, the durability of the electrocatalyst before and after the stability test was determined through the LSV. The electrocatalysts ’ double-layer capac - itance (Cdl) was evaluated through the CV curves in the potential win- dow of 0.00 V–0.25 V (non-Faradaic region) at various scan rates of 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, and 70 mV/s. Corresponding, electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) was determined through the Cdl values. The RHE potential was calculated from the Hg/HgO reference potential using the Nernst equation, as written as follows [25]: ERHEEHgEHgO0B059pHE• HgEHgO (2) where E• HgEHgO is the standard electrode potential of the Hg/HgO refer- ence electrode and is equivalent to 0.098 V. The overpotential (η) for HER efficiency was determined by deducing the hydrogen production system ’s onset thermodynamic potential of 0 V, as written in Eqn (3): Overpotential CηOnset potentialCERHEV␀0V (3) Meanwhile, the Tafel slope was obtained through the Tafel equation, as written in Eqn (4)[8]: ηblogja (4) where η is the overpotential, j is the current density, b is the Tafel slope, and a is the intercept parameter or exchange current density. 3.Results and discussion 3.1. Elemental analysis The microscopic examination of Ti3AlC2 MAX powder, shown in Fig. 2 (a), depicts a rough, compact, and densely packed layered struc- ture. The elemental analysis shown in the inset table indicates a composition dominated by titanium (Ti) at 64.18 wt%, followed by aluminium (Al) at 17.05 wt%, oxygen (O) at 11.23 wt%, and carbon (C) at 7.54 wt%. Similar observations were demonstrated in the previous study, where Ti3AlC2 MAX powder showed a group of sheet-like layers with no apparent interlayer spacing [26]. After the etching of Ti3AlC2 MAX using LiF/HCl solution through microwave-assisted hydrothermal treatment, the typical microscopic structure of Ti3C2Tx is depicted in Fig. 2 (b). The figure shows a crumbled, thin-layered structure with ridges and wrinkled structures resulting from removing the Al layer and the delamination of MXene sheets. This morphology of MXene agrees well with the previously published reference, which describes the sur- face of the Ti3C2Tx MXene as having wrinkled texture characteristics [27]. From the inset tables of Fig. 2 (a) and 2 (b), the effectiveness of the etching process is evident by the significant reduction in Al contents, from 17.05 wt% to 0.84 wt%, underscoring the successful formation of 2D Ti3C2Tx MXene. Additionally, the EDX analysis presented in the inset table of Fig. 2 (b)demonstrates the introduction of several new termination groups onto the Ti3C2Tx, namely –F, –O, and –Cl, with respective contents of 22.65 wt%, 17.76 wt%, and 0.17 wt%. This observation is closely linked to the use of LiF/HCl solution as the etching reagent, aligning with previous studies that emphasize the influence of etching conditions on the nature of surface termination groups formed within the MXene layers [11,26]. The morphological structure of GO, as shown in Fig. 2 (c), reveals a flake-like appearance with widely spread sheets, attributed to the elec- trostatic repulsion generated by the abundant functional groups on the GO’s surface. Following microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating without a reducing agent, the resulting rGO displays a thin, crumpled- sheet-like morphology with apparent folds and wrinkles, as depicted in Fig. 2 (d). A similar morphology transformation has been observed in previous studies, where GO reduced using ascorbic acid also produced rGO with a more wrinkled surface compared to the pure GO [28]. This crumpled structure likely arises from removing surface functional groups during the heat treatment. While the elemental compositions obtained from EDX indicate an increase in carbon content from 53.3 wt % (GO) to 62.6 wt% (rGO) and a corresponding decrease in oxygen content from 46.7 wt% (GO) to 37.4 wt% (rGO), it is noted that these values serve only as qualitative indicators of reduction trends due to the inherent limitations of EDS in accurately quantifying elements. The morphology of the Ti3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst depicted in Fig. 2 (e)features a slightly smooth surface with few cracks and ridges attributed to the Ti3C2Tx flakes layers interlayered with the smooth, thin paper-like sheets of rGO. This structure successfully integrates Ti3C2Tx and rGO sheets through microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating. Additionally, the detection of carbon and oxygen, with values of 48.73 wt% and 41.62 wt%, is attributed to the Ti3C2Tx and rGO, respectively. The abundance of oxygen in the electrocatalyst provides rapid ion and electron pathways, thereby improving the electrochemical performance of the Ti3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst for the HER [29]. The micrograph of the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst in Fig. 2 (f)illustrates thin, layered sheets with a slightly wrinkled and fractured surface, indicating a good integration between the Ti3C2Tx and rGO sheets. Additionally, the small white particles on the surface represent the presence of the Pt NPs. However, the morphology of Pt NPs in the SEM image is not apparent due to the resolution limitation of SEM, preventing detailed surface imaging. Nevertheless, the elemental composition shown in the inset table (Fig. 2 (f)) confirms the successful fabrication of Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst, identifying 9.37 wt% Pt NPs, 12.96 wt% Ti, 50.02 wt% C, 25.60 wt% O, 1.78 wt% F, and negligible traces of Al. 3.2. TEM and FESEM analysis The TEM image for Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, as depicted in Fig. 3 (a), fea- tures a thin and layered sheet structure, suggesting the successful inte- gration between the Ti3C2Tx and rGO. The rGO sheets in the figure appear relatively transparent, owing to the lower electron scattering associated with the carbon atoms distributed across the rGO layers. In contrast, the Ti3C2Tx layers appear darker despite their ultrathin nature, which is attributed to Ti atoms within the carbon layers. The higher atomic number of Ti leads to stronger electron scattering, making the Ti3C2Tx region darker in the TEM images. Similar observations have been reported in past studies, where the Ti3C2Tx sheets consistently appeared darker than the rGO due to the differences in the atomic number and electron scattering properties during the TEM analysis [30–32]. The TEM image also depicts several significant small dots within the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst, signifying the successful deposition of Pt NPs within the surfaces and edges of the electrocatalyst sheets. Based on the TEM image, it is evident that the sheets between the Ti3C2Tx and rGO are well-intercalated, forming a heterostructure framework which effectively prevents the restacking of the 2D layers. Additionally, the framework preserves excellent conductivity and a high surface area of 2D layers, facilitating ion and electron pathways during HER reactions. Furthermore, the well-distributed Pt NPs decorated F.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 5
