within the heterostructure framework offer additional active sites, promoting outstanding adsorption and desorption of ions and electron transport [33,34]. The successful integration of the Ti3C2Tx, rGO, and Pt NPs can be attributed to the uniform volumetric heating and localized “hot spot” effects provided by microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating [35,36], which significantly enhances the reaction kinetics among the materials. Notably, the MXene, GO and Pt precursor containing metal, metal car- bides, and carbon-based elements can act as effective heat susceptors, efficiently absorbing microwave energy and converting it into thermal energy within the bulk of the material [35,36]. The high energy sus- ceptance promotes rapid and uniform heating through strong in- teractions between the materials and the electromagnetic field, which thereby accelerates chemical reactions and promotes morphological and structural transformations [36]. For example, the electromagnetic field irradiation facilitates the formation of the chemical bonds between Ti3C2Tx, rGO, and Pt NPs, while also aiding the thermal reduction of GO to rGO. The negatively charged surface termination groups (–O, –F, –OH) on the Ti3C2Tx sheets promote string electrostatic and hydrogen bonding interactions with GO sheets, facilitating the reduction while ensuring stable anchoring within the composite structure [37,38]. Hence, the intense localized heating induces a phase transition in GO, changing its surface morphology from smooth to wrinkled and intro- ducing additional defects due to the loss of oxygen groups, resulting in an increase in electrochemical surface area. Simultaneously, the mi- crowave energy enhances the surface diffusion and nucleation of Pt precursors, promoting the in-situ formation of metallic Pt NPs and their deposition on the Ti3C2Tx/rGO composite surface [36]. The abundant functional groups on Ti3C2Tx and rGO surfaces assist in the nucleation and growth of Pt NPs, while the localized heating minimizes particle aggregation. Importantly, microwave-assisted hydrothermal synthesis not only enables the formation of novel microstructures but also modifies the physicochemical properties of the materials, which enhance their performance in electrochemical applications [37,38]. Fig. 3 (b)illustrates the surface morphology for the 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst, featuring a layered structure with some roughness, attributed to the integration of Ti3C2Tx and rGO. Small white particles distributed on top of the surface can also be seen, indicating a relatively lower Pt NPs loading onto the 5 Pt/s/rGO electrocatalyst. Meanwhile, the surface morphology for the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst, as shown in Fig. 3(c), depicts an apparent layered morphology accompa - nied by distinct crumpling and folding of the Ti3C2Tx and rGO sheets, indicating a well-interconnected layer establishment. This way, the figure comprises more white particles decorated on the sheet ’s surface, highlighting the intermediate Pt NPs loading, which is expected to improve better than the 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst, particularly on the ion and electron transportation during the catalytic activity of hydrogen production. The layered morphology of the 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst in Fig. 3 (d)exhibits distinct ridges and folds, indicating an interwoven structure between the Ti3C2Tx and rGO layers. Additionally, the increase in loading of Pt NPs (15 wt%) is apparent when more visible white particles are spread along the surface of the electrocatalyst. However, the 15 wt% loading of Pt NPs integrated within the Ti3C2Tx and rGO sheets creates an accumulation of Pt NPs, appearing as white spots at several regions, as shown in Fig. 3 (d). While the presence of Pt NPs is crucial for providing abundant active sites to boost the electrochemical performance, excessive loading can lead to NPs clustering. This clus- tering hinders the ion and electron pathways, negatively impacting the overall catalytic activity [39,40]. A recent systematic review highlighted several strategies for improving MXene-based electrocatalysts while mentioning that the abundant active sites of NPs can enhance the cat- alytic activity toward the HER [41]. Meanwhile, the macroscopic view of the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst at lower magnification as Fig. 3.TEM image of (a) Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO. High magnification FESEM images of (b) 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, (c) 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, and (d) 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO. (e) Low magnification FESEM image of 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO. EDX mapping of (f) Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst and (g–l) its corresponding elements.F.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 6
