The diffractogram of GO and rGO is presented in Fig. 4 (b). The figure exhibits GO with a distinct diffraction peak at 10.29•for the (002) plane with an interlayer d-spacing of 0.91 nm, attributed to the abundance of oxygen-functional groups on the basal plane of GO, suggesting the successful formation of GO from graphite through the modified Hum- mer’s method accordingly. A similar observation can also be seen in the past study, where the successful formation of GO through Hummer ’s method also exhibited a similar peak position at the (002) plane [44]. After the GO is subjected to the microwave-assisted hydrothermal heating (without the presence of a reducing agent), the peak intensity at 10.29•is reduced, accompanied by two new additional small peaks at 20.5•and 42.8•corresponding to 0.43 nm and 0.21 nm d-interlayer spacing for the (002) and (100) planes, respectively. These rGO peak positions are consistent with the findings from the previous research, focusing on the synthesis of rGO [45,46]. These results demonstrate the decrement of the d-interlayer spacing from 0.86 nm to 0.43 nm, sug- gesting the removal of oxygen-containing functional groups, and thereby affirming that the GO is reduced. However, the peak at 10.29• on the basal plane of rGO suggests the presence of residual GO within the rGO sheets, indicating that the reduction process is partially completed, resulting in partial reduction of rGO. The synthesized partial rGO is expected, as the synthesis process did not employ any reducing agents during the heat treatment. The partial reduction is further supported by the Raman spectra demonstrated in Fig. S1. The figure shows two distinct peaks at 1350 and 1590 cm␀1, representing the D and G bands, respectively. The G band represents the stretching of carbon atoms within the graphitic domains, expressing the graphitization degree of a material. Meanwhile, the D band represents the disordered carbon or defects within the carbon network of the material. Based on Fig. S1, the relative intensity ratio of the D band to the G band (ID/IG) for GO and rGO was 0.78 and 0.80, respectively. The slight increase in the ratio for rGO suggests a higher level of defects is introduced to the rGO during the reduction process, aligning with partial removal of oxygen-containing functional groups, which can be observed in the XRD pattern. These findings affirmed that the reduction process has indeed occurred, resulting in the formation of partial rGO. The XRD diffractogram for the Ti3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst obtained by incorporation between the Ti3C2Tx and GO solutions through the microwave-assisted hydrothermal method is depicted in Fig. 4 (c). Corresponding figure shows a prominent peak at 25.2•, corroborated by the existence of the rGO. This peak position aligns with the reported rGO from a previous study, which produced rGO through chemical treatment using various reducing agents, including ascorbic acid, hydrazine hy- drate, and sodium borohydride [47]. Additionally, two distinct peaks at 37.8•and 47.8•correspond to the characteristics of the Ti3C2Tx, aligning well with the previous findings [48]. The presence of both rGO and Ti3C2Tx within the Ti3C2Tx/rGO composite affirms the successful inte- gration of these materials, resulting in a 2D heterostructure composite. The peak at 10.29•is a characteristic of the interlayer spacing caused by oxygen-containing functional groups in GO. Its disappearance within the Ti3C2Tx/rGO composite shows the complete reduction of GO to rGO, indicating the disruption of the ordered layered structure. Similar ob- servations have been reported in previous studies, where the integration of MXene and GO facilitates GO reduction, likely due to surface termi - nation groups on MXene that enable it to act as a reducing agent [49,50]. Thus, this finding suggests that MXene may also play a role in promoting the reduction of GO in the fabrication of the composites. The XRD spectra for the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO in Fig. 4 (c)illustrates peaks at 25.2•and 54.2•(labelled as ♠) associated with the existence of rGO. The rGO peaks at 20.5•and 42.8•have been shifted to higher angles during Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO fabrication. The scenario of the peak shifting agrees with the previous research, indicating the reduction of the GO sheets [51]. Hence, the peaks at 37.8•and 47.8•(denoted as Φ), attributed to the characteristic of Ti3C2Tx, remain unchanged. Mean - while, the slight peak shifting from 60.4•to 62.16•corroborated the Ti3C2Tx, suggesting that minor alterations occurred to the Ti3C2Tx sheets due to the heat treatment. These findings align with previous findings where the MXene sheets were altered after the heat treatment [52]. The perseverance characteristics of Ti3C2Tx and rGO within the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO showed the successful integration of a 2D hetero - structure. The successful self-deposited Pt NPs (denoted as ◆) are confirmed with the appearance of peaks at 40•, 46.44•, and 67.53•at the (111), (200), and (220) crystallographic planes, respectively. These Pt NPs peak observations agree with JCPDS No. 01-087-0646 standard as well as the previous studies [53]. Similarly, the Raman spectra for the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO (Fig. S1) reveal characteristic peaks at 156, 401, 488 and 617 cm␀1, attributed to the Ti–C vibrations and thus confirming the preservation of the MXene structure. Notably, the absence of D and G bands in the Raman spectrum suggests a well-ordered carbon structure, which is due to thermal treatment during the synthesis, aligning well with the XRD findings and further supporting the successful integration of MXene with the carbon matrix in the composite. Additionally, the strong interaction between the Pt NPs and the conductive Ti3C2Tx/rGO, promotes efficient nucleation and growth of Pt, thereby facilitating the formation of Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO composite. Overall, from the XRD pattern, it can be observed that the rGO peak at 10.29•disappeared within the Ti3C2Tx/rGO and Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO composites. The disappearance confirms the complete reduction of GO during the composite fabrication and highlights the ability of Ti3C2Tx to act as an effective reducing agent [54]. Notably, these findings suggest that the surface termination groups attached to MXene sheets, like Ti–O and Ti–F, not only offer direct ion exchange sites, but also facilitate in-situ reduction of GO [54]. In addition, MXene offers a robust struc- tural framework that facilitates its integration with rGO and subsequent deposition of Pt NPs, indicating its dual role as a support and reductant during the synthesis. The reductive capability of MXene is further sup- ported by previous studies, such as the successful fabrication of MXe- ne/Ag composites, where Ti3C2Tx reduced Agto metallic Ag0, leading to Ag NPs deposition on its surface [55,56]. Similar behaviour was observed with other MXene types, including Nb2CTx and V2CTx, which also enabled the in-situ formation of Ag NPs on their surface without the need for additional reducing agents [54]. In contrast, when GO was treated alone under identical microwave- assisted hydrothermal conditions without any presence of a reducing agent, only partial reduction occurred. The partial reduction is evi- denced by the presence of the XRD peak at 10.29•with reduced in- tensity, indicating that some oxygen-functional groups remain. Such incomplete reduction is expected in the absence of strong reducing agents. Therefore, the variation in the XRD pattern between the rGO and Ti3C2Tx/rGO heterostructure composites can be attributed to the unique reductive and structural roles of MXene. This structural distinction does not impact the interruption of HER performance in this study, as rGO was employed only as a control, while the primary focus remains on the electrocatalytic behavior of the MXene-based composite systems. Moreover, the XPS survey spectrum for the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO in Fig. 4 (d)shows well-defined peaks at 76.08, 286.08, 460.08, 534.08, and 686.08 eV, correspond to the characteristic peaks of Pt, C, Ti, O, and F. These peaks are aligned with the prior research works [57–59]. Fig. 4 (e) portrays the deconvoluted spectrum of Ti 2p with two distinct peaks at 459.00 (Ti 2p3/2) and 465.00 eV (Ti 2p1/2), corresponding to the Ti–O and Ti–C bonding, respectively [60,61]. The spin-orbit splitting of 5.8 eV between Ti 2p3/2 and Ti 2p1/2 is consistent with previously reported XPS analysis of Ti-based compounds [62]. Hence, a minor yet distinct peak at 461.00 is attributed to the Ti2oxidation state, further corrob - orating the chemical composition of the material [62,63]. The decon - voluted C 1s spectrum of the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO (Fig. 4 (f)) reveals multiple components at 283.3, 284.62, 286.53, 288.95, and 291.74 eV corre - sponding to C–Ti [60], C–C [64], CHx/C–O [65], C––O [66] and O–C––O/C–F [67,68], respectively. These distinct peaks affirmed the diverse chemical states of carbon within the composite. The deconvoluted spectrum of O 1s of Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, as depicted F.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 8
