in Fig. 4 (g), shows three distinct peaks at 530.8, 532.6, and 533.86 eV, corresponding to Ti–O–Ti [69], Ti–OH [70] and C–OH [71], respec - tively. The Ti–O–Ti bonding originates from MXene at a bridging site, while the Ti–OH bonding is associated with F-related interactions [23]. Additionally, the C–OH peak can be attributed to adsorbed water or hydroxyl surface termination groups formed during washing. Further - more, the deconvoluted XPS spectrum of F 1s, shown in Fig. 4 (h), ex- hibits five distinct peaks at 685.00, 686.00, 687.09, 688.00, and 689.40 eV. These peaks correspond to Ti–F–Ti [63], TiO2x–Fx [60], Al–Fx [72], Ti–F–Ti [73] and C–F [74], respectively, highlighting the diverse F-related chemical states present within the composite. Lastly, the high-resolution XPS spectra of the Pt 4f region for the deconvoluted spectrum of the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO are presented in Fig. 4 (i). The spectrum features two primary peaks at binding energies of 71.38 eV [75] and 74.68 eV [76], corresponding to the Pt 4f7/2 and Pt 4f5/2 lines, respectively, which are characteristic of metallic Pt (Pt0). The presence of oxidized Pt species is confirmed by peaks at 72.38 eV [77], 75.89 eV [78], and 77.02 eV [59], which are associated with the Pt2 oxidation state. Another peak at 73.11 eV [79] further confirms the presence of Pt0. The peak at 78.28 eV corresponds to the Pt4oxidation state [80], indicating that some Pt NPs are not fully reduced. Despite oxidized Pt species, the atomic percentage distribution of Pt 4f states, as summarised in Table 1, reveals that Pt0 is the predominant species, ac- counting for 72.70 % of the total Pt content. In contrast, Pt2and Pt4 states contribute 18.87 % and 8.44 %, respectively. These findings confirm that metallic Pt (Pt0) is the predominant species in the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, demonstrating the successful deposition of Pt NPs. Pt NPs are essential for optimizing the electrocatalyst ’s performance to- ward the HER, ensuring improvement in the catalytic efficiency. 3.4. Hydrogen evolution reaction Corresponding electrochemical measurements examined the HER activity of the Ti3C2Tx, rGO, Ti3C2Tx/rGO, and Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO elec- trocatalysts, such as 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, and 15Pt/ Ti3C2Tx/rGO, in 1 M KOH at a scan rate of 5 mV/s at 25 •C. The LSV polarization curves for all the electrocatalysts are plotted in Fig. 5 (a) and evaluated with respect to the 20 % Pt/C electrocatalyst for the HER performance, as a reference. Fig. 5 (b)is the enlarged scale of the LSV curve for the Ti3C2Tx, rGO, and Ti3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalysts. Based on the LSV curves, it is unsurprising to find that Ti3C2Tx and Ti3C2Tx/rGO appeared to have relatively high overpotential values, requiring 1018 mV (η10 1018 mV) and 969 mV (η10 969 mV) to achieve a current density of 10 mA/cm2, respectively. Meanwhile, the rGO electrocatalyst, which serves as the more active electrocatalyst, requires a slightly lower overpotential of 429 mV (η10 429 mV) to reach 10 mA/cm2. Notably, the catalytic performance of Ti3C2Tx and Ti3C2Tx/rGO is outperformed by the rGO. Although the Ti3C2Tx and rGO are renowned for their high conductivity and high surface area [29,81], both face limitations when used individually as HER electrocatalysts. For rGO, the electrocatalytic activity towards HER is primarily attributed to the stable carbonyl group within the surface edges [82]. Additionally, the partial reduction of rGO in this work, as evident in the XRD analysis (Fig. 4 (b)), exhibits sufficient conductivity to facilitate HER activities, achieving an overpotential of η10 429 mV, and thereby boosting the hydrogen ion reduction dissociation. Although the partial rGO contains some remaining hydroxyl groups within the edges, which potentially might disrupt sp2 carbon domains due to localized electronic traps, their presence can be advantageous. These few residual functional groups and structural defects might offer tunable properties by providing an active site for anchoring ions during the hydrogen ion reduction and dissociation process [29]. The functional groups present on both Ti3C2Tx and rGO surfaces facilitate the formation of covalent bonds, hydrogen bonds and π-π stacking [37,83]. These interfacial in- teractions create additional electron transport pathways, thereby enhancing the HER activity of the electrocatalyst. Moreover, the alter- nating layered structure formed between the Ti3C2Tx and rGO provides a robust framework, as evidenced in Fig. 3(c–e), which further reinforces the structural integrity and preserves the desirable 2D properties of both materials [38,49]. A non predicted observation was made, where the Ti3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst exhibited a higher overpotential than the rGO electro - catalyst alone, suggesting inferior HER activities. This incident may be attributed to the partial detachment of the Ti3C2Tx/rGO composite from the GCE during the HER activities, leading to a small ECSA of 6.68 cm2. Nonetheless, the integration of rGO into Ti3C2Tx sheets still conferred improvements over the pristine Ti3C2Tx. This scenario can be attributed to enhanced electron transport and charge transfer kinetics rather than changes in ion adsorption/desorption behaviour. This conclusion is supported by the slightly lower overpotential (η10) and reduced Tafel slope observed for Ti3C2Tx/rGO, despite the minimal differences in Cdl and ECSA between these two materials. These findings suggest that the presence of rGO contributes to boosting electrical conductivity and fa- cilitates electron transport through structural synergy with Ti3C2Tx [49, 84]. Further, the presence of rGO sheets suppresses the restacking of Ti3C2Tx layers. Restacking prevention maintains accessible surface area and promotes rapid interfacial electron transport [38], which resulted in the observed decrease in overpotential for the Ti3C2Tx/rGO electro - catalyst. Although Ti3C2Tx/rGO exhibits a slightly higher Rct, the lower Tafel slope indicates better overall charge transfer kinetics [85]. This phenomenon is likely due to the good interlayer morphology of the heterostructure (as evident in Fig. 3 (c-e), which enables more efficient ion diffusion and electron movement during HER. Therefore, the observed enhancement is mainly governed by the improved electronic conductivity and interfacial charge transport within the Ti3C2Tx/rGO composite. Despite the numerous outstanding properties of Ti3C2Tx, it suffers from two significant problems, which are restacking of the sheets and susceptibility to oxidation, which lead to the formation of titanium di- oxide [86]. These issues destroy its 2D intrinsic properties, diminishing its electrical conductivity and catalytic activity [4]. Therefore, this study intended to improve the electrocatalytic activity of the electrocatalyst by integrating the Pt NPs into the Ti3C2Tx/rGO heterostructure com- posite. The incorporation of Pt NPs is expected to further boost HER performance due to the exceptional catalytic properties of Pt [87]. The polarization curves in Fig. 5 (a)illustrate that the Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalysts significantly improved the HER activity by exhibiting relatively low overpotential approaching the commercial Pt electro - catalyst. The low overpotential of the electrocatalyst indicates the ability of the electrocatalyst to have high current density, which is a vital property of a suitable HER catalyst in the reduction of protons (H) with a minimal energy loss [88,89]. As shown in Fig. 5 (a-b), the overpotential of 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, and 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO is 352 mV (η10 352 mV), 233 mV (η10 233 mV), and 259 mV (η10 259 mV), respectively. It is significant to note that the catalytic performance of 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO is close to that of 20 %Pt/C (η10 118 mV) and outperforms (superior or comparable performance with) the majority of MXene-based electro - catalysts in alkaline medium that have been reported before (Table 2). For instance, the pure Ti3C2Tx shows overpotentials of 541 mV [90] and Table 1 Binding energies and atomic percentages of the corresponding elements in the Pt 4f regions. Element Binding Energy (eV) Atomic Percentage (%) Pt (0) 71.4 35.60 Pt (0) 73.2 30.60 Pt (0) 74.5 6.50 Pt272.40 11.43 Pt277.0 3.98 Pt275.9 3.46 Pt478.40 8.44F.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 9
