344 mV [91] with Tafel slopes as high as 468 mV/dec [90] and 216 mV/dec [91]. The 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst in this work significantly reduces the kinetic barriers for HER with a η10 233 mV and a Tafel slope of 182 mV/dec. Other observations can be made where Ti3C2Tx-based hybrids, such as integrated Ti3C2Tx-ZIF-8 (η10 507 mV; Tafel 77mV/dec) [92] and Ti3C2Tx/Co (OH) 2 (η10 380 mV; Tafel  99mV/dec) [12], still require much higher overpotential than the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst. In summary, the incorporation of Pt NPs, Ti3C2Tx, and rGO offers synergistic enhancement for HER perfor - mance by significantly improving charge transfer efficiency and increasing the accessibility of active sites, which are two significant limitations in most of the MXene alone systems. The 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO can be an excellent choice for low over- potential, relatively high current density, and an eco-friendly synthesis approach involving the microwave-assisted hydrothermal technique. Thus, the results also indicate that 10 wt% is the optimal value for the Pt NPs to be integrated within the heterostructure of Ti3C2Tx/rGO and offer the best synergistic effect in enhancing the HER activity for the reduc - tion of proton (H) in alkaline media. For better pictorial presentation, the overpotential for the reported electrocatalysts in this study are illustrated in a histogram graph, shown in Fig. 5 (c), highlighting the excellent performance of the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst. Although the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst shows competitive performance among MXene-based systems, it still falls short of the benchmark activity exhibited by the commercial Pt/C electrocatalyst. The noticeable difference in overpotential between the two electro - catalysts can be largely attributed to the significantly lower Pt NPs content in the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO composite. Theoretically, 1 mg of the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst contains approximately 100 μg of Pt0, assuming complete reduction of precursors, whereas 1 mg of the com- mercial 20 wt% Pt/C contains 200 μg of Pt0. The difference in Pt loading likely contributes to the gap in the overpotential values observed. Despite lower Pt NPs content, the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO still presents a promising alternative electrocatalyst, owing to the synergistic effects Fig. 5.HER polarization curves for (a) all electrocatalysts and (b) Ti3C2Tx, rGO, Ti3C2Tx/rGO, and Pt/C in an enlarged image. (c) Corresponding overpotential values for all electrocatalysts at 10 mA/cm2 in a pictorial graph. (d) Corresponding Tafel plots. Table 2 HER activities comparison of MXene-based state-of-the-art electrocatalysts. Catalyst Electrolyte Substrate Overpotential, η10 (mV)Tafel slope (mV/ dec)Ref m-NiFe 2O4 1 M KOH GCE 584 187.5 [93] CoP@Ti 3C2- MXene1 M KOH GCE 243 66 [94] Ti3C2 flakes 0.5 M H2SO4GCE 385 188 [95] MoSe 2/ MXene0.5 M H2SO4GCE 350 200 [96] Ti3C2Tx@ZIF- 81 M KOH GCE 507 77 [92] Co–Ti3C2 1 M NaOH GCE 344 216 [97] Ti3C2Tx 1 M KOH Clip electrode541 468 [90] Ti3C2 1 M KOH Carbon paper344 216 [98] Ti3C2Tx/Co (OH) 21 M KOH GCE 380 99 [12] Co3�/Cr2CTx 1 M KOH GCE 404 112 [13] Co3�/V2CTx 1 M KOH GCE 458 98.1 Co3�/Ti2CTx 1 M KOH GCE 460 103 10Pt/ Ti3C2Tx/ rGO1 M KOH GCE 233 182 This workF.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 10
