between MXene and rGO, as well as the strong metal-support coupling within the heterostructure framework. The performance of this optimal electrocatalyst was further evaluated in acidic media and compared with its performance in alkaline media. As shown in the HER polarization curves (Fig. S2), the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst was unable to reach a current density of 10 mA/cm2, indicating limited activity under acidic conditions. In contrast, the electrocatalyst in the alkaline media required overpotentials of η50 426 mV and η70 516 mV to deliver 50 and 70 mA/cm2, respectively. Meanwhile Pt/C electrocatalyst recorded the overpotential of 247 mV to deliver 49 mA/cm2. The superior elec- trocatalyst activity in the alkaline media is attributed to surface termi - nations on the MXene sheets, facilitating the H–OH bond dissociation [83]. Hence, the presence of rGO suppresses MXene restacking and provides a conductive framework for electron transport [84]. The deposition of Pt NPs enhances the catalytic activity for the H adsorp - tion/desorption process [87]. On the other hand, MXene in the acidic media are susceptible to surface oxidation and dissolution of –OH/F terminations, resulting in low conductivity and reducing active-site accessibility [4]. Nevertheless, further optimization of NPs dispersion and porosity engineering could significantly enhance the catalytic ac- tivity for HER and help close the gap to the commercial benchmarks. The catalytic reaction of the electrocatalyst and the rate-determining steps were evaluated through the Tafel slope analysis, as illustrated in Fig. 5 (d). The Tafel slope reflects the relationship between overpotential and the logarithm of current density, providing insights into the reaction kinetics and identifying the rate-determining step. A lower value of the Tafel slope indicates more favorable kinetics, demonstrating that a smaller increase in overpotential is required to achieve a tenfold in- crease in current density, resulting in a higher reaction rate [91]. Based on the Tafel slope, the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst has the Tafel value of 182 mV/dec, which is lower than those pristine Ti3C2Tx (427 mV/dec), pristine rGO (338 mV/dec), Ti3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst (422 mV/dec), 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO (319 mV/dec), and comparable to 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO (185 mV/dec) electrocatalyst. The commercial Pt/C demonstrated an even lowest Tafel slope of 98 mV/dec. Relative to Pt/C, the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst therefore requires ~1.9 times more overpotential per decade increase in current, consistent with slower apparent HER kinetics. Hence, the potential difference (Δη10  141 mV) indicated that a higher driving force is needed for the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst to reach 10 mA/cm2. The higher Tafel slope for the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst suggested that the Volmer step is the rate-determining step, indicating that the intercalated ions on the electrode surface play a crucial role in accessing the surface for the hydrogen adsorption and desorption process. This scenario is evident as the higher the weight percentage of Pt NPs on the electrode surface (up to 10 wt% of Pt NPs), the lower the Tafel slope is achieved. These findings are similar to the previous literature, which reported a high Tafel slope with Volmer reaction as the rate-determining step [11]. Furthermore, the relatively high Tafel slope of 182 mV/dec observed for the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst suggests sluggish HER ki- netics, particularly during the initial hydrogen adsorption step (Volmer step). This phenomenon might be explained by the limitations in cata- lytic activity at the electrode surface, possibly due to partial Pt agglomeration on the GCE surface. Theoretically, the HER in the alka- line media involves the dissociation of water molecules to generate hydrogen, which introduces an additional kinetic barrier compared to acidic environments. So, when the electrocatalyst surface does not suf- ficiently facilitate water adsorption and activation, the water dissocia - tion reaction becomes slow, resulting in the Volmer step as a rate- limiting step and thereby exhibiting a high Tafel slope value. Despite the kinetics constraint, it is important to note that the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst still exhibits superior catalytic activity and plays a vital role in accessing the surface for adsorption/desorption of hydrogen, which is significant for the MXene-based electrocatalyst. The electrocatalysts ’ performance is further evaluated by calculating the ECSA through CV measurements conducted in the non-Faraday zone. Therefore, CV curves were measured against the different scan rates, including 10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, and 70 mV/s, as shown in the supple - mentary (Fig. S3). Subsequently, the Cdl are determined through linear fitting of current density versus different scan rates, as illustrated in Fig. 6 (a). The Cdl values have been calculated as 0.267, 0.177, 0.278, 2.25, 2.37, and 0.137 mF/cm2 for Ti3C2Tx, rGO, Ti3C2Tx/rGO, 5Pt/ Ti3C2Tx/rGO, 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, and 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, respectively. The ECSA value has been calculated from Cdl based on Eqn (5). From the given expression in Eqn (5), the Cdl is the double-layer capacitance while the Cs is the specific capacitance at the interface of the electrode (Cs  40 μF/cm2 for noble metal-based electrocatalyst) in KOH medium. The calculated ECSA values are 6.95, 4.43, 6.68, 56.25, 59.25, and 23.43 cm2 for Ti3C2Tx, rGO, Ti3C2Tx/rGO, 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/ rGO, and 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, respectively. The ECSA value is highest for the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst, indicating that 10 wt% of Pt NPs derived from the metal salts solution is the optimal loading. This composition dramatically boosted the catalytic activity of the electro - catalyst, leading to superior HER electrochemical performance. ECSACdlECs (5) The durability of the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst was evalu - ated through the chronopotentiometry plot at different current densities of 10 and 20 mA/cm2. The chronopotentiometry plot for 32 h, as depicted in Fig. 6 (b), confirms the excellent performance of the pro- longed HER application. The figure shows no prominent fluctuation for a potential drop during the durability test, attributed to the strong chemical bonding among the Pt NPs, Ti3C2Tx, and rGO, which facilitates a rapid reaction rate and excellent catalytic activity of the electro - catalyst. Hence, the stability of the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst was also analyzed through the LSV curves ’ behaviour before and after the durability measurement, as depicted in Fig. 6 (c). The figure shows no considerable differences in overpotential before and after the dura- bility test, and the findings are satisfactory. The firm and successful integration bonding between the Ti3C2Tx, rGO, and Pt NPs, as depicted in the TEM image (Fig. 3 (a)), allows a steady and continuous ion transport without compromising the catalytic performance for the hydrogen production, which has been seen during the durability and stability measurements. In addition to this, the present study extended analysis by examining the surface morphologies of the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst before and after the stability test. The electrocatalyst was electro - deposited onto F-doped tin oxide (FTO) substrate under comparable conditions, as described in the supplementary data. Prior to the stability test, the FESEM images of the electrocatalyst, as shown in Fig. S5(a-i, a- ii), reveal a layered MXene/rGO structure with noticeable wrinkling, which is advantageous for electron transport. Hence, bright NPs features observed on the stacked layers confirm the successful deposition of Pt NPs. After the 15 h chronopotentiometry at a fixed current density, the electrocatalyst morphology changed considerably. As depicted in Fig. S5 (b-i, b-ii), the initial distinct sheet-like features became thicker, rougher and more compact, with aggregated deposits surface. These changes can be attributed to long-term hydrogen bubble evolution, inducing restructuring, redeposition, and partial collapsing of the layered framework. Despite these morphological changes, the electrocatalyst remained strongly adhered to the FTO without evidence of large-scale peeling, suggesting robust mechanical stability. The corresponding chronopotentiometry profile over 15 h (Fig. S5c) showed only minor potential fluctuations, attributed to the transient hydrogen bubble accumulation and detachment during continuous current operation. Thus, the LSV curves for the electrocatalyst before and after stability test (Fig. S5d) revealed no significant increase in overpotential, under - scoring the excellent electrochemical durability of the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/ rGO electrocatalyst. Taken together with the earlier results on GCE, these findings confirm that the electrocatalyst exhibits remarkable sta- bility and maintains its catalytic performance on both electrode sub- strates (GCE and FTO), during prolonged HER operation.F.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 11
