The HER in alkaline media proceeds through a three-step mechanism as follows [81]: PtTi3C2TxH2Oe␀↔PtTi3C2TxHadsOH␀Volmer (I) PtTi3C2TxHadsH2Oe␀↔H2PtTi3C2TxOH␀Heyrovsky (II) PtTi 3C2TxHadsPtTi 3C2TxHads↔H2PtTi3C2TxTafel (III) The first step, the Volmer reaction, involves the adsorption of pro- tons onto the electrode surface (Step I). Subsequently, the second step can occur either in the sorption of the adsorbed species, as shown in the Heyrovsky reaction (Step II) or the combination of the adsorbed in- termediates, as represented in the Tafel reaction (Step III). The optimal electrocatalyst, 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, exhibited a Tafel slope of 182 mV/ dec, which is significantly higher than the theoretical value reported for the Heyrovsky (~40-60 mV/dec) and Tafel (~30 mV/dec) steps [99]. The higher Tafel slope suggests that the Volmer step, which involves the dissociation of molecules and the adsorption of hydrogen intermediates, is the rate-determining step. Thus, a higher Tafel slope of 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst reflects that the electrocatalyst re- quires more energy and time to dissociate the water molecules in the alkaline medium. Subsequently, the electrocatalyst may further the HER reaction through either the Heyrovsky (Step II) or Tafel (Step III) pathway. Based on the CV analysis, the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electro - catalyst demonstrates Cdl of 2.37 mF/cm2and ECSA of 59.25 cm2, demonstrating a high density of electrochemically active sites. Despite these favorable values, the Tafel pathway is considered less likely in alkaline media, as it requires the recombination of two adsorbed H␀, a challenging condition to be achieved in alkaline media due to the limited availability of protons. Conversely, the Heyrovsky step, which involves the reaction between a single adsorbent and a water molecule in the presence of an electron, is more feasible in typical conditions of alkaline HER. Additionally, the presence of Pt NPs further facilitates the charge transfer and promotes the hydrogen desorption, supporting the occurrence of the Heyrovsky pathway. Thus, despite the excellent ECSA and Cdl values, the HER 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst predomi - nantly follows the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism, with the Volmer step as the rate-determining step and the Heyrovsky step as the second step for hydrogen evolution. Similar observations have been reported where the electrocatalyst has also taken the route for the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism in the alkaline media [100]. The determination of the charge carrier of the electrocatalyst is identified through the arc size on the Nyquist curve. The smaller the arc size of the Nyquist curve, the higher the conductivity the electrocatalyst possesses, which enhances electron migration [11]. Fig. 6(d) illustrates the Nyquist plot derived from EIS for various electrocatalysts, including Ti3C2Tx, rGO, Ti3C2Tx/rGO and Pt-modified Ti3C2Tx/rGO. Generally, a small arc radius observed in the high-frequency region corresponds to the Rct, whereas a slope or linear tail in the low-frequency region is indicative of ion diffusion or mass-transport behavior. Based on the Nyquist plot, the Rct values for the Ti3C2Tx, rGO, Ti3C2Tx/rGO and 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO are 3.36, 5.27, 4.34 and 14.51 Ω, respectively. The corresponding Rct values are listed in Table 3. Among them, 15Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO exhibits the largest semicircular arc, suggesting that the high Pt loading (15 wt%) leads to increased charge transfer impedance, which might be due to the agglomeration of the NPs on the surface, as can be seen in the FESEM image (Fig. 3 (d)). The Fig. 6.(a) The corresponding Cdl of various electrocatalysts were obtained from the CV curves. (b) Chronopotentiometry durability of 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electro - catalyst at various current densities of 10 and 20 mA/cm2. (c) Stability before and after durability test of 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst. (d) Nyquist plots for HER performance through EIS measurement for all the fabricated electrocatalysts.F.E. Ab Latif et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                              International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  174 (2025)  151393 12
