agglomeration of the NPs on the electrode surface restricts electron migration [80], causing a higher potential required for hydrogen pro- duction and a higher Rct value. Meanwhile, the Nyquist plots for 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO and 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO display the absence of a defined semicircle in the high-frequency region (as seen in the inset of Fig. 6 (d)), suggesting a very minimal charge resistance occurred at the electrode-electrolyte interface. Therefore, the linear tail in the low fre- quency for 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst was steeper compared to 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO, suggesting more efficient ion diffusion, reflecting enhanced HER activities within the electrocatalyst. This phenomenon suggests that these electrocatalysts are predominantly governed by ion diffusion rather than interfacial electron transfer limitations. The linear region at low frequencies further confirms the presence of Warburg impedance, reflecting an ion-diffusion process. The ion-diffusion process for 5Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO and 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/ rGO electrocatalysts can be effectively modelled by a simplified equiv - alent circuit, as depicted in Fig. 6 (d). The circuit consists of only two elements, which are solution resistance (Rs) and Warburg impedance (Zw). The exclusion of Rct and Cdl is aligned with the Nyquist data, where the absence of a semicircle and the dominance of a Warburg tail affirm that charge transfer is not the limiting step. Additionally, the comple - mentary Bode phase plots (Fig. S4) reveal that the phase angle for 5Pt/ Ti3C2Tx/rGO and 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalysts reach ␀43•and ␀40•, respectively, indicating diffusion-dominated processes. Although Bode phase plots alone do not directly determine the proton diffusion, the phase angle values, which approach ␀45•, strongly suggest a diffusion-controlled reaction mechanism [101], which is typically suited for the HER process involving the proton transport to the active sites. Therefore, the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst demonstrates the steepest Warburg tails and favorable phase angle response compared to other electrocatalysts, indicating a synergistic effect among the Pt NPs, Ti3C2Tx and rGO. Hence, the addition of Pt NPs in the electrocatalyst enhances its performance for the HER reactions [81]. In this work, it is evident that when the electrocatalyst contains 10 % Pt NPs, the Warburg line of the electrocatalyst is prone to be steeper (towards the y-axis), suggesting a more likely accessible occurrence of HER. Furthermore, the in-situ integration of Pt NPs deposited from the Pt salts solution into the layers of 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst pro- vides more active sites, facilitating the ion adsorption and desorption during the proton (H) reduction. Eventually, the synergistic mecha - nisms formed between these three materials accommodate the interfa - cial effects, boosting the electrochemical HER performance. The interfacial effects include the stable formation of 2D sheet interfaces, additional active sites arising from the strong integration between Ti3C2Tx and rGO, and the establishment of short-range charge transport pathways within the heterostructure. These effects enhance electron migration efficiency. Besides, the reasonable distribution of Pt NPs within the heterostructure, as evident in the FESEM image (Fig. 3 (c)), facilitates rapid ion diffusion, elevating the electrocatalyst ’s electrical conductivity and stability. 4.Conclusions In this work, the development of Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO through microwave-assisted hydrothermal, represents a novel and significant advancement as an electrocatalyst design for HER application, particu - larly in alkaline media. The key finding demonstrates that optimal cat- alytic activity can be achieved with a minimal Pt loading of only 10 wt%, resulting in a low overpotential of 233 mV and a large electrochemical surface area of 59.25 cm2. The designation of the electrocatalyst was achieved through an eco-friendly, rapid, and cost-effective microwave- assisted hydrothermal synthesis, involving the simultaneous reduction of GO into rGO and in-situ deposition of Pt NPs using metal salt pre- cursors. The 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst shows significantly bet- ter performance than the reported pristine Ti3C2Tx. While some studies have integrated Ti3C2Tx with some other metals (such as CoP/Ti 3C2Tx and Co (OH) 2/Ti3C2Tx), achieving slightly improved catalytic activity, these systems often require higher metal loadings, lack structural simplicity and involve less environmentally friendly synthesis methods. In contrast, the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst in this study offers a balanced combination of high performance, structural simplicity, scal- able and green synthesis with minimal noble metal usage. Thus, the 10Pt/Ti 3C2Tx/rGO electrocatalyst is a promising and competitive candidate among the MXene-based HER electrocatalysts. It is worth noting that the excellent HER performance of the electrocatalyst is attributed to the synergistic interaction between the Pt NPs, Ti3C2Tx and rGO. These combinations offer enhanced charge transfer, abundant active sites and stable ion transport pathways. Thus, the electrocatalyst exhibits prolonged operational stability over 32 h at varying current densities, underscoring its durability for practical applications. This study introduces a new design paradigm by: (i) leveraging the oxygen functional groups in MXene and the presence of Pt NPs, which offer the additional catalytic sites, (ii) utilizing MXene as a conductive matrix to facilitate ion and electron pathways, and (iii) achieving high HER ac- tivity with minimal noble metal content. Looking ahead, further inves - tigation into tuning the degree of reduction of GO, defect concentrations of GO, and scalable synthesis techniques may unlock even higher elec- trochemical efficiencies, paving the way for sustainable, cost-effective hydrogen production technologies suitable for industrial deployments. CRediT author