Electrocatalysts possessing the ability to catalyze a wide range of re- actions (e.g. HER, OER, ORR etc.) at low costs and with high activity would be a major breakthrough in electrocatalysis [7]. Nevertheless, the task of creating versatile electrocatalysts that can effectively control many essential processes continues to be a major obstacle [8]. The field of catalysis has recently seen a surge in interest in Single- Atom Catalysts (SACs) [9], primarily due to their exceptional catalytic characteristics. These catalysts are characterized by a design that allows for a high density of active sites while utilizing a minimal quantity of metal [10]. This distinctive feature not only augments their effectiveness in catalytic processes but also contributes to the efficient use of metal resources [11]. The enhanced dispersion of noble metals (NMs) like Rh, Pt, Ir, and Au on clusters enables the production of various economical catalysts. This advancement in noble metal utilization is pivotal for creating cost-effective catalytic solutions in several catalytic applica - tions [12]. To boost the performance of metal-clustered catalysts, numerous theoretical and experimental investigations have focused on SACs [13]. Various findings suggest that reducing the size of metal particles increases their free energy, which in turn facilitates the crea- tion of SACs or metallic clusters [14]. Consequently, a substantial amount of research has been directed towards identifying the optimal cluster mechanism for SACs to prevent atom clustering while main - taining catalytic performance [15]. The introduction of strong metal- cluster interactions (SMSI) by introducing suitable clusters to enhance the local coordination environment and electrical characteristics is crucial for avoiding sintering [16]. It should be noted that SACs exhibit notable differences in reactivity compared to conventional catalysts that rely on extensive metal surfaces [17]. This offers intriguing possibilities in the field of catalysis. SACs are often clustered by various 2D materials like graphene, [18] nitrogen-doped graphene [19], carbon nitride, [20] MoS2 [21], metal oxides [22] and MXenes [23]. However, clusters like clusters based on POMs to stabilized SACs are rarely reported. Polyoxometalates (POM) clusters offer a highly effective model sys- tem to explore the functionality of SACs anchored to metal oxides [24]. This is due to the POM surface containing terminal groups (M O) and bridging groups (e.g., M␀O␀M). These specific groups on the POM surface facilitate coordination at the metal center, making POMs an ideal choice for studying SACs once the suitable active sites are identi - fied [25]. Polyoxotantalate (POT) clusters have demonstrated excep - tional capability as a host material for single-atom and complex transition metals. It has been reported that Rh/POT with a weight per- centage of 0.9 % exhibited consistent and robust catalytic activity for CO oxidation over an extended period [26]. Researchers have recently emphasized the exciting prospects of polyoxometalate (POM) derived materials for their use in electro - catalysis and energy storage applications [27]. Regarding POM-based SACs, Talib et al. conducted a theoretical examination of the series of transition metals at the four hollow sites of phosphomolybdic acid (PMA) polyoxometalate clusters. In this study they have demonstrated Pt1/PMA (␀0.02 eV) for best HER catalyst while excellent OER activity exhibited by Co1/PMA (0.45 V), Pt1/PMA (0.49 eV). The optimal ORR activity is shown by Fe1/PMA (0.42 V) [28]. A new approach to SAC generation was the subject of another investigation. Researchers suc- cessfully dispersed cobalt atoms using POM cluster traps, resulting in stable and very active SACs for ORR [29]. Zhuo-Ran Zhang et al. experimentally achieved exceptionally high hydrogen evolution cata- lytic activity at the most exposed nickel site of the polyoxometalate {NiSb 6O4(H2O)3[β-Ni(hmta)SbW 8O31]3}15␀(NiPOM), by anchoring NiPOM onto graphene oxide (GO). Ultimately, they integrated NiPOM/ GO with a CdS photoabsorber to develop a cost-effective and highly efficient hydrogen evolution system [30]. Notably, SACs may be firmly anchored at the lacunary sites of POMs and serve as active centers for diverse catalytic reactions [31–33]. The oxoanionic characteristics of POM clusters are essential for redox acid catalytic processes. The exceptional structural properties prevent the aggregation of anchored transition metals on various POM supports [34]. The Jun Li group has conducted a various POM-SACs based studies [35–38]. At ambient conditions, they observed that Fe1-SA PTA exhibited enhanced catalytic efficacy for the ethylene epoxidation process. The results suggest that POM cluster-based M1/PMA (where M1 represents Rh, Pt, Mo, Ru, etc.) SACs have considerable stability and are engaged in a range of signifi - cant reactions, particularly in hydrogenation processes. Multiple PTA- supported SACs were analyzed, revealing that Pt-PTA had superior catalytic activity in hydrogenation [39]. A comprehensive study of the electrical structures and catalytic mechanisms of these SACs informs the advancement of POM-based practical applications [40–42]. Here, we are focusing on Keggin-type Polyoxotantalate (POT) cluster consisting of a phosphorus (P)-rich Keggin core surrounded by 40 oxy- gen (O) and 12 tantalum (Ta) atoms. We thus affirm that no research has been conducted on this particular cluster. Based on these features and experimental evidence on relevant clusters, we suggest screening various TM adatoms clustered by the POT cluster as accomplished novel SACs for HER, OER, and ORR studies. In this work, the catalytic activity of 3d/4d/5d transition metals anchored on POT cluster is explored using DFT methods. The study highlighted Mn/POT and Pt/POT as excellent HER catalyst, exhibiting low overpotential |ΔGH*| values of ␀0.06 eV and 0.03 eV respectively. The OER is also well catalyzed by Pt/POT (0.33 V), Co/POT (0.43 V), and Rh/POT (0.67 V). These catalysts have overpotential that is com- parable to MoC 2 (0.45 V) [43], RuO 2 (0.42 V) [44], and IrO2 (0.56 V) [45] demonstrating strong OER activity. Furthermore, it was worth noting that both the noble metal Pt/POT and the non-noble metal Co/ POT exhibit impressive abilities in facilitating the ORR, displaying investigated overpotentials of 0.34 V and 0.35 V, accordingly. Further - more, we have found that the newly created catalysts, Co/POT, Rh/POT, and Pt/POT, are suitable candidates foremploying the solvation model. The low overpotential indicates that Pt/POT cluster working as tri- functional electrocatalysts for HER/OER and ORR. These findings highlight the catalytic significance of Pt/POT and Co/POT as versatile water splitting electrocatalysts for both OER and ORR. 2.Computational details All the calculations were performed using spin-polarized DFT methods with GGA-PBE exchange –correlation functional [46] employed via Vienna Ab Initio Simulation Package (VASP) [47,48] . Comprehen - sive DFT modelling aids in understanding the electronic, structural characteristics and the overall impact of adatoms over the POT cluster. The ion cores are strategically implemented with the Projector Augmented Wave (PAW) pseudo-potentials approach [49]. To explain valence electrons plane-wave cut-off energy of 450 eV is used. The Grimme ’s semiempirical dispersion-correction (DFT-D3) scheme was employed to investigate the long-range van der Waals interactions [50]. We employed VASPsol [38], to integrates solvation effect using a self- consistent continuum model, for conducting implicit solvation calcula - tions. For geometric optimization, the process adhered to a strict total energy convergence criterion of 10␀5eV. The relaxation of ions was permitted until the maximum force acting on them was attenuated to a threshold of 0.02 eV/Å. A vacuum space (20 Å) was utilized in the z-axis to eliminate interlayer interactions. In the examination of the Brillouin zone, a 1 x 1 x 1 Γ point mesh was employed [51]. Additionally, to accurately quantify electron charge transfer, the Bader charge analysis (q) methodology was applied [52]. The density of state analysis (DOS) calculations were also done using (NEDOS 2000) and post-processed using the VASPKIT package [53]. The Gibbs free energy change (ΔGH*) for each step of the reaction, the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) [37] was utilized as reference potential for HER. On using POT as a cluster we determined the adhesive properties of adatoms (TM 3d/4d/5d) as: Ebin EPOT adatom – EPOT – Eadatom                                                (1)F. Abbas et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computational  and Theoretical  Chemistry  1244  (2025)  115044 2
