ransfer, electron distributions, and chemical reac- tivity [62]. To gain deeper insights into the binding energy properties between adatoms and the POT cluster, we conducted an analyzed the PDOS relative to the Fermi energy level (EF). This analysis focused on Fig. 1.(a) Depicts the optimized configuration of the POT cluster, providing both top and side views. The primary emphasis is on pinpointing the ideal adsorption site for the transition metal (TM) element. The color scheme utilized distinguishes various elements: Ta is represented in sky blue, P in pink, Obin in gray, Ot in green, and Obri in red. (b) The theoretically computed binding energies of adatoms over the POT cluster. (c) The PDOS plot for doped TM (Pt as test) over the POT cluster. (d-g) Radial distribution function of POT cluster and modified with TM (e-h) The AIMD simulated temperature and energy patterns at 300 K of POT cluster along doped TM over the surface. It clearly shows the fluctuations of peaks remains constant over time (fs). (f-i) RSMD plots over simulations time to identify the structural changes. The calculated consistent behavior depicts our modified surface is dynamically stable.F. Abbas et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computational  and Theoretical  Chemistry  1244  (2025)  115044 4
