investigating the interaction between metal 3d and 3 s orbitals with neighboring oxygen 2p orbitals. The outcomes of this study, as illus- trated in Fig. S7-S10 in the ESI, reveal a robust affinity of adatoms and spin to towards the oxygen-2P orbitals within the POT cluster. Notably, at 4H site energy bands above the EF, the adatoms display a favorable interaction with the oxygen atom, showcasing both spin-up and spin- down. The PDOS peaks relative to the EF can also indicate the strength of the interaction between the adsorbate and the catalyst. Peaks close to the EF suggest stronger interactions, which may be relevant to the binding strength of intermediates in OER [63]. The main contribution from spin-up and spin-down states will depend on coordination envi- ronment of doped TM over the catalyst surface. The existence of a strong covalent metal-cluster interaction at the 4-H sites of POT is evidenced in the positioning of the PDOS peaks relative to the EF. 3.2. HER catalytic activity of TM/POT cluster To evaluate the HER performance of designed TM@POT catalysts, H atom is adsorbed over the modified cluster. We analyze the adsorption strength of H* atoms referred as ΔGH* at the extremely favorable 4H sites. These 4H sites serve as active centers, allowing us to assess the HER activity and calculate the Gibbs free energies for hydrogen adsorption ΔGH* using the Eq. (2). Our primary objective is to pinpoint the optimal catalyst, which exhibits a ΔGH* value close to zero during the HER process [64]. The adsorption of TMs on the cluster is considered energetically favorable only when it facilities the adsorption and desorption processes. According to Sabatier principle its nor to be high nor low [65]. This is particularly desirable for the HER as it promotes the strong binding interaction. However, excessively negative adsorption energies can be problematic. When adsorption is too strong, hydrogen molecules may struggle to desorb from the catalyst surface, resulting in a reduced HER rate. In extreme cases, the catalyst may become poisoned, causing the HER to cease entirely. Therefore, a delicate balance of adsorption energies is crucial to ensure optimal performance in HER catalysis. When the adsorption energies of adatoms indicate a positive scenario for hydrogen adsorption, it signifies a kinetically unstable sit- uation. These conditions are unfavorable to catalytic processes that depend on stable surface adsorption of reactants to progress efficiently. Generally, the effectiveness of a catalyst for the HER can be assessed by the standard rule, which requires the absolute value of the adsorption energy difference |ΔGH*| to be less than or equal to 0.20 eV [66,67] . Fig. 2(a •c) shows the calculated ΔGH* values for H atom adsorption on the POT cluster. Among the considered TMs, Sc, Ti, V, Cr, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Nb, Rh, Pd, Ag, Ta, Pt, and Au have positive ΔGH* values (F0.2 eV) at the 4H site, indicating quite higher values than the ideal for HER. This suggests that these catalysts are unsuitable for promoting HER due to their weaker hydrogen adsorption abilities. On the other hand, Mn, Fe, Mo, Tc, Ru, W, Re, Os, and Ir all exhibit negative ΔGH* values, indicating a strong interaction between H* and POT surface. Conse - quently, these catalysts tend to retain hydrogen atoms, hindering the HER performance on their surfaces. On performing the catalytic activity, we found, Pt/POT (ΔGH* 0.03 eV), Mn/POT (ΔGH* ␀0.06 eV), Ir/ POT (ΔGH* ␀0.13 eV), Ru/POT (ΔGH* ␀0.22 eV), Rh/POT (ΔGH* and Tc/POT (ΔGH* 0.18 eV) eV have ΔGH* values that are positioned around the optimal value, suggesting highly active HER performance. Based on these findings, the Pt/POT and Mn/POT electrocatalyst Fig. 2.(a-c) The Gibbs free energy profile (ΔGH*) for the HER over the POT cluster, specifically at the 4-H (Hollow) sites. The magnitude of the (ΔGH*) for the activity of HER is nearly negligible. (d) The volcano plot displays the exchange current (io) of HER