demonstrate superb catalytic activity for the HER, with an optimal |Δ GH*| value of 0.03 eV and ␀0.06 eV respectively. Our best catalyst Mn/ POT (ΔGH* ␀0.06 eV) and Pt/POT (ΔGH* 0.03 eV) even have better catalytic hydrogen production performance than well-known Pt (111) (ΔGH* ␀0.09 eV) [68]. In comparison to some recent studies, such as MoC-Pt shows (␀0.19 eV) on employing Pt site [69], these cluster pro- vide are providing exceptional HER activity. Our findings on the HER demonstrate superior performance compared to the newly studied Phosphomolybdic acid (M1/PMA), as the most effective catalyst with a V1/PMA value of (ΔGH* 0.08 eV) [28]. In Fig. 2d, the exchange current rate (i0) was plotted against the (ΔGH*) value using a volcano plot [70]. These two parameters were crucial in quantitatively evaluating the performance of the HER. Cata- lysts exhibiting positive (ΔGH*) values were situated on the right side of the volcano plot, suggesting difficulties in adsorbing protons, which consequently resulted in minimal exchange current rates (i0). On the other hand, catalysts with negative (ΔGH*) values, such as Mn, Fe, Mo, Tc, Ru, W, Re, Os, and Ir, were situated at the bottom left of the curve. These catalysts exhibited strong interactions with H*, leading to low exchange current rates (i0). Following Sabatier ’s principle [71], cata- lysts with near-optimal (ΔGH*) values occupied the top of the volcano curve including Mn/POT and Pt/POT. Notably, Mn/POT, Pt/POT, Ir/ POT, Ru/POT, Rh/POT catalysts demonstrated maximum exchange current rates (i0) and nearly optimal (ΔGH*) value indicating exceptional HER performance. The Bader charge (q) analysis of our best HER can- didates (Mn/POT and Pt/POT) clearly demonstrates that the Mn/POT catalyst (q 1.79 |e|) has a higher Bader value. This is attributed to the increased number of electrons transferred from the (TM) to the cluster surface. The magnitude of the transfer charge is entirely depending onnumber of electrons and the atomic radius of the TM. Furthermore, the electronic interactions resulting from the downward shiftof the d- band center (εd) significantly enhance the catalytic activity of the HER. Zhang and coworkers examined the downward shift of (εd) of the Pt 5d orbital in the PtNi 5-0.3 alloy when modified with Ni. This shift results in a reduction of the Pt-H adsorption energy, thereby lowering the Gibbs free energy. In our work, the downward shifting of (εd) is strongly in line with the literature, with Pt/POT catalyst (εd -1.23 eV) exhibiting higher downward shift of (εd) than Mn/POT catalyst (εd -0.23 eV). 3.3. Investigating OER and ORR catalytic activity of TM Embedded POT cluster Nørskov ’s computational hydrogen electrode framework was used to assess the catalytic performance of TM/POT clusters with adsorbed in- termediates for OER reactions [68]. The OER consists of four rigorous adsorption –desorption processes on the metal sites of the catalyst sur- face, as illustrated in Fig. 3and Eq. (5)•8. The energy calculations demonstrated that the *OH, *O, and *OOH radical intermediates have the ability to undergo stable adsorption over the surface, the values of adsorbate are depicted in Table S3. The initial stage involves the dissociation of H2O into *OH, where (*) represents the adsorption active site) and Hupon the catalyst surface. While in the second step involves the dissociation of *OH into *O and H species. In the third phase, an *O species reacts with another H2O molecule, resulting in the formation of *OOH and the release of a proton onto the surface. Finally, the fourth step is the dissociation of *OOH species into Hand O2, followed by the removal of the O2 fragment from the M/POT cluster. Fig. 3presents compelling evidence, illustrating the persistent occurrence of both proton release and electron emission throughout all elementary steps. The value of ΔG is computed for each reaction to ascertain the step that limits the overall potential. The overpotential should reach zero after two fundamental steps in an ideal electrocatalyst for OER which ought to possess a barrier to energy of 1.23 eV. Catalysts ’ varying successive energy barriers explain why their OER activities differ [72]. The Fig. 4illustrates that the (ΔGOH*) values associated with the initial proton-electron pairing in the M/POT systems (where M represents V, Cr, Mn, Nb, Mo, Tc, Ru, Ta, W, Re, Os, Ir) exhibit negative values, ranging from ␀0.18 to ␀2.00 eV. These negative magnitudes suggest a significant interaction between the adatoms and the hydroxylated *OH intermediate. However, the overpotential-limiting processes are the production of *OOH from *O during the third phase of the proton-coupled electron transfer and the release of the O2 fragment from the surface of the M/POT cluster in the concluding stage. Based on the calculated OER overpotential (ηOER) for different catalysts, such as Sc (1.63 V), Ti (1.71 V), Cr (1.09 V), Mn (1.02 V), Fe (0.97 V), Cu (1.34 V), Zn (2.14 V), Nb (1.20 V), Mo (1.52 V), Tc (1.51 V), Ru (1.63 V), Ag (1.56 V), Ta (1.51 V), W (1.84 V), Re (1.86 V), Os (2.03 V), Ir (1.66 V), and Au (1.01 V). Comprehensive Gibbs free energy pathway of all investigated metals with intermediates and energy required for each step are presented in Fig. S11-S14 of ESI. On the other hand, M/POT (Sc, Ti, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Rh, Pd, Ag, Pt, Au) catalysts exhibit positive ΔGOH* values, resulting in lower over- potential compared to the former catalysts. Among them, the Cu/POT, Ag/POT, and Au/POT catalysts stand out with the dissociation of H2O to *OH and Hin the first proton-electron coupling transfer step being unlikely due to the maximum step distance. It was investigated that Zn/ POT, Ag/POT, Cu/POT, Sc/POT Au/POT, Ni/POT, Pd/POT, Ti/POT exhibit very strong bonding properties between adatoms and *O at ΔG2 (second step) bond strength, making the formation of *OOH from O* at ΔG3 and the desorption of an O2 at ΔG4 at the fourth stage is more challenging. Considering the Ti (1.71 V), Cr (1.09 V), Mn (1.02 V), Fe (0.97 V), Cu (1.34 V), Zn (2.14 V), Nb (1.20 V), Mo (1.52 V), Tc (1.51 V), Ru (1.63 V), Ag (1.56 V), Ta (1.51 V), W (1.84 V), Re (1.86 V), Os (2.03 V), Ir (1.66 V), and Au (1.01 V), it can be concluded that these catalytic systems are also unsuitable for the OER activity. Notably, Pt/POT, Co/POT, Rh/POT, and Pd/POT Catalysts exhibit a remarkable limiting potential for OER, wherein ΔG3 (O* → OOH*) acts as the potential-determining step (PDS) to find the overpotential. Particularly, these catalysts achieve OER potentials of 0.33 V, 0.43 V, 0.67 V, and 0.86 V, respectively. The OER values of these catalysts, in particular, are comparable to those of well-known catalysts as RuO 2 (0.37 V) [73], MoC 2 (0.45 V) [43], IrO2 (0.56 V) [74], Pt/Ni-N 2C2 (0.27/ 0.40 V) [75], Ni/pd-C 4 (0.31/0.36 V) [75], and Rh-N (0.31 V) [76], exhibiting outstanding catalytic activity as shown in Fig. S10-S14 ESI. This shows that Pt/POT and Co/POT catalysts are quite effective for OER catalytic activity. By lowering the activation energy necessary for the process that includes the reduction of oxygen molecules (O2) to Fig. 3.The schematic representation of optimized intermediate geometries reveals a distinct 4-electron catalytic pathway for the OER occurring at the 4H site on the TMs/POT cluster. The reverse of the catalytic pathway is for the ORR.F. Abbas et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computational  and Theoretical  Chemistry  1244  (2025)  115044 6
