uitable for H2O production in the fourth step. The calculated binding strengths for W/POT (3.23 V), Re/ POT (2.66 V), Mo/POT (1.52 V), Ta/POT (2.46 V), Os/POT (2.03 V), Tc/ POT (2.02 V), Mn/POT (1.90 V), Ru/POT (1.82 V), V/POT (1.68 V), and Au/POT (1.34 V) have greater expected ORR values than the well- known Pt (0.45 V) catalyst [78] making O2 attachment and makes the process of activation difficult. Conversely, the non-noble metal Co/POT catalysts (0.35 V) and noble metal Pt/POT catalysts (0.34 V) have the lowest ORR values, and the fourth step is the potential-limiting step (*OH → H2O), is constructed in such a manner that the ORR is acceptable. In the same first step (O2 → OOH*) both Co/POT and Pt/POT becomes a potential limiting step. The electrocatalysts Co/POT and Pt/POT exhibit exceptional performance, surpassing well-known benchmark values such as Rh-N 4 (0.36 V) [79], Pd/Ni/Pt-C 4 (0.34 and 0.46 V) [80], and Ru-N 3Cl (0.36 V) [81], as illustrated in Fig. 5while in clustering information Fig. S11-S14 ESI. Consequently, Co/POT and Pt/POT hold tremendous promise as can- didates for ORR and OER catalytic applications. We will now focus on to the potential use of SACs in the aforementioned reactions after per- forming a comprehensive investigation of the catalytic execution of the OER and ORR. To get a better understanding of adsorbate with catalyst surface, PDOS [28] simulated spectra were used to investigate the arrangement of energy levels of metal d-orbitals, which play an important role in Fig. 4.(a-c) The Gibbs free energy profiles for the OER of 3d, 4d and 5d series transition metal adatoms were methodically calculated over POT cluster at (U 0 V). These plots, clearly pinpoint the step that limits the potential in each respective elementary reaction. It shows that Co/POT (0.43 V) and Pt/POT (0.33 V) showing excellent OER activity exhibit lowest overpotential value. Comprehensive Gibbs free energy profile along with catalytic pathway of all investigated 