promoting reaction pathways that impact the catalytic effectiveness of TM over the POT cluster. The d-band center (εd), which represents the average energy level of these d-orbitals in the transition metal atoms inside the catalyst, is critical for catalytic activity [82]. It controls the energy levels at which reactant molecules may interact with the surface of the catalyst. According to the Sabatier principle, obtaining optimum catalytic activity requires a balanced adsorption energy of reactants on the surface of the catalyst, ensuring neither too weak nor too strong interactions [71]. As a result, achieving the correct adsorption energy becomes critical. We estimated the adsorption energies and offered the relevant d-band center data for the HER (ΔGH*) and OER (ΔGOH*, ΔGO*, ΔGOOH*) steps in Table S2•S3 ESI. The d-band center is essential for assessing the adsorp - tion–desorption capacity of adsorbates towards determining the cata- lytic performance of HER and OER. According to the literature, a more negative d-band shift suggests that fewer electrons are present and the antibonding state are fully occupied, resulting in a weaker adsorption between the doped TM and the surfaces [83–85]. It is commonly accepted that, downward shift of d-band center facilities the H desorp - tion while the upward shift promotes the H adsorption. However, excessively strong or weak binding might have an impact on the elec- tronic configurations and coordination environment among doped TM and POT clusters, which is essential for the adsorption as well as desorption processes. In our study, a higher d-band centers exhibited by Zn/POT (εd -3.71 eV) cluster. It clearly indicates that; less electrons are available for the desorption process to facilities the rate of desorp - tion which is more significant to enhance the HER activity. That ’s the reason Zn/POT cluster exhibiting the higher HER activity (ΔGH* 1.91 eV). This higher catalytic activity is due to positive ΔEH*. The lowest d- band centers value is represented by Mo/POT (εd -0.01 eV); the adsorption capacity is little influenced by the established coordinating environment between the Mo and POT cluster, as well as the modulation of the electronic modes and energy of adsorption of the entire catalyst. Surfaces with d-band centers close to the EF have higher catalytic ac- tivity for certain processes owing to stronger adsorbate –surface in- teractions [86]. More negative of the d-band center value tends to exhibit the more strongly binding of hydrogen/oxygen intermediates over the POT cluster. We have not identified a linear relationship be- tween the d-band center and the catalytic activity. Consequently, we believe that based on above study and literature, a variety of additional parameters, such as the intensity of adsorbed intermediates, the Bader charge, and the binding energy, are involved in determining the cata- lytic activity. Utilizing the adsorption energies of different intermediate species, techniques based on descriptors are used to pinpoint and develop appropriate catalysts. This study also examines the association between Fig. 5.(a-c) The Gibbs free energy profiles for the ORR of 3d, 4d, and 5d transition metals were theoretically calculated over the POT cluster at (U 0 V). These plots, clearly pinpoint the step that limits the potential in each respective elementary reaction. It shows that Co/POT (0.35 V) and Pt/POT (0.34 V) exhibit the excellent ORR activity showing the lowest overpotential value. Comprehensive Gibbs free energy pathways of all investigated metals with intermediates are pre- sented in ESI (S11-S14) .F. Abbas et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computational  and Theoretical  Chemistry  1244  (2025)  115044 8
