oxygenated intermediates (ΔGO*) and the adsorption-free energy for different M/POT clusters. Shaobing et al. demonstrated that the down - ward shift of the d-band center lowers the adsorption barriers for OER intermediates and accelerates reaction kinetics, hence improving elec- trocatalytic performance [87]. Doped TM 3d-orbitals concurrently overlap with O-2p orbitals, therefore reducing the reaction energy barrier through a synergistic effect, which facilitates the deionization of *OH, thus adjusting the electronic structure towards improving the OER activity [88]. The adsorption energies fluctuate and the surface energy of the d-band center is affected by the bonding and anti-bonding elec- tronic configurations between the anchoring metal ’s d-band and the POT cluster, which are influenced by its interaction with oxygen in- termediates. In this study, the Pt/POT cluster exhibited the lowest en- ergy barrier (ΔG2 1.45 eV) with a d-band center value (εd -1.23 eV). It can be inferred that a balanced downward shift of the d-band center is more crucial in promoting the desorption step, which plays an important role in decreasing the energy barrier of intermediates in the search for the best OER catalyst. Based on the coefficients of determination (R2 0.971), it is evident that a significant linear correlation exists between ΔGOOH* and ΔGOH* [89]as illustrated in Fig. 6(a). Further investigations were conducted to explore the association between ΔGO* and ΔGOH* with the limiting po- tential of the M/POT catalyst ’s negative maximum potential. The find- ings demonstrate a distinct connection between ΔGO* and ΔGOH* and the negative maximum potential (␀ΔGmax) are explored, as illustrated through a volcano curve in Fig. 6(b). This curve reveals that certain M/ POT catalysts, such as Pt/POT and Co/POT, occupy the apex and exhibit the lowest overpotential ηOER values (0.33 and 0.43 V, respectively). Newly designed clusters situated near the curve ’s apex generally exhibit higher catalytic efficiency for the reaction being studied because they have the optimal adsorption energy that makes the surface reactive enough to facilitate the reaction. Metals to the left or right of the peak might be less efficient because they either bind the reactants too weakly or too strongly, respectively. Consequently, these catalysts Pt/POT and Co/POT display promising potential as highly effective electrocatalysts for OER, making them viable candidates to further enhance the catalytic activity of TM/POT clusters. 3.4. Continuum solvation model In addition, VASPsol was utilized to apply an implicit solvation model that took into consideration the impact of solvation on reactive intermediates [90]. In this section, we will discuss the solvent envi- ronment on catalysts that have already been identified as the best can- didates for OER and ORR. A popular method is the continuum solvation approach [91], which views the solvent as a uniform dielectric around the solute. The purpose of this was to identify the optimal values for the solvation parameters of the OER and ORR candidates that were considered in our study. When working with a large-scale dielectric medium, implicit solvent models are typically thought to be correct. Due to limited polarization and the inability of surface water molecules at the catalytic site to freely rotate, the dielectric constant is drastically reduced. Because water ’s dielectric influence is weaker at the interface, the implicit solvent model could exaggerate the solvent ’s impact on the Co/POT, Rh/POT, and Pt/POT catalyst set on the POT surface. As far as HER is concerned, the overpotential seems to be unaffected by the sol- vent [92]. Hence, it was deemed superfluous to include solvent effects in HER analysis. We observed that Pt experienced a lower OER overpotential in the solvent phase than Co and Rh. Pt has lower intermediate state energies than Co and Rh over the whole reaction coordinated with optimal en- ergy barriers, according to the calculated energy profile. This implies that Pt enables OER in a solvent environment with less energy need and stabilizes the transition states of the reaction intermediate, showing its enhanced catalytic efficiency. In comparison to Rh, the core of the Pt (εd) value is closer to the Fermi level. Pt has a greater ability to modify its electronic structure as a result, which facilitates the adsorption and release of chemical in- termediates in the OER. Pt/POT with overpotential values in OER and ORR are (0.27 V) and (0.21 V), respectively. As a result, the process has less overpotential. This reduced Gibbs free energy shift for the reaction shows that in the presence of a solvent, Pt provides a more favorable channel for OER, which may contribute to higher catalytic activity. Even though it concentrates on the ORR pathway, the last step entails reducing OH* to water and releasing the adsorbed species. Pt has the lowest overall energy trajectory, indicating that water formation is more energetically favorable in Pt. Rh falls somewhere in the middle, with a higher overpotential than Pt but a lower overpotential than Co as depicted in Fig. 7(a-b) . The slopes of the free energy step for each metal are indicative of the intermediates ’ binding energies. Pt possesses ideal binding energies that are neither too strong nor too weak, facilitating intermediate production and release. Co and Rh can connect too strongly or too weakly, complicating the process and increasing over- potential. Our investigation into solvents centered on examining the Fig. 6.(a) Scaling relationship between Gibbs free energies (ΔG) and intermediates (ΔGOH*). (b) Graphical representation of the maximum negative potential (␀ΔGmax) about the difference between ΔGO* and ΔGOH* toward catalyzing the OER activity.F. Abbas et al.                                                                                                                                                                