POT cluster and its relationship with catalytic properties. Within this framework, the Co/POT and Pt/POT were confirmed to behave as a bi- functional catalyst, with reduced overpotential values. 3.5. Water dissociation mechanism (HER-Alkaline) The least energy route for water dissociation provides mechanistic insight into the creation of hydrogen using water splitting (H2O → H OH␀). The examination of the minimum potential energy profile from the initial state (IS) to the final state (FS) via transition state (TS) pro- vides an understanding of the energy barriers and stability of interme - diate states. This information is essential for the development of highly efficient catalysts for the process of HER. The activation barrier refers to the energy differences between the reactants and products, specifically water molecules used in the initial state, and the greatest energy tran- sition state found along the reaction pathway after crossing the TS. This barrier is the threshold energy needed for water molecules to separate into hydrogen. The activation barrier in the hydrogen evolution reaction, specif - ically in the analysis of water dissociation, reveals the level of difficulty or ease in which the water molecule separates into Hand OH␀ions. Generating Hions are a crucial stage in HER since it is necessary for producing hydrogen. The activation energy barrier throughout water dissociation is illustrated in Fig. 8for the most effective HER catalysts, including Mn/POT, Tc/POT, Ir/POT, and Pt/POT. The calculated acti- vation energy (TS) required to overcome the barrier for the various best HER catalysts in this study including Mn/POT, Tc/POT, Ir/POT, and Pt/ POT are 0.42, 0.29, 0.23, and 0.60 eV, and exothermic reaction energies Fig. 7.(a-b) Figure shows the performance of Co, Rh, and Pt-doped POT surface catalysts in the presence of solvent (water) for each elementary stage of the OER and ORR free energy pathway. It highlights the energy requirements for each step and the overpotential of investigated Co/POT, Rh/POT, and Pt/POT. Fig. 8.DFT calculated the minimum potential energy (PE) profile for the water dissociation (H2O) kinetic mechanism to investigate the lowest activation barrier for best HER catalysts (Mn/POT, Tc/POT, Ir/POT, and Pt/POT).F. Abbas et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Computational  and Theoretical  Chemistry  1244  (2025)  115044 10
