are ␀1.62, ␀1.15, ␀0.35 and 0.20 eV, respectively. The energy of the TS is 0.23 eV for Ir/POT, among the metals listed, exhibits a substantially reduced energy barrier at the transition state, suggesting that it could potentially serve as the most efficient catalyst for this reaction, assuming that all other factors remain consistent. The optimized configurations of Mn/POT, Tc/POT, Ir/POT, and Pt/POT for studying the dissociation of water molecules and the breaking of OH␀and Hbonds are shown in Fig. S15of the ESI. The decreased energy at the transition state often corresponds to increased catalytic efficiency and accelerated reaction rates under similar circumstances. Effective catalysts decrease the amount of energy needed for water dissociation, hence enhancing the sustainability and economic feasibility of the process. Our calculated (Ir/POT) activation energy barrier is lower (0.23 eV) compared to recently reported work for the M1/PNb 12O40 cluster (0.92 eV) [35]. Our calculated HER catalytic activity (Mn/POT ␀0.06 eV) on the POT surface was found at the lowest ΔGH* in comparison to some recent works on POM-based materials [28]. 4.Conclusion To summarize our study, we develop a novel Keggin type Poly- oxotantalate (POT) cluster to examine the catalytic activity of single- doped transition metals to identify their catalytic activity towards HER, OER, and ORR. We first analyzed the best stable configuration site 4H using first principle thermodynamic calculations over the POT cluster. After finalizing the optimal active site, we, explore the binding energies, adsorption energies, intermediates energies, and free energy profiles of 2e␀and 4e␀catalytic pathways. The binding energies of the anchoring single atoms range from ␀2.21 eV to ␀10.82 eV. To gain deeper insight analysis of PDOS was performed relative to EF level focused on metal d and s orbitals with neighboring oxygen 2p orbitals results reveal a robust affinity of adatoms towards the O-2p orbital within the POT cluster. M/POT has been further studied in relation to the ORR pathways to enhance the understanding of catalytic activity. On the other hand, metal atoms with positive ΔGOH* values have substan - tially lower overpotential. However, the binding energy between adatoms and *OH is extremely high in the Cu/POT, Ag/POT, Au/POT, W/POT, Zn/POT, and Mo/POT systems, rendering them unsuitable for H2O production in the fourth step. Conversely, the non-noble metal Co/POT catalysts (0.35 V) and noble metal Pt/POT catalysts (0.34 V) exhibited the lowest ORR over- potential, and the fourth step, the rate-determining step (RDS) (*OH → H2O), is constructed in such a manner that the ORR is accessible. In the same first step (O2 → OOH*) both Co/POT and Pt/POT becomes a po- tential limiting step. Consequently, Mn/POT and Pt/POT hold tremen - dous promise as candidates for HER, OER, and ORR applications. Based on the coefficients of determination (R2) presented in this study (0.971), it is evident that a significant linear correlation exists between ΔGOOH* and ΔGOH*. The findings demonstrate a distinct connection between ΔGO* and ΔGOH* and the negatively charged maximum potential- determining steps are explored, as illustrated through a volcano curve. This curve reveals that certain M/POT catalysts, such as Pt/POT and Co/ POT, occupy the top and exhibit the lowest ηOER values (0.33 V and 0.43 V, respectively). The solvation model implementation reveals superior OER/ORR performance by Co/Rh/Pt catalysts. The water dissociation mechanism (H2O → HOH␀) through IS, TS, and FS for the Ir/POT cluster has the lowest activation barrier, calculated at 0.23 eV. The low overpotential implies that the Pt/POT cluster works as a trifunctional single-atom electrocatalyst for HER, OER, and ORR. The present work not only improves the utilization of POT clusters but also serves as a solid foundation for future experimentalists to develop multifunctional electrocatalysts. CRediT authorship contri