Thermodynamic stability, catalytic activity, kinetics and electronic properties of Polyoxometalate (TM@V-POM) for efficient electrochemical hydrogen evolution reactions: A DFT understanding Faheem Abbasa , Yongge Weia,b,* aDepartment of Chemistry, Key Lab of Organic Optoelectronics and Molecular Engineering of Ministry of Education, Tsinghua University, Beijing, 100084, PR China bState Key Laboratory of Natural and Biomimetic Drugs, Peking University, Beijing, 100191, PR China ARTICLE INFO Handling Editor: Dr A Iranzo Keywords: Single atom catalysis (SACs) Lindquist Polyoxometalates Density functional theory (DFT) Hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) Mechanistic studyABSTRACT Density functional theory (DFT) was employed to determine the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) efficiency, considering the influence of single-doped transition metals over the surface. We present the vanadium- substituted Pentatungstate, a Lindquist-type Polyoxometalates, namely the [VW 5O19]3- anion cluster func- tioning as a surface. The ideal active site is determined using benchmark research, revealing that the 3H site is the most advantageous for SACs. The structural stability of newly designed catalysts is investigated, giving cohesive and binding energies in the ranges of ␀8.15 to ␀9.05 eV and ␀4.75 to ␀23.99 eV, respectively. The lowest Bader charge value depicted by Au@V-POM is 0.99 ׀e׀ .Our research well-aligns with the computational hydrogen electrode (CHE) is the catalyst of choice for systems with overpotential values below (ΔGH* ≼0.30 eV). The V@V-POM catalyst demonstrated superior HER performance, surpassing that of the renowned Pt (111) HER catalyst in both the Volmer and Volmer-Tafel mechanisms. AIMD simulations were carried out to evaluate the thermodynamic reliability of the optimal HER catalyst. These findings revealed the need to identify the optimal active site for conducting mechanistic studies to enhance polyoxometalate-based single-atom catalysis (POM- SACs) for the production of H2 gas. 1.Introduction Given the rapid decline of energy supplies, there is a growing global focus on sustainable energy solutions to meet the demands of future generations [1–3]. Although many recent efforts have been made to create the intermittent character of renewable energy sources like solar and wind, these greatly restrict their practical use [4]. The water elec- trolysis rate, responsible for producing hydrogen as a renewable energy carrier, is expected to increase from 4 % to 22 % by 2050 [5]. Hydrogen is crucial for improving the consequences of global warming and substituting fossil fuels. The HER plays an essential role in electro - catalysis for establishing the link between structure and catalytic ac- tivity [6,7]. In acidic media, the evidence of the idea for reactions involving just one electron transfer is usually far more efficient than in alkaline ones. These reactions are crucial to other reactions like nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) or carbon reduction reactions (CRR) [8,9]. The expenditures of Basic H2 are relatively inexpensive, considering the almost boundless water supply. If the HER is successfully carried out in an alkaline environment, the availability of hydrogen will be almost limitless [10]. Pt-based materials serve as the industrial standard for HER catalysts. On the other hand, the high cost of Pt substantially im- pedes the broad use in the domain of HER [11]. Ruthenium (Ru) has excellent potential as an alternative to Pt in the role of HER catalyst due to its cheaper cost and comparable length of hydrogen bonds, around 65 kcal/mol [12]. Recent studies have shown that catalysts based on Ru exhibit more excellent stability and activity compared to catalysts based on Pt for the process of HER, in both acidic and alkaline conditions [13]. More improvements in catalyst performance are required to satisfy the requirements of practical applications [14]. SACs is an innovative approach that removes the differentiation between homogeneous and heterogeneous catalyst domains [15–19]. The unique feature of SACs has fascinating possibilities for catalytic implications since their reactivity may significantly differ from that of traditional catalysts made with large metal surfaces [20]. Furthermore, *Corresponding author. Department of Chemistry, Key Lab of Organic Optoelectronics and Molecular Engineering of Ministry of Education, Tsinghua University, Beijing, 100084, PR China. E-mail addresses: yonggewei@tsinghua.edu.cn , ygwei@pku.edu.cn , yonggewei@mail.tsinghua.edu.cn (Y. Wei). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect International Journal of Hydrogen Energy u{�~zkw! s{yo|kro>! ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2so https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2025.03.164 Received 25 October 2024; Received in revised form 9 March 2025; Accepted 10 March 2025  International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  117 (2025)  1–11 Available  online  13 March  2025 0360-3199/©  2025  Hydrogen  Energy  Publications  LLC. Published  by Elsevier  Ltd. All rights  are reserved,  including  those  for text and data mining,  AI training,  and similar  technologies.
