the range of transition metal combinations with various supports results in a costly and unsatisfactory screening process to find appropriate single-atom catalysts. Metal-organic frameworks, nitrogen-doped gra- phene, metal oxides implanted with single transition-metal (TM) atoms, g-C3N4 [21], MoS 2 [22,23], graphene [24,25], and MXenes [26–29] are often used as supports for SACs. Appropriate support should provide many anchoring points to ensure the stability of SACs. Thus, catalysts containing a single atom require the identification of a proper substrate that inhibits the aggregation of metals [30]. Polyoxometalates (POMs) are a distinct class of nanoclusters con- sisting of anions made up of transition metals from the d-block transition metals [31–33]. They exhibit remarkable performance in electron transport activities, which are crucial for HER. By substituting hetero - atoms, one may precisely modify the metal oxide complexes stability, redox, electrical, and magnetic properties. Lindqvist polyanions, deno- ted as [M6O19]n␀, are a prominent class of POMs. Some metal ions, such as NbV, TaV, MoVI, and WVI, can build Lindqvist structures because they can form polyanions in the 4d and 5d electron orbitals by combining with oxygen atoms using various oxidation states. The structures of these anions are very symmetrical with the Oh symmetry group [34,35]. Eri et al. [VW 5O19]3- investigated the Lindqvist-type POM by increasing the electrophilicity of the peroxo ligand via synergistic effects associated with tungsten atoms [34]. Herein, we report the vanadium-substituted Pentatungstate, a Lindquist-type Polyoxometalates [VW 5O19]3- anion cluster, as surface to study the catalytic performance of twenty-four transition metals using a DFT approach (Fig. 1). The immobilized transition metal is considered as SAC and abbreviated as TMs@V-POM for simplicity. No study to date have been reported to improve HER activity by applying an array of TM series over the V-POM surface. The Gibbs free energy (ΔGH*) have been investigated of an adsorbed hydrogen atom. This free energy profile offers a thermodynamic understanding of each reaction step along the reaction coordinates. In this study, few newly designed catalysts demonstrated excellent activities, comparable and even better to well- known HER catalyst Pt (111) at (ΔGH* 0.09 eV). In Volmer step, V@V-POM (0.03 eV) demonstrating the excellent HER activity by adsorbing first H* over the V-POM surface, while in Volmer-Heyrovsky Zn@V-POM Ru@V-POM (␀0.01) and Ti@V-POM (0.05 eV) are proved to be best catalytic candidates for HER. In the third step during the mechanistic study, we found V@-V-POM (0.000 eV), Pt@V-POM (0.02 eV), Os@V-POM (␀0.06 eV) and Ti@V-POM (0.08 eV) perform - ing exceptional HER performance. These results suggest that using V- POM support modified with single TMs can effectively improve the catalytic activity. Fig. 1.(a–b) DFT optimized configuration of investigated V-POM surface, focusing on doping with transition metal (TM) at three hollow active site (3H) to delve further analysis. (c–d) Ab initio molecular dynamic simulations at 300K for V-POM surface and modified with TM to visualize the thermal vibrations up to 5000 ionic steps. (e–f) The total, local, and projected density of state plots of V-POM surface are modified with TM to anticipate the energy levels, at Fermi level (E-E F 0).F. Abbas and Y. Wei                                                                                                                                                                                                                           International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  117 (2025)  1–11 2
