2.Computational methodology 2.1. Cluster model (Gaussian 16) Various investigated parameters for SACs such as geometry optimi - zation, electronic energy, cohesive energy, binding energy, formation energy, vibrational zero-point corrections of energy, free energy of the adsorbed hydrogen atom over the V-POM surface, HOM-LUMO energies with and without hydrogen adsorption and energy gaps were computed using the density functional theory (DFT) as implemented in the Gaussian 16 simulation package [36]. No negative frequency was found during the optimization, confirming that all the explored SACs are present at actual local minima of potential energy surfaces (PES). The properties and molecular models of all investigated complexes, the re- sults were displayed using Gauss View 6.1.1 [37], VESTA [38], and Origin Pro-2022 [39]. A benchmark study was performed to optimize the vanadium-substituted Pentatungstate [VW 5O19]3- anion cluster with different DFT basis sets at ωB97XD exchange-correlation functional [40] to get reliable outcomes. We used different basis sets for optimization like LANL2DZ, LANL2MB, and SDD. ωB97XD is preferred in complexes analysis because of a balanced approach to modeling complex electronic structures based on transition metals, accurately describing a broad range of non-covalent interactions, and managing the critical dispersion forces frequently essential to molecular complexes stability and behavior [41]. The basis set LANL2DZ is associated with more negative total energy, corresponding to the most stable electronic state under investigation as given in Table S1of the ESI�. The surface total energy serves as essential towards deciding the catalytic activity. In contrast, a negative energy level indicates a more stable configuration. The three-hollow site was doped with a single transition metal (TM), which served as a bridge between the three oxygen atoms (O12, O14, and O16), bridging oxygen atom (W1, O7, and W2) and the terminal oxygen atom (W1, O18). The atomic structures of three possible active sites over the V-POM cluster can be found in Fig. S1of the ESI�. These results show that the modified TMs with a 3H site have a much higher negative en- ergy than the other two binding sites, as presented in Table S2of the ESI�. 2.2. Periodic model (VASP) Spin-polarized DFT simulations were performed using VASP 5.4.4 [42,43] to provide the following outcomes: spin density, partial density of states (PDOS), Bader charge analysis, vacuum level, work function, d-band center, oxygen evolution activity (OER) and Ab initio molecular dynamic simulations of the best catalyst. To reduce the impact of the interlayer, contact in periodic systems, a 20 Å vacuum gap was intro- duced in the z-direction. Projector-augmented wave (PAW) pseudo-potentials were used to improve computational efficiency by accurately modeling the relation between the valence and core electrons [44,45]. This inquiry applied the generalized gradient approximation, to include the exchange-correlation function proposed by Perdew, Burke, and Ernzerhof (PBE) [46]. The cutoff energy was set at a termination value of 400 eV using a plane-wave basis. Ions were allowed to undergo decompression until their maximal force decreased to a value lower than 0.02 eV/e. The samples used to examine the Brillion zone consisted of 1 x 1 x 1 Γ points [47]. In this study, AIMD simulations were carried out at Canonical (NVT) ensemble focusing on temperature dependent proper - ties at constant temperature, number of particles and volume. The temperature was controlled via a Nos˘e-Hoover thermostat, which is ideal for maintaining a consistent thermal environment throughout the simulations using SMASS 0 at Nos˘e-Hoover thermostat (MDALGO  2). The following formula was used to compute the energy gap (Egap) between the HOMO-LUMO of a catalyst, both before and after the hydrogen adsorption. EgapELUMO␀EHOMO (1) EHOMO represents the energy of the highest occupied molecular orbital and ELUMO represents the energy of the lowest unoccupied mo- lecular orbitals. To evaluate how strongly adsorbate (hydrogen atom) interacts with the support, the adsorption binding energy was calculated using the following formula: ΔEBinETMIV␀POM–EV␀POMETM (2) Here, ETM@V-POM, EV-POM, and ETM are the energies of the catalyst with TM, the catalyst energy, and the energy of pure TM, respectively. The cohesive energy was computed to understand the stability of newly designed complexes as follows: EcohES–nOEOnWEWnVEVnTMETM (3) Where, ES is the total electronic energy of the catalyst with doped TM, while nO nW nV nTM are the numbers and nOEO nWEW nVEV  nTMETM are the individual electronic energies of oxygen, tungstate, va- nadium, and doped transition metal atom, respectively. The cohesive energy per atom (En) is: EnEEn Where n is the catalyst ’s total atom number present. The adsorption energy of hydrogen atoms (ΔEH) was computed when an H atom is adsorbed over TMs@V-POM support as follows: ΔEHETMH IV␀POM–ETMIV␀POM½EH2 (4) ETMH@V-POM , ETM@V-POM , and ½EH2 are the electronic energies of doped TM with and without adsorbed hydrogen atoms and isolated hydrogen molecules, respectively. Equation (4)determines the UL to assess the catalytic performance for hydrogen adsorption of the sug- gested SACs (TM@V-POM) for the hydrogen evolution process. ΔGH*ΔEHΔEZPE␀TΔSH (5) Equation (5)denotes the ΔGH* as the Gibbs free energy while ΔEH of adsorbed hydrogen energy on the V-POM as ΔEH. The ΔEZPE defines the energy at zero point both before and after the adsorption of H. T denotes the absolute temperature at 298K, and ΔSH represents the difference in entropy between the gas phase and adsorbate. 3.Results and discussion 3.1. Thermodynamic modeling and structural evaluation of TM@V-POM From the structural optimization process, it was found that the TM is coordinated to a site above the center of the triangle formed by oxygen atoms (O12_O14_O17) preferably known as 3H-site, which is thermo - dynamically more stable, exhibiting the highest negative energy in comparison to other two positions, as reported in Table S2of the ESI�. The investigated bond lengths at the 3H-bridge and terminal-site are (1.89 Å ␀1.89 Å ␀1.95 Å), (2.98 Å ␀2.06 Å) and 1.95 Å, respectively. The optimized configurations of the 3d,4d, and 5d TM series are depicted in Figs. S2–S4of the ESI�. It was observed that the relatively bond lengths between the three oxygen atoms and the doped TM are uniform throughout the addition of dopants. This indicates that elec- trons are symmetrically distributed around the metal atom with no surface strain involved, which is present at the potentially most stable configuration, leading to equivalent bonding interactions. Only the oxygen present at the 17 position reveals a slight increment due to a different coordination environment, and metal oxidation state. Upon examination of the 3d transition series, it was noted that the bond length increment at oxygen 17 for copper (Cu), zinc (Zn), titanium (Ti), and scandium (Sc) was observed in the following descending order: F. Abbas and Y. Wei                                                                                                                                                                                                                           International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  117 (2025)  1–11 3
