Cu@V-POM (2.01 Å) FZn@V-POM (1.99 Å) FTi@V-POM (1.96 Å) F Sc@V-POM (1.95 Å). In the case of the 4d transition series, the bond lengths are Ag@V-POM (2.30 Å) FNb@V-POM (2.26 Å) FPd@V-POM (2.18 Å) FMo@V-POM (1.93 Å). For the 5d transition series, the bond lengths are as follows: Au@V-POM (2.69 Å) FPt@V-POM (2.21 Å) F Ta@V-POM (2.16 Å) FRe@V-POM (2.08 Å). The little changes in the bond lengths might be caused by the covalent radii or due to high atomic mass. Before going to optimize all catalysts incorporating TM at the hollow site, the bond lengths were O12_TM (1.89 Å), O14_TM (1.89 Å), and O17_TM (1.95 Å). All the investigated bond lengths, bond angle between three oxygen atoms, and dihedral angle between three oxygen atoms and TM are given in detail in Table S2of the ESI�. Generally, shorter bond lengths between atoms release more energy due to solid bond formation. However, this relation may not be more direct due to other factors like steric effects, strain, and distortion caused by the co- ordination environment, considering the bond angles between the three oxygen atoms and the doped TM before getting the optimal configura - tions (61.56•). The bond dihedral angle between the three oxygen atoms and doped transition metals is 62.22•. The Binding energies (ΔEBin) of optimized structures are used to observe the thermodynamic stability of designed catalysts. This helps to predict how strongly TM adsorbs over the catalyst surface. The values of (ΔEBin) are present in (eV). All newly designed catalysts are given in Table S3of the ESI�. They reveal that all energies are negative. This depicts that doping TM at three hollow sites seems straightforward and energetically favorable [48]. A more negative binding energy indicates a stronger bond between the adsorbate and catalyst surface, making the catalyst more stable. In our study, the W@V-POM catalyst exhibits the highest binding energy maybe due to more contribution of d-electrons, more electronegativity of tungsten metal and high oxidation state which leads to more stable covalent bond. The highest binding energies of TM are as follows in descending order: W@V-POM (␀23.99 eV) FTa@V-POM (␀16.58 eV) FNb@V-POM (␀15.28 eV) FTi@V-POM (␀14.92 eV) FSc@V-POM (␀14.43 eV) FRe@V-POM (␀11.14 eV). The binding energies of cata- lysts range from ␀5.21 eV to ␀23.99 eV as graphically presented in Fig. 2 (a). If the adsorbate binds too strongly, it may hinder the catalytic activity. This is crucial for catalysis because the perfect catalyst binds reactants just enough to speed up the reaction without preventing the release of the products. It is possible to poison a catalyst with an adsorbate that has a very negative binding energy if it binds very firmly to it, blocking other reactants from binding and covering the catalyst ’s active sites. A less negative (positive) binding energy indicates a weaker binding and vice versa. The Sabatier principle, often used to explain this phenomenon, as- serts that an optimal level of adsorption strength enhances catalytic activity [49]. The interaction should not be too strong or too weak. The cohesive energies per atom are also computed to investigate the material strength, durability and catalytic activity of newly designed catalysts. The cohesive energies directly influence the catalytic activity. Energies must be in equilibrium to support the adsorption-desorption processes of adsorbed hydrogen atoms over the POM surface to achieve higher HER activity. The highest cohesive energies of TM are as follows in decreasing order: W@POM (␀9.05 eV) FTa@V-POM (␀8.77 eV) FNb@V-POM (␀8.72 eV) FTi@V-POM (␀8.70 eV) FSc@V-POM (␀8.68 eV) FCr@V- POM (␀8.66 eV). The cohesive energies of all newly investigated cata- lysts are reported in Table S4of the ESI�and Fig. 2 (b). Here, tungsten (W) metal shows the highest cohesive energy, which proves that it Fig. 2.(a) The Binding energies of all newly designed catalysts (TM@V-POM) (b) Cohesive energies (c) Formation energies (d) Hydrogen adsorption energy. All the energies are given in eV.F. Abbas and Y. Wei                                                                                                                                                                                                                           International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  117 (2025)  1–11 4
