ier molecular orbitals are directly related to the catalytic activity and the feasibility of reduction processes. A lower LUMO level is more energetically favorable for accepting electrons to reduce protons and convert them to hydrogen gas. A suitable arrangement of hydrogen energy levels to frontier orbitals can lead to ideal adsorption strength, which is important according to the Sabatier principle to enhance the efficiency and hydrogen production processes. The computed energy levels of HOMO and LUMO before and after hydrogen adsorption on TM for newly developed catalysts are presented in Table S5of the ESI�. Spin density maps were used to visualize electron-rich and deficient regions, as shown in Fig. S5of the ESI�. All the energy values of HOMO, LUMO, and energy gap (Eg) are given in eV. The energy gap of catalyst V-POM is 6.38 eV. Based on the electron density areas of HOMO and LUMO energy levels, the iso-surfaces of all recently studied catalysts are shown in Figs. S6–S7of the ESI�. 3.2.2. d-band center (εd) When discussing the electronic properties of doped TM, we mainly investigate the average contribution of d-orbitals of the atoms over the catalyst surface. The catalytic activity of adsorbing hydrogen atoms is closely related to its ability to facilitate the adsorption and desorption processes over the surface [50–52]. Obtaining lower overpotential, which is more demanding in practical applications, can be tuned using the d-band center approach. When the Fermi level is close to the d-band center, the d-orbitals of the transition metal can easily donate and accept electrons, facilitating the chemical reaction between the surface and TM. The d-band center values are comparably higher than the s and p-band centers, making it a strong candidate for catalytic activity. Following the Sabatier principle [49], values should be balanced, not too high or low. If the d-band value is too high, the catalyst may not perform optimally. In this study, d-band values of Zn@V-POM (␀3.79 eV) and Ag@V-POM (2.67 eV) are considered as a significant d-orbital contribution, sug- gesting that their d-band is closest to the Fermi level, but this does not mean they are the best HER catalysts. For HER, an optimal balance is necessary between the d-band value of catalysts are presented in Table S6of the ESI�. 3.2.3. Work function (ɸ) The minimum energy needed to transfer a single electron from a solid substance to a vacuum beyond the solid surface is determined by the work function, which is the difference in energy between the Fermi level and the vacuum level. It is important in catalysis to know how easily electrons are transferred from the catalyst surface to adsorbed hydrogen atoms. A lower work function requires less energy to move the electron, facilitating the release of electrons to hydrogen ions (protons) in the electrolyte to produce hydrogen gas. In the context of the POM surface, we observe that Tc@V-POM- POM (6.98 eV) and Ta@V-POM (6.95 eV) are considered as lowest work functions associated with more electron excitation with minimum energy. The values of vacuum level (eV), work function (eV) and Fermi energy (eV) are given in Table S6of the ESI�. After careful examination, it appears that Tc@V- POM and Ta@V-POM possess the lowest vacuum level and Fermi en- ergy, potentially making them more efficient for fast electron transfer with minimum energy utilization. 3.2.4. Bader charge (q) The Bader charge analysis helps identify the electron density distri - bution of single doped TM and their effective atomic charge over the surface. A positive value of q indicates a single atom lost electron density or distributes with the neighboring atoms, which can be considered an atom bearing a partial positive charge. Conversely, a negative q in- dicates that the atom has gained electron density from nearby atoms, corresponding to a negative partial charge. A catalyst with the correct q value might be more effective in aiding the reaction process to get a balanced state between the adsorption and desorption processes due to the well-distribution of the electron density to other atoms present on the surface. The calculated q of the 3d to 5d TM series (shown in Fig. 4d) reveal well-distributed electron density upon doping on the POM surface as follows: Cu@V-POM (0.04 e), Co@V-POMNi@V-POM (1.12 e), Sc@V-POM (1.14 e) and Fe@V-POM (1.33 e). Among the 4d transition series, Ag@V-POM (1.00 e and Pd@V-POM (1.03 e) exhibit the lowest positive q value. In the 5d transition series, Au@V-POM (0.99 e) exhibits the lowest q confirming that more electron density is transferred to the surface, possibly due to the intrinsic nature of the catalyst surface. The differences in q do not follow any direct relation in increment or reduction of charge across the series but vary from metal to metal. This entirely depends on the specific nature of metal-surface interfaces, electronic structure, and the overall coordination environment provided by the host surface. 3.2.5. Density of states (DOS) The catalytic performance of catalysts relies on how the 2p orbitals of oxygen of surface interact with the d-orbitals of TM. Throughout the study, the Fermi energy level remains at zero (E-EF 0). The PDOS plots are presented in Figs. S8–S11of the ESI�. We also plot the LDOS, TDOS, and PDOS for the best catalysts to compare their electronic distribution. In Figs. S12–S14of the ESI�, we visualized the total, local, and projected density of state plots for the V-POM surface. The TDOS tells about the surface ’s overall density of electronic states, while LDOS tells about where electronic changes are present using O-2p orbitals, V-3d, and W- 6s. The major contribution is observed between O-2p and V-3d orbitals due to high energy levels. The d-band center is also calculated to investigate the average contribution of d-orbitals for the V-POM d-band center is present at (εd 1.11 eV). The εd is more near to EF the d- electrons can participate more readily in bonding. In catalysis, the strength of adsorbate is also estimated by its εd. The V@V-POM exhibits the lowest value of the d-band center (εd 0.382 eV), signifying the role of V doping over the catalyst surface. The electronic states around the Fermi level may enhance the adsorption rate of hydrogen atoms onto the surface of the catalyst for HER.F. Abbas and Y. Wei                                                                                                                                                                                                                           International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  117 (2025)  1–11 5
