3.3. HER activity over V-POM (Volmer reaction) The HER activity was achieved by adsorbing single hydrogen atom (H*) over the V-POM surface referred as volmer step. The binding ca- pacity of TM estimated by (ΔGH*), which mainly relies on the energies of adsorbed hydrogen atoms (ΔEH*). The estimated (ΔEH*) are displayed in Fig. 2 (d)for the Volmer step. Because it is directly related to the ther- modynamic feasibility and efficiency of a reaction, determining the (ΔGH*) of a catalyst is an essential first step towards finding its catalytic activity. The (ΔGH*) of all investigated catalysts was determined using Eq. (5). The sign of (ΔGH*) represents whether the reaction is sponta - neous (ΔGH* D0) or non-spontaneous (ΔGH* F0) [53]. For catalysts to be catalytically active, they must have a sufficient energy gap that permits ease of electron removal. Relatively, strong adsorption clogs the active sites on the catalyst surfaces, leading to a significant decrease in catalytic activity over time. This can be explained based on the partial density of states analysis, the Bader charge of each atom involved as well as the position of the d-band center near the Fermi level. The adsorption energies (ΔEH*) are also significant in deciding about the (ΔGH*) value after hydrogen adsorption on the surface of the catalyst [54]. Regarding our newly developed catalysts, V@V-POM displays the lowest (ΔGH*) value at (0.03 eV) and the highest value for W@V-POM (␀3.04 eV). It was observed that the catalyst with an adsorption value in the range (of ␀0.21 to 0.1 eV) depicts a moderate (ΔGH*) value from (0.03 –0.34), which seems to be more beneficial for the catalytic process keeping the balance between adsorption-desorption processes with high catalytic efficiency. The values of zero-point energy, total energy, adsorption strength of adsorbed hydrogen atom on the surface of the catalyst, (ΔGH*) of catalysts, and Bader charge (e) of TM, are given in Table S7of the ESI�. The observed bond distances (Å) between doped TM and adsorbed hydrogen atom over the catalyst surface upon relaxation at the same level of theory are presented in Table S4of the ESI�. The highest increment in bond lengths is identified by Sc-H@V-POM catalyst (2.99 Å), while the lowest increment was for Fe-H@V-POM (1.47 Å). It is observed that all the good catalysts show metal-hydrogen bond lengths ranging from 1.52 to 1.58 Å [55]. The computed hydrogen electrode (CHE) [56] indicates whether a catalyst will behave as an efficient electrocatalyst if it shows a (ΔGH*) value less than D0.30 eV [57]. Then, it should be compared with the well-known optimal HER Pt catalyst having( ΔGH*) D0.15 eV [58,59]. Pt is considered the best HER catalyst because have the ability to adsorbed hydrogen atoms neither too weak nor too strong, which simultaneously facilitates the adsorption and desorption processes. In 3d series of TM, V@V-POM exhibited the lowest (ΔGH*) value (␀0.03 eV), with adsorption strength (␀0.21 eV) and Bader charge value (1.84 e) of doped individual V-metal over the V-POM surface. These results are also supported by the values of the s-band center (␀0.243 eV), p-band center (0.409 eV), and d-band center (0.380 eV) of the investigated V@V-POM catalyst. The catalytic efficiency is also based on the energy gap. The lower the energy gap between HOMO-LUMO, the higher the catalytic activity of the catalyst. The V@V-POM catalyst exhibits the lowest energy gap for the 3d transition metals (6.77 eV). A lower energy gap facilitates the ease of electron transfer, a crucial step to accepting protons to form hydrogen gas, thus enhancing the rate of reaction. The values of energy gap are given in Table S5of the ESI. The Mo@V-POM catalyst demonstrated the lowest (ΔGH*) value (0.14eV) in the 4d series, with adsorption strength of (␀0.10 eV) and a Bader charge value of (1.81 e). In 5d series, Au@V-POM exhibited the lowest (ΔGH*) value (0.12 eV), adsorption strength (␀0.12 eV), and lowest Bader charge value (0.99 e) of doped individual gold metal over the V-POM surface. These results are also supported by the values of the s-band center (0.208 eV), p-band center (0.147 eV), and d-band center (␀2.548 eV) of the investigated Au@V-POM catalyst. The energy gap of Au@V-POM before hydrogen adsorption (5.44 eV) and after hydrogen adsorption demonstrated the lowest energy gap (6.15 eV). The newly designed catalysts V@V-POM have shown superb catalytic activity in terms of hydrogen gas produc - tion utilizing the volmer-step, comparable to the well-known Pt elec- trocatalyst. Fig. 3(a–c) visually illustrates the HER (Volmer-step) for the three transition metal series, whereas the volcano plot (d) serves to find the best HER catalyst. 3.4. Mechanistic study for HER The initial adsorption of hydrogen via volmer-step (adsorption: H*  e␀Surface → Hads) is not sufficient for the formation of molecular hydrogen (H2), which is ultimate goal of HER. The HER is a two-step reduction. The main step (Volmer) is either followed by Volmer-Tafel (recombination: 2Hads → H2) or Volmer-Heyrovsky (desorption: Hads He␀→ H2). The (ΔEH*) for Volmer, Heyrovsky and Tafel step are computed by following the Eqs. (6)–(8), respectively. ΔE (1) EMH@V-POM – (EM@V-POM ½ EH2)                                      (6) ΔE (2) EMH@V-POM – (EMH@V-POM ½ EH2)                                    (7) ΔE (1) EHMH@V-POM – (EM@V-POM ½ EH2)                                    (8) Similarly, the (ΔGH*) for Volmer, Heyrovsky and Tafel step are computed using Eqs. 9–11, respectively. ΔG (1) ΔE (1) ΔEZPE - TΔSH                                                      (9) ΔG (2) ΔE (2) ΔEZPE - TΔSH                                                    (10) ΔG (3) ΔE (3) ΔEZPE - TΔSH                                                    (11) The values of (ΔEH*) and (ΔGH*) of TM@V-POM for Volmer, Heyr- ovsky and Tafel step presented in Table .1. According to CHE model, a catalyst considered to be prospective for efficient HER activity if it has Gibbs free energy less than 0.30 eV [60]. The graphical representation of the mechanistic inquiry can be seen in Fig. 4 (c). For the volmer step, the ΔGH*(1) value of V@V-POM catalyst is 0.03 eV, which is more close to attain the optimal configuration value (ΔGH* 0 eV). The catalysts Mo@V-POM, Au@V-POM, Os@V-POM, and Pt@V-POM have been found consistent with the CHE model after the adsorption of a single hydrogen atom on the surface. In the Volmer- Heyrovsky process, it has been noted that fifteen catalysts have a ΔGH* of less than D0.30 eV. It certainly implies that the Volmer- Heyrovsky process is applicable to these catalysts. The lowest ΔGH* value is depicted by Zn@V-POM Ru@V-POM (0.01 eV). During the Volmer-Tafel phase, nine catalysts are included within the parameters of the CHE model to identify viable catalysts for HER. The optimal HER catalytic activity is achieved by V@V-POM catalyst present at (ΔGH* 0 eV). This configuration is achieved by balancing the adsorption and desorption processes on the catalyst ’s surface. The catalytic activity can be assessed through the strength of (ΔEH*). It has been found that the catalyst V@V-POM exhibits (ΔEH*) values of ␀0.21 eV for the Volmer mechanism and ␀0.24 eV for the Volmer-Tafel mechanism. In this study, our catalyst V@V-POM demonstrated exceptional HER performance as evaluated by a mechanistic inquiry even better than the well-known Pt (111) catalyst (ΔGH* 0.09 eV). Table 1displays a comparison of (ΔEH*) as well as (ΔGH*) values for the Volmer, Volmer-Heyrovsky, and Volmer-Tafel processes for the HER over V-POM surface. Fig. 4(a and b) shows the volcano curves for the Volmer-Tafel and Volmer-Heyrovsky steps. The well-aligned catalysts with the CHE model are emphasized in bold. 3.5. Oxygen Evolution Reaction catalytic activity (OER) The primary objective of this study is to perform an extensive eval- uation of the engineered catalysts for the HER; yet, there is also an ex- amination for OER. We are only investigating for the best HER catalysts towards exploring a multifunctional electrocatalyst. The complete OER reaction comprises four intensive adsorption-desorption phases within F. Abbas and Y. Wei                                                                                                                                                                                                                           International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  117 (2025)  1–11 6
