the 4e␀catalytic pathway. The complete elementary OER pathway can be described by following equations. H2O(I) * → OH* e␀H(12) OH* → O* e␀H(13) H2O(I) O* → OOH* e␀H(14) OOH* → O2* e␀H(15) O2* → O2 (g) *                                                                         (16) Where, (*) denotes the active site over the V-POM surface for adsorption of intermediates (OH*, O*, OOH*). I and g represent the gas liquid and gas phase respectively. The Gibbs free energy for each step, can be calculated by using equation ΔG ΔE ΔZPE – TΔS ΔGU ΔGPH                                       (17) Where, ΔE, ΔZPE and ΔS displays the energy difference between adsorption energy, zero-point energy and entropy respectively. Under standard conditions, T is the absolute temperature (298.15K, P 1 bar, PH 0, U 0), Gu GPH 0. The CHE model is also considered, free energy for one electron-proton transfer can be estimated as 1/2 GH2 at applied potential U 0, vs SHE at PH 0. Free energy value (4.92 V) was taken into consideration to estimate the overall water splitting process. For each next OER step free energy 1.23 V is added theoretically to determine the overpotential value (ղOER). DFT calculated adsorption energies of all intermediates and ΔZPE and ΔS are depicted in Table S8 of the ESI. The rate determining step (RDS) is identifies by maximum ΔG value. The computed energy barriers for the OER intermediates to estimate the onset-potential are depicted in Table S9of the ESI. The OER catalytic pathway and RDS of all investigated OER catalyst depicted in Fig. 5(a and b). With the minimum RDS (ΔG4 2.27 eV) demon - strated by Mn@V-POM, it is an appropriate choice for determining the OER activity. Among all the catalysts that were examined, Mn@V-POM showed the lowest overpotential (ղOER 1.04 eV). The largest RDS is demonstrated by Ru@V-POM (ΔG4 4.78 eV), responsible for lowest OER activity present at (ղOER 3.55 eV). By taking into account the overpotential of OER and the straightforward Volmer reaction of HER, it is reasonable to estimate that the V@V-POM catalyst demonstrates bi- functional activity for both reactions. The DFT calculated OER activity for V@V-POM is (ղOER 2.11eV) which is lowest from the Mo@V-POM and Au@V-POM catalysts. Fig. 5 (c)shows the entire Gibbs free energy profile, with the RDS highlighted as red. Our research shows that V@V- POM is the most potential SAC for lowering the water oxidation overpotential. Fig. 3.(a–c) Free energy profile showing (ΔGH*) for adsorption of H* over the surface anchored by TM series. (d) The volcano plot for HER for the formation of TMH@V-POM (kinetic model). The top peak of the plot highlights the optimal HER catalytic activity.F. Abbas and Y. Wei                                                                                                                                                                                                                           International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  117 (2025)  1–11 7
