3.6. Ab-initio molecular dynamic simulation (AIMD) The AIMD simulations studied the stability of the newly designed catalysts exclusively for the identified optimal HER catalysts at certain temperatures to ascertain their viability for application. We observed a consistent trend with minimal changes for V@V-POM and Pt@V-POM regarding both temperature and total energy. Fig. 6(a–d) shows the variance in temperature and total energy at 300K for ionic steps up to 5000. The graphically representation illustrates that when V and Pt single metal atoms are anchored over the V-POM surface, the peak smoothness remains stable without drastic changes. The energy curves for Zn@V-POM exhibit disruptions in peak smoothness due to the ab- sorption of heat, resulting in energy alterations. The variations increase to a certain degree before normalizing, as expected, due to reaching a stable lower energy state. We observed the same for the Ru@V-POM catalyst. Nonetheless, the graphs indicate that the overall energy, despite fluctuations, remains in negative total energy, confirming its stability whenever exposed to heating. Thus, in our developed catalyst V@V-POM, the atomic structure exhibits little peak disturbance, attributable to its stable electronic configuration and thermal stability, which are essential for acquiring the practical applications of these studied catalysts. 4.Conclusion In conclusion, we have demonstrated the way single-doped transi - tion metals modify the surface coordination environment, boosting the HER catalytic activity. The catalytic activity of transition metals over the surface (TM@V-POM) has been supported by several factors such as binding energies, cohesive energies, hydrogen adsorption energy, Fig. 4.(a–b) The volcano plots for the Volmer-Tafel and Volmer-Heyrovsky over the V-POM surface. (c) The mechanistic pathway towards performing the Volmer, Volmer-Heyrovsky and Volmer-Tafel processes over the V-POM surface. (d) Following the trend between bader 