vacuum level, work function, s, p, d-band centers, zero-point energies, Bader charge and Gibbs free energy profile. The structural stability of newly designed catalysts has been investigated using cohesive and interaction energies in the following ranges: ␀8.15 eV to ␀9.05 eV and ␀4.75 eV to ␀23.99 eV, respectively. The lowest Bader charge value depicted by Au@V-POM was 0.99 e., facilitating the charge transfer. A maximum contribution of O-2p orbitals with Au-5d orbitals was ob- tained, making the Fermi level closer to the d-band center. In our study, the lowest Gibbs free energy was obtained for V@V-POM catalysts, both in vomer and volmer-Tafel step identified via mechanistic study. Among the three-transition metal series, we found several HER catalysts following the CHE model, confirming the balanced adsorption and desorption rates during catalytic reactions. While performing the OER on best HER catalyst, we observed that Mn@V-POM exhibiting the lowest RDS (2.27 eV) among all. The results are well supported by AIMD simulations that illustrate the thermodynamic stability of newly devel - oped catalysts at 300K. Our study indicates the importance of the active site identification, engineering the coordinating environment of a single TM over the V-POM surface via a mechanistic pathway, thereby facili - tating the development of high-performance POM-SACs. CRediT authorship contribution statement Faheem Abbas: Writing – original draft, Methodology, Investiga - tion, Data curation, Conceptualization. Yongge Wei: Writing – review & editing, Validation, Supervision, Software, Resources, Project Fig. 5.(a) Four proton coupled electron transfer (PCET) catalytic pathway for OER (b) Theoretically estimated RDS (mentioned by red arrow) for the best HER catalyst (c) Free energy profile of all six best HER catalyst towards estimating OER activity. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)F. 