High-efﬁcient and low-cost catalyst for hydrogen evolution reaction: Nickel phosphide nano-spheres S.Abdalla,1,a),b)Fahd Al-Marzouki,1,b)and A. Obaid2,b),c) 1Department of Physics, Faculty of Science, King Abdulaziz University Jeddah, P.O. Box 80203, Jeddah 21589, Saudi Arabia 2Department of Physical Chemistry, Faculty of Science, King Abdulaziz University Jeddah, P.O. Box 80203, Jeddah 21589, Saudi Arabia (Received 23 July 2016; accepted 3 February 2017; published online 7 April 2017) Catalysts have been a bottleneck in hydrogen evolution reaction (HER)—a half reaction of water splitting. Hitherto, the most efﬁcient HER catalyst in terms of catalytic capacity remains to be platinum group metals, especially platinum; however, rare metals’ exorbitant cost and scarcity inhibit their further applicationin industry, which therefore call for catalysts prepared from earth-abundant ele- ments. Herein, we introduce nickel phosphide nano-spheres (NPNs) bearing excel- lent HER activity. Prepared via a facile, inexpensive, and eco-friendly preparationprocedure, the as-synthesized NPNs have perfect over-potential to reach /C0100 mA/ cm 2(g100), with g100reaching 251 mV in 0.5 M H 2SO4. A series of durability tests, including cyclic voltammetry and chronoamperometry, also indicate NPNs’ favor-able stability in both acidic and alkaline conditions. After taking into account a string of factors, including NPNs’ mass density, handy preparation process, supe- rior catalytic activity, and favorable durability, the NPNs bear probable potentialityof taking traditional noble metals’ place as the HER catalyst and are worth studying further. Published by AIP Publishing. [http://dx.doi.org/10.1063/1.4979875 ] I. INTRODUCTION Water-splitting through electrolysis is considered a very promising approach for hydrogen generation under the backdrop of searching for the alternative of fossil fuels.1–5Redundant elec- tricity, regardless of its origins, e.g., wind power, solar power, hydro-energy, or traditional fossilfuels, can be stored as chemical energy, and hydrogen is considered as a perfect storage form. 6 Noble metal catalysts (often platinum) have so far been the most effective ones to be applied inwater splitting 7and other applications,8but their inherent drawbacks (rare and expensive) drive scientists to ﬁnd a cost-effective and abundant hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) catalyst whichat the same time is neck-and-neck with noble metals in terms of HER activity. 9–11 Micro- and nano-sized nickel phosphides have recently come into researchers’ notice, with Ni2P, Ni1 2P5, NiP 2,N i 5P4, NiP 2, and so forth being successfully prepared, their HER properties being proved excellent, and their catalytic mechanisms of efﬁciently activating HER being stud-ied. 2,6,12–14Their performances in a highly acidic environment are also considerably stable. Despite these breakthroughs, the frequently reported methods to prepare Ni-P nanoparticlesmostly require high temperature or the involvement of surfactants and organic nickel or phos-phorus sources. 13,15,16The introduction of nano-crystallization of Ni-P alloys and a speciﬁc Ni- P phase often requires the abovementioned reaction conditions through a thermal decompositionof organic precursors, and hydrothermal, solvo-thermal, or high-temperature solid phase reduc-tion methods, 15,17–19which complicate the preparation of Ni-P nanomaterials and hinder Ni-P alloys’ further application in large-scale commercial production processes. For example, one of a)Author to whom correspondence should be addressed. Elec tronic mail: smabdullah@kau.edu.sa. Tel.: 00966 5823438322. b)S. Abdalla, F. Al-Marzouki, and A. Obaid contributed equally to this work. c)saobaid@kau.edu.sa 1941-7012/2017/9(2)/023104/10/$30.00 Published by AIP Publishing. 9, 023104-1JOURNAL OF RENEWABLE AND SUSTAINABLE ENERGY 9, 023104 (2017)
============================================================
[Page 3/11]
============================================================
the biggest advantages of the solvo-thermal method is a certain Ni-P phase that can be easily achieved in a particular reaction condition, but actually, the reported morphology is not farfrom one another as expected. Although by far, only the HER mechanisms of Ni 2P and Ni 5P4 have been studied comparatively systematically,2,6,12and HER mechanisms of other Ni-P phases can be analogous to them;13,20therefore, a pure Ni-P phase does not deﬁnitely signify a greater HER property. In some cases, a mixture of different Ni-P may even have better HERcatalytic activity. 17In view of this, a balance should be struck between the preparation cost and the HER catalytic activity of Ni-P nanoparticles. It has long been proven that Ni has autocata-lytic reduction nature, through which Ni-P nanoparticles can be prepared in a comparativelymild condition. 21The newly reduced amorphous Ni-P alloys with NaH 2PO2as a reducing agent are able to transfer to other Ni-P phases through the heat treatment procedure. These properties were rarely reported in the preparation of Ni-P nanomaterials that are applied in the HER appli-cation. In this paper, we therefore introduce a facile preparation process of nickel phosphidenano-spheres (NPNs) and report their great HER properties. Inspired by the traditional chemicalnickel-plating process and nickel’s autocatalytic reduction property, the nano-sized nickel phos-phide was prepared through a facile, surfactant-free, and mild autocatalytic reduction reaction. 22 The as-synthesized NPNs’ catalytic properties after different heat treatment processes were studied. The as-prepared amorphous nickel phosphide na noparticles were heat treated at temperatures below 350/C14C for different durations. These nanopartic les, being sphere-like, have shown distin- guished HER properties in their amorphous state, and furthermore, after the annealing processes,the HER properties of these nanoparticles, mainly comprised of a combination of Ni 3Pa n dN i 5P2, were enhanced substantially. A ll the samples show outstanding catalytic properties in both H 2SO4 and NaOH in which they also have considerable stability. The minimum over-potential required for/C0100 mA/cm2on the cathode is 251 mV in H 2SO4and is achieved in the 8-h heat-treated sam- ple. The increased crystallinity and nickel phosp hide’s nature are shown after comparing the HER properties of hydrogen phosphide/nickel (HPN) before and after the heat treatment procedure. Thecrystallized phases of Ni xPyshould be considered better than the amorphous one in terms of HER activity. The comparatively low-cost raw materials of the NPNs, produced from earth-abundantelements, if studied searchingly, may p ave the way for their future application. II. EXPERIMENTAL A. Materials Nickel sulfate (NiSO 4), sodium hydroxide (NaOH), sodium hypophosphite (NaH 2PO2), pal- ladium chloride (PdCl 2), and acetic acid (CH 3COOH), all in analytical pure and without puriﬁ- cation, were purchased from National Chemical Reagent, Ltd., Shanghai, China. A Milliporewater system was applied to prepare deionized water throughout the experimental procedure. B. Preparation and annealing processes of NPNs The ﬁrst step is to prepare the a-Ni(OH) 2nano-wire precursor through a hydrothermal syn- thesis method.23The key point of forming a wire-like morphology is to mix two-fold excess NiSO 4into NaOH and heat-treat them at 120/C14C for 24 h. Drawing on these as-synthesized a- Ni(OH) 2precursors and capitalizing on NaH 2PO2as a reducing reagent, CH 3COOH as a pH reg- ulator, and PdCl 2as a nucleate agent, the NPNs were prepared through an auto-catalytic reduc- tion.22The adequately rapid reaction rate, comparatively high reaction temperature (90/C14C), as well as the addition of PdCl 2prior to NaH 2PO2(Ref. 24), would ensure a solid sphere morphol- ogy, rather than some novel morphologies like hollow spheres, nanotubes, or yolk-in-shell nano-spheres. The as-prepared NPNs were annealed at 350 /C14C and amid 5% H 2/Ar for different time periods: 0 h (non-heat treated), 4 h, and 8 h, respectively. A Pyris 1 ThermoGravimetric AnalyserTGA, working from room temperature (300 K) to 550 /C14C, was applied to get differential scanning calorimetry and thermo-gravimetric analysis (DSC/TGA) plots. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patternsof NPNs annealed for different time periods were obtained using a D/max-IIIA X-ray diffractom-eter, with 40 kV as the accelerating voltage and Cu-K a(k¼1.54056 A ˚) as the radiation source.023104-2 Abdalla, Al-Marzouki, and Obaid J. Renewable Sustainable Energy 9, 023104 (2017)
