were seen at 623 K (Ref. 25) Peeters et al.26have reported that the crystallite size of Ni 3P varies with temperature and annealing time. In particular, Ni 3P starts to crystallize at a tempera- ture above 200/C14C. In addition, Cui et al.27have shown that when annealing is conducted at a temperature higher than 400/C14C, agglomeration of the particles of this inter-metallic compound will occur. So, we have chosen the annealing temperature at 350/C14C. As can be seen in Fig. 2, the XRD pattern of the non-heat-treated sample shows a typical bread-like amorphous peak at around 45/C14, similar to Ni-P alloys reduced by NaH 2PO2at a com- paratively low temperature.19,28,29After 4 h, some degrees of crystallization behavior can be seen, with some peaks corresponding to Ni 3P. This trend has further progressed after an 8-h annealing, and apart from those already existing Ni 3P peaks (PDF 34-0501), some newly emerged peaks are related to the Ni 5P2phase (PDF 17-0225). Some undetermined metastable or amorphous peaks still exist after 8 h of heat treatment. Besides the yet unﬁnished heat treat-ment process which requires more energy and a longer time, the ICP data may shed light on this result. As can be seen in Table I, for the non-heat treated sample, nickel accounts for 74.62 wt. % and phosphorus represents 14.91 wt. %. The n(Ni): n(P) annealed samples showsimilar outcomes (2.65 and 2.77). These ratios would make it hard to become a pure nickel phosphide phase in a short time. In Fig. 3(a), the as-synthesized products are proved to be rounded solid nanoparticles. These NPNs are uniformly distributed, and although some of them overlap with one another, considering the surfactant-free preparation process, the disparity of the NPNs is still relatively favorable. Fig. 3(b) shows that the diameter of the NPNs is around 50 to 80 nm. The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) pattern in Fig. 3(c) shows that non-annealed NPNs are amor- phous, just like those of Ni-P electro-less alloys reduced by NaH 2PO2.25,29,30One can see from Figs. 3(d) and 3(e) the annealing effect after heat treatment processes (“8”-h-heat-treated NPNs), and accordingly, no signiﬁcant changes can be observed. The morphology of NPNs annealed for 4 hand 8 h remains ring-shaped, with still favorable disparity and similar size dis- tribution compared to the phase, which is also conﬁrmed by the fast Fourier transform (FFT)image in the inset. 31Scanning transmission electron microscopy images for different degrees of FIG. 2. XRD patterns of NPNs annealed by 4 h, 8 h, and the non-annealed sample. TABLE I. ICP results of the outcomes of heat treatment at different temperatures. Annealing time (h) Non-annealed 4 8 Ni (wt. %) 74.62 77.50 76.53 P (wt. %) 14.91 15.42 14.57 n(Ni):n(P) 2.71 2.65 2.77023104-4 Abdalla, Al-Marzouki, and Obaid J. Renewable Sustainable Energy 9, 023104 (2017)
