To test the durability of NPNs under HER conditions, a series of measurements were car- ried out in both acidic and basic conditions. The 8-h annealing sample, bearing the highestHER property, would ﬁrst undergo 500 sweeps of cyclic voltammetry (CV) after which the time-dependent test of 40 000 s would be carried out. The given over-potentials were 135 mV in H 2SO4and 350 mV in NaOH. Following the aforementioned durability test, the LSV would be carried out to re-test the HER activity of samples; (b) measured in 1 M NaOH. Following the aforementioned durability tests, the LSV would be carried out to re-test the HER activity of samples. In Fig. 6(a), after 500 sweeps of CV in H 2SO4, one can see that the HER activity did not undergo any signiﬁcant declines. This is because the original polarization curve is almost the same for the original 8-h-heat-treated sample and the sample after sweeps of CV, which is due to limited activity degeneration. In contrast, an increase in HER activity tested in NaOHcan be observed in Fig. 6(b). For the original 8-h-heat-treated sample, current reaches /C097.7 mA/cm 2when the over- potential is as high as 529 mV. When it comes to the time-dependent current density curvesshown in Fig. 7, stability varies in H 2SO4and NaOH conditions. For the sample tested in H2SO4under an over-potential of 135 mV, the output current, starting at /C026.1 mA/ccm2, declines with the extension of the testing time, eventually reaching /C018.6 mA/cm2. By contrast, the sample tested in NaOH (under 350 mV over-potential) demonstrates interesting behavior: it begins at /C040.2 mA/cm2, ﬂeetingly drops to less than /C027 mA/cm2, and then starts to regain much of its loss, ﬁnally ending at /C037.6 mA/cm2after 40 000 s. The polarization curves plotted after the potentiostatic experiment (also in Fig. 6), however, are interesting and more or less go against the time-dependent current density curves. g100in H 2SO4does increase marginally from FIG. 5. Polarization curves of NPNs heat treated by different times in 1 M NaOH. FIG. 6. Polarization curves of samples tested under different conditions: (a) measured in 0.5 M H and (b) measured in 1 M NaOH.023104-6 Abdalla, Al-Marzouki, and Obaid J. Renewable Sustainable Energy 9, 023104 (2017)
