251 mV to 259, but its counterpart in NaOH continues to promote its HER property, with its g100 descending from 459 mV to 450 mV. g100 tested in NaOH is able to make further pro- gression, which is found accidentally, that when electrodes were continually soaked in NaOHafter the potentiostatic experiment for more than 12 h, the g100. The results of durability tests can be explained by several reasons. First, the nanoparticles are mainly comprised of nickel and phosphorus elements, but there also exists a tiny amount of oxygen element in the form ofNiO or some more complex compounds on their surface due to reactants remaining on the sur- face during each step of the preparation process, 19,35,36which will have negative effects on HER properties. In the highly acidic condition, e.g., 0.5 M H 2SO4, oxide’s impact on the HER property is limited, because their resistance against H 2SO4is inferior to Ni-P alloys. Soon after the working electrode is soaked in the electrolyte, oxide will disappear in a short term, and Ni- P phases, bearing the main HER catalytic performance, will play their role; hence, NPNs in theacidic condition can display their optimal HER activity as soon as possible. Those NPNs arestill fractionally corroded by H 2SO4after 40 000 s of the potentiostatic experiment, thus explain- ing the decline of output current density under a constant over-potential. In NaOH, however, the removal of the oxide from the electrode surface is a comparatively slow course,35,36which often needs several or even a few tens of hours for the Ni-P catalyst to reach its highest HER activity. Furthermore, under the potentiostatic testing condition, the T-shaped working electrode inevitably absorbs air bubbles, which will periodically generate onto and escape from the elec-trode surface. The adsorption and escape behavior of air bubbles also contribute to the ﬂuctua- tion and unsteadiness when testing the CV and time-dependent current curves. FIG. 7. Time-dependent current density curves during 40000 s: (a) tested in 0.5 M H over-potential; (b) tested in 1 M NaOH, 350 mV as over-potential. FIG. 8. Tafel plots of NPNs tested in 1 M NaOH and 0.5 M H 2SO4.023104-7 Abdalla, Al-Marzouki, and Obaid J. Renewable Sustainable Energy 9, 023104 (2017)
