Drawing on the polarization curves obtained from the LSV curve of 8-h NPNs in both H2SO4and NaOH, Tafel plots can be obtained. Substituting over-potential gand current density j into the Tafel equation, we get g¼aþblogj, where b is the Tafel slope and “a” is the equa- tion’s intercept related to exchange current density j0. By ﬁtting the linear part of the Tafel plots (Fig. 8), the Tafel slope of the NPNs is 79 mV /C1dec/C01with a progressive increase in poten- tial. Under the acidic condition, the slope reveals that the HER is determined by both Volmer and Heyrosky reactions,37in which the Heyrosky reaction serves as the rate-limiting step; whereas in NaOH, the HER proceeds mainly through the Volmer reaction.37,38With the pur- pose of having better insight into the HER activity, the Nyquist plots of NPNs tested under dif- ferent over-potentials are drawn in Fig. 9. Generally, under the same over-potential, as can be seen in Figs. 9(a) and 9(b), the semi-circle drawn from the ﬁtted EIS data in H 2SO4has a smaller diameter than its counterpart tested in NaOH. With the increase in the absolute value of over-potential, a smaller transfer resistance is expected. Such a high-performance HER activity can be attributable to several reasons. First, good dispersity and nano-sized particles may pro-vide more active sites due to nanoparticles’ larger surface area, which serves as an indicator of better HER catalytic properties. Furthermore, transition metal phosphides, such as CoP, FeP, Cu3P, and Ni xPy, have weakly positive charged M dþ(M¼Co, Ni, Fe, and so on) and negative charged P.2,16,39,40The charged NPNs can be analogously considered to be hydride acceptors and proton acceptors in [NiFe] hydrogenase.13This ensemble effect has been proved and con- cluded in nickel phosphides like Ni2P, Ni12P5, and Ni5P4, which might also be applied to other Ni xPy. As to the enhanced HER activity led by the annealing process, this upgradation is more likely caused by crystallization rather than other factors. NPNs’ surface morphology and element distribution do not change too much after annealing. The inﬂuence of NPNs’ heat treat- ment under 5% H 2/Ar is also limited. First, actually the content of the NiO phase is lower than that can be observed, which is proved by XRD patterns in Fig. 2. Moreover, the NiO is not reduced by H 2. The molar ratio between nickel and phosphorus is an important factor resulting in different outcomes of the ensemble effect,6,17,20which also shows no regular changes after annealing. This is demonstrated in both ICP outcomes in Table S2 and EDS in Fig. S2 ( supple- mentary material ). As what annealing process will change is mainly dependent on the crystalli- zation process, and HER activity inﬂuenced by the element ratio is ﬁnite. Therefore, like other members in Ni xPy,N i 3P may also be a good HER catalyst. The NPNs in our system are not a pure nickel phosphide phase, but this result is still acceptable. One thing is that an outstanding HER activity seems to be universal in different nickel phosphides, among which even the amor-phous original Ni-P alloy demonstrates favorable HER activity. In addition, the presence of dif- ferent Ni-P alloys can also show a synergistic effect in favor of the more excellent HER. IV. CONCLUSION To sum up, through a facile auto-catalytic reduction reaction and subsequent annealing pro- cess, the NPNs have displayed distinguished HER activity in both acidic and alkaline FIG. 9. Nyquist plots of ﬁtted EIS data in (a) 0.5 M H 2SO4; (b) 1 M NaOH. The experiments are carried out under different over-potentials.023104-8 Abdalla, Al-Marzouki, and Obaid J. Renewable Sustainable Energy 9, 023104 (2017)
