conditions. A small over-potential for the 8-h annealing sample to reach a current density of 100 mA/cm2can still be achieved after a string of stability tests, showing stable durability. Their anti-corrosiveness in NaOH is reliable, a certain degree of performance improvementafter being soaked in NaOH for a long term, partly because of the removal of oxide. The state-of-the-art HER activity can be further veriﬁed by the Tafel plots and the EIS experiments.Considering the potentiality of the nickel phosphide being introduced as the catalyst for elec-trolysis, it is conceivable that these nickel phosphide nanoparticles may be able to be applied asHER catalytic materials with a good future prospect. SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL Seesupplementary material for the comparison of HER activities of a series of metal phos- phides, which takes into account the mass density of catalysts, substrate, and electrolyte. Fromthe data in Table S1 which shows NPNs’ superior HER activity and the NPNs’ low-cost prepa-ration process, it is found that the materials produced in this work have struck a balance amongdifferent necessary conditions. This is demonstrated in both ICP outcomes in Table S2 and EDS in Fig. S2. As what annealing process will change is dependent mainly on the crystallization process, HER activityinﬂuenced by the element ratio is ﬁnite. Therefore, like other members in Ni xPy,N i 3P may also be a good HER catalyst. Moreover, the NiO is not reduced by H 2. X-ray photoelectron spec- troscopy (XPS) spectra in Fig. S1 of NPNs of different annealing durations do not show thedecline of peaks corresponding to Ni 2þ. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS This project was supported by the King Abdulaziz City of Science and Technology, general direction of research grants—Project No. APR-35-143, and the authors also acknowledge withthanks the Deanship of Scientiﬁc Research, King Abdulaziz University, for technical support. The authors declare no conﬂicts of interest. 1J. Kibsgaard, Z. Chen, B. N. Reinecke