form of nanoparticles applied on large-surface-area support- ing substrates [11,12] . High active catalyst nanoparticles with a good electron transfer ability could be provided by idealsupport [13]. Thus, usage of Pt and Pt-based alloys have been decreased through decoration on two-dimensional (2D)layered transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) owing totheir abundance, low cost, and highly efﬁcient catalytic ac-tivity [14]. For instance, electrocatalysts consisting of a monolayer or nanoparticle of Pt on tungsten carbide (WC) [15], tungsten disulﬁde [16], molybdenum disulﬁdes [12] and tin disulﬁde [17] substrates were successfully fabricated with high efﬁciently catalytic activity in electrochemical reactions. The most catalyst supports, which are widely employed in researches, are carbon-based materials consisting of carbonblack, carbon nanotubes, carbon ﬁbers and graphene [18e21]. There are a number of interesting properties for carbon-basedmaterials, such as dispersion of metal nanoparticles due tothe high surface area, reactants and products transferencedue to porous structure and favorable electrochemical per-formance due to electrical conductivity [18]. Since two- dimensional graphene shows more unique properties thanother carbon nanomaterials, it can be considered as efﬁcientcatalyst support [22,23] . In the meantime, graphene is a hon- eycomb network contains two-dimensional sheets of sp 2 hybrid carbon atoms, which represents remarkable mechan- ical, thermal, chemical and high electrical conductive prop-erties as well as low-price and high availability [24e26]. However, the interaction between these graphene sheets andmetal nanoparticles is not signiﬁcant due to avoid of inﬂuentfunctional groups, excluding on the edges. As a solution of thisdrawback, the nanoparticles can be placed through the gra-phene nanosheets applying the bonds between them andconducting polymers like poly(3,4 eethylene dioxythiophene) (PEDOT). Thus, these stabilized homogeneous metal nano- particles can improve the HER to produce hydrogen gas in a lasting, efﬁcient manner. In addition to prevention of nano-particles agglomeration, PEDOT can facile the HER followingthe proton diffusion on the surface of the electrocatalyst. Toimprove the performance of this electrocatalyst, the graphenenanosheets can be more functionalized applying by the for-mation of sulfonate groups [27,28] . It has been conducted using different sulfonating agents including sulfuric acid (SA)[29,30] , chlorosulfonic acid (CSA) [31,32] ,4ediazoniobenzene sulfonate [33,34] , and 2 echloroethane sulfonic acid [35]. Among these sulfuric agents, CSA led to less graphene-structure destruction compared to the other ones, so, it is used to prepare edge-selective sulfonated graphene sheets (SG) and retain its aromatic system. Herein, a new nanocomposite of platinum nanoparticles (PtNPs) decorated on the conducting polymer of PEDOT andsulfonated graphene composite was synthesized. The syn-thesized nanocomposite was characterized using instru-mental techniques of Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR)spectroscopy, Raman spectroscopy, X-ray photoelectronspectroscopy (XPS), energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy(EDS), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), transmissionelectron microscopy (TEM), and X-ray diffraction (XRD) as well as electrochemical ones of cyclic voltammetry (CV), linear sweep voltammetry, and electrochemical impedance spec-troscopy (EIS). This nanocomposite was ﬁrst prepared by thedecoration of PtNPs on the different composing SG and PEDOT, assigned as PtNPs/PEDOT x-SG (1-x), and subsequently applied as HER electrocatalyst in acidic media. Experimental Materials and reagents 3,4eEthylene dioxythiophene (EDOT, Aldrich),Iron(III) chlo- ride hexahydrate (FeCl 3.6H 2O, Merck), natural graphite pow- der (Merck), sulfuric acid (H 2SO4, Merck), petoluene sulfonic acid (Merck), and potassium platinum(IV) chloride (K 2PtCl 6, Merck) were provided as analytical grade and used withoutfurther puriﬁcation. The double distilled water was utilized inall experiments. The HER electrocatalysts were examined in0.5 mol L /C01H2SO4solution. Apparatus The sulfonation of graphene nanosheets and its coupling of it with PEDOT were examined using FT-IR spectra obtained fromtheir KBr pellets of them by a Jasco e680 FTeIR spectrophotom- eter in IR spectral range of 400 e4000 cm /C01. Raman scattering of the prepared electrocatalyst was conducted using a Raman mi-croscope (TEKSAN, Iran), equipped DPSS Nd:YAG laser with532 nm emission. The size and morphology of generated nano-particles were investigated utilizing a scanning electron micro-scopy (SEM) (S e4160 Hitachi, Japan) and a transmission electron microscope (Philips CM 120, Netherlands). The spectral data obtained from EDS were recorded on a Philips XL30 instrument. X-ray diffraction (XRD) analyses were performed with a BrukerD8 Advance X-ray diffractometer using Cu eK aradiation and pattern characterizations of X 'PertHighScore (PANalytical B.V., the Netherlands). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) wasconducted by an ultrahigh vacuum (UHV) setup equipped with aconcentric hemispherical analyzer. Electrochemical studies ofHER electrocatalystswere conductedby an Autolabpotentiostat/galvanostat (PGSTAT302N, The Netherlands), equipped with athree-electrode cell of PtNps/PEDOT x-SG (1-x)/GCE as a working electrode, a saturated Ag/AgCl as a reference electrode and a Pt bar as an auxiliary electrode. As per the norm in the community in HER studies, the determined potential (vs. Ag/AgCl) was con-verted to reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) scale using theknown potential of Ag/AgCl (saturated KCl) of 0.204 V vs.N H E [36]. Preparation of sulfonated graphene An amount of 2.0 mL of chlorosulfonic acid (CSA) was care- fully added to 0.050 g of graphite powder in a round-bottomed ﬂask equipped with a cold-water condenser. The suspensionwas then thoroughly stirred and reﬂuxed for 12 h at 160 /C14C. The sulfonation of graphite created shiny black when thefunctionalization progressed. Afterward, the resulting sus-pension was fairly cooled. The resultant solid was thenaccumulated by centrifuging at 3000 rpm and subsequentlythoroughly rinsed with distilled water using Soxhlet-extraction system. At last, the SG was dried under vacuumconditions.international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2018) 1 e10 2 Please cite this article in press as: Abdolmaleki A, et al., Efﬁcient and stable HER electrocatalyst using Pt-nanoparticles@poly(3,4 e- ethylene dioxythiophene) modiﬁed sulfonated graphene nanocomposite, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2018), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.03.142
