pristine graphite was transformed into the few-layer gra- phene with defect-free basal planes of the SG platelets due to the edge functionalization. Besides, SG showed a wrinkledsurface which was presumably due to the formation of mo-lecular H-bonds among polar sulfonic acid molecules at theedges of the SG sheets [42]. As can be seen, the TEM images presented in Fig. 2 C and D clearly showed that the SG edges tended to scroll and fold due to the ﬂexible nature of the few-layered graphene [43]. SG exhibited the presence of the ﬂake- like morphology with defect-free areas of graphene since theselective functionalization exclusively occurred at the edgesof graphite without chemical alteration at the basal planes. The formation of PtNPs thorough PEDOT-SG was also exam- ined by TEM. The formed PtNPs, as dark circle points, arepartially homogeneously distributed thorough PEDOT linkedSG nanosheets showed as the string shapes ( Fig. 2 E). The size distribution of PtNPs showed mean size of 8.4 nm in size rangeof 4.2e12.8 nm ( Fig. 2 F). Subsequent XPS was utilized to investigate the chemical environment of the modiﬁed graphene structure ( Fig. 3 ). As can be seen in Fig. 3 A, the pristine graphite revealed a weak O1s peak at 534 eV, relative to the C1s peak at 287 eV (arising fromthe physically adsorbed oxygen) [42], which was increased for the chemically modiﬁed graphene, so, the edge functional groups could increase the quantity of oxygen in SG. The addi-tional S2p peak appeared at 171 eV, indicated that a signiﬁcantamount of sulfur (4.94 wt%) could be covalently introduced intoSG. The high-resolution XPS spectra indicated C1s of func-tionalized graphene with the peaks at 284.8 (C ]C), 285.6 (C eS), and 287.2 eV (C eO) (Fig. 3 B). The O1s core level of SG could bedeconvoluted into two curves at 532.5 (O eC) and 533.9 eV (O eS) (Fig. 3 C). The S2p core level of the SG could be divided into three peaks at 169.2 (S eC) and 169.7 eV (S eO) (Fig. 3 D), indicating a covalent nature for the C eS bond at the edges of SG [44]. XRD patterns were analyzed to obtain the interlayer and crystallization of SG ( Fig. 4 A). Besides, XRD patterns were applied to estimate the degree of delamination layers in theedge-selectively functionalized graphene. The characteristicpeak of the pristine graphite with the index of 002, located at26.5 /C14, was corresponded to interlayer d-spacing of 0.34 nm [45]. This peak was approximately decreased by 70% in the peak intensity, while it remained at the former location. The lower intensity in the same peak location can be due to the stacked layers of graphite sheets, functionalized by sulfonicacid groups at the edges and also delamination of graphiticlayers into SG. The graphite exfoliation into the few-layer SGwas through covalently grafting, as organic molecular wedges,directly onto graphite by an “unzipping ”mechanism and chemical exfoliation with CSA [46,47] . Increasing the peak width-at-half-maximum based on the Scherrer equation cor-responds to an increase of graphite layers when the graphitewas exfoliated to SG. Once again, this result conﬁrmed theedge-selective functionalization of SG with the minimal alteration of its basal plane. As can be seen in Fig. 4 B, the XRD patterns of PEDOT composite, PtNPs/PEDOT, PtNPs/SG andPtNPs/PEDOT 0.3SG0.7are showed in Patterns a to d, respec- tively. According to this ﬁgure and reference code of 00-001-1190 in X 'Pert HighScore software, PtNPs were successfully decorated on the composite since the platinum related peakswith their corresponding phasic indexes were assigned. Fig. 3eXPS spectra of pristine graphite and sulfonated graphene in accompany with their electron transitions.international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2018) 1 e10 5 Please cite this article in press as: Abdolmaleki A, et al., Efﬁcient and stable HER electrocatalyst using Pt-nanoparticles@poly(3,4 e- ethylene dioxythiophene) modiﬁed sulfonated graphene nanocomposite, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2018), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.03.142
