The elemental composition of PtNPs/PEDOT eSG nano- composite was characterized by EDS as exhibited in Fig. 5 .A s can be observed, the energy dispersive peaks related to oxy-gen, platinum, and sulfur are located at 0.46, 2.05 and 9.46, and2.37 keV, respectively. Accordingly, the sulfonation of gra-phene sheets and decoration of platinum nanoparticles couldbe veriﬁed by this EDX spectrum. Further investigation to evaluate PtNPs/PEDOT x-SG (1-x) nanocomposite, x is between 0 and 1, was done using cyclic voltammograms recorded at various compositions of GCEmodiﬁers in 0.5 mol L /C01H2SO4solution. According to Fig. 6 , the presence of PtNPs in the modiﬁers could be detected with theexistence of its reduction peak located at from 0.10 to 0.43 V vs.Ag/AgCl, depended on the portion of PEDOT and SG in thecomposition of modiﬁers. As can be seen in this ﬁgure, gener-ally, increases in SG levels led to low overpotential of platinumreduction whereas the optimum condition, i.e. lower over-potential of 0.42 V and higher reduction current of /C00.36 mA, was achieved at the surface of PtNPs/PEDOT 0.3SG0.7/GCE. Performance of PtNPs/PEDOT xeSG(1-x)/GCE as HER electrocatalyst The electrocatalytic HER performance of the various modiﬁed GCEs, including PtNPs/PEDOT/GCE, PtNPs/PEDOT 0.7-SG 0.3/ GCE, PtNPs/PEDOT 0.5-SG 0.5/GCE, PtNPs/PEDOT 0.3-SG 0.7/GCE, PtNPs/SG/GCE are respectively studied using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) and electrochemical impedance spectros- copy (EIS) in 0.5 mol L/C01H2SO4solution as indicated in curves (a-e) of Fig. 7 .Fig. 7 A shows LSV results of PtNPs/PEDOT xeSG(1-x)GCE modiﬁers for HER in 0.5 mol L/C01H2SO4at a scan rate of 10 mV s/C01. Besides as shown in Fig. 7 B, their electrochemical impedance spectra were recorded in 0.5 mol L/C01H2SO4solu- tion applying the potential of /C00.25 V vs. Ag/AgCl and poten- tial amplitude of 10 mV in the frequency range of10 MHz e100 kHz. As can be clearly seen in this ﬁgures, the study of HER at the surface of PtNPs/PEDOT 0.3-SG 0.7/GCE in acidic media represented lower overpotential, higher reduc-tion current, and lower charge transfer resistance comparedto the other HER electrocatalysts. On the other words, theprotons could be more facilely electrochemically reduced tohydrogen molecules using PtNPs/PEDOT 0.3-SG 0.7/GCE rather than other ones. Since impedance spectra recorded at PtNPs/PEDOT eSG/GCE indicates only one semicircle for HER, the conversion of protons to hydrogen could be occurred due toelectrochemical desorption of hydrogen on the surface of theelectrocatalyst. This semicircle was attributed to the charge transfer resistance, R CT, and the absence of Warburg effect, i.e., diffusion effect, was resulted because of high concentra-tion of the H 2SO4solution (0.5 mol L/C01). According to the above results, the long-term durability of PtNPs/PEDOT 0.3-SG 0.7/GCE for HER application was examined. Fig. 8 A indicates that this HER electrocatalyst evaluated hydrogen as a stable manner even after applying 500thcycle of potential. The mechanism of hydrogen evolution reaction was eval- uated using Tafel plot, as regressed by Log jjj¼/C0 8.109 h‒2.728 inFig. 8 B. This equation was conformed to Tafel equation, i.e., Logj¼logj 0‒2.3/b h, where j, j 0, b, and hare current, exchange current, Tafel slope, and overpotential, respectively. As re-ported in the literature, protons present in acidic media can beconverted to hydrogen molecules in accordance with threeprobable steps [48]. First, Volmer reaction by discharging protons and producing adsorbed hydrogen atoms with b of120 mV dec /C01(H3OþþMþee/MH adsþH2O); Second, Tafel reaction by chemical recombination of adsorbed hydrogenatoms to hydrogen molecules with b of 30 mV dec /C01(2MH ads/ 2MþH2); Third, Heyrovsky reaction by electrochemical desorption of adsorbed hydrogen atoms with b of 40 mV dec/C01 (MH adsþH3Oþþee/MþH2þH2O), where M is metallic Fig. 4e(A) XRD patterns of pristine graphite (a) and sulfonated graphene (b); (B) XRD patterns of PEDOT (a), PtNPs/PEDOT (b), PtNPs/SG (c) and PtNPs/PEDOT 0.3SG0.7(d). Fig. 5eEDX spectrum of PtNPs/PEDOT-SG.international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (2018) 1 e10 6 Please cite this article in press as: Abdolmaleki A, et al., Efﬁcient and stable HER electrocatalyst using Pt-nanoparticles@poly(3,4 e- ethylene dioxythiophene) modiﬁed sulfonated graphene nanocomposite, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy (2018), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2018.03.142
