Topics in Catalysis 1 3 above and several other works (see these reviews on metal catalysts [11– 13], metal sulfides [14], metal phosphides [15], metal nitrides [16], and boron and its compounds [17]), there are several DFT calculations that also tried to help with catalyst screening and finding the reaction path and reaction mechanism [18– 21]. We previously showed that the early transition metal nitrides of Sc, Ti, Y, Hf, Mo, and Ta in the (100) facets of the NaCl-type structure have no catalytic activity toward nitrogen reduction reaction [22, 23] and thus they are of better interest for HER [24 ] where we comprehensively explored the pathway towards HER and compared the rate and performance of these nitrides with Pt (111). We reported that the (100) facets of these mononi- tride surfaces can enhance HER at small overpotentials of − 0.09 to − 0.34 V, with the similar activation energies of the Tafel reaction reported on Pt (111) [24]. Since the syn- thesis of these nitrides is usually attributed to the formation of a polycrystalline structure with presence of multi facets, it is important to investigate other dominant facets too. In polycrystalline structures, the (100) and (111) facets are the most dominant texture orientations, and thus the focus here is on the performance analysis of the (111) facets of the NaCl-type structure. The same detailed DFT approach used for the (100) facets is used here to scrutinize the thermody - namics of the cathode reaction by calculating the differential binding free energy of hydrogen on the (111) surfaces. The Tafel mechanism was found to be the predominant and rate- limiting step for HER on the Pt (111) electrode around the equilibrium potentials [25], and as so it is investigated here. 2  Methodology The computational hydrogen electrode (CHE) approach pioneered by Norskov and co-workers represents a very promising way to enable modeling of electrochemistry by quantitatively incorporating insights from first-principles calculations [26]. The inclusion of quantum mechanical data imparts predictive capability and eliminates the need to make drastic simplifying assumption in the model. At the core of this approach is a specified reaction mechanism and the evaluation of the free energy of the corresponding elementary steps, ΔG=ΔH-T ΔS. The key gateway to introduce electronic structure information into the model is via the free energy of individual component in each of the elementary step. Thanks to its conceptual simplicity and computational efficiency, the CHE approach enabled both in-depth studies of reaction pathways and large-scale computational screening studies, dedicated to identifying optimal catalyst materials and facets. The CHE method has been used vastly over the last decade for different electro- chemical reactions to help the experiments. As few exam - ples, we can mention HER [27, 28],  CO2RR [29, 30], NRR [31], OER [ 29] and ORR [32 ], where its predictions have helped experiments to a great extent with regards to reac- tion mechanisms, operating conditions, material selections and so on. In order to estimate the adsorption free energy of hydrogen at pH = 0 and via the CHE method, we use: ΔG(0) = ΔE+ΔEZPE+TΔS,  where ΔE is the DFT-cal- culated reaction energy. The zero-point energy corrections (ΔEZPE) and entropy differences (ΔS) are calculated within a harmonic approximation for adsorbed species and their values are given in our previous publications [33, 34]. The CHE method is used for all electrochemical reaction steps involving n electrons, and this shifts the reaction free energy by − neU [26], which is the effect of an applied potential, U, for each step: ΔG(U) = ΔG(0) - neU. In order to make a valid comparison of the performance between the (100) and the (111) facets, similar approach and parameters as used before when studying the (100) facets [24] is used here for the (111). All DFT calculations are done via the Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP) code [35], using the RPBE exchange-correlation functional [36], a plane wave basis set with an energy cut-off value of 350 eV for representation of the valence electrons and a projected augmented wave (PAW) [37] representation of the core electrons. The climbing image nudged elastic band (CI-NEB) method [38] is used to obtain the activation free energies. The self-consistent electron density is determined by iterative diagonalization of the Kohn–Sham Hamiltonian, with the occupation of the Kohn–Sham states being smeared according to a Fermi–Dirac distribution with a smearing parameter of  kBT = 0.1 eV. A 4 × 4 × 1 Monkhorst–Pack k-point sampling is used for all the surfaces. The model is comprised of 40-atom nitride slabs in five layers. There are four metal and four nitrogen atoms in each layer. The bot- tom two layers are fixed, whereas the top layers as well as the adsorbed species are allowed to fully relax. Boundary conditions are periodic in the x and y directions, and sur - faces are separated by more than 12 Å of vacuum in the z direction. The structural optimization is allowed to converge until the forces in all direction on moveable atoms are less than 0.01 eV Å−1. Adsorption of hydrogen on the slabs is allowed on all possible sites and for all configurations to find the most energetically favourable intermediate. The Tafel mechanism is explored for  H2 formation. The Hey - rovsky mechanism is another mechanism for HER, but it was shown for transition metals that the Tafel reaction is the predominant  H2 formation step at small overpotentials (near the equilibrium potential, 0 V), while the Heyrovsky reaction becomes more dominant for more negative poten- tials of − 1.5 to − 2.0 V [25]. This means that the Heyrovsky mechanism can be a faster step at very negative potentials and since highly negative potentials are not interesting for HER, we can ignore it. With regards to the Volmer reaction, previous finding agrees well with the experiment in the fact
