Topics in Catalysis 1 3 that the barrier for the Volmer reaction is small and thus it can be considered to be in equilibrium during HER/HOR [25, 39]. At standard conditions, the chemical potential of hydrogen determines the hydrogen coverage on the surface, and including electric fields and water do not affect the ener - getics of adsorbed hydrogen as well as the Tafel reaction barrier [40– 42]. This is due to the fact that adsorbed H has small dipole perpendicular to the surface and contribution of electron transfer in the Tafel reaction is insignificant. The emphasis here is on the energetics of elementary reaction steps at the electrode surface where solvation effects from the electrolyte should not have a central role [40], thus we can only consider a surface slab and adsorbed hydrogen in the atomic model to considerably decrease the computa- tional demand. Nevertheless, the activation energy of the Tafel reaction is influenced by the H coverage caused by the electrochemical potential and thus this coverage dependence and its effect is fully studied in this work. The current densities of the Tafel reaction are then obtained as a function of the applied potential on the sur - face by: k=/u1D708exp(−Ea∕kBT). We apply normal attempt fre- quency of ν =  1013  sites−1  s−1, as the prefactor, because it agrees well with the measured absolute rate for HER when used together with the calculated activation energies [25]. The activation energy of the Tafel reaction is  Ea (eV), the Boltzmann constant is  kB, and the temperature is T. For Pt (111), the current density is calculated for a surface area of 6.64  ×  10–16  cm2 per Pt atom (A/N). Once i = ke(N/A) is applied, we can calculate the current densities (i). Full explanations of these were provided before when the (100) facets of these nitrides investigated [24]. The rates of HER on these (111) facets will be compared with that of Pt (111).3  Result and Discussions The Sabatier principle suggests that the best catalyst for HER has binding free energies of H near 0 eV. This is a known conventional descriptor, as ΔGH*, which is used when dif- ferent material is being inspected for HER. So reporting the adsorption free energy of hydrogen for different material can be a suitable estimation of the catalytic activity toward HER. However, for  H2 to evolve, at least 2*H atoms are required on the surface to evolve as  H2 via a Tafel mechanism. The H*-descriptor-based free energy diagram (FED) is the stand- ard approach for comparison of the catalytic activities. How - ever, the 2*H-descriptor-based FED (previously established [24]) offers different predictions of the catalytic activities of these materials compared to the H*-descriptor. By compar - ing these H-adatom and 2H-adatom schemes Fig.  1A and B are constructed where the FEDs for hydrogen evolution over the selected nitrides are shown for a single and double pro- ton adsorption, respectively. The catalytic activity of these surfaces is also compared with activity of Pt (111) as it is the most effective catalyst known. We know that near the equilibrium potential (U = 0 V), the reaction steps should not have large changes in the free energy. This immediately excludes YN and ScN as good catalysts in both schemes, because they form so strong bonds to H-adatom(s) that the H2 formation step becomes difficult/slow. However, TaN with 1H* adsorption free energy of − 0.06 eV is shown to be good via the former scheme and HfN with 2H* adsorption free energy of + 0.06 eV is shown to be good via the latter scheme. Since these both schemes illustrate different predic- tion (as expected), we will continue further analyses on each of these surfaces inclusively to find out which nitride could Fig. 1  The FEDs are constructed based on H*-descriptor (A) and 2*H-descriptor (B). The values for Pt (111) is  taken from ref [25] and is shown with dashed line. The studied (111) surfaces are shown with solid lines
