Topics in Catalysis 1 3 be of more interest for HER in this (111) oreintation and then later in comparison to their (100) counterparts. Some of the nitrides show higher affinity for H adsoprtion on the surface nitrogen (N) rather than metal sites in a way that a N atom in the surface layer is reduced to  NH3 [22, 23, 43, 44]. For earlier TMNs, however, we previously showed they are catalytically active for HER in the (100) facets where all metal and nitrogen sites on these surfaces were protonated and no *NH2 or *NH3 species were formed [24]. When studying the (111) facets of these nitrides (Fig.  2), it becomes evident that TiN, TaN, and HfN contribute to HER at less H coverage than their (100) facets. As stated earlier, there are four nitrogen and four metal sites on the surface of TMNs and full coverage of H attributes to eight H atoms (1 monolayer, 1ML). Each H binds one of these available sites without formation of any *NH2 species. This was the case for the (100) facets, but for the (111) facets and as shown in Fig. 2, TiN, TaN, HfN, and MoN only needs 0.75 H cover - age on the surface for HER. However, both YN and ScN in the (111) facets require more than a full coverage for HER becasue few *NH2 species are also formed on their surfaces along the reaction path towards HER. As seen from Fig.  2, TaN moderately adsorps the first H but both YN and ScN do it much stronger (as shown by Fig.  1A. For TaN to build up a 0.25 ML coverage on the surface, a potential of − 0.45 V is required to get the second hydrogen atom adsorbed. HfN, instead, only needs small potential of − 0.06 V to build up similar coverage on the surface and evolve  H2. Hence in this analysis, HfN should be more active compared to TaN, which agrees well with the 2H* descriptor scheme shown in Fig.  1B. For MoN, the first three H adsorption steps are exergonic and should hap- pen spontaneously. For ScN and YN the H adsoprtion on the surface is very exergonic which can indicate that these latter surfaces might bind protons strongly and thus proton detachment and recombination might become difficult and unlikely. To answer this, more detailed calculations will be needed and this will be discussed further below where the activation free energies are analysed. When studying the effect of coverage on these surfaces, it is seen that higher coverage of H is formed on the surface as the applying potential goes to more negative values. This is expected and illustrated in Fig.  3A. Figure  3B shows the activation energy of H recombination toward HER as a func- tion of coverage. The general trend is that HER becomes -3-2-1012Differ ential adsorption freeenergy /eV (H++e-)transferr edScN TiN TaN HfN MoN YNHERHER HERHER HER HER ATaHANHBNHCNHANH 2HER ANHBNHCNHDNHANH 2HER ANHBNHCNHDNHANH 2BNH 2CNH 2DNH 2AYH HERANHBNHCNHDNHANH 2HER ANHBNHCNHDNHANH 2HERANHBNHCNHDNHANH 2BNH 2CNH 2DNH 2AScHH ER1 23 4 56 7 8 0 9 10 Fig. 2  Differential free energy of H adsorption. At each hydrogena- tion step, the most stable intermediate is shown: *NH or *MH. The top-view structure of the NaCl-type structure in the (111) facets is shown as the inset, and the labelling from A–D represent how the H-adatoms prefer to bind metal atoms. The labelling from A–D on the nitrogen atom is not shown for the sake of clarity of the inset, but it is similar to that of metal atoms. The surface unit cell has been repeated in the x and y directions. Metal atoms are represented by cyan spheres and nitrogen atoms by dark blue spheres Fig. 3  Applied potential (U/V) as a function of the hydrogen coverage on the surface of nitrides (A). Activation energy  (Ea/eV) for the Tafel reaction in HER as a function of the hydrogen coverage (B). The values for Pt (111) were  adapted from ref [25]
