Topics in Catalysis 1 3 facile as higher H coverage is formed on the surface but the activation energy for all these nitrides is higher than that for Pt (111) with the activity of TaN and HfN being the closest to Pt (111). As stated earlier and showed in Fig.  2, adsorp- tion of 2*H atoms on MoN, TiN, ScN, and YN is exergonic but when we calculate the activation free energy of hydrogen recombination for  H2 evolution, we can see that this step is kinetically difficult on these surfaces with  Ea higher than 2 eV. These analyses show that ScN, YN, TiN, and MoN show so high affinity to hydrogen adsorption that desorp- tion becomes difficult and the surface should get poisoned by the intermediates. TaN and HfN, however, perform better for  H2 evolution. Figure  4A illustrates the activation energy for the Tafel reaction on the (111) nitrides as a function of the electrode potential. When the applied potential goes to more negative values, the coverage of proton on the surface gets higher. This contributes to lower activation energy for the 2H*→H2 reaction, and this is clearly seen here too. A small overpo- tential of around 0.06 V is required for TaN to reach 1/8 coverage and 0.45 V to build 2/8 coverage of H. The cal- culated barrier of this Tafel reaction is 1.35 eV which is around 0.5 eV higher than that calculated for Pt (111) and TaN(100) which was 0.84 eV. HfN, however, is predicted to need an overpotential of 0.35 V to reach 2/8 coverage. This means that HfN should be a better candidate from the thermodynamic perspective, but the calculated barrier of its Tafel reaction is more than twice (3.05 eV) as that on TaN, and therefore TaN should offer a better kinetics than HfN. Overall, near the equilibrium potential, none of these (111) surfaces show rates close to what Pt(111) demonstrates. TaN is the most active surface compared to the other nitrides, though. This is interesting to mention here that when com- paring the (100) and (111) facets of these nitrides, TaN is the most active candidate amongst all with the (100) being much more promising than the (111). Figure  5 shows the experimental volcano plot made for the metals (adapted from ref [ 25, 45]) on which the cal - culated theoretical current density of the Tafel reaction for TaN in both the (100) and (111) facets are located where the experimentally measured exchange current density obtained Fig. 4  Activation energy  (Ea/eV) (A) and theoretical current densi- ties of the Tafel reaction (B) calculated as a function of the electrode potential, U, on the (111) facets of the nitride and compared with Pt (111)  adapted from ref [25]. In order to reach 2/8 coverage of H on TaN, − 0.45 V needs to be applied and this naturally results in more H coverage, thus some of the points are overlaying at − 0.45  V for TaN Fig. 5  Volcano plot of the HER for various pure metals, TaN in both the (111) and (100) facets are the most promising surfaces. The data for TaN (100) is taken from our previous work [24]. The rates for TaN are calculated, but the rest of the values are experimental. The ΔGH* value for TaN is calculated for 0.25 ML of H coverage at 1 bar of  H2 and 298 K. Experimental data were taken from ref [25, 45], where the circular data points are the measured exchange current density (open data points for (111) facets but filled data points for polycrystalline) plotted versus the calculated free energy of H adsorp- tion. The metals on the left side of the volcano have high H coverage (1 ML) and the metals on the right side low H coverage (0.25 ML). The (100) facet of the TaN is a better facet compared to the (111) with regards to both thermodynamics and kinetics
