Topics in Catalysis 1 3 on pure metals is compared with the theoretically predicted exchange current densities. The ΔGH* descriptor is calcu- lated with DFT. The 1 ML is used for the left leg of the volcano and the top of the volcano whereas 0.25 ML is used for the right leg of the volcano and the illustrated nitrides. The (100) facets of TaN is a more active surface for HER compared to its (111), with regards to both thermodynamics and kinetics. All the other nitrides of (111) facets studied here are located well below −  30 A.cm−2 and thus not shown in the volcano plot. The experimental studies on the HER performance of Tantalum nitride are mainly limited to  Ta3N5 and  Ta5N6 structures as of today. To mention couple of examples, Xio et al. [46] achieved a current of around − 0.008 Amp on a single-crystal  Ta3N5 nanomesh for photocatalytic HER at overpotential of 0.3 V, while the (100) facets of TaN reported here should contribute to around − 5 Amp.cm−2 at overpotential of 0.1 V, and its (111) facets to around − 14 Amp.cm−2 at overpotential of 0.45 V. Also Zhang et al. investigated HER performance of  Ta5N6 electrocatalysts where 0.02 Amp.cm−2 was achieved at overpotential of around 0.11 V [47]. These early transition metals all form very stable compounds with nitrogen. The most important of these are the mononitrides  (M1N1) that form a NaCl-type cubic structure which are generally resistant to chemical attack and stable at high temperatures in inert or reducing atmosphere [48]. Another point is that since HER happens at near-zero potentials, the stability of these systems will not be compromised compared to, for example,  CO2RR or NRR where much more negative potentials are usually applied. Different crystallite size of these nitrides might contrib- ute to different surface area and thus different activity by affecting on surface roughness and thickness, which should be investigated experimentally. However, at any given crys- tallite size there will be yet similar (100) and (111) fac- ets involved which we have investigated their performance for HER here. The determining factor might be the ratio between the availability of these different facets in the syn- thesized crystal structure, for example if there are more (100) facets available in the final structure, more activity will be expected but if there are more of the (111) present, less activity will be anticipated. 4  Conclusion We had previously explored the possibility of using earlier transition metal nitrides as catalysts for HER where TaN (100) in the NaCl-type structure was predicted to be the most promising surface. Usually, in catalyst synthesis, these surfaces are mainly produced in a polycrystalline structure where texture orientations other than (100) could be of dom- inance like the (111), so this facet have deeply been studied as well. The findings show that among the studied (111) facets, HfN is thermodynamically better than TaN but not kinetically. More importantly, the (100) was the most active facets compared to (111) with regards to both thermodynam- ics and kinetics with TaN being the most promising. There- fore, surface engineering becomes of utmost importance in using these nitride surfaces for HER, and more efforts should be focused on synthesizing these surfaces in single- crystal structures to achieve optimum performance. Acknowledgements  Financial support is acknowledged from the Icelandic Research Fund grant numbers 185051051-3, 207056-051, 207056-052, and the Research Fund of the University of Iceland. The calculations were carried out on the Icelandic high-performance com- puter, Garpur. Funding Icelandic research Fund grant numbers 185051051-3, 207056-051, 207056-052, and the Research Fund of the University of Iceland. Data Availability The datasets generated during and/or analysed dur - ing the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Code Availability The VASP code was used for the DFT calculations of this manuscript and the corresponding details of that are provided in the manuscript. Declarations Conflict of interest Not applicable. References: 1. Jamesh MI (2016) Recent progress on earth abundant hydrogen evolution reaction and oxygen evolution reaction bifunctional electrocatalyst for overall water splitting in alkaline media. J Power Sources 333:213–236. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1016/j. jpows our. 2016. 09. 161 2. Turner JA (2004) Sustainable hydrogen production. Science 305(5686):972–974. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1126/ scien ce. 11031 97 3. Zeng K, Zhang D (2010) Recent progress in alkaline water elec- trolysis for hydrogen production and applications. Prog Energy Combust Sci 36(3):307–326. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1016/j. pecs. 2009. 11. 002 4. Han N, Liu P, Jiang J, Ai L, Shao Z, Liu S (2018) Recent advances in nanostructured metal nitrides for water splitting. J Mater Chem A 6(41):19912–19933. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1039/ C8TA0 6529B 5. Chen WF, Muckerman JT, Fujita E (2013) Recent developments in transition metal carbides and nitrides as hydrogen evolution electrocatalysts. Chem Commun 49(79):8896–8909. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1039/ c3cc4 4076a 6. Xie J, Xie Y (2016) Transition metal nitrides for electrocatalytic energy conversion: opportunities and challenges. Chem—A Eur J 22(11):3588–3598. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1002/ chem. 20150 1120 7. Han Y, Yue X, Jin Y, Huang X, Shen PK (2016) Hydrogen evolu- tion reaction in acidic media on single-crystalline titanium nitride nanowires as an efficient non-noble metal electrocatalyst. J Mater Chem A 4(10):3673–3677. https:// doi. org/ 10. 1039/ c5ta0 9976e
