3  Abstract  In light of the ever-growing global energy demand, photocatalytic water splitting has emerged as a promising avenue for sustainable and persistent energy sources. However, the quest for an optimal photocatalyst suitable for industrial-scale applications remains a strenuous challenge. The journey to identify the optimal photocatalyst for the water splitting reaction has been extensive and remains ongoing. While the search started with the use of inorganic semiconductors based on metal oxides, such as TiO2, many new and promising materials, such as Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs), have started to attract the attention of the scientific community. However, in order to be able to improve the efficiency of any photocatalyst, it is important to first understand how the reaction is taking place, in other words, it results imperative to understand the reaction mechanism. The aim of the following review is to study and analyze different experimental techniques that can be used for the elucidation of the reaction mechanism covering both water splitting’s half reactions: hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and overall water splitting (OWS). This work starts with the fundamentals of photocatalytic OWS under solar irradiation, followed by the systematical evaluation of distinct MOF-based photocatalysts, classifying them based on the specific metal ion in their composition which facilitates standardized comparisons. The mechanistic investigation of photocatalysts is then detailed, employing various spectroscopic techniques. While a higher focus has been given to the analysis of the mechanistic study on MOFs, other important photocatalysts counterparts are also explored, as they have helped to cement the bases in which new materials can be studied. Furthermore, by comparing results obtained for conventional photocatalysts (e.g., metal oxide semiconductors) with those obtained for newer materials like MOFs, we attempt to
============================================================
[Page 5/147]
============================================================
4  show the great amount of information that can be extracted for the elucidation of reaction mechanisms. This systematic approach aims to help better investigate the mechanistic study and designing the next generation of photocatalysts for HER, OER, and OWS. 1. General introduction  The surge in carbon dioxide emissions from fossil fuels has led to severe environmental crises, prompting a focus on economically viable, carbon-free renewable energy sources such as geothermal, wind, solar energy, and even biomass [1–5]. However, societal reliance on fossil fuels persists, highlighting the urgent need for a viable, easily stored, and transported renewable energy vector. Because of its exceptional characteristics as a clean and environmentally friendly substance [6], hydrogen gas has emerged as one of the most extensively investigated alternative energy carriers which is used in various applications including ammonia and methanol synthesis and petroleum refining [7–9]. Currently, most hydrogen is generated via steam methane reforming (SMR), emitting gigatons of CO2 annually [10], prompting the emergence of blue hydrogen as a partial solution, utilizing traditional methods with reduced emissions. However, for a truly carbon-free alternative, clean hydrogen production methods such as water electrolysis are crucial, albeit expensive yet highly efficient, with purple and green hydrogen identified as main types using nuclear or renewable energies respectively [11,12]. Thus, finding an environmentally friendly and cost-effective hydrogen production route is crucial.  For instance, the  direct use of sunlight through photocatalysis, for hydrogen production without prior electricity conversion, holds significant potential [13,14]. It presents a promising solution due to its natural abundance and cleanliness. Photocatalytic water splitting, is the process of producing hydrogen using photoactive catalysts from water to
============================================================
[Page 6/147]
============================================================
5  hydrogen and oxygen components through two half-reactions: the oxidation of water forming molecular oxygen (OER) and the reduction of protons generating hydrogen (HER).  Generally speaking, for efficient photocatalytic overall water splitting (OWS), a photocatalyst must have a conduction band (CB) edge more negative than the redox potential observed for of H+/H2 (0.0 V vs. NHE) and a valence band (VB) edge that is more positive than the redox potential of O2/H2O (1.23 V), requiring a minimum band gap energy of 1.23 eV. However, meeting these criteria doesn't ensure effective catalysis as reaction efficiency also hinges on thermodynamic and kinetic parameters such as active surface sites, charge transfer, diffusion, and electron-hole recombination rate, emphasizing the necessity of tuning these factors for the development of high-performance photocatalysts [15,16]. This photocatalytic performance is usually assessed through the conversion efficiency and catalysts lifetime. However, comparing reaction rates across photocatalysts can be challenging due to variations in illumination conditions [17]. Concepts like quantum yield, apparent quantum efficiency (QE), and solar-to-hydrogen efficiency (STH) offer more reliable comparisons between systems [18,19]. Along the years numerous semiconductors have been investigated in the field of photocatalysis for their cost-effectiveness and suitable band structures. However, they are only active under UV irradiation due to their large band gaps. Some examples are TiO2, SrTiO3, and KTaO3 that  face challenges in electronic conductivity and QE [17]. Huge efforts to boost their efficiencies have focused on textural design, doping, photosensitizer integration, and forming semiconductor heterojunctions [18,20]. Here are some common reported cocatalysts used to promote HER Pt [21], Ni [22], Cu [23], Cr2O3 [24], MoS2 [25], Cu3P [26], etc., while the OER presents better results by using some metal oxides such as IrO2 [27], RuO2 [28], NiOx [29], CoOx [30], among others. Due to the challenge of finding a
============================================================
[Page 7/147]
============================================================
6  single solid material with both suitable band alignment and band gap for efficient HER and OER, a Z-scheme junction was proposed as an alternative  [31]. Two different photocatalysts are used each of them greatly efficient in either the HER or the OER so, Gibbs free energy is reduced and a wider range of visible light may be utilized.  After that, the development of metal complexes for OWS in homogeneous catalysis was also explored [32]. Initially, homogeneous reactions involved multicomponent systems with photosensitizer, electron mediator, catalyst, and sacrificial agent [33–35]. However, rapid photodegradation limited their activity. Supramolecular photocatalysts, single-component systems, were the  catalytic centers and chromophores  are linked through hydrogen bonding, van der Waals forces, and weak interactions allowing to have the absorption of light and the reactivity in the same structure were then explored [36–41]. Despite numerous reported photocatalysts, both solid semiconductor and molecular, most fail to meet the necessary standards for industrial viability, which demand a minimum of 10% solar-to-hydrogen conversion efficiency (STH) and a lifespan of 5 to 10 years to achieve competitive hydrogen pricing [42].  In the last few decades, a vast quantity of photocatalytic materials, composites, complexes, and heterojunctions have been developed for the HER, OER and OWS (Figure 1). However, the drawbacks associated with rapid electron-hole recombination, reduced specific surface area, poor harvesting of visible light, and insufficient durability, still limit the expansion of semiconductor photocatalysts. Meanwhile, molecular photocatalytic systems seem to solve some ailments, like the increase of active site’s number, easier transfer of charge carriers, and structural tunability yet they still suffer from rapid decomposition, low stability, and inability to recover for multiple reaction cycles, making them unreliable for industrial scale-up.
============================================================
[Page 8/147]
============================================================
7  Recently, a series of hybrid ordered porous materials have gained more attention in the area of photocatalysis. In the border between solid semiconductors and molecular photocatalysts, Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) represent the convergence of the best of both worlds. With high surface areas, porosity, tunable chemical structures including photoactive inorganic moieties, efficient charge transfer and well dispersed active sites, MOFs have a great potential as microreactors and photocatalysts for OWS. Despite being relatively new compared to other photocatalysts developed over decades, research on MOF photocatalysts needs further exploration (Figure 1).  Even if their behavior may be different, the knowledge obtained from the development of semiconductors, Z-schemes, molecular catalysts, the understanding of the photocatalytic process could be used as a base not only for a better understanding of the OWS reaction in presence of MOFs, but also to better tune the chemical and the geometrical features of MOFs, to enable even more efficient photocatalysts.  In this review, a huge focus will be given to the advances that have been made in the elucidation of the mechanism for HER, OER and OWS reactions using semiconductors-based oxides and MOFs as photocatalysts. This primarily stems from the challenges faced in mechanistic investigations of MOF-based photocatalysts, attributed to the difficulties associated with the bulkiness of the MOF structure. For this, we will detail the most common characterization techniques used for this purpose. Meanwhile, we will compare the best photocatalysts investigated, by normalizing their hydrogen yield. The influence of MOF stability and purity on photocatalytic behavior will be further discussed as it provides crucial insights into reaction kinetics, charge transfer processes, and surface interactions, thereby guiding the rational design and optimization of MOF-based photocatalysts for diverse applications in environmental remediation, energy conversion, and chemical synthesis.
