8    Figure 1. Timeline showing some of the most important developments that have marked the evolution of the photocatalytic water splitting reaction using a) semiconductors (light blue); b) metal-complexes (green); c) Z-scheme composites (orange); and d) MOFs (red).  2. MOFs-based photocatalysts: properties and characteristics As a relatively newly developed class of porous 3D materials, MOFs have received great attention in recent years. The Metal-Organic Frameworks (MOFs) are a family of materials consisting of metal ions or oxo-clusters (or chains, layers…) that are linked together by organic molecules or ligands to form an extended micro- or meso-porous coordination polymers with a well-ordered/crystalline structures [43,44]. They can be produced through different synthetic methods including hydro/solvothermal, microwave, ultrasonication, electrochemical and mechanical processes and their particle size/shape tuned [45]. MOFs have a modular design that allows the functionalization and tunability of their structure to suit different applications including gas adsorption or storage, gas separation [46], biomedical [47], sensing [48], proton conduction [49], catalysis [50], among many others. They can be characterized by a) a high surface area that can reach up to 7,500 m2 g-1, b) the presence of open metal sites or polar functional groups that can give certain acidity to the
