[Page 10/147]
============================================================
9  framework (Lewis sites) [51] and/or Brønsted acid sites, c) modular pore size and shape, and d) tunable chemical surfaces [52]. Thus, MOFs are considered more and more as photocatalysts owing to their unprecedented tunability not only in terms of both metal centers and organic ligands, but also for the possibility to exploit their openings to accommodate active species in specific spaces and contact with the framework. This quasi-unique flexibility allows to fine-tune the MOFs’ properties to match specific photocatalytic applications by selecting appropriate metal ions and ligands. Therefore, in contrast with conventional semi-conductor, MOFs’ structure can be engineered to have the desirable electronic, optical, and redox properties, in view of optimizing then their performance as photocatalysts. For instance, MOF-based photocatalysts bearing photoactive organic ligands and/or MxOy oxo-clusters enable them to effectively absorb light in the visible or UV range [53]. Furthermore, MOFs exhibit sometimes a flexible structure that can be modified easily by functionalizing the ligands [54] or by doping/exchanging metal ions of the clusters [55]. As a result, the region of light absorption and the potential of the band gap of the MOF can be easily regulated to fit for a specific photocatalytic reaction. In addition to that, due to the outstanding porosity of MOFs and their large pore sizes, photosensitizer and/or co-catalysts can in some cases be incorporated into the frameworks, boosting their photocatalytic activity [56].  However, one of the main drawbacks that has prevented a wider and faster advancement of MOFs as photocatalysts is their limited chemical stability compared to other materials (metal oxides and perovskites). However, with the continuous research and exploration on these structures and the constant synthesis of novel MOFs, more robust and chemically stable frameworks have been synthesized [57]. Some of these structures have presented high
============================================================
[Page 11/147]
============================================================
10  stability in different ranges of pH and solvents, allowing their reusability in several catalytic cycles, making them more cost efficient [58–60]  On the other hand, a greater number of MOFs have been tested as photocatalysts for the HER, OER and OWS reactions.  Table 1 presents a summary of some of the most relevant MOFs that have been reported in the literature for the HER, OER, and the OWS. by normalizing the hydrogen and oxygen yields. Thus, allowing appropriate comparison across different systems. In the following section some of the most remarkable MOF-photocatalysts that have been used for these reactions will be discussed.  Given the conditions at which the water splitting reaction takes place, there are a couple of important factors that should be considered when choosing a MOF for this specific photocatalytic application. The first condition is that MOFs should be stable in the presence of water; it is even preferable that the material can maintain its crystalline structure and porosity at different ranges of pH. In presence of water, there exists a competition between the organic linker/metal ions and the water molecules that can end up in the hydrolysis of coordination bond [61]. The second specification is the MOFs should preserve its structure under light irradiation, in particular in the UV range, although for OWS water might absorb part of the UV thus decreasing the risk of long term photodegradation. It has been reported that several MOFs can indeed suffer from degradation upon extended exposition to UV light irradiation, especially considering the photo-sensibility of the organic molecules that conform the ligands of the MOF. Mateo et al. studied the long-term photostability of MOF containing carboxylate linkers (BDC = benzene dicarboxylic acids). MIL-101, UiO-66 and MIL-125 were tested for this experiment showing that the BDC linker can be photo-decarboxylated in the presence of UV-vis irradiation evolving into CO2. The level of
============================================================
[Page 12/147]
============================================================
11  degradation showed to have a structural dependency [62]. We will shed more light on the effect of the MOFs stability and purity, latter in this review. However, there are some common strategies that could be followed for the obtention of more chemically stable MOFs. As a general rule, by strengthening the coordination bond between the organic (ligand) and the inorganic (metal ions or oxo-clusters) secondary building units, it is possible to obtain more robust frameworks. For example, high-valence cations (such as Zr4+, Ti4+, and Cr3+) form stronger coordination bonds with hard bases (such as a carboxylate group). It is possible to obtain a MOF that presents a higher hydrothermal stability if the  interactions between the metal ions and the organic ligands are strengthened [57]. A more detailed analysis on the parameters that influence the stability of a MOF could be found elsewhere [63–65].  For this reason, it is not surprising that most of the MOFs that have been tested for OWS consist of frameworks containing high-valence cations and polycarboxylate linkers. MIL-125(Ti) and UiO-66(Zr) are two of the most investigated materials for the HER, OER and OWS. In the next section, we analyze some of the most common MOFs built containing high-valence cations that have been reported in the literature for the production of hydrogen and oxygen from water.
