18   2.1. Titanium based MOFs (Ti-MOFs) Similar to what was observed for the photocatalytic properties of semiconductor TiO2, in the case of MOFs, MIL-125(Ti) (MIL = Materials from Institute of Lavoisier) was one of the first Ti-based frameworks showing photocatalytic activity. MIL-125(Ti) is composed of Ti8O8(OH)4 cyclic octamers connected by 1,4-benzenedicarboxylates as linkers, forming a structure that presents both tetrahedral (inner diameter of 6.1 Å) and octahedral (inner diameter of 12.5 Å) cages, and a triangular window of 5–7 Å (Figure 2). For instance, Serre et al. reported that MIL-125(Ti) presented a change in color (white to purple) under UV-visible light irradiation. Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectroscopy showed the formation of reduced Ti3+ species responsible for the red shift. It was also observed that the sample was able to photochemically oxidize alcohols to aldehydes [101]. Since then, Ti-MOFs have been one of the most used metal-organic frameworks based photocatalyst. Figure 2. Structure of MIL-125 and MIL-125-NH2. Color code: Ti, purple polyhedra; C, grey; O, red. The redox capacity to transition between Ti3+ and Ti4+ was reported as well by other authors and is one of the properties that make Ti-MOFs so appealing in photocatalytic applications.
============================================================
[Page 20/147]
============================================================
19  However, with a high valence band and a smaller ionic radius than Zr4+, Ti4+ presents a strong affinity to oxygen, which, while useful in the construction of more stable MOFs, makes the synthesis of new frameworks a considerably challenging endeavor [57,102].  With the struggle to synthesize new Ti-MOFs most of the photocatalytic studies were mainly focused on MIL-125(Ti). However, analogous to TiO2, the photoactivity for most Ti-MOFs can only be observed under UV irradiation [54]. In consequence, many reports have focused on the structural modification of MIL-125(Ti) in order to improve its photocatalytic properties and increase the range of light harvesting ability. The addition of co-catalysts, photosensitizers, doping and formation of heterojunctions and/or adding functionalized groups within the structure or to the organic linker are some of the strategies that have been used to improve the photocatalytic activity of Ti-MOF-based photocatalysts. E.g., the use of the amine-analogue MIL-125(Ti)-NH2 instead of MIL-125(Ti) (the -NH2 moieties being grafted on the aromatic ring from the ligand) has been one of the most commonly used modifications which allows to lower the optical band gap from 3.6 eV to 2.6 eV with the addition of the amine group to the organic linker, also leading to a better stability in water [54].  Remiro-Buenamañana et al., for example, used MIL-125(Ti)-NH2 for the OWS reaction obtaining 49 and 23 μmol.g−1 of H2 and O2 respectively, under UV−vis irradiation for 22 h. However, when Pt and RuOx were added as cocatalysts to the pristine MIL-125-(Ti)-NH2, the production of hydrogen and oxygen increased to 218 and 85 μmol.g−1, respectively, under the same conditions [103]. This study proved that the presence of binary cocatalysts can promote both the production of H2 and O2. The reproducibility of various catalytic systems involving cocatalysts with MIL-125(Ti) was examined solely through characterization, lacking an actual catalytic test. This absence of a catalytic test raises concerns regarding the
============================================================
[Page 21/147]
============================================================
20  stability of these MOFs in the context of photocatalytic OWS. Additionally, an observable reduction in the crystallinity of the MOFs was inferred from  transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and X-ray diffraction pattern (XRD) patterns of the catalysts after the reaction, compared to their initial states [103]. The incorporation of a cocatalyst into the MOF to enhance the photocatalytic activity of the material is a very recurrent technique that has been used by many research groups. Another example is the study performed by An et al. [104]. In this case, cobalt phosphate (CoPi) and Pt were deposited in the larger cage of MIL-125(Ti). The MIL-125-CoPi-Pt material gave the production of 42.33 μLh-1 of H2 and 21.33 μL.h-1 of O2. The presence of the CoPi and the Pt helped to stop the fast electron–hole recombination by inducing the migration of the holes towards the CoPi while the electrons migrate to the Pt  nanoparticles (NPs) [104].  The study on MIL-125(Ti)-NH2 continued with Antonino et al. who used the oxygen plasma to induce mesoporous or microporous defects into the crystals of the Ti-MOF and improve the overall photocatalytic water splitting under visible light irradiation and using labeled H218O. Different irradiation times were tested, showing optimal results at 10 min of contact between the oxygen plasma and the MIL-125(Ti)-NH2. However, treatments longer than 10 min decreased the photocatalytic activity of the sample. The authors attributed the higher performance of the defective MIL-125(Ti)-NH2 to a smaller particle size. According to them the decrease in size allows a better diffusion of light in the sample as well as changes in the energy band alignment [94]. The chemistry of Ti-MOFs is quite challenging due to the tough competition between making Ti-ligand bonds and the formation of Ti oxides in solution. However, a larger number of Ti-MOFs have aroused these past few years. And in most cases new Ti-MOFs have been developed for the OWS reaction. One of the most efficient MOFs that has been reported so
============================================================
[Page 22/147]
============================================================
21  far is the IEF-11 (IEF = IMDEA Energy Framework), which consists of a porous titanium(IV) squarate metal–organic framework. This MOF, presenting a layer of interconnected TiO5 and TiO6 polyhedra (Figure 3), showed a band gap equal to 2.45 eV. The system Pt/IEF-11 showed a production of 658 µmol.g–1 for H2 and 302 µmol.g–1  for O2 after 22 h reaction time. The photocatalytic activity of this material was attributed to the arrangement of 2D Ti-O-Ti motifs which allows an efficient charge separation [97]. Figure 3. Crystal structure of IEF-11. (a) overall view of the structure along the c axis. and (b) a squarate inker Ti-O layers. Color code: Ti, purple polyhedra; C, grey; O, red. Besides the overall water-splitting reaction, Ti-MOFs have also been used for hydrogen evolution. Ayala et al., for example, presented the study of the photocatalytic properties of the titanium structure COK-47 (COK = Centrum voor Oppervlaktechemie en Katalyse) for the hydrogen evolution reaction  [105]. The structure of COK-47 consists of Ti-O sheets (2D secondary building units) connected by biphenyl-dicarboxylate (bpdc2-) forming a 3D framework (Figure 4). A microwave-assisted synthesis was used in order to obtain small particles while preventing the formation of missing ligand defects. This sample was identified as COK-47ISO (ISO=isotopic). The photocatalytic tests were performed in a batch reactor, with 1h of irradiation time, under different illumination sources (Light-Emitting Diode
