23  observed, with a modest reusability (three cycles). According to the electrochemical results, Ru-SAs/NPs@NTC has a lower recombination rate of charge carriers than Ru-NPs/SAs@NTC. However, Ru-NPs/SAs@NTC showed superior H2 production which was not clearly justified or explained [107]. In our opinion, this phenomenon can be due to the dehydrogenation of impurities (proton donor) embedded in the MOF framework. A more detailed analysis on the influence of impurities as sources of confusion during a reaction will be discussed in section 6.  Kampouri et al. presented another example where MIL-125(Ti)-NH2 (Figure 2) was used for the synthesis of a visible-active heterojunction, in this case, by coupling it with Ni2P NPs [108]. A relatively high AQE was recorded (27 % at 400 nm) using a 300 W Xenon (Xe) lamp and trimethylamine (TEOA)as sacrificial agent. The stability of the proposed system in triethanolamine is a major concern that limits the activity [108].  Eosin Y (EY) has also been used as a photosensitizer to enhance the light-absorption capacity of MIL-125. The system MIL-125(Ti)-NH2-g-C3N4/NiPd NPs/ EY-sensitized was prepared and used for the HER, with good hydrogen production results (8.7 mmol.g-1.h−1). The characterization of the sample showed that MIL-125(Ti)-NH2-g-C3N4/NiPd NPs has an enhanced photocurrent density (0.7 μA.cm−2) compared to MIL-125(Ti)-NH2 (0.5 μA.cm−2). This is mainly due to the heterostructure formed between C3N4 and MIL-125(Ti)-NH2 that further boosts the separation and transfer of charge carriers. However the recyclability of the sample can be a long term concern, with a dramatic decrease in the photocatalytic activity after the third reaction cycle (50 % lower than what was originally observed) [75].  Chen and his coworkers investigated the use of a multivariate titanium-based metal organic framework/covalent organic frame work composite: Ti-MOF/COF composite with a good visible light absorption capacity, an appropriate band gap, and a huge surface area for
