25   Figure 6. Structure of the Ti3-BPDC-Ir photocatalysts. [109]  2.2. Zirconium based MOFs (Zr-MOFs) At the contrary to the Ti-based MOFs, Zr-MOFs are much more widely diverse and their chemistry allows more versatility due the lower charge density of Zr4+ and higher connectivity compared to Ti4+ enabling an easier chemistry to handle when making MOF materials. However, deeper research on Zr-MOFs has demonstrated that they have limited photocatalytic activity as their charge transfer properties are not as efficient as those that can be observed in other MOFs (e.g., Ti-MOFs) [110]. This is mainly due to the fact that the reduction from Zr4+ to Zr3+ is generally more challenging due to the higher oxidation state and stability of zirconium compared to that of Ti4+/Ti3+. It's noteworthy to emphasize that while TiO2 has been extensively studied as a photocatalyst, its efficacy is limited to UV-driven reactions. ZrO2 has also been widely used for different photocatalytic applications due to its high stability and its tunable band gap that can go from 3.25 to 5.1 eV depending on the synthesis method [111]. This metal oxide has also presented a conduction band with highly negative vales which makes it a promising photocatalyst for H2 production [112]. In the case of MOFs, due to the relatively strong bond that is formed between the zirconium and oxygen, and, thus, the associated chemical stability, the Zr-carboxylate MOFs, such as UiO-
============================================================
[Page 27/147]
============================================================
26  66(Zr) which is among the earlier MOFs studied for the photocatalytic HER, are still widely used in photocatalytic OWS. As a result, some synthetic strategies, similar to that used for MIL-125, have been implemented in order to tune the photochemical behavior of the UiO-66 based structures and increase its photoactivity. One of them involves the addition of a second or even third metal into the framework (doping) which can help the adsorption of visible light and facilitate the ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT).  Under this premise, Melillo et al. [95], for example, proposed the synthesis of a series of UiO-66(M) represented in Figure 7, with M = Zr, Zr/Ti, Zr/Ce, Zr/Ce/Ti, and Ce, which were tested as photocatalysts for OWS, although there was no demonstration of the successful loading of all the heteroelements within the constitutive oxo-cluster of UiO-66. The H2 (9.58 μmol.g-1.h-1) and O2 (4.59 μmol.g-1.h-1) evolutions with the trimetallic UiO-66 (Zr/Ce/Ti) under UV irradiation using 300 W Xe lamp were higher than the one observed with the monometallic counterparts. This enhancement arises from the efficient separation of charge carriers provided by the multi-metal conformation. However, as mentioned in this study, the quantity of metal could not be controlled limiting therefore a quantitative comparison or normalization between the various composites [95].
