27   Figure 7.  Structure of UiO-66 with different possible functionalization. Color code: Zr, blue polyhedra; C, grey; O, red. Another alternative for materials’ doping is the addition of cocatalysts.  Accordingly, Zhang et al. investigated the use of Pt@NH2UiO(Zr)-66@MnOx (PUM) as a photocatalyst for overall water splitting [96]. The typical structure of the MOF is shown in Figure 7. Results confirmed that loading of both Pt and MnOx NPs has helped to improve the light absorption range and the charge transfer rate. The photoluminescence (PL) and the UV-vis spectroscopy results proved that the PUM sample has higher absorbance capacity and better charge transfer rate than the Pt@NH2-UiO-66 (PU) and NH2-UiO-66 (UM). This was directly related to the higher photocatalytic activity for HER and OER that was observed within the sample. In the PUM sample, the photo generated electrons are transferred directly from the NH2-UiO-66 to the Pt nanoparticles where reduction of water takes place generating H2. Meanwhile, the generated holes are transferred to the MnOx where reduction takes place to generate O2. As a result, this synergetic effect between the three components enhances significantly the photocatalytic OWS [96].  Many studies have as well focused on the coupling of  Zr-based MOF with a photoactive
============================================================
[Page 29/147]
============================================================
28  material such as CdS, TiO2, or graphitic carbon nitride (g-C3N4) in order to form functional heterojunctions that can enhance the charge (hole/electron) separation and prevent their fast recombination during the reaction [113]. For example, the ternary composite UiO-66/CdS/reduced graphene oxide (RGO) was synthesized by Lin et al. and used for photocatalytic hydrogen production [69]. Results confirmed that the hydrogen evolution rate with the UiO-66/CdS/reduced GO (13.8 mmol.g-1.h-1) is superior to the UiO-66/CdS composite (11.2 mmol.g-1.h-1) under 300 W Xe lamp and using NaSO3/Na2S as sacrificial agent. In this case, the main role of graphene oxide is to increase the transfer efficiency of charge carriers between the UiO-66 and the CdS [69]. The recyclability tests were also conducted on the photocatalyst and showed enhanced photocatalytic activity throughout six successive cycles. However, it is important to mention that photocorrosion of CdS particles is one of the major concerns for the use of this material in photocatalysis [69].  Following the previous report, Shin et al. studied the photocatalytic activity of MoS2/UiO-66/CdS composites under visible light irradiation. High photocatalytic performance was recorded with an evolution of 32.5 mmol.g-1.h-1   of H2 and apparent quantum efficiency (AQE) of 26.5 % at 420 nm. However, results showed inferior recyclability compared to UiO-66/CdS/reduced GO where only four cycles were conducted [68]. Zhang et al. investigated the photocatalytic activity of αNiS-βNiS/CdS linked to UiO-66 hybrid for HER [66]. Remarkable reusability was reported reaching up to 10 successive cycles with negligible decrease in the photocatalytic activity. Electron spin resonance (ESR) measurements confirmed the efficient construction of a Z- scheme heterojunction and a good charge carriers separation [66].  More different UiO-66 composites have been prepared and tested for the photocatalytic evolution of hydrogen. UiOS-CuCdS/ZnS (where UiOS-66 = thiol-UiO-66) [70],
