29  Au@UiOS-66@ZnInS2 [72], Pt@CdS/UiO-66 [114], UiO-66-(COOH)2/MoS2/ZnIn2S4 [115] and Ni2P@UiO-66-NH2/ZnCdS [67] are some of the many examples of UiO-66 composites that have been reported in the literature. Although most of these composites give a relatively high hydrogen evolution as well as a good reusability, sometime the cost of production is very high and the sustainable character low, for example when gold NPs are required, which results in an overall unfavorable system. The use of covalent organic framework (COFs) combined with MOFs such as UiO-66, is another recent strategy that has been explored for a possible increase in the efficiency of the photocatalytic reaction. In particular, Zhang et al. conceived a rational design of a MOF/COF hybrid structure by covalently linking UiO-66-NH2 to the surface of TpPa-COF. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements showed high transfer efficiency of charge carriers compared to the pristine UiO-66-NH2. The TpPa-COF possess a light harvesting ability under visible light irradiation. Once the sample is irradiated with light, the excited electrons of the TpPa-COF are transferred to the HOMO of the UiO-66-NH2 via the covalent junction [73]. This study illustrates the possibility of well-developed systems for photocatalytic activities based on covalent bonding.  Finally, the use of light sensitizers is another effective method to prompt the catalytic activity of MOF-based photocatalysts. Hao et al. proposed the use of EY (5% wt.) with MoS2QDs/UiO-66-NH2-GO for the hydrogen evolution reaction [116]. Despite the multiple parameters that were modified during the reaction, such as the addition of amino group to UiO-66, loading it with CdS quantum dots, and using EY as a photosensitizer, a relatively low hydrogen evolution rate was recorded (0.84 mmol.g-1.h-1) with a high AQE = 40.5 % at 430 nm [116]. This entails the synergetic effect of graphene and MoS2 QDs along with UiO-66-NH2. UiO-66-NH2 has a relatively high surface area (712 m2.g−1) and highly porous
