41  reported on the mechanism of the photocatalytic HER, OER and OWS, reported on MOF structures and their semi-conductor photocatalysts analogues, although data remains much scarcer than the former. It's crucial to emphasize that, as mentioned earlier, the OER poses a greater challenge, making investigations into its mechanism even more arduous. This clarifies why, in the section dedicated to OER, only a limited number of mechanistic studies have been conducted exclusively on semiconductors, with few focusing on MOF-based materials. 3. Mechanism elucidation of photocatalytic OWS using various technical approaches In order to enhance the performance of a reaction process, it is necessary to first have a good understanding on the different steps that conform it. This way, it is possible to identify both the assets and the limitations of the catalyst, in order to design a new generation of materials that can surpass their predecessors. The comprehensive characterizations of the reaction and catalyst, incorporating techniques such as electron transfer studies and spectroscopic analysis, ultimately contribute to the development of a consistent and well-described mechanism. Through these in-depth investigations, researchers can propose a detailed understanding of how the photocatalytic process unfolds and the role of the catalyst in facilitating the desired reactions. This mechanistic understanding is crucial for further advancements in the rational design and optimization of efficient photocatalysts. For this reason, in the area of heterogeneous catalysis, and for most chemical reactions, it is important to identify the mechanism taking place during the reaction and recognize the different steps that lead to the formation of the desired product.  A real comprehension of a mechanism includes not only the identification of the active sites and intermediates that are involved in the reaction, but also their specific role in the overall
============================================================
[Page 43/147]
============================================================
42  process. The understanding of this mechanism gives an insight, at a molecular level, of the reaction, which can be used to improve the efficiency of the system (e.g. conversion and selectivity) [128].  The most common characterization processes involve the study of the catalyst before and after the reaction that took place, which allows to identify the modifications induced in the material by the process itself. However, the conditions of these ex-situ characterizations are far from the real reaction conditions such of temperature, pressure, presence of solvents, etc. Therefore, to truly understand a material during a reaction, it is required to perform in situ and operando characterizations. When performing an in-situ characterization, the material can be found in defined conditions that will give certain enlighten on the behavior of the catalyst during real reaction conditions. Meanwhile, during an operando study, it is possible to analyze the catalyst while the reaction is taking place [129]. This in situ and operando conditions have been adapted for a great number of characterization tools such as EPR, NMR, XAFS, XPS, UV/vis, FT-IR and Raman spectroscopies. Figure 15 presents several characterization techniques that have been employed to unravel reaction mechanisms, specifically focusing on the HER, OER, and OWS reactions. Despite the large number of photocatalysts that have been tested for the HER, OER and OWS reaction, there are only few studies that have focused on the investigation of the mechanism of the reaction.  Even more, from these studies, the one that are dealing with the use of MOFs as photocatalyst are even more scarce. One of the main issues that has limited these studies is the correct incorporation of the irradiation source into an operando or in situ system, without interfering in the final measurement [129].  Different characterization techniques can be used in order to obtain specific evidence on the reaction mechanism[130], notably when their time resolution is compatible with the intrinsic
============================================================
[Page 44/147]
============================================================
43  temporal dimension of the physical phenomena concerned. For example, vibrational spectroscopies can provide information such as the changes taking place on the surface of the catalyst including the formation of specific intermediates, at a very fast rate [131]. . Studying the electron transfer is also crucial to understand the reaction taking place as the photocatalysis process is based on the formation of charge carriers and electron transitions. Identifying radical intermediates, such as reactive oxygen species in oxygen evolution reaction (OER) or hydrogen radicals in hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), is crucial and could be achieved by magnetic spectroscopies like electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR).  Although MOFs are a relatively new type of material, they have been investigated as photocatalysts for several decades. While the understanding of their mechanism reactions is still limited, researchers can draw upon the experimental studies conducted on other photocatalysts to guide their investigations. Additionally, certain behaviors observed in semiconductors, such as metal oxides, can be extended to the metal oxo-clusters present in MOFs. Meanwhile, the ligand to metal charge transfer mechanisms that have been observed on MOFs could be compared to the charge transfer processes observed in molecular catalysis. This approach enables researchers to leverage existing knowledge and make progress in unraveling the mechanisms underlying MOF photocatalysis. The following section introduces a selection of commonly used characterization techniques for studying HER, OER, and OWS. Each technique is explained in detail including the underlying information it aims to convey. Additionally, examples from the literature are provided to demonstrate how these techniques have been utilized to characterize various photocatalysts and extract valuable information for mechanism elucidation.
