44   Figure 15. Common characterization techniques for the elucidation of the photocatalytic OWS, HER, and OER mechanism.   3.1. Vibrational spectroscopies in HER, OER or OWS A vibrational spectroscopy, as its name indicates, is a technique used to measure the vibrational energy of a chemical bond on certain molecules or compounds. The energy of the vibration of a chemical bond will depend on a series of factors such as the mass of the atoms and their spatial distribution, as well as the length and strength of the bond. All these factors result in vibrational frequencies that are distinctive for a specific type of bond, which is greatly advantageous for their experimental identification [132]. The two most important vibrational spectroscopies are infrared (IR) spectroscopy and Raman. However, even if both IR and Raman techniques deal with changes in vibration modes of a structure, there are some fundamental differences between these two spectroscopies. One of the most important distinctions is that a change in the dipole moment of the molecule is required to have an IR active vibration mode, while a change in the polarizability of the molecule is necessary for a
============================================================
[Page 46/147]
============================================================
45  Raman active mode [133]. A more detailed description of both IR and Raman spectroscopies is provided in Figure 16. Figure 16. Difference between infrared (IR) and Raman vibrational spectroscopies in an anharmonic oscillator.   3.1.1. Infrared (IR) spectroscopy The IR spectroscopy is a powerful technique for providing structural information on the molecules and reaction intermediates [134]. By recording the absorption and transmission of the IR radiation of a sample, it is possible to monitor quantitatively the evolution of the reaction species in real time without perturbating the reaction, by correlating the band intensities with the concentration of the species. This allows investigating the reaction mechanism in various catalytic and photocatalytic reaction [130,132,135–138]. Ida et al. investigated the photocatalytic HER mechanism over rhodium doped TiO2 nanosheets using the complementary information that can be obtained from FTIR, NMR and the density functional theory (DFT) calculations. In this study they detected the Rh-H and O-H stretching vibrations after treating the Rh-doped titania nanosheets powder with H2 or deuterium (D2) (Figure 17.a). A resolved peak appeared at 3400 cm-1 which was attributed
============================================================
[Page 47/147]
============================================================
46  to the O-H stretching vibration due to the adsorption of water molecules on the nanosheets. An increase in the intensity of the O-H stretching vibration as well as a new peak at 2016 cm- 1 that was ascribed to Rh-H was observed after the treatment with H2. A shift on these bands was observed to 2500 and 1432 cm-1 for O-H and Rh-H respectively after treatment with D2 (Figure 17.a). This was consistent with the DFT calculations whereby the O-H and Rh-H stretching vibration were anticipated to shift from 3400 to 2473 cm-1, and from 2016 to 1432 cm-1 respectively. Those findings proved that Rh-H bonds exist on the surface of the nanosheet representing an intermediate in the HER mechanism. This was further confirmed by the H-NMR analysis whereby a peak at -8 ppm attributed to the hydride (Rh-H), and it was absent in the non- doped titania nanosheet. Additional FTIR spectra recorded for the Rh doped titania samples before and after UV irradiation, showed that the O-H peak intensity decreased after irradiation while that of Rh-H remained constant (Figure 17.c and d). Thus, it was deduced that the adsorbed water molecules are dissociated, and the Rh-H bonds are formed. As the Rh-H represents the hydride ion, an heterolytic mechanism was proposed as 𝐻!+𝐻""→𝐻# [139].
