48  from FA has garnered considerable interest.  In a recent study, Hamoud et al. [140] were able to study by operando FTIR, simultaneously, the surface of a Zr-based MOF and the gas phase released during the reaction. More specifically, UiO-66(COOH)2 post-metalated with copper (UiO-66(COO)2-Cu) (Figure 18.a) was studied for the photocatalytic FA dehydrogenation under continuous vapor flow and visible-light. Interestingly, high performance (5 mmol.g-1.h-1) and great selectivity (100 % of dehydrogenation), with no CO detection in the gas phase was obtained. Furthermore, the production of equimolar amounts of CO2 and H2 and high stability with no deactivation over 3 reaction days (Figure 18.h and i) were only attained with UiO-66(COO)2-Cu unlike UiO-66-Cu. Results showed that a short induction time is indispensable to reach the steady state revealing a possible restructuring phenomenon that takes place at the surface of the MOF (Figure 18.b-e). The IR analysis of the surface of UiO-66(COO)2-Cu revealed the formation of new bands at 1785, 1798 and 1855 cm-1 during the first minutes of the reaction, which corresponds to the characteristic bands of the anhydride moiety (Figure 18.f). To gain more mechanistic insight of the restructuration from UiO-66-(COOH)2 to UiO-66-(COO)2-Cu and the formation of the anhydride bridge, isotopic labeled H13COOH was used under the same reaction conditions to prove the origin of the resulting products in the gas phase (H2 and CO2). Results showed that the produced CO2 is selectively from H13COOH (Figure 18.g) while no shifts were observed by the surface species, proving that the formation of the anhydride bridge is selectively from the cross coupling of the MOF carboxylates. Nanoclusters of highly stable Cu(I)/Cu(0) active sites of the catalyst in the first cycle was further demonstrated using HR-TEM and XPS techniques. Based on all the above results, a mechanism of the possible restructuring pathway of UiO-66-(COOH)2-Cu in FAc reforming and the formation of the stable Cu(0)/Cu(I) binary system was proposed as shown in Figure 18.j.
