58  In a recent study, in-situ surface enhanced Raman spectroscopy (SERS) was used to investigate the OER mechanism by cobalt oxyhydride (CoOOH) using KOH as an electrolyte [146]. The use of other characterization techniques (XAS, electrochemical) showed that, under OER conditions, the dominant species of the catalyst are Co(IV), which were denoted as CoO2. The in-situ Raman, used for identification of the reaction intermediates, showed the presence of a broad peak in the range of 800 to 1200 cm-1 (max at 1075 cm-1). This peak was attributed to the Co-superoxide species which showed a similar IR band at 1013 cm-1 in spinel Co3O4 for photocatalytic water splitting. 18O-labeling experiments were performed to confirm these results. After electrolysis, the peak observed at 581 cm-1 corresponding to the A1g vibrational mode of CoO2 shifted by 26 cm-1 in comparison to the 16O sample verifying the full exchange with labeled 18O in CoO2. Similarly, the broad peak ascribed to the CoOO- was shifted by 50 cm-1. This exchange, worked as an indication that oxygen of the lattice may be participating in the OER.  In addition, to check if the oxygen atoms of the superoxide can exchange with the oxygen of the electrolyte before or during OER, an experiment consisting in the consecutive exchange for 18O and 16O was performed (Figure 24.a). The results showed that the Raman peaks attributed to the A1g and the Eg of both the OO- and CoO2 were moved to higher frequencies upon OER (Figure 24.b). Thus, both oxygen atoms of the superoxide may exchange with the O atoms of the OH- in the electrolyte. Afterwards, D labelling experiments showed the presence of a peak of the A1g vibrational mode of the CoO2 at 571 cm-1 that was shifted by 10 cm-1 compared to the KOH electrolyte. This was a prove of the presence of OH groups in CoO2 and the exchange of OH with OD in the electrolyte. This shift was attributed to the hydrogen bonding or interaction with water or OH -. This was not the case for the