62  this context, examining both the electronic and optical properties of the photocatalyst is essential. However, due to sample bulkiness and the absence of an open window in certain frequency ranges, which can limit the visibility of distinctive vibrational peaks, IR spectroscopies have not been extensively employed in the study of MOF-based photocatalysts, particularly in the realm of photocatalytic water splitting. As seen in the previous section of this review, our focus shifted to elucidating mechanisms in other catalysts, particularly semiconductors, where FTIR studies are well-established. FTIR is recognized as a crucial technique for identifying reaction mechanisms and intermediates. By addressing this gap in knowledge, we aim to contribute to the improved design of these highly significant photocatalysts. 3.2. Electron spectroscopies 3.2.1. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) is a quantitative characterization technique frequently employed in materials science to study the surface chemistry of a sample (top 10 nm).  XPS consists in the irradiation of a sample with a high energy beam (X-ray) which leads to the emission of photoelectrons from the surface of the material. These electrons will exhibit different energy values depending on the elements they come from, and their chemical environment. By measuring the kinetic energy of the electrons that scaped the material, it is possible to determine their binding energies (BE). These binding energies, as well as the measurement of the number of electrons emitted, are used to identify the oxidation states and abundance of the elements present in the material. The ability to study changes in oxidation states makes of XPS a very powerful technique for the analysis of a great variety of catalysts [148–150].
============================================================
[Page 64/147]
============================================================
63  Some catalytic processes that occurred during a reaction are taking place in the surface of the catalyst, which make surface characterization techniques, such as XPS, of vital importance for the understanding of reaction mechanisms. Favaro et al. [151], for example, used operando ambient-pressure X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (AP-XPS) as the main technique to study the reaction mechanism for OER, taking place at the solid/liquid interface of an electrocatalyst consisting of biphasic Co(OH)2/Co3O4. For this reason, biphasic Co(OH)2/Co3O4 films were prepared via plasma- enhanced atomic layer deposition (PE-ALD). This electrocatalysts consisted of a nanocrystalline spinel Co3O4, covered by a thin layer of disordered Co(OH)2. When analyzing this biphasic film under electrochemical OER conditions, it was possible to observe the conversion of the Co(OH)2 overlayer into  CoO(OH), which was detected to be highly active for the oxygen evolution reaction. This was confirmed by the use of AP-XPS and analyzing the valence band (VB) region, where the 3d band at 2.1 eV corresponding to the presence of Co2+ was reduced. At the same time, a shift in the binding energy for the O 1s core-level of chemisorbed H2O was observed during the reaction. This change from 532.2 to 532.6 eV was attributed to the change of water adsorption from Co(OH)2 to CoO(OH), confirming the structural conversion. Finally, it was also possible to identify the presence of an emerging feature on the BE of Co 2p3/2 and O 1s under OER conditions. The occurrence of this feature was attributed to the presence of highly active Co4+ sites, that are only observed under catalytic conditions [151].  In a similar study, Xiao et al. used quasi-operando XPS to study the effect of the presence of oxygen vacancies in the electrocatalytic activity of Co3O4 for the OER [152]. For this study, a rich oxygen vacancy Co3O4 sample (VO- Co3O4) was prepared by treating a pristine spinel Co3O4 nanosheet with Ar plasma. Several XPS spectra were obtained for the VO- Co3O4 sample increasing the applied potential up to 1.75 V. The deconvolution of the O 1s peaks
============================================================
[Page 65/147]
============================================================
64  showed the presence of 3 signals named O1, O2, and O3. The O1 peak at 530.0 eV was attributed to the oxygen in the metal oxide (Co-O), the O2 signal at 531.0 eV corresponds to the presence of surface chemisorbed oxygen, while the smallest O3 signal between 532.5 and 533.5 eV was designated to adsorbed water and contamination from organic compounds. The authors used the signal ratio O2/O1 as a method to estimate the number of surface vacancies in the sample, with higher ratios representing a larger number of oxygen vacancies. During the experiment, it was observed that the number of oxygen vacancies decreased when the applied potential was increased. The authors proposed that this decrease in surface VO was occurring due to the adsorption of OH ions which were bonding with Co sites and generating active Co-OOH• species during OER.  This proposal was further confirmed by analyzing the Co 2p XPS spectra, which confirmed an increase in the relative percentage of Co-O bond during OER. Therefore, it was concluded that the presence of oxygen vacancies in the VO- Co3O4 sample help to promote the formation of surface Co-OOH sites during the OER which are crucial for a higher production of oxygen[152].  3.2.2. Auger Electron Spectroscopy (AES) Similar to XPS, Auger Electron Spectroscopy (AES) serves as a surface-analysis technique that provides information on the composition and electronic state of the elements at the surface layer of a sample. The depth analysis of AES is typically around 2-5 nm. AES is based on the irradiation of a sample with high-energy electrons (3-20 keV) which causes the removal of core electrons from the atoms in the sample. With the emission of a photoelectron, the atom is left with a core hole that needs to be filled.  As a consequence, the atom suffers a relaxation process, where an electron with a lower binding energy drops into the available position. The relaxation of the atoms results in the emission of another electron, which receives de name of Auger electron. This Auger electron is characteristic of the element that
============================================================
[Page 66/147]
============================================================
65  emitted it, so it is used to identify the composition of the material. The number of transitions possible during the Auger process will depend on the energy level of the core hole, and the selection rules for the possible transitions. A KLL peak, for example, indicates that an electron from the K-shell was ejected, while an electron from the L-shell dropped to the core level, resulting in the ejection of a second electron (the Auger electron) from the L-shell [153,154]. The main advantage of Auger Electron Spectroscopy over XPS is that AES uses an electron beam instead of X-rays. This smaller beam can be easily focus on a specific area of the sample, giving AES a better spatial resolution in comparison to XPS [155]. However, the electron beam may also lead to the charge of the surface of the sample, which results in the loss of resolution and the shift of the peaks in the spectra [153,154].   The use of AES in the study of the OWS, HER, and OER has been limited, and in most cases, it is only used as a complementary technique for XPS. Zhang et al. [156], for example, used AES as a way to confirm the presence of Cu0 in a CuCo/CDs (CD: carbon dots), used for the photocatalytic overall water splitting reaction. In this case, XPS analysis was performed in the sample to study the oxidation state of Cu before and after the photocatalytic reaction. However, due to the similarity in the binding energies of Cu+ and Cu0, the Cu 2p spectrum could not be used to discern between these two oxidation states. Therefore, AES was used as a confirmation technique, where a LMM spectrum showed a peak at 918.6 eV, establishing the presence of Cu0. These results, combined with the photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy analysis, confirmed that the CuCo bimetallic nanoparticles presented surface plasmon resonance (SPR), which can only be observed in zero-valence copper. The proposed mechanism suggested that the excitation of CuCo generated SPR which then decayed to the formation of separate electrons and holes by Landau damping [156].
