66  3.3. Other techniques  3.3.1. UV-visible spectroscopy UV-vis spectroscopy is a widely recognized characterization technique that has been used for many years to study the electronic transitions in both bonding and nonbonding electrons in a great variety of samples [157]. Operando and in-situ UV-vis spectroscopy becomes a very useful technique when studying active species or sites that show electrochromic or photochromic properties. Görlin et al. for example, studied the Ni-Fe(OOH) catalyst redox behavior by performing in situ UV-vis spectro-electrochemistry studies. This catalyst has shown promising results for the electrochemical oxygen evolution reaction (OER). However, it has been reported, that the presence of Fe is required for the activation of NiOOH [158]. In order to have a better understanding on the influence of Fe in Ni-based OER electrocatalyst, Görlin et al. prepared Ni−Fe oxyhydroxide electrocatalysts with different Ni/Fe ratios. In this case, Ni(OOH) presents a strong coloration when applying an OER catalytic potential, which can be followed by UV-vis spectroscopy with the appearance of a broad absorption band above 450 nm. This band is not present in the FeOOH catalyst and is considerably reduced for the Ni-Fe sample. In fact, the Ni45Fe55 sample did not present the potential-induced coloration that was observed for the pure Ni catalyst. These results show that Fe is helping suppress the charge transfer of Ni2+→Ni3+/4+ at the applied catalytic potential. This was confirmed by XAS studies, where a higher fraction of Ni2+ sites were observed in the Ni-Fe catalysts. In other words, the presence of Fe in a Ni-based catalyst alters the absorption of visible light which is related to the oxidation of Ni2+[159].  3.3.2. Photoluminescence (PL) Photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy is often used to study the electronic structure of photocatalyst. The principle of this technique consists in irradiating a sample with light of a
============================================================
[Page 68/147]
============================================================
67  high enough energy to promote the photoexcitation, where an electron is transferred from a ground to an excited state [160]. When the system relaxes and the electron returns to its ground state, the photon that is emitted can be registered resulting in a photoluminescence spectrum. The energy of this emitted photon will be identical to the difference in energy between the two levels that were involved in the transition. In consequence, this technique allows the measurement of the energy difference between HOMO and LUMO levels in a molecular photocatalyst, or in the case of a semiconductor, it can be used to measure the value of a band gap [161]. At the same time, in the case of semiconductors, it is also useful to determine the presence of defects or oxygen vacancies in the sample which can play an important role in OER and OWS reaction [162].  Electrochemical characterization as well as photoluminescence spectroscopy allowed to study the reaction mechanism taking place in various material such as the HER over a CdS-decorated MIL-53(Fe) whose structure is illustrated in Figure 26.a. The reaction was carried out using benzyl alcohol as sacrificial agent [163]. A photocatalytic activity of H2 production equal to 2334 μmol.g-1.h−1, CdS/MIL-53(Fe) was obtained. The higher photocatalytic activity of CdS/MIL-53(Fe) compared to its individual components is attributed to the enhanced efficiency of charge separation. PL spectroscopy reveals that CdS/MIL-53(Fe) exhibits the weakest fluorescence intensity, while the flexible microporous iron(III) terephthalate MIL-53(Fe) shows the strongest fluorescence intensity (Figure 26.a). This indicates that the charge transfer efficiency follows the order of CdS/MIL-53(Fe) > CdS > MIL-53(Fe), which correlates with the observed photocatalytic activity. In addition, when comparing the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) results of CdS/MIL-53(Fe) with single CdS, it is observed that CdS/MIL-53(Fe) shows a smaller arc radius and inferior resistance in charge transport (Figure 26.b). This indicates that the incorporation of CdS particles into the MIL-53(Fe) MOF effectively improves the
============================================================
[Page 69/147]
============================================================
68  efficiency of carrier transport. Based on this information the overall mechanism over CdS/MIL-53(Fe) was proposed as shown in Figure 26.c Figure 26. ( (a) Photoluminescence spectra; (b) photocurrent test; (c) Schematic illustration of the proposed mechanism over CdS/MIL-53(Fe). Color code: Fe, green polyhedra; dicarboxylate linker, yellow bridges. Figure modified from reference [163].  Chen et al. [164] prepared a Calix-3-sensitized Pt@UiO-66-NH2 catalysts that was used for the photocatalytic hydrogen production under visible light irradiation, although this is doubtful here that the Calix moieties is loaded inside the cavities of the microporous MOFs, therefore more lying either at the external surface or lying into mesoporous MOF defects. The study of the reaction mechanism was performed using steady-state photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy under a modified UiO-66 structure. When the Calix/Pt@UiO-66-NH2 was measured and compared to the Calix-3 solid, it was clearly observed that the emission peak was considerably smaller than the free dye, which indicated a decrease in the recombination of excited charge pairs. At the same time, a slight blue shift of 37 nm was observed for the emission peak, which was attributed to interfacial electron transfer from the excited Calix-3* to the UiO-66-NH2 framework. However, to identify the hydrogen precursor, an
