70  of unoccupied states, and chemical bond hybridization, whereas the EXAFS enables the detection in the variation of bond lengths and the coordination environment of atoms or ions.  Tsai et al. used operando EXAFS and XANES measurements to investigate the HER and OER mechanism of alkaline water splitting over a FeCoNi electrocatalyst. The XANES measurements under the HER conditions showed a white line spectrum similar to those of their metal oxides with the increase in the over potential. This indicates that the metals are in the Fe3+, Co2+ and Ni2+ oxidation states.  A great decrease in the number of coordinated oxygen atoms surrounding the Ni and Co atoms was observed. This is attributed to the formation of a bridge between the two metal centers (Co2+–μ-H–Ni3+). This is followed by the reduction and elimination of two adjacent hydrides to give H2.  Combining these results with the anodic shift of Ni2+/Ni3+ couple in the CFeNiP, allowed the authors to suggest that the electron deficient Fe3+ prompts the electrochemical reaction to generate the active sites (Co2+ and Ni2+). It also acts as a Lewis acid in the medium, increasing the proton transfer to the desired active site. Hence, the overall mechanism presented in Figure 28.a was proposed. According to this mechanism, the first step of the reaction consists in the generation of Ni2+ and Co2+ species by the dissociation and one electron reduction in the presence of Fe3+ in the water. The following step is a Volmer step in which a proton gains an electron forming an adsorbed hydrogen atom. The reaction occurring is Co2+Ni2+/Fe3+ + H2O +𝑒""  à Co2+–μH–Ni2+/ Fe3+–OH, followed by the generation of H2. Afterwards, the OER mechanism was also studied from the operando XANES data, it was observed that the oxidation state of the Fe3+ increased from 2.9 to 3.6 upon increasing the potential from 300 to 600 mV.  Also, both Ni2+ and Co2+ acquired two positive shifts at 2.2 and 1.4 eV respectively, whereas the shift observed with Fe3+ was much higher reaching 3.3 eV. Both Co-O and Ni-O encountered a slight decrease in the bond length as inferred from
