71  the EXAFS. However, a dramatic decrease in the Fe-O bond was observed and could be strongly correlated to the increase in its oxidation number of Fe3+. The increase in the oxidation state of Fe3+ as the overpotential rises is attributed to the increase in the number of Fe4+ species. It may be present in the form of iron(IV)-oxo(Fe4+=O/ Fe3-O●) hybrid resonance species which acts as the active intermediate for the OER. The cathodic shift observed with Ni2+/Ni3+ couple indicates that the Ni3+ can be stabilized by the electron-dense Co2+ and as a result the Co-Ni matrix can stabilize the (Fe4+=O/ Fe3-O●) active intermediate. The OER mechanism (Figure 28.a) was proposed whereby the surface (Fe4+=O/ Fe3-O●) resonant species are the key for the formation of the O-O bond either by radical-radical coupling of contiguous (Fe4+=O/ Fe3-O●) motifs (intermediate A) or through the OH- attack on the Fe4+ induced electrophilic center (intermediate C) yielding the proposed bridged/terminal peroxide intermediates Fe3+–(μ-O2)–Fe3+ and Fe3+–OOH. Another proposed pathway leads to the formation of intermediate B (Figure 28.b) by the coupling of neighboring OH- groups. Coordination to Co/Ni removes the weakened proton of the terminal peroxide, transforming the terminal peroxide (Fe3+–OOH) into a bridged peroxide (Fe3+–(μ-O2)–M, M = Co, Ni). The successive proton-coupled electron transfer (PCET) occurs leading to the destabilization of  the bridged peroxide (Fe3+–(μ-O2)–M, M =Fe, Co, Ni), resulting in the evolution of oxygen along with the restoration of Fe3+–OH active sites [165].
