============================================================
[Page 77/147]
============================================================
76  the probe that passes through the sample first. After certain time delay, that can usually go from nanoseconds to femtoseconds, the second pulse, the pump mainly the laser, is sent to the sample, where some of the  electrons are excited  to higher  energy  levels, creating an excited state absorption [166,167]. By changing the time delay between the pump and the probe pulses, it is possible to study the fast changes that are occurring in the system in presence of irradiation [168]. This technique is mainly used in photocatalysis to elucidate the microscopic mechanisms of photogenerated charge carriers, to study the transfer/recombination of electrons and holes, reaction kinetics and to get indirect information of trap states. For instance, Melillo et al. conducted a study on the photocatalytic activity of UiO-66(Zr, Ti, Ce) for overall water splitting. The researchers observed hydrogen (H2) and oxygen (O2) production of 230 µmol.g-1 and 110 µmol.g-1 after 24 h of irradiation, respectively, under UV irradiation. The mechanism of this photocatalytic process was investigated using various techniques, including UV-vis spectroscopy and transient absorption spectroscopy (TAS). In their study, Melillo et al. examined the transient signals recorded at 400 nm in the microsecond time scale upon 266 nm laser excitation under an argon atmosphere. They analyzed five different samples: UiO-66(Zr), UiO-66(Zr/Ti), UiO-66(Zr/Ce), UiO-66(Zr/Ce/Ti), and UiO-66(Ce) with their temporal profiles as shown in Figure 31.a and b. The researchers also conducted quenching experiments using methanol and dichloromethane. The results showed two distinct behaviors in the presence of the quenchers. For the monometallic samples UiO-66(Ce), UiO-66(Zr), and for UiO-66(Zr/Ce/Ti), the transient signals disappeared when exposed to both methanol and dichloromethane. This indicated that the transient signals corresponded to the combined absorption of electrons and holes as illustrated in Figure 31.c and d, being the most efficient the trimetallic MOF. In contrast, the
============================================================
[Page 78/147]
============================================================
77  two bimetallic samples, UiO-66(Zr/Ti) and UiO-66(Zr/Ce), exhibited different behaviors. The transient species in UiO-66(Zr/Ti) were quenched only in the presence of methanol. However, in the presence of dichloromethane, UiO-66(Zr/Ti) showed an increase in the lifetime and intensity of the transient signals. On the other hand, UiO-66(Zr/Ce) showed a moderate quenching of the transient signals but no significant effect on their lifetime. These findings suggested that the main driving factor for these transient signals in UiO-66(Zr/Ti) was the photogenerated holes, which were quenched by methanol, but experienced reduced electron-hole recombination in the presence of dichloromethane, an electron scavenger. The results obtained from time-resolved spectroscopy exhibited good agreement with the generation of the charge-separated state, which decayed within a few microseconds. The decay process showed two kinetics: a fast component completed within 1 microsecond, accounting for 65% of the initial signal, and a slower component spanning a few microseconds (Figure 31.b). Comparing the dynamics of UiO-66(Zr) and UiO-66(Zr/Ti/Ce), no significant difference was observed, as the transient signals and the percentage of the slowest component were similar. Therefore, the authors suggested that the increased efficiency in water splitting observed in the trimetallic sample (UiO-66(Zr/Ti/Ce)) was primarily due to the kinetic factor rather than the thermodynamic considerations of the band gap of the highest occupied crystal orbital (HOCO) and lowest unoccupied crystal orbital (LUCO), as all samples generated oxygen and hydrogen. This indicates that UiO-66(Zr/Ti/Ce) exhibits highly effective charge separation and a lower degree of charge carrier recombination, resulting in a higher density of electrons and holes available for the water splitting reaction [95].
