============================================================
[Page 80/147]
============================================================
79  spectroscopies is that for EPR the magnetic field induces the splitting of degenerate electronic spin states, while NMR concerns the nuclear spin states [169]. Transitions between these new states are possible by irradiating the samples with the appropriate energy which gives rise to the magnetic resonance phenomenon. Consequently, for EPR, resonance frequency is found in the microwave range, while for NMR it is in the region of radio frequencies [134].  3.4.1. Electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) The electron paramagnetic spectroscopy (EPR), also called electron spin resonance (ESR), is a technique used for the characterization of paramagnetic species, that is, species that contain unpaired electrons, such as free radicals, semiconductors, or some transition metal ions [170]. In the case of photocatalysis, EPR helps in the elucidation of mechanisms by identifying the radical intermediates that are involved during the reaction. Thus, EPR is the most used technique to detect and study the evolution of the free radicals as the reaction proceeds. Since the lifetime of radicals is usually very brief, their detection is regularly performed using spin traps that selectively react with a known radical to form a more stable species (adduct) [171]. The formation of a long lived adduct, allows the accumulation of these species in the reaction mixture up to a concentration that can be detected by EPR [172]. The spin traps that are most commonly used are organic nitrone or nitroso compounds which include molecules such as 5-diethoxyphosphoryl-5-methyl-1-pyrroline N-oxide (DEPMPO), 5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO), 2-methyl-2-nitrosopropane (MNP), N-tert-butyl-a-phenylnitrone (PBN), 2-methyl-2-nitrosopropane (MNP), 3,5- dibromo-4-nitrosobenzene sulfonic acid (DBNBS), among others [173]. The strength of the interaction between the spin trap and the radicals will be different depending on the nature of the reacting species which results in the formation of different spectral shapes [174].
============================================================
[Page 81/147]
============================================================
80  Fu et al., for example, used DMPO to study the reaction mechanism for the photocatalytic production of hydrogen using a 2D/2D WO3/g-C3N4 heterojunction [175]. The EPR spectra measured in water showed the presence of DMPO-•OH signals for WO3, but not for g-C3N4 indicating that the holes generated from the graphitic carbon nitride do not have the required potential to obtain •OH species (Figure 32.a). The WO3/g-C3N4 system presented an EPR spectrum similar to the one obtained for WO3, indicating that the holes in the heterojunction are staying in the valence band of the WO3. When the experiment was repeated in presence of methanol as a scavenger, the EPR spectra showed the presence of DMPO-•O2− signals for g-C3N4 and the heterojunction, but not for WO3 (Figure 32.b). In this case, the results suggest that the electrons photogenerated in g-C3N4 have the potential required to reduce O2 into O2•− and that, in the heterojunction, they are staying in the C3N4 and not being transferred to the conduction band of the WO3. Considering these results, it was proposed that the photocatalyst followed a S-scheme charge transfer mechanism where, after irradiation with light, the electrons from the conduction band of the WO3 are recombined with the holes in the valence band of the g-C3N4, leaving the electrons in the conduction band of the g-C3N4, free for the reduction reaction for the photocatalytic H2-production (Figure 32.c).
