90  charge transfer from the amine linker to the Ni sites which probably leads to the formation of EPR-silent Ni(0) species. This behavior is more noticeable when air is used instead of N2 due to the addition of electron acceptors that promote the charge transfer. These results show that the Ni(I) sites in the sample Ni1−S/MOF can accept electrons more efficiently, resulting in a more effective reduction of protons and enhanced photocatalytic activity. In accordance with these results, the mechanism shown in Figure 39.b was proposed. Upon irradiation, the photosensitive amino-functionalized linker absorbs photons followed by the process of charge separation. The holes are located in the amino group according to the N+-centered radical signal in EPR, while the electrons are transferred to the Zr6-oxo-cluster and the Ni(I) center where the proton reduction takes place. Figure 39. (a) EPR spectra of Ni1−S/MOF and Ni1−Sox/MOF in nitrogen  atmosphere; (b) Photocatalytic mechanism proposed for hydrogen evolution over Ni1−S/MOF. [183] Nasalevich et al. also studied the formation of Ti(III) species on Ti-MOF photocatalysts [184]. In this case, a cobaloxime was encapsulated within NH2-MIL-125(Ti) identified as Co@MOF. The mechanistic aspects of hydrogen evolution using this system were determined by EPR spectroscopy. Figure 40.a shows the results obtained for pristine NH2-MIL-125(Ti) before and after 30 min of exposure to light (>420 nm visible light irradiation). A signal at g = 1.94 was observed upon irradiation, corresponding to the formation of
