91  paramagnetic Ti(III) formed by ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT). Meanwhile, the sample Co@MOF, showed a very intense signal at g = 4.09 (Figure 40.b), similar to the reference CoBr2 (Figure 40.c) indicating the presence of residual high-spin Co(II) impurities in the MOF composite. Exposure to light for 22 h for Co@MOF results in a very strong and broad signal which was assigned to the high-spin Co(II) species. These reveled that, in the dark, the Co species in Co@MOF can be found mainly as Co(III) which is a diamagnetic cation and not detectable with EPR. However, when the sample is in contact with light Co(III) is converted to high-spin Co(II). On the basis of the EPR observations, two mechanisms were finally proposed for the pristine MOF (Figure 40.d) and the Co@MOF (Figure 40.e and f). For NH2-MIL-125(Ti), upon irradiation, the linker absorbs a photon creating the charge carriers. After charge separation takes place Ti(III) species are formed due to the electron transfer to the cluster, while the hole can be found at the organic linker. The hole reacts with the triethylamine (TEA) while the reduction takes place in the cluster (Figure 40.d). Nonetheless, when the Co complex is encapsulated in the sample, the electron is rapidly injected into the cobalt species, which results in the reduction of Co(III) to Co(II), as observed in the EPR results. Lastly, the proton reduction takes place in the Co centers, resulting in the formation of molecular hydrogen. A more successful electron transfer between the different components of the composite may explain the higher efficiency in the H2 evolution for the Co@MOF which demonstrated an enhancement of 20-fold superior to that of NH2-MIL-125(Ti) photocatalyst.
