============================================================
[Page 94/147]
============================================================
93  to an external magnetic field, a difference in energy between the previously degenerate nuclear spin states is formed. The transition frequencies between these states are influenced by the electron distribution around the nucleus, which results useful for the identification of the structure of a molecule. These NMR couplings interactions are anisotropic, which means that they depend on the orientation of the sample and the direction of the applied magnetic field. When the sample is found in a solid state, it is necessary to spin it around an axis tilted at an angle of 54.7° from the magnetic field. This is known as the magic-angle spinning (MAS) NMR and is used to decrease some of the anisotropic nuclear interactions and to give more intense and narrow signals [185].  NMR can serve as a complementary technique for studying reaction mechanisms. As of our knowledge, this technique has not yet been employed in the investigation of MOF-based photocatalysts for HER, OER, or OWS. Nevertheless, there are intriguing examples of its potential application in semiconductor research, rendering it highly useful for exploring similar studies on MOF-based materials. For example, Yang et al. [186] used in-situ solid-state NMR, in complementary with EPR,  to investigate the photocatalytic mechanism of water splitting on Pt/TiO2 catalysts where methanol is used as a hole acceptor. 1H magic-angle spinning (MAS) NMR spectrum of TiO2 showed the presence of two types of surface hydroxyl groups; one of them being a bridging hydroxyl (OHB), the second one a terminal hydroxyl (OHT). While most of the Pt is deposited on the OHT species, H2O seems to preferentially adsorb on OHB. When testing the co-adsorption of water and methanol on the catalyst, this technique provide evidence of the formation of OHB–(H2O)n–CH3OH complexes that are in proximity to the Ti-OHT species. At the same time, the use of in-situ 17O MAS NMR techniques combined with EPR, showed that during light irradiation, the hydrated OHB species can trap the photogenerated h+, leading to the formation of
============================================================
[Page 95/147]
============================================================
94  paramagnetic species Ti-O• – → Ti-O2• – → Pt-O2– (Figure 41). These EPR spectroscopy results also revealed that the paramagnetic Pt-O2– species act as oxidizers during the photocatalytic reaction producing CO2 from the absorbed methanol (Figure 41; Process a). In absence of methanol, these Pt-O2- species can interact with either H+ or H2 producing H2O2 or H2O, which reduces the efficiency of the HER (Figure 41; Process b) and confirms the importance of using a hole scavenger during the reaction. Figure 41. Proposed Mechanism for Water Splitting on Pt/TiO2basing on the NMR-EPR results [186] The primary challenges associated with the NMR technique resemble those encountered with EPR. These include the complexity of setting up photocatalytic experiments, ensuring homogeneous irradiation of the photocatalyst, and achieving an acceptable accumulation time for spectra.
