95  4. Combined techniques for mechanism elucidation  In some cases, for the proposal of a full reaction mechanism, more than one characterization technique is used for photocatalytic, HER, OER and OWS. In this respect, each technique provides a piece of the puzzle, supplying complementary information in order to finally obtain a full vision of the picture and the complete elucidation of a mechanism. In this section, we present some studies where complementary techniques are used in order to investigate the mechanism of the photocatalytic reaction and understand each step observed.  Zhang et al., for example, was concerned by the photocatalytic performance of Pt@ NH2-UiO-66@MnOX for OWS. First, EPR and spin trapping reagents were used for the analysis of the radical species in this reaction. DMPO, was used as a spin trap for hydroxyl radicals (OH●) which were found to be intermediate species in the water oxidation process over Pt@NH2UiO-66@MnOx (PUM) (Figure 42.a). The results showed the signal corresponding to the presence of DMPO–●OH adducts was more intense on the sample NH2UiO-66@MnOx (UM) than on the pristine NH2-UiO-66, suggesting that more ●OH radicals are generated when MnOx is present as oxidation cocatalyst (Figure 42.b). A similar trend was observed with the Pt@NH2UiO-66 (PU) sample where the presence of a reduction cocatalyst helps consume the photogenerated electrons preventing the fast recombination of the charge carries and enabling the production of more •OH radicals. However, the highest signal for DMPO-OH adducts was observed with the PUM sample where the heterojunction that is formed between the MOF and the cocatalysts Pt and MnOx NPs can greatly suppress the recombination of charge carriers and enhance the photocatalytic oxidation reaction of water. To better investigate the effect of spatially separated cocatalysts on the high activity of PUM, time resolved photoluminescence spectroscopy (TR-PLS) was used to monitor the
============================================================
[Page 97/147]
============================================================
96  fluorescence decay on the different MOF samples as shown in Figure 42.c. Both PU (τave = 0.413  ns) and UM (τave = 0.245  ns) samples resulted in longer charge carrier lifetimes than pristine NH2-UiO-66. Similarly, the PUM recorded the highest charge carrier lifetime (τave = 0.508 ns) which explains the highest charge separation efficiency in the target reaction. This also implies a longer apparent diffusion distance that facilitates the migrations of electrons and holes toward the interface to promote the photocatalytic reaction. In addition to that, TAS was employed to better understand the electron transfer processes observed in the reaction. A 365 nm pump laser with a white light probe (550-700 nm) to get the TA spectra of NH2-UiO-66 at different probe delays (Figure 42.d). with the corresponding TA kinetics for the different samples (Figure 42.e). By comparing to pristine NH2-UiO-66, all of PU, NH2- UiO-66@Pt (UP), MnOx@NH2-UiO-66 (MU), and UM display an acceleration of TA decay kinetics, which might be credited to the opening of a supplementary channel of charge carriers transfer from the NH2-UiO-66 to cocatalysts. Moreover, in comparison to Pt/MnOx@NH2-UiO-66 (PMU) and NH2-UiO-66@Pt/MnOx (UPM) samples, the PUM sample exhibits the shortest mean relaxation lifetime. This indicates that the approach of loading two different cocatalysts with spatially separated methods is more effective in reducing the recombination of photogenerated electrons and holes. Consequently, the overall mechanism for the OWS was described as follows (Figure 42.f): electrons and holes are generated from the UiO-66-NH2 upon irradiation with visible light then the electrons are transferred to the Pt NPs were the reduction reaction leads to the formation of H2. At the same time, the holes are extracted by the MnOx were oxidation of water takes place generating OH● to produce O2 [96].
