98  Furthermore, the activity of Ti3C2 nanosheets supported on UiO-66-NH2 was studied for the photocatalytic HER and the reaction mechanism was investigating by combining various techniques [187]. The photocatalyst was synthesized by intercalation of the Ti3C2 nanosheets that were then attached to the porous UiO-66-NH2 using the one-pot hydrothermal strategy. The resulting composite named TU10 has a superior photocatalytic activity that is 8 times higher than pristine UiO-66-NH2 (Figure 43.a). In their study different techniques were used to analyze the activity as well as the mechanism of the reaction. EPR measurements (Figure 43.e), DFT calculations, time resolved spectroscopies along with different electrochemical measurements were performed. In this part we will mainly shed the light on the TRS whereby the charge carrier dynamics was studied by steady state PL and TR-PLS. Both pristine UiO-66-NH2 and TU10 showed an emission peak at around 450 nm once excited by a 375 nm however the intensity of the peak is much lower in the TU10 sample (Figure 43.b).  This proves that the recombination of electrons and holes is greatly retained due to their extraction by the Schottky junctions. Furthermore, TRPLS curves were analyzed using a double-exponential function to determine the lifetimes Figure 43.c shows that the short lifetime (τ1-TU10 = 61 ps), long lifetime (τ2-TU10 = 315 ps), and intensity-average lifetime (τave-TU10 = 77 ps) of TU10 were shorter compared to pure UiO-66-NH2 (τ1-UiO = 81 ps, τ2-UiO = 387 ps, τave-UiO = 97 ps). This reduction in photoluminescence lifetime indicates the presence of an efficient non-radiative pathway in TU10. This phenomenon can be attributed to the transfer of photo-generated electrons from UiO-66-NH2 to Ti3C2 nanosheets through the Schottky junction, facilitating spatial separation and transfer of electron-hole pairs as illustrated in Figure 43.f. Furthermore, the stability of the catalyst was assessed and it was proved to be stable during three successive cycles (Figure 43.d).
