103  In the last few decades, the use of computational simulation techniques (density functional theory, Monte Carlo, and molecular dynamics) for the understanding and prediction of physical and chemical properties in material science has gained a lot of interest in the scientific community. In the case of photocatalytic systems, density functional theory (DFT) raises as the most commonly used theoretical calculation method [192]. This quantum mechanical tool has already been widely used to study the ground and excited state of several photocatalysts allowing to calculate precise values for ionization energies, band gaps, band edge-levels, and optical absorption  [193].  Even more, computational methods could also be used to forecast reactions pathways, hence, helping in the elucidation of photocatalytic reaction mechanisms. Different computational and modeling methods can be used to predict preferred adsorption geometries, adsorption strengths, and bonding energies, which are of great interest in the understanding of a mechanism. Zhang et al., for example, constructed two different models for DFT calculations in order to understand the difference in photocatalytic performance between Co3O4 and P-doped Co3O4 (P@Co3O4) [194]. These two photocatalysts were obtained through the calcination of a ZIF-67 precursor. In the case of the P-doped catalyst, a second calcination was performed in the presence of NaH2PO2 to finally obtain the P@Co3O4 material.  Figure 45.a-d show the electrostatic potential (ESP) calculated for the intermediate layer of both Co3O4 and P@Co3O4. These results showed that the inclusion of P in the material, by partially substituting the O atoms, destroys the symmetry of the original crystal plane, resulting in an aperiodic distribution of the ESP. A more negative ESP could also be observed. All these promotes de bridging of isolated electronic states, as well as the formation of sites for the adsorption and activation of water molecules. At the same time, Figure 45.e and f show the total density of states (DOS) calculated for Co3O4 and P@Co3O4. In these
============================================================
[Page 105/147]
============================================================
104  figures, it can be observed that the bandwidth for the P@Co3O4 is greatly reduced. Simultaneously, the phosphorus incorporation can result in the delocalization of electrons and the acceleration of charge transfer in the photocatalyst. These two factors contribute to a better transfer of electrons to the substrate and, therefore, to a higher hydrogen production. Considering these computational modeling results, it was proposed by the authors, that the reaction follows a molecular synergetic reaction mechanism (Figure 45.g). In this mechanism, the dye molecule EY acts as an electron transporter, where it passes through a series of excited states (EY → EY1* → EY3*), to finally form a reactive free radical (EY–•) which transfer electrons to the CB of the photocatalyst. Meanwhile, in the photocatalyst, a lone pair of electrons in the P 3p orbital absorbs a proton form a molecule of water, creating a transition state with the adsorbed H2O (I).  The electrons from the O-H bond are then transferred to the oxygen atom. Hydroxyl anions are released, and a protonated transition state is formed (II). Finally, electrons are transfer to the reactive site by the EY molecule, resulting in the coupling of two protons and the formation of a H2 molecule (III). In a similar study, Sun et al. used DFT calculations to perform a mechanistical study on a 2D nickel, iron-MOF array (NiFe-MOF), which was used as an electrocatalyst for water splitting [195]. These calculations allowed to reveal the active sites of the electrocatalyst, where the presence of Ni and Fe sites create a strong synergetic effect that can bring down the free energy barrier of the hydroxyl dissociation step in the OER, as well as determining the limiting steps for the OER (adsorption of the OH– ions on the surface of the NiFe-MOF) and the HER (H* desorption).
