106  proposed, DFT calculations were used to confirm its feasibility [109]. Figure 46 shows the calculated energy change along the reaction coordinate for the photocatalyst. According to these results, the rate-determining step of the reaction is the formation of the TiIV-OH species. The energy to overcome this barrier is provided by the electron transfer of the reduced Ir-photosensitizer. The final hydrogen formation step was shown to be energetically downhill. The total energy change of the full reaction was calculated to be equal to -0.21 eV, which is in good agreement with the experimental value of 0.18 eV. This way, the computational analysis helped to confirm the experimentally proposed mechanism [109]. Similar to this example, many other author have combined the use of experimental and computational techniques to elucidate the mechanism for the hydrogen evolution reaction [144,145,187,196]. Figure 46. DFT calculated energy changes [109].   The analysis performed by Ayala et al. for the photocatalyst COK-47(Ti) (with Pt used as co-catalyst) is another clear example on how the experimental and computational modeling results can be used to complement each other and reveal the final HER mechanism [105]. In this case, DFT calculations were used to study the electronic structure of COK-47. The band structure analyses showed the presence of an indirect band gap with a value equal to 2.19 eV which is comparable to the value of 2.85 eV obtained through experimental tests. Partial
============================================================
[Page 108/147]
============================================================
107  projection of total density states (DOS) for the individual atoms were also obtained (Figure 47.a). In this case, it was revealed that the sample presents two types of carbons, one corresponding to those on the benzene rings and the other one corresponding to the -COO species of the ligand. The C atoms from the rings contribute to the valence band maximum and are responsible for the excitation of electrons under irradiation. This partial DOS results also showed that many different O states from the TiO6 octahedra can be located at deeper energies in the valence band. With these results, it was proposed that the material presents a dual-excitation mechanism, where visible light excites electrons at the biphenyl ligand, transferring them to the Ti-SBU, while UV-illumination is needed to excite electrons from deeper in the valence band, as those observed for the O atoms. Therefore, the electrons and holes formed with UV-light will be located at the inorganic SBU, where a faster recombination can occur. In order to confirm this proposed dual-excitation mechanism, •OH-trapping experiments were performed. In this experiment, •OH radicals are produced during the reaction, and can be monitored by using terephthalic acid (BTC). The BTC will trap the radicals forming a fluorescent hydroxyl terephthalic acid that can be followed by PL spectroscopy (Photoluminescence Spectroscopy). When a sacrificial agent, such as methanol or TEOA, is added to the reaction, the formation of •OH radicals is hindered and a quenching in the PL signal can be observed. Figure 47.b shows that under visible irradiation the PL quenching is similar for both sacrificial reagents (methanol and TEOA). However, under UV-irradiation, the quenching of the signal is more pronounced for methanol than for the TEOA (Figure 47.c). A possible explanation is that under visible light, the photoactive species are generated in the ligand, where the electron is transferred to the Ti-SBU. The hole remains in the ligand where it can react with both methanol and TEOA. However, when UV-illumination is used, the electron and the hole are generated and located in the TiO6-SBU.
============================================================
[Page 109/147]
