109  At the same time, it has been proposed that the use computational modeling through the use of machine learning, could allow the design and prediction of new photocatalytic materials and MOF structures that can present higher efficiencies in the water splitting reaction [193]. Chong et al., for example, performed deep and detailed DFT calculations and computational analysis on the viability and potential performance of phosphorus-doped ZnIn2S4 (P-ZIS) on the photocatalytic water splitting reaction [197]. This approach is quickly becoming a reality and could present a turning point in the way photocatalysts are being designed. A fast-theoretical screening of promising photocatalytic materials could help accelerate the finding of more efficient and active MOFs that is so crucially required. DFT studies have indeed encounter significant advancements in the recent years, leading to a deeper understanding of the reaction mechanisms by investigating electronically excited-states (EESs) known as time dependent-DFT (TD-DFT). In 1984, Runge & Gross set the foundations of modern TDDFT deriving a Hohenberg-Kohn–like theorem for the time-dependent Schrödinger equation[198]. This theory involved calculating photo-absorption spectra, or more broadly, analyzing the interaction between electromagnetic fields and matter. Consequently, given that the photocatalytic reactions involve complex time scale processes it is then beneficial employ this kind of calculations to better understand the different steps of the photocatalytic reaction including light absorption, charge transfer, surface reactions and the recombination of charge carriers. For instance, Wu et al. used this technique to investigate the ligand to metal charge transfer (LMCT) of the Ce- based MOFs. Their  study was based on the  doping of the Ce6O4(OH)4 node with  Zr or Ti to decrease the photon absorption energy (Eabs)of this system which is described as the energy change upon exciting an electron from the highest occupied crystal orbital (HOCO) to the lowest unoccupied 4f crystal orbital (LUCO) in the UiO-66-Ce-based frameworks.[199]. This absorption energy is then corelated to the energy
============================================================
[Page 111/147]
============================================================
110  of the ligand to metal charge transfer (ELMCT) and Eg which is the HOCO–LUCO gap as follows: Eabs = Eg - ELMCT. . Results showed that the ELMCT of  UiO-Ce-Ti and the UiO-66-Ce-Zr increased to -0.55 and -0.77 respectively compared to that of pristine UiO-66-Ce of value -1.4 eV. Furthermore, ligand functionalization was also explored as an approach to tune the ELMCT for promoting the visible light absorption capability of the catalyst for the OWS reaction. Results showed that the ELMCT values of all functionalized Ce-UiO-66 samples are negative, yet they exhibit variations when compared to the ELMCT of unfunctionalized Ce-UiO-66. UiO-66 Ce-(Ti4)-I, reported to be the most promising visible-active photocatalyst for water splitting using a single photocatalyst based on the above calculations. In another study, Zhong and his research group investigated the role of Pt in Pt/20%-MIL-125-(SCH3)2   with (-SCH3) being the methylthio functional group on the terephthalic acid linker [H2BDC- (SCH3)2], for the photocatalytic H2 evolution from water. The activity of Pt/20%-MIL-125-(SCH3)2 was proved to be100 times higher than that of pristine MIL-125-(SCH3)2. TD-DFT calculations were employed for the understanding of the properties of the composite structure, particularly the electron transfer within the composite system at the interface and the excited state properties [200]. Results demonstrated that the charge transfer occurs from the cluster to the MOF surface through the new Pt-O bonds formed upon the adsorption. In addition, the Bader charge analyses that is used to quantify the amount of charge transferred across the interface gave a value of a net charge of + 0.2 e on the Pt13 cluster. Consequently, partial positive charges accumulate on the metal cluster, facilitating the effective separation of photogenerated electrons and holes. After that the process of light absorption in the bulk of the system was then studied by TD-DFT. Results showed that the vertical excitation wavelength for the first excited state is 465 nm, agrees with experimental value of 420 nm. Furthermore, the process of the ligand-to-metal charge
