[Page 112/147]
============================================================
111  transfer (LMCT) was also investigated. As the electron density of HOCO was distributed mainly on the HBDC-(SCH3)2 unit, while the LUCO orbital was mainly on the Ti atoms, under irradiation at 420 nm the charge will then be transferred from the ligand (HBDC-(SCH3)2 unit) to Ti atoms that will be further reduced. Ultimately, through the utilization of DFT and TD-DFT, incorporating the Pt13 cluster into 20%-MIL-125-(SCH3)2 proved to significantly alter its electronic properties, consequently enhancing photocatalytic efficiency. After all what has been debated, it’s clear that by integrating standard DFT, which provides insights into ground state electronic properties, with TD-DFT, which elucidates excited state electronic structure, photoexcitation processes, and electron transfer mechanisms, we can expedite and enhance the development of rational photocatalysts for efficient OWS , HER and/or OER photocatalytic reactions.  However this density functional theory (DFT) modeling of photocatalysts still faces some challenges in accurately describing electronic properties, including band gaps and charge transfer dynamics, crucial for understanding photocatalytic mechanisms. Modeling surface reactions and interfaces, along with the accurate prediction of adsorption energies and reaction pathways, are key challenges due to the complexity of surface interactions and the involvement of solvent effects. Additionally, the computational demands associated with simulating large-scale systems and long timescales pose significant challenges. 6. Impact of structure’s stability and purity of the MOF-based photocatalysts As discussed above, the potential of MOFs in photocatalysis, in general, and more specifically, in photocatalytic hydrogen production, is therefore proven. Nevertheless, one must remain very cautious when interpreting the data, and in particular in elucidating any mechanistic insights. Indeed, the stability and the purity (or the exact chemical composition)
============================================================
[Page 113/147]
============================================================
112  of the MOF in play, are two major points that need to be thoroughly considered all over the catalytic study.  In general, the chemical stability of MOFs is not an absolute measurement but it is to be assessed in respect to the conditions and constraints in play for the regarded application [201]. If a considerable progress has been made to achieve highly chemically stable MOFs in water and aqueous media, the photo(chemical)stability of MOF, in particular under irradiation, can still be detrimental. This is for instance the case of some carboxylate-based MOFs (i.e., due to photodecarboxylation). In contrast, azolate-based MOFs seems to be more photo-resistant [62] which could be very useful for the design of durable MOF-based photocatalysts. This being said, the presence of water (which absorbs the UV light) is expected to significantly attenuate the effect of UV irradiation on MOFs.  It is, thus, important to keep monitoring the structural integrity of the MOF over the catalytic reaction. In some cases, the lack of stability can be quick and obvious (leaching, amorphization, etc.), although the partially degraded MOF, sometimes passivated, and/or the degradation product might still be active and stable over time under operation such as commonly observed for materials in electrocatalysis. However, in others, the kinetics of degradation can be very slow and not easily detected. This may lead to misinterpretation of the results where, for instance, oxides or defective sites could appear and become at the origin of the activity, instead of the MOF with its initial structure. It is very common to rely on PXRD to assess the stability of MOF when comparing the patterns before and after the catalytic tests. However, relying on a sole characterization technique is not sufficient. In this typical scenario, partial amorphization/degradation cannot be easily detected. Hence, it is recommended to combine different techniques (e.g., PXRD, chemical analysis, solid state
============================================================
[Page 114/147]
============================================================
113  NMR, electron microscopy, IR spectroscopy, gas sorption, etc.) to carefully evaluate the microscopic and macroscopic evolutions. Besides, knowing the exact chemical composition, is also extremely important in order to properly assign a given property/activity to the right functional group/site. For instance, it is well known that for some MOFs, defective sites (i.e., absence of ligands leading to potential open metal site/Lewis acids) can occur and their rate may vary from a preparation to another, while one can also rationally promote them on purpose [94]. Thus, well identifying the defective sites is a key aspect. In addition to their possible direct role in catalysis, these defective sites can also serve as anchoring spots for active metal ion sites with a close contact with the inorganic building unit for enhanced catalytic activities [202,203].  Careful spotting the presence of organic modulator (e.g., acetates, formates, etc.) as well as any residual organic solvents, coordinated or not, is also of high importance, particularly for Ti or Zr-MOFs that often contain, grafted on their constitutive oxo-clusters, a significant amount of these inhibitors. This is specifically true for water photolysis where these organic molecules, or sometimes inorganic impurities, can serve as sacrificial agents promoting partial reactions and leading to over- or misinterpretation of the activity. It may lead, for instance, to assigning an HER photocatalyst as that of OWS, due to the inherent presence of sacrificial agent instead of the sole reactant itself. It is however to be noted, that the presence of some modulators can be very beneficial when leading to create specific imprinted sites after their removal. This is, for example, the case of formates in the photoactive Ti-MOF, MIP-177, that has led to selectively achieve the dehydrogenation reaction (over dehydration) of formic acid to produce green H2 [60]. Overall, it is of extreme importance to carefully assess the chemical composition and the structural features of MOF based catalysts, before, during and after the catalytic studies.
============================================================
[Page 115/147]
============================================================
114   7. Conclusions and Perspectives  In this review, we analyzed different photocatalysts used for the water splitting reaction for the production of hydrogen and oxygen and their reaction mechanisms. Slightly higher emphasis was given to the new and promising hybrid porous materials, the so-called MOFs. An overview of the evolution through the years of different photocatalysts for the HER, OER, and OWS reaction was presented, starting with conventional semiconductors, passing to supramolecular systems, to finally arrive to the more recent MOF-photocatalysts. Afterwards, some examples of the most efficient MOFs for each photocatalytic reaction were analyzed. Subsequently, a small description was presented of some of the most common experimental techniques used for the elucidation of reaction mechanisms in a great variety of photocatalysts. These explanations were accompanied with some examples from the literature, illustrating the use of these techniques for the understanding of specific parts of the reaction. The importance of complementing these techniques with each other for a wider view of the reaction mechanism was also emphasized. Similarly, the use of computational modeling in tandem with the experimental results was explored. Finally, some critical points related to the use of MOFs in photocatalysis, such as the concept of stability, was also discussed.  All this thorough study shows how MOFs used for photocatalytic applications are still in an early development stage when compared to their more conventionally used counterpart, the semiconductors. While this relative newness is encouraging, because it means that we have not yet reached the peak of their potential and that there is a lot of space for improvement, it also implies that there is still a lot we don’t understand of their behavior as photocatalysts.
============================================================
[Page 116/147]
============================================================
115  However, this review also exhibited that, despite the more extensive research that has been done on more well-established photocatalyst such as metal oxide semiconductors or perovskites, our comprehension of their reaction mechanisms is still far from being complete. This highlights the importance of continuing the search of photocatalytic materials, and the study of their reaction mechanisms as an attempt to broaden our understanding of the OWS reaction.  The following list presents some of the parameters that we consider could be taken into consideration for the improvement of the study of, not only MOF photocatalyst, but any other photocatalytic material that is being tested in the water splitting reaction, and any other photocatalytic process.  1. In order to achieve this higher understanding of the materials, it is required to comprehend how the reaction is taking place and what is the role of each component that is participating in the reaction. For this reason, more mechanistical studies should be included when developing a new photocatalyst. The proposal of reaction mechanisms, based on specific studies for their elucidation should be encouraged. While some information can be found in the literature, in many other cases, evidence provided results insufficient for a deeper understanding of the reaction. This is particularly noticeable for MOF-photocatalyst when compared to the studies performed with semiconductors.  2. One of the most important factors that limits the activity of the OWS reaction, is the sluggish kinetic that it presents. Further studies need to be performed to better understand the origin of this sluggish behavior. In-situ and operando techniques are essential for this target as they allow to explore the material in conditions more similar to the once observed during the reaction. The number of studies that use in-situ and operando techniques, especially those focusing on vibrational spectroscopies, are very limited. However, more studies using these
============================================================
[Page 117/147]
============================================================
116  techniques are necessary to identify the weaknesses in the photocatalysts and in consequence find solutions to improve them. Therefore, the use of these studies needs to be encouraged in order to create a larger database that can be used for a wider range of materials.  3. At the same time, the analysis in this review evidenced the need for the standardization of the photocatalytic studies as well as the reporting of the results. Many different variables can be found when comparing the different results obtained by different research groups. Differences in concentration, irradiation source (wavelength, radiant flux, etc.), temperature and irradiation time, are some aspects of the reaction that will affect the results obtained. As a result, the compilation and comparison of results become more complicated, and hinder the analysis of the data. At the same time, it becomes more problematic to find trends on the behavior of different materials that could be useful to identify structural properties that can be crucial for the development of more efficient photocatalyst.  4. Computational modeling is a very powerful technique that should be used with consciousness to make the most of the information that can be extracted from it. The cooperative use of experimental techniques with theoretical calculation could be of great use in the elucidation of reaction mechanism. While the experimental characterizations help in the mechanistical study by identifying the different steps or intermediates of the reaction, the computational calculations (DFT, Monte Carlo, and molecular dynamics) can help confirm the feasibility of the proposed mechanism, as well as calculate some other parameters such as bond energies, adsorption configurations and rate determining steps. As it was discussed in the text, some research groups already use the combination of these two tools, however these studies are still in the minority. Nevertheless, modeling surface reactions and interfaces, alongside accurately predicting adsorption energies and reaction pathways, are key
============================================================
[Page 118/147]
============================================================
117  challenges owing to the intricate nature of surface interactions and the influence of solvent effects. 5. The stability of the MOFs, especially when in constant contact with water and light irradiation, is also an important aspect that needs to be examined in close attention. This aspect has limited the widespread of MOFs as photocatalysts as they would not be able to be used in consecutive reaction cycles. However, some MOFs are starting to be reported that can withstand long reaction conditions, showing that it is possible to find ideal MOFs for photocatalytic applications. The development of more stable MOFs is one of the focal points for many research groups. Many new and more stable MOFs are being reported each year.  As it can be seen, there are still many factors that need to be taken into consideration to improve the photocatalytic use of semiconductors and MOFs in the water splitting reaction for the efficient production of hydrogen. Even more work needs to be done in order to have better understanding of the mechanism taking place for this reaction. However, we are hopeful that in the next few years, more mechanistical studies could be presented in the literature and that they could be of great use for the future development of more efficient photocatalysts.  Acknowledgment Authors acknowledge the ANR (MOFtoH2 project - ANR-21-CE50-0022) and European Union under Horizon 2020 research and innovation programmes under the GA 101022649 (METHASOL) and the GA 101084131 (MOF2H2) for the financial support.
