RESEARCH ARTICLE www.small-journal.com AcceleratingtheHydrogenEvolutionKineticswithaPulsed Laser–SynthesizedPlatinumNanocluster–Decorated Nitrogen-DopedCarbonElectrocatalystforAlkaline SeawaterElectrolysis VelusamyMaheskumar,AhreumMin,AnujKumar,RajaArumugamSenthil, CheolJooMoon,andMyongYongChoi* Eﬃcientanddurableelectrocatalystsforthehydrogenevolutionreaction (HER)inalkalineseawaterenvironmentsareessentialforsustainable hydrogenproduction.Zeoliticimidazolateframework-8(ZIF-8)issynthesized throughpulsedlaserablationinliquid,followedbypyrolysis,producing N-dopedporouscarbon(NC).NCmatrixservesasaself-template,enabling Ptnanoclusterdecoration(NC-Pt)viapulsedlaserirradiationinliquid.NC-Pt exhibitsalargesurfacearea,porousstructure,highconductivity,N-rich carbon,abundantactivesites,lowPtcontent,andastrongNC-Ptinteraction. ThesepropertiesenhanceeﬃcientmasstransportduringtheHER. Remarkably,theoptimizedNC-Pt-4catalystachieveslowHERoverpotentials of52,57,and53mVtoattain10mAcm−2inalkaline,alkalineseawater,and simulatedseawater,surpassingcommercialPt/Ccatalysts.Inatwo-electrode systemwithNC-Pt-4( −)ǀǀIrO2(+)ascathodeandanode,itdemonstrates excellentdirectseawaterelectrolysisperformance,withalowcellvoltageof 1.63mVtoattain10mAcm−2andremarkablestability.Thisstudypresentsa rapidandeﬃcientmethodforfabricatingcost-eﬀectiveandhighlyeﬀective electrocatalystsforhydrogenproductioninalkalineandalkalineseawater environments. V.Maheskumar,R.A.Senthil,M.Y.Choi DepartmentofChemistry(BK21FOUR) ResearchInstituteofAdvancedChemistry GyeongsangNationalUniversity Jinju52828,RepublicofKorea E-mail:mychoi@gnu.ac.kr A.Min,C.J.Moon,M.Y.Choi Core-FacilityCenterforPhotochemistry&Nanomaterials GyeongsangNationalUniversity Jinju52828,RepublicofKorea A.Kumar Nano-TechnologyResearchLaboratory DepartmentofChemistry GLAUniversity Mathura,UttarPradesh281406,India TheORCIDidentiﬁcationnumber(s)fortheauthor(s)ofthisarticle canbefoundunder https://doi.org/10.1002/smll.202403314 DOI:10.1002/smll.2024033141.Introduction In recent years, energy crises and envi- ronmental issues have risen dramatically, primarily owing to the exhaustion of fos- sil fuel resources and the escalating im- pacts of climate change, creating an ur- gent need for clean and renewable en- ergy solutions.[1–3]Green hydrogen en- ergy resources are regarded as viable clean energy carriers owing to their environ- mentally friendly nature, large gravimet- ric energy density, absence of CO2emis- sions,andabundantstoragecapabilities.[4,5] Among the various approaches for hydro- genproduction,electrocatalyticwatersplit- ting stands out as a highly eﬀective and sustainable technique for producing high- purity clean hydrogen on a large scale, with numerous advantages, including low cost, high eﬃciency, absence of carbon emission, and ecological responsibility.[6–8] However, thus far, high-eﬃciency catalysts have been primarily utilized along with high-purity water in alkaline electrolytic cells for small-scale hydrogen production applications, posing limitations in terms of freshwater availability for large-scale industrial water splitting.[9–12]As a result, there is an urgent demand for large- scalehydrogenproductionstrategiesemployingearth-abundant and long-term sustainable alternatives. Seawater, constituting 96.5% of the world’s natural water resources, emerges as a promisingalternativetohigh-purityfreshwaterforhigh-capacity hydrogen production; while, also enhancing the ionic conduc- tivity of electrolytes.[13–15]However, the electrolysis of seawater presents signiﬁcant challenges, particularly due to electrode corrosion facilitated by the presence of chloride ions. This corrosion severely compromises the electrocatalytic eﬃciency and durability, necessitating the development of robust and corrosion-resistantelectrocatalystsforseawaterelectrolysis.[16,17] Consequently, the design of high-performance, stable electro- catalysts that exhibit both alkaline and corrosion resistance for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) remains a formidable challenge. Small2024,20,2403314 ©2024Wiley-VCHGmbH 2403314(1of14)
