www.advancedsciencenews.com www.small-journal.com Noble metal catalysts, speciﬁcally platinum (Pt)-based elec- trocatalysts, continue to demonstrate exceptional catalytic performance, with Pt representing the most eﬃcient metal owingtoitshighintrinsiccatalyticactivityandexcellentreaction kinetics.Thissuperiorityisattributedtoitsimproveddesorption andadsorptionGibbsfreeenergyduringintermediatereactions (ΔGH*) of the HER process.[18,19]Further, Pt-based catalysts excel in acidic electrolyte conditions for HER owing to their highly beneﬁcial reaction kinetics.[20]Nevertheless, Pt exhibits unsatisfactoryperformanceunderalkalineconditionsbecauseof slowreactionkineticsandtheextraenergyrequiredtoovercome theO ─Hbondcleavageobstaclewithinwatermolecules,which limits its potential for a wide range of applications.[21,22]Fur- ther, the high expense and poor durability of Pt-based catalysts restrict the possibilities for large-scale hydrogen production. Hence,themostimportantapproachliesindevelopingPt-based catalysts by incorporating minimal amounts of Pt with robust and inexpensive supporting materials.[23–25]These strategies reducethecostofthecatalystandimproveitscatalyticeﬃciency, stability, and suitability for alkaline seawater conditions, mak- ing it suitable for large-scale hydrogen production. In recent decades,extensiveresearchhasfocusedonPt-basedcatalystsfor HER, speciﬁcally Pt nanoclusters and nanoparticles supported on various support materials, including metal sulﬁde, metal carbide, metal hydroxide, metal oxide, metal phosphide, and diﬀerent kinds of carbon.[26–29]Among these, N-doped carbon (NC)-based materials obtained from metal–organic frameworks (MOFs)havedemonstratedhighlyeﬃcientcatalyticperformance for HER. MOFs, constructed using organic linkers and metal cations, have garnered signiﬁcant attention owing to their large surface area and ability to produce nanomaterials with pre- cisely controlled electronic structures for utilization in various ﬁelds.[30,31]AmongthevarioustypesofMOFs,zeoliticimidazo- late frameworks (ZIFs) have captured much interest in recent years. ZIF-8isasubclassofMOFsgeneratedbycombiningZnmetal cations and imidazolate organic linkers. ZIF-8 oﬀers numer- ous advantages, including a large speciﬁc surface area, excep- tional porosity, enhanced N content, an ordered porous struc- ture, and the ﬂexibility to tailor its structure and composition. The N-doped carbon (NC) matrix with high electrical conduc- tivity and good mechanical stability can be obtained from ZIF- 8throughahigh-temperaturepyrolysisprocess.[32,33]Numerous nanoparticles,nanoclusters,andsingleatoms(includingPtnan- oclusters or nanoparticles) have been incorporated into this NC matrix generated from ZIF-8, exhibiting beneﬁcial HER perfor- mance.Forexample,Chenetal.utilizedPtnanoclusterstodec- orate the surface of NC matrix, resulting in superior electrocat- alyticHERperformanceandenhancedstability.Thissuccesscan beattributedtothepresenceofporousNCmatrix,encapsulated Ptnanoclusters,andconductivecarbonnanotubearrays.[34]This designed catalyst achieved a small overpotential of 27.42 mV to reach 10 mA cm−2, surpassing commercial Pt/C. Likewise, Mingyueetal.presentedtheirﬁndingsontheuseofporousNC to support bimetallic alloy PtNi nanoparticles, which served as anextremelyeﬀectiveHERcatalyst.Thiscatalystattainedanul- tralow overpotential of 27 mV at 10 mA cm−2and had a very small Tafel slope of 17.7 mV dec−1under acidic conditions, at- tributed to the promotion by the porous NC and the alloy syn-ergybetweenPtandNi.[35]However,thepreparationofNCfrom ZIF-8 with decorated nanoparticles or nanoclusters remained a formidable challenge. Typically, traditional chemical synthe- sis methods such as hydrothermal, coprecipitation, and sol–gel techniques have been widely employed to fabricate the ZIF-8 framework.[36–38]However, these methods are hindered by sev- eral limitations, including the generation of hazardous byprod- ucts, expensive precursors, time-consuming processes, and the needforsurfactantstocontroltheshapeofthedesiredproducts. To address these challenges, the pulsed laser technique had re- cently emerged as a groundbreaking approach, oﬀering numer- ous advantages such as rapid one-pot synthesis, improved time eﬃciency, elimination of toxic byproducts, and obviation of the needforsurfactants.[39–41]Ourgrouphadleveragedpulsedlaser methods to engineer nanostructures tailored for environmental andenergyapplications,culminatingintherecentdevelopment of MOF structures.[42–44]For instance, we conducted a study on the Ir-doped Co3O4@NC matrix derived from ZIF-67 utilizing pulsedlaserablationinliquid(PLAL).Thismatrixexhibitedex- cellent bifunctional catalytic eﬃciency owing to the synergistic interaction among Ir, Co3O4, and the NC matrix, as well as its high electrical conductivity and large surface area.[45]Neverthe- less,limitedstudieshavereportedonthesynthesisofZIF-based structures using the PLAL approach. Moreover, the majority of previous studies have utilized chemical reduction methods for decoratingPtnanoclustersoncarbonsubstrates,employingcap- ping and reducing agents.[46–49]The utilization of these agents can inhibit surface-active sites during the electrochemical reac- tions, leading to decreased electrocatalytic activity. To avoid this issue, we employed the pulsed laser irradiation in liquid (PLIL) approach to decorate Pt nanoclusters on the NC matrix without requiringreducingandcappingagents.Speciﬁcally,thereareno existing studies on the synthesis of Pt nanoclusters decorating NCusingthepulsedlasertechnique.Inspiredbytheaforemen- tionedpoints,weaimedtofabricatePtnanoclustersonthesur- faceofNCusingaPLILtechniqueandtoexploretheirelectrocat- alyticactivityinseawaterHERperformance. Herein, we developed a self-supported NC matrix decorated with Pt nanoclusters through a simple and rapid pulsed laser technique, followed by a pyrolysis process. The resulting mate- rialexhibitedexcellentelectrocatalyticperformanceforHERand seawaterelectrolysis.ThePtnanoclusterswereintegratedbyim- mersingthemontotheNCmatrixthroughaPLILprocess,ensur- ingtheirhomogeneousdispersiononthesurfaceoftheNCma- trix,ﬁrmlydecoratingduringtheensuingreductionprocess.The discoveredcatalystsexhibitedanexcellentelectrocatalyticperfor- mancecomparedtothePt/Cinbothalkaline(1mKOH)andalka- lineseawater(1mKOHwithseawater)electrolyteenvironments. Impressively, the NC-Pt-4 catalyst required ultralow overpoten- tialsof52and57mVtoattaincurrentdensitiesat10mAcm−2 withoutstandingstabilityinalkalineandalkalineseawaterelec- trolytes.ThisoutstandingHERperformancecouldbeattributed tothedecorationofrobustPtnanoclustersontothehighlyporous NCmatrix.Whenassembledinatwo-electrodesystemwithNC- Pt-4(−)ǀǀIrO2(+), serving as the cathode and anode, it demon- strated outstanding performance in direct seawater electrolysis, achievingalowcellvoltageof1.63Vat10mAcm−2,alongwith exceptionallong-termstability.Thisstudyoﬀersapotentialtech- nique for developing low Pt nanoclusters decorated within the Small2024,20,2403314 ©2024Wiley-VCHGmbH 2403314(2of14) 16136829, 2024, 40, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/smll.202403314 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [28/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
