www.advancedsciencenews.com www.small-journal.com phase purity of the as-synthesized catalysts were investigated using powder X-ray diﬀraction (PXRD). Figure 1b; FigureS2, Supporting Information show the XRD patterns of the as- synthesizedNC,NC-Pt,andZIF-8catalysts.TheXRDpatternof pristineZIF-8polyhedronsexhibitsseveraldiﬀractionpeaksthat closelyresembleeachotherandareconsistentwiththeprevious reports,indicatingtheformationofpuresodalite-typestructures with no other impurities observed in the ZIF-8 (Figure S2a, Supporting Information).[50]After the pyrolysis of ZIF-8, the resulting NC matrix showed a characteristic diﬀraction peak at ≈26.2°,correspondingtothe(002)planesofgraphitizedcarbon (Figure1b).[51]Further, upon decorating the NC catalyst with diﬀerent percentages of Pt nanoclusters, new diﬀraction peaks were detected at 39.8 °in the patterns of NC-Pt catalysts These peaksareascribedtothe(111)planesoftheface-centeredcubic (fcc)Ptphase,[52]suggestingthesuccessfuldecorationofPtnan- oclustersontotheNCcatalyst.Inaddition,theintensityofthePt diﬀractionpeakincreasedwithincreasingPtcontent,indicating an increase in peak intensity. Raman spectroscopy was used to investigate the degrees of graphitization and defect creation on the NC and various Pt-decorated NC catalysts. As shown in Figure1c,twodistinctpeaksappearedat ≈1351and1583cm−2, correspondingtotheDbandandGband,respectively,of sp2hy- bridizedcarbonmaterials.The ID/IGratioiscommonlyutilized toquantifydefectsorthelevelofgraphitizationincarbon-based materials, which is established from the intensities of the D and G bands. The ID/IGratios for the catalysts NC, NC-Pt-1, NC-Pt-2, NC-Pt-3, and NC-Pt-4 were 1.03, 1.02, 1.01, 0.99, and 0.97 respectively. Notably, the smallest ID/IGratio observed for NC-Pt-4isattributedtothepresenceofhigherPtcontentalong with nitrogen within the matrix, leading to a high degree of graphitization and enhanced electrical conductivity in NC-Pt-4. Consequently, this enhancement was expected to increase the HERperformance.AsdepictedinFigure S2b,SupportingInfor- mation,theRamanspectraoftheZIF-8revealedmultiplepeaks at684,1147,1178,1458,and1506cm−1,whichcorrespondedto thevibrationalmodesofthe2-methylimidazoleligand.[53] Theelectronicstatesandsurfacechemicalcompositionsofthe as-synthesizedcatalystswereinvestigatedusingX-rayphotoelec- tron spectroscopy (XPS). As revealed in Figure 1d, the XPS full survey spectrum of the as-synthesized ZIF-8, NC, and NC-Pt-4 catalystsdemonstratestheexistenceofZn,N,C,andPt,consis- tent with the elemental mapping results mentioned previously. The high-resolution Zn 2 pspectra displayed in Figure S3,S u p - portingInformationfortheZIF-8catalystweredeconvolutedinto doublet peaks at 1021 and 1044 eV, related to the Zn 2 p3/2and Zn2p1/2spinorbitalsassociatedwithZn2+,respectively.[54]After the carbonatation process, the Zn 2 pspectra of NC and NC-Pt- 4catalystsexhibitedthedisappearanceofZnpeaks,attributedto Znevaporationduringhigh-temperaturepyrolysis.Figure 1edis- playsthehigh-resolutionXPSC1 sspectraoftheas-synthesized ZIF-8resolvedintotwoseparatedpeaksat284.5and285.8,which could be ascribed to C ═Ca n dC ─N, respectively;[55]whereas, thehigh-resolutionC1 sspectraoftheNCandNC-Pt-4catalysts had three deconvoluted peaks at 284.6, 286.1, and 288.3 corre- spondingtotheC ═C,C─N,andC ═O,respectively.[56]Thehigh- resolution N 1 sspectrum of the ZIF-8 (Figure 1f) was resolved intotwodeconvolutionpeaks,correspondingtoZn ─N(398.9eV) and C ─N (400.2 eV).[57]The high-resolution N 1 sspectra of theNC and NC-Pt-4 catalysts revealed three distinctive deconvolu- tion peaks at 400.6, 399.4, and 398.5 eV, assigned to graphitic- N, pyrrolic-N, and pyridinic-N, respectively.[58]InFigure1g, the high-resolutionXPSspectraofPt4 fintheNC-Pt-4catalystindi- cate distinct peaks. Particularly, the peaks at observed 71.2 and 73.6 eV were associated with the Pt metallic state, whereas the peakslocatedat73.2and76.5eVwereattributedtothePt2+state, resulting from the surface oxidation of the Pt metal.[59]Mean- while,noPtpeakswereidentiﬁedintheZIF-8andNCcatalysts. TheseXPSresultsprovidestrongevidencefortheeﬀectivesyn- thesisofNCderivedfromZIF-8anddecoratedwithPtnanoclus- ters,utilizingtheadvancedpulsedlaserprocess. ThesurfacemorphologyandmicrostructureoftheZIF-8,NC, andPt-decoratedNCcatalystswereanalyzedusingﬁeldemission scanningelectronmicroscopy(FE-SEM).Figure S4a,Supporting Information shows the FE-SEM image of the ZIF-8 catalysts, revealing their uniform polyhedral morphological structure with an average size of 200 nm, synthesized using the PLAL technique. After carbonatation during the high-temperature pyrolysis of ZIF-8, it transformed into the NC matrix; while, retainingitsoriginalpolyhedronmorphologicalshape,asshown inFigure2a,b. Similarly, Figures 2cto3d;F i g u r e s S5–S7, Supporting Information show that various percentages of Pt nanoclustersdecoratedontheNCcatalystsmaintainedthesame morphologicalstructure.Further,thesurfacemorphologyofthe catalysts was investigated utilizing transmission electron mi- croscopy(TEM)analysis.AsshowninFigure S4b,c,Supporting Information, the TEM image of the ZIF-8 catalysts displayed a smooth surface within the polyhedron structure. Conversely, Figure2erevealssurfaceirregularitiesintheTEMimageofthe NCcatalysts,whichcanbeascribedtotheevaporationofZndur- ing the high-temperature pyrolysis process in the NC catalysts. high-resolution TEM (HR-TEM) images of the ZIF-8 and NC catalystsdisplayanamorphousnature,asshowninFigure S4d, SupportingInformation;Figure 3f.AsdisplayedinFigure 2g,the NC-Pt-4catalystshowedauniformdecorationofPtnanocluster onthesurfaceoftheNCmatrix.Thisarrangementpromotesthe electron and mass transfer kinetic during the electrochemical process,resultinginanincreasedHERperformance.Tofurther conﬁrm the HR-TEM investigation, the NC-Pt-4 catalyst dis- playedanevendistributionofPtnanoclustersontheNCmatrix supports,withanaveragePtparticlesizeof2–3nm,asshownin Figure2h.Anadditi