d and natural seawater under alkaline conditions. The performance of the as- synthesizedNC-Ptcatalystswastestedinalkalineelectrolyteen- vironmentsunderbothsimulated(1mKOH +0.5mNaCl)and natural seawater (1 m KOH +natural seawater). In Figure4a, the LSV polarization curves in simulated seawater demonstrate that the NC-Pt-4 catalyst exhibited excellent HER performance, achievingcurrentdensityvaluesof10and50mAcm−2withvery low overpotentials of 53 and 199 mV, respectively. This perfor- mancesurpassesthatofNC(414and588mV),NC-Pt-1(231and 478mV),NC-Pt-2(81and272mV),andNC-Pt-3(65and232mV) andisalmostasgoodasthatof20wt%Pt/C(46and204mV).In alkaline seawater electrolytes, the introduction of KOH to nat- ural seawater led to the precipitation of insoluble hydroxides. The formation of these precipitates was studied through XRD and EDX analyses, as illustrated in Figure S9, Supporting In- formation. The XRD study indicated the presence of Ca(OH)2 and Mg(OH)2as the precipitated compounds, which was con- ﬁrmed by EDS investigation showing the presence of Ca, Mg, and K. The seawater used for this study was collected from the Namildae Sea in Sacheon, South Korea (pH =7.96). The HER performance of the as-synthesized NC-Pt catalyst was analyzed in 1 m KOH with seawater (pH =13.65). Figure 4breveals thatNC-Pt-4 continued to exhibit the greatest catalytic performance in alkaline seawater, requiring overpotentials of 57 and 214 mV toachievecurrentdensityvaluesof10and50mAcm−2,respec- tively.Thesevaluesareconsiderablysmallerthanthoseforother as-synthesizedcatalystsandarealsonearlyidenticaltothoseof20 wt%Pt/C,whichrequires56and234mVat10and50mAcm−2, respectively. To further expose the reaction kinetics of the as-synthesized catalysts using simulated and alkaline seawater electrolytes, we calculatedthecorrespondingTafelslopeandconductedEISchar- acterizations.Notably,asillustratedinFigure 4c,d,theTafelslope valuescalculatedfortheas-synthesizedcatalystsinvariouselec- trolytes reveal that the NC-Pt-4 catalyst exhibited smaller Tafel slope values of 73.1 and 82.6 mV dec−1compared to other as- synthesizedcatalysts.Thesevaluesindicatefasterreactionkinet- icsandaVolmer–HeyrovskyreactionmechanismfortheNC-Pt- 4 catalyst during the HER process, regardless of the electrolyte used.Figure 4eshowstheEIScharacterizationconductedtode- termine RctusingtheNyquistplotforsimulatedseawater,reveal- ing that NC-Pt-4 showed the lowest Rctvalue of 2.1 Ωthan the othersynthesizedcatalysts.Whentestedwithalkalineseawater, NC-Pt-4 still exhibited a smaller Rctvalue (2.9 Ω) compared to NC-Pt-1,NC-Pt-2,andNC-Pt-3,asshowninFigure 4f.Tofurther assess the relative improvement in HER performance utilizing theNC-Pt-4catalystinbothsimulatedandalkalineseawaterelec- trolytes, EIS was conducted over a range of overpotentials from 30 to 150 mV versus RHE, along with associated Bode plots to verify 𝜏e.A ss h o w ni nF i g u r e S10, Supporting Information, the increaseinoverpotentialledtoareductionin Rctvalues,andthe Bode plots indicated a shorter lifetime for the NC-Pt-4 catalyst in both simulated and alkaline seawater electrolytes compared tootheras-synthesizedcatalysts.Theseresultsdemonstratethat theNC-Pt-4catalystpossessedahigherchargetransferrateand shorter electron lifetime compared to other as-synthesized cat- alysts. In addition, the intrinsic catalytic eﬃciency for HER, as representedbyMAinbothsimulatedandalkalineseawaterelec- trolytes,ispresentedinFigure 4g,h.TheNC-Pt-4catalystdemon- strated a larger MA in simulated seawater (20.7 A g−1)a n da l - kaline seawater (17.7 A g−1) compared to the other control cat- alysts.Further,theECSAvaluesweredeterminedbyestimating CdlfromCVpolarizationcurvesmeasuredatvariousscanrates within the range of 10–50 mV s−1, as displayed in Figures S11 andS12,SupportingInformation.InFigure S13,SupportingIn- formation,theresulting CdlvaluesfortheNC-Pt-4catalystinboth simulated and alkaline seawater electrolytes were 22.9 and 22.1 mFcm−2,respectively.Basedonthese 