ent densities of 10 and 50 mA cm−2, respectively. How- ever, in alkaline seawater, the overpotential of the NC-Pt-4 cata- lystincreasedby ≈58and117mVat10and50mAcm−2,respec- tively. This could be due to the blocking of electrode surface ac- tivesitesbytheformationofMg(OH)2andCa(OH)2precipitates, whichresultedfromthepresenceofMgandCametalsinseawa- ter.Moreover,theelectrocatalyticstabilityoftheNC-Pt-4catalyst was assessed using a multicurrent step CP test under diﬀerent electrolyte conditions at various current densities. As shown in Small2024,20,2403314 ©2024Wiley-VCHGmbH 2403314(8of14) 16136829, 2024, 40, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/smll.202403314 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [28/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
