www.advancedsciencenews.com www.small-journal.com To further investigate the structural and morphological changes in the NC-Pt-4 catalyst after a 50-h long-term CP test at50mAcm−2,weconductedcomprehensivecharacterizations, includingXRD,SEMwithEDS,andXPS,bothbeforeandafter thestabilitytestsforHERevaluationinvariouselectrolytes.The XRDpatterns,showninFigure 5b,revealnosigniﬁcantchanges in the crystalline structure of the NC-Pt-4 catalyst, conﬁrming the preservation of its crystallinity after the HER stability test acrossdiﬀerentelectrolytes.Further,SEMimages,asdisplayedin FiguresS16–S18,SupportingInformationillustratethatthepoly- hedralmorphologicalstructureoftheNC-Pt-4catalystremained intact before and after the HER stability test in 1 m KOH and simulatedseawater.However,theSEMimageinFigure S19,Sup- portingInformationindicatedthataftertheHERstabilitytestin alkaline seawater, the polyhedral structure of the NC-Pt-4 cata- lyst exhibited signiﬁcant agglomeration due to mineral deposi- tion from the seawater on the electrode surface. EDS mapping analysisshowsauniformdistributionofN,C,O,andPtwithin the catalyst structure and highlights the deposition of Mg, Ca, and K on the surface post-testing. XPS analysis was performed toexaminethebindingenergystatesandsurfacechemicalstruc- tureoftheNC-Pt-4catalystafterthestabilitytestsinvariouselec- trolytes.TheXPSfullsurveyspectrum,showninFigure 5c,dis- playsnoalterationinthecompositionorvalencestateofthecata- lystafterthetests.High-resolutionXPSspectraofC1 s,N1s,and Pt 4f, presented in Figure 5d–f, conﬁrm the presence of these elements on the surface of NC-Pt-4, suggesting that no signiﬁ- cantsurfacereconstructionoccurred.Notably,thePt4fpeaksex- hibitedaslightnegativeshiftafterthestabilitytestsindiﬀerent electrolytes,indicatingchangesinthechemicalenvironmentand electronic structure of the Pt catalyst, along with a slight reduc- tionofPtspeciesduringtheHERprocess.[60]Inalkalineseawater conditions,asevidencedinFigure S20,SupportingInformation, XPSanalysisconﬁrmsthepresenceofCa(OH)2,attributedtothe seawaterminerals.Remarkably,theseresultsunderscoretheNC- Pt-4catalyst’ssuperiorelectrocatalyticstabilityandstructuralen- durance, positioning it as a promising candidate for sustained HERinindustrialhydrogenproductionfromseawater.TheNC- Pt-4catalystdemonstratedoutstandingHERperformancecom- paredtorecentlyreportednoble-metalelectrocatalystsundervar- iouselectrolyteconditions,asshowninFigure 5g;TableS1,Sup- portingInformation. 2.5.ElectrocatalyticOWSPerformanceinVariousElectrolytes Encouraged by the remarkable electrocatalytic performance of NC-Pt-4 in various electrolytes for HER, we proceeded to ex- plore its oveall water splitting (OWS) performance. To investi- gate this, we assembled a two-electrode electrolyzer using NC- Pt-4(−)ǀǀIrO2(+) with NC-Pt-4 and IrO2as the cathode and an- ode, respectively, and evaluated its performance in various elec- trolytes.Asshownin Figure6a,theLSVpolarizationcurvesofthe NC-Pt-4(−)ǀǀIrO2(+)electrolyzerindicatethatacurrentdensityof 10mAcm−2canbereachedwithaminimalvoltageof1.60Vin the1mKOHelectrolyte.UpontheadditionofNaCltothealka- lineelectrolyte(1mKOH +0.5mNaCl),theelectrolyzerconsis- tentlyrevealedsuperioractivity,comparabletoitsperformancein pure alkaline electrolyte, as displayed in Figure 6b. In the pres-ence of natural seawater with the 1 m KOH electrolyte, the po- tential of the electrolyzer demonstrated a slight rise to 1.63 V (Figure6c),possiblyowingtotheexistenceofnaturallyoccurring mineralsinseawater.Subsequently,theOWSperformanceofthe NC-Pt-4(−)ǀǀIrO2(+) electrolyzer was notably superior to that of the Pt/C( −)ǀǀIrO2(+) electrolyzer for various electrolytes, as dis- playedinFigure 6d.Inaddition,theelectrocatalyticstabilityofthe NC-Pt-4(−)ǀǀIrO2(+)electrolyzerwasassessedatacurrentdensity of50mAcm−2invariouselectrolytesthroughCPmeasurements, asdisplayedinFigure 6e.Theresultsdemonstratedonlyaminor increaseincellvoltageafter50hinboth1mKOHand1mKOH +0.5mNaClelectrolytemediums.However,amorepronounced increase in cell voltage was observed after 50 h in a continuous alkalineseawatermediumatthesamecurrentdensity,likelydue to the formation of Mg(OH)2and Ca(OH)2precipitates within the alkaline seawater electrolyte during the OWS. In compari- son,theOWSstabilitytestofthestate-of-the-artPt/C( −)ǀǀIrO2(+) couple was also performed in various electrolytes, as shown in FigureS21, Supporting Information. A gradual decrease in cell voltagewasobservedduringthecontinuouselectrolysisprocess, indicating the unsatisfactory long-term stability of the commer- cial Pt/C catalyst. Figure 6fshows a schematic illustration of a two-electrode water electrolysis system employing alkaline sea- water as the electrolyte, with NC-Pt-4( −)ǀǀIrO2(+) as the elec- trolyzer electrodes. Moreover, we have conducted experiments with a membrane electrolyzer using an H-cell equipped with a Naﬁon membrane to assess device performance. The results from the membrane electrolyzer, displayed in Figure S22,S u p - porting Information, indicate that the cell voltage of the mem- braneelectrolyzerwashigherthanthatofthemembrane-lesswa- terelectrolyzer.Thisdiﬀerencecanlikelybeattributedtothein- creased resistance encountered through the Naﬁon membrane, inadditiontopotentiallossesrelatedtoiontransportatthemem- braneinterface.Notably,theassembledNC-Pt-4( −)ǀǀIrO2(+)elec- trolyzer exhibited superior OWS performance when compared topreviouslydevelopednoble-metalelectrocatalysts,asshownin Figure6g;TableS2,SupportingInformation.Thisstudysuggests thepotentialofpulsedlaser-producedNC-Ptcatalystforeﬃcient hydrogenproductionthroughalkalineseawaterelectrolysis. 2.6.TheHERMechanismInvestigation Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were employed to elucidate the superior HER performance of the NC-Pt catalyst. The DFT-optimized geometric structures of the NC, NC-Pt, and Pt-C catalysts are depicted in Figure7a, showing that N atomsweredopedintothecarbonstructurebyreplacingCsites. Further, the successful incorporation of Pt onto the NC and C surfaces was facilitated by bonding with C atoms. To explore theelectronicstructureoftheNC-Ptcatalyst,thecharge-density diﬀerencewascalculated,asshowninFigure 7b.Itwasobserved thatchargetransferoccurredfromPttothesurroundingCand Natoms,whichisattributedtothezero-valencestateofPt.The partial density of states (PDOS) for the NC, NC-Pt, and Pt-C catalysts presented in Figures 7c–e, reveal that the p orbitals of C and N, as well as the d orbitals of Pt in the NC-Pt catalyst, were notably closer to the Fermi levels compared to those in the NC and Pt-C catalysts. These results suggest a stronger Small2024,20,2403314 ©2024Wiley-VCHGmbH 2403314(10of14) 16136829, 2024, 40, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/smll.202403314 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [28/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
