Carbon 224 (2024) 119061 Available online 19 March 2024 0008-6223/© 2024 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.Accelerating water dissociation at hierarchical nanostructured electrocatalyst for efficient hydrogen evolution Beibei Chen1, Dou Wang1, Shusheng Wei, Juan Wang* Shanghai Key Laboratory of Materials Protection and Adv. Mater. in Electric Power, Shanghai University of Electric Power, Shanghai, 200090, China ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Electrochemical hydrogen evolution Three-dimensional (3D) nanostructure Charge transfer kinetics Pt nanoparticles ABSTRACT The sluggish kinetics of the water electrolysis reaction is a key issue for hydrogen production, so the rational design of a high-efficiency hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) electrocatalyst with various structures has attracted extensive attention. Herein, a coaxial hierarchical three-dimensional (3D) nanostructure, Pt@Mo –S–Ni- CNTs, was prepared. In this nanostructure, carbon nanotubes (CNTs) as the core, Mo–S–Ni ultrathin nanosheets as shells, and ultrasmall metal nanoparticles (NPs) were grown uniformly on the surface of Mo–S–Ni nanosheets (NSs). At a current density of 10 mA m␀2, the catalyst under investigation has remarkable hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) activity under acidic conditions. The overpotential (η10) is measured to be 61.2 mV, while the Tafel slope is determined to be 40.2 mV dec␀1. Meanwhile, in an alkaline medium, η10 is 80.5 mV and the Tafel slope is 52.3 mV dec␀1. It also shows great mass activities of 1.32 A mg␀1 and 1.86 A mg␀1, which are higher than those of commercial Pt/C HER catalysts. Impressively, the excellent HER activity and durability of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs are mainly attributed to the close binding effects among Pt, MoS 2, and NiS. Theoretical simulations show that the MoS 2/NiS interfaces facilitate H2O dissociation and that Platinum (Pt) enhances the absorption of hydrogen atoms (H) and increases the charge transfer kinetics of the Mo–S–Ni, thus significantly improving the HER activity. This work highlights the importance of engineering interface nanosheets based on transition-metal dichalcogenides for enhanced HER performance. 1.Introduction Hydrogen, an ecological and value-added product, has attracted considerable interest [1,2]. Electrochemical water splitting is a notable technique for hydrogen production, distinguished by its versatility and ease, among the wide range of available technologies [3,4]. However, the exorbitant cost and limited availability of catalysts impose sub- stantial limitations on its widespread use in the commercial sector [5]. To address the challenges associated with large scale hydrogen gener - ation, it is imperative to develop a stable and energy-efficient catalytic agent [6]. According to existing knowledge, catalysts based on platinum are generally considered to be the most effective in promoting the hydrogen evolution process (HER), especially in solutions with corrosive properties [7,8]. The surface atomic ratio and surface energy of the catalysts are affected by geometric factors such as the size and morphology of nanometals [9,10]. Through the geometric effect, the number of effec- tive active sites per unit of area increases [11–14]. Therefore, changing the morphology of noble metal catalysts and adjusting the size of noble metals can improve catalytic activity [15–17]. However, scientific research has confirmed that due to their small particle size and large surface energy, metal nanoparticles in the process of chemical reactions are more susceptible to agglomeration, oxidation, and loss [18,19]. Therefore, it is necessary to find an excellent support or substrate to anchor metal particles and improve metal utilization. The potential of 2D MoS 2 as a support material for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is attributed to the existence of coordinatively unsaturated sulfur atoms located at the edge locations [20,21]. The catalytic activity of MoS 2 is not found in its ideal in-plane region [22]. Based on a previous study, the incorporation of platinum atoms (Pt) in ultrathin molybdenum disulphide (MoS 2) nanosheets of has been ach- ieved, signifying a noteworthy progression in the field of hydrogen generation with enhanced efficacy [20]. However, the tendency of ul- trathin MoS 2 nanosheets to aggregate leads to a reduction in the avail- able surface area to anchor individual atoms, which compromises their cyclic stability. In general, for the design and modification of Pt-based *Corresponding author. E-mail address: wangjuan@shiep.edu.cn (J. Wang). 1 Both authors contributed equally to this manuscript. Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Carbon u{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2mk~l{z! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.carbon.2024.119061 Received 5 November 2023; Received in revised form 20 February 2024; Accepted 19 March 2024
