Carbon 224 (2024) 119061 2catalysts, it is not only to reduce the loading of Pt but also to ensure that the efficiency and stability of the catalyst are maintained at an excellent level and finally applied to the water electrolysis hydrogen production system [23]. In this study, a coaxial hierarchical structure of Mo–S–Ni/CNTs was fabricated and selected as the substrate for the immobilization of Pt nanoparticles. The coaxial hierarchical structure of Mo–S–Ni nanosheets offers favorable dispersibility and high specific surface area, making it conducive for the anchoring of Pt nanoparticles. Furthermore, the catalytic activity can be increased as a result of the modified electronic structure arising from interactions between NiS, MoS 2, and Pt. Through electrochemical tests, Pt@Mo –S–Ni/CNTs displays an overpotential (61.2 mV in acidic media and 80.5 mV in alkaline medium at the current density of 10 mA cm␀2). In addition, outstanding durability (less than 5% change in overpotential) was obtained. Furthermore, by density functional theory (DFT), an increase in the charge density area was induced along the interface of the MoS 2/NiS heterostructure, and Pt deposition also results in local charge redistribution, which could Fig. 1.The (a–b) SEM images of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs with different magnification. (c–d) TEM images of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs with different magnification. and (e) The corresponding EDX mapping images of C, O, S, Ni, Mo, Pt elements in Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs. Fig. 2.(a) TEM image of particle distribution on Mo–S–Ni sheet. (b) HRTEM image of Pt, MoS 2 and NiS.  B. Chen et al.
