Carbon 224 (2024) 119061 4furnace and again heated at 450 •C for 200 min in a He atmosphere to reduce the Pt precursor to metallic Pt nanoparticles. 3.Results and discussion 3.1. Morphology and structure of materials Fig. 1a–d illustrate the results related to the distribution of morphological, structural, and elemental characteristics of the hybrid materials denoted as Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs. The Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs cata- lyst was examined by scanning electron microscopy (SEM), revealing a coaxial core-shell structure. This structure consists of a carbon tube serving as the core, with hierarchical Mo–S–Ni nanosheets acting as the coating on the exterior surface. These findings are depicted in Fig. 1a and b. In addition, the ultrasmall Pt nanoparticles were preferentially deposited on the Mo–S–Ni nanosheets, which was further proved by the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images (Fig. 1c and d) with different magnifications and energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) re- sults in Fig. 1e. Small nanoparticles were homogeneously dispersed over the ultrathin nanosheet. The introduction of defects and deformations by thermal pretreatment of hydrothermal Mo–S–Ni nanosheets im- proves the capacity to attach and stabilize platinum nanoparticles [24]. The corresponding EDS image visually shows the distribution of each element. What ’s more, Fig. S1 and Fig. S2 are the SEM, TEM, and EDS images of the CNTs, MoS 2-CNTs, NiS-CNTs, and Mo–S–Ni-CNTs. Firstly, it can be seen from Figs. S1a and e that the length of CNTs is several microns and the surface morphology is smooth. Then SEM and TEM images of MoS 2-CNTs (Figs. S1b and f), NiS-CNTs (Figs. S1c and g), and Mo–S–Ni-CNTs (Figs. S1d and h) all showed a coaxial-core-shell struc- ture with ruffled nanosheets of MoS 2, NiS, and Mo–S–Ni enveloping the CNTs. Furthermore, the EDS image of these materials (Fig. S2) also proves this. Fig. 2a also shows the dispersed Pt nanoparticles on the thin edge of Mo–S–Ni-CNTs nanosheets. The Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs catalyst exhibits distinct lattice fringes as observed in the high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM) pictures, as shown in Fig. 2b. The lattice spacings of 0.62 nm, 0.26 nm, and 0.225 nm are associated with the (002) plane of MoS 2, (101) plane of NiS, and (111) crystal plane of. Pt, respectively. The obtained findings provide a clear demonstration of the effective deposition of platinum (Pt) onto the fabricated hetero - structure composed of molybdenum-sulfur-nickel (Mo–S–Ni). Addi- tionally, it is seen that nickel sulphide (NiS) chemically interacts with molybdenum disulphide (MoS 2) through the formation of Mo-(S) –Ni heterointerfaces. As shown in Fig. 3, the structures and crystalline Fig. 5.LSV curves of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs, Mo–S–Ni-CNTs, MoS 2-CNTs, NiS-CNTs and Pt/C in (a) 0.5 M H2SO4 and (b) 1 M KOH; (c) Tafel plots in 0.5 M H2SO4 and (d) 1 M KOH. B. Chen et al.
