Carbon 224 (2024) 119061 5phases of CNTs, MoS 2-CNTs, NiS-CNTs, Mo–S–Ni-CNTs, and Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs were characterized by XRD. All materials have a diffraction peak at 2θ 26.34•, which corresponds to the (002) crystal plane of the graphite layer in the CNTs (JCPDS Nos. 65–6212). In addition, MoS 2-CNTs have four strong diffraction peaks at 2θ 14.04•, 2θ 29.89•, 34.34•, 45.44•and 53.09•for NiS-CNTs, belonging to the (100), (101). (102) and (110) lattice planes of NiS (JCPDS No. 77–1624). Furthermore, the 32.87•, 39.23•, and 58.28•, corresponding to the (002), (100), (103), and (110) planes of MoS 2 (JCPDS No. 37–1492). Four distinct characteristic peaks were observed at characteristic peaks corresponding to the above materials can also be found in Mo–S– Ni- CNTs and Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs. In addition, the Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs composites have two faint characteristic peaks at 2θ 38.96•and 45.65•, which may be caused by the low content of Pt, which can correspond well to the (111) and (200) crystal planes of Pt (JCPDS No. 88–2343). The chemical composition and electronic structure of Pt@Mo –S–Ni- CNTs were further studied by XPS, and the bonding state between elements was investigated. The two main peaks at 232.65 and 229.55 eV in Fig. 4a can be assigned to Mo 3d3/2 and Mo 3d5/2 in the Mo4state, respectively, indicating that this sample still exists in the form of 2H–MoS 2 species [25]. The observed peak at 235.75 eV could be attributed to the presence of Mo6, which can commonly be related to the oxidation process of the catalyst surface upon exposure to atmo - spheric conditions. Fig. 4b shows the high-resolution XPS spectra of Pt 4f, which helps determine the chemical state of the Pt nanoparticles in the Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs catalyst. The Pt 4f spectrum is mainly divided into two peaks, which are high-energy doublets at 72.4 eV and 75.7 eV, both pointing to divalent Pt ions (Pt–O bonds) [23,26]. It is obvious that Pt nanoparticles exhibit a metallic state in this material, which is beneficial for enhancing electrocatalytic activity [27]. Fig. 4c shows the S2p spectrum. The element S can be divided mainly into two high-intensity characteristic peaks at 162.4 and 163.55 eV, corre - sponding to S2-2p3/2 and S2-2p1/2 of negative divalent S, representing MoS 2 and the presence of NiS. This shows that most of the S element in the sample exists in the form of a negative divalent. From this we can conclude that sulfur co-exists primarily in the form of MoS 2 and NiS [28]. It is evident from the data presented in Fig. 4c and d that both the S2p and Ni2p peaks exhibited positive shifts simultaneously. In partic - ular, the S2p peak had a considerable shift, which may be attributable to the strong interaction between NiS, MoS 2, and Pt. It was shown that the binding energy of the nanosheets changed after the Pt nanoparticles were anchored, indicating a strong electron transfer ability between the. Pt nanoparticles and the Mo–S–Ni nanosheet support. The electro - chemical catalytic activit of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs for the hydrogen evo- lution reaction (HER) were assessed using a linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) technique. The measurements were conducted in 0.5 M sulfuric acid (H2SO4) and 1 M potassium hydroxide (KOH), both at ambient temperature. All of the collected data underwent IR correction, irradi - ation, and the potentiometry measurements were calibrated using a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). For comparison, pure MoS 2-CNTs, pure NiS-CNTs, Mo–S–Ni-CNTs and commercial Pt/C were also measured under the same conditions. As shown in Fig. 5a and b, the linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) po- larization curves indicate that Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs achieve a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 in 1 M KOH solution and 0.5 M H2SO4 solution with overpotentials of 61.2 mV and 80.5 mV, respectively, which are significantly lower than the rest of the reference catalysts (Mo–S–Ni- CNTs, MoS 2-CNTs, NiS-CNTs). All of this demonstrated that the modified. electrodes of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNT with a lower loading of Pt can Fig. 6.Mass activities of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs and Pt/C in 0.5 M H2SO4 and 1.0 M KOH. Fig. 7.EIS of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs and Pt/C in (a) 0.5 M H2SO4 (b) EIS in 1 M KOH. (Cdl). The level of Cdl positively correlated with the degree of site activity. Based on the observed linear relationship between the scan rate and current, possesses the largest. B. Chen et al.
