Carbon 224 (2024) 119061 6provide excellent HER activity. The Tafel slope fitted at the initial po- tential is one of the indicators that reflects the electrocatalytic reaction kinetics of this catalyst with a strong reference function [29]. The magnitude of the slope can infer the rate-determining step of the hydrogen evolution reaction. It is widely recognized that a lower tafel slope and a higher exchange current density correspond to enhanced kinetic processes of the electrocatalyst and improved catalytic activity [30]. The Volmer, Heyrovsky, and Tafel processes are commonly employed as rate-determining stages in various chemical reactions [31]. The corresponding theoretical Tafel slopes associated with these pro- cesses are measured to be 120, 40, and 30 mV dec␀1, respectively [25]. As seen in Fig. 5c, the Tafel slope of the Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs sample is around 40.2 mV dec␀1 in 0.5 M H2SO4 solutions, which is much lower than the Tafel slopes for MoS 2-CNT (132.8 mV dec␀1), NiS-CNTs. (170.8 mV dec␀1), and Mo–S–Ni-CNTs (53.5 mV dec␀1). This indicates that the hydrogen production process follows the Volmer- Heyrovsky mechanism [32]. Meanwhile, the Tafel slopes of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs and Pt/C in a 1 M KOH solution (Fig. 5d) are measured to be 52.3 and 49.3 mV dec␀1, respectively. However, the Tafel slopes of MoS 2-CNTs and NiS-CNTs are as high as 95.1 and 107.2 mV dec␀1, which shows that the kinetic rate of the HER reaction on the surface of Pt @ Mo–S–Ni-CNTs and Pt/C is similar and much higher than on the surface of MoS 2-CNTs and NiS-CNTs (Fig. 5d). Meanwhile, ac- cording to previous research results, Pt NPs can promote electron transfer to the active S site, and the interface charge polarization be- tween MoS 2 and Pt NPs increases the electron density of the S site, thus enhancing the activity of HER, corresponding to the LSV results [33]. In Fig. 6, the mass activities of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs have been iden- tified as 1.32 A/mg Pt (over potential: ␀30 mV) in 0.5 M H2SO4 and 1.86 A/mg Pt (over potential: ␀110 mV) in 1 M KOH, respectively. The HER Fig. 8.Chronopotentiometric curve for Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs at a current density of 10 mA cm-2 in (a) 0.5 M H2SO4 and (b) 1.0 M KOH. Fig. 9.Theoretical investigations. Computational models and localized electric field distribution of (a) Pt10/MoS2(001) (b) Pt10/NiS(101) (c) Pt10/MoS2(001)-NiS (101). (d) Calculated energy barriers of water dissociation kinetic and (e) adsorption free energies of H* on the surface of the catalysts. (yellow: S, orange: Mo, red: Ni, and gray: Pt; yellow and green contours represent the charge accumulation and depletion, respectively). (A colour version of this figure can be viewed online.) B. Chen et al.
