Carbon 224 (2024) 119061 7activity of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs at lower loadings exceeds that of com- mercial Pt/C, suggesting that even with reduced amounts of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs, stronger HER activity may be achieved compared to 20% commercial Pt/C. Additionally, the assessment of electrochemical surface area of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs relies heavily on the measurement of double-layer capacitance. electrochemically active area, with Cdl values of 102.5 mF/cm␀2 and 98.2 mF/cm␀2 in alkaline and acid environments, respectively. This indicates that Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs exhibits complete exposure of the edge locations in Mo–S–Ni nanosheets, thereby exposing abundant active sites. Furthermore, the decoration of the anchored Pt NPs in- creases the number of active sites to a greater extent. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was used to validate the rapid electrode reaction kinetics of Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs hetero - structure. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) plots for all samples in both acidic and alkaline solutions are depicted in Fig. 7a and b, respectively. The corresponding equivalent circuits are illustrated in the inset of each figure. Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs shows a remarkable low charge transfer resistance (Rct) of 10.3 Ω and 35.6 Ω in acidic and alkaline electrolytes separately, exhibiting high Faradaic efficiency and rapid electron transfer during HER [20]. During HER, it exhibits rapid electron transfer and high Faradaic efficiency [21]. This may be attrib - uted to the enhancement of the hierarchical core-shell structure of Mo–S–Ni-CNT and the sufficient contact between Pt nanoparticles and 1D CNTs, which is favorable for electron transfer and transport between the interfaces [33,34]. The long-term durability of the Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs catalyst for HER was further studied in acidic and alkaine media by performing a continuous HER at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 for 12 h. As shown in Fig. 8a and b, the potential growth of the catalyst does not Fig. 8. Chronopotentiometric curve for Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 in (a) 0.5 M H2SO4 and (b) 1.0 M KOH. exceed 4% after 12 h. In addition, as shown in Fig, S3, we also tested the hydrogen evolution stability of CNTs, MoS 2-CNTs, NiS-CNTs and Mo–S–Ni-CNTs under acidic and alkaline conditions, and comparing with Fig. 8., we found that Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs had better hydrogen evolution stability. DFT calculations (Fig. 9) were performed to understand the syner - getic enhanced HER mechanism at the nanoparticle level. Charge den- sity distributions were used to determine whether there was an elevated charge density region formed at the interface of the MoS 2/NiS hetero - structure. The deposition of Pt leads to a localized redistribution of charge, perhaps resulting in an increased adsorption capacity for H2O molecules and H atoms [35]. Generally, the complete pathway of hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) may be represented by a three-state diagram consisting of initial adsorption of water molecules (H2O*), the subsequent formation of intermediate hydrogen species (H*), and ulti- mate production of molecular hydrogen gas (H2) [36]. The Pt@MoS 2/- NiS hybrid catalyst demonstrates a low energy barrier of 0.18 eV for the dissociation of H2O into *OH and H* species. This energy barrier was determined using the Ab Initio Cluster-Continuum Model. The results highlight the significant contribution of the MoS 2/NiS interfaces exposed on the catalyst ’s surface in facilitating the Volmer step of H2O dissociation. In the subsequent step, the ΔGH* of the hybrid catalyst Pt@MoS 2/NiS (␀0.08 eV) is closer to 0 eV, compared to Pt10/MoS 2 (001) (␀0.21 eV) and. Pt10/NiS (101) (0.37 eV), as shown in Fig. 3b. The platinum (Pt) sites supported by MoS 2/NiS interfaces serve as proton acceptors, facilitating the recombination of dissociated protons (H*) and the subsequent development of hydrogen gas (H2). This is attributed to the near-zero hydrogen binding energy and the strong electron supply capacity of these Pt sites, which is obtained from the locally improved charge distribution. 4.Conclusions In conclusion, a 3D Pt@Mo –S–Ni-CNTs coaxial hierarchical structure has been made, which acts as a highly active and long-lasting HER electrocatalyst. The electronic interaction between Pt and Mo–S–Ni- CNTs can effectively inhibit the migration and agglomeration of Pt particles in the catalytic process and ensure the high dispersion and stability of Pt species. The introduction of Pt improved the charge- transfer kinetics of Mo–S–Ni-CNTs, which enhanced the HER perfor - mance. The DFT calculation also confirmed the synergy in the hetero - interface of NiS and MoS 2, which is favorable to HER. This work can provide new ideas for designing hybrid catalysts for hydrogen produc - tion in the future. CRediT authorship contribution statement Beibei Chen: Conceptualization, Data curation, Methodology, Soft- ware, Writing – original draft. Dou Wang: Conceptualization, Data curation, Formal analysis, Methodology, Validation. Shusheng Wei: Conceptualization, Data curation, Formal analysis. Juan Wang: Data curation, Funding acquisition, Investigation, Methodology, Project administration, Supervision, Writing – review & editing. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgements This work was financially supported by Science and Technology Commission of Shanghai Municipality (22ZR1425600 and 19DZ2271100). Appendix A.Supplementary data Supplementary data to this article can be found online at https://doi. org/10.1016/j.carbon.2024.119061 . References [1]J.K. Norskov, C.H. Christensen, Toward efficient hydrogen production at surfaces, Science 312 (2006) 1322 –1323 . [2]S. Bai, C. Wang, M. Deng, M. Gong, Y. Bai, J. Jiang, Y. Xiong, Surface polarization matters: enhancing the hydrogen-evolution reaction b