Accurate design of spatially separated double active site in Bi 4NbO 8Cl single crystal to promote Z - Scheme photocatalytic overall water splitting Kailong Gaoa, Hongxia Guoa,⇑, Yanan Hub, Hongbin Heb, Mowen Lia, Xiaoming Gaob,⇑, Feng Fub,⇑ aFaculty of Materials and Manufacturing, Beijing University of Technology, Beijing 100124, China bYanneng-Yanda Comprehensive Energy Industry Technology Research Institute, Shaanxi Modern Industrial College of Green and Efﬁcient Utilization of Energy Resources, Shaanxi Collaborative Innovation Center of Clean Utilization of Low Rank Coal, Shaanxi Key Laboratory of Chemical Reaction Engineering, Department of Chem istry and Chemical Engineering, Yan’an University, Yan’an 716000, Shaanxi, China article info Article history:Received 5 June 2023Revised 4 August 2023Accepted 17 August 2023Available online 4 September 2023 Keywords: Spatially separated double active sitesFeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs Photocatalytic water oxidation Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution Hydrogen evolution co-catalyst PtRu@Cr 2O3 Z - Scheme photocatalytic overall watersplitting systemabstract The efﬁciency of photocatalytic overall water splitting was mainly limited by the slow reaction kinetics of water oxidation. How to design effective surface active site to overcome the slow water oxidation reac- tion was a major challenge. Here, we propose a strategy to accelerate surface water oxidation through thefabrication spatially separated double active sites. FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs photocatalyst with spatially separated double active site was prepared by hydrogen reduction photoanode deposition method. Due to the high matching of the spatial loading positions of FeCoPi and OVs with the photogenerated chargedistribution of Bi 4NbO 8Cl and corresponding reaction mechanisms of substrate, the FeCoPi and OVs on the (001) and (010) crystal planes of Bi 4NbO 8Cl photocatalyst provided surface active site for water oxi- dation reaction and electron shuttle reaction (Fe3+/Fe2+), respectively. Under visible light irradiation, the evolution O 2rate of FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl OVs was 16.8 lmol h/C01, as 32.9 times as Bi 4NbO 8Cl. Furthermore, a hydrogen evolution co-catalyst PtRu@Cr 2O3was prepared by sequential photodeposition method. Due to the introduction of Ru, the Schottky barrier between PbTiO 3and Pt was effectively reduced, which pro- moted the transfer of photogenerated electrons to PtRu@Cr 2O3thermodynamically, the evolution H 2rate on PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3increased to 664.8 times. On based of the synchronous enhancement of the water oxidation performance on FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs and water reduction performance on PtRu@Cr 2O3/ PbTiO 3, a novel Z - Scheme photocatalytic overall water splitting system (FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs) medi- ated by Fe3+/Fe2+had successfully constructed. Under visible light irradiation, the evolution rates of H 2 and O 2were 2.5 and 1.3 lmol h/C01, respectively. This work can provide some reference for the design of active site and the controllable synthesis of OVs spatial position. On the other hand, the hydrogen evo-lution co catalyst (PtRu@Cr 2O3) and the co catalyst FeCoPi for oxygen evolution contributed to the con- struction of an overall water splitting system. /C2112023 Science Press and Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences. Published by ELSEVIER B.V. and Science Press. All rights reserved. 1. Introduction Photocatalytic overall water splitting could produce clean and sustainable hydrogen energy, which had the advantages of low cost, simple equipment and easy expansion of scale. So, it had become one of the most promising solutions to solve energy short- age and global warming [1–3] . In the past few decades, people hadbeen committed to developing and improving the hydrogen pro- duction system based on particle photocatalyst, especially in increasing light absorption and optimizing photogenerated charge separation. Unfortunately, the available solar hydrogen production efﬁciency (STH 1.1%) still did not meet the minimum requirement of practical application (10%) [4–6] . The water oxidation reaction involved four electron transfers, O–H bond breaking and the for- mation of high activation energy barrier of O–O bond, which were recognized as the bottleneck reaction of POWS [7–9] . At the same time, water oxidation reaction was also the core step of photocat- alytic CO 2reduction and N 2ﬁxation. Therefore, the development of https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jechem.2023.08.038 2095-4956/ /C2112023 Science Press and Dalian Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Sciences. Published by ELSEVIER B.V. and Science Press. All rights re served.⇑Corresponding authors. E-mail addresses: hxguo@bjut.edu.cn (H. Guo), ydgaoxm@126.com (X. Gao), yadxfufen@126.com (F. Fu).Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Journal of Energy Chemistry journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/jechem
