stable and efﬁcient water oxidation photocatalyst was the key to realize visible light-driven POWS and production of solar fuel. However, there were relatively few studies on photocatalytic water oxidation. Recently, layered Sillén-Aurivillius phase perovskite Bi 4NbO 8Cl was a new mixed anionic photocatalyst, the crystal structure was formed by alternating accumulation of [(Bi 2O2)2+(Cl)/C0] (Sillén layer) and [(Bi 2O2)2+(NbO 4)3/C0] (Aurivillius layer) along the c- axis. The built-in electric ﬁeld in the Sillén layer and the polariza- tion region in the Aurivillius layer could promote the separation of photogenerated charges [10–14] . Especially, because of the highly dispersed O 2 porbital in valence band, Bi 4NbO 8Cl showed excellent anti-oxidation stability in the photocatalytic water oxi- dation reaction, considered as a promising photocatalyst for water oxidation [15–18] . However, the lack of surface active sites seriously limited water oxidation performance. It was worth that the spatial positions needed to be considered more carefully in the design of surface active sites compared with the number increase of the speciﬁc structures on the surface of photocata- lysts. Generally, under the light excitation, the electronic state of the active site and the space distribution of photogenerated charge on the surface of photocatalyst should be highly matched. According to previous studies, when the oxidation or reduction assistant was loaded to the correct position, they could play the most roles. If it was only a simple random distribution, it not only increased the possibility of charge recombination, but also caused serious side reactions or reverse reactions [19–23] . In addition, the reverse reaction of the electron shuttle medium (Fe3+/Fe2+, +0.77 V vs. SHE, pH = 0) was more advantageous than the oxida- tion of H 2O( O 2/H2O, +1.23 V vs. SHE, pH = 0) in thermodynamic. So, the close redox sites inevitably leaded to the reverse reaction of the electron shuttle medium. Therefore, it was an attractive strategy to accelerate the surface water oxidation reaction by accurately designing the effective active sites of speciﬁc reactions and maintaining the spatial separation. Up to date, only a few photocatalytic water oxidation systems based on Bi 4NbO 8Cl had been developed, and all relied on noble metal co-catalysts, such as Flux-Bi 4NbO 8Cl/RuO 2(or Pt) [24], RuO x:Bi4NbO 8Cl[25] and Rh-Bi 4NbO 8Cl[26]. The intrinsic activity of surface active site depended largely on the local atomic structure, coordination environment and elec- tronic state. For photocatalytic water oxidation reaction, it was a multi-proton coupling four-electron transfer process involving two water molecules (2H 2O*?/C15OH*?/C15O*?/C15OOH*?/C15OO*?O2). The gradual formation of different intermediates mean that the active center needed to have the function of self-regulating coor- dination environment to adapt to different stages of elementary reactions [27–29] . The blocking of the elementary reaction at any stage leaded to other side reactions, such as the formation of/C15OH by single-hole oxidation of H 2O or the formation of H2O2by double-hole oxidation. Therefore, in order to ensure the complete occurrence of the four-electron oxidation reaction chain from H 2Ot oO 2, it was usually necessary to select the mul- tivalent transition metal center to adjust electronic state and pro- vide water oxidation reaction site [30–32] . The reversible valence cycle (+2/+3/+4) of cobalt catalytic center could meet the multi- step molecular mechanism of O 2/H2O, which attracted people widespread attention. Recently, Li et al. developed a new type of spatially separated dual-cocatalysts (FeCoO x, Ir) [33].A sa cocatalyst of water oxidation, FeCoO xexhibited better charge separation and OER performance than CoO xbecause the intro- duced Fe atom could well adjust the geometry and electronic structure of the Co active site. When BiVO 4modiﬁed by double cocatalysts (FeCoO x, Ir) was further combined with H 2evolution photocatalyst (ZrO 2/TaON or MgTa 2O6-xNy/TaON), it showed arecord POWS activity in the suspended particle system of inor- ganic semiconductor materials. At 420 nm, the apparent quan- tum efﬁciency and solar hydrogen production efﬁciency reached 12.3% and 0.6%, respectively. However, the chemical structure and formation mechanism of FeCoO xhad not been fully clariﬁed. Therefore, it was of great signiﬁcance to increase the sites of water oxidation reaction by selectively depositing FeCoO xon the oxidation crystal surface of single crystal semiconductor Bi4NbO 8Cl. Oxygen vacancies (OVs), as typical anionic vacancies, had excel- lent characteristics of reduction site [34–36] due to their unsatu- rated coordination structure and abundant localized electrons. It had been widely used in photocatalytic molecular oxygen activa- tion [37,38] ,N2ﬁxation [39,40] ,C O 2reduction [41,42] and electron sacriﬁce reaction [8]. In terms of electron transfer, OVs can satisfy the single electron transfer from Fe3+to Fe2+. However, up to date, OVs were usually constructed by one-step high-temperature calci- nation or chemical reduction, which led to the random distribution on the surface of photocatalysts. Therefore, the internal inﬂuence of the location of OVs on the catalytic performance remained in the density functional theory (DFT) theoretical calculation and the adjustment of crystal surface exposure for a long time [43– 47]. Emphatically, the comprehensive understanding of the rela- tionship between OVs and performance not only related to the local atomic geometry of OVs, but also should consider the space charge aggregation effect. However, OVs with electron capture capability could only play the role of promoting charge separation and providing reduction sites when they were located on the sur- face of the electron-enriched crystal. Therefore, it was an attractive strategy to construct OVs on the reduced crystal surface of single crystal semiconductor Bi 4NbO 8Cl to accelerate the electron shuttle reaction (Fe3+/F e2+,I O 3/C0/I/C0, [Fe(CN) 6]3/C0/[Fe(CN) 6]4/C0). At the same time, it was also faced with the huge challenges brought by the controllable synthesis of oxygen vacancies and the visualization of distribution. In this study, water oxidation photocatalyst FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl- OVs with spatially separated double active sites was prepared by hydrogen reduction-photoanode deposition method. The FeCoPi and OVs provided active site for the water oxidation reaction and the electron shuttle reaction, respectively. The correct spatial load- ing positions of FeCoPi and OVs enhanced the built-in electric ﬁeld in the space charge region below the Bi 4NbO 8Cl, which promoted the separation of photogenerated charges. The water oxidation performance of FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs has been signiﬁcantly improved. The hydrogen evolution co-catalyst PtRu@Cr 2O3had been synthesized by sequential photodeposition method. Due to effective regulation of Ru and Pt to work function, the hydrogen evolution rate of PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3was signiﬁcantly increased. Furthermore, FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs and PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3was used as oxygen evolution photocatalyst and hydrogen evolution photocatalyst, respectively, Z - Scheme photocatalytic Overall water splitting system successfully was constructed with Fe3+/ Fe2+. The results revealed the importance and effectiveness of rea- sonable construction of double active site for accelerating surface water oxidation reaction and promoting bulk charge separation. 2. Experimental 2.1. Materials BiOCl precursor was prepared by hydrothermal method. Except for NaCl and CsCl purchased from Aladdin reagent, other reagents used were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagents Co., Ltd. All chemicals were of analytical grade, and no further puriﬁcation.K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 569
