2.2. Synthesis of single crystal Bi 4NbO 8Cl Using the eutectic mixture of cesium chloride and sodium chlo- ride as the ﬂux (the molar ratio of CsCl/NaCl was 65:35, the melt- ing point Tm= 486 /C176C), the single crystal Bi 4NbO 8Cl was synthesized by ﬂux method. Fully grinded 0.75 mmol Bi 2O3, 0.5 mmol BiOCl, 0.25 mmol Nb 2O5and ﬂux CsCl/NaCl (solute con- centration Bi 4NbO 8Cl/(Bi 4NbO 8Cl + ﬂux) was 5 mol%) in an agate mortar. Then put the mixture into 25 mL alumina crucible, annealed in mufﬂe furnace at 750 /C176C for 6 h at heating rate of 2 /C176- C min/C01. After natural cooled to room temperature, washed thor- oughly with deionized water and anhydrous ethanol, collected bright yellow solid by centrifugation, and dried in vacuum at 60/C176C for 12 h, single crystal Bi 4NbO 8Cl was obtained. It was recorded as BNC. 2.3. Synthesis of Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs OVs were introduced through the gas–solid reaction of H 2(g) - Bi4NbO 8Cl (s) with H 2generated from the thermal decomposition of NaBH 4as the reducing atmosphere. 0.2 g Bi 4NbO 8Cl powder and 0.6 g NaBH 4were evenly distributed at both ends of 20 mL porcelain boat, which was placed in a tubular furnace. Then, Ar gas was ﬂowed (10 mL min/C01) and heated at 420 /C176C for 2 h (2 /C176- C min/C01). After the reaction, washed with deionized water and anhydrous ethanol several times, and dried in vacuum at 60 /C176C for 12 h, Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs was obtained, recorded as BNC-OVs. 2.4. Synthesis of FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs FeCoPi was loaded on the photoanode of Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs by in- situ photodeposition. 0.2 g Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs powder was dispersed in 50 mL phosphate buffer solution (25 mM PBS, pH = 6) containing 0.5 mM K 3[Fe(CN) 6], and then 1 mL CoSO 4(1 mg mL/C01) aqueous solution was added dropwise under magnetic stirring. The mixed solution was irradiated with a 300 W xenon lamp for 2 h. FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs were obtained by centrifugation and wash- ing, named as FeCoPi/BNC-OVs. 2.5. Synthesis of PbTiO 3nanoplates PbTiO 3nanoplates were prepared by hydrothermal method [48]. 1.6 g TiO 2(P25), 16.83 g KOH and 8.28 g Pb(NO 3)2were addedto the 50 mL deionized water, stirred for 30 min. Subse- quently, the obtained suspension was transferred to 100 mL Teﬂon lined stainless steel reactor and heated at 200 /C176C for 12 h. After the reaction, PbTiO 3nanoplates was obtained by washing with deion- ized water and drying vacuum at 60 /C176C for 12 h. 2.6. Synthesis of PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3 PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3was prepared by sequential photodeposi- tion method. 0.2 g PbTiO 3powder was dispersed in 100 mL 20 vol% methanol aqueous solution. 4.2 mL 1 mg mL/C01H2PtCl 6 (Pt/C241wt%) aqueous solution was added under magnetic stirring. Then, the mixed suspension was irradiated with 300 W xenon lamp for 1 h to complete the photodeposition of Pt. Subsequently, 2.87 mL of 1 mg mL/C01RuCl 3(Ru/C240.7 wt%) aqueous solution was added, and the photodeposition of Ru was completed by continu- ing light irradiation for 2 h. Finally, 5 mL 1 mg mL/C01K2CrO 4aque- ous solution was added and lighted for 4 h, the photodeposition of Cr2O3was completed. PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3was obtained by cen- trifugation and washing. Using the same photodeposition process, Pt/PbTiO 3was obtained by adding only H 2PtCl 6aqueous solution, Ru/PbTiO 3 was obtained by adding only RuCl 3aqueous solution, and PtRu/PbTiO 3was obtained by sequentially adding H 2PtCl 6and RuCl 3 aqueous solutions. 2.7. Characterization The microstructure of the sample was observed by JSM-7610F scanning electron microscope (SEM). The high resolution image (HR-TEM) of the sample was tested using JEM-F200 transmission electron microscope. The crystal structure of the sample was deter- mined by XRD-7000 X-ray powder diffractometer. Cu- Katarget source, Ni ﬁlter, k= 0.15418 nm, scanning speed of 4 /C176min/C01, accel- erating voltage of 40 kV, applied current of 30 mA. The functional groups on the sample surface were analyzed by IRAFFINITY-1S Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR). The chemical structure of the sample was tested by LabRAM HR Evolution Raman spectrometer.Thermo Scientiﬁc ESCALAB Xi + X-ray photoelectron spectrometer was used to determine the element composition and chemical state of the photocatalyst surface, C1s = 284.8 eV as the standard. The electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) test was carried out on the Bruker EMXplus 10/12 spectrometer. The ultraviolet visible dif- fuse reﬂectance spectrum (UV–Vis DRS) of photocatalyst was determined by UV-2550 ultraviolet visible spectrophotometer, ref- erence standard as BaSO 4, scanning range of 200–800 nm. The Zeta potential of the sample was determined by the Malvern Zetasizer Nano ZS90 Zeta potential analyzer. ThermoFisher Nexsa in-situ irradiation X-ray photoelectron spectrometer was used to monitor the chemical state changes of samples under dark and light condi- tions. The morphology and thickness of the sample were measured by Bruker Dimension Icon atomic force microscope, and the surface potential changes of the sample under dark and light were mea- sured under Kelvin probe mode. The surface photovoltage of the sample was tested by CEL-SPS1000 surface photovoltage spec- trometer. The time-resolved ﬂuorescence decay spectrum was measured by the FLS920 transient. 2.8. Photocatalytic water splitting Photocatalytic water oxidation was carried out in Labsolar-6A (Perfect Light). 50 mg photocatalyst was dispersed into 100 mL FeCl 3aqueous solution (5 mM, adjusted pH to 2.4 with HCl). Sub- sequently, the reactor was connected to a closed gas circulation system, and controlled the reaction temperature to 283 K through circulating cooling water. The 300 W xenon lamp with 400 nm cut- off ﬁlter was used as the visible light source. Before the start of the photocatalytic reaction, the air in the suspension and glass system was completely removed through argon replacement and multiple vacuum pumping. Photocatalytic hydrogen evolution was carried out in the same Labsolar-6A. 50 mg photocatalyst was dispersed into 100 mL of 20 vol% methanol aqueous solution. Z-Scheme photocatalytic Overall water splitting system was carried out using FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs and PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3 as photocatalyst for O 2and H 2evolution, respectively. Using the same experimental setup, 50 mg FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs and 50 mg PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3were dispersed into 100 mL 2 mM FeCl 3 aqueous solution (pH = 2.4). The generated O 2and H 2were mea- sured by online gas chromatography. 2.9. Theoretical calculations We have employed the Vienna Ab Initio Package (VASP) [49,50] to perform all DFT calculations within the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) using the PBE [51] formulation. We have chosen the projected augmented wave (PAW) potentials [52,53] to describe the ionic cores and take valence electrons into account using a plane wave basis set with a kinetic energy cutoff of 400 eV.K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 570
