Partial occupancies of the Kohn-Sham orbitals were allowed using the Gaussian smearing method and a width of 0.05 eV. The elec- tronic energy was considered self-consistent when the energy change was smaller than 10/C05eV. A geometry optimization was considered convergent when the force change was smaller than 0.02 eV Å/C01. Grimme’s DFT-D3 methodology [54] was used to describe the dispersion interactions. The equilibrium lattice constants of orthorhombic Bi 4NbO 8Cl unit cell were optimized, when using a 5 /C25/C21 Monkhorst- Pack k-point grid for Brillouin zone sampling, to be a= 5.504 Å, b= 5.538 Å, c= 27.907 Å. We then use it to construct two surfaces. One is Bi 4NbO 8Cl (001) surface model (model 1) with p(1/C21) peri- odicity in the /C2and ydirections and one stoichiometric layer in the zdirection separated by a vacuum layer in the depth of 15 Å in order to separate the surface slab from its periodic duplicates.Model 1 comprises 16 Bi, 4 Nb, 32O and 4Cl atoms. During struc- tural optimizations, a 4 /C24/C21 k-point grid in the Brillouin zone was used for k-point sampling, and the bottom half stoichiometric layer was ﬁxed while the top half was allowed to relax. The other one is Bi 4NbO 8Cl (010) surface model (model 2) with p(1/C21) peri- odicity in the /C2and ydirections and two stoichiometric layers in thezdirection separated by a vacuum layer in the depth of 15 Å in order to separate the surface slab from its periodic duplicates. Model 2 comprises 32 Bi, 8 Nb, 64O and 8Cl atoms. During struc- tural optimizations, a 4 /C21/C21 k-point grid in the Brillouin zone was used for k-point sampling, and the bottom stoichiometric layer was ﬁxed while the top one was allowed to relax.The adsorp- tion energy ( Eads) of adsorbate A was deﬁned as Eads=EA=surf—Esurf—EAðgÞ where EA/surf ,Esurfand EA(g)are the energy of adsorbate A adsorbed on the surface, the energy of clean surface, and the energy of isolated A molecule in a cubic periodic box with a side length of 20 Å and a 1 /C21/C21 Monkhorst-Pack k-point grid for Bril- louin zone sampling, respectively. 3. Results and discussion 3.1. The construction of spatially separated FeCoPi and OVs The traditional one-step high-temperature calcination or chem- ical reduction method will inevitably lead to the random distribu- tion of OVs on the surface of photocatalyst. In order to introduce OVs only on the photocathode surface of Bi 4NbO 8Cl single crystal photocatalyst, a photoanode protection method was developed. The preparation process was shown in Fig. 1 (a). Firstly, Bi 4NbO 8Cl single crystal photocatalyst was synthesized by ﬂux method. Then, PbO 2protective layer was loaded on the (001) crystal face of Bi 4NbO 8Cl single crystal by photoanode depo- sition. Finally, Bi 4NbO 8Cl with OVs only on (010) crystal face was obtained by removal PbO 2coating through hydrogen reduction. OVs with the ability to capture electrons could only play a promot- ing role when they were located on enriched electron crystal planes, which was researched by the experiment of photocatalytic molecular oxygen activation (the detailed discussion in supple- mentary information ). Furthermore, FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs photo- catalyst was synthesized by the charge separation effect between the crystal planes of low symmetry single crystal particles. Firstly, argon was used as carrier gas and protective gas. When the tubular furnace was heated to 420 /C176C, NaBH 4would decompose and gener- ate hydrogen. The resulting hydrogen reacted with Bi 4NbO 8Cl and OVs was formed. At this time, there were OVs on the (001) and (010) crystal face of Bi 4NbO 8Cl, that is, OVs were randomly dis- tributed on the crystal face of Bi 4NbO 8Cl. Subsequently, FeCoPi was selectively loaded on the (001) crystal face by photoanodedeposition and simultaneously covered the OVs on the (001) crys- tal face. Finally, OVs (010)/Bi 4NbO 8Cl/FeCoPi (001) was obtained. The spatial loading position of OVs and FeCoPi was consistent with the photogenerated charge migration path and aggregation site of single crystal Bi 4NbO 8Cl. Therefore, the OVs located on the reduced crystal plane (010) and FeCoPi located on the oxidized crystal plane (001) provided a great possibility for the formation of spatially sep- arated double active sites. Bi 4NbO 8Cl was recorded as BNC. The spatial loading position of FeCoPi on the surface of single crystal Bi 4NbO 8C was visually detected by SEM and TEM. As shown inFig. 1 (b and c) and Fig. S3 (a), BNC was composed of regular square nanoplates and had a clear exposed crystal surface. From Fig. 1 (d), the mutually perpendicular lattice stripes were clearly observed. The crystal plane spacing of 0.275 and 0.273 nm was cor- respond to the (020) crystal plane and (200) crystal plane of BNCrespectively, indicating that the top exposed crystal plane of BNC was (001) plane, and the side exposed crystal plane was (010) plane and (100) plane (the crystal space model was shown in Fig. 1 e). Compared with the ordered lattice stripes of BNC, the (001) crystal surface of BNC-OVs had a local lattice disorder struc- ture (yellow circle in Fig. S3 d), because of the surface unsaturated coordination caused by OVs [41,42] . After loading FeCoPi by pho- toanode deposition, it could be clearly observed from SEM of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs ( Fig. 1 f,Fig. S3 f and g), a large number of nanodots distributed on the (001) crystal surface. There was no obvious nanoparticle attachment on its side ( Fig. 1 h), which fully showed that FeCoPi was selectively loaded on the (001) crystal plane of BNC, and also covered OVs (001) (the crystal space model was shown in Fig. 1 i). The EDX showed the uniform distribution of Fe and Co elements on the FeCoPi/ BNC-OVS (001) crystal plane, which further indicated that FeCoPi completely covered the OVs on the BNC(001) crystal plane ( Fig. 1 k–p). The TEM in the vertical direction of the square nanoplate ( Fig. 1 j) further provided direct evidence for the spatial selective loading of FeCoPi. In addition, TEM of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs ( Fig. 1 g) showed that there was no change in the morphology and structure of BNC during the calcination pro- cess. SEM after deposition of PbO 2protective layer on BNC single crystals was shown in Fig. S4 (a and b). The (001) crystal plane was covered with a large number of PbO 2particles. Only BNC nanoplates with larger transverse sizes were not completely cov- ered by PbO 2particles, which was due to the small internal electric ﬁeld driving force of pure BNC and the short carrier diffusion dis- tance, which made it difﬁcult for the photogenerated charge to migrate to the surface of BNC with larger sizes. Partial coverage of the PbO 2protective layer could prevent (001) crystal plane reduction to some extent. The SEM image after removing the PbO 2protective layer was shown in Fig. S4 (c and d). The nanopar- ticles on the surface of BNC-OVs (010) were signiﬁcantly reduced compared to PbO 2/BNC, indicating that the PbO 2protective layer was removed. XRD of the sample was shown in Fig. 2 (a), all diffraction peaks of BNC could be well indexed to orthogonal Bi 4NbO 8Cl (JCPDS No.84–0843), where the diffraction peaks at 12.3 /C176and 32.5 /C176corre- sponded to (004) and (020) crystal planes, respectively. It indicated the successful preparation of pure phase Bi 4NbO 8Cl. In addition, the characteristic of peak of narrow and strong diffraction intensity revealed the formation of Bi 4NbO 8Cl single crystal. After introduc- ing OVs through calcination, the diffraction peaks of BNC-OVs and BNC-OVs (010) were still consistent with that of BNC, indicating that the calcination temperature of 420 /C176C would not cause the phase change of original Bi 4NbO 8Cl. No diffraction peaks of lead oxide species were observed in the XRD patterns of BNC-OVs (010), indi- cating removal of the PbO 2protective layer. After deposition of FeCoPi, no new diffraction peak was observed, which was due to the low amorphous nature and content of FeCoPi. Worthwhile, (FeCoO x, Ir)/BiVO 4was synthesized by Li et al. [33], 0.2 g BiVO 4K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 571
