was dispersed in 50 mL phosphate buffer containing [Fe(CN) 6]3/C0 and CoSO 4(25 mM, PBS, pH = 6), and then 300 W xenon light was irradiated for 2 h for in-situ photodeposition. According to the characterization results, the chemical structure of the water oxidation catalyst was ﬁnally determined as FeCoO x(nanocompos- ite of FeOOH and CoOOH). However, according to other researchreports [55–58] , the photocatalyst was dispersed in PBS containing Co 2+could form CoPi water oxidation co-catalyst under light. Therefore, it was reasonable that the FeCoO xsynthesized by Li et al. not only had the composition characteristics of FeOOH and CoOOH, but also should have the chemical composition of CoPi. The FT-IR spectrum was shown in Fig. 2 (b). The absorption peaks at 456 and 558 cm/C01were the tensile vibration of the Bi–O bond, and the absorption peaks at 614 and 722 cm/C01were the tensile vibration of the Nb–O bond in the NbO 4layer [59–61] . The absorp- tion peak at 3458 cm/C01was the –OH vibration in the water mole- cules adsorbed on the surface of the photocatalyst, and enhanced absorption peak intensity may be related to the introduction of OVs. BNC-OVs had a new absorption peak at 1634 cm/C01, which could be attributed to the –OH group adsorbed on the unsaturated coordination OVs. After photodeposition of FeCoPi, a new vibration band centered at 1050 cm/C01appeared in FeCoPi/BNC-OVs, which belonged to the characteristic absorption peak of PO 4group [62–64]. Evidently, PO 43/C0also participated in the formation of FeCoO x. In addition, the absorption peak appeared at 2107 cm/C01was attrib- uted to the successful loading of FeCoPi. The Raman spectra of BNC, BNC-OVs and FeCoPi/BNC-OVs were shown in Fig. 2 (c). The peaks at 100 and 156 cm/C01were corresponded to the Bi–O bond in the [Bi2O2]2+layer, and the peak at 247 cm/C01was caused by the bend- ing vibration of the Nb–O–Nb bond in the NbO 6octahedron. While the peak at 600 cm/C01was agreed to the octahedral bond sharing an angle between the [Bi 2O2]2+layer [13,65] . The Raman spectra of BNC, BNC-OVs and FeCoPi/BNC-OVs were similar, which showed that the heating process and light radiation process would not lead to the chemical structure change of BNC (as shown in the illustra- tion of Fig. 2 c). In order to further conﬁrm the successful loading of FeCoPi and the introduction of OVs, the chemical state and composition were analyzed by XPS. In the XPS full spectrum of BNC and FeCoPi/BNC- OVs ( Fig. S5 a), the elements of Bi, Nb, O, Cl, Fe, Co and P were detected, indicating that FeCoPi was successfully loaded onto the (001) plane of BNC single crystal. The high-resolution P 2 ppeak at the binding energy of 133.2 eV ( Fig. 2 d) showed that P existed in the form of phosphate radical [57,58] . This further showed that FeCoPi was composed of FeOOH, CoOOH and PO 43/C0. The formation mechanism and chemical composition were shown in supplemen- Fig. 1. (a) Scheme of preparation process of Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs (010) and FeCoPi/Bi 4NbO 8Cl-OVs. (b–e) BNC and (f–j) SEM and TEM of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs and spatial model of single crystal. (k–p) EDX of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs.K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 572
