tary information , and the chemical formula could be expressed as Fe3Co(PO 4)4. Compared with BNC, the Bi 4 fpeak and Nb 3 dpeak of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs shifted 0.4 and 0.5 eV to the direction of highbinding energy respectively ( Fig. 2 e and f), indicating that there was a strong interaction between FeCoPi and BNC. At the same time, the ﬂow direction of electrons from BNC to FeCoPi revealed the upward energy band bending at the BNC contact interface. From The HR-XPS of Fe 2 pof FeCoPi/BNC-OVs ( Fig. 2 g), the two characteristic peaks at 711.7 and 725.3 eV belonged to Fe 2 p 3/2 and Fe 2 p1/2of Fe3+, respectively. In the O 1 sHR-XPS of BNC and BNC-OVs ( Fig. 2 h), the peaks at 529.7, 531.0, and 531.8 eV corre- sponded to lattice oxygen, OVs, and surface adsorbed oxygen, respectively. Compared with BNC, the percentage of OVs in BNC- OVs increased from 8.2% to 19.2%, which was attributed to the con- tribution of –OH adsorbed on the surface OVs [8,66] . In addition, in the Bi 4 fHR-XPS ( Fig. 2 e) and Nb 3 dHR-XPS ( Fig. 2 f) of BNC-OVs, compared with BNC, after the introduction of OVs, BNC-OVs had a slight shift in the direction of low binding energy, which was related to the low metal state formed by OVs. EPR of the sample was shown in Fig. 2 (i), BNC-OVs had a typical EPR signal centered ong= 2.003, which corresponded to the Zeeman effect of unpaired electrons trapped in oxygen vacancies [37,67] , fully showed that OVs had been successfully introduced into BNC. In addition, com-pared to BNC-OVs, the EPR signal intensity of BNC-OVs (010) was weaker, indicating that the concentration of OVs on BNC-OVs (010) was low, which was related to present of OVs only in the (010) crystal plane. 3.2. Effect of active sites of FeCoPi and OVs FeCoPi and OVs located on the (001) and (010) crystal face of single crystal BNC provided necessary space conditions for the for- mation of spatially separated double active sites. However, whether FeCoPi and OVs could play a speciﬁc active site role in the photocatalytic water oxidation needed further discussed. Firstly, the density of states and electrostatic potential of (001) and (010) crystal face were calculated by using density functional theory (DFT). As shown in Fig. 3 (a–c), the position of CB on the BNC (001) surface was higher than that on the (010) crystal face, and the electrostatic potential on the (001) and (010) crystal face was 5.85 and 6.57 eV, respectively, indicated that photogenerated elec- trons tended to transfer from (001) to (010) in thermodynamics, while photogenerated holes tended to transfer from (010) to (001) [68–70] . So, the photogenerated electrons and holes gathered on the (010) and (001) crystal face, respectively. In order to con- ﬁrm the spatial distribution of photogenerated charges, the selec- Fig. 2. XRD patterns (a), FT-IR spectra (b), Raman spectra (c) of BNC, BNC-OVs, BNC-OVs (010) and FeCoPi/BNC-OVs samples. (d) P 2 p, (e) Bi 4 f, (f) Nb 3 d, (g) Fe 2 pHR-XPS spectrum of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs. (h) O 1 sHR-XPS spectra of BNC and BNC-OVs. (i) EPR patterns of BNC, BNC-OVs (010) and BNC-OVs.K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 573
