tive photodeposition experiments of Ag and PbO 2were carried out on BNC using AgNO 3and Pb(NO 3)2as precursors [71]. As shown in Fig. 3 (d–f), compared with the smooth surface of the original BNC, Ag nanoparticles were deposited on (010) crystal planes, while PbO 2particles were mainly deposited on the (001) crystal planes, separately. Therefore, the photodeposition directly reﬂected that the photocatalytic reduction and oxidation reactions occurred on the (010) and (001) crystal face, respectively, which were consis-tent with the DFT results. Importantly, the anisotropic charge dis- tribution was consistent with the spatial loading position of FeCoPi and OVs, the water oxidation assistant FeCoPi was located on the hole enrichment surface, and the OVs with the ability of electron capture were located on the electron enrichment surface. Accord- ing to UV–Vis DRS spectrogram ( Fig. 3 g), the calculated band gap (E g) of BNC was 2.42 eV [72,73] . From Mott-Schottky curve (Fig. 3 h), the CB potential and VB potential of BNC were calculated to be /C00.35 and +2.07 V, respectively. The crystal face energy level position and charge transfer of BNC were plotted as shown in Fig. 3 (i). In the solution environment with pH = 2.4, because the VB potential of BNC was corrected than O 2/H2O (+1.09 V vs. SHE) (the oxidation driving force was DEOdf= 0.98 V), and the CB poten- tial was more negative than Fe3+/Fe2+(+0.77 V vs. SHE) (the reduc- tion driving force was DERdf= 1.12 V). it was thermodynamically feasible to use the photogenerated holes and electrons of BNC to drive water oxidation and electron shuttle reaction.The absence of oxygen atoms usually leaded to the accumula- tion of a large number of local electrons near OVs, forming electron-rich sites [39,41,74] . Because of the coordination unsatu- rated nature of OVs, the local electrons were expected to be injected into the Fe 3 dorbit of Fe3+through charge interaction, thus reduced Fe3+. In order to clearly analyze whether OVs could be used as reducing active sites of Fe3+, the charge density differ- ence of FeCl 3-BNC-OVs (010) model was calculated. As shown in Fig. 4 (a and b) the depletion of electrons on OVs and the accumu- lation of electrons on Fe3+indicated that the charge was trans- ferred from OVs to adsorbed FeCl 3. Therefore, OVs as active sites could provide more electrons to reduce Fe3+. OVs could also become adsorption sites of H 2O molecules in FeCl 3aqueous solu- tion, the charge density difference of H 2O-BNC-OVs (010) model was further calculated. As shown in Fig. 4 (c), the charge interaction between H 2O molecule and OVs was signiﬁcantly weakened, and the accumulation of electrons near OVs indicated that the electrons could not be used to reduce H 2O. At the same time, the adsorption energy Eadsof FeCl 3and H 2O molecules at OVs were calculated as/C03.38 and /C00.85 eV, respectively, which fully showed that OVs could provide the active sites of Fe3+reduction. DFT was used to calculate the density of crystal plane states of BNC-OVs (010). As shown in Fig. 4 (d), VB was mainly composed of highly dispersed O 2porbitals rather than Cl 3 porbitals, and CB was mainly composed of Bi 6 porbitals. This unique VB structure gave anti-hole autoxida- Fig. 3. DFT calculates (a) band structure and (b, c) electrostatic potential of BNC (001) and (010) surfaces. (d–f) SEM of in-situ photodeposition of Ag and Pb O2on BNC. (g) UV– Vis DRS of BNC, BNC-OVs and FeCoPi/BNC-OVs. (h) Mott-Schottky diagram of BNC in 0.5 M Na 2SO4(pH = 2.4) solution. (i) Schematic diagram of energy level position and charge transfer on BNC (001) and (010) surfaces.K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 574
