and 164.7 eV belonged to Bi 4 f7/2and Bi 4 f5/2of BNC-OVs. Under light irradiation, the binding energy of Bi 4 fhad a signiﬁcant neg- ative shift ( /C00.3 eV). In-situ HR-XPS of Nb 3 dof FeCoPi/BNC-OVs was shown in Fig. 4 (h). With absent of light irradiation, the two peaks at 206.8 and 209.6 eV belonged to Nb 3 d5/2and Nb 3 d3/2of BNC-OVs. Under light irradiation, the binding energy of Nb 3 d had a signiﬁcant negative shift ( /C00.4 eV). It was consistent with the change of chemical state of Bi element, indicating that the pho- togenerated hole ﬂowed into FeCoPi from BNC-OVs under light irradiation [76]. The opposite result was observed in the Co 2 p spectrum, as shown in Fig. 4 (i). In the absence of light, the two peaks at 781.1 and 797.0 eV belonged to Co 2 p3/2and Co 2 p1/2of FeCoPi. Under light irradiation, the binding energy of Co 2 phad a signiﬁcant positive shift (+0.4 eV), which indicated that the elec- tron density of FeCoPi in FeCoPi/BNC-OVs decreased under lightirradiation, which meaned that photogenerated holes were enriched at Co site. It demonstrated that FeCoPi could effectively extract photogenic holes in BNC and provided active sites for water oxidation reaction in the photocatalysis. However, in the Fe 2 pISI- XPS spectrum ( Fig. 4 j), the chemical state change opposite to that of Co was observed. Under light irradiation, the binding energy of Fe 2pshifted by /C00.6 eV, forming a low valence Fe +(3/C0x). Therefore, Fe was not directly used as the water oxidation site, but con- tributed to regulate the electronic geometry of Co site in OER. At the same time, the valence change of Fe3+/Fe+(3/C0x)could serve as the internal electron transfer medium, thus promoting the rapid transmission of photogenerated holes. As shown in Fig. 4 (k), the water oxidation ability of FeCoPi was evaluated. Compared with the original BNC, FeCoPi/BNC and FeCoPi/BNC-OVs showed lower initial potential and greater current response, indicating that FeCoPi as an active site could signiﬁcantly promotes the water oxi- dation process [33,77] . Therefore, a collaborative OER process pro- moted by Fe and Co atoms in FeCoPi was proposed, as shown in Fig. 4 (l). The Co site was directly the place where the water oxida- tion reaction took place. While the Fe site not only promoted the fast transport of photogenerated charges through valence state conversion, but only regulated the geometric electronic structure of Co in the OER process. Summary, FeCoPi/BNC-OVs photocatalyst with spatially sepa- rated double active sites had been successfully constructed through precise design and regulation. FeCoPi located on BNC (001) surface provided active sites for water oxidation reaction, and OVs located on BNC (010) surface provided active sites for electron shuttle reaction (Fe3+/Fe2+). At the same time, the separa- tion of FeCoPi and OVs in space could effectively avoid the occur- rence of reverse reaction of O 2and Fe2+. 3.3. Photocatalytic oxygen evolution performance Under visible light （k> 400 nm), the water oxidation perfor- mance was evaluated, and the results were shown in Fig. 5 (a). The original BNC showed extremely low water oxidation activity due to serious lack of water oxidation sites and Fe3+reduction sites, and the O 2evolution rate was 0.5 lmol h/C01. After loading FeCoPi and OVs, the water oxidation performance was signiﬁcantly enhanced, and the O 2evolution rate was 16.8 lmol h/C01, 32.9 times the original BNC. The signiﬁcantly improved water oxidation per- formance was due to the increase in the number of double active sites and spatial separation. At the same time, the O 2evolution rate of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs with spatially separated double active sites was much higher than that of the water oxidation sites (FeCoPi/BNC) or Fe3+reduction sites (BNC-OVs). In addition, the water oxidation activity of BNC OVs (010) was slightly lower than that of BNC OVs, because the lead chloride oxide on the (001) crystal plane had a greater inhibition on water oxidation than OVs. The effect of content ratio of CoPi/FePi in Fe xCo(PO 4)1+x(0 < x< 3) on thewater oxidation performance of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs was further dis- cussed. As shown in Fig. 5 (b), the generation of FePi in FeCoPi depended on the photodeposition of Co2+, the added amount of Co2+directly determined the amount of CoPi or FeCoPi deposited on the surface of BNC-OVs, while the the proportion of CoPi/FePi content in FeCoPi was adjusted by amount of [Fe(CN) 6]3/C0. In the active site saturation region (Sras), with the increasing of [Fe (CN) 6]3/C0concentration, the FePi in FeCoPi increased. In the range of [Fe(CN) 6]3/C0concentration < 0.5 mM, the tight horizontal contour revealed the effective regulation of FePi on the intrinsic activity of CoPi. After the concentration of [Fe(CN) 6]3/C0exceeded 0.5 mM, the O2evolution rate of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs no longer increased with the addition of [Fe(CN) 6]3/C0, but remained unchanged. It showed that the proportion of FePi in FeCoPi had reached the upper limit, which might be related to that Fe 3Co(PO 4)4was the most stable state of FexCo(PO 4)1+x(0 < x< 3). In addition, due to the insufﬁcient number of water oxidation active sites and the light shielding effect of excessive FeCoPi in the left and right sides of Sras, the O 2evolution rate was relatively low. To further explain the key active components of FeCoPi, CoO x, FeCoO x, CoPi and FeCoPi were deposited in situ on the surface of BNC. As shown in Fig. 5 (c), the oxygen yields were 15.3, 21.0, 9.1 and 76.2 lmol h/C01g/C01, respectively. Among them, the promotion effect of FeCoPi for water oxidation was far better than that of CoO x, FeCoO xand CoPi. It indicated that the addition of Fe played a key role in the catalytic activity of FeCoPi. OVs with abundant local electrons became effective reduction sites through strong Coulomb electrostatic action on Fe3+cations. To further verify this fact, the surface reaction rates in the presence of different elec- tron acceptors were investigated. As shown in Fig. 5 (d), when the cation Fe3+and Ag+were used as electron acceptors, the O 2 evolution rate as 16.8 and 10.8 lmol h/C01, respectively. When anions [Fe(CN) 6]3/C0and IO 3/C0were used as electron acceptors, the O2evolution rate were 3.2 and 1.2 lmol h/C01, respectively. The surface reaction rate of cations as electron acceptors was signiﬁ- cantly higher than that of anions. A scheme of the experiment to measure AQE was shown in Fig. 5 (e), where the outside of the reactor was wrapped in tinfoil to prevent reﬂection of the inci- dent light into the environment. The height of the light source to the reaction liquid level was controlled at 8.8 cm. Considering the uneven intensity distribution of the circular incident light from the center to the edge position, the average value along the radius direction was taken as the ﬁnal light intensity. Table S2 provided the test results and calculation process of each parameter. The AQE of photocatalytic O 2evolution on FeCoPi/ BNC-OVs was shown in Fig. 5 (f). The values of AQE at 365, 400, 420, 450, and 500 nm were 0.37%, 0.27%, 0.25%, 0.24%, and 0.08%, respectively, which was consistent with the trend of UV–vis DRS, indicating that the water oxidation reaction of FeCoPi/ BNC-OVs was driven by absorbed photons. In addition, the O 2 evolution rate of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs was signiﬁcantly better thanthat of most previously reported inorganic oxygen evolution pho- tocatalysts supported with noble metals ( Fig. 5 g), such as TiO 2/ IrO x, R-TiO 2: N, F/RuO 2, m-BiVO 4-OV/rGO, TaON/RuO 2,a-Fe 2O3/ Au, Bi 4NbO 8Cl/Rh and PbBiO 2Cl/(Fe,Ru)O x.Table S3 provided detailed conditions and corresponding references. The stability of the photocatalyst was important parameter to evaluate the performance of the photocatalyst. Four OER cycles were performed on the BNC-OVs photocatalyst, and the results were shown in Fig. 6 (a). The O 2evolution rate remained basically the same during the four cycles, which were 1.73, 1.77, 1.65 and 1.60lmol h/C01, respectively. The EPR of BNC-OVs after the OER cycling experiment was shown in Fig. 6 (b), where the typical signal of OVs centered at g= 2.003 could still be observed, and no signif- icant decreased in signal intensity occurs. The results indicated that OVs had good chemical stability during the reaction. TheK. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 576
