results of the OER cycling experiments for FeCoPi/BNC photocata- lysts were shown in Fig. 6 (c). After four OER cycles, the oxygen evolution rate decreased from 3.81 to 2.28 lmol h/C01, which might be due to the chemical structure transformation or dissolution of FeCoPi during OER. HR-XPS spectra of FeCoPi/BNC after OER cycling, as shown in Fig. 6 (d–f). There was no signiﬁcant shift of binding energy or appearance of new signal peaks for Fe 2 p,C o 2pand P 2 ppeaks, indicating that the main reason for the decreased activity was the dissolution of FeCoPi in FeCl 3aqueous solution (pH = 2.4) rather than photocorrosion. SEM images of FeCoPi/BNC after OER cycling were shown in Fig. 6 (g). Although the activity decreased, the uniform distribution of a large number of nanodots on the crystal plane of BNC (001) was still observed. The Fe3+concentration in 5 mM FeCl 3aqueous solution was high, so the dissolution rate of FeCoPi during OER mainly depended on the release of Co3+and PO 43/C0into the solution. The dissolution mechanism of FeCoPi in the process of OER was as follows (Eqs. (1)–(5) ). BNC ð001Þþhv!e/C0þhþð1Þ FeIII 3CoIIðPO4Þ4þhþ!FeIII 2FeIVCoIIðPO4Þ4 ð2Þ FeIII 2FeIVCoIIðPO4Þ4!FeIII 3CoIIIðPO4Þ4 ð3ÞFeIII 3CoIIIðPO4Þ4þOOH ads!FeIII 3CoIIðPO4Þ4þO2þHþð4Þ 3FeIII 2FeIVCoIIðPO4Þ4þ8HCl !FeIV 3ðPO4Þ4þ3Co2þþ8HPO2/C0 4 þ6Fe3þþ8Cl/C0ð5Þ In FeCoPi, the Fe atom was responsible for the fast transport of the photogenerated charge, and the Co atom was the site where the OER reaction taken place. First, the BNC generated photogener- ated electrons (e/C0) and holes (h+) under photoexcitation. Then, h+ was accepted by the Fe atom in FeCoPi and the valence state waschanged from + 3 to + 4. Subsequently, the Fe atom transmitted the trapped h +to the Co atom and restored to the + 3 valence state, while the valence state of the Co atom changed from + 2 to + 3. Finally, the OOH intermediate adsorbed on the Co(III) site received the h+and completed the fourth step of OER and released O 2. In the above process, Fe(IV) transported the h+to Co(II) more slowly. Therefore, Co2+ions and PO 43-ions would slowly dissolve through Eq.(5)in FeCl 3solution with pH = 2.4. Based on the above mecha- nistic analysis, the addition of HPO 42/C0to FeCl 3solution seemed to be an effective strategy to alleviate FeCoPi dissolution. To prevent Fe3+hydrolysis, we attempted to perform OER in PBS solution at pH = 2. Unfortunately, PO 43/C0in PBS solution reacted with Fe3+to form white ﬂocculent FePO 4(Fig. 6 h). FeCl 3was replaced by K3[Fe(CN) 6] in order to avoid the reaction of PO 4with the electron Fig. 5. (a) Time course of photocatalytic O 2evolution of the prepared sample. (b) Photocatalytic O 2evolution rate of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs prepared with different amounts of Co2+ and [Fe(CN) 6]3/C0. (c) Oxygen yield with different water oxidation co-catalysts deposited on the surface of BNC. The reaction conditions: 50 mg photocatalyst, 100 mL F eCl 3 aqueous solution (5 mM, pH = 2.4), visible light irradiation k> 400 nm, temperature 283 K. (d) Photocatalytic O 2evolution rate on FeCoPi/BNC-OVs in the presence of different electron acceptors. (e) Scheme representation of the experiment in AQE measurement. (f) AQE and UV–vis DRS of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs at 293 K. (g) O 2evolution rate on FeCoPi/ BNC-OVs with different oxygen evolution photocatalysts.K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 577
