sacriﬁce. The results of the OER cycling experiments of FeCoPi/BNC in PBS (25 mM, pH = 6) solution containing 5 mM K 3[Fe(CN) 6] were shown in Fig. 6 (i). The rate of O 2evolution during the three OER cycles remained basically the same at 1.61, 1.64 and 1.56 lmol h/C01, respectively. The signiﬁcant improvement in the stability of FeCoPi was attributed to the high concentration of PO 43/C0reacting with reoxidized Co3+to form CoPO 4precipitate, which repaired the dis- solution of FeCoPi in time. 3.4. Strong local electric ﬁeld induced by FeCoPi and OVs Effective spatial separation and migration of photogenerated charges to corresponding surface active sites were the premise of surface reaction [77]. Therefore, it was necessary to further under- stand the contribution of FeCoPi and OVs to photogenerated charge separation and transfer. Firstly, the spatial separation of photogen- erated charges at nanometer scale was studied by the in-situ pho- toradiation Kelvin probe force microscope (KPFM). KPFM images of BNC and FeCoPi/BNC-OVs were shown in Fig. 7 (a and b). Under light irradiation, the surface potential of BNC increases by 8.6 mV along the cross section of the dotted line ( Fig. 7 c). It indicated thatthere was an upward band bending in the space charge region (SCR) of BNC (001) [78]. Noteworthyly, after loading FeCoPi, the surface potential of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs along the cross section ofthe dotted line increased by 18.1 mV ( Fig. 7 d), which was 2.1 times of BNC. It showed that the loading of FeCoPi made the energy band bending of BNC (001) surface more intense, accompanied by the signiﬁcant enhancement of the built-in electric ﬁeld in the space charge region [79]. The result was also conﬁrmed in the surface photovoltage spectra of BNC, BNC-OVs and FeCoPi/BNC, as shown inFig. 7 (e). After loading FeCoPi on the (001) crystal planes, the SPV signal of FeCoPi/BNC increased by about 0.32 mV. The increased SPV signal indicated that a large number of photogener- ated holes were migrated towards the surface of the photocatalyst. Furthermore, the loading of FeCoPi further caused the upward of band bending of BNC (001) [80]. After introducing OVs on the sur- face of BNC, the SPV signal of BNC-OVs decreased by about 0.96 mV. The reduced SPV signal could be attributed to the migra- tion of photogenerated electrons to the surface of photocatalyst [81–83] . It showed that the introduction of OVs caused the down- ward of band bending of BNC(010). So, the spatially separated FeCoPi and OVs not only provided redox sites, but also induced Fig. 6. (a) OER cycling experiments on BNC-OVs photocatalysts. (b) EPR pattern of BNC-OVs after OER cycling. (c) OER cycling experiments on FeCoPi/BNC photo catalysts. (d) Fe 2p, (e) Co 2 p, (f) P 2 pHR-XPS spectrum of FeCoPi/BNC after OER cycling. (g) SEM images of FeCoPi/BNC after OER cycling. (h) Photographs with time after addition of 5 mM FeCl 3in phosphate buffer solution (25 mM PBS, pH = 2). (i) OER cycling experiments of FeCoPi/BNC in phosphate buffer solution (25 mM PBS, pH = 6) containing 5 m MK 3[Fe (CN) 6].K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 578
