and enhanced the built-in electric ﬁeld in the space charge region below the crystal surface. The mechanism of enhanced photogen- erated charge separation was shown in Fig. 7 (f). FeCoPi and OVs located on BNC (001) and (010) caused upward and downward band bending in the space charge region, respectively. Due to the increase of energy band bending, the built-in electric ﬁeld was sig- niﬁcantly enhanced (1.28 mV), which provided a strong driving force for the separation and migration of photogenerated charges [84–86] . Under photoexcitation, photogenerated holes and elec- trons were correctly guided to the (001) and (010) surfaces of BNC by the built-in electric ﬁeld, and then captured by FeCoPi with hole collection capability and OVs with electron capture capability respectively, and ﬁnally participated in speciﬁc surface catalytic reaction. The time-resolved ﬂuorescence decay spectrum, transient photocurrent response spectrum and electrochemical impedance spectrum directly reﬂected the effective separation of charges dri- ven by the built-in electric ﬁeld, and the results were shown in Fig. 7 (g–i). The ﬂuorescence decay curve ( Fig. 7 g) conformed to the double exponential decay function, and the average ﬂuores- cence lifetime of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs ( save) was 0.34 ns, which was shorter than that of BNC (0.44 ns). Therefore, FeCoPi/BNC-OVs had more efﬁcient photogenerated carrier transport performance [87,88] . In the transient photocurrent response spectrum (Fig. 7 h), the photocurrent density of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs increased, indicating that the separation and transfer of photogenerated charges had been signiﬁcantly improved. In the electrochemical impedance spectroscopy ( Fig. 7 i). The reduction of the Nyquistcurve radius of FeCoPi/BNC-OVs indicated that it had lower charge transfer resistance and effective charge transfer. 3.5. Water oxidation enhancement mechanism The water oxidation enhancement mechanism of FeCoPi/BNC- OVs was shown in Fig. 8 . Driven by the internal electric ﬁeld, the photogenerated electrons and holes of BNC migrated rapidly to the (010) and (001) surfaces, respectively. Because the space load- ing position of FeCoPi and OVs was highly consistent with the pho- togenerated charge distribution of BNC (FeCoPi with hole collection capability located at the hole enrichment surface, and OVs with electron capture capability located at the electron enrich- ment surface). Therefore, a large number of photogenerated elec- trons and holes were collected by OVs and FeCoPi to form electron-rich OVs and hole-rich Co sites, respectively. Subse- quently, the hole-rich Co center promoted the complete occurrence of the water-oxidation four-electron reaction chain by self- regulating the coordination environment, and the electron- enriched OVs accelerated the reduction of Fe3+by strong Coulomb electrostatic action. Due to the increase in the number of double active sites, the spatial separation and the enhanced built-in elec- tric ﬁeld, the water oxidation activity of BNC had been signiﬁcantly improved. The mechanism of water oxidation was shown in Eqs. (6)–(10) . H2OþCo/C3!OH adsþHþþe/C0ð6Þ Fig. 7. (a) 1–3, (b) 1–3 AFM images, dark KPFM images and light KPFM images of BNC and FeCoPi/BNC-OVs, respectively. (c, d) Height and surface potential distri bution curves of BNC and FeCoPi/BNC-OVs along the cross section of the dotted line. (e) SPV spectra of BNC, BNC-OVs and FeCoPi/BNC. (f) The photogenerated cha rge separation mechanism before and after loading FeCoPi/OVs (dotted line) and FeCoPi/OVs (solid line). (g) Time-resolved ﬂuorescence decay spectra of BNC and FeC oPi/BNC-OVs (kex= 371 nm, kex= 522 nm). (h) Transient photocurrent response spectrum. (i) Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy.K. Gao, H. Guo, Y. Hu et al. Journal of Energy Chemistry 87 (2023) 568–582 579
