Surprisingly, when Ru was deposited on the surface of Pt co- catalyst, the hydrogen evolution rate of PtRu/PbTiO 3increased to 18.26 lmol h/C01. After depositing Cr 2O3on the surface of PtRu co-catalyst, the hydrogen evolution rate of PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3 was 59.83 lmol h/C01, which was 664.8, 299.2, 76.7 and 3.3 times than that of Pt/PbTiO 3, Ru/PbTiO 3, Pt@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3and PtRu/ PbTiO 3, respectively. The signiﬁcant increase in hydrogen evolu- tion activity indicated a signiﬁcant synergistic effect between Pt and Ru. A possible synergistic mechanism was proposed as shown inFig. 9 (b). because the work function (4.5 eV) of PbTiO 3was lower than that of Pt (5.6 eV), the electrons of PbTiO 3transferred to Pt, and strong upward band bending occurred at the contact interface, thus formed a high Schottky barrier ( ESB1= 1.1 eV). Under photoexcitation, the transfer of photogenerated electrons from the CBM of PbTiO 3to Pt required crossing the upward bend- ing part of the energy band, which was thermodynamically difﬁ- cult [89]. When Ru was introduced on the surface of Pt, the work function of Pt was reduced. Pt 0.59Ru0.41(the proportion was deter- mined by the addition amount of photo deposition) had not only sufﬁcient electron transfer driving force, but also low Schottky barrier ( ESB2= 0.6 eV). Therefore, the introduction of Ru was ben- eﬁcial for the transfer of photogenerated electrons to PtRu [90]. For Ru, although the Schottky barrier ( ESB3= 0.2 eV) formed was relatively low, the electron transfer was relatively slow dynamically due to insufﬁcient driving force of electron transfer. In addition, Ru could effectively inhibit reverse reaction (ORR). With the signiﬁcantly improving of water oxidation performance on FeCoPi/BNC-OVs and water reduction performance on PtRu@Cr 2O3/PbTiO 3, Z-Scheme photocatalytic overall water split- ting system was constructed by mediating of Fe3+/Fe2+shuttle ions. As shown in Fig. 9 (c), FeCoPi/BNC-OVs and PtRu@Cr 2O3/ PbTiO 3was used as an oxygen evolution photocatalyst (OEP) and a hydrogen evolution photocatalyst (HEP), respectively. The valence transition of Fe3+/Fe2+completed the electron transfer and photocatalytic process cycling between OEP and HEP. As shown in Fig. 9 (d), under visible light irradiation, the Z- Scheme system produced O 2and H 2in stoichiometric ratios with evolution rates of 1.3 and 2.5 lmol h/C01, respectively. The slight decrease in gas evolution rate in the third OWS cycle was due to the slight dissolution of FeCoPi. 4. Conclusions To sum up, a new FeCoPi/BNC-OVs photocatalyst for water oxi- dation was constructed through the precise design and regulation of spatially separated double active sites. Due to the increase of the number of surface active sites and the effective separation of bulk charge, FeCoPi/BNC-OVs showed signiﬁcantly enhanced water oxi-dation performance under visible light irradiation, and the O 2evo- lution rate was 16.8 lmol h/C01, 32.9 times than that of original BNC. The reasonable design of the reaction site was emphasized from the aspects of the space distribution of photogenerated charge and the molecular reaction mechanism, and the key function of the space loading position of active site was revealed. In addition, the controllable synthesis of OVs and the elucidation of new water oxidation promoter FeCoPi will help to develop more efﬁcient photocatalysts. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing ﬁnan- cial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to inﬂuence the work reported in this paper.Acknowledgments This work was supported by National Natural Science Founda- tion of China (22369022) and Technology Innovation Leading Pro- gram of Shaanxi (2022QFY07-03). Data availability statement The data that support the ﬁndings of this study are available from the corresponding author upon reasonable request. Appendix A. Supplementary data Supplementary data to this article can be foun