Multiphase lattice engineering of bimetallic phosphide-embedded tungsten-based phosphide/oxide nanorods on carbon cloth: A synergistic and stable electrocatalyst for overall water splitting Debendra Acharyaa,1, Kisan Chhetria,1, Ishwor Pathaka, Alagan Muthurasua, Roshan Mangal Bhattaraib, Taewoo Kima,c, Yagya Raj Rosyaraa, Dae Woo Leec, Tae Hoon Koa,c,*, Hak Yong Kima,c,* aDepartment of Nano Convergence Engineering, Jeonbuk National University, Jeonju 54896, Republic of Korea bDepartment of Chemical Engineering, Jeju National University, Jeju 63243, Republic of Korea cDepartment of Organic Materials and Fiber Engineering, Jeonbuk National University, Jeonju 54896, Republic of Korea ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Carbon cloth (CC) In-situ phosphorization Multiphase lattice Bimetallic phosphide Overall water splittingABSTRACT The hierarchical design of the three-dimensional (3D) nanoarchitecture, comprising multiple phases within an interconnected network on a conductive substrate, offers a high specific surface area, abundant exposed active sites, and rapid electron transport. In our study, we synthesized a hybrid catalyst by integrating (Fe,CO)-P nanoparticles into uniformly grown W-(O,P) nanorods on carbon cloth (CC) using a straightforward method, demonstrating notable electrocatalytic performance. The as-synthesized (Fe,CO)-P/W-(O,P)@CC exhibited remarkable performance in both the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) for overall water splitting. It achieves overpotentials of 210 and 61.3 mV for the OER and HER, respectively, at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 in a 1 M KOH solution. As a bifunctional electrocatalyst in overall water splitting, (Fe,CO)-P/W-(O,P)@CC needs only 1.50 V to attain a current density of 10 mA cm␀2, surpassing traditional Pt/ C@CC and IrO2@CC counterparts (1.53 V @ 10 mA cm␀2). The synergistic effects between (Fe,Co)-P and the W- (O,P)@CC nanohybrid heterostructure enhance the charge transfer, further promoting the activity of this hybrid electrocatalyst. This study reveals a promising avenue for advanced water electrolysis applications. 1.Introduction The splitting of water into hydrogen (H2) and oxygen (O2) is a promising energy conversion method for producing clean and sustain - able energy [1–6]. In a standard water-splitting electrolyzer, there are two electrodes, with the cathode facilitating the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and the anode facilitating the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [7]. The electrocatalyst allows for the continuous production of hydrogen and oxygen with high conversion efficiency, guided by ther- modynamics [8,9] . Nevertheless, the use of an electrocatalytic water splitting process for practical applications faces challenges due to its significant overpotential and sluggish kinetics, particularly because it requires two and four electron transfers to drive the HER and OER, respectively [10]. To date, electrocatalysts based on noble metals such as Pt, as well as their alloys such as ruthenium oxide (RuO 2) and iridium dioxide (IrO 2), have been utilized globally as top performers for the electrocatalytic activity such as HER and OER [7,11 –13]. However, their limited availability and high cost hinder their widespread practical application [14]. Current research endeavors focus on developing cost- effective catalysts that can efficiently drive both the OER and HER concurrently within the same electrolyte [15]. While Most of the re- ported catalysts demonstrate noteworthy individual performances in either the OER [16] or HER [17], a catalyst optimized for OER may not be as effective for the HER and vice versa. Only a few catalysts can effectively enhance both reactions in the same electrolyte medium, highlighting the complexity of designing catalysts that are efficient for overall water-splitting [18,19] . Moreover, cost-effective electrocatalysts often face challenges such as resistivity/high low conductivity and poor durability, requiring significant advances to compete effectively with precious metal catalysts in terms of water-splitting efficiency [20]. *Corresponding authors at: Department of Nano Convergence Engineering, Jeonbuk National University, Jeonju 54896, Republic of Korea. E-mail addresses: kth1745@jbnu.ac.kr (T. Hoon Ko), khy@jbnu.ac.kr (H. Yong Kim). 1These authors contributed equally to this manuscript. Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Chemical Engineering Journal u{�~zkw! s{yo| kro>! ÐÐÐ1 ow�o�to~1m{y 2w{mk�o2mou https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2024.155832 Received 25 June 2024; Received in revised form 24 August 2024; Accepted 13 September 2024  Chemical  Engineering  Journal  499 (2024)  155832 Available  online  21 September  2024 1385-8947/©  2024  Elsevier  B.V. All rights  are reserved,  including  those  for text and data mining,  AI training,  and similar  technologies.
