[Page 2/11]
============================================================
Numerous studies have explored alternatives based on nonnoble metals, such as transition metal oxides (TMOs) [21–23], and their de- rivatives, such as transition metal sulfides (TMSs) [24,25] and transition metal phosphides (TMPs) [26,27] , which are receiving considerable interest due to their distinct nanostructure and electronic properties [28]. Extensive experimentation has been conducted on various TMPs, such as MoP, FeP, CoP, Ni2P, Cu3P, and WP, to expand their applications [29–31]. During the hydrogen catalytic process, the phosphorous and metal sites of transition metal phosphides (TMPs) act as active sites for accepting protons and hydrides [30]. This characteristic enhances their activity for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). Additionally, tran- sition metal-based phosphides exhibit outstanding performance in the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) [32]. The active sites in these phos- phides are believed to be surface oxy/hydroxides (M-OOH, where M  Fe, Co, Ni), and phosphates are formed in-situ during the OER process [33]. However, the overall water-splitting performance of monometallic phosphides still falls short of expectations. To enhance the efficiency of catalytic electrode materials in electrochemical water splitting, adjust - ing the absorption energy of reaction intermediates through element incorporation into the crystal lattice is the most effective method for optimizing the electronic configuration and improving kinetics for bet- ter electrocatalytic activity [34]. Numerous studies have shown that multi-metallic phosphides derived from oxides or hydroxides exhibit superior electrocatalytic properties to single-metal phosphides because of synergistic effects [35]. Zhang et al. synthesized a bifunctional elec- trocatalyst, CoP/Ni 2P@HPNCP, by carbonizing Co/Ni-LDH@ZIF-67 followed by an oxidation and phosphorylation strategy. This catalyst, comprising a hollow porous N-doped carbon polyhedron with embedded CoP and Ni2P nanoparticles, provides additional active sites and mod- ulates the electronic structure of the catalyst. As a result, it exhibits excellent performance in both pH-based universal HER and alkaline OER [36]. Kandel et al. prepared a noble-metal-free electrocatalyst, Ni2PM- MnP@Co 2P, from LDH composed of hybrid Ni2PM-MnP nanosheets coupled with Co2P nanoflowers. This catalyst, reinforced on nickel foam and featuring a unique heterostructure architecture, demonstrates high competence in both the HER and OER [37]. Moreover, growing catalysts directly on conductive substrates such as carbon cloth (CC) and per- forming phase transformations through in-situ techniques are also favorable. The hierarchical design of the three-dimensional (3D) nano- architecture with an exclusive interconnected network on the substrate provides a high specific surface area, abundant exposed active sites, and fast electron transport [38]. This method is particularly favored because it facilitates fast electron transfer and eliminates the need for binders such as Nafion or other polymers [39]. Based on the above findings, a (Fe,CO)-P/W(O,P)@CC hetero-phase structure was successfully developed by using a hydrothermal method followed by an in-situ phosphidation technique on CC. The (Fe,CO)-P/W (O,P)@CC catalyst, with its 3D hierarchical nanorod structure and ample active sites from integrated multiphase materials on web-like interconnected nanorods, offers a seamless interface with the current collector CC due to its unique morphology. This design makes it a ver- satile, cost-effective, and binder-free solution for efficiently catalyzing both the HER and OER without requiring further modifications. The starting material, WO 3, played a key role in creating the final (Fe,Co)-P- integrated W-(O,P)@CC structure. It acted as a bridge, promoting a unique synergistic effect during the catalytic process, which facilitated rapid charge transfer and enhanced the catalyst ’s stability [23]. The different work functions of the components at the interface allowed for electronic coupling and balanced interfacial electron transfer, ensuring the stability of the adsorption energy of the intermediates [40]. Simi- larly, the high conductivity resulting from charge separation in moder - ately converted tungsten phosphide (W-P) from WO 3 not only enhances the active site but also significantly hinders electron delocalization in the metals due to the high electronegativity of the phosphide (P) atom. This impediment offers a reduction in the charge-transfer resistance. As expected, (Fe,CO)-P/W(O,P)@CC demonstrated outstanding electrocatalytic activity for both the OER and HER in an alkaline solu- tion and exhibited remarkable stability. The binder-free, unique, and robust design of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC offers a novel solution for creating practical and effective materials. Its unique architecture en- sures outstanding performance for electrochemical water splitting at low potentials. 2.Experimental design 2.1. Chemicals and materials The required materials are explained in the Electronic Supporting Information (ESI) file, Section 1.1. 2.2. Preparation of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC on carbon cloth (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC was synthesized from WO 3@CC, and the synthesis process of WO3@CC is described in the ESI file, Section 1.2. WO 3@CC was converted into (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC through an in-situ phosphorization process. In this process, WO 3@CC was placed in a Teflon tube with 1 mmol of cobalt nitrate, 1 mmol of iron nitrate, and 2 mmol of triphenylphosphine (PPh 3) solution (used as a phosphorous source) at 200 •C for 10 hr. Subsequently, the sample was sequentially washed with ethanol and deionized water and dried at 60 •C for 6 h, and the resulting sample was named (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1. Another sample, (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-2, was prepared using 2 mmol of cobalt nitrate, 2 mmol of iron nitrate, and 4 mmol of PPh 3 solution following the same method. For comparison, Co-P/W-(O,P)@CC, Fe-P/W-(O,P) @CC, and W-(O,P)@CC were synthesized separately under identical experimental conditions, except iron salt, cobalt salt, and other pre- cursors were added. Detailed explanations and material characteriza - tions have been added to the ESI file, Sections 1.3 and 1.4, respectively. 2.3. Electrochemical measurements All electrochemical evaluations of the synthesized electrode mate- rials were conducted using 1 M KOH at room temperature on a Versa - STAT4 electrochemical workstation (AMETEK, Inc., USA). In a three- electrode system, the synthesized nanorods grown on CC ((Fe,Co)-P/ W-(O,P)@CC) were used as working electrodes with a geometric area of 1 ×1cm2. Without the addition of any conductive additive or binder, Pt wire served as the counter electrodes, and KCl solution-saturated Ag/ AgCl reference electrode. For the overall water-splitting device test in a two-electrode system, two identical pieces of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC electrodes (1 * 1 cm2) were employed as both the cathode and anode. Benchmark IrO2 (for the OER) and Pt/C (for the HER) were deposited onto the carbon cloth by mixing the catalyst with a PVDF binder in NMP for comparative analysis. All reported potential data in this study are referenced to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), and calibration was performed using the Nernst equation: ERHE EAg/AgCl 0.059 × pH0.197 V. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) polarization curves were recorded at a scan rate of 2 mV s␀1, while electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was performed within a frequency range of 10␀2 Hz to 105 kHz at a 10 mV RMS amplitude. The stability of the optimized electrode materials was assessed through chronoamperometry (CA) and/or chronopotentiometry (CP) tests. The mathematical equations for the electrochemical calculations are available in the ESI file, Section 1.5. 3.Results and discussion 3.1. Synthesis and structural analysis Fig. 1represents the strategy for engineering a freestanding bifunc - tional electrocatalyst for water splitting. The uniform growth of (Fe,Co)- P/W-(O,P) on CC with a unique morphology was achieved using a two- step synthesis process (a hydrothermal process followed by an in-situ D. Acharya et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Chemical  Engineering  Journal  499 (2024)  155832 2
============================================================
[Page 3/11]
