phosphidation process). The outstanding electrical conductivity, flexi- bility, and 3D structure of the used CC can provide sufficient space for ion penetration and transport, promoting the adsorption and desorption of the electrocatalyst surface. Initially, uniformly and densely cross- linked WO 3 nanorods with smooth surfaces and unique morphologies were synthesized on an activated carbon cloth substrate by a strong acid. In the second step, these WO 3 nanorods were treated with iron nitrate, cobalt nitrate, and PPh 3 solution for in-situ phosphorization at 200 •C for 10 h. The morphology and structure of the synthesized electrocatalyst were characterized by FE-SEM and EDS mapping. Fig. S1(a, b, and c) presents the FE-SEM images of WO 3@CC. The FE-SEM shown in Fig. 2(a) reveals WO 3 nanorods with smooth surfaces, uniquely and gently interconnected on carbon cloth (CC). As shown in Fig. 2(b, c, and d), the surface structure of the WO 3 nanorods changed, and nanomaterials were easily observed after the in-situ phosphorization process. Densely cross- linked nanorods grown with interconnected 3D networked nanoweb structures with abundant void spaces are suitable for electrochemical reactions and electrolyte absorption. The elemental mapping of the (Fe, Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 electrocatalyst (Fig. 2(e, e1, e2, e3, e4, and e5)) proves the uniform distribution of W, Co, Fe, O, and P within the pre- pared electrode materials. The FE-SEM images of W-(O-P)@CC (Fig. S1 (d, and e)) were obtained by adding PPh 3 solution without metal salts, and high-resolution imaging and elemental mapping (Fig. S1(f, f1, f2, and f3)) confirmed the partial in-situ phosphidation of WO 3@CC without metal salts. As the concentration of cobalt, iron, and phosphorus sources increases during the in-situ phosphorization process, the nanomaterials become densely dispersed on the nanorod network, resulting in small and limited void spaces, which can reduce the kinetics of electrocatalytic reactions and electrolyte absorption (Fig. S2(a, b, and c)). The FE-SEM images (Fig. S2(d, and e)) and elemental mapping (Fig. S2(f, f1, f2, and f3)) of Co-P/W-(O,P)@CC, as well as the FE-SEM images (Fig. S2(g, and h)) and elemental mapping (Fig. S2(i, i1, i2, and i3)) of Fe-P/W-(O,P) @CC, reveal a regular nanorod morphology within a web-like struc- ture. No significant aggregation was observed, which was attributed to the presence of the single metals Co and Fe during the in-situ phos- phorization process. These findings highlight the effectiveness of the in- situ phosphidation process in maintaining a well-defined structure. Similarly, the FE-SEM images (Fig. S3(a, b)) and elemental mapping (Fig. S3(c, c1, c2, c3)) of (Fe,Co)-P@CC reveal uniformly attached nanoparticles on the carbon cloth (CC) surface, with no significant ag- gregation observed. These small, well-distributed nanoparticles confirm that the nanoparticles observed in (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC are a result of (Fe,Co)-P formation during the in-situ phosphidation process. This highlights the effectiveness of in-situ phosphidation in creating a uni- form nanoparticle distribution. TEM analysis was also performed to investigate the morphology of the prepared materials, which was validated via powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis that confirmed the crystalline phase of (Fe, Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1. In the XRD pattern presented in Fig. 3(a), the broad peak at approximately 2θ of 24•corresponds to the carbon cloth. The diffraction peaks at 2θ values of 13.95•, 24.28•, 28.11•, and 55.58• match well with the (100), (110), (200), (212), and (222) lattice planes (written in greenish yellow) of hexagonal WO 3 (PDF#85-2460), respectively. In addition, diffraction peaks at 2θ values of 34.51•, 49.96•, and 58.25•correspond to the (111), (150), and (200) planes (marked in red) of orthorhombic P8W4 (PDF#96-153-7274), respec - tively. Similarly, the peaks observed in the XRD pattern at 2θ values of 23.20•, 36.96•, 71.40•, and 78.38•are assigned to the (200), (310), (530), and (620) planes (written in sky blue) of cubic CoP 4 (PDF#20- 0336), and the peaks at 2θ values of 17.48, 47.42, 56.24, and 63.5• indicate the (100), (210), (102), and (220) planes (written in black) of hexagonal Fe2P. The XRD patterns of Fe-P/W-(O,P)@CC (Fe,Co)-P@CC, W-O@CC, W-(O,P)@CC, and Co-P/W-(O,P)@CC displayed in Fig. S4(a, and b) for comparison analysis, showed their respective phases of WO 3, P8W4, CoP 4, and Fe2P at distinct levels. The observed peaks in (Fe,Co)-P/ W-(O,P)@CC-1, (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-2, W-(O,P)@CC, Co-P/W-(O,P) @CC, and Fe-P/W-(O,P)@CC correspond to WO 3, indicating partial phosphidation during the in-situ phosphidation process. In addition, the nanorods were separated from (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 by sonication and analyzed further using transmission electron microscopy (TEM). TEM analysis (Fig. 3(b, c, and d)) revealed highly interconnected nanorods, consistent with the observations from the FE-SEM analysis. The HR-TEM image (Fig. 3(e)) confirmed the presence of multiple lattice phases of different compounds. Specifically, in the HR-TEM image of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1, lattice fringes with interlayer distances of 0.64 nm and 0.25 nm correspond to the WO 3 (100) and P8W4 (111) planes, respectively. Additionally, lattice spacings of 0.38 and 0.20 nm are observed, corresponding to the CoP 4(200) and Fe2P(210) planes, respectively. The XRD and TEM findings confirmed the coexistence of the multiphase structure of WO 3/P8W4/CoP 4/Fe2P in (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O, P)@CC-1, indicating successful synthesis of multi lattice-phase nano- materials on a conductive substrate (CC). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was further used to evaluate the surface composition and chemical states of the as-prepared catalysts. Fig. 4(a) illustrates the presence of Co, Fe, W, P, and O in the (Fe,Co)-P/ W-(O,P)@CC-1 nanorods, indicating the partial conversion of tungsten oxide into its phosphide as well as the integration of cobalt and iron phosphides on the nanorods during in-situ phosphidation. In Fig. 4(b), the high-resolution Co 2p spectrum displays two peaks located at 779.2 and 793.7 eV due to Co-P formation, with additional peaks at 782.4 and 798.3 eV representing the oxidized state of cobalt [41]. The satellite peaks at 785.9 and 802.8 eV correspond to the binding energies of Co 2p3/2 and Co2p 1/2, respectively [42]. In Fig. 4(c), the two peaks observed at binding energies of 707.4 and 720.2 eV in the deconvoluted Fe 2p spectrum are ascribed to the Fe-P species, and the peaks at 710.4, Fig. 1.Schematic representation of electrocatalyst synthesis for overall water splitting.D. Acharya et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Chemical  Engineering  Journal  499 (2024)  155832 3
