benchmark electrocatalysts. 3.2.1. OER catalytic activity test Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) was used to evaluate the OER ac- tivity of W-(O,P)@CC, Fe-P/W-(O,P)@CC, Co-P/W-(O,P)@CC, (Fe,Co)- P@CC, (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-2, and (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1, which are nanohybrids with marketable IrO2 catalysts and bare CC at a scan rate of 2 mV s␀1 (Fig. 5(a)). (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 displayed over- potentials (η) of 210, 269, and 299 mV at 10, 50, and 100 mA cm␀2, respectively, which are lower than those of other prepared nanohybrid materials. The variation in overpotentials among W-(O,P)@CC, (Fe,Co)- P@CC, (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1, and bare CC under identical reaction conditions (Fig. 5(b)) suggests that the reduced overpotential (η) of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 nanohybrid mainly arises from synergistic interactions between its electroactive (Fe,Co)-P and W-(O,P) constitu - ents integrated within the CC substrate. The superior electrocatalytic performance of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 in the OER compared to that of alternative counter catalysts (Table S1) can be largely attributed to the synergistic effects generated by the multiple active phases between (Fe,Co)-P and W-(O,P). This interaction facilitates atomic restructuring, adjustment of adsorption-free energies, and efficient charge transfer capabilities. To gain deeper insights into the catalytic effectiveness of the (Fe,Co)-P and W-(O,P) nanohybrids, Tafel curves were fitted to the LSV curve using the Tafel correlation equation η b log(J/J 0) (Fig. 5 (c)). Here, J0 represents the exchange current density, J denotes the current density, and b signifies the Tafel slope. The Tafel slope of the (Fe, Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC nanohybrid was 62.2 mV dec␀1, which is lower than those of all other synthesized catalysts, indicating the enhanced OER kinetics of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 nanohybrids. Under the same testing conditions, the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 nanohybrids were compared with IrO2/CC, revealing lower η and b values (88.3 mV dec␀1 for IrO2/CC) (Fig. 5(b, c)). This indicates the superior performance of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 compared to that of conventional IrO2 cata- lysts. The integration of (Fe,Co)-P within W-(O,P) nanorods with distinct architectures and unique morphologies indicates the promising poten - tial of these nanorods as effective electrode materials for the OER in alkaline media. Previous results and experimental analyses suggested that multiphase engineering significantly enhances the OER activity [51]. Therefore, this enhancement is also achieved by improving the electrical conductivity and increasing the number of electroactive sites due to the W-(O,P) and (Fe,Co)-P in the prepared electrode, thereby accelerating the OER. All recorded LSV curves underwent IR correction, and the series resistance was estimated using EIS. The Nyquist plots of all synthesized electrocatalysts are illustrated in Fig. 5(d). The smaller diameter of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 indicates a lower Rct among all the prepared electrocatalysts, signifying a faster charge transfer process and superior conductivity of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1. This enhanced conductivity facilitates outstanding catalytic activity by adsorbing and generating intermediate reactants. Furthermore, the availability of charge carriers and the charge storage capacity at the interface for electrochemical reactions were assessed using electrochemical double- layer capacitance (Cdl). The Cdl value, which correlates linearly with the ECSA (ECSA Cdl/Cs, where Cs is the specific capacitance), was calculated based on the CV curves (Fig. S6). As depicted in Fig. 5(e), the higher Cdl value (7.5 mF cm␀2) of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 compared to that of the other prepared electrocatalysts indicates a more active interface and sites, potentially resulting in enhanced electrochemical activity. Analyses of the Tafel slope, EIS curves, and Cdl suggest that the Fig. 5.(a) OER polarization curves with iR correction, (b) overpotential bar diagram and (