heterointerfaces of (Fe,Co)-P and W-(O,P)@CC can provide a greater electrochemically active surface area and an improved electro - de–electrolyte interface, thereby promoting effective reaction kinetics in the OER process. Fig. 5(f) shows a multistep chronoamperometry curve obtained without iR correction. As the current density increased, the potential adjusted accordingly and stabilized rapidly, indicating the excellent mass transfer properties of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 catalyst. The long-term stability test of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 catalyst was carried out at a constant current density of 10 mA cm␀2 for 100 h. The slight decrease in the initial current density (92.86 % retention) even after continuous OER testing for 100 h indicated the stable nature of the electrocatalyst (Fig. 5(g, and h)). The LSV curves depicted in Fig. 5 (i) confirm the catalyst ’s high durability in a 1 M KOH solution, with only a slight decrease in current density and a corresponding increase in overpotential observed after the OER test. After the stability test, the catalyst retained its dense structure and morphology without any discernible damage, which is corroborated by the consistent elemental composition observed in the EDS mapping analysis depicted in Fig. S7. Furthermore, post-XPS analysis was conducted after prolonged OER to examine the chemical states of the material. Changes in the chemical composition of the surface were observed in the XPS spectrum of (Fe, Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 after the OER stability test (Fig. S8). The fitted Co 2p (Fig. S8a) and Fe 2p (Fig. S8b) spectra exhibited slight shifts, implying that the initial phosphide was primarily oxidized to (oxy)hy - droxide during the OER [52]. The high-resolution W 4f spectrum reveals an interesting evolution of tungsten ’s valence states, showing the transformation from W6to W5along with the presence of W4in W-P. Additionally, a significant shift of W6toward lower binding energy suggests a strong π-interaction between O2– and high-valence W atoms, facilitating a smooth electronic transition [53]. In the fitted P 2p spectra (Fig. S9a), a significant shift in the intensity of the P signal is observed after 100 h of stability, with the dominant peak being P-O. This shift may indicate the depletion of Co-P, Fe-P, and W-P species on the catalyst surface. Additionally, the reduced P 2p signal after the 100-hour sta- bility test suggested the formation of MOOH on the catalyst surface during the OER process [54]. The XPS spectra of O 1s displayed in Fig. S9b show three peaks: lattice oxygen (529.84 eV), surface oxygen (530.91 eV), and adsorbed H2O (533.08 eV). These peaks indicate an affinity for H2O and oxygen adsorption and suggest the partial formation of MOOH during long-term OER analysis in an alkaline solution.3.2.2. HER catalytic activity test The effectiveness of the W-(O,P)@CC, Fe-P/W-(O,P)@CC, Co-P/W- (O,P)@CC, (Fe,Co)-P@CC, (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-2, and (Fe,Co)-P/W- (O,P)@CC-1 nanohybrid materials in catalyzing the HER was studied using LSV in a 1.0 M KOH solution (Fig. 6(a)). The η values determined for these nanohybrids were 147.3 mV for W-(O,P)@CC, 118.4 mV for Fe- P/W-(O,P)@CC, 101.4 mV for Co-P/W-(O,P)@CC, 89.9 mV for (Fe,Co)- P@CC, 70.9 mV for (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-2, and 61.3 mV for (Fe,Co)- P/W-(O,P)@CC-1, with corresponding J values of 10 mA cm␀2 (Fig. 6 (b)). In contrast, the bare carbon cloth showed minimal HER perfor - mance compared to that of the synthesized electrode materials. The overpotential of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 heterostructure closely approximated that of the Pt/C@CC catalyst (47.3 mV) and was lower than that of all prepared electrocatalysts and recently developed HER- based electrocatalysts (Table S2). The HER overpotential of the Pt-C catalyst can vary due to the operating conditions and material purity, which are specifically influenced by the precursor amount, temperature, electrolyte concentration, and material drop-cast volume. The Tafel slope serves as a critical metric for HER kinetics, with a lower Tafel slope indicating faster reaction kinetics at the electrolyte –electrode interface due to the reduced activation energy requirement at low overpotentials. Therefore, the HER kinetics were also evaluated using Tafel slope analysis based on log (j) versus overpotential (η) plots (Fig. 6(c)). The (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 nanohybrid exhibited a Tafel slope of 65.7 mV dec␀1, comparable to that of commercial Pt/C@CC (37.6 mV dec␀1) and superior to that of other synthesized catalysts, including W-(O,P) @CC (146.3 mV dec␀1), Fe-P/W-(O,P)@CC (130.4 mV dec␀1), Co-P/W- (O,P)@CC (100.1 mV dec␀1), (Fe,Co)-P@CC (93.4 mV dec␀1), and (Fe, Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-2 (77.4 mV dec␀1) (Fig. 6(c)). These results indicate that the HER catalysis mechanism of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 closely resembles that of commercial platinum. The lower Tafel slopes observed for both the commercial Pt/C and (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 catalysts suggest that both catalysts exhibit low activation energy requirements, improving the HER kinetics. Consequently, when (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P) @CC-1 catalyzes the HER, it follows the Volmer –Heyrovsky pathway in alkaline electrolytes, as noted in prior studies. Typically, the HER in an alkaline solution involves hydrogen adsorption on active metal sites (MM-H*), the electrochemical dissociation of water into OH␀ions, and subsequent desorption from active metal sites [55]. M H2O e␀→ MM-H* OH– (Volmer step) Fig. 6.(a) HER polarization curves with iR correction, (b) overpotential bar diagram, (c) corresponding Tafel slopes of all prepared electrocatalysts, bare CC and commercial Pt/C@CC, (d) EIS measurements, (e) multistep chronopotentiometry test at different current densities from 10-130-10 mA cm␀2 of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P) @CC-1, (g) long-term CA test over 100 h (insets; retention calculation at different times, and LSV curve measurements before and after the stability test).D. Acharya et al.                            