MM-H* H2O e␀→ H2 OH– M (Heyrovsky step) MM-H* MM-H* → 2 MH2 (Tafel step) Furthermore, the charge transfer resistances at the electro - de–electrolyte interfaces for the HER catalytic activities of all the pre- pared electrocatalysts were analyzed further using electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements. The Nyquist plot shown in Fig. 6(d), resembling a semicircle, was fitted with an equivalent circuit model (inset: Fig. 6(d)). For the HER, the smaller diameter semicircle of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 indicated the lowest charge transfer resis- tance (Rct) compared to that of the other prepared electrocatalysts, suggesting faster electron transfer at the electrode –electrolyte in- terfaces. The Multistep chronopotentiometry test of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P) @CC-1 demonstrated a stable response even under fluctuating applied currents (Fig. 6(e)). This observation indicated excellent mass transport and high compatibility of the nanorods within the web-like structure on the conductive surface of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 catalyst. The catalytic stability of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 was tested using contin - uous chronoamperometry (CA) analysis at a constant overpotential of 79 mV (Fig. 6(f)). The inset in Fig. 6(f) illustrates a marginal decrease in the current density even after 100 h, demonstrating excellent durability with no substantial change in the LSV curves. The charge-transfer resistance after the long-term chronopotentiometry test closely matched the initial values observed before the stability test (Fig. S10), indicating consistent charge-transfer potential. Furthermore, FE-SEM and EDS analysis of the morphology and corresponding elemental mapping of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 following the HER stability test showed no significant changes in morphological features (Fig. S11). Similarly, the deconvoluted peaks in the high-resolution XPS spectra of Co, Fe, W, and P showed minimal changes after the HER stability test (Fig. S8and Fig. S9). The observed slight shift in binding energy toward lower levels, coupled with the prominent peak of surface oxygen (530.65 eV) in the high-resolution O 1s spectra (Fig. S9), indicates the adsorption of H2O and oxygen during HER tests. These alterations are likely attributed to inevitable redox reactions occurring on the electrode surface during prolonged exposure to higher current densities in an aqueous alkaline environment. These findings highlight the ability of the material to maintain its physiochemical properties after long-term durability testing under HER conditions. Therefore, the (Fe,Co)-P/W- (O,P)@CC-1 hybrid structure serves as a robust electrode material, demonstrating excellent HER performance among phosphide-based electrocatalysts (Table S2). 3.2.3. Overall water splitting performance of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 (␀)||(Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1( ) Considering the excellent OER and HER activities of (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O, P)@CC-1, a symmetric electrode system was constructed using it as both Fig. 7.(a) Schematic representation and digital photograph of the 2-electrode system for the overall water splitting device, (b) linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves for (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1, with the inset showing the voltage calculations at various current densities, (c) long-term stability test (CP) results at 10 and 50 mA cm␀2, (d) LSV curves before and after long-term CP tests, and (e, and f) FE-SEM images of the anode and cathode materials after continuous CP tests at 10 and 50 mA cm␀2, respectively.D. Acharya et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Chemical  Engineering  Journal  499 (2024)  155832 8
