an anode and a cathode for overall water splitting in 1 M KOH (Fig. 7 (a)). The performance of this system was compared with that of an electrolyzer prepared by pairing IrO2/CC and Pt/C/CC electrodes. The LSV measurements of both devices were carried out at a scan rate of 2 mV s␀1. An electrolyzer based on the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1( ␀)||(Fe, Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1( ) configuration demonstrated outstanding per- formance, requiring a cell voltage of only 1.50 V to achieve a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 (Fig. 7(b)). The observed voltage for water electrolysis using (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 is lower than that of the noble metal-based catalyst Pt/C@CC||IrO 2/C@CC (1.53 V) (Fig. 7(b)), indicating the superior performance of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 (, ␀) device. The above result is also one of the best for transition metal- based multiphase electrocatalysts for overall water splitting in a basic solution (Table S3). The long-term durability of the electrodes was assessed through a chronoamperometry test by applying a controlled two-step current density ranging from 10 to 50 mA cm␀2 ((Fig. 7(c)). This approach allowed for the evaluation of electrode stability under varying current densities, providing valuable insights into electrode long-term perfor - mance and strength. Notably, the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 pair exhibited exceptional stability during a 100 h CA stability test at 10 and 50 mA cm␀2. At a low current density of 10 mA cm␀2, there was minimal voltage loss, with a slight variation from 1.87 to 1.90 V (1.5 %). Simi- larly, at a high current density of 50 mA cm␀2, the voltage decreased by only 4.2 % (from 1.88 to 1.96 V). However, following continuous sta- bility testing at 10 and 50 mA cm␀2 using the same electrode, there were slight, barely perceptible changes in both voltage and current density after prolonged exposure to the alkaline solution ((Fig. 7(d)). After the long-term stability test, the morphological features of the (Fe,Co)-P/W- (O,P)@CC-1 hybrid heterostructure were also examined via FE-SEM analyses. According to the FE-SEM images in Fig. 7(e, and f), there was no significant deterioration of the nanomaterials from the cathodic and anodic (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1, apart from some slight surface roughness and destruction even after continuous CA at two different current densities (10 mA cm␀2 for 100 h and again 50 mA cm␀2 for 100 h using the same electrode). These findings indicate that the hybrid (Fe, Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 heterostructure retains its structural integrity under prolonged operational conditions. Furthermore, the evolution of H2 and O2 gases at the cathode and anode, respectively, was monitored using an H-cell with a two-electrode setup (Fig. 8(a)). The recorded volumes of H2 and O2 generated are consistent with the experimental data, and the gas volume –time curves for the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 (, ␀) electrolyzer demonstrate a linear relationship with a volume ratio of approximately 2:1 for H2 and O2 (Fig. 8(b)). The added video in the Supplementary file (video-S1) further validates H2 and O2 gas evolution at the cathode and anode, respectively, as monitored using an H-cell with a two-electrode setup. These data highlight the potential of simply synthesizing a dual-functional (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 electrocatalyst as a practical and economical option for overall water-splitting appli - cations, achieving approximately 100 % faradaic efficiency for both H2 and O2 production. Thus, the fabricated (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 electrocatalyst demonstrates high efficiency and cost-effectiveness and represents a promising solution for sustainable hydrogen and oxygen production through water splitting. The remarkable electrocatalytic performance of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 heterostructure can be attributed to several factors. First, the formation of this unique hetero- phase significantly increases the specific surface area of the catalyst, providing numerous exposed electrocatalytically active sites. Second, incorporating multiple metals, such as Co, Fe, and W, improves the electronic structure of the electrocatalyst, enhancing its electrochemical properties. Furthermore, the unique architecture and morphology reduce the charge-transfer and electrolytic resistance, enhancing the efficiency of charge transport. This improvement is attributed to the integration of (Fe,Co)-P nanomaterials on the highly interconnected, web-like nanorods of W-(O,P)@CC. Finally, the binder-free approach enhances the reaction kinetics and ensures strong mechanical adhesion, further boosting the electrocatalytic activity. These combined attributes account for the exceptional HER, OER, and overall water-splitting per- formance of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 hybrid heterostructure, making it a promising candidate for electrochemical catalysis applications. 4.Conclusion In summary, we designed an active and stable bifunctional electro - catalyst for both the OER and HER composed of (Fe,Co)-P nanomaterials on web-like nanorods of W-(O,P)@CC. Using a hydrothermal process followed by in-situ phosphidation, we achieved a hybrid structure on conductive carbon cloths with improved electrocatalytic active sites and an expanded active surface area. This facilitates rapid electron and ion transmission at the electrode –electrolyte interface. Electrochemical measurements showed that (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC-1 required only a 210 mV overpotential for the OER and a 61.3 mV overpotential for the HER to reach a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 in 1.0 M KOH. This approach, which focuses on electrocatalyst design with a unique morphology, supports the large-scale production of cost-effective (Fe, Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC multiphase structures, essential for O2 and H2 generation in renewable energy applications. As a bifunctional electro - catalyst in overall water splitting, (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P)@CC requires only 1.50 V to reach 10 mA cm␀2, outperforming traditional Pt/C@CC and IrO2@CC (1.53 V @ 10 mA cm␀2). The synergistic effects between (Fe, Co)-P and W-(O,P)@CC enhance charge transfer, promoting electro - catalytic activity. The successful development of the (Fe,Co)-P/W-(O,P) Fig. 8.(a) Photograph of a laboratory-made H-cell setup for the estimation of Faradaic efficiency and (b) theoretical and experimental volumes of H2 and O2 generated at 10 mA cm␀2 in an H-cell at various times.D. Acharya et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Chemical  Engineering  Journal  499 (2024)  155832 9
