@CC-1 bifunctional electrocatalyst opens several promising avenues for future research and application in the field of renewable energy. The findings highlight the importance of structural design and material synergy in advancing catalytic performance, and this approach can be extended to other transition metals such as Ni, Mn, V, and Mo to develop a diverse range of phosphide and oxide-based heterostructures, thereby improving performance in catalysis, energy storage, and conversion technologies while offering a pathway towards more sustainable and cost-effective energy solutions. CRediT authorship contribution statement Debendra Acharya: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Software, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Kisan Chhetri: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Visualization, Software, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptu - alization. Ishwor Pathak: Visualization. Alagan Muthurasu: Data curation. Roshan Mangal Bhattarai: Software, Data curation. Taewoo Kim: Formal analysis. Yagya Raj Rosyara: Methodology. Dae Woo Lee: Data curation. Tae Hoon Ko: Visualization, Validation, Data curation, Conceptualization. Hak Yong Kim: Writing – review & edit- ing, Visualization, Validation, Supervision, Funding acquisition, Conceptualization. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Data availability No data was used for the research described in the article. Acknowledgments The National Research Foundation of Korea (NRF) and the Korean government (MSIT) (project numbers (2019R1A5A8080326 and 2022R1A2C2007676) financially supported this research. Appendix A.Supplementary data Supplementary data to this article can be found online at https://doi. org/10.1016/j.cej.2024.155832 . References 