10688 |  Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys., 2025, 27, 10688–10697 This journal is © the Owner Societies 2025 Cite this: Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys., 2025, 27, 10688Engineering electronic structures of Nb 6Ix superatomic clusters by metal atom incorporation: a first-principles study † Dolan Acharya,aSoumyadeep Bhattacharyya,aRenna Shakirband J. Karthikeyan *a Akin to the conventional building blocks of solids, the elements in the periodic table, the iodine capped Nb6Ix(x= 12–18) octahedron cluster is a well-known ‘‘superatomic building block’’ for designing not only cluster assemblies like Prussian blue analogues and hybrid perovskites but also for new catalysts. Recent achievements within the realm of atomic clusters using experimental and theoretical approaches have shown synthesis and precise tuning of magnetic and electronic properties of small octahedronclusters viaatom-by-atom substitution. In this work, state-of-the-art density functional theory (DFT) calculations highlight the feasibility of tuning the valence electron concentration (VEC) by endohedraldoping of 3d-block transition metals (M = Sc–Zn) inside the core of the homo-iodide Nb 6octahedron. The energetics of various structural isomers imply that all other M atoms can be doped within the Nb 6Ix cluster with high I atom concentrations, except Sc and Ti. In-depth analysis shows the cohesive energyper atom and the formation energy (FE) of the clusters correlate with various structural and electronic parameters of these clusters exhibiting electronic shell closures with 18 and 24 VEC, with a substantial HOMO–LUMO gap within the range of 0.72 to 1.76 eV using HSE06 hybrid functionals. Interestingly, theFE calculations demonstrate that the MNb 6I18cluster can be formed even under M-rich conditions, hav- ing robust formation stability. We further explain how the Gibbs free energy ( DGH) of adsorbed hydrogen correlates with different d-band centres of Nb and M atoms to highlight the impact of electronic struc-ture on the catalytic activity towards the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). Thus, it is possible toachieve potential HER catalysts with very small DG Hfor VNb 6I18to CoNb 6I18comparable to the Pt(111) surface. This work provides the atomic structure, stability, and electronic properties of endohedral doped Nb6Ixclusters that can pave the way for various potential applications in catalysis, molecular electronics and spintronics, and for further experimental research. 1 Introduction Atomic clusters1are an ever-fascinating class of zero-dimensional structures due to their tunable physical and chemical properties originating from structural and electronic confinements.2Interest- ingly, certain atomic clusters have exceptional stability due toeither an electronically or st ructurally closed shell, 3,4despite becoming less stable when the dimension is reduced. The electro-nic shell closing 5c a nb ea c h i e v e db yt u n i n gt h eV E Ci nt h e cluster.6The VEC of metal clusters can be varied by changing the number of metal atoms and the ligands that shield their surface from further agglomeration.7The atomic shell closing is achieved with a specific number of atoms required to form a polyhedron with closely packed smooth surfaces.8Inherently, an endohedral cage with a structurally closed atomic shell cansimultaneously achieve the electr onic shell closing by an ingenious choice of appropriate filler atom. Since it is conceivable to attainaDepartment of Physics, National Institute of Technology, Durgapur, 713209, India. E-mail: kjeyakumar.phy@nitdgp.ac.in bDepartment of Sciences & Humanities, Rajiv Gandhi Institute of PetroleumTechnology, Jais, Amethi, Uttar Pradesh, 229304, India †Electronic supplementary information (ESI) available: Measurement of the HOMO–LUMO gap for pristine Nb 6Ixand centre site of MNb 6I17with PBE and HSE06 respectively (T1); force generated by HSE06 (T2); calculation of band-center (E1); measurement of average Nb–Nb bond distance ( d Nb–Nb ) (S1, T3); the energy level and HOMO–LUMO gap for pristine Nb 6Ix(S2); calculation of average bond distances (T4); atomic energy of 3d-block M atoms (S3); chemical potential(S4); molecular dynamics (S5 and S6); measurement of relative energy ( E R)f o r different I x(T5); DOS of Cu and Zn doped Nb 6I18(S7); the d-band center for Nb(4d) and M(3d) (T6); HOMO–LUMO gap for MNb 6I18(S8); calculation of zero point energy (ZPE) (E2); calculation of vibrational entropy ( DS) (E3–E5); adsorp- tion of second H 2in clusters (S9); onset potential for HER (T7). See DOI: https:// doi.org/10.1039/d5cp00018aReceived 2nd January 2025, Accepted 7th April 2025 DOI: 10.1039/d5cp00018a rsc.li/pccpPCCP PAPER Published on 08 April 2025. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 4/15/2026 1:43:35 AM. View Article Online View Journal | View Issue
