This journal is © the Owner Societies 2025 Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys., 2025, 27, 10688–10697 |  10689atomic and electronic (double) s hell closures together in the endohedral cage, they are competent candidates for designing superatoms, such as superalkali or superhalogen.9,10 It was first demonstrated in 1985 that lanthanide elements may be packed within large carbon cages, also called fullerenes.11,12 Following the endohedral carbon cages, Si and Ge,13which were not previously known to form atom ic cages, have been predicted to form endohedral cages with a transition metal (M) in the centre.14–16S u c hMe n c a p s u l a t e dC ,S io rG ec a g e so ft i n ys i z e like M@Si nand M@Ge n(n=1 4 – 1 6 ;M=T i ,Z r ,H f ,F e ,R u ,O s ) containing less than twenty atoms form a variety of structures,such as Frank–Kasper polyhe drons, capped decahedrons, fullerene-like cages, and even cubes and rings. 17–20Immediately after this, many theoretical works were conducted to search forsimilar atom-filled cages of other elements and compounds. 21–23 Recently, such theoretically predicted M@Si 16cages have been synthesized on a large scale.24Their formation and thermody- namic stabilities are not only just governed by the size of the endohedrally doped M but also man y other electronic factors, such as the electron transfer, the bonding properties, the VEC of the cluster, geometric factors such as the symmetry of the cage, andthe coordination number. 25–27 When the atomic cages are tiny, containing less than 10 atoms, the endohedral doping process becomes more complexbecause the smaller central void can accommodate only tinyatoms such as H, Be, B, C, and N. For example, the endohedral doping of tiny p-block atoms such as B, 28C, and N inside the liganded octahedron of second-row M atoms, such as Y, Zr, Nb, and Mo are experimentally known from the past fewdecades. 29,30,31Interestingly, there are also cases where even larger metal atoms can be inserted into liganded Y, Zr32,33and Bi34octahedrons. In such instances, the strong binding between the metal atom and the cage is suﬃcient to oﬀset the strainenergy caused by the insertion of the larger atom. 34 These halide- or chalcogenide-liganded octahedron clusters can be synthesized using various methods, including high-temperature techniques, 35,36immobilization approaches (as in the case of Ta 6Br12clusters),37and depolymerization or excision reactions, which allow for the soft chemical modification of thecounter ion and ligand environment. 38In addition to these approaches, several solution-based chemical methods havebeen developed for the functionalization of diﬀerent octahedral clusters like Re. 39These liganded octahedron clusters are dis- persible in homogeneous media and can be directly utilized in applications such as photocatalytic hydrogen generation,40,41or grafted with two-dimensional materials like graphene,41as well as organic and inorganic polymeric materials.42They have been employed in advanced technologies such as transparent UV-NIRblocking smart windows, 31,43–45and sensors.46 Despite the intelligible motivation for doing so, liganded Nb6octahedrons are yet to be studied for endohedral doping of M atom inside its voids. The most commonly used ligandsfor the stabilization of these oc tahedron clusters are halogens (Cl, Br, and I), 45cyanide,47isothiocyanides,48water,43or organic ligands49–51of different sizes. Hence, we study Nb 6octahedron clusters capped with I atoms (x ). The DFT calculations will explorethe structural and electronic stability of pristine and M-dopedNb 6Ixclusters with different VECs. We tune the VEC of the cluster by choosing different 3d-block M atoms and varying the xvalues. Thus, it is possible to achieve clusters with specific oxidation statesdriven by the electronic shell closing effects. 52It has been experi- mentally demonstrated that halogen and CN liganded Nb 6clusters with specific oxidation states can form Prussian blue analogs.53,54 Thus, the metal-inserted liganded Nb 6octahedron cluster is a vital building block for growing one-, two- and three-dimensionalcompounds with desirable proper ties to widen their applications in fields as mentioned earlier. 55I np u r s u i to ft h e s ea p p l i c a t i o n s , such Nb 6octahedron clusters can be assembled on metal surfaces such as Au to tailor their surface properties.56 Ultimately, our DFT calculations are amenable to answer the following questions: (1) Which M (Sc–Zn, from the 3d series)atoms can be endohedrally doped inside the Nb 6octahedron rather than being adsorbed on its surface? (2) How do thecapping atoms play a role in the endohedral doping process? (3) How do the cohesive and formation energies correlate with various structural and electronic factors? Moreover, we findthat the VEC dependent finely tuneable d-band centre positionsof Nb and the endohedrally doped M atoms are key descriptorsin deciding the catalytic activity of the cluster for interestingredox reactions. Hydrogen is touted as the fuel of the future; weexplore HER activity from the hydrogen adsorption Gibbs freeenergy ( DG H), which shows a strong correlation with the d-band centre and electronic density of states (DOS) of MNb 6I18. 2 Computational methods The spin-polarized DFT calculations are carried out on optimizedMNb 6Ixstructures using the pseudopotential-based projector- augmented wave method within the framework of generalized- gradient approximation (GGA) by Perdew–Burke–Ernzerhof exchange–correlation (PBE) functional as implemented in theVienna ab initio simulation package (VASP). 57,58The clusters considered in this study consist of a maximum of 25 atoms.The clusters are modelled inside a big cubic unit cell with alattice parameter of 23 Å to exclude the interactions betweenadjacent units effectively. In the electronic minimization process,the wave functions are converged to 10 /C06eV with a 500 eV plane wave kinetic energy cut-off. Since the system is non-periodic, weuse only the high symmetry Gamma point in the Brillouin zonefor structural optimization and electronic calculation. All atomsare relaxed until the absolute atomic forces are less than0.01 eV Å /C01without considering any symmetry. To improve convergence in the case of partially occupied eigenstates at theFermi energy level ( E f), modest Gaussian smearing of sigma 0.1 eV has been used for both relaxation and density of states (DOS) calculations. Also to get experimentally reliable highest occupied molecular orbital–lowest occupied molecular orbital(HOMO–LUMO) gaps, we have used the HSE06 functional 59in the main manuscript, while the PBE results are tabulated inTable T1 (ESI †). The average Hellmann–Feynman forces 60,61on each endohedral cluster after electronic convergence in thePaper PCCP Published on 08 April 2025. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 4/15/2026 1:43:35 AM. View Article Online
