hest HOMO–LUMO gap of1.38 eV as its VEC is 18. For octahedrons, it is well known that VEC = 18 and 24 provides magical stability. 6,66,67 When the apical I aatoms are added, the Nb atoms are buckled outward, increasing the dNb–Nb bond distance and hence the volume of the Nb 6octahedron ( V0). To understand this, we have plotted V0vs. x in Fig. 1(h) and the average Nb–Nb distance (dNb–Nb )vs. xin Fig. S1 (ESI †). The increasing trend in V0represents the stretching of Nb–Nb bonds due to the decrease in the number of electrons required for its bonding. Consequently, the stretching of Nb–Nb bonds will decrease the cohesive energy per atom (CE) of the cluster, as shown in Fig. 1(i). The CE is calculated usingeqn (1): CE¼EðMÞþ6/C2EðNbÞþx/C2EðIÞ ½/C138 /C0 EMNb 6Ix ðÞ m(1) where E(M), E(Nb), E(I), and E(MNb 6Ix) are the energies of isolated M, Nb, I atoms and total energy of the MNb 6Ixcluster, respectively, and mis the total number of atoms present in the cluster. The E(M) term will be omitted in (1) for the CE calculation of pristine Nb6Ixclusters. Since the Nb–Nb bond strength decreases with the x value, the CE energy per atom of the cluster decreases linearly withthexvalue, albeit a stable hump for Nb 6I14is observed. This is due to the magical stability of the Nb 6I14cluster, which has completely filled three-centre two-electron (3c–2e/C0) bonding states as shown in the inset in Fig. 1(j). Due to the bizarre multi-centre bonding in this cluster, we observe an electronic shell closing for VEC = 16, in addition to 18. Due to this magical stability, thiscluster has the highest HOMO–LUMO gap of 1.33 eV, as shownin Fig. S2 (ESI †). With the further addition of I aatoms, the HSE06 gap of the clusters decreases due to reduced chemicalstability. From the preceding discussion, it is evident that due tothe stretching of Nb–Nb bonds after the addition of more I aatoms, Fig. 1 The ball-and-stick models (a)–(g) represent the optimized structures of the pristine Nb 6Ix(x= 12–18) clusters; (h) volume of the Nb 6octahedron (V0)i nN b 6Ix; (i) cohesive energy per atom CE (eV per atom) of Nb 6Ixclusters; (j) three-centre two-electron (3c–2e/C0) bonding orbital in Nb 6I14. The green and red balls represent Nb and I atoms, respectively. The dashed line in (i) connects the CE of the x=1 2a n d x= 18 cases.PCCP Paper Published on 08 April 2025. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 4/15/2026 1:43:35 AM. View Article Online
