10692 |  Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys., 2025, 27, 10688–10697 This journal is © the Owner Societies 2025doped clusters can be formed using formation energy (FE) calculations. Hence, the FEs of the clusters are calculated using eqn (2) for both 3f- and centre site configurations in M-rich and M-poor conditions. They are plotted in Fig. 3(a). FE = E(MNb 6Ix)/C0[E(Nb 6Ix)+m(M)] (2) where E(MNb 6Ix) and E(Nb 6Ix) are total energies of the metal- doped and pristine Nb 6Ixclusters, and m(M) is the chemical potential of the M atom. We calculate that m(M) is the energy per M atom in its bulk structure for the M-rich conditions. TheM-rich limit reflects the FE values of the experiment in whichthe M precursor is taken at a very high concentration, and theM atoms can form bulk metal particles before they react withthe Nb 6I18clusters. For the M-poor condition, the m(M) is the energy per M atom in its dimer cluster, assuming that M isdoped from the M 2dimer available in the reaction. The M-poor limit assumes that the precursors of the M atom are at a lowconcentration and the M atoms are released slowly in such away that a maximum of two metal atoms can meet each other toform a dimer before it reacts with the Nb 6I18clusters. The calculated FE values of both 3f- and centre sites are negative in M-poor conditions as depicted in Fig. 3(a), due to strong exothermic reaction of M 2dimers with the Nb 6I18 cluster. However, the FE values of mainly the 3f-site configu- ration in M-rich conditions are positive, and those of centresites are negative. This shows that M atoms residing on thesurface without diﬀusing into the endohedral voids willagglomerate to a bigger particle in M-rich conditions. The 3fconfiguration of ScNb 6I18has the lowest FE due to the high reactivity of Sc. Further, the FE of 3f-configurations increases up to Cr as dictated by the chemical potential of M atoms. Then, it drops suddenly for the 3f-site of Mn due to a strongbinding between Mn and Nb atoms. Smaller atoms sponta-neously pass through the I atoms to stay near the Nb atoms.From Mn to Zn, the monotonous increase in FE of the 3f-siteconfiguration is due to the variation in the binding between Nband M atoms controlled by the number of unpaired electrons inthe outer orbitals. From this, it is clear that the FE of the 3f-sitedepends on the chemical potential of the M atoms without any eﬀect from the atomic radius for the pre-Mn atoms. Still, the FE of post-Mn atoms depends on the radius and the bindingbetween Nb and M atoms. The variation of chemical potentialfor all the M atoms is plotted in Fig. S4 (ESI †). However, one may consider that the encapsulation of the M atom can destabilizethe Nb octahedral cage. To prove that these Nb octahedrons arestable during the M insertion process at experimentally relevanttemperatures of solid-state reactions such as 700 K and 1000 K, we have performed AIMD simulations at various temperatures for 700 K and 1000 K. Further details are provided in Fig. S5 andS6 (ESI †). During the AIMD simulation, after a few steps (1350 steps) near the 3f sites, the M atom crosses the Nb plane andenters the octahedral cage. Notably, the M atom remains con-fined within the cage and does not escape after the insertion,even at elevated temperatures. We extended our analysis to understand the thermody- namics of the metal insertion process by correlating the FEvalue of the centre site configuration with other parameters,such as the chemical potential of M atoms, the atomic radius,and the number of unpaired electrons with the M atoms. Forthis purpose, we have calculated the radius deviation parameter(DR) and plotted it in Fig. 3(b). This DRmeasures how much d Nb–M is lower than the sum of elemental radii of Nb and M using eqn (3): DR=[dNb–M /C0(RNb+RM)] (3) where the standard elemental radii RNband RMare taken from ref. 68. We did not consider the metallic radii of the M atoms as they intrinsically aﬀect M–M bonding. The DRplot trend shows that the binding between the Nb and M atom decreases signifi- cantly for the post-Mn atoms due to fewer unpaired electronsavailable for the Nb–M bonding. However, the d Nb–Nb plot in Fig. 3(b) shows an increase in Nb–Nb binding (decrease in dNb–Nb value) from Ti to Mn, which will synergistically facilitate theformation process with the lowest FE value for Mn. For the Fig. 3 (a) Formation energy FE (eV) for MNb 6I18in the 3f- and center-sites as labeled. (b) On the left side y-axis, DRand the right side y-axis, dNb–Nb and dNb–M in Å are plotted for all the M atoms. The solid and dashed lines in (a) indicate the FE for M-rich and M-poor conditions, and the black dashed line shows FE = 0 eV. The dashed pink line in (b) shows the dNb–Nb distance in pristine Nb 6I18, and the numbers on the top x-axis signify the VEC for the cluster.PCCP Paper Published on 08 April 2025. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 4/15/2026 1:43:35 AM. View Article Online
