 a s e sa st h e xvalue decreases, as tabulated in Table T5 (ESI †)f o rt h ec e n t r es i t es t a b l e cases. Moreover, the FE increases as the xvalue decreases. This shows that the centre site doping is feasible in I-rich Nb 6clusters (x= 18–16), and for the I-poor limit ( x= 12–15), there is no possibility of doping metal at the centre of the cluster. 3.6 Electronic structure of MNb 6I18clusters As the MNb 6I18clusters show good formation stability and high relative stability against the surface for the 3f-site, we have studied their electronic and catalytic properties. To understandthe electronic structure of these clusters, we have plotted theHSE06 functionals employed DOS for MNb 6I18(M = V–Ni) clusters as shown in Fig. 5. For Cu and Zn doped cases, theDOS are given in Fig. S7 (ESI †) as their d-states are very deep. The DOS of the doped clusters show that the majority of Nb-4d Fig. 4 Genesis of endohedral doping stability in Nb 6Ix(x= 12–18) clusters with different 3d-block M atoms. M atoms stable in the centre-site and 3f-site are shown by the green and red circles, respectively. Metals with anextremely minuscule energy difference between the centre and 3f sites areindicated by the grey circles. The dotted white line covers the stable clusters for endohedral M doping. The yellow circular ring shows the magic clusters with VEC = 18 and 24. Fig. 5 Density of states (DOS) employed with HSE06 functional of MNb 6I18, for M = V–Ni. The energy values in the x-axis are calibrated with respect to the Fermi energy ( Ef) of respective clusters. The dotted lines show the position of Ef. The red, green and blue coloured states represent the partial density of states of I(5p), Nb(4d), and M(3d) orbitals, respectively. The spin-up and spin-down states are denoted by the up and down arrows, respectively.Paper PCCP Published on 08 April 2025. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 4/15/2026 1:43:35 AM. View Article Online
