ap with the Nb orbitals of the cluster. This reducestheir CE with poor formation enthalpy. 3.7 Catalytic activity of M doped clusters for HER Further, we studied the catalytic activity of these clusters for the HER by calculating the DG Hof hydrogen adsorption as shown in Fig. 7(a). An ideal HER catalyst must be able to adsorb protons, reduce themon the surface, and subsequently release H 2gas with an optimal interaction. This requires DGHt ob ec l o s et oz e r oi nt h ec o m p u t a - tional hydrogen electron (CHE) model. DGHis defined as: DGH=DH+DZPE/C0TDS (4) Fig. 6 Calibration of the HOMO and LUMO positions with respect to the vacuum energy of MNb 6I18clusters. The top of the cyan bar shows the position of the HOMO, and the bottom of the grey bar shows the position of t h eL U M O .T h ep o s i t i o n so ft h eD B C so ft h eN ba n dMa t o m sa r ed e l i n e a t e d by green and blue coloured lines, respectively, and are listed in Table T6 (ESI †). Fig. 7 (a) Gibbs free energy change ( DGH) for HER at pH = 0 in MNb 6I18(M = V–Zn) clusters. The coloured dotted lines highlight superior catalytic activity for M = V and Ni compared to Pt(111). (b) The variation in DGHfor hydrogen adsorption in MNb 6I18clusters with different M atom incorporation, where DGH1,DGH2,DGH3denote the adsorption of single H, second H, and H 2molecule by the cluster.PCCP Paper Published on 08 April 2025. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 4/15/2026 1:43:35 AM. View Article Online
