This journal is © the Owner Societies 2025 Phys. Chem. Chem. Phys., 2025, 27, 10688–10697 |  10695where, DH¼EMNb 6I18H ½/C138 /C0 EMNb 6I18 ½/C138 /C0EH2½/C138 2(5) In these equations, E[MNb 6I18–H] and E[MNb 6I18] represent 1the total energies of the cluster with and without the adsorbed H atom, respectively. E[H2] is the total energy of the H 2 molecule. DZPE is the difference in zero-point energy between the adsorbed H atom and half of the H 2molecule in the gas phase, and DSis the change in entropy at room temperature ( T= 298 K), detailed calculations are given in eqn (E2) (ESI† ). The entropy of the free H 2gas molecule is taken from the NIST database,69and only the vibrational entropy is considered for the adsorbed H atom as describedin eqn (E3)–(E5) (ESI† ). For the H adsorption study on M doped Nb 6I18clusters, we consider the 3f-site as other corners and edges are completely covered by the I atoms. Due to the smaller radius of the H atom, the H-atom goes perfectly inside the center of the void of thepristine cluster and exhibits significantly a large negative DG H of/C01.56 eV, indicating a strong chemisorption of H at the center of the cluster. In contrast, for the endohedrally dopedcluster, the H atom stays at the 3f-site to bind with central Mand Nb atoms through the hole in the 3f-site. Thus, theelectronic states of M and Nb atoms will play a vital role in theDG Hvalues and, consequently, the HER activity. To infer this, the DGHvalues of MNb 6I18(M = V–Zn) are plotted against the M atoms in Fig. 7(b) for different H adsorption. For the first hydrogen adsorption, DGH1increases from /C00.05 eV for V to a maximum positive value of +0.45 eV for Fe, which can beattributed to the downward shift of the DBC of the M atoms.After Fe, it decreases gradually to /C00.41 eV up to Cu, which can be attributed to the upward shifting of the DBC of Nb. The Zn has a large positive DG Hdue to its magical stability, though its d-band centre is higher than Cu. For the second protonation, the DGH2follows an inverse trend compared to DGH1, except in the case of V. This can be attributed to the fact that the cluster, which is highly reactive toward the first protonation, does not exhibit the same reactiv-ity toward the second protonation. Since DG H2is always higher thanDGH1, and the absorption of the second proton is the rate- limiting step. The second H atom preferentially adsorbs nearNb atoms close to the first H atom, as illustrated in Fig. S9(ESI† ). The onset potential of HER for diﬀerent clusters is tabulated in Table T7 (ESI †). We have plotted the DG H2of Pt(111) for the comparison. Our calculated DGH2for the Pt(111) surface is in agreement with other reports.70,71From the rate-determining step, it can be understood that V, Fe, andCo incorporated Nb 6I18haveDGH2close to that of the Pt(111) surface. The corresponding onset potentials for H-atom adsorp-tion are listed in Table T8 (ESI† ). For which V, Fe, and Co have a lesser onset potential relative to CuNb 6I18. The discussion above clearly shows that the catalytic activity of this cluster isfine-tunable by adjusting the electronic structure by choosingdiﬀerent M atoms.4 Conclusions In summary, this comprehensive DFT study has elucidated theatomic and electronic structures of pristine Nb 6Ixas well as endohedrally doped MNb 6Ix(M = Sc–Zn; x= 12–18) clusters. The additional apical I atoms decrease the CE of the pristinecluster with great enhancement in the cluster volume. This enhanced volume and decreased CE of Nb 6Ixfacilitate the endohedral doping process in the I-rich limit. The bulged Nb6I18cluster facilitates the endohedral doping of almost all the M atoms (V–Cu, except Zn) with a higher CE than thepristine counterpart and Sc and Ti are found to be experimen-tally not viable for incorporation into the core of the octahe-dron due to the large atomic radius (structural strain) and weakbinding with the Nb atoms. The calculated CE values of MNb 6I18reveal an inverse dependence with the size of M atoms as the CE increases from Ti to Ni. Cu and Zn show very low CE due to their weak binding with Nb atoms, as evidenced by aneffective increase in Nb–M distance ( d Nb–M ) and a weak over- lapping between the Nb 4d, and 3d states of Cu and Znobserved from the DOS calculations. Further, we calculate theFE values for M-rich and M-poor conditions to explore thesynthetic conditions required for the formation of this cluster. Even in metal-rich conditions, FEs of centre-site doped cases are exothermic for many metals (Cr–Zn) and show exceptionalformation stability. There is correlation between FE and DR, the average bond distance between Nb–Nb and Nb–M. Endohedraldoping feasibility has been further explored for clusters withx= 12–17, showing that centre-site doping is favoured in I-rich clusters ( x= 16–18) but less likely in I-poor clusters ( x= 12–15), except when VEC = 24. The electronic properties of the thermo- dynamically stable MNb 6I18clusters align with the magic elec- tronic shell closure, with CrNb 6I18displaying a large HOMO– LUMO gap of 1.76 eV at VEC = 18 and ZnNb 6I18showing a gap of 1.45 eV at VEC = 24. The DOS for VNb 6I18and CoNb 6I18near the Efsuggests exceptional HER activity, with minimal DGH1for first protonation values of /C00.04 eV and 0.03 eV, respectively. The band centre position of both the Nb and M atoms dictates the DGHvalues. The VNb 6I18to CoNb 6I18clusters exhibit promising HER activity with a lower DGH2for the rate-limiting second proton adsorption step compared to the Pt(111) surface. Thisresearch highlights the potential and feasibility of endohedraldoping to fine-tune the electronic and catalytic properties ofoctahedral cluster structures for diverse applications. We hopethis study will inspire and guide experimentalists to investigateNb 6clusters with different M impurities. Author contributions D. A. performed data curation (computational), graphing, formal analysis, investigation of results, writing and reviewing. S. B. and R. S. contributed equally to preparing graphs andanalyzing and validating computational data. J. K. contributed tographing, analyzing, and investigat ing results, and writing, review- ing, conceptualizing, and supervising the research. All authors haveread and agreed to the published version of the manuscript.Paper PCCP Published on 08 April 2025. Downloaded by Beijing University of Chemical Technology on 4/15/2026 1:43:35 AM. View Article Online
