Chemical Engineering Journal 433 (2022) 134571 Available online 7 January 2022 1385-8947/© 2022 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.Achieving an efficient hydrogen evolution reaction with a bicontinuous nanoporous PtNiMg alloy of ultralow Noble-metal content at an ultrawide range of current densities Yuexi Zhenga,1, Pengfei Shanga,1, Feng Peib, Guang Mac, Zhiguo Yea,*, Xinyuan Penga,*, Duosheng Lia aSchool of Materials Science and Engineering, NanChang Hangkong University, NanChang 330063, PR China bState Grid JiangXi Electric Power Research Institute, NanChang 330096, PR China cGlobal Energy Interconnection Research Institute Co., Ltd., Beijing 102209, PR China ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Bicontinuous nanoporous alloy Nanoscale Kirkendall effect Hydrogen evolution reaction Electrocatalytic activity Long-term stability ABSTRACT Electrochemical water splitting powered by green electricity generated from intermittent geothermal, wind and solar power is a promising approach to realize large-scale hydrogen production owing to its sustainable and clean characteristics. Developing electrocatalysts with high electrocatalytic activity and long-term stability for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at an ultrahigh current density (F1000 mA cm␀2) still needs a breakthrough. Here, a bicontinuous nanoporous PtNiMg alloy with an ultralow noble metal content for the HER is fabricated by a facile high-temperature sintering method based on the nanoscale Kirkendall effect. A bicontinuous nanoporous alloy electrode with an ultralow content of approximately 1 at % Pt for the HER in an alkaline medium achieves an extremely low overpotential of 22 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 and a Tafel slope (30.9 mV dec-1) of the Volmer-Tafel mechanism. The alloy electrode for the HER at a wide current density of 1–2000 mA cm␀2 displays superior stability in alkaline media. The 3D bicontinuous nanoporous structure, perfect synergistic effect of Ni and Pt species, high structural stability and excellent conductivity of the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy achieve outstanding electrocatalytic activity and long-term stability at an ultrawide range of HER current densities. This study greatly improves the electrochemical performance of Pt-based electrocatalysts for the HER via a novel fabrication approach and provides the possibility of large-scale industrial application for H2 production. 1.Introduction The development and utilization of renewable and clean energy is always a foremost challenge for modern human society [1,2] . Hydrogen has been widely regarded as one of the most promising energy resources in the 21st century owing to its sustainability and environmentally friendly characteristics [3,4] . In particular, the HER (hydrogen evolu - tion reaction) of the cathode powered by green electricity during elec- trochemical water splitting in a neutral [5], acidic [6,7] or alkaline media [8] is a carbon –neutral and sustainable process in comparison with the traditional route [7,9] . Precious Pt electrodes for HER are generally known to exhibit ultrahigh electrocatalytic activity in acidic media [10-12] and have poor electrocatalytic activity owing to the sluggish kinetics of the Volmer step, which is an intermediate reaction that produces hydrogen adsorption (Had) on neighbouring Pt sites [13- 16]. It is thus clear that the sluggish kinetics and the scarcity of noble Pt as HER electrocatalysts impede the large-scale application in alkaline media [17]. Therefore, exploiting more efficient electrocatalyst mate- rials for the HER in alkaline media is very urgent for the current in- dustrial development of water splitting [18]. Nonnoble metal-based materials have been widely studied as promising substitutes for HER electrocatalysts, especially Fe, Co, and Ni of VIII 3d transition metals with diverse types (i.e., phosphides, sulfides, oxides, borides and selenides) [19-22] . However, these nonnoble metal- based electrodes for HER still have some disadvantages, including un- desirable HER activity and ordinary stability in alkaline media. There - fore, alloying Pt with various transition metals is still an effective strategy to improve the HER activity and decrease the cost of *Corresponding Authors. E-mail addresses: yezhiguo2008@163.com (Z. Ye), 70317@nchu.edu.cn (X. Peng). 1 Yuexi Zheng and Pengfei Shang contributed equally to this work. Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Chemical Engineering Journal u{�~zkw! s{yo| kro>! ÐÐÐ1 ow�o�to~1m{y 2w{mk�o2mou! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cej.2022.134571 Received 24 October 2021; Received in revised form 24 December 2021; Accepted 4 January 2022
