Chemical Engineering Journal 433 (2022) 134571 2electrocatalysts. Many research groups have successfully synthesized Pt- based bimetallic electrocatalysts with excellent electrocatalytic activity and good stability in alkaline media. Ni-Pt alloys with various forms (such as oxides and hydroxides) in alkaline media exhibit a low over- potential and Tafel slope owing to the synergistic effect of Pt and Ni species [13,18,23-25] , and Ni in the alloy can favourably adsorb OH to accelerate the dissociation of water molecules in the Volmer step [13,17,26] . Hence, the adsorption/desorption of Had more easily reacts on adjacent Pt sites because the bonding energy of Pt-H is neither too strong nor too weak in alkaline media [27-29] . In this architecture, Ni provides active sites for cleaving H-OH bonds, and Pt facilitates the combination of generated hydrogen intermediates into H2 molecules. In addition, nanostructural electrocatalysts (nanowires, nanosheets, nanoparticles, etc.) for HER possess ultralarge electrochemical active surface area (ECSA), producing higher electrocatalytic activities than traditional electrocatalyst materials [8,30-37] . Sun and coworkers [24] synthesized a Ni(OH) 2-PtO 2 hybrid nanosheet array with an ultralow Pt content by a hydrothermal method, achieving an HER overpotential of 31.4 mV for a current density of 4 mA cm␀2 in 0.1 M KOH solution. Liang and coworkers [14] employed a one-step solvothermal strategy to syn- thesize Pt-Ni bimetallic truncated octahedral nanocrystals and then transformed these nanocrystals into Pt-Ni heterostructures, displaying an HER overpotential of 48 mV at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2. In this work, we report a one-step high-temperature sintering method for the synthesis of bicontinuous nanoporous ultralow-Pt- content Ni-based alloys based on the nanoscale Kirkendall effect [38- 44]. Spherical Mg powders are employed as a nanopore-making agent, which is favourable for forming a solid/liquid interface, increasing the diffusion rate of atoms/vacancies and decreasing the sintering temper - ature during the high-temperature sintering process [45]. The as- prepared alloy electrode exhibits superior electrocatalytic activity and outstanding stability at ultralarge HER current densities, providing a novel direction for the design and fabrication of advanced electrocatalysts. 2.Results and discussion Fig. 1a displays a schematic diagram of the fabrication process for bicontinuous nanoporous PtNiMg alloys with ultralow noble metal content by using a one-step high-temperature sintering method. The distinctive nanoporous architecture is formed based on the nanoscale Kirkendall effect owing to the unequal diffusion rate of atoms/vacancies between Pt/Ni, Pt/Mg and Ni/Mg during the high-temperature sintering process. Fig. 1b exhibits the X-ray diffraction patterns of nanoporous PtNiMg alloys sintered at 900 •C with different platinum nominal compositions of 0.05 wt%, 0.1 wt%, 0.3 wt%, 0.5 wt%, 1 wt%, 3 wt% and 5 wt% (denoted as 0.05-PtNiMg-900, 0.1-PtNiMg-900, 0.3-PtNiMg- 900, 0.5-PtNiMg-900,1-PtNiMg-900,3-PtNiMg-900 and 5-PtNiMg-900, respectively). The actual atom content of Pt, Ni and Mg in these alloys was analysed by ICP-OES and was basically consistent with the nominal weight percentage (see Table S1). Fig. 1b displays the XRD patterns of the 0.05-PtNiMg-900, 0.1-PtNiMg-900, 0.3-PtNiMg-900, 0.5-PtNiMg- 900, 1-PtNiMg-900, 3-PtNiMg-900 and 5-PtNiMg-900 alloys. As shown in Fig. 1b, the diffraction peaks of all the as-prepared alloys located at 44.3•, 51.7•and 76.2•correspond to the (111), (200) and (220) facets of metallic Ni with the body-centred-cubic (bcc) structure (JCPDS No. 70–1849), respectively. The diffraction peaks located at 40.5•, 44.0•, 46.8•, 56.9•and 73.2•correspond to the (106), (144), (203), (204) and (217) facets of a hexagonal MgNi 2 intermetallic compound (JCPDS No. 65–4318), respectively. Clearly, a small quantity of Pt does not change the nanoporous PtNiMg alloy structure. Hence, the nanoporous PtNiMg alloy consists of a cubic Ni solid solution and a small MgNi 2 phase. Fig. 2a and b shows the FESEM images of the nanoporous 1-PtNiMg- 900 alloy. As shown in the figure, the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy displayed a 3D-interconnected bicontinuous nanoporous structure with a pore diameter of approximately 10 ~ 50 nm. The formation of the nano- porous 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy could be ascribed to the nanoscale Kirken - dall effect based on the unequal diffusion rates of species (Pt, Ni, Mg) and vacancies on the solid/liquid and solid/solid interface during high- temperature sintering [42,43] . Moreover, few Pt species are homoge - neously dispersed into the bulk of Ni-based solid solution, which possibly forms a single Pt atom in the alloy bulk, favourably increasing the intrinsic electrocatalytic activity and utilization of the HER in alkaline media. The 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy possesses a large BET surface area of 1.29 m2 g␀1 and an average pore diameter of 9.95 nm (see Figure S1 and Table S2), increasing the number of exposed active sites derived from nanoscale Kirkendall porosity. The Pt content influenced Fig. 1.(a) Schematic diagram of the fabrication process for bicontinuous nanoporous PtNiMg alloy with ultralow noble metal content. (b) XRD patterns of 0.05- PtNiMg-900, 0.1-PtNiMg-900, 0.3-PtNiMg-900, 0.5-PtNiMg-900,1-PtNiMg-900,3-PtNiMg-900 and 5-PtNiMg-900 alloys. Y. Zheng et al.
