Chemical Engineering Journal 433 (2022) 134571 4PtNiMg-900 ageing can be attributed to the formation Ni(OH) 2 from Ni, which further proves that the surface structure of Ni changes during the HER process. As shown in Fig. 3b, the Ni 2p spectrum of the 1-PtNiMg- 900 alloy before ageing shows two peaks of Ni 2p3/2 at 855.3 eV and Ni 2p1/2 at 873.1 eV, corresponding to Ni0and NiO. After ageing, the Ni 2p3/2 (Ni 2p1/2) spectral signals appear only at 855.6 eV (873.3 eV), which is consistent with Ni2derived from a Ni(OH) 2 phase, indicating that the Ni species on the alloy surface have changed after undergoing HER. The Mg 2p spectrum of the unaged 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy in Fig. 3c presents two peaks at 49.58 eV and 50.82 eV, corresponding to signals of Mg0and Mg2(MgO), respectively [46]. However, the XPS spectrum of the aged sample in Fig. 3c can be fitted to a single peak of Mg 2p at 49.47 eV, corresponding to the binding energy of Mg2in Mg(OH) 2, which is in line with the previous analysis [42,43] . Moreover, the O 1 s spectrum of the unaged sample in Fig. 3d contains four different peaks located at 529.6, 531.1, 531.9 and 533.4 eV, which correspond to Mg-O, Ni-O, Pt-O and adsorbed water, respectively. After ageing, the O 1 s spectrum exhibits four main peaks at 529.6, 531.1, 531.9 eV and 533.4 eV, corresponding to the signals of O in Mg(OH) 2, O in Ni(OH) 2, Pt-O and adsorbed water, respectively. Obviously, the peak intensity of hy- droxyl groups plays a dominant role, which should be attributed to the formation of an (oxy)hydroxide layer on the surface of the aged sample. These analyses indicate that an (oxy)hydroxide layer composed of Ni, Mg, Pt and O species during the HER process is formed on the surface of the aged 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy. Fig. 4a exhibits the LSV curves of pure Ni-900, 0.05-PtNiMg-900, 0.1- PtNiMg-900, 0.3-PtNiMg-900, 0.5-PtNiMg-900,1-PtNiMg-900,3- PtNiMg-900 and 5-PtNiMg-900 alloys and commercial Pt/C electrodes at a scan rate of 1 mV s␀1. As shown in the figure, the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode for the HER possesses an extremely low overpotential of 22 mV to drive a current density of 10 mA cm␀2, which is far smaller than those values of 0.05-PtNiMg-900 (107 mV), 0.1-PtNiMg-900 (62 mV), 0.3-PtNiMg-900 (42 mV), 0.5-PtNiMg-900 (47 mV), 3-PtNiMg-900 (34 mV), 5-PtNiMg-900 (39 mV) and Pt/C (31 mV). The PtNiMg alloy electrodes with different Pt content sintered at different temperatures were also measured to identify the optimal electrocatalytic activity by performing LSV curves (see Figure S4). Clearly, the temperature of 900 ℃ is suitable for the fabrication of nanoporous PtNiMg alloy electrodes. The HER overpotential of 22 mV for the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode at 10 mA cm␀2 is nearly the lowest value in the present reported references (see Table S3). The 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode for the HER may achieve an optimal atom ratio of Ni to Pt for absorbing OH and H on the inner/outer alloy surface, respectively, resulting in the highest electro - catalytic activity among all the PtNiMg alloy electrodes with different compositions and sintering temperatures. Hence, the admirable HER electrocatalytic activity of the nanoporous 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode should be ascribed to a 3D bicontinuous nanoporous structure, perfect synergistic effect of Ni and Pt species, more exposed active sites and high Fig. 3.(a) Pt 4f, (b) Ni 2p, (c) Mg 2p and (d) O 1 s XPS spectra of the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy before and after ageing for 200 CV cycles at a potential range of 0–0.287 V (vs. RHE) with a scan rate of 10 mV s␀1 in 1 M KOH solution. Dashed white, solid orange and solid lines represent the experimental data, spectral envelopes and deconvolution spectra, respectively. Y. Zheng et al.
