Chemical Engineering Journal 433 (2022) 134571 6electrodes during the HER process. Fig. 4c shows the EIS of all as- prepared electrodes. The data in Figure 6c are fitted with an equiva - lent circuit of Rs(QdlRct) (see Table S4). Rs, Qdl and Rct represent the solution resistance, double-layer capacitance and charge-transfer resis- tance during the HER process, respectively. As shown in Fig. 4c and Table S4, the nanoporous 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode has an extremely low Rct value of 0.05 Ω cm␀2, which is far smaller than 0.05- PtNiMg-900 (3.41 Ω cm␀2), 0.1-PtNiMg-900 (0.52 Ω cm␀2), 0.3- PtNiMg-900 (0.36 Ω cm␀2), 0.5-PtNiMg-900 (0.11 Ω cm␀2), 3-PtNiMg- 900 (0.06 Ω cm␀2) and 5-PtNiMg-900 (0.10 Ω cm␀2) , indicating a lower charge transfer resistance and higher intrinsic electrocatalytic activity for the HER [26,42,43,53,54] . According to the formula of the turnover frequency (TOF) based on the mass of Pt, the TOF of the 1- PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode is 0.153 ×10-3 s␀1, which is larger than the TOF of other electrodes (see Table S5), further confirming more excellent electrocatalytic activity. From charge-transfer resistance and TOF efficiency, the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode is more efficient for HER in comparison with other nanoporous alloy electrodes. The 1- PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode also possesses an outstanding Faradaic ef- ficiency of 99.5% in an alkaline medium (see Figure S5). The ECSA of all as-prepared electrodes can be qualitatively evaluated by the double layer capacitance (Cdl) derived from the CV data in Figure S6 by performing in a region of 0.303 –0.403 V (vs. RHE) with a scan rate of 10 mV s␀1 –100 mV s␀1. Fig. 4d exhibits the Cdl values of all as-prepared electrodes measured in a 1 M KOH solution. As shown in the figure, the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode achieves a much larger Cdl of 75.6 mF cm␀2 than the Cdl values of 0.05-PtNiMg-900 (1.41 mF cm␀2), 0.1-PtNiMg-900 (1.74 mF cm␀2), 0.3-PtNiMg-900 (3.74 mF cm␀2), 0.5- PtNiMg-900 (15.3 mF cm␀2), 3-PtNiMg-900 (66.4 mF cm␀2), 5-PtNiMg- 900 (48.3 mF cm␀2) and Pure Ni-900 (1.35 mF cm␀2), possessing more exposed active sites for the HER. The long-term stability of electrocatalysts for the HER is a key indi- cator for practical applications, especially at large current densities (F1000 mA cm␀2). Fig. 4e displays the durability of all as-prepared electrodes in a 1 M KOH electrolyte at multicurrent densities of 1–200 mA cm␀2. As shown in the figure, the overpotential of various 1- PtNiMg-900 alloy electrodes with different Pt contents for the HER nearly does not fluctuate for 50,000 s at multicurrent densities of 1, 10, 50, 100 and 200 mA cm␀2, confirming the superior stability. In partic - ular, the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode also exhibits extremely low overpotentials of 7, 22, 40, 52 and 73 mV at current densities of 1, 10, 50, 100 and 200 mA cm␀2, respectively, achieving the optimal elec- trocatalytic activity in the reported data (see Table S3). The chro- nopotentiometric curve of the nanoporous 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode for 100 h at a current density of 50 mA cm␀2 further confirms the long- term durability for the HER (see Figure S9). The 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode was also subjected to more severe measurements at a current density of 2000 mA cm␀2 in a two-electrode electrolytic cell (see Figure S10). It is obvious that the bath voltage of 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode rapidly decreases during the initial period and then remain a stable stage over time. After ageing, the structure and surface morphology of the nanoporous 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode has not nearly a change (see Figure S7 and Figure S8), indicating the superior stability at an ultralarge current density. The LSV curves of the 1- PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode after ageing for 100 h at 2000 mA cm␀2 are shown in Fig. 4f. The 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode for the HER has an even smaller increase in electrocatalytic activity than the unaged sample, meeting the requirement of large-scale practical application, especially at ultralarge current densities. Undoubtedly, the electro - catalytic activity and stability of the 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode are competitive with those of advanced electrocatalysts in an alkaline so- lution (see Table S3). 3.Conclusions In summary, a bicontinuous nanoporous PtNiMg alloy of ultralow noble metal content with preeminent performance for the HER was successfully synthesized via a one-step high-temperature sintering method based on the nanoscale Kirkendall effect. The 1-PtNiMg-900 alloy electrode for the HER in an alkaline medium achieves an extremely low Tafel slope of 30.9 mV dec-1 and a low overpotential of 22 mV for 10 mA cm␀2. The electrode for HER was subjected to a current density of 2000 mA cm␀2 for 100 h without compromising the electro - catalytic activity, ascribed to the merits of a 3D bicontinuous nano- porous structure, perfect synergistic effect of Ni and Pt species, high structural stability and excellent conductivity. This study provides a highly durable electrode with superior electrocatalytic activity, fulfilling the practical application for large-scale H2 production. 4.Experimental section Nickel (17.2 μm, F99.7%) and magnesium (75 μm, F99.9%) pow- ders were purchased from Aladdin Chemistry Corp (Shanghai, China). Platinum (D10 nm, F99.9%) powder was received from Kaida Chemical Engineering Co., Ltd. (Shanxi, China). Materials synthesis. Ni and Pt powders with different weight ratios were weighed to prepare a bicontinuous nanoporous PtNiMg alloy with an ultralow noble metal content (see Table S6). The spherical Mg powder, accounting for 10 wt% of the total mass of Ni and Pt powders, was added as a pore-making agent to achieve the nanoscale Kirkendall effect. Ni, Pt and Mg powders of different weight proportions were mixed in a planetary ball mill (QM-3SP4, Nanjing University Instrument Plant, NanJing, China) at a rotation speed of 250 rpm for 6 h. Next, green compacts (Ф 12 mm ×6 mm) were obtained by cold pressing the mixed powders at a uniaxial pressure of 580 MPa for 2 min. Then, the green compacts placed in an alumina crucible were sintered in a tube furnace (OTF-1200X, Hefei Kejing Materials Technology Corp.). Before sintering, the furnace tube was pumped with highly pure argon (F99.999%) to remove any residual air for 30 min at a rate of 30 ml min␀1. Finally, the green compacts were sintered at 800–1000 •C for 1 h under an argon flow rate of 10 ml min␀1 and naturally cooled to room temperature. Material characterizations. The phases, composition, morphol - ogies and microstructure of the as-prepared alloys were determined by using X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 ADVANCE), field-emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM, ZEISS Sigma HD), transmission electron microscopy (TEM, FEI Talos F200X) and energy-dispersive X- ray spectroscopy (EDS, Oxford Instruments). The surface atomic valence states of the as-prepared alloy electrodes before and after ageing were analysed by using X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Axis Ultra DLD, Shimadzu-Kratos). The precise element content in the samples was identified by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES, PerkinElmer 8300), and the BET specific surface areas and average pore diameter were analysed by using N2 absorbing ␀desorbing isotherms (Micromeritics ASAP 2460). Electrochemical measurements. An electrochemical workstation (CHI-660C, Shanghai CHI Instruments, Inc.) was employed to determine the electrochemical properties of the as-prepared alloy electrodes at room temperature in a 1.0 M KOH medium. The as-prepared alloy electrodes, a graphite rod and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE) were used as the working electrode, a graphite rod and a saturated calomel electrode (SCE), respectively. All the tested potentials were calibrated to the potential of a reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) according to the method [43]. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves were obtained with a scan rate of 1 mV s␀1. The iRs-corrected Tafel slopes were calculated according to the LSV and electrochemical impedance spec- troscopy (EIS) data. EIS was conducted in the frequency range of 100 kHz to 0.01 Hz at an amplitude of 5 mV. The ECSA can be calculated from the CV curves tested at a nonFaradaic potential with different scan rates from 10 to 100 mV s␀1. The stability of the nanoporous alloy electrode was determined at consecutive constant current densities of 1–200 mA cm␀2. A two-electrode cell was employed to achieve long- Y. Zheng et al.
