Keywords: Hydrogen evolution reactionFree energyOxygen vacancyBand center level Strain engineeringthe other hand, O- p zband center level is responsible for the hydrogen adsorption free energy in the case of hydrogen adsorbed with the surface oxygen atom. Unexpectedly, forMn doping cases, the pre-adsorbed hydrogen could activate surface O atom with additionalhydrogen adsorption free energy being close to zero. In addition, the strain engineeringalso could effectively adjust HER activity in defected nanosheets. ©2019 Hydrogen Energy Publications LLC. Published by Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. Contents Introduction . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00 Methods . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00Results and discussions . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00 Crystal structure and adsorption site of TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00 HER catalytic activity . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00 The effect of biaxial strain on the HER catalytic activity . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00 Conclusion . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00Acknowledgements . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00Supplementary data . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00References . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 00 Introduction Molecular hydrogen has become a promising energy carrier to replace traditional fossil fuel, due to its high energy density, abundance and environmental friendliness [ [1e3]]. As the half reactions of photocatalytic water splitting [ 4], the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is an important pathway to convertsolar energies into chemical energies in the form of molecularhydrogen [ 5]. Hence, an efﬁcient and stable catalyst is required to achieve high HER activity and platinum (Pt) hasbeen considered as a potential catalyst with high HER activityexcept for its scarcity and valuableness [ 6]. Besides, another primary requirement for photocatalytic HER is that the con-duction band minimum of catalyst should be higher than the reduction potential of H þ/H2. In addition, photocatalytic efﬁ- ciency is also susceptible to the recombination of photoex-cited electron-hole. With the boom in graphene successfully prepared, two- dimensional (2D) layered materials, such as 2D transition-metal dichalcogenides (TMDs), and metal carbides and ni-trides (collectively known as MXenes), have attracted muchattentions over the past decades [ 7e9]. Generally speaking, 2D nanosheets usually are prepared from their layered bulkphase with lateral dimension from several hundred nano-meters to several micrometers. They could provide much more reaction sites and better carrier separation ability, which is beneﬁcial for the HER process [ 10e12]. Recently, as a most promising candidate in photocatalyst, the lepidocrocite-type TiO 2(LNS-TiO 2) nanosheet has captured interest in photocatalytic applications, since it hasbeen successfully exfoliated through soft-chemical proced-ures [ 13]. Compared with the bulk TiO 2(e.g., anatase and rutileTiO 2) and other 2D TiO 2nanosheets, LNS-TiO 2nanosheets have advantages in high stability [ 14,15], reduced electron- hole recombination rate [ 16] and suitable band edge align- ment relative to the water redox potential [ 17]. Hence, LNS- TiO 2nanosheets show great potentials to be as a highly efﬁ- cient photocatalyst for HER. Unfortunately, too weak bonding interaction between the proton and catalyst surface results in low H 2production efﬁ- ciency in LNS-TiO 2[18,19]. In order to improve the HER ac- tivity, transition metal (TM) doping has been theoretically andexperimentally conﬁrmed to be a feasible method to tune the hydrogen adsorption ability of LNS-TiO 2nanosheets. During the doping process, almost all dopants could distribute veryclose to the surface of single layered TiO 2nanosheet due to its ultrathin thickness compared with the bulk TiO 2[19]. There- fore, most of the dopants can be directly involved with theadsorbates during the catalytic reaction, potentiallyimproving photocatalytic efﬁciency than that of bulk TiO 2[19]. It has been reported that the photocatalytic hydrogen pro-duction rate increases about 10 times in Rh-doped LNS-TiO 2 nanosheets with single Rh atoms as reaction centers [ 19]. Cr doping has also been theoretically considered to adjust thehydrogen adsorption ability close to Pt metal in LNS-TiO 2 nanosheets without defects [ 18]. However, as far as we know, TM doping usually introduces oxygen vacancies in oxides dueto low valence state, which has been conﬁrmed in previousliteratures [ 20,21]. More importantly, oxygen vacancy defect was proposed in Rh doped LNS-TiO 2nanosheets by Ida [ 22]. Therefore, some critical issues still should be explored forgetting more insights into the HER behavior of defected LNS-TiO 2nanosheets. For example, what is the most stable hydrogen adsorption conﬁguration in LNS-TiO 2nanosheetsinternational journal of hy drogen energy xxx (xxxx) xxx 2 Please cite this article as: Yuan J et al., Activated HER performance of defected single layered TiO 2nanosheet via transition metal doping, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2019.11.191
