[Page 3/8]
============================================================
with oxygen vacancy (LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets)? How does the hydrogen adsorption ability change? Since the 4 dtransition metal of Rh is rare and much cost, the 3 dtransition metals were chosen as dopants in our work for calculations. On the other hand, Mn-, Co-, Fe- and Ni-doped single layered TiO 2nanosheets have been success- fully prepared in the experiment, yet there is no relevant re-ports on HER activity found for them [ 23e25]. Therefore, our investigation with 3 dtransition metals for hydrogen evolution is viable and critically needed by employing ﬁrst-principlescalculations. And the mechanism of tunable activity of HERwas explored in details. Moreover, interestingly, we found that the pre-adsorbed hydrogen atom which occupied oxygen vacancy is beneﬁcial for improving the activity of additionalhydrogen adsorption at the surface oxygen atom in Fe-, Co-and Mn-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets. In addition, the HER performance of LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets is also found to be susceptible to the strain engineering. Methods All calculations here were performed with Vienna ab initiosimulation package (VASP) based on the density functionaltheory [ 26,27]. The exchange correlation energy was descrip- ted by Perdew-Burke-Ernzerhof (PBE) of generalized gradientapproximation (GGA). The cut-off energy of 400 eV was set forthe plane wave expansion. The convergence threshold of totalenergy was 10 /C05eV, and all structures are fully relaxed until the residual forces on each atom are smaller than 0.01 eV/ /C23A. A vacuum slab of 18 /C23A was added in the z direction to prevent interaction between neighboring periodic slab units. TheBrillouin zone was sampled by Monkhorst-Pack k-point mesh of 4 /C23/C21 for the 3 /C23/C21 supercell with one oxygen va- cancy. Spin-polarization was applied in all calculations. Thevan der Waals (vdW) interactions is taking into account byusing the DFT-D2 scheme [ 28,29]. The hydrogen adsorption free energy ( DG H*) is considered as an effective descriptor to evaluate the HER activity [ 30]. In this work, the hydrogen adsorption free energy of defectedTiO 2nanosheet is deﬁned as: DGH*¼DEH*þDEZPE/C0TDSH (1)where DEH*is the hydrogen binding energy which can be ob- tained as follow: DEH*¼EðhostþnHÞ/C0E½hostþðn/C01ÞH/C138/C0EðH2Þ=2 (2) in which E(host þnH) and E[host þ(n-1)H] are the energies of LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheet with nand ( n-1) adsorbed hydrogen atoms, and E(H2) is the total energy of one hydrogen gas molecule. Then, DEZPEis the zero point energy difference be- tween the adsorbed state ( EZPE) and the gas phase ( EH2 ZPE), i.e., DEZPE¼EZPE/C01/2EH2 ZPE. Additionally, because the vibration entropy of H* in the adsorbed state is relatively small, theentropy difference of 1/2H 2is taken as DSHz/C01/2 S0 H2, where S0 H2is the entropy of H 2in the gas phase at the standard con- ditions [ 31]. Besides, in order to simulate the biaxial strain of LNS-TiO 2- Ovnanosheets, the lattice relaxation was just performed along one in-plane direction for the strained crystal. And the spec-iﬁed in-plane strain was deﬁned as ε¼(a-a 0)/a0, with aand a0 are strained and unstrained equilibrium lattice constants along a-direction, respectively. Results and discussions Crystal structure and adsorption site of TM-doped LNS- TiO 2-Ovnanosheets The LNS-TiO 2nanosheet has two inequivalent O atoms which are two-fold or four-fold coordinated with Ti atom respec-tively, while Ti atom is fully 6-fold coordinated [ 32]. This im- plies the LNS-TiO 2nanosheet is relatively stable among the reported TiO 2nanosheets [ 14,15,33]. Generally, if TM atoms are introduced as isolated dopants at the Ti4þsites in the LNS- TiO 2nanosheet, oxygen vacancies will prefer to form around the dopant. For example, it has been conﬁrmed that oxygen defect formation energy in Rh-doped LNS-TiO 2nanosheet is lower than that in undoped LNS-TiO 2nanosheet [ 22]. And the crystal structure for TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets is shown in Fig. 1 (a). Through theoretical calculations, one hydrogen adsorbed on top of TM atom (site I) or on the surface oxygen (site II,O ads), were considered as the most stable structural conﬁgu- rations for different doping models [ 22]. Then, with calculated hydrogen binding energies from Eq. (2), the relative stability of Fig. 1 e(a) Crystal structures of TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets. (b) There are two different adsorption conﬁgurations in the TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets. One is the hydrogen adsorbed on top of TM atom. The other is one hydrogen adsorbed on the surface oxygen. Color code: Ti: blue; O: red; TM: brown; H: white. The spherical dotted line represents anoxygen vacancy and hydrogen pre-adsorption sites in (a) and (b), respectively. (For interpretation of the references to color in this ﬁgure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (xxxx) xxx 3 Please cite this article as: Yuan J et al., Activated HER performance of defected single layered TiO 2nanosheet via transition metal doping, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2019.11.191
