single hydrogen atom adsorption is estimated for TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets as listed in Table 1 . The hydrogen atom is favorable to adsorb at site I in most cases, while thehydrogen binding energies of Ni-, Cu- and Zn-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets are lower at site II. Therefore, the HER activity of oxygen defected LNS-TiO 2nanosheets will be further discussed below with obtained stable adsorptionconﬁgurations. HER catalytic activity Firstly, the HER activity for the hydrogen adsorbed at site I inTM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets is estimated by the hydrogen adsorption free energy DGH*. Generally speaking, the value of DGH*close to 0 eV has been considered as the high efﬁciency for HER catalysts [ 31]. In contrast, more positive (negative) DGH*indicates that the catalyst exhibits relatively weaker (stronger) stabilization for the proton bonding, whichleads to lower HER activity. The calculated free energy for theﬁrst one hydrogen ( DG 1H*) adsorbing at site I in TM-doped models has been presented in Fig. 2 . Obviously, the binding strength of hydrogen bond is sensitive to the dopants. And theoptimal value of DG1H*can be achieved by Fe doping ( /C00.12 eV) in the LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets, rather competitive to that of Pt (DGH*¼/C00.09 eV) [ 30]. It is known that, for transition metal, the density of states near the Fermi level mainly originates from the d-states. The dominated hybridization between H 1 sstates and the narrow transition metal d-states will form a fully ﬁlled bonding orbital (s) and a partially ﬁlled antibonding orbital ( s*). As a conse- quence, the classical d-band model could be considered as a good descriptor for the adsorbate-metal interaction. The d- band center level ( εd) is deﬁned as εd¼Zþ∞ /C0∞E,DðEÞdE Z/C0∞ /C0∞DðEÞdE(3) where D(E) is the density of states (DOS) of TM d-orbitals [34,35]. The model identiﬁed the position of the center of the d- orbitals and the degree of ﬁlling of the d-orbitals [ 36]. The d- band center level shifts down deeper with respect to the Fermilevel, which results in higher d-electron occupation in s* states. According to the molecular orbital theory [ 37,38], the bond order, which represents the binding strength betweenadsorbate and surface atom, is determined by the d-electron number in s* state when sstate is fully ﬁlled. Therefore, the hydrogen binding strength gradually weakens as the d-band center level of TM atom becomes lower. On the basis of the above d-band model, the calculated d- band center level of TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets has been depicted in Fig. 3 (a). The calculated d-band center level of TM atom shifts up from Zn-doped to Cr-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ov nanosheets expect for the Ni and V dopants, which results in astrong binding strength between hydrogen and TM atom. Thisfurther lowers the hydrogen adsorption free energy of nano-sheets which is well consistent with the evolution tendency ofDG 1H*in this work. The decrease of d-band center level is generally dependent on the number of d-orbital electrons of dopants. That is why the HER activity of LNS-TiO 2-Ovnano- sheets could be well modulated by different transition metaldopants. (The detailed electronic structure analysis for d-band center variation in different TM doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnano- sheets has also been presented in the Supporting Informa- tion.) However, as for Ni-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheet, it provides a relatively shallow d-band center level, but its hydrogen adsorption free energy is higher. This behavior maybe attributed to the absence of an unpaired electron in the lowspin d 8electron conﬁguration of Ni in the LNS-TiO 2-Ovnano- sheet [ 39], as shown in Fig. 3 (b). The magnetic moments and spin density distributions have been shown in Table S1 and Fig. S2 , respectively. Moreover, for V-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheet, a deeper d- band center level was found although it has a relatively strong hydrogen binding ability. This can be explained by the change of H-TM bond lengths below. Table 2 lists the calculated H eTi and H-TM bond lengths for TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnano- sheets after H adsorption. It can be clearly seen that H-TMlength is relatively short in this work, which is similar to thecase in Rh-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets [ 22]. In other words, the hybridization interaction between H atom and TMatom is dominated in these nanosheets. Nevertheless, there isTable 1 eHydrogen binding energies ( DEH*, eV) of undoped and TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets at different adsorption sites. undoped and TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-OvnanosheetsHydrogen binding energies ( DEH*, eV) site I site II pure /C01.09 /C00.63 V /C01.24 /C00.06 Cr /C01.56 /C00.88 Mn /C00.44 /C00.11 Fe /C00.39 /C00.19 Co /C00.48 /C00.19 Ni 0.27 /C00.68 Cu 1.07 /C00.89 Zn 1.61 /C00.05 Fig. 2 eCalculated DG1H*at site I in TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ov nanosheets.international journal of hy drogen energy xxx (xxxx) xxx 4 Please cite this article as: Yuan J et al., Activated HER performance of defected single layered TiO 2nanosheet via transition metal doping, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2019.11.191
