much less difference between H-TM and H eTi lengths in V- doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheet. This indicates that the inter- action between H atom and Ti atom is not negligible. Hence,the hydrogen adsorption ability in V-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ov nanosheet is stronger than that predicted by the d-band cen- ter level.On the other hand, HER activity of defected nanosheets with hydrogen at site II is also investigated here. Fig. 4 shows the calculated DG1H*for hydrogen at site II in TM-doped LNS- TiO 2-Ovnanosheets. Since the hydrogen adsorption takes place on the surface O site, the pzband center level of O adsis responsible for the variation of DG1H*[38,40,41]. The deﬁnition ofpz-band center level ( εpz) is similar to that of d-band center level, i.e., orbital contribution of TM-3 dis substituted by O-2 pz. According to the molecular orbital theory, the higher pz-band center level indicates the stronger interaction between theadsorbate and surface oxygen. Therefore, the p z-band center level variation with DG1H*is depicted in Fig. 5 . It is observed that the pz-band center level becomes shallower from Zn- to Cu-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets except for Ni case. Simi- larly, this is also attributed to the absence of an unpairedelectron in the p z-orbital of Ni-doped nanosheet. The calcu- lated magnetic moments and spin density distributions havealso been shown in Table S1 and Fig. S2 , respectively. Besides the above discussions on HER activity of the ﬁrst hydrogen atom adsorption, the cases for the second hydrogenadsorption was also analyzed. Taking into account thehydrogen binding energies in Table 1 , the second hydrogen atom will prefer to adsorb with the surface oxygen atom(adsorption site II) when oxygen vacancy was occupied by pre-adsorbed hydrogen atom. Since the ﬁrst hydrogen atom hasalready adsorbed with the surface oxygen for Ni-, Cu- and Zn-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets, they are not included here for analyzing. Similarity, the hydrogen adsorption free energy for the second hydrogen atom ( DG2H*) is also calculated with Eq. (1). And adsorption binding DE2H*is estimated as below: DE2H*¼Eðhostþ2HÞ/C0EðhostþHÞ/C0EðH2Þ=2 (4) where E(host þ2H) and E(host þH) are the energies of LNS- TiO 2-Ovnanosheets with and without the second hydrogen atom. The obtained DG2H*and pz-band center level of TM- doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets are presented in Fig. 6 . Inter- estingly, compared with the conﬁgurations without hydrogenadsorbed at oxygen vacancy site, the surface O atom has been Fig. 3 e(a) Free energy for HER ( DG1H*) at site I and relevant d-band center level in TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-OVnanosheets. Purple dashed circles mark abnormal condition. (b) Calculated DOS of Ni atom in doped LNS-TiO 2-OVnanosheet. The vertical yellow dashed line indicates the Fermi level. (For interpretation of the references to color in this ﬁgure legend, the reader isreferred to the Web version of this article.) Table 2 eCalculated H eTi and H-TM bond lengths for TM- doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets after H adsorption. TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets Bond length ( /C23A) H-TM H eTi V 1.77 1.88 Cr 1.69 1.93Mn 1.62 1.98Fe 1.56 2.05 Co 1.51 2.14 Ni 1.48 2.29Cu 1.46 2.62Zn 1.62 1.94 Fig. 4 eCalculated DG1H*at site II in TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ov nanosheets.international journal of hydrogen energy xxx (xxxx) xxx 5 Please cite this article as: Yuan J et al., Activated HER performance of defected single layered TiO 2nanosheet via transition metal doping, International Journal of Hydrogen Energy, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2019.11.191
