activated for HER in Mn-, Co- and Fe-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ov nanosheets with hydrogen occupying oxygen vacancy. More importantly, Mn- and Co-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheetsexhibit much close to 0 eV of DG2H*(0.004 eV and /C00.06 eV, respectively), indicating superior HER activity in these nano-sheets. And the p z-band center level of O adsis still responsible for the variation of DG2H*, as shown in Fig. 6 . In addition, the shift of pz-band center level between after and before hydrogen occupying oxygen vacancy could be attributed tothe charge redistribution resulting from the case likehydrogen doping in the nanosheets. It can be clearly observedin the calculated charge density difference in Fig. S3 . The effect of biaxial strain on the HER catalytic activity For two-dimensional materials, the strain engineering is also usually seen as an effective way to modulate the properties bychanging the bond strength [ 42e45]. Thus, it is quite necessary to investigate the effect of external strain on the HER activityof LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets. In this work, the biaxial strain is applied for Fe-doped LNS-TiO 2-OVnanosheet as an example. The calculated ﬁrst hydrogen adsorption free energy at sites I was shown in Fig. 7 (a), and the relationship between d-band center level and biaxial strain ( ε) were also presented. Apparently, a non-linear DG1H*tendency is observed with Fig. 5 e(a) Free energy for HER ( DG1H*) at site II and relevant pz-band center level in TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-OVnanosheets. Purple dashed circles mark abnormal condition. (b) Calculated DOS of O adsin Ni-doped LNS-TiO 2-OVnanosheets. The vertical yellow dashed line indicates the Fermi level. (For interpretation of the references to color in this ﬁgure legend, thereader is referred to the Web version of this article.) Fig. 6 eThe calculated DG2H*andpz-band center level of Oadsin TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets. Fig. 7 eCalculated DG1H*andd-band center level (a) as well as DG2H*andpz-band center level (b) vs biaxial strain