increasing the strain. The optimal value of hydrogen adsorp- tion free energy ( /C00.09 eV) is obtained at strain of /C02%. Despite for the intricate variation, the d-band center model mentioned above is still suitable to explain the HER activity of strainedstructures. The high d-band center level indicates a low hydrogen adsorption free energy, whereas the shift-down ofd-band center means a weakened binding strength between adsorbate and surface atom. On the other hand, the DG 2H*at sites II and its pz-band center level were also calculated, as shown in Fig. 7 (b). Under compressive strain, the DG2H*almost remain the same ( /C00.25 ~ /C00.24 eV), and the corresponding pz- band center level is also nearly unchanged. When tension is applied, there are slightly up-shift of DG2H*and deeper pz-band center level. It also should notice that the ﬁrst hydrogenadsorption is more sensitive to the imposed biaxial strain thanthe case of second hydrogen adsorption. Conclusion In summary, the HER activity of TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-OV nanosheets have been systematically studied by the ﬁrst- principles calculations. As for the ﬁrst hydrogen adsorption, the hydrogen atom prefers to adsorb with transition metal viaoxygen vacancy or directly with the surface O atom. And theHER activity at sites I can be engineered to the optimal valueby introducing the Fe dopant. The variation of hydrogenadsorption free energy is well understood in light of theclassical d-band center model. Similarly, the calculated hydrogen adsorption free energy of sites II can be correlatedwith O- p zband center level. On the other hand, it is found that although the Co- and Mn-doped LNS-TiO 2-OVnanosheets exhibit a relatively strong binding strength for the ﬁrst hydrogen adsorption, they show excellent HER activities for the second hydrogen adsorption. This is attributed to thecharge redistribution caused by the pre-adsorbed hydrogenadsorption at oxygen vacancy. Moreover, HER performancecan be further modiﬁed by imposing external biaxial strain,which is also dominated by the d-band center or p z-band center models. In a word, these ﬁndings unveiled in our workare conductive to better understand the insights of HER pro-cess and design new two-dimensional catalyst for hydrogenproduction applications. Acknowledgements This work was supported by the National Nature ScienceFoundation of China (51972227) and (11804023), and the Nat-ural Science Foundation of Tianjin (18JCQNJC02700). Appendix A. Supplementary data Calculated partial density of states (PDOS) of TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ov nanosheets (TM ¼V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu and Zn, Fig. S1 ). The calculated magnetic moments M(mB) of TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets ( Table S1 ). The spin densitydistribution (isosurface value ¼0.01 e Ả/C03) for TM-doped LNS- TiO 2-OVnanosheets ( Fig. S2 ). The charge density difference for TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-Ovnanosheets with the hydrogen occu- pying oxygen vacancy ( Fig. S3 ). Atomic position of TM-doped LNS-TiO 2-OVnanosheets without and with H adsorption, as well as Fe-doped LNS-TiO 2-OVnanosheet with biaxial strain. Supplementary data to this article can be found online at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2019.11.191 . references [1]Lewis NS, Nocera DG. Powering the planet: chemical challenges in solar energy utilization. Proc Natl Acad Sci 2006;103:15729 e35. [2]Thomas IL, Dresselhaus MS. Alternative energy technologies. Nature 2001;141:332 e7. [3]Lu S, Yang S, Hu X, Liang Z, Guo Y, Xue Y, Cui H, Tian J. Fabrication of TiO 2nanoﬂowers with bronze (TiO 2(B))/ anatase heterophase junctions for efﬁcient photocatalytichydrogen production. Int J Hydrogen Energy 2019;44:24398 e406. [4]Lasia A. Mechanism and kinetics of the hydrogen evolution reaction. Int J Hydrogen Energy 2019;44:19484 e518. [5]Zou X, Zhang Y. Noble metal-free hydrogen evolution catalysts for water splitting. Chem Soc Rev 2015;44:5148 e80. [6]Hai X, Zhou W, Chang K, Pang H, Liu H, Shi L, Ichihara F, Ye J. Engineering crystallinity of Mo