Applied Surface Science 545 (2021) 149013 Available online 13 January 2021 0169-4332/© 2021 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.Full Length Article Activating PtSe 2 monolayer for hydrogen evolution reaction by defect engineering and Pd doping Li-Juan Maa,*, Haoming Shenb aKey Laboratory of Magnetic Molecules & Magnetic Information Materials Ministry of Education, and the School of Chemical and Material Science, Shanxi Normal University, No. 1, Gongyuan Street, Linfen 041004, China bDepartment of Materials Science and Engineering, College of Engineering, Peking University, Beijing 100871, China ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Hydrogen evolution reaction PtSe 2 monolayer Intrinsic defects Pd doping Density functional theory calculation ABSTRACT Platinum diselenide (PtSe 2) has been successfully fabricated with a hope of catalytic applications. However, the active sites of pristine PtSe 2 are only located at the edges while the sites in basal plane are largely inactive. To change such situation, in this study we introduce Pd dopant and vacancies to activate the basal plane for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). Based on systematic density functional theory calculations, we find that Pd doping reduces the band gap and enhances the catalytic ability, and the divacancy sites (VSe2 and VPt2) in pristine PtSe 2 monolayer and the monovacancy sites (VSe, VPd) as well as the divacancy sites (VPt2, VPdPt) in PdPtSe 4 monolayer significantly improve the catalytic activities comparable to that of Pt(111). A band-center model is applied to better understand the difference in HER activities, where the chemical bond energies of H (ΔG1) show an approximately linear relationship with the energy of d/p level of Pt/Se. The strategy proposed here would be applicable to other two-dimensional materials. 1.Introduction The electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is a key re- action in fuel cells and water splitting [1–3]. The most common mech - anism of HER are Volmer-Heyrovsky and Volmer-Tafel reactions. These reactions can be described in the following reaction steps: * He␀→ H*(Volmer reaction) H*He␀→H 2 ↑ * (Heyrovsky reaction) 2H*→ H2 ↑ 2* (Tafel reaction) The Tafel slope obtained by the polarization curve can qualitatively determine which is the reaction rate-limiting steps. In general, when the Tafel slope is greater than 120 mV/dec, Volmer reaction is the rate control step of the HER. Response, when the Tafel slope is greater than 40 mV/dec and less than 120 mV/dec, Heyrovsky reaction is the rate control step. When the Tafel slope is less than 30 mV/dec, the Tafel reaction is the rate control step. In general, the smaller the Tafel slope is, the faster the reaction rate is, and the less potential the catalytic elec- trode is [4,5] . To date, numerous inorganic materials including phosphides [6], sulfides [7,8] , carbides [9] and other materials [10], have been widely explored. However, the Volmer␀Heyrovsky mechanism is operative for these catalysts due to the high available Tafel slope of 40 mV/dec. In comparison, platinum (Pt) can catalyze HER through a Volmer␀Tafel mechanism with an energy barrier as low as ~0.80 eV, leading to a Tafel slope of only 30 mV/dec [11]. Though precious Pt and Pt-group metals have small Tafel slopes [12,13] for HER, the utilization is badly limited due to their high costs and scarcities [14]. Among various potential alternatives, two-dimensional (2D) nano- materials have become one of the most promising candidate materials due to their intriguing features, which include a large specific surface area, band gaps, electronic properties, highly exposed active edge sites, ease of intercalation and modification, and outstanding electrochem - istry activities [15–24]. However, most pure 2D electrocatalysts are not active enough because of the poor intrinsic activity, low density of active sites, and weak conductivity [8,25,26] . Thus, numerous efficient optimization strategies (including defect constructing, heteroatom doping, surface/ interface building) have been developed to make 2D nanomaterial substrates achieve higher catalytic activities [27–33]. These commonly substitute or disrupt the intrinsic atoms at the edge areas and basal planes to form more defect sites, which can work as the active sites. For *Corresponding author. E-mail addresses: malijuan19852223@163.com (L.-J. Ma), shenhaoming@pku.edu.cn (H. Shen). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Applied Surface Science u{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2k|���m! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apsusc.2021.149013 Received 11 October 2020; Received in revised form 31 December 2020; Accepted 8 January 2021
