Applied Surface Science 545 (2021) 149013 2example, N, P, S, Br, and O heteroatoms were investigated as the dopants of 2D carbon-based electrocatalysts to facilitate the electrocatalytic processes [34–38]. Heteroatoms Fe, Co, or Ni were doped in 2D TMD- based electrocatalysts such as MoS 2 to activate inert basal planes by greatly increasing the number of active sites [39–42]. On the other hand, introducing S vacancies can not only modify the band gaps but also create additional exposed Mo edge sites [43–45]. Recently, a new family of layered materials, namely platinum dis- elenide (PtSe 2) and palladium diselenide (PdSe 2) were successfully fabricated and have been extensively studied both experimentally [46–52] and theoretically [53–55] due to the potential applications in thermoelectric materials [56–59], optoelectronic devices, optoelec - tronic sensors, and field-effect transistors [60,61] . In addition, the hexagonal phase PtSe 2 and PdSe 2 show excellent potentials as catalysts [62], especially for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) [63–68]. Nevertheless, the catalytical active sites are mainly located at the edges while the sites in a basal plane are largely inactive [63–65]. To increase the active sites, Lau and coworkers synthesized edge-rich and large-area PtSe 2 via direct selenization of a Pt substrate [64]. In addition, theoretical calculation demonstrated that the B-doping, single vacancy and double vacancy can effectively increase the active sites on the inert basal plane of monolayer PtSe 2, PdSe 2, PdTe 2, and PtTe 2 [69]. Based on the second law of thermodynamics, point vacancies inevitably exist in all types of materials and are especially evident in the 2D ma- terials grown by chemical methods due to the imperfections of the growth process [70]. Hong et al. presented point defects are widely exist in monolayer MoS 2 prepared by mechanical exfoliation, physical and chemical vapor deposition [71] and made a comprehensive joint experiment –theory investigation. Very recently, Gao et al. [72] identified the most stable single and double vacancies in 1T-PtSe 2 and indicated that both Se and Pt single vacancies preferred to combine to form double vacancies. Zheng et al. [73] investigated intrinsic point defects in ultrathin PtSe 2 layers by using first-principles calculations and STM/STS. Five dominant types of point defects, such as mono-selenium vacancy (VSe), diselenium vacancy (VSe2), mono-platinum vacancy (VPt), diplatinum vacancy (VPt2), and anti-site defects with a Se atom substituting a Pt atom (SePt) have been observed and identified [73]. Abundant intrinsic defects can tailor the magnetic, optical, electronic properties, and create new functionalities that are absent in pristine PtSe 2 [74]. Ma et al. [75] demonstrated that the introduction of intrinsic defects was an effective means for improving the photocatalytic water-splitting activities of PtSe 2 monolayer. As mentioned earlier, both heteroatom doping and engineering de- fects are considered as an efficient approach to modulate the electronic states, control the surface/interface properties, and even affect their extrinsic and intrinsic activities. Then in this study, except for the above defects, we introduce Pd dopant to activate the basal plane for HER. It is fascinating to see whether the intrinsic defects will bring additional active sites and activate the inert basal planes for HER and what will happen if half of the Pt atoms in the 1T-PtSe 2 monolayer are replaced by Pd directly. The 5 intrinsic defects of PtSe 2 monolayer we choose are VSe, VPt, VSe2, VPt2, and SePt anti-site defects, which have already been widely observed in experiments. The 9 corresponding intrinsic defects of PdPtSe 4 monolayer (Pd : Pt 1 : 1) are constructed as well. Based on systematic density functional theory calculations, we find that Pd dopant reduces the band gap and enhances the catalytic ability; the divacancy sites (VSe2 and VPt2) in pristine PtSe 2 monolayer and the monovacancy sites (VSe, VPd) as well as the divacancy sites (VPt2, VPdPt) in PdPtSe 4 monolayer significantly improve the catalytic activities comparable to that of Pt(111). A band-center model is applied to better understand the difference in HER activities. More importantly, activities in both Heyrovsky and Tafel reactions are analyzed. 2.Calculation method The Vienna Ab-initio Simulation Package (VASP) [76] is used in this work. For structure optimization, Perdew␀Burke␀Ernzerhof (GGA- PBE) functional [77] is adopted for the exchange-correlation interac - tion. The projector augmented wave (PAW) method [78] is employed to describe the electron-ion potential. The kinetic energy cutoff energy is set to a 450 eV for the plane-wave basis. A total energy convergence criterion is 10␀5 eV and the Hellman␀Feynman force convergence is 0.01 eV/Å. Supercells consisting of 4 ×4 ×1 and 2 ×2 ×1 unit cells are used for PtSe 2 monolayer and PdPtSe 4 monolayer, respectively. The corresponding Brillouin zone is sampled with Monkhorst-Pack k-point mesh [79] of 4 ×4 ×1 and 4 ×3 ×1, respectively. The vacuum space is set to 20 Å along the z-direction. The Heyd –Scuseria –Ernzerhof (HSE06) hybrid functional [80] is adopted for accurate electronic structure cal- culations. The semiempirical dispersion-corrected DFT force-field approach (DFT-D3) [81] is adopted to describe the weak interaction. The climbing image nudged elastic band (CI-NEB) method [82] is used to search for the transition states of H adsorption and locate the mini- mum energy paths. The phonon spectrum is determined to use the finite displacement method which is implemented in the PHONOPY code [83]. Ab initio molecular dynamics (AIMD) simulation for 5000 steps with a time step of 2 fs is performed using the canonical ensemble with the Nos˘e-Hoover heat bath scheme [84] (300 K) to confirm thermal stability. Generally, the Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption (ΔGH) is a key descriptor to evaluate the electrocatalytic HER activity for Volmer reaction [58,3] . ΔGH ΔEH ΔEZPE - TΔS                                                             (1) ΔEH E (optimized H*) – E(optimized *) ␀1/2E (H2)                        (2) The standard hydrogen electrode is the reference. ΔEH is the adsorption energy of the H atom. ΔEZPE is the zero-point energy differ - ence between the gas-phase state and the adsorbed state, which has been ignored, considering that the phonon contribution to the free energy is quite small. TΔS is the gas-phase entropy contribution of an H2 at 298 K (0.40 eV). 3.Results and discussions 3.1. Geometric and electronic structures of perfect PtSe 2 and PdPtSe 4 monolayer PtSe 2 thermodynamically favored 1T-phase in experiment [48] and our calculations show that 1T-PtSe 2 (Figure S1b) is 0.01 eV per unit lower in energy than 2H-PtSe 2 (Figure S1a). As shown in Fig. 1a, 1T- PtSe 2 monolayer belongs to the P3m1 space group with the Pt layer sandwiched between two Se layers. Our calculated lattice constant of the primitive unit cell is 3.75 Å, which is in good agreement with another computational result of 3.70 Å [55] and the experimental result of 3.73 Å [85]. Including the spin-orbit coupling (SOC), the PBE calculation gives a band gap of 1.15 eV (Fig. 1c), which suits better with the pre- vious datum of 1.17 eV [48] and 1.18 eV [86]. After doping the equivalent proportion of Pd atoms, the most stable PdPtSe 4 monolayer (Fig. 1b) belongs to the P2/M space group (other meta-stable configu - rations are given in Figure S2). Its lattice constant of the primitive unit cell is a 3.75 Å, b 6.49 Å. PdPtSe 4 monolayer has a smaller band gap than the corresponding PtSe 2 monolayer (Fig. 1d), indicating that the Pd doping will improve the conductivity and ensure rapid electron trans - port during the HER process. There are no imaginary modes in the phonon band (Fig. 1e), suggesting that the PdPtSe 4 monolayer is dynamically stable. Furthermore, The AIMD simulation plotted in Fig. 1f shows that the PdPtSe 4 monolayer remains intact after being heated at 300 K for 10 ps. L.-J. Ma and H. Shen
