ated due to the binary element system and the lower symmetry. As shown in Figs. 4 and 5 point defects similar in PtSe 2 monolayer (VSe, VSe2, VPt, VPt2, SePt) as well as the other 4 point defects are included. The 4 different defects are mono-palladium va- cancy (VPd), dipalladium vacancy (VPd2), mono-platinum combined mono-palladium vacancy (VPdPt), and anti-site defects with a Se atom substituting a Pd atom (SePd). A comprehensive comparison of the HER performances of PtSe 2 monolayer and PdPtSe 4 monolayer are listed in Table 1. There are two different structures generated by removing a Se atom (Figure S5). The most stable is shown in Fig. 4a, with the Se defect occupying the center of the Pt-Pd-Pd triangle. The another VSe defect is 0.22 eV higher in energy (Figure S5) with the Se defect occupying the center of the Pt-Pt-Pd triangle. There are 6 different H adsorption sites are predicted for each VSe defect (Figure S6). Comparing the ΔGH of the 12 possible active sites, it can be found that the electrocatalytic activities for Volmer reaction are Pt site FPd site FSe site. The Pt site in the VSe-2- 5 defect had the highest HER activity with ΔGH of 0.15 eV. VSe2 defects are generated by losing the second Se atom near the first VSe in 1T-phase PdPtSe 2 monolayer. The relative energies of the fourteen possible VSe2 defects are listed in Table S7. The most stable VSe2 defect (VSe2-2-6 in Figure S7) and its most stable structure for H adsorption are shown in Fig. 4b and Fig. 5b, respectively. The VSe2-2-6 has a Pt atom that lies between two Se vacancies and its ΔGH is 0.15 eV. In fact, each VSe2 defect will produce 5–10 possible H adsorption sites. We found that there are three optimal H adsorption sites in the sub-stable VSe2-2-7 defect, which is 0.22 eV higher than the VSe2-2-6 structure. Their ΔGH are ␀0.035, ␀0.04, and ␀0.04 eV. The HER activities of the other VSe2 defects have been also studied as shown in Figure S8. Fig. 3.(a-e) The most stable configurations for a single H atom absorbing at the site of VSe, VSe2, VPt, VPt2, and SePt in PtSe 2 monolayer. The yellow, orange, white, and blue balls refer to the upper Se, lower Se, H, and Pt atoms, respectively. (f) The free energy diagram for HER on different active sites in PtSe 2 monolayer. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the web version of this article.) Table 1 Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption (ΔGH) for Volmer reaction and structure reconstruction energy (ΔER) of PtSe 2 and PdPtSe 4 monolayer caused by H adsorption. PtSe 2 ΔGH (eV) ΔER (eV) ΔG1 (eV) PdPtSe 4 ΔGH (eV) ΔER (eV) ΔG1 (eV) VSe 0.18 0.10 0.09 VSe 0.15 0.29 ␀0.14 VSe2 ␀0.05 0.13 ␀0.19 VSe2 0.25 0.11 0.13 VPt ␀0.24 0.20 ␀0.45 VPt 0.87 0.60 0.27 VPd 0.13 0.12 0.01 VPt2 0.10 0.61 ␀0.51 VPt2 ␀0.07 0.85 ␀0.92 VPd2 ␀0.25 0.15 ␀0.41 VPdPt 0.05 0.48 ␀0.42 SePt 0.86 0.43 0.44 SePt 2.15 – – SePd ␀0.43 – –  L.-J. Ma and H. Shen
