Applied Surface Science 545 (2021) 149013 7The values of ΔER and ΔG1 of different defects are listed in Table 1. The Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption (ΔGH) of VPt in PtSe 2 monolayer is ␀0.25 eV, but the chemical bond energy (ΔG1) is ␀0.45 eV, which indicates that the energy of structural deformation can’t be ignored. Previous studies have shown that structure reconstruction of two-dimension material has a significant effect on the catalytic perfor - mance [93]. As shown in Table 1, the ΔER of point defects in PtSe 2 and PdPtSe 4 monolayer are in the range of 0.10 –0.61 eV and 0.11 –0.85 eV, respectively. Small ΔER indicates better structural stability and less structure relaxation. The ΔER of the 5 defects (VSe, VSe2 in PtSe 2 monolayer and VSe2, VPd, VPd2 in PdPtSe 4 monolayer) are close to zero, which are conducive to the H atom fast adsorption and release. Meanwhile, the 1s orbital of H overlaps with the 3pz orbital of Se as well as the dz2 orbital of Pt. The chemical interaction causes charge transfer from the PtSe 2/PdPtSe 4 monolayer to the H atom. The extra charges will occupy the anti-bonding orbital and lead to weaker hydrogen bonding strength (See Table S9, and Figure S10 for more detailed information). So, the higher the number of transferred elec- trons, the lower the binding energy, as shown in Figure S9. As we all know, the intrinsic activity of a catalyst is closely related to its underlying electronic structure. To gain an in-depth understanding of the diverse HER activity, analyses of the density of states (DOS) of the active sites are very helpful. Here, only the d-projected DOS of Pt atoms or the p-projected DOS of Se atoms are analyzed since these orbitals play a decisive role in the HER activity. Previous studies have explored the variation of ΔGH with the d/p -band center (εd or εp) on different intrinsic electronic structures [94,95] . Only when there ’s very small deformation energy, the ΔGH show an approximately linear relationship with the energy of d level of Pt. As shown in Fig. 6d, the ΔGH values do not display a declining trend with the increase of εd due to the structure reconstruction energy that cannot be ignored. Interestingly, the ΔG1 values of all investigated models show an approximately linear trend to εd (Fig. 6f). And the closer to the fermi level the εd is, the stronger the H␀Pt bond is. For the case of H interacting directly with the Se atoms, the p-pro - jected DOS of the Se atoms should be considered. Comparing Fig. 6c with Fig. 6e, compared with ΔGH values, the ΔG1 values are correlated linearly with the εp of Se atoms. An appropriate εp can ensure an appropriate strength of the H␀Se bond and even good catalytic activity for the HER. Fig. 6e shows that Pd doping makes both the single-metal defect site (VPd, VPt) and bimetallic defect site (VPd2, VPdPt) have bet- ter catalytic activity for HER. This amendatory band-center model is proposed as an efficient descriptor for a broad class of defective systems with large deformation energy. To directly compare the catalytic performance of PtSe 2/PdPtSe 4 monolayer, the exchange current densities (i0) are calculated based on Norskov ’s assumption [96], which can describe the intrinsic rate of proton transfer from the solvent to the catalytic surface [97–99]. If the proton transfer is exothermic, corresponding to ΔGH ≦ 0, the i0 at pH  0 and T 298.15 K is: i0␀ek01 1exp␀ΔGHEkT where k0 is 200 s␀1 site␀1, which gives a reasonable overall magni - tude of the rate [96,100] ; If the proton transfer is endothermic (ΔGH F 0), i0 will be expressed as: i0␀ek01 1exp␀ΔGHEkTexp␀ΔGHEkT Fig. 7 shows a volcano curve plotted by the exchange current den- sities as a function of ΔGH. Generally, the closer to the peak, the better catalytic performance. It can be observed that divacancy sites (VSe2 and VPt2) on pristine PtSe 2 monolayer and the monovacancy sites (VSe, VPd) as well as the divacancy sites (VPt2, VPdPt) on PdPtSe 4 monolayer with a very negative/positive ΔGH locates on the bottom left/right, corre - sponding to a very low exchange current. Very interestingly, VSe2 on pristine PtSe 2 monolayer and VPdPt on PdPtSe 4 monolayer are located on the peak of the volcano curve and exhibit even higher exchange current densities (10␀17 A/site) than the Pt (111) catalyst (ΔGH ␀0.09 eV and 10␀18 A/site) [11,100] . This evidence once again confirms that the catalytic properties of PtSe 2 monolayer could be significantly enhanced by introducing Pd dopants and vacancies. 3.5. The HER mechanism According to the preceding Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption and the electronic structure analyses, the VSe2, VPt2 in PtSe 2 monolayer and VSe, VPd, VPt2, VPdPt in PdPtSe 4 monolayer present excellent HER performance for Volmer reaction. It’s interesting to see whether these 6 structural defects can also activate the Tafel reaction and which pathway is more favored. As shown in Fig. 8a, the Tafel reaction is a chemical desorption process of H2. Since multiple H adsorption sites exist in PtSe 2/PdPtSe 4 monolayer, the most stable adsorption sites of the second H atom are optimized (Figure S11) and the corresponding absorption free energy (ΔGH) of H are calculated. As shown in Fig. 8b, the second H adsorption on VPt2 in PtSe 2 monolayer and VSe in PdPtSe 4 monolayer is endothermic, indicating the Tafel reaction is prohibited. Though it is exothermic for the second H adsorption on VPt2 and VPdPt in PdPtSe 4 monolayer, the interaction is too strong that H2 desorption is thermodynamically unfavorable. For VSe2 in PtSe 2 monolayer and VPd in PdPtSe 4 monolayer, the ΔGH are still close to 0 eV, indicating the good activities for Tafel reaction. The H–H distances between two neighboring adsorbed H atoms are 1.74 Å and 2.03 Å, respectively. Therefore, VSe2 on PtSe 2 monolayer and VPd on PdPtSe 4 monolayer are promising active centers not only for the Heyrovsky re- action but also for the Tafel reaction. To gain a profound understanding of the kinetics of H atom recom - bination and desorption as H2, we further calculated the energy barriers on VSe2 on PtSe 2 monolayer and VPd on PdPtSe 4 monolayer. As shown in Fig. 8c and d, the energy barriers to break the Pt/Se␀H bonds and to form the H␀H bond are as large as 2.17 eV and 2.07 eV, respectively, lower than those of Ti2NO2 and Nb2NO2 (2.51 and 2.57 eV) [101] , but higher than that of Pt (0.80 eV) [11], graphene/MXene heterostructures (1.33 –1.56 eV) [102] , and MoS 2 edges (1.00 –1.50 eV) [93,103,104] . Too large energy barriers will reduce the hydrogen production rate and cause low turnover frequency. Therefore, the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism is more preferred. 4.Conclusions In summary, to improve the catalytic performance, we introduce Pd Fig. 7.Volcano curve of exchange current (i0) as a function of the Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption (ΔGH). L.-J. Ma and H. Shen
