Applied Surface Science 545 (2021) 149013 8dopants and vacancies to activate the basal plane for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). Detailed calculations are carried out for 5 intrinsic point defects on PtSe 2 and 9 intrinsic point defects on PdPtSe4 which lead to the following conclusions: (1) For point defects in PtSe 2 monolayer, the SePt anti-site defect has low activity due to the weak binding strength of H while the VSe2 and VPt2 site present high activity and even comparable to Pt; (2) Pd doping reduces the band gap and enhances the catalytic ability; (3) The catalytic properties can be significantly enhanced by divacancy sites (VSe2 and VPt2) on pristine PtSe 2 monolayer and the monovacancy sites (VSe, VPd) as well as the divacancy sites (VPt2, VPdPt) on PdPtSe 4 monolayer; (4) The chemical bond energy ΔG1 rather than the H adsorption energy ΔGH of all investigated models show an approximately linear trend in relation to the energy of d/p level of Pt/Se; (5) For VSe2 on PtSe 2 monolayer and VPd on PdPtSe 4 monolayer, the free energy (ΔGH) for Tafel reaction is also close to 0 eV, indicating the good HER activity on the studied sites for the Tafel reaction; (6) The Tafel reactions on these sites are prohibited due to higher energy barriers and the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanisms are preferred. These findings pro- vide better understanding on the activation of PtSe 2 monolayer for hydrogen evolution reaction. CRediT authorship contribution statement Li-Juan Ma: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Formal analysis, Investigation, Data curation, Writing - original draft, Writing - review & editing, Visualization. Haoming Shen: Conceptualization, Methodology, Writing - review & editing. Declaration of Competing Interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgments This work is partially supported by the 1331 Engineering, Y