Activation of efficient hydrogen evolution on two-dimensional Kagome structured perthiolated coronene (PTC) metal-organic frameworks (MOFs): A first-principles study Dan Xinga, Minghao Jiaa, Pengbo Dingb, Junguang Taob, Shuo Zhangb, Lixiu Guana,* aSchool of Science, Hebei University of Technology, Tianjin, 300401, China bSchool of Materials Science and Engineering, Hebei University of Technology, Tianjin, 300132, China ARTICLE INFO Handling Editor: Dr J Lobato Keywords: Two-dimensional materials Metal-organic frameworks First-principles calculations Hydrogen evolution reactionABSTRACTS The rapid development of hydrogen energy relies heavily on cost-effective hydrogen production. Catalytic water splitting through hydrogen evolution reactions (HER) presents a compelling avenue for generating high-purity hydrogen, necessitating the deployment of electrocatalysts that are both economical and highly active. Here, we conducted a comprehensive first-principles analysis to evaluate the HER performance of perthiolated coro- nene (PTC)-based metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), with a particular emphasis on the impact of replacing Fe with a variety of transition metals (TM). Our findings reveal that the Gibbs free energies of PTC-TM (TM Pt, V, and Mn) monolayers under alkaline conditions are ␀0.07 eV, ␀0.11 eV, and ␀0.14 eV, respectively, indicative of their promising HER performance. An in-depth examination of the mechanisms behind their superior HER catalytic activity has revealed that the hybridization between d-orbitals and p-orbitals plays a crucial role in facilitating effective charge transfer and enhancing conductivity. Analysis based on crystal orbital Hamilton population (COHP) and integrated crystal orbital Hamiltonian population (ICOHP) calculations demonstrate the close correlation between HER properties and the occupancy of bonding and anti-bonding states. This study highlights the significant potential of PTC-V and PTC-Mn monolayers as catalysts that offer a compelling com- bination of low cost and high efficiency. 1.Introduction Hydrogen energy is an efficient, clean, and pollution-free solution that plays a crucial role in facilitating the transition to a low-carbon economy as a high-density energy carrier. Hydrogen production through water electrolysis has emerged as a promising method for the large-scale generation of high-purity hydrogen (H2), owing to its high efficiency, low energy consumption, and environmental friendliness [1–3]. Selecting the appropriate catalyst is crucial for reducing reaction barriers and enhancing the efficiency of the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). Platinum (Pt) is often regarded as the most stable and efficient catalyst for HER due to its near-zero hydrogen adsorption free energy. However, its high cost and scarcity limit its applicability in large-scale industrial settings [4,5]. Additionally, the HER process tends to pro- ceed more readily in acidic environments with high Hconcentrations. However, these conditions require stringent storage and present safety challenges that hinder their broad application. In light of these considerations, there is a growing interest in developing catalysts that can effectively facilitate water decomposition and achieve optimal HER activity under alkaline conditions. However, in neutral or alkaline conditions, the reaction process is typically slow due to the retarded hydrolysis kinetics. Therefore, the quest for low-cost, high-efficiency HER catalysts that perform well in alkaline media has become a critical area of research [6,7]. Single-atom catalysis (SAC) is recognized for its high selectivity, exceptional performance, and the increased surface free energy it offers. However, the enhanced surface energy can inadvertently lead to the aggregation of metal atoms into clusters, diminishing the activity of the catalyst [8–10]. Consequently, research attention has shifted towards two-dimensional (2D) materials, which possess abundant surfaces and edges. These features reveal more catalytically active sites, significantly enhancing the contact efficiency and reaction rate with reactants [11]. For instance, transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) such as MoS 2, VS2, and WS2 are utilized as catalysts for HER. Their large specific *Corresponding author. E-mail address: lixiuguan@hebut.edu.cn (L. Guan). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect International Journal of Hydrogen Energy u{�~zkw! s{yo|kro>! ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2so https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2024.12.315 Received 11 September 2024; Received in revised form 3 November 2024; Accepted 18 December 2024  International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  100 (2025)  191–200 Available  online  23 December  2024 0360-3199/©  2024  Hydrogen  Energy  Publications  LLC. Published  by Elsevier  Ltd. All rights  are reserved,  including  those  for text and data mining,  AI training,  and similar  technologies.
