surface areas not only increase the active sites, but also facilitate surface charge transfer, thereby accelerating the kinetics of the HER process [12–15]. Furthermore, the electronic structure of 2D materials is highly tunable. Employing strategies like doping, defect engineering, or surface modification, researchers can meticulously adjust the band structure, Fermi level, and charge transport characteristics to fine-tune the HER performance [15]. This adaptability opens up a broad range of oppor - tunities for 2D materials in HER catalysis, enabling tailored solutions for diverse reaction scenarios. For instance, the introduction of sulfur va- cancies or the doping of metal atoms into MoS 2 has been shown to notably enhance its electrical conductivity and HER activity [16–21]. Additionally, 2D materials exhibit excellent mechanical and chemical stability, which helps maintain the integrity of their structure and properties in harsh reactive environments. Such stability ensures the reliability and durability of 2D materials in sustained operations, laying a solid groundwork for their practical deployment in HER catalysis [22, 23]. In 1995, the pioneering work of the Yaghi group introduced metal- organic frameworks (MOFs) to the scientific community, opening a new era in MOFs research [24]. The following years have witnessed an exponential growth in scholarly interest, with a surge in investigations dedicated to the structural engineering and functional exploration of MOFs, establishing them as a cornerstone in the realm of materials sci- ence. MOFs are distinguished by their crystalline nature and are defined by their periodic network structure, which arises from the orchestrated self-assembly of inorganic metal ions or clusters and organic ligands. Renowned for their ultra-high specific surface area, adjustable pore di- mensions, diverse chemical functionalities, and commendable thermal stability, MOFs have revolutionized a multitude of fields, such as gas storage, separation processes, catalysis, sensing technologies, and drug delivery systems [25,26]. MOFs are designed with the flexibility to precisely tailor their structures and properties to meet the specific re- quirements of different applications by altering the metal center, organic ligand, or synthesis conditions. Among their many attributes, certain MOFs have demonstrated an exceptional capacity for the efficient adsorption and storage of hydrogen, coupled with superior catalytic properties. These qualities render them as potential candidates for clean energy storage in the future [23,27,28]. As the field of nanostructured catalysts continues to evolve, 2D MOFs have risen to prominence, showcasing exceptional catalytic prowess in HER. Architecturally, 2D MOFs are characterized by their layered design, which endows them with a substantial specific surface area and abundant porosity, which facilitate the diffusion of reactants and the progression of catalytic reactions. Huang et al. synthesized the 2D MOF- Ni3(Ni3≡HAHATN) 2 and, through density functional theory (DFT) cal- culations, confirmed that its nanosheets exhibit high electrical conduc - tivity, a compact electron transport band gap, and an advantageous in- plane porous configuration [29]. These features collectively enhance its HER activity. The electronic characteristics and catalytic efficacy of MOFs can be engineered by selecting appropriate metal centers and organic ligands [30,31]. For example, the choice of metal centers, such as Pt, Ru, and Ni, significantly influences the catalytic performance, as the electronic structure and the coordination environment of these metals are directly correlated with their catalytic activity. For instance, bifunctional electrocatalyst has been reported for the dissociation of C–N and H–O bonds, composed of a 2D nickel-organic framework (Ni-MOF) combined with nitrogen-doped carbon nanotube-encapsulated nickel nanoparticles (Ni-MOF@Ni-NCNT) [32]. The HER performance of Ni-MOF@Ni-NCNT was found to be superior to that of both Ni-MOF and Ni-NCNT, demonstrating a reduction in over- potential of 224 mV and 900 mV at 50 mA cm␀2, respectively. This enhancement can be attributed to the synergistic effects whereby Ni-MOF, Ni nanoparticles, and Ni-NX-C facilitate water adsorption, dissociation, and adsorption/binding, respectively [32]. Additionally, 2D CoP/Co-MOF heterostructure nanosheets were fabricated as self-supporting electrodes [33]. The optimized 2D CoP/Co-MOF demonstrated exceptional HER activity across a broad pH range, requiring only overpotentials of 52, 106, and 26 mV at 10 mA cm␀2 in acidic (0.5 M H2SO4), neutral (1.0 M PBS), and alkaline (1.0 M KOH) solutions, respectively. Furthermore, the CoP/Co-MOF exhibited impressive durability in various pH environments. This outstanding HER performance is attributed to modifications in the electronic structure and an increased specific surface area, which together enhance the charge transfer rate and expose a greater number of active sites [33]. Recently, a conjugated 2D MOF material, synthesized via a sol- vothermal approach and based on fully perthiolated coronene (PTC) and iron (Fe) metal centers, has been developed. This material exhibits a high level of π-d conjugation and complete electron delocalization, endowing it with high electrical conductivity and semiconductor prop- erties [34]. Furthermore, PTC-Fe has been observed to exhibit ferro- magnetic behavior at low temperatures [35]. The findings from this study have sparked further research aimed at enhancing its perfor - mance, offering innovative pathways for the development of novel HER catalysts and highlighting the promising potential for future applications. In this study, we delved into the properties of PTC-TM (TM Sc, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Mo, and Pt) monolayers through first- principles calculations. Our findings reveal that PTC-V, PTC-Mn, and PTC-Pt monolayers stand out for their exceptional electrical conduc - tivity and prowess in HER. To unravel the mechanisms behind their remarkable HER performance, we conducted a comprehensive analysis of their electronic structures, crystal orbital Hamilton population (COHP), and integrated crystal orbital Hamiltonian population (ICOHP). This in-depth examination has shed light on the structural and electronic factors that are pivotal to catalytic activity, offering a framework for the strategic design and refinement of MOF-based HER catalysts. Our studies demonstrate the potential of PTC-Mn and PTC-V monolayers as cost-effective and high-performing catalysts, posi- tioning them as promising candidates for industrial applications. 2.Calculation details Our calculations were conducted using first-principles methods based on density functional theory (DFT) as implemented in the Vienna ab initio calculation program (VASP) [36,37]. the intricate interplay between ions and electrons was effectively managed through the pro- jector augmented wave (PAW) method [36], while the exchange-correlation effects were addressed using the generalized gradient approximation (GGA) as proposed by Perdew, Burke, and Ernzerhof (PBE) [38]. To ensure a comprehensive sampling of the first Brillouin zone, a 3 ×3 ×1 Monkhorst –Pack grid centered at the Γ point was employed, complemented by a plane wave expansion cutoff energy set at 400 eV. The geometric optimization was carried out until the forces acting on each atom were reduced to below 0.05 eV Å␀1, and the total energy change was less than 10␀4 eV. To accurately account for the nonlocal van der Waals (vdW) forces, we incorporated the DFT-D3 approximation, which provides a reliable correction for interlayer in- teractions [39]. The Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption under alkaline con- ditions, denoted as ΔGOH*H*, was calculated using the following Eq. (1) [40–45]. ΔGOH*H*ΔEOHHΔEZPE␀TΔSH (1) Here, ΔEOH H signifies the energy required to increase the coverage of one hydrogen atom in an alkaline environment. It is calculated as the chemisorption energy of hydrogen under alkaline conditions: ΔEOHHEHSOHH␀EHS␀EH20 (2) In Eq. (2), EHS OH H and EHS represent the total energies of the structure system with and without H atom adsorption, respectively, while EH2O is the total energy of the gas-phase H2O molecule. The zero- D. Xing et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  100 (2025)  191–200 192
