point energy change, ΔEZPE, is derived from the following relationship: ΔEZPEEHSn1H* ZPE␀EHSnH* ZPE␀1 2EH2 ZPE (3) where EHS(n1)H* ZPEand EHS nH* ZPEare the zero-point energies (ZPE) of adsorption of (n 1) and nH atoms on the catalyst surface. EH2 ZPE is the zero-point energy of the gas-phase hydrogen. We adopted gas- phase H2O at a pressure of 0.035 bar as the reference state, ensuring equilibrium with liquid H2O at 300 K and providing a stable reference for our analysis. The ZPE for H2 and H2O molecules, as well as for H* and OH* adsorbed on the pure PTC-TM surface, were calculated from the vibrational frequencies obtained by diagonalizing the Hessian matrix. The calculated vibrational frequencies showed minimal sensitivity to different configurations, allowing us to use a uniform value for all cor- rections. The entropy change for hydrogen adsorption, ΔSH, is approx - imated as ΔSH 1/2S H2, where SH2 is the entropy of molecule hydrogen in the gas phase at standard conditions. Entropy contributions from various reaction intermediates were referenced from the literature [46–48]. The detailed results of zero-point energy and entropy differ - ences from the VASP calculations are presented in Table S1of the Supporting Information. Consequently, Eq. (1)can be simplified to ΔGOH*H* ΔEH 0.62 (eV). The detailed adsorption configurations for various systems are described in Fig. S1of the Supporting Information. 3.Results and discussion 3.1. Electronic structure sof PTC-TM (TM Sc, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Mo, and Pt) monolayers MOFs are widely recognized for their exceptional mechanical robustness, substantial adsorption capabilities, and impressive electrical conductivity. In current work, we concentrate on the PTC-Fe 2D MOFs family, which is composed of polycyclic aromatic monomers. These monomers have PTC ligands bonded to Fe metal atoms through S pairs, creating S–Fe linkages. Fig. 1provides a visual representation of the original PTC-Fe MOFs, showcasing both the top and side views. Within the 2D PTC-Fe MOF structure, the combination of the π-plane molecule with the tetragonal FeS4 node leads to an enhanced π-d conjugation and complete electron delocalization across the hexagonally ordered PTC-Fe framework. To explore the impact of various transition metals (TMs) on the MOFs ’ properties, we substituted the Fe elements in PTC-Fe with a range of TM elements, denoted as PTC-TM, with the aim of enhancing the catalytic performance. This strategic structural configuration capi- talizes on the interaction of π-d orbitals within the 2D planes, estab - lishing a conjugated system that is defined by a hexagonal lattice structure and characterized by van der Waals layer stacking. This design endows the PTC-TM system with superior electronic properties, setting the stage for its potential applications in catalysis and beyond. Fig. 1(b–d) and Fig. S2provide an insightful overview of the band structures for a range of PTC-TM systems, showcasing their unique electronic profiles. PTC-TM systems with TM Pt, Co, Cr, Ni, Mo, Cu, and Zn, as illustrated in Fig. 1(b) and Figs. S2(b, c, e-h), demonstrate metallic behavior. This is evidenced by a significant electron density at the Fermi level (EF) and excellent electrical conductivity. On the other hand, PTC-V, PTC-Sc, and PTC-Fe, as depicted in Fig. 1(c) and Figs. S2(a and d), reveal spin polarization features, exhibiting half-metallic nature. While the spin-up channel remains conductive, the spin-down channel exhibits a pronounced band gap, notably around ~0.41 eV for PTC-V as depicted in Fig. 1(c). Such attributes are highly beneficial for spintronics applications, enabling the fine-tuned control of electron flow across different spin channels. Among these, PTC-Sc is observed to have the most substantial bandgap, while PTC-Fe has the narrowest. Specifically, it is only PTC-Mn, as showcased in Fig. 1(d), that manifests semi- conductor behavior, characterized by a minuscule bandgap of approxi - mately 0.07 eV. This small bandgap is crucial for ensuring adequate conductivity, thereby facilitating efficient charge transfer. The diverse properties that emerge from the incorporation of different TMs not only enrich the landscape of these materials but also highlight their potential for further in-depth exploration and application. In our quest to further delineate the electronic properties of the PTC- TM series (with TM Sc, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Mo, and Pt), we have conducted a comprehensive density of states (DOS) analysis. The total DOS (TDOS), as depicted in Fig. 2and S3, reveals a continuous distribution of electronic states at the EF, a indication of robust electrical conductivity that aligns with the aforementioned band structure find- ings. Notably, PTC-Pt, PTC-V, and PTC-Mn, highlighted in Fig. 2(a–c), demonstrate pronounced electron density peaks in proximity to EF. These peaks in the electron states near EF are indicative of an elevated conductivity, a trait that is highly desirable for efficient electronic transport. Conversely, the DOS profiles of PTC-Sc, PTC-Mo, and PTC-Co, as illustrated in Fig. 2(d–f), peak at energies considerably below EF. This positioning results in an abundant electron occupation that impedes the desorption process of the intermediate molecular species, such as H, thereby influencing the overall catalytic dynamics. Notably, PTC-Pt, as illustrated in Fig. 2(a), exhibits a significant electron density peak near the EF, indicating a high occupancy of states. The presence of an abundance of free electrons in these states serves to facilitate the HER by reducing the kinetic barrier for protons to combine Fig. 1.(a) The top and side views of PTC-Fe monolayers. (b–d) The band structures of PTC-TM (TM Pt, V and Mn), respectively. Carbon atoms are represented by light brown and sulfur atoms by yellow, allowing for an intuitive understanding of the atomic composition and arrangement within the PTC-TM monolayers. The orange and blue dotted circles represent the H and HO adsorption sites, respectively, for PTC-Fe monolayers. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)D. Xing et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  100 (2025)  191–200 193
