and form hydrogen molecules, thereby enhancing the efficiency of HER. While antibonding states may not directly contribute to the electron occupancy, they can act as bridges for electron transfer from occupied states to reactants or intermediates, playing a pivotal role in the catalytic process. PTC-Mn and PTC-V, featured in Fig. 2(b) and (c), respectively, demonstrate substantial electron density peaks in the unoccupied states just above EF. These peaks are indicative of an improved electron transfer efficiency and exhibit excellent conductivity, which in turn enhances their catalytic activity for HER. The unoccupied states above EF are crucial for accepting electrons from reactants, thus promoting their HER performance. In contrast, the electron density distribution of PTC-TM (with TM Zn, Cr, Ni, Cu, Fe), as depicted in Fig. S3, remains relatively flat and devoid of distinct peaks, particularly in the vicinity of EF. The majority of electrons in these materials are concentrated in states that are already occupied, which does not favor an enhanced HER performance. The lack of unoccupied states near EF implies a lower propensity for electron transfer, a critical factor in the HER process. This underscores the importance of electronic structure in dictating the cat- alytic performance of these materials, highlighting the need for further material engineering to optimize their electronic properties for improved HER catalysis. Fig. 3and S4offer a detailed examination of the spin density dis- tribution across various PTC-TM monolayers, shedding light on the deliciated electronic characteristics of these materials. It is evident that monolayers of PTC-TM, with TM being V, Mn, Co, Ni, and Fe, as depicted in Fig. 3(b, c, and f) and Figs. S4(c and e), exhibit a noticeable deficiency of spin-polarized electrons. This deficiency suggests a reduced potential for spin-dependent electronic applications. Conversely, the incorpora - tion of specific TM atoms leads to a marked accumulation of spin-up electrons in PTC-TM monolayers with Pt, Sc, Zn, and Cr, as illustrated in Fig. 3(a and d) and Figs. S4(a and b). In a distinct case, PTC-Cu, shown in Fig. S4(d) , exhibits a concentration of spin-down electrons. These concentrations of spin-polarized electrons are primarily attributed to the presence of TM and S atoms, highlighting the pivotal role of these atoms in dictating the spin density distribution. Subsequent to the substitution of TM atoms, the adjacent C atoms manifest varying degrees of spin polarization. This variation arises from the redistribution of electrons, which in turn influences the electronic structure of the material. Such redistribution of spin density not only induces magnetism but also en- hances the catalytic activity, a crucial factor for the performance of these materials in HER catalysis. Significantly, the spin-polarized orbitals encircling the TM atoms are characterized by a pronounced d-orbital signature, indicative of their involvement in the material ’s magnetic and electronic properties. Meanwhile, the orbitals surrounding the S atoms exhibit a clear p-orbital character, further underscoring the complex interplay between the constituent atoms and their orbitals in shaping the overall electronic behavior of PTC-TM monolayers. This intricate bal- ance of spin-polarized states and orbital characteristics is essential for Fig. 2.Total density of states (TDOS) for the PTC-TM monolayers with each panel dedicated to a specific transition metal: (a) Pt, (b) V, (c) Mn, (d) Sc, (e) Mo, and (f) Co. The black dashed line indicates the position of the EF, which is set to 0 eV. Fig. 3.The spin density distribution across PTC-TM monolayers is meticulously detailed for Pt (a), V (b), Mn (c), Sc (d), Mo (e), and Co (f). The isosurface threshold is set at 3.0 ×10␀7 e⋅Å␀3. The red and blue regions represent the spin-up and spin-down electron densities. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)D. Xing et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  100 (2025)  191–200 194
