tailoring the materials for specific catalytic applications. 3.2. HER performance of PTC-TM (TM Sc, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Mo, and Pt) monolayers In theoretical calculations, the Gibbs free energy is a crucial parameter for evaluating the HER activity of materials. It reflects the interaction strength between hydrogen atoms and the catalyst surface. An ideal catalyst should have a balanced affinity for hydrogen adsorp - tion, not too strong to hinder desorption, nor too weak to prevent effective adsorption. A lower ΔGOH*H* value indicates a stronger ten- dency for hydrogen to desorb, which is conducive to higher hydrogen production rates and improved HER performance. Catalysts with ΔGOH*H* values close to zero are thus considered to have more desir- able HER activity. To ensure accuracy, a threshold for excellent HER performance has been set at |ΔGOH*H*| ≼0.30 eV, which is within an acceptable range of error. Under alkaline conditions, the HER process typically follows the Volmer –Heyrovsky or Volmer –Tafel mechanisms. The three critical steps involved in these pathways are as follows [49–51]. Volmer stepBH2Oe␀*→H*OH␀(4) Heyrovsky stepBH2Oe␀H*→H2OH␀* (5) Tafer stepBH*H*→H22*(6) where * and H* denote the active site on the catalyst and the interme - diate hydrogen adsorbed on the catalyst site, respectively. The efficiency of these steps and the overall HER process is influenced by the catalyst ’s ability to lower the activation energy and stabilize the intermediates, which is directly related to the Gibbs free energy change associated with the adsorption and desorption of hydrogen. Catalysts that can optimize these steps will exhibit superior performance in facilitating the HER, making them ideal candidates for hydrogen production technologies. We have conducted a computational assessment of H* adsorption on a variety of PTC-TM (TM Sc, V, Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Mo, and Pt) substrates under alkaline conditions. The specific adsorption sites for H and HO are illustrated in Fig. 1and Fig. S1. The results, presented in Fig. 4(a) and detailed in Table 1, indicate that PTC-TM monolayers with TM Pt, V, Mn, Ni, and Fe are particularly promising for HER appli - cations. Of particular note, PTC-V, PTC-Mn, and PTC-Pt exhibit ΔGOH*H* values of ␀0.11 eV, ␀0.14 eV, and ␀0.07 eV, respectively, all of which are nearly zero. These minimal negative ΔGOH*H* values are indicative of a low energy barrier for H* desorption, which is correlated with their superior HER performance. Conversely, other systems present a considerably higher absolute ΔGOH*H* value, surpassing 2 eV, indicative of their less favorable HER activity. Our computational analysis points to PTC-V, PTC-Mn, and PTC-Pt as the most effective catalysts for HER, with PTC-Pt notably outperforming the benchmark metal Pt(111) at ␀0.09 eV. This suggests that PTC-V and PTC-Mn could serve as viable, cost-effective alternatives to precious metal catalysts, especially under alkaline conditions. Moreover, these results highlight the significant potential of MOFs-based structures as catalysts for HER. Their tailored electronic properties and tunable active sites offer a promising avenue for the development of advanced, efficient, and economical HER catalysts, potentially revolutionizing the hydrogen production landscape. To further evaluate the catalytic performance across various systems, we have computed the exchange current density, a parameter that, to an extent, mirrors the rate at which protons are transferred to the catalyst surface [52–54]. If the proton transfer is exothermic (ΔGOH*H* D0), the exchange current density under standard conditions, defined as pH  0 and T 300 K, is expressed as follows: i0␀ek01exp␀ΔGOH*H*EkT␀1(7) Here, k0 represents the rate constant, approximately 200 s␀1 site␀1, which is considered universal for ideal HER catalysts, and k denotes the Boltzmann constant. Conversely, for endothermic reactions (ΔGOH*H* F0), the exchange current density is determined by: i0␀ek0exp␀ΔGOH*H*EkT1exp␀ΔGOH*H*EkT␀1(8) Utilizing Eqs. (7) and (8), we have calculated the exchange current densities for a range of catalysts, with the results graphically presented Fig. 4.(a) Gibbs free energy (ΔGOH*H*) diagram for various TMs in alkaline conditions. (b) log(i 0) and ΔGOH*H* volcano plots for PTC-TM monolayers. For comparison, the value for pure Pt (111) is also included.Table 1 ΔGOH*H* of different TM atoms on PTC-TM monolayers in alkaline conditions. System ΔGH*HO* (eV) PTC-Cu 0.51 PTC-Ni 0.26 PTC-Co ␀2.61 PTC-Pt ␀0.07 PTC-Mn ␀0.14 PTC-Zn ␀0.53 PTC-V ␀0.11 PTC-Fe ␀0.35 PTC-Cr ␀0.71 PTC-Sc ␀3.27 PTC-Mo ␀4.29D. Xing et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  100 (2025)  191–200 195
