in Fig. 4(b). For a benchmark comparison, the exchange current density for Pt(111) has also been determined. The relationship between log(i0) and ΔGOH*H* exhibits a classic volcanic profile, a concept deeply ingrained in the field of heterogeneous catalysis [55]. As illustrated in Fig. 4(b), PTC-V, PTC-Mn and PTC-Pt are located near the top of the volcanic curve, signifying their exceptional HER activity. This strategic placement suggests that these catalysts possess an optimal balance of hydrogen adsorption and desorption energies, which is crucial for effi- cient HER catalysis. Their proximity to the peak of the volcano curve underscores their potential for further investigation and development as high-performance HER catalysts. 3.3. Mechanism of high HER activity on PTC-TM (TM Pt, V and Mn) monolayers Based on the Gibbs free energy and theoretical exchange current density calculations previously discussed, it has become clear that PTC- Pt, PTC-V, and PTC-Mn manifest exceptional HER activity under alka- line conditions. In pursuit of a comprehensive understanding of the mechanisms underlying the enhanced HER performance of PTC-TM (TM Pt, V, and Mn) monolayers, an exhaustive analysis has been conducted. This includes an examination of the total density of states (TDOS) and projected density of states (PDOS) to explore the electronic hybridization among adjacent atoms. Furthermore, crystal orbital Hamilton population (COHP) and integrated crystal orbital Hamiltonian population (ICOHP) methodologies have been engaged to procure direct insights into the dynamics of hydrogen bonding [56–58]. By delving into these multiple facets, we have unraveled the intrinsic factors that are pivotal to the notable HER activity observed in these materials. Fig. 5illustrates the density of states (DOS) spectra for the H- adsorbed PTC-TM (TM Pt, V, and Mn) systems, which have been identified as superior performers in HER activity. To further elucidate the factors contributing to the variability in HER activity, Fig. 6presents the DOS spectra of PTC-TM (TM Sc, Mo, and Co) systems that exhibit non-ideal HER performance. Strikingly, the PTC-V and PTC-Mn mono - layers exhibit pronounced electronic states at the EF, predominantly stemming from the d orbitals of the TM atoms, with a substantial contribution from the S p orbitals. The presence of these electronic states is indicative of an efficient charge transfer mechanism within the ma- terial, which in turn enhance its HER properties as observed in Fig. 5. In contrast, the PTC-Sc, PTC-Mo, and PTC-Co monolayers, which are characterized by less effective HER performance, display less pro- nounced electronic states at EF, particularly in the d orbitals of the Sc, Mo, and Co atoms. This results in a less distinct electronic structure and a reduced ability to facilitate charge transfer and conductivity. This comparative analysis evidences the critical role of the hybridization between the d orbitals of the TM atoms and the p orbitals of the S atoms. Such hybridization fosters a synergistic effect, enhances electrical con- ductivity, and ultimately elevates the HER properties of the material. In Fig. 5(a, f, and k), a significant electron density peak is observed near EF. Interestingly, the electron states near EF are diminished relative to those in the pre-H adsorption state, as depicted in Fig. 2(a–c). Moreover, the electronic states below EF are shifted to even lower energy levels, and a local electron density peak emerges at a higher energy above EF. This energy redistribution is ascribed to the fundamental mechanism of bond formation. When H atoms are adsorbed onto the PTC-TM (TM Pt, V, and Mn) surfaces, electronic-state coupling in- duces hybridization, with energy levels descending to form a stable bonded state and ascending to create an antibonded state. The bonded states are sufficient lower than EF, leading to full occupancy, while the Fig. 5.TDOS and PDOS for the PTC-TM monolayers. Panels (a–e) are dedicated to depicting the PTC-Pt monolayer. Panels (f–j) correspond to the PTC-V monolayer, Panels (k–o) are assigned to the PTC-Mn monolayer. The red vertical dashed lines signify the position of EF. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)D. Xing et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  100 (2025)  191–200 196
