antibonded states are generally situated around EF, resulting in partial occupancy. Furthermore, in comparison to the TDOS of PTC-TM (TM  Sc, Mo, and Co) presented in Fig. 6, the electronic states near EF are primarily contributed by the d orbitals of the TM atoms and the hy- bridization of p orbitals from S. The presence of these electronic states is indicative of efficient charge transfer within the material, which in turn enhances its HER properties. This finding is in line with Fig. 3, where the substantial spin density of TM and adjacent S atoms is closely correlated with the catalytic activity. The DOS analysis elucidates that HER cata- lytic activity is intricately connected to the interplay between the TM and S atoms, as well as the antibonding state, highlighting the in- terdependencies in the electronic structure that govern the material ’s catalytic properties. In first-principles calculations, quantifying the bond strength of a specific chemical bond has often been a challenging endeavor. This complexity stems from the intricate coordination environments in which atoms within a periodic system reside, complicating the isolation and analysis of interactions between individual pairs of atoms. COHP anal- ysis has risen as a powerful method to navigate this challenge, as it elucidates the contributions of bonding and anti-bonding states to the band structure, playing a crucial role in the field of materials science [15,59]. To attain a more profound comprehension of the electronic structure and the strength of chemical bonds within the PTC-TM system, we have computed the COHP between the adsorption intermediate hydrogen atom and the PTC-TM monolayer. This computation was facilitated by the LOBSTER package [60,61], with the findings graphi - cally encapsulated in Fig. 7and S5. The COHP analysis provides a detailed account of the bonding characteristics, offering insights into the stability and HER reactivity of the PTC-TM system. It is a well-established principle that a positive COHP value is indicative of an anti-bonding state, while a negative COHP value de- notes a bonding state. Typically, bonding states are found below the EF, and anti-bonding states are located above EF. The occupancy of anti- bonding states has been identified as a measure of bond strength [15]. An increased occupancy of anti-bonding states can lead to the instability of adsorbed hydrogen, thereby weakening the bond strength between hydrogen and the substrate. In contrast, a lower occupancy of anti-bonding states results in a stronger bond between the substrate and the adsorbate. Thus, the occupancy of anti-bonding states is a pivotal indicator of the electrocatalytic performance for HER. Consequently, we have conducted an in-depth analysis of the anti- bonding states within the COHP framework. Our findings reveal that the spin-up and spin-down COHPs for the majority of PTC-TM mono - layers effectively coincide, with the majority of these states being in the anti-bonding region, as shown in Fig. 7and Fig. S5. PTC-TM (TM Sc, Mo, Co, Zn, Ni, Cu, Cr, and Fe) shows high anti-bonding state occupancy and a deficiency of bonding states, which is detrimental to H adsorption on the catalyst surface. This situation may raise the activation energy barrier, hinder the efficient transfer of electrons necessary for the HER from the catalyst surface to the reactants, and consequently lower the HER performance, as depicted in Fig. 7(d–f) and Fig. S5. In a striking contrast, PTC-TM (TM Pt, V, and Mn) monolayers display minimal anti-bonding state occupancy, with most of these states positioned above EF, indicating a scarce filling of anti-bonding states, as illustrated in Fig. 7(a–c). This favorable electronic state interaction leads to stable H atom adsorption during the HER process for PTC-TM (TM Pt, V, Mn), which contributes to their enhanced electrocatalytic performance. On the other hand, the ICOHP is derived from the integration of COHP within a specified energy window. This metric offers profound insights into the bonding strength and provides a comprehensive assessment of the overall effectiveness of interactions between any pair of atoms [62–64]. Table 2presents the spin-up ICOHP values for the PTC-Pt, PTC-V, and PTC-Mn monolayers within the integration range of ␀8 to 7 eV, which are found to be ␀1.15 eV, ␀1.12 eV, and ␀1.07 eV, respectively. Comparatively, the ICOHP values for other PTC-TM (TM  Sc, Cr, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn, Mo, and Pt) monolayers are observed to be less negative than those of the PTC-V and PTC-Mn monolayers. It is recog - nized that a more negative ICOHP value is indicative of a stronger bond strength between the substrate and H atom. While this is advantageous for H adsorption, it can impede H desorption, a critical step in the HER process. This principle applies equally to spin-down states. Therefore, systems with lower ICOHP values do not exhibit superior HER perfor - mance. In brief, the ICOHP spectra further corroborate that the PTC-V and PTC-Mn monolayers possess excellent HER performance, a conclu - sion that is consistent with the results obtained from the Gibbs free energy of hydrogen adsorption (ΔGOH*H*). This consistency enhances the reliability of our analysis and highlights the significant role of electronic structure in dictating the catalytic properties of these materials. 4.Conclusion In conclusion, our investigation into the HER activity of 2D PTC-TM MOF monolayers under alkaline conditions using DFT, has yielded insightful results. The ΔGOH*H* for PTC-Pt, PTC-V, and PTC-Mn were ascertained to be ␀0.07 eV, ␀0.11 eV, and ␀0.14 eV, respectively. These values, which are nearly zero, suggest an optimized balance for hydrogen adsorption and desorption, and are indicative of their enhanced conductivity and closely associated with their superior HER performance. The COHP analysis has unveiled a robust correlation be- tween the occupancy of bonding and anti-bonding states and the HER performance. Specifically, a lower occupancy of anti-bonding states and a higher occupancy of bonding states are beneficial for efficient HER catalysis. Additionally, the ICOHP analysis has shown that net bonding capacity is a crucial factor influencing HER performance. Specifically, a more negative ICOHP indicates a higher occupation of anti-bonding states, which correlates with poorer catalytic performance. Our work has not only reaffirmed the potential of MOFs as an emerging class of ordered crystalline materials in the design and application of HER cat- alysts but also expanded the horizon of materials science. The compre - hensive insights garnered from this study serve to deepen our understanding of the HER catalytic process. Furthermore, they lay the groundwork for the future development of more efficient and sustain - able energy conversion systems, contributing to the advancement of clean energy technologies. CRediT authorship contribution statement Dan Xing: Writing – original draft, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. Minghao Jia: Investigation, Formal analysis. Pengbo Ding: Investigation, Formal analysis. Junguang Tao: Investigation, Funding acquisition, Formal analysis. Shuo Zhang: Formal analysis. Lixiu Guan: Writing – review & editing, Supervision, Investigation, Conceptualization.Table 2 ICOHP values of PTC-TM monolayers in spin-up and spin-down channels. TM ICOHP (eV) Spin-up Spin-down V ␀1.12 ␀1.12 Mn ␀1.07 ␀1.19 Pt ␀1.15 ␀1.15 Cu ␀2.95 ␀2.95 Co ␀2.98 ␀3.00 Ni ␀3.09 ␀3.09 Zn ␀1.15 ␀1.15 Fe ␀2.92 ␀2.93 Cr ␀2.84 ␀2.83 Sc ␀3.09 ␀3.09 Mo ␀3.14 ␀3.18D. Xing et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  100 (2025)  191–200 198
