Active site of the Pt electrodes modified with purine bases for hydrogen evolution reaction in alkaline solution Syunnosuke Tanakaa, Hiroaki Hattoria, Tomoyuki Koganezawab , Tomotaka Nakatanib , Rosantha Kumarab , Osami Sakatab , Kenji Miyatakec,d , Nagahiro Hoshia , Masashi Nakamuraa,* aDepartment of Applied Chemistry and Biotechnology, Graduate School of Engineering, Chiba University, Yayoi-cho 1-33, Inage-ku, Chiba, 263-8522, Japan bCenter for Synchrotron Radiation Research, Japan Synchrotron Radiation Research Institute (JASRI), Sayo-gun, Hyogo, 679-5198, Japan cClean Energy Research Center, University of Yamanashi, 4 Takeda, Kofu, 400-8510, Japan dDepartment of Applied Chemistry, Waseda University, Shinjuku, Tokyo 169-8555, Japan ARTICLE INFO Handling Editor: Dr. E.A. Veziroglu Keywords: Hydrogen evolution reaction Electrical double layer Platinum Purine bases Surface electrochemistryABSTRACT The hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) on Pt electrodes can be activated by controlling the hydrophilic/hy - drophobic structure in the electric double layer (EDL). Identifying the factors governing the HER activity is essential for developing practical electrocatalysts. In this study, we investigated the HER activity of the Pt electrodes modified with purine bases. With increasing purine coverage, the HER activity of purine-modified Pt (110) increased linearly to 4.2 times higher than that of the unmodified surface. X-ray diffraction analysis indicated that the water molecules interacting with the purine were precisely located in the missing rows of the reconstructed Pt(110). These water molecules facilitated hydrogen donation to the topmost Pt atoms to activate the HER. We additionally attempted to activate the HER by the purine modification on the Pt stepped surface of commercial Pt/C. The purine modification and the oxidative roughening of Pt/C increased the activity by 3.2 times compared to that of untreated and unmodified Pt/C. 1.Introduction Hydrogen is a promising source of clean energy with its combustion and the fuel cell reactions producing water as the only product. Highly efficient water electrolysis using renewable energy sources is required for the widespread use of hydrogen energy. A variety of electrocatalysts based on both noble and base metals have been developed for water electrolysis [1–5]. Noble metals such as Pt are used as the cathode for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) because of their small overpotential in acidic solutions. The Volmer step for the hydrogen adsorption is rapid under acidic conditions [6–8]. In alkaline solutions, however, water becomes a reactant in the HER, which give rise to a large overpotential because the Volmer step must dissociate the OH bond of water [7–12]. Therefore, the reduction of the overpotential using highly active elec- trocatalysts is essential for the development of low-cost water electrol - ysis systems [13–16]. The processes whereby the reactants and reaction intermediates undergo adsorption/desorption onto/from the surface govern the kinetics of electrocatalytic reactions. Specifically, the atomic arrange - ment of the surface affects the rate-determining step of the elementary processes of the HER, including the stability of adsorbed hydrogen. Consequently, the HER activity increases in the order Pt(111) DPt(100) DPt(110) in both acidic and alkaline solutions [17]. In an electrical double layer (EDL), the electrolytes and solvents form specific structures by interacting with the electrode surface, which affects various elec- trochemical reactions [18–22]. The HER activity also depends on the ionic species and their concentrations in the EDL. In a solution of alkali metal hydroxides, the countercations significantly influence the HER activity, which decreases in the order LiFNaFKFRb[23,24]. The addition of organic molecules and cations such as caffeine and tetraalkylammonium also increases the HER activity [24–26]. These interfacial species alter the structure of the reactant water molecules in the EDL [27]. The caffeine molecule, which is one of the purine bases, contains π-conjugated electrons, pyridinic nitrogen, methyl group, and carbonyl group, any of which may contribute to the HER activation. For example, *Corresponding author. E-mail address: mnakamura@faculty.chiba-u.jp (M. Nakamura). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect International Journal of Hydrogen Energy u{�~zkw! s{yo|kro>! ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2so https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2025.151318 Received 5 March 2025; Received in revised form 17 July 2025; Accepted 30 August 2025  International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  172 (2025)  151318 Available  online  4 September  2025 0360-3199/©  2025  The Authors.  Published  by Elsevier  Ltd on behalf  of Hydrogen  Energy  Publications  LLC. This is an open access  article  under  the CC BY license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/  ).
