the HER on Pt(111) was activated in a caffeine-added solution of KOH (0.1 M), whereas caffeine lowered the HER activity in 0.1 M HClO 4 [28]. This suggests that, under alkaline conditions, the rate-determining Volmer step in alkaline conditions (H2O e␀→ Had OH␀) [7, 10–12], is promoted by adding caffeine. The HER activity of a Pt elec- trode modified with 1-methylimidazole, which is a component of the caffeine molecule, was demonstrated to be comparable to that of caffeine [26,29]. These studies suggest that, in the Volmer step, water molecules that are hydrogen-bonded to the pentagonal pyridinic nitrogen of the adsorbed imidazole are located at a position that facilitates the Grot- thuss mechanism [13]. The structural optimization of the pyridinic ni- trogen by controlling the molecular orientation of nitrogen-containing additives would be expected to result in further activation. Surface analysis using single-crystal electrodes is helpful for under - standing the adsorption structures and active reaction sites [30,31]. However, the incorporation of these active surface structures of single-crystal electrodes into nanoparticles for practical implementation would be challenging. Recently, we constructed complex defect struc- tures on a Pt(111) surface using electrochemical oxidation/reduction cycles [32]. These new types of defects have (100) and (111) steps with bowl-type structures that show high activity for the oxygen evolution reaction. This preparation method could be adapted for use with Pt nanoparticles. In this study, we investigated the effects of purine bases (Fig. 1; caffeine, xanthine, purine, theophylline, and theobromine) on the HER activity using single-crystal Pt electrodes in alkaline solution. The ability of these purine bases to activate the HER was studied to determine which part of the molecular structure contributes to the activation and to understand the effect of each substituent. The interfacial structure of the purine adsorbed on Pt(110), which activates the HER, was deter - mined using infrared reflection absorption spectroscopy (IRAS) and specular X-ray crystal truncation rod (CTR) scattering. Furthermore, we attempted to construct a HER-active structure on commercial Pt/C by the oxidation/reduction of the surface Pt atoms. The new method involving the annealing and H2O2 treatment of Pt/C created specific surface structures containing (111) and (100) steps without the aggre - gation of Pt particles. This approach, in combination with purine modification, enhanced the HER activity by 3.2 times. 2.Results and discussion 2.1. HER activities on single crystal Pt electrodes modified with purine bases The single-crystal Pt electrodes used in this study were characterized using cyclic voltammetry (CV) in 0.05 M H2SO4, as shown in Fig. S1. The CV of Pt(110) is consistent with that of the previous reported reconstructed Pt(110)-(1 ×2) [33]. The peak potentials and the charge densities for the under potential deposition of hydrogen (Hupd) are identical to those of ideal well-defined surfaces [34–36]. To evaluate the HER activity in an alkaline solution, LiOH, which has the highest HER activity in alkali metal hydroxide solutions [23,24], was used as the supporting electrolyte. Fig. 2(a) and Fig. S2show the CVs on the low-index planes of Pt in 0.1 M LiOH containing 10␀4 M purine bases (caffeine, xanthine, purine, theophylline, or theobromine). The CVs in 0.1 M LiOH without the purine bases exhibit specific peaks characteristic of the adsorption/desorption of hydrogen and hydroxide, which corre - spond to those reported previously [37]. Fig. 2(b) shows the current density at ␀0.1 V vs RHE estimated from the linear sweep voltammo - grams (LSVs) in 0.1 M LiOH containing 10␀4 M purine bases (Fig. S3). In 0.1 M LiOH, the HER current density is the highest on the Pt(110) sur- face compared to that on the Pt(111) and Pt(100) surfaces [24]. In 10␀4 M purine bases containing solutions, the activity of Pt(110) is the highest and is 3.4 times higher than that without purine. The current density for the adsorption/desorption of hydrogen and hydroxide is decreased by the adsorption of purine bases. The coverage (θ) of the adsorbed purine bases was estimated using the following equation: θ1␀Qadd QwEo where Qadd and Qw/o are the charge densities between 0.05 and 0.4 V in 0.1 M LiOH containing 10␀4 M purine bases and 0.1 M LiOH, respec - tively, for the positive sweep. Fig. 2(c) shows the coverage dependence of the HER activity on Pt(110) as measured from the LSVs at different concentrations of purine bases (from 10␀7 M to 10␀2 M). Different pu- rine bases yielded maximum HER activity at the different coverages. Specifically, the HER activities of purine and theophylline are 4.2 and 5.0 times higher than those in the solution without purine bases, respectively (Fig. 2(c)). Two trends were observed: the HER activities of caffeine, theophylline, and theobromine with methyl groups reached their maxima and then decreased, whereas the HER activities of xanthine and purine without methyl groups increased linearly with increasing coverage. This could be understood by considering that pu- rine bases with methyl groups may aggregate because of hydrophobic interactions between the methyl groups, which would obstruct the active sites. This led us to investigate the adsorption structure of purine, which did not cause HER deactivation at high coverage. 2.2. Interfacial structure of purine adsorbed on Pt(110) in LiOH solution For the potential-modulated IR measurements of the electrochemical interface, if the IR absorption of the adsorbed species does not change between the sample and background spectrum, the IR intensity will cancel out in the differential spectrum. Therefore, the background spectrum was obtained by fully covering Pt(110) with adsorbed carbon monoxide (CO), and the sample spectra of the adsorbed purine were acquired after the adsorbed CO was completely oxidized. The mea- surement was conducted as follows: the background spectrum was recorded by saturating the Pt(110) with adsorbed CO at 0.1 V, after which the sample spectra were collected by adsorbing purine on Pt(110) after the oxidation of CO by CV between 0.05 and 0.9 V. This approach enabled us to extract the differential spectra for the signal from adsorbed purine against that of the purine-free surface. Fig. 3(a) shows the potential dependent IR spectra of Pt(110) in 0.1 M LiOH deuterium oxide (D2O) solution containing 10␀2 M purine. D2O was used to avoid overlap with the water bending mode around 1600 cm␀1. The negative-going IR bands at 2040 cm␀1 and 1857 cm␀1 are assigned to the CO stretching mode of CO adsorbed at the atop and bridge sites, respectively [38]. The positive-going IR bands at 1530 cm␀1 and 1410 cm␀1 are assigned to the symmetric COO stretching mode of adsorbed CO32␀that are bidentate and monodentate coordinated to Pt, respectively [39]. Fig. 1.Chemical structure of caffeine, theophylline, theobromine, xanthine, and purine.S. Tanaka et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  172 (2025)  151318 2
