The positive band that appears at 1587 cm␀1 is assignable to the CC stretching mode of purine. In alkaline solution (pH 13), the purine anion without the hydrogen atom at N9 predominantly exists, considering the pKa of purine [40]. Purine is chemically stable even under harsh alkaline conditions such as 10 M NaOH at 100 •C for 1 h [41]. Fig. S4shows the IR spectrum of 0.1 M LiOH in D2O containing 10␀2 M purine in the solution phase. Strong and weak bands appear at 1604 cm␀1 and 1553 cm␀1, respectively. The band at 1587 cm␀1 is bipolar in shape and the weak negative dip results from the purine anion in the solution. The frequency of the positive-going band (1587 cm␀1) was red-shifted compared to the peak at 1604 cm␀1 of the purine anion in the solu- tion phase, indicating that the purine anion is specifically adsorbed on Pt (110). Fig. 3 (b)shows the potential dependence of the band intensity at 1587 cm␀1. The band intensity decreases with increasing electrode po- tential from 0.1 V to 0.8 V and then recovers reversibly as the potential decreases. The CV of Pt(110) in 0.1 M LiOH containing 10␀4 M purine shows no characteristic peak between 0.05 and 0.9 V (Fig. 2(a)), indi- cating no oxidative dissociation of purine on Pt. The vibrational mode at 1587 cm␀1 has a dynamic dipole moment in the molecular plane. The appearance of this band indicates that the molecular plane of the purine is tilted with respect to the surface according to the surface selection rule of IRAS: only the vibrational modes with a dynamic dipole moment perpendicular to the surface are IR active [42]. The potential depen - dence of the band intensity shown in Fig. 3(b) suggests that the mo- lecular plane of the purine is more tilted relative to the surface at positive potentials. Specular CTR scattering was measured to determine the projected electron density in the direction normal to the surface. Fig. 4(a) shows the specular CTR profiles of Pt(110) in 0.1 M LiOH containing 10␀2 M purine at 0.6, 0.2, and ␀0.05 V vs RHE. The flow type electrochemical cell used for this CTR measurement enabled the in situ detection of diffracted X-rays during the evolution of hydrogen gas [27]. The CTR intensities in the solution containing 10␀2 M purine differ in shape around L 0.8␀1.4 from those in 0.1 M LiOH without purine reported previously [27], indicating that purine is adsorbed on the surface. The interactions between Pt complexes and purine bases have been extensively studied in the medical field, including in relation to cancer therapy [43–46]. Pyridinic nitrogen atoms, such as the N7 of guanine and the N1 or N3 of adenine, are preferentially coordinated to Pt sites [45,47,48]. Purine anions have four nitrogen sites (N1, N3, N7, and N9) that can coordinate with the surface. Because the density functional theory (DFT) calculations predicted that the N9 site possesses the highest basicity in the purine anion [49], we considered the structure adsorbed on the Pt surface via the N9 site of the purine as the initial model. Structural optimization using specular CTR profiles was conducted using an interfacial atomic model comprising adsorbed purine, water oxygen, and Pt layers. To optimize the adsorbed purine, the relative atomic positions of the purine molecules determined by DFT calcula - tions were fixed, and the distance of the purine molecule from the sur- face and the molecular orientation were refined, assuming that the N9 atom of the purine was coordinated to the Pt surface. Fig. 4(b) shows the electron density in the direction normal to the surface. The structural parameters of the optimized model are listed in Table S1. The molecular plane of adsorbed purine was tilted by approximately 20•, 30•, and 60• relative to the surface at 0.6, 0.2, and ␀0.05 V, respectively. This sup- ports the IR result shown in Fig. 3(b), where the band intensity weakens at positive potentials because the molecular plane is tilted toward the surface. The N9 nitrogen of purine is located at the distances of 3.0 ± 0.5 Å, 2.2 ±0.5 Å, and 2.4 ±0.3 Å from the topmost Pt surface layer (Pt1st) at 0.6, 0.2, and ␀0.05 V, respectively. Previous DFT calculations have indicated that Pt complexes bind to adenine and guanine at a distance of approximately 2.0–2.1 Å [48,50]. Fig. 4(c) shows a sche- matic model of adsorbed purine on the Pt1st at ␀0.05 V. The occupancy factor of 0.31 –0.37 for the Pt1st implies that the 1 ×2 reconstructed structure with the missing row is dominant with some Pt rows missing. Considering the steric hindrance by the hydrogen atoms bound to C8, a structure in which the molecular plane is parallel to the Pt row of Pt Fig. 2.(a) Cyclic voltammograms of Pt(110) in 0.1 M LiOH containing 10␀4 M purine bases. The scanning rate is 0.050 V s␀1. (b) Current densities at ␀0.1 V vs RHE estimated from the LSVs from 0 V to ␀0.1 V. The scanning rate is 0.010 V s␀1 with a rotation speed of 1600 rpm at 293 K. (c) Relationship between the current densities at ␀0.1 V and the coverages of purine bases on Pt(110). Fig. 3.(a) IR spectra of Pt(110) in 0.1 M LiOH deuterium oxide solution containing 10␀2 M purine. The background spectrum is CO-adsorbed Pt(110) at 0.1 V vs RHE. IR spectra were obtained sequentially at different potentials, as indicated by the arrows. (b) Potential dependence of band intensity at 1587 cm␀1.S. Tanaka et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  172 (2025)  151318 3
