(110) is not appropriate. The occupancy of the purine anion at ␀0.05 V is higher than that at 0.2 and 0.6 V. At ␀0.05 V, the molecular plane of the adsorbed purine becomes more aligned with the direction normal to the surface, thereby increasing the number of available adsorption sites. The electron density independent of the change in orientation of adsorbed purine appears at a distance of approximately 1.5 Å from the Pt1st layer, which is assigned to the oxygen of water in the missing row of Pt(110). If water is located at the center of the missing row, the atomic distance between oxygen and Pt1st is approximately 2.5 Å. The bond length of O–Pt of the adsorbed water monomer on Pt(111) was predicted to be 2.3␀2.4 Å by first principles calculations [51,52]. In our previous study, our X-ray diffraction analysis revealed that water is adsorbed on Pt(110) in LiOH at a distance of 2.09 –2.2 Å from the topmost Pt row [27]. Therefore, water, which prefers adsorption to the top sites on the Pt surface, is relegated to the missing row because of the presence of the adsorbed purine. In alkaline conditions, the Volmer process (H2O e␀→ Had OH␀), which involves the dissociation of the OH bond, is the rate-determining step for the HER on the Pt electrode [7,9–12]. Moreover, the local diffusion of generated OH␀and reactant H2O at the interface also affects the HER activity because the interfacial structure is significantly different from that of the bulk phase. The restructuring of the interfacial hydrogen-bonding network promotes fast OH␀transfer, such as Grotthuss-type motion, which enhances the HER activity [26,53,54]. Fig. 5shows a schematic model of the hydration structure of the purine anion adsorbed on Pt(110), based on structural analysis using X-ray diffraction. In previous DFT calculations, the water cluster hydrating the purine was reported to forms a cage-like structure via hydrogen bonding with the purine nitrogen [55]. According to the X-ray diffraction results Fig. 4.(a) Specular CTR profiles of Pt(110) in 0.1 M LiOH containing 10␀2 M purine at 0.6 (blue), 0.2 (red), and ␀0.05 V (green) vs RHE. The solid lines represent structure factors calculated from the optimized model. (b) Electron density profiles of the optimized model of purine adsorbed on Pt(110) at 0.6, 0.2, and ␀0.05 V. The relative atomic positions in the purine molecule were fixed, and the center position and the molecular orientation were optimized. The orientation angle at which the molecular plane of purine is parallel to the surface is defined as 0•. (c) Schematic model of adsorbed purine on Pt(110) at ␀0.05V. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.) Fig. 5.Schematic model of the hydration structure for purine adsorbed on reconstructed Pt(110) based on structural analysis using X-ray diffraction. The dashed yellow and blue lines indicate hydrogen bonding between the nitrogen atoms (N1 and N3) of adsorbed purine and water molecules. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the Web version of this article.)S. Tanaka et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 International  Journal  of Hydrogen  Energy  172 (2025)  151318 4
