described above, N3 of the adsorbed purine interacts with the hydrogen atom of water in the missing row. Moreover, additional water molecules may interact with N1 and N7 of adjacent adsorbed purine anions, as shown in Fig. 5. We could not confirm the presence of additional water, either because of overlap with the projected electron density of purine or statistically disorder. Although the water molecules farther away from the surface do not directly participate in the Volmer process, they may facilitate the diffusion of OH␀by hydrogen bonding with reactive water. HER can also be activated by bulky hydrophobic cations at the interface, implying that OH␀transfer is facilitated by the formation of hydrogen bonds within the hydration shell of the hydrophobic cation [24]. Sun et al. reported that a Pt electrode modified with imidazole, which is a component of caffeine, exhibited enhances HER activity [26]. They also suggest that the diffusion of OH␀was improved by the hydrogen-bonding network of interfacial water interacting with adsor - bed imidazole. Thus, HER activation is indirectly supported by the enhancement of OH␀diffusion at the interface. We investigated the HER activity of purine-modified Pt(110)-(1 ×1) to clarify the role of the water molecules in the missing rows of Pt(110)- (1 ×2). Fig. S5shows the HER activity of Pt(110)-(1 ×1) and -(1 ×2) in 0.1 M LiOH containing purine. Although the HER activity of unmodified Pt(110)-(1 ×2) was similar to that of unmodified Pt(110)-(1 ×1), the HER activity of purine-modified Pt(110)-(1 ×2) was approximately twice that of the purine-modified Pt(110)-(1 ×1) at a purine coverage of 0.8. This result also suggests that water in the missing row of Pt(110)-(1 ×2) contributes to the high HER activation by purine. The purine- modified Pt(110)-(1 ×2) can accommodate water molecules as hydrogen suppliers in the missing row (Fig. 5). These confined water molecules form hydrogen-bonded chains with water molecules inter- acting with adsorbed purines, which facilitate rapid OH␀hopping via the Grotthuss mechanism. Such specific interfacial ion transport may lower the local activation barrier for the HER to enhance its activity, thereby contributing to the superior activity on the (1 ×2) surface. 2.3. HER activation of Pt/C with defects in LiOH solution containing purine As mentioned above, the results obtained using single-crystal Pt electrodes demonstrated that the purine-modified (110) structure of Pt effectively activated the HER. We also attempted to construct a Pt sur- face with a (110) structure on the commercial Pt/C catalyst to activate the HER via purine modification. A previous study reported that the electrochemical oxidation and reduction of single-crystal Pt electrodes can produce defect structures, such as steps and vacancies [32]. Thus, defective Pt/C was prepared by chemically and thermally oxidizing the surface in H2O2 solution and air, respectively. Quaternized poly (arylene perfluoroalkylene)-4 (QPAF-4) was used as an anion exchange co- polymers and binder to prepare the Pt/C ink [56]. The trimethy - lammonium group of QPAF-4 activates HER on a Pt electrode [24]. Fig. 6(a) and (b) show the CVs of Pt/C after surface oxidation treatments in 0.5 M H2SO4 and 0.1 M LiOH, respectively. After thermal treatment at 573 K, the peaks at 0.12 V and 0.27 V increase in sulfuric acid solution, which are assigned to the hydrogen adsorption/desorption at the (110) structure and the edge of the (100) terrace, respectively [57]. The peak for (110) structure at 0.25 V increases in alkaline solution after chemical and thermal treatments, indicating that Pt/C heated at 573 K in air has a defective structure on the Pt surface. The thermal treatment of Pt particles supported on carbon causes particle aggregation. Table S2presents the electrochemical surface area (ECSA) of Pt/C after thermal treatment, estimated from the charge density of hydrogen adsorption/desorption in 0.1 M HClO 4. The ECSA of the 