because the surface impurities were oxidatively removed. The decrease in the ECSA of Pt/C heated at 623 K compared to that at 523 K was attributed to the agglomeration of Pt particles. The aggregation caused by the heating of Pt/C was also evident in the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images, as shown in Fig. S6, in which the average particle size increased from 2 nm at 523 K to 5 nm at 623 K. On the other hand, chemical treated of Pt/C with H2O2 before thermal treatment at 573 K (H2O2–573 K Pt/C) suppressed aggregation and increased the ECSA. The TEM image of H2O2–573 K Pt/C shows that the average particle size is comparable to that of the as-received Pt/C (Fig. S6). Fig. 6(c) and (d) compare the surface and mass activities for HER, respectively, of as-received Pt/C and H2O2–573 K Pt/C modified with caffeine or purine at ␀0.1 V vs RHE estimated from the LSVs in 0.1 M LiOH containing 10␀4 M purine bases (Fig. S7). The surface and mass activities of H2O2–573 K Pt/C were 2.3 times higher than those of the as- received Pt/C. This result indicates that the (110) structure constructed by the thermal and chemical treatments enhanced the HER activity, as Pt (110) showed the highest HER activity among the low-index planes of Pt. The top site of Pt(110) stabilizes the overpotentially deposited hydrogen (Hopd), which is an intermediate in the HER [58,59]. The HER activities of H2O2–573 K Pt/C are further enhanced in 0.1 M LiOH containing caffeine and purine. The HER activity of H2O2–573 K Pt/C with purine is the highest, and the surface and mass HER activities are 3.0 times higher as compared to that of as-received Pt/C. As is the cases fo single-crystal Pt electrodes in Fig. 2(c), the HER activity of H2O2–573 K Pt/C does not decrease with increasing purine concentrations (Fig. S8). The overpotentials of the HER on the purine-modified Pt(110) electrode is lower than that of Pt(110)/Ni(OH) 2 (Table S3). This result indicates that the comparatively low overpotential contribute to a sur- face engineering strategy for the alkaline HER. Thus, the microscopic properties of single-crystal electrodes are applicable to nanoparticle catalysts, and provide insights into the use of surface modifications to improve the HER activation. 3.Conclusions In this study, we investigated the effect of purine bases (caffeine, xanthine, purine, theophylline, and theobromine) on the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) using single-crystal Pt electrodes. The results obtained from these electrodes were extended to commercial Pt/C. The HER activity of the reconstructed Pt(110) in 0.1 M LiOH containing purine was 4.2 times higher than that without purine. The HER activity of purine-modified Pt(110) increased linearly with purine coverage and did not deteriorate even at high purine coverage. The interfacial struc- ture of the HER-activated Pt(110) with purine was revealed by IR spectroscopy and X-ray CTR scattering. The molecular orientation of purine depended on the electrode potential, with the molecular plane of purine orientated nearly perpendicular to the surface at ␀0.05 V, the potential at which hydrogen is produced. Water molecules interacting with the adsorbed purine were located in the missing row of Pt(110)-(1 ×2), suggesting that they formed hydrogen bonds with other water molecules located in the outer layer. This hydrogen-bonding structure of water molecules might facilitate the diffusion of OH␀, which activates the HER. These findings were also applied to the commercial Pt/C. The (110) structure was constructed on the Pt/C surface via chemical treatment with H2O2 and thermal treatment without aggregation of the Pt particles. Furthermore, the modification of chemically and thermally treated Pt/C with purine increased the HER activity by 3.0 times compared to that of the as-received Pt/C. CRediT authorship contribution statement Syunnosuke Tanaka: Writing – original draft, Investigation, Fund - ing acquisition. Hiroaki Hattori: Investigation. Tomoyuki Kogane - zawa: Writing – review & editing, Investigation. Tomotaka Nakatani: Writing – review & editing, Investigation. Rosantha Kumara: Writing – review & editing, Investigation. Osami Sakata: Writing – review & editing, Resources. Kenji Miyatake: Writing – review & editing, Re- sources. Nagahiro Hoshi: Writing – review & editing, Supervision. Masashi Nakamura: Writing – review & editing, Supervision, Meth - odology, Funding acquisition, Conceptualization. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgment X-ray diffraction measurements were carried out at SPring-8 with the approval of Japan Synchrotron Radiation Research Institute (JASRI) under proposal Nos. 2022A1291, 2022B0582, and 2023A1796. This study was supported by the Sasakawa Scientific Research Grant, JST SPRING (JPMJSP2109), the ENEOS Hydrogen Trust Fund, the Steel Foundation for Environmental Protection Technology, and the Japan Society for the Promotion of Science (JSPS/KAKENHI, 23K23155). Appendix A.Supplementary data Supplementary data to this article can be found online at https://doi. org/10.1016/j.ijhydene.2025.151318 . References [1]Guo F, Macdonald TJ, Sobrido AJ, Liu L, Feng J, He G. Recent advances in ultralow-pt-loading electrocatalysts for the efficient hydrogen evolution. Adv Sci 2023;10:2301098. https://doi.org/10.1002/advs.202301098 . [2]Jiang B, Liu S, Cheng L, Zhou L, Cui H, Liu M, et al. Mass synthesis of Pt/C catalysts with high Pt loading for low-overpotential hydrogen evolution. Int J Hydrogen Energy 2024;58:268 –78. https://doi.org/10.1016/J.IJHYDENE.2024.01.183 . [3]Luo X, Yuan P, Xiao H, Li S, Luo J, Li J, et al. Effects of intrinsic defects in Pt-Based carbon supports on alkaline Hydrogen Evolution reaction. ACS Appl Mater Interfaces 2024;16:26044 –56. https://doi.org/10.1021/ACSAMI.4C00690 . [4]Li Q, Luo W, Cui X, Shi J. Rapid and in situ active sites regeneration for OER activity recovery and greatly prolonged water-splitting performance. Angew Chem Int Ed 2025;64:e202500303. https://doi.org/10.1002/ANIE.202500303 . [5]Li Q, Chen C, Luo W, Yu X, Chang Z, Kong F, et al. In situ active site refreshing of electro-catalytic materials for ultra-durable hydrogen evolution at elevated Current density. Adv Energy Mater 2024;14:2304099. https://doi.org/10.1002/ AENM.202304099 . [6]Markovi ˘c NM, Ross PN. Surface science studies of model fuel cell electrocatalysts. Surf Sci Rep 2002;45:117 –229. https://doi.org/10.1016/s0167-5729(01)00022-x . [7]Sheng W, Gasteiger HA, Shao-Horn Y. Hydrogen oxidation and evolution reaction kinetics on platinum: acid vs alkaline electrolytes. J Electrochem Soc 2010;157: B1529. https://doi.org/10.1149/1.3483106 . [8]Sheng W, Zhuang Z, Gao M, Zheng J, Chen JG, Yan Y. Correlating hydrogen oxidation and evolution activity on platinum at different pH with measured hydrogen binding energy. Nat Commun 2015 61 2015;6:1 –6. https://doi.org/ 10.1038/ncomms6848 . [9]Liu Z, Li Z, Li J, Xiong J, Zhou S, Liang J, et al. Engineering of Ru/Ru 2P interfaces superior to Pt active sites for catalysis of the alkaline hydrogen evolution reaction. J Mater Chem A 2019;7:5621 –5. https://doi.org/10.1039/C8TA11635K . [10] Tang MT, Liu X, Ji Y, Norskov JK, Chan K. Modeling hydrogen evolution reaction kinetics through explicit water-metal interfaces. J Phys Chem C 2020;124: 28083 –92. https://doi.org/10.1021/ACS.JPCC.0C08310 . [11] Bender JT, Petersen AS, Østergaard FC, Wood MA, Heffernan SMJ, Milliron DJ, et al. Understanding cation effects on the hydrogen evolution reaction. ACS Energy Lett 2023;8:657 –65. https://doi.org/10.1021/acsenergylett.2c02500 . [12] Durst J, Siebel A, Simon C, Hasch ˘e F, Herranz J, Gasteiger HA. New insights into the electrochemical hydrogen oxidation and evolution reaction mechanism. Energy Environ Sci 2014;7:2255 –60. https://doi.org/10.103