Ceramics International 49 (2023) 1020–1029 1021spontaneous polarization through the ab plane [9]. Among the ferroelectric materials in the BLSFs family, relaxor-type ferroelectrics show much interest due to their application in energy storage. Low remnant with high saturated polarization and frequency- dependent diffused phase transition characteristics makes them unique from normal ferroelectrics [12–14]. In this respect, BaBi 2Nb2O9 (BBN) (m 2) is an important and well-known relaxor ferroelectric, where microscopic distortion with the disorder of Ba- and Bi-cations lead to high polarization retention [15]. Studies show that cation disorder and local charge imbalance in BBN create polar nano-regions (PNRs) [12, 16]. Here, Bi2O2 layers take an important role in the polarization and electric conduction of ceramic materials. The 6s2 stereo active lone pair of electrons in bismuth is responsible for the generation of important stereochemistry and steric-related properties [17]. Again, the lower stability of the oxygen ions in this layer favors the formation of oxygen vacancies than the perovskite blocks [18,19]. This type of oxygen va- cancy is responsible for fatigue generation [20]. Researchers introduce suitable cations like trivalent rare earth, transition metal in the place of Bi3to enhance the electrical properties of BLSFs. The involvement of stereo-active cations like Pb2, Sb3, Te4, Sn2varies the dielectric properties without obstructing the crystal structure [17,21–23]. The presence of 5s2 lone pair containing Sb3(5 mol%) in the place of bis- muth improves ferroelectricity and piezoelectric coefficient of SrBi 2Nb2O9 (SBN) [17]. The partially or fully replacement of rare earth elements like La3[24], Pr3[25], Sm3[26], Nd3[23] for Bi3in SBN have been already investigated by the researchers. The involvement of La3or Pr3in SBN changes it from normal to relaxor ferroelectric. La3 doping in the Bi2O2 layer of SrBi 2Ta2O9 destroys the strain-stress equi- librium of sublattice and enhances remnant polarization [27]. Kannan et al. show that the doping of Sm3in BBN leads to a decrease of dielectric constant and dielectric maxima with the retain of relaxor diffused phase characteristics [28]. Simultaneous doping of La3and V5in the place of Bi3and Nb5in BBN shows the enhancement of Curie temperature with an increasing level of substitution [29]. Recently, perovskite based materials such as LnBaCo 2O5δ are reported to show excellent HER performance [1]. Ferroelectric perovskites like K0⋅5Na0⋅5NbO 3 has recently been reported for the hydrogen evolution reaction under solar irradiation [2]. However, there has been seldom report to date about the effect of polarization on electrocatalytic HER. Therefore, HER study on perovskite and layered perovskite ferroelectric materials are of great importance for energy storage application purpose. As a promising dopant, yttrium shows a high value for the improvement of densification and fatigue properties in ferroelectric materials. Yttrium substitution in the place of Bi in (Bi0⋅5Na0.5)TiO 3 enhances diffused phase transition characteristics of the material [30]. The presence of yttrium in Bi3⋅2Y0⋅8Ti3O12 thin films leads to high remnant polarization, low coercive field, and fatigue-free characteristics Fig. 1.(a) Powdered X-ray diffraction study, (b) Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy and (c) Room temperature Raman spectrum of BBYN0.1 ceramics calcined at 900 •C, 20 h. M.K. Adak et al.
============================================================
[Page 3/10]
============================================================
Ceramics International 49 (2023) 1020–1029 1022up to greater than 108 switching cycles [31]. Involvement of yttrium at Bi-position of SBN causes the formation of relaxor characteristics with diffused phase transition. This type of doping is also owing to the reduction of saturated polarization, remnant polarization, and coercive electric field. The presence of Y3acts as an inhibitor of grain growth which leads to the formation of high dense ceramics [32]. In the present study, we introduce 5 mol% Y at the Bi position in the layered structure of BBN to improve their dielectric and polarization characteristics. The single-phase structure ceramic is prepared through a solid-state reaction process and the sintered material is used to characterize through structural and electrical analysis. The layered structured material is also used for the investigation of hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) under the electrocatalytic pathway. This type of HER shows high importance in terms of alternatives of fossil fuel consumption and environmental concern [33]. To the best of our knowledge, such types of BBN-based materials are not used previously for HER application. 2.Experimental procedure Conventional solid-state reaction method is used to prepare BaBi 2- xYxNb2O9 (x 0.10, abbreviated as BBYN0.1). In this synthesis process, the starting raw chemicals are Ba(NO 3)2 (Merck India, ≽99%), Bi2O3 (Merck India, ≽99%), Nb2O5 (Sigma-Aldrich, 99.9%), Y2O3 (Merck India, 99.99%). The chemicals are weighed according to their stoi- chiometric ratio and mixed well. The mixture powder is then ground using ethanol in a mortar pestle. The resulted powder is then packed in an alumina crucible and calcined at 900 •C for 10 h in a muffle furnace. After calcination, the powder is again ground using ethanol in a mortar pestle and calcined for 2nd time at 900 •C for 10 h. The synthesized material is then subjected to XRD for phase analysis. The calcined powder is then mixed with binder polyvinyl alcohol (5 wt%) and con- verted into a pellet with the help of a manual hydraulic press. The pellet is then sintered at 900 •C in an alumina crucible for 8 h under the air atmosphere. The overall synthesis process is given in Fig. S1. The solid- state reaction of the above process is given as [34]. BaNO 321B9 2Bi2O3Nb2O50B1 2Y2O3→BaBi 1B9Y0B1Nb2O9NO 3↑ NO 2↑ (1) The crystal structure and phase formation of BBYN0.1 is identified using a powder X-ray diffraction study. BRUKER D8 advanced eco is used with λ 1.5406 Å, 2θ 20•to 80•in a scanning rate 2•(2θ/min, θ Bragg angles). The Fourier Transform Infrared spectrometer of Per- kinElmer (Spectrum two) has been used to analyze the functional group of the synthesized ceramic material. JEOL-JSM 5800 model of scanning electron micrograph (SEM) is used to view the material surface and grain size orientation under 20 KV at room temperature. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) of Hitachi-600 is used to investigate morphological information. For electrical characterization, both sides of the sintered pellet are polished and electrodes by high purity silver paste (Aldrich, South Korea). The silver-coated pellet is then dried at 180 •C for 2 h in an oven to remove moisture if any. The dielectric and ferro- electric measurement of the silver-coated pellet is done by AixCCT systems with aixPlorer software version 3.0.47.0. For the electrochemical measurement, Gamry potentiostat is used in a standard three-electrode setup. During the measurement, Hg/HgO, and Pt electrodes are used as reference electrodes and counter electrodes respectively in 1 M KOH electrolyte. Here, nickel foam (NF) is used as a substrate of the working electrode for its porous structure with high conductive characteristics. A 1 ×1 cm2 area of NF is used for its loading area where the different amount of catalyst ink (BBYN0.1 10% Nafion, see SI) has been loaded for electrochemical measurement. According to the catalyst loading, BBYN1 and BBYN2 are marked according to 6.6 and 27.4 mg catalyst loading respectively. 3.Results and discussion The powder X-ray diffraction pattern of BBYN0.1 is given in Fig. 1(a). The diffractogram shows that all the diffraction peaks are well-matched with the standard powdered diffraction pattern of BBN [Joint Commit - tee on Powder Diffraction Standards (JCPDS) No. 86–1191]. The pattern Fig. 2.(a) FESEM image, (b–g) mapping, (h) EDX spectra of sintered pellet of BBYN0.1 ceramics measured at room temperature.  M.K. Adak et al.
