Ceramics International 49 (2023) 1020–1029 1023establishes a single-phase orthorhombic structure with an A21am (36) space group, where WinPLOTR software (version: February 2021) is used for the hkl list generation. The absence of a secondary phase in- dicates that the Y-ion successfully enters the layered position of bismuth. The comparable smaller radius of Y3ion (1.04 Å) concerning volatile Bi3(1.17 Å) suggests easy substitution [35]. The lattice parameters are calculated from the XRD pattern, which are a 5.42 Å, b 5.68 Å, and c 25.57 Å. The unit cell volume of the crystalline structure is 787.19 Å3. The following Scherrer ’s formula [36,37] is used to determine the crystallite size of BBYN0.1 D0B9λ βcosθ(2) where D is the crystallite size, λ is the wavelength of the X-ray used (λ  1.5406 Å for Cu Kα), β is the full width at half maxima, and θ is the angle of diffraction. The calculated average crystallite size of the material is 61 nm. In the XRD diffraction, the highest intense peak (115) indicates the solid-state synthesized BBYN0.1 is (112 m 1) type BLSFs [38]. The sites of all the diffraction peaks of BBYN0.1 are coincident with the perovskite structure of BBN in the standard card without any lattice distortion. The involvements of Y3in the layered structure have several significant rules in the improvement of ferroelectric properties like reduction of oxygen vacancies and pinning of domain walls. This type of displacement has a probability for the generation of larger internal stress, which may enhance the remnant polarization in the material [39]. Single-phase orthorhombic structure is also found for SrBi 2–xYxNb2O9 (x 0.0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8), where slight shifting of the XRD peaks to higher value is also observed [32]. The presence of a smaller ionic radius (Nd3, Gd3) in place of Bi3leads to the reduction of orthorhombicity in Bi5Ti3FeO 15, the inherent mechanism works by the partial substitution of bismuth ions and A-site cations [40]. FTIR spectra of solid-state synthesized BBYN0.1 is given in Fig. 1(b). The characteristic absorption bands are observed at 622 cm␀1, 810 cm␀1, 1638 cm␀1, 2350 cm␀1, and 3420 cm␀1 wavenumber regions. The band position at 3420 cm␀1 and 1638 cm␀1 indicates O–H stretching and bending vibration mode of water molecule respectively. The pres- ence of water molecules is probably due to the presence of moisture in the sample or in the sample compartment [41]. The band near 2350 cm␀1 indicates the asymmetric stretching of CO2, which may be from atmospheric or adsorbed CO2 [42,43]. The area between 1100 cm␀1 to 1200 cm␀1 indicate the presence of O–O stretching frequency [44]. The peak near 535 cm␀1 indicated the stretching vibration of Y–O bonds, confirming the incorporation of Y in the layered structure [45–47]. The band position at 820 cm␀1, and 622 cm␀1 is responsible for the presence of the Nb–O bond in NbO 6 octahedra [48]. The room temperature Raman spectrum of BBYN0.1 from 250 cm␀1 to 1000 cm␀1 Raman shift is shown in Fig. 1(c). The high-frequency bands are related to the inter-group binding energy i.e. binding energy for NbO 6 octahedron [49,50]. The binding mode at 568 cm␀1 indicates the symmetric stretching vibration of NbO 6 octahedra (Nb–O) [51,52]. Another mode at 863 cm␀1 is raised due to symmetrical metal-oxygen stretching vibration of NbO 6 octahedra [49,52]. The presence of Y–O vibrational mode is confirmed from the Raman shift of 311 cm␀1, 434 cm␀1 and 466 cm␀1 [53,54]. The morphological and topological information of the BBYN0.1 is investigated through a FESEM study. The SEM image of the sintered pellet is given in Fig. 2(a) (Fig. S2). The figure shows well-packed grains are present with spherical morphological characteristics. Image J soft- ware is used to calculate the average grain size of the material which is 405 nm. The grains are compact with each other and asymmetrically distributed throughout the image, which indicates various kinetic movement is present across the boundaries [55,56]. The high value of grain size is due to the high and long duration of heat treatment. As a result, the grain growth process is built up during the sintering process that leads to disappear of small grains [57]. Kiran et al. showed that the yttrium substitution at bismuth position in SBN acts as a growth inhib - itor and promotes the material as high dense ceramics [32]. The elemental mapping (Fig. 2(b–g), Fig. S3) shows the homogeneous dis- tribution of corresponding elements Ba, Bi, Y, Nb, and O throughout the Fig. 3.(a) Survey spectra, and high-resolution spectra of (b) Ba 3d, (c) Bi 4f, (d) Y 3d, (e) Nb 3d, and (f) O 1s.  M.K. Adak et al.
