Ceramics International 49 (2023) 1020–1029 1024sample surface. Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) (Fig. 2(h), Fig. S4) confirms the presence of all elements of the materials with the right composition ratio. The oxidation state, chemical environment of BBYN0.1 elements at the surface are investigated through XPS analysis [58]. The XPS survey spectra of the BBYN0.1 and the high-resolution deconvoluted spectra of Ba3d, Bi4f, Y3d, Nb3d, and O1s are presented in Fig. 3 (a - f). The measured survey spectra are matched with previously reported data (Fig. 3(a)) [59]. The spin-orbit doublet of Ba 3d peaks are observed at 795.95 eV and 780.56 eV, which are due to 3d3/2 and 3d5/2 of Ba2 respectively (Fig. 3(b)) [60]. The presence of peaks around 779.0 eV is responsible for the presence of Ba2ions in the previous studies [58,61]. The chemical shifting towards higher binding energy indicated the presence of oxide species which enhance its oxidation state and corre - sponding binding energy [60]. The Bi 4f spectra show two characteris - tics strong signals at 158.78 eV and 163.98 eV for 4f7/2 and 4f5/2 respectively (Fig. 3(c)) [62,63]. The Y 3d core level peak shows two distinct characteristics bands at 157.65 eV and 158.67 eV for 3d5/2 and 3d3/2 respectively (Fig. 3(d)) [64]. The corresponding peaks indicate the presence of Y3only that indicate the existence of Y2O3 [65]. The Nb 3d spectrum shows three distinct peaks as shown in Fig. 3(e). The decon - voluted spectra show four peaks at the binding energies 209.85 eV, 207.24 eV, 205.87 eV, and 203.69 eV. The binding energies 207.24 eV and 209.85 indicate the presence of 3d5/2 and 3d3/2 respectively, which are related to the Nb5-O due to surface oxidation [66,67]. The O1s deconvoluted spectra show two peaks at 528.96 eV, 530.81 eV binding energies (Fig. 3(f)). The higher binding energy indicates the presence of adsorbed surface oxygen species with a lower coordination number [68]. 3.1. Dielectric and ferroelectric study Fig. 4 (a-b) shows the change of dielectric constant and dielectric loss at various temperatures for 1 kHz, 10 kHz, 100 kHz, and 1 MHz fre- quency. The dielectric constant value steadily increases with rising temperature and reaches a maximum at a point, which is known as Curie temperature or temperature maxima (Tm). For 1 kHz frequency, Tm is found at 135 •C and gradually shifted towards a higher temperature region with increasing frequency (Table 1). This type of Tm shifting characteristic of the material at different frequencies is known as relaxor behavior. The dielectric curve also exhibited diffused phase transition characteristics at each frequency range. This broad temperature dependence is due to the involvement of Y3randomly at different do- mains. The phase transition of different domains occurs in a broad temperature range [30]. This kind of diffused phase transition is also Fig. 4.(a) Dielectric constant vs. Temperature and (b) dielectric loss (tanδ) vs. temperature plots and (c) Plots of ln (1/ε - 1/εmax) versus ln (T-T max) for BaBi 1- ⋅9Y0⋅1Nb2O9 sintered at 900 •C, 8 h at different frequencies. Table 1 Dielectric constant and dielectric loss at Curie temperature and room tempera - ture (25 •C) under 1 kHz, 10 kHz, 100 kHz, and 1 MHz frequency for BBYN0.1 composition. Frequency (Hz) Tm (•C) ε at Tm Loss at Tm ε at 25 •C Loss at 25 •C 1 kHz 135 199 0.063 149 0.14 10 kHz 190 183 0.005 119 0.11 100 kHz 205 175 0.041 112 0.09 1 MHz 255 173 0.082 102 0.10  M.K. Adak et al.
