Ceramics International 49 (2023) 1020–1029 1025observed for [(YxBi0.5␀x)Na0.5]TiO 3 (x 0–20 mol%) and diffused characteristics improve gradually with the raise of yttrium doping [30]. Kiran et al. pointed out the diffused phase characteristic relaxor behavior of SBN due to the disorder of the bismuth layer by the sub- stitution of Y3ion. Bi5␀xYxTi3FeO 15 (x 0.0, 0.1, 0.3, 0.5 and 0.7) show phase transition at 300 •C and become more prominent with the presence of more Y3ion, which may be due to the role of space-charge polarization [35]. At 1 kHz and 10 kHz frequency range, BBYN0.1 show second-order phase transition, this can be described by Landau - –Ginzburg –Devonshire mean-field theory [69,70]. The lower value of the dielectric loss was found at low temperatures and increases rapidly at high temperatures. Here, the involvement of charge carriers take an important role through the contribution of oxygen vacancies [71]. In the dielectric loss curve, the tangent loss peaks are appeared at the below of Tc point, an indication of ferroelectric domain walls contribution [11, 72]. The degree of diffuseness or relaxation in phase transition is analyzed by the use of Curie-Weiss law as follows [17,73]. [1 ε␀1 εm] 1 CT␀Tmγ(3) where εm indicates the maximum value of dielectric constant at the temperature maxima Tm, C is the Curie constant, and γ indicates the degree of diffuseness. The degree of diffusion is calculated from the slope of linear fit plotting of log ( 1 ε␀1 εm) vs. log (T-T m) at different fre- quencies. The obtained γ values are 1.42 (±0.02), 1.52 (±0.03), and 1.72 (±0.03) at 1 kHz, 10 kHz, and 100 kHz respectively (Fig. 4 (c)). The values indicated the ferroelectric characteristics nature shifted to relaxor ferroelectric characteristics with increasing frequencies [73]. The higher value of relaxation is probably owing to the large local po- larization through structural disorders between layered cation and ‘A’ site cation [73]. The impedance spectroscopy is performed to understand the role of defects during the conductivity of the material through grain and grain boundaries. The Nyquist plot of BBYN0.1 for a wide range of frequencies (42 Hz–1 MHz) at different temperatures is shown in Fig. 5 (a). The single semicircle characteristics specify the grain conductivity of the material [73–75]. The enhancement of temperature reduces the resis- tance characteristics of the material, reflected in the arcs of the semi- circles. The intercept of the arcs gradually decreases with increasing temperature, which is the features of the NTCR (negative temperature coefficient of resistance) behavior of the material [71]. The non-Debye type relaxation is identified through the presence of semicircles center at below of the real axis. This type of relaxation generates owing to the presence of multiple relaxation processes in the material [76]. The plot of imaginary and real impedance vs. frequency at different temperatures is given in Fig. 5 (b) and (c) respectively. The real impedance value at low frequency is frequency independent for a certain range and after that, the value gradually decreases significantly. The Z∕value gradually decreases with increasing temperature and at the high frequency, it Fig. 5.(a) Cole-Cole plot, (b) Z∕vs. frequency plot, (c) - Z∕∕vs. frequency plot, (d) relaxation with the inverse of the temperature, (e) dc conductivity vs. 1000/T, and (f) ac conductivity vs. frequency plot of BBYN0.1 ceramic. Table 2 Variation of electrical parameters (bulk) as a function of temperature. σdct RbA(6) Temperature (•C) fmax (kHz) Z∕(Ω) Cb (pF) 500 0.53863 4.04E06 7.32E01 510 0.61191 3.59E06 7.25E01 520 0.78976 2.84E06 7.10E01 530 1.15797 2.22E06 6.19E01 540 1.31553 1.78E06 6.80E01 550 1.49452 1.42E06 7.50E01 560 2.48949 1.11E06 5.76E01 570 4.14685 8.97E05 4.28E01 580 4.71107 7.07E05 4.78E01 590 5.35207 5.45E05 5.46E01 600 6.08028 4.34E05 6.03E01 The DC conductivity of BBYN0.1 is determined throu