Ceramics International 49 (2023) 1020–1029 1026becomes frequency and temperature independent. This type of charac - teristic is responsible for the release of space charge by the reduction of barrier properties [60,77–79]. The imaginary impedance value at the low-frequency region grad- ually increases and reaches a maximum value for each temperature. The peak maxima position gradually shifted towards higher frequency [Fig. 5(c) arrow line, Table 2] with increasing temperature implying temperature-dependent dielectric relaxation [76]. The broadening of the peak maxima with rising temperature indicates the spread of the relaxation process. The Z∕∕value gradually decreases after peak maxima and merges at high-frequency region. The merging characteristics at the high-frequency region are also observed in the previously published literature [60,80]. At higher frequency regions, grain oxidation occurs quickly by relaxing the conduction path and neutralizing the space charge carriers [60]. The relaxation time (τ) associated with Fig. 5 (d) is calculated using the following equation 2πfmaxτ1 (4) where fmax indicates the relaxation frequency. The activation energy of the relaxation is calculated through the Arrhenius equation as follows ττ0eEa kT (5) where τ0 indicates the pre-exponential factor, Ea is the activation en- ergy, k is the Boltzmann constant, and T is the absolute temperature. The activation energy (1.45 eV) is calculated from the slope of linear fitting lnτ vs. 1000/T plot as shown in Fig. 5 (e).where σdc is the dc conduc - tivity of the material, t and A is the thickness and area of the pellet respectively that is used for electrical measurement, Rb is the bulk resistance. Rb is calculated from the Nyquist plot [81]. The plot of dc conductivity vs. 1000/T is given in Fig. 5 (e). The activation energy (1.33 eV) of the plot is obtained from the slope of linear fitting using the Arrhenius equation [71]. The obtained activation energy is closely related to the relaxation activation energy, which indicates dc conduc - tivity of the material is controlled by oxygen vacancies. AC conductivity of BBYN0.1 is investigated within the frequency range 42 Hz to 1 MHz at different temperature regions (Fig. 5 (f)). The value of AC conductivity is calculated through the following equation σacωε0εtanδ (7) where ω (2πf) is the angular frequency, ε0 is the permittivity at vac- uum, ε is the relative permittivity, and tanδ is the dielectric loss. The AC conductivity gradually increases with increasing frequency and tem- perature. The higher conductivity at the high-frequency region is due to the initiation of the relaxation process that confined the charge carriers to the barrier. The same type of relaxation mechanism is reported by Jiang et al. [71]. The ferroelectric P-E hysteresis loop of BBYN0.1 under an applied field of 5 kV/cm, 10 kV/cm, 15 kV/cm, and 20 kV/cm at room tem- perature is shown in Fig. 6 (a). The maximum polarization value grad- ually increases with increasing the applied electric field, but the remnant polarization and coercive field remain much lower, resulting in a slim hysteresis loop (Table 3). This type of slim characteristic is applicable for energy storage performance [73]. The fatigue characteristics of BBYN0.1 is investigated for the po- tential device application purpose [82]. The fatigue study is done under a 500 V applied field, 50 Hz frequency for 106 times repeated a number of polarization cycles. The normalized polarization vs. switching cycles is shown in Fig. 6 (b). The plot shows excellent fatigue-free character - istics without degradation of polarization. The P-E hysteresis curve of the 1st cycle and 106 cycles show no difference that reflects its better potential device application (Fig. S5). The fatigue-free nature is possibly due to the lower value of charge defects ’ diffuseness through different crystal cells [83]. This performance means promising material for non-volatile random-access memory [84]. The same type of fatigue-free characteristics is observed for ferroelectric mica/Ag-ITO/ - Bi3⋅25La0⋅75Ti3O12/ITO structures (ITO indicates indium tin oxide) were composed of different crystals by (Bi2O2)2and (Bi1⋅25La0⋅75Ti3O10)2- layers lead to lower diffuse of charge defects [83]. The leakage current density study at room temperature shows a smaller value that indicates improve short-circuit current with fill factor (Fig. S6) [85]. The lower leakage current density by the incorporation of lanthanide elements in the place of bismuth is previously discussed by Sun et al. where the introduction of the La3and Pr3leads to lower oxygen vacancies [86]. 3.2. Electrochemical HER study The electrochemical hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is investi - Fig. 6.Ferroelectric hysteresis loops of the BBYN0.1 ceramics measured at a maximum applied electric field of 5 kV/cm, 10 kV/cm, 15 kV/cm, and 20 kV/cm at 30 •C, (b) Polarization vs. switching cycles. Table 3 Coercivity, Remnant and saturated polarization value of BaBi 1⋅9Y0⋅1Nb2O9 ce- ramics sintered at 900 •C for 8 h at different electric field. Temperature At 30 •C Electric Field 5 kV/ cm 10 kV/ cm 15 kV/ cm 20 kV/ cm Coercivity (kV/cm) 1.07 2.18 2.77 2.86 Remnant polarization (μC/cm2) 0.03 0.06 0.09 0.08 Spontaneous polarization (μC/ cm2) 0.14 0.30 0.49 0.64  M.K. Adak et al.
