Ceramics International 49 (2023) 1020–1029 1027gated with the ferroelectric BBYN0.1. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) study is performed at a scan rate of 1 mV/s [33]. The BBYN1 and BBYN2 loading show the overpotential 180 mV and 186 mV respectively at 10 mA cm␀2 current density where bare NF substrate exhibit 246 mV overpotential at the same current density (Fig. 7 (a)). The higher amount of loading of BBYN0.1 leads to the suppression of active sites that re- duces the catalytic efficiency of the material. In the electrochemical kinetic study, the Tafel plot has been plotted according to the following Tafel equation [87]. ηblogja (8) where η and j indicate the overpotential and current density respec - tively. The Tafel slope of BBYN1 and BBYN2 is calculated according to the above equation which is 113 and 122 mV dec␀1, respectively, as shown in Fig. 7 (b). The smaller value of the lower doping substrate indicates the higher activity of the material. The relatively better elec- trocatalytic activity of the BBYN1 electrode has been used to determine its electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) [88]. The measurement of cyclic voltammetry in a non-Faradaic region under different scan rates (10, 50, 100, and 150 mVs␀1) leads to its corresponding double-layer capacitance (Cdl) value (Fig. 7(c), Fig. S7). A Cdl value of 0.67 mF cm␀2 is calculated that gives 0.39 ECSA (per cm2) value. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) of the electrodes is shown in Fig. 7(d) (Fig. S8). The Nyquist plots show the charge transfer resistance (Rct) 3.5 Ω for both BBYN1 and BBYN2 samples. The lower value of Rct indicates rapid charge transfer kinetics of the material [89]. The corresponding fitted Bode plot of BBYN1 and BBYN2 are given in Fig. 7 (e) and 7 (f) respectively [90,91]. 4.Conclusion The nanocrystalline BBYN0.1 has been synthesized using the solid- state route through 5% yttrium substitution in the place of bismuth of BBN. The well matched PXRD pattern confirms the pure phase of the synthesized material. The FTIR and Raman studies verified the corre - sponding metal-oxygen stretching frequency. The crystallite size of the material is 61 nm, calculated from the PXRD pattern using Scherrer ’s formula. In the FESEM image (pellet sample) the high value of grain size is observed owing to the aggregation of a particle at higher sintering temperature. In the dielectric study, Curie temperature is found at 135 •C, which is shifted at higher temperature with increasing fre- quency, indicating relaxor characteristics. The Curie-Weiss law is applied to determine the diffusivity which is become higher with increasing frequency that reflected the relaxor characteristics. In the impedance spectroscopy study, materials show grain conductivity only with non-Debye type relaxation. The ferroelectric P-E hysteresis study show a slim hysteresis curve that can be applied for energy storage purpose. The materials also show fatigue-free characteristics under 106 switchable cycles with the lower value of leakage current density. The yttrium substituted BBN is used for the HER study that shows a 180 mV overpotential value at 10 mA cm␀2 current density. The Tafel slope value supports a facile electro-kinetics in the material. The Nyquist plot of the EIS study indicates lower charge transfer resistance of the material that supports the superior HER activity of the catalyst. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgement MKA thanks the Indian Institute of Technology Delhi, India for a Post-doctoral fellowship IITD/IES1/2021/329/72 dated 17.06.2021s . Appendix A.Supplementary data Supplementary data related to this article can be found at https:// doi.org/10.1016/j.ceramint.2022.09.077 . Fig. 7.(a) Polarization curves from LSV at different loading of BBYN0.1 on NF substrate, (b) Tafel plot of BBYN1 and BBYN2, (c) plots of Δj versus scan rate to get Cdl for BBYN1 and to determine the ECSA value, (d) Nyquist plot from the EIS data, and (e–f) Bode plot of BBYN1, BBYN2. M.K. Adak et al.
