Impact of temperature, frequency, and electrical field on the polarization of three-layer ferroelectric Ba2Bi2Nb2TiO12 aurivillius phases and its electrocatalytic activity for water splitting Mrinal Kanti Adaka,b,* , Debasish Mondala, Ajit Dasa,c, Debasis Dhaka,* aDepartment of Chemistry, Sidho-Kanho-Birsha University, Purulia 723104, India bDepartment of Chemistry, Indian Institute of Technology Roorkee, Roorkee 247667, India cDepartment of Chemistry, Balarampur College, Balarampur, West Bengal, 723143, India ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Ba2Bi2Nb2TiO 12 Ferroelectric Hysteresis Water splitting Energy storage efficiency NanomaterialsABSTRACT Aurivillius-type bismuth layered perovskite has garnered attention due to its ferroelectric polarization for energy storage applications. This work designs a three-layer ferroelectric Ba2Bi2Nb2TiO12 (BBNT) Aurivillius ceramic material that follows a high dielectric constant in the lower frequency region, which increases gradually with increasing temperature. The P-E hysteresis loop of the BBNT material is analyzed by the variation of electric field, temperature, and frequency. The slim characteristics of the hysteresis loop, with a higher Ps/Pr ratio (~20) at a lower frequency (0.1 Hz), indicate higher energy storage characteristics than those associated with temperature or electric field variation. A high recoverable energy density (Urec) of 0.026 J/cm3 with 89.7 % efficiency is recorded at 0.1 Hz frequency condition. The fatigue nature is also analyzed up to 106 cycles, which indicates a negligible loss of polarization. The energy conversion performance of the BBNT is also investigated in terms of its electrocatalytic activity for producing green hydrogen through water splitting. The calcined BBNT material exhibits a 172 mV overpotential for the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at 10 mA cm-2 when reviewed in a three-electrode setup under 1.0 M KOH. A long-term chronoamperometry analysis finds the stable electro - catalytic nature during green hydrogen production. The BBNT material demonstrates promising performance in both ferroelectric and electrocatalytic studies, indicating strong candidature for alternative energy storage and conversion applications. 1.Introduction Ferroelectric materials are extensively employed in modern elec- tronic devices owing to their spontaneous and reversible electric po- larization [1,2]. Lead zirconium titanate (PZT) is commonly utilized in commercial purposes, but lead toxicity and low Curie temperature limit its applications [3,4]. In this regard, newly designed Bi-based layered structured ferroelectrics (BLSFs), Aurivillius phase materials display fascinating qualities in non-volatile random-access memories (NVRAM) and high-temperature applications. The attention of ferroelectric ma- terials is also noticeable in the electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution re- action (HER), an alternative source of fossil fuels [5,6]. The general formula of BLSFs is demonstrated as [Bi2O2]2[An-1BnO3n1]2-, where n indicates the number of separated perovskite blocks by bismuth oxide layers [7–11]. In the formula, ‘A’ represents bi- or tri-valent cations like Ba2, Sr2, Ca2, Bi3, Pb2etc., where ‘B’ cations are related to high valence d0 transition metals like Ti4, Nb5, W6, Ta5etc [3,12]. The distortion of the [BO 6] octahedron and off-centering of the A-site cation originate from ferroelectric dipolar characteristics. These Aurivillius materials have generated significant interest in the scientific community due to their structural flexibility, high Tc, large remanent polarization, small coercive field, and possibility of forming lead-free materials [13, 14]. Three-layer Bi4Ti3O12 (BIT) Aurivillius ferroelectric shows sponta - neous polarization features, which have been varied previously through structural and layer tunability to obtain the desired functionality [1]. The displacements of Bi3cations and distortion of TiO6 are present in the noncentrosymmetric space group of BIT, which are responsible for their spontaneous polarization characteristics. The modifications on the perovskite layer and the cations at the ‘A’ and ‘B’ sites may influence *Corresponding authors. E-mail addresses: adakkantimrinal@gmail.com (M.K. Adak), debasisdhak@gmail.com (D. Dhak). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Surfaces and Interfaces u{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1�mt ozmont~om�1m{ y2u{�~zkw2 ��~qkmo�/k zn/tz�o~qkm o� https://doi.org/10.1016/j.surfin.2025.108309 Received 9 August 2025; Received in revised form 25 November 2025; Accepted 10 December 2025  Surfaces  and Interfaces  80 (2026)  108309 Available  online  11 December  2025 2468-0230/©  2025  Elsevier  B.V. All rights  are reserved,  including  those  for text and data mining,  AI training,  and similar  technologies.
