ferroelectric as well as Aurivillius structure. In this regard, highly substituted materials like Bi2Sr2Nb2TiO12 (BSNT) are explored with their enriched dielectric and ferroelectric characteristics [1,15]. Pure BSNT material acts as an electronic conductor at high oxygen activity, while the incorporation of Fe at the Ti site absorbs ionic conduction characteristics [16]. Compositional variation of BSNT as Sr1.25Bi2.75Nb1.25TiO12 leads to strong frequency dispersion in the relaxor ferroelectric characteristics owing to the disruption in the long-range ferroelectric ordering [17]. Bella et al. reported Mn-doped Bi2Sr2Ta2TiO12 Aurivillius phase as Sr1⋅5Bi2⋅5Ta2Ti0⋅5Mn0⋅5O12, where relaxor nature of the materials is brightened as the 6s2 lone pair of Bi3is more influenced by doping [18]. The non-centrosymmetric crystal structure improves electrical dipole formation that sponsoring relaxor ferroelectric behavior through A-site (Sr2/Bi3) and B-site (Ta5/Ti4/Mn3) disorder. Partial and full substitution of Sr2with smaller Ca2ions leads to the crystal structure distortion that influences BO6 octahedron deformation with the improvement of Curie tempera - ture and hysteresis polarizations [15]. 50 % replacement of Sr from BSNT by Ca2shows polar B2cb orthorhombic phase [1,15], where the presence of Ba2follow nonpolar I4/mmm space group [1]. Such types of doping produce relaxor nature of the materials, which is evidenced from the dielectric and P(E) studies. The incorporation of doped cations creates disorder at the crystallographic site owing to their substitution at the Bi3sites of [Bi2O2]2layer, which becomes more prominent in presence of Ba2[1]. Spontaneous polarization nature of the ferro- electric materials favored them in high-performance energy storage applications. In this regard, properties like recoverable energy density (Urec) and energy conversion efficiency (η) are highly significant [19, 20]. The energy conversion performance of the ferroelectric materials as an electrocatalyst is significant yet rarely explored. The involvement of ferroelectric materials as electrocatalysts in the electrocatalytic water splitting is highly interesting and fruitful owing to their spontaneous polarization nature under high voltage. In this regard, limited research work is performed on ferroelectric materials like Bi2Fe4O9 [21], BaBi 1- ⋅9Y0⋅1Nb2O9 [3]. The investigation based on three-layer BLSFs BBNT material has not been studied in the electrocatalytic water splitting to date. Herein, a three-layer Aurivillius phase BLSFs BBNT is considered to investigate dielectric and ferroelectric characteristics. The article first reported the complete substitution of Ba atoms as Ba2Bi2Nb2TiO12 and their electrocatalytic activity for water splitting. Though a lot of research works were performed on their structural variability through doping of metal ions and layer numbers, their detailed dielectric and ferroelectric investigations are lacking [22,23]. The current manuscript attentions this limitation not only through the material ’s characteriza - tion but also its electrical and electrocatalytic properties. The effect of ferroelectric polarization by different applied electric fields, tempera - ture, and frequencies is noted here. The excellent polarization charac - teristics are used for the electrocatalytic HER activity under alkaline conditions. Such type of work may contribute information about their catalytic suitability in the electrocatalytic water splitting for the search for an alternative to noble metal-based catalysts. 2.Experimental procedure 2.1. Raw chemicals Three-layered BBNT material is synthesized through the precursor solution decomposition method. The used raw chemicals for the syn- thesis process are Bi2O3 (Merck India, ≽99.0 %), Ba(NO 3)2 (Merck India, ≽99.0 %), Nb2O5 (Sigma-Aldrich, 99.9 %), TiO2 (Merck, ≽99.0 %), tartaric acid (Merck, GR grade), ammonium hydroxide (Merck, 25.0 %), triethanol amine (TEA) (Merck, GR grade), nitric acid (Merck, 69.0 %), and hydrofluoric acid (Merck, 48.0 %). The chemicals are used directly in the synthesis process without further purification. The double- distilled water is used to prepare the stock solutions of the chemicals. In the synthesis process, Ti-tartarate, Nb-tartarate, and bismuth nitrate precursor solutions are prepared from TiO2, Nb2O5, and Bi2O3, respectively. 2.2. Synthesis process In the preparation of Ti-tartarate solution from TiO2, the precursor chemical is dissolved in dilute hydrofluoric acid (F7M) under a water bath with continuous stirring (8h) to obtain a clear solution of TiF4. The clear acidic solution of TiF4 is then treated by a dilute NH4OH (1:1) solution to obtain hydrous titanium oxide precipitation. The obtained white residue is collected and freed of fluoride ions by repeated washing with dilute ammonium hydroxide and water. The fluoride-free hydrous titanium oxide residue is then transformed to a clear solution of Ti- tartarate by dissolving the residue in a minimum quantity of tartaric acid aqueous solution with continuous stirring. The strength of the Ti- tartarate aqueous solution is determined by assaying the aqueous solu- tion at 700•C for 3h. A similar process is used to prepare Nb-tartarate solution from Nb2O5, where the conversion of Nb2O5 to NbF 5 takes a larger time (F24h). To prepare a Bi(NO 3)3 solution from Bi2O3, dilute nitric acid solution is used as medium, into which the required amount of precursor is added under continuous stirring. The desired BBNT ma- terial is synthesized through the precursor solution decomposition method as schematically presented in Scheme 1. A suitable volume of Bi (NO 3)3, Ba(NO 3)2, Nb-tartarate, and Ti-tartarate solutions is mixed in a beaker according to the compositional ratio of 2:2:2:1. TEA is then added to the resultant solution and mixed well with continuous stirring to get a homogeneous solution. The amount of TEA is maintained as three times higher than the total number of metal ions used during the synthesis process. The pH of the mixed precursor solution is maintained at five and it is heated on a hot plate at a fixed temperature of 200•C. A complete dehydration of the precursor solution furnishes a black, fluffy carbonaceous mass, which is calcined at 700•C, 3h to finalize the desired BBNT material. The related mechanistic pathway about the chelation and formation of fluffy mass during precursor solution decomposition method is studied by Dhak et al [24]. 2.3. Characterizations The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of the investigated material were collected on a Rigaku SmartLab spectrometer using Cu Kα X-rays of wavelength 1.5406 Å. The analysis is performed within the scan range of 20o – 80o (2θ) at a scanning rate of 2o (2θ/min, θ Bragg ’s angle). The chemical bond formation in the BBNT material is studied using a Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) instrument from PerkinElmer (spectrum two, L1600300). The FTIR spectrum is recorded by forming a KBr pellet under transmission mode in the 4000 to 450 cm-1 wavenumber region. The Raman spectrum of the calcined material was studied through a Raman spectrometer of Horiba Evolution of Horiba Jobin Yvon, France. The Raman spectrum analysis is performed under an Argon ion laser at 514 nm. The surface chemical state of the materials is investigated through X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), which was guided by JPS-9030, JEOL, Tokyo, Japan, with a monochromatic Al Kα X-ray source. The surface morphology is analyzed using a field emission scanning electron microscopy (FESEM) instrument of JEOL JSM5800 under 20 kV at room temperature. The surface elemental mapping and compositional ratio of the investigated material are stud- ied through energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) and conducted by using an FESEM instrument. The surface area analysis of the material is conducted by Quantachrome Instruments, USA, where the materials are outgassed at 120•C for 5 h under vacuum and then placed for N2 sorption study at 77K, 1 bar. The BET model is used to measure the specific surface area. The electrical characterizations of the BBNT ma- terial are performed on the sintered pellet. For the sintered dense pellet formation, the as-synthesized BBNT powdered material is mixed well with 5 wt % polyvinyl alcohol and pressed by a hydraulic press, where M.K. Adak et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  80 (2026)  108309 2
