10 mm die punch is used. The obtained pellet is sintered at 900•C for 8 h in a muffle furnace. Both sides of the sintered pellet are then silver- coated (Aldrich, South Korea) and dried at 180•C, 2 h in an air oven to remove any moisture present on the flat surfaces. The silver-coated pellet is then used to measure dielectric and ferroelectric properties of the BBNT material using AixCCT techniques through AixPlorer software version 3.0.47.0. 2.4. Electrocatalytic investigation The electrocatalytic activity of the BBNT material for HER is inves - tigated using a CHI instrument (model 620E SN:I1214). A three- electrode setup is employed during the electrocatalytic study under 1.0 M KOH, where Hg/HgO, Pt rod, and BBNT-loaded nickel foam (NF) are used as the reference, counter, and working electrode, respectively. For the preparation of the working electrode, 1 ×2 cm2 NF was cut from the NF sheet (0.5 mm thickness) and washed with 0.1 M HCl, water, and acetone consequently to remove the impurity of the market available NF [25,26]. The washed NF is dried in an air oven at 50•C for 6h. For the preparation of catalyst ink, BBNT powder material (10 mg) is taken in a glass vial with the addition of 200 μL ethanol and 20 μL Nafion (5 wt %). The catalyst ink is then sonicated for 15 minutes to get a homogeneous solution. The prepared catalyst ink is then loaded on the 1 ×1 cm2 area of the NF and dried at room temperature (30 •C) for overnight. The Scheme 1.A schematic diagram of the synthesis process of BBNT material through the precursor solution decomposition method. Fig. 1.(a) PXRD, (b) FESEM image of sintered pellet, (c) FESEM image of calcined powder, (d) EDX mapping, and (e) EDX spectrum of the BBNT material.M.K. Adak et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  