distance between the Pt rod and the working electrode is maintained as 1 cm in a 50 mL 1.0 M KOH electrolyte solution. 3.Results and discussion The crystallographic structure and symmetry of the synthesized Aurivillius phase BBNT material are studied through PXRD, as given in Fig. 1a [22]. The detected diffractogram indicates the crystalline nature of the investigated material and follows the diffraction pattern of the parent Bi4Ti3O12 material, as per the JCPDS data file number 01-072-1019 (Joint Committee on Powder Diffraction Standards (JCPDS)) (Fig. S1) [27–29]. The three-layer structure of the BIT com- pounds [30] is modified both in the ‘A’ and ‘B’ sites by Ba and Nb atoms, and the resultant molecule is showcased in Scheme 2. In the present case, the A site of Bi3is replaced by Ba2and resides in the (Bi2O2)2 layer. Similarly, the two-layer position of Ti4is replaced by Nb5 atoms. However, the cation doping in the ‘A’ and ‘B’ sites is considered based on the preferred position rather than the layered position, and previous literature proved it [1,30]. The PXRD pattern identifies a single-phase orthorhombic structure with the B2ab (#41) space group. The associated lattice parameters are a b 5.4 Å, and c 32.8 Å, with 956.4 Å3 unit cell volume. Though the shifting of the diffraction peaks from the reported data indicates the distortion of the parent structure owing to the involvement of different foreign ions (Fig. S1). The pres- ence of Ba2at the A sites and NbO 6 octahedra at the B-sites leads to a higher degree of structural distortion. The highest intense peak is identified at the lower diffraction angle (2θ 29.2o) and corresponds to m 3 Aurivillius phases. This observation agrees with (1 1 2m1) reflection related to (117) peaks. Thomas et al. reported such a type of lattice distortion to the lower 2θ angle with increasing the x value of Bi2-xPbxLa2Ti3-xNbxO12 [23]. However, an unidentified impurity is observed at 2θ 27.7o, which may be due to the formation of some metal oxides or other phases, which is also reported by the previous articles [18]. The distortion of the BBNT material from the ideal phase is also investigated through the Goldschmidt tolerance factor (t) as given below [1] tRARO⎪⎪⎪ 2♠ RBRO(1) In Eq. 1, RA, RB, and RO indicate the ionic radius of A-site cation (Ba), B-site cation (Nb, Ti), and O anion, respectively. For an ideal perovskite structure, the tolerance factor equals 1, which indicates no rotation. The calculated tolerance factor for the three-layered BBNT material is 1.07, considering RBa 1.61 Å, RNb 0.64 Å, RTi 0.61 Å, and RO 1.26 Å [31]. For the same type of material, this tolerance factor for Bi2Sr(A) TiNb 2O12 is reported as 0.962, 0.977, and 0.993 for A Ca2, Sr2, and Ba2, respectively [1]. So, the presence of Ba2at the A site leads to the strong displacement, which is reflected in the PXRD patterns shifting to a lower 2θ value. The average crystallite size of the synthesized material is calculated using Scherrer ’s formula [32], which is 34 nm. The surface morphology of the BBNT sintered pellet is studied using FESEM, as given in Fig. 1b (Fig. S2). The observed surface implies a well-packed grain with plate-like morphological microstructure, a typical characteristic of bismuth layered Aurivillius ceramics owing to the high grain growth in the vertical direction along the c-axis [15,33]. The average grain size distribution is in the range of 200-300 nm, as calculated using ImageJ software (Fig. S3). The large grain size of the sintered plate indicates the grain growth process during the long-time high-temperature treatment [34]. The surface structure of the calcined powder BBNT material is also investigated through FESEM study. It shows an irregular agglomerated plate-like shape within the average grain size range of 120 (±5) nm (Fig. 1c). The presence of a bigger grain size in the sintered pellet indicates the occurrence of several kinetic movements during sintering at higher temperatures for a long time duration across the boundary [3,35]. The presence of elements in the material and their composition are ascertained through the EDX anal- ysis. Fig. 1d-i shows the EDX elemental mapping of Ba, Bi, Nb, Ti, and O, which indicates uniform dispersion and high solubility of all elements in the synthesized BBNT material. The related energy spectrum is given in Fig. 1j, where the presence of constituent elements is observed at the related energy values. The EDX mapping and spectrum-related atomic and weight percentages of sintered BBNT material are given in Fig.s S4 - S5, corresponding to the synthesized material composition. The FTIR spectrum of as-synthesized BBNT material is shown in Fig. 2a. The band positions between 3556 to 3410 cm-1 correspond to the -OH stretching, and 1619 cm-1 suggests the -OH bending vibrations [26,36]. The presence of -OH in the BBNT material may be due to surface-adsorbed moisture or moisture in the sample compartment. The bands near 1100 – 1200 cm-1 correspond to the O-O stretching frequency [37]. Two distinct modes are noticed at the lower wavenumber region at 866 and 612 cm-1, corresponding to the symmetric and asymmetric B-O bonds, respectively [33]. Previous reports indicate these bands are associated to the Nb-O bond in the NbO 6 octahedra [38]. The room temperature Raman spectrum of the BBNT material from 100 to 1000 cm-1 spectral region is shown in Fig. 2b. Strong, intense peaks are observed at 152.5, 235.8, 546.7, and 883.0 cm-1, besides some weak Scheme 2.Ball-and-stick presentation of (a) BIT [30], and (b) BBNT viewed along ‘a’ direction.M.K. Adak et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  80 (2026)  108309 4
