peaks at 292.0, 465.0, and 670.6 cm-1. The Raman mode at 152.5 cm-1 indicates Nb z-axis vibrations, whereas 235.8 cm-1 corresponds to Bi3 or A-site vibration [39]. The symmetric stretching of the NbO 6 octahe - dron is reflected in the Raman mode of 883.0 cm-1, which is also related to the orthorhombic phase as discussed in the PXRD pattern [3,39]. The phonon vibration of Ba-O bonds appeared at 546.7 cm-1, where phonon modes for Ti-O bonds appeared by 292.0 cm-1 [40]. The band position at 670.6 cm-1 indicates the deformation of the BO4 tetrahedron [41]. The appearance of broadband positions may be due to the phonon confine - ment and the nanocrystallinity nature of the BBNT material [41,42]. The N2 sorption and pore size distribution of the BBNT material are given in Fig. 2c and Fig. 2d, respectively. The textural characteristics of BBNT were investigated by the N2 sorption isotherm at -196 •C as given in Fig. 2c. The N2 sorption isotherm of the as-synthesized BBNT calcined powder material follows a low gas uptake in the lower pressure region. The specific surface area of the BBNT material is 12 m2/g, calculated using the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method, where mesopores (2.5 and 5.1 nm) are identified in the crystalline material. The pore size distribution is plotted in Fig. 2d (Fig. S6). The total pore volume of 1.4 × 10-2 cc/g was estimated at a P/P 0 0.99. The chemical environment of Ba, Bi, Nb, Ta, and O elements of the BBNT material is investigated through an XPS study (Fig. 3, Fig. S7). The XP survey spectrum is shown in Fig. S7, which confirms the pres- ence of all the constituent elements and matches the previous reports [3, 43]. The deconvoluted high-resolution spectrum of Ba 3d is given in Fig. 3a, where the spin-orbit doublet of Ba 3d is observed at the binding energy of 780.1 eV for Ba 3d5/2 and 795.4 eV for 3d3/2, respectively [3]. The Bi 4f XP spectrum (Fig. 3b) shows two strong deconvoluted peaks at the binding energy of 158.3 (Bi4f 7/2) and 163.6 eV (Bi4f 5/2), with a spin-orbit separation of 5.3 eV. According to the previous literature, these peaks correspond to the Bi3[44]. Simultaneously, the deconvo - luted spectrum indicates two less prominent peaks at the binding en- ergies of 160.7 and 166.1 eV, owing to the surface charging induced by the polarization changes within the crystal [45]. Fig. 3c shows the deconvoluted high-resolution of the Nb 3d XP spectrum. The four identified peaks are observed at the binding energies of 203.2, 204.9, 206.8, and 209.4 eV. The value of binding energy at 206.8 and 209.4 eV is related to the 3d5/2 and 3d3/2, respectively, and suggests the presence of Nb5-O, owing to surface oxidation [46,47]. The other two peaks at the lower binding energy correspond to the lower oxidation state of Nb [48]. The high-resolution XP spectrum of Ti 2p is given in Fig. 3d, where spin-orbit doublets are found at the binding energy of 458.2 (Ti2p 3/2) and 463.8 eV (Ti2p 1/2) [49,50]. The high-resolution XP spectrum of O1s shows two deconvoluted peaks at 529.9 and 531.0 eV, as shown in Fig. 3e, where the first one is related to lattice oxygen, and the second one corresponds to adsorbed hydroxyl oxygen on the BBNT surface [51]. Dielectric and ferroelectric study . The sintered silver-coated pellet (thickness 2.15 mm, diameter 10 mm) of BBNT material is used to analyze its dielectric and ferroelectric properties with the variation of frequency and temperature. The investigation reflects the temperature stability and dipole polarization characteristics of the dielectric ce- ramics [2,52]. The dielectric constant and dielectric loss of the BBNT material at the frequency region of 100 Hz, 1 kHz, and 10 kHz within the temperature region of 30•C to 600 •C are given in Fig. 4a and b, respectively. The dielectric constant of the material gradually increases with rising temperature and becomes prominent after 450•C for 100 Hz, which becomes less noticeable at higher frequencies. The higher dielectric nature is noticed at higher temperatures for lower frequencies due to enough response time through channel direction, which becomes less for higher frequencies [53]. The inertness of dielectric nature at higher frequencies may be related to the relaxation of dipoles and steady polarization deficiency [54]. The room temperature dielectric constant at 100 Hz, 1 kHz, and 10 kHz was determined as 80, 75.5, and 74 with tangent loss of 0.03, 0.06, and 0.07, respectively. The dielectric loss also follows the same pattern of dielectric constant, i.e., loss tangent grad- ually increases with rising temperature and shows a prominent nature at lower frequencies. The same type of dielectric characteristics is observed in previously investigated dielectric materials [1,7,55,56]. The dielec - tric constant of the BBNT material at 600 •C for 100 Hz is 15200, in contrast to 79 at 10 kHz. A similar type of frequency-dependent Fig. 2.(a) FTIR spectrum, (b) Raman spectrum, (c) N2 sorption isotherm, and (d) pore size distribution of BBNT material.M.K. Adak et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  80 (2026)  108309 5
