dielectric loss is noted at 600 •C as 8 for 100 Hz, which becomes 0.36 at 10 kHz (Table S1). The dramatic enhancement of dielectric parameters at higher temperatures is due to the increase in both ionic and electronic conductivities, which is a usual feature of the Aurivillius material [57]. The dielectric constant value does not show any distinguishable tem- perature maxima peaks up to 600 •C, indicating ferroelectric to para- electric transition peak is present at higher temperatures or less than room temperature. Sahu et al. reported Sr0.7Bi0.2TiO3, that does not show any temperature maxima, but the presence of a diffused phase transition within the broad temperature region suggests the existence of nano polar regions (PNRs) [58]. The observed characteristics indicate the relaxor ferroelectric characteristics, where the presence of PNRs corresponds to the chemical inhomogeneity or charge imbalance in the crystal structure. The parent BIT exhibits temperature maxima at 675 •C [59], which also supports the current BBNT material. Again, the higher dielectric constant value could mask the transition temperature [1]. Bi4Ti2Nb0.5Fe0.5O12 exhibited a dielectric maximum above 600 •C, which becomes shifted to a lower temperature with the substitution of Mn in the site of Ti [60]. In another study, Bi4Ti2.75(FeNb) 0.125O12 shows temperature maxima at 660•C with normal ferroelectric characteristics [33]. Sutra et al. exhibited the lower temperature dielectric maxima as -60 •C, -136 •C, and -69 •C for A Ca2, Sr2, Ba2substitution in Bi2Sr (A)TiNb 2O12, respectively [1]. The impedance spectroscopy of the BBNT material is measured to investigate its electrical characteristics, and related plots are shown in Fig. 4c-4f. The Nyquist plot of BBNT material for a wide temperature range from 500•C to 600•C is shown in Fig. 4c. The single semicircular arc is identified in all cases related to grain conductivity in the sintered pellet [61]. The radius of the arc gradually reduces with increasing temperature, owing to the reduction of the material ’s resistance and activation of more charge carriers with rising temperature [2,62]. Such types of reduction in the intercept of the arc are known as the NTCR (negative temperature coefficient resistance) behavior of the material [63,64]. The centre of the semicircles is present below the x-axis, which designates non-Debye type relaxation in the investigated BBNT material owing to the presence of different types of relaxation processes which generated during frequency and temperature variation [65]. Although, previously reported study suggests that in the single semicircular arc, the small and large intercept at the high and low frequency regions implies the grain and grain boundary conductivity, respectively [66]. The plots of real impedance (Z’) and imaginary impedance (-Z’’) vs. frequency in the function of 500 •C to 600 •C temperature are shown in Fig. 4d and e, respectively. The plot of real impedance vs frequency indicates a sharp decrease in Z’ value with increasing frequency, but Fig. 3.XP spectra of (a) Ba 3d, (b) Bi4f, (c) Nb 3d, (d) Ti 2p, and (e) O 1s spectra of the BBNT material.M.K. Adak et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  80 (2026)  108309 6
