Fig. 5b indicates that the Ps/Pr value is boosted to an optimal limit of 0.7 kV and then decreases at higher applied electrical fields. In Fig. 5c, the P- E hysteresis loop at different temperatures from 25 •C to 200•C under 5Hz frequency is plotted. The enhancement of temperature leads to narrow characteristics of the P-E hysteresis loop, indicating a reduction in the energy barrier for domain reorientation [73]. The transformation to slim hysteresis corresponds to the relaxor nature of the material, where microdomains of the material are translated into nanodomains [74]. The rise in temperature leads to large lattice vibration that reduces the charge separation. The temperature variable P-E loop indicates the maximum polarization, and remanent polarization gradually decreases and becomes saturated at higher temperature regions (Fig. 5d). The related Ps/Pr value follows an ‘S’ type pattern, where the value is minimized at 50 •C and gradually increases and reaches a saturation value of 8.5 at 180 •C. The P-E hysteresis loop with the variation of frequencies from 0.1 Hz to 20 Hz under a 0.5 kV applied electric field at room temperature is given in Fig. 5e. The slim shapes of the hysteresis loop are observed at lower frequencies, which become broader in nature at higher frequencies. The slim hysteresis loop is related to the better sensitivity towards external electric fields and is reasonable for higher energy storage performance [75,76]. The obtained open saturated loop at low frequency indicates back switching of domains after saturation, which is disturbed at higher frequency, possibly owing to leakage through mobile defects [77]. The corresponding maximum polarization and remanent polarization values vary according to Fig. 5f, where the maximum Ps/Pr value (~20) is noticed at a lower frequency (0.1 Hz) that becomes saturated after 5 Hz frequency. Such type of electric field, temperature, and frequency variation is a crucial factor for application in sensor and actuator devices [77]. The fatigue characteristics of the BBNT material are analysed to understand the substantial reduction during switchable polarization. Fig. 5g shows the fatigue characteristics of the material studied under 500 V applied field, 5 Hz frequency for 106 times repeated number of polarization cycles. The fatigue of ferroelec - tric material is related to the charges and diffusion defects that prohibit the nucleation of domains during charge-discharge activity [78]. The plot shows a negligible change after 106 writing/erasing cycles at 5 Hz. The quiet fatigue-free nature of the BBNT material is due to the lower diffusive nature of charge defects among different crystal cells of the bismuth layered structure [79]. The log current density vs. voltage curve is shown in Fig. S8. The leakage current density shows a low magnitude of 10-12 order within the measured voltage range of 100 V to -100 V at room temperature, which suggests very low leakage for the BBNT ma- terial [3]. The energy storage capability of the BBNT material is calculated from the observed hysteresis curve at different electric fields, tempera - tures, and frequency regions. The recoverable energy density (Urec), energy loss (Uloss) and efficiency (η) are derived from the following equations [80,81] Urec̃Ps PrEdP (5) Uloss̃Ps PrEdP (6) ηUrec UrecUloss×100% (7) Here, E indicates the applied electric field. So, the large difference between Ps and Pr is desirable to improve the energy storage perfor - mance of the investigated material. Fig. 6a represents the P-E loop of BBNT material, where charging, discharging process, recoverable en- ergy density, and energy loss are directed through color regions. The variation of Urec and Uloss with the applied electric field is presented in Fig. 6b, which indicates the enrichment of both parameters with increasing electric field. However, a maximum efficiency of 55.2 % is recorded at 400 V/cm, which gradually decreases at higher electric fields. In case of temperature variation as shown in Fig. 6c, the Urec becomes prominent at a higher temperature than Uloss, but does not show much distinction in characteristics between them. Interestingly, the energy efficiency is boosted gradually from 50•C (η 40.7 %) and reaches a maximum point at 150•C (η 77.4 %), after that a constant efficiency is noted. The decline in nature of Urec as well as Ps with increasing temperature from 25•C to 70•C suggests the increment of polar nanoregions that inhibit domain orientation, which becomes Fig. 6.(a) Schematic presentation about the charging, discharging, Urec, and Uloss in a P-E hysteresis plot; The variation of Urec, Uloss, and efficiency in terms of (b) electric field, (c) temperature, and (d) frequency for BBNT material.M.K. Adak et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                Surfaces  and Interfaces  80 (2026)  108309 9
