almost constant at higher temperatures [82]. The high differentiation is noticed during frequency variation as given in Fig. 6d. A high Urec (0.026 J/cm3) is recorded at 0.1 Hz frequency with a lower Uloss (0.003 J/cm3) that leads to 89.7 % efficiency for the BBNT material. The decrement of energy efficiency at higher frequency owing to sluggish dielectric response with the frequency variation [19]. In case of polar - ization study, the presence of structural distortion or oxygen vacancy leads to the formation of non-centrosymmetric and polarized defects, which influence polarization enhancement in the investigated material [83,84]. Electrocatalytic HER activity . The ferroelectric BBNT material is also explored for its activity in the electrocatalytic water splitting under alkaline conditions (Fig. 7a). Such an investigation is essential for the green hydrogen generation as a clean energy source to reduce energy demand. In the PXRD study, peak shifting is observed, which indicates the generation of microstrain in the crystal structure. Such a type of strain causes the transfer of the d-band centre of the transition metal related to the Fermi level [85]. This phenomenon optimizes the binding energy of intermediates during the electrocatalytic reaction. Again, the presence of oxygen vacancies regulates the unsaturated coordination sites of the BBNT material and acts as new active sites that accelerate the electrocatalytic activity [86]. In a one-cell three-electrode setup, the cyclic voltammetry is oper- ated to activate the investigated BBNT material within the potential region of 0 to -1.5 V at 5 mV/s. The linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curve is shown in Fig. 7b, exhibiting higher activity of the BBNT ma- terial than the NF substrate. The measured overpotential for BBNT material is 172 mV at 10 mA cm-2 where NF shows a higher value of 252 mV. The ferroelectric polarization of the BBNT material significantly influences its electrocatalytic activity. In the presence of an electric field, the internal electric field of ferroelectric material creates banding at the BBNT/electrolyte interface that promotes the separation of charged species. Such types of charge separation improve the HER activity of the investigated material [87]. The kinetic study of the BBNT material is analyzed through the Tafel equation, as given below [88]. ηblogja (8) In the above equation, η and j is the overpotential and current den- sity, respectively. The plot of logj vs. overpotential is given in Fig. 7c, which indicates 109 mV dec-1 Tafel slope, which shows faster kinetics than the substrate. The Tafel slope value indicates that the HER kinetics for BBNT materials follow the Volmer-Heyrovsky pathway [89,90]. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) of the BBNT material shows a single semicircular nature, with a charge transfer resistance of 3.2 Ω (Fig. S9). The electrochemically active surface area (ECSA) of the BBNT materials is studied through CV scan (10, 25, 50, 100, 150, and 200 mV/s) at the potential region of 0.05 mV to -0.05 mV (Fig. 7d). The double-layer charge transfer resistance (Cdl) is calculated to be 1.01 mF cm-2 from the linear plot between scan rate and current density, as shown in Fig. 7e. The stability of the BBNT ceramic material is inves - tigated through a chronoamperometry study at the applied potential of -0.18 V (vs. RHE) (Fig. 7f). The obtained current density displays a constant value of -10 mA cm-2 up to 12h time duration, which suggests the stable nature of the material. 4.Conclusion The three-layer ferroelectric Ba2Bi2Nb2TiO12 (BBNT) Aurivillius material is synthesized through the precursor solution decomposition method. The PXRD study reveals a shift in the diffraction pattern to- wards lower angles compared to the parent material, indicating distor - tion in the crystal structure. The BBNT material exhibits a well-packed, plate-like morphology. The XPS analysis confirms the presence of all constituent elements in their respective valence oxidation state. In the dielectric investigation, a higher dielectric constant is observed for low frequencies and at higher temperatures. The dielectric constant value does not show any distinguishable temperature maxima peaks up to 600 •C. The Nyquist plot within the temperature region of 500•C to 600•C indicates a single semicircular arc, corresponding to the grain conduc - tivity nature. The unsaturated P-E hysteresis loop is influenced by the application of external electric field, temperature, and frequency. The Ps/Pr ratio finds volcano trends for electric field variation, where it follows ‘S’ characteristics trend in temperature variation. However, the slim and saturated nature of the P-E hysteresis loop with the highest Ps/ Pr (~20) is observed at a 0.1 Hz frequency under room temperature conditions, suggesting the highest energy storage characteristics in the material. A high Urec of 0.026 J/cm3 with 89.7 % efficiency is recorded at 0.1 Hz frequency condition. The electrocatalytic activity of BBNT materials signifies their activity for HER under alkaline conditions. A 172 mV overpotential is observed at 10 mA cm-2, indicating the mate- rial’s electrocatalytic nature under alkaline conditions for generating green hydrogen fuel. The chronoamperometry study suggests the stable catalytic nature of the BBNT material with constant current supply at the applied potential of -0.18 V (vs. RHE). The investigation reports, for the first time, a three-layered BBNT material and its dielectric, ferroelectric, Fig. 7.Electrocatalytic HER performance of BBNT material (a) three-electrode set up, (b) LSV plot (0 to -1.5 V, 1 mV/s), (c) Tafel plot, (d) CV cycles for ECSA (0.05 V to -0.05 V), (e) Cdl plot, and (f) 12h chronoamperometry study.M.K. Adak et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                