rsc.li/materials-aAs featured in: See Vipin Adavan Kiliyankil, Wei Mao, Bunshi Fugetsu et al., J. Mater. Chem. A , 2024, 12, 28731. Showcasing research from Professor Sakata-Fugetsu’s laboratory, Institute for Future Initiatives, The University of Tokyo, Tokyo, Japan. Edge sites on platinum electrocatalysts are responsible for discharge in the hydrogen evolution reaction The edges and steps of platinum electrocatalysts get oxidized and act as atomic barriers where electrons gather within the metallic terrace (111, 110, and 100) domains. These electrons are then released to hydronium ions in the electrical double layer through the oxidized sites, leading to the generation of hydrogen atoms. Edge-rich platinum electrocatalysts have been developed using single-walled carbon nanotube-based nano-fabrics as templates and supports. Their beneﬁ  cial electrocatalytic properties have been demonstrated through the processing of hydrogen evolution reactions at high current densities. Registered charity number: 207890
