1 From nano to macro: Hierarchical Platinum Superstru ctures synthesized using 1 bicontinuous microemulsion for hydrogen evolution r eaction 2 3 Elijah T. Adesuji 1,2 , Laila Khalil 3, Marcelo Videa 2,* , Margarita Sánchez-Domínguez 1,*  4 1Centro de Investigación en Materiales Avanzados, S.  C. (CIMAV), Unidad Monterrey, Alianza 5 Norte 202, Parque de Investigación e Innovación Tec nológica, Apodaca 66628, N.L. México 6 2Departament of Chemistry and Nanotechnology. School  of Engineering and Sciences, 7 Tecnologico de Monterrey, Ave. E. Garza Sada 2501, Monterrey 64849, N.L., México 8 3Smart Hybrid Materials Laboratory, Advanced Membran es and Porous Materials Center, King 9 Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUS T), Thuwal, 23955-6900 Saudi Arabia 10 11 Abstract 12 The possibility to harness the intrinsic properties  of bicontinuous microemulsions (BCME) has 13 led to their application as nanocages in the synthe sis of nanocoral-shaped Pt hierarchical 14 superstructures (PtHSs). We employed two different platinum precursors (oil-soluble and water- 15 soluble) and sodium borohydride (NaBH 4) was used as reducing agent. PtHSs were observed t o 16 be pure and nanocrystalline, with small crystallite  size (4.5-6.5 nm). XPS showed that the 17 nanostructures are composed of Pt (0). The electroc hemical surface areas obtained were 8.7 18 m2/g, 35.1 m2/g and 20.9 m2/g for PtH-O (synthesized using oil soluble precurs or), PtH-A2 and 19 PtH-A4 (synthesized using 2 and 4% water soluble Pt  precursor), respectively. Linear sweep 20 voltammetry (LSV), Tafel plots and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) were used to 21 study the hydrogen evolution reaction process. PtH- A2 showed an overpotential of 68 mV at 10 22 mA cm -2, a Tafel slope of 30 mV dec -1 and a low charge transfer resistance of 3.39 Ω. The 23 stability of the PtHSs was tested before and after 1000 LSV cycles with minimal loss of current 24 density. The improved electrochemical properties re corded, are due to the unique Pt hierarchical 25 superstructure, which is key for the electrocatalyt ic performance. Through this research, the 26 “template effect” of bicontinuous microemulsions to wards the synthesis of metallic hierarchical 27 superstructures is demonstrated for the first time.  28
