2 Keywords : bicontinuous microemulsion, nanoreactor, Pt hiera rchical superstructure, HER, 1 electrocatalyst 2 3 *corresponding authors: margarita.sanchez@cimav.edu .mx , mvidea@itesm.mx  4 5 1. Introduction 6 Hierarchical superstructures (HSs) are made up of n anoscale building blocks (nanocrystals, 7 nanoparticles) that self-assemble to extend in more  than one dimension, which results from the 8 successful manipulation of materials from nano to m eso and all the way up to the macroscale. 9 They are characterized by the arrangement of the bu ilding blocks in a consistent fashion as to 10 give a unique architectural and reproducible design . HSs exhibit unique and collective physical 11 and chemical properties different from those of the  monomorphological structures, for example 12 highly effective transfer of both mass and charge, thanks to the long ways present in their 13 interconnected nanostructure [1, 2]. One of the mos t important advantages of hierarchical 14 superstructures is their high surface area, which i s important in electrocatalysis and other 15 applications [3]. A range of hierarchical superstru ctures made of various materials, including 16 metals, semiconductors, heterogeneous composites, h ydroxides and organic materials have been 17 successfully prepared [1, 4-6]. However, new method s for the synthesis of metal nanostructures 18 of different architectures are necessary. These met hods are aimed at providing the reproducibility 19 and control over properties such as the composition , size, shape and morphology, surface 20 species, stability, and isolability, which are dete rminant for advanced applications of 21 nanostructures [7].  22 Platinum, a precious transition metal with the elec tronic configuration of [Xe]4 f14 5d96s1, is 23 considered to exhibit effective catalytic activity in alcohol oxidation reaction[8, 9], fuel cells [10 , 24 11], petroleum cracking [12], and electron transfer  reactions [5]. Platinum metals or bimetallic 25 alloys with nanoarchitectural morphologies exhibit exceptional and unique properties, such as 26 affinity for hydrogen, oxygen and various other mol ecules [13]. This extends their applications 27 to heterogeneous catalysis and electrocatalysis [14 ], and in particular fuel cells [15, 16]. 28 Electrochemical processes such as hydrogen evolutio n reaction (HER) has been widely reported 29 and the use of Pt or Pt alloy nanomaterials as elec trocatalysts for electron transfer and rapid 30
============================================================
[Page 6/42]
============================================================
3 kinetics has been  well investigated [17]. The use of Pt and Pt-alloyed nanomaterials facilitate 1 fast reaction rate in acidic media, possessing low Tafel slope, minimum onset overpotential and 2 optimal hydrogen binding energy which helps to over come the high energy barrier and 3 kinetically sluggish reaction which is typical of H ER [17, 18].  4 Fabrication of Pt and Pt-based nanostructures are c onducted to improve catalytic performance 5 and reduce the usage of Pt. Nanoporous platinum str uctures [3, 19], two- and three-dimensional 6 platinum nanodendrites [20], Pt-Ru mesostructures [ 21], hierarchical porous platinum 7 nanoflowers and assembly of platinum nanochains [8] ,  have been reported as highly active. 8 Thus, emphasis on the development of soft, facile, self-assembly routes for the formation of Pt 9 HSs is paramount. Among reported methods are therma l vapor transport and condensation 10 technique [22], liquid-liquid transfer method [23],  chemical corrosion and electroless deposition 11 [3], solvothermal method [24], and galvanic replace ment reaction [25]. However, these methods 12 present high-energy requirements, non-facile synthe tic paths, and are inadequate for scale up. 13 The fabrication of metal nanostructures using one o f these methods over a large area with high 14 uniformity remains a major issue.  15 The use of microemulsion (ME) systems is an efficie nt alternative in the synthesis of 16 nanostructures. ME have been applied as confined re action media, and soft template, for the 17 chemical synthesis of small nanoparticles, nanowire s and nanorods  [26-31]. ME are 18 thermodynamically stable colloidal dispersions in w hich two immiscible liquids (typically water 19 and oil) coexist due to the presence of a monolayer  of surfactant molecules with balanced 20 hydrophilic–lipophilic properties [32]. These collo idal systems are optically isotropic, with a 21 very small characteristic size (2–50 nm). ME can ex ist as oil-swollen direct micelles dispersed in 22 water (o/w ME), water-swollen inverse micelles disp ersed in oil (w/o ME) or with both aqueous 23 and oily continuous domains as interconnected spong e-like channels (BCME), depending on the 24 oil/water ratio and the hydrophilic – lipophilic ba lance (HLB) of the surfactant. The surfactant- 25 stabilized nanocavities provide a cage-like effect that limits particle nucleation, growth and 26 agglomeration [33].  27 The controlled synthesis of Pt nanoparticles [34] a nd flower-like hierarchical nanostructures of 28 crystalline ZnO and ZnO 2 nanoparticles [35], were reported in o/w ME media;  whereas the 29 synthesis of iron oxide NPs [36], zinc oxide NPs [2 2], silver NPs [37, 38], Pt, Au and Fe NPs 30
