5 a final mixture containing 1 wt % of Pt in the oil phase. The BCME was then formed using 27.36 1 % Synperonic TM 91/5, 20.64 % Milli-Q water and 52 % Isooctane (wit h 1 wt % Pt). The mixture 2 was subjected to vortex agitation for few seconds, resulting in a transparent, fluid, optically 3 isotropic liquid phase. Characterization of the mic roemulsion by electrical conductivity as a 4 function of temperature is discussed in Supporting Information (Fig. 1S). The reaction vial was 5 maintained at 22 oC. Thereafter, 1 mL of sodium borohydride solution with an appropriate 6 concentration was prepared. 100 µL of the reducing agent was withdrawn (corresponding to a 7 2:1 NaBH 4: Pt stoichiometry) and slowly added to the ME in a  period of two seconds, and then 8 vortexed.  The final mixture was kept at 22 oC without mechanical agitation for 48 hours (Fig. 9 1A and B). The platinum hierarchical nanostructure (PtHSs) formed was denoted as PtH-O. The 10 same procedure was followed using Chloroplatinic ac id hexahydrate as Pt precursor instead of 11 Pt-COD. The platinum hydrophilic salt was dissolved  in Milli-Q water. Final concentrations of 2 12 and 4 wt % of Pt in the aqueous phase of the BCME c ontaining 20.64 % aqueous phase, 27.36 % 13 Synperonic TM 91/5, and 52 % Isooctane were prepared. PtHSs synth esized using 2 and 4 % of the 14 water-soluble Pt precursor are denoted as PtH-A2 an d PtH-A4 respectively.  15 16 17 Fig. 1A: Schematic illustration for the synthesis o f Platinum hierarchical nanostructure using 18 water-soluble platinum precursor. 19 2.3. Recovery and isolation of PtHSs 20 The microemulsion fraction containing the PtHSs was  dissolved in isopropanol, while the other 21 part free of PtHSs by visual inspection, was decant ed and discarded. The precipitate was then 22 washed with Milli-Q water and Isopropanol six times . An acidic solution containing HNO 3: H 2O 23 at a 50:50 weight ratio was used to wash the obtain ed product. Additional washing was done