8 morphology of the formed structures in this work is  in conformity with the inherent nature of 1 bicontinuous microemulsion in which both aqueous an d oily continuous domains exist as 2 interconnected sponge-like channels [28]. Scheme 1 and 2 illustrate that our reaction method was 3 able to direct the nucleation, growth and self-asse mbly sequence of the materials. Also, because 4 of the nanoconfined reaction media in which microem ulsion is known for [43], the growth of the 5 nanostructures was tailored to conform to the nanoc hannels of the reaction media. In as much as 6 the metal precursor is fully dissolved in the appro priate phase, reduction to the zerovalent state is 7 facilitated by contact due to reactant diffusion wi thin the channels [43].  8 Furthermore, the purity and elemental constituents of PtH-O, PtH-A2 and PtH-A4 was confirmed 9 by energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX). Iden tification as well as quantification of 10 elements in nanomaterials is one of the main uses o f EDX technique [44]. The EDX spectra 11 displayed the Pt content in the nanomaterials as ra nging from 87-100 % (Fig. 3S). The absence 12 of other elements showed that our synthesized PtHSs  is without impurities nor residual surfactant 13 from the synthesis procedure, aside the elemental c arbon from the carbon tape used in the EDX 14 measurement. 15 16 17 3.2. TEM Analysis 18 The TEM images provide deeper insight into the PtHS s nanomaterials nature, morphology, 19 shape, size and d-spacing [45]. The latter is usually determined fro m high-resolution TEM 20 (HRTEM) image.  The hierarchical and interconnected  arrangement of nanospherical materials 21 making up PtH-O, as well as the nanoneedle-like nat ure of both PtH-A2 and PtH-A4 were 22 confirmed using the TEM micrographs (Fig. 3A, C and  E). A high-resolution TEM image of the 23 consistent nanomaterials were recorded (Fig. 3B, D and F) and well-defined fringes were 24 observed. The HRTEM micrographs indicated that PtHS 