16 Nyquist EIS spectra were fitted by ZSimpWin 3.60 so ftware. The experimental data (different 1 geometry) and the fitted data (solid curve) are clo sely correlated with a small deviation at low 2 frequencies. This suggested to be a result of compl ex mass transformation [41] due to kinetics of 3 HER process [76] accompanied by electron transfer p rocesses. The equivalent circuit is shown in 4 Fig 8A (inset) and the Nyquist plot had two differe nt semicircles. R s is the electrolyte resistance, 5 the arc at high frequencies is ascribed to the resi stance of the adsorption hydrogen atom (R ads ), 6 while the arc in the low frequencies region corresp onds to the charge-transfer resistance (R ct ) 7 [77], CPE (constant phase element) 1 is the capacitance at higher frequencies which is suggested 8 to be due to the hydrogen atom adsorption process, CPE 2 is the double layer capacitance, while 9 n1 and n 2 are normally between 0 and 1, which represent the deviation from the ideal 10 capacitance. In EIS, the phase angle can be represe nted by θ = (n–1) π/2; for a perfect resistor θ = 11 0 (n=1), for a perfect capacitor θ = –π/2 (n=0), and for a diffusion-like process θ = –π/4 (n=0.5)  12 [64, 78]. These values were measured for PtHSs (Tab le 3S). The CPE also helps to reduce the 13 deviation from the non-ideality of the electrocatal yst surface.  14 The electrochemical values recorded for PtH-A2 modi fied electrode which include R s, R ads , and 15 Rct  at an overpotential of -65 mV are 1.16, 3.65, and 3.39 Ω respectively. These values, when 16 compared with reported works (Table 2) showed that PtH-A2 is a better electrocatalyst. The 17 resistance values of the electrocatalysts (PtHSs) a t -65 mV are summarized in Fig. 8B, in which 18 the diameter of the semicircle at low frequencies o btained by simulated electrochemical fitting 19 revealed the charge transfer resistance R ct  of PtH-A4, PtH-A2 and PtH-O to be 3.00, 3.39 and 20 8.54 Ω, respectively. Smaller resistance values for R ct  implies higher electron transfer and faster 21 electrocatalytic reaction rate, and a good correlat ion is found between the values and the ECSA 22 values determined  [41, 65].  We can deduce that Pt H-A4 and PtH-A2 have faster electron 23 transfer capability than PtH-O. This also shows tha t the higher surface area (35.1 m 2/g) and the 24 lower Tafel slope (30 mV dec -1) of PtH-A2 are well supported by a rapid electron transfer rate. 25 In summary, PtH-A2 was found to be a better electro catalyst compared to the other two 26 nanomaterials tested in this work. Aside the Nyquis t plot obtained at the overpotential of -35 27 mV, two arcs are observed at all the overpotentials  (Fig. 5S-9S) which also confirms that the 28 equivalent circuit was characterized by two time co nstants (high and low-frequencies region) 29 [64, 76]. As shown in the Fig. 7S -9S, the semi-cir cle at the low frequencies region was observed 30 to decrease with increase in the applied potential,  while the high frequencies semi-circle is not 31
