18 on the surface of the Pt nanomaterials for the Hydr ogen adsorption/desorption. We suggest that 1 this interconnected hierarchical structural changes  greatly improved the surface area, electron 2 transport and consequently the catalytic ability of  our synthesized nanostructures. Similar 3 observation was reported by Zhang et al.  and Jiang et al.  [60, 81].    4 Secondly, determination of the size of PtHSs from t he FWHM using Scherrer equation, Table 5 1S, shows that PtH-O had the highest crystallite si ze than the others. We suggest that the size of 6 PtH-O might play a role in the lower ECSA value it exhibited. This was also observed in the 7 ECSA values of Pt nanoflowers prepared with diethan olamine as reported by Ooi and Aziz [57].  8 The effect of crystallite size is directly related to the catalytic ability. 9 3.6. Mechanism of formation 10 11 The mechanism of formation of the nanomaterials syn thesized in this work can be illustrated 12 using the Scheme 1 and 2 below. In principle, BCME offer the possibility to incorporate 13 precursors into either the water phase or the oil p hase. The chemical reaction between the 14 reducing agent (NaBH 4) and the platinum precursor dissolved in the oil ( Scheme 1) or water 15 (Scheme 2) channels of the microemulsion (which ser ve as a nanocage) forms Pt monomers 16 immediately upon mixing. The monomer concentration increases up to the point of spontaneous 17 nucleation (primary platinum particles), which occu rs over a critical supersaturation 18 concentration [C] C, followed by diffusion-controlled particle growth [82]. Surfactants stabilize 19 the particles formed and their morphologies are dir ected by the bicontinuous microemulsion 20 channels. There is an important difference between the method using lipophilic precursor versus 21 hydrophilic precursor: in the first case, the react ion must be initiated and propagated at the 22 oil/water interface, since the precursor is dissolv ed in the oil channels and the reducing agent is 23 dissolved in the water channels; whereas, in the la tter case, both reactants are dissolved in the 24 aqueous channels, resulting in higher mobility of t he ions and easier contact. This creates the 25 appropriate interactive medium between the metal io n and the reducing agent. Equation 2 26 represents the chemical reaction taking place. The morphological arrangement showed that the 27 structure is of nanocoral shape, when the reaction is carried out solely in the water channels. 28 In the case of the lipophilic platinum precursors, the precursor and reducing agent are in different 29 phase, the reduction reaction will not easily proce ed. This means there will be gradual alignment 30 and self-assembly of the platinum particles formed based on the rigidity of the BCME structure 31
