www.advancedsciencenews.com www.small-journal.com notonlyprovideanewdirectionfordirectlygrowingTM-doped TMD electrocatalysts on electrodes but also represents a highly promising approach to fabricating eﬃcient electrocatalysts for oxygen evolution and reduction, CO2reduction, and nitrogen reduction. 4.ExperimentalSection PreparationoftheTiOx/TiSubstrate :Tifoils(0.127mmthick,99.99% AlfaAesar)werewashedwithisopropanolandacetonetoremoveorganic residues on the surface and then partially oxidized in air at 500 °Cf o r 15min.TheoxidizedTifoilsweretakenoutandusedasgrowthsubstrates. Forfabricatingthe3Delectrocatalyst,Timesh(100-mesh,AnpingOfolan) wasused.TheTimeshwascleanedwithisopropanolandacetoneandthen oxidizedviahydrothermalmethodin0.15mNaOHat120 °Cfor2h.The obtained coarsely oxidized 3D Ti mesh was cleaned using ethanol, dried inanoven,andusedasagrowthsubstrate. Growth of TM-Doped TMD Electrocatalyst :Liquid precursors for the growth of various TMD materials (MoS2,W S2,W S e2,a n dR e S2) were prepared by respectively dissolving sodium molybdate dihydrate (Na2MoO4·2H2O,≥99.5%, Sigma Aldrich), sodium tungstate dihy- drate (Na2WO4·2H2O,≥99%, Sigma Aldrich), and sodium perrhenate (NaReO4, Sigma Aldrich, 380989) in aqueous solutions. Then, 0.05 m Na2MoO4,0.05mNa2WO4,and0.1mNaReO4wereusedtogrowMoS2, WS2(WSe2),andReS2,respectively.TogrowTM-dopedTMDs,vanadium (III) chloride (VCl3, Sigma Aldrich, 208272) and cobalt (II) chloride hex- ahydrate (CoCl2·6H2O, Sigma Aldrich, 769495) were added to precursor solutions. For V2.3%:Co6.7%-doped ReS2growth, the liquid precursor so- lution was prepared by mixing 0.1 m NaReO4, 0.05 m VCl3, and 0.05 m CoCl2·6H2O solutions at a volume ratio of 1:1:1. For V:Co-doped MoS2 growth, the liquid precursor solution was prepared by mixing 0.05 m NaMoO4, 0.05 m VCl3, and 0.05 m CoCl2·6H2O solutions at a volume ratio of 1:1:1. The ammonium sulﬁde solution (NH4)2S (40–48 wt% in H2O, Sigma Aldrich) and selenium pellets (Sigma Aldrich 209623) were used as S and Se sources, respectively.[34,35]The liquid precursor solu- tionswerespunonTiOx/Tifoilsat3000rpmfor1min,andtheresultant growth substrate was placed in the center of a quartz tube in a chemical vapor deposition (CVD) furnace. The furnace was heated to each growth temperature,namely,ReS2:650 °C,MoS2:700 °C,andWS2:750 °C,under anAratmosphereataﬂowrateof350sccm.ForgrowingeachTMDma- terial, (NH4)2Sa n dH2at ﬂow rates of 30 and 5 sccm, respectively, were introducedfor15min.Aftergrowth,thesupplyof(NH4)2Swasstopped, and the furnace was naturally cooled under Ar and H2atmosphere. The WSe2ﬁlm was grown using a two-zone CVD system, where the Se pellet and growth substrate were placed in each furnace.[36]Each furnace was heated to 400 °C and 750 °C under N2and H2atmosphere at ﬂow rates of600and15sccm. Growth of 3D TM-Doped TMD Electrocatalyst :The oxidized 3D Ti mesh was soaked in the liquid precursor solution used for preparing V2.3%:Co6.7%-dopedReS2andthendriedatroomtemperature(rt).Finally, thesameCVDprocedurewasusedtosynthesizeV2.3%:Co6.7%-ReS2(area: 1.5×2cm2)ontheoxidized3DTimesh. PreparationofPt/C :Forthesynthesisof3DPt/C@carboncloth,5mg Pt/C(20wt%ofPtonVulcanXC72Sigma-Aldrich)wasdispersedin900 μL ethanol containing 100 μL Naﬁon solution (5 wt% Thermo Fisher Scien- tiﬁc) to form a homogeneous ink. 200 μL of the resultant ink was coated onthecarboncloth(1 ×4cm)anddriedinairtoyieldPt/C@carboncloth electrode(0.25mgcm-2). Preparation of RuO2on Nickel Foam :Thepreparationof3DRuO2on nickel foam (NF) was done according to the previous report.[37]Ap i e c e of NF (1 cm ×4 cm) was cleaned in 3 m HCl and ethanol, then rinsed withwater.Subsequently,thecleanedNFwasimmersedin40mLofHCl solution and then transferred into a securely sealed 50 mL Teﬂon-lined stainless-steelautoclave.Thisautoclavewasmaintainedat180 °Cfor6h. Afternaturalcoolingtoroomtemperature,thesamplewascollected,sub- jected to multiple rinses with water and ethanol, and further dried underanAratmosphere.TheNi(OH)2/NFobtainedwasimmersedin20mLof 5×10−3mR u C l3.xH2O (Sigma Aldrich) aqueous solution overnight. Af- terward, the sample was collected and dried in a vacuum oven at 60 °C for 12 h to yield Ru(OH)3/Ni (OH)2/NF. Under an Ar atmosphere, the obtained sample was heated to 300 °C in a tube furnace and kept at that temperaturefor2hresultingintheformationofRuO2/NiO/NF. MaterialCharacterization :ThemorphologyanduniformityoftheTM- doped TMD ﬁlms were analyzed using a scanning electron microscope SEM(JSM-7100F,JEOL).Theopticalpropertiesofthesampleswereeval- uated by using micro-Raman spectroscopy with an excitation energy of 2.33eV(NTEGRASpectra,NT-MDT).X-raydiﬀractionwasconductedus- ing a Rigaku Smartlab X-ray diﬀractometer using Cu K 𝛼radiation (45 kV, 200mA)atrt.X-rayphotoemissionspectroscopy(ESCALAB250,Thermo Scientiﬁc)wasemployedtocharacterizetheelementalcompositionofthe as-grownV:Co-ReS2beforeandaftercatalyticmeasurements.Theatomic structures of TMD samples were analyzed by annular dark-ﬁeld scan- ning transmission electron microscopy (ADF-STEM, JEM-ARM 200CF, Jeol) at 80 keV after transfer onto a TEM grid (PELCO, 200 mesh, cop- per, 1.2 μm holes) using the viscoelastic polymer support layer trans- fermethod.[38]ProcessingofADF-STEMimaging,Wienerﬁltering,strain analysis,andEDXchemicalmappingwereperformedaccordingtothepre- viousreport.[32] ElectrochemicalMethod :Electrochemicalmeasurementswerecarried out in a three-electrode system using a ZIVE SP2 electrochemical work- station (ZIVE Lab, Korea). A graphite rod (935-00014, Gamry), saturated calomel electrode (SCE) (013693, ALS Japan) and as-prepared catalysts on TiOx/Ti (area: 2 ×2c m2) were used as the counter, reference, and workingelectrodes,respectively,foracidicandneutralmedium,whilethe reference electrode used in alkaline media was Hg/HgO (013694, ALS, Japan).Polarizationcurvemeasurementswereconductedin0.5mH2SO4 (pH ≅0),1.0mPBS(pH ≅7)(SigmaAldrich),and1.0mKOH(pH ≅14) solutionsatroomtemperature.Theelectrolyteswerede-aeratedbypurg- ing with N2for 30 min to eliminate dissolved oxygen prior to perform- ingtheelectrochemicalexperiment.Thecatalyticbehaviorwasconducted using linear sweep voltammetry (LSV), cyclic voltammetry (CV), electro- chemicalimpedancespectroscopy(EIS),chronoamperometry,andaccel- erated degradation tests. All polarization curves were measured in the rangeof0.0to −0.8V (vsRHE) ata scanrateof5mVs−1. Electrochem- icalimpedancespectroscopy(EIS)wasperformedinthefrequencyrange of 100 kHz to 0.1 Hz with an amplitude of 10 mV s−1and at a constant potential of −0.25 V (vs RHE). With the aid of software Zview, a simple Randles circuit was employed to ﬁt the EIS data. The potentials were ref- erencedwithareversiblehydrogenelectrode(RHE). ERHE=ESCE+0.0592×pH+E0 SCE(1) ERHE=EHg∕HgO+0.0592×pH+Ereal Hg∕HgO(1 M KOH)(2) whereERHEis the converted potential value versus RHE, ESCEand EHg/HgOare the potential readings from the potentiostat, and E0SCEand Ereal Hg∕HgO(1 m KOH)are the experimentally determined standard electrode potential of SCE and Hg/HgO electrode potential respectively.[39]resis- tancetestwasconductedpriortothemeasurements,andtheohmicdrop wascorrectedwith85% iRcompensation.Thereportedcurrentdensities werenormalizedtothegeometricalareaoftheelectrodeortheamountof catalystused. AEM Water Electrolyzer Assembly and Measurements :The anion ex- change membrane (AEM) water electrolyzer assembly was done by cou- plingRuO2onNF(anode,electrodesizeof4.9cm2),V:Co-ReS2onTiOx/Ti mesh(cathode,electrodesizeof4.8cm2),andananionexchangemem- brane (AEM, X37-50 Grade T, Dioxide Materials). The single cell was op- erated with an electrolyte of 1 m KOH at 50 °C at a rate of 48 mL min−1. TheAEMwaterelectrolyzermeasurementswereperformedusingaZIVE SP2electrochemicalworkstation(ZIVELab,Korea). Computational Methods :The spin-polarized DFT calculations were conducted using the Vienna ab initio simulation package.[40]The exchange-correlation interactions are treated by a generalized gradient approximationwithPerdewBurkeErnzerhof-typeparameterization.[41]The Small2024,20,2308672 ©2023Wiley-VCHGmbH 2308672(9of11) 16136829, 2024, 22, Downloaded from https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/smll.202308672 by Beijing University of Chemistry Technology, Wiley Online Library on [30/03/2026]. See the Terms and Conditions (https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/terms-and-conditions) on Wiley Online Library for rules of use; OA articles are governed by the applicable Creative Commons License
