Adsorbate-Mediated Deposition of Noble-Metal Nanoparticles on Carbon Substrates for Electrocatalysis Yiting Zheng, Ruichun Luo, Yuan Xu, Lei Zhang, Pan Liu, and Qing Chen * Cite This: https://dx.doi.org/10.1021/acsaem.0c00706 Read Online ACCESS Metrics & More Article Recommendations *sıSupporting Information ABSTRACT: Here, we demonstrate a general method of mediating the deposition of metal nanostructures on carbon substrates via reductive adsorbates. The adsorbates, quinone molecules, can achieve a well-de ﬁned surface coverage on a range of carbon substrates through one step of soaking. Upon electrochemical reduction, the adsorbates can function as surface-con ﬁned reductants to reduce metal cations for uniform anchorage of metal nanoparticles on carbon, which is not easily achievable via other electrochemicaldeposition. We apply the method to deposition of Ag and Pt and show the close correspondence between the coverage of theadsorbate and the amount of deposition as well as the dependence of the deposit structures on the quinone molecular structures. Wefurther demonstrate the stability and the catalytic activity of the deposited Pt for catalyzing the hydrogen evolution reaction. KEYWORDS: quinone molecules, adsorbate-mediated deposition, thin-layer deposition, hydrogen evolution reaction, Pt nanoparticles, ultralow Pt loading ■INTRODUCTION Among the ways of building metal nanostructures, under- potential deposition (UPD) paves a unique path toward the deposition of a precise amount of metal atoms on another metal substrate.1,2It seeds the deposition with a monolayer of sacriﬁcial metal atoms, adsorbed at the potential not too low to trigger its bulk deposition.3,4When in contact with cations of more noble metals, the monolayer serves as a reductant in the galvanic replacement for the more noble metals underinterface-controlled kinetics, leading to a conformal morphol- ogy and an often exotic deposit/substrate interface that are diﬃcult to achieve via electrochemical, salt impregnation, and solvothermal means.5−8The process is analogous to atomic layer deposition (ALD), but instead of vacuum chambers or sophisticated machinery, it requires only routine setups in an electrochemistry lab, whose convenience and low cost aremajor advantages.9,10 One critical task in electrocatalysis that UPD cannot accomplish is the deposition of metal on carbon. Most electrocatalysts do not exist in monoliths. They are placed ontoa porous carbon substrate to provide continuous paths of electrical conduction and mass transports. The best example is the catalyst layer in a fuel cell or an electrolyzer, where Pt or itsalloy is anchored to a network of connected carbon black (CB).11−13With the inert, conductive, and cheap carbon as an indispensable support for a metal electrocatalyst, there are not many ways to control the interface between the two.14−16Most often, catalyst particles are binded to carbon support with an adhesive, which inevitably covers a part of the active site. Alternatively, we can deposit thin layers of catalyst nanostructures directly to the carbon support via ALD,17,18 whose application is however limited by the costly and toxic precursor. Here, we develop a method of metal deposition on carbon in analogy to the UPD-mediated deposition. Reductive quinone derivatives, which adsorb strongly on carbon with well-de ﬁned surface coverages,19−21mimic the roles of the UPD layers. These adsorbates can undergo reversible electrochemical Received: March 31, 2020 Accepted: June 2, 2020 Published: June 2, 2020 Article www.acsaem.org © XXXX American Chemical Society Ahttps://dx.doi.org/10.1021/acsaem.0c00706 ACS Appl. Energy Mater. XXXX, XXX, XXX −XXX Downloaded via UPPSALA UNIV on June 14, 2020 at 08:06:17 (UTC). See https://pubs.acs.org/sharingguidelines for options on how to legitimately share published articles.
