reactions at potentials low enough to deposit a range of metals under surface-limited kinetics. In this work, we employ ﬁve quinone derivatives for the deposition of silver and platinumon carbon black and carbon paper. We show that this methodcan lead to the controllable deposition of ﬁne nanostructures of high catalytic activities for hydrogen evolution. ■RESULTS AND DISCUSSION Quinone Adsorption. We hypothesize the adsorbate- mediated deposition to follow a mechanism as stated below.Quinone of a su ﬃciently low redox potential to reduce metal cations usually adsorbs onto carbon at the oxidized state, QQ+*→ * where Q stands for quinone in general, *is the electrode surface, and Q *is the quinone adsorbate. The electrochemical reduction of quinone to hydroquinone (H 2Q) Q2 H2 e H Q2 *+ + → *+− should precede the metal deposition, assuming two-electrontransfer reaction and no di ssociation of the phenolic hydroxides in the hydroquinone in an acid or neutralelectrolyte, given the high pK a. If metal cations of a higher reduction potential are now added to the electrolyte,spontaneous charge transfer will occur as follows nnn2HQ M M2QHn 2*+ → + *++ + We assume that the quinone stays adsorbed on either the carbon or the deposited metal until the deposit reaches a highcoverage when the quinone may desorb if it bonds weakly tothe metal. The process resembles UPD-mediated deposition asthe site of charge transfer will lose its reducing power. Thediﬀerence is that the quinone is not immediately replaced, whose role in the wetting between the carbon and the metalremains unclear. Nevertheless, this interface-controlled depo-sition has the potential to achieve smooth deposits that isdiﬃcult to attain via typical electrodeposition. In the above mechanism, the reduction potential and surface coverage of a quinone molecule are two important factors fordeposition. In our previous work, we have shown that onporous carbon electrodes, quinone adsorbates display similarelectrochemistry to their solvated counterparts and that thesurface coverage is weakly dependent on the size of themolecule. 21The behaviors are similar to quinone adsorbates on planar carbon electrodes. Here, we choose ﬁve diﬀerent molecules of di ﬀerent reduction potentials, but all low enough for the deposition of typical noble-metal catalysts. We beginour investigation on carbon black. The quinone moleculeswere dissolved in 0.5 M H 2SO4to a concentration of 0.01 − 0.50 M to achieve saturated adsorption on the carbon blacksurface. The quinone-adsorbed carbon black was then pastedon a glassy carbon electrode with the help of Na ﬁon binder. The electrode was then rinsed thoroughly to remove quinonesolution in the pores. Because of the strong adsorption and theslow desorption kinetics, quinone adsorbates stay on the carbon surface. Figure 1 shows the cyclic voltammetry (CV) of the quinone- adsorbed carbo black electrodes. All curves have very smallseparations between the cathodic and anodic peaks, character-istic of adsorbed molecules. The peak potentials are close tothe formal potentials of the solubilized counterparts. Allelectrodes display one pair of peaks except that ofbenzoquinone (BQ) adsorbates, which likely goes through two consecutive steps of one-electron reduction. We can derivethe saturated surface coverage ( Γin mol/cm 2) from the charge under either the reduction or the oxidation peak normalized tothe weight of the carbon black ( Qin C/g). Given a gravimetric speciﬁc area ( A g) measured by gas adsorption, Γcan be calculated from Qthrough the following equation Q nF AQgΓ= (1) where nQis the number of electrons in quinone reduction. The results are shown in Table S1 . 5,8-Dihydroxy-1,4-naphthoqui- none (DHNQ) shows the highest coverage of 55.4 pmol/cm2, and anthraquinone-2,7-disulfonic acid, disodium salt (AQDS)shows the lowest coverage of 22.6 pmol/cm 2. The value seems to decrease with an increase in the size of the molecule as wewill expect. The values of surface coverage agree with ourprevious study on porous carbon paper and cloth, as well as theliterature on planar carbon electrodes. 19,21All adsorbates show high stability in extensive cycles of CV except 1,4-naphthoquinone-2-sulfonic acid, potassium salt (NQS), andBQ, which undergo gradual decomposition and likelydesorption. 22−24 Silver Deposition. We then apply the stable adsorbates to deposit Ag and use this simple one-electron charge transferprocess to investigate the deposition mechanism. The quinonewe choose is AQDS for its su ﬃciently low redox potential and rapid charge transfer kinetics. 19The AQDS-adsorbed carbon black was reduced electrochemically at −0.42 V vs the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) in 0.50 M sulfuric acid.A AgNO 3solution was then added to the cell to react with the reduced adsorbates (experimental procedures are illustrated inFigure S1 ). The step of deposition involves the electron transfer between the hydroquinone and the metal cation,without the need of a potentiostat. As shown by the open-circuit potential ( Figure S2a ), the deposition completes within a minute, after which the potential stabilizes at a valuecharacteristic of Ag metal. The amount of Ag deposited is quanti ﬁed electrochemically. We apply linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) to the Ag-deposited carbon black electrode ( Figure 2 ). 25The waves at low and high potentials correspond to the oxidation of the as-reducedAQDS adsorbate and the Ag deposit. The charge associatedwith the adsorbates is 0.36 mC, lower than that beforedeposition (0.47 mC) likely due to desorption. The high-potential wave has a charge of 0.41 mC, very close to that of Figure 1. Cycle voltammetry (CV) of quinone molecule adsorption on carbon black, all performance in 0.5 M H2SO4at a scan rate of 40 mV/s.ACS Applied Energy Materials www.acsaem.org Article https://dx.doi.org/10.1021/acsaem.0c00706 ACS Appl. Energy Mater. XXXX, XXX, XXX −XXXB
