the quinone adsorbate, which con ﬁrms its role as the reductant. The loading of Ag is thereby calculated to be 4.6%. The morphology of the Ag deposit is characterized by transmission electron microscopy (TEM), as shown in theinset of Figure 2 . On the surface of carbon black particles, we see Ag particles of size around 100 nm, seemingly attachedtightly to the carbon particles. The size of the Ag deposit isapparently larger than a few monolayers, as one would inferfrom the deposition mechanism. Meanwhile, at a lowermagni ﬁcation of scanning electron microscopic (SEM) images, we can ﬁnd regions with little deposition ( Figure S3a ), suggesting nonuniform quinone adsorption and/or deposition.Although nonuniform adsorption could happen as regions of the pasted carbon electrode were covered by the binder, it would not necessarily lead to a thick layer of Ag deposit.Nonuniform deposition or the growth of localized islands ismore likely, given the poor wetting between carbon and Ag.Despite the immobile source of electrons, the good electricalconductivity of carbon and the high surface di ﬀusivity of Ag atoms may facilitate the formation of large Ag crystallitesinstead of a thin, uniform Ag ﬁlm. Although the observed morphology deviates from our expectation based on the analogy to UPD-mediated deposi-tion, 26the method still shows two unique advantages over conventional chemical or electrochemical deposition. Com-pared to the chemical deposition with a dissolved reductant,the method leads to the direct anchorage of a controllableamount of Ag deposit to carbon. Compared to electrochemicaldeposition (an example given in Figure S4 ), quinone-mediated deposition covers the carbon surface more uniformly owing tothe surface-limited electron source.Pt Deposition. The above deposition mechanism infers generality, which we then exploit for the deposition of Pt,which as an electrocatalyst often interfaces with carbon. We repeated the procedure with a solution of H 2PtCl6.A ta concentration of 1 mM, the deposition ﬁnished in 2 min, as shown by the open-circuit potential of the carbon electrode(Figure S2b ). The morphology is however di ﬀerent from that of the Ag deposit. In Figure 3 a, we can ﬁnd∼20 nm wide clusters of deposits, which comprise ﬁne secondary particles with lattice fringes attributed to Pt metal. The particles aresmaller than the carbon black substrate ( Figure S5a ). The length scale is signi ﬁcantly smaller than that of Ag likely for two reasons. First, the same reductant would result in a larger driving force for Pt deposition and thus a smaller size of critical nuclei. Second, surface di ﬀusion, the most likely kinetics of spontaneous coarsening, is slower for Pt. 27As a comparison, we tried to deposit Pt on adsorbate-free carbon black electrochemical at the voltage where the mediated deposition took place. However, we could not ﬁnd any Pt after the deposition, which suggests that the adsorbate may have alsofacilitated the nucleation. To understand how this morphology evolves, we lowered the concentration of PtCl 62−to 0.1 mM for slower deposition so that we can characterize the morphology at an earlier stage.As shown by the change of the open-circuit potential ( Figure S2a), the deposition indeed proceeded at a much slower rate. We stopped the deposition at 100 s and 60 min and retrievedthe electrodes for characterization, as shown in Figure 3 b,c, respectively. The longer-duration deposition produces clusterswith secondary particles similar to that from 1 mM PtCl 62−, except that the cluster size is smaller on average. For the shorter-duration sample, we observe small Pt particlesuniformly deposited on the carbon black. Therefore, wepostulate that the clusters evolve through the aggregation ofthe small particles, similar to the agglomeration of Pt particles in fuel cell cathodes, 28as supported by the lower population of the Pt clusters ( Figure 3 c) than that of the Pt nanoparticles (Figures 3 a,b and S6). A change in the reductant, the quinone adsorbate, can lead to a signi ﬁcant change in the morphology of Pt deposit, as shown in Figures 3 d and S5c. Reduction by AQS results in nanoparticles uniformly dispersed on carbon black ( Figure S5b), similar to that observed in the short-duration deposition by AQDS. Reduction by DHNQ for 60 min leads to ∼5n m thick, compact layers of Pt on the carbon black with gooduniformity. If the deposition is carried out for 30 min, the Figure 2. LSV of Ag-deposited carbon black at a scan rate of 40 mV/ s. The inset is a transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of the Ag deposit (dark contrast) on carbon black particles (lightcontrast), with a scale bar of 20 nm. Figure 3. TEM images of Pt deposited via adsorbed quinone. (a −d) Deposits from 1 mM H2PtCl6for 60 min, 0.1 mM H2PtCl6for 60 min, and 0.1 mM H2PtCl6for 100 s all mediated by AQDS, and from 0.1 mM H2PtCl6for 60 min mediated by DHNQ. Scale bars are 5 nm, except those in the insets (50 nm).ACS Applied Energy Materials www.acsaem.org Article https://dx.doi.org/10.1021/acsaem.0c00706 ACS Appl. Energy Mater. XXXX, XXX, XXX −XXXC
