morphology appears similar, although the Pt layer seems thinner ( Figure S5c ). This morphology, distinct from the clusters in the case of AQDS, may be understood in terms oftwo properties of the adsorbates. The higher surface coverageof DHNQ yields a larger amount of Pt deposit conformallycovering the carbon surface, and the high redox potential ofDHNQ provides a small driving force for nucleation, whichlikely reduces the chance of detachment and aggregation of thesmall nuclei. Although the amount of Pt is too small toquantify with methods like elemental analysis, we can estimateit based on eq 1 by assuming that the charge associated with the Pt reduction is the same as that we put into the quinoneadsorbate, which should be fairly accurate given themechanism and what we observe for the Ag deposition. WithQstill being the charge from the quinone adsorbate (normalized to the carbon weight), M Mthe molar weight of the metal, and nMthe number of electrons transferred during the metal deposition, we can estimate the loading of Pt ( W%, normalized to the weight of the carbon black) with WQM nF%M M= (2) It gives a theoretical loading of 6.40% for DHNQ and 2.72% for AQDS. We further characterize the two types of Pt deposits with UPD for the speci ﬁc area. We used Ag-UPD on Pt as Cu- or H-UPD is interfered by the electrochemical signals fromresidual quinone adsorbates in the electrode.3From Figure S7a,b , we can estimate the areas of Pt to be 0.24 and 0.75 cm2 for the deposits from AQDS and DHNQ, respectively. Dividing the two values by the weights of Pt estimated fromeq 2, we can derive the speci ﬁc areas of Pt to be 24 and 75 m 2/ g, respectively. The speci ﬁc area achieved by DHNQ is low despite the larger amount of Pt deposited, as we would expectgiven the morphology. Hydrogen Evolution Reaction. The two deposition processes provide us with two distinct carbon-supported Ptnanostructures, which can ﬁnd immediate applications in the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). The Pt clusters cancontribute to a higher speci ﬁc area, whereas the Pt layer should be more robust. We characterized two samples deposited byAQDS and DHNQ (from 1 mM PtCl 62−for 30 min), respectively, for their catalytic activities toward HER. With the same carbon loading on a rotating disk electrode, the twoperform similarly in terms of current per geometric area(Figure 4 a). However, if we convert it to the current per Pt area, the di ﬀerence is outstanding, with the one deposited with DHNQ delivering a much better activity ( Figure S8b ). A possible explanation of this di ﬀerence is the di ﬀerent surfaceorientations of the Pt deposits. Although we cannot determine the orientation accurately with TEM, we have observed evidence that the deposit from the DHNQ adsorbates may terminate at a lower-index plane (110) ( Figure S5c ), which beneﬁts the catalysis of the HER. 29Meanwhile, if we normalize the current by the weights of Pt, AQDS stands out as more of the Pt atoms are on the surface ( Figure 4 b). Both outperform commercial 20% Pt on carbon (Pt/C). A spurious factor here is the residual quinone adsorbate, which can obscure themeasured catalytic activity. We performed a control test on commercial Pt black with AQDS adsorbed onto it. The results show that the adsorbate does not change the LSV curves (Figure S8a ), which rules out the impact of the adsorbate in the measured activity. The Pt layer deposited via DHNQ also displays a higher durability, as assessed through extensive cycles of the HER. The dashed lines in Figure 4 a show the 1000th LSV cycles of the two samples, where we see a bigger loss of activity in theone deposited with AQDS due to the weaker attachment of the clusters, which can gradually fall o ﬀthe electrode. The Pt layer instead anchors to the carbon black well. It nonetheless shows an increase in the overvoltage, as the carbon black itself cannot stay on the electrode stably, evident from the black particlessuspended in the solution after the durability test. Overall, the Pt deposit mediated by DHNQ shows more promise, whose loading can be optimized in the future to achieve a better gravimetric activity. Finally, the quinone-mediated deposition applies to other carbon substrates as well. Through the same procedure, we can deposit Pt on commercial carbon paper. With the help of either AQDS or DHNQ adsorbate, we achieve a microscopic uniformity in coating ﬁbers in the carbon paper with a low loading of Pt ( Figure S4b,c ). Thermogravimetric analysis estimates a Pt loading of 0.39% ( Figure S9 ), consistent with what we would estimate based on the assumption that all AQDS lose the charge to deposit Pt. Although SEM could not reveal the microstructures clearly, we suspect the morphologyto resemble that on carbon black. DHNQ also achieves a visibly higher amount of deposit owing to the higher saturation coverage. The deposition is limited to the ﬁbers at the exterior, as the hydrophobic carbon paper does not let in any solution. However, if we bake the paper in air to partially oxidize thesurface, we can achieve uniform deposition even inside the paper, rendering a three-dimensional structure full of Pt nanoparticles. These fabrication strategies will allow the method to be applied to a broad range of catalysis applications. Figure 4. (a) Durability test of DHNQ- and AQDS-promoted Pt growth on carbon black. (b) LSV curves of DHNQ, AQDS, and 20% Pt/C in 0.5 MH 2SO4at a scan rate of 5 mV/s.ACS Applied Energy Materials www.acsaem.org Article https://dx.doi.org/10.1021/acsaem.0c00706 ACS Appl. Energy Mater. XXXX, XXX, XXX −XXXD
