■CONCLUSIONS In summary, we have demonstrated a new method of depositing metal nanostructures on carbon substrates. Wehave no reason to believe the method to be limited to the casesexamined here. We also show that the deposits anchor to the substrates stably, although the robustness depends on the speciﬁcs of the method, including the quinone adsorbate. The adsorbate determines the morphology, the amount ofdeposition, and likely the surface orientation of the depositthrough its redox potential, surface coverage, and possibly other speci ﬁc interactions with the metal being deposited. The method, with its unique ability to achieve robust and tunableinterfaces between metal and carbon, will ﬁnd its applications in catalysis and electrocatalysis, as we show with theoutstanding HER activity with the Pt deposits. ■EXPERIMENTAL SECTION Fabrication. All chemicals were used as received. Quinone solutions were prepared respectively using hydroquinone (Sigma-Aldrich, >99%), 5,8-dihydroxy-1,4-naphthoquinone (DHNQ) (Alfa Aesar, 97%), anthraquinone-2,7- disulfonic acid, disodium salt (AQDS) (TCI, >97%), sodium anthraquinone-2-sulfonate (AQS)(TCI, >97%) and 1,4-naphthoquinone-2-sulfonic acid, potassium salt(NQS) (Sigma-Aldrich, >97%). They were dissolved in 0.5 M H 2SO4 (VWR, 95%). To prepare quinone-adsorbed electrodes, 1 mg ofcarbon black (Ketjemblack ECP 600JD) was ﬁrst dispersed in a mixture of 50 μLo fN a ﬁon solution (5%, Sigma-Aldrich), 250 μLo f ethanol, and 250 μL of water and ultrasonicated for 1 h. Ink (5 μL) is then dripped onto a polished glassy carbon electrode (PINE AFE5T050GCPK, 5 mm diameter, 0.196 cm 2face area) and dried at room temperature. The electrode was then soaked in a quinonesolution with 0.5 M H 2SO4(VWR, 95%), and then pure 0.5 M H2SO4 solution and deionized water were used in sequence to wash o ﬀthe quinone solution. For the deposition, a quinone-adsorbed electrode was ﬁrst electrochemically reduced in deaerated 0.5 M H 2SO4in a three- electrode cell. Ag/AgCl and Pt wire served as the reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. A solution of metal salt (AgNO3, Sigma-Aldrich, 99.8%, or H2PtCl6, Aldrich, 8 wt % in H2O) was added to the cell for the reduction. After the synthesis, the weight of Agrestored by quinone on carbon black was measured by the electrochemical method. Applied potential was provided by LSV in 0.5 M H 2SO4to oxidize Ag at about 0.565 V. Characterization. All electrochemical characterization was conducted with a potentiostat (Autolab 302N). UPD was conductedvia LSV at a scan rate of 5 mV/s in either 0.5 mM silver sulfate(Aladdin, 99.7%) or CuSO 4(Sigma-Aldrich, 99.6%). Morphologies of the deposits were characterized by SEM(JEOL- 7100F) and HR-TEM (JEOL-2100 and JEOL-ARM200F). EDS was carried out on JEOL-7100F. Thermal weight loss was measured by a thermogravimetric analyzer (TGA, TA-Q5000) at a heating rate of 10 K/min under air condition. ■ASSOCIATED CONTENT *sıSupporting Information The Supporting Information is available free of charge at https://pubs.acs.org/doi/10.1021/acsaem.0c00706 . Summary of quinone adsorbate properties; schematic of deposition procedure; electrochemical data of thedeposition, ECSA measurements, and HER; additionalSEM and TEM characterizations of Ag and Pt deposits;and TGA ( PDF)■AUTHOR INFORMATION Corresponding Author Qing Chen −Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, Department of Chemistry, and The Energy Institute, HKUST, Clear Water Bay, Kowloon, Hong Kong;HKUST-Shenzhen Research Institute, Shenzhen 518057, P. R.China; orcid.org/0000-0003-3106-9281 ; Email: chenqing@ust.hk Authors Yiting Zheng −Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, HKUST, Clear Water Bay, Kowloon, Hong Kong; orcid.org/0000-0002-9022-4301 Ruichun Luo −School of Materials Science and Engineering, Shanghai Jiao Tong University, Shanghai 200240, P. R. China Yuan Xu −Department of Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering, HKUST, Clear Water Bay, Kowloon, Hong Kong L