Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 Available online 13 May 2024 0010-8545/© 2024 Elsevier B.V. All rights reserved.Advanced noble-metal/transition-metal/metal-free electrocatalysts for hydrogen evolution reaction in water-electrolysis for hydrogen production Chunxia Wanga,1, Wenxuan Guoa,1, Tianle Chena, Wenyi Lua, Zhaoyi Songa, Chengcheng Yana, Ying Fenga, Fuming Gaoa, Xiaona Zhanga, Yupeng Raoa, Lanting Qiand, Shengming Xub, Guoyong Huanga,*, Yun Zhengc,*, Wei Yanc, Jiujun Zhangc,* aState Key Laboratory of Heavy Oil Processing, College of New Energy and Materials, China University of Petroleum (Beijing), Beijing 102249, China bInstitute of Nuclear and New Energy Technology, Tsinghua University, Beijing 100084, China cInstitute of New Energy Materials and Engineering, School of Materials Science and Engineering, Fuzhou University, Fuzhou 350108, China dDepartment of Chemical Engineering, Waterloo Institute for Nanotechnology, University of Waterloo, Waterloo, Ontario N2L 3G1 Canada ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Hydrogen evolution reaction Noble metal-based electrocatalysts Transitional metal electrocatalysts Metal-free electrocatalysts ABSTRACT Hydrogen energy serves as an ideal alternative energy carrier to fossil fuels in the future clean energy system. The hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) occurring at the cathode of water electrocatalysis has been considered as a promising approach to produce hydrogen over the past decades due to zero carbon emission. To gain a deep insight into the advanced electrocatalyst, herein, we provide a comprehensive review with an emphasis on the recent state-of-the-art advances of HER electrocatalysts in view of controlling their local electronic structure, creating defect nanostructures, exposing active sites, altering d band center, and constructing heterogeneous structure. Initially, we start with a brief introduction of the fundamental electrocatalytic mechanisms and the critical electrochemical parameters for evaluating HER. Then, we systemically discuss three representative classes of electrocatalysts, comprised of highly active noble-metal including single atom, nanoparticles, and clusters, earth-abundant transition metal such as transition metal sulfides, phosphides, carbides, nitrides, and oxides, and metal-free functional nanomaterials involving different dimensions of carbonaceous nanomaterials, boron nitrogen, and phosphorous non-carbonaceous nanomaterials by adjusting their tunable electronic struc- tures and architectures/morphologies with a particular emphasis on the structure –function relationships. Finally, we point out the current challenges and opportunities in view of achieving highly active and stable electro - catalysts towards practical applications. 1.Introduction The vast majority consumption of fossil fuels has caused widespread concern about the energy shortage and unfavourable environment damage. With respect to this, exploring clean and sustainable energy sources and developing new energy storage and conversion technologies have been emerging [1–3]. Among the energy storage and conversion technologies, batteries, supercapacitors, and electrocatalytic water splitting to produce hydrogen have been recognized as reliable, efficient and practical options [4–6]. Especially, by using water-electrolysis technique to produce hydrogen is recognized as a clean and sustain - able substitute to traditional energy carriers owing to its high repro - ducibility, zero emission, and eminent energy density [7,8] . However, the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) occurs at the cathode of water- electrolysis differs greatly in different media (acidic, alkaline, neutral). In alkaline conditions, the HER process involves the dissociation of water to form adsorbed hydrogen (Hads) and the combination of Hads with H to form molecular H2. The dissociation step requires high energy, resulting in a high overpotential, often considered as the rate- determining step [7]. In acidic media, proton adsorbed on the elec- trode surface is the key step. However, many electrocatalysts degrade significantly in harsh acidic electrolytes due to oxidation and dissolution [9]. In neutral media, the Hads originates from the dissociation of H2O, highlighting the involvement of identical active intermediates in both alkaline and neutral environments [10]. To address this issue, research focuses have been devoted to developing electrocatalysts by lowering *Corresponding authors. E-mail addresses: huanggy@cup.edu.cn (G. Huang), yunzheng@fzu.edu.cn (Y. Zheng), jiujun.zhang@fzu.edu.cn (J. Zhang). 1 These authors contribute equally. Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Coordination Chemistry Reviews u{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2mm~! https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccr.2024.215899 Received 11 December 2023; Accepted 21 April 2024
============================================================
[Page 2/30]
============================================================
Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 2activation energy and enhancing conversion efficiency via optimized intermediate-site interactions. At present, platinum (Pt)-based nano- materials are considered as the ideal HER electrocatalysts due to its high catalytic activity in a wide pH range and the near zero Gibbs free energy for hydrogen absorption [11]. However, the scarcity, high cost, and insufficient stability of Pt limit their large-scale application [12]. Therefore, tremendous efforts have been devoted to exploring earth abundant, cost effective, highly active, stable and sustainable electro - catalysts over the past decades. As summarized in Fig. 1a, the literatures of HER electrocatalyst increase rapidly in the past decades. To reduce electrode overpotential and strengthen water-splitting efficiency, a wide range of electrocatalysts including highly active noble-metal [13], earth-abundant transition metal oxides [14], chalco - genides [15], phosphides [16], nitrides [17], and carbides [18], as well as functional metal-free nanomaterials [19], have been exploited and evaluated for HER. As for the first main type, noble metals such as Pt, Pd, Rh, and Ir are commonly used in the HER process due to their optimum binding ability with hydrogen and hence display excellent HER activities regardless of the structure [20]. Moreover, changing the geometry and electronic properties of noble metal electrocatalysts through size tuning into metal single atoms, nanoclusters and nanoparticles can increase atomic utilization, optimize free energy for hydrogen adsorption, and offer highly active electrocatalysts [21]. However, it should be noted that Au and Ag have not been widely utilized in HER due to high free energy of hydrogen adsorption and low current density. Nevertheless, Au (or Ag) nanoclusters (NCs) composed of several tens to hundreds of metal atoms with large surface area also serve as potential HER elec- trocatalysts [22]. Typically, the inherent activity and increased active site density of noble-metal electrocatalysts can be realized by reducing the particle size [23], engineering crystalline structure [24], alloying with transition metals [25], or immobilizing them on a conductive substrate [26] to prevent aggregation. With respect to next main type of electrocatalysts, transition metal electrocatalysts are relatively affordable compared to noble metals, and they feature with abundant reserves, long-term stability, and reasonable catalytic activity, however, they exhibit moderately favourable Gibbs free energy for hydrogen adsorption in comparison with noble metals such as Pt [27,28] . Furthermore, many transition metal-based HER electrocatalysts exhibit thermodynamic instability in strong acidic and alkaline environments. For instance, pure Ni/C experiences redox Fig. 1.a. Statistics of publications of electrocatalytic HER catalyst from 2008 to 2022 through the search formula TS (“electrocatalytic hydrogen evolution catalyst ”), using the Web of Science: https://webofknowledge.com/ on 15 May 2023. b. Statistics diagram of Tafel slope versus overpotential for various HER electrocatalysts summarized in this review. C. Wang et al.
============================================================
[Page 3/30]
============================================================
Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 3reactions with electrolytes in strong acid, leading to alterations of original active sites. Since transition metals possess unfilled d orbitals in their valence shells, which facilitates their combination with other metallic or non-metallic elements to achieve a stable state [29]. The interaction between Ni and lanthanum-cerium oxide (LCO) ensures notable stability under large current density [30]. Additionally, the growth of NiCo layered double hydroxide (LDH) ultra-thin nanocrystals on a three-dimensional conductive support also facilitates effective electrolyte/ion diffusion and enables a high stability over 72 h [31]. With respect to this, the adsorption/desorption ability of transition metal electrocatalysts towards hydrogen, the number of active sites and capacity of electron transport can be adjusted by changing their local electronic structure, constructing heterogeneous structure, controlling morphology, creating porous or vacancy defect nanostructures, and doping or complexing with conductive substrates due to their control - lable structure [32]. Regarding the third main type, metal-free electrocatalysts have versatile composition chemistry, abundant sources, and the lowest price but suffer from the least performance. However, they are considered as the remarkable and fascinating electrocatalysts due to their considerable electron transport capacity, high surface area, and adjustable catalyti - cally active sites [33]. Their catalytic HER performance can be further enhanced by regulating electronic structures and increasing active sites either through heteroatom doping or structural engineering. In the past five years, some reviews have summarized efficient strategies for developing highly efficient HER electrocatalysts from different perspectives including structure engineering, activity modu - lation, and surface modification. To be specific, Hu et al. [20] provided a review in view of synthetic approach and morphology characterization of transition metal dichalcogenides-based electrocatalysts and presented a series of detailed strategies for long term performance. Fu et al. [29] discussed the relationship of structure and activity between Pt-Co electrocatalysts by combining synthestic methods and formation mechanisms. Luo et al. [34] summarized the fabrication of hetero - structures for water splitting by coupling different interface compo - nents. Different from the mentioned topics that focus on only one or two type(s) of electrocatalysts, a comprehensive and systematic review highlights the fundamental mechanism, the modification strategies, the relationship between structure and electrocatalytic performance of versatile functional HER electrocatalysts are highly desirable. In this review, a comprehensive overview is demonstrated in view of recent advancement of noble metal, transition metal compounds, and metal-free electrocatalysts and their catalytic HER Tafel slopes, as summarized in Fig. 1b. This paper begins with a brief introduction of mechanisms and typical evaluation parameters for electrochemical performance of HER. Then, three types of noble metal electrocatalysts, which are classified to single atoms, nanoclusters, and nanoparticles, are reviewed considering the size, active sites, surface modification and stability. Meanwhile, various kinds of transition metal compounds involving transition metal sulfide, phosphide, carbide, nitride, and oxide are also summarized in detail. Furthermore, recent advances in carbo - naceous as well as carbon-free electrocatalysts are also discussed in view of their different components, morphologies, microstructures as well as active sites (Fig. 2). This review concludes with the proposed insights into current major challenges and their anticipated solutions with the goal of stimulating continuous innovation to provide perspectives on advanced electrocatalyst fabrication and the potential for future breakthroughs in the development of HER electrocatalysts. 2.Fundamentals of hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) 2.1. HER mechanism The mechanism of HER proceeds in two different pathways accord - ing to acidic and alkaline conditions (Fig. 3a) [35]. At present, it is generally accepted that the HER process can be described as follows: in case of acidic electrolytes, after an electrochemical discharge in the first step of Volmer reaction, an adsorbed hydrogen intermediate (H*) on the active site is formed. Subsequently, H2 is formed by the adjacent adsorbed H* intermediates (Tafel reaction). Alternatively, the adsorbed H* can combine with a proton accompanied with an electron transfer reaction to form H2 (Heyrovsky reaction) [11]. However, in addition to the above mentioned Volmer-Tafel and Volmer-Heyrovsky process, the HER process in alkaline environments involves the dissociation of water, which is highly influenced by the dissociation of water molecules to produce protons, resulting in a high energy barrier, therefore, dissoci - ation of water is a dominant factor that affects the HER performance [20,36] . To summarize, the HER can be explained by two mechanisms: the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism and the Volmer-Tafel mechanism. During HER process, the binding energy between the electrocatalyst and the H* dominates the reaction process, too weak interaction results in a subdued of Volmer reaction process; while too strong binding energy hinders the desorption of H*, leading to the occupied active sites and the inhibited desorption process (Volmer-Heyrovsky). The Tafel slope (Fig. 1b) of the polarization curve is used to predict the HER reaction mechanism in which different electrocatalysts differ greatly during the HER process. Given that the adsorption free energy toward hydrogen (ΔGH*) is one of the essential factors used to estimate activity of an electrocatalyst. Density functional theory (DFT) can provide a promising way to calcu - late ΔGH* by modelling the intermediate species formed on the elec- trocatalyst surface during HER [37]. For example, Lou et al. [38] reported the plot of exchange current density (log j0) versus ΔGH* of different pure metals and bimetals, showing a “volcano ” curve. A nearly zero value of ΔGH* (zero) means a better HER activity (Fig. 3b), there - fore, Pt with an almost zero value of ΔGH* located at the top of the volcano demonstrates the most premium HER activity. 2.2. Cathode HER overpotential The measurement of cathode HER overpotential (η) involves deter - mining the potential difference between the experimental value and the thermodynamically determined potential value. To evaluate the effi- ciency of electrocatalysts, the overpotential measured at a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 is commonly employed as a benchmark [11]. A lower overpotential required to reach the target current density Fig. 2.Schematic illustration of topics mentioned in this review.  C. Wang et al.
