Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 4indicates superior performance. However, it is important to note that the overpotential alone cannot be solely considered as an indicator to evaluate the electrocatalyst ’s performance. This is due to the fact that different loading amounts of active sites yield significantly different activity. 2.3. Tafel slope Tafel slope is a useful tool for identifying the rate-determining steps (Volmer, Tafel, and Heyrovsky) in the HER. Plotting η versus logarithm | j| (η b log (j/j0), here j represents the current density and j0 is the exchange current density) yields a Tafel slope from the linear range of the Tafel diagram [20]. A smaller value of b indicates a smaller η required to increase current density, indicating a faster electron trans - port kinetics. However, the determination of the Tafel slope is chal- lenged by inconsistencies with the expected behavior. Studies have shown that the Tafel slope extracted from linear fitting methods is highly dependent on the loading and potential. The sluggish H2 diffusion within the catalyst layer leads to elevated local H2 pressures, making H2 reoxidation non-negligible. This phenomenon influences the potential and consequently affect the accuracy of Tafel slope. In practical appli - cations, the determination of rate-determining steps requires the com- bination of DFT calculations for adsorption energies to achieve more accurate assessments [39]. 3.Various electrocatalysts for HER 3.1. Noble metal electrocatalysts Noble metals such as Pt, Pd, Ru, Ir, and Rh, all of them can be assigned to platinum group metals (PGMs). As depicted in Fig. 3b, PGMs positioned at the top of the volcano curve with an almost zero free en- ergy for hydrogen adsorption [38], generally, they are highly active, however, their durability and stability still face challenges [29]. Fig. 3.HER mechanisms. a. The Volmer-Tafel HER mechanism (left) and Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism (right) on the electrode surface 