Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 5Commercial Pt/C catalysts show degradation rates of 0.96 and 1.58 mV h␀1 after continuous electrolysis for 120 h in acidic and alkaline solu- tions, respectively. These degradation rates are higher than those of single-atom Ir anchored on mesoporous graphitic carbon nitride (Ir-g- CN) [40]. As for noble metal electrocatalysts, Fig. 4 shows that the size effect has a huge impact on their electrocatalytic activity. When the particle size is reduced to less than 5 nm, the energy spectrum splits into discrete energy levels, resulting in substantial changes in its physical and chemical properties as well as electronic properties [41]. In this section, the recent developments in noble metal electrocatalysts with different sizes are summarized and analyzed. 3.1.1. Noble metal single atom electrocatalysts Optimizing the performance of electrocatalysts involves manipu - lating the amount and composition of active sites. Researchers often aim to improve the density of active sites of the electrocatalyst via reducing particle size. Altering the atomic and electronic structure can also enhance the hydrogen adsorption capacities of electrocatalysts and improve its electrocatalytic HER activity [42,43] . Reducing noble metal nanoparticles (NPs) to single atoms (SACs) can not only expose active Fig. 4.Geometric and electronic structures of metal electrocatalysts with different dimensions, from single atom to clusters and nanoparticles. Copyright 2018, ACS. Reproduced with permission from ref [41] Fig. 5.Typical researches for carbonaceous substrate of SACs electrocatalysts. a. Fabrication of the Pt single atoms on graphene edges. b. and c. HAADF-STEM images of Pt/VG Ar-5. d. Calculated projected density of states (PDOS) [52]. e. Synthetic process of Pt1/NCNS. f. k3-weighted Fourier transform EXAFS spectra. g. Corresponding N proportions and Pt loadings [44]. h. The synthetic process of Pt1/Co 1NC [60]. i. Raman spectra. Copyright 2