Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 6sites, raise atom accessibility, but also optimize electrochemical per- formance owing to low coordination induced high chemical activity of single-atom [44–46]. In 2011, Qiao et al. [47] immobilized single Pt atoms on iron oxide nanocrystals and created an innovative single-atom electrocatalysts with exceptional atom efficiency. This electrocatalyst demonstrated both remarkable activity and preferential oxidation of CO. Since then, various kinds of single atom based electrocatalysts with small size of less than 5 nm have been emerged [12]. However, these individual atoms usually suffer from aggregation induced unfavourable stability and reduced activity [48]. Therefore, researchers have put on extensive efforts to improve the stability of the electrocatalysts by optimizing the morphological structure, and regulating the surface- active sites and electron transfer properties of the electrocatalysts. Carbonaceous substrate-based SACs electrocatalysts . Carbon- based materials are crucial in desirable electrocatalyst supports because of their high surface area, prominent electron conductivity, superior chemical stability, and tunable porous structure [49]. In this part, the carbon substrates are briefly introduced, including graphene, carbon nanotubes, carbon nitride, and carbon materials based on met- al–organic frameworks (MOFs) for noble metal electrocatalysts. Graphene is extensively utilized as a substrate for diverse noble metal single atom electrocatalyst because of its adjustable electronic structure and good chemical stability. As one of the most representative noble metal electrocatalysts, Pt has a “quasi-zero ” onset overpotential located near the volcano ’s peak [50]. Attaching isolated Pt single atoms to graphene substrate while preserving a uniform distribution is chal- lenging, because graphene substrate and Pt atoms usually adopt a weak interaction [51]. However, the edges of graphene show a highly local- ized density of states, which increases the electronic activity and facil- itates binding with Pt atoms to disperse metals. Amal et al. [52] exploited this property by anchoring highly dispersed Pt on edge-rich graphene (Fig. 5a). As demonstrated in Fig. 5b and c, Pt coordinated to the edge sites of graphene can enhance *H adsorption by coupling the d-band center to the Fermi level (Fig. 5d). Pt with ultra-low mass loadings (0.71 µg cm␀2Pt) displays an overpotential (η) of 150 mV and a Tafel slope of 36 mV dec␀1. Additionally, oxygen-rich functional groups of graphene oxide can be coordinated with metal to improve dispersion ability. An atomically dispersed Rh electrocatalyst (Rh@NG) with a Rh loading of 8.45 µg cm␀2 was prepared by one-step pyrolysis approach, in which the Rh single atoms were coordinated with oxygen-rich functional groups and nitrogen bonds. The resultant Rh@NG showed a η of 29 mV in H2SO4 and 33 mV in KOH solution at 10 mA cm␀2, while the Tafel slope reached up to 22 and 30 mV dec␀1 [53]. Graphite carbon nitride (g-C 3N4) is highly stable, while the N/C coordination framework can bind effectively with metal atoms, pre- venting the aggregation of metal atoms at high temperature [44]. Single atom Ru anchored on mesoporous g-CN (Ru-g-CN) was synthesized by Liu et al [40]. The abundant N groups on the surface of g-CN firmly Fig. 6.Typical researches for metallic substrate of SACs electrocatalysts. a. Synthetic process of Ru/g-C 3N4-C-TiO 2. b. AC-STEM of Ru/g-C 3N4-C-TiO 2. c. |ΔGH2O| for hydrogen evolution [63]. d. Synthesis scheme of Ru1/D-NiFe LDH. e. Overpotentials at specific current densities [64]. f. Synthetic route for IrSA-NS-Ti 3C2Tx. g. XPS spectra of Ti 3 s and Ir 4 f. h. The calculated Gibbs hydrogen adsorption free energy (ΔGH*) diagram [69]. Copyright 2021, ACS, Copyright 2021, Nature, and Copyright 2021, RSC. Reproduced with permission from ref [63][64][69] C. Wang et al.
