Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 8the binding of Pt atoms. Simultaneously, the coordination of pyrrole N could anchor Pt more easily, providing an extra small η of 15 mV with a Tafel slope of 29.1 mV dec␀1. MOFs can be fabricated through assembling metal ions with ligands to form porous materials. The pyrolysis of MOFs produces a carbon substrate with numerous pores and substantial surface area, resulting in a homogeneous distribution of metal ions throughout the structure. However, the abundance of anchor sites is insufficient [58]. Nitrogen/ carbon defects are designed to anchor single atoms to prevent aggre - gation and improve dispersion. A Zn-based zeolitic imidazolate frame - work (ZIF-8), which is a typical MOF with a high content of nitrogen and carbon, was utilized as an ideal carbon support [59]. The study led by Feng et al. [60] demonstrated the use of defect-rich Co-doped porous carbon as the substrate for Pt precursors to produce atomically dispersed Pt and Co atoms (Pt1/Co 1NC) under a mild electrochemical reduction process (Fig. 5h). It is well known that the larger ratio of ID/IG indicates the more defects of the carbon material. As shown in Fig. 5i, the ID/IG ratio is increased after the incorporation of Co to NC compared to NC. The Co1NC allowed the exposure of defects to anchor Pt single atoms, the resultant Pt1/Co 1NC showed a η of 4.15 mV at 10 mA cm␀2, achieving a high mass activity of 32.4 A mg␀1, which is 54 times higher than that of commercial 20 wt% Pt/C. Metallic substrate-based SACs electrocatalysts. Transition metal oxides, sulfides, hydroxides, and MXenes, etc. can be used as the carriers for single atoms due to their thermal stability, good electrical conduc - tivity and reducibility. The transition metal substrate could form met- al–metal bond, firmly anchoring the atoms while changing their electronic structure [61]. Reducible oxides such as TiO2 are widely used in hydrolysis reactions due to their facile formation of oxygen vacancies to trap metal sites for the dissociation of H2O [62]. Shao et al. [63] developed a high-density Ru single atom electro - catalyst (12.4 wt% Ru) with oxygen vacancies on g-C3N4-C-TiO 2 support using an assembly-calcination approach, as shown in Fig. 6a. This approach prevented single atom aggregation induced by high surface energy. As expected, the remaining oxygen vacancies (yellow circle in Fig. 6b exhibit a low Gibbs free energy (Fig. 6c, |ΔGH2O|, 0.19 eV), which favoured the breakdown of HO-H bonds, while single Ru atoms favoured the hydrogen adsorption. At 10 mA cm␀2, the Ru/g-C 3N4-C- TiO2 delivered a η of 107 mV. Transition-metal-based layered double hydroxides with ultra-thin thickness and high surface area can also an- chor single atoms by defect engineering to create vacancies. Single-atom Ru could be homogeneously anchored on defect-rich NiFe LDH (Ru1/D- NiFe LDH) by electrodeposition and etching process, as shown in Fig. 6d. By carefully controlling the local environments of active sites and de- fects, Ru1/D-NiFe LDH exhibited an excellent performance (Fig. 6e) with an ultra-low η of 18 mV at 10 mA cm␀2, exceeding commercial Pt/C [64]. 2D transition metal carbides and nitrides (MXene) show large surface area and satisfied electrical conductivity. Their inherent highly unstable active vacancy defects and strongly electronegative lattice carbon or surface oxygen can be used to anchor single atoms [65,66] . Single-atom Pt could be loaded onto MXene nanosheets through a rapid thermal shock technique. The Pt-SA anchored on the oxygen vacancies could decrease the adsorption energy and the hybridization strength with hydrogen, achieving a ΔGH* close to 0 eV and exhibiting an excellent electrocatalytic activity [67]. However, 2D MXene nanosheets are prone to induce self-agglomeration of single atoms [68]. This issue can be solved by assembling nanosheets into 3D structures. For example, Tan et al. [69] anchored Ir atoms on 3D porous Ti3C2Tx MXene and regulated the coordination interaction between metal and substrate by N, S co- doping (Fig. 6f). The high-resolution XPS spectra (Fig. 6g) reveal that Ir 4f peak is located between Ir0 and Ir4, indicating the electronic in- teractions occurred between Ir and N, S. DFT results (Fig. 6h) suggest that the ΔG(H*) value of IrSA-NS-Ti 3C2Tx electrocatalyst is closer to 0, showing that the coordination of N and S with Ir can reduce hydrogen adsorption and enhance the intrinsic activity. IrSA-2NS-Ti 3C2Tx exhibits a low η of 50.5 mV at 10 mA cm␀2 in alkaline conditions. More recently, the tip effect was found to have the ability to improve the electrocatalytic performance by introducing locally enhanced elec- tric field and reducing the interfacial energy barrier [70]. MoS 2 has a tunable electronic structure and can be divided into a metallic 1 T phase and a semiconductor 2H phase, which helps improve the reaction ac- tivity by covalent bonding with metals. Compared with the less conductive 2H-phase MoS 2, the metallic 1 T phase can promote the formation of tip structures. Cao et al. [71] directly anchored Ru single atoms onto 1 T-MoS 2 with a tip structure to achieve efficient hydrogen evolution. The anchoring of Ru single atom caused a local structural phase transition of MoS 2, while the synergistic effect with S vacancies led to a nearly zero of hydrogen adsorption energy. In alkaline media, 5.0 %-Ru-MoS 2 electrocatalyst showed a η of 76 mV at 10 mA cm␀2. 3.1.2. Noble-metal nanoclusters based electrocatalyst Constructing metal clusters with a high surface-exposed atomic ratio is another impressive approach to minimize the amount of noble metals and strengthen the electrocatalytic activities. Noble metal nanoclusters are usually around 2 nm in size with precisely defined elemental com- positions and atomic structures. A large fraction of the atoms in the clusters can directly interact with the substrate, which makes them rich in catalytic active sites that can effectively catalyze electrochemical HER [72,73] . Pt nanocluster based electrocatalysts. Compared to bulk Pt-SACs, Pt nanoclusters with Pt-Pt bonds can enrich electron density on Pt atoms by hindering electron transfer. Additionally, charge transfer direction may be reversed, which further increases electron density on the Pt species, reduces the d-band center, and enhances H* intermediates desorption [74]. Lu et al. [75] anchored Pt nanoclusters on oxygen va- cancies riched porous TiO2 nanosheets (VO-rich Pt/TiO 2) by a simple pyrolysis method (Fig. 7a). The oxygen-rich vacancies in porous TiO2 could promote both hydrogen spillover and enhance charge transfer (Fig. 7b, from 0.367 to 0.406 electrons), which made Pt-NCs absorb H* easily for proton reduction. The resultant VO-rich Pt/TiO 2 displayed a high mass activity of 45.28 A mgPt␀1, which was obviously superior than commercial Pt/C. Similar to TiO2, introducing nanomaterials with diverse morphology to disperse and anchor Pt-based species is a viable approach to expose active sites and enhance utilization efficiency. For example, Mai et al. [76] proposed a spray drying method to fabricate wrinkled Ti3C2Tx MXene containing Pt clusters (Pt/Mxene), as shown in Fig. 7c. The fol- ded structure of MXene nanosheets can prevent them from restacking and ensure that the Pt clusters are sufficiently exposed. Pt/MXene electrocatalyst shows a superior HER performance, which is comparable to 20 wt% Pt/C (Fig. 7d and e). In addition, the doped N could enhance the electrical conductivity and metal-carrier interactions. As displayed in Fig. 7f, g and h, the porous MOFs-derived N-doped carbon arrays with a high ID/IG ratio (1.05) (Fig. 7f) offers plentiful defect sites to trap and disperse the Pt clusters. N-6 (Fig. 7i) acts as the Lewis acid, and can facilitate hydrogen ions’ adsorption and transfer, which improves the electrocatalytic HER performance (Fig. 7j) [77]. Other precious metal-based nanocluster electrocatalysts. The stability of Ru/Rh/Ir nanoclusters can be improved by anchoring effect and space confinement of carbon materials [78]. Ru is highly similar to Pt in geometry and electronic structure, and the energy of Ru-H bond (~65 kcal mol␀1) is close to Pt-H, which is a promising alternative to Pt [79]. Li et al. [80] created a novel Ru nanocluster called Ru-S/N-C by pyrolyzing Ru complexes within a supramolecular framework, which was anchored on N/S-encoded carbon nanosheets. The charge-density difference calculations and ΔGH* values were employed to investigate the coordination of Ru-S bonds and the synergistic interactions between N, S, C and Ru, demonstrating that an optimized hydrogen adsorption to Ru and a changed coordination surrounding of Ru could be observed, which accelerated HER kinetics. Rh and Ir are close to Pt in the volcano diagram with low Gibb ’s free C. Wang et al.
