Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 10Zhang et al. [86] constructed nanosheet supramolecular assemblies (BOPs) with weak reduction properties driven by the macrocyclic molecule cucurbituril (CB) and 3D aromatic-like closo-[B 12H12]2␀, as shown in Fig. 8a. The highly dispersed Pt nanoparticles are located in the sheet-like superimposed BOPs framework (BOPs@Pt) (Fig. 8b), and the pyrolytic BC3N@Pt provides a η of 23.1 mV with a Tafel slope of 61.07 mV dec␀1 in acidic medium at 10 mA cm␀2. The efficiency of electrocatalysts can be enhanced via controlling the anchoring structure of the carbon materials. For example, Guo et al. [87] successfully synthesized highly defective graphene nanospheres (GNs) by an arc discharge method and then used them as Pt nanoparticle supports. The GNs had a high density of lattice disorder and oxygen- containing functional groups, resulting in an uniformly distributed Pt nanoparticle dispersion. In acidic media, the Pt/GNs electrocatalysts showed an excellent catalytic HER performance with a lower η of 25 mV and a Tafel slope of 33 mV dec␀1. By alloying transition metals with Pt can modify the d-band center, thereby optimizing the binding energy of hydrogen adsorp - tion–desorption [88]. For instance, Sun et al. [89] reported Co single atoms modified Pt nanoparticles on nitrogen-doped carbon nanotubes (NCNTs) by atomic layer deposition, as shown in Fig. 8c. The d band centers (Fig. 8d-f) of Pt(111) (␀1.94 eV) and Co SA-modified Pt(111) (␀2.04 eV, ␀2.30 eV) demonstrate that the electronic structure of Pt is influenced by the modification of Co atoms, leading to a weakened hydrogen adsorption strength, resulting in high activity and stability when compared to commercial Pt/C under acidic conditions. Other precious metal-based nanoparticle electrocatalysts. Ru can display a weak water-dissociation capability which limits its activity in alkaline media. Transition-metal hydroxides can be used as the ac- celerators to dissociate H-OH bonds [64]. For example, Ren et al. [90] used a conductive nickel foam to anchor (Fe,Ni)(OH) 2 nanosheet arrays and reduced Ru3ions to Ru nanoparticles to form Ru/(Fe,Ni)(OH) 2/NF (RFNOH). The resulting composite featured a 3D structure with high conductivity and super-hydrophilic surface, allowing for high-cur - rent–density electron transfer of gases on its surface, demonstrating a low η of 13 mV at 10 mA cm␀2. The presence of heteroatoms could activate electrochemical reactions on the surfaces of graphite with the edge sites of these heteroatoms, demonstrating superior electrocatalytic properties [91]. Kim et al. [92] employ edge-selectively nitrogenated graphitic nanoplatelets (Ru-ENG) to anchor Ru nanoparticles. Ru3was mainly adsorbed on the edges, and the strong Ru-N bonds could improve the catalytic activity and structural stability. Ir and Rh are good catalytic HER electrocatalyst due to their catalytic behaviours which are similar to Pt [93]. Schmuki et al. [94] anchored an ultrafine metal Ir nanoparticles to conductive plasma intercalated TiO2 nanotube (H-TNT) arrays, where H-TNT acted as a carrier and partici - pated in the reduction of Ir3. Hydrogenated TiO2 could modulate the electronic structure of Ir, thereby facilitating hydrogen formation with a η value of 63 mV for Ir@TNT at 100 mA cm␀2. A novel high-quality 2D boron nanosheet was designed by ultrasound-assisted liquid-phase exfoliation, and Rh nanoparticles were uniformly anchored on its sur- face, as shown in Fig. 8g. Compared with Pt@B(1 04), the energy barrier of HER can be significantly reduced by Rh complexing with B(104) (Fig. 8h), resulting in a η value of 66 mV and a Tafel slope of 56 mV dec␀1 for Rh NP@BNS [95]. In summary, noble metals-based single atom, nanocluster, and nanoparticle have emerged as a new category of electrocatalysts featured by ultra-small size, high atomic utilization, exposed active sites, unique electronic structure. Table 1 summarizes the HER activities of versatile noble metal electrocatalysts at different loading amounts. The size effect and the metal-substrate interaction can be used to regulate the surface chemistry of the substrate and optimize the metal-substrate anchoring effect to achieve a highly stable electrocatalyst. 3.2. Transition metal compounds based electrocatalysts For non-noble metal-based HER electrocatalysts, significant progress has been made in recent years [20,96 –98]. In particular, transition metal compounds involving transition metal sulfides [99], phosphides [100] , carbides [101] , nitrides [102] and oxides [103] , are widely studied for catalytic HER electrocatalysts in terms of their multi-valence, special electronic structures and physical properties. Transition metals (mo- lybdenum, cobalt, nickel, tungsten, etc.) have a Pt-like electronic structure near the Fermi energy level, appropriate metal hydride bond (M␀H) strength and the ability to activate H* and H2O by adjusting their d electronic structure, making them a promising alternative to noble metals for electrocatalytic HER [104 –107]. Unlike noble metals whose ΔGH* value is close to zero, non-precious metals with moderate ΔGH* face a difficulty to effectively produce a high efficiency. Therefore, several approaches have been proposed to optimize the adsorption of hydrogen by changing the local electronic structure, directly con- structing heterogeneous structures, synthesizing porous or defective nanostructures, and increasing conductivity by complexing with conductive substrate [108] . Table 1 Summary of the HER activities of various precious metal based electrocatalysts. Category Electrocatalysts Support Mass loading Electrolyte (Solution media) η10 (mV) Tafel Slope (mV dec␀1) ΔGH* (eV) Ref Single atom Pt/VG Ar-5 Graphite paper 0.71 µgPt cm␀2 1 M KOH 150 36 ␀0.26 [52] Rh@NG Glassy carbon 8.45 µgRh cm␀2 0.5 M H2SO4 29 22 —— [53] Ru-g-CN —— —— 54 ␀0.08 [40] Pt-HNCNT —— 15 29.1 —— [57] Pt1/Co 1NC —— 4.15 17 —— [60] Ru-g-C 3N4-C-TiO 2