Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 113.2.1. Transition metal disulfides (TMDs) 2D TMDs usually consist of MS2 type, where M is transition metal element (Co, Ni, Fe or Mo) and S is sulfur element. They feature with the intercalated structures of one M atomic layer sandwiched by two S atomic layers (Fig. 9a and b), displaying a continuously tunable band gap [109,110] . TMDs have a wide range of compositions, lattice struc- tures, and distinct electronic structures. The abundant defect layers, tunable electronic structures and diversity of morphologies make tran- sition metal sulfide exhibit improved catalytic HER performance [111] . In TMDs, MoS 2 is the most widely studied electrocatalyst due to its moderate hydrogen adsorption energy (ΔGH*), abundant sulfur edge sites and super stability. Although bulk MoS 2 has negligible electro - catalytic HER activity, theoretical and experimental approaches have demonstrated that the nanosized MoS x can accelerate HER with high efficiency [112] . For example, Huang et al. [114] prepared ultra-small MoS 2 monolayers (~2.76 nm) and aligned them vertically on 3D porous honeycomb-like carbon plates (Fig. 9c and d). The conductivity of 3D porous carbon plates could increase the electron and mass transfer, while the decreased ratio of terminal S22␀and bridging S22␀/apical S2␀ could expose more S-edges. The MoS 2 monolayers demonstrated an outstanding electrocatalytic HER performance by a η value of 126 mV at 10 mA cm␀2. However, the ultrasmall nanosheets are prone to agglomeration. Regarding this, Xu et al. [113] fabricated a 3D ordered structure (MoS 2@C) composed of 1–3 layers of MoS 2 nanocrystals on carbon cloth that could effectively avoid agglomeration. Similarly, bulk CoS x and FeSx with lower catalytic HER activity can be improved by dispersing on a high-surface area carrier. For example, Qiao et al. [115] synthesized a cobalt disulfide-porous carbon foam (CoS 2-CF-X, X  1, 2, 3, and 4) through hydrothermal method. Since porous carbon could provide a good electrical conductivity and CoS 2 was uniformly dispersed on porous carbon, the CoS 2-CF-4 exhibited a η value of 121 mV. The microwave assisted method can preserve the layered structure of MoS 2 and enable homogeneous distribution of NiSx and CoS x on the surface of layer-expanded MoS 2, which substantially enhances the HER performance in alkaline solution [116] . Chemical doping can substantially modify the electronic structure, improve reaction kinetics, optimize binding strength with in- termediates, and tune surface properties of the electrocatalyst for cata- lytic HER performance [117] . Hydrothermal synthesis of transition metal (Ni, Fe, Co)-doped MoS 2 nanosheets was reported by Venkatesh et al. [118] The as-obtained electrocatalyst exhibited a different cata- lytic HER performance in the order of Ni-MoS 2 (240 mV) FCo-MoS 2 (252 mV) FMoS 2 (279 mV) FFe-MoS 2 (315 mV). The low HER activity of Fe-doped MoS 2 arises from the agglomeration of Fe atoms on the surface of MoS 2. Bimetallic doping could give Co/Pd co-doped MoS 2 composites (Fig. 10a) without aggregation or edge enrichment. The doped structures are shown in Fig. 10b-g with significant structural distortions caused by Co and Pd co-doping, while atomic structure deformation is due to the combined effect of spin-polarized (SP) and Jahn-Teller (JT) distortion [119] . Non-metal with high electronegativity could polarize neighbouring atoms, thereby influence the electron density and the d-band center [120] . The addition of O could change the valence and conduction band densities, making the electronic structure of pristine MoS 2 tuned with an improved electrical conductivity [121] . When N was introduced to Ni3S2 nanosheets to form N-Ni 3S2, a η value of 155 mV and a Tafel slope of 113 mV dec␀1 were obtained [122] . Moreover, MoS 2 typically varies between a metastable metallic 1 T phase (trigonal) and a semiconducting 2H (hexagonal) phase with Fig. 9.MX2 str