Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 16materials is another method to improve the quantity of active sites. In this regard, NiCoP-CoP/NF [148] and Ni2P@CoP/NF [149] nanowire heterostructures have emerged recently. Their excellent catalytic HER performance is ascribed to appropriate hydrogen adsorption free energy and the additional active sites provided by the heterostructures. Besides, integrating carbon-based materials with TMPs to form heterogeneous structures can improve the electrocatalyst ’s conductivity, regulate ΔGH*, prevent oxidation, and increase active sites. The carbonization of Ni/CoLDH@ZIF-67 can result in a bifunctional electrocatalyst featured with CoP and Ni2P nanoparticles on porous N-doped carbon polyhedron (CoP/Ni 2P@HPNCP) (Fig. 11d) [150] . The hollow CoP and Ni2P het- erostructures are able to expose active sites, while the synergistic interaction of N-doped carbon layers promotes electron transport and structural stability with negligible current densities after 20 h, showing superior activity (Fig. 11e and f). In addition, graphene oxide (GO) containing rich oxygen groups with strong binding affinity can be combined with ZIFs to construct a new nanocomposite. However, the premix procedure of metal ions with GO usually causes agglomeration. Guo et al. [151] synthesized CoP@NC/ GR nanosandwich heterostructures could solve the agglomeration issue. The enhanced catalytic HER activity was attributed to the graphene interlayer which provides a continuous conductive pathway, therefore accelerating the interface electron transfer. As a result, such as CoP@NC/GR demonstrated a η value of 105 mV and the Tafel slope of 47.5 mV dec␀1. 3.2.3. Transition metal carbides, nitrides, oxides Transition metal carbides (TMCs) have garnered a significant interest owing to their unique Pt-like structure and superior corrosion resistance. In TMCs, the transition metal elements mainly include Mo, Ni and W. The incorporation of carbon atoms can induce lattice constant expan - sion, d band contraction, and closer Fermi energy level [152] . In a pioneering work, Mo2C with a significant catalytic activity was prepared [153] , demonstrating a Tafel slope of about 56 mV dec␀1 in alkaline solution. Since W possesses a d-band structure similar to that of Pt, WC shows a similar electronic density of state with Pt and exhibits outstanding thermal/chemical stability [154] . However, the prepara - tion of metal carbides may need a high temperature and produces un- desirable particle size and low surface areas [155] . Song et al. [156] innovatively constructed a self-supporting integrated electrode by decorating porous WCx nanoparticles on a multilayer stacked carbon cloth (WC x@CC). The porous structure of this catalyst could accelerate the mass transfer and offer more accessible active sites, consequently, exhibiting a small HER overpotential along with a Tafel slope of 79 mV dec␀1. Construction of heterostructure could well accelerate electro - catalytic HER activity. A facile strategy [157] was designed by Mu et al. to fabricate a heterostructure (Ni-Ni 3C/CC) by depositing Ni nano- particles on Ni3C nanosheets on carbon cloth (CC) (Fig. 12a). Benefited from the coupling of Ni and Ni3C (Fig. 12b), the adsorption energies for hydrogen and water are optimized (Fig. 12c), while the introduction of conductive CC facilitates electron transfer and improves reaction ki- netics. Ni-Ni 3C/CC generates a η value of 98 mV and low Tafel slope of 88.5 mV dec␀1, outperforming other documented Ni3C-based electro - catalysts in alkaline medium. Besides, heteroatom doping to mono - metallic carbides can regulate the electrocatalytic HER performance. WC was incorporated into the N-doped carbon shells after Mo doping to form 2D hybrid electrocatalyst (Mo-WC@NCS) (Fig. 12d). The Mo doping result in a reduced d-band center (Fig. 12e), thereby accelerate hydrogen adsorption (Fig. 12f), eventually facilitate HER process with a η value of 221 mV and a Tafel slope of 95 mV dec␀1 [158] Transition metal nitrides (TMNs) possess high electrical conductivity and distinct electronic structure. In addition, the coexistence of three bonds (metal bond, ionic bond, and covalent bond) gives TMNs an excellent corrosion resistance and high adaptability in acidic or alkaline medium [159] . TMNs and TMCs have similar physicochemical properties by virtue of their similar electronic structures [160] . Specif - ically, the contraction of d-band state of metal in TMNs can lead to a similar electronic structure with noble metals. However, compared to noble metals (e.g. Pt) as presented in Fig. 3b, both TMNs and TMCs exhibit unsatisfactory HER performance due to the higher adsorption free energy (ΔGH*) toward hydrogen. Accordingly, the heteroatom doping typically can be used to distort the atom arrangement, redis- tribute the electron density, and thus regulate the performance [159] . As observed, Co4N in the presence of Co-Co and Co-N interactions show the d-band contraction [161] . The vanadium-doped Co4N (V- Co4N) could tailor the d-band center (Fig. 13a) and enable the d-band center far away from the Fermi energy level (Fig. 13b), therefore reducing the adsorption energy of hydrogen, and facilitating hydrogen desorption. A η value of 37 mV and a Tafel slope of 44 mV dec␀1 could be obtained which showed remarkably better activity than Co4N (93 mV, 117 mV dec␀1) at 10 mA cm␀2 [162] . Besides, N and P also have the similar electronic structures. The M␀H bond can be weakened if P with lower electronegativity is doped [163] . In a P-doped Mo2N (P-Mo-N), P provides more reactive sites, while the prepared P-Mo-N has a large number of exposed crystalline surfaces, which can weaken the Mo-H bond. Thereby, P-Mo-N exhibits a significantly low overpotential, indi- cating that the presence of P can promote proton transfer and catalytic HER performance. Heterostructure in TMNs is important in promoting the catalytic HER performance. For example, the 2D porous Mo2N-Co xN with increased surface area and mass transfer capacity was fabricated (Fig. 13c), exhibiting outstanding catalytic HER performance with a η value of 29 mV [107] . As shown in Fig. 13d and e, the charge redistribution occurs in the Mo2N-CoN heterostructure, facilitating the intermediates ’ adsorption and/or desorption, substantially improving the electro - catalytic HER activity. Additionally, the Ni3N/Ni heterostructure elec- trocatalyst loaded onto nickel foam (Ni3N/Ni/NF) was synthesized by Sun et al. [164] , demonstrating a η of 12 mV in alkaline electrolyte. They attributed the high activity to the facilitated electron transfer from the nickel foam and the lower adsorption free energy of hydrogen at the interfacial sites of Ni3N/Ni/NF. Transition metal oxides (TMOs) are extensively employed in cata- lytic HER due to their easy availability, large surface areas and diverse nanostructures [50]. In TMOs, the transition metal elements mainly include Ni, W and Mo. TMOs have more active surface sites to facilitate adsorption due to enormous atomic edges and dangling bonds. How- ever, the inherent low electrical conductivity, TMO-based electro - catalysts in general show insufficient catalytic HER activity. As studied by Luo et al., introducing O vacancies to mesoporous MoO 3 could pro- mote charge transfer and ion conduction, and enhance adsorbed ability towards water molecules [165] . Meanwhile, Zhang et al. [166] syn- thesized self-supported NiO nanorods (NRs) by O vacancy engineering. The O vacancy engineering could optimize the electronic structure and significantly enhance the electronic conduction of NiO NRs, whose η value was 113 mV with a Tafel slope of 100 mV dec␀1 in alkaline medium. Despite being regarded as a candidate to replace Pt for catalytic HER, the performance of WO 3 remains inadequate due to high ΔGH, limited accessible active site, and low electrical conductivity. DFT calculations revealed that O vacancies in WO 3 could introduce gap states and tend to show high conductivity [167] . Therefore, by endowing with desirable ΔGH and high conductivity, the WO 3 nanosheets with large active sur- faces and rich O vacancies displayed a η of 38 mV as well as a low Tafel slope of 38 mV dec␀1 at 10 mA cm␀2. Hybridization of metals with oxides has been found to be able to enhance catalytic HER activity through synergistic effects. Oxides can accelerate H-OH bond breaking and the Volmer step, and metals can help adsorb hydrogen in- termediates and promote Heyrovsky/Tafel step [168] . Ni/NiO-cp with abundant O vacancies also has a large number of metal/oxide interfaces, which was synthesized by an electrochemical deposition method (Fig. 13f). The unique worm-like structure (Fig. 13g) of Ni/NiO-cp and C. Wang et al.
