Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 20network, the electronic structure can be effectively modified and surface defects are created [187,188] . As an example, Fig. 14c illustrates the embedding of N and S into hollow carbon tubes (NSCHCT) by carbon - izing melamine-trithiocyanurim nanorods wrapped with polydopamine. This approach promotes active site formation and enhances mass transfer [189] . The optimized NSCHCT requires a η of 360 mV and a Tafel slope of 179 mV dec␀1 at 10 mA cm␀2 in alkaline medium. Therefore, effective integration of CNTs with heteroatom-doped carbo - naceous materials induce a synergetic effect between them, accerlerat - ing HER [190] . Multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) can facilitate fast elec- tron transport since their inner multilayers are protected from damage [191] . As illustrated in Fig. 14j, an ultrathin N, P co-doped carbon (NPC) is successfully constructed on the surface of MWCNTs by using condensation reaction and acid-base interaction [192] . The doping of heteroatoms can reduce the ΔGH* value (Fig. 14k), enhance H* adsorption, and strengthen electron transfer capacity (Fig. 14l). The as- obtained nanomaterials of the NPC layer and CNTs exhibit abundant active sites and large surface area, therefore contribute to enhanced catalytic HER activity with a η of 167 mV and a Tafel slope of 81 mV⋅dec␀1 in acidic electrolyte. A 3D framework composed of porous N/ S co-doped carbon nanotubes (CNTs) and graphene was synthesized from guanine-sulfate and OCNTs [193] . The close contact or juncture between open nanotubes and few-layer graphene could create hierar - chical networks with numerous channels, large surface area, and exceptional conductivity. 3.3.3. 2D carbon based electrocatalysts Graphene-based electrocatalysts. Graphene can be a metal-free electrocatalyst with satisfactory conductivity, ample inner/interlayer space, large surface area, high stability, and good corrosion resistance [194,195] . Nevertheless, the electrocatalytic activity is inadequate due to the zero-band feature of high purity of prime graphene [196] . Maz˘anek et al. [197] reported that ultrapure graphene showed a greater overpotential than that of reduced graphene made by both chemical and thermal techniques. Although intrinsic graphene is commonly regarded as an inactive HER electrocatalyst due to its electrochemical inertness, chemical doping can optimize its conductivity and wettability as well as produce more active sites and defects to activate its HER performance [195,198] . Besides, the doping of higher electronegativity of N can alter the charge density around carbon atoms in graphene, affecting the chemical adsorption and electron transfer [199] . For example, Li et al. [200] boosted the electronic conductivity by doping N to form N-doped gra- phene, which delivered an enhanced catalytic HER performance and long-term stability. N-doping could create defects or disordered struc- ture on the carbon layer, resulting in additional active sites that were beneficial to catalytic HER activity. Double-defect graphene-based ma- terials (DDGMs) were generated by N doping and KOH etching [201] . This double-defect structure allowed the relatively high ID/IG of G-N5- E6 (Fig. 15b). Defects are presented in the left corner of Fig. 15c (blue circle, Fig. 15d), in which the defects can be used to improve conduc - tivity and promote charge transfer. When employed in acid medium, such DDGMs showed stronger catalytic HER activity than that of the single defect. The DDGMs delivered a η and a Tafel slope of 0.245 V and 141 mV dec␀1 at 10 mA cm␀2, respectively. Additionally, coupling graphene with carbonaceous materials to obtain nanocomplex has been proven to be effective in promoting cat- alytic HER activity owing to more edge defects, high specific surface area as well as improved conductivity [202] . A graphene nano- scrolls@CNTs (GNS@CNTs) electrocatalyst with a caterpillar-like 3D structure was formed by growing CNTs branches on the inner, inter- layer, and surface of graphene nanoscroll [203] . Its abundant hierar - chical pore and conductive internetwork could induce spatial confinement effect and effectively facilitate the mass transport and electron transfer, resulting in an optimized catalytic HER performance. Moreover, producing numerous defects on the carbon nanocomplex can further improve the electrocatalytic HER performance. Indeed, a defect-rich CQDs/graphene hybrid was developed by in-situ sol- vothermal treatment (Fig. 15e) and Raman spectroscopy proves the presence of defects (Fig. 15f). This catalyst showed a η of 194 mV and a Tafel slope of 80 mV dec␀1 at 10 mA cm␀2 [204] . CQDs and small sized graphene sheets have excellent electron acceptor/donor properties, which not only enhance the charge transfer rate but also offer multiple active edges and defect sites. Graphdiyne based electrocatalysts. Graphdiyne (GDY), which con- tains sp- and sp2-hybridized carbon atoms, has a variety of active sites due to its uneven surface charge distribution [205] . GDY has been widely explored as a 2D carbon material in HER electrocatalysis, since each benzene ring is connected to six neighbouring benzene rings in GDY, which allows for vertically aligned arrays of GDY, facilitates electron transport and enhances catalytic HER performance [206,207] . Li et al. [208] synthesized ultrathin GDY nanosheets (UGNS/CF) grown on a 3D carbon fiber network (UGNS/CF) , which showed a superior HER pactivity in both acidic and basic electrolytes. The performance of electrocatalyst could be further improved by introducing non-metallic heteroatoms into GDY framework [209] . Among them, the addition of heteroatoms such as B, N, F, S and P could replace C atoms and thus adjust the local charge distribution, which was considered to be a po- tential method to modify hydrogen adsorption [210] . For instance, F atoms could be doped into GDY to form 3D porous fluorographenediyne nanostructure (FGDY) [205] . The great C-F bonds could bring about charge polarization and increase electron density of active sites of FGDY, contributing to high electron-transfer ability and water adsorption/ desorption. As a result, the HER performance was significantly improved. Heteroatom doping, particularly co-or/and tri-doping, can obviously adjust the electronic structure of nearby carbon, lower the work function of carbon and thereby boost their electrocatalytic performance [211] . For example, Zhao et al. [212] invented 3 types of B and N co-doped GDY into few-layer GDY (BNFLGDY), (B, N, FLGDY, B first), and (N, B, FLGDY, N first). The N, B, FLGDY gave the highest catalytic HER performance (a η of 84 mV, and a Tafel slope of 89 mV dec␀1) because of the synergistic effects from N and B, in which N directly could lower the overpotential, whereas B contributed to the increased current density. Besides, the GDY plane could be activated by various doping of P and S atoms, thus improving HER performance [213,214] . Incorporating a P or S atom into GDY also resulted in altered charge transfer and enhanced electrical conductivity in comparison with the pristine one. Further - more, the models of GDY with P and S doping were found to have extraordinarily high HER activity with near-zero GH* values. Electronic structure regulation can induce electron or spin redistri - bution in sp2 conjugated carbon to create active sites which can be amplified in the subsequent process to improve catalytic HER perfor - mance of carbon-based electrocatalysts [215,216] . For example, Zhang et al. [217] achieved an electronic structure modulation through a chemical etching strategy without introducing foreign elements (Fig. 15g). Introducing sp2-hybridized carbon –oxygen bonds (Csp2-O) into GDY results in the total density of states (TDOS) of O-GDY (Fig. 15h) close to Fermi energy level, and the ΔGH* of O-GDY (Fig. 15i) is lower than that of P-GDY, which can enhance surface electron transfer and charge density of acetylene bonds, and promote catalytic HER kinetics. The as resulted GDY shows a η at 101 mV along with a Tafel slope of 54 mV dec␀1 at 10 mA cm␀2. The presence of acetonlyic bonds in GDY can lead to infinite active sites and increase intrinsic activity when participating in the electro - chemical process [218] . Moreover, the natural cavity structure of GDY can facilitate mass transfer and increase electrochemical active surface area, which is superior to traditional carbon nanomaterials [219] . To strengthen the superior role of acetylenic bond in GDY, Wang et al. [220] changed the acetylene bond ratio and spatial distribution, and synthesized two types of GDY, e.g. TPM-GDY and TPN-GDY. This TPM- C. Wang et al.
