Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 233.3.5. Biomass carbon based electrocatalysts Biomass carbons with diverse natural structures and multi-channel structures can accelerate electron transport efficiency. Meanwhile, the rich functional groups on the surface of biomass can provide a huge space for surface modification [233,234] . Biomass such as cauliflower leaves [235] , human hair [236] , ginkgo leaves [237] , and soybean [238] have been explored as the materials for preparing HER electrocatalysts. As identified, nanofibers with large specific surface area, high porosity, and adjustable structure are suitable for electrocatalysts. Sepiolite nanofibers are 1D natural clay minerals with a unique structure consisting of a central octahedral sheet of magnesium oxide linked to an inverted tetrahedral sheet of silicon oxide. Such materials can give regularly shaped fiber channels with large surface area and rich porosity, favouring HER [239] . For example, Wang et al. [240] suc- cessfully synthesized N-doped porous carbon nanofibers from silk fibroin by KCl activation. Due to the synergetic effect of porous structure and fiber morphology, remarkable catalytic HER performance was ob- tained in acidic conditions. Similarly, the activated porous carbon with abundant porous struc- tures derived from biomass can be synthesized via carbonization and chemical activation, which have also been explored as HER electro - catalyst [241] . As shown in Fig. 17a, treating human hair ashes by KOH activation at high temperature affords graphitized mesoporous activated carbon (AC) nanobundle. It can be seen that the HH-AC-600 nanobundle is amorphous structure which is shown by the high-resolution TEM image (Fig. 17b), while HH-AC-700 shows partially graphitized crystals (Fig. 17c). The obtained samples after higher temperature activation displayed textural porosity with an average dimension of 3.47 nm, and delivered an extremely low η of 16 mV and a Tafel slope of 51 mV dec␀1 at 10 mA cm␀2 in acid medium [236] . On the other hand, naturally occurring organic frameworks in plants can also be transformed into porous carbon through carbonization. The morphology of corn stalks after carbonization is shown in Fig. 17d and e, its naturally ordered channels can promote solvent penetration and ion diffusion during the catalytic HER process [242] . Biomass-derived carbon can be modified by changing its electronic structure through heteroatom doping [234] . Corn stalks are a widely available, clean, and renewable source of biomass, whose combustion process produces layered structure smoke ash. Liu et al. [243] doped the smoke ash with N to prepare porous carbon nanosheets. Due to the extensive surface area and abundant pyridinic/pyrrolic N, a catalytic current density of 10 mA cm␀2 with 195 mV was obtained. Besides, Magnolia liliiflora flower can be calcined with thiourea to yield S and N- rich carbon layers (S/N-CLs) composite for HER (Fig. 17f) [244] . The as- obtained S/N-CLs displays noteworthy performance with a η of 75 mV and a Tafel slope of 73 mV dec␀1 in acid electrolyte, which is due to the N, S functional groups induced defects (Fig. 17g). However, natural N, S heteroatoms exist in biomass can be directly doped into the carbon skeleton as the active sites [235] . For example, Zhou et al. [245] pre- pared a 3D porous structure from natural honey with a small amount of amino acids, which was advantageous for active sites and specific sur- face area, thereby promoting the catalytic HER process. 3.3.6. Non-carbonaceous metal-free electrocatalysts BN is a compound consisting of alternating B and N atoms, which can form h and r or c and w lattices, corresponding to hexagonal and rhombic or cubic and wurtzite lattices, respectively. Among them, the structural similarity between h-BN and graphene with the sp2 hybridi - zation has been extensively studied [246,247] . Boron carbon nitride (BCN) is a layered material with conductive property between conduc - tive graphene and insulating BN. The synergistic effect between het- eroatoms such as B and N can perturb electron distribution and enhance catalytic activity by changing the charge or spin distribution of sp2 carbon. Furthermore, variations in the B, C, and N content can lead to defect formation and further tune the electronic structure [248] . Kumar et al. [248] used a new thermally assisted chemical method to synthesize BCN. The material exhibited a porous graphene-like structure with high conductivity which promoted charge transfer. As identified, B and N can synergistically enhance the electro - catalyst ’s hydrogen adsorption, while the defect sites in the carbon Fig. 18.Typical researches for non-carbonaceous metal-free electrocatalysts. a. Schematic fabrication of BCN@GC sheets. b. TEM of BCN@GCs [250] . c. Schematic colloidal synthesis of P-RPNPs. d. P-RPNP stability measurement [253] . Copyright 2021, ACS, and Copyright 2020, RSC. Reproduced with permission from ref [250][253] C. Wang et al.
