Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 24network acts as active sites. For example, Dong et al. [249] developed a new type of nitrogen-boron –carbon ternary nanosheets (NBCs) by using g-C3N4 as the precursor. In these nanosheets, the molar ratio of N and B contributed to good electronic conductivity and enabled moderate cat- alytic HER activity. The vertical stacking of graphene with h-BN was found to increase the number of interlayer active sites [246] . As dis- played in Fig. 18a, Liu et al. [250] developed carbon-rich graphene capsule-decorated boron –carbon-nitride sheets (BCN@GC) (Fig. 18b) by thermally decomposing the mixed pyridine borane precursor. The increased surface area of the carbon-rich ternary hybrid structure offered more active sites for hydrogen adsorption/desorption in- teractions. Furthermore, this hybrid structure could modify electrical conductivity, leading to superior catalytic HER activity. Phosphorus function as a HER electrocatalyst independently due to its low electronegativity. Among three different types of phosphorus allotropes, red phosphorus (RP) shows great promise as HER electro - catalyst due to its low electronegativity, chemical stability and non-toxic nature [251] . However, phosphorus nanoparticles are hydrophilic, which renders them unstable during the process of water decomposition [252] . To solve this problem, a monodispersed porous RP nanoparticle (P-RPNP) is prepared through a one-pot hot injection method (Fig. 18) [253] . P-RPNPs were found to exhibit a η of 218 mV at 10 mA cm␀2 along with a good durability (Fig. 18d) comparable to carbon-based metal-free materials. RP was then combined with rGO-g-C 3N4 to enhance the electrical conductivity. It was found that an appropriate amount of RP could generate more active sites, and the synergy between the graphitic and pyridinic N of g-C3N4 could improve catalytic HER activity [252] . Biomass-derived carbon electrocatalysts show diverse structures and abundant sources, but their electrocatalytic activity is normally lower than metal-based electrocatalysts. Compared to biomass-based carbon electrocatalysts, nitrogen boride and RP have high chemical and cata- lytic stability, but their catalytic HER activities are still not satisfied. It is crucial to change the electronic structure, expose the active sites, alter the Fermi level, and construct layered porous structures with substantial surface area through doping, vacancy defect engineering, morphology control, and composite to improve HER performance. The updated types of metal-free electrocatalysts and the effects of modification strategies on electrocatalyst performance are listed and compared in Table 3. 4.Summary, challenges and perspectives 4.1. Summary of this review Hydrogen has been identified as the most environmentally friendly and promising energy carrier. Sustainable hydrogen production through water-electrolysis seems to be a prerequisite for a carbon-free hydrogen economy. In order to facilitate the advancement of HER electrocatalysts, Table 3 Summary of HER activities of various metal-free electrocatalysts. Category Electrocatalysts Modification Support Electrolyte (Solution media) η10 (mV) Tafel Slope(mV dec␀1) ΔGH* (eV) Ref C60 C60/SWCNTs Heterostructure Glassy carbon 0.5 M H2SO4 0.1 M KOH 320 380 73.2 120.8 —— [176] 10 % F/BCN 0.5 M H2SO4 222 87 0.03 [177] CDs N-CQDs-17 h Doped Glassy carbon 0.5 M H2SO4 341 126 —— [181] CD/CF-5 Morphology Carbon cloth 280 87 —— [182] CNTs LSZ-FCNT Doped Glassy carbon 0.5 M H2SO4 276 197 —— [185] HST-FCNT 307 157 —— [185] BSA-FCNT 324 161 —— [185] CLG-FCNT 332 108 —— [185] NSCHCT 0.1 M KOH 360 179 0.13 [185] CNT@NPC-900 0.5 M H2SO4 167 81 —— [192] NSG@CNT-2 Rotating-disk electrode 1 M KOH 350 89 —— [193] Graphene NG Doped Glassy carbon 1 M KOH 259 73.2 —— [200] G-N5-E6 Doped/ Vacancy- defective 0.5 M H2SO4 245 141 —— [201] GNS@CNTs Morphology 365 165 —— [203] G-EX-ST Vacancy-defective 1 M KOH 194 80 —— [204] GDY UGNS/CF Morpho