Coordination Chemistry Reviews 514 (2024) 215899 25this paper reviews a brief introduction of the mechanisms as well as elucidates critical electrochemical parameters of overpotentials and Tafel slopes of catalytic HER processes. A comprehensive survey for important types of noble metals, transition metal compounds, and metal-free materials for electrocatalysts of HER during the past five years is presented. In view of Tafel slopes, overpotentials and stability, the intrinsic activities and modification strategies of different types of electrocatalysts are deeply discussed in this paper. 4.2. Challenges and perspectives Despite significant progress in the development and modification of a wide range of electrocatalysts for HER process has been made, there are still challenges in achieving satisfactory activity and stability that would be practical and usable. Several major points can be discussed as follows: (1) Further exploration of size effects and rational design of noble metal electrocatalysts. For noble metals-based electro - catalysts, there are still some issues in terms of (i) how to adjust the metal distribution level at different particle sizes, (ii) how to improve the bonding strength between noble metals and sub- strate through coordination microenvironment regulation, (iii) how to prevent the deterioration and shedding of the active components of the electrocatalyst to improve the stability, and (iv) how to reveal the distribution state of the active sites and the structure–activity relationship. Therefore, it is essential to develop advanced size-controlled strategies such as orientation guided anchoring, defects trapping and space confinement, and explore the coordination or coupling effects between precious metals and substrates to build stable, uniformly dispersed, highly active noble metal catalysts. (2) Further providing guidance on the design and optimization of transition metal electrocatalysts by combining advanced technologies. As for transition metal electrocatalysts, the agglomeration and stability need to be improved for achieving their satisfactory catalytic HER activity. Moreover, the electronic structure and the transformation mode of the active sites are still unclear in terms of different morphological, synergistic and electronic structure characteristics through the construction of nanostructures and modification methods. Hence, developing in- situ characterization strategies such as X-ray absorption spec- troscopy (XAS) to detect the dynamic changes in atomic-scale structure under operating conditions, in-situ synchrotron radia - tion Fourier transform infrared (SR-FTIR) spectroscopy for monitoring the co-evolution of phases during the process, and combination with DFT calculations and experiments to monitor the electrochemical behavior on the surface of electrocatalysts is necessary for understanding the structural transformation of electrocatalysts during modification, the role of reaction in- termediates at active sites and their reaction pathways. (3) Improvement of machine learning assisted approach. In recent years, experiment protocols such as doping, defect con- struction, heterostructure formation, and mechanism investiga - tion have been devoted to fulfilling the requirements of HER electrocatalysts with high activity and stability. The exploration of machine learning assisted approach to predict the effective active centers through metal–metal and metal-carrier inter- configuration of electrocatalysts is now in the early stage and highly desirable. Therefore, developing appropriate procedures and algorithms for machine simulation and theoretical prediction to meet the required robust electrocatalyst would guide the design of the advanced electrocatalysts with high atom utiliza - tion, and enable low electrode overpotentials and good long-term durability as well as chemical/catalytic stability. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing fi