Advanced porous platinum group metal nano-structural electrocatalysts for water electrolysis, fuel cells and metal-air batteries Ruiwen Zhang, Jialin Sun, Yizhe Chen, Qian Shen, Cheng Ding, Shiming Zhang*, Jiujun Zhang* Institute for Sustainable Energy/College of Sciences, Shanghai University, Shanghai 200444, China ARTICLE INFO Keywords: Electrocatalysts Platinum group metal nanostructures Porous ORR/OER/HER/AOR electrocatalysisABSTRACT Platinum-group-metals (PGMs) demonstrate exceptional practical performance in various electrocatalytic re- actions, but their widespread application is hindered by challenges such as high cost, scarce resource, and insufficient electrocatalytic activity and stability. Porous PGM-based nanostructure electrocatalysts have emerged as the future choices in view of the effective improvement in surface area increase, active site exposure, precious metal utilization, and mass transfer acceleration. In this review, a comprehensive overview of porous PGM-based nanostructures has been summarized, including the porosity characteristics, the morphology clas- sifications across zero-, one-, two-, and three-dimensional structures, the construction strategies including soft- template, hard-template, self-template, and surface-induction methods, and the electrocatalytic applications in key electrode processes involving oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), oxygen evolution reaction (OER), hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), and alcohol oxidation reaction (AOR). For in-depth atomic-level insights into these porous nanostructures, some significant challenges and potential perspectives are proposed for the advancement of porous PGM-based nanostructure electrocatalysts. This review aims to offer profound viewpoints on porous PGM-based nanostructures, thereby effectively guiding the development of high-performance electrocatalysts and exploring their electrocatalytic applications in diverse electrochemical energy technologies including water electrolysis, fuel cells, and batteries. 1.Introduction With rapid economic growth and strong energy demand, excessive fossil fuel consumption has raised significant concerns about energy shortages and environmental pollution [1–3]. Therefore, exploring renewable energy and energy storage and conversion technologies has become necessary. Among them, electrochemical energy tech- nologies —including water electrolysis for hydrogen production, fuel cells, and metal-air batteries —have gained recognition as promising, efficient, and practicable options [4–7]. Electrocatalysis plays a pivotal role in their performance across consumer electronics, new energy ve- hicles, and power stations, and so on [8–14]. The key electrocatalytic reactions on the electrodes, such as the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR), oxygen evolution reaction (OER), hydrogen evolution reaction (HER), and alcohol oxidation reaction (AOR), all have insufficient electrode reaction kinetics. For the purpose of speeding up the reaction rates for practicability, using both highly active and stable electrodes/ electrocatalysts is necessary. Platinum-group-metals (PGMs) including ruthenium (Ru), rhodium (Rh), palladium (Pd), osmium (Os), iridium (Ir), and platinum (Pt), serve as highly efficient electrocatalysts, exhibiting excellent performance in various electrocatalytic processes such as ORR, OER, HER, and AOR [15–17]. However, their wide application is hindered by challenges such as high cost, scarcity, and both insufficient activity and stability. Recent advancements have focused on enhancing the performance of PGM- based electrocatalysts through various strategies such as pore-forming, morphology nanostructuring, alloying/doping, and surface modifying [18–20]. In particular, porous PGMs-based nano-structural materials have emerged as promising high-performance electrocatalysts. On the one hand, the unique porosity characteristics can significantly promote the active site exposure, precious metal utilization, and mass transfer acceleration to enhance electrocatalytic activity; on the other hand, the diverse morphology dimensions can obviously facilitate the structure immobility, metal-support interaction enhancement, and ripening/ corrosion reduction to enhance electrochemical stability [21–24]. Also, the incorporation of cheap transition-metals/non-metals can greatly reduce the precious metal content to decrease the material cost, while the modification of surface structures can effectively regulate the *Corresponding authors. E-mail addresses: smzhang@shu.edu.cn (S. Zhang), jiujun.zhang@i.shu.edu.cn (J. Zhang). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Coordination Chemistry Reviews u{�~zkw! s{yo|kr o>!ÐÐÐ1ow�o �to~1m{y2w{m k�o2mm~ https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ccr.2025.216958 Received 19 March 2025; Accepted 2 July 2025  Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 Available  online  7 July 2025 0010-8545/©  2025  Elsevier  B.V. All rights  are reserved,  including  those  for text and data mining,  AI training,  and similar  technologies.
