geometric/electronic property to improve the catalyst performance [25–28]. The design of porous nanostructures not only significantly improves the activity and stability of electrocatalysts but also advances the understanding and knowledge of electrocatalysis. Achieving these objectives requires a comprehensive perception of PGM-based catalysts, encompassing various aspects such as porous feature, morphological structure, and synthetic strategy, positioning them as viable future choices for electrocatalytic applications in a range of electrochemical energy technologies. In this review, a comprehensive overview of porous PGM-based nanostructure electrocatalysts is organized with a focus on the porosity characteristics from different aspects related to morphology classifications, construction strategies, and electrocatalytic applications. Firstly, the porosity characteristics are extracted. Particularly, smaller pores can increase the catalyst ’s electrochemical surface area (ECSA), while larger pores facilitate mass transport. Moreover, the morphology classifications of porous PGM-based nanostructures with different di- mensions (0D, 1D, 2D, 3D) are discussed, highlighting the distinct morphologies and specific roles of each structure. Furthermore, ac- cording to different nano-dimensions, construction strategies are intro- duced, including soft-template methods, hard-template methods, self- template methods, and surface-induction methods. The review empha - sizes the design idea, synthesis solution, and performance optimization approach for producing these nanostructures. Additionally, a detailed discussion is provided on electrocatalysts for ORR, OER, HER, and AOR. Lastly, the challenges and future perspectives are presented to provide valuable guidelines for further development of efficient and stable PGM- based electrocatalysts. 2.Porosity characteristics of porous PGM-based nanostructures In the electrocatalysis process, the transport of reactants from the bulk solution to the catalyst surface and the removal of products from the catalyst surface back into the bulk solution, can be heavily influ- enced by the structural characteristics of the catalyst itself [20]. Compared with nonporous catalysts, porous catalysts have been exten - sively investigated owing to the significantly increased surface area and notably enhanced mass transfer. The pore size distribution, which in- cludes micropores (D2 nm), mesopores (2–50 nm), and macropores (F50 nm), significantly influences the performance of porous PGM- based catalysts. When the total pore volume remains the same, the microporous and mesoporous structures can exhibit larger specific sur- face areas to provide sufficient active sites for the adsorption and acti- vation of reactants, thereby improving catalytic efficiency [29,30]. Moreover, the porous structures of mesopores and macropores can enhance the transport of reactants and products to facilitate mass transfer, therefore enhancing transport efficiency [31]. The balance and design of these pore sizes are crucial in optimizing the catalytic per- formance by improving both the active site exposure and the mass transport processes. For example, Snyder et al. [32] synthesized the nanoporous Ni/Pt alloy nanoparticles, which possessed abundant voids with diameters of about 2 nm. The micro/meso-porous nanoparticle catalyst showed ORR mass activity nearly four times in comparison to the commercial Pt/C catalyst. Also, Wang et al. [33] found that the PtPdFeCoNi high-entropy alloy with a double-gyroid mesostructure kinetically lowered the overall ORR energy barrier. The excellent elec- trochemical performance in ORR electrocatalysis and zinc (Zn) –air battery benefits from the combined advantages of mesoporous structure, optimized high-entropy alloy surface, and ordered intermetallic phase. These results corroborate the great potential of porous PGM-based nanostructure catalysts in enhancing electrocatalytic performance. Furthermore, the synergistic design of porosity with diverse nano- structural dimensionalities plays a pivotal role in optimizing the prop- erties of porous PGM-based nanostructure electrocatalysts. In zero- dimensional nanostructures, the small size of nanoparticles leads to a high density of exposed surface active sites. However, the mass transfer efficiency is low because of the restricted mass transfer path. Moreover, the zero-dimensional nanostructures are prone to particle aggregation and size change, which leads to decreased stability [34]. In contrast, one-dimensional nanostructures exhibit axial mass transfer character - istics and high electrical conductivity. When combined with a porous framework, these structures further optimize the efficient transport of reactants, products, and electrons. Meanwhile, the radial structure in- hibits catalyst aggregation, dissolution, and Ostwald ripening [23]. Two-dimensional nanostructures offer significant surface interface ef- fects. Their porous planar surfaces and exposed edge sites provide extensive active regions with excellent accessibility, effectively pro- moting mass diffusion and improving overall reaction efficiency [35,36]. Three-dimensional porous nanostructures, characterized by their highly open architectures, maximize both internal and external surface accessibility. The presence of interconnected porous channels facilitates rapid molecular diffusion and reactant accessibility, thereby markedly enhancing reaction kinetics [37]. Additionally, the continuous metal frameworks present in two-dimensional and three-dimensional nanostructures contribute to improved mechanical and electro - chemical stability, supporting long-term catalytic stability [38,39]. 3.Morphology classifications As is well-known, the morphology, crystal type, composition, and surface structure of PGMs are crucial in determining their electro - catalytic activity and stability [40]. Considering that catalytic reactions primarily occur at the surface of the PGM catalysts, one effective approach is to reduce precious metal usage, increasing the catalyst ’s surface area by reducing particle size and designing a porous structure [40]. Nanostructured materials, as a subject of nanotechnology, are comprised of submicron or nanoscale units in at least one dimension [41]. Based on their structural characteristics, nanomaterials are clas- sified into zero-dimensional (0D), one-dimensional (1D), two- dimensional (2D), and three-dimensional (3D) categories, where the focus is primarily on their external dimensions, often overlooking the internal structure. These different dimensions constructed by geometric structure engineering can increase available active sites and expose high-activity facets. Also, the design of these structures can suppress undesirable processes such as metal dissolution and Ostwald ripening [42]. Furthermore, the introduction of porous structures into the PGM catalysts can effectively increase the surface area and accelerate the mass transfer of reactants/products [43]. In addition, the combination of alloying and nanostructuring can synergistically improve the elec- trocatalytic performance of PGM-based catalysts [23,44]. 3.1. Zero-dimensional nanostructures The 0D nanoparticles are particularly attractive due to their unique advantages, including high dispersion and excellent activity because of their absorption bandwidth, narrow emission band, symmetry, small size, and high surface energy [45,46]. Fig. 1a illustrates the hollow- structured PtNi nanoparticles with well-controlled size [47]. The porous/hollow nanostructures have the characteristics of high seed utilization, abundant exposed catalytic sites, abundant edges and cor- ners, abundant structural defects in grain boundaries and atomic va- cancies, and abundant pore channels, which can significantly enhance the ORR dynamics and enable the fast and effective transport of re- actants [47–49]. 3.2. One-dimensional nanostructures Compared to bulk metallic materials, 1D nanostructures such as nanostructured tubes/wires/rods have attracted considerable attention for their superior catalytic performance [45,50]. These structures offer a defined axial channel that facilitates efficient electron and mass trans - port, greatly improving conductivity [51]. The anisotropic nature of 1D R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 2
