structures also favors close surface interactions with carbon supports, enhancing metal-support interactions and boosting both activity and stability [52]. Moreover, the longitudinal axis of these structures makes them more resistant to dissolution and aggregation, offering higher durability than conventional nanoparticles under similar conditions [44,53]. From the structural perspective, 1D Pt-based nanocrystals are particularly promising due to their fewer low-coordination sites and surfaces resembling the Pt(111) facet, enhancing ORR activity and longevity. [54]. The exposed facets of these structures are essential in determining the interactions at the interface of the Pt surface and adsorbed molecular species. Among these facets, the Pt(111) facet stands out for its superior ORR activity in perchloric acid solutions due to its ability to reduce the coverage of hydroxyl species, driven by its unique electronic structure [54]. 3.2.1. Nanotubes Recently, a new class of electrocatalysts based on porous Pt or Pt alloy nanotubes have been developed. These nanotubes are typically 3–8 nm in wall thickness, 30–80 nm in tube diameter, and 5–30 μm in length [55]. Pt nanotube catalysts have proven to be significantly more durable than Pt/C during the 0–1.3 V (vs. RHE) potential cycling. This improved durability is due to the elimination of carbon corrosion and Fig. 1.The various porous metal nanostructures with different dimensions. (a) TEM image of hollow structured PtNi/C. Reproduced with permission [47]. Copyright 2018, Elsevier. (b) STEM image of Pt-Ni ultrathin holey nanotube. Reproduced with permission [23]. Copyright 2022, American Chemical Society. (c) TEM image of PtNi bunched nanocages. Reproduced with permission [51]. Copyright 2019, Science. (d) HAADF-STEM image of Pt3Te3Rh2 nanorods. Reproduced with permission [59]. Copyright 2022, Royal Society of Chemistry. (e) TEM image of the prepared porous Pd square nanosheets. Reproduced with permission [61]. Copyright 