reduced aggregation, dissolution, and ripening. The enhanced ORR performance is generally linked to changes in crystal planes, coordina - tion number (CN), and electronic structure of surface Pt atoms. Ding et al. [40] reported the creation of porous Pt-Ni-P nanotube arrays, which provide uninterrupted charge transport pathways, enhancing electrocatalytic activity and carbon monoxide (CO) tolerance for Pt- based alloy catalysts. Another notable example is the synthesis of ul- trathin holey nanotube (UHT)-structured Pt-M (M Ni, Co) catalysts, which successfully met the dual U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) tar- gets of high activity and durability [23]. The Pt-Ni UHT had rough and holey tube walls with a thickness of only 2–3 nm (Fig. 1b). In addition, Yin et al. [56] fabricated PtM (M Co, Ni) mesoporous nanotubes (PtM MNTs) with an average of 100 nm in diameter. These nanotubes featured continuous mesoporous walls with pores around 20 nm, showing excellent electrochemical performance in both ORR and methanol oxidation reactions (MOR). 3.2.2. Nanowires Although hollow nanotubes offer structural advantages, they are prone to collapse over time, resulting in a significant decline in catalytic activity and insufficient stability. In contrast, porous Pt-based alloy nanowires, reinforced by internally connected nanoparticles, provide a more robust framework that mitigates structural degradation [57]. These nanowires feature abundant high-index atomic steps, which significantly enhance electrocatalytic performance. Cao et al. [57] pre- pared porous Pt-Cu nanowires that demonstrated remarkable improve - ments in both ORR activity and durability. Furthermore, Wang et al. [58] reported the porous carbon nanofibers loaded Pt nanowires (PtNW/PCNF) catalyst, where the mesoporous architecture of the car- bon nanofibers played a key role in enhancing PEMFC performance. Also, the nanowires-interlaced three-dimensional assembly could be attractive for electrocatalysis because of the large surface area and high stability [51]. Tian et al. [51] developed 1D bunched Pt-Ni alloy nanocages. As shown in Fig. 1c, the combination of hollow structure and dimensional architecture in the bunched Pt-Ni alloy nanocages makes the efficient and durable electrocatalyst. Typically, Jin et al. [52] engineered a high-performance electrocatalyst by constructing meso - porous Pt@Pt-skin Pt3Ni core-shell framework nanowire. This design featured an ultrathin, anisotropic Pt nanowire core with atomic-scale jagged edges, enveloped by a mesoporous Pt-skin Pt3Ni shell. The unique structural configuration enabled exceptional Pt utilization while significantly enhancing ORR activity and durability, making it a highly efficient catalyst. 3.2.3. Nanorods The synthesized porous Pt-based nanorods have also exhibited excellent catalytic ORR properties, including activity and durability [44,59]. For instance, Bu et al. [59] designed an innovative type of porous Pt-Te-Rh nanorods with one-dimensional building blocks and three-dimensional open surfaces/interfaces, which are highly efficient as cathodic ORR electrocatalysts. These nanorods exhibited a well- defined morphology, with an average diameter of approximately 26.9 ±2.6 nm and a length of 99.0 ±8.0 nm (Fig. 1d), optimizing both catalytic efficiency and structural stability. 3.3. Two-dimensional nanostructures Compared with zero- and one-dimensional nanomaterials, two- dimensional nanostructures offer superior electronic and photonic properties, unique surface-interface effects, and a high contact area with support, all of which can improve catalytic performance [35,60]. Liu et al. [61] introduced Pd nanosheets with an extensively porous archi - tecture, demonstrating superior performance in liquid fuel electro - oxidation and significantly enhanced ORR activity compared to conventional Pd black catalysts. As illustrated in Fig. 1e, these nano- sheets possess a highly porous structure that maximizes the surface-to- volume ratio, thereby greatly boosting electrocatalytic efficiency. Since the successful stripping of graphene in 2004, the ultra-thin 2D materials have attracted great attention, showing the high ratio of active surfaces to improve atomic utilization [62]. In addition, the increased interfacial area between two-dimensional nanostructures and support can achieve good ORR stability, which has become a hot topic in catalysis research [63]. Wang et al. [60] engineered ultrathin porous nanosheets with exceptional catalytic performance, demonstrating high mass activity and stability in methanol and ethanol oxidation reactions. Their remarkable efficiency stems from the synergistic effects of their porous architecture, atomically thin structure, and optimized electronic properties, which collectively enhance reaction kinetics and durability. Chen et al. [64] developed PdCuNiP ultrathin porous nanosheets that exhibited outstanding electrochemical performance for ethanol oxida - tion reaction (EOR) under alkaline conditions. Their ultrathin nature allowed for the formation of a highly distorted structure, effectively enhancing the electronic properties of active metal sites and signifi - cantly improving both catalytic activity and stability. Similarly, Feng et al. [65] reported that ultrathin, highly flexible porous Pt nanosheets demonstrated remarkable improvements in ORR performance. Beyond these advances, metallene —a novel class of two-dimensional materials made of metallic elements at atomic thickness —can also be a promising candidate for electrocatalysis and energy conversion. Metallene struc- tures provide abundant active metal sites, high atomic efficiency, and excellent conductivity, making them particularly advantageous for cat- alytic applications [35,66]. As shown in Fig. 1f, Yu et al. [35] fabricated defect-rich ultrathin porous Pd metallene nanosheets, achieving both high ORR efficiency and long-term stability in alkaline electrolytes. Furthermore, Deng et al. [67] designed two-dimensional Ir metallene with a porous and defective architecture, which facilitated a large sur- face area, enhanced mass transfer, and excellent conductivity, collec - tively leading to significantly improved electrochemical performance. 3.4. Three-dimensional nanostructures The 3D architecture of catalyst surfaces ensures close contact with reactant O2, thereby enhancing electrocatalytic activity. Simulta - neously, the porous skeleton in the 3D structure provides exceptional structural integrity, effectively mitigating collapse and particle agglomeration during catalytic reactions. This robust design greatly enhances the electrocatalyst ’s long-term stability and durability. The proximity of the 3D catalyst surface to the reactant O2 in the skeleton structure can improve the electrocatalytic activity [38,68–72]. As shown in Fig. 1g, Zhang et al. [38] presented PtCu nested skeleton cubes, which exhibited exceptional activity and stability. Sievers et al. [68] developed nano-porous, self-supported Pt-Co oxide networks, featuring a high surface area and delivering exceptional performance in ORR. Lin et al. [69] synthesized PtFe cubic clusters with distinct three-dimensional geometries and porous characteristics (Fig. 1h). These unique struc- tures outperform traditional cubic nanomaterials in ORR catalysis, of- fering improved stability through the interaction of adjacent nanocubes. The heterogeneous element distribution within the structure creates a distinctive local coordination environment, generating abundant active sites and enhancing adsorption and catalytic efficiency. Dutta et al. [70] reported porous Pt nanoflowers that exhibited outstanding electro - catalytic activity and long-term stability, while Qiao et al. [71] demonstrated the remarkable electrocatalytic properties and superior anti-poisoning performance of ternary PtCuRu nanoflowers. Zhang et al. [72] synthesized the porous PtIr alloy nanobowls (Fig. 1i) composed of 2.4 nm ultrafine nanoparticles with a highly open structure and abun - dant internal and external usable surfaces that give them outstanding electrocatalytic properties in HER. 4.Construction strategies As recognized, porous PGM nanostructures can be integral to R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 4
