contemporary energy conversion and storage technologies, owing to their outstanding physicochemical properties and superior catalytic performance [20]. By adjusting their nanostructures across diverse dimensional scales (0D, 1D, 2D, and 3D), these catalysts can markedly enhance electrocatalytic activity and stability. Electrocatalysts with different dimensional nanostructures can be classified according to their construction strategies, including the different methods of soft-template, hard-template method, self-template method, and surface-induction. Moreover, various approaches are commonly employed in the con- struction of porous nanostructures, such as the use of etchants like NaClO [73], HNO 3 [74], Br␀[22], and acetic acid [50]; the double- templates strategy [75–77]; as well as electrochemical processes including electrical replacement [57], atomic diffusion under heat treatment [78], and the Ostwald ripening [79]. The combination of these methods offers significant advantages in preparing porous nano- structured PGM electrocatalysts with diverse morphologies, providing infinite possibilities for improving activity and stability. This section outlines the strategies for constructing porous PGM nanostructures based on different dimensions, summarizing the design principles, syn- thesis approaches, and performance optimization paths, with the aim of offering theoretical insights for the construction of porous nano- structured PGM catalysts. 4.1. Soft-template methods The soft-template method uses amphiphilic molecules, like surfac - tants and block copolymers, as structure-directing agents to guide nanostructure formation. This process typically involves three main stages. Initially, when the concentration of surfactant or block copol - ymer surpasses the critical micelle concentration, molecular self- assembly occurs, resulting in the formation of various structures, such as spherical micelles, columnar micelles, or liquid crystals. Subse - quently, the metal precursor interacts with the soft-template and, under the action of a reducing agent, metal ions are guided to form ordered porous nanostructures. Finally, a mild reagent (typically water or ethanol) is employed to remove the soft-template, thereby yielding the final porous nanostructures [80,81]. Common surfactants serving as soft-templates for porous nano- structures typically include cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB), cetyltrimethylammonium chloride (CTAC), dioctadecyldimethyl ammonium chloride (DODAC), and didodecyldimethylammonium chloride (DDDAC). Specific porous nanostructures are formed through the interaction between strongly charged anionic inorganic substances and these cationic surfactants [81–85]. As shown in Fig. 2a, Chen et al. [82] used the CTAB micelles assembled in ultra-pure water as a soft- template, with ascorbic acid (AA) acting as a reducing agent to facili - tate the formation of a PtCu-alloy@Pt-skin core/shell nanostructure. After template removal with ethanol and water, a distinct three- dimensional porous network structure was obtained, significantly enhancing ORR activity and stability. Similarly, Lv et al. [85] employed dimethylamine borane and boric acid as the reducing agents and boron sources, respectively, to synthesize uniform nanosized Pd-B mesoporous nanospheres (MNSs). By utilizing DODAC as the surfactant template, they successfully fabricated MNSs with a three-dimensional dendritic structure and center-radial pore channels, which contributed to enhanced catalytic performance. The cylindrical mesophase formed by the amphiphilic DODAC micelles constrained the mobility of the Pd precursor, controlling nucleation and growth of the Pd-B alloy and the overall diameter of the Pd-B MNSs from 32 nm to 120 nm. After washing with ethanol and water, these nanostructures demonstrated a large surface area, excellent surface characteristics, and exceptional perfor - mance in the EOR. Moreover, An et al. [84] prepared hollow PdCu nanospheres in an aqueous medium, employing tetrabutylphosphonium bromide (Bu4PBr) as a surfactant. Their catalytic activity was tunable via composition adjustment, with a uniform Pd-Cu distribution. The hollow PdCu/C structure showed promise as an alkaline cathode electrocatalyst. The soft-template synthesis method offers flexibility by utilizing various surfactants, which self-assemble into different micelles based on filling parameters, thereby facilitating the design of tailored nanostructures. As depicted in Fig. 2b, Lv et al. [81] precisely engi- neered the porous structures of multi-component PdCuBP mesoporous nanospheres by utilizing surfactants with distinct amphiphilic proper - ties, such as DODAC, CTAC, and DDDAC, as soft-templates. Through this method, three kinds of mesoporous materials with different porous structures were prepared, including 3D opened/interconnected den- dritic mesopores (dMSs), 1D cylindered mesopores (cMSs), and 0D spherical mesopores (sMSs). Among these, the dMSs with a dendritic mesoporous structure demonstrated superior mass activity, stability, catalytic kinetics, and anti-poisoning ability in EOR tests. Furthermore, Li et al. [83] employed CTAC as the soft-template, combined with Na2PdCl 4 as the electrolyte, and used a conductive gold (Au) substrate as the working electrode for electrodepositing a Pd film at a constant po- tential. After thoroughly removing the surfactant by soaking the film in ethanol, a continuous Pd film with vertically aligned mesochannels was obtained, showing excellent electrocatalytic performance for formic acid oxidation. Block copolymers are special polymers composed of two or more polymer segments with different properties. Compared with simple surfactants, their higher relative molecular mass enables the synthesis of mesoporous nanostructures with larger pore sizes and thicker pore walls [86]. For example, Jiang et al. [87] utilized F127 as a soft template and pore guide to synthesize mesoporous Pt nanospheres (11 nm pores) with tunable shape and size, demonstrating excellent electrochemical per- formance in methanol oxidation and oxygen reduction. Iqbal et al. [88] employed Pluronic P123 as the soft-template to deposit PdCu films with mesoporous structures in a three-electrode system. In water, Pluronic P123 self-assembled into spherical micelles with an average diameter of 13 nm. In this case, the hydrophobic polypropylene oxide (PPO) block stabilized the micelles, while the hydrophilic polyethylene oxide (PEO) block interacted with the metal ion complexes through hydrogen bonding and electrostatic forces, facilitating their solvation and attrac - tion. Due to the difference in deposition potential, the films were elec- trochemically deposited on the substrate. At this stage, the PdCu alloy crystallized around the micelles, causing the spherical micelles to merge into cylindrical structures. This resulted in mesochannels aligned perpendicularly to the substrate. Wang et al. [89] adopted a micelle- assisted replacement method, using polystylene-b-poly (ethylene oxide) (PS-b-PEO) as a pore-forming agent. By dissolving the copolymer in a mixed solution of THF-ethanol-water, spherical micelles were formed with 1,3,5 triisopropylbenzene (TIPBz) as a swelling agent to increase the size of the nucleus with an average pore size of 60 nm. After washing the soft-template with ethanol, a Pd film with a continuous porous structure was synthesized, serving as an ideal electrocatalyst for the electrochemical synthesis of ammonia. The material ’s structure can be precisely controlled by adjusting the ratio of two different soft- templates. For example, Wang et al. [90] employed CTAB as the pri- mary soft-template, with Pluronic F127 serving as an assistant template, to facilitate the self-assembly of stable micelles. After adding K2PtCl 6, metal ions adsorbed on the micelles surface, while Br␀interacted with [PtCl 6]2␀in the solution to form [PtBr 6]2␀to generate (CTA) 2PtBr 6␀ metallomicelles. The lipophilic groups (CTA) in the CTAB was coated on the nanoparticle the surfaces, resulting in quasi-spherical particles with F127 for increasing the pore size. This led to the preparation of well-dispersed mesoporous Pt spheres with a 5:1 mass ratio of CTAB/ F127, which showed excellent electrochemical activity for EOR. Other surfactants have also been utilized to construct various porous nanostructures, further enhancing the tunability of the material ’s morphology and catalytic properties. For example, Xu et al. [91] employed oleylamine as the surfactant, co-reductant, and solvent to synthesize PdIr nanoporous aggregates. In this system, oleylamine acted as a ligand to induce alloy assembling and form three-dimensional nanoporous structures. Lyotropic liquid crystals (LLCs) were formed R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 5
