when a low viscosity diluted surfactant solution penetrated into a confined space, enabling the deposition of mesoporous metal within the narrow regions of the template [87]. Liu et al. [92] reported the fabri- cation of a nanoporous, coral-like Pt nanowire electrode for methanol and ammonia oxidation reactions. The synthesis process, depicted in Fig. 2c, involves the electrodeposition of dense Pt nuclei onto the surface of carbon fiber cloth with phytantriol liquid crystal acting as the tem- plate. This process consisted of in-situ ordered growth of Pt nanowires that contained a large number of pores. Phytantriol in aqueous solution could form a stable inverse hexagonal (HII) liquid crystal with self- assembled aqueous channels. Pt ions diffused along these channels, guiding radial growth from Pt nuclei to form coral-like Pt nanowires. The resulting nanowires exhibited excellent activity and stability in methanol and ammonia oxidation. 4.2. Hard-template methods The hard-template method uses a hard-template with a specific shape and size to guide the formation of porous PGM nanostructures. In this approach, the precursors of PGMs are introduced into the pores of the hard-template and then converted into metals or metal compounds to form the active site of electrocatalysts. This method enables the stable and orderly synthesis of porous PGM nanostructures. However, the use of hard-templates is constrained by limitations in template selection and requires a cumbersome post-processing process to remove the templates [93]. PGM nanostructures with different shapes can be prepared by employing mesoporous silica as a hard-template, which possesses a robust skeleton and high thermal stability. For example, Kibsgaard et al. [93] synthesized an highly extended 3D mesoporous Pt network elec- trocatalyst for ORR using silica as the hard-template. Firstly, they pre- pared the hard-template of silica double gyroid (DG) thin film. Subsequently, Pt was deposited from an H2PtCl 6 aqueous solution into the DG silica through the pulsed electrodeposition. Ultimately, the silica template could be removed through hydrofluoric acid (HF) etching, yielding an extended 3D mesoporous Pt network electrocatalyst with a characteristic DG morphology. Kim et al. [94] utilized 100 nm spherical mesoporous silica nanoparticles as hard-templates to fabricate porous nanostructures. As depicted in Fig. 3a, the silica features a 3D inter- connected cubic Ia3d double gyroid mesostructure. Metal salts impreg - nated into the pores undergo hydrogen reduction and heat treatment, forming self-supported mesostructured Pt-based bimetallic (M Ni, Fe, Co, Cu) nanospheres. The silica template is then removed via HF etching. The final nanostructure catalyst exhibits exceptionally excellent ORR electrocatalysis and extremely high performance in single-cell configurations. Moreover, the soft-template is often employed as a pore-guiding agent to direct the arrangement and deposition of PGM nanostructures. When combined with the hard-template, this strategy can facilitate the construction of the hollow porous PGM nanostructure [95]. For example, Wang et al. [75] utilized SiO2 nanorods hard- template to generate hollow structures and F127 micelles soft- template to direct the surface for forming mesoporous pores. After treatment with HF solution to remove the SiO2 template, the synthesized hollow PtPd nanorods with mesoporous shells were uniform in size, with mesoporous pores of approximately 14.5 nm, exhibiting excellent MOR activity and stability. Similarly, Kani et al. [96] synthesized periodical arrays of dendritic Pt nanospheres by using the hard-template of large mesoporous silica (LP-FDU-12) and a precursor solution containing surfactant octaethylene glycol monohexadecyl ether (C16EO8). KIT-6 molecular sieve, with Ia3d symmetry, is known for its ordered mesoporous structure. Its unique pore and mesoporous network make it an ideal hard-template that can precisely regulate the material morphology and structure for the synthesis of well-defined nano- structures. As shown in Fig. 3b, Wang et al. [97] utilized KIT-6 as a hard- Fig. 3.Illustration of hard-template strategies. (a) Schematic illustration of synthesis process for Meso-PtM, with H and O atoms represented by white and red balls, respectively. Reproduced with permission [94]. Copyright 2016, John Wiley and Sons. (b) Schematic stepwise synthesis of core-shell Meso-Pt@Ag nanocrystals. Reproduced with permission [97]. Copyright 2023, John Wiley and Sons. (c) Synthesis of Pt nanowires with large mesoporous pores. Reproduced with permission [99]. Copyright 2016, Springer Nature. (d) Diagram of the growth mechanism of hollow dendritic Ag/Pt alloy nanoparticles. Reproduced with permission [101]. Copyright 2017, Royal Society of Chemistry. (For interpretation of the references to colour in this figure legend, the reader is referred to the web version of this article.)R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 7
