template to prepare a series of few-layer Ag-coated ordered mesoporous Pt nanocrystals (Meso-Pt@Ag) with varying Ag shell thicknesses. This was accomplished through a method that combines the hard-template KIT-6 with in-situ reduction. Firstly, the uniformly nucleated Pt ions were reduced to form mesoporous Pt@KIT-6. Then, after etching with HF solution, the Meso-Pt nanocrystals were obtained. The subsequent in- situ reduction method filled the Meso-Pt nanocrystals with hydrogen to obtain the core-shell Meso-Pt@PtH x, after which Ag ions were reduced to metal Ag by activated H atom, forming a uniform Ag shell on the Pt core. By controlling the AgNO 3 concentration, the various Meso-Pt@Ag nanocrystals with varying shell thicknesses were prepared. The meso - porous Pt@Ag 2 nanocrystals showed excellent EOR performance. Moreover, Wang et al. [33] also used KIT-6 as a hard-template to syn- thesize the ordered mesoporous high-entropy intermetallics with a double-gyroid mesostructure and a pore size of 4.1 nm, which showed high activity and stability in both alkaline ORR electrocatalysis and single rechargeable Zn-air battery. Anodic aluminum oxide (AAO) nanoporous templates enable cost- effective integration of millions of nanostructures into large-scale ar- rays and can be well controlled in terms of size [98]. When combined with other hard/soft-templates, AAO can be beneficial for forming porous nanostructures with the tailored characteristics. For instance, Zhang et al. [99] found the use of AAO in combination with silica nanospheres to create mesoporous Pt nanowires with large ordered pores. The typical synthesis process is illustrated in Fig. 3c. Silica nanospheres self-assemble in AAO membrane channels, forming an AAO-silica composite. Pt is deposited into voids via chemical reduction, and the AAO and silica are removed with dilute HF, yielding mesoporous Pt nanowires with ordered pores. Pore size is tunable to 15, 30, or 45 nm based on silica diameter. These mesoporous Pt nanowires exhibit strong activity and excellent tolerance to poisoning for MOR. Similarly, Ding et al. [76] developed an AAO-phytantriol dual-template electrodeposi - tion process to synthesize mesoporosity-decorated freestanding Pd nanotube arrays (P-Pd NTA). AAO served as the hard-template for nanotube growth, with gold sputtered to initiate Pd seed nucleation, while phytantriol acted as a soft-template, coating the AAO inner walls as the working electrode. This approach enabled high site density and controlled Pd nanotube electrodeposition using PdCl 2 and HCl as pre- cursors. The self-assembled phytantriol network, with nucleophilic hy- droxyl groups, enhanced electrostatic interactions with PdCl n(n-2)- anions, ensuring uniform and stable deposition. After removing the soft- template, a large number of mesoporous holes were produced, result - ing in P-Pd NTA with excellent electrocatalytic capabilities for formic acid and formate oxidation. Ag and Te nanoparticles or nanowires can be used as the hard- template to prepare unique one-dimensional nanoporous nano- structures. Using Te nanowires as hard-templates, Fu et al. [100] pre- pared PtCu alloy nanotubes (BANTs), featuring a porous surface. After the Te nanowire was removed by dispersing the product in 1 M NaOH, the finally obtained nanotube had a diameter of approximately 20 nm. The resulting PtCu BANTs showed better ORR activity and stability compared to commercial Pt/C catalyst. Sui et al. [101] took spherical Ag nanoparticles as the hard-templates to create hollow spheres via galvanic replacement of Ag with PtCl 62␀. By employing polyethylene glycol hexadecyl ether (Brij58) surfactant micelles as the soft-templates, porous dendrites were formed, as illustrated in Fig. 3d. The prepared hollow dendritic Ag/Pt nanoparticles showed high electrocatalytic performance for MOR. Similarly, Deng et al. [102] employed Ag nano- wires and F127 as dual-templates for fabricating mesoporous H-AgPdPt nanotubes by HNO 3 etching, which showed excellent ORR activity and durability. In addition, NaCl crystals can indeed serve as effective hard- templates for the fabrication of 2D nanoporous materials, offering a straightforward yet efficient approach for producing highly porous structures with enhanced catalytic properties. For example, Fan et al. [103] utilized NaCl crystals as the hard-templates to prepare 2D porous Pt nanosheets. During the synthesis process, H2 was used to reduce PtCl 62␀ions, depositing Pt onto NaCl crystals. Subsequently, the NaCl hard-template was then removed by washing with water, yielding 2D porous Pt nanosheets, showing high ORR activity and stability. 4.3. Self-template methods The self-template method is a straightforward approach for synthe - sizing porous PGMs-based nanostructures, where the template itself is utilized directly to create the porous nanostructures. This strategy offers several advantages over other template methods. One key benefit is the elimination of additional template removal steps, simplifying the syn- thesis steps. Furthermore, since no surfactants are used during the synthesis process, the resulting metal-based nanostructures have clean surfaces, which is crucial for achieving high catalytic performance [41]. By using the self-templates of nanoparticles [104], nanocubes [78], nanowires [57], nanorods [79], and metal-organic framework (MOF) structures [105], specific morphologies of PGM nanostructures can be engineered through various techniques, such as electrical replacement [57], atomic diffusion under heat treatment [42,78], and Ostwald ripening [79]. Moreover, the self-template process enables precise tun- ing of the electronic structure of PGMs, thereby enhancing their catalytic performance. Using nanoparticles and cubes as the self-templates, structures like nanocages and nanobowls with well-defined shapes can be prepared [72,78,104]. For example, Zhang et al. [78]took Pd nanocubes and octahedra as self-templates, coating them with four atomic layers of Pt. Selective Pd etching produced Pt nanocages with subnanometer walls and tunable facets for ORR, offering high activity and durability. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations (Fig. 4a) suggest Pd removal from intermixed Pt shells creates surface vacancies. Also, Zhang et al. [72] synthesized porous Pt3Cu alloyed nanobowls via one-pot hydrothermal method, using N, N-Methylenebisacrylamide (MBAA) as a structure- guiding agent. As shown in Fig. 4b, Pt-MBAA nanospheres acted as self-templates for Pt3Cu nanoparticle deposition, forming asymmetric open-shell nanobowls with stronger robustness for MOR. Yao et al. [104] used AgCl nanoparticles as self-templates for synthesizing hollow porous platinum ‑silver double-shelled nanocages (Pt-Ag DSNCs) with the aid of MBAA. By adjusting the MBAA dosage and Pt/Ag ratio, they tuned the morphology from single-shell to double-shell nanocages, exhibiting superior MOR activity and stability. Using nanowires and nanorods as self-templates allows the growth of porous nanostructures similar to nanowires and nanotubes. For instance, Cao et al. [57] employed Cu nanowires as self-templates to prepare porous PtCu nanowires with varying Pt/Cu ratios through a galvanic replacement reaction with H2PtCl 6 under ultrasound. The PtCu alloy grew inward, consuming the Cu template and forming porous nano- wires. This porous structure, along with appropriate lattice compression and an adjusted electronic structure, could improve ORR activity and durability for the prepared Pt0.5Cu0.5 porous nanowires. Also, Xu et al. [42] took Cu nanowires as the self-template. After Cu nanowires were treated in NaOH solution, CuO nanosheets grew perpendicularly on the surface at high density, providing support for Pt nanoparticles. Subse - quent heat treatment in nitrogen at 400 •C could form the nanotube structures by facilitating the migration of Cu and Pt atoms. The final porous PtCu nanotubes were successfully synthesized by treatment in an HNO 3 solution. Fig. 4c illustrates the preparation process of porous PtCu nanotubes. Three kinds of porous PtCu nanotubes are synthesized by varying the dosage of Pt and polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP), resulting in structures composed of hollow nanospheres, solid alloy, and Pt-rich skin nanoparticles, respectively. The porous PtCu nanotubes comprising alloy nanoparticles and hollow nanospheres showed the best catalytic activities for MOR and ORR, respectively. In another example, after synthesizing Te nanowires by the hydrothermal method, Li et al. [106] further synthesized porous Ir-Te nanowires using these Te nanowires as the self-template, which exhibited excellent OER performance and R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 8
