outstanding stability. They also extended this approach to synthesize Ru-Te, Rh-Te, and Pt-Te nanowires. Wang et al. [79] synthesized 1D Pd porous nanotubes by first growing dimethylglyoxime-Pd(II) complex (DMG-PdII) nanorods, and then reducing them at 200 •C in hydrogen atmosphere. The Pd porous nanotubes were further phosphated to obtain porous palladium phosphide (PdxPy) nanotubes with different phosphide contents. The one-dimensional porous structure was induced by the continual inside-outside Ostwald ripening effect of Pd particles in the reduction process. The resulting Pd3P porous nanotubes showed high electrooxidation activity for formic acid. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) offer a unique structure with customizable porosity and composition, which can be tailored through controlled pyrolysis. This process can allow multiple metal species to coexist in the same framework, thereby improving the electrocatalytic performance of the resulting materials through their synergistic effect [105]. Typically, Li et al. [105] utilized ZIF-67 as the self-template to synthesize the Pd@PdO-Co 3O4 nanocubes. In this approach, Pd (CH 3COO) 2 was diffused into the zero-type cavities of ZIF-67, triggering a nucleation process that led to the formation of metallic Pd nano- particles. Upon pyrolysis, the Pd-encapsulated ZIF-67 was converted into Pd@PdO-Co 3O4 nanocubes. The resulting material exhibited excellent catalytic activity and stability for OER and ORR, owing to the synergy of PdO and the robust hollow structure of interconnected Co3O4 crystallites. 4.4. Surface-induction methods The surface-induction method enables the precise anisotropic growth of PGM-based nanostructures by leveraging the selective in- teractions between specific crystal facets/surfaces of PGMs and mole- cules such as CO [22], NH3 [107], halide ions [107], or specific functional groups (e.g., -COOH) [108]. These interactions induce spatial confinement effects that direct the growth of these characteristic nanostructures. This approach offers several distinct advantages. Firstly, it provides strong morphological controllability. Secondly, the prepa - ration procedures are simplified. When CO is employed as a surface- inducing agent, it often works synergistically with oxidative etching systems (for example, Fe3/O2 [22] or Br␀/O2 [66]), facilitating the formation of porous architectures. Moreover, CO-induced growth is conducive to the generation of ultrathin nanostructures, thus enhancing the atomic utilization efficiency. Significantly, both CO and NH3 possess dual functionality. They not only serve as surface-inducing agents but also function as reducing agents for metal precursors [22,66,107]. This effectively streamlines the synthesis process and highlights the unique potential of surface-induction methods for fabricating porous PGM- based nanostructures. Gas atmospheres, such as carbon monoxide (CO) and ammonia (NH 3), can act as surface-induction agents to precisely control the nano- morphology of metal nanostructures. For instance, Luo et al. [48] confirmed that under an oxygen atmosphere, transition metal oxides could be continuously removed, while the counter-diffusion of metals in N-PtCuCo nanospheres could be driven to form N-Pt 7Cu porous hollow nanospheres. Zhang et al. [109] introduced the confining agent of CO to controllably grow the Pd nanosheets. The confinement effect caused CO to strongly adsorb on Pd(111) facets, restricting growth in the (111) direction and forming ultrathin nanosheets (~1.5 nm). Furthermore, the strong reducing nature of CO also led to perforated Pd nanosheets, featuring crystalline/amorphous heterostructures. Similarly, Pei et al. [22] synthesized the catalyst by using a reducing agent of tungsten hexacarbonyl (W(CO) 6), where the CO generated by in-situ decompo - sition was selectively absorbed on the (111) crystal surface, which could control the nanosheet thickness to less than 10 atomic layers. As shown Fig. 5.Illustra