in Fig. 5a, the presence of O2 and Br␀enables to form the porous structures because of the strong oxidation and etching of the Ni-rich nanosheets. The resulting ultrathin porous PdPtNi nanosheets exhibi - ted high electrocatalytic activity for AOR. In a different approach, Huang et al. [66] synthesized a Fe-doped ultrathin porous Pd metallene (Fe-Pd UPM) with a few 2D atomic metallene layers for ORR. During the synthesis process, the decomposition of Fe2(CO) 9 produced CO, which selectively bonded with Pd atoms to form nanosheets. The oxidation of Fe2to Fe3and the etching of Pd led to form a porous structure. Lu et al. [107] proposed a novel synthetic strategy where an NH3 gas at- mosphere combined with halides (F␀, Cl␀, Br␀) was used to selectively construct porous Pt nanodendrites with different structures, as shown in Fig. 5b. The NH3 molecules facilitated anisotropic growth by coordi - nating with Pt2to produce a new complex to lower the reduction po- tential of the Pt precursor and reduce the reaction temperature. The halides are selectively absorbed on certain low-index facets, allowing for the morphological control of the Pt nanodendrites. The resulting Pt nano dendrites showed improved ORR activity and durability in comparison to commercial Pt/C catalysts. Also, amino compounds with amino and halogen ions were shown to act as surface-limiting agents, further enhancing the morphology control. In addition to gases, some functional groups of certain compounds can also act as surface-induction agents to control the morphology of nanostructures. As shown in Fig. 5c, Wang et al. [110] adopted a simple one-pot co-reduction method, using 3-aminopyrrolidine dihydro - chloride (3-APDH) as a structure-director. The Cl␀and amino groups in 3-APDH worked together to guide the growth of a 3D porous PtAg hollow chain-like network with an adjustable composition. This mate- rial exhibited both remarkable ORR activity and stability. Fan et al. [108] utilized citrate and PVP to synthesize layer-controlled Pt-Ni porous nanobowls. The carboxyl group in sodium citrate selectively adsorbed onto specific crystal facets or positions, promoting the for- mation of nanobowls under intense agitation, while PVP stabilized the assembly of the bowl-shaped nanoparticles. 5.Electrocatalytic applications PGM nanostructures can occupy a core position in electrochemical energy technologies to promote the conversion and storage of clean energy [111,112]. Their superior catalytic activity and stability make them indispensable for critical processes [113], such as ORR, OER, HER, and AOR. Despite their exceptional performance, high prices and resource limitations hinder their practical application. Therefore, structural design —particularly the creation of porous nano- structures —can effectively enhance the performance of PGM-based catalysts while simultaneously reducing material costs [114]. Addi- tionally, bifunctional catalysts in porous PGM nanostructures can facilitate the simultaneous catalysis of multiple reactions, greatly improving catalyst efficiency and broadening their range of applica - tions. This approach enhances performance while offering significant economic and practical benefits [115,116]. This section will focus on the latest research progress in porous nanostructured PGM-based electrocatalysts for various electrocatalytic reactions. The unique porous nanostructures can not only significantly enlarge the catalyst area, exposing more catalytic sites, but also optimize the mass transfer process, improving catalytic efficiency by fine-tuning the pore size and distribution [117]. Furthermore, the construction of porous structures typically leads to the exposure of low-coordinated active atomic sites and promotes the formation of high-index facets and grain boundary (GB) defects, which are structural features that are widely recognized for significantly enhancing the electrocatalytic properties of porous PGM-based nanostructure electrocatalysts [118–120]. Additionally, when combined with alloying strategies, the surface properties of catalysts and their electronic structures can be optimized, leading to significant improvements in catalytic performance [74]. Here, a systematic summary has been provided on various applications of porous nanostructured PGM-based electrocatalysts in key electrochemical reactions. This discussion will span both theoretical and practical aspects, offering valuable insights and guidance for researchers. 5.1. Oxygen reduction reaction ORR is a key process in electrochemical energy technologies like fuel cells and metal ‑oxygen/air batteries. Due to its multi-electron, multi- step nature and sluggish kinetics, it often limits the overall reaction rate. Consequently, it can be crucial to coat the cathode with efficient and durable electrocatalysts to boost ORR kinetics. Pt catalysts are widely regarded for their exceptional activity and are currently the most representative catalysts for ORR [111,121]. Among the PGMs, Pd demonstrates similar performance to Pt, along with superior methanol resistance, positioning it as a promising alternative for ORR electro - catalysis [122]. However, the high cost and limited availability of PGMs pose significant challenges to their broad utilization in catalysis [123]. This has prompted researchers to focus on strategies that reduce the PGM loading while simultaneously enhancing ORR activity [124]. Currently, morphology control has emerged as a key avenue for improving catalytic performance for PGM-based catalysts. With respect to this, extensive studies have focused on developing porous nano- structures across various dimensions, including 0D nanoparticles [45], 1D nanowires [52], nanorods [44], and nanotubes [102], 2D nanosheets [125], and 3D structures such as nanospheres [26], nanocages [78], nano-dendrites [107], nano-frames [126]. These structures integrate the benefits of porous architectures and various dimensionalities, with their synergy enhancing ORR activity and stability. 5.1.1. Single metal for ORR Wang et al. [127] proposed that synthesizing nanostructured Pt with controlled size and shape could significantly enhance catalytic perfor - mance, leading them to prepare dendritic Pt nanoparticles with a high surface area. Since then, various single metal nanostructures have been synthesized to improve the ORR activity and stability. Kibsgaard et al. [93] developed mesostructured Pt thin films, demonstrating superior ORR activity and durability than carbon-supported Pt nanoparticles. This enhancement was attributed to their continuous double gyroid (DG) network that minimized undercoordinated Pt sites. Fan et al. [103] constructed freestanding porous Pt nanosheets, which in an alkaline medium demonstrated a half-wave potential (E1/2) of 0.896 V vs. RHE, outperforming commercial Pt black in ORR activity and stability. Particularly, the highly porous 2D structure exposed more surface atoms, while the interlaced wavy nanowires prevented dissolution, Ostwald ripening, and aggregation during the ORR process. Further - more, highly distorted porous PGM-based nanostructures containing low coordinated atoms, formed as a result of surface distortion effects, can significantly enhance both the ORR activity and stability [65]. For instance, Feng et al. [65] prepared porous Pt nanosheets via the elec- trochemical erosion of PtTe 2 nanosheets. During this process, significant atomic rearrangement occurred at the Pt surface, resulting in a highly distorted structure and improved Pt atom utilization. As depicted in Fig. 6a–c, the dissolution of Te led to the formation of numerous line defects and step structures between adjacent crystalline domains, along with the creation of kink sites at grain boundaries. To further understand the correlation between structure and electronic activity, twisted surface models of electrochemically eroded nanopores, boundaries, and Pt va- cancies (denoted as m1 and m2) were constructed via DFT calculations (Fig. 6d, e). These models revealed that the low-coordination Pt sites (m1) exhibit distinct electron transfer characteristics and can act as electron-depleting centers, thereby facilitating efficient charge transfer with adsorbed oxygen species. This electronic modulation pathway significantly enhances ORR performance, as illustrated in Fig. 6f–i. In parallel, the structure of Pd catalysts, which shares similarities with Pt, has gained attention because of its high resistance to CO R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 11
