alloying PGMs with transition metals can significantly reduce precious metal loading while simultaneously improving catalytic activity. This enhancement occurs due to the alteration of oxygen intermediates binding on metal surfaces, which is influenced by strain and ligand ef- fects [130]. For example, Chen et al. [126] fabricated Pd-Pt hollow framework structures, which exhibited optimal oxygen adsorption en- ergy resulting from both facet and composition effects. The catalyst exhibited an impressive mass activity of 1.86 A mgPt␀1 for ORR. Liu et al. [131] synthesized Au@PdPt porous core-shell nanowires, where Pt’s unsaturated d-orbitals and Pd’s fully occupied d-orbitals coupled. The Au core segregated to the PdPt shell, filling defective sites, while the trimetallic composition accelerated O2 adsorption and activation. Compared to mesoporous Pt nanoparticles and commercial Pt/C cata- lysts, these nanowires exhibited a more positive onset potential (Eonset) and a higher E1/2. Combining porous alloy nanostructures, Pt-skin structures, and high- index surfaces can greatly improve ORR electrocatalysis. The formation of Pt-skin structures generates lattice strain, enhancing catalytic activity and inhibiting the dissolution of surface Pt atoms. This structural modification contributes to both improved activity and stability [52,82,132]. Based on the design of nanostructures with controllable compositions and morphologies, Jin et al. [52] synthesized the meso - porous Pt@Pt-skin Pt3Ni core-shell frame nanowire catalyst, which exhibited excellent ORR performance, achieving a remarkable mass activity of 6.69 A mgPt␀1 and a specific activity of 8.42 mA cm␀2, respectively. Moreover, the catalyst showed excellent durability, main - taining high stability after 50,000 cycles. As shown in Fig. 8a, the 3D open mesoporous structure increases the ECSA and accelerates the transport of reactants, while the Pt-skin prevents subsurface Ni loss during cycling via a place-exchange mechanism. The Pt-skin thickness is typically about 1–1.5 nm (Fig. 8b and c). Compared to the bulk Pt(111) lattice spacing of 2.27 Å, the Pt-skin structure exhibits a compressive strain of approximately 4.9 %. This strain modifies the surface electronic structure, leading to a reduction in oxygen adsorption energy, as illus- trated in Fig. 8d. Consequently, this effect significantly enhances both the ORR activity and stability. In addition, high-index facets (HIFs) feature a greater number of undercoordinated atoms and unsaturated chemical bonds due to their high density of atomic steps, ledges, and kinks, which serve as additional catalytic sites. These high-index sites facilitate enhanced reactant adsorption and promote effective charge transfer, thereby significantly improving the ORR activity [133–135]. Li et al. [136] synthesized PdPtCu ultrathin nanorings with abundant (311) high-index facets, which were rich in rough edges and abundant defects on the inner and outer surfaces. These nanorings demonstrated an ECSA of 92.2 m2 gPGM␀1, resulting in mass and specific activities of 1.97 A mgPGM␀1 and 2.39 mA cm␀2, respectively. The surface local atomic configurations (Fig. 8e) were constructed to perform DFT calculations. The results in Fig. 8f revealed that the charge migrates from Cu atoms to Pd or Pt atoms, with PdPtCu(311) exhibiting the lowest d-band center and the most moderate ΔEO*. This electronic structure effectively weakens the excessive binding of oxygen species on the catalyst surface. Typically, the high-index facets and Cu alloying could enhance ORR activity. 5.2. Oxygen evolution reaction The OER is a key half-reaction in electrochemical systems like water- electrolyzers and metal-air batteries. However, it remains a significant challenge because of its sluggish kinetics and substantial overpotential [137]. As a four-electron redox process, OER demands high catalytic efficiency to become viable for practical applications in energy con- version and storage systems [138]. Currently, iridium oxide (IrO 2) is the best-performing material for OER in acidic environments, but it has a high price and limited reserves, significantly hindering its large-scale application. Ruthenium oxide (RuO 2) offers an alternative with rela- tively higher activity; however, its electrochemical stability remains a critical challenge [139]. Despite progress in developing non-precious metal catalysts, they still lack sufficient activity and stability for prac- tical use [140,141]. Recently, efforts have focused on optimizing OER electrocatalysts ’ activity and stability, including the design of various nanostructures such as 1D porous nanowires [106], 2D porous nano- sheets [139], 3D porous nanocrystals [142], nanospheres [140], nanocages [143], etc. These innovative structures have led to notable breakthroughs, enhancing the efficiency of energy conversion and storage technologies. 5.2.1. Single metal/metal oxide for OER For single metal/metal oxide electrocatalysts used in OER, designing porous structures and optimizing nano-scale dimensions have proven to be highly advantageous. Bao et al. [144] synthesized 3D Ir porous nanohollows (p-NHs), which exhibited an impressive mass activity of 1.75 A mgIr␀1 at 300 mV overpotential under acidic conditions, 6.25 times higher than commercial Ir/C. As an anode for acidic water split- ting, Ir p-NHs achieved 1.50 V at 10 mA cm␀2. Compared to solid Ir nanospheres (NSs), porous Ir p-NHs showed higher surface electron density at Ir sites (Fig. 9a), indicating electronic modulation induced by porosity. DFT calculations (Fig. 9b, c) revealed an optimal OER activity at generalized coordination numbers (GCNs) of 3.0–3.7, highlighting the role of *OOH adsorption in guiding the design of open-structured Ir- based catalysts. Furthermore, increasing grain boundary density is an effective strategy to significantly increase active site density for OER. This is attributed to the looser atomic arrangement at the grain boundaries compared to the interior of the grain, as well as the presence of various defects such as vacancies, dislocations, and deformations, which can further facilitate catalytic activity [145]. During porous structure formation, the introduction of grain boundary defects has an important impact on the modulation of the electronic structure of the materials, which in turn significantly modulates their OER catalytic properties. For example, He et al. [139] proposed a grain boundary engineering strategy by preparing ultrathin porous RuO 2 nanosheets enriched with grain boundaries (GB-RuO 2) to address the long-term stability issues of RuO 2. As shown in Fig. 9d–i, the interplanar dis- tances near the grain boundary was significantly stretched, providing an important way to regulate the electronic structure of RuO 2. Compared with commercial RuO 2 (Com-RuO 2), the GB-RuO 2 maintained the structural stability under high potential conditions, effectively sup- pressing the peroxidation behavior of Ru sites (Fig. 9j–l). Differential electrochemical mass spectrometry (DEMS) analysis further confirmed the inhibitory effect of the lattice oxygen oxidation mechanism (LOM) by grain boundary, thus significantly enhancing the catalytic stability of GB-RuO 2 (Fig. 9m). This catalyst exhibited a low overpotential (η10  187 mV), a small Tafel slope (34.5 mV dec␀1), and stable operation in 0.1 M HClO 4 solution for more than 550 h. 5.2.2. Metal alloy for OER Alloying Ir and Ru with 3d transition metals is a promising strategy for designing new porous OER catalysts, as it tunes the bond strength by modifying their electronic structure. This approach not only optimizes the intrinsic activity of Ir and Ru but also reduces their consumption [106,140]. For instance, Wang et al. [146] synthesized polycrystalline Ni-Ir nanocages, boosting the efficiency of Ir atoms. In acidic solution at a 300 mV overpotential, the Ni2.53Ir catalyst ’s mass activity reached 114.7 mA mgIr␀1, 2.1 times higher than commercial Ir black. Also, Wang et al. [147] developed nanoporous Ir-based alloy nanowires, with IrNi showing the best activity. It achieved an extremely low overpotential, driving 10 mA cm␀2 at 283 mV and high mass activity of 0.732 A mg␀1 at 1.53 V vs. RHE. First-principles calculations (Fig. 10a–d) showed that alloying Ir with 3d transition metals shifts the Ir d-band center nega- tively, weakening intermediate adsorption and enhancing OER activity. This highlights the impact of the 3d transition metal and Ir alloy ligand effect on OER performance. Additionally, the unique porous structure can produce more defects and further enhance the electrocatalysis R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 15
