[142,148]. Pi et al. [142] synthesized 3D porous Ir-Cu nanocrystals (P- IrCu x NCs), where surface Ir-OH species around abundant pores enhanced oxygen attraction. The optimized P-IrCu 1.4NCs catalyst showed a current density of 10 mA cm␀2 at 311 mV overpotential and maintained stability for 10 h in 0.05 M H2SO4 solution. Moreover, Mahmood et al. [149] investigated the relationship between exposed Ir defects in porous Ir-based alloy nanosheets and their electrocatalytic performance. The Ir-rich defects in IrOx@IrCu(111) functioned as elec- tron donors, modulating the local coordination environment and elec- tronic structure of Ir, thereby enhancing OER activity (Fig. 10e). Din et al. [140] also improved activity and stability by Mn doping, creating 3D ternary IrRuMn porous spheres, achieving 10 mA cm␀2 at 260 mV overpotential in acidic conditions, surpassing other Ir-based catalysts.5.3. Hydrogen evolution reaction During water electrolysis, the electrochemical HER at the cathode produces hydrogen gas as a clean fuel via a two-electron transfer. This process reduces protons and water molecules in acidic and alkaline media, respectively [150]. Due to its slow kinetics, efficient catalysts are needed to lower the energy barrier and overpotential [151]. In acidic media, abundant hydrogen ions enhance hydrogen production, facili - tating the reaction [152]. However, in alkaline electrolytes, the absence of intermediates for hydrogen adsorption during the Volmer step limits HER efficiency [153]. Pt can be widely recognized as the most efficient HER catalyst because it is on top of a volcano [152,154]. However, its high cost, scarcity, and low activity in alkaline electrolytes restrict its widespread commercial use. Therefore, there is a pressing need for developing the cost-effective, highly active, and stable catalysts. Ru has Fig. 9.(a) High-resolution XPS Ir 4f spectra of Ir porous nanohollows (p-NHs) and Ir nanospheres (NSs); (b) Surface phase diagram of IrO2 at pH 0