garnered attention as a viable alternative to Pt because its hydrogen binding strength in alkaline media is similar to Pt, and it is more affordable [155]. Palladium (Pd) shares crystal structures and atomic sizes similar to Pt. However, its strong hydrogen affinity is not conducive to the desorption of adsorbed hydrogen. Pd- and Ru-based catalysts with unique structures and excellent properties remain to be explored [156]. Non-precious metal catalysts are potential alternatives but often face acid corrosion and lower stability than Pt-based catalysts. These cata- lysts tend to exhibit higher overpotentials and lower stability [151]. To enhance the HER performance of precious metals, various porous nanostructures, including 1D nanowires [157], 2D nanosheets [116,158,159], as well as 3D nanospheres [160], nanodendrites [161], and nanobowls [162], have been studied for their potential to improve both activity and stability through structural modifications. 5.3.1. Single metal for HER Constructing porous nanostructures to increase surface area is an effective solution to enhance HER activity. For example, Drouet et al. [163] synthesized porous Ru nanomaterials with highly accessible metal surfaces, achieving high activity and durability with a low overpotential of 83 mV at 10 mA cm␀2. Jang et al. [164] fabricated a holey 2D Pt nanosheet catalyst featuring a sandwiched structure (2D-Pt/NiFe layered double hydroxide NiFe-LDH/2D-Pt), which enhanced Pt utili- zation and synergistically accelerated HER kinetics. The NiFe-LDH facilitated water adsorption –dissociation, while Pt optimized hydrogen adsorption –desorption (Fig. 11a–c). Consequently, the catalyst exhibi - ted a mass activity 6.1 times higher than that of commercial 20 % Pt/C. Furthermore, modifying unsaturated sites by tuning the CN enhances H* adsorption and optimizes HER performance. For example, Jin et al. [165] explored the impact of metal CN on the activity of holey 2D Ru nanosheets. DFT calculations (Fig. 11d–g) showed that HER activity in Ru nanosheets depends on surface coordination, with *H adsorption Gibbs free energy (ΔGH) linearly related to the CN (Fig. 11d). The most efficient HER activity occurred on a surface with a metal CN of ~10.2 and two defects (2,2′), which promoted favorable H2 release by 0.53 eV compared to a perfect surface (Fig. 11e). This is due to the higher d-band center (Fig. 11f) and more favorable H–H bond formation on the defected sites (Fig. 11g), supporting the Volmer –Tafel mechanism for Fig. 10.(a) Schematic illustration showing the transformation of crystal structure of Ir at more than 1.20 V vs. RHE; (b) Projected DOS of Ir 5d bands of IrO2 and IrMO x models; (c) Optimized geometry of adsorption structure of O, OH, and OOH intermediates on IrNiO x (110) model; (d) Relationship between the activity (indicated by logarithm of the as-measured current density at 1.55 V vs. RHE) and the binding energies of O, OH, and OOH species. Reproduced with permission [147]. Copyright 2019, American Chemical Society. (e) The calculated electron localization functions (ELFs) for Ir(111), Ir@IrCu(111) and IrOx@IrCu(111) nanosheets. Reproduced with permission [149]. Copyright 2023, John Wiley and Sons.R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 17
