exhibited markedly enhanced HER activity over its body-centered cubic Fe–Pd alloy (bcc Fe–Pd) and commercial Pt/C catalyst. DFT calculations (Fig. 12a–d) revealed that this metastable phase modulates the coordi - nation environment and electronic structure of Pd, weakens the Pd–H interaction, and significantly lowers the energy barrier for water dissociation, thereby promoting HER kinetics in alkaline media. Guo et al. [170] prepared P-doped porous Pt nanodendrites by post- phosphating reaction. Compared to pure Pt nanodendrites, P-doping could enhance reaction kinetics for HER and MOR by facilitating elec- tron transfer from Pt to P (Fig. 12e). During HER (Fig. 12f), P sites preferentially adsorbed hydrogen ions (Volmer step), while neighboring Pt sites, due to partial electron transfer, strongly banded with Hads, enabling efficient molecular hydrogen production in the subsequent Tafel steps. Yao et al. [171] synthesized a nanoporous Pd–Ag surface alloy for HER in different media. Ag incorporation optimized Pd–H binding strength, balancing water dissociation and hydrogen formation, while the bicontinuous skeleton enhanced stability. Chen et al. [155] developed ternary PdCuRu porous nanoplates, where metal electron coupling, a porous structure, and Ru’s water dissociation ability enabled a low overpotential (40.7 mV) and a small Tafel slope (37.5 mV dec␀1) in alkaline solutions, significantly outperforming commercial Pt/C. Mao et al. [159] enhanced the HER performance of porous PdIr bimetallene through N doping, which induced lattice strain to reduce hydrogen binding energy and improved Hadsorption. The porous N-PdIr bimetallene exhibited low overpotentials of 26, 34, and 59 mV in acidic, alkaline, and neutral electrolytes at 10 mA cm␀2, with excellent stability and durability. Additionally, Ren et al. [156] introduced Si into Pd to form nanoporous PdSi hybrids, where Si modified Pd’s electronic structure and adsorption energy while shaping the porous morphology. Pd3Si outperformed Pt and Pd in HER activity in acidic solutions. 5.4. Alcohol oxidation reaction The electrooxidation of methanol and ethanol is crucial for energy conversion, particularly in direct alcohol fuel cells (DAFCs) [172–175]. The MOR involves methanol adsorption and dehydrogenation [104], while EOR follows two pathways: the 4-electron C2 pathway, leading to partial oxidation, and the 12-electron C1 pathway, achieving complete oxidation to CO2 with higher energy output [97]. However, the C1 re- action pathway requires C–C bond cleavage [74], and the AOR can produce CO intermediates that poison catalysts and hinder efficiency [97,104]. Pt is the most effective catalyst for alcohol oxidation, but its cost and scarcity hinder DAFC commercialization. The catalytic per- formance of Pt-based nanostructures depends on surface structure, size, and morphology, driving research into nanostructured Pt materials [104,176]. Moreover, Pd offers a promising alternative due to its CO tolerance and high activity in alkaline media. However, Pd suffers from dissolution issues, limiting its stability, which has driven the exploration of strategies to control the morphology of Pd-based nanomaterials [177,178]. Rh nanoparticles have also shown potential as catalysts for Fig. 12.DFT calculations of fcc Fe–Pd and bcc Fe–Pd: (a) hydrogen free energies; (b) H2O dissociation energies; (c) pDOS; (d) differential charge density. Repro - duced with permission [169]. Copyright 2024, Elsevier. (e) Schematic illustration for atomic-level P-doped effect for boosting the electrocatalytic performance of both HER and MOR; (f) Proposed electrocatalytic mechanisms for HER. Reproduced with permission [170]. Copyright 2022, John Wiley and Sons.R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  C