methanol oxidation due to their more negative peak potential in alkaline media compared to Pt and Pd, suggesting that they could offer a promising solution for MOR [177]. Currently, porous precious metal catalysts in the forms of 1D nanotubes [41,42], nanorods [179], and nanowires [180,181], 2D nanosheets and nanofilms [22,80,182], as well as 3D nanospheres [177], nanocages [183], nanoparticles [184], nanoflowers [185], and nanobowls [186], have been extensively syn- thesized, showing excellent electrocatalytic performance for AOR. 5.4.1. Single metal for AOR The study of noble metal nanostructures in special forms aims to unravel the relationship between their specific surface areas and active sites. This constitutes a key challenge and opportunity for optimizing the efficiency of AOR. Zhang et al. [187] synthesized 3D ordered macro␀/ mesoporous Pt, where the macropore walls endowed it with a larger ECSA, exhibiting electrocatalytic MOR activity superior to that of Pt black. Kang et al. [177] prepared a hollow Rh nanospheres electro - catalyst with a dendritic, porous structure, offering a large surface area and excellent MOR activity. Kong et al. [188] fabricated highly porous, self-supporting Pd bulks, showing potential for direct ethanol fuel cells. 5.4.2. Metal alloy for AOR In Pt- and Pd-based alloy catalysts, ligand, geometric, and ensemble effects are key to optimizing AOR performance [189,190]. Ding et al. [179] synthesized porous Pt-Pd hollow nanorod arrays, where hetero - geneous interfaces and porosity enhanced activity and durability. Yao et al. [104] synthesized hollow porous platinum ‑silver (PtAg) double- shell nanocages and investigated the effects of composition and morphology on MOR. It was proposed that Ag doping could promote the subsequent oxidation of CO during MOR through a bifunctional mech - anism and release more active sites of Pt. The porous double-shell structure allowed for efficient reactant/product transports and helped prevent aggregation or dissolution, leading to a PtAg electrocatalyst with more than twice the specific activity of commercial Pt and improved stability. Zhang et al. [191] constructed the hollow nano- porous Au/Pt core-shell catalysts. The porous hollow structure and Au–Pt synergistically improved activity and stability for alcohol oxida - tion. Alloying noble metals with non-precious metals (e.g., Mn [192], Fe [193], Co [194], Ni [108], Cu [72], Te [195], and Bi [122]) reduces costs, adjusts electronic structures, and creates new active sites. For example, Han et al. [122] prepared PtBi nanorings with low-coordinated atoms and ~4 % tensile strain (ε), exhibiting MOR and EOR mass ac- tivities 9.4 and 8.5 times higher than Pt/C. Their open structure pro- vided abundant active sites, while tensile strain and ligand effects enhanced *CH 2OH adsorption, boosting electrocatalytic activity. As shown in Fig. 13a–c, applying 4 % tensile strain to the PtBi system decreased the Gibbs free energy change of the potential-determining step (ΔGPDS*), increased the d-band center (Fig. 13b), and enhanced the charge transfer between *CH 2OH and Pt (Fig. 13c). Metalloids, such as boron (B), can change the metal-metal lattice spacing and thus alter the catalytic performance. For example, Lv et al. [85] fabricated Pd–B mesoporous nanospheres with 3D-dendritic pore channels, reducing toxic intermediate adsorption and enhancing EOR performance in alkaline solutions. Phosphorus (P) [40], with abundant valence electrons, also boosts AOR electrocatalyst activity and stability by promoting uniform size distribution and increasing Pt0 content. Ternary alloying further augments catalytic activity. In addition, the porous structure can significantly enhance the catalytic activity and selectivity of AOR by exposing high-index facets and introducing grain boundary defects [74,119]. Typically, Fan et al. [74] developed 2D PtRhPb mesoporous nanosheets that exhibited exceptional EOR perfor - mance in alkaline conditions, with high activity (7.8 A mg␀1) and excellent C1 product selectivity (70 % of Faradaic efficiency). This su- perior performance stems from the synergistic effects of the mesoporous, quasi-single-crystalline 2D architecture and the trimetallic composition, which together address kinetic limitations and improve COads tolerance through optimized OHads-mediated pathways (Fig. 13d). Furthermore, the in-situ attenuated total reflection-infrared (ATR-IR) spectroscopy indicated enhanced C–C bond cleavage on the PtRhPb surface, favoring C1 product formation (Fig. 13e). As shown in Fig. 13f, the proposed electrocatalytic mechanism of these PtRhPb nanosheets in promoting EOR electrocatalysis under alkaline conditions is based on the unique features of their metal mesoporous structure. This mesoporous structure creates a confined microenvironment for OHads intermediates, thereby optimizing C–C bond oxidative cleavage. The trimetallic PtRhPb composition effectively separates the chemisorption of two carbons and OHads intermediates, synergically promoting C–C bond cleavage and enhancing EOR electrocatalysis with high activity and selectivity (Fig. 13g). 6.Summary, challenges, and perspectives 6.1. Summary In conclusion, this review aims to explore the performance of PGM- based electrocatalysts in different electrocatalysis fields, focusing on the role of porous structures. PGMs, as indispensable catalyst materials in many electrocatalytic half-reactions, can be designed to produce many unique porous nanostructures. These designs can significantly enhance the catalyst ’s surface area, exposing more active sites and optimizing catalytic efficiency, while also reducing the reliance on expensive metals. More importantly, the porous nanostructure can effectively enhance the mass transfer of the reactants and products, further accel - erating the reaction rates. This paper summarizes the porosity charac - teristics, morphology classifications, construction strategies, and electrocatalytic applications of porous PGM-based nanostructures. Regarding the porous structures, their unique characteristics include high surface areas, abundant high-index facets, excellent electronic/ ionic transfer ability, and good stability relative to solid nanoparticles. Typically, porous nanostructures can be classified into various mor- phologies of 0D, 1D, 2D, and 3D. Furthermore, their construction stra- tegies are discussed, mainly including soft-template methods, hard- template methods, self-template methods, and surface-induction methods. Additionally, the electrocatalytic applications of porous nanostructured PGM-based electrocatalysts in various electrode re- actions, such as ORR, OER, HER, and AOR, are presented, providing robust theoretical foundation and cutting-edge research progress for future advancements in these electrochemical fields. Moreover, beyond the aforementioned half-reactions, porous PGM-based nanostructures are also anticipated to exhibit enhanced activity and stability across a broad spectrum of other electrocatalytic processes. For instance, in the direct ammonia oxidation reaction, these nanostructures efficiently catalyze the conversion of ammonia into nitrogen and water. In the nitrate reduction reaction, they facilitate the effective reduction of ni- trate pollutants to ammonia. During the carbon dioxide (CO 2) reduction reaction, they enable the electrochemical transformation of the green - house gas CO2 into high-value fuels and chemicals. Particularly, compared to the solid nanoparticles, these porous nanostructures with their inherent porosity and nanometer-scale dimension, can be benefi - cial to active-site exposure and precious-metal utilization, significantly optimizing the electrocatalytic kinetics. As a result of their superior performance in the aforementioned half-reactions, porous PGM-based nanostructures have demonstrated substantial promise for application in diverse energy conversion and storage devices, including electro - lyzers, fuel cells, zinc–nitrate batteries, and metal –air batteries. 6.2. Challenges and perspectives The significance of constructing and applying porous PGM-based nanostructures is highly evident. More importantly, the structural modification of PGMs has become a key research focus, with the design and synthesis of porous nanostructures being central to enhancing R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 20
