physical/chemical properties and improving electrocatalytic perfor - mance. Nevertheless, there remain several significant challenges in fabrication and application of porous PGM-based nanostructures, such as: (1) imprecise control over nanostructure morphology, pore archi - tecture, and chemical composition; (2) high cost of precious metals; (3) incontrollable surface structures and contentious electrocatalytic mechanisms; (4) insufficient stability and durability issues of porous nanostructures; (5) bottlenecks in large-scale preparation; (6) diffi- culties in industrial production and gaps from laboratory to market. While porous nanostructured PGM-based electrocatalysts show signifi - cant promise in enhancing catalytic performance, overcoming these challenges will be essential for their successful application in large-scale, practical electrocatalytic processes. To consciously address these chal- lenges, this review provides some potential perspectives for the advancement of porous PGM-based nanostructure electrocatalysts, as follows: (1) Further developing the innovative construction and synthesis strategies to achieve innovative porous nanostructures aimed at significantly enhancing their electrocatalytic performance. Particularly, achieving consistent control over the size, shape, and distribution of pores is a considerable challenge. Variations in pore size and structure can lead to heterogeneous active sites, affecting the overall catalytic performance. Developing reliable, scalable methods to synthesize well-defined porous structures is essential for improving reproducibility and performance. The further large-scale preparation of porous PGM-based electro - catalysts that meet the stringent requirements for high activity and stability in industrial applications remains constrained by critical limitations inherent in current construction strategies. Although template-assisted and surface-induction methods have proven effective in producing porous PGM-based nanostructures with large electrochemical surface areas and rapid mass transfer characteristics, the precise regulation and optimization of pore architectures during synthesis still pose significant technical challenges. Moreover, the processes involved in template removal and porous structure construction are often associated with high energy consumption, complex procedures, and low efficiency. The substantial consumption of raw materials and elevated production costs further hinder the scalability and practical deployment of these electrocatalysts. To overcome these bottlenecks, future efforts should prioritize the development of simple, fast, low-cost, and environmentally friendly strategies for large-scale synthesis. Key directions include: optimizing the fabrication processes of porous nanostructures to reduce energy consumption and procedural complexity, integrating artificial intelligence and automation technologies to achieve precise design and controllable synthesis of porous nanostructures assisted by templating or surface-induction agents, as well as developing novel template removal technologies, such as safer and more sustainable etching agents, to minimize environmental impact and operational hazards. (2) Further strengthening the electrocatalytic mechanisms and advanced characterization techniques to understand the structure-activity relationships. Justifiably, a comprehensive and in-depth analysis of the formation mechanisms of porous nano- structured PGM-based electrocatalysts, coupled with a detailed understanding of their electrocatalytic mechanisms, can offer valuable theoretical insights. Also, the high-resolution in-situ/ operando techniques can provide valuable information about the clear identification of active sites, real-time supervision of reac- tion intermediates, and detailed exploration of electrocatalytic mechanisms. Additionally, the integration of simulations and calculations can provide deeper insights into the structure- activity relationships while leveraging artificial intelligence, machine learning, DeepSeek large language model, and big data analytics to accelerate the discovery and optimization of catalyst materials. (3) Further reducing the use of precious metals to prepare the cost- effective low/non-noble metal catalysts. Despite their enhanced electrocatalytic performance, porous PGM-based electrocatalysts face challenges related to high material costs and complicated synthesis methods. Developing more cost-effective and scalable fabrication techniques, as well as utilizing less expensive mate- rials, are crucial for the widespread adoption of these catalysts in industrial applications. (4) Further tailoring surface properties to maximize the efficiency of electrocatalytic processes. The performance of porous nano- structures is strongly affected by their surface chemistry and electronic structure. Achieving optimal adsorption/desorption kinetics for various reactants and intermediates remains chal- lenging. In specific electrocatalytic reactions, alloying PGMs with transition-metals/non-metals and regulating the surface structure of alloy catalysts, such as introducing high-index crystal facets and increasing grain boundaries, can promote electrocatalytic performance. However, controlling the distribution and stability of dopants within porous structures is difficult, often leading to heterogeneous active sites and inconsistencies in performance. Achieving homogeneous doping at the atomic scale is critical for optimizing catalytic activity. (5) Further enhancing the long-term stability and durability of porous PGM-based electrocatalysts to achieve their successful implementation in practical applications. Although porous nanostructures offer advantages like increased surface area and improved active site exposure, they are often susceptible to instability when subjected to harsh electrochemical environ - ments. Phenomena such as pore collapse, material degradation, and catalyst sintering can result in a progressive loss of catalytic performance. (6) Further gaining an in-depth understanding of the degradation mechanisms of porous nanostructures under complex operating conditions to promote their large-scale application. Porous PGM- based nanostructure electrocatalysts exhibit excellent stability attributable to their nanostructures of diverse dimensions. However, their structural stability still encounters significant challenges when subjected to high current densities and extreme pH conditions. Under such conditions, the dissolution of metal active sites leads to a loss of electrochemically active surface area, while support corrosion results in the collapse of the porous framework and subsequent catalyst detachment. To address these stability issues, future efforts should focus on the rational design of advanced nanostructures, including the construction of novel porous architectures, the engineering of alloyed/core-shell structures, and the development of highly robust support mate- rials. These strategies are expected to significantly enhance the durability and operational stability of porous PGM-based nano- structure electrocatalysts. (7)Further achieving a desirable balance between activity and sta- bility in porous PGM-based nanostructure electrocatalysts to meet the demanding operational requirements of energy con- version and storage devices. First, the dissolution behavior and electrochemical stability of the active metals should be system - atically evaluated using electrochemical techniques, such as cy- clic voltammetry under high-potential conditions. Second, the integration of advanced in situ/operando spectroscopic tech- niques, including X-ray absorption spectroscopy, Raman spec- troscopy, etc., can provide critical insights into structural evolution and degradation mechanisms during the catalytic process. Finally, theoretical simulations and calculations should be utilized to elucidate the reaction pathways and guide the rational design of catalysts with improved activity and stability.R. Zhang et al.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   Coordination  Chemistry  Reviews  543 (2025)  216958 22
