(8) Further deepening the industrial production and application of porous PGM-based nanostructure electrocatalysts to integrate them into practical systems. One of the major challenges in advancing porous PGM-based nanostructures lies in bridging the gap between laboratory-scale research and industrial-scale pro- duction. Although these catalysts show great promise in the lab, their large-scale synthesis, cost-effectiveness, and long-term performance under real-world operating conditions remain sig- nificant obstacles. To expand the scope of their applications within the field of electrocatalysis and increase their practical value, ongoing research is necessary to optimize their synthesis routes, scalability, and stability. The integration of porous PGM- based electrocatalysts into practical systems, such as water elec- trolysis, fuel cells, and metal-air batteries, is essential for the effective and sustainable development of these materials in electrochemical energy technologies. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Acknowledgments This work was supported by the Natural Science Foundation of Shanghai (Grant No. 23ZR1423900) and the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 22272105). Data availability No data was used for the research described in the article. References [1]S. Chu, A. Majumdar, Nature 488 (2012) 294–303. [2]C. Zhang, Y. Yang, X. Liu, M. Mao, K. Li, Q. Li, G. Zhang, C. Wang, The Innovation 4 (2023) 100518 . [3]S.V. Venkatesan, A. Nandy, K. Karan, S.R. Larter, V. Thangadurai, Electrochem. Energy Rev. 5 (2022) 16. [4]X.-W. Lv, W.-W. Tian, Z.-Y. Yuan, Electrochem. Energy Rev. 6 (2023) 23. [5]J. Zhao, H. Liu, X. Li, Electrochem. Energy Rev. 6 (2023) 13. [6]E. Berretti, L. Osmieri, V. Baglio, H.A. Miller, J. Filippi, F. Vizza, M. Santamaria, S. Specchia, C. Santoro, A. Lavac