Dalton Transactions FRONTIER Cite this: DOI: 10.1039/c9dt04793j Received 17th December 2019, Accepted 22nd January 2020 DOI: 10.1039/c9dt04793j rsc.li/daltonAdvanced water splitting electrocatalysts viathe design of multicomponent heterostructures Yafei Cheng,aHongbo Geng*aand Xiaoqing Huang *b The design of multicomponent heterostructures for electrocatalysts is a promising strategy for enhancing electrocatalytic activities. This signi ﬁcant achievement, which is made possible by multicomponent heterostructure engineering, mainly arises from the ability to balance the adsorption and desorption ofintermediates. Herein, the design of multicomponent heterostructures is highlighted by presenting recent achievements in electrocatalysis, focusing on the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evol- ution reaction (OER). Finally, challenges and perspectives relating to multicomponent heterostructureengineering are presented. Introduction Faced with growing problems relating to energy supplies and environmental issues, green and sustainable energy has neverbeen as in demand as it is today. 1Electrochemical catalysis technology, which can supply useful energy in an environmen- tally friendly way, is considered to be the brightest choice foreasing the present awful situation. 2Electrocatalysts are to electrochemical catalysis technology as water is to fish. Looking for cost-e ﬀective and high-performance electrocata- lysts is a long-term goal that promotes progress in the field of electrochemical catalysis technology.3–5Thanks to booming levels of investigations and studies, a number of strategies hasbeen proved to be useful for exploring excellent electrocata- lysts, such as decreasing the size to the nanoscale, controlling the morphology, modifying the surface electronic environ-ment, and chemical doping. 6–8 As one of the oldest rules in catalysis, Sabatier ’s principle suggests that an e ﬃcient electrocatalyst should possess suit- able adsorption energy.9That is to say, the binding energy between intermediates and electrocatalysts should neither be too high nor too low. Unfortunately, no actual electrocatalystspossess the ideal binding energy for intermediates in a given reaction. Therefore, optimizing the binding energy between intermediates and electrocatalysts at a suitable value canenhance the electrocatalytic activity. However, there is also a case that disobeys Sabatier ’s principle. 10In this case, the reac- tion procedures are separated so that they occur at di ﬀerent parts of an electrocatalyst. The part with high binding energytakes charge of the adsorption of intermediates and the part with low binding energy is responsible for the subsequent de- sorption of intermediates. The electrocatalytic activity in this case highly depends on the migration rate of intermediatesfrom the adsorption site to the desorption site. Recently, the design of multicomponent heterostructures for electrocatalysts is considered as a promising way to boost the electrocatalyticactivity due to its ability to balance the adsorption and desorp- tion of intermediates, as shown in Fig. 1. In the case obeying Sabatier ’s principle, di ﬀerent components are chosen to combine together so as to optimize the formation of hetero- structures with suitable binding energy values. For the case disobeying Sabatier ’s principle, the heterostructures formed viathe coupling of a high binding energy component and a low binding energy component can act as “delivery men ”, facil- itating the transfer of intermediates. Consequently, the design Fig. 1 Multicomponent heterostructure engineering can boost electro- catalytic activities viabalancing the adsorption and desorption of intermediates.aSchool of Materials Engineering, Changshu Institute of Technology, Changshu, Jiangsu 215500, China bCollege of Chemistry, Chemical Engineering and Materials Science, Soochow University, Suzhou, Jiangsu 215123, China. E-mail: hxq006@suda.edu.cn This journal is © The Royal Society of Chemistry 2020 Dalton Trans. Published on 27 January 2020. Downloaded by Universite Paris Descartes on 2/14/2020 3:10:10 PM. View Article Online View Journal Dalton Transactions 前沿 引用：DOI:10.1039/c9dt04793j 接收于2019年12月17日，接受于 2020年1月22日 DOI:10.1039/c9dt04793j rsc.li/dalton先进水分解电催化剂通过多组分异质结构设计 叶飞,a耿红波*a和黄晓庆*b 多组分异质结构电催化剂的设计是一种增强电催化活性的有前景的策略。这一重大成果得益于多组分异质结 构工程，主要源于平衡中间体吸附和脱附的能力。在此，通过介绍电催化领域的最新成就，重点突出多组分 异质结构的设计，聚焦于析氢反应（HER）和析氧反应（OER）。最后，介绍了与多组分异质结构工程相关 的挑战和展望。 引言 面对日益增长的能源供应和环境问题，绿色和可持续能源的需求 从未如此之高。1电化学催化技术以环保的方式提供有用能源， 被认为是缓解当前严峻局势的最佳选择。2电催化剂对电化学催 化技术而言，正如水对鱼一样重要。寻找具有成本效益和高性能 的电催化剂是一个长期目标，推动电化学催化技术领域的发展。 3–5得益于蓬勃的研究和探索，已经证明许多策略对于发现优异 电催化剂是有用的，例如将尺寸减小到纳米级、控制形貌、修饰 表面电子环境以及化学掺杂。6–8 作为催化领域中的一项古老规则，萨巴蒂尔原理指出，高 效的电催化剂应具备合适的吸附能。9也就是说，中间体与电 催化剂之间的结合能既不能太高也不能太低。然而，实际上没 有哪种电催化剂能在特定反应中拥有理想的中间体结合能。因 此，将中间体与电催化剂之间的结合能优化到合适值可以提高 电催化活性。但是，也存在违反萨巴蒂尔原理的情况。10在这 种情况下，反应过程被分开，以便它们发生在电催化剂的不同 部位。具有高结合能的部位负责中间体的吸附，而结合能较低的部分则负责中间体的后续 脱附。在这种情况下，电催化活性高度依赖于中间体从吸附位 点迁移到脱附位点的迁移速率。最近，由于其在平衡中间体吸 附和脱附方面的能力，将多组分异质结构设计用于电催化剂被 认为是一种很有前景的方法来提高电催化活性，如图1所示。在 遵循萨巴蒂尔原理的情况下，选择不同的组分组合在一起，以 优化具有合适结合能值的异质结构的形成。对于违反萨巴蒂尔 原理的情况，通过高结合能组分和低结合能组分的耦合形成的 异质结构可以作为“送货员”，促进中间体的转移。因此，多 组分异质结构的设计 图1多组分异质结构工程可以通过平衡中间体的吸附和解吸来提高电催化活 性。a材料工程学院，常熟理工学院，江苏常熟215500，中国b化学化工与材料 科学学院，苏州大学，江苏苏州215123，中国。E‑mail: hxq006@suda.edu.cn 本期刊受©英国皇家化学学会2020年保护 Dalton交易 Published on 27 January 2020. Downloaded byUniversite Paris Descartes on2/14/2020 3:10:10 PM. 在线查看文章 查看期刊
